      "results": {
        "code": "SUCCESS",
        "msg": [
          {
            "type": "TABLE",
            "data": "Database ID\tNRW File Identifier\tcreatedate\tTitle\tResource Identifier\tNRW Internal Location\tNRW Internal Custodian\tAbstractEN\tRecord in Public view\n17106\tEXT_DS119265\t2024-05-28T12:11:56.142Z\tWelsh Government: Shellfish Water Protected Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323635\tX:\\EU Directives\\Shellfish Water Directive\\Shellfish Water Protected Areas 2013.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Shellfish Water Directive\\Shellfish Waters Pre 2013.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Shellfish Water Directive\\Shellfish Water Protected Areas 2022.lyr\tMark Charlesworth, SUW Senior Advisor Wales, WFD and Marine Team, NRW (mark.charlesworth@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)\tThe Water Framework Directive requires the specification of protected areas for the protection of economically significant species. This dataset shows the location of coastal and brackish waters that have been designated as Shellfish Waters under the EC Shellfish Waters Directive (2006/113/EEC). This dataset is wholly owned by Welsh Government but has been made available to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) who are required to make it open on behalf of Welsh Government.\tPublic record\n18178\tNRW_DS116233\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.601Z\tFlood Defences without Standardised Attributes (archived data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323333\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Defences without attributes.lyr Stored in AMX but made available On the Y drive\tMark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group, FIRM\tThis dataset has been superseded by the dataset \"Flood Defence Structures\" (NRW_DS125266).  This dataset shows flood defences that have been built to protect against flooding from rivers and the sea. The defences shown provide different levels of flood protection and this is recognised in the risk classification shown in the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map. Flood defences that are not yet shown and the areas that benefit from them will be gradually added.   Flood defences do not remove the risk of flooding however and can be overtopped or fail in extreme weather conditions.\tPublic record\n17231\tEXT_DS122166\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.453Z\tDŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water: South Wales Fire Hydrants (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313636\tx:\\Designations\\Emergency Services\\South Wales Fire Hydrants.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset has been supplied to Natural Resources Wales via Welsh Government and is owned by Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water. This dataset details the location of fire hydrants around South Wales.\tPublic record\n17632\tNRW_DS109736\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.056Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373336\t[A] Paper reports for each survey available from CCW library (see CCW library catalogue/related titles field for more information). Gower 2001 survey available as hardcopy in CCW heathland ecologist\u0027s office.  [B] Species data held in Recorder 6 - surveys tagged as Phase 2 Lowland Heathland  [C] GIS data held on X Drive: X-Layer_Files\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_heathland - see GIS metadata in the contains field [D] H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\SherryJ\\Lheathland\\Survey\\LHS original quadrat data  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tIn the absence of a Heathland Ecologist Liz Howe has suggested that Peter Jones is the best contact; Peter Jones, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) is the basis used for the selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and is used to define Annex 1 habitats for the selection of Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive (EU Directive 92/43).  This project comprises of detailed data related to the Phase 2 plant community survey for Lowland Heath across Wales. The project covers dry and maritime heath covered by the heathland section of the NVC and wet heath covered by the section of the NVC. Two non-NVC communities the calcareous grass and heath and shingle heath are also described.  This project had three main purposes: - To provide an overview of the Welsh lowland heathland resource allowing local conservation programs particularly local Biodiversity Action Plans (BAP) partnerships to assess and interpret their resource in a Welsh context.  - To identify heathland types which are underrepresented in the SSSIs series.  - To identified gaps in knowledge and highlight areas for further study.\tPublic record\n19097\tNRW_DS125467\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.631Z\tNVC Upland Surveys Ceredigion and Powys 1991-1993 (paper records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343637\tOffsite-archive box no. CCW/SERNB/51.\tdata custodian not known; contact Data Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tRaw survey data field maps \u0026amp; notes quadrat cards   (potentially ad hoc surveys?)  The various papper record files cover: Claerddu Elendydd SSSI (Cwmanddwr Common LLuest Abercarnon Glascwm \u0026amp; Gladesty Hill SSi Gamrhiw Pen y Garreg Ty Mawr Great Gors Upper Cwm Pastures Nantybeddau Nant yt Henfran SO67753 Rogesrstone Pasture SO 07123 Dolfawr Pastures Claerwen Traedrhiw Drain Ty Mawr\tPrivate record\n17441\tNRW_DS87129\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.207Z\tMilford Haven and Daugleddau Estuary Tidally Exposed Seabed and Shores (2002)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313239\t[A] Data is archived under Project No. 79, media No\u0027s 223 and 224 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to [B] Marine Recorder survey: MRCCW80000000003\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW.\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. In addition to this the Habitats Directive (1992) requires the establishment of a European network of important high-quality conservation sites Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). The Habitats Directive identifies Reefs as a major category of habitats that should be considered for inclusion in SACs. Tidal rapids not only forms part of the reef environments classification but is also highlighted as an important site under the UKBAP habitats list. Tidal rapids is used to describe a broad range of high energy environments including deep tidal streams and tide-swept habitats. JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) defined rapids as ‘strong tidal streams resulting from a constriction in the coastline at the entrance to or within the length of an enclosed body of water such as a sea loch. Depth is usually shallower than five metres. The data collected during this survey is being used to help define the conservation objectives for the cSAC reef features in Wales and to inform management of this feature. One of the actions listed in the Habitat Action Plan for tidal rapids is to complete survey and recording of the extent quality and composition of the habitats and communities of tidal rapids.\tPublic record\n19115\tNRW_DS125458\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.128Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Regulations Cycle 3 Classification\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343538\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment\tThis dataset shows the boundaries and baseline classification undertaken in 2021 for The Water Framework Directive (WFD) Cycle 3. It is an update in classification for all water bodies in Wales following on from the cycle 1 and cycle 2 classification rounds.    The Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England \u0026amp; Wales) Regulations 2017 (referred to as the WFD Regulations) provide a framework for managing the water environment in England and Wales. The Directive aims for “good status” of all ground and surface water (rivers lakes transitional water and coastal waters). Under the WFD Regulations a river basin management plan must be prepared for each river basin district. The plan includes environmental objectives and a summary of the programmes of measures required to achieve those objectives.\tPublic record\n17476\tNRW_DS99344\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.118Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Inventory\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939333434\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_heathland Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\phase_2\\lowland_heathland\tInternal Custodian:  Jane Stevens - TEG, NRW (Used to be Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.)  Metadata contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a GIS dataset consisting of a inventory of the phase 2 lowland Heathland inventory.  National Vegetation Classification (NVC) is the basis used for the selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and is used to define Annex 1 habitats for the selection of Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive (EU Directive 92/43).  This project comprises of detailed data related to the Phase 2 plant community survey for Lowland Heath across Wales. The project covers dry and maritime heath covered by the heathland section of the NVC and wet heath covered by the section of the NVC. Two non-NVC communities the calcareous grass and heath and shingle heath are also described.  This project had three main purposes: - To provide an overview of the Welsh lowland heathland resource allowing local conservation programs particularly local Biodiversity Action Plans (BAP) partnerships to assess and interpret their resource in a Welsh context.  - To identify heathland types which are underrepresented in the SSSIs series.  - To identified gaps in knowledge and highlight areas for further study.\tPublic record\n17879\tNRW_DS111296\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.884Z\tPhotographic collection from Yr Wyddfa Environmental Change Network (ECN) site\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323936\tDigital images are kept on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff, whilst the slides are housed in filing cabinets in NRW\u0027s ECN team office.\tInternal Custodian: Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW. Victoria Bowmaker, Environmental Change Network Data Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW / Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tA collection of slides scans and digital photographs taken at the Environmental Change Network sites on Snowdon. The purpose of this data capture was to keep a photographic record of the site in order to study changes over time.\tPublic record\n18525\tNRW_DS121612\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.530Z\tStatutory Plant Health Notices\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363132\tX:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Statutory Plant Health Notices.lyr   lyr   (this one is for people who just view the data – like a normal GIS user)  X:\\Editing\\Statutory Plant Health Notices EDIT.lyr   (this one is for people who can have access and edit the sites a bit like a working area… you cannot view the data in the editing folder if you cannot edit it (only the GIS team can view this data))\tInternal Data Custodian: Callum Stone       Data contains:  Personal Data Legal concerns This data contains information that may be deemed as personal sensitive information, ie legally binding Statutory Notices.\tPhytophthora ramorum (P. ramorum) is a fungus-like pathogen called a water mould. It causes extensive damage and death to a wide range of trees and other plants. This spatial data is owned by Natural Resources Wales and shows the spatial extent of sites served with a Statutory Notice (destruction or containment) under the Plant Health (Forestry) Order 2005.\tPublic record\n17513\tNRW_DS101867\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.160Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Meteorological Records Wooltack Point\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383637\tData also available for download through the following link; http://environmental-change.ccw.gov.uk/?page_id\u003d513 Data is held locally at the SKomer Office (DELL 709) A data backup is held one the Skomer mnr West Region drive/shared docs/data/weather\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Weather data collected from the Coastguard lookout station on Wooltack point Pembrokeshire from May 1993 onwards. The continuous weather data can be used for monitoring climate change and factors effecting species within the MNR.\tPublic record\n19019\tNRW_DS125199\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.481Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313939\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Coastal Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2 clause 7 of the WFD defines coastal waterbodies as \u0027...a surface water on the landward side of a line every point of which is at a distance of one nautical mile on the seaward side from the nearest point of the baseline from which the breadth of territorial waters is measured extending where appropriate up to the outer limit of transitional waters\u0027.   Coastal waters were defined by territorial waters 1 nautical mile from the Mean High Water coastline taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2. The delineation between coastal and estuarine waters was delineated by the Environment Agency defined transitional waterbodies. Waterbodies are also split and assigned to River Basin Districts. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics risk classification and proposed objectives.   This dataset covers the layer for Cycle 3 of the Water framework Directive.\tPublic record\n19667\tNRW_DS161312\t2024-10-17T14:31:22.261Z\tNRW Fisheries Enforcement Teams\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333132\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Fisheries Enforcement Teams.lyr\tHolly Dyke, Waste and Enforcement Team\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Enforcement Team boundaries. The purpose of this data is to capture and define boundaries to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities. It will be used to ensure enquiries and incidents etc are passed to the correct team.\tPrivate record\n17546\tNRW_DS101980\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.141Z\tIntertidal Marine Mud and Muddy Gravel Characterisation in the Menai Strait\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393830\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 3, Media No 100\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSeveral rich marine mud and muddy gravel habitats were identified within the Menai Strait by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Marine Intertidal Phase 1 survey team and a sampling programme initiated in order to better characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats.  Marine mud and muddy gravel habitats are an important feature of several candidate Special Area of Conservation (SACs) including the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay cSACs. Species lists for these habitats within the Menai Strait have not been compiled since 1982. This report details sampling work and laboratory analysis undertaken in order to characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats. Several rich marine mud and muddy gravel habitats were identified within the Menai Strait by the CCW Marine Intertidal Phase 1 survey team and a sampling programme initiated in order to better characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats.\tPublic record\n18543\tNRW_DS121726\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.232Z\tWINS Water Quality: Constraints for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373236\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Constraints for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. What the map shows: This map shows constraints to intervention types for reduction of excess nutrients from diffuse sources entering the water environment. The number of constraints is combined across all land management intervention types so an area with one constraint for one type of action and two constraints for another type of action will show as having three constraints.\tPublic record\n17806\tNRW_DS111134\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.511Z\tMonitoring Tide-Swept Sponge Communities in the Menai Strait, Anglesey and Warrior, Pembrokeshire\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313334\tElectronic data archived under project 217, media number 801.  A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG drive. Streamed video footage is held on the imaging drive.  Mini DV tapes are stored in the fireproof safe.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSponges are an important component of benthic communities in temperate waters and can become dominant in suitable conditions. Monitoring takes place at 2 sites within the Menai Strait; Coleg Normal and Nelson\u0027s Column and at one site in the Milford Haven (Warrior) Pembrokeshire. Sponge communities in the Menai Strait are dominated by large relatively fast-growing species such as Halichondria panicea Esperiopsis fucorum and Haliclona oculata which thrive in rapid flowing turbid waters. The purpose of this data capture was to record the abundance and \u0027luxuiousness\u0027 of the sponge communities to in order to give an indication of the favourable conservation status of benthic communities within the Special Areas of Conservation (SAC\u0027s).\tPublic record\n17620\tNRW_DS109835\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.059Z\tTremadog Bay Subtidal Macrobenthic Study\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383335\t[A]  The data is archived in Project no. 143 (media 469). [B]  Species data is also held in Marine Recorder.   [C]  Specimens are stored at EMU.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Habitats Biologist, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Area of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the \u0027Favourable Conservation Status\u0027 (FCS) of habitats and species as defined in the Directive. In the case of SACs the features to which FCS applies are the habitats and/or species listed in Annex I and Annex II of the Habitat Directive for which the individual site has been selected. Infaunal monitoring work is required to provide management feedback and enable reporting on the FCS of infaunal aspects of SACs Special Protection Areas (SPAs: Birds Directive) and Special Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSI).  The aim of this data capture was to gather repeatable data to provide reference conditions and to support the identification of the two characteristics of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau large shallow inlet and bays feature.  Tremadog Bay a large shallow Inlet and Bay within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.  Two important characteristics of this feature have been identified.  These are:  1. The variety of subtidal sediment communities within the large shallow inlet and bay 2. The species richness and diversity of the sublittoral mixed sediment south west of Pwllheli\tPublic record\n17741\tNRW_DS110467\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.655Z\tMorfa Gwyllt Lagoon Soft Sediment Infauna Survey (2006 - 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343637\tAll data is archived under project no. 241 and  media no 765.   A working copy is held on S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Lagoons\\Morfa Gwyllt\tInternal Custodians:  Marine Technical Specialist, MFSG Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tCoastal lagoons are areas of shallow coastal salt water wholly or partially separated from the sea by sandbanks shingle or less frequently rocks. Lagoons show a wide range of geographical and ecological variation; five main sub-types have been identified in the UK on the basis of their physiography as meeting the definition of the Annex I habitat type. Annex 1 habitat types are those habitats which are listed under the EC Habitats Directive and form part of Special Area of Conservation (SAC) interest features. Morfa Gwyllt lagoon in classed as one of these features within the Pen Llyn a`r Sarnau/ Lleyn Peninsula and the Sarnau SAC. Morfa Gwyllt lagoon is a small percolation lagoon that consists of a depression in a shingle bar across the mouth of the Afon Dysynni in mid Wales. This is the only example of a percolation lagoon in Wales. The substrate is a mosaic of medium sand over/amongst shingle with muddier patches within the deeper pockets and scattered larger pebbles. Three lagoonal specialists have been found at this site: the amphipod Sphaeroma hookeri the bryozoan Conopeum seurati and the alga Chaetomorpha linum. This dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Morfa Gwyllt lagoon collected to aid the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex 1 feature.\tPublic record\n18880\tNRW_DS124692\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.540Z\tNational Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363932\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2018.lyr  x:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2018 from OSMM.lyr   Spreadsheet: 2018s0011 - FRAW Project Report - Appendix T03 - NRD2018_Exclusions_v2.xlsx (also knows as AddressBase_products_classification_scheme_nrd2018_AEP7.xlsx), this spreadsheet is required for anyone who wants tocompare the 2018 NDR with previous versions of the data\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh / GIS and Spatial Data Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors typically residential and commercial property intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management. It is a spatial dataset produced from Ordnance Survey (OS) AddressBase® Premium (ABP) and OS MasterMap® (MM) data with information on property type floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centre’s Multi-Coloured Manual (MCM) codes attributed.   The version of the NRD described here is the NRD 2018 created as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) project. The NRD 2018 builds on and supersedes the previous NRD 2014 (and NRD 2011 before this). Production of the dataset was streamlined as part of the FRAW project whilst maintaining conceptual consistency. Several additional data attributes have been added to facilitate flood damage calculations in the impacts analysis element of the FRAW project. Consequently the NRD 2018 comprises two parts – a base NRD which has a very similar structure to NRD 2014 and an additional attribute table with extra information useful for impacts analysis and economic damage calculations.  Several standard NRD and non-standard attributes have been updated to support the national assessment of flood risk.  Some aspects of this are bespoke for Wales and may differ to the NRD datasets used in England.  Further details of this are included in the project report but the main changes are:   - MCM code – addition of a caravan and bungalow code  - Settlement name – name of settlement to aid understanding  - Ground level – based on analysis from from the DTM  - Property value – creation of a residential property value   - Property classification – attribute based on MCM codes and JBA analysis   - Population – required for basic reporting of flood metrics and for Danger score calculation within NRW’s Communities at Risk Register or ‘CaRR’  - SFVI_CAT2a – to identify communities that are more vulnerable to the adverse health and social effects associated with floods  - Individual property protection  - Warning and alert areas  - Defended areas and SoP\tPublic record\n18309\tNRW_DS116347\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.913Z\tGreat Crested Newt Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333437\t[A]  Species Data held in Recorder 6: - CCW - HQ Terr - Main Assessment of Great Crested Newts at Johnston and Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley SAC CCW000630000000E    - CCW - HQ Terr - Initial Newt Surveys at Johnstown SAC and Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley SAC CCW0004800000001    - CCW - HQ Main Assessment of Great Crested Newts at Halkyn Mountain SAC CCW000480000000E      The following datasets are sensitive and restricted from public access. - Various - Historical Newt Surveys at Johnstown SAC and Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley SAC - Various - Historical Newt Surveys at Halkyn Mountain SAC  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tLiz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW  Johnstown, and Deeside and Buckley Special Areas of Conservation. - Sites were monitored by contractor Jan Wilkinson. At Halkyn Mountain Special Area of Conservation sites were monitored by contractor Young Associates.\tThis dataset comprises of Natural Resources Wales (NRWs) monitoring data for Great Crested Newts on Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales where Great Crested Newts are a special feature. It also includes records of other amphibians. This dataset forms part of the Wales-wide Common Standards Monitoring. Currently the monitoring covers three sites in North East Wales: -  Johnstown Newt Sites SAC -  Deeside and Buckley Newt Sites SAC -  Halkyn Mountain/Mynydd Helygain SAC The aim of this data capture was to assess the condition of designated SAC features based on site-specific performance indicators and to record aspects of their management both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales.  The reports also include collations of historical records however these are not included here as they are not owned by NRW and are therefore omitted from this published dataset.\tPublic record\n17465\tNRW_DS99459\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.952Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Scattered Vegetation Layer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343539\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Scattered.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\phase_1\\allwales_ph1\\p1colour_sca.tab\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing point data showing the location of scattered vegetation mapped during the Phase 1 Habitat Survey. The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. This dataset is an all - Wales electronic dataset created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.  Uniformly distributed scattered vegetation is also captured as a polygon attribute in the vegetation layer.\tPublic record\n18061\tNRW_DS115547\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.833Z\tSevern Estuary Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Area (SPA): Intertidal Mudflats and Sandflats Condition Assessment 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353437\tData archived as Project 435, Media 1472 (File path:  K:\\Archive\\Media1401to1500\\Media1472).   Species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder through Natural England Snapshot  [MRNE013700000005]\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting.\tThe Severn Estuary is the largest example of a coastal plain estuary in the United Kingdom. It has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EU Habitats Directive a Special Protection Area (SPA) under the Birds Directive and as a Ramsar site under the Convention on Wetlands of International Importance.    The primary focus of this study relates to monitoring the intertidal mudflats and sandflats feature of the Severn Estuary SAC by assessing the relevant attributes for the purpose of condition assessment. However monitoring of prey availability within the Severn Estuary SPA was also a secondary objective.    The survey was undertaken in three blocks between the 13th October and 14th November 2012.\tPublic record\n17602\tNRW_DS108734\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.995Z\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of 5 Woodland Proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) in North East Wales (E5)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038373334\tArchived under Project No. 208, Media No. 573\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset consists of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of woodlands on five proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) in North East Wales.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide consistent survey information to aid assessment of SSSI status and subsequent site-support work.\tPublic record\n18982\tNRW_DS125080\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.209Z\tNRW Local Model Manager\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303830\tGIS Corporate Data Store X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe ‘NRW Local Model Manager’ shows the location of NRW’s detailed local flood models which can be requested via datadistribution@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk.\tPublic record\n18729\tNRW_DS123349\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.092Z\tRiver Ecosystem Grade\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333439\tx:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\River Ecosystem Grade.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18088\tNRW_DS115617\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.808Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath, North Atlantic Wet Heath and Rhynchosporion Habitats in Preseli SAC (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363137\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\21 European dry heath within the Preseli SAC\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe collection of this dataset was commissioned by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to monitor the condition of the Northern Atlantic wet heath with Erica tetralix European dry heath and Rhynchosporion habitats within the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The original aim of the project was to monitor the condition of some of the Annex I habitats for which the site (Preseli SAC) has been designated against performance indicators provided by CCW. These habitats are Northern Atlantic wet heaths with Erica tetralix (H4010) European dry heaths (H4030) and Depressions on peat substrates of the Rhynchosporion (H7150). The monitoring follows on from a detailed habitat survey of the site undertaken jointly by CCW\u0027s Phase II survey team and under contract by earlier in 2005.\tPublic record\n17679\tNRW_DS110096\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.675Z\tDevelopment and Field Trials of a Drop-Down Video System (2003)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303936\tZ:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media101to200\\Media162\tNRW Marine Monitoring Team, NRW. (original project commissioned by Rohan Holt - Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group)\tEnvision Mapping was contracted by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to develop a purpose built drop-down video system suitable for marine monitoring work. Detailed specifications were prepared and the system was manufactured by C-Technics a specialist underwater video systems manufacturer. The system was trialled during the manufacturing process and modifications made prior to the system being completed. Upon completion the system was commissioned at three locations in Wales. This tested the system in field conditions for both operational suitability and also enabled the system to be modified to best suit the requirements of CCW.\tPublic record\n18239\tNRW_DS116295\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.097Z\tWales Coast Path\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323935\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Wales Coast Path.lyr\tData Custodian: Quinten Grimley, Coastal Access Project Officer, Access Delivery Team, Sustainable Communities, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of the Wales Coast Path (WCP). The 870 mile long Wales Coast Path was officially opened in May 2012. It runs from the outskirts of Chester to Chepstow and together with Offa\u0027s Dyke Path forms a route of over 1000 miles roughly around the edge of Wales.\tPublic record\n17835\tNRW_DS111182\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.969Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Pectenogammarus planicrurus in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007 - Onwards\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313832\tData is archived within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 554 and Project 410. Media No. 1430.  Marine Monitoring data for each year is also organised in legacy drives  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tPectenogammarus planicrurus is the only amphipod which is a permanent resident of shingle beaches and it was found on the steep gravel or pebble beach fronting the promenade at Pwllheli during Phase 1 surveys undertaken by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) during June 1996.  This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out in 2007 to investigate whether P. planicrurus was still present in the area.  Monitoring of this feature is ongoing and is repeated on a yearly basis.   The purpose of this data capture is to confirm the continued presence and monitor the population of this scarce amphipod species at Pwllheli repeating work carried out in 2004.\tPublic record\n18414\tNRW_DS117691\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.328Z\tSurvey for the Blue Ground Beetle Carabus intricatus at Coed Maesmelin and other Woodlands in the Neath Valley, Glamorgan (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137363931\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (David Boyce)\tThe chance finding of several adult Blue Ground Beetles Carabus intricatus in a garden on the edge of Neath in 2012 and 2013 led to the discovery of a population in the nearby Coed Maesmelin Woodland Trust reserve in January 2015 during a NRW-funded survey. Three adults were recorded and recommendations were made to improve the quality of the woodland habitat for the beetle by re-introducing grazing and cutting back bramble to provide more open conditions.  Survey work in February 2016 focussed on finding additional populations of the beetle in woodlands within close proximity to Coed Maesmelin. A total of eight sites were visited during this second phase of survey work but no beetles were found although some areas in the upper Mellte and Nedd valleys and at Craig Gwladys Country Park appeared to have highly suitable habitat. Coed Maesmelin was also re-visited with just a single adult being found here on 4th February 2016. Bramble growth appeared to be even stronger than in the previous year and given the absence of other records from the Neath area implementation of a programme of remedial management that aims to improve the habitat quality here is required urgently.\tPublic record\n18221\tNRW_DS116250\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.177Z\tReservoir Flood Map Maximum Flood Outline Extent\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323530\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Maximum Flood Extent.lyr\tData custodian - Phillip Morgan, Team Leader, Reservoir Regulation\tData layer showing individual reservoir flood maps for 225 Large Raised Reservoirs including attributed data. Individual reservoirs may have up to 5 flood maps associated with them based on separate breach locations.   The data shows the maximum extent of flood should the reservoir be breached although the location of the reservoir can be inferred it is not explicitly shown on the maps.     Reservoir flooding is extremely unlikely to happen. There has been no loss of life in the UK from reservoir flooding since 1925.   All large reservoirs must be inspected and supervised by reservoir panel engineers. As the enforcement authority for the Reservoirs Act 1975 in Wales we ensure that reservoirs are inspected regularly and essential safety work is carried out.    An online viewable version of this dataset is available here: https://flood-risk-maps.naturalresources.wales/?locale\u003den\tPublic record\n17140\tEXT_DS121553\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.296Z\tEuropean Environment Agency (EEA): CORINE Land Cover (CLC) 2012 - Peat and Urban (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353533\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Corine Land Cover 2012 Peat.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Corine Land Cover 2012 Urban.lyr\tMetadata Contact: GIS and Data Services Team. Internal Contact: Mark Pugh - Flood risk management  External responsibility lies with - European Environment Agency (EEA) under the framework of the Copernicus programme - copernicus@eea.europa.eu\tThese dataset was produced under the Copernicus Initial Operations (GIO) which forms part of the Copernicus Data Services in the European Environment Agency. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to these datasets under an open licence. CORINE Land Cover (CLC) was specified to standardize data collection on land in Europe to support environmental policy development.  Natural Resources Wales has access to the following CORINE Land Cover 2012 Datasets: - Peat - Urban\tPublic record\n19146\tNRW_DS161271\t2024-05-28T13:04:09.317Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Bog Restoration\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323731\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Bog Restoration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report. What the map shows: This map shows the number of constraints to bog restoration for natural flood management. Constraints to hard engineering solutions are not considered.\tPublic record\n18498\tNRW_DS121392\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.327Z\tWoodland Grant Scheme\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333932\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 1.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 2.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 3.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones- Spatial team Leader\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provided incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. The scheme was run in 3 phases with the last phase ending in 2006.   Woodland Grant Scheme 1 operated between April 1989 and April 1992.  Woodland Grant Scheme 2 operated between April 1992 and September 1994 Woodland Grant Scheme 3 operated between September 1994 and April 2006  Spatial data has been digitised against OS raster backdrops  by the former Forestry Commission wales.\tPublic record\n17064\tEXT_DS100789\t2024-05-28T12:10:09.814Z\tCadw: Scheduled Ancient Monument (SAM) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373839\tX:\\Designations\\CADW\\Scheduled Ancient Monuments.lyr\tGIS Service Desk  External Contact: Records Officer at CADW\tNatural Resources of Wales (NRW) are supplied a number of historic and cultural datasets under a licence from Cadw. Cadw is the Welsh Government\u0027s historic environment service working for an accessible and well-protected historic environment for Wales. They were set up with the aim of conserving Wales\u0027s heritage helping people understand and care about their history and helping to sustain the distinctive character of Wales.  This is a spatial dataset containing basic information concerning Scheduled Ancient Monuments (SAM) that lie within the boundaries of Wales. The data has been provided as a guide to the SAMs. Areas have been interpreted from paper maps taken from original legal documents and therefore remain indicative only. Archaeological sites of national importance are given legal protection by being placed on a \u0027Schedule\u0027 of monuments. Under the Ancient Monuments and Archaeological Areas Act 1979 the Welsh Ministers are required to compile and maintain this schedule.\tPublic record\n17376\tNRW_DS24352\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.855Z\tUnmanaged Semi-Natural Vegetation in Welsh Uplands\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333532\tG drive of Upland Ecologist, TSG.\tBarbara Jones, Upland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.\tLists of upland sites (often small enclosures) in Wales fenced off from grazing.  The dataset contains site name grid reference habitat type present grazing species found grazing pressure management regime and actions such as burning brush cutting monitoring and  publications referenced. Also has a section for fencing and canopy and regeneration species.  The database was commissioned by the Uplands Habitat Working Group in 1998.  Data entry was frozen in 1999.\tPublic record\n17998\tNRW_DS114725\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.070Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Coastal Saltmarsh\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373235\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Seagrass Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to coastal saltmarsh only. Coastal saltmarshes in the UK comprise the upper vegetated portions of intertidal mudflats lying approximately between mean high water neap tides and mean high water spring tides. Saltmarshes are usually restricted to comparatively sheltered locations in five main physiographic situations: in estuaries in saline lagoons behind barrier islands at the heads of sea lochs and on beach plains. The development of saltmarsh vegetation is dependent on the presence of intertidal mudflats.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPublic record\n17596\tNRW_DS108658\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.849Z\tAn Assessment of Juniperus communis (Juniper) Sub-species Hemisphaerica in Britain using Molecular Markers\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363538\tCCW library, TSG archive and Welsh national libraries\tAndy Jones (Robert A Jones) Flowering Plant and Fern Specialist, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of genetic analysis aimed at ascertaining the distinction of subspecies of Juniper comminis (juniper) a morphologically variable dioecious species native to the British Isle. Within Britain the extremes of this variation are recognised by two sub-species ssp. communis and ssp. nana but many intermediate forms are known and two further sub-species are also recognised by some- ssp. depressa and ssp. hemisphaerica. It has been suggested that these sub-species should be considered as varieties of J. communis however because of the rarity of the ssp. hemisphaerica in Britain further clarification of its taxonomic status was desirable. The ssp. hemisphaerica while found in mountainous areas of southern Europe and north-west Africa is confined to two main small populations on low maritime cliffs in the UK. Other population also consisting of less than 10 individuals is found on Ramsay Island on the Pembrokeshire coast south Wales. In addition 4 other individuals from Wales have been considered to show morphological characters that are indicative of ssp. hemisphaerica the populations being at Skrinkle Haven (Pembrokeshire) and Holyhead Mountain (Anglesey). The aims of the project were: - to ascertain whether plants named as Juniperus communis ssp. hemisphaerica form a cohesive genetic group distinct from Juniperus communis ssp. communis;  - to assess the extent of clonal growth in British populations of ssp. hemisphaerica. The dataset also confirms the status of a UK BAP species to determine future conservation effort.\tPublic record\n17362\tNRW_DS46100\t2024-05-28T12:21:21.883Z\tLichens of Conservation Concern - Site Data for Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313030\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas  The Threatened Lichen Database is basically defunct now - all data for Threatened Lichens are stored along with those for all other bryophytes by the British Lichen Society and there is no point in NRW storing them separately as that just leads to duplication and errors.\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team (Formerly Alan Hale, Lower Plant Ecologist, CCW)\tA collation of distribution data for 123 species of Red Data Book and National Biodiversity Action Plan lichens. This dataset is historic and is not being updated. New records and corrections are being collated in the Wales Threatened Lichens dataset (see separate metadata entry). The purpose of this data capture as to assist in informing the process of conservation objective setting.\tPrivate record\n19111\tNRW_DS125465\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.033Z\tNorth Wales Marine Observation Scheme (Paper Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343635\tPaper Data stored in off site storage – Combined with CCW/NRB/118\tUnknown. Catalogued by Data Services Team as part of Records and paper data records management project May have value if mobilised\tVolunteer based survey of marine observations carried in sublittoral zone around Anglesey and Llyn Peninsular\tPrivate record\n17057\tEXT_DS102091\t2024-05-28T12:10:09.483Z\tRSPB: Reserves (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303931\tX:\\Designations\\RSPB\\RSPB Reserves.lyr   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\external_agency\\rspb_reserves\\rspb_reserves.TAB\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a GIS dataset containing the spatial boundaries of  Royal Society for the Protection of Birds reserves across the UK. This is an external third party dataset owned by RSPB and licensed to NRW for internal use only.  The RSPB Reserve Boundaries dataset contains the boundaries of all land managed leased or owned as part of RSPB reserves.  Areas for which other rights are held or which are not classed as RSPB reserves are excluded (for exceptions please see individual site records).  Some reserves are only represented by an indicative boundary due to confidentiality issues surrounding the definitive boundary.\tPublic record\n17819\tNRW_DS110960\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.998Z\tSublittoral Survey of Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula 1983\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393630\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder as JNCCMNCR10000186. NRW only holds a snapshot of this data - contained in All Wales snapshot.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBardsey Island and the adjacent coast of the Llyn Peninsula was one of seven areas proposed by the Nature Conservancy Council for establishment as a statutory marine nature reserve. This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out in 1983 to describe the range of sublittoral habitats and communities present in nearshore sublittoral areas of Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula. The purpose of this data capture was to survey and describe the range of habitats and communities present in nearshore sublittoral areas and to assess the scientific interest and make recommendations on the conservation importance of the areas surveyed.\tPublic record\n17382\tNRW_DS24350\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.035Z\tWelsh Parkland Survey (Phase 1)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333530\t\tMike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW (Formerly Adrian Fowles, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, CCW)\tDatabase of Phase 1 Welsh Parkland Survey detailing number of over-mature trees and aerial photograph information for 201 parkland pasture and woodland sites in Wales.\tPrivate record\n18659\tNRW_DS123245\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.378Z\tSurfacewater Abstractions\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323435\tx:\\EU Directives\\Surface Water Abstraction Directive\\Surfacewater Abstractions.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18503\tNRW_DS121357\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.183Z\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Riparian Woodland Potential\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333537\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Riparian Woodland Planting Potential.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Riparian Woodland Potential is our best estimate of locations where tree planting may be possible on smaller floodplains close to flow pathways and effective to attenuate flooding. The dataset is designed to support signposting of riparian areas not already wooded. The dataset is based upon a 50m buffer of available OS Open Data river networks.   A set of open access constraints data was used to erase areas which contained existing woodland watercourses peat roads rail and urban locations.\tPublic record\n19781\tNRW_DS161318\t2024-11-18T15:23:24.503Z\tWest Anglesey fragile sponge and anthozoan communities dive survey, 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333138\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\2023 Anglesey Sponge and Anthozoan\tNatasha Lough - Specialist Advisor, Marine Ecology\tThis resource contains data from surveys of fragile sponge and anthozoan (FSAN) communities off North and West Anglesey collected during dive surveys in April-May 2023. Divers were tasked with re-surveying up to 17 sites previously surveyed as far back as 1996 to determine the continued presence of reported FSAN communities namely of the biotope CR.HCR.XFa.ByErSp.DysAct (mixed turf of bryozoans and erect sponges with Dysidea fragilis and Actinothoe sphyrodeta on tide-swept wave exposed circalittoral rock. This data was collected as FSAN communities are listed under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 and this area around Anglesey has been proposed as an Area of Search (AoS) in the Marine Protected Area (MPA) Network Completion Programme by Welsh Government. The data comprises of:  • 3GB of images (covering each of the 20 survey sites) • 4GB of videos taken during dive surveys • A Survey Photo Catalogue (Excel spreadsheet) with details of each photo and video (e.g. filename subject notable species biotope) • A Master metadata Excel spreadsheet\tPublic record\n17264\tEXT_DS123020\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.598Z\tWG Fisheries (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303230\tX:\\Biological\\Freshwater Fisheries\\WG Fisheries.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.  There is no information about the ownership of this collated dataset\tPrivate record\n17447\tNRW_DS87133\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.278Z\tWest Pembrokeshire tidally exposed seabed and shores (2003)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313333\t[A] Data is archived as Project No. 79, media No.\u0027s 223 and 224 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 224) [B] Raw biological data in Marine recorder survey MRCCW80000000005\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.   Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. Tidal Rapids is one of those habitats.  Tidal Rapids are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan feature and rocky Reefs are listed as an Annex I feature under the Habitats Directive (1992). These features are of conservation importance and the data collected during this survey is used to help define conservation objectives and inform decisions regarding protection and management.  Tidal Rapids are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan feature and rocky Reefs are listed as an Annex I feature under the Habitats Directive (1992). These features are of conservation importance and the data collected during this survey is used to help define conservation objectives and inform decisions regarding protection and management.\tPublic record\n20569\tNRW_DS161355\t2025-04-11T07:53:51.837Z\tWelsh Acoustic Marine Mammal Survey (WAMMS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333535\tData will be stored on the Crown Estate Marine Data Exchange Portal.\tAlex Zalewski - Marine Ecology Advisor\tThe Welsh Acoustic Marine Mammal Survey (WAMMS)  aim was to develop an effective and robust method for measuring underwater noise and the occurrence of cetaceans (whales dolphins and porpoises) in marine protected areas (MPAs) around the Welsh coastline. To test and demonstrate these monitoring methods an 18-month pilot study was undertaken as part of the project in the North Anglesey Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). As well as illustrating how monitoring could be carried out elsewhere in Welsh marine waters this pilot study provided baseline data on ambient noise and marine mammal detections at the site.  Three monitoring locations were selected within the North Anglesey Marine SAC: one at Holyhead deep two off Point Lynas and one situated 1.7km off the coast and the other 5.2 km at the boundary of the SAC.\tPublic record\n17285\tEXT_DS124512\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.766Z\tThreatened Lichen species (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353132\tThe data is downloaded to a spreadsheet, stored in the DMS: teams\u0026gt;evidence\u0026gt;species\u0026gt;lich\u0026gt;Lichen Information\u0026gt;Lichen Statuses (EVID-357-7).  This data is for internal use only and is not to be disseminated. The data has been collated into a gdb along with other information Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Non-vascular Plants\\Non-vascularPlants.gdb\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tBritish Lichen society collation of the available records for rare and threatened lichens and lichenicolous fungi in Wales from 1700 to 2012 for purposes of conservation future monitoring and research. Habitat and substrate information is included where available. Species are included if they are nationally rare or scarce or have a conservation status other than least concern data deficient or not evaluated.\tPublic record\n17911\tNRW_DS111813\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.875Z\tAtlas of the Marine Mammals of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383133\t[A]  This dataset is archived as Project 200, Media 565 (edition 1) and Media 1317 (edition 2) on backed-up server based storage at NRW, Maes y Fynnon. [B]  The report is available from NRW libraries (see related titles field above) and within the NRW Reports Archive located on the DMS.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tWelsh water support nationally and internationally important populations of marine mammals (whales dolphins and seals) which use the area at various stages of their life histories such as feeding and breeding. Since the early 1990s the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has been a major supporter of studies that have recorded the distribution and abundance of marine mammals in Wales.   These studies often undertaken using dedicated networks of volunteers have helped to inform conservation objectives for Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) monitoring targets and the assessment of proposed developments and impacts.  This dataset brings together the best available effort-related survey data on marine mammals in Welsh waters and adjacent parts of the Irish Sea to provide the highest resolution analysis carried out to date of the spatial and temporal variation in their distribution. The Atlas contains distribution maps including variations both seasonally and over the long terms of the five most common species (harbour porpoise bottlenose dolphin short-beaked common dolphin Risso\u0027s dolphin and minke whale).  The purpose of this data capture is to describe the temporal distribution spatial distribution and relative abundance of all marine mammal species sighted detected or stranded in Welsh waters.   This dataset is concentrated on species regularly sighted and is designed to help meet obligations under the EU Habitats Directive. In addition is help former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now Natural Resources Wales (NRW)  advise on marine activities including renewable energy exploitation fisheries waterborne recreation and oil and gas exploitation. The study area consists primarily of Welsh territorial seas out to the median line with Ireland and the Isle of Man and English territorial seas.  It was assumed that data contributed were reliable with respect to species identification and had been checked for errors in date time and position fields; however factors affecting sight ability survey characteristics and ecological factors may have affected sighting rates. Survey effort remains inadequate in all but a few small areas.\tPublic record\n18249\tNRW_DS116268\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.806Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323638\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbodies Catchments Cycle2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2 are a polygon dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive.   Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams rivers and possibly lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. Since rivers are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to the WFD river waterbody to which it relates.  Updated to 2018 version.\tPublic record\n18951\tNRW_DS124885\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.568Z\tWales Green Infrastructure Dataset 2021\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383835\tX:\\Social Economic\\Greenspaces\\Wales Green Infrastructure.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Pippa Jones, Geospatial Advisor, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Pippa Jones, Geospatial Advisor, NRW\tThe Wales Green Infrastructure (GI) Data Set shows every bit of Wales including wetlands and water that is not built upon or covered with a man-made surface. It shows areas that are designated for nature conservation (such as Sites of Special Scientific Interest Local Nature Reserves and local wildlife sites) and areas with a legal right of public access. At a lower level of confidence the data set can show: a typology of sites (e.g. open space amenity space etc.); management quality (e.g. Green Flag Awards); and accessibility (e.g. public parks country parks etc). It shows where green space exists near where people live if it is likely to be natural and whether people are likely to be able to get into it.\tPublic record\n18931\tNRW_DS124828\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.673Z\tIndustrial and Commercial Waste Survey Wales 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383238\tDMS\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Marcus Fogarty, Project Manager  Metadata Point of Contact:  Marcus Fogarty, Project Manager\tThe purpose of this study was to produce information on the quantities origins (by industry sector and geographic region) and fate (management method) of industrial \u0026amp; commercial (I and C) wastes generated by businesses in Wales in 2018.  This information is required by Welsh Government for a variety of reasons including:  reporting on waste generation in compliance with the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002; informing the development of national waste policy; monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets; informing waste planners and the regulator; and providing data to the waste management industry to inform investment decisions.  The precision for the total waste generated is +/- 4.54% at 90% confidence.\tPublic record\n18457\tNRW_DS120494\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.799Z\tGroundwater Vulnerability (2018)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230343934\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Simplified Groundwater Vulnerability.lyr  X:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Combined Groundwater Vulnerability.lyr   Associated layerfiles with this metadata are: Groundwater Vulnerability Groundwater Vulnerability Local Information Groundwater Vulnerability Solid Rock Risk\tDave Jones\tGroundwater vulnerability maps reflect improvements in data mapping and understanding of the factors affecting vulnerability. The maps show the vulnerability of groundwater at a location based on the hydrological geological hydrogeological and soil properties within a 1 kilometre square grid. The maps provide a separate assessment of the vulnerability of groundwater in overlying \u0027superficial\u0027 rocks and those that comprise the underlying \u0027bedrock\u0027. The main aim of the maps is as a high level screening tool to give NRW planners consultants and other users an indication of whether a proposed development or activity is likely to be acceptable (e.g. located in an area of low vulnerability or over unproductive strata) or of potential concern (e.g. located in an area of high vulnerability). The maps can also be used to inform and target environmental management and incident response so that preventative and/or remedial actions can be taken as early as possible to protect groundwater.  Two main rock types are recognised: - Superficial (drift): permeable unconsolidated (loose) deposits such as sands and gravels.  - Bedrock: solid permeable formations such as sandstone chalk and limestone.  The vulnerability of these two rock types may differ due to their structure and location. Where both types are present the maps display the most vulnerable category of the two.\tPublic record\n17905\tNRW_DS112384\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.567Z\tGround Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay 2009 - 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132333834\tData is archived under project no 273 (media 990).\tInternal Custodian: - Marine Ornithologist, NRW. - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16000 individuals: Wetlands International 2006) of the biogeographic population of common scoter Melanitta nigra. Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters ( less than10m) in Carmarthen Bay. Common scoter are also listed as a BAP species. Purpose of data capture was to collate data to feed into the condition monitoring and surveillance of Carmarthen Bay SPA and contributes to Common Standards Monitoring.\tPublic record\n18386\tNRW_DS119124\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.049Z\tNational Marine Character Areas (NMCA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313234\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\National Marine Character Areas.lyr\tJohn Briggs- Landscape Architect, PPG.\tSeascapes like landscapes reflect the relationship between people and place and the part it plays in forming the setting to our everyday lives. Marine Character Areas highlight the key natural cultural and perceptual influences that make the character of each seascape distinct and unique. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) were commissioned on behalf of Welsh Government to identify the character of Wales’ seascapes at a broad-scale. We divided our inshore waters into 29 Marine Character Areas. This work provides the strategic spatial evidence on seascapes to inform the Wales National Marine Plan which is being prepared by Welsh Government. Each area profile includes a description of the key characteristics including their natural cultural and perceptual influences as well as land - sea inter-visibility maps.\tPublic record\n19626\tNRW_DS161306\t2024-10-04T07:40:30.750Z\tSurveys for additional populations of Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae in Pembrokeshire and west Carmarthenshire in 2023 and 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333036\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe current two-year survey visited a further eleven sites in Pembrokeshire and one in west Carmarthenshire. A single Scaly Cricket nymph was found in a pitfall trap at Monkstone Beach near Tenby in October 2023. An additional seven nymphs were recorded at the same site in October 2024 again in pitfall traps suggesting that a resident population is present. This represents the 4th known locality in Wales all of which are in Pembrokeshire and the 6th in the UK. The current survey suggests that there are few suitable beaches for Scaly Cricket and that even where suitable habitat is present it is not always utilised by the species which seems to have a very restricted distribution. The survey further highlights the importance of the Marloes Sands population as it is clearly the largest in Wales. Populations at Marloes Sands West Dale Bay and Monkstone Beach remain very vulnerable to habitat loss due to coastal erosion and sea-level rise.\tPublic record\n17263\tEXT_DS122691\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.598Z\tEuropean Commission: Copernicus Sentinel 2 Data June 2018 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232363931\tX:\\Basemapping\\Remote Sensed Data\\Sentinel 2\\Sentinel 2 June 2018 index.lyr ,  X:\\Basemapping\\Remote Sensed Data\\Sentinel 2\\Sentinel 2 image June 2018.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management Business contact: Helena Sykes, Lead Specialist Advisor, Monitoring Analysis, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17968\tNRW_DS114499\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.599Z\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring: North Llyn Ground Truthing (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343939\tMaintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Fynnon as project no. 313 media no. 1236. Videos are maintained in the archive but can be accessed through GIS layers on the M drive.\tInternal custodian: Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA drop-down video survey carried out in North Wales. This work was carried out by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and focused on the North Llyn area.  Subsequent analysis of the video taken was undertaken by Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd on behalf of CCW. This work was conducted as part of a ground truthing exercise through which the extent and quality of Modiolus mussel beds could be assessed.\tPublic record\n17785\tNRW_DS110512\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.231Z\tMonitoring and Surveillance of National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353132\tIndividual NRW NNR warden offices. Data is fed into Countryside Monitoring System (CMS)  which is held on an external server accessed through a network connection. Contact Tom Hellewell about this.   The reserve staff and wardens hold a lot of the data - and also use a lot of the SAC and SSSI monitoring data to inform their site management plans.  September 2021 update - We still ask the NNR managers to update the data in CMSi. This was last done in November 2019. In the past we’ve tried to get it updated every 2 or 3 years – but looking to extend this to every 5 or 6 years as things really weren’t changing much. CMSi is good for at least another 2 years. There are plans to migrate the data to another system and the provisional plan was to do some development work on AMX to allow for this. However, this didn’t make the cut for this years IT development and no guarantee it will for next year either. Also not sure how this is affected by the AMX monitoring module – which I understand has been put on hold.\tInternal Custodian: Thomas Hellawell, Lead Specialist Advisor – Estates, Estate Planning Team Formerly David Allen, Environmental Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs) contain examples of some of the most important natural and semi-natural terrestrial and coastal ecosystems in Great Britain. They are managed to conserve their habitats or to provide special opportunities for scientific study of the habitats communities and species represented within them. In addition they may be managed to provide public recreation that is compatible with their natural heritage interests. NNRs are declared by the statutory country conservation agencies under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 and the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981.  This dataset contains the monitoring and surveillance of work carried out on National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in areas such as water quality water level water chemistry evapotranspiration water chemistry meteorology soil temperature atmospheric chemistry habitats and species. Examples of particular site monitoring include the Coppicing Surveillance at Coedydd Aber or surveillance of vegetation changes following the removal of grazing from Cwm Idwal  (see \u0027contains\u0027 field for more information).\tPublic record\n17805\tNRW_DS111146\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.946Z\tResults of a Study into the Use of a Phase 2 Sampling Grid as a Monitoring Strategy for the Mawddach Estuary.  (1999 CCW Marine Monitoring trials)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313436\tData held in NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000739). MNCR paper forms are held in NRW off-site storage.Report available from NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above) Archived as project 314, media 1238.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe aim of this survey was to investigate the feasibility of using a phase 2 sampling grid methodology to determine the proportion of muddy and sandy communities and the distribution of major communities within the estuary.  The Mawddach is one of the best examples of a small drying bar-built estuary in Britain and with its mountainous catchment area it has some of the lowest nutrient inputs in Wales. In order to be able to report on the condition of the estuary a number of attributes have been identified which require monitoring. This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out to trial a sampling strategy and methodology that addresses two of these attributes: - The relative proportion of muddy and sandy communities. - The distribution of major communities within the estuary.\tPublic record\n19138\tNRW_DS125531\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.371Z\tTranquillity \u0026amp; Place Wales Sound Environment Part 1 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353331\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Sound Environment Part 1 2023\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape\tThe nationally consistent terrestrial Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource identifies the strategic and local resource in remote rural peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent practice and provision for well-being benefits. There are 6 themes.    Tranquillity is associated with the degree to which places and ecosystems deliver a state of quiet calm peace and well-being. This can be described as a relative abundance perception or experience of nature natural landscapes and features and/or a relative freedom from unwanted visual disturbance signs of human influence and artificial noise.    Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Part I delivers Theme 4 mapping sound environments where natural sounds are more prominent than noise and are appropriate to context. It includes sounds other than road and railway noise.\tPublic record\n17261\tEXT_DS123025\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.537Z\tESTYN: Independent Schools (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303235\tX:\\Social Economic\\Education\\ESTYN Independent Schools.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17838\tNRW_DS111173\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.455Z\tSublittoral Monitoring Trials of Sarn Badrig Reef (1997 - 1998)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313733\tData held in NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (1998 survey- JNCCMNCR40000773, 1999 quadrat survey MRCCW30000000006, 1999 transect survey JNCCMNCR40000776 ). Report available from NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above) MNCR paper forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tMarine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW\tThe data was captured in order to assess the suitability of the biotope classification monitoring method trialled. Sublittoral monitoring trials were done to investigate the extent of biotopes on Sarn Badrig reef- the most extensive of three Sarnau reefs within Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC. In total three surveys were been carried out to investigate the biotopes present.  1) A 1998 survey to evaluate the deployment of divers for measuring the extent of the Sarnau reef biotopes.  2) A 1999 survey of Sarn Badrig reef using quadrat methodology.  3) A 1999 transect survey of the reef.\tPublic record\n17812\tNRW_DS110890\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.909Z\tMonitoring the Dynamics of the North Llyn Modiolus Reef\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383930\tAll data is archived under project no.218, media no. 584. Working copies of the data is held on shared MFSG drive\tInternal custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG.  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tModiolus Reefs are a feature of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Information is gathered about the dynamics of the reef through the recording of spat/larval settlement.  This is used as an indicator of current/recent influences on the reef and can provide an early warning of potential problems. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the dynamics of the reef. This contributes to reporting of favourable conservation status of the SAC feature. Data will be used in conjunction with acoustic methods which look at the extent of the feature (reef) and photo quadrat methods that look at the health of the feature.\tPublic record\n18896\tNRW_DS124772\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.134Z\tCuRVe Condition WaterQuality\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373732\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tWater is a supporting abiotic component of functioning habitats. The quality of the water available is not only intrinsically important for the flora and fauna living directly within water ways but also has a wider effect on the habitats drawing from these water sources. For this project river water quality was chosen as a factor as rivers permeate throughout Wales thereby having a large influence on many ecosystems.  The output shows the relative resilience of each reporting square based on the condition of the freshwater available to the area through rivers. As this data was originally collected on a catchment-by-catchment basis gradients between catchment boundaries are quite sharp with intermediate values only occurring where one reporting square overlaps multiple river waterbody catchments.\tPrivate record\n17396\tNRW_DS64578\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.335Z\tSpecies Licence Return Datasets\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353738\tRecorder 6 database Operational Support Group, CCW, Bangor.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tDylan Lloyd, Environmental Surveillance Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW possibly should now be Carys Roberts\tThis dataset was collected for recording and monitoring species which require licences. It is a collation created using licence return forms and is made up of a number of species including amphibians and reptiles dormouse bats plants badgers and otters.\tPublic record\n18318\tNRW_DS116337\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.024Z\tHydropower Permits\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333337\tData Extracted from Permitting And Licencing System (PALS) Data Stored in NALD from 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-26 and then Permitting And Licencing System from 2016-07-01\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest - Permitting Centre Wales, Cardiff\tPermits issued for Hydropower Generation sites. NRW assesses and licences hydropower schemes for the water they abstract and to protect the local and wider environment; this may require issuing one or a combination of Abstraction Impoundment and Transfer licences dependant on the scheme/site. This dataset details the permits issued for new varied or renewed hydropower schemes.\tPublic record\n18920\tNRW_DS124844\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.572Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Fen Orchid Monitoring 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383434\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Fen Orchid locations 2020.lyr    X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Slack boundaries Fen Orchid 2020.lyr    Species data held in DMS as document LAND-75481194-1312  (SOL-JBAU-LL-XX-DB-EN-0001-S3-PO1-Species_records.xls) Full DMS File path: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/Protected-Sites/so-life/D1%20Physical%20Monitoring/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Flandman%2FProtected%2DSites%2Fso%2Dlife%2FD1%20Physical%20Monitoring%2FKenfig%20Fen%20Orchid%20monitoring\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000DC881C721DC6C9439F0968E82C157F72\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BC00F4132%2D066F%2D4E23%2D8D53%2D597F2DDD6C62%7D\tInternal Custodian: Toni Henwood, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales, Sands of LIFE Project Internal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tLocation and phenology of fen orchids in January 2016 North slack and Lpost Extension Scrape as recorded in August 2020. Boundaries of the slacks are also included. Species abundance data is also recorded in separate spreadsheets.\tPrivate record\n18925\tNRW_DS124833\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.819Z\tThe Freshwater and Terrestrial Protected Sites Baseline Evaluation Project Results\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383333\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/en-gb/ourwork/Pages/Baseline-Evaluation-results-fuel-calls-for-partnership-approach-to-a-nature-rich-future-for-Wales.aspx\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dylan Lloyd, (Thomas Wright, Sue Byrne )  Metadata Point of Contact:  Dylan Lloyd, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning.\tThe Freshwater and Terrestrial Protected Sites Baseline evaluation project (henceforth known as the ‘Baseline evaluation project’) uses existing evidence to derive where possible ‘indicative’ feature condition assessments across the range of freshwater and terrestrial features on protected sites (SAC SPA SSSI) in Wales.\tPrivate record\n18097\tNRW_DS115578\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.846Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath in Cwm Doethie-Mynydd Mallaen candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2003)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353738\tAssociated data is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\03 Cwm Doethie European Dry heath\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Cwm Doethie-Mynydd Mallaen candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) supports dry heath and sessile oak woodland both of which are features of international importance. This dataset includes data collected during a survey and monitoring programme of the dry heath feature at Cwm Doethie-Mynydd Mallaen cSAC undertaken in 2003 and 2004.  The purpose of the contract was to assess the condition extent and distribution of the dry heath or potential dry heath within the boundary of the cSAC and to monitor the condition of selected plots against a set of performance indicators. The monitoring result would establish the present condition of the feature.\tPublic record\n18266\tNRW_DS116330\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.074Z\tMonitoring of Pesticides and Trace Organics in Water 1992 - 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333330\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\tDataset Custodian: Martin Stephens       This dataset is not to be published to data.gov.uk as already published through data.gov.uk by the EA.\tA pesticide is defined under the Food and Environment Protection Act (1985) as \u0027any substance preparation or organism prepared or used for destroying any pest\u0027. Pesticides include herbicides fungicides insecticides molluscicides rodenticides growth regulators and masonry and timber preservatives. They are not confined to agriculture but are also used on roads and rail tracks in homes and gardens as sheep dips for the protection of public health and for many other purposes. Monitoring for pesticides is guided by statutory requirements to monitor concentrations of specific pesticides listed in certain Directives. We also undertake non-statutory monitoring of pesticides when investigating known or predicted local problems and pollution incidents. Trace Organics are organic compounds (including pesticides) detectable at low levels.  As with pesticides our monitoring of these substances is guided by statutory requirements.\tPublic record\n17769\tNRW_DS110520\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.310Z\tOther Protected Animals Licence Return Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353230\tPaper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, CCW\tDylan Lloyd, Environmental Surveillance Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) collation of licence return data for work carried out in Wales affecting protected animal species. This collation includes invertebrates such as but not limited to marsh fritillary crayfish and fairy shrimp. The collation was created to add to the picture of protected animals distributions in Wales to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. The dataset does not represent a systematic coverage/survey of Wales. Location of records is generally random depending on the requirements of the licence holders.\tPublic record\n17544\tNRW_DS101979\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.109Z\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Sites (Polygon)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393739\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Map Sites.lyr .    Archived as project 204, media 569.\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial dataset showing the mapped location of sites. Includes information exported form the Phase 1 Intertidal database and includes selection unit area length dates of visits digitiser and biotope richness. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tPublic record\n19184\tNRW_DS000003\t2024-05-28T13:12:50.305Z\t\t\t\t\t\tPublic record\n19093\tNRW_DS125499\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.896Z\tMapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Spatial Planning in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343939\tX:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Birds.lyr,     X:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Fish.lyr,    X:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Habitats Benthic Species.lyr,   X:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Mammals.lyr\tLee Murray - Specialist Advisor Marine Spatial Planning\tEnvironmental Considerations spatial datasets for marine planning. These datasets are derived from multiple datasets indicating the presence of protected features in Welsh waters. The data is presented on 1 km2 hexagonal grids.    Operational phase was considered for all sectors for all feature groups and includes maintenance activities.  The construction phase was only considered for sectors where potential construction impacts are substantially different from those during operation (tidal stream wave tidal range Floating offshore wind and cabling).  However for the Bird feature group construction phase was only considered for tidal range and cabling. The scoring methodology included three main stages:   1. The occurrence score reflects either the presence or absence or standardized abundance (where abundance data was available) of features (i.e. species habitats or protected sites) and were scored from 1 to 3 on a continuous scale where 1 was the lowest density and 3 the highest for each feature.    2. The conservation importance score is an estimate of the level of protection each feature has based on the legislation that confers a feature protection. It is important to note that all the features included in this work are conferred protection under various legislation and all are important considerations in the planning of developments. This step was scored from 1 to 5.     3. An impact score to reflect potential impacts on features based on the likely pressures generated by each sector was applied for the operational pressures of each of the focus sectors and separate to this construction pressures for some sectors. Where there was considered to be a lack of evidence then a higher score was applied. The current approach did not assess specific technologies individually but considered the potential pressures that could originate from a sector and their potential to negatively affect each of the features. This step was scored from 1 to 3.   A total score was calculated for each feature in each cell and these were summed for each 1 km2 hexagonal grid cell providing a relative ‘environmental considerations’ score for each feature group per sector. The approach taken to the scoring differed slightly between feature groups based on the available evidence\tPublic record\n17923\tNRW_DS113445\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.989Z\tModelling the Predicted Spread of a Carpet Sea Squirt ( Didemnum vexillum ) around the Welsh Coast (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133343435\tData is archived as Project No. 298, media No. 1208 (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 1208)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Data Services, NRW\tDidemnum vexillum is an invasive sea squirt that is not native to UK shores. It was first detected in Europe in 1991 and has since spread to several countries (including France Ireland and the UK). The species has been located in Wales Scotland and England and there is concern D. vexillum may have negative impacts on biodiversity and shellfish interests.    Predicting the spread of an invasive species is crucial when assessing possible management actions. The potential impacts of the species on both biodiversity and commercial interests need to be studied and a cost-benefit approach taken to decide on the best course of management for that species.    Geographic Information System (GIS) offers a fast efficient way to map this predicted spread. The results of this mapping can then be used to focus on areas where D. vexillum may conflict with conservation and commercial interests.\tPublic record\n18437\tNRW_DS119221\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.209Z\tAfforested Deep Peat\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323231\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\Afforested Deep Peat.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tAreas in Wales where deep peat sites have been restored to forest. This dataset comprises the total afforested deep peat including modified deep peat in Wales and is categorised as Broadleaved or predominantly broadleaved Conifer or Predominantly Conifer and Transition. The datasets gives information on soil type along with the species (broadleaved and conifer) replanted or whether the transformation of the site is still in transition.  Peatland in pristine or good condition provides a range of critical ecosystem services including biodiversity carbon storage and sequestration regulation of stream base flows water runoff and downstream flood peaks and nutrient regulation and retention. Peatlands are also sinks and sources of several natural greenhouse gases particularly carbon dioxide (CO2) and methane (CH4). More than 75% of deep peat soils in Wales are covered in semi-natural vegetation. Most of this is upland blanket bog with significant amounts of fen and flush and locally lowland raised bog. These are all UK Biodiversity Action Plan priority habitats with UK and Welsh targets for habitat management and restoration.  In order to deliver the habitat restoration targets set out in the Welsh Environmental Strategy there is a need to progressively restore semi-natural habitat in afforested areas. Restoration measures should seek to better integrate the key objectives of nature conservation soil carbon protection carbon sequestration and water quality.\tPublic record\n17966\tNRW_DS114453\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.647Z\tIntertidal Mapping Project (Updating Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343533\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1 Intertidal H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1201to1300\\Media1217 Archived under project 305, media 1217. The data is available on the CCW corporate GIS (M) drive.\tInternal Custodians: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Spatial Data and GIS Team\tThe Phase 1 Intertidal project was completed in 2005 and maps finalised (see metadata record 24333) however since 2005 selected of areas have been re-mapped as up-to-date information was required for casework and other needs. There is a need to incorporate these newly mapped areas with the original Intertidal data layer and make it available as the most up-to-date data source.  A decision was made to only incorporate newly mapped areas or sites and not individual habitats such as Sabellaria reefs.   The purpose of this data capture was to update the Phase 1 Intertidal biotope and target note layers.   From 2005 onwards for updates but the original sites were mapped from April 1996.  The methodology and the maps have undergone quality control independently but the combined final output has not.  The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted. An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under NRW licence.\tPublic record\n17816\tNRW_DS110891\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.943Z\tBritannia Bridge Mussel Survey (1997)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383931\tMarine Recorder survey: MRCCW3000000002F\t\tThe purpose of this data capture was to provide information and data towards the casework requirement relating to the sublittoral mussel bed. Surveys took place around Britannia Bridge in the Menai Straits.\tPublic record\n18522\tNRW_DS121673\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.448Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Enhancing Surface Roughness\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363733\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Enhancing Surface Roughness.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: This map shows opportunities for enhancing surface roughness for example by relaxing grazing pressure restoring semi-natural habitat increasing species diversity or through complete change in land use (e.g. woodland planting). Priority is given to areas that are well connected to the hydrological environment and/or currently have a low surface roughness. The opportunity spaces shown in the map are the areas that remain after the constraints have been applied. Habitats of high conservation value and high economic value have been considered a constraint as in these areas it is unlikely to be possible or desirable to increase the surface roughness. The opportunity space on this map shows opportunities that remain after all constraints have been applied.\tPublic record\n18442\tNRW_DS119170\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.239Z\tIndustrial and Commercial waste survey Wales 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313730\tData and Report Link:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/wasters/waar/IC%202012%20Survey/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tJohn Fry, Waste Advisor john.frycyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThe purpose of this study was to produce information on the quantities origins (by industry sector and geographic region) and fate (management method) of industrial and commercial (I and C) wastes generated by businesses in Wales in 2012. This information is required by Welsh Government for a variety of reasons including: reporting on waste generation in compliance with the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002; informing the development of national waste policy; monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets; informing waste planners and the regulator; and providing data to the waste management industry to inform investment decisions.  The precision for the total waste generated was +/- 7.9% at 90% confidence.\tPublic record\n17354\tNRW_DS23780\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.797Z\tWelsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey (WPIS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3233373830\t[A]  Biological Records are held in Recorder 6  - Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey CCW0006600000005 tagged as part of the Welsh Invertebrate Database.\tData Custodian: Mike Howe\tThe purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of peatland invertebrate species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions. The Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey sampled invertebrates from peatlands throughout Wales during a 3 year period.  The survey assessed the distribution of invertebrates species in peatland and studied the effects of site management on the assemblage of invertebrates present.  Around 106000 records of invertebrates were found in peatland habitats in Wales along with environmental information for each sampling station. All data relating to the WPIS is now held in NRWs Welsh Invertebrate database (WID).  The survey was based on the whole of Wales and a report is available for each of the following vice counties Anglesey Brecon Caernarfon Carmarthen Ceredigion Denbigh (inc. Wem Moss Shropshire) Glamorgan Merioneth Monmouth Montgomery Pembroke Radnor. Data was collected between 1987-1989.  New records have not been added to the WPIS itself as this dataset is now part of the Welsh Invertebrate Database.\tPublic record\n18523\tNRW_DS121670\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.503Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Bog Restoration\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363730\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Bog Restoration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: This map shows opportunities for bog restoration. The main opportunity for bog restoration is the restoration of existing bog habitat currently in degraded condition. Additionally peat soils under coniferous plantations are highlighted which can be a potential opportunity particularly if the peat soil is in good condition. Existing bog habitat is shown for reference. Hard engineering solutions to flooding are not considered.\tPublic record\n17275\tEXT_DS124754\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.316Z\tNational Flood Asset Dataset for Wales: Environment Agency Flood Assets held in AMX–Flood (Historic legacy data) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373534\tAMX\tRichard Kelland - now moved posts\tmetadata to be provided by custodian.   AMX flood asset data store contains data that has been split into three types according to data owner (NRW EA others)\tPrivate record\n17615\tNRW_DS108730\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.105Z\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of Selected Woodland Proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) in Wales (E4)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038373330\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland\\PH2_Woodland_Quadrats.lyr  Archived as Project No.  207, Media No. 572\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) selection criteria for woodlands are based on the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) such that the best areas of each community should be represented within each Area of Search (AoS). NVC information is therefore required for pSSSls.   This dataset comprises of a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of woodland at 13 sites on 12 proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) throughout Wales covering a total of 305 ha. The purpose of this data capture was to provide consistent survey information to aid assessment of SSSI status and subsequent site-support work.\tPublic record\n17875\tNRW_DS111212\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.865Z\tSurvey of the Littoral Zone of Great Britain\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323132\t[A] Paper reports are available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. [B] Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000265). NRW only have a snapshot of this data\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: JNCC  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBetween 1970 and 1980 NCC contracted a survey on the littoral zone of the coast of Great Britain. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a series of reports prepared under this contract as well as data in Marine Recorder. The purpose of this data capture was: -  To record the distribution of habitats and the associated organisms in the littoral zone of Great Britain in order to verify existing information fill gaps in knowledge and ensure uniformity in the description.  -  To make a photographic record of the main types of littoral habitats and the associated plant and animals.  -  To develop suitable criteria for the assessment and selection of key areas in terms of their scientific and conservation value and assess the information collected by the criteria and so select key areas and to zone the rest of the coast in relation to the key areas.\tPublic record\n18793\tNRW_DS123438\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.918Z\tWater Catchment National Forest Inventory (NFI) Identifier\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343338\tx:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Water Catchment NFI Identifier.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17353\tNRW_DS24232\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.854Z\tImportant Welsh Bat Roosts Database (BATSITES-WALES) 1946 - 2001\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323332\t[A]  Species data is held in Recorder 6. CCW-HQ-Terr- Important Welsh  Bat Roosts Database (BATSITES-WALES)  CCW0003800000003\tInternal Custodian:  Mammal Ecologist, Bats and Riparian mammals, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of information on important bat roosts from across Wales mostly related to Greater and Lesser Horseshoe Bats and other rare species. It includes roost location and other site details and results of repeat monitoring and surveillance. This database was originally created by Nature Conservancy Council as a UK-wide dataset. Welsh records were transferred to Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 1991. There has been no systematic updating since 1999. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts to assist CCW\u0027s conservation work. In particular to provide information when dealing with development proposals to support in the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tPublic record\n17802\tNRW_DS111142\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.810Z\tSublittoral Monitoring Trials of North Pen Llyn 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313432\tPaper report available from NRW library.  Data on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000772).  MNCR survey forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe data was captured in order to assess the suitability of the biotope classification monitoring method trialled. A measure biotope richness as an attribute of north Pen Llyn reefs was measured over three surveys transects between July and August 1998.  Surveyors were experienced in Phase 2 survey methodology and each record was carefully re-examined by an experienced operator to ensure that data were comparable to those assigned to biotopes in the national classification therefore were correctly assigned.\tPublic record\n17977\tNRW_DS114775\t2024-05-28T12:37:06.807Z\tA Marine Education Framework in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373735\t[A] Data is archived as project 344, media 1320 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301 to 1400) [B] Reports and documents are also available in Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsty Lindenbaum, Senior Specialist Advisor Marine, Sustainable Places Land \u0026amp; Sea  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis project is the first step in the development of a Marine Education Framework in Wales to provide a strategic approach for integrating marine experiences both inside and outside the classroom with the Welsh curriculum and subject specifications. The purpose of this data capture was to identify links between Welsh educational policies subject specifications and curricula and the marine environment.\tPublic record\n17633\tNRW_DS109803\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.036Z\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Access Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383033\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Access points.lyr    Archived as project 204, media 569 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 569).\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a static spatial dataset. The dataset contains details on access points used during the phase 1 intertidal survey including site ID OS grid references access type status e.g. public/private contact details of landowners and notes.  Phase 1 intertidal surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.  This particular dataset provides information on access points used by the surveyors to access the various sites across Wales.\tPublic record\n20373\tNRW_DS161347\t2025-03-06T16:49:33.383Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Regulations Cycle 3 Classification (interim 2024)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333437\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England \u0026amp; Wales) Regulations 2017 (referred to as the WFD Regulations) provide a framework for managing the water environment in England and Wales. The Directive aims for “good status” of all ground and surface water (rivers lakes transitional water and coastal waters). Under the WFD Regulations a river basin management plan must be prepared for each river basin district. The plan includes environmental objectives and a summary of the programmes of measures required to achieve those objectives.  This dataset shows the boundaries and interim classification undertaken in 2024 for The Water Framework Directive (WFD) Cycle 3. It is an update in classification for all water bodies in Wales following on from the Cycle 1 Cycle 2 and 2021 Cycle 3 baseline classification rounds.\tPublic record\n17172\tEXT_DS121667\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.631Z\tCanal and River Trust: Canals and Rivers Infrastructure (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363637\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Canals and rivers trust aqueducts.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Canals and rivers trust bridges.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Canals and rivers trust canals.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Canals and rivers trust embankments.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Canals and rivers trust locks.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Canals and rivers trust reservoirs.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team       A copy for the third party data licence is available here: https://canalrivertrust.org.uk/refresh/media/thumbnail/26503-CRT-Data-Licence.pdf  It is very similar to a CC-BY\tThis is a collation of spatial datasets related to canals and river\u0027s infrastructure that run through Wales. Natural Resources Wales obtained this data from the Canal and Rivers Trust through an end user licence. The Canal and River Trust are a charity who look after over 2000 miles of waters across the UK. Natural Resources Wales only have access to the welsh relevant data points.\tPublic record\n18327\tNRW_DS116698\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.298Z\tAquifer Designation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136363938\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Aquifer topology superficial.lyr  X:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Aquifer topology bedrock.lyr\tDave Jones-  Groundwater Team\tAquifer designation consists of two datasets.  These are NRW Aquifer topology superficial [Aquifer Designation Superficial Deposits] and NRW Aquifer topology bedrock [Aquifer Designation Bedrock Geology]. Aquifer designations identify the potential of the geological strata to provide water that can be abstract and have been defined through the assessment of the underlying geology. The designations are attributed directly to British Geological Society (BGS) 1:50K. Bedrock geology (formerly known as \u0027solid\u0027 geology by BGS) is a term used for the main mass of rocks forming the Earth and present everywhere whether exposed at the surface in outcrops or concealed beneath superficial deposits or water. Geology less than 1.8 million years in age are referred to as Superficial Deposits. Superficial deposits are the youngest geological deposits formed during the most recent period of geological time the Quaternary which extends back about 1.8 million years from the present. They rest on older deposits or rocks referred to as Bedrock.\tPublic record\n19133\tNRW_DS125561\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.246Z\tModelled Distributions and Abundance of Cetaceans and Seabirds of Wales and Surrounding Waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353631\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Maximum Numbers,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Numbers by Month,   X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Maximum Numbers,   X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Numbers by Decade,   X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Numbers by Month.\tTom Stringell - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Species - For Mammals.   Matthew Murphy - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Species - For Seabirds\tThe data resource is a package of GIS shapefiles of modelled maximum and monthly (mean) densities of cetacean and seabird species presented in Natural Resources Wales’ Evidence Report No 646: Modelled Distributions and Abundance of Cetaceans and Seabirds in Wales and Surrounding Waters. Refer to report for further information.\tPublic record\n19009\tNRW_DS125268\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.382Z\tSaltmarsh Transect Lines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323638\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh\tKathryn Borrowdale\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. The lines in these layers show the location of the saltmarsh transects surveyed.\tPrivate record\n19101\tNRW_DS125452\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.888Z\tAuthorised Landfill Sites Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343532\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Historic Landfill Sites Points.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning\tThis dataset shows the location of landfill sites which are currently authorised by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under The Environmental Permitting (England and Wales) Regulations 2010. This data is a subset of information from NRWs permiting database. The dataset includes waste and installation permits that fall into the following category’s.   • A1: Co-Disposal Landfill Site;  • A2: Other Landfill Site taking Special Waste;  • A4: Household Commercial and Industrial Waste Landfill;  • A5: Landfill taking Non-Biodegradable Wastes;  • A6: Landfill taking other wastes;  • A7: Industrial Waste Landfill (Factory cartilage);  • 5.2 A(1) a): Waste Landfilling; \u0026gt;10T/D with capacity\u0026gt;25000T excluding inert waste;  • 5.2 A(1) b): Waste Landfilling;  • Any other Landfill to which the 2002 regulations apply;  • L04: Non Hazardous Landfill;  • and L05: Inert Landfill.\tPublic record\n17200\tEXT_DS121883\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.233Z\tMet Office: Average Rainfall (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383833\tx:\\Physical\\Meteorological\\Met Office Rainfall Annual Averages 1Km.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Meteorological\\Met Office Rainfall Annual Avg Contours 1941-1970.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial datasets for annual average rainfall obtained from the Met Office. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have obtained this dataset via a bespoke licence from the Met Office. The Met Office is the UK National Meteorological Service set up to research the possibilities of forecasting the weather and now uses more than 10 million weather observation from across the UK to create advanced atmospheric models to predict upcoming forecasts and longer term climate change scenarios.\tPublic record\n18875\tNRW_DS124682\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.531Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Substation with Hardstanding\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363832\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Substation with Hardstanding.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Substation with Hardstanding. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18208\tNRW_DS116264\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.297Z\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas - Bathing Waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323634\tUpdated every 4 years  X:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTR Sensitive Areas Bathing Waters.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Geraint Weber\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017 (WFD Regulations 2017) requires specification of protected areas for those areas designated as nutrient sensitive areas.     The Urban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas - Bathing Waters layer is a replacement layer of the Welsh only sites previously designated under the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive. It shows the extent of Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulation  (UWWTR) sensitive areas Bathing Waters.     The Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulations regulates the collection and treatment of waste water from homes and from industry. In the UK the directive is implemented through the Urban Wastewater Treatment regulations 1994. Under these regulations water bodies where treatment more stringent than secondary is necessary to fulfil the requirements of the Bathing Waters Directive should be designated as sensitive areas by Defra.   This dataset shows areas currently designated as UWWTD bathing water sensitive areas.\tPublic record\n17257\tEXT_DS123027\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.505Z\tHigher Education Classes for Local Authorities (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303237\tX:\\Social Economic\\Education\\LA Higher Education Classes.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.  There is no information about the ownership of this collated dataset.\tPrivate record\n18789\tNRW_DS123437\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.907Z\tForest Thinning Coupes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343337\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Thinning Coupes.lyr\tLikely to be Geraint Morgan or Arpana Chunilal\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17586\tNRW_DS109396\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.491Z\tThe Long-term Viability of Restored Lowland Heathland in Pembrokeshire\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039333936\tCCW library (Ffynnon Reference: DCT-09-049345) - this just gives the location of the report.\tJane Stevens, TEG (Formerly Jan Sherry CCW)\tThis dataset contains the results of questionnaires and interviews conducted with farmers involved with the management of six restored lowland heathland sites in Pembrokeshire. It includes an assessment of the agricultural value of restored sites versus un-restored sites i.e. had the restoration scheme added value; an assessment of the ecological environmental and financial implications of a range of long-term management options and an assessment of the various mechanisms available to support long-term heathland management. The purpose of this data capture was to determine whether the heathland restoration scheme was seen by the agricultural community to have added value to the sites; to determine what levels of support including financial support and other resources graziers deemed they required in order to continue to manage the sites in line with nature conservation objectives within the schemes and to identify any potential future developments which may support or impede the management of heathland.\tPublic record\n18403\tNRW_DS117501\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.189Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Woodland sub-compartment data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137353031\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest SubCompartments.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary Metadata contact: Keith Harri Jones\tThe NRW Woodland Sub-compartment Database (SCDB) is to be our authoritative data source giving us information for recording monitoring analysis and reporting which supports decision-making on the whole of the NRW woodland estate.   Information from the SCDB is used by NRW wider government industry and the public for economic environmental and social forest-related decision-making. Furthermore it supports forest related national policy development and government initiatives and helps us meet our national and international forest-related reporting responsibilities.     The Inventory SCDB will help provide a definitive measure of trends in extent structure composition health status use and management of NRW land holdings. We require this to meet national and international commitments to report on the sustainable management of forests as well as to help us through the process of business and Forest Design Planning. As well as helping with the above the SCDB helps us address detailed requests from industry government non-government organisations and the public for information on our estate.   NRW\u0027s growing national and international responsibilities and the requirements for monitoring and reporting on a range of forest statistics have highlighted the technical challenges we face in providing consistent national level data.\tPublic record\n17100\tEXT_DS111947\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.522Z\tCadw: Historic Parks and Gardens (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131393437\tX:\\Designations\\CADW\\Historic Parks and Gardens X:\\Designations\\CADW\\Historic Parks and Gardens\\Historic Parks \u0026amp; Gardens - Essential Setting.lyr  X:\\Designations\\CADW\\Historic Parks and Gardens\\Historic Parks and Gardens.lyr  X:\\Designations\\CADW\\Historic Parks and Gardens\\Historic Parks and Gardens - Gardens and Kitchen Gardens.lyr  X:\\Designations\\CADW\\Historic Parks and Gardens\\Historic Parks and Gardens - Significant View.lyr  X:\\Designations\\CADW\\Historic Parks and Gardens\\Historic Parks and Gardens - Walks Seating.lyr   Used to be in the Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\historic_landscapes\\CADW\\Parks_\u0026amp;_gardens\\    \u0026gt;\u0026gt;\u0026gt; multiple MapInfo tables\tGIS Service Desk John Briggs, Landscape Architect, PPG, NRW External Contact: Nigel Davies, Cadw\tThis is a third party spatial dataset owned by Cadw who are the Welsh Governments historic environment services working for an accessible and well-protected historic environment for Wales. Wales has a rich inheritance of historic parks and gardens. They form an important and integral part of the historic and cultural fabric of the country.  Starting in 1992 CADW has undertaken a comprehensive survey of historic parks and gardens in Wales. Parks and gardens thought to be of national importance have been included in the Register of Parks and Gardens of Special Historic Interest in Wales and this dataset contains the digitised boundaries for the areas included in the Register.  The register was compiled in order to aid the informed conservation of historic parks and gardens by owners local planning authorities developers statutory bodies and all concerned with them. This spatial dataset NRW has access to as part of the Historic parks and Gardens are as follows:  - Historic Parks and Gardens - Essential Setting - Historic Parks and Gardens - Historic Parks and Gardens - Gardens and Kitchen Gardens - Historic Parks and Gardens - Significant View - Historic Parks and Gardens - Walks Seating\tPublic record\n17960\tNRW_DS114466\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.664Z\tTy Gwyn Marsh - National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey Saltmarsh Survey (2000)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343636\tAssociated data is held at; M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Ty_Gwyn_marsh_2000\tInternal Custodian: NR Conservation Officer-Sites Team, NRW. Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tA standard Phase 2 NVC survey was undertaken at Ty Gywn Marsh as part of the Morfa Harlech Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) designation process.\tPublic record\n17801\tNRW_DS111147\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.800Z\tMacroinvertebrates in the Glaslyn / Dwyryd Estuary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313437\tA copy of the report is available in NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above).   The survey data has been entered into JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000633). NRW only has a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: JNCC\tThis dataset contains the results of a macro-invertebrate survey of the Glaslyn/Dwyryd estuary undertaken in 1978 in order to better understand the ability of the estuary to receive wading birds that may be displaced by barrage construction. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the capacity of the estuary to receive wading birds that might be displaced from other estuaries on the Irish Sea flyway by barrage construction. Data capture also helped to obtain data relevant to the management of the adjacent Morfa Harlech National Nature Reserve and enabled collection of data on the marine fauna of the area.\tPublic record\n17098\tEXT_DS112026\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.561Z\tRegister of Historic Landscapes: Historic Landscape Character Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132303236\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Historic Landscape.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap2017\\LANDMAP Historic Landscape.lyr\tGIS Service Desk Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group, NRW\tCare should be taken when using this data. The Register of Landscapes of Historic Interest in Wales has been divided into several areas called Historic Landscape Character Areas. This dataset contains the boundaries of these areas. The dataset is jointly owned by the Archaeological Trusts and Cadw. Cadw is the Welsh Governments historic environment service working for an accessible and well-protected historic environment for Wales. The datasets are essential for NRW in planning casework to determine if particular developments will have an impact on a historic landscape. In collaboration with Cadw and the Archaeological Trusts jointly promotes the use of the Register and the Historic Landscape Character areas in the planning and development system in which historic landscapes are a \u0027material consideration\u0027.\tPublic record\n18198\tNRW_DS116223\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.670Z\tAd hoc North Wales Moth Records 2013 to 2014\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323233\tData is stored in Recorder 6 under the Welsh Invertebrate Database. Recorder survey: Ian Sims sabbatical moth records 2013 - 2014 CCW000800000000Z.\tGIS Service Desk Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist MFSG, NRW External Custodian and Originator: Ian Sims, RSPB North Wales.\tThis is a third party dataset held by Natural Resources Wales. This dataset was collected as part of a sabbatical taken by Ian Sims. It consists of moth records and sightings obtained between 2013 and 2014 in North Wales. In all over 3113 records of over 11535 moths of 408 species were recorded including 11 Nationally Notable species Sandhill Rustic (Luperina nickerlii gueneei) (Na/RDB) Ashworth’s Rustic (Xestia ashworthii) (Na) Sand Dart (Agrotis ripae) (Nb) Anerastia lotella (Nb) Blomer’s Rivulet (Discoloxia blomeri) (Nb) Scarce Water-veneer (Donacaula mucronellus) (Nb) Homoeosoma nebulella (Nb) Marsh Oblique-striped (Hypenodes humidalis) (Nb) Shore Wainscot (Mythimna litoralis) (Nb) Pediasia aridella (Nb) and Pseudopostega crepusculella (Nb). Of the remaining species 69 were classified as  \u0027Local\u0027 327 as \u0027common\u0027 with one \u0027Immigrant\u0027. 42 BAP species were identified along with 7 Endangered and 32 Vulnerable species. At least an additional four species were identified to family level only.  This data is now part of Natural Resources Wales\u0027 Welsh Invertebrate Dataset.  This datasets is already available on the NBN Gateway and therefore is not required to be published by Natural Resources Wales.\tPublic record\n17063\tEXT_DS100804\t2024-05-28T12:10:09.873Z\tEnvironment Agency (EA): Licenced Water Abstraction - Water Resources Abstraction Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383034\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\NALD Licence Abstraction Areas.lyr\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as polygons showing Licenced Water Abstraction data information.  This will not updated. Legacy dataset for licences for water abstraction and discharges.\tPrivate record\n18952\tNRW_DS124884\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.563Z\tNRW Woodland Names\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383834\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW Woodland Names.lyr\tData Custodian: Guy Bennett, Specialist Advisor NRW Estate Programming  Data Originator: Geraint Morgan, Specialist Advisor, Forester System\tThis theme contains woodland names used across NRW to differentiate different parts of a Forest Block. Each section is part of a wider forest block and will coincide with one of seven NRW Operations Areas aka Regions. Each polygon can be referred to as a ‘section’.\tPrivate record\n17730\tNRW_DS110124\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.570Z\tCondition of Crustose Coralline Red Algae in Pembrokeshire After the Sea Empress Disaster\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313234\tData are held within the Marine Archive as Project No.71, and Media No. 60 (File Path: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media060) Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.   NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey Number MRCCW10000000074)\tMetadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tFollowing the Sea Empress oil spill observations on the shores of West Angle Bay and Bluck\u0027s Pool and The Pole at Castlemartin showed that some populations of crustose coralline algae had become bleached and white. Collections were made at this time and during the following year to determine the nature of the bleaching and to follow the recovery or otherwise of these plants.   The purpose of this data capture was to determine the nature of the bleaching in crustose coralline algae and to follow the recovery or otherwise of these plants.    Two Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) were chosen for regular monitoring the north shore of West Angle Bay 510 41.8\u0027 N 50  06.5\u0027 W and Bluck\u0027s Pool and The Pole Castlemartin at 510 38\u0027 N  50 03\u0027 W. Further observations and specimens were obtained from Blaise Bullimore on Skomer (510 44\u0027 N 50 17\u0027 W) Manorbier (510  38.5\u0027 N 40 48.35\u0027 W) Worm\u0027s Head Gower (510 33.5\u0027 N 40 19\u0027 W) Porth Trecastell Anglesey (53 0 12\u0027 N 40 25.9\u0027 W)  and Bardsey Island (520 45\u0027 N 40 45\u0027W).\tPublic record\n17332\tNRW_DS24299\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.711Z\tInvertebrate Surveys conducted on Welsh Parklands\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323939\t[A]  Data is held in Recorder 6 Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey  CCW0006600000005\t\tTHIS HAS NOW BE MERGED INTO VARIOUS SURVEYS WITHIN RECORDER 6 UNDER THE WELSH INVERTEBRATE DATABASE (WID). THIS DATASET WAS ARCHIVED BECAUSE THE DATA WAS NOT EASILY IDENTIFIABLE IN RECORDER 6. THIS DATASET SHOULD NOT BE CONFUSED WITH THE WELSH PEATLAND INVERTEBRATE SURVEYS (WPIS).   This is a biogeographical dataset for Welsh saproxylic arthropods (invertebrates). Data generated during the Nature Conservancy Council. Welsh Parkland Invertebrate Survey (WPIS) of 130 wetland sites in Wales plus Wem Moss in Shropshire. This datasets consists of all records from sites with a suffix (WPIS) or with recorder as Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey.  The purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions.  Survey is comprised of two parts: North Wales and South Wales.\tPublic record\n17150\tEXT_DS121613\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.835Z\tJNCC: Offshore Special Area of Conservation (SAC) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363133\tX:\\Designations\\JNCC\\JNCC Offshore SAC WGS84.lyr\tKeith Jones, Spatial Evidence Manager, Evidence, Knowledge and Advice        data downloads: http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/protectedsites/SACselection/gis_data/terms_conditions.asp\tThe spatial dataset containing the boundaries of Special Areas of Conservation designated in Welsh waters beyond 12 nautical miles offshore. The dataset is owned by Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have access to this dataset under an Open Government Licence.\tPublic record\n18430\tNRW_DS119162\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.194Z\tDrinking Water Protected Areas Safeguard Zones - Groundwater\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313632\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Safeguard Zones (Groundwater).lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Jones - Technical Specialist Groundwater and Contaminated Land dave.jones@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tDrinking water safeguard zones are designated areas in which the use of certain substances must be carefully managed to prevent the pollution of raw water sources that are used to provide drinking water.   For at risk drinking water protected areas we may establish Safeguard Zones. These non-statutory Safeguard Zones are areas where activities can impact adversely on the quality of water abstracted in the drinking water protected area. Action to address pollution is targeted in these zones so that extra treatment of raw water can be avoided.   Safeguard Zones are a joint initiative between Natural Resources Wales and water companies. Safeguard Zones are one of the main tools for delivering the Drinking Water Protected Area objectives of the Water Framework Directive.\tPublic record\n18461\tNRW_DS119301\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.090Z\tSection 42 Fungi Register\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333031\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Fungi S42 Inventory 2014.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Fungi S42 point.lyr   These excluded records are on the Y Drive  The spatial data have been created from this excel document: Evidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Fungi \u0026gt; Fungus Information \u0026gt; Fungus Statuses  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/fun/Fungus%20Information/Fungus%20Statuses/Section%2042%20draft%20sites%20inventory%20fungi%20ex%20UWA.XLS\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tA set of records of fungi listed on Section 42 of the NERC Act localised as accurately as possible with imprecise records excluded.\tPublic record\n18439\tNRW_DS119140\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.160Z\tMacrophyte Survey of Welsh Lakes 2016\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313430\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Leafpacs calculator files within folder [Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt; Internal Research \u0026gt; Lake Leafpacs data and spreadsheets \u0026gt; ENSIS Surveys 2016] in Microsoft Excel format [Lake_name_LEAFPACS 2 0_metriccalculator_Month_Year] and summary information in MS Excel format [Site_metrics_Summary_2015.xls]\tNRW Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems Tristan Hatton-Ellis\tThis project set out to collect process and supply to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) ecological and environmental data from a network of 14 lake sites across Wales in support of NRW\u0027s integrated monitoring programme for protected sites (Special Areas of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)) the Water Framework Directive Nitrates Directive Biodiversity Action Plans and other legislative and policy drivers. In particular the surveys are aimed at informing management and restoration of protected sites and facilitating delivery of River Basin Management Plans.  Using standard methods lakes were surveyed to assess the species and abundance of aquatic plants growing within and directly around the lake and to measure water clarity dissolved oxygen and temperature within the lakes.  The results of the aquatic plant surveys are suitable for the purposes of assessing site condition for Habitats Directive standing water features and SSSI status. The lake metrics are applicable for the production of ecological quality ratios from which the lakes may be classified in accordance with the requirements of Water Framework Directive (2000/60/EC).\tPublic record\n18455\tNRW_DS120747\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.208Z\tKiWQM Closed Stations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230373437\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Closed Stations.lyr  Source data is stored in WISKI\tSimon Webb, Stuart Kay       MJ  - I don\u0027t think the the access and use is correct in this entry as only third party data may be restricted, should look at the access and use constraint statements for the KiWQM overarching dataset  - GIS team to check\tThe KiWQM Sampling Stations GIS layer provides a route of discovery for the spatial element of all data relating to water quality measurements that have taken place in and around Wales. It is a record of locations where water sampling has taken place.  ***Replaced by metadata entry 120006**** which has created a combined open and closed station dataset\tPrivate record\n17212\tEXT_DS122416\t2024-05-28T12:17:03.417Z\tEnvironment Agency (EA): River Level, River Flow, Groundwater Level and Rainfall Data held in WISKI 7 Core Hydro and Telemetry (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343136\tThis data is help within the hydro modules in WISKI\tAlison Hanson - Team Leader for this area of work  Liam Perrott - technical specialist for hydrometry       Data from 2014 in Water Resources Team DMS location.\tHydrometric data collected by the Enivronment Agency (EA) at locations in the vicinity of the England / Wales border.  Data is collected by EA and therefore owned by EA.  Due to proximity to Wales often located within cross border river catchments Natural Resources Wales (NRW) also have an interest and operational use for the data.\tPublic record\n17139\tEXT_DS119306\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.325Z\tForestry Commission England (FCE): National Forest Estate Ownership England (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333036\tx:\\Ownership\\Forestry Commission\\FCE Forest Ownership.lyr  x:\\Ownership\\Forestry Commission\\FCE Legal Boundary.lyr  x:\\Ownership\\Forestry Commission\\FCE_Forest Deeds.lyr   SDE Feature Class: NRWGDBTP.GISDATA.FC_NFE_OWNERSHIP\tGIS Service Desk\tThis collection of dataset details the location of external legal boundaries of land within FC ownership and the acquisitions that have created the Forestry Commission England (FCE) Forestry Estate. This dataset is owned by Forest Commission England (FCE) and has been licenced to NRW under and OGL Licence.\tPrivate record\n18497\tNRW_DS121363\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.305Z\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Runoff Attenuation Features 3.3% AEP\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333633\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Runoff Attenuation Features 3% AEP.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tRunoff Attenuation Features Potential is our best estimate of locations of high flow accumulation across the land surface or in smaller channels where it may be possible to temporarily store water and attenuate flooding during high flows.  The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas to target enhanced storage. It is based upon the Risk of Flooding from Surface Water datasets and identifies areas of high flow accumulations for the 3.3% Annual Exceedance Probability surface water maps. The areas of ponding or accumulation are between 100 and 5000 metres squared and have been tagged where they fall on an area of slope steeper than 6% as gully blocking opportunities.   All the potential areas have been constrained so that they are not in urban areas or on roads rails or canals.\tPublic record\n17665\tNRW_DS109983\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.434Z\tWater Turbidity Data and Macroalgal Depth Distributions on a Transect in the Menai Strait\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393833\tData is archived in the NRW Marine archive as Project No 13, Media numbers 24,31 and 83 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\24 etc etc)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tPresentation of data and analysis of patterns of water turbidity in the Menai Strait between 1961 and 1999 as well as depth distribution patterns of macroalgae (Telegraph Cable transect) at intervals from 1956 to 1999. Analysis of any relationship between the depth distribution of macroalgae and water turbidity patterns. The purpose of this data capture was to ascertain changes in turbidity and depth distribution patterns of macroalgae.\tPublic record\n17116\tEXT_DS115861\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.215Z\tPhase 1 Intertidal Biotope Mapping for Severn Estuary 2002 - 2004 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383631\tArchived as Project 186 Media 528, 1185 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 501to1000\\Media 528 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1101to1200\\Media 1185)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine  Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  GIS Service Desk  External Contact: Natural England\tThis is a 3rd party spatial dataset for Phase 1 intertidal biotope data for the English side of the Severn Estuary as an addition to the Welsh side which was completed by Countryside Council for Wales. The Severn Estuary has long been regarded as a site of international natural heritage importance but managing the wide variety of significant wildlife interests against a background of increasing human pressures represents huge challenges for the UK statutory conservation agencies. The study aim was to provide a powerful management GIS tool to support decision making processes and allow rapid assessments of the effects of human activities on local intertidal ecological features. The project involved the construct a Geographical Information System (GIS) database populated with Phase I level intertidal biotope data for the entire Severn Estuary site (English side). The study aim was to provide a powerful management GIS tool to support decision making processes and allow rapid assessments of the effects of human activities on local intertidal ecological features.  The objectives of the study are summarised below: - Conduct Phase I mapping field surveys to classify and map (at 1:5000 scale) intertidal biotopes along the English side of the Severn Estuary possible Special Area of Conservation (pSAC) including Bridgwater Bay Special Protection Area (SPA). - Present a consistent biotope dataset for intertidal areas throughout the site and to compliment similar previous mapping work of sub-tidal environments (Posford Duvivier) and saltmarshes (Dargie 1998).  - Construct a Geographical Information System (GIS) database present a report giving a descriptive account of the site highlighting significant habitats and species and providing an interpretation of observations. - Present a report giving a descriptive account of the site highlighting significant habitats and species and providing an interpretation of observations.\tPublic record\n17811\tNRW_DS110962\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.866Z\tLittoral Survey of Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula 1983\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393632\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored in JNCC\u0027s copy of  Marine Recorder as - JNCCMNCR10000205\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, NRW External Custodian: JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBardsey Island and the adjacent coast of the Llyn Peninsula was one of seven area proposed by the Nature Conservancy Council for establishment as a statutory marine nature reserve. The purpose of data capture was to describe the range of habitats communities and species present on littoral rock within the proposed marine nature reserve and to assess the scientific interest and conservation importance of the areas visited. This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out in 1983 to describe the range of habitats communities and species present on littoral rock on Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula.\tPublic record\n18892\tNRW_DS124789\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.196Z\tGreen Flag Awards Boundary data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373839\tGIS Corporate Data Store: X-Layer_Files\\ Recreation and Access\\Green Flag\\Green Flag sites poly.lyr\tPeter Frost - Advisor -  Planning, Landscape, Energy, Decarbonisation\tThe boundaries of all sites that held a Green Flag or Green Flag Community Award in the 2019-2020 Award Year.\tPublic record\n17102\tEXT_DS111949\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.617Z\tCadw: World Heritage Sites (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131393439\tX:\\Designations\\CADW\\World Heritage Sites X:\\Designations\\CADW\\World Heritage Sites\\World Heritage  Arcs of View.lyr  X:\\Designations\\CADW\\World Heritage Sites\\World Heritage  Essential Setting.lyr  X:\\Designations\\CADW\\World Heritage Sites\\World Heritage  Significant View.lyr  X:\\Designations\\CADX:\\Designations\\CADW\\World Heritage Sites\\World Heritage Sites.lyr   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\historic_landscapes\\World_Heritage_Sites\tGIS Service Desk  John Briggs, Landscape Architect, EPP, NRW External Custodian: CADW - the Welsh Government\u0027s historic environment service working for an accessible and well-protected historic environment for Wales.\tNatural Resources of Wales (NRW) are supplied a number of historic and cultural datasets under a licence from Cadw. Cadw is the Welsh Government’s historic environment service working for an accessible and well-protected historic environment for Wales. They were set up with the aim of conserving Wales’s heritage helping people understand and care about their history and helping to sustain the distinctive character of Wales.This is a spatial dataset containing the digitised boundaries of World Heritage Sites. World Heritage Sites are places that the World Heritage Committee of UNESCO (United Nations Educational Scientific and Cultural Organization) has inscribed on a list of international sites because of their outstanding universal value the importance of which is so great as to transcend national boundaries. Wales currently has three world heritage sites - the Castles and Town Walls of Edward I in Gwynedd at Caernarfon Conwy Beaumaris and Harlech in north-west Wales; Blaenavon Industrial Landscape in south-east Wales; and Pontcysyllte Aqueduct and Canal in north-east Wales.  This spatial dataset for NRW is split into 4 different spatial layers: - Arcs of View - Essential Setting - Significant View - World Heritage Sites\tPublic record\n17370\tNRW_DS24424\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.901Z\tFeatures data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343234\tSafle (Protected Sites) Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.  (Formerly held on ISIS)\tInternal Custodian: Holly York, Advisor 2, Protected Sites (SSSIs), Biodiversity, Ecosystems \u0026amp; Geodiversity\tThis dataset holds information on qualifying and non-qualifying features (species and habitats) on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs). The purpose of this data capture is to maintain and have access to information on Wales\u0027 qualifying non-qualifying and notified features (species and habitats)  on SSSIs SACs and SPAs. Data entry began in 2005 and is ongoing as new sites are designated and existing sites redesignated.\tPublic record\n17820\tNRW_DS111013\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.007Z\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Photographic Collection\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303133\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1 Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Map Photos.lyr  for GIS layer showing  where location photos were taken Digital and scanned images stored on MFSG shared drive Z:\\Archive\\legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Phase1_Database\\IntertidalPhotographs\\Validated\u0027. Pre-digital images stored in the Hafod Elfyn fireproof room.  Digital data is also archived as project 204, media 569 (File path K:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science.\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA collection of photographs taken during the Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Mapping Survey of Wales and associated Microsoft Access database for viewing and displaying the images.  Supplementary visual information for the Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Mapping Survey of Wales. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.   All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tPublic record\n18717\tNRW_DS123351\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.794Z\tHistoric Gauging Station\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333531\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\Historic Gauging Station.lyr\tAlison Hanson, Team Leader, Hydrometry \u0026amp; Telemetry, Supra Area Hydrometry \u0026amp; Telemetry\t\tPrivate record\n19125\tNRW_DS125601\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.547Z\tNutrients Review 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363031\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tOn the 1st April 2021 The Water Resources (Control of Agricultural Pollution) (Wales) Regulations 2021 came into force. The regulations revoke and replace the Nitrate Pollution Prevention (Wales) Regulations 2013. The objective of tthe new regulations is to reduce losses of pollutants from agriculture to the environment by setting rules for certain farming practices.  With the implementation of the new Regulations at WG’s request NRW appointed consultants (Arup) to undertake a review of nutrients in water bodies across Wales to develop a national scale understanding of the current baseline nutrient water quality and assess potential nutrient risks. The nutrient review covers both nitrate and phosphate which are considered to be the key nutrients contributing to poor water quality. The review has assessed the following water bodies in Wales: · Groundwater; · Surface water (rivers); · Lakes; and · Transitional and Coastal (TraC) waters.  The methodology used in the review broadly followed the previous method used for the 2017 NVZ review (NRW_DS116240) but has been adapted to incorporate the assessment of phosphate. The methodologies represent a consistent and practical approach to idenfication of potentially polluted waters across Wales.\tPublic record\n19120\tNRW_DS125605\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.283Z\tNutrients Review - Phosphorus and Nitrogen Source Appointment Percentages\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363035\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nitrogen and Phosphorus Source Apportionment by Land Use Type (%).lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tRelative contribution to total nitrogen and phosphorus load from each land use type in the transitional surface water coastal and  lake catchment (%)\tPublic record\n19430\tNRW_DS161283\t2024-08-13T14:34:56.920Z\tWales Integrated Natural Beauty Mapping 2024 – GIS dataset\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323833\tWaiting to be loaded on X:Drive (13/08/2024)\tAlex Hardie-Brown\tThe Wales Integrated Natural Beauty Mapping is part of the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Designated Landscape Programme which receives funding from the Welsh Government. This project was initiated to create a consistent applicable methodology and evidence base for informing landscape interventions across Wales. As the Welsh government\u0027s designating authority for landscape and statutory advisor on landscape matters NRW has developed this mapping tool using the recognized natural beauty criteria outlined in NRW’s Procedural Guidance 2022.  The mapping tool aims: -To map natural beauty across Wales -To integrate landscape analysis with nature recovery resilience to climate change and well-being -To identify areas to focus detailed analysis for future landscape interventions\tPrivate record\n18807\tNRW_DS123892\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.558Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales: Species Datasets\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233383932\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas. This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  Update from Kathryn Birch - September 2021 \"2004-2011 species records on NBN Atlas. There is now a lot more species data (2012 to 2021) that needs to go onto NBN Atlas. I haven\u0027t had time to find out how this needs to be done. Probably something to sort out in 2022 when the Peatland survey project ends\".\tInternal Custodian: Kathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species Team\tThis dataset was part of the Phase 2 Habitats surveys conducted across Wales. It consists of a detailed survey of plant communities in lowland peatland vegetation. The survey was conducted at Phase 2 (plant community) level of herbaceous mire habitats predominantly within the lowland environment of Wales. Key project outputs include site-based assessments of community type distribution and ecology and evaluative accounts of vegetation and its condition. Strategic outputs include Area of Search/regional/all Wales synthesis and analysis of data to inform site selection conservation and restoration. This part of the Phase 2 survey includes species datasets collected by Countryside Council for Wales and Natural Resources Wales in-house expert Phase 2 survey team. Peer-based quality assurance is carried out within the team and by CCW\u0027s Peatland Specialist. Regular meetings are held to ensure consistent community and species determination and survey protocol application.\tPublic record\n18593\tNRW_DS122707\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.875Z\tNDF Hotspots for generating ecosystem servcies that deliver public goods\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373037\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\Hotspots for generating ecosystem services that deliver public goods.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset shows areas in Wales that provide a large number of ecosystem service benefits. The services selected to be shown in this map were chosen because of their relevance to development planning and because they may not be immediately apparent in the same way as for example agricultural productivity. The following services were chosen; flood regulation water quality vegetation carbon soil carbon and landscape aesthetics. This dataset is a useful high level representation of significant areas for ecosystem service generation.  When interpreting this data it needs to be borne in mind that areas that are not highlighted may still make an important contribution to ecosystem services that were not included in the analysis such as agriculture.  The ecosystem services used to create this dataset deal with a number of key issues that have been identified as important in planning policy including understanding environmental risks addressing landscape change and adapting to the effects of climate change.\tPrivate record\n17588\tNRW_DS109025\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.796Z\tOther Mammals Licence Return Data (1905 - 2007)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039303235\tSpecies Data held in Recorder 6: Other Mammal Licence Returns CCW0008000000006    Paper Records: Dylan Lloyd, Operational Support Group, Maes y Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tCollation of records from CCW licence returns for work carried out on scheduled mammal species sites in Wales. The collation was created to add to the picture of protected mammal distributions in Wales to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. This dataset includes sightings of Bank Vole Eurasian Common Shrew Eurasian Pygmy Shrew Eurasian Red Squirrel Eurasian Water Shrew European Water Vole Field Vole Harvest Mouse Polecat Wood Mouse and Yellow-necked Mouse.\tPublic record\n18840\tNRW_DS124655\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.017Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Substation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363535\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Substation.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm Substation Location. The location of the on-site substation. This is where transformers change the 33kV input from the wind turbines to a 132kV rating for use in the National Grid. This the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18164\tNRW_DS115896\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.775Z\tMonitoring invertebrates by finger searching and pitfall trapping after excavation of the surface vegetation in three slacks at Newborough Warren - Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI (2014).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383936\tData to be provided and added to Recorder 6.\t\tThe purpose of this data capture was to monitor the colonisation of excavated inland dune slacks by specialist pioneer invertebrates on Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve (NNR).  This monitoring data was a continuation of the work undertaken in 2013. All invertebrates captured in the taxa Orthoptera Heteroptera Auchenorrhyncha Coleoptera Aculeata Diplopoda Chilopoda Araneae and Opiliones were identified and counted. The numbers of the individual species are recorded in tables after being corrected for trapping effort. There was little evidence of any change from 2013 in the fauna on the exposed dune habitat. In the slack habitat exposed by excavation a marked increase in the abundance of the rove beetle Bledius subniger was revealed by finger searching and mapping the distribution of its casts. Pitfall trapping in Slack 4 also revealed the abundance of the ground beetles Dyschirius impunctipennis and D. politus that are known predators of B. subniger. Another ground beetle Bembidion pallidipenne characteristic of embryonic slack habitat was also abundant. It is tentatively concluded that this fauna has greatly increased probably from breeding in 2013. Some observations are given to illustrate the bias inherent in pitfall trapping and the danger of too easily comparing abundance between species that are caught in pitfalls. There seemed to be no detectable increase in species of Heterocerus and Dryops. Slack 4 had two distinct areas of slack habitat that had been created by the excavation. In the southern part there was a higher organic content in the soil and greater plant cover and diversity. This contrasted with the northern part which had little plant cover and a low organic content in the soil. These differences were reflected in a marked difference in the fauna of the two areas. The southern half contained species found in the unexcavated slacks that appear to require some shade but were probably eliminated from the other excavated slacks.\tPublic record\n18999\tNRW_DS124992\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.023Z\tDrinking Water Protected Areas for Groundwater in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393932\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Groundwater Drinking Water Protected Areas.lyr\tDave Jones - Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tAll groundwater bodies in Wales are designated as drinking water protected areas (DrWPAs). This aims to protect groundwater from over-abstraction and to prevent deterioration in groundwater quality that could increase the treatment of drinking water. Drinking Water Protected Areas are required to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017.\tPublic record\n18268\tNRW_DS116329\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.294Z\tConsented Discharges to Controlled Waters with Conditions\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333239\tData is extracted from the Permitting and Licencing System (PALS)\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest, Permitting Centre Wales Cardiff\tThis dataset provide details of permit details as required under the Environmental Permit Regulations. Information is held for all permit holders and covers all substances that are controlled. The data come from Natural Resources Wales public Register.   The data set contains 3 tiers of information:    Tier 1 - Site and General Information relating to the permit operator discharge address and type. The date of permit issue effective and revocation. Information where the effluent enters the environment (such as river coastal groundwater) for each permit.     Tier 2 - Outlet and release Data on the effluent type e.g. Sewage effluent Storm Overflow Trade. The location is supplied for the effluent and the outlet in OS Nation Grid Reference format. Further detail is provided on the permit type and treatment type.    Tier 3 - Determinand /release detail Limits  Further detail is provided on the amount that can be discharged and in which time period in months. Release details/determinands are the substances and numerical limits that make up the effluent. This could include chemical biological and physical limits. The permitted limits are included for each determinand /release detail. Data is provided for each effluent and may contain one or more determinands depending on the complexity of the discharge.\tPublic record\n17117\tEXT_DS115864\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.284Z\tCardigan Bay Marine Wildlife Centre Welsh Bottlenose Dolphin (Tursiops truncatus) Photo-identification Catalogue (2005-2012) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383634\tArchived as Project 414, Media 1432 - data supplied in 2011 Archived as Project 453, Media 1495 - data supplied 2012/2013\tGIS Service Desk  Tom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist, NRW       Data is provided to NRW as a condition of the European Species Licence.  We have listed and would normally treat as third party data and therefore not for re-use (especially as this data I snot current) but in reality it depends on the T\u0026amp;Cs listed in the EPS licences, which do allow re-use and are as follows: 252.     Within four weeks after the expiry of this licence the Licensee shall submit to NRW copies of catalogued images and associated data obtained, subject to the terms and conditions of this licence. NRW has the Licensee’s permission to store, copy, use, and release or publish  any biodiversity records associated with the issue of this licence. Where NRW’s policy on public access to data states that the information is sensitive, publication and public access will be restricted in accordance with both the Environmental Information Regulations (2004) and NRW\u0027s guidance on access to data on sensitive biodiversity features. Where NRW\u0027s policy on public access to data states that the information is sensitive any such release will be under restrictive licence conditions in accordance with NRW’s guidance on access to data on sensitive biodiversity features. NRW may share the biodiversity records supplied with conservation organisations and research programmes selected by it. The licensee agrees that such third parties will have use of the images and associated data for the purposes of research programmes contributing to the conservation management of the species concerned; these purposes will include storage, copying, use, and publication where required. Please note that an inadequate return will prejudice future licence applications.   Additional guidance is provided for the documentation of images as part of the licence \\Protocols to avoid excess disturbance during photo-identification studies for SWF and CBMWC licensees.doc\\\"  Available from the species licence team.\"\tThe conservation status of bottlenose dolphin (Tursiops truncatus) has been categorised by Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora (CITES) as data deficient indicating that inadequate information exists for this species concerning its distribution and population status. The only two areas in UK waters that have been identified as having the physical and biological factors essential to the life and reproduction of a population of bottlenose dolphins Tursiops truncatus are Cardigan Bay Wales and the Moray Firth Scotland.  The bottlenose dolphin Tursiops truncatus population of Cardigan Bay off the west coast of Wales has been estimated to consist of around 125 individuals. The dolphins appear to use the inshore waters of Cardigan Bay for both feeding and reproduction and in the summer months calves and juveniles are often observed with adult individuals or groups.  The dataset contains photographic images of cetaceans from Cardigan Bay Pwllheli and Fishguard. This photo-identification catalogue consists of an identification sheet for each bottlenose dolphin that has been successfully identified by volunteer researchers at Cardigan Bay Marine Wildlife Centre. The recognition of individual bottlenose dolphin is essential to the studies associated with animal behaviour and ecology.  Photo-identification was conducted to create a permanent record of bottlenose dolphins encountered around Cardigan Bay and facilitate in the identification of individual bottlenose dolphins as an individual enabling future identification.\tPublic record\n17269\tEXT_DS122680\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.661Z\tGB outline\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232363830\tx:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\GB outline.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18171\tNRW_DS115894\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.775Z\tA survey of the spider Agroeca dentigera on Dyfi National Nature Reserve, with a particular focus on Ynylas Dunes (2013 to 2014).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383934\tData being transferred to Recorder 6.  Report is available through the library. Reports Archive \u0026gt;  NRW Evidence Reports  KNOW-1206538853-47 NRW Evidence Report No. 054 - A survey of the spider Agroeca dentigera on Dyfi NNR, with a particular focus on Ynyslas Dunes in 2013 and 2014\tContact the Invertebrate Ecologist in NRW for additional information.   Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe sand dune system at Ynyslas part of Dyfi National Nature Reserve (NNR) is the only known UK locality for the IUCN Vulnerable liocranid spider Agroeca dentigera. The main purposes of the survey work in 2013 and 2014 was to re-locate the spider last recorded in 2002 and attempt to define its habitat preferences and ecological requirements by searching the dunes and other potentially suitable habitats within the NNR.  The main purposes of the survey work in 2013 and 2014 was to re-locate the spider last recorded in 2002 and attempt to define its habitat preferences and ecological requirements by searching the dunes and other potentially suitable habitats within the NNR. The aim of this survey was to determine the current status and distribution of the spider Agroeca dentigera.\tPublic record\n17277\tEXT_DS124586\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.388Z\tFlood Hazard Research Centre (FHRC): multi-coloured manual (MCM) codes (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353836\tData is held on Flood team DMS  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/teams/kdim/Data-Management/dai/Third%20Party%20Data%20Licenses%20Agreements/Middlesex%20University%20(RE%20MCM%20codes%20for%20NRD%20data)/MCM%20licence%20to%2030%20April%202020.docx?d\u003dw8347a6146492437abf82c4b8117109a0\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\tCustodian: Mark Pugh\tMiddlesex University Flood Hazard Research Centre (FHRC): multi-coloured manual (MCM) codes for use in Natural Receptors Database 2019 only\tPublic record\n18630\tNRW_DS123219\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.429Z\tSubtidal with Dee and Severn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323139\tx:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\Habitat\\Subtidal with Dee and Severn  15AUG2012.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\Habitat\\Subtidal with Dee and Severn 9FEB2012.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\tPrivate record\n18326\tNRW_DS116720\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.115Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) North Wales Bat Roosts\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373230\tThis dataset is managed by Cofnod on behalf of NRW - it is published on the NBN Gateway.\tMetadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  External Contacts and Data Custodian: Aisling May  - Rheolwr Data/Data Manager       aisling.may@cofnod.org.uk Cofnod, Intec, Ffordd y Parc, Parc Menai, Bangor, Gwynedd. LL57 4FG.  (01248) 672603 www.cofnod.org.uk\tThis dataset comprises of bat records from North Wales including monitoring data from known bat roosts and some incidental bat rescue records. This dataset is now held and managed by Cofnod North Wale Environmental Information Service.\tPublic record\n18890\tNRW_DS124793\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.044Z\tUnchecked Rain Gauge Data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Gauges\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373933\tWiski H\u0026amp;T\tLiam Perrot and Alison Hanson\tThis dataset covers monitoring data that is updated on our systems on a minimum of a daily update cycle. This is increased during times of flooding etc. Measurements of the height (m) of water in a river lake or coastal site taken using automatic field devices are given usually every 15 minutes. Information is available for NRW river monitoring sites throughout Wales as well as some reservoirs and coastal sites.\tPrivate record\n17110\tEXT_DS119198\t2024-05-28T12:11:56.505Z\tWelsh Government: Phytophthora Core Disease Zone (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313938\tX:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Phytophthora Core Disease Zone.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s):  Andrew Wright Tree Health Team Leader  (andrew.wright@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Andrew Wright Tree Health Team Leader  (andrew.wright@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)\tPhytophthora ramorum (P. ramorum) is a fungus-like pathogen called a water mould. It causes extensive damage and death to a wide range of trees and other plants. This spatial data is owned by Welsh Government (WG) and shows the boundary for the Phytophthora ramorum disease Core Disease Zone.  The boundary data is used to determine the Statutory Action required for infected sites and is based on OS data for physical features such as roads rivers. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) draft this on-behalf of WG who then quality check the data and get it signed off for use by the Welsh Tree and Plant Health Steering Group. The master or top copy of this dataset is held by Welsh Government and a copy is retained by the NRW Tree Health Team.\tPublic record\n18226\tNRW_DS116242\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.357Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323432\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Lake Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 1 (2008 -2013) is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Article 2 clause 5 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...a body of standing inland surface water\u0027. This dataset contains data related to the physical characteristics risk classification and proposed objectives that can be linked to water bodies by their unique identifiers.\tPublic record\n17066\tEXT_DS100682\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.173Z\tEnvironment Agency (EA): Local Environment Agency Plans (LEAPs) boundaries (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030363832\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\physical\\hydrology\\environment_agency\\agency_infrastructure\\leaps_250k.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) for internal use only.  The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains Local Environment Agency Plans. LEAP\u0027s are areas for which the Agency draws up specific action plans. For further information please contact your local Environment Agency Office.\tPublic record\n18355\tNRW_DS116466\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.540Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Pumping Stations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343636\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Pumping Stations.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tPumping Stations within Powysland and Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge IDD\u0027s mapped as point features\tPublic record\n18898\tNRW_DS124779\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.154Z\tCuRVe Ecosystem Resilience Overall\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373739\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows current relative resilience across Wales based on five attributes (Diveristy Extent Condition Connectivity Adaptability).\tPrivate record\n17528\tNRW_DS100821\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.247Z\tWoodland Grant Scheme 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383231\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 1.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provides incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. WGS1 operated between April 1989 and April 1992. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects in the form of polygons. The dataset identifies areas approved by grant type within each WGS Scheme. The list below provides a summary of grant type captured: SCHEME BOUNDARY -The boundary of the area(s) included within the scheme. NEW PLANTING APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new planting has been approved under NP NB NS NC NK NQ and/or NU. NEW NATURAL REGENERATION APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new natural regeneration has been approved under NN NL and/or NZ. REPLANTING/REGENERATION/MANAGEMENT - Areas within the contract where replanting restocking by natural regeneration or management has been approved under RP RN RC RG RQ EN MG and/or SM. COMMUNITY WOODLAND CONTRIBUTION - Areas within the contract approved for Community Woodland Contribution. Abbreviations used above: NP New planting NB New planting with better land supplement NS New planting accepted into the 1988 set-aside scheme NC New planting of coppice NK New planting of coppice with better land supplement NQ New planting short rotation coppice NU New planting short rotation coppice with better land supplement NN New planting by natural regeneration NL New planting by natural regeneration with better land supp NZ New planting by natural regeneration with set-aside RP Restocking by replanting RC Restocking with coppice (planting not regrowth) RQ Restocking with short rotation coppice (planting not regrowth) RN Restocking by natural regeneration EN Restocking by existing natural regeneration MG Management grant SM Special management grant. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects in the form of polygons.   Purpose of data capture: The scheme provided incentives for people to manage woodlands on sites all over Great Britain.  Temporal coverage: June 1988 to June1991.\tPrivate record\n17427\tNRW_DS98742\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.452Z\tCountry Parks\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373432\tX:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Country Parks.lyr\tJont Bulbeck - Team Leader, Outdoor Access \u0026amp; Recreation\tThere are over 250 recognised Country Parks in England and Wales. Most Country Parks were designated in the 1970s under the Countryside Act 1968 with the support of the former Countryside Commission. In more recent times there has been no specific financial support for Country Parks directly and fewer have been designated. Most are managed by local authorities although other organisations and private individuals can also run them. A Country Park is an area designated for people to visit and enjoy recreation in a countryside environment. The purpose of a Country Park is to provide somewhere for visitors who do not necessarily want to go out into the wider countryside. Visitors can enjoy a public open space with an informal atmosphere as opposed to a formal park as might be found in an urban area. For this reason Country Parks are usually found close to or on the edge of built-up areas.\tPublic record\n18440\tNRW_DS119169\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.317Z\tConstruction and Demolition waste survey Wales 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313639\tData:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/wasters/waar/CD%202012%20Survey/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx   Report: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/wasters/waar/CD%202012%20Survey/Wales%20Waste%20Survey%20Report%20-%20CD%20Final.pdf\tJohn Fry, Waste Advisor John.Fry@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk       John Fry: john.fry@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThe purpose of this data collection was to produce information on the quantities origins (by industry sector and geographic region) and fate (management method) of Construction and Demolition (C and D) wastes generated by businesses in Wales in 2012. This information is required by Welsh Government for a variety of reasons including: reporting on waste generation in compliance with the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002; informing the development of national waste policy; monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets; informing waste planners and the regulator; and providing data to the waste management industry to inform investment decisions.  The precision for the total waste generated was +/- 33.2% at 90% confidence.\tPublic record\n18431\tNRW_DS119163\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.236Z\tForestry Commission Wales (FCW) Landholdings\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313633\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex FCW Landholdings.lyr\tData Custodian: Ian Medcalf GIS Officer, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact: Ian Medcalf GIS Officer, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset for internal use only containing information on areas of land in which former Forestry Commission Wales (FCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have a right to manage. This provides an indicative representation of land owned by former FCW in Wales. The dataset contains only polygons which includes information on site name and unique codes. Content varies for each parcel of land but often includes personal details which are regarded as sensitive.  The purpose of the data is to provide location and boundary information primarily for use by the estates department.\tPublic record\n18444\tNRW_DS119133\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.398Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313333\tX:\\EU directives\\WFD\\Cycle 1\\river catchments.lyr\tData custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Environmental reporting team\tThe \u0027WFD River Waterbody Catchments\u0027 are a polygon Shapefile dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams rivers and possibly lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. Delineation of the river catchment boundaries primarily utilised the CEH Flow Grid hydrological model run with CEH Integrated Hydrological Digital Terrain Model (IHDTM) data so as to determine water drainage into river stretches. Upstream catchment size was calculated within an ESRI GIS environment using the extension Arc Hydro.\tPublic record\n18155\tNRW_DS115995\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.127Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: East Gwynedd, Gower and Glamorgan 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393935\tData stored in Recorder 6 Additional sites 1998 (E Gwynedd, Gower, Glamorgan). Prosser \u0026amp; Wallace CCW0007300000001\tMetadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW  Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in East Gwynedd Gower and Glamorgan. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around East Gwynedd Gower and Glamorgan. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tPublic record\n17982\tNRW_DS114766\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.163Z\tSurveys of Potential Oystercatcher Prey, Pembrey Sands (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373636\tData is archived as project 342 and media 1313.\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Maritime Ornithologist, NRW. Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata contact:  Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) are studying the food requirements of oystercatcher and knot to develop monitoring targets and to assess the implications of shellfish fisheries on the bird populations.  This data was captured to provide data on abundance and distribution of potential oystercatcher prey on Pembrey Sands. The data collected and its results will primarily be used for site monitoring and also for casework. As part of that study surveys of the cockle and mussel stocks in the Burry Inlet have been carried out every autumn since 2004 using a standard methodology.  Significant populations of oystercatcher also feed in the Three Rivers and along Pembrey Sands and it is known that they are part of the same population that feed in the Burry Inlet SPA.  Data from the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) on oystercatcher distribution along the Pembrey coast showed that they concentrate in the central section of the Sands and confirmed that they are feeding at low tide.  Sediment fauna that are found along the Pembrey coast include a variety of shellfish (but not cockles and mussels) and a number of large bodied polychaete worms that may be potential prey for oystercatcher.  A survey of potential oystercatcher prey along the Pembrey Sands was carried out in late September 2009.  It showed high densities of the bivalves Donax vittatus and Angulus vittatus and the polychaetes Lanice conchilega and Owenia fusiformis distributed across the lower and middle shore.  A repeat survey was commissioned by CCW in autumn 2011. In the future this data may be combined with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tPublic record\n19510\tEXT_DS161293\t2024-09-10T14:39:07.496Z\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2022 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323933\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 10m Classified Pixels.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 25m Rasterised Parcels.lyr\tGIS Team\tLCM2022 is the tenth UK land cover map produced by UKCEH (others include LCM1990 2000 2007 2015 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021). Land cover is given as 21 UKCEH Land Cover Classes based on Biodiversity Broad Habitats (BAP see Jackson 2000). LCM2022 was created by classifying Classification Scenes comprising Sentinel-2 Seasonal Composite Images combined with a series of Context Layers designed to reduce spectral confusion. The classification process is automatic using a technique we call Bootstrap Training combined with a Random Forest classifier.   NRW has access to the 10m Classified Pixel and the 25 m Pixel Rasterised Parcel Datasets\tPrivate record\n17491\tNRW_DS100358\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.452Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Mosaic Polygons\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333538\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_mospolys.lyr\tCustodian - Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW Jane Stevens, Habitat and Ecosystem Resource Analyst, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Team\tThis is a spatial dataset of grassland mosaic polygons. These polygons are a gridsquares coloured in chequerboard fasion according to Vegetation_type for display purposes only. The records where collected during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales ( 1987 - 2004 ). Digitising this datasets allowed the detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation to be accessible more widely. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed. Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). CCW now forms part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  The main limitation of the data are the age of records. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends and also through agricultural treatments or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\tPublic record\n17120\tEXT_DS116286\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.151Z\tForesty Commission Great Britain (FCGB): National Forest Estate - Forest District Boundaries (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323836\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\FCGB Forest Districts.lyr\tGIS Service Desk  Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team Leader, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset based on a generalised version of the Great Britain wide forest district information. Natural Resources Wales has the whole of the UK Boundaries which includes Forestry Commission England and Scotland and Wales.\tPublic record\n18273\tNRW_DS116322\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.722Z\tQuality Controlled Daily Rain Gauge Data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Gauges\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333232\tHydrometry and Telemetry syste\tAlison Hanson- Hydrometry and telemetry.  Data is provided on request. Metadata- Data services Team       Currently data is only on request as has to be extracted by the H\u0026amp;T team\tThis dataset contains daily rainfall totals given in mm to 1 decimal place for all rainfall stations included within this dataset. The list of stations to be defined by the file contents with no separate index of stations. The period for counting rainfall will be in line with the water day which is 24 hour period between 9am and 9am. Stations which do not meet quality control specification eg missing or invalid data will be omitted from the published file.\tPublic record\n17961\tNRW_DS114587\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.563Z\tBlack grouse (Tetrao tetrix) National Surveys (Welsh data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353837\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and available by the NBN Gateway\tInternal Custodian: Sian Whitehead, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team NRW\tThe male black grouse (Tetrao tetrix) population in Britain was surveyed in 1995/96 and 2005 in order to estimate population size.   The surveys were based on counts of males displaying in the spring. In Wales and Staffordshire the entire known population was surveyed in 1995. In northern England and Scotland in 1995/96 and 2005 and in Wales in 2005 a stratified random sample of 5 km squares from the breeding range was surveyed. As such the dataset covers around 10% of the UK population overall and does NOT contain all black grouse leks in Britain in the survey years; its aim was to produce a reliable population estimate. Please note that apart from the 1995 Welsh data the dataset does NOT contain all leks of black grouse because only a random subsample of the breeding range was surveyed.  Data have been thoroughly checked for geographical and other errors.\tPublic record\n18487\tNRW_DS121564\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.658Z\tFlood mitigation woodland opportunities for Dyfi, Rhonndda and Tawe Area Trails\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353634\tX:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\Dyfi Catchment.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\Rhondda Catchment.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\Tawe Catchment.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Patrick Green (patrick.green@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)  Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team  This dataset was done under contract and the external contractor was Forest Research: Huw Thomas huw.thomas1@forestry.gsi.gov.uk       Information from Huw Thomas from Forest Research the contractors of this dataset:  I’ve made some enquires and the only dataset which may present a problem would be the NSRI soils data and any dataset we created using the NSRI soil data so anything which selected areas on HOST class for example. In this case, Maps 16 to18. FR and FC are permitted to make use of any soil and soil derived data to meet the objectives of our agency so we can make the data visible via online map browsers and supply printed images but we cannot share the data unless the recipients have the same licence with the NSRI as we do (which the Environment Agency has). If NRW has the same licence agreements as the EA that will be fine also. 3rd parties can not have the spatial data unless they have a licence with NSRI or pay NSRI for the use of your data. However they can view the data.\tThis is a derived spatial dataset created under and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) contract by Forest Research. The creation of this dataset was ommissioned by NRW for three Area Trails Dyfi Rhonndda and Tawe as part of the work related to the Sustainable Management of Natural Resources. This dataset outlines opportunities for woodland creation to help mitigate flooding in the three catchments; Dyfi Rhonndda and Tawe.\tPublic record\n17201\tEXT_DS121890\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.087Z\tNational Trust: Land Access (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383930\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\National Trust\\National Trust land that has limited access.lyr  x:\\Recreation and Access\\National Trust\\National Trust land that is always open.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThese datasets detail area of access or limited access to National Trust Land. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this data through an Open Government Licence.\tPublic record\n18901\tNRW_DS124781\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.155Z\tCuRVe Extent PatchDynamics\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373831\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tHabitat resilience does not only depend on the amount of semi-natural habitat available but also on the spatial configuration of this habitat.  The output shows on a relative scale from 0 to 100 how patchy the distribution of the semi-natural habitat within the reporting square is (one large vs several small).\tPrivate record\n18483\tNRW_DS121594\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.243Z\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) F Proposed Turbines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353934\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA F Proposed Turbines.lyr\tCustodians:  James Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tPublic record\n18347\tNRW_DS116471\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.332Z\tMarine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Species in Wales (archived 2018)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343731\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP Species Archived (Consists of 32 layer files)\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis archived dataset consists of 32 datasets and has been superseded by Environment (Wales) Act Section 7: Marine Species as part of the newer legislative requirements. This dataset is associated with the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) which was published back in 1994 and was the UK Governments response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act (applicable to England and Wales) 2006 it is required that a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027. The NERC Act in Wales is being superseded by the Environment Act (Wales) 2016. Under Section 7 of the Environment Act (Wales) These datasets will provide a basis on which to create a biodiversity list to meet the requirements of the Environment Act (Wales) set out under Section 6 and 7 which require biodiversity and resilience of ecosystem duty and biodiversity lists and duty to take steps to maintain and enhance biodiversity are required respectively. This includes the publishing of a list of living organisms of principal importance in Wales for the purpose of maintaining and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. These datasets will contribute towards the identification of marine organisms of principal importance in Wales and will be kept under review in order to keep the list up-to-date as per Section 7 of the Environment Act (Wales).\tPrivate record\n18202\tNRW_DS116207\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.666Z\t2.1 Otter TEST *** This is not a real dataset ***\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323037\tThe location of the data internally. EXAMPLE: Data is archived as project 450, media 1372. (Or) GIS Corporate Data Store: X-Layer_Files\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Ramsar.lyr  Or Paper copies held in Mammal Ecologist Office, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\tinternal Contact test       add notes here\t*** This metadata entry is NOT REAL data. ***   It is just acting as a test metadata entry for use internally within NRW and as an example for staff who are entering metadata.  Test welsh character Coed Dol-gôch.    EXAMPLE (Test for formatting): The aim of this data collection was to monitoring for otter spraints holts and sightings on all major river systems and their tributaries across Wales.  Surveying was carried out between April and October between 2005 and 2015. Each river was visited only once each year. The river systems covered included Usk Tywi and Wye all of which are designated under the Habitats Directive as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  The main objectives of analyses were to:  - Determine whether the distributions had changed over the monitoring period.  - Determine the number of otter signs had changed in abundance over the monitoring period.\tPrivate record\n17907\tNRW_DS111793\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.663Z\tCommon Scoter (Melanitta nigra) Infaunal Data Collected During Academic Research Projects\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131373933\tData is stored as Project No.45 media number 221, and Project 2 media number 3.  The MSc project is archived as project 4 media number 227.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader Metadata Contact: Data Services Team\tCarmarthen Bay an extensive shallow embayment off the south coast of Wales UK encompasses a wide range of rich and diverse coastal and marine habitats. Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important wintering ground for the Common Scoter duck (Melanitta nigra) and is widely regarded as the most important such site in the UK causing the site to be proposed as a Special Protection Area (pSPA) under the EC Birds Directive. The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs especially during the winter although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps) worms echinoderms isopods amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs. The large concentrations of this species that occur during the moulting period and in winter are highly vulnerable to oil spills human disturbance and the degradation of food resources as a result of oil exploration. The need for a comprehensive baseline survey of the macro fauna communities of Carmarthen Bay became a priority for government agencies such as the Countryside Council for Wales in the wake of the Sea Empress oil spill February 1996. The long-term presence of certain infaunal species and the stability of the areas populations of the species may be a factor in the importance of the Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important overwintering site for the common scoter.  This dataset consists of an extensive series of surveys of the macrofauna around Carmarthen Bay and was collated to identify environmental and / or biotic conditions relating to areas where scoter number were high or low. The majority of this dataset is data generated as part of a Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) funded PhD for the University of Wales Swansea known as \\Carmarthen Bay infauna scoter project\\\". It also includes data related to a University of Wales Swansea MSc Environmental biology dissertation entitled \u0027Common Scoter Melanitta nigra in their wintering habitats; an investigation into the factors influencing their finer scale distribution\u0027. Data was collected by students with CCW involvement or supervision.\"\tPublic record\n18486\tNRW_DS121555\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.650Z\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Natural Processes Constraints\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353535\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\\tVictoria Greest\tThis appears to be an unnecessary entry as there is no x drive layer called \"Natural Processes Constraints\".  Possibly it is a duplication of Woodland constraints dataset (NRW_DS121359) or Natural Processes woodland (NRW_DS121556)\tPrivate record\n18179\tNRW_DS116232\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.602Z\tFlood Map: Flood Zone 3 (archived data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323332\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Zone 3.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FRIM, NRW.\tThis dataset has been archived and has been superseded by Flood Map for Planning (NRW_DS125076).   The Flood Map shows the areas across Wales that could be affected by flooding from rivers or the sea. It also shows flood defences and the areas that benefit from them.   Flood Map is designed to raise awareness among the public local authorities and other organisations of the likelihood of flooding and to encourage people living and working in areas prone to flooding to find out more and take appropriate action.   The Flood Map includes several layers of information this layer is: Flood Zone 3 which is NRWs best estimate of the areas of land with a 100 to 1 (or greater) chance of flooding each year from rivers or with a 200 to 1 chance (or greater) of flooding each year from the sea.\tPublic record\n18118\tNRW_DS115834\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.791Z\tAllis (Alosa alosa) and Twaite (Alosa fallax) shad monitoring data sampled from the rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi using Mitochondrial DNA techniques (2013).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383334\tProject Number: 436 Media number: 1473\tInternal Contact: Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe river Wye Usk and Tywi are or form part of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) designed under the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC) for several features including the Twaite and Allis shad. The dataset contains the results from DNA analyses conducted on 226 fish eggs sampled from the river Wye Usk and Tywi. The aim of the project was to use genetic techniques to address the weaknesses identified in the methods already adopted by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for sampling shad eggs.  The main objectives of analyses were to: - Determine whether the collected eggs belonged to Alosa spp. - Determine the proportion of Twaite and Allis shad using three different genetic markers.\tPublic record\n17273\tEXT_DS124785\t2024-05-28T12:19:15.952Z\tWelsh Government: Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) data (3rd Party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373835\tTo be added - Data to be downloaded from CEH data portal and added to the X drive\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team External  Custodian: James Skates - Welsh Government       NRW has just negotiated access to all the data 22/06/2022.  Matt is going to discuss with Barny if we need to keep the NRW land version or whether the full data will replace it or whether we will need metadata entries for all three Reduced resolution Open data downloaded from CEH (THis metadata entry_ - suggest renaming to incl Open in the title and adding to the abstract for each of the entries whether they are open data or restricted data) Full resolution data for NRW land Full resolution data for all Wales\tGlastir is the Welsh Government\u0027s sustainable land management scheme which pays for environmental goods and services aimed at: Combating climate change; Improving water quality and managing water resources; Improving soil quality and management; Halting biodiversity loss; Managing landscapes and historic environment and improving public access to the countryside; Woodland creation and management.  The Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) has been commissioned by the Welsh Government to assess the performance of Glastir and ran from 2013 to 2016. The data layers are as follows: • Vegetation plot data • Topsoil meso-fauna data • Topsoil particle size distribution • Landscape and habitat area data • Landscape point data • Landscape linear feature data • Pond quality metrics • Topsoil physio-chemical properties • Headwater stream quality metrics • Birds • Pollinators\tPrivate record\n17071\tEXT_DS24266\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.869Z\tDatabase of Welsh Fens (Fenbase 6) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323636\tCCW VMware: H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\JonesP\\FENBASE\tGIS Service Desk Peter Jones Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW Kathryn Birch Habitat Survey and Evaluation Ecologist Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tDatabase of Welsh Fens which was collected as part of a larger inventory of UK Fens data (Fenbase) by the Department of Animal and Plant Science University of Sheffield. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and therefore Natural Resources Wales (NRW) does not possesses the underlying access code and therefore the data base cannot be altered except for adding removing and editing data records.\tPublic record\n18913\tNRW_DS124776\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.167Z\tCuRVe Diversity SemiNaturalHabitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373736\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tHabitat diversity forms an important component of expected resilience as the variety of available ecological niches in the area means that the area is likely to harbour greater species and genetic diversity.  The output shows the diversity of semi-natural habitats on a relative scale from 0 to 100 within each reporting square. In the interpretation of these outputs it is important to consider that only diverse habitat mosaics will show with the highest values whilst areas dominated by few classes will show with low values regardless of whether or not they are semi-natural.\tPrivate record\n17554\tNRW_DS101925\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.526Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Digital Photographs\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393235\tMost of the slides are in a filing cabinet in in the office of CCW\u0027s Grassland Ecologist. The digital photos are stored in the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group. H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV\\PHASE II PHOTO AND IMAGE ARCHIVE The DVDs are stored in the Marine Fireproof Safe (project T007, Media T_Media007- Media T_Media019)\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, TEG, NRW Jonathon Rothwell, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThis metadata entry relates to the photographs taken during the grassland surveys of Wales. This links with the The metadata entry \u0027Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Photo _ GIS Dataset\u0027 which details a GIS dataset containing notes about the photo locations.\tPublic record\n20114\tNRW_DS161329\t2025-01-21T13:45:15.493Z\tData collected for: Sands of LIFE Socio-Economic Impact Assessment (post-intervention)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333239\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n17815\tNRW_DS110958\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.924Z\tLittoral Survey of Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Formally a Marine Nature Reserve ( MNR ) 1982\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393538\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection at Maes-y-Fynnon, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored in JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder as - JNCCMNCR10000160. NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team External Custodian: JNCC  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The results of a survey aimed at describing the major littoral habitats and selected special sites together with their communities found around Skomer Island and the Marloes peninsula. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the range and distribution of littoral habitats and communities to aid in the drafting of the management plan necessary for the establishment of a Statutory Marine Nature Reserve.\tPublic record\n17553\tNRW_DS101897\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.527Z\tWales Wetland Survey 1977-1982\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383937\tAlso see entry for FenBase (holds the quadrat/species data).  Metadata entry: 24288 * Field maps, Quadrat cards and slides stored in MyF filing cabinet (see Kathryn Birch) * Scanned versions of field maps in DMS:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/WLPS19771982/Wales%20Wetland%20Survey%201977-1982%20GIS%20Table%20and%20Document%20Tracker.xlsx?d\u003dw890221785d3940d6ad3b6958d613eda5\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1 * Quadrat species data held in FenBase but extraction to accessible system expected by Autumn 2023 * Tracker in DMS:: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/WLPS19771982/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx * GIS table:  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales\\Wales Lowland Peatland Survey Sites 1977-1987.lyr\tKathryn Birch Or Peter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  (Formerly, Peter Jones as Peatland Ecologist)\tThis project was a major survey of wetlands in Wales. It was a joint venture between NCC Wales HQ and CST to describe classify and evaluate the lowland wetlands of Wales on a floristic basis. In all around 270 wetland sites were surveyed across Wales. The dataset is comprised of sketch maps quadrat records proformas  and a report (Ratcliffe \u0026amp; Hattey 1982). In part to inform Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) notification.  This dataset is being mobilised in 2023\tPublic record\n17774\tNRW_DS110521\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.656Z\tPlant Licence Return Data (2003 - 2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353231\tPaper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group and Andy Jones, CCW.   Species data held on Recorder 6 Plant Licence Returns CCW0008000000003.\tSenior Species Officer, Casework Team, NRW\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) collation of licence return data for work carried out in Wales affecting protected plant species. The collation was created to add to the picture of protected plant distributions in Wales to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.\tPublic record\n18159\tNRW_DS115875\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.739Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer - Voronoi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383735\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Vegetation voronoi.lyr . Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\allwales_ph1 \\phase1_veg_voronoi.tab\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial polygons showing the boundaries and attributes of vegetation polygons.   A voronoi procedure was applied to the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer - GIS dataset to subdivide upland mixed habitat polygons into their component habitats as identified by the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Habitat Layer - GIS dataset. New polygons derived by the voronoi procedure have Voronoi_UID\u0027s prefixed \u0027v_\u0027. New polygons created from Habitat Layer points mapped with a string of habitats e.g. D.1.1 + D.2/E.2.1 are attributed \u0027mosaic\u0027 in the Phase 1 Code field and the component habitats and their proportions are listed in the Mosaic browser table (see metadata for All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mosaic Layer voronoi - GIS dataset). The two tables link on the Voronoi_UID field. The Phase 2 Tools were used to create a mospolys (mosaic polygons) layer to which the Phase 1 legend can be applied for better visualisation in GIS.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales 1km square dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage and the maps were digitised as part of the mapping of Open Access land. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.   Voronoi was carried out to enable full representation and visualisation of the components of complex mixed-habitat upland polygons in the vector data.\tPublic record\n17738\tNRW_DS110473\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.523Z\tCwm Idwal Vegetation Change Surveillance Project 1999-2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343733\ta) Hardcopy of final report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). Word version is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist. Annual interim reports also exist.  b) Raw data is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist c) Photographs available from CCW\u0027s upland ecologist\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (exCCW Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tThe results of a five year surveillance project carried out in Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR) from which grazing has been excluded on a long term experimental basis.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish a detailed monitoring system to determine the long term impact of grazing removal on a range of vegetation communities; and to determine the nature and direction of vegetation recovery in terms of its structure and composition.\tPublic record\n19537\tNRW_DS161294\t2024-09-17T10:12:47.684Z\tCopy of template Template for Dataset Metadata in NRW 1.0 format created at 2024-09-17T11:12:47.684+01:00[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323934\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n18902\tNRW_DS124775\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.186Z\tCuRVe Connectivity Overall\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373735\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows resilience based on connectivity using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tPrivate record\n18910\tNRW_DS124774\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.196Z\tCuRVe Connectivity FeatureLength\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373734\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tConnective features in this instance refers to habitat types that are typically long and narrow. These habitats may act as corridors or stepping stones facilitating species movement through oftentimes heavily modified and otherwise impermeable landscapes.    The output shows the relative quantity of connective features present within each square.\tPrivate record\n18787\tNRW_DS123435\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.778Z\tCydcoed Grants\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343335\tx:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Cydcoed Grants.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n19028\tNRW_DS125204\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.431Z\tDuty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Non-Flood and Non-Land Management Incidents\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323034\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Duty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Non-Flood and Non-Land Management Incidents.lyr\tSally Davies - Incident Management Team\tNRW currently has Duty Tactical Manager (DTM) rotas covering the North South East and South West areas of Wales. This dataset shows the areas of DTM responsibility for non-Flood or Land Management incidents (e.g. pollution incidents office closures etc).\tPublic record\n18633\tNRW_DS123216\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.626Z\tTBLFish\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323136\tx:\\Biological\\Freshwater Fisheries\\Dee Bullhead\\TBLFish font point.lyr , x:\\Biological\\Freshwater Fisheries\\Dee Bullhead\\TBLFish point.lyr\tTristan Hatton-Ellis, Lead Specialist Advisor, Freshwater Habitats and Species, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group\t\tPrivate record\n18915\tNRW_DS124773\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.654Z\tCuRVe Connectivity EcologicalNetworks\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373733\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows the number of ecological networks represented within each reporting square. In some squares 5 out of 6 ecological networks are present generating areas that act as major nodes between various habitat networks across Wales.\tPrivate record\n18456\tNRW_DS120294\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.482Z\tBadger Licences\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230323934\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\Badger licences.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Iwan G. Hughes, Team Leader, Species \u0026amp; Carbon Permitting\tThe dataset includes details of Badger licences issued from 2002 to date.  Information includes the licence number applicant location activity and dates of validity.\tPrivate record\n17538\tNRW_DS101725\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.436Z\tAge and Growth Analysis of Native Oyster Beds (Ostrea edulis) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373235\tArchived at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon as Project No. 77, Media 104  (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 101to200\\Media 104) and also copies are held at Pembroke Dock.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe native flat oyster Ostrea edulis is a UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species for which a UK action plan exists. Native oysters have a long history of over-exploitation in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Harvested beds in Wales used to be present off Porthcawl Swansea Bay and Mumbles Milford Haven Cardigan Pwllheli and the Menai Strait. All of these fisheries declined during the early 1900\u0027s due to intense exploitation possibly combined with disease and poor recruitment. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to Milford Haven Swansea Bay and Porthcawl although at a much reduced scale. The remaining oyster populations in Wales are under threat through increased fishery effort in the existing oyster fishery and from diversification of fishing activities in the fisheries. Dredging activity used in harvesting sublittoral oysters causes physical damage to the seabed and associated marine biodiversity. Oyster beds in Milford Haven occur in the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and form part of the features for which the SAC is designated.  The present work reports on the morphometric characteristics the age structure growth and settlement of Ostrea edulis populations from seven sites in Milford Haven in Pembrokeshire (Wales UK). Information gained from the project will contribute to an ecological understanding of native oyster beds and provide management advice for the remaining native oyster beds in Wales.\tPublic record\n18907\tNRW_DS124771\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.142Z\tCuRVe Condition SoilErosionRisk\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373731\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tSoil is one of the most fundamental factors underlying ecosystem health.  The output shows erosion risk on a relative scale from 0 to 100 from a purely soil-centric perspective; the values express how resilient the soil would be without landcover and important consideration when considering the soils; resilience to land use change. Areas with peaty soils show as NoData areas in this factor as the erodibility of peat is driven by the condition of the landcover to such an extent that the DEFRA methodology was not applicable to these soil types.\tPrivate record\n17878\tNRW_DS111397\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.815Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Subtidal Monitoring Project - Animal Communities on Stones\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333937\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.   Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000056)\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe results of subtidal monitoring undertaken on animal communities found on stones and pebbles at a site within Skomer Marine Nature Reserve. The purpose of this data capture was to observe and record seasonal settlement and colonisation of pebble habitat by marine fauna and record the changes in abundance and where possible the condition of the animals collected over a year.\tPublic record\n17129\tEXT_DS115167\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.422Z\tJNCC Acoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of two sites off North West Anglesey 2009 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313637\tArchived as Project 381, Media 1392. Subsequent drop down video surveys carried out in the same areas are: Archived as Project 268, Media 1309.\tGIS Service Desk  External Contact: Offshore Survey Manager, JNCC\tIn summer 2009 JNCC commissioned Cefas to carry out a multibeam echosounder survey within two Areas of Search (AoS) for offshore Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) off the North West coast of Anglesey. These areas were selected by JNCC as historic data and evidence suggested that Modiolus modiolus beds (a subtype of Annex I reef) had been recorded within these areas. The purpose of this data capture was to obtain multibeam (MBES) coverage of two areas of search in order to carry out seabed interpretation and identify potential drop-camera locations for possible Modiolus modiolus biogenic reef presence. The interpretation of these data was to be used to identify potential drop-camera locations.\tPublic record\n18149\tNRW_DS115997\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.524Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Brecknock and Carmarthen 2000\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393937\tData stored in Recorder 6 CCW0007300000003.\tMetadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Brecknock and Carmarthen. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Brecknock and Carmarthen. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tPublic record\n20113\tNRW_DS161328\t2025-01-21T13:43:48.040Z\tSands of LIFE LiDAR Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333238\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n19851\tNRW_DS161320\t2024-12-02T15:17:22.567Z\tA Survey of Lichens on Pine Snags at Cors y Llyn National Nature Reserve\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333230\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data/Snag%20Lichens%20at%20Cors%20y%20Llyn/NRW%20Evidence%20Report%20761%20-%20Cors%20y%20Llyn%20pine%20snag%20lichen%20survey%202023%20data.xlsx?d\u003dwfe1b9fba22b64297ab5b2b787fa4cda2\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tCors y Llyn National Nature Reserve in Powys was surveyed for lignicolous lichens on dead Scots Pine Pinus sylvestris snags on three dates in November 2023. Lichens were systematically recorded on 24 Scots Pine Pinus sylvestris snags and on one birch Betula snag with additional casual records made on other Scots Pines birches and Heather Calluna vulgaris. Forty-six lichen species 10 lichenicolous fungi and 2 non-lichenised fungi were recorded during the survey of which 33 species were new site records.\tPrivate record\n18904\tNRW_DS124770\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.184Z\tCuRVe Condition SACs\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373730\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tDue to European reporting requirements SACs are amongst the best monitored conservation designations in Wales making them one of the few areas with direct measurements of condition acquired through field work. In field assessments of habitat condition are one of if not the most valuable contribution to the attribute Condition.    The output shows the percentage of SAC features in favourable condition within the area; areas outside of SACs take a NoData value.\tPrivate record\n17540\tNRW_DS101780\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.730Z\tA study of the Milford Haven maerl bed (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373830\tData is archived under project no.145, media no.\u0027s 471-478 Paper MNCR forms stored in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Biodiversity Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tMaerl beds are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat and are also included in the biodiversity duty of public bodies introduced by the NERC Act 2006. Maerl is a collective term for several species of red seaweed with hard chalky skeletons. It is rock hard and unlike other seaweeds it grows as unattached rounded nodules or short branched shapes on the seabed. Maerl beds are an important habitat for many different types of marine life which live amongst or are attached to the surface of Maerl or burrow in the coarse gravel of dead maerl beneath the top living layer. Maerl beds can be of importance to sustainable fisheries providing nursery grounds for commercial species of fish and shellfish. In Milford Haven the maerl species Phymatolithon calcareum forms the only living maerl bed in Wales. P. calcareum is listed in the EC Habitats Directive Annex Vb. The aim of this project was to collate high quality images of the Milford Haven maerl bed to create a base line for long term monitoring for Biodiversity Action Plan reporting. There is high confidence in this dataset as the surveyors are amongst the most experienced marine biologists in the UK.\tPublic record\n17478\tNRW_DS99400\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.296Z\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Photo Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343030\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Map Photos.lyr  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\phase1_intertidal  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media569 Archived under project no. 204, media 569.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tThis is a static spatial dataset.  Phase 1 level intertidal survey photograph index to complement a seamless layer of communities within the intertidal zone. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tPublic record\n18427\tNRW_DS119251\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.039Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Water Quality Data Archive held in WISKI (KiWQM)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323531\tWISKI Database - Kister Water Quality Module\tNRW Water Quality Data Analyst: Martin Stephens       In the near future refineries and steelworks data (under PPC regime) will be added. Simon Webb will clearly mark these as third party on KiWQM as these must not be shared with customers; they are classed as commercially confidential/sensitive. Samples provided by welsh water as part of legislative requirements.\tWater quality data is held in the WISKI Kisters Water Quality Module (KiWQM). The Water Quality Archive provides a central repository for all data relating to water quality measurements that have since the 1960\u0027s been collected from over 22000 sampling points with 2500 active today. Samples are taken from coastal or estuarine waters rivers lakes ponds canals or groundwater. Additional data has been provided by third parties as public record. The data measures a wide range of water quality parameters such as nutrients metals and physio-chemical determinants for both archived and processed data and these are used to assess discharge monitoring against discharge permits investigation of pollution incidents or environmental monitoring. Whilst this data is used to meet the requirements of The Water Framework Directive and Bathing Water Quality assessments the system is currently unable to record anything about compliance.\tPublic record\n17928\tNRW_DS113087\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.618Z\tIntertidal Survey Mapping (phase 1) from Pen-ychain to Criccieth, Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133303837\tThe data are archived as Project No 289, Media No 1192 [K:\\Archive\\Media 1101-1200\\Media 1192]. Arc LayerY:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Pen Llyn SAC Monitoring.gdb\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team. Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a habitat survey of the intertidal area from Pen-ychain to Criccieth Castle on the south coast of the Llyn Peninsula. This work supported the detailed SAC monitoring program by providing a more up to date baseline survey of the intertidal area. It provided CCW with information to help deal with current and future casework in relation to coastal defence and reporting particularly regarding BAP habitats and species of conservation concern. One such Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat are the Sabellaria alveolata reefs which are formed by the honeycomb worm Sabellaria alveolata a polychaete which constructs tubes in tightly packed masses with a distinctive honeycomb-like appearance. These reefs can be up to 30 or even 50 cm thick and take the form of hummocks sheets or more massive formations. Reefs are mainly found on the bottom third of the shore but may reach mean high water of neap tides and extend into the shallow subtidal in places. The current report aims to fill these knowledge gaps for the survey area.  The purpose of this data capture is to update previous intertidal mapping in the area and to specifically provide an updated extent of honeycomb worm reef Sabellaria alveolata. The specific project aims were to identify natural temporal changes that have been witnessed since the original Phase 1 Intertidal Mapping Project in 1996-1997.\tPublic record\n17495\tNRW_DS100241\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.430Z\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Sabellaria alveolata Honeycomb Worm\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323431\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan\\Marine\\Marine Bap Habitats Sabellaria Alveolata Reef.lyr\tContact: GIS and Spatial Team\tSabellaria alveolata reefs are formed by the honeycomb worm Sabellaria alveolata a polychaete which constructs tubes in tightly packed masses with a distinctive honeycomb-like appearance. These reefs can be up to 30 or even 50 cm thick and take the form of hummocks sheets or more massive formations. Reefs are mainly found on the bottom third of the shore but may reach mean high water of neap tides and extend into the shallow subtidal in places. This is a spatial dataset made up of known locations of the Sabellaria alveolata Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats across Wales. The data are derived from the Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCWs) intertidal Phase 1 survey data held by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) from the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) project and from ad-hoc records by individuals. The aim of this data capture digitally is to represent the distribution of the BAP habitat across wales.\tPublic record\n18261\tNRW_DS116287\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.049Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Recreation Routes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323837\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\NRW Forest Recreation Routes.lyr\tCustodian: Heather Crump, National Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThe usage name and grade of the series of recreation segments that link together to form linear recreation features for example mountain bike trails.  -  Walking:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Nature trail heritage trail education trail forest walk.  -  Cycling:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Mountain bike route road bike downhill cross-country family rough route.  -  Equestrian:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Carriage route standard. -  Forest Drive:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Toll Toll free.  -  Running:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment.  -  Huskies:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment.  -  Emergency Services:  Must be an agreed access route for emergency services (Fire ambulance mountain rescue).  -  Rally:  Must be an approved rally route.  Other Route Types are further split into Route Subtype; Nature Trail Sculpture Trail Heritage Trail Sensory Trail Education Trail Play Trail Downhill Cross Country Family Carriage Route Toll Toll Free Fire Ambulance Mountain Rescue Forest Walk Trim Trail All ability Access Trail Mountain Bike Road Bike Standard Cross Country Ski Segway Other. Where appropriate routes are graded; Easy Moderate Difficult; Green - Easy Blue - Moderate Red - Difficult Black - Severe Orange - Bike Park Forest road or similar.\tPublic record\n18909\tNRW_DS124766\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.125Z\tCuRVe Condition PosPlantIndicators\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373636\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tFor many habitats specific species associated with the habitat are known to only occur if the habitat is in good overall condition. These species can be considered indicators of good habitat condition.    This factor shows on a relative scale the diversity of positive plant indicator species associated with a given habitat are present within that habitat group for each 1 km reporting square.\tPrivate record\n18078\tNRW_DS115294\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.763Z\tSAC Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey River Usk and River Wye (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323934\t[A]  Data is archived as project 403, media 1420 [B]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Shad Egg Survey, River Usk and River Wye, 2012 - CCW - Report No. 13/8/3 CCW000800000000Q.\t\tThe River Usk and Wye are designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EC 92/43/EEC Habitats Directive for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. In the UK significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn Usk Wye and Tywi.  The River Usk and Wye have been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for shads and other features including salmon lampreys and bullhead.  The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the assessment of  Favourable Conservation  Status along the Usk and Wye to supplement the main shad spawning distribution condiftion assessment for the Usk (previous surveys conducted in 2010 and 2011) and the Wye (previous surveys were conducted in 2011). 7 sites were chosen along the length of the River Usk and 10 along the length of the River Wye. Both surveys were conducted between high river flows events and work was carried out by qualified Countryside Council for Wales monitoring staff.\tPublic record\n19547\tEXT_DS161295\t2024-09-18T09:31:46.974Z\tCopy of template Template for Dataset Metadata in NRW 1.0 format created at 2024-09-18T10:31:46.974+01:00[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323935\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n18429\tNRW_DS119234\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.069Z\tKnow Your River - Salmon and Sea Trout Catchment Summary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323334\tMost up to date reports should be on the NRW website - https://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/research-and-reports/terrestrial-and-freshwater-species-reports/know-your-rivers-salmon-and-sea-trout-catchment-summaries/?lang\u003den  Internal NE/NW docs  are held - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/anass/fr/Salmon%20action%20plans/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx  SW/MID KYR - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/anass/fr/Salmon%20action%20plans/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Fanass%2Ffr%2FSalmon%20action%20plans%2FSW%20Know%20Your%20Rivers\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120006992F85D1E299A439F90D9D45A521C69\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B8EF66B5A%2DB381%2D4FD9%2DA72E%2D8B9041DFF0D5%7D  SC/SE - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/anass/fr/Salmon%20action%20plans/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Fanass%2Ffr%2FSalmon%20action%20plans%2FSouth%20East%20Know%20Your%20Rivers\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120006992F85D1E299A439F90D9D45A521C69\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B8EF66B5A%2DB381%2D4FD9%2DA72E%2D8B9041DFF0D5%7D\tInternal Contact: Robert Evans, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team   Metadata Point of Contact:  Data Services Team\tThe Know Your River documents give a brief overview of Salmon and Sea trout stocks on major rivers throughout Wales. Documents contain the following data: Rod Catches - data is graphed. Rod catch data is collected through anglers. All salmon and sea trout rod catch should be recorded by rod license holders. This data is used to estimate overall fish runs on catchments. Conservation objectives - data is graphed. This uses the rod catch data to estimate the spawning stock on a catchment. This is then compared against what we believe is the stock level needed for a healthy population on that catchment. This data influences byelaws for catchments e.g rivers not reaching conservation limit can have 100% catch and release imposed on them. Juvenile salmonid data - Any electro-fishing surveys carried out on a catchment are recorded on a map using the National Fisheries Classification Scheme to highlight the quality of the site. This data is collected due to the National Fisheries Monitoring Programme.\tPublic record\n17426\tNRW_DS98741\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.451Z\tBiosphere Reserves\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373431\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Biospheric Reserves.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of biosphere reserves in Wales. The Biosphere Reserve is an international designation made by UNESCO based on nominations made by more than 110 countries. These sites are chosen to conserve examples of areas which are characteristic of the world\u0027s natural regions. They must also be areas where people are important components of everyday life. The biosphere is rapidly changing under the influence of human activity so meeting their needs whilst conserving the area\u0027s natural processes and wildlife is of the utmost importance.\tPublic record\n18122\tNRW_DS115773\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.871Z\tThe status and distribution of the shrill carder bee Bombus sylvarum in the Kenfig National Nature Reserve (NNR) - Port Talbot (2013).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373733\t[A]  The data will become available in Recorder 6  [B]  The report located in the DMS Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports  NRW Evidence Report No. 023 - The status~ distribution of the shrill carder bee in the Kenfig - Port Talbot area in 2013.  KNOW-1206538853-15\t\tThe shrill carder bee (Bombus sylarum) was once relatively widespread across southern England and lowlands Wales favouring flower-rich habitats such as sand dunes and established grasslands and heathlands. However there has been a recent decline in the population. The main purpose of this survey was to provide an up-to-date account of the current status of the species with an emphasis placed on determining the distribution limits by re-visiting sites with previous records and surveying sites supporting flower-rich dunes grasslands and other habitats.  The surveying took place in the Kenfig-Port Talbot area in Glamorgan between late July and early September 2013.  This dataset provides an up-to-date account of the distribution of shrill carder bee (Bombus sylarum) across these sites around South Wales. It is a collection of sightings of shrill carder bee and botanical surveys. The botanical surveys had a particular emphasis on plants associated with the foraging behaviour of shrill carder bees. A total of 17 species of bumblebee including shrill carder bee were recorded during the survey with a total of 936 recorded observations of 2122 individual bumblebees.\tPublic record\n18823\tNRW_DS123773\t2024-05-28T12:56:22.175Z\tIntertidal Biotope Map\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233373733\t\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\tPrivate record\n17281\tEXT_DS124707\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.718Z\tMedian line to 12 Nautical Miles (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373037\tx:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Median line to 12 NM.lyr\tGeospatial team, Knowledge Information Management Group\t\tPrivate record\n18905\tNRW_DS124765\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.155Z\tCuRVe Condition Overall\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373635\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows resilience based on condition using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tPrivate record\n18790\tNRW_DS123432\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.713Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Land Parcels\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343332\tx:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Land Parcels.lyr\tLouise Harris, Specialist Advisor, Geospatial, Knowledge Information Management Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17684\tNRW_DS110069\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.936Z\tAssessment of the Impact of Hydraulic Cockle Dredging on the Macroinvertebrate Faunas of Traeth Lafan (1993-1994)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303639\t[A] Data is held as Report  (project?) No.184, media 523 (File path: K\\:Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 523 [B] Survey data in Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000693)\tMetadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW Internal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis dataset contains the results of a macroinvertebrate and sediment survey conducted post hydraulic cockle dredging around Traeth Lafan North Wales. The purpose of this data collection was to study the effects of hydraulic suction dredging on benthic invertebrate macrofaunas substrate conformation and sediment composition on Traeth Lafan. The survey was located at Traeth Lafan the northern entrance to the Menai Strait (Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC)).  A survey of the macroinvertebrates and sediments of Traeth Lafan North Wales was undertaken following extensive hydraulic cockle dredging in autumn 1993. The survey covered the site allocated for dredging and part of the hand gathering area which was used as \u0027control\u0027 against which changes on the dredged ground could be compared. A follow up survey using the same sampling grid and rationale was carried out twelve months later in November 1994 to ascertain the recovery state of the area after a full season\u0027s recruitment.\tPublic record\n17364\tNRW_DS46084\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.017Z\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Invertebrate Data for Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436303834\t[A]  Original data is held in Recorder 6 within the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID).  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of all confirmed occurrence records of UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species reported from Wales. The aim of the data was to collate information on UK BAP invertebrates in Wales and inform Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) partners. Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. Spatial references dates and species identifications have been validated by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) invertebrate experts checking printed lists of records and literature references have been added where possible.\tPublic record\n17636\tNRW_DS109814\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.051Z\tLower Limit of Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383134\tLegacy CCW Archive (File path: Z\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\LAT-revised.TAB\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis is a GIS dataset line dataset depicting an approximation of the Lowest Astronomical Tide through digitising along a combination of the OS 1:50000 intertidal boundary and the lowest survey points of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) marine intertidal phase 1 mapping survey. The purpose of this data capture was to define the lowest boundary of the intertidal zone which has relevance when defining Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) boundaries for intertidal features. All SSSI\u0027s designated after the CCW Phase 1 mapping survey use this as their lowest limit.\tPublic record\n19666\tEXT_DS161311\t2024-10-17T09:24:41.780Z\tCopy of template Template for Dataset Metadata in NRW 1.0 format created at 2024-10-17T10:24:41.78+01:00[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n18387\tNRW_DS119100\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.263Z\tMarine Article 17 Reporting Habitat Features\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313030\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine  Consists of 18 layers held within the same location.  These three datasets have been updated in 2018. X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine\\Art17 Subtidal Reef Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine\\Art17 Subtidal Reef Polygon.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine\\Art17 Welsh Sandbanks.lyr  Data is also available via the WG web mapping portal \"DataMapWales\". JNCC also have a UK version of the composite Article 17 datasets that is available from their website.\tInternal Contact: Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tReporting under Article 17 of the EU Habitats Directive requires member states to report on progress towards achieving favourable conservation status for habitats and species of Community Importance. The assessment of conservation status does not only relate to that component of the habitat area or species population to be found in Special Areas of Conservation but to the totality of the habitats and species throughout the United Kingdom.   The results of NRW\u0027s feature monitoring work and other evidence collected (survey) or collated (other data sources) on feature extent feed in to the process of mapping and reporting under Article 17.   JNCC coordinate and provide UK-level feature reports under Article 17; England Wales Northern Ireland and Scotland submit country-level data and information to inform these reports.   This dataset only consists of the Welsh component of the Article 17 mapping.\tPublic record\n20570\tNRW_DS161356\t2025-04-11T11:25:56.162Z\tImpacts of bait collection on Welsh Marine Protected Areas (MPAs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333536\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/BaitCollectn/Outputs/ABPmer%20survey%20raw%20data?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;e\u003d0X2cxO\tAlex Zalewski, Marine Ecology Advisor\tSurvey to assess the impacts of three bait collection methods on Welsh MPAs - bait digging (Sites : Angle Bay Four Mile Bridge Penmon - Beaumaris) boulder turning (Penmon-Beaumaris West of Great Orme) crab shelters (Deganwy Bangor Pier). Samples taken from control and impact sites. Infaunal cores sediment cores benthic megafauna (i.e. razor clam cockle) quadrat worm cast count seagrass coverage boulder epifauna boulder turning classification\tPrivate record\n20280\tNRW_DS161340\t2025-02-18T10:42:24.354Z\tThe status of the Grizzled Skipper Pyrgus malvae at Dinham Meadows SSSI and the wider Mod Caerwent Training Area in 2023 \u0026amp; 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333430\t\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tWaiting for report completion\tPrivate record\n19602\tNRW_DS161297\t2024-09-26T10:07:35.491Z\tForester Conservation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323937\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Conservation.lyr\tArpana Chunilal, Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tThis layer is a copy of the Master Conservation Sites layer; where the site location is sensitive it is obscured by the application of the internal obfuscation value set in the Master Conservation Sites layer. It is a read only layer on ForesterWeb which is available to all users. The layer contains details of locations species and inspection records for all conservation sites on the National Forest Estate. The individual records are recorded as points polygons and lines. It is collected to create a record of all species on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate but also to be used for constraints in coupe planning and for habitat restoration projects.\tPrivate record\n17429\tNRW_DS98736\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.468Z\tAreas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONBs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373336\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\AONB Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty.lyr  Paper maps are stored in map store Maes y Ffynnon Unitary Local Authorities may also keep a copy of paper map of AONB for their area.\tInternal Custodian: Carole Thornley, Lead Specialist Adviser Landscape, Designated Landscapes Programme\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONBs) statutory landscape designation in Wales. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has a legal duty to maintain and make available AONB boundaries.  Following recommendations from a review commissioned by Welsh Government in 2024 AONBs were rebranded as National Landscapes. Both names of AONB and National Landscapes are currently in use. Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty remains the legal name for this statutory designation.  AONBs are designated for the purpose of conserving and enhancing the natural beauty and differ from National Parks in that they do not have a recreation remit. AONBs are managed by their constituent Local Authorities.  NRW is the designating authority to create any new AONB in Wales or boundary variations to existing ones however these must be confirmed by Welsh Ministers. NRWs statutory powers derive from the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act (NPAC) 1949 and Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. Any new AONBs designated by NRW would now be under powers derived from the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000.  Five AONBs have been designated in Wales: Gower (1956) was the first AONB designated in England and Wales Llŷn (1957) Anglesey (1967) and Wye Valley (1971) which straddles the border with England. The Clwydian Range AONB was designated in 1985 and extended by the then Countryside Council for Wales (a predecessor organisation to NRW) in 2011.  The data has been held digitally since mid-1990s. The boundary has been transferred to OS MasterMap by NRW and has been subject to joint checking by NRW staff and the relevant Local Authority staff.\tPublic record\n19396\tNRW_DS161281\t2024-08-01T08:13:34.915Z\tSpecies License Mitigation Sites 1994\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323831\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\tIwan G Hughes Species Permitting Team Leader\tThe database lists the spatial locations of protected species mitigation sites throughout Wales. These sites were proposed by applicants for licences to take or disturb protected species or damage or destroy their breeding sites  resting places or places of shelter for the purposes of demonstrating their applications would not be detrimental to the maintenance of the favourable conservation status (FCS) of the applicable local population of protected species in the long term. The majority of protected species mitigation sites have been for European protected species and consequently the majority of the sites are to facilitate the conservation of bats hazel dormouse and great crested newt The information has been collated by (a) The scanning of plans illustrating the location of mitigations as part of licence applications to the Welsh Government (WG) Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW); and (b) The submission of Geographical Information System (GIS) shape files as a condition of licences issued by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2017 (as amended) As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPrivate record\n18515\tNRW_DS121677\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.814Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Catchment Woodland Planting\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363737\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Catchment Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report. This map shows the constraints preventing catchment woodland planting. The constraints map gives an indication of the number of reasons for not planting woodland for NFM in an area. The following types of constraints have been considered: biophysical practical preservation of exisiting urban areas conservation cultural economic and legislative.\tPublic record\n18354\tNRW_DS116467\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.548Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Land Drainage Consents\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343637\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Land Drainage Consents.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tLand Drainage Consents within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge Lower Wye and Powysland IDDs mapped in the form of polygons in this vector dataset. Mapped for the purpose of Flood Risk Management. Enables the identification of previous Land Drainage Consents and aids in assessing new Land Drainage Consents in particular producing the site specific conditions.\tPublic record\n17142\tEXT_DS120344\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.624Z\tSevern Trent: Utilities Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230333434\tX:\\Utilities\\Water\\Severn Trent Sewer Lines.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Water\\Severn Trent Sewer point.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Water\\Severn Trent Water Mains.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Water\\Severn Trent Water Point.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Water\\Severn Trent Water Mains Operational Area.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Water\\Severn Trent Sewer Operational Area.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a collation of datasets detailing the extent and location of water utilities owned or managed by Severn Trent Water plc which is the parent company for several Water Companies (Severn Trent Water Hafren Dyfrdwy and Severn Trent Services).  This dataset only covers the area under management of Hafren Dyfrdwy (Severn Dee) Water Company. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this collation for internal use only.\tPublic record\n17348\tNRW_DS24267\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.635Z\tSevern Estuary Database Phase 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323637\tArchived as Project No. 427, Media No. 1464, 1465 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1464 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1465)  Update August 2021 by Nicola Rimington :\\ This database was used to inform WG policy on marine aggregate extraction that was published in 2004 and has since been superceded by the Welsh National Marine Plan 2019. It is worth retaining a copy for reference but there is limited value in sharing it internally or externally given that things have significantly moved\\\"\"\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis data is made up of the results from evaluating the nature conservation resource of the sediment environment in the Severn Estuary and Bristol Channel. This database was created for the Marine Aggregates Resource Management Planning System (MARMPS) a National Assembly project in order to create a decision-support tool for the Environment Minister when making decisions on aggregate extraction licences.\tPublic record\n18399\tNRW_DS118733\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.065Z\tDevelopment Advice Maps (DAM)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373333\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone A.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone B.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone C1.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone C2.lyr\tMark Pugh\tThe Development Advice Map (DAM) shows areas at risk of flooding from rivers and the sea for the purposes of land-use planning. The DAM supports Planning Policy Wales and Technical Advice Note (TAN) 15 to guide new development away from areas at risk of flooding wherever possible. Together they form a precautionary framework to guide planning decisions. The DAM should be considered as a trigger for identifying which development proposals may need to undertake a more detailed assessment of flooding risks and consequences in line with the policy advice set out in TAN15.    Zone C (the 1000yr extreme flood outline) was last updated in January 2020. No further updates are planned to the DAM and you are advised to contact Natural Resources Wales (NRW) about the availability of more up-to-date information.    Zone B (areas known to have flooded in the past) was originally published in 2004 and revised in 2017.  When using the DAM the preview defaults to Zone B only.  You will need to use the legend control to switch on the other zones.    NRW has developed a new Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) which was published in September 2021.  This indicates undefended flood extents over the next 100yrs taking into account the impacts of climate change. This will replace the DAM in June 2023.  Although the Flood Map for Planning is not referred to in current planning policy it can be considered as the most up to date information on flooding risks.     Due to technical Issues the Web Services and Map Browser for the Development Advice Map are currently not available. If you wish to browse the data see the NRW Flood Risk Viewer https://maps.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk/Html5Viewer/Index.html?configBase\u003dhttps://maps.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk/Geocortex/Essentials/REST/sites/Flood_Risk/viewers/Flood_Risk/virtualdirectory/Resources/Config/Default\u0026amp;layerTheme\u003d2\tPublic record\n18146\tNRW_DS116002\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.157Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Pembrokeshire 1995\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303032\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000007\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Pembrokeshire. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tPublic record\n18453\tNRW_DS121120\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.203Z\tMonitoring Invertebrate features on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs): assessing and reviewing the status of the troglobitic spider Porrhomma rosenhaueri in Ogof y Ci and Lesser Garth Cave, South Wales in 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231313230\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tData Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThis dataset was collected to assess whether Porrhomma rosenhaueri was still present in the Ogof y Ci cave system and if so to consider the viability of establishing a Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) protocol for monitoring the population within the cave; and to revisit the CSM established for the Lesser Garth Cave to collate further data on the condition of both the Porrhomma rosenhaueri population and the ecological condition of the cave.   The cave-dwelling spider Porrhomma rosenhaueri (L. Koch) is unique to the British fauna as it is considered to be the only species of paleotroglobitic spider present and whilst the cavernicolous fauna of the UK has undoubtedly been under-recorded sufficient work has occurred to suggest that Porrhomma rosenhaueri is genuinely rare.The spider has a very limited distribution in the UK and is only known from two sites in South Wales Ogof y Ci near Merthyr Tydfil and Lesser Garth Cave near Cardiff. The Ogof y Ci population was first discovered in 1971 but since this period no recorded survey work on the spider has taken place and the status of the spider within the cave was unknown. The Lesser Garth Cave population was discovered in 1979 during an investigation of the cave for the cave dwelling isopod Proasellus cavaticus. The cave has since been surveyed for the spider on a number of occasions initially in 1997 as part of an environmental impact assessment and more recently in 2009 in order to establish a Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) protocol for the longer-term monitoring of the spider population in the cave.\tPublic record\n18438\tNRW_DS119141\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.220Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Recreation points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313431\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Designated Areas\\NRW Forest Recreation Points.lyr\tCustodian: Heather Crump, National Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis is a dataset of physical recreation feature on the ground best depicted as point features. These features a divided into eight Categories.   - Signage - NRW branded signs information and navigational aids which have management activity and investment (e.g. trailhead waymarker information board)  - Built feature - Purpose built and installed NRW asset which has management activity and investment (e.g. Gate stile bin barrier)  - Forest furniture - Purpose built and installed NRW asset which has management activity and investment (e.g. Bench seat picnic table). Exercise - Purpose built and installed FC asset which has management activity and investment (e.g. Activity point).  - Play equipment - Purpose built and installed NRW asset which has management activity and investment (e.g. Play component structure).  - Viewpoint - A feature which has management activity and investment. Art feature - Purpose built and installed NRW asset or agreed feature which has management activity and investment (e.g. Art work sculpture).  - Forest entrance - Regularly used feature to gain access to the forest/NRW estate (e.g. Car park or layby access point).  - Miscellaneous - Features which do not fall into one of the existing feature types but required to be captured for management purposes.  Within each Category there are a number of Asset types and Asset Subtypes.\tPublic record\n17410\tNRW_DS46322\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.435Z\tGrassholm Northern Gannet (Morus bassanus) Survey (2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436333232\tphotos and excel key to jpgs are located at N:\\USERS\\MurphyM (was GrayC)\\SPAs \u0026amp; SSSIs\\SPAs\\Grassholm\\Grassholm survey 2004\\  H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\MurphyM\\SPAs \u0026amp; SSSIs\\SPAs\\Grassholm H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\MurphyM\\contracts\\Completed projects\\Grassholm  Report on H:TSG/Gray_C/Contract reports/Report 604 Gannets 2004 Will be held JNCC seabird database and NBN\tMatty Murphy, Senior Maritime Ornithologist, Natural Resources Planning/Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group, NRW (Formerly Catherine Gray, Ornithologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tThis dataset consists of a count of the breeding northern gannet (Morus bassanus) population on Grassholm in 2004. The purpose of this data capture was to assess population estimates for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Special Protection Areas (SPA) monitoring and to update the UK population estimate. The projected aimed to make the first complete survey of this gannetry since 1999 and to: -  assess current numbers conservation status and number of Apparently Occupied Site (AOS)  -  contribute to the decadal survey of the UK gannet population and the wider north Atlantic gannet colonies and  -  establish survey protocol for future site condition monitoring.\tPrivate record\n18600\tNRW_DS122690\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.414Z\tGeomatics AP index 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232363930\tx:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Geomatics AP index 2018.lyr\tKeith Jones\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17436\tNRW_DS88070\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.164Z\tBat Roosts Database - North East Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838303730\tThis dataset is held and managed by Cofnod. They hold the top copy of the dataset and therefore requests should go directly to them, via the NBN Gateway.  Historically: CCW data was stored in Recorder on the Mold server Rec2002 - Mold. MapInfo layer held on m:/species/North East/Mamamals/Bats/AllBats_NEWales_validated.tab.   Additional MapInfo layers are available for each of the species within the same folder.\tMatthew Ellis - see Distributor above for contact details. External Contact: Aisling May  - Rheolwr Data/Data Manager       aisling.may@cofnod.org.uk Cofnod, Intec, Ffordd y Parc, Parc Menai, Bangor, Gwynedd. LL57 4FG.  (01248) 672603\tThis dataset is the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regional database of bat roosts for all species of bats found in North East Wales.  This includes the site name grid reference species date and abundance where available. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts to assist CCW\u0027s conservation work. In particular to provide information when dealing with development proposals to support the monitoring and notification of SSSIs and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tPublic record\n17535\tNRW_DS100829\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.363Z\tWoodland Grant Scheme 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383239\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 3.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provides incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. The dataset identifies areas approved by grant type within each WGS Scheme. The list below provides a summary of grant type captured:  - Scheme Boundary: The boundary of the area(s) included within the scheme.  - New Planting Approved: Areas within the contract where new planting has been approved under New planting (NP) New planting with better land supplement (NB) New planting accepted into the 1988 set-aside scheme (NS) New planting of coppice (NC) New planting of coppice with better land supplement (NK) New planting short rotation coppice (NQ) and/or New planting short rotation coppice with better land supplement (NU).  - New Natural Regeneration Approved: Areas within the contract where new natural regeneration has been approved under New planting by natural regeneration (NN) New planting by natural regeneration with better land sup (NL) and/or New planting by natural regeneration with set-aside (NZ).  - Replanting/Regeneration/Management: Areas within the contract where replanting restocking by natural regeneration or management has been approved under Restocking by replanting (RP) Restocking by natural regeneration (RN) Restocking with coppice (planting not regrowth) (RC) RG Restocking with short rotation coppice (planting not regrowth) (RQ) Restocking by existing natural regeneration (EN) Management grant (MG) and/or Special management grant (SM).  - Community Woodland Contribution - Areas within the contract approved for Community Woodland Contribution.\tPrivate record\n17356\tNRW_DS24258\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.895Z\tStanding Waters Database (Lakes)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323538\tThe NRW copy is held at N:\\DuiganC\\lakesdb\\JNCC.MDB in MFSG.\tSenior Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW.\tThe Standing Waters Database mainly contains data on the relative abundance of lake plants.   This data can be used for: 1. Site data storage  2. Identification of rare plant sites  3. Identification of Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs)  4. Could be used in Local BAPs  5. Classification scheme used forms the basis of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) site selection  6. Provides data for Regional staff if casework arises on a site  7. Information used in SSSI management plans.\tPrivate record\n19426\tNRW_DS161282\t2024-08-12T11:35:12.357Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD River Basin Districts Cycle3.lyr\tInternal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts (RBD) is a spatial dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2 clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027…the area of land and sea made up of one or more neighbouring river basins together with their associated groundwaters and coastal waters…\u0027. River Basin Districts have been delineated by using River Catchments as \"building blocks\" that have been aggregated together within a GIS ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries. Coastal and transitional waterbodies are also merged and assigned to a river basin district. River Catchments were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model.\tPublic record\n18912\tNRW_DS124778\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.212Z\tCuRVe Diversity Topographic\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373738\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tTopography is one of the main biophysical drivers behind habitat establishment.  The output expresses on a relative scale how topographically diverse the landscape within each of the reporting squares is. Areas with higher values can be expected to contain habitats at different elevation levels encompassing areas with shallow slopes as well as steeply rising land. Furthermore areas with the highest values are expected to have slopes facing in different cardinal directions affecting the amount and timing of sunlight they receive.\tPrivate record\n17729\tNRW_DS110121\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.547Z\tOil contamination of gannets and their nests on Grassholm subsequent to the sea Empress oil spill.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313231\tReport is held in the Marine Archive as Project No. 70 and Media No. 48.  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Marine Ornithologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tGrassholm situated 23 km WNW of the entrance to Milford Haven supports the worlds third largest gannetry with an estimated population of 30000 pairs.  Gannet nests are perennial constructions 30-60 cm high built out of seaweed grass and items of floating debris collected from the sea.  Almost all gannet nests therefore contain man-made materials often in substantial quantities. Dataset contains results of investigations to assess whether nest material contaminated with oil from the Sea Empress was incorporated into gannet nests on Grassholm and if so whether there was any effect on fertility or productivity in contaminated nests. At the time of the Sea Empress incident it is estimated that 80 percent of the adult breeding population of gannets on Grassholm had returned to the colony.  On a ship based survey between the 25-29/2/96 immediately after the spill only 0.39 percent of 1279 gannets recorded showed some degree of oiling and a similar percentage were observed carrying nest material.  The small proportion carrying nest material suggests that at that time they had not started nest building.\tPublic record\n20533\tNRW_DS161353\t2025-04-04T08:43:01.493Z\tCommon Standards Monitoring of nationally important cave invertebrate assemblages in Ogof Draenen and Ogof Ffynnon Ddu, 2023 \u0026amp; 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333533\tData will be stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder 6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tTo be completed when report is finalised.\tPrivate record\n19604\tNRW_DS161299\t2024-09-26T11:14:05.358Z\tForester Other Trails\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323939\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Other Trails.lyr\tArpana Chunilal, Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting.\tThis layer holds additional trail data which is NOT held in the Rec-Walking Rec-Mountain biking and Rec- Miscellaneous routes. It is collected to create a record of trails that are not assigned any recreational status and do not fall into any other categories of layers i.e. public right of way bridal ways etc. The attributes only contain the name of the trail and it has no other identifying features. It is spatially represented as lines only. It is used for constrains in coupe planning.\tPrivate record\n20281\tNRW_DS161341\t2025-02-18T10:44:03.876Z\tA survey for the water beetles Haliplus variegatus, Hydrochus ignicollis, Hydrovatus clypealis and Limnebius aluta on selected Anglesey wetlands in 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333431\t\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tWaiting for report completion\tPrivate record\n18377\tNRW_DS116744\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.519Z\tIndexing Paper Record Cards for ad hoc Marine Species Sightings around Wales (Paper Records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373434\tIn Data Services Room in cardboard box labelled EIT Biol Data - Do No Throw Archived as Project 471, Media 1548\t\tThis is a paper records dataset which is currently stored with the Data Services Team. These paper recording cards were put together by W.E Jones and are made up of numerous records collated from various reports related to the flora and fauna around Wales. Some of these records have been taken from reports and put into the Marine Recorder but the paper records contain further information and records which have not be digitised. Some of these data may already be available in JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Records for example 1982 Littoral hard substrata of Menai Strait (see Parent ID for further information).\tPrivate record\n17536\tNRW_DS100822\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.373Z\tWoodland Grant Scheme 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383232\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 2.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Services Team ..........Keith Harri Jones\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provides incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. WGS2 operated between April 1992 and September 1994. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects in the form of polygonsThe dataset identifies areas approved by grant type within each WGS Scheme. The list below provides a summary of grant type captured: SCHEME BOUNDARY -The boundary of the area(s) included within the scheme. NEW PLANTING APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new planting has been approved under NP NB NS NC NK NQ and/or NU. NEW NATURAL REGENERATION APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new natural regeneration has been approved under NN NL and/or NZ. REPLANTING/REGENERATION/MANAGEMENT - Areas within the contract where replanting restocking by natural regeneration or management has been approved under RP RN RC RG RQ EN MG and/or SM. COMMUNITY WOODLAND CONTRIBUTION - Areas within the contract approved for Community Woodland Contribution. Abbreviations used above: NP New planting NB New planting with better land supplement NS New planting accepted into the 1988 set-aside scheme NC New planting of coppice NK New planting of coppice with better land supplement NQ New planting short rotation coppice NU New planting short rotation coppice with better land supplement NN New planting by natural regeneration NL New planting by natural regeneration with better land supp NZ New planting by natural regeneration with set-aside RP Restocking by replanting RC Restocking with coppice (planting not regrowth) RQ Restocking with short rotation coppice (planting not regrowth) RN Restocking by natural regeneration EN Restocking by existing natural regeneration MG Management grant SM Special management grant.\tPrivate record\n19047\tNRW_DS125323\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.592Z\tRiver Wye Special Area of Conservation Sonde Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333233\tZ:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Sonde Data\\Wye\\Processed\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Johnston, Environmental Reporting Team Leader (Temporary reassignment, please contact Geralene Mills)  NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Dave Johnston, Environmental Reporting Team Leader, NRW\tA number of automatic monitors are in place in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation. Data is collected for seven water quality parameters at 15 minute intervals on a semi-permanent basis to assess the state of the river and changes over time.\tPublic record\n17695\tNRW_DS110038\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.632Z\tSurvey of the Tentacled Lagoon Worm (Alkmaria romijni) at Carew Castle Mill Pond (2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303338\tThe report and data collected under this project is archived as Project 142, Media 467. Data is also available in Marine Recorder\tInternal Custodian: Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe tentacled lagoon worm (Alkmaria romijni) is a nationally scarce protected species found in lagoonal habitats in the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation. In Wales it has only been recorded from two saline lagoon sites in Pembrokeshire: Neyland Weir Pool (Westfield Pill) and Carew Castle Mill Pond.  Elsewhere in the UK it had been recorded from only a handful of sites in lagoons and estuaries along the English Channel and southern North Sea and internationally between Morocco and the Baltic where it is not uncommon. A romijni is limited to brackish water habitats and threats to these habitats include agricultural run-off and sewage coastal development and coastal defence as well as pollution from industrial discharges. There were three main purposes of this data capture:  -  To establish a repeatable baseline survey of the tentacled lagoon worm (Alkmaria romijni) at Carew Castle Mill Pond. -  To undertake a spatial survey of salinity at the sediment-water interface -  To note field observations on the integrity of the inflow/outflow system or any activities at the site that might be considered to have an adverse effect on the integrity of the site as a saline lagoonal habitat.\tPublic record\n18227\tNRW_DS116246\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.353Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Monitoring Network Cycle 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323436\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Monitoring Network Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) Monitoring Network Cycle 1 (2008-2013) is a point dataset that contains the location of monitoring sites used by Natural Resources Wales in the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). The network consists of numerous monitoring sites used to record various parameters. These data are intended to show the location and extent of the network and does not hold any actual monitored data.\tPublic record\n18383\tNRW_DS116782\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.568Z\tAd Hoc Bat Records from around Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373832\tData is stored in Recorder 6.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tLiz Halliwell, Mammal Ecologist Woodland and Farmland Mammals, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset combines the bat records Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds as a top copy within their copy of Recorder 6. It includes Important Welsh Bat Roosts Database (BATSITES-WALES) bat roosts data from Pembrokeshire hibernacula and day / night roost records Welsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat summer roost surveillance database and some regional records from Pembrokeshire and south Wales (BATDATA-WALES). This dataset gives an indication of the abundance of each species at each location and gives the annual summer surveillance records for selected nursery roost sites.  The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts to assist NRW in conservation work. In particular to provide information when dealing with development proposals to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tPublic record\n17280\tEXT_DS124708\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.406Z\tWelsh Government (WG) Cardigan and Tremadog Bays Annex I boulder/cobble reef towed video, multibeam and sidescan Sept 2019 for Assessing Welsh Fishing Activity (AWFA) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373038\tto be stored in Central Archive - to be confirmed by Stu Kay/Mark Diggle\tHarry Goudge  - Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer)\t*************Data provided by WG under an OGL.  NRW intend to analyse the data and store for internal and external use.  Any products will belong entirely to NRW however raw data remains under WG ownership *******************    Survey aim was to improve confidence and confirm the presence or absence of Annex I boulder and cobble reef (stony reef) within areas of Tremadog and Cardigan Bays where historically demersal fishing was undertaken within SACs. NRW has previous survey evidence of low or medium quality (10-40% cover) stony reef in some of these areas although confidence in this data varies and therefore needs validation and where found to be present reef extent needs confirming and mapping.    Survey output included:   * Raw processed \u0026amp; georeferenced multibeam (MBES) and sidescan (SS) survey data and metadata to include survey period start and end times locations and any explanatory notes.  * Raw video and stills image files; ship or USBL GPS logs showing locations of all video transects.   Drop-down video (DDV) survey metadata to include:     DDV tow codes start and end times (i.e. camera first on and last off seabed); Start and end times/coordinates for areas of boulder/cobble reef;    details of species and habitats visible whist viewing the onboard video feed;  and any explanatory notes that will assist with future use of the imagery data.\tPrivate record\n18077\tNRW_DS115295\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.791Z\tThe Function and Restoration of Biogenic Reefs Formed by Sabellaria alveolata\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323935\tArchived as Project 404, Media 1421, 1425, 1426 and 1427\tGIS Service Desk Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, NRW\tThe honeycomb worm Sabellaria alveolata is an example of a species that forms large well-consolidated reef structures. However as the known area of S. alveolata covers less than 0.5 % of the coastline of the United Kingdom it is considered a fairly rare species. Individuals and reefs are typically found on the low to mid-shore of the intertidal zone but have also been found in subtidal areas. In sufficient densities individual tubes of S. alveolata coalesce to form biogenic structures that may reach several kilometres in length. These reefs can provide an important habitat for other benthic vertebrates and invertebrates (La Porta and Nicoletti 2009) as well as offering lesser studied benefits including sediment entrainment/consolidation and potentially reducing wave energy transfer to the top of the shore. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the role S. alveolata has on coastal dynamics along with providing new baseline data directed towards the monitoring of the biogenic reefs.\tPublic record\n18906\tNRW_DS124780\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.070Z\tCuRVe Extent Overall\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373830\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows resilience based on extent using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tPrivate record\n17647\tNRW_DS110019\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.255Z\tField observations on the disturbance of grey seals in North Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303139\tReport and associated data are archived as Project No. 34, Media No\u0027s 76,182-185\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Officer, NRW.\tMany studies quantify the impacts of non-lethal human disturbance on animal behaviour to which animals respond by deploying what are interpreted as anti-predator strategies. These strategies are recognised as diverting time and energy from behaviours that would have occurred in the absence of human influences. In this report levels of disturbance to grey seals are measured and these take into account the variety of seal vigilance levels as the prelude to / trigger of gross disturbance. Gross disturbance here denotes the stampeding of a seal or seals into the sea where they were assembled on land or where seals \u0027crash dive\u0027 when already in the sea.  The purposes of this data capture were to: -  Record the haul-out patterns of grey seals at Henllwyn Bay on Ynys Enlli/Bardsey when subject to no disturbance caused by people in order to show examples of normal behaviour. To record examples of disturbance caused by human activity on land in the sea and in boats at Henllwyn for comparison with \u0027normal\u0027 / undisturbed behaviour.  -  Describe variables in seal behaviour observed over several tide cycles.  -  Outline some aspects of survey design to best measure disturbance.  -  Outline some site-specific management measures designed to reduce the frequency of disturbance events while also respecting the desires of people to have good views of and insights into the behaviour of grey seals at haul-out sites.  -  Provide a short review of papers and reports on seal disturbance at haul-out sites on the shores of the northeast Atlantic. This will examine management measures that have been or are about to be deployed elsewhere and the ways that such measures are being or will be monitored.\tPublic record\n18075\tNRW_DS115518\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.744Z\tBardsey Seal Counts\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353138\tArchived within the Marine Archive as Project No. 396, Media No. 1413\tInternal Custodian: Tom Stringell, Marine and Coastal Advice Team, NRW.   RSPB Custodian: Jo Porter\tGrey seals Halichoerus grypus are among the rarest seals in the world: the UK population represents about 40% of the world population and 95% of the EU population. Globally there are three reproductively-isolated stocks of grey seal: a west Atlantic (northern North American) stock; a Baltic stock; and an East Atlantic stock. The latter extends from Iceland and northern Norway southwards to northern France with the majority breeding around Great Britain and Ireland.    The extent of connectivity within the Irish Sea grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) population has been investigated every year since 2006 using  photo-identification and bio-logging techniques.  The purpose of this data capture was to survey Bardsey seal population fluctuations. Surveys were undertaken by both trained staff and volunteers.\tPublic record\n17617\tNRW_DS109861\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.881Z\tSaltmarsh Features at Cefni and Aberffraw 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383631\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media234 Archived as Project 53, media 234\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of a survey which used the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Anglesey (Cefni and Aberffraw). The purpose of this data capture is to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map areas of saltmarsh on the southern shore of Anglesey that were put forward as a potential Special Area of Conservation (pSAC) as part of the Glannau Mon: Cors Heli/ Anglesey Coast saltmarsh.\tPublic record\n19606\tNRW_DS161301\t2024-09-26T20:06:00.894Z\tForester Recreational Walking\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333031\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Recreational Walking.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tThis layer holds recreational walking routes across the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. The data collected shows a range of suitable walking routes that range from Forest trails to Heritage trails. The data is spatially recorded as lines only and it is used as a constrains layer in coupe planning.\tPrivate record\n17253\tEXT_DS123214\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.984Z\tWales Marine Plan Areas (3rd Party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323134\tX:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Marine plan area Wales WGS84.lyr ,  X:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Wales National Marine Plan Area.lyr  (British National Grid projection)\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.  There is also no information about the creator or ownership of these datasets.  Welsh Government?\tPrivate record\n17574\tNRW_DS102032\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.492Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of LR.Rkp.SwSed Pools in the Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303332\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 555 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2004 IECS\\Cei Bach rockpools\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tRockpools are an important sub-feature of the \u0027Reef\u0027 feature of the Cardigan Bay SAC in that they provide habitats of high species richness often with species that are restricted to rockpool habitats only. The nature of rockpools particularly those with sediment in the bottom provides additional challenges to developing a repeatable and reliable method for monitoring of these features. This dataset contains the results of a survey investigating the extent distribution and species richness of LR.Rkp.SwSed (sand scoured) rockpools at Llanina Point Cei Bach. Rockpools are an important sub-feature of the \u0027Reef\u0027 feature of the Cardigan Bay SAC in that they provide habitats of high species richness often with species that are restricted to rockpool habitats only. Taxonomic identification carried out by experienced intertidal surveyors.  Positional accuracy using the dGPS is expected to be 1-5 metres. The aims for the survey of the rockpools at Llanina Point Cei Bach were to: - map and assess the current extent and distribution of LR.Rkp.SwSed pools within this area - assess the species richness of these rockpools - record any presence of Japanese wireweed Sargassum muticum at the site.\tPublic record\n18783\tNRW_DS123431\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.726Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Access Routes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343331\tx:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Access Routes.lyr\tJames West, Team Leader, Integrated Engineering Team NW, Integrated Engineering North West\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18911\tNRW_DS124784\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.193Z\tCuRVe Connectivity River Modifications\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373834\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows the resilience value derived from the amount of hard engineering affecting rivers within the reporting squares. These values are based on the expected quantity of physical modifications but do not take into account the type of modification and how strongly it impacts upon the river’s capacity to act as connective feature.\tPrivate record\n17531\tNRW_DS101833\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.196Z\tFluvial Audits of Welsh River Systems\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383333\tArchived as Project 257, Media 951:   See the separate metadata entries Fluvial Audit of the Upper Usk Tributaries (URI: 110182)  Fluvial Audit of the River Dee (110183)  Fluvial Audit of the Afon Tywi (URI: 111562)  Fluvial audit of the River Wye and tributaries (URI: 111860)\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW)\tGeomorphological assessments that utilised historical information contemporary field survey and scientific literature resources have been undertaken for various Welsh river systems of high conservation importance including the Usk Wye Dee and Tywi. The purpose of these fluvial audits it to record the morphological features and to identify and understand the geomorphological processes that influence and control the channel activity and the morphological quality of the river systems.   Specific aims of each project include (but are not limited to): - Developing an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the key Welsh river systems. - Examining changes to the channel planform over time using historical maps and aerial photographs. - Investigating channel and catchment changes using historical records and consultation with Environment Agency (EA) staff and to develop a time chart which identifies Potentially Destabilising Phenomena (PDP\u0027s). - Identifying the major geomorphological features and processes occurring by undertaking a field survey of present erosion deposition bank protection flow types and sediment sources. - Identifying the key areas of channel instability (geomorphological activity) and determine the possible factors affecting this instability in particular those contributing to any severe bank erosion evident. - Determining the presence and absence of geomorphological features associated with the SAC species habitats.\tPublic record\n17814\tNRW_DS110931\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.895Z\tDatasets from the South-West Britain Sublittoral Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393331\tCCW\u0027s copy of marine recorder (see survey titles and keys in \u0027abstract\u0027) .Paper reports are available from CCW library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe South-west Britain Sublittoral Survey was a three-year project carried out between 1976 and 1979 by the Field Studies Council for the Nature Conservancy Council. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds the data from a selection of surveys that make up the project .These are listed under Alternative Title which also includes: - 1978 SWBSS Gower sublittoral survey (JNCCMNCR10000069) - 1978 SWBSS Skokholm survey (JNCCMNCR10000076) The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the nearshore sublittoral ecosystems present from Pembrokeshire around to Dorest and to assess the conservation interest of these sites. The area included in the survey is the old county of Pembrokeshire the south coast of Wales and the coast of Avon Somerset Devon Cornwall and Dorset.\tPublic record\n17537\tNRW_DS101837\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.442Z\tLlyn Tegid automatic monitoring station\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383337\tStored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.  Archived as Project 195 (Media 540, 541, 596, 953 and 1212),  project 347 (media1324), project 446 (media1487), project 462 (media 1523)\tInternal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tYear-to-year changes in the weather have a major effect on the water quality in many freshwater lakes. For example periods of heavy rain transport more nutrients from the surrounding land while periods of warm calm weather during the summer promote the growth of cyanobacteria. Llyn Tegid in Snowdonia is a deep lake that periodically develops short-lived blooms of cyanobacteria. An automatic station was installed in Llyn Tegid in October 2006 to monitor its response to extreme variations in the weather. This station is fitted with a range of meteorological sensors a chain of water temperature sensors to record the mixing characteristics of the lake and optical sensors to monitor the spatial distribution of the algae. This dataset includes raw data on parameters recorded by the station at hourly intervals such as conductivity barometric pressure mean chlorophyll a temperature at varying depths wind speed and direction solar radiation etc. The reports include an analysis of this data and a review of technical performance of the station. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the response of Llyn Tegid to variations in the weather and support more detailed studies on the climatic sensitivity of the lake.\tPublic record\n18914\tNRW_DS124783\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.640Z\tCuRVe Diversity Overall\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373833\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows resilience based on diversity using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tPrivate record\n18903\tNRW_DS124782\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.167Z\tCuRVe Extent SemiNaturalHabitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373832\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tSemi-natural habitats with their natural and diverse assemblage of flora and fauna are inherently more resilient than habitats modified strongly by human management making this an important factor to include for the attribute Extent.  The output shows the proportion of semi-natural habitats in the reporting square.\tPrivate record\n17502\tNRW_DS100359\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.737Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Photo\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333539\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_photos.lyr   Images are on DVD in the Data Services Safe.\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons. Photo locations recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (1987-2004). A detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. This dataset has all Wales coverage. Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).\tPublic record\n17946\tNRW_DS114691\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.170Z\tMerthyr Mawr Warren: Potential for Dune Reactivation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363931\tData is archived as project no. 331, media 1290\tMarine and Coastal Physical Scientist, NRW.\tThis dataset is a compilation of work carried out to assess the geomorphological feasibility of sand dune reactivation at Merthyr Mawr Warren based on a review of background environmental data air photographs LiDAR data a site visit and laboratory analysis of a limited number of sediment samples.   The Merthyr Mawr dunefield is a sediment-starved system which is undergoing re-distribution of sandy sediment from the north-western part to the south-eastern part of the system. Photographic evidence indicates that bare sand and active dunes covered a large part of the area between the late 19th century and the late 1960’s after which time the dunes became progressively stabilized by vegetation. The period of high dune mobility corresponded with a period of coastal erosion. No long-term wind or wave data are available for the area but it is hypothesized that coastal erosion frontal dune blowout development and inland dune mobility were encouraged by high average wind and wave energy conditions and perhaps a higher frequency of severe storms. Conversely the period of inland dune stability between the 1970’s and the present day was associated with beach accretion and foredune progradation. It is hypothesized that these changes were driven by lower average wind / wave energy conditions and lower frequency of severe storms.   Bare sand and active parabolic dunes have only been maintained in areas where there is high visitor pressure or other anthropogenic disturbance. The purpose of this data capture was to help inform decisions that may result in potential re-mobilisation work including impact on existing habitats and stakeholder interests.\tPublic record\n18458\tNRW_DS120063\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.849Z\tNorth West Anglesey Modiolus – Drop Down Video Survey Sept 2009 \u0026amp; 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230303633\t[A] The data is stored in the marine archive  as project 268, media 965-979 (videos), media 1307 \u0026amp; 1308 (CMACS video and image grabs), 1309 (analysis and outputs). File path \u003d K:\\Archive\\ Media 900 -1000\\Media 965 etc. etc. [B] The report is still in draft but a copy is in the DMS  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/proj/_layouts/15/DocIdRedir.aspx?ID\u003dEVID-201-24 [C] Marine Recorder surveys : [see MRCCW31900000002 and JNCC survey MRCON02100000003 in the snapshot only)\tInternal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team        Distributor contact details: Miss Liz Morris Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd, 41 High Street, Menai Bridge, Anglesey, LL59 5EF 07868 705017 or 01248 712277 liz@marine-ecosol.com www.marine-ecosol.com  Analysis was undertaken on the following CCW-held video files: 20090925Tape1NWAngleseyModiolusSurface.avi 20090925Tape2NWAngleseyModiolusSurface.avi 20090928Tape3NWAngleseyModiolusSurface.avi  Data supplied by the contractor, Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd. are: 2 Modiolus_CCW_Metadata.xls \u003d the metadata taken on the day of survey, together with summary details of ALL habitat and subsection divisions 3 Modiolus Data Entry.xls \u003d ALL raw results / data scored by the contractor. Columns highlighted in red are definite Modiolus reef; highlighted in cream are areas of potential reef; text in red are QA duplicate scores 4 Summary of NW Anglesey Modiolus Reef only Sept09.xls \u003d this excel table summarises the areas of confirmed and potential Modiolus reef only.\tThe area to the North and West of the Isle of Anglesey (North Wales UK) is one where horse mussel Modiolus modiolus beds are thought to occur. In the past several surveys have recorded aggregations of Modiolus in this area but in general the density and extent of Modiolus has not been accurately mapped. Modiolus beds are important because they are part of the Habitats Directive Annex I Reef habitat are on the OSPAR list of threatened and declining habitats and are a Biodiversity Action Plan habitat. Modiolus beds appear to be extremely sensitive to physical disturbance and are thought to have declined in extent and quality. A collaborative project between the Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) and the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) (now Natural Resources Wales) began in 2009 to investigate the possible presence of Modiolus modiolus beds and other reef habitat at two locations to the north and west of Anglesey. These locations were chosen based on historical evidence of the presence of the horse mussel Modiolus modiolus. Area NWA1-North is between 32 and 38km north of Holyhead and has an area of almost 14km2. It is characterised by a deep trench running ENE through the study area which is approximately 105m at its deepest point which shallows towards the north and south to depths of approximately 45 and 60m respectively. Area NWA2-West is located between 23 and 28km WNW of Holyhead and is almost 20km2 in area. The shallowest point is in the SE of the study area where there is a plateau at approximately 50m depth which then drops off towards the NW to the base of a slope at approximately 90m deep.   The two areas were surveyed using a Multibeam Echosounder and drop down video. The Centre for Environment Fisheries and Aquaculture Science (CEFAS) carried out a Multibeam Echosounder survey in July 2009 and the resulting data was interpreted for bedrock quaternary bedforms sediments and habitat features by CEFAS. Four sites in water deeper than 100m were surveyed using a stills camera by the Centre for Marine and Coastal Studies in June 2010. CCW then surveyed selected areas of interest using a drop down video during September 2009 and January 2010. The video footage was collected to identify Annex 1 Horse Mussel (Modiolus) reef habitat by Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd who developed methods by which to score areas of potential reef to confirm presence of live Modiolus reef and where possible quantify live Modiolus. 9 video transects were analysed. First they were split into rough sections of different habitat. Areas of potential Modiolus reef were identified scored for Modiolus and Alcyonium (a known proxy for live Modiolus in the right habitat) and then JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) style habitat surveys were conducted. MNCR style habitat surveys were also undertaken on sections of habitat which were either flat unconsolidated reefs of dead Modiolus shell or habitats where no Modiolus were present. All habitats longer than 1 minute in length were further divided into 1 minute subsections (to keep the surveyors’ attention and increase the accuracy of scoring large numbers of Alcyonium).    In total 158 subsections of habitat were analysed (92 scored for Modiolus; 66 subsections for MNCR phase 2 style habitat survey only). 2 areas of certain live Modiolus reef were identified and confirmed whilst another 7 subsections of habitat showed signs of live Modiolus that may be within areas of potential reef.  This could be due to the ‘banded’ habitats identified where unconsolidated Modiolus shell gravel embedded lag cobbles and pebbles and live Modiolus reef were identified in ‘bands’ across a possibly gradually undulating seabed. 10% of the one minute sections were resurveyed by different surveyors for Quality Assurance (QA of counts and MNCR). As much of the video scoring could be considered ‘semi-quantitative’ and partially subjective QA did not aim to fi ...\tPrivate record\n19155\tNRW_DS125697\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.468Z\tSlope for Landspreading\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363937\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\Slope for landspreading.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): To be determined.   Metadata Point of Contact: Paul Challender, Lead Specialist Industry \u0026amp; Waste Regulation\tThe Slope to Landspreading layer is a dataset derived from height data. It provides a colourised visual representation of pertinent slope angles for the regulation of landspreading activities.\tPublic record\n17770\tNRW_DS110517\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.519Z\tBadger Licence Return Data (1997 - 2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353137\tExcel spreadsheet available from Environmental Information Team, NRW.  Paper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, NRW. Some data is stored in Recorder 6\tSenior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW (Adam Cole-King)\tThis dataset consists of former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCWs\u0027) collation of licence return data for work carried out affecting badgers and their setts across Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to: -  add to the picture of  badger distributions in Wales - collect relevant data in one place and  - mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.   The dataset does not represent a systematic survey coverage  of Wales and is instead based upon ad hoc records collected from licence returns. Location of records is generally random depending on the requirements of the licence holders.  Records are provided by licence holders who have very variable recording ability. The dataset has been subject to some basic validation to eradicate erroneous outlying records and inputting errors.\tPublic record\n19632\tNRW_DS161310\t2024-10-08T11:01:46.644Z\tLocal Seascape Character Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333130\tThe working master file is stored at Y:\\Landscape\\Seascapes_LSCA\\LSCA.gdb - waiting to be loaded to X:Drive\tJohn Briggs - Specialist Advisor: Landscape\tA single all-Wales geospatial database showing Local Seascape Character Areas (LSCA). Spatial extent is to 12 nautical miles out to sea (coincident with the National Wales Marine Plan for Inshore Waters boundary). In addition coastal land areas are included (seascape being about the connections between land and sea). Land areas vary in distance from the coast line. They include the whole extent of NRW’s Flood Map for Planning Extreme Tidal Inundation plus a range of land of a coastal type such as cliffs and slopes dune systems ports and coastal settlements and a wider coastal setting area. Area boundaries reflect changes in character which may in practice be a broad zone of transition and so should not be taken as a sudden change in character.   Boundaries in the marine area are highly generalised (to 1:250000 accuracy) reflecting available evidence and are informed by a variety of different baseline datasets and field observations. Boundary lines inland are similarly generalised (but to 1:25000 accuracy) reflecting the greater availability of more specific spatial evidence. Inland boundaries follow identifiable features such as edges of built up areas changes in land cover or land use field patterns and changes in topographic pattern. Boundary lines are freshly drawn rather than being ‘snapped’ to baseline datasets and any apparent exception is unintended.  Attribute table data includes: area geographical name and number; various typologies; from which source assessment the area is derived; to which National Marine Character Area the LSCA relates; and some evaluations related to different factors. There are various URLs to link to published written profiles for areas where they exist. (These extend to several pages per area and are available for about 70% of the LSCA). NRW manages and publishes the dataset ‘once for Wales’ to promote easier access for users than the formerly fragmented datasets arising from different assessments in different areas.  In addition our LSCAs attribute each area to a National Marine Character Area in effect creating a nesting hierarchy between the two scales of assessment. LSCA introduces a new nationally consistent area numbering system to achieve this. Although baseline data has not been copied directly a number of baseline datasets have informed the characterisation both for identifying area boundaries and to inform the written profile documents. These include: UKHO rastor charts Ordnance Survey 1:25000 scale rastor map; Lidar mapping of land; NRW Flood map for Planning; Slope for Land-spreading data; BGS Seabed sediment data; Landcover Map 2020; NRW Inter-tidal survey; LANDMAP Visual and Sensory layer.   Other datasets consulted included: MMO Land-sea inter-visibility; Key variations in Marine Habitats Wave Energy and Water Depth (a dataset created for the National Marine Character Areas preparation); Seabed sediments; Land with sea views and Sea visibility from land. Some fields in the LSCA attribute table are blank at the time of writing. This reflects limited resources and it is intended to fill out all blanks in due course with further work and updates (no timescale). The attribute table and polygons at least provide a common structure into which future data can be added. It is often 3rd party work that uses the datasets that results in new data arising which are in turn be fed back into the LSCA master dataset.\tPrivate record\n18874\tNRW_DS124706\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.499Z\tNRW Flood Technical Services areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373036\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Flood Technical Services areas.lyr\tLucy Edwards\tMap of Wales split into 2 sections each section covers the area of responsibility for each of the Flood technical services team area.\tPrivate record\n19040\tNRW_DS125344\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.277Z\tWye Valley Woodlands Project 1850 -1980 - distribution of woodland species vascular plants and bryophytes - Paper Survey records\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333434\tOff site storage Box CCW/SERBC/209\t?\tPaper survey records relating to the Wye Valley Woodland vascular species and bryophytes survey.  This is a collation of existing records and new records. The survey was done in two parts.  Records compiled by Stephanie Taylor 1979 and in 1978. 1980 NCC commissioned Ian Bolt to collect records for additional sites.\tPrivate record\n20240\tNRW_DS161336\t2025-02-12T11:13:46.570Z\tPre-assessed Areas for Wind Energy\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333336\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pre-assessed areas for wind energy.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tAreas with a presumption in favour of large-scale wind energy development. Data highlights whether the area is suitable for wind solar or both.\tPrivate record\n17340\tNRW_DS24282\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.985Z\tMarine and Estuarine Fish Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323832\tThis data is stored on the DMS: Advice \u0026gt; Marine \u0026gt; Fisheries Science \u0026gt; Reports \u0026gt; Fin Fish \u0026gt; Marine and estuarine fishes of wales Entire Dataset  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/fishsci/Reports/Fin%20fish/Marine%20and%20esturine%20fishes%20of%20wales%20Entire%20Dataset.xls?d\u003dwd944d693e99c4492a4b447f54c33fe9e\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tMarine and estuarine fishes are important components of the coastal environment and their presence/absence is seen as indicative of the state of inshore seas. In view of the conservation significance of marine fishes as a major component of the marine and estuarine environment the conservation agencies of the UK have statutory obligations to examine current conservation requirement of the marine fish fauna and to establish and maintain a monitoring programme by which to review their status. Other literature sources were obtained through the National Lending Library inter-library loan scheme.\tPublic record\n18597\tNRW_DS122453\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.432Z\tEnvironment (Wales) Act Section 7 and OSPAR: Marine Habitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343533\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\tGIS and Spatial Data Team  Internal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team       There are two data layers  Oyster Beds Points and Polygones that are not included in the open data download.\tUnder the Environment (Wales) Act (2016) Section 7 requires biodiversity lists to be produced. These lists include types of habitats which are of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 for the purpose of maintain and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This list supersedes the duty in Section 42 of the NERC Act 2006. In addition to this marine habitats can be protected under the OSPAR Convention which has established a list of \u0027threatened and/or declining species and habitats\u0027 in the North-East Atlantic.   This spatial dataset details the extent and location of those marine habitats classed of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 under Section 7 and are regarded as \u0027threatened or declining\u0027 under OSPAR in Wales.   Habitats include:  Carbonate Reefs Estuarine Rock Honeycomb Worm Sabellaria alveolata Intertidal Underboulder Mud habitats in deep water Musculus Discors Green crenella Beds Blue Mussel Beds (listed under OSPAR aswell) Fragile Sponge and Anthozoans (listed under OSPAR aswell) Horse Mussel Modiolus Bed (listed under OSPAR aswell) Intertidal Mudflats (listed under OSPAR aswell) Maerl Beds live and dead (listed under OSPAR aswell) Sabellaria spinulosa reef (listed under OSPAR aswell) Saline Lagoons (listed under OSPAR aswell) Saltmarsh  Seagrass Beds (listed under OSPAR aswell) Seapens And Burrowing Megafauna (listed under OSPAR aswell) Peat Clay Exposures (listed under OSPAR aswell) Sheltered Muddy Gravel (listed under OSPAR aswell) Subtidal Mix Mud Sediments (listed under OSPAR aswell) Tide Swept Channels (listed under OSPAR aswell) Oyster Beds (OSPAR only).\tPublic record\n17369\tNRW_DS24428\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.840Z\tLicencing held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343238\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tSpecies Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW\tDetails of the species licences requested and granted by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to individuals and organisations. CCW\u0027s Operational Support Group received licence applications from individuals/companies/organisations detailing who is requesting it for what type of activity affecting which species where etc. The Species Protection Administrator enters the information into the Licensing ISIS (Oracle) database before sending the application to specialists for consultation. The licence is then either issued or not if issued details of the licence are added to ISIS.\tPublic record\n19039\tNRW_DS125350\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.238Z\tLimestone grassland survey 1987 (paper data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333530\toff-site storage, ref CCW/NRM/R-53\t\tPaper survey sheets of a 1987 survey of limestone grassland in grid reference area SJ and SH\tPrivate record\n19608\tNRW_DS161303\t2024-09-26T20:14:47.404Z\tForester Watercourses\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333033\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Watercourses.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tThis layer is a copy of the detailed river networks layer. It also contains additional features added in by NRW staff. The quality of the data is satisfactory as it is not quality controlled and it is not monitored. It is used for constrains in coupe planning.\tPrivate record\n19018\tNRW_DS125213\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.482Z\tGlastir Woodland Creation agreements/contract data and related schemes 2022-2027\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323133\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Glastir Woodland Creation 11 EoI Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Woodland Creation Planning Pilot Scheme (WCPPS).lyr\tGary.kingsbury@gov.wales RPW IT Data Management Metadata Point of Contact: Gary.kingsbury@gov.wales\tEach set of agreement/contract data contains 3 elements (shapefiles). There will be many sets of data delivered to NRW when they become available. 1. EoI (Expresion of Interest) – Welsh Government (WG) constructed from whole Land Parcel Identification Layer (LPIS) polygons covering the land where the applicant wants to construct planting compartments 2. Planting compartment (polygons) created by independent Forestry Planners – need to be within the EoI 3. Fences (polylines) - independent Forestry Planners – need to be within the EoI Once checked and confirmed correct by NRW – the data will be used in WG systems to create payments for Glastir Woodland Creation.\tPrivate record\n17545\tNRW_DS101981\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.110Z\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Sites (Line)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393831\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Sites.lyr .   Archived as project 204, media 569.\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environment Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThis is a spatial dataset comprising of data for the mean high water mark around the coast of Wales divided into line lengths reflecting the phase 1 survey sites. Information provided includes site ID site name name of Special Area of Conservation (SAC) the site is part of selection unit and length. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.   All work has been undertaken by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tPublic record\n20811\tNRW_DS161360\t2025-05-20T10:56:09.693Z\tMarine Protected Areas Compensation Habitat\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333630\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Compensatory Habitats.lyr\tKirsten Ramsay, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis data is a map of the extent of marine areas in Welsh waters that have been identified as compensatory habitat within Welsh waters and along Welsh coastlines under Regulation 68 (Compensatory measures) of The Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2017.   These areas are afforded the same protection and management requirements as the SAC Natura 2000 designated sites.   This also includes areas delivered by the National Habitat Creation Programme.\tPublic record\n19109\tNRW_DS125466\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.994Z\tSaproxylic invertebrates at Carn Gafallt RSPB Reserve in 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343636\tWID – Natur Am Byth Surveys\tMike Howe\tA survey of the dead wood (saproxylic) invertebrate fauna of the Carn Gafallt Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB) Reserve was undertaken over four days in 2022. This work is part of the Natur am Byth! partnership project which aims to promote the recovery of a range of key species in Wales.\tPublic record\n17249\tEXT_DS123459\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.987Z\tWelsh schools (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343539\tx:\\Social Economic\\Education\\Welsh schools.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.  There is no information about the ownership of this collated dataset\tPrivate record\n17733\tNRW_DS110101\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.783Z\tThe Impact of the Sea Empress Oil Spill on the Prosobranch Mollusc Paludinella littorina\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313031\tReports are held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 21 and Media No. 56 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media056).   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.  NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [MRCCW10000000089]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tIn March 1996 the Sea Empress Environmental Evaluation Committee (SEEEC) was set up by the government to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on the environment.  This contract was issued as part of the SEEEC monitoring programme designed to evaluate the impact of oil from the Sea Empress on local marine life.   Data collected in order to ascertain whether Paludinella littorina still lived at its known sites at Broad Haven and on Caldey Island after the Sea Empress oil spill and to obtain more information on the extent and density of the populations. The objectives were to:   1.  Ascertain whether Paludinella littorina is still living at its known sites at Broad Haven and on Caldey Island and to obtain more information on the extent and density of the populations.  2.  Attempt an assessment of the impact of oil damage if any and subsequent recovery to habitats at known Paludinella littorina sites.  3.  Locate and investigate other sites for Paludinella littorina on Caldey Island.  4.  Evaluate methods for monitoring and recommend a protocol for future monitoring work.\tPublic record\n18926\tNRW_DS124830\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.547Z\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ) Groundwater Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383330\tX:\\EU Directives\\Nitrates Directive\\NVZ Groundwater Points.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18792\tNRW_DS123430\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.934Z\tHeads of the Valley woodland\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343330\tx:\\Resource Management\\Heads of the Valley\\Heads of the Valley existing woodland groups.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Heads of the Valley\\Heads of the Valley existing woodland.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Heads of the Valley\\Heads of the Valley potential new woodland.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n19012\tNRW_DS125266\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.655Z\tFlood Defence Structures\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323636\tZ:\\Archive\\Water Management\\Flood Risk Mgmt\\FRA\\Maps\\FD Locations and X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Defence Locations\tBen Hext - lead specialist advisor Flood Risk Analysis\tThe National Flood Asset Database records flood infrastructure in Wales managed by Lead Local Flood Authorities (LLFAs) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW). It includes many embankments walls flood gates culverts and inlet screens (which catch debris) but not all flood infrastructure maintained or owned by these parties. This infrastructure has been constructed to help manage the risk of flooding from rivers and the sea. They do not remove the risk of flooding and can be overtopped blocked or fail in extreme weather conditions.   Three datasets are available as open data:  1. Flood defence Locations. These show the linear alignment of flood defence asset that have been built to protect against flooding from rivers and the sea. It includes the type length maintainer and an ID. These defence locations are also shown in Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) and Find flood defence structures near you maps. They may relate to an Area Benefiting from Flood Defence (FRAW) and TAN15 zone (FMfP).     2. Culverts (Polyline); These show an indicative location of underground culverts for general guidance only. Attributes include maintainer owner length and ID. Due to the underground nature and history of many culverts the locations may not be accurate complete or up to date.     3. Culvert openings and Screens (Points). These show an indicative location of culvert openings and screens (often referred to as Trash or Debris Screens). Attributes include maintainer owner and ID. Locations and details may not be accurate complete or up to date.     We will regularly update the information when new and improved data becomes available. If you have any queries or concerns about location maintenance condition or records of flood assets check with the owner or maintainer.\tPublic record\n18018\tNRW_DS114840\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.542Z\tCountryside Survey (Survey data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383430\tData held by the Countryside Survey on their website (http://www.countrysidesurvey.org.uk/home).\tMetadata point of contact: Data Services Custodians: CEH and NERC Contact: Countryside Survey Project Office, Centre for Ecology and Hydrology, Lancaster  Environment Centre, Library Avenue, Bailrigg, Lancaster LA1 4AP\tCountryside Survey in 2007 was funded by a partnership of government-funded bodies led by the Natural Environment Research Council (NERC) and the Department for Environment Food and Rural Affairs (Defra).  Countryside Survey is a unique study or ‘audit’ of the natural resources of the UK’s countryside. The Survey has been carried out at discrete intervals since 1978 (2007 1998 1990 1984 and 1978). The Countryside Survey provides scientifically reliable evidence about the state or health of the UK\u0027s countryside today. By comparing present findings with previous surveys it is possible to identify change (and the relative rate of change) in the countryside. This evidence is used to help form policies that influence management of the countryside both now and in the future.  The purpose of this data capture is to provide a \u0027snap-shot\u0027 of the health of the countryside in the UK and a comparable dataset to track changes.\tPublic record\n17944\tNRW_DS112540\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.970Z\tUnitisation Boundaries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353430\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Unitization Boundaries.lyr\tChristine Edwards, Lead Specialist Advisor: Protected Sites Programme, Protected Sites\tDesignated sites in Wales include but is not limited to Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Special Protection Areas (SPA) Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) and Ramsar sites. These sites have been divided into management units. This dataset contains the boundaries of these units and includes information such as designation type unit name unit area national grid reference and former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regions.   Digitising began in 2007 and is on going as and when new sites are created or existing sites are altered. The purpose of these management units is to have a coherent plan in order to manage sites in a practical manner to ensure that work is done to get sites and their corresponding features into a favourable condition as per the requirements of the protected sites.\tPublic record\n18631\tNRW_DS123223\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.441Z\tFreshwater fish sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323233\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Freshwater Fish Cleddau Sites.lyr       x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Freshwater Fish Lamprey West.lyr      x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Freshwater Fish Teifi Sites.lyr          x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Freshwater Fish Tywi Sites.lyr        x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Freshwater Fish West Cleddau Sites.lyr\tTristan Hatton-Ellis, Lead Specialist Advisor, Freshwater Habitats and Species, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group\t\tPrivate record\n17731\tNRW_DS110119\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.717Z\tSandy Shore Meiofauna of South West Wales-Impact of the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313139\tReport and data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 67, media No. 43 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media043),  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.   NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [MRCCW10000000072]\tNRW Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Seems more likely that Kirsten Ramsay or Matt Green would be a suitable contact\tThe high species diversity of meiofaunal communities imply a complex and intricate ecological web. However relatively little information has been recorded on the ecological interactions of meiofauna. This is largely due to the practical difficulties involved in the study of these organisms. These difficulties arise from the small body size of meiofauna their cryptic habitat and the taxonomic difficulties caused by their high species diversity. Various authors have suggested that meiofauna are potentially useful indicators of anthropogenic perturbations of the marine environment (Heip 1980; Moore and Bett 1989; Platt and Warwick 1980; Vincx and Heip 1991). This data was captured with the aim of assessing the impact of pollution from the Sea Empress oil spill on the structure and composition of meiofaunal communities of intertidal sandy beaches.\tPublic record\n17243\tEXT_DS121993\t2024-05-28T12:17:32.794Z\tWildlife Trust: Reserves (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393933\tx:\\Designations\\Wildlife Trusts\\Brecknock Wildlife Trust Reserves.lyr  x:\\Designations\\Wildlife Trusts\\Gwent Wildlife Trust Reserves.lyr  x:\\Designations\\Wildlife Trusts\\Monmouth Wildife Trust Reserves.lyr  x:\\Designations\\Wildlife Trusts\\North Wales Wildlife Trust Reserves.lyr  x:\\Designations\\Wildlife Trusts\\Radnorshire Wildlife Trust Reserves.lyr  x:\\Designations\\Wildlife Trusts\\South and West Wales WT Reserves.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a collation of spatial datasets owned by the Wildlife Trusts in Wales licenced for use to Natural Resources Wales. This datasets show the location and distribution of reserves across Wales that are owned or managed by each Wildlife Trust.\tPublic record\n17471\tNRW_DS98785\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.152Z\tProtected sites : Old SSSI 1949 Act\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373835\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SSSI 1949 Act.lyr\t\tThis is a spatial dataset. This data set is SSSI notified under the 1949 W\u0026amp;ATC Act only. They were not re-notified under the 1981 Wildlife act.\tPublic record\n18091\tNRW_DS115607\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.092Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Monitoring Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Fochno candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363037\tData is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis dataset has been obtained through monitoring of Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Fochno candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC).  The primary objectives were to map the extent and then assess the condition of three Annex 1 habitats (of the EC Habitats and Species Directive) namely \u0027Active Raised Bog\u0027 and the \u0027Depressions on peat substrates of the Rhynchosporion\u0027 (Rhynchosporion habitat) that forms part of it together with \u0027Degraded Raised Bog\u0027. Assumed to be high as undertaken by experienced Surveyors.\tPublic record\n18542\tNRW_DS121727\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.169Z\tBullhead (Cottus gobio) population condition assessment for three Welsh SAC rivers: Afonydd Cleddau, River Usk \u0026amp; River Wye. Habitats Directive reporting cycle 3 2013 – 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373237\ttop copy of data held in BIOSYS  Data also stored on W drive (H Garrett)  Report stored on the DMS as Evidence Report No. 266 -  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/Running%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Ffm%2FRunning%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203%2FAll%20SAC%20rivers%20Bullhead\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000EE02D3CE2ED1AE4085983FEDF88CC2E5\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B90B85581%2DA33A%2D4F0D%2DB9D4%2DBCB28FC39C08%7D\tHeather Garrett (heather.garrett@cyfoenaturiolcymru.gov.uk) (main author of report) Paul Hyatt, Freshwater monitoring co-ordinator, Senior Monitoring Officer Paul Greest,  Technical Specialist Fisheries.\tBy-catch records of adult \u0026amp; juvenile bullheads from other fish surveys and invertebrate kick sampling sites\tPublic record\n17916\tNRW_DS114436\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.538Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2007 to 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343336\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-342 is archived as Project No 199, Media Nos 1221 and is maintained on a backed-up server based storage at NRW . H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2010 ASML Pembs\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. In Pembrokeshire Marine SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features include Reefs. The specific area sub-feature of interest covered here are the rockpools of Pen-y-holt on the wave exposed intertidal limestone reefs of South Pembrokeshire.  The aim of this data capture was to support a report of the Favourable conservation status of SAC features through intertidal monitoring. This data capture is related to studies carried out in these rockpools between 2007 and 2010.\tPublic record\n17130\tEXT_DS115179\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.520Z\tSwansea Bay Tidal Lagoon Geophysical Investigation (February 2012) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313739\tArchived as Project 384, Media 1398\tData Services Team Sean Evans/ Laura Grant, Team Leader Marine Ecologist or Marine Ecologist, NRW\tA hydrographic and geophysical survey of the proposed Swansea tidal lagoon in Swansea Bay was carried out by Titan Environmental Surveys Ltd in the winter of 2011/12. This included singlebeam echosounder sidescan sonar surface tow boomer and magnetometer surveys. All data were referenced to OSGB36 datum using OSTN02 datum transformation and seabed bathymetry was reduced to chart datum using the UKHO Vertical Offshore reference frame (VORF) model. The dataset shows a gently sloping seabed of thin sand and gravels overlying a sequence of fine sediments of up to 20m thickness on top of boulder clay. An outcrop of boulder clay divides the site into E and W zones with featureless smooth sands in the E and predominantly gravel in the W. No bedforms large enough to produce bathymetric features were identified. The purpose of this data capture was to carry out a hydrographic and geophysical survey of the proposed Swansea Tidal Lagoon site in Swansea Bay.  Data provided to Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) for internal business use only - for consultation on the Swansea Bay Tidal lagoon casework .  Data is considered confidential and may not be disseminated.\tPublic record\n17377\tNRW_DS24337\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.962Z\tWelsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus hipposideros) Summer Roost Surveillance Database (1987-2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333337\tSpecies data: Held in Recorder 6 database   - CCW-HQ-Terr-Welsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat Summer Roost Surveillance 1987 to 2006  CCW0003800000002   - CCW-HQ-Terr-Welsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat Summer Roost Surveillance 2006 to 2011  LC0003850000006H\tInternal Contact: Jean Matthews, Mammal Ecologist Bats and Riparian Mammals, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe monitoring of Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) is required under the EC Habitats Directive and contributes towards the UK Biodiversity Action Plan for the species. This dataset comprises of two summer surveillance collations for Welsh Lesser Horseshoe bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros). The two collations date from 1987 to 2006 and from 2006 to 2011. They contain the results of annual summer surveillance of selected Lesser Horseshoe bat maternity and nursery roost sites. It includes a range of associated environmental data such as temperature and weather conditions. The purpose of this data capture was to provide standardised information for planning and development control purposes. Data is used to support notification and subsequent monitoring of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). This dataset is now classed as static as no data is currently added to these records. Instead these records are now sent directly to the Bat Conservation Trust as part of their National Bat Monitoring Programme.\tPublic record\n18130\tNRW_DS115627\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.776Z\tMonitoring Invertebrate Features on Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs): Tetragnatha striata on Newport Wetlands National Nature Reserve (NNR) Gwent (2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363237\tData held at the Gwent Levels Reserve - location to be confirmed\tKevin Dupe, Reserve Manager Newport Wetlands Team Leader, South East Region, NRW Mike Howe, Invertebrate Specialist, MFSG, NRW\tThe spider genus Tetragnatha is represented in Britain by six species: Tetragnatha extensa T. montana T. nigrita T. obtusa T. pinicola and T. striata. This dataset concerns Tetragnatha striata a species which is listed as Nationally Notable B. The Spider Recording Scheme holds 322 British records of Tetragnatha striata. However in Wales it has only been recorded from five locations: Cors Goch National Nature Reserves (NNR); Conwy RSPB Reserve; Parc Menai Bangor; Kenfig NNR; Newport Wetlands NNR. It was first discovered at the Newport Wetlands NNR in 2001 when a population was found inhabiting emergent Phragmites at what is now known as Reedbed-6. The spider constructs a small orb-web on reeds but is highly cryptic folding itself along a reed stem when at rest.  The data is used to detail the distribution of this feature across the NNR and proposes a Common Standards Monitoring protocol to enable its condition to be monitored and assessed in the future.\tPublic record\n20334\tNRW_DS161345\t2025-02-26T09:04:07.534Z\tAn insect survey of Ynyslas Dunes in July 2024, with a focus on the Pantaloon Bee Dasypoda hirtipes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333435\t\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tMike Howe to Provide\tPrivate record\n18134\tNRW_DS115626\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.845Z\tModelling the Sensitivity of Seabed Habitats to Combined Fishing Effects around Anglesey, North Wales (2011-2013).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363236\tArchived as project 444, media 1485\tCustodian:  Was Clare Eno, potentially Colin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries,  Marine and Fisheries Welsh Government or Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Habitats, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group may be able to provide information.   Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tA pilot study has been conducted to determine whether the theoretical combination of habitat sensitivity to fishing activity of different gear types. This theory was converted into a model and run using combined fishing activity data collected through the FishMap Mon pilot project to predict the sensitivity of habitats.    The objective of this data capture was to determine whether the data on resistance to fishing activity data for each fisheries habitat developed during the NRW habitat sensitivity project could be applied to the impact of cumulative fishing activity on each habitat.   Specific aims of the modelling project were to:  - Calculate footprint versus resistance curves for each fisheries habitat and fishing activity using the revised activity footprints calculated for the FishMap Mon project  - Develop a methodology to model combined fishing effects  - Test the methodology on Red Wharf Bay and Conwy Bay  - Produce an output layer at 1 km grid resolution  - Produce thematic maps    The source GIS data was collated during the FishMap Mon project using a standard questionnaire and post-processing method. The resistance and intensity definitions were supplied by the FishMap Mon project team. The curves were calculated using an automated process to ensure processing was identical for all habitats and visually verified to ensure a good fit.   This project was a pilot to determine feasibility of the use of such models therefore the output data is suggestive of the habitat sensitivity.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning Portal.\tPublic record\n18017\tNRW_DS115062\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.432Z\tPredicting the Impacts of Objective 1 Funding on Selected Aquatic Natura 2000 Sites in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303632\tData is archived as project 359, media 84.   Data is also available on the CCW M drive at the following location M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\marine_reports\\natura_2000\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Spatial Plan Energy and Infrastructure Team, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe aim of this project was to provide Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) with a clear understanding of where developments funded by the Structural Funds under Objective 1 may potentially impact sites along the Welsh coastline with aquatic Natura 2000 designations under the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC). The project has involved a number of steps including; the identification of objective one structural funding projects likely to impact on the Natura 2000 sites in Wales; the identification of Welsh aquatic maturity thousand sites and the identification of potential impacts and sensitivities based on the available information contained within Regional Action Plans (RAPS) and Local Action Plans (LAPS) and the list of priorities measures and projects in each plan. The Objective 1 funded projects have been analysed and grouped into the following categories; fisheries industrial developments infrastructure developments tourism initiatives farming initiatives forestry biodiversity and conservation initiatives and regional development. As part of this project an interactive GIS tool has been developed which identifies the Welsh aquatic maturity thousand sites within the Objective 1 funded areas and the nature conservation features for which the sites are designated. The GIS tool can be manipulated to indicate the generic impacts resulting from the likely types of Objective 1 funded projects to the features of interest at the site.  This study develops a GIS tool that aims to identify where developments funded under the Structural Funds from Objective 1 may impact Welsh coastlines that occur within Natura 2000 sites.  Although the data was based on the best information available at the time of capture the base maps (Special Area of Conservation (SAC) features and boundaries) are now considered out-of-date.  Newer sources of data are currently available.\tPublic record\n17161\tEXT_DS121845\t2024-05-28T12:15:04.988Z\tOil and Gas Authority (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383435\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Oil and Gas Authority\\OGA Licences WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Oil and Gas Authority\\OGA Wells WGS84.lyr\tGeospatial Team\tThese datasets are wholly owned by the Oil and Gas Authority and contain spatial data detailing the extent and location of licencing blocks Licences Sub Areas and Wells across the terrestrial and marine areas of Wales and England.  Licences: A Licence is a geographically defined area for prospecting exploration or extraction of petroleum resources. The Oil and Gas Authority’s licensing system covers oil and gas within Great Britain its territorial sea and on the UK Continental Shelf (UKCS). The Petroleum Act 1998 vests all rights to the nation’s petroleum resources in the Crown but the OGA can grant licences that confer exclusive rights to ‘search and bore for and get’ petroleum. Licences fall into several categories. The principal distinctions are between onshore and offshore licences and between exploration licences (which cover exploration alone) and production licences (which cover both exploration and production). The OGA has discretion in the granting of licences to help maximise the economic recovery of the UK’s oil and gas resources. Licence data is represented by 3 different datasets; Licences Licence Blocks and Sub Areas. Licence polygons represent the whole licence and have one polygon per licence. Licenced Blocks and Sub areas represent finer divisions of these licences and may have many polygons per licence. Most Licenced Blocks are split with several sub areas sometimes with differing operators and beneficiaries. Licence types included are detailed as follows:  Landward Exploration Licences have the prefix ‘LX’ (e.g. LX001). CCS Licences have the prefix ‘CS’ (e.g. CS001) and Gas Storage Licences have the prefix ‘GS’ (e.g. GS001). Other prefixes (e.g. PEDL001 EXL001 PL001 ML001) indicate a Landward Production Licence of various vintages.  Wells: A wellbore is a path of drilled footage from the Well Origin to a terminating point. The location of each wellbore in this dataset is the wellbore’s well origin. A well origin is the location on the surface of the earth or sea bed where the drill bit penetrates the earth to establish or rework a well. Definitions for wellbore and well origin taken from PPDM ‘what is a well’: https://whatisawell.ppdm.org/. Data collection starts once a well has been spudded when the operator submits a spud notification on WONS. The OGA will issue an official well number via WONS which is to be used for all data and records resulting from the well’s drilling. All well data requirements are set out in the documents Petroleum Operations Notices (PON) 9 (offshore) and PON 9b (onshore). Onshore operators have a statutory obligation to also supply well data to the British Geological Survey (BGS). Visit the BGS website for more information.\tPrivate record\n18040\tNRW_DS115271\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.793Z\tDistribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activity Project Outputs\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323731\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive at Project 376, Media 1396 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1396)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe objective of the associated project was to summarise how the fisheries habitats developed during the countryside Council for Wales (CCW) habitat sensitivity project intersected with Special Area of Conservation (SACs) SAC features and fishing activity. This sets the fisheries sensitivity work in the context of the Habitats Directive. The data used for this project was in the process of being revised therefore the aim of the project was to produce a toolkit which would allow the analysis to be repeated in future. The outputs from this project included: 1. MapBasic scripts 2. Set of processed output layers 3. Set of Excel spreadsheet summarising the data 4. Overall report archive appendix and technical appendix. The outputs were to be used by CCW to determine whether sensitive habitats were subject to fishing activity and whether these situations occurred within SACs and SAC features. The data could also be used as a baseline for discussions with fishermen.  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tPublic record\n17773\tNRW_DS110513\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.657Z\tCondition monitoring of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353133\tVarious locations.\tInternal Custodian: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team  Matty Murphy  - NRW\u0027s main contact for Bird Data\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive) and for regularly occurring migratory species. Once sites have been designated the country agencies work with owners occupiers and others to secure their management and protection regime. One element of this work was a programme of species monitoring across the site. This is a collation of existing monitoring of bird species across SPAs. This work is often undertaken by contractors or partners such as the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) and the RSPB. The RSPB have previously collated all the data on behalf of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The first round of assessments occurred between 2001-2006. Second round undertaken by funded partnership post within RSPB for assessment period 2007 - 2013.\tPublic record\n17639\tNRW_DS109654\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.049Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sea Water Turbidity Recording\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039363534\tSkomer MNR West Region drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic/sediment\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Sechi disc readings have been taken since 1992. Samples of suspended sediment have been collected from 2 sites since 1994 and turbidity probes / loggers have been used (with limited success) since 2002. The purpose of this data capture was to provide a long-term record of sediment load in the water column and water clarity for the site.\tPublic record\n17479\tNRW_DS99333\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.377Z\tGrazing Marsh Site Boundaries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939333333\t[A] X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Coastal_and_Floodplain_Grazing_Marsh.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian: Jonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat \u0026amp; Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team.   This metadata may not be complete for more information please contact Clare Burrows.\t*** This data is no longer accessible  therefore this metadata is under review 02/08/2024 ****  This is a spatial dataset containing the boundaries for grazing marsh and floodplain grasslands areas for Wales. These have been collated from the inventory and review of grazing marsh and floodplain grasslands. The aim of this data capture was to produce an inventory of the priority UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat grazing marshes and flood plains. Information not previously available due to the composite nature of the habitat. Confidence in the site outlines is good. Verification of the habitat coding was undertaken at the time of digitising but errors have subsequently been found.\tPrivate record\n18629\tNRW_DS123221\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.288Z\tMostyn channel buoys\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323231\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine Reports\\Mostyn\\Mostyn channel buoys point.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine Reports\\Mostyn\\Mostyn channel buoys line.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Port of Mostyn Casework\\Mostyn Channel Buoy Line.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Port of Mostyn Casework\\Mostyn Channel Buoys.lyr\tData originally supplied by Paul Brazier, due to role changes, now managed within the Marine Area Advice and Management Team, DPAS and Marine Services\t\tPrivate record\n17630\tNRW_DS109749\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.049Z\tCockle and Mussel Stock (bird food) Surveys in the Dee, Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373439\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive as Project No. 212, Media 151,577,578\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive) and for regularly occurring migratory species. The Dee Estuary Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet SPA support nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders as well as important cockle fisheries. The removal of cockles and the associated disturbance caused by fishing them needs to be balanced with bird food requirements. The aim of the project is to assess the food requirements of wading birds such as oystercatcher and knot that over-winter in these areas in order to set monitoring targets and to assess the implications of cockle and mussel fishing scenarios to the bird populations. The purpose of this data capture was to  provide data on stock abundance and distribution across the whole of the  survey areas. The data collected and its results will primarily be used for site monitoring and also for casework. Longer term work on the Burry Inlet may combine this data with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tPublic record\n17445\tNRW_DS87132\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.271Z\tNorth West Anglesey tidally exposed seabed and shores (2003-2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313332\t[A] Data is archived under Project No. 79, media No.\u0027s 223 and 224 (K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 224) [B] Species data are in Marine Recorder 5 (MRCCW80000000004)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. In addition to this the Habitats Directive (1992) requires the establishment of a European network of important high-quality conservation sites Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). The Habitats Directive identifies Reefs as a major category of habitats that should be considered for inclusion in SACs. Tidal rapids not only forms part of the reef environments classification but is also highlighted as an important site under the UKBAP habitats list. Tidal rapids is used to describe a broad range of high energy environments including deep tidal streams and tide-swept habitats. JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) defined rapids as ‘strong tidal streams resulting from a constriction in the coastline at the entrance to or within the length of an enclosed body of water such as a sea loch. Depth is usually shallower than five metres. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the features in North West Anglesey to help inform and define conservation objective and decisions regarding their protection and management.\tPublic record\n18923\tNRW_DS124838\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.699Z\tWater Resource Availability\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383338\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Resource Availability Wales.lyr\tEmma Amos Water Resource Planning Team\tThe Water Resource Availability data is based on a nationally consistent method and provides a national picture of Water Resource Availability for each Water Framework Directive Cycle 2 water body. Local Water Resource Availability for abstraction licensing is provided in our Abstraction Licensing Strategies (ALS) published on our website.  ALS outline the implications for abstraction licensing of the different water resource availability colours.  This national picture of Water Resource Availability may be different to local assessments in ALS particularly for regulated rivers and rivers designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) where local requirements may mean that results have been overridden.\tPublic record\n17162\tEXT_DS121848\t2024-05-28T12:15:04.878Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): British Pits, Active and Historic Quarries (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383438\tx:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS British Pits.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\\BGS Active quarries.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\\BGS Historic slate quarries.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\\BGS Significant building stone quarries.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) is a UK provider of geoscientific data information and knowledge. Natural Resources Wales has licenced access to a number of BGS datasets. These particular licenced spatial data products are known as BRITPITS active quarries historic slate quarries and significant building stone quarries and they depict the location of surface and underground mineral workings across the UK.\tPublic record\n17147\tEXT_DS121615\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.676Z\tJNCC: Offshore Special Protection Area (SPA) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363135\tX:\\Designations\\JNCC\\JNCC Offshore SPA WGS84.lyr\tKeith Jones, Spatial Evidence Manager, Evidence, Knowledge and Advice\tThe spatial dataset containing the boundaries of Special Protection Area designated in Welsh waters beyond 12 nautical miles offshore. The dataset is owned by Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have access to this dataset under an Open Government Licence.\tPublic record\n18794\tNRW_DS123419\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.948Z\tMostyn Marine Energy Park (MEP) New Development\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343139\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine Reports\\Mostyn\\Mostyn MEP new development.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Port of Mostyn Casework\\Mostyn MEP New Development.lyr\tData originally supplied by Paul Brazier, due to role changes, now managed within the Marine Area Advice and Management Team, DPAS and Marine Services\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17456\tNRW_DS99705\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.431Z\tLettings by Natural Resources Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939373035\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\Lettings by NRW current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\Lettings by NRW expired.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\Lettings by NRW proposed.lyr\tNatural Resources Wales GIS Team\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all current proposed and expired Land Agency Lettings by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). This is generally letting out of our land or giving people licences to use our land for certain activities. It is always money coming into NRW. The dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name owner scale captured expiry date unique codes.\tPublic record\n18418\tNRW_DS119270\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.360Z\tForest Management Coupes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323730\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Management Coupes.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan  Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset shows management coupes. These are areas of intended management and may not be implemented when indicated or at all. The feature description field indicates the probable silvicultural system in use and the year field when that action will take place or be completed by.  A related layer is restocking coupes which show what happens to the area after management has taken place\tPublic record\n19628\tNRW_DS161307\t2024-10-04T09:12:46.217Z\tSpecies in Peril in Wales 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333037\tData is a spreadsheet collection of a range of species and link will be supplied by Mike Howe\tMichael Howe\t\tPrivate record\n17095\tEXT_DS113077\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.418Z\t2 FULL FIELD TEST - marine data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133303737\tArchive in media 205, project 102\tGabrielle Wyn, Intertidal Team Leader, Marine and Freshwater Science Group.       test\tTest export to MEDIN WAF 31-07-2012. Test that metadata date is reflecting the last data it was edited 28-09-2012    Testing OLIBWeb 24/01/2014  Based on the GIS non  native layers  Purpose of data capture: To test the system  Methods of data capture: Not real data but based on non native GIS layers metadata.  Geographic coverage: Wales  Temporal coverage: 1900 to 2010.  Some records are recorded to the day whereas some can only be attributed to month or year.  Confidence in data: The majority of records are collected by specialists. Ad-hoc records are verified where possible.\tPublic record\n17736\tNRW_DS110478\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.229Z\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Mynydd Llangatwg and Mynydd Llangyndir\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343738\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist.  Raw data and map images are also held within the report.\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial and Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader - NRW (used to be Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tThis dataset comprised of the results from a survey of an upland site in and adjacent to the Brecon Beacons National Park carried out during May 2002 by the Central Environmental Surveys on behalf of the former Countryside Council for Wales. Within the report the survey is divided into two sites. One site includes the area within the boundary of Mynydd Llangatwg Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). The other site includes land to the south-west of the Mynydd Llangatwg SSSI.\tPublic record\n18001\tNRW_DS114723\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.129Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Blue Mussel Beds (Mytilus edulis) (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373233\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Blue Mussel Beds Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Blue Mussel Beds Polygon.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at H:\\Science\\MSFG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Additional Word documents are available as project 282, media 1098.  GIS layers are both point and polygon.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Data Services Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Blue Mussel (Mytilus edulis) beds on sediments only. Blue mussel (Mytilus edulis) beds are small blue mussels which can form extensive beds with living and dead mussels sand and mud bound together by the mussel’s sticky byssus threads. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n18527\tNRW_DS121656\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.541Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Watercourses with overhead powerlines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363536\tx:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Watercourses with OH Power lines.lyr\tKeith Jones, Spatial Evidence Manager, Evidence Knowledge and Advice\tContains the watercourses within the Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels IDD that have overhead powerlines above or near them.\tPublic record\n17126\tEXT_DS115369\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.392Z\tAudit of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) within the Mynydd Mawr Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Extended Landscape for Habitat Management in Carmarthen County Borough Council (CBC) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333639\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Adrian_f\\MapInfo Data\\Marsh Fritillary\\Habitat Quality Surveys\\Mynydd Mawr\\CCC~RGS MMawr Final GIS 2010\tGIS Service Desk Mike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW Formerly:  Adrian Fowels in CCW\tThe Marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) priority species and has been listed by the Welsh Government as species of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity in Wales (Section 74 species Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000). This species is now confined to the south-western counties of England south and west Wales western Scotland and Ireland. There is some evidence to suggest that the species range is continuing to contract along eastern edge having previously been lost in central and eastern parts of England. It has also become scarcer within this range with healthy populations now only appearing to persist where there is sufficient habitat to support their meta-populations (dynamic network of colonies) in which the species naturally occurs. Nevertheless in parts of this range its status maybe poorly known due to recording issues. For species existing in meta-populations as is the case with many of our scarce butterflies the quality and abundance of available habitat within colonising distance of local populations is extremely important. In Wales there is increasing emphasis on mapping these patches of habitat to complement surveys of marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia adults and larval webs.  This data consists of an ArcMap polygon layer illustrating the condition of habitats for marsh fritillaries in the landscape outside the Mynydd Mawr Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Project Area to help inform planning decisions with regard to Favourable Conservation Status of the SAC Feature in order to provide scientifically-justified guidance to inform planning decisions.\tPublic record\n18205\tNRW_DS116010\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.996Z\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Carmarthenshire and Llanelli Woodlands (1994 - 2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303130\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000003 Carmarthenshire and Llanelli Woodlands.\tCustodian: Woodland Ecologist.\tThis is a dataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 4607 observations in woodlands of conservation interest across Carmarthenshire and Llanelli woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.    The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tPublic record\n17267\tEXT_DS123021\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.588Z\tESTYN: Further Education (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303231\tX:\\Social Economic\\Education\\ESTYN Further Education.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17258\tEXT_DS122471\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.467Z\tLocal Authorities (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343731\tX:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Unitary Authorities.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Services Team\tThis data shows the Local (Unitary) Authority boundaries for Wales.\tPublic record\n19168\tNRW_DS125630\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.800Z\tPembrokeshire Sea Cliff Vegetation Survey 1974-1975 - (paper data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363330\tAdd in here the off-site storage location/box number\t\tSea cliff plant community data based on Dr A.J.C Malloch (Lancaster University) survey methodology.  Pembrokeshire coast was split into sections and plan communities described for each section.  Work carried by NCC.  Likely to be a small portion of a larger survey (the rest of the survey data is currently unaccounted for).  Areas of the Pembrokeshire coast split into Units 304-358 361-400 401-409.  May be related to NCC report (Malloch 1971)\tPrivate record\n17511\tNRW_DS101871\t2024-05-28T12:25:24.980Z\tCollated Saltmarsh Survey of Wales (1994 - 2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383731\tData is archived under Project No. 252, media No.\u0027s 905 and 1099\tInternal Custodian: Coastal Ecologist, NRW  Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tThis now static dataset was collected to monitoring the progress of saltmarsh development across Wales between 1994 and 2002. The dataset comprises of a collation of biological data recorded across different saltmarsh environments. This dataset is now archived as more up-to-date information is available about saltmarsh habitats across Wales. However this still forms an important baseline dataset in which to monitor the health and species richness across saltmarshes and overtime. This will help inform conservation management requirements for the important saltmarsh habitats. The more up-to-date surveys can be found in the following metadata entry: 114725 Priority Marine Habitats of Wales (coastal saltmarsh).\tPublic record\n19089\tNRW_DS125505\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.825Z\tRed Squirrel (Sciurus vulgaris) abundance and habitat preference survey data – Anglesey 2021/2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353035\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Red%20Squirrel/Research%20Papers/NRW%20Evidence%20Report%20672%20-survey%20data%20(Red%20Squirrel%20abundance%20and%20habitat%20preference%20-%20Anglesey).xlsx?d\u003dw01227aac5cc5427b85dd2d31e5e2155f\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tCustodian: Becky Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor  - Terrestrial Mammals, Terrestrial Ecosystems Groups\tProject undertaken to analyse the use of camera traps in determining population abundance of red squirrel in different habitat types on Anglesey. Two sites were used in this survey; Pentraeth and Newborough   The data contains trap locations identified by What3 words and includes details of habitat types and numbers of squirrels caught.\tPublic record\n17779\tNRW_DS110502\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.139Z\tA collation of National Vegetation Classification (NVC) data for Woodlands in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353032\tRaw data spreadsheet and excel spreadsheet on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/wood/Woodland%20Types%20and%20Classification/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FTerrestrial%2DHabitats%2Fwood%2FWoodland%20Types%20and%20Classification%2FNVC%2FSurvey%20data%2FSURVEY\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000238FEB7B97F86443BBC09DF6F9B378DE\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BC1284E03%2DE26B%2D453C%2DB036%2DB0055ED6D8F8%7D  MapInfo table located at M:\\GIS_Users\\jim_l\\NVC\\nvc report - this needs to be translated before it is put on the X Drive. Paper reports held in CCW libraries (Regional and HQ - see related titles field).\tInternal Custodian:  Jim Latham, Woodland Ecologist, NRW (James Latham, Woodland \u0026amp; Spatial Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group) Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe National Vegetation Classification (NVC) became established as the standard classification scheme for semi-natural woodlands in Britain and has wide applications to nature conservation programmes. The NVC is the basis for selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and has been adopted as a fundamental tool for planning and creating new native woodlands. This dataset is a collation of Woodland NVC data from former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) strategic surveys individual site surveys records from site visits interpretation of pre-NVC surveys and contributions from other bodies. This purpose of this data capture was to provide a useful platform from which to review current understanding of woodland NVC in Wales and give a snapshot of the woodland resource at the end of the 20th Century.  The total area surveyed is around 11500 ha from 802 sites across Wales. This data collation was also used to identify future research and survey needs. Areas requiring additional survey and any gaps in the understanding of communities could also be highlighted.\tPublic record\n17686\tNRW_DS110073\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.316Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Recreational Activities Occurring in and around Skomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303733\tImages -  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\littoral view points ,   SOP - DMS:  (Location to be provided by Helen Carby)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.    Records of all the recreational activities occurring in and around Skomer MNR. Including; diving angling canoe trips boating activity and any incidents. The purpose of this data capture was to document the usage of the MNR.\tPublic record\n17866\tNRW_DS111435\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.230Z\tResurvey of Permanent Quadrats at Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Formally a Marine Nature Reserve ( MNR ) Following the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343335\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000092)\tMetadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The results of a survey carried out to re-examine permanent quadrats and transects established in Skomer Marine Nature Reserve in 1992 (see separate metadata \"Establishment of permanent quadrats at Skomer Marine Nature Reserve\") following the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996.\tPublic record\n19712\tNRW_DS161313\t2024-11-04T09:03:58.060Z\tA survey to determine the status of the Depressed River Mussel Pseudanodonta complanata at eight monitoring stations within the River Wye (Lower Wye) SSSI in 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333133\tData stored in the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tData from a survey of the River Wye taken over a 3 day period from August 17th - 19th 2022. Sampling methodology followed that established in the previous survey in 2007 with eight monitoring sites visited from Backney Common (north of Ross-on-Wye) downstream to Bigsweir covering a 50km stretch of the River Wye. At each site 60-minute visual surveys were undertaken in the river using a bathyscope to spot mussels buried in sediments or dislodged from them using a pronged rake. The number of mussels located during that period were counted measured photographed and then returned to the river.\tPublic record\n17549\tNRW_DS101965\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.181Z\tIntertidal Designated Site Assessment Documents and Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393635\tThese reports and data are archived as Project No 270, Media No 569 and 981\tInternal Custodian; Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team\tData derived from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey have been used to evaluate the relative value of each survey site according to the JNCC intertidal Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) guidelines. The purpose of this data capture was to make recommendations and objectively evaluate the nature conservation value of stretches of the intertidal zone throughout Wales in order to create new designations for SSSI and provide evidence and identify additional of intertidal features of interest to identify features to existing SSSIs. This in turn helps to develop Site Management Statements and Management Plans for existing SSSI and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).\tPublic record\n17640\tNRW_DS109802\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.943Z\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383032\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Species.lyr .   Archived as project 204, media 569.\tCustodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a GIS dataset containing Phase 1 level intertidal species index (point data). The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team.   During the survey species were recorded in one of three ways:  1) in target notes   2) in species lists for certain (mainly specialised and nationally important) biotopes found on a site or  3) in species list for a whole site therefore precision is very variable.   Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tPublic record\n18484\tNRW_DS121591\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.377Z\tA comparison of the dune slack invertebrate faunas of Newborough Warren, Morfa Dyffryn and Morfa Harlech (2015)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353931\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tInternal Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist  Data Custodian: Data Services Team\tThis dataset are records from comparison surveys of invertebrate species from Newborough Warren where sand dune restoration occurred and Morfa Dyffryn and Morfa Harlech where natural sand dune habitat occurs. Following management works on Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve in 2013 to create pioneer dune slacks and other early-successional habitats invertebrate surveys had demonstrated a rapid dispersal/colonisation by seven of sixteen beetle species which Natural Resources Wales regards as requiring these conditions. Additional excavations in March 2015 provided an opportunity to find how quickly this colonisation occurred. Within two months three pioneer dune slack beetles - Bembidion pallidipenne Bledius subniger and Dyschirius politus - were recorded in pitfall traps set in this area all of which had previously been difficult to locate on the dune system. As it was suspected that the colonising fauna of the excavated slacks had its origin in the estuaries to the north and south of Newborough Warren surveys for Bledius beetles were undertaken in the Cefni and Braint estuaries by mapping the casts thrown up by their activities. Similar surveys were undertaken along the Afon Artro at Morfa Dyffryn and the Glaslyn and Dwyryd estuaries north of Morfa Harlech. It revealed that the Bledius colonies occurred in limited areas and seemed to be associated with sand newly deposited by wind or water and of a low organic content. Diatoms were found abundantly in the intestines of the Bledius beetles.  Pioneer slack habitat is at a premium on all Welsh dune systems other than Morfa Dyffryn where mobile dunes with drifting sand help to create pioneer slack as waves of sand move in front of the prevailing wind. This 2015 survey compared the invertebrate faunas of these naturally-occurring slacks with the artificially-engineered slacks on Newborough Warren. The opportunity was also taken to sample two slacks on Morfa Harlech a well-vegetated slack on Morfa Dyffryn and two unexcavated slacks on Newborough Warren. The survey was made at 20 sites with five pitfall traps at each and the total data set consisted of 100205 individual invertebrates spread across 505 species. The taxa used in this survey consisted of beetles spiders harvesters millipedes centipedes woodlice and a few orthopteroid insects. This study has divided a previous definition of pioneer slack on Morfa Dyffryn into pioneer slack (damp almost bare yellow sand at the foot of an eroding dune) and post-pioneer slack (upwind of pioneer slacks and more vegetated with bare sand having a black crust). Whilst there are biases with pitfall trapping and some limitations on drawing conclusions from analyses pitfall data was examined by Principal Components Analysis (PCA) after splitting the data into 13 sub-sets based on taxonomic groupings. The ground cover of the vegetation was assessed in the three slacks sampled on Morfa Dyffryn to determine the differences between pioneer and post-pioneer slacks with PCA separating them into two distinct clusters. The analysis also groups the excavated slacks on Newborough Warren close to the Morfa Dyffryn pioneer slack. This is particularly so where the data set is presented in the form of histograms of the numbers of selected species across the 20 sites.  An analysis of the sixteen beetle species regarded by NRW as being associated with pioneer slacks using information from this study and other sources (published literature species records in the NBN Atlas) suggests that several are often if not exclusively associated with the post-pioneer slack habitat. The pioneer slack beetle fauna is more properly restricted to four species - Bembidion pallidipenne Bledius fergussoni Bledius subniger and Dyschirius politus - which are also found in the estuarine habitat at the edge of the salt marsh.  A small survey of the tiger beetle Cicindela maritima found it breeding in numbers in the damp sand of the pioneer slack ...\tPublic record\n18488\tNRW_DS121565\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.906Z\tSurvey data from the 2017 Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR) Surveillance - vegetation response to grazing removal, 9th round of surveillance.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353635\tData is held on the NRW DMS within the Protected Sites, Eryri SSSI location.  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landmanps1/psg/ersn/Monitoring%20%20Surveillance/Surveillance/Cwm%20Idwal%20-%20Post%20grazing%20exclusion%20surveillance/2017\tInternal Custodian: Philip Oliver, Environment Officer, Anglesey \u0026amp; Arfon Environment Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Philip Oliver, Environment Officer, Anglesey \u0026amp; Arfon Environment Team\tThis is the data behind the ninth surveillance undertaken to report on the effect of stock exclusion since 1999 in the Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR). This surveillance of vegetation response is the first over a five yearly period and the fourth since the recording methodology was changed in 2006. During this surveillance round both the revised methodology and some elements of the original methodology were used enabling comparison further back than 2006 and interpretation of the changes to date in terms of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). Field data has been gathered in 2017 for 11 of the original (1999) 30 survey plots to feed into this ongoing surveillance program. The data gathered records the frequency of plant species selected species groups and other variables (bare ground exposed peat bare rock litter and sheep/goat droppings) from the five \u0027fixed\u0027 quadrats within each of the eleven surveillance plots covered by the 2006 baseline. The data gathered is primarily intended for use in comparing vegetation change with previous rounds of this surveillance program and is not intended for any other purpose. The data is in the form of an Excel workbook with sheets setting out: - plot locations within the NNR - quadrat locations within plots (5 quadrats per plot) - plot vegetation heights - individual quadrat species and other variable (e.g. bare ground litter droppings) presence. - individual quadrat species\u0027 % cover values - individual cell recordings (twenty 25x25cm cells in each quadrat) for species present. - Landscape photography location and framing information - Plot and quadrat photography location and framing information.\tPrivate record\n19465\tNRW_DS161289\t2024-08-27T10:08:44.721Z\tWinter Gull Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323839\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mneb/Liaison%20%20BTO/Winter%20Gull%20survey%20WiNGs/BTO%20WiNGs%20data?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tMatthew Murphy: Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Species\tThese data were collected as part of the Winter Gull Survey (WinGS) project which is a UK wide survey coordinated by the BTO and funded by the BTO Defra and country nature conservation bodies. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is the owner of the Welsh part of the data. The project aims to produce revised wintering population estimates for non-breeding gulls  in the UK and its constituent countries and also to assess numbers of each species in autumn.   Information will be provided for all gull species but with a focus on Black-headed Gull Common Gull Mediterranean Gull Ichthyaetus melanocephalus Lesser Black-backed Gull Herring Gull and Great Black-backed Gull as the six most numerous and widespread species that occur in the UK during the non-breeding seasons. Mediterranean Gulls have been added as an additional target species for WinGS recognising their increased use of UK inland and coastal waters across breeding and non-breeding seasons.\tPrivate record\n19044\tNRW_DS125331\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.262Z\tGenetic ancestry of introduced populations of sand lizard (Lacerta agilis) in Wales.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333331\tData is held within science report 596 in NRW library - Data outputs associated with this project are archived in the Supercomputing Wales system “Hawk”, physically located at Cardiff University, with additional local back-up copies stored in the Environment Centre Wales, Bangor University on server–based storage at Natural Resources Wales.\tMatthew Ellis, Senior Advisor Casework Team NRW  Sam Dyer, Specialist advisor: Terrestrial habitats and species\tThe purpose of this investigation was to use a state-of-the-art whole genome sequencing approach to test the three most plausible hypotheses for the origin of the Gower population of Lacerta agilis. Assessing the origin and ancestry of Gower sand lizards is a fundamental step in assessing the need and desirability for conservation action to preserve the population or in the case of an introduced population of a non-native lineage to consider the case for removal.  The date is presented in a report format with tables of DNA sequencing results presented in table and graphs\tPublic record\n18027\tNRW_DS114892\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.693Z\tEcological Effects of Re-Building Beaches in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383932\tData is archived the marine archive as project 354, media 1333 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1333)\tInternal Custodian: Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Process \u0026amp; Coastal Management, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tBeach re-building - also known as beach nourishment is the process of adding sediment to a beach to make it higher and wider. In an era of sea level rise beaches are under serious threat - many are already adversely affected by manmade defences that have reduced the natural sediment supply. Beaches are a key natural social and economic resource for Wales - they play a vital role in sustaining coastal tourism and underpin important nature conservation and landscape sites and acts as \u0027natural\u0027 coastal defences dissipating wave energy helping to protect both natural and manmade structures. During 2009/2010 a Steering Group comprising The Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) The Crown Estate British Marine Aggregates Producers Association (BMAPA) Welsh Government and the Environment Agency Wales (EAW) commissioned a Pilot Study to begin investigating the issues relating to the use of aggregates (sand shingle gravel etc.) for beach nourishment in Wales specifically in relation to the potential requirements for the quantity of material that may be needed to maintain Welsh beaches at current and future risk. The study is in essence a short data and literature review to establish the current level of knowledge regarding the ecological impacts (both positive and negative) of beach nourishment on the intertidal ecology of Welsh beaches.  The potential impacts of more \u0027traditional\u0027 hard engineering and the use beach control structures are relatively well understood by coastal engineers while the potential impacts of beach nourishment are less well known by those responsible for all aspects of beach management.  The aim of the study is to look at the ecological effects (including potential benefits or adverse effects) of re-building beaches. This considers the most appropriate places for beach re-building the methods used (including timing of works) and the suitability of different types of material.\tPublic record\n17219\tEXT_DS122406\t2024-05-28T12:17:03.633Z\tDŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water: Reservoir Level Data Weekly (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343036\tDMS Location:   https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/waterman/wrmr/ww/Reservoir%20Storage%20Data/Weekly%20DCWW%20data?csf\u003d1\tdata contact - Zhong Zhang\tThis dataset is supplied to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) by Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water. Welsh Water owns 91 reservoirs varying in size from 2 acres to 1026 acres.\tPrivate record\n17127\tEXT_DS115367\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.302Z\tAudit of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) Habitat Management in Bridgend County Borough Council (CBC) area August 2002 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333637\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Adrian_f\\MapInfo Data\\Marsh Fritillary\\Habitat Quality Surveys\\SWales LA surveys (RSmith) revised\tGIS Service Desk  Mike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW Formerly: Adrian Fowles in CCW\tThe Marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) priority species and has been listed by the Welsh Government as species of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity in Wales (Section 74 species Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000). This species in now confined to the south-western counties of England south and west Wales western Scotland and Ireland. There is some evidence to suggest that the species range is continuing to contract along eastern edge having previously been lost in central and eastern parts of England. It has also become scarcer within this range with healthy populations now any appearing to persist where there is sufficient habitat to support their meta-populations (dynamic network of colonies) in which the species naturally occurs. Nevertheless in parts of this range its status maybe poorly known due to recording issues.  For species existing in meta-populations as is the case with many of our scarce butterflies the quality and abundance of available habitat within colonising distance of local populations is extremely important. In Wales there is increasing emphasis on mapping these patches of habitat to complement surveys of marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia adults and larval webs.   This data consists of a MapInfo polygon layer illustrating the condition of habitats for marsh fritillaries in Bridgend. The purpose of this data capture was to ascertain the extent and condition of habitat capable of supporting populations of marsh fritillaries in order to provide scientifically-justified guidance to inform planning decisions. Confidence in this data is high because the data was collected under contract by experienced surveyors.\tPublic record\n18563\tNRW_DS122387\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.864Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Powysland English Watercourses\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232333837\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\PowysLand\\IDD Powsyland English Watercourses.lyr\tSpatial Data Team, Louise Harris\tWatercourses within the English section of Powysland Internal Drainage Board now administered by Hoople. These watercourses drain into Powysland IDD watercourses within Wales. Mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for the purpose of water level management flood defence maintenance and planning operations/work programming.\tPrivate record\n19006\tNRW_DS125294\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.257Z\tBala Sluices Water Level Records\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323934\t2 off-site storage archive boxes - number tbc\tDebbie Evans/Rob Bissell Water Resources Team  off-site storage box ref: EAW/BALASLU/1 and  EAW/BALASLU/2 Ta\tBala Sluices Water Level Records\tPrivate record\n18368\tNRW_DS116790\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.363Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Artificial Waterbodies Canals Cycle 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373930\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Artificial Waterbodies Canals Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal Contacts: Internal custodian - Rob Blackridge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Artificial Waterbodies Canals Cycle 1 (2008 - 2013) is a spatial (polyline) dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive.  As stated within Article 2.8 of the WFD \u0027artificial water body\u0027 is defined as a body of surface water created by human activity.  Canals are therefore reported as a discrete artificial waterbody dataset. Each waterbody has been assigned \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 which is a unique identifier that enables a link to WFD attributes.\tPublic record\n17467\tNRW_DS99419\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.098Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Habitat Layer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343139\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Habitat.lyr ,   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\Lookup_tables\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.      Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing point data showing the location of habitats mapped in mixed habitat polygons during the Upland Vegetation Survey.   The habitat codes have been translated from Ratcliffe and Birkes to Phase 1. Corrections made in 2014 are: missing points added for Cothi Tywi and Mynydd Mallaen and Pwllpeirian and points removed from single habitat and uniform mixed habitat polygons in Berwyn. This update was done when the voronoi version of the Phase 1 veg layer was created.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tPublic record\n17338\tNRW_DS24283\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.943Z\tGreat Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) Sites in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323833\t[A]  Species data held in Recorder 6 CCW - HQ Terr - All Wales Great Crested Newt Sites CCW000480000000C  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tLiz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tThis datasets contains the location and abundance or presence of Great Crested Newts (Triturus cristatus) across Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the distribution and abundance of Great Crested Newt as it is an Annex II species listed under the EC Habitats Directive. This dataset covers the whole of Wales although the majority of records occur in Ynys Mon and on the borders of east Wales.  The dataset has been subject to validation (checking for inputting errors). Records range in accuracy from 2 to 8 figure grid references. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.\tPublic record\n19741\tNRW_DS161315\t2024-11-11T10:12:22.984Z\tForester Acquired and Disposed rights\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333135\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\tArpana Chunilal\t1. Acquired rights Acquired rights is a key editable layer that contains data on acquired rights on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate (WGWE). This is a sensitive dataset as it contains transaction referencesnames and titles. The attributes are linked to a spatial shape that shows the extent of the acquired right(s). When any rights are disposed of they are amended on this layer and they automatically appear in the ‘Disposed Rights’ layer. This data is collected and digitised by NRW staff and it is quality controlled by the estate surveyors.  2. Disposed rights  Disposed rights is linked to Acquired Rights and all data within is derived from Acquired Rights. The layer is browse only thus data can only be viewed and not amended.\tPrivate record\n18561\tNRW_DS122304\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.849Z\tTerrestrial Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) 1km Distribution Maps for Priority Habitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232333034\tAll these distrubutions start with: X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\LBAP_Priority_Habitat_\tInternal Custodian Pete Jones but Stuart Smith is also very helpful.  Metadata: GIS Team\t*** This data is no longer accessible therefore this metadata is under review 07/08/2024 ****  Priority Habitats of Wales 1km squared distribution maps show the area totals for each habitat between 2002 and 2003. Priority habitats of Wales is mainly based on the combined results of the Lowland Habitat Survey of Wales and the Welsh Upland Vegetation Survey undertaken by the Nature Conservancy Council and subsequently the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) between 1979 and 1997. This data are the results from these surveys which have been compiled and provides information on habitat extent for individual 1 km squares across the entire land of Wales.\tPrivate record\n17872\tNRW_DS111292\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.739Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ) Sediment Interface Study 1994-1995\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323932\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.   Data available on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000059)\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. A detailed survey of the shores of Skomer Marine Nature Reserve was carried out in 1982. This dataset comprises of the results of surveys at two sites within Skomer MNR during 1994 and 1995 designed to establish monitoring sites and collect baseline survey data on the macrofauna and megafauna of the sediment/water interface in shallow subtidal regions. The purpose of this data capture was to initiate monitoring of the sediment/water interface in shallow subtidal regions taking into account both macrofauna obtained from samples and visible megafauna.\tPublic record\n18871\tNRW_DS124732\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.519Z\tOtter Road Mortality Survey (ORMS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373332\tX Drive GIS Layer: X:\\Biological\\Otter_Road_Mortality_Survey_1971_To_2021.lyr  DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Cardiff%20University%20Otter%20Project/ORMS%20Access%20Database%20Export%20-%20v3.xlsx?d\u003dw1bbd568441bc42ab93330890c936c318 MS PowerBI: https://app.powerbi.com/groups/20b3821f-8d6d-445e-a3b2-928cd79199d9/reports/5f564b08-4cf7-4b0c-aa3b-0a726901cc9e?ctid\u003d8865ef0f-acde-487c-bf17-5cb50375d757 ArcGIS Online:https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/518736dfc090454f83bbd905bf732ff3 MS Access: L:\\EAW\\Legacy Databases\\OtterRoadMortality\\ORMDb.mdb Paper files: Basement office in boxes on shelves behind Janet Buckles old desk\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian: Hilary Foster - Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species, Freshwater Habitats \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Contact: Olly Ishmael, Advisor 2,  Geospatial Services (Formerly Janet Buckles - Retired)\tThe Otter Road Mortality Survey (ORMS) database was initiated after the UK Otter Biodiversity Action Plan (which arose from the Rio Summit of 1992) identified that otter road deaths were a significant factor causing loss or decline in otter populations in the UK. The database was initiated in association with a report commissioned to identify blackspots in Wales (written by Geoff Liles and published in March 2000).  Members of the Public who spot dead otters and report them were originally asked to complete a paper form to provide observational details. These were subsequently recorded in the ORMS Access database.\tPublic record\n18994\tNRW_DS124948\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.038Z\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Turbines Point Location\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393438\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Turbines.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm (as built) wind turbine point locations. This shows all 27 turbines on the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm. Each Vestas wind turbine is 3.6MW in power. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPrivate record\n17692\tNRW_DS110070\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.551Z\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring 2007 - 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303730\t[A] Data archived as project no. 35, media 1328.   (K:\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1328) - older data is held in media no. 175. [B]  Fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  [C]  Videos are maintained in the archive within multiple Media folders (see Archive inventory) but an index can be accessed through GIS layers X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat video points\tCharles Lindenbaum, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Marine Archive: Monica Jones, Data Services Team Leader\tThe aim of this data capture was to report on the conservation status of the habitats and species within Wales\u0027 marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and enable reporting of their favourable conservation status as well as to inform site management.  Drop-down video surveys of SACs across Wales. Works is carried out by Royal Haskoning under contract to CCW and focuses on Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and Pembrokeshire Marine SAC.    This project also aims to improve design and implementation of the project harmonise the work in SACs with Water Framework Directive monitoring in overlapping areas and develop and refine Quality Control and Quality Assurance for drop-down video techniques.\tPublic record\n17223\tEXT_DS122284\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.289Z\tEnvironment Agency (EA): River Quality Objectives (RQO) Compliance (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323834\tX:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\RQO Compliance.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is owned by Enivronment Agency (EA). Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this data as part of a Data Sharing Agreement with EA. River Quality Objectives (RQOs) were based on the River Ecosystem (RE) Classification Scheme which was introduced in 1994 to replace the National Water Council\u0027s methodology.\tPublic record\n20306\tEXT_DS161342\t2025-02-21T10:59:41.681Z\tGazetteer and Bibliography of the Mines and Quarries of Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333432\tY:metalmines\\NWCROdata\tDave Johnston - Senior Specialist Advisor, Abandoned Metal Mines\tJeremy Wilkinson’s Gazetteer and Bibliography of these mines and quarries. The gazetteer currently has entries for more than 4670 documented mine quarry and other site names plus entries for more than 4300 other sites (unnamed levels pits quarries shafts etc.) identified from historic maps.\tPrivate record\n17890\tNRW_DS112404\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.013Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal species diversity layer (neighbourhood analysis)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343034\tThe data is archived as project 260, Media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team The metadata point of contact is the Data Service Team.\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the intertidal range is measured using species richness average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is used to present these metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. The purpose of this data capture was to establish intertidal species diversity from known data along the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out taking into account the source of the data its age spatial taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tPublic record\n17884\tNRW_DS111205\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.890Z\tSurveys of Harbours, Rias and Estuaries in Southern Britain - Taf, Tywi and Gwendraeth Estuaries.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323035\tPaper report (volume 1) is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000258)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Nature Conservancy Council commissioned the Field Studies Council to undertake studies of the marine inlets of southern Britain including harbours rias and estuaries in order to gain information on the ecosystems present. The study took place between 1985 and 1988 and covered 24 inlets. This dataset contains the results of a survey of the Taf Tywi and Gwendraeth estuaries. The purpose of this data capture was to: - Describe areas in terms of their physical attributes;  - Review the results of previous studies;  - Review marine resource usage e.g. fisheries diving activities etc; and - Catalogue available information and to collect information and photographs on the habitats and species present.\tPublic record\n17941\tNRW_DS112417\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.962Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal species diversity layer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343137\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity is measured using species richness average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 20 km is used to present these metrics.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out taking into account the source of the data its age spatial taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tPublic record\n18316\tNRW_DS116336\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.977Z\tWaste Permit Returns Data Interrogator\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333336\tWaste Returns database and PALs database\tDataset point of contact: John Fry, Lead Specialist Advisor, Waste Policy\tAll operators of regulated waste management facilities have to provide us with details of the quantities and types of waste they deal with i.e. waste received into site and waste sent on from site to other facilities or processes. This data is used to monitor compliance but has historically been used by the EC DEFRA and local authorities to assist in planning for new waste facilities and for monitoring against statutory targets. We have provided this data in an interrogatable format.\tPublic record\n18579\tNRW_DS122101\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.917Z\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitats Cycle 1 (2007)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313031\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2007\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.   Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset collated as part of the Habitats Directive reporting cycle 1 for Article 17 terrestrial habitats. Article 17 required a report to be sent to the European Commision every 6 years follwoing an agreed format. JNCC collate theses on behalf of the UK Country Agencies and this data was sent to JNCC for the collation.   Article 17 reports are an assessment of conservation status of the habitat and species targetted by the directive. The assessment is made based on information on status and trends of species populations or habitats and on information on main pressures and threats. An important component of the Article 17 report is a map of habitat or species distribution mapped in 10x10 km grid.\tPublic record\n17886\tNRW_DS112409\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.475Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species Diversity Layer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343039\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity is measured using species richness average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 10 km is used to present these metrics.    An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out taking into account the source of the data its age spatial taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from mean high water datum to 12 nautical miles offshore.\tPublic record\n17371\tNRW_DS45523\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.835Z\tPeregrine breeding population surveillance in Pembrokeshire (1981-2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435353233\tData is stored in Recorder 6 45 VC Pembs: Peregrine breeding sites CCW000080000004.  The data is published on the NBN Gateway within the Stackpole National Nature Reserve Species Inventory and Ad Hoc sightings from around Pembrokeshire (extracted from Recorder 6). See Parent ID.\tInternal Custodian: Paul Culyer - Senior Reserve Manager, Operational Services, South, NRW (Used to be Bob Haycock - Senior Reserve Manager, Stackpole, NRW.)  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis is a non spatial datasets map up of species data from the surveillance of Peregrine (Falco peregrinus) breeding populations around Pembrokeshire south Wales. The surveys cover both coastal and inland monitoring and just contains Peregrine data points. There were both local and national reasons for this data collection. Local Purposes: a) To maintain records of Pembrokeshire breeding population of peregrines to service update/data requirements for local peregrine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) in partnership with other members of Pembrokeshire Bird Group.  b) To assess the condition of peregrine as an Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) feature in Castlemartin Cliffs and Dunes SSSI for Castlemartin Range Integrated Land Management Plan. c) To maintain records of concentrations of peregrine relevant to management of recreation (sea cliff-climbing and coasteering)   National Purposes:  a) To contribute to decadal national/international surveys - normally managed by the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO).  b) To contribute to all Wales database maintained by the RSPB via Wales Raptor Study Group (when requested).\tPublic record\n18042\tNRW_DS115254\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.893Z\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) / Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Sidescan Data Collation and Archiving Project\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323534\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\Marine_Acoustic_data\\sidescan_data M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\Marine_Acoustic_data\\sidescan_data\\Sidescan_Index.tab Archived as Project 392, Media 1408.\tGIS Service Desk / Dave Tavner - Marine Mapping Scientist, Evidence Analysis Subtidal Team Leader, MFEG, NRW External Custodian:  Jim Burnell, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University\tAcoustic survey data for Welsh waters is collected by a variety of different organisations including Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the Bangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS). The survey location encompasses the Irish Sea including Liverpool Bay Isle of Man and Northern Irish coast. The majority of data collected from January 1998 onwards is stored in disk format at SOS and requires a specialised disk reader to be used. In order to make these data more accessible to SOS CCW and subsequently Natural Resources Wales (NRW) this project was carried out in two parts. The first part translated all the data into a new digital format and also produced GeoTiff images in both low and high resolution. The second part catalogued all the data available in a spreadsheet and mapped it spatially into a GIS based spatial index for easy visualisation and query. The data is now more readily available for use on projects such as for marine monitoring work or habitat mapping. This will give staff an easy to use spatial index of data available to them as well as additional information such as linked student dissertations or other projects.\tPublic record\n17228\tEXT_DS122123\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.424Z\tOceanWise: Nautical Boundaries (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313233\tx:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Nautical limits.lyr  x:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Marine plan area Wales WGS84.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis collation of datasets is managed and owned by OceanWise and licenced to NRW for internal use only. This data collation consists of two layers detailing the extent of Welsh waters and jurisdiction boundaries at 3 6 and 12 nautical miles.\tPublic record\n18496\tNRW_DS121398\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.252Z\tUK Coastal Flood Forecasting (UKCFF) Tide Gauge Network\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333938\tAlthough NRW is supplied the whole dataset, the Mumbles head data is the only data held in WISKI 7 Hydro and telemetry.\tAndy Wall Alison Hanson Liam Perrott, Technical Specialist Hydrometry       Not to publish to data.gov.uk\tThe UK Coastal Flood Forecasting (UKCFF) is a partnership formed by the Environment Agency (EA) Scottish Environment Protection Agency (SEPA) Department for Agriculture and Rivers Agency Northern Ireland (RAN) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Through the partnership the authorities commission and deliver the capability to monitoring and forecasting of coastal conditions that underpin coastal flood forecasting and flood risk modelling throughout the UK.   The network includes 43 gauges most of which are related through the national levelling network to Ordnance Datum Newlyn. The whole network is owned by the Environment Agency who operates the network on behalf of the UKCFF partners. Data are collected processed and archived centrally to provide long time series of reliable and accurate sea levels. The British Oceanographic Data Centre (BODC) act as Data Archive Centre for this data.\tPublic record\n19002\tNRW_DS124943\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.255Z\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Substation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393433\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Substation.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm Substation Location. The location of the on-site substation. This is where transformers change the 33kV input from the wind turbines to a 132kV rating for use in the National Grid. This the final true data received from the developer ((RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17539\tNRW_DS101839\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.684Z\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Management Reports\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383339\tThis data is stored on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Geodiversity/gcr/Data%20%20Statistics/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Geodiversity/gcr/SitePages/Home.aspx  reports are held here: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/rl/CCW%20GCR%20Site%20Management%20Reports/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tRaymond Roberts, North Region Earth Science Officer, CCW\tMulti-page reports produced for each Geological Conservation Review (GCR) site. The reports contain geological and conservation information comprehensive site photography and local information. The Geological Conservation Review looked at identifying geological sites across the UK that make up a unique contribution to Britain’s Earth Heritage. The Geological Conservation Review started in 1997 when a more systematic approach to site selection occurred. The objective of the review was to identify those sites needed to show all the key scientific elements of the Earth heritage of Britain.\tPublic record\n18384\tNRW_DS116723\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.471Z\tWelsh Permitted Waste Operations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373233\tCustomer Relationship Management (CRM)  Permitting and Licencing (PALS).\tData point of Contact: Tim Morris Waste Advisor\tThe EU Waste Framework Directive (2008/98EC) provides the legislative framework for the collection transport recovery and disposal of waste. The directive requires all Member States to take the necessary measures to ensure waste is recovered or disposed of without endangering human health or causing harm to the environment and includes permitting registration and inspection requirement.   Article 28 of the Waste Framework Directive specifically requires Member States to publicly make available any data relating to waste facilities.   Natural Resources Wales (NRW) issue waste permits across Wales to anyone who deals with waste of any kind. This dataset contains details obtained from the returned permits of the waste facilities located across Wales. These waste sites are categorised into groups with each sub-type of waste site being linked to the overarching category.\tPublic record\n20084\tNRW_DS161327\t2025-01-20T11:05:24.317Z\tSands of Life (SoLIFE)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333237\tMuch of the data resources are stored in the DMS here - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/Protected-Sites/so-life/D1%20Physical%20Monitoring/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTgyMjcuMjAyMjIiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D\u0026amp;CID\u003dec5979a1%2D702b%2D0000%2D415a%2D9285ebb2dfae\u0026amp;cidOR\u003dSPO\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Flandman%2FProtected%2DSites%2Fso%2Dlife%2FD1%20Physical%20Monitoring%2F00%20SoLIFE%20Data%20Archive   Data available in a GIS format is found here - X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life  Users should see individual Sands Of Life Datasets for specific locations\tKathryn Hewitt - Lead Specialist Advisor, Sustainable Farming Scheme\tSands of LIFE was a major conservation project to rejuvenate sand dunes across Wales which ran to June 2024.  The project’s aims were to recreate natural movement in the dunes and revitalise habitats which are home to some of our rarest wildlife.  A range of datasets were created as part of this project which included data for Great Crested Newts Sand Lizard Fen Orchid\u0027s Invertebrates and Bryophytes. For more information on data created from this project please see child metadata entries. This data includes survey results pre-intervention and post-intervention.\tPublic record\n18485\tNRW_DS121588\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.391Z\tThe status and distribution of the scarab beetles Rhysothorax rufa and Onthophagus nuchicornis on Welsh dunes in 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353838\t[A]  Report is held in the DMS report Archive [B]  Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tInternal Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team\tThe Endangered Red Dune Crawler Rhysothorax rufa has only been recorded from a handful of dune systems in England and Wales and has not been recorded in England for over fifteen years. In Wales it is known from Kenfig Burrows where a single adult was found on 20th July 1994 and from Merthyr Mawr Warren where single adults were recorded in June 1981 and 21st May 2006. Adults feed on detritus in sand. A survey in 2017 failed to find the beetle at either location. Current management at both Kenfig Burrows and Merthyr Mawr Warren is considered sympathetic for the conservation of this species.  The Vulnerable Dillwyn\u0027s Dung Beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis was formerly widespread across England and Wales. It has declined dramatically in England and is now known from just five of 30 previous locations where it is recorded infrequently and often as single individuals. In Wales it is restricted to coastal sand dune systems and there are records from fifteen sites from Merthyr Mawr Warren in the south-east to Morfa Harlech in the north-west. Larvae can be found in the dung of a range of mammals but are probably dependent upon that of large herbivores such as cattle ponies and sheep. A survey in 2017 of the known sites utilising both hand-searching and baited pitfall traps found Onthophagus nuchicornis at seven localities - Kenfig Burrows (975 adults) Merthyr Mawr Warren (36) Morfa Harlech (1) Oxwich Burrows (4) Pembrey Burrows (32) Pennard Burrows (4) and Whiteford Burrows (5). The population on Kenfig Burrows is the strongest in the UK. Elsewhere absence and low population levels are strongly correlated to cessation of grazing and the quantity and quality of available dung. Management including grazing regimes are considered sympathetic for the conservation of this species at three sites (Kenfig Burrows Merthyr Mawr Warren and Whiteford Burrows) although there is room for improvement. Increased access for cattle to mobile dunes and disturbed areas of open sand would undoubtedly benefit Onthophagus nuchicornis. Management recommendations are made for all currently-occupied sites.\tPublic record\n17259\tEXT_DS122506\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.506Z\tEnvironment Agency: All Wales 2017 Saltmarsh Zonation Polygons (WFD Cycle 3) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353036\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh Areas\\All Wales 2017 Saltmarsh Zonation Polygons (WFD Cycle 3).lyr\tMarine Monitoring Officer\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. Saltmarsh zonation polygons are determined in a desk based study using aerial imagery. This data is Open but owned by the Environment Agency.\tPublic record\n17967\tNRW_DS114564\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.585Z\tMorphodynamic Modelled Outputs for the Mawddach Estuary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353634\tData outputs associated with this project are archived as Project No. 318, Media No\u0027s 1253 and 1262 to 1266  (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1253 etc.)\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis report summarises the third stage of a modelling research study undertaken by Bangor University investigating the hydrodynamic and morphodynamic response of representative Welsh estuaries (which include both past and potential future changes to the estuaries as well as their present form) to a range of mean and extreme climatic conditions. In particular coastal flood risk and potential for erosion and accretion has been assessed through the medium of high resolution computational models. The Mawddach estuary lies within the Snowdonia National Park north Wales and its landscape and environment represents one of the key natural assets which Wales possesses. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the response of the estuary to future tidal and atmospheric changes. The model has also been used to investigate the response of the estuarine dynamics to potential management strategies such as embankment re-alignment and re-location.\tPublic record\n17141\tEXT_DS120801\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.737Z\tPlantlife International: Important Arable Plant Area Data for Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230383031\tX:\\Biological\\Plant Life\\Plant Life Arable plant species point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Plant Life\\Plant Life Arable plant species poly.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Plant Life\\Plant Life IAPA richness.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Plant Life\\Plant Life IAPA stat tetrad grid.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team External Contact: Beth Halski, Biological Data and GIS Manager, Plantlife International - The Wild Plant Conservation Charity Email: beth.halski@plantlife.org.uk\tPlantlife International collects and holds a large quantity of data on plants and habitats of conservation importance throughout the UK from a network of individuals and organisations. The Important Arable Plant Area (IAPA) are based on the presence of particular threatened species exceptional plant assemblages and priority habitats and this method is used to highlight arable farmland in the UK of three levels of importance: Sites of European Importance; Sites of National Importance; Sites of County Importance. The datasets include: - The Welsh Important Arable Plant Area (IAPA) Status Tetrad data contains information at the 2x2 km scale for the presence of individual UK IAPA species which has been totalled to provide the IAPA score for each tetrad. This has been assessed against the broad-soil present in each tetrad to conclude whether each tetrad is of County National or European Importance for UK IAPA plants and the number of rare and threatened arable plants (Criteria A) has been summed. - The Wales Arable Plant Species Data which contains information on 30 arable plants that are rare and threatened in Wales. Each species has either or both a point or polygon Shapefile containing location and date information. - The Wales Field IAPA and Richness dataset contains information of the presence of each UK IAPA species at a field level. This includes the data of the last survey for each field the last time when each species was recorded in the field the UK IAPA score for the field a count of the IAPA species in each field (species richness) and the area of each field. - The Wales vice-counties IAPA map contains information of the Important Arable Plant Area (IAPA) score for each Welsh vice-county.\tPublic record\n18003\tNRW_DS114731\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.220Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Intertidal Underboulder (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373331\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Intertidal Underboulder Points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Intertidal Underboulder Polygons.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.    The data is related to Intertidal underboulder communities only. Intertidal underboulder communities are unique environments from the rest of the shoreline due to residing in damp and shaded areas which provide refuge on an otherwise hostile beach. These environments are characterised by sea mats sponges sea squirts barnacles coat-of-mail shells tube worms and tufts of pink coralline seaweed encrust the undersurfaces.  Sea slugs such as the sea-lemon feed on encrusting sponges and the European cowrie eats sea squirts.  Brittlestars porcelain crabs (which are actually lobsters) and squat lobsters also cling to the undersides of boulders. The undersurfaces are an important refuge for the eggs of fish dog whelks and sea slugs. Crabs and fish hide amongst the boulders and they may be a particularly important shelter for young edible lobsters.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n18805\tNRW_DS123984\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.252Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Incidental Records\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233393834\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data\tInternal Custodian: Jennifer Owen, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team       Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tIncidental records are used to record species of importance (may be rare invasive of local value) which were not within the confines of the original survey method or were outside the range (spatially) of the original survey.\tPrivate record\n17186\tEXT_DS121956\t2024-05-28T12:15:59.617Z\tPlantlife International: Important Plantlife Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393536\tX:\\Biological\\Plant Life\\Plantlife International\\Important Plantlife Areas.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tPlantlife International collects and holds a large quantity of data on plants and habitats of conservation importance throughout the UK from a network of individuals and organisations. This data collations is owned by Plantlife International and has been licence to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for use. The Important Plant Area (IPA) programme is intended to inform and underpin existing international regional and national conservation programmes and legislation. Ultimately Plantlife aim to have Important Plantlife Areas (IPAs) acting as a benchmark for determining whether the strongest protection under any existing legislation is being afforded to the most important sites for plants.\tPublic record\n18941\tNRW_DS124811\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.873Z\tSurface Water Catchments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383131\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Surface water catchments.lyr\tSimon James Flood Risk Analysis (FCRM) David Tarrant Flood Risk Analysis (FCRM) Mark Pugh\tThe datasets shows surface water catchment areas.  The data was produced to create the National Hazard Maps for Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses (2019) as part of the National Flood Hazard Maps (2019)\tPrivate record\n17590\tNRW_DS108805\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.063Z\tMarine Monitoring Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383035\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring points.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring areas.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring points.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring transects.lyr   Archived as project 345, media 1321.\tContact: GIS and Spatial Data Team / Marine Monitoring Team, NRW\tSite location of Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRWs) Marine Monitoring Sites within Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  This is general location of the sites where marine monitoring takes place and does not show the individual sample locations. It includes the location of monitoring sites for subtidal rock subtidal sediment intertidal rock intertidal sediment lagoons marine climate monitoring sites marine birds and bird food sites seals and cetacean monitoring sites as well as areas where acoustic and drop down video monitoring takes place. Information is also available for potential sea cave monitoring sites however this is not currently a NRW monitoring programme. The purpose of this data capture was to provide an overview of Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) Marine SAC Monitoring Sites. This is a Wales-wide but concentrated in the SAC and Special Protection Areas (SPAs). However additional sites will be added as they are identified.\tPublic record\n17585\tNRW_DS101997\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.809Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rocky Reefs in Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393937\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 556 (K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters (Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2005 IECS\\BedrockQuadrats Marine Recorder file: MRCCW30200000018 Arcview layers available in Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now forming part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The aim of this data capture was to establish a set of permanent monitoring sites representative of the rocky shore intertidal habitats within the Haven Waterway in order to implement a long-term monitoring programme. The purpose of this data capture and project was to collect and present baseline data on the current status of six sites within the Milford Haven waterway for future comparisons of distribution and abundance of rocky shore organisms.  As part of the Pembrokeshire Marine SAC monitoring of the Haven waterway is required to provide regular reporting on the condition of the Habitats Directive features within the SAC.\tPublic record\n17262\tEXT_DS122695\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.538Z\tNorthern Ireland outline\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232363935\tx:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Northern Ireland outline.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18475\tNRW_DS119362\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.490Z\tPowys Intensive Livestock Units 2004 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333632\tX:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Powys intensive livestock units 2004 2017.lyr\tRachel Jarvis - conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team\tA comprehensive dataset of the locations of intensive livestock units (poultry and pigs) in Powys operational since 2004.  This is an ongoing working dataset and will be updated periodically to reflect any new developments across Powys. For internal staff use only.  See also Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2008 2017 - NRW_DS119363 and Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2017 only - NRW_DS119364.\tPublic record\n17189\tEXT_DS121944\t2024-05-28T12:15:59.620Z\tOceanWise: Marine Raster Charts (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393434\tX:\\Basemapping\\Marine Charts\\Marine Raster Data - BNG.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Marine Charts\\Marine Raster Data - WGS84.lyr\tGIS and Spatial data Team\tThis collation of datasets is managed and owned by OceanWise and licenced to NRW for internal use only. Raster Charts comprises of the well-known and popular nautical chart series from the UK Hydrographic Office supplied as individual image files for easy loading and use in GIS.\tPublic record\n17831\tNRW_DS111181\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.824Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Transects in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313831\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 549 [A]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\MarineMonitoring\\MarineMonitoring Reports\\No 58 - Carm Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Data [C] CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder  [D] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\GIS [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Images\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.    Between 1999 and 2001 the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) undertook a Phase 1 biotope mapping survey of the intertidal areas of the Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   This dataset includes the results of surveys of the biotopes present along eight transects around Carmarthen Bay. Thirteen transects had been surveyed with a very similar methodology in 2005.  This survey repeated one of those transects and established seven new transects. The purpose of this data capture was to provide sufficient information to identify the biotopes present and describe the composition of the communities; and to establish sites for long-term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods.\tPublic record\n17152\tEXT_DS121623\t2024-05-28T12:14:11.092Z\tNational Trust: Neptune Coastline Campaign (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363233\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\National Trust\\Neptune Coastline Campaign Land Use 2014.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\National Trust\\Neptune Coastline Campaign Land Use 1965.lyr\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team GIS and Spatial Dataset\tThese spatial datasets details over 50 years of land use change around the coast of Wales in areas owned by the National Trust. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this data through an Open Government Licence (OGL).\tPublic record\n18260\tNRW_DS116269\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.035Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323639\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Rivers Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team  Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 2 are a spatial (polygon) dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. \u0027WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2\u0027 is a subset extracted from the NRW\u0027s Detailed River Network (DRN).   Updated to 2018 version\tPublic record\n18004\tNRW_DS114734\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.352Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Mud Habitats in Deep Water (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373334\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Mud in Deep Water WGS84.lyr      Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tData Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Mud habitats only. Mud habitats in deep water are fairly stable environments typically found below 20 meters where currents are slow and they are not affected by waves. This can be found offshore and in sheltered inshore environments such as sea lochs.  Most of the animals that live here burrow below the surface.  The animal communities vary according to the levels of silt clay sand and nutrients found in the mud. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n17309\tEXT_DS125586\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.699Z\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Plus - Crops (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353836\t\t\tDeveloped in collaboration with Remote Sensing Applications Consultants Ltd (RSAC) the maps exploit satellite data - Copernicus Sentinel-1 C-band SAR (Synthetic Aperture Radar) and from 2016 onwards Sentinel-2 optical data.   The crops from 2016 onwards are winter wheat (including oats) spring wheat winter barley spring barley oilseed rape field beans potatoes sugar beet maize and improved grass. Other cereals root crops early potatoes and vegetables are grouped in a class called ‘other’ together with a small number of parcels which could not be classified. From 2020 solar fields (sites with solar panels at ground level) are also included.  UKCEH Land Cover® plus: Crops is based on the Land Cover Map parcel framework. Every parcel which is larger than 2ha and categorised as agricultural land is coded with crop type information. Where parcels contain two or more crops preliminary processing is applied to subdivide parcels into separate cropping units.\tPrivate record\n18162\tNRW_DS115844\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.663Z\tSOS N Llyn and Tremadog Bay Fishers Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383434\tArchive Project: 419  Media: 1437 and 1491 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1437 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1491)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\tPrivate record\n18531\tNRW_DS121655\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.498Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Mowing One Side\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363535\tx:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Mowing One Side.lyr\tKeith Jones, Spatial Evidence Manager, Evidence Knowledge and Advice\tWatercourses within the IDD that are flailed (Mowed) one side due to access restrictions and environmental reasons. Produced to aid the production of the works programme and to ensure watercourses are managed in agreement with stakeholders.\tPublic record\n18311\tNRW_DS116339\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.920Z\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333339\tThis dataset sits within Kieco - WISKI module.   [A]  This data is available on Sharefile and is classed as Open Data and static at the moment, until we can get an update out of WISKI.\tNicola Wiltshire, Environmental Monitoring Officer 1, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW Mel Lacan, Senior Environmental Monitoring Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW       Notes from 17/07/2018 The RHS data only currently goes up to 2014. When EAW split from EA we got an excel spreadsheet which contains the 1994 - 2004 data, extracted from EA\u0027s Ecosys module. This RHS acts as summary dataset, which is adequate for most people to use, and it includes indices of habitat type. This data is now stored in Keico (WISKI) which contains a lot of data from 2003 onwards. Pre 2003 the methodology was different so it has been hard to get the data into keico, Mel Lacan is hoping that by the end of the year, the data will be entered and they can start reporting again, therefore updating this dataset.\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) is Natural Resources Wales standard for collecting data on the physical character and quality of river habitats across the Wales. This dataset provides survey results carried out from 1994 to 2014.  The bulk of surveys were carried out between 1994 to 1997 and 2006 to 2008. Surveys are still carried out for specific drivers for example assessing habitat availability and Water Framework Directive.\tPublic record\n17945\tNRW_DS112565\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.920Z\tOpen Access - Open Country (CRoW Act)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353635\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Open Country 2014.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Open County 2004.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Chris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tThe CRoW Act provides for public access on foot to certain types of land amends the law relating to public rights of way increases measures for the management and protection for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and strengthens wildlife enforcement legislation whilst providing for better management of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB). Open Country 2014 is digitised to OS MasterMap. This data was part of the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 (CroW Act). When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access  it should be used in conjunction with Open land Open Country Registered Common Land Other Statutory Access Land and Other Dedicated Land from the same years. Registered Common Land is a faithful copy of Local Authority commons.\tPublic record\n18379\tNRW_DS116722\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.507Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mospolys Layer voronoi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373232\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Mospolys voronoi.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset of Phase 1 mosaic polygons. The polygons are a grid of squares coloured in chequerboard fashion according to Phase1_Code and in proportion to their extent to enable the visualisation of mosaics in a GIS. The mosaic polygons were created by the NRW Phase 2 Tools from the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer - Voronoi - GIS dataset and the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mosaic Layer Voronoi - GIS dataset.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales 1km square dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage and the maps were digitised as part of the project to map Open Access land. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales. Voronoi was carried out to enable full representation and visualisation of the components of complex mixed-habitat upland polygons.\tPublic record\n18513\tNRW_DS121700\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.760Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Floodplain Woodland Planting\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373030\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Floodplain Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report. What the map shows: This map shows the opportunity space for planting floodplain woodland to deliver NFM benefits. It is important to note that most effective use of these opportunity spaces would be to plant deep-rooting species particularly broadleaved species in order to fully realise NFM potential.\tPublic record\n17569\tNRW_DS102058\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.214Z\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Chemistry Spatial Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303538\tX:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Chem Network.lyr\tPlease contact Nick Davies ( Environment Agency)\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to NRW for internal use only.   The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains River quality chemistry. GQA covering England \u0026amp; Wales displayed in varying thickness according to flow and colour according to (year 2000) and chemistry.\tPrivate record\n18884\tNRW_DS124694\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.510Z\tAn assessment of the condition of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior at Whiteford Burrows NNR, October 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363934\tReport to be stored in DMS Reports Archive Data to be stored in the Wales Invertebrate Database (WID) in Recorder 6  -\tMike Howe       Metadata to be supplied and data location of raw spreadsheets to be confirmed.  Date to be entered into Recorder 6 - 15/01/2019\tSingle day assessment visit was undertaken in October 2018. which allowed an overview site-survey combined with off-site bulk sample analysis to assess most of the sand dune/salt marsh transition zone and allowing the field assessment of the snail at 11 sites which also involved the removal of bulk vegetation samples for off-site determination of snail presence. The results revealed V. angustior to be present at seven sites including several in the northern transition zone an area in which the snail had not been recorded previously and which had been overlooked for study since the 1993 survey. The 2018 results demonstrated the presence of the snail over a linear distance of 1.9km extending the previously recorded range by 1.2 km a 171% increase\tPublic record\n19629\tEXT_DS161308\t2024-10-04T10:27:37.541Z\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2022 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333038\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 10m Classified Pixels.lyr X:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 25m Rasterised Parcels.lyr\tGeospatial (GIS) Team\tLand Cover Map 2022 (LCM2022) is the tenth UK land cover map produced by UKCEH (others include LCM1990 2000 2007 2015 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021). Land cover is given as 21 UKCEH Land Cover Classes based on Biodiversity Broad Habitats (BAP see Jackson 2000).   LCM2022 was created by classifying Classification Scenes comprising Sentinel-2 Seasonal Composite Images combined with a series of Context Layers designed to reduce spectral confusion. The classification process is automatic using a technique we call Bootstrap Training combined with a Random Forest classifier.     NRW has access to the 10m Classified Pixel and the 25 m Pixel Rasterised Parcel Datasets\tPrivate record\n18085\tNRW_DS115276\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.974Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog monitoring at Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323736\tAssociated data for the 2006 monitoring report is held at NRW at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\29 European Dry Heath at Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC  Electrinic copies: Held on exCCW Document Store - Fynnon: Word Report  DCT-12-519992 Habitat Condition Assessment of the European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog within the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC 2012. Excel Spread Sheet of results: DCT-12-318185 SAC Monitoring 2012 Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt Dry Heath Wet Heath Blanket Bog Results\tData Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring is undertaken at the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC in order to assess the condition of the three designated Annex I habitats present. Data for monitoring cycles 2006 and 2012 is available.  The purpose of this data capture is to assess the condition of European Dry Heath Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog habitats within the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC.\tPublic record\n17813\tNRW_DS110947\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.891Z\tMonitoring of Algal Diversity and Richness on the Glacial Moraine of Sarn Badrig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393437\tAll electronic data is archived under project 221, media no. 587.   Specimens are held in the Marine Monitoring Team Herbarium and scanned versions of these are held on the MFSG drive.   Incidental video is stored on the imaging machine.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSarn Badrig (St Patrick\u0027s Causeway) is a belt reef running in a south-westerly direction approximately 18 km out from Mochras Point near Harlech. This reef forms part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) protected under the EC Habitats Directive. The reef is composed of glacial moraine deposits and is exposed in patches at low water along most of its length.  The reef structure is very mobile and subject to tidal currents wave action and sand scour and therefore many of the seaweeds found on the reef are annual or perennial species able to survive the scoured conditions.  These include a number of rare species that would be overgrown by species that would normally establish on more stable reef environment. The purpose of this data capture was to map the distribution of substratum types and select biological indices.  Also to assess and determine a suitable methodology to monitor algal richness within a benthic habitat of dynamic substrata.  The site will only be assessed for its algae richness and diversity every 2 years.\tPublic record\n17271\tEXT_DS124768\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.047Z\tEnvironment Agency (EA): Area of Interest (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373638\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\EA Area of interest.lyr\tGeospatail Team\tThis dataset is owned by Enivronment Agency (EA). This is a dataset that is used to define the areas by which NRW extract Water Quality Sampling Data and Water Quality Permits to send to the Environment Agency as part of the Data Exchange Agreement.\tPublic record\n18312\tNRW_DS116352\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.925Z\tMarine Non Native Species (NNS) records from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Monitoring Research and Ad-hoc Sightings ( extracted from Marine Recorder )\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333532\tNon Native Species Data - Sargassum muticum around Wales [A] Data is archived under Project No. 151, media no\u0027s. 230 and 485 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media201to300\\Media 230 and K:\\Archive\\Media 401to500\\Media 485) [B] Species Data is held in Marine Recorder: 1998-ongoing CCW Sargassum muticum records for Wales MRCCW30000000047  Across Wales Marine Non-Native Species Survey around Welsh Marinas  [C] Data is archived as project 321 and media 1269 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1269) [D] Species data is stored in Marine Recorder 2011 CCW/SOS Across Wales Non-Native Species Survey MRCCW31300000002.  Comprehensive reassessment of Non Native Species in Welsh marinas [E]  Species data is held in Marine Recorder: 2014 NRW Comprehensive reassessment of NNS in Welsh marinas MRCCW32900000002 [F]  Currently stored at Z:\\Achive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_Drive\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder\\MarineRecorderData\\MRCCW329_BishopsGroup_MBA\\MRCCW329_NBNDATA.mdb   [G] This data is now available on the NBN Atlas [H] Also available as a GSI layers: X:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\tInternal Custodian: Jennie Jones, Officer 2 (Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tThis dataset comprises of marine surveys undertaken by the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) (and former Countryside Council for Wales) for Marine non-native species currently held in Marine Recorder. These datasets have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway. The purpose of this data capture was to collate and monitor the spread of non-native species in Wales. The surveys represent on-going non-native monitoring sightings and ad-hoc records of invasive and non-native species supplied to NRW or noted outside of other research and monitoring work. This published dataset is kept separate from other species survey data provided by NRW to allow it to be updated more often. This data will contribute to the information already available in the UK non native species portal.\tPublic record\n19107\tNRW_DS125460\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.956Z\tEryri SSSI Bryophyte Survey 2015\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343630\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FEryri%20Bryophyte%20Survey%20BBS%202015\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Terrestrial Habitats and Species Specialist Advisor\t18 cwms and two summits in Eryri SSSI were surveyed by 30 bryologists from the British Bryological Society including 9 national experts between 7th and 12th June 2015. The survey was coordinated by the Natural Resources Wales non-vascular plant ecologist.\tPublic record\n17515\tNRW_DS101860\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.331Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sediment infauna community data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383630\tSkomer MNR. West Region drive shared document/data/biological/sediment/infauna.     Data updated every 4 years    Data is stored in NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (survey numbers MRCCW10000000058, MRCCW10000000080, MRCCW10000000095, MRCCW30900000002, MRCCW30900000003, MRCCW3000000005F)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Species abundance data from grab samples within Skomer MNR from 1993 onwards. Complimented by sediment grain size analysis and various oil content measures. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the sediment infauna community within Skomer Marine Nature Reserve.\tPublic record\n18381\tNRW_DS116796\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.510Z\tInvertebrates Recorded on Exposed Riverine Sediments at Llanelltyd on the Afon Mawddach in 2016.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373936\tData is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6 Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: Adrian Fowles)       The contact (address) details of the Originator, Adrian Fowles, are withheld as personal data.\tIn order to alleviate flooding problems on the Afon Mawddach at Llanelltyd Natural Resources Wales is considering the removal of gravel from shingle bars immediately upstream and downstream of the bridge. Data was collected to evaluate the current significance of these bars for Exposed Riverine Sediments invertebrates and to indicate the possible impact of gravel removal on the Exposed Riverine Sediments fauna.  This dataset holds specialist invertebrate species of Exposed Riverine Sediments recorded on three dates in July and September 2016. Comparisons are made with a similar survey carried out on the Tan-lan bar in 2003 and Exposed Riverine Sediment Quality Indices are presented.   Unseasonal floods during August and September may have limited the range of species but it would also appear that changes to the topography of the Tan-lan (upstream) sediment bar may also be responsible for limiting the area of habitat. The current survey did not yield sufficient species to allow a reliable index to be calculated but there is no reason to suppose that the Llanelltyd shingle bars are no longer important to the Exposed Riverine Sediments assemblage on the river.\tPublic record\n20028\tNRW_DS161324\t2025-01-08T09:23:11.062Z\tNational Environment Programme - 2024 Price Review Final Determination\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333234\tWaiting for X:drive location.\tRuth Johnston \u0026amp; Inoka Manatunga - Integrated Water Planning Team\tThe Price Review 2024 (PR24) Final Determination (FD) National Environment Programme (NEP) dataset is a Wales-wide spatial point dataset that identifies the environmental legislative obligations on water companies operating in or benefitting from Wales’s natural resources. These include five water companies; Dwr Cymru Welsh Water Hafren Dyfrdwy United Utilities Severn Trent Water and South Staffordshire and Cambridgeshire Water. Delivery of the identified actions occurs in the Asset Management Plan (AMP) period. The PR24 NEP will be delivered in AMP8 from 1 April 2025 to 31 March 2030. These actions are intended to ensure compliance with new and changed environmental obligations relevant to the water companies. This dataset is published reflecting the version of the NEP used in Ofwat’s final determination for PR24. It is correct as of December 2024. During its delivery in AMP8 the NEP actions may be subject to change.\tPrivate record\n18953\tNRW_DS124889\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.574Z\tPublic Service Boards\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383839\tX:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Public Service Boards.lyr\tData Custodian: Oliver Ishmael, Geospatial Advisor\tThis dataset shows the boundary of Wales\u0027 19 Public Service Boards (PSB). The PSB improve joint working across all public services in each local authority area in Wales. Each board must carry out a well-being assessment and publish an annual local well-being plan.   The plan sets out how they will meet their responsibilities under the Well being of Future Generations (Wales) Act. Further Information: https://gov.wales/public-services-boards https://www.futuregenerations.wales/work/public-service-boards/\tPublic record\n18459\tNRW_DS120044\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.909Z\tCondition assessment of the Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa in Llyn Tegid in 2014\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230303434\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive  Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database - Recorder 6\tCustodian: Mike Howe, invertebrate ecologist NRW.  External Contact / Originator - was put here because it looks as if this might be his home address as well - therefore in Responsible Organisation Martin Willing is C/O NRW. Dr Martin J Willing 14 Goodwood Close MIDHURST West Sussex GU29 9JG\tAs part of the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Common Standards Monitoring programme this report documents the results of monitoring for the Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa during 2014 at Llyn Tegid using the Conservation Objective produced for this snail (Willing 2004 2006 2010). Llyn Tegid was visited in late October 2014 and over two days eight survey-stations situated around the lake were monitored (those used on nine previous occasions since 1999) for the presence of the snail (and associated molluscs) using the sampling procedures as detailed in the Conservation Objective. M. glutinosa was found at all 7 sites where it had previously been recorded in numbers that using Condition Assessment standards allows the status of the snail to be judged as in a Favourable Condition in the lake in 2014. The population has been in Favourable Condition since standardised monitoring first took place in 2002. The habitat at all of the sites was also judged to be in ‘good condition’ in 2014. Results from surveys undertaken since April 2000 are also included in the report. Following status monitoring work a live sample of M. glutinosa was taken under licence from NRW from the lake survey to allow captive observation of the snails to be undertaken and to investigate the possibility of captive breeding of the snail. Initial observation of the captive Myxas has revealed that the snails emerge from beneath stones in darkness and feed upon encrusting algae both on stones and glass tank sides. Myxas bred in captivity at the end of the experiment.\tPublic record\n18538\tNRW_DS121844\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.060Z\tThe current status and distribution of the beetles Anthonomus conspersus, Aulacobaris lepidii and Thinobius newberyi on selected Welsh Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383434\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database - Recorder 6\tCustodian: Dr. Mike Howe - Invertebrate Ecologist  Contractor: Adrain Fowles, his home address is the same as his business address - contact Mike Howe for more information\tThe three species selected for this contract are all Individually Qualifying Features on their respective SSSIs yet they had not been recorded on those SSSIs for at least twenty years. There is no evidence that Anthonomus conspersus or Aulacobaris lepidii had been looked for in the intervening period and although there was some survey effort in 2004 which could have yielded records of Thinobius newberyi this is a difficult species to find. With habitat conditions for all three ostensibly remaining suitable there was no reason to suppose that any of their populations had become extinct.  Over such a long period however confirmation of their continued presence is clearly desirable.  The rowan weevil A. conspersus was found to be strongly established at Coed Boch y rhaeadr in Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SSSI with adults recorded throughout the wood and on the adjacent ffridd. Historically the area was probably managed as pasture woodland and numerous ancient rowan trees are scattered throughout the more densely wooded parts of the site as well as the open hillside. A detailed survey of the tree resource was not possible in the time available but a rough estimate suggests that there are more than 300 trees present. A map of the denser concentrations is provided. For at least 20 years grazing stock has been fenced out of the wood as part of an agri-environment scheme and this has led to an increase in birch scrub on the lower slopes. At present there are sufficient clearings to provide suitable conditions for A. conspersus but consideration should be given to some management to retain these open spaces in the wood.  The yellow crucifer weevil A. lepidii was recorded twice on the Dee Meanders in Afon Dyfrdwy (River Dee) SSSI in the 1990s but has not been seen since. Five sites were selected for survey from aerial photographs where exposed sand and gravel was obvious on the riverbanks as A. lepidii breeds in the roots of crucifers growing in sandy sediments. A further two sites were visited that had been identified as containing potential habitat during a previous survey. The species was not found during any of these surveys and vegetational succession had taken place at both of the recommended sites such that little or no exposed sand was seen. Hostplants of the weevil were generally scarce on the remaining sites and at four of the five trampling by livestock was noticeable with the hostplant crucifers being heavily grazed. It is probable that A. lepidii still occurs along this stretch of river but the disturbance to the shingle bars is a concern and consideration should be given to fencing some of them to give protection to the shingle invertebrate fauna.  The shingle rove beetle T. newberyi is a feature of Gro Ty\u0027n yr Helyg SSSI on the Ystwyth and Rheidol Shingles \u0026amp; Backwaters SSSI on the Rheidol. At the original site on the Afon Ystwyth Gro Ty’n-yr-helyg the river has altered course to remove most of the former bar. Suitable sediment is deposited further downstream but this is much smaller in extent than previously. On the Afon Rheidol the original site of records in the 1990s is no longer suitable as dense willow carr has become established over the whole area. Efforts to reach other shingle bars in the floodplain were frustrated by deep water or dense scrub and the only bar investigated at Abercwmdolau had a high silt load in the sediment whereas clean sand or fine gravel are thought to be required by the species. Hand-searching and excavation on both rivers failed to discover any examples of the target species but this is a very difficult species to find and its absence from this survey should not be taken as evidence of extinction. The current state of the Rheidol is worrying but future flood events may rework sediments to create new bars. On the Ystywth potential sites up and downstream from Ty’n-yr-helyg should be investigated to assess their potential for shingle invertebrates.\tPublic record\n17518\tNRW_DS101862\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.549Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sediment Surface Epifauna Community Surveys\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383632\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/sediment/ epifauna.    2001 data also available in Marine Recorder (MRCCW16800000009).   2004 data is also available in Marine Recorder (MRCCW30900000004).\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference data team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  The aim of this data capture was to describe and monitor the sediment surface community within the Skomer Marine Reserve. The project was an investigation into the sediment surface communities from two sites at Skomer MNR.\tPublic record\n17208\tEXT_DS121877\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.477Z\tForestry Commission Great Britain (FCGB): National Forest Inventory Felled and Windblown (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383737\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory Felled 2016.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory Felled 2017.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory Felled 2018.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory Felled 2021.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory Windblow 2016.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory Windblow 2017.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory Windblow 2018.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory Windblow 2021.lyr\tGeospatial Services Team\tThis is a subset of the National Forest Inventory and specifically details the felled and windblown datasets.\tPrivate record\n17144\tEXT_DS121617\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.646Z\tSites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363137\tX:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Sites of Interest for Nature Conservation (SINC).lyr  X:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Wrexham Wildlife Sites.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Flint Wildlife Sites.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Denbigh Wildlife Sites.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Conwy Wildlife Sites.lyr\tKeith Jones GIS team (keith.jones@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of the boundaries of all Sites of Interest for Nature Conservation (SINCs) also known as Wildlife Sites in Wales owned by the Local Authorities and licenced to Natural Resources Wales. SINC\u0027s are intended to provide comprehensive coverage of sites of nature conservation value. SINC\u0027s do not have statutory protection (unless they are also SSSIs or LNRs) but local authorities are expected to take account of the need to protect them in deciding their planning and development policies. SINC\u0027s sites are designated by local authorities in cooperation with local wildlife trusts and other local stakeholders. Information includes site name site description reason for designation total area (ha) and which local record centre area the site is in.\tPublic record\n18478\tNRW_DS121596\t2024-05-28T12:50:12.948Z\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G East Proposed Turbines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353936\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G East Proposed Turbines.lyr\tCustodians: James Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the wind farm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tPublic record\n17680\tNRW_DS110095\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.693Z\tSkomer Island National Nature Reserve (NNR) - Resurvey of Lichen Quadrats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303935\tArchived at Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media044.    Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset is made up of the results from a survey of lichen distribution and cover on Skomer Island following the Sea Empress oil spill. Pembrokeshire is of the highest significance for coastal saxicolous and terricolous lichens and Skomer is a key site in Countryside Council for Wales’ (CCW\u0027s) lichen monitoring programme.   After the Sea Empress oil spill SEEEC decided the Skomer lichens should be re-surveyed. Record type distribution and extent of lichens within the 40 quadrats established on Skomer Island. Compare these records with those from earlier surveys and determine the nature of any changes that have taken place. Interpret any changes as far as possible with special consideration given to those that may be attributable to oil pollution. Re-mark quadrats by indicating their precise position.\tPublic record\n19447\tNRW_DS161285\t2024-08-19T11:08:35.712Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323835\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Management Catchments Cycle 3.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS\tWFD Management Catchments Cycle 3 is a dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Management catchments are the unit of geography for which action plans are drafted in implementing the WFD. This process was conducted by using expert judgement in consultation. WFD Management Catchments have an action plan published that relates to all waterbodies that fall within its boundaries.\tPublic record\n18049\tNRW_DS115170\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.429Z\tSea Watch Foundation Welsh Bottlenose Dolphin Photo-Identification Catalogue (1980-onwards)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313730\tArchived as project 382, media 1394. All data is held in separate media number #1410 (2005-2012) This data encompasses the catalogued report used for identification held as Media 1394, and the photo-identification catalogue itself including left and right photographs of individuals is held as Media 1410 subsequent data for 2013 Archived as project 441, media 1481 Data for 2014-2016 archived as project 479, media 1556\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tLong term studies of individuals through photo-identification (frequently termed photo - ID) of naturally marked cetaceans can reveal much needed information using a technique that is non invasive. It provides information on group structure site fidelity movement patterns and population size. In conjunction with other studies long term photo ID can also enhance descriptions of life history parameters such as age at sexual maturity calving intervals reproductive and total life spans. In order to provide a photo identification catalogue of individual dolphins seen in Welsh Waters to provide further information of dolphin pods in Wales.\tPrivate record\n19011\tNRW_DS125258\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.658Z\tWales Construction and Demolition waste arisings survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323538\thttps://naturalresourceswales.sharefile.eu/share/view/s65d2984bde9a4d5aabf47af3a0d7ef4a/fo74f4e3-fe88-43d1-a02d-7c9a96c06426\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Fry, Project Executive, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  John Fry, Project Executive, NRW\tThe purpose of the study was to provide information on the types quantities origins (by Construction and Demolition sector and geographic region) and fate (management method) of Construction and Demolition (C\u0026amp;D) wastes generated by businesses and the public sector in Wales in 2019. This is a stand-alone survey and supersedes the data from the last survey for the 2012 calendar year.   This information is required for a variety of reasons including:     • statutory reporting on waste generation in compliance with Part 2 of Schedule 1 of the Waste (England and Wales) Regulations 2011 and up until 2020 the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002;  • informing the development of national waste policy;  • monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets;  • informing waste planners and the regulator;  • supporting investment decisions such as the selection of the type and scale of new waste management facilities required by local authorities and the waste industry;  • updating the evidence base for the State of Natural Resources Report Wales assessment (Natural Resources Wales 2021) and;  • providing businesses with a better understanding of their current waste practices and for developing sustainable waste management support strategies by Welsh Government for improving environmental performance of businesses.    The precision for the total waste generated in Wales was +/- 16.7% at 90% confidence.  Results from all surveys are subject to limitations with respect to the quality of estimates produced. Whilst these limitations do not alter the results or the statistical data they should be borne in mind by users of the data.  Information on waste management in the report is generally reliable but the accuracy of the results is limited to information available to surveyed producers on the final destinations of their waste. The accuracy is limited owing to the complexities of waste management routes and difficulties linking final fate back to source.    Further information including on the survey confidence is contained in the published survey report. The detailed survey technical appendices are also available on request.\tPublic record\n18062\tNRW_DS115537\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.006Z\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the River Wye (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353337\tArchived as project number 434 Media Number: 1471\tInternal Custodian: Freshwater Monitoring Coordinator, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tThe River Wye has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The data supports a condition assessment for the population of brook river and sea lamprey (protected under Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive 92/43/EEC) for the second Habitats Directive monitoring report cycle (2007-2012). This dataset combines the survey results from our lamprey surveys conducted by the former Environment Agency Wales (now part of Natural Resources Wales) and Dwr Cymru/Welsh Water. This data collection was required as part of the second Habitats Directive monitoring report cycle (2007-2012). 48 sites were surveyed along the following water courses; Afon Elan Aran Bidno Camddwr-Irfon Cammarch Chwerfri Cledan Cnyffiad Duhonw Dulas Brook Edw Garth Dulas - Irfon Gwenwst Hafrena Irfon Llunfi Marteg Nant Gwesyn Nant y Dernol Sgithwen bridge South Dulas - Irfon Wye.\tPublic record\n19001\tNRW_DS124997\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.218Z\tLocations where Coastal Adaptations measures may affect Coastal Access over the lifetime of the Shoreline Management Plans\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393937\tNRW File Storage Z:DRIVE - Archive - Access Recreation \u0026amp; Education - Access and Recreation Programmes and Projs - Wales Coast Path \u0026amp; National Trails\tNicola Rimington - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Process and Coastal Management\tThe purpose of the survey was to determine which coastal defences and coastal footpaths could be affected by the implementation of a Shoreline Management Plan policy unit classified as No Active Intervention or Managed Realignment. For the affected areas of footpaths the objectives of the survey were to record the total lengths of footpath affected the Shoreline Management Plan policy unit and Local Authority area the footpaths are located and when the coastal footpaths could be affected.  This data from this survey contains the following outputs •A spreadsheet of all affected footpaths which are listed by a unique path identifier as well as the associated costal coastal defence identifier and each intersecting policy unit. This also includes the The local authority in which the section of path is located and the length (in metres) of path is also included. •GIS shapefiles of potentially affected footpaths identified in this study. •GIS shapefiles of coastal defences and the ‘affected zone’ of potential influence of defences used in the study.\tPrivate record\n19274\tNRW_DS000001\t2024-06-26T11:14:56.464Z\tTemplate for Dataset Metadata in NRW 1.0 format\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n20573\tNRW_DS161357\t2025-04-17T08:09:33.799Z\tA Survey of Welsh populations of the Grizzled Skipper Pyrgus malvae, 2023-2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333537\t\t\tTo be added once report is completed.\tPrivate record\n17634\tNRW_DS109830\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.053Z\tMarine intertidal Phase 1 Final Report and Analysis Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383330\tData is archived as project 204, media 1423 (File path \u003d K:\\Archive\\Media 1401-1500\\Media 1423)\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis dataset contains analysis data in the form of excel spreadsheets (survey dates contacts recreational popular areas details of sea defences etc) and MapInfo tables (biotope distribution- including rare scarce nationally important and specialised biotopes biotope richness mapping of northern and southern species non native species LBAP areas bait digging and boulder turning locations angling man made substrata intertidal designated sites etc). It also contains many images taken during surveying which are stored as JPEGs. These are made up of the results of a 10 year survey of the intertidal zone of Wales including detailed information on a wide variety of features and activities e.g. the distribution of habitats and species non-native species man-made substrata bait collection. It provides comprehensive baseline data for monitoring change or for the identification of locations sensitive to certain anthropogenic impacts - a critical capacity in the context of climate change.    The data also provides information to support a notification programme for intertidal Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) thereby providing a series of intertidal designated sites across Wales.  It also underpins the selection of the internationally important Special Area of Conservation (SAC) site series.  Intertidal mapped data provides an integrated information resource for Local Authority structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications. The maps give important regional information about habitat and biotope distribution. They are used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) as a basis for the development of sensitivity mapping and as a baseline for developing monitoring of intertidal features in marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC).  Sites are mapped once and therefore are a \u0027snapshot\u0027 in time.\tPublic record\n18158\tNRW_DS115880\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.615Z\tPhase 1 Intertidal Data from Dee\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383830\tData is archived under project 204 media 1185 (File path; K:\\Archive\\Media 1101to1200\\Media 1185)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\tPrivate record\n17611\tNRW_DS108651\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.110Z\tLANDMAP Landscape Habitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363531\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Landscape Habitats (Boundaries).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Landscape Habitats (Classification).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Landscape Habitats (Evaluation).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset.   The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    Geographic dataset showing habitat landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information including General Description Evaluation Recommendations Area Boundary Evaluation and Bibliography. A LANDMAP Quality Assurance Procedure is in place to ensure that LANDMAP Information meets an agreed standard of good practice and that data is accurate and consistent across Wales.\tPublic record\n18477\tNRW_DS121593\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.075Z\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) A Proposed Turbines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353933\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA A Proposed Turbines.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tPublic record\n17199\tEXT_DS121874\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.197Z\tForestry Commission England (FCE): Forest Recreation (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383734\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\FCE Recreation Areas.lyr  x:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\FCE Recreation Points.lyr  x:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\FCE Recreation Routes.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial dataset owned by Forest Commission England (FCE) detailing the extent and location of places where forest recreation occurs in England. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this dataset under an Open Government Licence (OGL).\tPublic record\n19940\tNRW_DS161322\t2024-12-17T11:46:26.630Z\tTest\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333232\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n18555\tNRW_DS121718\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.927Z\tWINS Woodland Planting: Constraints for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373138\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Constraints for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows the number of constraints an area has to woodland planting to where rapid turnover woodland could be planted for the biomass and timber industries. The constraints map gives an indication for the number of reasons for not planting woodland in an area. The following types of constraints for woodland planting have been considered: biophysical practical preservation of existing urban areas conservation cultural economic and legislative.\tPublic record\n17497\tNRW_DS100238\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.434Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1Kmsq\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323338\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 1kmsq V2.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 1kmsq V3.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 1kmsq V3 centre point.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive - M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\ph_1_database\\Version_3\\Ph1_1kmsq_v3_centroids, data and polygons\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset made up of point dataset of Phase 1 habitat areas in every 1 km square in Wales.   The data are derived from a programme of field recording that was begun by the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) in 1979 and continued by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) after restructuring in 1991. The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level.   This electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage (see metadata for All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1kmsq - Access database). The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.   Habitat areas are summed over regions and districts within 1 km squares. Version 2 is a point layer of 1 km square centroids; habitats are labelled with Phase 1 lettered and alphanumeric codes; a \u0027modbog\u0027 column identifies whether modified bog (E.1.7 E.1.8 and E.4) is derived from blanket bog or raised bog; habitat areas are given for upland lowland and total. Version 3 was derived to facilitate data disaggregation and reporting. In it areas of heath-grass mosaics (D.5 D.6 and D.7) are split 50:50 between the appropriate heathland and grassland classes; modified blanket bog is labelled E.1.6.1.2 and modified raised bog is labelled E.1.6.2.2; the Phase 1 fen categories are combined as E.3 and modified fen as E.3.1; the saltmarsh categories are combined as H.2.6; limestone pavement is combined with basic rock; intertidal habitats are removed because they were mapped in greater detail by CCW\u0027s Phase 1 Intertidal Survey (1996-2005). Version 3 comprises a non-mappable browser table of habitat areas per 1 km square a table of 1km square centroids and a table of 1 km square polygons. The three tables link on the grid square field.\tPublic record\n17719\tNRW_DS110120\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.536Z\tThe Abundance and Distribution of Waterfowl Within Milford Haven After the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313230\tReport and data are held within the Marine Arcive as Project No. 69, Media No\u0027s. 45-47  (File Path: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media045 ,  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media046 ,  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media047)  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Headquarters, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy Marine Ornithologist, NRW. /  Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW (now Lucie Haines).  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis study was put in place to assess whether the oil spill was to impact the nationally important waterfowl populations found in the area of Milford Haven.   Two polluted (Angle Bay and Pembroke River) and two relatively unpolluted \u0027control\u0027 sites (the Carew/Cresswell and Cleddau Rivers) were monitored intensively over the three winters after the oil spill. The four sites have been monitored following standard Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS) methodology to assess the numbers of waterfowl present and following standard British Trust for Ornithology all-day (across the tidal cycle) count methodology to assess the feeding areas most used.   The data was captured to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on waterfowl populations in the Milford Haven area.   Counts during winter took place between November and March as the estuary is most important for birds during the winter period.  The migration and early autumn periods have been excluded because numbers of birds can fluctuate widely from year to year for reasons not associated with the estuary.  Each site was counted twice a month with one count on a spring tide and one on a neap tide where possible.  In the first year only one count was carried out at each site in November. Historical data was used from 1982-1999.\tPublic record\n18825\tNRW_DS123458\t2024-05-28T12:56:22.312Z\tWetland Catchments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343538\tx:\\Resource Management\\Wetland Opportunities\\Wetland Catchments.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18647\tNRW_DS123295\t2024-05-28T12:53:50.248Z\tCorine Land Cover 2012 Urban\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323935\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Corine Land Cover 2012 Urban.lyr\tMark Pugh\t\tPrivate record\n18791\tNRW_DS123424\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.914Z\tPort of Mostyn Sediment areas within development\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343234\tX:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Port of Mostyn Casework\\PM Sediment areas within development.lyr\tData originally supplied by Paul Brazier, due to role changes, now managed within the Marine Area Advice and Management Team, DPAS and Marine Services\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17760\tNRW_DS110284\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.506Z\tVolunteer Diver Seagrass Zostera marina Surveys, Llyn Peninsula and Milford Haven (2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323834\t[A] Data is archived as project 229, media no. 598 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 598) [B ] A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive - [NB this may be out of date information]  [C] Marine Recorder Survey (MRMCS01200000011)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team. Internal Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn areas where conditions are suitable seagrass beds form and these are considered nationally important in the UK. Seagrasses are thought to help stabilising the seabed in addition to providing organic matter shelter and food for a variety of fish birds and invertebrates. Seagrass beds are vital nursery grounds for flatfish and in some areas also for cephalopods. They are also known to be sensitive to changes in water quality and human disturbance. Seagrass beds have been designated as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan Habitat (underpinned by the Convention of Biological Diversity Rio Earth Summit 1992) and as a Habitat of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity in Wales (NERC Act 2006: Section 42).   The survey was due to be expanded in 2009 to other areas within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and methodologies refined. 1. Develop methods to look at damage to seagrass beds caused by anchor chains compared to permanent moorings. 2. \u0027Ground-truth\u0027 aerial photographs of Porth Dinllaen and Milford Haven subtidal seagrass beds. 3. Test methods of measuring extent of large seagrass beds using volunteers. 4. Estimate density and measure depth of live seagrass in different parts of the bed. 5. Make note of any unusual feature of the seagrass bed.\tPublic record\n17896\tNRW_DS111815\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.448Z\tConnectivity of Marine Habitats - A Review of Vulnerable Species (1960 - 2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383135\tArchived as project number 259, media number 955 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 955)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services\tThe ‘connectivity’ of marine benthic organisms is important as it has potential implications for designation and management of MPAs and is also likely to feed into work on the management of the wider environment through Marine Planning which will be developed in Welsh waters in the near future. Connectivity is defined here as the exchange of sufficient organisms via dispersal and movement to sustain populations.  Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) contracted Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd. (Marine EcoSol) to undertake the first stages of analysis primarily to review species that may be vulnerable to low connectivity or fragmentation in Welsh waters. The vulnerability to connectivity project (presented in the current report) aimed to identify marine invertebrates macroalgae plants and small territorial fish which are theoretically vulnerable to low connectivity and fragmentation and flag those that might need to be carefully considered when managing the marine environment around Wales. This dataset contains the results of  the \u0027vulnerability to connectivity project\u0027  a five staged review of existing information which highlighted species that may be vulnerable to poor connectivity or fragmentation in Welsh Waters based on information easily available in 2009. Connectivity is now generally recognised as an important criterion in the design of networks of Marine Protected Areas. Historical data was reviewed up to November 2008 with the earliest data point recorded in 1960.\tPublic record\n18788\tNRW_DS123423\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.906Z\tPort of Mostyn Operational Land Mean High Water (MHW)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343233\tX:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Port of Mostyn Casework\\PM Operational Land MHW.lyr\tData originally supplied by Paul Brazier, due to role changes, now managed within the Marine Area Advice and Management Team, DPAS and Marine Services\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18142\tNRW_DS115651\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.848Z\tEuropean Union (EU) Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC) reporting under Article 17 reporting round 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363531\tAssociated data is held in the Terrestrial Archive: at W:\\Terrestrial Archive\\T_Media001to100\\T_Media002\tInternal Custodian, contact relevant specialists or the Enterprise Data Management Specialist Advisor\tEvery six years Member States of the European Union are required (by Article 17 of the Directive) to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC). The reporting format set by the European Commission requires a separate analysis for each species and each habitat in each biogeographic region which that country covers. This is the archive version of the databases and supporting evidence which was sent to JNCC added to the archive 15/04/2014. A live version of the databases are stored on a corporate drive and are in use by the relevant specialists. The purpose of this data capture was to meet the Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW\u0027s) obligations under Article 17 of the Habitats Directive. This dataset covers all of Wales and covers the six year reporting period to 2013.  All data was entered by specialists based on expert opinion and supporting data sources.\tPublic record\n19716\tNRW_DS161314\t2024-11-04T11:54:35.896Z\tThe status and distribution of the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae at Freshwater West in 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tData held in the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder 6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tFollowing the discovery of three nymphs of the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae on the shingle beach at Freshwater West in Pembrokeshire in August and September 2022 a survey was undertaken in 2023 to determine its current status and distribution.\tPublic record\n18549\tNRW_DS121717\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.669Z\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373137\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where rapid turnover woodland could be planted for the biomass and timber industries.\tPublic record\n17613\tNRW_DS108652\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.083Z\tLANDMAP Visual Sensory\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363532\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Visual Sensory (Boundaries).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Visual Sensory (Classification).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Visual Sensory (Evaluation).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset. The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    Geographic dataset showing visual and sensory landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information including General Description Evaluation Recommendations Tolerance to Change Area Boundary Evaluation and Assessment.    This dataset maps the landscape as perceived through our senses based on the physical attributes of landform and land cover. The perceived characteristics of the landscape are primarily determined visually but the senses of hearing smell and touch are also considered.\tPublic record\n19607\tNRW_DS161302\t2024-09-26T20:10:55.211Z\tForester Rides\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333032\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Rides.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support\tThis layer holds information about ride features on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. Rides are a network of established tracks across the woodland developed to aid timber extractions. It is collected to form a record of all the rides on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. The data is spatially recorded as lines only. It is used by operational staff to identify  existing rides so they can be utilised in operations.\tPrivate record\n17597\tNRW_DS108653\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.904Z\tLANDMAP Historic Landscape\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363533\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap2017\\LANDMAP Historic Landscape.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Historic Landscape.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset. The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    This is a geographic dataset showing historical landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information including General Description Evaluation Recommendations Area Boundary Evaluation and Bibliography. This dataset focuses on archaeological and historical sites and how they relate to each other and to the surrounding landscape. The features mapped include those resulting from past human activity and structurally prominent patterns and features that contribute to the historic character of the present landscape.   Information is gathered by LANDMAP in order to inform sustainable decision making and to enable landscape to be taken into account during decision making processes. Historic Landscape data can be used to assess the speed direction and effect of change and to provide an understanding of how the landscape has evolved. A LANDMAP Quality Assurance Procedure is in place to ensure that LANDMAP Information meets an agreed standard of good practice and that data is accurate and consistent across Wales.\tPublic record\n18590\tNRW_DS122914\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.835Z\tNew birds\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393134\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\New birds.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n17924\tNRW_DS113767\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.078Z\tFeasibility Study on the Use of Dispersants in the Bristol Channel in the Event of an Oil Pollution Incident (2002)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133373637\tData is archived in the NRW data archive as project 299, media no.1209\tContact the Data Services Team Internal Custodian: Marine and Freshwater Evidence Manager, NRW\tThe approval and use of oil spill dispersants is regulated in UK waters. The Food and Environment Protection Act 1985 requires that a licence be issued for the deposit of any substance or article in the sea or that an exemption be granted under the Deposits in the Sea (Exemptions) Order 1985. The licensing authority for England and Wales is DEFRA (Department of Environment Food and Rural Affairs (formerly MAFF - Ministry of Agriculture Fisheries and Food). Specific permission to use oil spill dispersants in shallow water (defined as areas of sea where the water depth is less than 20 metres as marked on Admiralty charts or within 1 nautical mile of such area) must be granted by DEFRA. The role of the EG will be to provide information about the sensitivities of resources in the area to oil pollution so that DEFRA can make a reasoned decision on dispersant use. The BCSEG considered many different relevant factors including the oil spill risks likely spilled oil behaviour characteristics of the Bristol Channel and the shoreline and marine resources that could be affected by spilled oil. The consequences of dispersing or not dispersing spilled oil were also considered. As a result of these deliberations the areas of the Bristol Channel and Severn Estuary have been designated as dispersant use or non-use zones.These zones are marked on maps for ease of reference. These zones are: - Zone A Where dispersants can be used at any time of the tide - Zone B Where dispersants can be used (but only with DEFRA\u0027s specific permission) on an outgoing tide from High Water until High Water + 5 hours - Zone C Where dispersants can be used (but only with DEFRA\u0027s specific permission) on an outgoing tide from High Water until High Water + 2 hours - Zone D Where dispersants are not to be used at any time   In the event of oil pollution occurring in the Bristol Channel an Environment Group (EG) will be activated to consider the environmental consequences of the spill and the most suitable ways in which damage can be minimised. The use of oil spill dispersants is a response method that might be used. The Bristol Channel Standing Environment Group (BCSEG) commissioned this report to describe the factors in the Bristol Channel that need to be considered when deciding to use or not to use oil spill dispersants.\tPublic record\n19116\tNRW_DS125609\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.072Z\tNutrient Review - Groundwater Interpolated Future and Current Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363039\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Quality Interpolation.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tFuture (2031) and current (2019) predicted groundwater quality for phosphate and nitrate to understand spatial patterns in the groundwater dataset and predict phosphate and nitrate concentrations at unmonitored locations across Wales\tPublic record\n18013\tNRW_DS115036\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.897Z\tVegetated Shingle Site Boundary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303336\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1348 Archived as Project 363 and media 1348.  Needs to be transferred over from Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\VegetatedShingle\\\tInternal Custodian: Monica Jones, Data Services, NRW (Contract Support Officer) Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tA polygon layer showing the boundary of vegetated shingle sites.  A GIS layer was generated in 2012 from paper maps from the 1993 JNCC report by Sneddon and Randall.  The purpose of this data capture is to generate an electronic version of the data and make it easily available for reporting purposes.  The surveys focused on the vegetation of stony banks and did not attempt to map strandline communities; therefore the site boundary is not a true representation of the extent of the Shingle Ridge.\tPublic record\n17225\tEXT_DS122194\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.406Z\tForestry Commission Great Britain (FCGB): Recreation Designations (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313934\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Designated Areas\\GB Recreation Designations.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones  GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Forestry Commission Great Britain (FCGB) and is licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for use. This spatial datasets shows the extent and locaiton of designated recreational forestry sites around Great Britain.\tPrivate record\n18193\tNRW_DS116212\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.634Z\tNative Freshwater Species Surveys Across Wales (1978 to 2019)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323132\t[A] This data is available on the NBN Atlas.  [B] Original data held on Wiski-Kieco\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe purpose of this data capture was to primarily monitor the distribution of freshwater invertebrates.  The data comprises primarily of the Welsh abstract of freshwater invertebrates. It comes from BIOSYS the national ecology database for freshwater species and is related to the aquatic stages of the invertebrates. Most data have been collected from main rivers as part of an extensive routine monitoring network. The data are restricted to a selection of families which contain one species only.  The records cover the whole of Wales. Sites are recorded at a minimum precision of 100m. The distribution of records is biased towards main rivers and areas with historical water quality issues. This is especially true of older records. The sampling methodologies and taxa identified are the same at all sites.    NB This data set has been expanded to include all freshwater data from 1978 to 2019 at all taxonomic levels. Includes river \u0026amp; lake macroinvertabrates river \u0026amp; lake diatoms and river macrophytes.\tPublic record\n17749\tNRW_DS110319\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.339Z\tWest Wales Grey Seal Census 1991 to 1994\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333139\tDataset is archived as Project 137 (media number 393) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media393\\Report Paper report is available from CCW library (see related titles field) 2 boxes off-site of original paper data: CCW/WRPB/003 - Seal Census ( 1992 - 1994 ), CCW/WRPB/004 - Seal Census ( 1992 - 1994 )\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW\tThe large breeding colonies of grey seals in northern Britain have been censused annually in recent years by the Sea Mammal Research Unit but the relatively small population in Wales had not been the subject of a population estimate since 1974 and there had never been a comprehensive census of all mainland and island sites sustained through a pupping season.  The purpose of this data capture was to locate and document all grey seal breeding sites during the main pupping season from September to December 1991 and to estimate pup production at these sites. To estimate the total number of cows in the West Wales population identify \u0027areas\u0027 where seals are subject to disturbance by man and assess the quality of the breeding haul-out environment.\tPublic record\n18881\tNRW_DS124687\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.514Z\tCommunities at Risk Register 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363837\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Communities at Risk Register (CaRR) has been developed to provide an objective means of identifying risk and prioritising flood risk management activities at a Wales-wide community level. It applies a standard methodology across all flood sources to calculate a theoretical ‘danger score’ that allows comparative risks to be quantified and ranked (from High to Low).    The CaRR comprises a spreadsheet that identifies and ranks individual communities for  1. a natural ‘undefended’ scenario and  2. a managed scenario (based on the presence of defences and flood warning).   It is complemented by a GIS layer which defines the individual Community polygons and is attributed with danger scores community rankings and other metric information.\tPublic record\n18443\tNRW_DS119155\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.371Z\tWales Waste Information\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313535\tData is stored in WISKI and also on the DMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/reporting/was/SWMA/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Irina Nicholas, Environmental Reporting team, NRW John Fry, SUW Advisor Wales, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Irina Nicholas, Environmental Reporting team, NRW\tThis is a summary of the types and quantities of waste handled by permitted waste management facilities in Wales.\tPublic record\n19117\tNRW_DS125608\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.092Z\tNutrient Review - Groundwater and Surface Water Locations Scoped in and Out of Nitrate and Phosphate Assessments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363038\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Quality Monitoring Locations.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Water Monitoring Locations.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tThis dataset shows NRW Nutrient Review 2023 surface water and groundwater monitoring sites excluded and includes from the nitrate and phosphate water quality assessment following data processing and application of data rules.  For the included data The current (2019) and predicted (2031) at each location is provided along with the method used to assess the concentrations.\tPublic record\n18252\tNRW_DS116284\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.981Z\tFelling Licence Applications\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323834\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Felling Licences\\Felling Licence Applications.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team\tAnyone wishing to fell trees must ensure that a licence or permission under a grant scheme has been issued by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) before any felling is carried out or that one of the exceptions apply.  The applicant would normally need to get permission from the NRW to fell growing trees. This is usually given in a Felling Licence or an approval under a Dedication Scheme.   This dataset provides a record of all approved Felling License Applications in Wales and type of license approved.  The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has personal information redacted.\tPublic record\n17358\tNRW_DS24185\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.895Z\tBat Roosts Database - South Wales (BATDATA-WALES) 1982-1997\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234313835\t[A] Species Data held in Recorder 6:  CCW-HQ-Terr-Bat Roosts Database - Wales (BATDATA-WALES) CCW0003800000001\tInternal Custodian: Jean Matthews,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tThis is a former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) regional dataset of bat roosts in South Wales containing approximately 600 records. Includes the location of roosts roost type species records and public enquiries and/or details of visits to roosts by CCW staff or other bat experts. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts. This data has been used to provide information when dealing with development proposals to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.  Original data was created by Nature Conservancy Council as part of a UK-wide dataset and Welsh records were transferred to CCW in 1991. This dataset has not been validated and there may be some errors in grid references and place names.\tPublic record\n18415\tNRW_DS118482\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.435Z\tAfon Teifi and Afon Eden Condition Assessment (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138343832\tData is stored in WISKI\tDr Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Ecus Ltd) Melanie Lacan, Senior Environmental Monitoring Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW, NRW\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) was carried out in 2016 at 27 sample sites on the Afon Teifi and six sample sites on the Afon Eden. The data was collected to feed into condition assessment of the Afon Teifi and Afon Eden according to Common Standards Monitoring guidance for Rivers for the following physical river habitat quality attributes: Channel planform Habitat Modification Score Bank vegetation naturalness Riparian zone vegetation naturalness Large woody debris In-channel structures Fine sediment Siltation Negative indicators: alien/locally absent. species\tPublic record\n18900\tNRW_DS124769\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.148Z\tCuRVe Condition PressureOnAir OzoneAmmonia\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373639\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tAir is one of the most important supporting features of ecosystem resilience. Clean air affects every habitat from freshwater to forests and upland habitats.     On a relative scale from 0 to 100 the output shows which areas are the most resilient to an increase in air pollution i.e. areas where an increase in air pollutant emissions is less likely to have an immediate effect on ecosystem condition. In interpreting this dataset it is important to note that values are based on emission of pollutants not on concentration of pollutants in the air.\tPrivate record\n18020\tNRW_DS114945\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.590Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Saltmarsh Extent in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393435\tArchived as project 199 media 1350.   Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae).   Specific areas of interest include saltmarsh fringing the Burry Inlet.  The Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. Data was captured to map the 2009 boundary for comparison with previous boundaries; record patches away from the main saltmarsh; take viewpoint photographs to enable a quick comparison of boundaries on future visits.  All surveys completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high.\tPublic record\n18556\tNRW_DS122451\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.464Z\tForest Blocks\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343531\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Blocks.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Primary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tWithin each Operational Area Woodland is initially divided into Forest blocks these are usually a specific number per woodland blocks. Sometimes very large areas of woodland are broken down into several blocks for management purposes. These geographic blocks are used to identify distinct groups of woodland within an operational area. Planning Staff use this block information to produce production forecasts at block Level. In 2004 Blocks were renamed Design Plan Units but Blocks numbers are still used in Forester.\tPublic record\n18897\tNRW_DS124777\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.894Z\tCuRVe Diversity SSSIs Proportion\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373737\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tSites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) are areas of land notified for their special scientific interest. They support an important range of biological geological and physiographical features. The most important areas for habitat and species conservation at both national and international levels lie within them and all are considered of national importance for nature conservation.    The proportion of land within SSSIs in each 1 km reporting square is used as factor for the Diversity factor.\tPrivate record\n18480\tNRW_DS121589\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.209Z\tMonitoring Wetland Invertebrates on Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve, Radnorshire (2018)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353839\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tInternal Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist  and  Rhys Jenkins   Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThis work presents the findings of wetland invertebrate monitoring work undertaken in 2017 at Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve Radnorshire. Previous surveys have identified the four wetland habitats of bare mud ground layer open water and wetland flora as key wetland invertebrate assemblage features. Previously in 2007 six monitoring stations were set up and timed searches undertaken using a range of collecting methods with the aim of developing standardised future monitoring. Target wetland invertebrate species were then chosen for each key habitat. For the reserve to be in favourable condition for its wetland invertebrate assemblage any monitoring would have to record at least 50% of the target taxa.   This work follows on from invertebrate monitoring undertaken in 2007 and 2012. A total of 438 records of 226 invertebrate species were recorded during the current 2017 monitoring. Of the 115 target species previously recorded only 26 species (23%) were taken in 2017 marking a steady decline over the 10 year monitoring period (68 species [59%] in 2007; 37 species [32%] in 2012). This shows that all of the four key wetland habitat features are in unfavourable condition and the percentages of the target wetland species are continuing to decline.  It is recommended that the levels of grazing need to be increased to provide bare mud and open water habitat and that scrub management should continue to keep succession in check.\tPublic record\n17207\tEXT_DS121865\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.485Z\tCanal and River Trust: Ownership (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383635\tx:\\Ownership\\Canals and rivers trust\\Canals and rivers trust Ownership Line.lyr  x:\\Ownership\\Canals and rivers trust\\Canals and rivers trust Ownership.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Canals and rivers trust feeders.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial third party dataset detailing the extent and location of Canal and River Trust Ownership in Wales. The Canals and Rivers Trust has licenced Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tPublic record\n17973\tNRW_DS114452\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.709Z\tRestoration of Native Oyster, Ostrea edulis, in South Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343532\tData is archived under project no. 306, media no. 1220 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1220)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team [previously  -Aethne Cooke (ex CCW), Marine Biodiversity Liaison Officer, NRW] Metadata Contact: Data Services, Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, KSP, NRW\tNative oyster populations throughout Europe suffered a serious decline in the late 19th and early 20th centuries due predominantly to over fishing and associated habitat loss but exacerbated by anthropogenic and environmental pressures of pollution disease and a series of cold winters. Historic accounts place Ostrea edulis at the centre of important local fisheries in South Wales back to the Roman occupation of Britain and describe a fishery supporting 200 vessels landing over 9 million oysters at its peak in the 19th century. Ostrea edulis are found on a wide variety of firm and stable seabed habitats where clean hard surfaces exist for larval settlement but it is likely that more complex habitat forms have historically existed including oyster banks and reefs. The aim of this study was to identify the scope and potential approaches for restoration of native oyster populations and habitats in South Wales. The associated report describes the options for practical approaches for restoration of the native oyster in its natural habitat in South Wales to meet UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) targets for recovery.\tPublic record\n17883\tNRW_DS111294\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.904Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools Following the Sea Empress Oil Spill 1996-97\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323934\t[A] Paper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. [B] Data available on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000076)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tFollowing the Sea Empress grounding in Milford Haven on February the 15th 1996 it was decided that a programme of monitoring was required to assess the impact of the oil on the coasts of Pembrokeshire and Carmarthenshire. This dataset contains the results of rockpool monitoring carried out for a year following the spillage to assess community changed caused by the oil spill. The data collected was used to assess community changes caused by the oil spillage. It was thought rockpool monitoring was particular suitable for this purpose because rockpools are diverse in physical characteristics occur at different height on shores contain a range of biota and are relatively easy to re-locate.\tPublic record\n17809\tNRW_DS110894\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.939Z\tMarine Sites: Data Collation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383934\ta) archived as Project 27, media no 81 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 81)   b) Marine Recorder - SURVEY NUMBERS NOT RECORDED IN METADATA\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBetween October 2001 and June 2002 the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) contracted Sea-Scope to source and collate any Welsh marine biological data which were not in CCW\u0027s possession. The main aim of the Project was to collate extant data enabling them to be accessible to CCW data management systems and linking to other data collation projects nationally. Priority was to be given to data acquired from Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) particularly those relating to SAC moderation features. A higher priority was to be given to sourcing datasets from areas which had limited representation on the MNCR database in terms of data already held. Also datasets collected before 1980 with restricted phyla or with suspected inaccuracies in species identification were to be given a lower priority.\tPublic record\n17722\tNRW_DS110118\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.533Z\tCommon scoter (Melanitta nigra) Monitoring in Carmarthen Bay following the Sea Empress Oil Spill. April 1997 to March 1998.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313138\tReport is held as Project No. 26, media 72\tInternal Custodian: - Patrick Lindley Maritime Ornithologist, NRW - Mike Camplin Team Leader, Specialist Monitoring, NRW.  Metadata contact: - Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Common Scoter Melanitta nigra is listed in Annex II/2 of the EC Directive on the Conservation of Wild Birds (79/409/EEC) and occurs on the red list of the UK\u0027s Species of Conservation Concern. Carmarthen Bay southwest Wales is by far the most important wintering site in Wales. On 24 February 1996 large amounts of oil from the stricken Sea Empress tanker entered Carmarthen Bay.  Following the oil spill a monitoring programme was immediately established by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to record numbers and distribution of Common Scoters in Carmarthen Bay. Data collected to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on common scoter populations in Carmarthen Bay Wales.\tPublic record\n17724\tNRW_DS110159\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.450Z\tMonitoring of Lichen Communities at Stackpole National Nature Reserve (NNR)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313539\tArchived at K:\\archive\\Media301to400\\Media372\tInternal Custodian 1: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian 2: Paul Culyer, Senior Officer Land Management, Conservation Land Management Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tStackpole Warren is noted for its communities of calcicolous terricolous lichens which occupy heavily rabbit-grazed ground with a poor cover of vascular plants. However available evidence suggests that the amount of open ground and short grazed turf at Stackpole Warren has been decreasing for at least 3 decades. The purpose of this data capture was to examine the terricolous lichen sites at Stackpole Warren for signs of damage which might be attributable to oil pollution from the Sea Empress; to extend monitoring coverage of the terricolous lichen sites at Stackpole National Nature Reserve (NNR); and to assess the success of management in maintaining the notable species and communities.\tPublic record\n17133\tEXT_DS120572\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.203Z\tDudley Stamp Land Utilisation Survey for Wales 1933-1949\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230353732\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Dudley Stamp Land utilisation survey 1933 to 49.lyr\tGIS Service Desk Jonathan Rothwell, Terrestrial Mapping Specialist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Barnaby Letheren, Specialist Advisor Enterprise Data Management, Evidence Knowledge and Advice, NRW\tThis spatial dataset - for the Wales geographic area only - is held by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). The dataset is a digital version of the 1 inch to 1 mile \u0027Dudley Stamp Maps\u0027 that provide a pre-war land survey. The Environment Agency carried out the digitisation of the survey data for all of Great Britain.\tPublic record\n18007\tNRW_DS115077\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.556Z\tCollection Activity and Crab Survey in the Menai Strait (2009)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303737\tData is archived as project 199, media 550.  Species data is also available in Marine Recorder and the NBN Gateway.   A copy of the working data is available at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2009 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\\Data\tData Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services NRW\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive.  In the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide and Reefs. Specific areas of interest include areas of tide-swept bedrock and boulders and areas of tide-swept Fucus serratus on mixed sediment along the Menai Strait. In previous studies it was noted that a portion of the boulders had been turned by bait collectors. Surveys to assess bait collection activity and crab counts were carried out along the length of the shoreline of the Menai Strait using student volunteers. Data quality and confidence considered high.\tPublic record\n17125\tEXT_DS115522\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.294Z\tSwansea Bay and Carmarthen Bay Coastal Group Beach Monitoring Campaign 2013 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353232\tData is archived as project 433, media 1470\tGIS Service Desk  Ceri Seaton, Marine and Coastal Physical Scientist, NRW\tData comprises of beach profile measurements for both Swansea Bay and Carmarthen Bay area. This data is available for Internal NRW use only.\tPublic record\n19820\tNRW_DS161319\t2024-11-29T12:10:24.175Z\tFurther studies to assess the distribution of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior on Oxwich Burrows, Nicholaston Burrows, Whiteford Burrows, Three Cliffs Bay \u0026amp; Swansea Dunes in September 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333139\tRecorder 6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tMike Howe to supply\tPrivate record\n17996\tNRW_DS114713\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.021Z\tAssessing the Vulnerability of Marine Habitats in Wales to the Impacts of Climate Changes (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373133\tData is archived as project 328, media 1287.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Ecologist, MFEG, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tClimate change is recognised as a major environmental issue and the process of global change and climate will impact upon many aspects of everyday life presenting a serious threat to the natural environment. In response to the scientific and political consensus of a need to tackle climate change the UK government bought into law the climate change act 2008. This requires a Strategic Climate Change Risk Assessment (SCCRA) to be carried out every five years the first of which will need to be completed by November 2011.   This project was carried out to develop a methodology to assess the vulnerability of Welsh marine habitats to the impact of climate change and to use this method to undertake an assessment of welsh marine habitats. This assessment helped enable the identification of those habitats most at risk from climate change and it is envisaged that this information will be used to inform management plans and government policy and decision making.\tPublic record\n17121\tEXT_DS115368\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.248Z\tAssessment of Rhos Pasture Habitat in Caerphilly County Borough Council (CBC) in respect of its Suitability and Condition for the Priority Butterfly Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333638\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Adrian_f\\MapInfo Data\\Marsh Fritillary\\Habitat Quality Surveys\\SWales LA surveys (RSmith) revised This data should be obtained from it\u0027s owner Caerphilly County Borough Council (CBC) to ensure you have the most up-to-date copy of the data.\tGIS Service Desk  Dr Mike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW   Formerly: Adrian Fowles in CCW\tThe Marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) priority species and has been listed by the Welsh Government as species of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity in Wales (Section 74 species Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000). This species in now confined to the south-western counties of England south and west Wales western Scotland and Ireland. There is some evidence to suggest that the species range is continuing to contract along eastern edge having previously been lost in central and eastern parts of England. It has also become scarcer within this range with healthy populations now any appearing to persist where there is sufficient habitat to support their meta-populations (dynamic network of colonies) in which the species naturally occurs. Nevertheless in parts of this range its status maybe poorly known due to recording issues.  For species existing in meta-populations as is the case with many of our scarce butterflies the quality and abundance of available habitat within colonising distance of local populations is extremely important. In Wales there is increasing emphasis on mapping these patches of habitat to complement surveys of marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia adults and larval webs.   This data consists of a MapInfo polygon layer illustrating the condition of habitats for marsh fritillaries in Caerphilly County Borough Council (CBC). The purpose of this data capture was to ascertain the extent and condition of habitat capable of supporting populations of marsh fritillaries in order to provide scientifically-justified guidance to inform planning decisions. Confidence in this data is high because the data was collected under contract by experienced surveyors.\tPublic record\n17505\tNRW_DS100107\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.762Z\tLakes Inventory\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313037\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Lakes inventory.lyr  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\physical\\hydrology\\lakes\\welsh_lakes.tab\tTristan Hatton-Ellis\tThis is the Welsh subset of an inventory of standing waters (freshwater lakes and lochs) derived from Ordnance Survey digital map data at a scale of 1:50 000 . The inventory basic physical data such as location surface area perimeter and altitude. Catchment areas were computed for water bodies with a surface area larger than 1 ha from a digital terrain model (DTM) using customised routines in a geographical information system (GIS).\tPublic record\n18434\tNRW_DS119181\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.225Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey on the River Usk (2013 - 2018)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313831\tDMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitat Directive 3rd reporting cycle \u0026gt; Shad Fish Species - 3rd Reporting Cycle \u0026gt; Usk. DMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 3 \u0026gt; Usk Shad \u0026gt; Workbook Usk shad cycle 3 2013 onwards.xlsx\tNRW Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator: Heather Garrett.\tThis report assesses the spawning extent population attribute of Allis and Twaite shad on the River Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC) using a kick sampling monitoring method for shad eggs at routine sampling points using an Natural Resources Wales (NRW) standard protocol (Garrett 2015) during the months of May - June in 2013 2015 and 2016. This is one of the population attributes for condition assessment from the Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance for freshwater features (Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) 2015). The two remaining population attributes are never assessed by NRW because of the invasive techniques for sampling juvenile and adult life stages of these two species. The environmental attribute targets for water quality flow river habitat etc will be assessed when sufficient data has been collated and is available.   Overall the shad spawning distribution target was not met on the River Usk SAC in the third reporting cycle. This preliminary assessment is classed as a Fail. For freshwater species and habitats the overall assessment of the feature as \u0027Favourable\u0027 or \u0027Unfavourable\u0027 (JNCC 2017) incorporates both abiotic and biotic attributes. This overall assessment will be made when in addition to the population attributes the environmental attributes (water quality flow river habitat barriers to migration etc.) for the SAC river have also been assessed. This analysis will be documented in a summary table for each SAC river and each of its features.\tPublic record\n18535\tNRW_DS121668\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.632Z\tWINS Area Statement Opportunity Maps\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363638\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\\tRussell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)\tThe Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS Project (Previously known as SCCAN) showed a range of national opportunities to manage natural resources more sustainably in the SoNaRR report. This current phase has looked at a more local level to provide input to the Area Statement process.The result is a comprehensive map atlas of opportunity demand and constraint maps with accompanying spatial data to facilitate broad cross-sectorial discussions on sustainable management of natural resources (SMNR). SCCAN aims to promote more holistic management of natural resources through spatial mapping of opportunity areas for land management interventions. The mapping focusses on priorities of the Welsh Government Natural Resources Policy (2017) arising from the Environment (Wales) Act (2016) which puts an emphasis on taking a place-based approach and delivering nature-based solutions. The five policy themes considered by the project: Ecological Resilience and Connectivity; Marine and Freshwater Water Quality; Natural Flood Management; Woodland Planting (for various objectives); Urban and Peri-Urban Green Infrastructure.\tPublic record\n19000\tNRW_DS124942\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.124Z\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Turbines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393432\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Turbine Building Line.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm (as built) wind turbine building lines. This shows all 27 turbines on the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm. Each Vestas wind turbine is 3.6MW in power. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPrivate record\n17748\tNRW_DS110342\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.330Z\tEcological impacts of shoreline clean-up during the Sea Empress Oil spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333432\tArchived as project no 122 (media no 363) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media363 Report available from CCW library (see library catalogue/\u0027related titles\u0027 field)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of an assessment of the effects of shoreline clean-up activity following the Sea Empress oil spill concentrating on conspicuous species of rocky shore algae and animals coastal vegetation and signs of physical disturbance. The Sea Empress ran aground on the 15th of February 1996. Shoreline clean-up activities at over 100 sites around south-west Wales began on the 16th of February and were scaled down gradually from July 1996 through to summer 1997.  The purpose of this data capture was to carry out: -  An assessment of the environmental impacts of each individual clean up technique;  - Compare the impacts with those described from previous spills and experimental studies;  - Make recommendations on the suitability and application of techniques in different situations; - Assess the overall impact of the clean-up activity in relation to the impacts of the oil pollution itself; and - Make recommendations for future monitoring and studies of the effects of clean-up activities.\tPublic record\n18276\tNRW_DS116317\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.866Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Rocky Shore Macroalgal Species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333137\tBioSys WISKI KiEco\t\tInformation regarding the presence of macroalgal species on rocky shores monitored for the Water Framework Directive ecological assessment of transitional and coastal waters of Wales. Monitoring follows the Reduced Species List method outlined in the UK Technical Advisory Group method statement. Data are from intertidal rocky shores. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data collected and owned by the Natural Resources Wales.\tPublic record\n18641\tNRW_DS123046\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.846Z\tForest Road Access\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303436\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Forest Road Access.lyr\tPeter van-Velzen, Planning Manager, Wales Harvesting \u0026amp; Marketing\tThe point theme contains the access points or gateways into forest or woodland managed by Natural Resources Wales providing access to the forest roads network.\tPublic record\n18245\tNRW_DS116291\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.414Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Ownership\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323931\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\NRW Forest Ownership.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset illustrates the acquisitions that have created the NRW Forestry Estate as we know it today. This information is obtained from the Deed Management System which has been designed for the maintenance and reporting of the NRW land holdings.\tPublic record\n20509\tNRW_DS161352\t2025-03-31T14:53:23.287Z\tAn Evaluation of the Current Use of Farmed vs Wild-caught Ragworm bait in Wales - Survey Results\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333532\tQuestionnaire Results stored here - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B7170031E-0717-4C32-886C-54DE0ABCCE4B%7D\u0026amp;action\u003dedit\u0026amp;source\u003dhttps%3A%2F%2Fcyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fmar%2FMNN22-25%2FBaitCollectn%2FForms%2FNRW%2520View.aspx%3Fcsf%3D1%26web%3D1%26CID%3D466c16f7%252Dc54f%252D4285%252Dbe1e%252Ded89e9fa3bf1%26RootFolder%3D%252Fteams%252Fadvice%252Fmar%252FMNN22%252D25%252FBaitCollectn%252FCode%2520of%2520Conduct%2520and%2520Farmed%2520Bait%2520Workstream%26FolderCTID%3D0x012000911566D4BD76D243A66C042306FD1A40%23InplviewHash9effeb0a-6b03-4b90-aca0-0063f964c6b2%3DPaged%253DTRUE-p_SortBehavior%253D0-p_FileLeafRef%253DChris%252520Smith%252520%25252d%252520Dragon%252520Baits%25252emsg-p_ID%253D327-FolderCTID%253D0x012000911566D4BD76D243A66C042306FD1A40-PageFirstRow%253D31\tDewi R Evans - Specialist Officer, Intertidal Management\tThis is the survey outputs of a study evaluating the current use of farmed vs wild-caught ragworm bait in Wales identifying any barriers to the use of farmed ragworm bait and with those barriers in mind recommending measures that can be taken to encourage the use of farmed ragworm bait.   The data is an excel spreadsheet detailing questionnaire results which were undertaken by Bangor University under commision by Natural Resources Wales\tPublic record\n17691\tNRW_DS110061\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.533Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Scarlet and Gold Cup Coral Balanophyllia regia\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303631\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/cup corals ,  Images - dell1590 C/doc and settings/MONITORING/balanophyllia\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Cup corals are slow growing filter feeders which are susceptible to changes in water quality and planktonic food supply.  Scarlet and gold star-coral Balanophyllia regia is a Lusitanian species and Skomer MNR is close to the northern edge of its range in the UK. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor Scarlet and Gold cup coral.\tPublic record\n19100\tNRW_DS125456\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.858Z\tReservoir Regulations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343536\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Regulation.lyr\tGerallt Richards,  Reservoir Regulation Team\tA layer showing the reservoirs in Wales. Reservoirs with a capacity greater than 10000 cubic metres are regulated as large raised reservoirs. This layer provides information such as the capacity of the reservoir dam height and ownership details. Further information can be obtained from the Reservoir Regulation Team.\tPublic record\n17828\tNRW_DS111184\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.582Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Dyfi Estuary in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313834\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media 549 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\ Media 549. [A] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 59 - PLAS Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Data [C] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\GIS [D]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey No. MRCCW16300000009) [E] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture is to establish sites for long-term monitoring and collect baseline and reference data using appropriate methods.  Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary. Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau has representative examples of bar-built estuaries in north-west Wales and includes the Glaslyn/Dwyryd Mawddach and Dyfi estuaries.\tPublic record\n17548\tNRW_DS101978\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.151Z\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of Selected Alder Woods on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales (JA1)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393738\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland\\PH2_Woodland_Sites.lyr  Archived as Project 209 Media Number 574\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of alder woods at 11 sites on 10 Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) throughout Wales.    The aim of this survey was to provide a better understanding of the plant communities of Welsh alder woods a rather neglected habitat.\tPublic record\n18819\tNRW_DS123983\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.928Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macrophytes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233393833\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data\tAdvisor - Data Services Team.  Graham Rutt  SMEs       Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI Data sent to NBN Feb 2020\tMacrophytes (water plants visible to the naked eye) can indicate the impact of pressures such as increased nutrients flow pressures channel alterations etc. The species present and their abundance give an indication as to the health of a river. This dataset is made up of species lists and their percentage cover value for each survey conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\tPublic record\n19113\tNRW_DS125457\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.958Z\tThreat of landfill sites to Marine Protected Area habitat features in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343537\tX:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\Coastal landfill sites posing a threat to MPA features\tHarriet Robinson - Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning\tThe map package shows the potential overall threat coastal landfill sites in Wales pose to Marine Protected Area (MPA) habitat features under a present day epoch (2005-2025) a medium term epoch (2025-2055) and a long-term epoch (2055-2105).   Overall threat presented in the map package is a combination of the risk of waste being released from the landfill site and the vulnerability of MPA habitat features to waste if released from these sites. The map package ultimately shows the results of a high-level assessment on the potential impacts of coastal landfill sites to MPAs. The study aimed to provide an initial assessment of this impact to highlight where regional or site-specific investigations should be undertaken to further understand the threat coastal landfill sites pose to MPA features.   The boundaries of the Operational Areas and MPAs are also included in the map package.   Excel files of the attribute tables from the GIS map package are also provided.   More information on the rationale methods results and assumptions can be found in the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Evidence Report 673.\tPublic record\n17963\tNRW_DS114501\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.610Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Marine Benthic Species and Habitat Collation Held in Marine Recorder\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353031\tMarine Recorder is currently available through the legacy CCW VMWare.  A back up is held on the legacy CCW MFSG drive along with copies of the most recent snapshot (H:\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder)  Copies of the NRW snapshot is also held at JNCC and the NBNDate file is archived at DASSH.  Some paper MNCR forms are held in off site storage  (Box CCW/MNCR/1, Box CCW/MNCR/2. Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Contact / Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  Internal Data Contact: Marine and Freshwater Evidence Manager, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) currently holds and collates current and former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) marine benthic species biotope habitat and particle size analyses data in Marine Recorder.  A snapshot of the data may be made available through the use of the Marine Wizard and is also available on the NBN Gateway. NRW data are merged with snapshots of datasets from across Wales supplied from other data providers (e.g. JNCC NE DASSH and Seasearch) using the suite of tools provided with the program and made available for internal use. The purpose of marine data capture through the use of Marine Recorder is to provide access to all marine benthic species and habitats data at NRW through one program.  From 2024 the data will be migrated to the new Marine Recorder Online tool.  A subset of data that is suitable for public use is published through the NBN Atlas.\tPublic record\n18857\tNRW_DS124762\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.472Z\tSSSI NH3 Critical Levels\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373632\tx:\\Resource Management\\Air Quality\\SSSI NH3 Critical Levels.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species\tThis layer provides a Critical Level (CLe) for ammonia against which impacts on a SSSI should be assessed. The value is the lowest CLe for a feature on that site. The layer gives the SSSI name SSSI code and ammonia Critical Level. The ammonia CLe can be: 1: a 1µg/m3 NH3 Critical Level indicates a SSSI has N-sensitive bryophytes/lichens as a feature or has bryophytes/lichens as key components of a habitat feature of that SSSI: 3: a 3µg/m3 NH3 Critical Level indicates a SSSI has N-sensitive vegetation or has invertebrate features that are dependent on N-sensitive vegetation. NS: all features on a site are considered not to be particularly sensitive to ammonia; this does not mean that other aspects of a development will not have negative impacts on a SSSI for example through water pollution or disturbance.\tPublic record\n17351\tNRW_DS24278\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.743Z\tCoastal Surveillance Unit (CSU) monitoring programme\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323738\tArchived as Project No. 256, Media No. 918 (File path: K\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 918)\tInternal Custodian: Gabrielle Wyn, Team Leader, All Wales Marine Advice Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Coastal Surveillance Unit (CSU) monitoring programme is comprised of 10 databases including those for abundance records for around 1000 coastal species.  The purpose of the monitoring (funded by Shell UK Ltd.) was to detect any changes that might have occurred as a result of a Shell oil pipeline that runs from a terminal at sea off Amlwch across Anglesey to the mainland. Monitoring was carried out over 10 years from 1973 - 1983 with a resurvey in 1993.\tPublic record\n18334\tNRW_DS116512\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.680Z\tMacrofaunal Community Monitoring on Inlets across Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353132\tData is currently being entered into Marine Recorder by a contractor – Marine Ecosol\tInternal Custodian:  Mike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge \u0026amp; Advice  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tLarge shallow inlets and bays are habitat complexes which comprise an interdependent mosaic of subtidal and intertidal habitats. Several of these habitat types (1140 Mudflats and sandflats not covered by sea water at low tide 1110 Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time and 1170 Reefs) are listed as Annex I habitats in their own right.   In Wales inlets occur in a number of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). This includes Carmarthen Bay and Estuary SAC which is located off the south Wales Pembrokeshire Marine SAC which is located around Pembrokeshire south Wales; and Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau SAC which is located on the south side of the Llyn Peninsula north-west Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to examine the indication of anthropogenic change on macro-faunal communities around shallow inlets and bays. This dataset comprises of infaunal species counts sediment grain size and chemical compositions.\tPublic record\n17468\tNRW_DS99549\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.021Z\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Target Notes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939353439\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\.PHASE1_INTERTIDAL_TARGET_NOTES.lyr  Data is archived as project 204, media 569\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a static spatial dataset. Phase 1 level intertidal habitat and substrata information in the format of target notes (point data) to complement a seamless layer of communities within the intertidal zone.  The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.  All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.  The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted\tPublic record\n19114\tNRW_DS125497\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.129Z\tLichen survey of three coastal SSSIs on the Llŷn Peninsula, Gwynedd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343937\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoastal%20Lichens%20Lleyn\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tBaseline survey of coastal lichens covering three SSSI on the western Llŷn Peninsula Gwynedd; Glannau Aberdaron Mynydd Penarfynydd and Porth Ceiriad Porth Neigwl ac Ynysoedd Sant Tudwal. It will involve coverage of all three SSSI but complete coverage is not anticipated given their size and difficult terrain.\tPublic record\n19004\tNRW_DS125075\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.662Z\tAberdovey Sand Dune drone survey 2021\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303735\tx:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Drone footage\\Aberdovey Sand Dunes 32WDF18RA AGC 2021.lyr\tKatrina Jenkinson, Senior Officer (Ecologist), Dwyfor and Meirionnydd Environment Team\tThis drone survey data was collected as part of a Section 16 Management Agreement (Environment (Wales) Act 2016) between Aberdovey Golf Club and NRW. The aim was to have baseline data on the sand dune line and produce an up-to-date accurate map of the current coastline.  The survey will be repeated at the end of this agreement / start of a renewal.\tPublic record\n18645\tNRW_DS123035\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.822Z\tWelsh Lakes Survey and Monitoring\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303335\tReports are storted in Library Data is stored in various places, some is in KiEco - LEAFPAC spreadsheets are on the DMS Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt;  Internal Research \u0026gt; Lake Leafpacs data and spreadsheets Older data is in the Lakes Database.\tInternal Data Custodian:  Tristan Hatton-Ellis\tThis is a compilation of lake survey and monitoring data carried out by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and it’s predecessor bodies Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Environment Agency Wales (EAW) since 1992. It consists mainly of aquatic plant (macrophyte) surveys supported by water chemistry and dissolved oxygen temperature and Secchi depth data. The datasets cover a wide range of lakes in Wales focussing on protected sites (Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) lakes) and on larger waterbodies monitored for Water Framework Directive (WFD). A selection of lakes that are not protected but likely to be of biodiversity importance are also included.\tPublic record\n17606\tNRW_DS107792\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.107Z\tSAC Monitoring: Shad Monitoring Data for Rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi (2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373932\t[A]  Data is archived as project 377, media 1387.   [B]  Species data also available in Recorder 6 - Shad Monitoring, Usk, Wye \u0026amp; Tywi Catchments 2006 - CCW -  Report no.40 CCW0002800000005.\t\tThe purpose of this data capture was to monitoring of the Rivers Wye Usk and Tywi for the presence of Shad.  These Rivers are designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) partly because they support internationally important populations of twait shad.  Allis Shad is also a feature of the sites and a secondary reason for selection as a SAC.  Records of other species of freshwater and estuarine fish are also recorded as part of this dataset. This provides a collation of existing knowledge habitat and shad data and provides evidence to design and implement a suitable juvenile monitoring programme for their condition assessment.  There were 5 survey sites along the River Usk 5 sites along the River Wye and 5 sites along the River Tywi.  Further data is available in paper format in the associated report with historical records of eggs surveys extending back to 1980.  This historical data is collated from the Environment Agency.\tPublic record\n18165\tNRW_DS115866\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.746Z\tLlyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys (2014)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383636\tData is archived as project 458, media 1519\tInternal Contact: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains the results of a project undertaken in 2014 to carry out a hydroacoustic survey and measurement of environmental variables. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the status of gwyniad and its habitat in Llyn Tegid.\tPublic record\n17882\tNRW_DS111220\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.899Z\tCollation of Information on Species and Habitats in the Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) 1975-1991\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323230\t[A] Paper report is available from NRW Reports Archive on the DMS. [B] Species Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (Survey number: MRCCW10000000033). Snap\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. This dataset is a collation of previously unpublished information concerning species and habitat records from Skomer MNR and review and assess the conservation importance of faunal species (emphasis on difficult groups of marine infauna e.g. sponges and sea squirts). Some of the information dates from 1975 however most has come from an expedition to Skomer in July 1987 and a series of diving workshops at Dale Fort Field Centre between 1988 and 1991. The data is chiefly from the seas around Skomer but records from nearby areas of the Pembrokeshire coast are also included.\tPublic record\n17984\tNRW_DS114771\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.169Z\tAcross Wales Marine Non-Native Species Survey around Welsh Marinas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373731\t[A]  Data is archived as project 321 and media 1269.   [B]  Species data is stored in Marine Recorder 2011 CCW/SOS Across Wales Non-Native Species Survey MRCCW31300000002.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Ecologist,  NRW. External Custodian:  Stuart Jenkins, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe dataset is part of an Ongoing rapid assessments for presence/absence of marine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) in Welsh marinas. A sub-section of probable invasive marine species was determined using a mixture of Welsh high-priority species and species previously found by the Marine Aliens 2 Group. The purpose of this data capture was to look at the spread of invasive non-natives in Wales and provide early warnings for new introductions with a possible view to employing eradication techniques.\tPublic record\n20433\tNRW_DS161350\t2025-03-19T09:41:02.437Z\tInland Fisheries River Restoration Strategic Allocation of Funds\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333530\tTo be confirmed\tRhodri Powell, Specialist Advisor River Restoration, Freshwater Ecosystems and Fisheries Management  there is no requirement for Data Map Wales but the data is to be included in an AGOL application.\tThis data is two layers consisting of; Habitat Restoration and Other Remedial delivery associated with the Inland Fisheries Habitat Restoration grant.   Delivery is carried out by River Trusts and summary data is collated by Afonydd Cymru.\tPublic record\n17978\tNRW_DS114776\t2024-05-28T12:37:06.830Z\tVolunteer Resource Packs for Marine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Species and Habitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373736\tData is archived as project 346, media 1322.   The report is available in Ffynnon. Contract Science Report No 987.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Biodiversity Ecologist, NRW. (Footage taken by Damon-stanwell Smith)  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA project designed to develop a series of \"volunteer packs\" to provide guidance for volunteers to take measurements on the \u0027extent\u0027 and \u0027condition\u0027 of 8 Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats and species as follows; Subtidal seagrass beds;  Mat mussel beds; Crayfish; crawfish or spiny Lobster; Fragile sponge and anthozoans communities on subtidal rock habitats; Intertidal seagrass beds; Honeycomb worm reefs; Peat and clay exposures; Natural blue mussel beds on sediment. This project was create and data captured to allow volunteers to contribute to BAP habiat and species recording by providing robust and scientific data to a described standard.  The project includes a report on the scope and discussion of best methods for volunteers for each habitat and species existing distribution maps and GIS layers for the 8 habitat and species images of each habitat and species draft resource packs and recording forms.\tPublic record\n17526\tNRW_DS101779\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.238Z\tA Survey of Native Oyster Beds (Ostrea edulis) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373739\t[A] Data is archived as Project No. 285, media no\u0027s 104,111 and 112 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 101-200\\Media 104 etc.) [B] Marine Recorder Survey (MRCCW30000000032)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe native flat oyster Ostrea edulis is a UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species for which a UK action plan exists. Native oysters have a long history of over-exploitation in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Oyster fisheries around Wales declined during the early 1900\u0027s due to intense exploitation possibly combined with disease and poor recruitment. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to only a few locations. The remaining oyster population in Wales are under threat through increased fishery effort in the existing oyster fishery and from diversification of fishing activities in the fisheries.  The purpose of this data capture was to gather data on the distribution and abundance of oysters in locations known to currently support native oyster beds; and to collect samples of oysters for age analysis and to gather data on the marine biodiversity associated with oyster beds. This dataset also helps towards a further understanding of the ecology of native oysters as well as informing management decisions (part of the UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP).\tPublic record\n17149\tEXT_DS121609\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.834Z\tOffice of National Statistics (ONS): Spatial Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363039\tX:\\Social Economic\\Office of National Statistics  to see a variety of data layers.   For Admin boundaries:  x:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\NUTS Level 1.lyr ,  x:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\NUTS Level 2.lyr ,  x:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\NUTS Level 3.lyr\tInternal Custodian: GIS Service Desk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) obtains a large variety of spatial data from the Office of National Statistics (ONS). The majority of the dataset obtained from ONS are for Wales plus border areas however some datasets do cover the whole of England and Wales.\tPublic record\n17830\tNRW_DS111187\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.581Z\tIntertidal Sediment Survey of Cosheston Pill with Reference to Shelduck Distribution\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313837\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000658)\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW  External Custodian: JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tCosheston Pill provides an important sanctuary for a number of wildfowl and wader species including redshanks curlew and shelduck. Previous studies had shown that in Cosheston Pill shelduck appeared to frequent certain areas of the Pill preferentially- this dataset contains the results of an intertidal sediment survey designed to investigate this preference further. The purpose of data capture was to assess whether or not shelduck feeding area preference in Cosheston Pill were related to variation in the distribution of their prey species and therefore related to spatial variations in the characteristics of the sediments.\tPublic record\n18279\tNRW_DS116316\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.947Z\tSeagrass Taxa and Abundance\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333136\t[A]  Data extracted from Biosys [B] X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Areas [C] X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Quadrats\t\tInformation regarding the presence and percentage cover of seagrass species at specific marine monitoring points held within the legacy Environment Agency Wales BIOSYS database. This is now NRW\u0027s store for legacy freshwater EAW data. EAW used this as the main database for storing manipulating and reporting data from freshwater and marine biological surveys at any taxonomic level. These data represent ground-truthing monitoring for the ecological assessment of seagrasses within transitional and coastal waters of Wales. Seagrass data on BIOSYS are updated as new monitoring data are made available. Monitoring for seagrass occurs June to September with records being updated after the sampling season. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data managed and / or owned by Natural Resources Wales.\tPrivate record\n19752\td5829acd-4c02-4542-b5ea-6c8951a346f4\t2024-11-12T14:23:49.109Z\tDuplicate 3.0 Otter TEST Data Discovery *** This is not a real dataset *** FULL FIELD EXAMPLE\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-353832393430323435343235363839353133343634-4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-353832393430323435343235363839353133343634\tINTERNAL LOCATION TEST: Archived as Project 360, Media #1590. GIS layer: X:\\Layer_Files\\Biology\\Species\\Example_MEDIN_Compliant_Full_Field.lyr.\tData Custodians SME: John Smith, Otter specialist. Geospatial Custodian: Joe Bloggs, Geospatial Officer.\tTHIS IS NOT REAL DATA. THE FOLLOWING ELEMENTS HAVE NOT BEEN TESTED Vertical Datum - we are waiting on encoding advice from MEDIN THE FOLLOWING ELEMENT IS STORED IN THE METADATA and XML BUT IS NOT VISIBLE iN MAIN VIEW/PDF/PERMALINK. However this will be addressed at a later point with the Datum issue. Vertical CRS type e.g. EPSG 5701 • This is NRW\u0027s test dataset to ensure staff can refer back to a fully valid record to help themselves solve validation errors • It is also to ensure all elements are displaying and exporting to multiple XML schemas. The Metadata is written to be compliant with the NRW MEDIN and GEMINI Standard. • It may temporarily be in public view when checking view formats for the public but will be removed later.\tPrivate record\n17983\tNRW_DS114765\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.152Z\tCockle and Mussel Stock (Bird Food) Surveys in the Burry Inlet 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373635\tData is archived as project 341,media 1312\tInternal Custodians:  Maritime Ornithologist, NRW  and   Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive) and for regularly occurring migratory species. The Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders. It also supports an important cockle fishery. The removal of cockles and the associated disturbance caused by fishing them needs to be balanced with bird food requirements. The aim of the project is to assess the food requirements of wading birds that over-winter in the Burry Inlet Special Protection Areas (SPAs). Data Collection is repeated annually. The purpose of this data capture was to provide data on stock abundance and distribution across the whole of the Burry Inlet. The data collected and its results will primarily be used for site monitoring and also for casework. In the future this data may be combined with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tPublic record\n17301\tEXT_DS125725\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.315Z\tCrown Estate offshore wind leasing round 5 PDA WGS84\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373235\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Crown Estate offshore wind leasing round 5 PDA\tThe Crown Estate, 1 St James\u0027s Market, London, SW1Y 4AH 020 7851 5000 enquiries@thecrownestate.co.uk http://www.ccw.gov.uk\tIn support of The Crown Estate’s Offshore Leasing Round 5 this data describes a final scenario of Project Development Areas (PDAs) for the leasing round.\tPrivate record\n19103\tNRW_DS125454\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.799Z\tHabitat Map of Wales resurvey pilot – field survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343534\tCurrently in Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Natural_Resources_Planning\\Biodiversity_Fisheries_Geodiversity\\Jon Rothwell\\P1_2022\\Checking\\Final GDB and LYR Files   But getting moved to X:Drive - location not decided yet.\tStuart Smith, Lead Specialist Advisor, NRW and Jon Rothwell, Specialist Advisor, NRW\tPhase 1 survey of 30 tetrads in Carmarthenshire and Neath Port Talbot. Digitised in the field using Qfield (android software a data capture application linked to QGIS).   Data collected as part of the Pilot for Habitat Map of Wales resurvey project.\tPrivate record\n18197\tNRW_DS116003\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.613Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Pembrokeshire 1996\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303033\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000008\tMetadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Pembrokeshire. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tPublic record\n17796\tNRW_DS111152\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.489Z\tPembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Sea Salinity Time Series Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313532\tAll the salinity data is archived under project 225, media 591.  A working copy of the files is stored at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Temperature and salinity loggers\\Salinity Loggers (starr oddi) by year and location.  Metadata about the loggers is stored in Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tWithin Pembrokeshire Special Area of Conservation (SAC) an integrated monitoring strategy at a site level is employed to produce a view of the status of each feature within the SAC as a whole. The Data is captured as part of the Across-Wales Diving Survey designed to gather information on Reef features across the Marine SAC\u0027s in Wales.  In order to ascertain whether observed changes are due to natural factors or anthropogenically created factors a suite of environmental factors also need to be considered such as turbidity temperature salinity and water chemistry. Salinity loggers have been placed at sites across Pembrokeshire Marine SAC.  The data is collected continuously although this has not been processed to produce daily averages due to the low confidence in the data. Additional salinity recordings are taken at Skomer MNR but these are covered in a separate metadata entry. Salinity is also recorded in saline lagoons within Wales. Metadata about this data is held in a separate entry. Gathering physical data such as temperature provides context and background information for the condition assessment of each of the reef features monitored.\tPublic record\n18148\tNRW_DS115998\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.489Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Ceredigion 2001\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393938\tData stored in Recorder 6 CCW0007300000004\tMetadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW  Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Ceredigion. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Ceredigion. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tPublic record\n19276\tNRW_DS161272\t2024-06-28T08:33:41.858Z\tDrinking Water Protected Area\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323732\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Groundwater Drinking Water Protected Areas.lyr x:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Protected Areas Lakes.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Protected Areas River Catchments.lyr\tDave Jones, Suzanne Lawn\tAny water bodies in Wales used to provide drinking water are designated as Drinking Water Protected Areas (DrWPAs). The designation aims to protect the water and prevent any deterioration in quality that could increase the required treatment of water to meet Drinking Water Standards.   Drinking Water Protected Areas need to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017.   This data shows the designated Drinking Water Protected Areas in Wales and is split into two separate layers for Surface Water Drinking Water Protected Areas and one for Groundwater Drinking Water Protected Areas. This aims to protect from over-abstraction and prevent deterioration in water quality that could increase the treatment of drinking water.   Within these Drinking Water Protected Areas we work with water companies to identify raw water sources that are \u0027at risk\u0027 of deterioration which would result in the need for additional treatment.\tPublic record\n20802\tNRW_DS161359\t2025-05-16T12:53:31.642Z\tNational Peatland Action Programme (NPAP) - Water Vole and Otter Records\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333539\tNot confirmed, but probably X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Watervole point.lyr\tRobert Bacon, Senior Specialist Officer, National Action Programme for Peatlands\tThis data shows evidence of water vole and otter activity in point data form. Surveys have been undertaken by our contractor to inform National Peatland Action Programme approaches to peatland restoration. No limiting information.\tPublic record\n17582\tNRW_DS102003\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.751Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Biotope SLR.FserX.T in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303033\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 552 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities on optical media and is maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Fynnon. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  SLR.FserX.T is a tide-swept algal biotope typically found on sheltered lower shore boulder cobble and pebbles on muddy sediments that are subject to enhanced tidal water movement. It is considered nationally uncommon. This dataset contains the presence/absence of the biotope SLR.FserX.T (Fucus serratus with sponges ascidians and red seaweeds on tide-swept eulittoral mixed substrata).  The purpose of this data capture was to determine the presence/absence of SLR.FserX.T biotope previously identified at Whiteford Point during a CCW Phase 1 survey and to ascertain the condition of the habitat.\tPublic record\n18812\tNRW_DS123465\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.928Z\tWind Farms\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343635\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Farm Points.lyr ,  x:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Farm Polygons.lyr\tThe data was originally supplied by Julie Avery.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18986\tNRW_DS125005\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.684Z\tVisitor count data at a selection of NRW sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303035\tLineTop: ‘Z:\\Archive\\Access Recreation \u0026amp; Education\\Recreation Data \u0026amp; Knowledge\\LineTop\\CSV Archive\\Data Transfer Prep\\For_Chambers’. Grafana: data is downloaded as CSV from the Grafana portal.\tDavid Liddy, Specialist Advisor Recreational Safety\tFor the purpose of monitoring its visitor centres National Nature Reserves (NNR) and bike trails Natural Resources Wales contracted Linetop LTD to provide data and reporting on visitor numbers.   Reports are produced quarterly and provide a breakdown of visitor numbers in each site and reserve.  This data includes:   Site cards - which provide details of counter placements and specification.   Raw Count data - raw counts from sensors.  Reports - summary data provided as MS Word documents at regular intervals.  This metadata entry also covers the Grafana Chambers Electronics downloadable visitor counter data which is time stamp and number count data which is affectively counting the same thing as the Linetop data.\tPublic record\n17696\tNRW_DS110063\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.589Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Devonshire Cup Coral Caryophyllia smithii\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303633\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/cup corals ,  Images - dell1590 C/doc and settings/MONITORING/cup corals\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Cup corals are slow growing filter feeders which are susceptible to changes in water quality and planktonic food supply. Caryophyllia smithii is a common feature of the sub-littoral benthic community of south-western Britain. Counts of cup corals have been done since 1993 using the images collected from the sponge assemblage project.  The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the population for changes in densities and to look for evidence of recruitment.\tPublic record\n18471\tNRW_DS120116\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.307Z\tRiver Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Lamprey Species Survey 2013 – 2015\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230313136\tKiECO database (WISKI)  Some data is also available on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/Running%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Ffm%2FRunning%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203%2FWye%20Lamprey\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000EE02D3CE2ED1AE4085983FEDF88CC2E5\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B90B85581%2DA33A%2D4F0D%2DB9D4%2DBCB28FC39C08%7D\tSophie Gott, Fisheries Technical Specialist, NRW Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW\tRiver Wye SAC lamprey species population condition assessment using revised Common Standards Monitoring attribute targets (JNCC 2015). Analysis of lamprey larvae surveys conducted using electrofishing technique and an analysis of population spatial extent density and age structure for the sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) river lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis) \u0026amp; brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri).\tPublic record\n17307\tEXT_DS125713\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.653Z\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Wales wide marine non-native species distribution data from iRecord (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373133\tGIS data is on x drive (both full and redacted copies): X\u0026gt;Biological\u0026gt;Non-Native Species\u0026gt;iRecord  Spreadsheets with both full and redacted copies of the data are located here: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/New%20records/Data%20from%20iRecord  Data licence stored: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/New%20records/Data%20from%20iRecord/TERMS_NRW%20download%20from%20iRecord_2023_08_17.pdf\tLaura Grant, Specialist Advisor – Marine Ecology\tThis data contains marine non-native species distribution in Wales.  The data was downloaded from iRecord (https://irecord.org.uk/ which is administered by CEH) using a pre set up query. The list of 102 non-native species of interest was provided to CEH in advance. The boundary is set to include species up to 12nm and includes a small inland buffer. The downloaded data contains data input to iRecord at all verification levels including species which have not yet been verified.\tPublic record\n17192\tEXT_DS121875\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.190Z\tForest Research: Soil Map Coverage (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383735\tx:\\Physical\\Soils\\FCE Soil Map Coverage.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Soils\\Local Soil Information.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Forestry Commission Great Britain and licence to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for use. It shows the local soil map coverage information. These datasets remain relatively static.\tPrivate record\n17737\tNRW_DS110477\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.528Z\tUpland Vegetation Survey of the Brecon Beacons Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343737\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\tPete Jones - Terrestrial Ecosystems Team Leader... Used to be Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tGeographic coverage: Brecon Beacons SSSI\tPrivate record\n17302\tNRW_DS12182\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.399Z\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G West Felling Plan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3132313832\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G West Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the developer for the wind farm development  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tPublic record\n18174\tNRW_DS115843\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.756Z\tMerthyr Mawr Topographic Surveys Conducted as part of the Sand Dune Rejuvenation Trials (2012 - 2015).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383433\tArchive Project Number: 419  Media number: 1437 [Data from 2013 surveys]  1491 [Data from 2014 surveys]  1533 [data from 2015 surveys]\tInternal Contact: Marine and Coastal Physical Scientist, Evidence Analysis Group.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to assess the topographic mobility of the Merthyr Mawr National Nature Reserve sand dunes and to monitor the sand dune rejuvenation.  Merthyr Mawr Warren sand dune rejuvenation trails began in December 2012. The dataset consists of points were topographic elevations were determined many of which were on profile lines which were profile lines. Key features were defined based on the limits. These features included ridge crests standing water new vegetation growth and the position of the frontal sand dunes.\tPublic record\n17093\tEXT_DS112485\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.287Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): Maps and Memoirs (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343835\tThe CCW MapInfo layer is available on Open Datasets when MapInfo is started up.  Selected memoirs and reports are held in CCW libraries and by Area Earth Science Officers.  X:\\Physical\\Geological\\ X:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\tGIS Service Desk Raymond Roberts, Senior Earth Scientist Geologist/ Earth Science Officer, NRW\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) maps and memoirs are the principal geological data sources in Britain. Traditionally the 1:50000 maps have been accompanied by explanatory volumes called Memoirs however now maps are often published with less comprehensive sets of explanatory notes.   Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a basic digital GIS layer of BGS geological data at 1:250000 scale and selected memoirs and reports are held by NRW libraries.\tPublic record\n17976\tNRW_DS114807\t2024-05-28T12:37:06.701Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Dee (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383037\t[A]  Data is archived as project 348, media 1325.   [B]  Species data in Recorder 6 [CCW000800000000A]\tInternal Custodian:  Tristan Hatton Ellis - Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tRiver Brook and Sea Lamprey are a designated (Annex II) feature of the River Dee Special Area of Conservation. The purpose of this data collection was to assess the conservation status of the Annex II features including species composition distribution abundance and size class composition are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment.  Monitoring was undertaken on the Dee Catchment in 2011.\tPublic record\n17177\tEXT_DS121661\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.626Z\tAerial Photography Great Britain (APGB): Welsh Digital Aerial Photography and DSM/DTM models supplied by GetMapping and Bluesky International Limited (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363631\tService: X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\WMS services\\Getmapping 12.5cm WMS 2009 to 2019.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\WMS services\\Getmapping 25cm WMS 2009 to 2019.lyr   Data - Aerial Photos: X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Aerial Images 2000.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Air photo index 2001.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Aerial Images 2009.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Air photo index 2009.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Aerial Images 2013-14.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Air photo index 2013.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Air photo index 2013 14.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Aerial Images 2013-14_NIR.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Aerial Images 2013 to 2017.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Air photo index 2013 to 2017.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Aerial Images 2017.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Air photo index 2013 to 2018.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Aerial Images 2013 to 2018.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Aerial Images 2013 to 2019.lyr   Data - DEM 5m (single band 32 bit float GeoTIFF) X:\\Basemapping\\Height\\APGB_5m_DEM.lyr  (also available in Z:\\Processing\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Management\\1 - Geospatial\\APGB_5m_DTM\\Output\\Wales_5m_DEM.tif)\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tGetMapping and Bluesky International supplied NRW this data under licence for the following years; 2000 2009 2013-14 2013-17 2015 2017 2018 2020 and 2021. In 2006 the Aerial Images were produced by Cowi see metadata number 121879.  Aerial Photography Great Britain (APGB) is a raster dataset containing aerial imagery produced by Getmapping and Bluesky International. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to the Welsh extent of this dataset as a member of the APGB. The licencing conditions mean that NRW can only use this data for internal use. APGB (Aerial Photography for Great Britain) comprises aerial photography and height data (2m DSM 5m DTM 5m contours) for government. The data itself is captured processed and delivered by Bluesky International Ltd and Getmapping Plc two of the premier aerial survey companies in the UK on a rolling three year update programme.  This date is available as a service as well as holding the actual data\tPublic record\n19085\tNRW_DS125370\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.379Z\tDevelopment and implementation of a monitoring project for two rare spiders (Heliophanus dampfi and Lasaeola prona) on bogs within Dyfi SSSI\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333730\tStored on the WID in Recorder 6\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThis project surveyed the arachnid (spider harvestman and pseudoscorpion) fauna of five bogs within Dyfi SSSI - Cors Fochno Ynys Greigiog Tynohir Gogarth Bog and Ynys-hir - during 2021 and early 2022. The primary aim was to develop new monitoring protocols based on vacuum sampling for the two Nationally Rare spiders Heliophanus dampfi (IUCN Vulnerable) and Lasaeola prona (IUCN Endangered). No monitoring with the development of targets for these two species is known from other UK sites. Both species are qualifying features of Dyfi SSSI.\tPublic record\n18795\tNRW_DS123421\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.939Z\tMostyn Marine energy Park (MEP) Outline\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343231\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine Reports\\Mostyn\\Mostyn MEP outline.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Port of Mostyn Casework\\Mostyn MEP Outline.lyr\tData originally supplied by Paul Brazier, due to role changes, now managed within the Marine Area Advice and Management Team, DPAS and Marine Services\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17825\tNRW_DS111139\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.294Z\tMonitoring of Kelp Depth as an Indication of Turbidity in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Saranac Special Area of Conservation (SAC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313339\tElectronic data archived under project 220, media number 586. A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG drive. Streamed video footage is held on the imaging drive. Mini DV tapes are stored in the fireproof safe\tInternal Contact: Mike Camplin, Marine Monitoring Team Leader, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tTurbidity provides an indication of water clarity and water quality.  The depth to which light can penetrate is reduced with increased turbidity which may be the result of natural processes in coastal and estuarine waters but can also be caused by anthropogenic causes such as coastal construction and dredge spoil dumping.  Curvie (Laminaria hyperborea) is known to be susceptible to the impacts of elevated turbidity levels and therefore changes in L. hyperborea can be an indicator of changes in turbidity.  Grazing of L. hyperborea by the common sea urchin Echinus esculentus may also have an impact of numbers of kelp plant and therefore numbers of this species present must also be taken into consideration. The purpose of this data capture was to use depth measurements for the distribution of kelp Laminaria hyperborea as a monitoring indicator for medium/long-term changes in turbidity level.  Also to measure the overall \u0027luxuriousness\u0027 (biomass) of the algal turf of the rocky reef.  This information will assist with reporting on the favourable conservation status of reef features in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau.\tPublic record\n18506\tNRW_DS121713\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.243Z\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Urban Accessible Greenspace\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373133\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Urban Accessible Greenspace.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows the catchment areas for accessible greenspaces highlighting greenspaces that are likely to have a more ‘natural’ feel. It also shows urban areas that do not currently have access to greenspace (demand) and these have been prioritised according to the number of people and the overall deprivation in the area.\tPublic record\n18169\tNRW_DS115840\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.718Z\tNewborough Topographic Surveys conducted as part of Sand Dune Rejuvenation Trials (1950-2015)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383430\tArchive Project Number: 421  Media number: 1439  [Data from 2013 surveys January and May]  1474  [Data from 2013 surveys August]  1489  [Data from 2014 surveys]   1535 [Data from 2015 Surveys]\tMarine and Coastal Physical Scientist, Evidence Analysis Group. Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group NRW.\tThere were three main purposes for this dataset:  -  To provide information on the development of remobilising and monitoring the dune system;  -  Conduct a baseline topographic survey to provide local pre-works and height information as part of the remobilisation and monitoring;  -  Provide temporal comparisons of topographic profiles and mapping the limits of define features  -  Temporal comparison of topographic profiles and mapping limits of define features.  Newborough sand dune system forms part of the Newborough Warren Ynys Llanddwyn Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the Abermenai to Aberffraw Dunes Special Area of Conservation (SAC). This dataset holds a variety of physical chemical and historic data related to rejuvenation and monitoring works completed on the dune system. The data includes topographic surveys sediment sampling LiDAR surveys drone aerial photography surveys historic aerial photos and maps.\tPublic record\n17552\tNRW_DS101928\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.528Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Site packs paper maps and field notes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393238\tMost of the files are kept in filing cabinets in the office of CCW\u0027s Grassland Ecologist - Stuart Smith, who refers to these on a daily basis.  The electronic copies are held on the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group. Copies of the site packs have also been distributed to the relevant regional offices.  The paper copies are held together in TSG files. The site files and other info on Ffynnon has not been superseded by the Recorder database as the latter only contains the quadrat data and site logistical info (e.g. not maps, descriptions, evaluations). Some files relating to sites in south Glamorgan and Gwent are kept in CCW\u0027s Cardiff office.\tData Custodian: Stuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains site packs from over 1070 sites surveyed or resurveyed during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales 1987-2004 and since. The site packs contain for each site site summary sheets (general site information and plant community area summations) site descriptions vegetation maps and plant community (quadrat and stand) data recorded using the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The data was collected to provide detailed information for each site to supplement published summary information of the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales.\tPublic record\n18821\tNRW_DS123464\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.945Z\tWind Turbines in Pembrokeshire Coast National Park\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343634\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\PCNP Wind Turbines.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.  This data appears to illustrate where applications for wind turbine have been lodged for the Pembrokeshire Coast National Park and their status.\tPrivate record\n18635\tNRW_DS123207\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.646Z\tOpportunistic Macroalgae Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323037\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Macroalgae Areas\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tWater Framework Directive opportunistic macroalgae area data for the following waterbodies from 2007 onwards; Afan Alaw Artro Braint Cefni Clwyd  Conwy Dyfi Dysynni Erch Foryd Ffraw Glaslyn Gwaun Holyhead Bay Holyhead Straits Loughor Mawddach Menai Strait MHI MHO Neath Nyfer Ogmore Swansea and Teifi. This data was mapped in a desk based study using aerial imagery.\tPrivate record\n18054\tNRW_DS115176\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.483Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera nolitii) Beds at Angle Bay in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313736\tData is archived as project 199, media 546.   A working copy of the data is held at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2008 ASML Pembs\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. For SACs in Wales Zostera noliti is a sub-feature of the Annex II habitat mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at high tide. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas such as lagoons and channels which are sheltered from significant wave action.   This data was captured to support reporting on conservation status of the Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) sub-feature eelgrass zostera noltii.  Pembrokeshire Marine SAC was selected for eight Habitats Directive Annex I habitat types and five Annex II species. The aim of this survey was to initiate eelgrass Zostera noltii monitoring at Angle Bay in Milford Haven.\tPublic record\n18474\tNRW_DS119303\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.389Z\tThreatened Lichen Species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333033\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Lichens Threatened Species 2015.lyr   The excel spreadsheet associated with this file is saved in the following location on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Lichens \u0026gt; Lichen Information \u0026gt; Lichen Statuses \u0026gt; Wales threatened lichens 2015 all records https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Information/Lichen%20Statuses/Wales%20Threatened%20Lichens%202015%20all%20records.xlsx\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tA set of records of lichens listed as threatened in GB and/or Wales localised as accurately as possible with records of less than 100m resolution excluded.\tPublic record\n18167\tNRW_DS115841\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.797Z\tDefining and mapping calaminarian grassland in mid-Wales (2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383431\t[A]  The data is available on the DMS. Reports Archive \u0026gt;  NRW Evidence Reports.  NRW Evidence Report No. 024 - Defining and mapping calaminarian grassland in mid-Wales 2014   KNOW-1206538853-16 [B]  The data is stored in Ffynnon - Folder NRWF-14-003465 Document NRW14-118779 [C]  The data is also stored on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Terrestrial Habitats \u0026gt; Lowland Grassland \u0026gt; Projects \u0026gt; Calaminarian - definition 2013 Document ID - EVID-271-338\tGrassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe 2012/13 reporting for the status of habitats under the EU Habitats Directive indicated a lack of essential data for H6130 Calaminarian grasslands of the Violetalia calaminariae  calaminarian grassland. This instigated the collection of data to identify areas of this type of habitat in mid-Wales. This dataset consists of botanical surveys conducted as a preliminary study associated with calaminarian grasslands in mid-Wales. The aim of this data collection was to gather information in order to identify lichen-rich forms of calaminaria grassland in mid-Wales with a view to obtaining information on extent of these areas in the future.\tPublic record\n18299\tNRW_DS116444\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.363Z\tFreshwater Fish ad hoc Records for Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC) reporting under Article 17\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343434\t[A]  This data is available on NBN Atlas [B]  Collation of records extract from numerous databases and ad hoc records.\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tFreshwater fish species records collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff contractors or members of the public. N.B. this dataset is an interim upload to allow Article 17 reporting to be completed. The data are being entered into Marine Recorder and a regular export will be carried out once this is complete. This export will replace this dataset. There are numerous reasons for this data capture. This dataset is a compilation of numerous records from different sources compiled for Habitats Directive Article 17 reporting related to freshwater fish species. Every six years Member States of the European Union are required (by Article 17 of the Directive) to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive. The reporting format set by the European Commission requires a separate analysis for each species and each habitat in each biogeographic region which that country covers.  This dataset is a collation of records that constitutes a gap filling exercise rather than a coherent dataset in itself. Consequently it should not be viewed as being spatially or temporally representative. However it may be used in combination with other data to make assessments of the range and / or occurrence of relevant species.  We have high confidence in the taxonomic quality of the records.\tPublic record\n17646\tNRW_DS110024\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.048Z\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) Acoustic Habitat Survey (multibeam) of Habitat and Feature Distribution in Pembrokeshire Marine Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303234\tAll data is archived at 102, 392, 515, 516, 517, 518, 519, 520, 954.  Copies are also available in the Pembroke Dock and Skomer offices.  A copy of the georeferenced tifs are also available on the MFSG drive as seamless layers at: S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Pembs Multibeam.   Fireproof safe - Stack 3, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian -  MFSG (NRW) Metadata point of contact - Data Services\tPembrokeshire Marine candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) is a large area including open coast and the Milford Haven Waterway that has been identified as being of European marine conservation importance.  Features of this site include marine species and seabed habitats.  The purpose of the Acoustic Habitat Survey was to provide information on the nature and characteristics of the seabed within defined areas of the Pembrokeshire coastline.  The dataset contains the results of a project to gather topographical and seabed characterisation information to improve the knowledge of the site and to aid in future site management and feature monitoring.  Primary focus- areas of topographical distinction such as reefs other hard substrata and sandbanks. Areas covered in the Pembrokeshire cSAC survey include; Skomer Grassholm Hats \u0026amp; Barrels Welshmans Bay East side of Skokholm St Ann\u0027s Head Milford Approaches and the majority of the Milford Haven estuary upstream as far as Picton Point.\tPublic record\n18908\tNRW_DS124767\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.193Z\tCuRVe Condition PressureOnAir Acidification\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373637\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tAir is one of the most important supporting features of ecosystem resilience. Clean air affects every habitat from freshwater to forests and upland habitats.  On a relative scale from 0 to 100 the output shows which areas are the most resilient to an increase in air pollution i.e. areas where an increase in air pollutant emissions is less likely to have an immediate effect on ecosystem condition. In interpreting this dataset it is important to note that values are based on emission of pollutants not on concentration of pollutants in the air.\tPrivate record\n20656\tNRW_DS161358\t2025-05-02T13:09:06.139Z\tCopy of record NRW Local Model Manager created at 2025-05-02T14:09:06.08+01:00[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333538\tGIS Corporate Data Store X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe ‘NRW Local Model Manager’ shows the location of NRW’s detailed local flood models which can be requested via datadistribution@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk.\tPrivate record\n18643\tNRW_DS122973\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.822Z\tRiver Wye catchment (Wales) species rich grassland creation opportunity maps\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393733\tX:\\Biological\\Floodplain Habitat Restoration\\River Wye catchment (Wales) opportunity\tStuart Smith - Grassland Ecologist.\tA detailed GIS analysis of land use Phase 1 habitats National Vegetation Classification (NVC) community data soils and designated sites for the River Wye catchment within the NRW mid-Wales operational area boundary was undertaken.   The potential hydrological suitability of each soil type for species-rich floodplain vegetation was assessed usng HOST classes. A trial of GIS-based Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis was undertaken to identify potential sites for restoration of species rich floodplain grassland within the Wye catchment.  Target NVC communities for restoration were selected (MG4 MG5 MG8 and M22-26) and criteria likely to affect restoration success identified. GIS vector datasets relevant to each criterion were then assembled or created and assigned a suitability score. A weighted analysis was then undertaken for each of the target communities resulting in a series of maps one for each plant community type indicating areas suitable for restoration.\tPublic record\n18463\tNRW_DS119297\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.083Z\tOver Head Telephone Lines on Natural Resource Wales (NRW) Land\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323937\tX:\\Utilities\\Telecoms\\OH Telephone Lines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains Overhead Telephone Lines on Natural Resource Wales (NRW) land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured overhead telephone line information.\tPublic record\n17317\tEXT_DS125513\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.244Z\tPackaging Regulations Approved Schemes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353133\tNot stored by NRW. EA Maintain this dataset https://npwd.environment-agency.gov.uk/PublicRegisterSchemes.aspx?ReturnUrl\u003d%2fPackagingPublicRegisterLinks.aspx%3fReturnUrl%3d%252fdefault.aspx\tKate Llewellyn -  Officer 2 - Once in Ops Waste Regulation\tProducers are manufacturers converters importers pack-fillers and sellers of packaging who supply to another part of the chain or to the end user. The Producer Responsibility Obligations (Packaging Waste) Regulations 2007 (as amended) oblige companies to recycle packaging waste to meet national targets. Producers\u0027 recycling obligations are based on packaging handled in the previous year.  Businesses must pass two threshold tests to become obligated producers. They must have a turnover of £2million or above and handle 50 tonnes or more of packaging in a calendar year.  Environment Agency store this data and Public Register of Compliance Schemes Reports are available via the National Packaging Waste Database. https://npwd.environment-agency.gov.uk/PublicRegisterSchemes.aspx?ReturnUrl\u003d%2fPackagingPublicRegisterLinks.aspx%3fReturnUrl%3d%252fdefault.aspx\tPublic record\n19777\tNRW_DS161317\t2024-11-13T17:03:28.863Z\tClocaenog Forest Red Squirrel Abundancy data 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333137\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Red Squirrel/Clocaenog/Population Abundance Contract 2023_2024/Data Associated with Abundancy report/Detections_original_survey_29-10-24.csv  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Red%20Squirrel/Clocaenog/Population%20Abundance%20Contract%202023_2024/Data%20Associated%20with%20Abundancy%20report/Detections_targetted_survey_29-10-24.csv\tRebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Mammals\tThis study involved the assessment of red squirrel occupancy and habitat preferences within Clocaenog Forest. A stratified random sampling strategy was employed along with a ‘targeted’ survey to maximise red squirrel detections.  This data consists of spreadsheets detailing red squirrel detections via trail cameras.   One spreadsheet is the original non-targeted/random approach the other is the targeted approach.\tPublic record\n18938\tNRW_DS124831\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.657Z\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ) Shallow Sandy Soil\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383331\tX:\\EU Directives\\Nitrates Directive\\NVZ Shallow Sandy Soil.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18190\tNRW_DS116007\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.604Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Rayers and Harding 2001\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303037\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000011.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around the Gower. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tPublic record\n17683\tNRW_DS110072\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.970Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Littoral Communities Macro scale\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303732\tImages -  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\littoral view points ,   SOP - DMS:  (Location to be provided by Helen Carby)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.   This data documents gross changes in shores and shore communities by means of viewpoint photography; identify shore or parameters requiring further or specific monitoring. The purpose of this data capture was to document gross scale changes in zonation and beach profiles.\tPublic record\n18520\tNRW_DS121657\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.446Z\tMunitions Marine Licence Applications\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363537\tX:\\Editing  Restricted use\tShelley Vince, Senior Permitting Officer (shelly.vince@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk).  Custodianship believed to have moved to Joseph Thomas.\tThe records contain coordinate data associated with current marine licence applications in Wales. This data allows the locations of licence applications to be mapped to assess their impacts on designated sites and combined effects with other proposals.\tPrivate record\n17431\tNRW_DS88158\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.055Z\tBat Roosts Database - Montgomeryshire\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838313538\t[A]  Data is archived as Project No. 175, Media 509  [B]  Copy also held in the Newtown Office database CCW Montgomeryshire General Bat Files WA0001500000001O    [C]  A Master copy held at Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park Biodiversity Information Service (BIS) Local Environmental Records Center\t\tThis dataset contains records for all species of bats recorded in roosts within Montgomeryshire. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts in order to assist Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRWs)  conservation work. In particular to provide information when dealing with development proposals to support the monitoring and notification of SSSIs and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species. This dataset contains records for all species of bats recorded in roosts within Montgomeryshire.  The data also indicates the abundance of each species at each location. Data was extracted from files as part of an exercise carried out by the Local Record Centre on behalf of former Countryside Council for Wales.  A top copy of this data is held by the Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park Biodiversity Information Service (BIS) Local Record Centre. An older copy of the data is held on NRW\u0027s copy of Recorder 6 under CCW Montgomeryshire General Bat Files WA0001500000001O however this has not been updated since 2003.\tPublic record\n18005\tNRW_DS114737\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.363Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Peat and Clay (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373337\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Peat and Clay Points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Peat and Clay Polygons.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Peat and Clay only. Seabeds formed of exposed peat or clay or in some cases both are uncommon.  Where they do occur they have been found between the tides as well as fully underwater.  They can be buried by sand or other sediments and then exposed again on a regular basis. These unique and fragile habitats are irreplaceable as they were formed millions of years ago from ancient lakebeds and forested peatland.  They are threatened by coastal infrastructure development cable laying dredging and other activities that disturb the seabed mussel fisheries and the collection of the piddocks for bait.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n18196\tNRW_DS116013\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.664Z\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Welsh Wet Woodlands 1995\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303133\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000004 Welsh Wet Woodlands\tWoodland Ecologist, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 3247 observations in woodlands of conservation interest in across welsh wet woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.    The standard woodland NVC survey methodology was used.    The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities and to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tPublic record\n18092\tNRW_DS115595\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.157Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Freshwater Pearl Mussel on the Afon Eden cSAC (2003 and 2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353935\tMonitoring data for 2003 and 2011 cycles are held in the Marine Archive as Project No. 336, Media No. 1302.  Paper survey forms for 2011 monitoring are stored in regional registered files (Meirionnydd SH73.5.24)\tFreshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW. Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW.\tFreshwater Pearl mussels are protected under national and international legislations. Afon Eden Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is the only site in Wales where pearl mussel forms the primary reason for selection of the Special Area of Conservation. The data forms part of ongoing monitoring of this site. The Afon Eden was surveyed to assess the current population status of Freshwater Pearl Mussel compared to that of a previous survey in 1997. The aim of this project was to undertake monitoring of the freshwater pearl mussel on the Afon Eden candidate (SAC) in order to be able to report on the condition as a requirement of the EC Habitats and Species Directive. There have been two monitoring cycles since the work in 1997 both in 2003 and 2011. The only data held for monitoring undertaken in 2003 are photographs taken of the site.\tPublic record\n17551\tNRW_DS101896\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.444Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy gravels (LMX.Psyllid) in Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383936\tData is archived under project No. 199, media 362\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tMuddy gravels consist of shores of mixed sediments ranging from muds with gravel and sand components to mixed sediments with pebbles gravels sands and mud in even proportions. Mixed sediments which are predominantly muddy tend to support infauna similar to mud and sandy mud shores.  Although sheltered muddy gravel habitats occur principally in estuaries rias and sea lochs in areas protected from wave action and strong tidal streams the environmental conditions of the Menai Strait are unusual in that the water is relatively turbid and although the area is largely sheltered from wave action it experiences extremely strong tidal currents reaching up to 8 knots in places during spring tides. The muddy gravel LMX.Psyllid biotope has been mapped along the west side of the Menai Straits with the mixed sediment shores between Beaumaris and Lleiniog being highly productive rich in animal and plant species. The muddy gravel LMX.Psyllid contains an unusually high number of syllid polychaetes and form a nationally important biotope uncommon within the UK. The aim of this data capture was to identify the boundary of muddy gravel and muddy gravel with canopy of tideswept Fucus serratus.  - To determine what exactly constitutes the boundary of muddy gravel - To measure the extent of the muddy gravel biotope - To determine a physical damage index for muddy gravels - To take samples at old and recently impacted areas of muddy gravel.  - To characterise the infaunal community and identify the degree and type of change using previous data.\tPublic record\n18238\tNRW_DS116294\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.110Z\tTraditional Orchards\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323934\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116294 X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Traditional orchards.lyr   (no longer exists - has it been replaced by X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Priority Habitat Traditional Orchards.lyr )\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  [Former Internal Custodians: Hillary Miller (VES) / Jim Latham, Terrestrial Ecosystem Services, NRW]          (I think this dataset is now part of the Section 7 OSPAR habitats, for some reason it has also been published on DMW in its own right and therefore has its own metadata entry. Also see NRW_DS122418).\tThis is a spatial dataset contain the location of Traditional Orchards sites around Wales. Traditional orchards are listed as a UK BAP priority habitats which are those habitats that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).   Traditional orchards are structurally and ecologically similar to wood-pasture and parkland with open-grown trees set in herbaceous vegetation but are generally distinguished from these priority habitat complexes by the following characteristics:   - the species composition of the trees these being primarily in the family Rosaceae;  - the usually denser arrangement of the trees;  - the small scale of individual habitat patches;  - the wider dispersion and greater frequency of occurrence of habitat patches in the countryside.   Traditional orchards include plantings for nuts principally hazel nuts but also walnuts. Management of the trees is the other main feature distinguishing traditional orchards and wood-pasture and parkland. Trees in traditional orchards are or were grown for fruit and nut production usually achieved through activities such as grafting and pruning; whereas timber has been the main product from trees in wood-pastures and parkland mostly derived from pollarding or selective felling. Grazing or cutting of herbaceous vegetation are integral to orchard management as they are in wood-pastures and parkland.\tPublic record\n17804\tNRW_DS111145\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.953Z\tMonitoring of Subtidal Rocky Reef in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313435\tElectronic data archived under project 223, media number 899. A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG drive. Mini DV tape is stored in the fireproof safe and streamed material on the Imaging PC.  Still images are stored on the MFSG drive and copies are available on DVD12 and DVD21 in the fireproof safe (until room available in the electronic archive).  Specimen samples are stored in the NRW lab and will be archived with The National Museum of Wales in due course.\tInternal Custodian: Mike Camplin, Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the results from an integrated monitoring strategy on a site level carried out to provide an overview of the status of reef feature within the Pembrokeshire MarineSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) as a whole.  Work is concentration on tide-swept infralittoral and circalittoral rock. 7 sites across the SAC selected and permanent monitoring quadrat established within each of the biotope complexes.  Within the upper parts of the Milford Haven sites were established at Beggars Reach; Warrior and Dockyard Bank.  Near to the mouth of the Haven; sites were established at Chapel Reef; Thorn Island; and Junkos Reef.  A site was also established within the off-shore islands of The Smalls.\tPublic record\n18340\tNRW_DS116495\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.130Z\tWoodland Grant Scheme (WGS) Walkers Welcome\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343935\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\WGS Walkers Welcome.lyr\tCustodian: GIS and Spatial Services Team..........Keith Harris Jones\tThe former Forestry Commission now Natural Resources Wales provide the following grants under the Woodland Grant Scheme for woodland owners who welcome the public into their woods: i) Community Woodland Contribution (CWC) ii) Annual Management Grant (AMG) iii) Woodland Improvement Grant (WIG). This dataset shows the locations of the Walkers Welcome areas which are intended to help inform the public of the woodlands where the owner has agreed to general access.\tPublic record\n18820\tNRW_DS123463\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.934Z\tHydro Scheme Application Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343633\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Hydro Scheme Application Sites.lyr\tMike Pitcher, Senior Land Agent Team Leader, Energy Delivery Programme\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18998\tNRW_DS124929\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.051Z\tVisible Settings of Landscapes in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393239\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Visible settings of landscapes in Wales.lyr\tJohn Briggs, Specialist Advisor: Landscape, NRW\tNature of data: showing the extent of areas visible from a range of landscape-scale polygons and view points.    Purpose of data: To assist strategic site choices when planning visually prominent development types. A web page with general introduction and links to map images produced from the data is available from Natural resources Wales. This is new data output believed to be seminal for Wales.    Resolution and extent: The visibility mapping uses a 30m resolution open-source digital terrain model for the whole of Wales out to maximum viewing distance of 35km including overlaps to/from England where relevant. The 30m digital terrain model was sourced from the Shuttle Radar Topography Mission (Van Zyl 2001) and processed using the Quantum GIS plugin developed by Cuckovic (2016). See page 23 of technical report (via the link above) for more detail.\tPublic record\n18008\tNRW_DS115074\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.623Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Boulders Habitat in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303734\tData is archived as project 199, media 550.  Species data is also available in Marine Recorder and the NBN Gateway.  A copy of the working data is available at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2009 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\\Data\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. To do this CCW has developed a programme of feature condition monitoring.In Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide and Reefs. Specific areas of interest include areas of tide-swept bedrock and boulders and areas of tide-swept Fucus serratus on mixed sediment along the Menai Strait.  The overriding objective of the survey was to establish the reference condition for the Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC using established monitoring stations (and establishing additional monitoring stations) to describe natural and unnatural changes in the communities. This will enable the development of conservation objectives for the appropriate management of the features of the SAC. This data was collected to build on the surveys conducted in 2005 2007 and 2008 to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data.\tPublic record\n20398\tNRW_DS161348\t2025-03-11T12:07:01.017Z\tEryri Montane Lake Bryophyte Survey 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333438\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FEryri%20Lake%20Bryophytes%202024\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\u0026amp;InitialTabId\u003dRibbon%2ERead\u0026amp;VisibilityContext\u003dWSSTabPersistence\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: non-vascular plants\tRocks along the shores of montane lakes in Eryri have been found to be an important bryophyte habitat supporting a range of rare and threatened bryophytes that grow only in this habitat.  Due to the limited niche of the species of this habitat and in some cases very small populations they are especially vulnerable to disturbance and a single disturbance event could lead to irreparable damage to or even loss of the Welsh population of some species. Therefore it is of high importance to fully document the bryophyte flora of the shores of these montane lakes and identify rocks that support rare and important bryophytes and are vulnerable to disturbance.   Seven montane lakes within the Eryri SSSI were surveyed in September 2024 spread between the three main massifs of Yr Wyddfa Glyderau and Carneddau.   Lakes were prioritised for survey based on the quality and quantity of recent bryophyte records. The full shore of each lake was walked and the locations of any rare or otherwise notable bryophytes encountered were recorded.   Important boulders were identified and documented with any damage present noted. A full species list was recorded from the shore of each lake.\tPublic record\n17826\tNRW_DS111190\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.367Z\tSublittoral Survey of Sarn Badrig, Sarn-y-Bwch and Cynfelin Patches 1986\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313930\tPaper reports are available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000125). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe results of a sublittoral survey of the mid Wales sarnau (reefs) carried out in 1986 to more fully describe the sublittoral communities habitats and species present on Sarn Badrig and the two smaller reefs to the south.  The purpose of this dataset was to describe the sublittoral and sublittoral fringe communities on and around the mid-Wales sarnau (reefs) and to assess their scientific interest and nature conservation importance.\tPublic record\n17745\tNRW_DS110469\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.701Z\tThe condition and conservation of montane heath and summit heath vegetation in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343639\tHardcopy of report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). PDF is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  (ex-CCW, Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist.) Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of an investigation into the relationship between species composition grazing impacts nitrogen pollution and nitrogen status of montane heath communities of the Carneddau massif in Snowdonia.  The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the links between species composition grazing impacts nitrogen pollution and nitrogen status of montane heath communities on the Cardeddau massif. The main objective was to provide information on the current status of the communities in order to indicate whether overgrazing is likely to be the main factor in their decline and whether a reduction in grazing levels might result in the restoration of these communities in North Wales.\tPublic record\n18985\tNRW_DS125083\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.387Z\tMaterials Facilities Reporting\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303833\tWaste Returns Database\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Fry, Waste Policy, EPP Metadata Point of Contact: waste.returns@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThis displays the quarterly Operator reported input and output sampling data that qualifying Materials Facilities are required to provide by the regulations. This excludes additional information reported by sites that is not required to be sampled.   Data prior to 2020 was previously published for England and Wales on the WRAP Portal.\tPublic record\n17143\tEXT_DS120011\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.701Z\tWestern Power: Utilities Electricity Components  (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230303131\tX:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Western Power Poles.LYR x:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Western Power Underground Powerlines.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Western Power Overhead Powerlines.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Western Power Towers.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Western Power Substations.lyr\tGIS Service Desk: Louise Harris\tThis collation contains information around the location of the electrical components owned or managed by Western Power.  This is a collation of a limited range of data owned by Western Power downloaded from Western Power’s DataPortal and licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal re-use for specific purposes only i.e. network planning and design. Currently this data will not be updated.  The data must not be used for asset location/health and safety plans when conducting operational field work and to prevent accidental damage to the assets. Please refer to www.lsbud.co.uk to download pdf plans intended for this type of use.\tPublic record\n18987\tNRW_DS125074\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.702Z\tFlood Map for Planning (FMfP) TAN15 Defended Zones\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303734\tGIS Corporate Data Store X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) includes climate change information to show how this will affect flood risk extents for rivers the sea and surface water and small watercourses over the next century. It shows the potential extent of flooding assuming no defences are in place.  It also indicates areas at risk from coastal erosion areas that have flooded in the past and key information on the location of flood defence infrastructure.     The FMfP can be considered as the best available information on flood risk and will replace the Development Advice Map for planning purposes when the Welsh Government implements the revised TAN15.  Although it currently has no official status for planning purposes it is a useful data source when considering flood risks.  The coming into force of the new TAN 15 and the Flood Map for Planning has been suspended due to a further consultation on the TAN.  Technical advice note (TAN) 15: development flooding and coastal erosion - https://www.gov.wales/technical-advice-note-tan-15-development-flooding-and-coastal-erosion  The FMfP introduces a new layer called the TAN15 Defended Zone.  This shows areas that benefit from risk management authority owned flood defence infrastructure that have a minimum Present Day level of protection of:     • 1% (1 in 100) annual event probability (AEP) for rivers or   • 0.5% (1 in 200) annual event probability (AEP) for the sea.     Flood defences built after 1 January 2016 must also include design freeboard and an allowance for climate change.\tPublic record\n18035\tNRW_DS114818\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.718Z\tManagement Measures for Self-Propagated Future Recovery of Crawfish, Palinurus elephas, in Welsh Waters.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383138\tReport archived as project 353, media 1332 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1332)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: NAFC Marine Centre  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe crawfish Palinurus elephas is a large benthic crustacean found mainly on rocky seabeds from the Azores in the south to Norway in the North and throughout the Mediterranean.  This species is commercially valuable and has been targeted by fisheries for over a century; however fisheries in Wales and beyond have seen a steep decline in landings since the 1960s which coincided with the introduction of net fisheries for this species.   The crawfish is a priority species for the UK Biodiversity Action Plan and is also an important component of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) designated for rocky reefs.  It has been identified by the National Assembly for Wales as one of the species of \"principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\" in Wales and is listed in the Natural Environment Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  This act requires that steps be taken that are \u0027reasonably practicable to further the conservation of the living organisms\u0027 listed and to \u0027promote the taking by others of such steps\u0027.  It is therefore important to understand the potential impact of fisheries management measures and other management measures on the recovery of this species both in designated areas and within the wider context of Welsh waters as a whole including the potential impact of regulations or management measures in adjacent waters.   The purpose of this data capture was to investigate management measures for the self-propagated future recovery of crawfish Palinurus elephas in Welsh waters. The overall aim of this survey and reporting work was to establish the potential effectiveness of existing and possible management measures (both fishery and nature conservation based) to enable self-propagated recovery of P. elephas populations in Welsh waters within the context of their declined status.  This was to be carried out through a review of relevant literature on this and other crustacean species to determine using the best available data and information the potential benefit of the different management measures.\tPublic record\n17897\tNRW_DS112379\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.536Z\tGround Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay 2008 - 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132333739\tData is held in the Marine Archive as project no 272 (media 989).\tInternal Custodian: - Marine Ornithologist, NRW. - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16000 individuals: Wetlands International 2006) of the biogeographic population of common scoter Melanitta nigra. Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters (less than 10m) in Carmarthen Bay. Common scoter are also listed as a Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has supported ground based counts since 1996 following the Sea Empress oil spill. The purpose of this data capture was to carry out one scoter count of Carmarthen Bay in January and February 2009. This work feeds in to the condition monitoring and surveillance of Carmarthen Bay SPA and contributes to Common Standards Monitoring.\tPublic record\n17215\tEXT_DS122392\t2024-05-28T12:17:03.539Z\tOrdnance Survey: Open Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232333932\tx:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\ESRI hosted OS data\\OS Open Background.lyr  x:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\ESRI hosted OS data\\OS Open carto.lyr  x:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\ESRI hosted OS data\\OS Open greyscales.lyr  x:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\ESRI hosted OS data\\OS Open Raster.lyr  x:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\ESRI hosted OS data\\OS VML carto.lyr  x:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Products\\OS Open Greenspace.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Products\\OS Open Rivers.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Products\\OS Open Roads.lyr  x:\\Basemapping\\Addressing\\OS Open Names.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Unitary Authorities Wales.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Ordnance Survey (OS) and licenced for use by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) via an open data licence issued by OS. OS open data products (OS OpenData) are a set of free digital maps of Great Britain available for anyone to use for any purpose. The maps include data on roads rivers and boundaries. This dataset includes layers derived from those available from OS.\tPublic record\n17764\tNRW_DS110343\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.509Z\tSurvival Rate of Rehabilitated Guillemots\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333433\tArchived as project no 96 (media no 371) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media371 Report available from CCW library (see library catalogue/\u0027related titles\u0027 field)\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Marine Ornithologist  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe results of a study on the post-release life expectancy and oiled and cleaned guillemots has caused concern for seabird rehabilitation. This lead to considerable concern that seabird rehabilitation programmes are of low conservation value and may even prolong the suffering of the rescued birds. The study aimed to: - Calculate the survival rates of oiled cleaned and released guillemots - Compare these with the natural survival rates of guillemots which have not been oil cleaned and released - Assess whether the survival of rehabilitated birds has increased in recent years as cleaning techniques have improved - Critically examine the quality of the available data and make recommendations for future improvements.\tPublic record\n17146\tEXT_DS121542\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.671Z\tScottish Power: Utilities Electricity Components (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353432\tX:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Scottish Power De-Energised Overhead Lines.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Scottish Power De-Energised Underground Cables.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Scottish Power Overhead Lines.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Scottish Power Poles.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Scottish Power Substations.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Scottish Power Towers.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Scottish Power Undergound Cables.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis collation of datasets is owned by Scottish Power. The data have been licenced to Natural Resources Wales for internal use only. Scottish Power own and manage a number of electrical components both on and underground helping to supply electricity to households across Wales.\tPublic record\n17492\tNRW_DS100178\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.378Z\tOpen Access - Registered Common Land (CRoW Act)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313738\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Registered Common Land 2004.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Registered Common Land 2014.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Chris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tThe CRoW Act provides for public access on foot to certain types of land amends the law relating to public rights of way increases measures for the management and protection for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and strengthens wildlife enforcement legislation and provides for better management of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB). Under Section 4 of the CRoW Act it states the following: It shall be the duty of Natural Resources Wales (formerly Countryside Council for Wales) to prepare in respect of Wales maps which together show; (a)  all registered common land and (b)  all open country.   It is a redacted version of the \u0027Common Land (CROW Act) Fully Attributed Dataset\u0027 see separate metadata entry for details. Digitisation took place in 2014 for all Wales. This dataset does not include Urban Commons Commons with Deeds for Access 1899 Commons Act Provisional Order Confirmation or Village Greens. When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access  it should be used in conjunction with Open land Open Country Registered Common Land Other Statutory Access Land and Other Dedicated Land from the same years. Contact your local authority for access to and information about Registers of Common Land.\tPublic record\n18094\tNRW_DS115610\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.174Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Tywi Catchment Area (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363130\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\17 Otter in the Afon Tywi\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned this monitoring to assess the condition and conservation status of the European otter Lutra lutra within the Afon Tywi Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and catchment. The otter which has made a significant recovery in numbers from a rapid decline in the 1950\u0027s though to the early 1970\u0027s is still considered to be rare and across most of Europe is endangered. Indeed in many countries it is now considered extinct making the populations in Wales all the more significant.  The results of this project combined with those of similar projects instigated by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) on other SAC river catchments over the past two years and in future years will provide a valuable and unique picture of the otter in Wales.\tPublic record\n19147\tNRW_DS125743\t2024-05-28T13:04:09.754Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Fish Monitoring Data - Geospatial\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373433\tX/Monitoring/Kieco\tPaul Hyatt Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting\tFreshwater fish are used as an indicator of stock status water and habitat quality artificial obstacles to migration and river flow requirements due to specific life-cycle requirements.   This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the freshwater river fish surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  For the non-geospatial delimited data format please see NRW_DS125358 \"KiEco Freshwater Ecology: Fish Monitoring Data\".\tPublic record\n19177\tNRW_DS125626\t2024-05-28T13:04:35.898Z\tEnvironmental Permits for disposal of waste sheep dip to land\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363236\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Environmental Permits for disposal of waste sheep dip to land.lyr\tDave Jones - Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tThis dataset provide details of permit details as required under the Environmental Permit Regulations issued for the disposal of waste sheep dip to land. The data comes from Natural Resources Wales Public Register. The published datasets only includes permits with an \u0027Effective\u0027 status; revoked expired or surrendered permits are not included.\tPublic record\n18927\tNRW_DS124809\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.673Z\tNRW Integrated Engineering Teams\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383039\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Integrated Engineering Teams.lyr\tMike Thomson, Specialist Advisor, Incident ManagementIncident and Emergencies, Flood and Operational Risk Management\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Integrated Engineering Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Integrated engineering Teams post Organisation Design July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tPrivate record\n18466\tNRW_DS119363\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.324Z\tPowys Intensive Livestock Units 2008 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333633\tX:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Powys intensive livestock units 2008 2017.lyr\tRachel Jarvis - conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team\tA comprehensive dataset of the locations of intensive livestock units (poultry and pigs) in Powys operational since 2004.  This is an ongoing working dataset and will be updated periodically to reflect any new developments across Powys. For internal staff use only.  See also Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2004 2017 - NRW_DS119362 and Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2017 only - NRW_DS119364.\tPublic record\n19105\tNRW_DS125474\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.797Z\tSurveying for the Bog Pseudoscorpion Microbisium brevifemoratum on the raised bog at Cors Fochno (Dyfi SSSI)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343734\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThis project surveyed Cors Fochno in October/November 2022 using standardised vacuum sampling for the very rare Bog Pseudoscorpion Microbisium brevifemoratum(Ellingsen 1903). A total of sixty-two minutes of vacuum sampling was undertaken during the survey at 17 sample points. In total five specimens of M. brevifemoratum were found at two sample points during the survey. A single adult at SN63849084 (29th October 2022) represents a new location with a further four adults found at SN64029087 (22nd November 2022) very near to where the species was found in 2021.\tPublic record\n18946\tNRW_DS124914\t2024-05-28T12:59:08.999Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Skomer Marine Conservation Zone plankton survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393134\tT:\\shared drive\\data\\biological data\\plankton  The data series has been submitted for archive at DASSH and may be onward published as open data to the NBN, EMODnet or eur/OBIS and will be added to regulaly\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Burton - Marine Environmental Assessment Officer  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Burton - Marine Environmental Assessment Officer\tTime series of consistent plankton samples taken using a Zooplankton net (200um mesh) from a single point on the North side of Skomer island. The net is hauled vertically from 40m and the methods used are designed to replicate the methods used by the Plymouth Marine Laboratory at the L4 plankton sampling station. Taxa are enumerated and recorded to various levels of taxonomic resolution depending on the group. Different taxonomists have been used throughout the time series but the data has been truncated to a standard species list.  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPublic record\n17148\tEXT_DS120645\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.803Z\tNetwork Rail: Level Crossings (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230363435\tX:\\Transport\\Railway\\Network Rail level crossings.lyr\tExternal data owner - Network Rail  The request for this data came from: Nicolas Pritchard, Flood Risk Strategy Officer, KSP - Sustainable Communities Internal Custodian GIS and Spatial Data Services\tNational Rail have more than 6300 level crossings on their rail network with multiple crossing types at some sites. They have a legal duty to assess manage and control the risk for every crossing. Natural Resources Wales have a welsh and boarders cut of this spatial dataset on the corporate data stores.\tPublic record\n17799\tNRW_DS111148\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.721Z\tMenai Strait Sublittoral Survey 1982 (Paper Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313438\tA copy of the report is available in NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above). The survey data has been entered into JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000293). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.  Paper Data and associated additional survey cards stored in off site storage – box no. CCW/NRB/118\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Paper Data: contact Records Team or Data Services Team Electronic Data: Marine Recorder Snapshot distributed by JNCC\tThis dataset consists of the result of a sublittoral survey carried out in the Menai Strait during 1982 to investigate the habitats and communities present. The dataset contains site descriptions (name location depths surveyed site features etc) lists of all algae and animal species observed and habitat data. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the sublittoral ecosystems of the Menai Strait and to determine the range and distribution of habitats and communities present within the Menai Strait and on adjacent open coasts.  Original survey forms are held by NRW mobilised data is available through the JNCC distributed Marine Recorder snapshot\tPublic record\n18541\tNRW_DS121728\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.138Z\tAbandoned coal mine discharges\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373238\tx:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Abandoned Coal Mine Discharge.lyr\tHaf Peskett, Technical Officer Groundwater and Contaminated Land, Geoscience / Knowledge Strategy and Planning\tPoint location abandoned coal minewater discharges recorded by former Environement Agency Wales based on NGR. Recorded as excel data converted to point shape files on GIS. Data is used to locate coal minewater discarges across Wales.  Data has not been updated for a number of years and won’t be up to date.   The data is generally for internal use only. Some of the data may have been provided by the coal authority.\tPrivate record\n17183\tEXT_DS121965\t2024-05-28T12:15:59.327Z\tUNESCO Geoparks (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393635\tX:\\Designations\\UNESCO\\UNESCO Geoparks.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tUNESCO Global Geoparks are single unified geographical areas where sites and landscapes of international geological significance are managed with a holistic concept of protection education and sustainable development. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this dataset through the Breacon Beacons National Park Authority and Isle of Anglesey County Council  (IOACC).\tPublic record\n17464\tNRW_DS99421\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.820Z\tPhase 2 Sand Dune Survey of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343231\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Sand_dunes Site reports are available from NRW\u0027s library.\tInternal Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing a collation of all mapped sand dune habitats in Wales. Over 40 NVC Phase 2 surveys were carried out on sand dunes across Wales. This dataset is a collation of data from these surveys grouped together for mobilisation purposes and to provide information on a Wales-wide scale. One of the major strategic initiatives of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The purpose of this data capture was to help define the sand dune vegetation resource of Wales. The surveys were initiated in the late 1980\u0027s and continued until the mid \u002790\u0027s. Digitisation of the maps took place in 2004.\tPublic record\n17651\tNRW_DS110015\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.424Z\tWelsh coastal habitats as calving and nursery grounds for the harbour porpoise (Phocoena phocoena)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303135\tData is held as Project No.65, media 33 Fireproof safe - Stack 01, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.\tRob Penrose (CSIP)\tThe results of an investigation into strandings data for evidence that harbour porpoises breed and calve in Welsh coastal waters. Harbour porpoise calves are recorded annually off the Welsh coast from mid-May onwards.  However published survey data is lacking for the region and the distribution and relative abundance of calves have not been reported. This project present data from harbour porpoise strandings on the Welsh coast collected through the collaborative UK and Celtic Marine Mammal Strandings Project examines the incidence and seasonality of calf and neonate strandings and compare stranding rates in Wales with England Scotland and Ireland. The only data associated with this project is held within the report. Raw data is held by CSIP.\tPublic record\n18021\tNRW_DS114930\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.611Z\tAcoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of Turbot Bank Pembrokeshire (March 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393330\tData is archived as Project 362, media 1347.   Data is also available through the UK Hydrographic Office.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Mapping Scientist, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA multibeam echosounder survey was commissioned by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in March 2012 over Turbot Bank Pembrokeshire. This was completed by EMU Ltd at the end of March 2012 to International Hydrographic Organisation (IHO) Order 1a standards. The data have been verified by the United Kingdom Hydrographic Office (UKHO) and fully tidally corrected to chart datum. The dataset shows the dominant bedform over the bank to be large to very large dunes with bedrock visible on the eastern edge of the study area. Further data use and analysis by CCW should help to delineate features for mapping purposes and monitor change over time.  The purpose of this data capture is to obtain bathymetry and backscatter datasets of the study area with 100% coverage.\tPublic record\n18934\tNRW_DS124803\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.510Z\tNRW Forestry Operations Teams\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383033\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Forestry Operations Teams.lyr\tGary Eccleston, Advisor 2, Geospatial Knowledge, Information Management\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Forestry operations Teams post Organisation Design July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tPrivate record\n18689\tNRW_DS123302\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.101Z\tWoodland Types Vaccinnium\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333032\tx:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Woodland Types Vaccinnium.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones\t\tPrivate record\n18454\tNRW_DS120507\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.228Z\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) A Felling Plan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230353037\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA A Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPublic record\n17171\tEXT_DS121680\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.549Z\tThe Crown Estate: Offshore Tidal and Wave Energy Site Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363830\tx:\\Resource Management\\The Crown Estate\\Offshore Wave Site.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\The Crown Estate\\Offshore Tidal Sites.lyr\tInternal Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team Internal Custodian: James Moon, Lead Specialist Advisor: Offshore Renewable Energy Programme, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team       The dataset is available to download here:  https://www.thecrownestate.co.uk/en-gb/resources/maps-and-gis-data/\tThis dataset shows the location of offshore tidal and wave energy sites for renewable energy projects issued on Crown Estate owned seabed. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to a number of Crown Estate datasets associated with the offshore renewable industries associated with tidal and wave energy. NRW can use this dataset under the terms and conditions of a bespoke Crown Estate Licence which means we can only use this data for internal business use.  NRW has access to two dataset: Tidal Sites and Wave Sites.\tPublic record\n18943\tNRW_DS124815\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.897Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Sand Lizard surveys\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383135\t[A] X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Sand Lizards 2019.lyr  [B] Spreadsheet version in DMS LAND-75481194-1033 and a copy provided to LERC (Cofnod)\tInternal Custodian: Toni Henwood, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales, Sands of LIFE Project Internal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tLocations of lizard sightings during pre-intervention surveys for the Sands of LIFE project at Tywyn Aberffraw and Newborough Warren. Data was collected between April and October 2019.\tPublic record\n18473\tNRW_DS120006\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.321Z\tKiWQM Sampling Stations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230303036\tData is stored in WISKI New layer on GIS X:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Stations.lyr\tData custodian is Martin Stephens  Simon Webb, Stuart Kay       OLLY - i can confirm that this relates to X:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Stations.lyr GARY needs to confirm if this entry, it didn\u0027t seem to be related to a specific dataset, but potentially does now.  X:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Stations.lyr???? MJ  - I don\u0027t think the the access and use is correct in this entry as only third party data may be restricted, should look at the access and use constraint statements for the KiWQM overarching dataset  - GIS team to check\tThe KiWQM Sampling Stations GIS layer provides a route of discovery for the spatial element of all data relating to water quality measurements that have taken place in and around Wales. It is a record of locations where water sampling has taken place.  Formerly this data was held as 2 layers; Open stations (Metadata # 120746) and Closed stations (Metadata # 120747). Information for sampling locations for stations in England are documented in a separate metadata entry: 120732 (Environment Agency (EA): KiWQM English Stations (3rd Party Data))\tPrivate record\n17159\tEXT_DS121849\t2024-05-28T12:15:04.816Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): Mass Movement and Landslide data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383439\tx:\\Physical\\Geological\\Landslide Records.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mass Movement Hazard on Forestry Land.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) is a UK provider of geoscientific data information and knowledge. Natural Resources Wales has licenced access to a number of BGS datasets. These licenced dataset include mass movement and landslip data.\tPublic record\n17964\tNRW_DS114458\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.645Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata Reefs in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343538\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 199, Media No 1228. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW-Maes y Fynnon\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats). Honeycomb worms build tubes from sand and shell fragments. They are found on the lower part of rocky seashores but they also need a sand supply for tube-building. They are found on the lower part of rocky seashores but they also need a sand supply for tube-building.  Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones. Of these Estuaries and their constituent mudflats sandflats and reefs large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to monitoring the extent of Sabellaria alveolata reefs. Field work was carried out during five days of spring tides 6th to 10th July 2009. Data quality and confidence considered high as all surveys were completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.\tPublic record\n18540\tNRW_DS121783\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.124Z\tCarmarthen Bay common scoter (Melanitta nigra) data from the winter 2016 - 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373833\tY:\\Marine\\Marine_Ornithology\\SPAs \u0026amp; SSSIs\\SPAs\\Camarthen bay SPA\\Camarthen Bay.gdb\tCustodian and metadata point of contact: Matthew Murphy, Senior Marine Ornithologist\tThis is data of common scoter locations collected by contractors APEM from aerial survey in the winter of 2016 to 2017. Carmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was the first fully marine SPA in the UK; it includes areas of sea coast and estuary that support a wide range of different marine habitats and wildlife. The SPA was designated in June 2003 solely for wintering common scoter (Melanitta nigra) and is the most important individual wintering site in Britain and Ireland for this species. To properly manage and report on the condition of the SPA accurate population estimates are needed.\tPublic record\n18419\tNRW_DS119284\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.413Z\tGood for People Toolkit\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323834\tV:\\Good For People GIS Toolkit Documentation: V:\\Good For People GIS Toolkit\\Documentation Project Files: V:\\Good For People GIS Toolkit\\project_files Info also available on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/are/commu/sp/Guidance/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?InitialTabId\u003dRibbon%2EDocument\u0026amp;VisibilityContext\u003dWSSTabPersistence\tMetadata / Custodian Contact: Ben Anscombe GIS and Spatial Data Team. KIM have the technical responsibility for the tool.  Ros Owen Recreation, Health and Wellbeing Team Leader  is also a key contact as she worked on gathering organisational requirements.\tThis is not a specific dataset but rather the Good for People Toolkit is a spatial modelling framework developed to aid the bringing together and combining of data on a modelling platform producing key evidence for use by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).   This metadata entry can be supplied alongside data outputs although more specific metadata may be written if required for specific outputs.   Outputs from the model could be in the form of spreadsheets spatial layers or maps but all require some form of interpretation and knowledge for them to be understood by users.     About the Model: In 2014 NRW commissioned AECOM to develop a GIS based toolkit that would help to inform decisions around the use and targeting of NRW resources in order to best deliver priorities and outcomes that are \"Good for People\".   The Good for People GIS Toolkit is a spatial tool designed to help the Good for People programmes and the associated Enabling Plans: Outdoor Access and Recreation; Communities and Regeneration; and Education and Skills.   The toolkit addresses 18 research questions specified by NRW in relation to these plans by allowing data to be viewed in combination. These cover:  Q01 - Deprivation;  Q02 - Physical and Mental Ill-health;  Q03 - Unemployment;  Q04 - Education;  Q05 - Community Facilities;  Q06 - ESTYN (annual inspection report for individual education settings and for educational authorities);  Q07 - NEET and Pre-NEET (Not in Education Employment or Training);  Q08 - Access to Natural Green Space;  Q09 - Urban Tree Canopy Cover;  Q10 - Air Quality;  Q11 - Flooding (criteria not mapped at time);  Q12 - Access to NRW Land;  Q13 - Recreation Provision;  Q14 - Crime and Anti-Social Behaviour;  Q15 - Tourism;  Q16 - Active Travel;  Q17 - Slope;  Q18 - NRW and Partner Managed Land.   The toolkit can be used in a number of different ways. It has been designed to allow users to select the data layers that are most relevant to their purposes. Users can be confident that as an NRW-wide tool all NRW managers will be using the same evidence in a visual format so that plans for different areas can be compared fairly. The Good for People GIS Toolkit should be used where possible to support strategic and operational decision making within NRW in particular to strengthen decisions involving the needs of local communities and the benefits they derive from using and engaging with the natural environment.\tPublic record\n17767\tNRW_DS110872\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.130Z\tAcoustic Monitoring of South Llyn (East of the Tudwels) and Subsequent Infauna Sampling\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383732\t[A]  Electronic data is also archived under project 236, media no 802: (K:\\archive\\Media 801-900\\Media 802)  Copies of edited material are also archived under media 802. [B]  A working copy of data is held on  S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Sidescan\\tudwals 2009.   [C]  Images are held on DVD19 and videos are on the I drive.  [D]  Samples collected were lost: samples that got lost by the couriers (citylink)  then found and destroyed by them. which could be why [E] has not been completed [E]  Species data to be supplied as spreadsheet and entered to Marine Recorder\tInternal Custodian: Charlie Lindenbaum, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team    Metadata Contact: Data Services Team       18/01/2016 - HR has contacted Matt Green and Charlie Lindenbaum about the location of the infaunal sampling data as it currently states that this data is held by Hebog. Once located this data should be entered into Marine Recorder, or at least be added to the import list. Reply stated that the samples were lost in the post.\tA side-scan sonar survey was carried out in Tremadog Bay following reports of possible scallop dredging within the Sea Fisheries Scallop Exclusion Zone.  The acoustic survey was followed up by infaunal sampling to look at the effect of disturbance of the substrata within the area. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate reports of possible scallop dredging within the Exclusion Zone and its effects on biodiversity within the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Exclusion Zone.  Confidence in this dataset is high. The side-scan data is processed to reduce errors (e.g. positional corrections).\tPublic record\n17797\tNRW_DS111150\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.547Z\tWelsh saxicolous lichen and bryophyte community data from a survey of upland Britain\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313530\tData is stored on NRW\u0027s upland ecologist\u0027s g:drive. Paper report is available from NRW library (see related titles section above).\tInternal Custodian: Jane Stevens  (used to be: Upland Ecologist, NRW)  Internal Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe British uplands have two groups of lichen species of outstanding conservation interest: 1) species of western oceanic areas and 2) species of calcareous rock at high altitudes. Phytosociological data were collected to describe the terrestrial lichen and bryophyte vegetation of rock or saxicolous habitats within 14 Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) within upland Britain with special reference to four saxicolous habitats listed in Annex I of the EU Habitats Directive. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds the Welsh data as well as copies of the project report. The work aimed to improve our knowledge of the lichen and bryophyte communities of rock habitats in upland Great Britain with particular regard to certain habitats which have importance in nature conservation legislation. The work had the following main objectives: 1. To describe the lower plant assemblages of Annex I rock habitats in selected sites which are Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). 2. To provide a preliminary classification of the lower plant communities of rock habitats in upland Britain (excluding freshwater habitats).  These objectives were achieved by extensive sampling of rock habitats using phytosociological methods within selected SACs. Those visited were in Wales: Brecon Beacons Cadair Idris Eryri and Berwyn and South Clwyd Mountains.\tPublic record\n17164\tEXT_DS121685\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.010Z\tHistoric England: Historic Areas and Buildings in England (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363835\tX:\\Designations\\English Heritage\\English Heritage Scheduled Ancient Monuments.lyr   X:\\Designations\\English Heritage\\English Heritage Listed Buildings.lyr  X:\\Designations\\English Heritage\\Engish Heritage Parks and Garderns.lyr\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a collation of spatial datasets associated with historic area and buildings that sit along the English side of the Welsh border and so are still of interest to Natural Resources Wales (NRW). NRW has obtained these dataset from Historic England under an Open Government Licence. Historic England are a public body that helps people care for enjoy and celebrate England\u0027s spectacular historic environment.\tPublic record\n17176\tEXT_DS121643\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.677Z\tCefas: Shellfish Classification Zones (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363433\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\CEFAS\\CEFAS Shellfish Classification Zones 2015.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\CEFAS\\CEFAS Shellfish Classification Zones Pre 2015.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\CEFAS\\CEFAS Shellfish Classification Zones.lyr\tMetadata - Geospatial Team  Internal Contact: Rowland Sharp, Senior Advisor, SW Area Operations (Marine)\tThis is a third party spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of Shellfish Classification Zones of England and Wales. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this data from Cefas under an Open Government Licence (OGL). NRW holds historic versions of the data as well as up to date versions of the data. For instance: - CEFAS Shellfish Classification Zones 2015 (SWF Hygine) - CEFAS Shellfish Classification Zones pre 2015 (Bivalve) - CEFAS Shellfish Classification Zones\tPublic record\n18960\tNRW_DS124878\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.048Z\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Wind Turbines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383738\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Turbines.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThese shapefiles relate to the location of wind turbines within Cefn Croes windfarm. Data initially received from Developer.\tPrivate record\n18958\tNRW_DS124879\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.133Z\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Sub Station \u0026amp; Control Building\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383739\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Substation and Control Building.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThese shapefiles relate to the Cefn Croes Sub-Station + Control Building. Data initially received from Developer.\tPrivate record\n18962\tNRW_DS124877\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.069Z\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Wooden Bridge\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383737\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Wooden Bridges.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThese shapefiles relate to the Wooden Bridge within Cefn Croes.\tPrivate record\n18547\tNRW_DS121720\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.570Z\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Planting Large/Medium/Small Woodlands\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373230\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Planting Large Medium \u0026amp; Small Woodlands.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: This map shows areas that are suitable for woodland planting subdivided into woodland size categories identifying opportunities to plant large medium or small-scale woodlands based on the number of land owners.\tPublic record\n18945\tNRW_DS124810\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.864Z\tNRW Waste Regulation Teams\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383130\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Waste Regulation Teams.lyr\tSophie Lewis, Team Member, Customer Management/Customer Hub\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Waste regulation Team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Integrated engineering Teams post Organisation Design July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tPrivate record\n18251\tNRW_DS116277\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.983Z\tRisk of Flooding from Rivers and Seas (RoFRS) Postcode Flood Likelihood Category\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323737\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116277\tDataset custodian: Nick Steele, Technical Specialist (FRA), Flood Risk Analysis/FCRM / (previously) Mark Pugh, Flood Risk Manager – Assets and Reservoir Enforcement, FORM Metadata contact: Data services team\tThis dataset is a product of a national assessment of flood risk for England produced using local expertise. This dataset is produced using [Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Sea] which shows the chance of flooding from rivers and/or the sea based on cells of 50m. Each cell is allocated one of four flood risk categories taking into account flood defences and their condition. This dataset uses OS address data and Royal Mail postcode data to show how many properties are in each of four flood risk categories in each postcode based simply on the category allocated to the cell that each property is in.\tPublic record\n17865\tNRW_DS111468\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.917Z\tEffects of the Sea Empress oil spill on seaweed animals of the West Angle tide pools (1996-1997).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343638\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above BarCode 14750 Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor. Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000085)\tInternal Custodian and metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe results of a survey investigating the effects of oil pollution on the species richness of mobile fauna living in association with the algal turf in the West Angle Tide Pools. The study compares data collected from the polluted pools at West Angle with data collected from an unpolluted tide pool system in South Devon. The purpose of this data capture was to discover the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the fauna of the Coralline alga turf fringing the tide pool system at West Angle Bay Pembrokeshire.  Samples were collected over a 14 month period beginning in April 1996. Samples were taken in April June July and October 1996 and in March and June 1997. Samples were taken from the Devon site for April June and July 1996.\tPublic record\n17870\tNRW_DS111195\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.792Z\tGallows Point Sublittoral Survey 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313935\tThe data is available on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000698).  MNCR forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe aim of this data collection was to collect baseline data on the habitats and species present in the area adjacent to the proposed marine development. Prior to this site visit there were no survey data from the site. This dataset contains the results of a site visit undertaken by CCW to record baseline data on the habitats and species present at Gallows Point in view of a proposed marina development in the area.\tPublic record\n18307\tNRW_DS116331\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.914Z\tEnvironmental Permitting Regulations - Waste Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333331\tThe data is stored from 2013-04-01 to 2015-12-31 in REGIS and  from 2016-01-01 to 2016-07-01 in Permitting And Licencing System.\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest/ Tim Morris - Permitting Centre Wales, Cardiff\tA waste management licence is a legal document issued under the Environmental Protection Act 1990. A licence authorises the treatment keeping or disposal of waste in or on the land. Once we have issued a licence neither the activities nor the area of land may be changed unless the licence is modified.\tPublic record\n17334\tNRW_DS24290\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.817Z\tPhoto Monitoring of Statutory Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323930\tSlides from photomonitoring pre-dating digital photography are archived in approximately 10 filling cabinets in NRW\u0027s archive (Bangor). Several plastic crates and carboard boxes of protected sites monitoring images in a store-room (F29) in Maes y Ffynnon\tInternal Custodian: Tom Harrison, Environmental Assessment Officer, NE EAAT.  (Originally managed by Stuart Matley, contact Monitoring Team, Operational Support Group, NRW)  Update from Tom Harrison 2021 - This dataset needs a custodian at a higher level to take responsibility for it.  Sarah Gorton has moved some of the images from the SSSI photomonitoring dataset from plastic crates into slide hangers in filing cabinets in Maes y Ffynnon.  These have been partly catalogued (to be completed post-COVID).  The other images are still in boxes/crates or their previous filing cabinets in Maes y Ffynnon.\tSystematic photo-monitoring of statutory sites including data paper records and photographic images. Steps are being taken to make the data available as a MapInfo layer with pdf links to a scanned paper record and a georeferenced point of where the photos were taken. Most images are from the 1980s onwards however there are some historical images. The files are referenced by Special Site of Scientific Interest (SSSI) name SSSI code year and the film number. The purpose of this data capture and archiving is to record and create a collation of the change of statutory sites over time.\tPublic record\n18544\tNRW_DS121724\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.240Z\tWINS Water Quality: Constraints for Reducing Sedimentation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373234\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Constraints for Reducing Sedimentation.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: This map shows constraints to intervention types for reduction of sediment entering the water environment. The number of constraints is combined across all land management intervention types so an area with one constraint for one type of action and two constraints for another type of action will show as having three constraints.\tPublic record\n18961\tNRW_DS124882\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.065Z\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Communications Mast\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383832\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Communications Mast.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThese shapefiles relate to the Communications Mast located within Cefn Croes\tPrivate record\n19317\tNRW_DS161276\t2024-07-15T09:05:20.167Z\tDigital grain sizing of Welsh shingle beaches\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20%20External%20Research/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2fteams%2fevidence%2fMarine-Evidence%2fmcps%2fEvidence++External+Research%2fShingleSourcesData\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000686B1A13B2902E43B637F5C8EB33B608\u0026amp;View\u003d%7b4CA932F0-0F0A-47A2-B5BF-FD4C292536A3%7d\u0026amp;xsdata\%3d\u0026amp;sdata\u003dOWhpVEVIbWZjM2lWNCtSRlA0OFZjTDNadFFicHVtN0NXVEovQUU3cVU0WT0%3d\tIain Fairley, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team, NRW.\tThis dataset consists of particle size analysis of five shingle beaches around the coast of Wales. The five beaches are (from north to south): Pensarn; Morfa Dinlle; Borth; Amroth; Leys Beach (Aberthaw).   Particle size information was collected using digital grain size analysis. Both an excel file containing the particle size analysis images of grain size histograms and the raw digital data are provided.  The data was collected to enable assessment of suitable quarry sources of shingle for beach renourishment.\tPublic record\n17169\tEXT_DS121681\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.499Z\tThe Crown Estate: Offshore meteorological equipment WGS84 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363831\tX:\\Resource Management\\The Crown Estate\\Offshore meteorological equipment WGS84.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to a number of Crown Estate datasets. NRW can use this dataset under the terms and conditions of a bespoke Crown Estate Licence which states we can only use this data for internal business use. This spatial dataset details the location of offshore meteorological equipement around the UK.\tPublic record\n17168\tEXT_DS121689\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.499Z\tJNCC: UK SeaMap Predictive Habitat Model (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363839\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\UK SeaMap\\UK SEAMAP predictive habitat model.lyr\tMarine Evidence Team, JNCC euseamap@jncc.gov.uk\tThe UKSeaMap is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of habitats from UK Waters. This map is generated by JNCC as a by product from the EMODnet Seabed Habitats Consortium. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have access to this data through an Open Government Licence.\tPublic record\n18360\tNRW_DS116836\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.640Z\tOtter Distribution Maps of Wales (1977-1980)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383336\tPaper format only - Held in in Evidence \u0026amp; Analysis files.   Maps are to be scanned and stored in DMS\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team       The paper maps will be scanned in-house (Worked carried out by Rhonwen Williams for the Terrestrial Mammal Group).  Long-term it would be useful to digitise the data into GIS either in-house or by offering to a third party (e.g. otter group, LERC).  The metadata is to remain as internal view only until available in an electronic format.  To check that this is not the same as the data described by EA as AFA224\tHand drawn maps showing the extent of rivers where otters are distributed.  Appears to be repeat surveys of 15 Rivers (with some additional sub-sites/tributaries).  Survey carried out 1977-1978 1979-1980 1980.\tPrivate record\n18512\tNRW_DS121699\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.757Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Riparian Woodland Planting\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363939\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Riparian Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. What the map shows: This map shows the combined constraints to planting riparian woodland for NFM benefits.\tPublic record\n18816\tNRW_DS123456\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.917Z\tTEMP Regulated Dams and Reservoirs\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343536\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\TEMP Regulated Dams and Reservoirs.lyr\tMatthew O\u0027Brien, Senior Specialist Advisor, Reservoir Safety Group, National Sevices - Reservoir Safety\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n20356\tNRW_DS161346\t2025-03-05T09:37:35.539Z\tGwent Levels - Resilient Ecological Networks\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333436\tWaiting for CDS Load\tLiz Hancooks\tGwent Levels Nature Networks · Nature Network Boundary · Core Zone · Nature Recovery Zone These files are the mapped products of the Gwent Levels Nature Network including the layers which define resilient ecological networks in this area: Core and Nature Recovery Zone.   The output of the project has identified 42 core zones covering 18979 hectares (ha) and 86 nature recovery zones covering 2214 ha as two GIS layers. These broadly comprise freshwater fen bog semi-natural grassland and native woodland ecosystems.\tPrivate record\n18024\tNRW_DS114860\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.608Z\tColonisation, Growth and Interactions of Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383630\tData is archived as projects 323 and 324 and media 1340 and 1341 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1340 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1341)\tStuart Jenkins, Bangor University, School of Ocean Sciences. Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) is a Invasive Non Native (INN) filter feeding marine invertebrate that forms large colonies carpetting hard structures on which it grows.  Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) was only confirmed in British waters in 2008.  However there are strong concerns that it will quickly colonise a much wider area as it has been found to do in other countries such as New Zealand and the USA.  After an initial survey to identify the extent of colonisation an eradication programme was undertaken by the Countryside Council for Wales. A programme of monitoring was initiated to measure the success of the eradication experiment and subsequent recolonisation and a research project undertaken in conjunction with Bangor University.  The purpose of this data capture was to assess patterns of settlement growth colonisation and species interactions of Didemnum vexillum within Holyhead Marina from season to season.  Also to look at how different disturbance magnitudes have negative or positive effects on Didemnum vexillum settlement and recruitment.\tPublic record\n17166\tEXT_DS121678\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.255Z\tThe Crown Estate: Offshore Aggregates Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363738\tx:\\Resource Management\\The Crown Estate\\Offshore Aggregates Application.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\The Crown Estate\\Offshore Aggregates Exploration.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\The Crown Estate\\Offshore Aggregates Licences.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\The Crown Estate\\Offshore Aggregates Option.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Services Team       The dataset is available to download here:  https://www.thecrownestate.co.uk/en-gb/resources/maps-and-gis-data/\tThis dataset shows the location of Marine Aggregate Agreements issued on Crown Estate owned seabed. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to a number of Crown Estate datasets associated with the offshore aggregates industry. NRW can use this dataset under the terms and conditions of a bespoke Crown Estate Licence called \u0027the Data Means Aggregate Production Licence\u0027.  The data can be used for internal business use only.\tPublic record\n18282\tNRW_DS116319\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.126Z\tSaltmarsh Species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333139\t[A]  Extracted from BIOSYS\t\tInformation regarding the presence and percentage cover of saltmarsh angiosperms (flowering plants) at specific marine monitoring points held within the former Environment Agency Wales\u0027 welsh abstract of the BIOSYS database (our main database for storing manipulating and reporting data from freshwater and marine biological surveys at any taxonomic level). This data is now managed and held by Natural Resources Wales. These data represent ground-truthing monitoring for the ecological assessment of saltmarsh within transitional and coastal waters of Wales. Welsh saltmarsh data originally held on BIOSYS are now updated as new monitoring data are made available through Natural Resources Wales. Monitoring for saltmarsh occurs June to September with records being updated after the sampling season. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data collected/owned by Natural Resources Wales.\tPublic record\n18038\tNRW_DS115272\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.815Z\tSensitive Marine Habitats Data Derived from Stereo Baited Remote Underwater Video System (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323732\tData is Archived under project 398 media 1415 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1415)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset was collected using Baited Remote Underwater Video Systems (BRUV). This was the first known study of its kind in temperate waters and involved extensive method development. The BRUV surveys collected information on fish assemblages over three sensitive habitats (kelp seagrass meadows and horse mussels). Surveys took places in the coastal waters off North Wales (Llyn Peninsula) and South Wales (Pembrokeshire) within Special Areas of Conservation as designated under the EU Habitats Directive. BRUVs were used as a non-destructive and non-invasive method to assess fish communities within Wales. This project was aimed as investigating the fish species assemblages present in each habitat type and determine the age structure of these.\tPublic record\n18026\tNRW_DS115035\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.608Z\tVegetated Shingle Habitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303335\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping  Archived as Project 363 and media 1348. (File path) K:\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1348 Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_ M_DRIVE:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\VegetatedShingle\\\tInternal Contact: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Internal Custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset showing the boundary of shingle habitats and the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) equivalent code.  A GIS layer was generated in 2012 from paper maps from the 1993 JNCC report by Sneddon and Randall.  The purpose of this data capture is to generate an electronic version of the data and make it easily available for reporting purposes.\tPublic record\n17912\tNRW_DS111788\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.071Z\tPredicting the displacement of common scoter Melanitta nigra from benthic feeding areas due to offshore windfarms\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131373838\tThis dataset is maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW HQ as Project 188 media numbers 530 \u0026amp; 531.\tInternal Custodian: Matt Murphy, Ornithologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis study used field observations and surveys combined with an individual-based modelling approach to predict the change in over-wintering mortality rates of common scoter that would result from the displacement of birds from potential feeding habitat through the avoidance of windfarms in Liverpool Bay. The purposes of this data capture were to: -  Develop a model to assist in predicting the effect of offshore windfarms on common scoter due to habitat loss and change. -  Link the non-breeding distribution of common scoter with environmental variables at selected sites -  Identify the characteristics of preferred feeding areas for common scoter within these sites.\tPublic record\n17580\tNRW_DS102026\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.539Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Number and Extent of Sea Caves, Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303236\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 559 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\SeaCavesFriog\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tSubmerged or partially submerged sea caves are included as an Annex I type within the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC). These caves may vary in size and exposure and thus the communities associated with them vary considerably depending on the structure and extent of the cave system. Sea caves generally provide habitat for colonies of encrusting animal species but may also have shade-tolerant algal communities at their mouth. Caves on the shore and in the shallow sublittoral zone are frequently subject to conditions of strong wave surge and tend to have floors of coarse sediment cobbles and boulders. These materials are often highly mobile and scour the cave walls. Caves that are subject to strong wave surge are characterised by communities of mussels Mytilus edulis barnacles Balanus crenatus cushion sponges encrusting bryozoans and colonial ascidians depending on the degree of water movement and scour at particular points in the cave system. The UK has the most varied and extensive inshore sea caves on the Atlantic coast of Europe. In Wales sea caves are important within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and are therefore a qualifying feature but not a primary reason for the site selection. As such there is a requirement to assess the number and extent of intertidal sea caves within this SAC. Further background information on the surveying of sea caves in Welsh SACs can be found in Bunker and Holt (2003).  Sea caves are important within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and are a qualifying feature. As such there is a requirement to assess the number and extent of intertidal sea caves within this SAC.  The specific aims of the survey were to identify and map the location of sea caves along the coastline use digital photography to record their extent and to measure the threat from in-fill by the railway company.\tPublic record\n17659\tNRW_DS109898\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.354Z\tAssessment of the Recreational Use and Conservation Value of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383938\ta) MapInfo tables on CD held by CCW HQ library (copy held by Jan Sherry) b) Report and data files held as paper copy and CD by CCW HQ library (copy held by Jan Sherry) c) Excel files on CD held by CCW HQ library (copy held by Jan Sherry)\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Formerly Jan Sherry Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage ecology landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales.  This dataset is a collation of all biological data associated with 25 coal spoil sites in the South Wales valleys. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a strategic assessment of conservation recreation and education use and potential of these sites.  Specific aims included initial classification of coal spoil types collation of species and habitat data to allow an initial assessment of conservation value assessment of recreational and amenity use and assessment of the potential for enhancements.  The area covered by this work included the South Wales valleys. The current dataset covers Torfaen Rhondda Cynon Taf Caerphilly Blaenau Gwent and Merthyr County Borough Council (CBCs).\tPublic record\n17160\tEXT_DS121847\t2024-05-28T12:15:04.933Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): 50k Onshore Geology (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383437\tx:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 50k Artificial.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 50k Linear.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 50k Mass Movement.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 50k Bedrock.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 50k Superficial.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) is a UK provider of geoscientific data information and knowledge. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has licenced access to a number of BGS datasets. This particular spatial data is a collation of licenced data from BGS Geology 50k: DiGMapGB-50 all of which depict the data at a scale of 1:50000. This data is split into the following themes: - Bedrock Geology - Superficial deposits - Mass movement - Artificial ground - Linear Features\tPublic record\n17581\tNRW_DS102036\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.743Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera noltii) Beds in Carmarthen Bay 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303336\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-291 is archived as Project No 199, Media No 546 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 501 to 600\\Media 546) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Zostera Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe seagrass Zostera is a flowering plant typically found in sheltered areas with a muddy / sandy substratum. Zostera beds are of significant ecological importance in that they support a diverse community of infaunal and epibenthic species algal and epiphytic species and provide refuge and feeding sites for fish and crustaceans (particularly juveniles). Furthermore Zostera beds are an important food source for wildfowl (e.g. Brent geese). Zostera beds can be an important factor in coastal defence. The root networks stabilise the sediment while the plants encourage sedimentation through sediment particle trapping and a certain degree of wave attenuation. This together with their role in supporting commercially valuable fish and crustacean species means that Zostera beds are also of economic importance. However human activity in the coastal zone (pollution dredging boat mooring etc) can affect its distribution or even destroy the beds completely. The purpose of this data capture was to determine and map the extent density and condition of Zostera noltii identified in previous years at Llanrhidian Sands and Penrhyn Gwyn in the Burry Inlet record any damage or disease of the Zostera record the presence of epiphytes and ephemeral algal species on the beds record the presence of mussel crumble and develop an index of damage.\tPublic record\n18806\tNRW_DS123982\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.454Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Lake Phytoplankton\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233393832\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data\tInternal Custodian: Harriet Robinson / Holly Self - Data Services Team.  Internal Custodian: Jennifer Owen, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team        Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tPhytoplankton form the base of many food webs in lakes. This method uses the principle that an increase in nutrients (particularly phosphorus) leads to an increases in phytoplankton biomass and a change in the taxonomic composition often leading to an increased occurrence of cyanobacteria (blue-green algae.   Only the collection data is held on the KIECO database.\tPrivate record\n17217\tEXT_DS122442\t2024-05-28T12:17:03.583Z\tMarine Recorder Snapshot supplied by Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343432\tH:\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder\tBryan Tozer - or Monica Jones Data Services Team\tMarine Recorder is the database application used by Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) and the organisations such as Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to store marine benthic sample data such as species physical attributes and biotopes. JNCC periodically compiles and combines all local Marine Recorder Snapshots into a single UK-wide version and supplies this to NRW so they can incorporate it into their version of Marine Recorder. JNCC compile the data from various third party providers including but not limited to various country agencies.\tPublic record\n18956\tNRW_DS124874\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.767Z\tWales National Forest\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383734\t\tPrimary Metadata contact: Heather Crump Estates Recreation Planning Team\tExtent of the National Forest as of the end of the first phase 2021. Boundaries derived from Forester data.\tPrivate record\n18450\tNRW_DS121231\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.919Z\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323331\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tThe project created a toolbox of mapped data and methods which enable operational staff in Wales to identify potential locations for Working with Natural Processes (WWNP).    Data has been produced for each intervention covered by the project. The final outputs include the following datasets:    • Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential:   • Riparian Woodland Planting Potential   • Wider Catchment Woodland  • Floodplain Reconnection Potential   • Runoff Attenuation Features 3.3% AEP   • Runoff Attenuation Features 1% AEP  • Woodland Constraints\tPublic record\n17361\tNRW_DS46129\t2024-05-28T12:21:21.741Z\tArctic-Alpine Plant Monitoring for Effects of Climate Change\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313239\tUnknown\tJonathon Rothwell, Terrestrial Mapping Specialist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  The data was collected orginially by: Alex Turner, H Roberts, and B Jones.\tThis data consists of 6 arctic-alpine species monitoring results. The purpose of this data capture was to determine the long-term effects of climate change on a specific group of arctic-alpine species.\tPrivate record\n17455\tNRW_DS99611\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.523Z\tNatural Resources Wales Easements Over NRW Land\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363131\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land current line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land current poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land proposed line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land proposed poly.lyr\tNatural Resources Wales GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a  spatial dataset showing the easements for Natural Resources Wales (NRW) over NRW land used for Access and pipelines. Easements are rights of access across land (can be for people / pipelines).  Data are both completed and proposed. Includes information on site name owner scale captured expiry date unique codes. This is to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and to provide a complete table showing all proposed and completed easements across NRW land.\tPublic record\n18014\tNRW_DS115065\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.018Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy Gravel, Sediments and Rocky Shore in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303635\tData is archived a project 199, media 249.  Species data is also held in Marine Recorder and on the NBN Gateway.  A working copy of the data is stored at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2008 ASML PLAS\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones. The major features of intertidal interest are Estuaries and their constituent mudflats sandflats and reefs Large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds populations of the shingle dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata. Specific areas of interest for are the intertidal sediments and rocky areas in the Glaslyn and Dwyryd Estuaries.  There were two purposes for this data capture. The first was related to sediments. Sediment data was collected to test monitoring methodology establish sites for long term monitoring and collect baseline and reference data. The second was related to the Data collected on the rocky shore to establish sand levels at key rocky areas - Port Meirion Ynys Gifftan and Cei Ballast.\tPublic record\n18514\tNRW_DS121698\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.694Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Riparian Woodland Planting\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363938\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Riparian Woodland Planting .lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets.  What this map shows: This map shows the opportunity space for planting riparian woodland for NFM benefits. It is important to note that most effective use of these opportunity spaces would be to plant deep-rooting species particularly broadleaved species in order to fully realise the NFM potential.\tPublic record\n18765\tNRW_DS123375\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.736Z\tFR Peat Restoration Ecology Suitability\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333735\tx:\\Physical\\Peat\\FR Peat Restoration Ecology Suitability.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18036\tNRW_DS115275\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.514Z\tBangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Towed Video Seabed Surveys (2009 - 2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323735\tArchived as Project 334, Media 1293 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1293)  Species data held in Marine Recorder [MRCCW3160000000A, MRCCW3160000000C, MRCCW3160000000D, MRCCW3160000000E]\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe primary aim of the survey work and reports by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats and primarily whether \u0027stony reef\u0027 habitat was present and then to assess the effects of scallop dredging on those habitats. The secondary aim of this data capture was to improve seabed habitat mapping for Cardigan Bay and assign biotopes. Surveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in 2009 2010 and 2011. Work included side-scan surveys (December 2009) grab sampling of sediments for particle size analysis (December 2009 and June 2010) and drop-down video and stills camera tows (all four surveys). The drop-down imagery dataset comprises: Survey 1 (December 2009): 72 tows (4743 stills); Survey 2 (June 2010): 59 tows (4720 stills); Survey 3 (December 2010): 13 tows (1364 stills); Survey 4 (April 2011): 47 tows (5347 stills). Video tows were approximately 10 minutes in length with still images taken every 10 seconds along the length of the tow. The videos and still images from the first two surveys were analysed by SOS and subsequently converted by Coastal Assessment Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to a format suitable for entry into Marine Recorder including assignment of biotopes. The videos and still images from the last two surveys were analysed by CALM to describe habitats species and biotopes. Grab samples were taken from each site during the first two surveys but only the sediment particle size analyses data from the first survey are included in this dataset. Sidescan survey data are not included with this dataset. The four surveys described were not directly associated with any other programme and no further work is currently planned.  However it is important to note that staff in the SOS are still working on the data collected during this survey and are planning to produce publications from it.\tPublic record\n17934\tNRW_DS112739\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.829Z\tMarine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Species in Wales (Archived 2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373339\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP Species Archived.     Data is archived under Project No. 231, Media 600.   This dataset has now been updated and superseded by a more up-to-date version of this dataset known as Section 7 species.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Biodiversity Liaison Officer (Ben Wray?), NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) was published back in 1994 and was the UK Government’s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006 it is required that the a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027. The UK BAP is one of the main focuses of this work.  As part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 CBD and NERC Act there have been a number of initiatives to identify those species and habitats whose UK populations may be under threat and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity.  The list includes all marine UK BAP priorities as well as additional habitats and species to reflect those in need of conservation action in Wales. For each species and habitat data provided it should include a map of the distribution around the Welsh coast and supporting text with information on the ecology rarity legislative protection occurrence in statutory sites and known threats. This data was captured to contribute towards providing all public bodies and Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) partnerships with information on the marine habitats and species included in the Section 42 list. This work included intertidal habitat searches for biotopes in the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Phase 1 dataset done using 1997 biotope classification codes. Biotopes associated with \u0027Section 42\u0027 habitats were compiled using the 2004 biotope codes. Subtidal habitats were plotted using records of biotopes and/or species from Marine Recorder (Snapshot data dated November 2007).\tPrivate record\n18489\tNRW_DS121548\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.905Z\tSurveys for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve (NNR) / Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the Afon Penrhos floodplain and for Geyer\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Geirch NNR in 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353438\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tMetadata Contact: Data Services  Internal Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist\tA survey was undertaken in October 2017 to determine the eastern distribution limits of Desmoulin\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on the Afon Penrhos floodplain following a previous survey covering the western part of the site in 2016. Virtually all potentially suitable habitat was surveyed. Although most new survey areas proved negative an additional 0.5ha of V. moulinsiana habitat was identified this being contiguous with the occupied habitat block lying to the immediate west surveyed in 2016. Areas of habitat not supporting the snail were judged to be too dry and/or too acidic to support the snail. It is calculated that the minimum total area of the Afon Penrhos floodplain occupied by V. moulinsiana is 11.07ha with a possible maximum of 13.2ha if a number of adjoining but potentially suitable un-surveyed areas also support the snail. The Afon Penrhos population is not only the largest of three in Wales but also probably one of the largest in mainland Britain. The significance of the population here is further enhanced by recent considerable population losses at several Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in southern England and on the nearby Corsydd Llyn SAC (Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve (NNR) / Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)). Now that the probable full extent of the population on Afon Penrhos is known it is recommended that the site should be notified as a SSSI to reduce any risk of loss or damage due to changes in site management.\tPublic record\n17175\tEXT_DS121635\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.642Z\tSea Visibility From Land (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363335\tX:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Sea Visibility From Land.lyr\tJohn Briggs, Landscape Architect (john.briggs@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk) Steven Newstead, Data and GIS for SMNR Officer (steven.newstead@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)       MMO metadata found here: https://data.gov.uk/dataset/6f46c292-4eab-4870-95af-39aecc9ab3c2/marine-management-organisation-visibility-of-sea-from-land\tThe Marine Management Organisation (MMO) has produced a dataset which models the sea visibility from land to understand the extent of intervisibility (the ability to see in a direct line of sight from one position on the earth\u0027s surface to another). This dataset is a clip (Wales only) from the MMO\u0027s full dataset that is available for download on the governments spatial data catalogue under an OGL licence (see resource locator).\tPrivate record\n18507\tNRW_DS121712\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.286Z\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Air Pollution\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373132\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Air Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: The map shows demand areas for greenspace based on locations with air quality issues with air quality being based on annual average PM2.5. Along a colour gradient from dark green to dark brown the demand increases based on a combination of three factors: the intensity of air pollution problem population density and deprivation.\tPublic record\n17547\tNRW_DS101931\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.146Z\tIntertidal Infauna of the Usk and Wye and Severn Estuaries 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393331\tThe data are archived as Project No 406, Media No 1424.  A copy is also stored at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Severn\\2004 Usk and Wye Infauna 2004\tIntertidal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tAs part of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey. This collection of sediment samples were completed to aid the identification of the biotope of the mud sediments in the two sheltered estuaries of the Usk and Wye and the more dynamic environment of the Severn estuary.  The data were used to inform the biotope mapping and identification of these estuaries for the CCW intertidal phase 1 mapping survey.\tPublic record\n17462\tNRW_DS98775\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.819Z\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373735\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPA Special Protection Areas.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas SPA.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\pSPA Proposed Special Protection Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of all Spatial Protection Areas (SPAs) in Wales. The EC Birds Directive of 1979 requires member states to establish SPAs to conserve the habitats of two categories of birds:  i) Species which are rare or vulnerable of which there are forty-eight in the UK.   ii) Some migratory species which visit our shores regularly.    SPAs in Wales are identified by NRW in conjunction with the UK Joint Nature Conservation Committee and designated by the First Minister for the Welsh Assembly Government.   These sites are also protected through being Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs). The 1994 Conservation Regulations also provide a means of protecting such areas at sea. SPAs together with SACs will contribute to a European Union network of protected sites to be known as \u0027Natura 2000\u0027 (N2K sites). SPAs have been designated over a number of years from 1982 to the present day and are on-going.  The data has been held digitally since the mid1990s.\tPublic record\n18957\tNRW_DS124880\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.065Z\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Site Boundary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383830\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Site Boundary.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThese shapefiles relate to the Cefn Croes Windfarm Site Boundary as per Lease Agreement dated 20.11.2003. Data initially received from Developer. This area forms part of the existing data held for ‘Wind Energy Development Boundary’ within the Utilities layer of MyMap.\tPrivate record\n18968\tNRW_DS124875\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.242Z\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Export Cable 132kV\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383735\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Export Cable.lyr\tDave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThese shapefiles relate to the Cefn Croes Windfarm Export Cable – 132kV within Welsh Government Woodland Estate This area forms part of the existing data held for ‘Electricity Powerlines on NRW Land’ – Legend: ‘Overhead’ within the Utilities layer of MyMap.\tPrivate record\n17975\tNRW_DS114500\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.639Z\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring: Puffin Island Survey (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353030\tMaintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Fynnon as project no. 313 media no. 1237. Videos are maintained in the archive but can be accessed through GIS layers on the M drive.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of drop-down video surveys of sites around Puffin Island Anglesey. Work was carried out by former Countryside Counicl for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) within the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  Subsequent analysis of the video taken was undertaken by Marine Ecological Soultions Ltd on behalf of CCW. The project was designed to validate existing HABMAP data of the area surrounding Puffin Island.   Methods of data capture: Drop-down video is useful for gathering large volumes of data across wide areas of seabed and often to depths beyond which divers\u0027 observations can be safely made.  Detailed methods and site locations are described in CCW Marine Monitoring Report 24a.  Amendments to these methods include the addition of a HD video camera system manufactured by C-Tecnics in place of the SD camera previously used. A Canon EOS 400D digital SLR was also used in a housing manufactured by Rovtech Systems.  Photographic stills were taken every 11 seconds along the tows to assist with species and habitat validation.\tPublic record\n18115\tNRW_DS115576\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.103Z\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Tremadog Bay (2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353736\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466. Marine Recorder: 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Tremadog Bay, July MRCCW31600000017\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: (Mike Kaiser)  Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences,  Bangor University.\tSurveys of seabed in Tremadog Bay carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in July 2013 as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (1 tow) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was about 6 km offshore of Abersoch.  The tow length was 430m and seabed consisted primarily of coarse sand.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.  The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Tremadog Bay and assign biotopes. High - experienced surveyors followed by experienced video and stills analysis.\tPublic record\n17097\tEXT_DS110043\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.525Z\tMarine Mammal and Marine Turtle Strandings (Welsh Coast) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303433\tArchived as Project 168 (media 497, 498, 499), project 171 (Media 501)\tGIS Service Desk  Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW\tThis dataset contains information on marine mammal and turtle strandings along the Welsh coast. As contributors to the project former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) hold a copy of the data up to 2006. The project is run by the Marine Environment Monitoring group and originally received sponsorship from DEFRA Natural History Museum and funding from Special Areas of Conservation (SAC\u0027s). However the projects funding was cut. The purpose of this data capture was to obtain information on disease and cause of death within marine mammal and turtle populations along the Welsh Coastline.\tPublic record\n17173\tEXT_DS121663\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.631Z\tWelsh Government: Active Travel Approved Routes (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363633\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Active Travel\\Active Travel Approved Routes.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the location of existing active travel routes for walking and cycling. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) obtained a copy of this dataset from Lle where is has been published by Welsh Government and made available under a Public Sector Mapping Agreement.  Please note - this dataset contains spatial errors with some data being mapped in the wrong place.\tPublic record\n20310\tNRW_DS161343\t2025-02-24T09:57:18.679Z\tCompliance Assessment of Welsh River Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) Against Water Quality Targets (2020 - 2023)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333433\tX:\\Resource Management\\Compliance Assessment Welsh River SACs\\Water Quality Targets (2020-2023)\tSuzanne Hearn, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems \u0026amp; Fisheries Management\tThese data identify the compliance of Welsh river Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) regarding targets for eight water quality attributes: Phosphorus Dissolved Oxygen Biochemical Oxygen Demand Total Ammonia Unionised Ammonia Trophic Diatom Index pH and Acid Neutralising Capacity.   The targets and methods of assessment are set at a UK level by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee as published in the Common Standards Guidance for Rivers 2016 and form part of the conservation objectives for the riverine SAC features.   There are nine SACs in Wales with riverine features (known in their respective Core Management Plans as):   • River Dee and Bala Lake  • Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn  • Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion (Afon Glaslyn)  • Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd  • Afon Teifi  • Afonydd Cleddau  • Afon Tywi  • River Usk  • River Wye   For the purposes of water quality reporting Natural Resources Wales (NRW) divides rivers into units called water bodies. A total of 119 (of a possible 127) water bodies each with a single sampling point were assessed for one or more attribute. The River Dee and Wye were assessed in the Welsh part of the SAC only.   Water quality data collected by NRW’s routine surveillance monitoring programme for a four-year period (1st January 2020 – 31st December 2023) were extracted from the NRW water quality database (KiWQM) to assess compliance with targets.   Data was not available for all attributes for all water bodies due to the risk-based design of NRW’s monitoring programmes. For attributes/waterbodies with insufficient data to determine compliance over the period 2020-2023 but where a result was produced in the previous assessment (using 2017-2019 data) the previous result has been rolled forward.\tPublic record\n17190\tEXT_DS121943\t2024-05-28T12:15:59.688Z\tOceanWise: Marine Themes Vector Maps (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393433\tThe various data layers are stored in the following folder: X:\\Basemapping\\Marine Charts\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis collation of datasets is managed and owned by OceanWise and licenced to NRW for internal use only. Marine Themes Vector comprises of authoritative data from different public sector data agencies including the UK and other hydrographic offices. The dataset uses different sources of data available for a particular feature and scale range derived either from the production Electronic Navigational Charts (ENCs) or from original source datasets where available. Marine Themes Vector is supplied as individual themed layers corresponding to marine reference data or base mapping required for general situation awareness planning site selection and investigation and outline engineering design.\tPublic record\n18237\tNRW_DS116298\t2024-05-28T12:43:54.927Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Operational Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323938\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Operational Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gary Ecclestone, Open Access GIS Officer, GIS and Spatial Information Services Team\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Operational Areas.    This dataset shows the six main operational areas:   - North East Wales  - North West Wales  - Mid Wales  - South East Wales  - South West Wales  - South Wales Central    These are also known as the same areas used for Area Statements.    It is the post OD (Organisational Design) catchment boundaries for the six NRW Operational Areas (or places).\tPublic record\n18869\tNRW_DS124735\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.521Z\tStatus of Dillwyn’s Dung Beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis, Morfa Harlech 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373335\tRecorder 6 - WID\tMike Howe\tDillwyn’s Dung Beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis was formerly local but widespread across England and Wales. It has declined dramatically in England and since 2000 is extant at just five of the forty known locations. In Wales this species has fared better but is restricted to grazed coastal sand dune systems. Historically known from fifteen sites it was still present at half of these in 2017. The beetle feeds on the dung of a range of mammals but is probably dependent for breeding on medium to large herbivore dung such as cattle ponies and sheep.  A survey in 2017 of all the known sites in Wales found Onthophagus nuchicornis at seven localities – Kenfig Burrows (975 adults) Merthyr Mawr Warren (36) Morfa Harlech (1) Oxwich Burrows (4) Pembrey Burrows (32) Pennard Burrows (4) and Whiteford Burrows (5). The authors regard the populations at Kenfig Burrows and Methyr Mawr to be the best in the UK. Elsewhere absence and low population levels are strongly correlated to the cessation of grazing and the quantity and quality of available dung on sand.  Following the 2017 survey a more intensive survey of Morfa Harlech which represents a northern outlier for the beetle was undertaken in 2019 to determine the status of Onthophagus nuchicornis at this site.\tPublic record\n17955\tNRW_DS114595\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.809Z\tTwite (Carduelis flavirostris) Breeding Season Survey, Wales 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353935\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and the NBN Gateway\tInternal Contacts: Data Services Team, NRW  Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThis survey updated a survey undertaken in 2002 by Snowdonia National Park and the National Trust. In Wales twite are restricted primarily to northern Snowdonia around the valley of Nant Ffrancon and Llyn Ogwen bounded by the Carneddau to the north and east by Elidir Fawr and Y Garn to the west and by the Glyder range to the south where breeding has been recorded since 1944 with post breeding flocks of over 150 birds in the recent past (1999 - 2000). This dataset also contains records other species in addition to twite which were recorded during the survey. This survey updated a survey undertaken in 2002 by Snowdonia National Park and the National Trust to assess current status and provide reliable indication of trend in range and abundance between the reference years (2002 - 2008).\tPublic record\n17446\tNRW_DS87128\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.275Z\tMenai Strait tidally exposed seabed and shores (2002)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313238\t[A] Data is archived under Project No. 79, media No\u0027s 223 and 224 (File paths are  K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 [B] Species data stored in Marine Recorder v5 (Survey number: MRCCW80000000002) [C] Paper MNCR forms in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. In addition to this the Habitats Directive (1992) requires the establishment of a European network of important high-quality conservation sites Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  The Habitats Directive identifies Reefs as a major category of habitats that should be considered for inclusion in SACs. Tidal rapids not only forms part of the reef environments classification but is also highlighted as an important site under the UKBAP habitats list. Tidal rapids is used to describe a broad range of high energy environments including deep tidal streams and tide-swept habitats. JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) defined rapids as ‘strong tidal streams resulting from a constriction in the coastline at the entrance to or within the length of an enclosed body of water such as a sea loch. Depth is usually shallower than five metres.  The data collected during this survey is being used to help define the conservation objectives for the cSAC reef features in Wales and to inform management of this feature.  One of the actions listed in the Habitat Action Plan for tidal rapids is to complete survey and recording of the extent quality and composition of the habitats and communities of tidal rapids.\tPublic record\n17165\tEXT_DS121665\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.157Z\tAPBMer: Data from the Atlas of UK Marine Renewable Energy Resources (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363635\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\ABPMer Tidal stream WGS84.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\ABPMer Wave height WGS84.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) has accesses to some of the datasets which form part of the Atlas of UK Marine Renewables Energy Resources which is owned by ABPmer. Working with major marine data holders Met Office and Proudman Oceanographic Laboratory (POL) ABPmer’s Data Team managed the delivery of the Atlas bringing the data together into GIS data layers (maps). NRW has access to this data through a data licencing agreement which is signed upon download.\tPublic record\n18508\tNRW_DS121703\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.492Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Combined Woodland Planting\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373033\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Combined Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: This map shows all types of opportunities for woodland planting that are likely to deliver NFM benefits. The combined layer is based on the constrained input layers of three types of woodland planting: Riparian/Drainage channel shelter belts floodplain woodland woodland throughout the wider catchment.\tPublic record\n20192\tNRW_DS161335\t2025-02-04T13:57:20.684Z\tTranquillity \u0026amp; Place Theme 6 full resource - connecting sound and visually tranquil places\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333335\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place  Associated Spreadsheet can be found here - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/Landscape/tranq/TP/1.%20Tranquillity%20%26%20Place%20Theme%206%20full%20resource%20Final/Theme%206%20Stats.xlsx?d\u003dw666689ff9a0e4776a5e11cd4b9090e98\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor, Landscape\tThe Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource was developed using mapped ‘themes’. You can view information and data relating to the Tranquillity and Place themes on the Tranquillity and Place Story Map. - https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/865c1876d9f64280a3dfc6e2769a46a5  • Theme 1 - relative abundance perception or experience of nature natural landscapes and greenspaces. • Theme 2 - relative freedom from intrusive visual disturbance and human influence. • Theme 3 - relative dark skies.  • Theme 4 – sound environments where natural sounds are more prominent than noise and are appropriate to context.  • Theme 5 - visually tranquil places (Themes 1 2 and 3) • Theme 6 – the full tranquillity and place resource (Themes 1 2 3 and 4). Theme 6 full resource identifies the strategic and local resource in remote rural peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent practice and provision for well-being benefits.\tPublic record\n18505\tNRW_DS121391\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.350Z\tAssessing Sensitivity of Welsh Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs) Features to Unregulated and Partially Regulated Activities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333931\tDMS Evidence\u0026gt;Marine\u0026gt;Projects\u0026gt;Contracts\u0026gt;Assessing sensitivities of Welsh MPA features to unregulated activities\tInternal Data Custodian: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team   Metadata Contact: Data Services Team        Extra info from Laura Grant: There are a couple of reasons why we don’t want to let the public to get hold of this data at the moment. It is part of an interim project and it needs further work before publication.  If the spreadsheets were released now then they could be taken out of context.  Some of the spreadsheets contain data supplied by Natural England.  NE state ‘: We are currently exploring charging for certain advice on operations products, particularly where we have used contracts to conduct some of the assessments as a way of recouping some of this cost. With this in mind, it is very important that this information is not shared any wider than yourselves.  Some of the spreadsheets contain data supplied by MarLIN.  There is ongoing discussion as to who owns the data and MarLIN currently don’t want the information released in the form it appears in these spreadsheets.  DO NOT SET FOR EXPORT.\tThis dataset is associated with the outputs from \u0027Assessing Sensitivity of Welsh Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Areas (SPA) Features to Unregulated and Partially Regulated Activities\u0027 contract which is a pilot project part of a wider programme of work. The data outputs are a series of spreadsheets which accompany a report and are split to mirror the outputs of 3 Work Packages.  The data which constitutes these spreadsheets is composed of work by consultants ABPmer alongside existing data obtained from a Natural England led contract and the Marine Biological Association. WP1_Activity_Pressure matrix_070318: Contains definitions of activities used in the study whether there is evidence to support a link between each activity and pressure whether the pressures associated with an activity and a level that needs to be considered further a review of the current pressure benchmarks and an assessment of the activity-pressure against the benchmarks to determine the likelihood of an activity exceeding the benchmark pressure. WP2_Benthic_Activity_Pressure_Feature_070318: Contains the pressure-feature relationship for each activity (for biotopes) and the accompanying sensitivity scores. WP2_Benthic_Sensitivity_Summary_070318: Gives the maximum sensitivity of the activity - feature (biotope) combination. WP2_Mobile_Activity_Pressure_Feature_070318: Contains the pressure-feature relationship for each activity (for mobile species) and the accompanying sensitivity scores. WP2_Mobile_Sensitivity_Summary_070318: Gives the maximum sensitivity of the activity - feature (mobile species) combination. WP3_All_Sites_Features_Max_Sensitivity_070318: Contains the sensitivity scores for each activity vs annex 1 habitat / mobile species (within each relevant Welsh SAC /SPA)\tPublic record\n18511\tNRW_DS121705\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.708Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Demand for Natural Flood Management\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373035\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Demand for Natural Flood Management.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. What the map shows: The map shows catchments feeding into urban areas at elevated risk from fluvial flooding; these are demand areas for natural flood management.\tPublic record\n17952\tNRW_DS114596\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.576Z\tTwite (Carduelis flavirostris) national sample survey in the UK in 1999 (Welsh data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353936\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and the NBN Gateway\tInternal Contacts:  Data Services Team, NRW Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThis dataset contains results from the first comprehensive survey of breeding twite in the UK. The aim of this survey was to provide a baseline population estimate of breeding twite in the UK. A random subsample of the UK breeding range was surveyed to arrive at a population estimate and as such the dataset does NOT contain all breeding locations of twite in the UK in 1999. There were an estimated 10 000 pairs of breeding twite in the UK in 1999 with the range lying between 6000 and 15 000 pairs. The majority 9948 (95 percent confidence intervals 6293-14 586) pairs was recorded in Scotland and England. Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) and English Nature (now Natural England) helped fund this survey as part of the Statutory Conservation Agencies/RSPB Annual Breeding Birds Scheme (SCARABBS).\tPublic record\n17631\tNRW_DS109823\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.038Z\tShellfish Stocks, Oystercatcher and Knot (bird) Modelling Data for the Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan and the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers, Phase 1 (1993-2001)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383233\tThe data associated with this project is archived as Project 158 (Media number 103)\tInternal Custodian:  Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThe Dee Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet are designated Special Protection Areas (SPAs) for nationally and internationally important populations of wintering wildfowl and waders. In order to set conservation objectives and assess the sites for Favourable Conservation Status information is required on numbers of birds using the sites and the food supplies they require to survive the winter and attain adequate body condition for Spring Migration. The three SPAs also support economically important cockle fisheries that may affect oystercatchers directly by removing some of the food available to them and indirectly by displacing them from the best cockle beds to less suitable feeding areas.  This is phase 1 of the project that will assess and use existing data to run an Individual Behaviour-based Model (IBM) to answer key questions relating to target setting condition monitoring and management of each of the three sites.  There were two main objectives to the project.  The first to assess the power of existing surveys to detect change in the cockle populations at each site and recommend possible improvements.  The second objective was to determine how each site was used by the populations of oystercatchers that roosted there.  The models can be used to establish the target baseline quality habitats in terms of invertebrate populations but also in terms of the ability of the site to support the bird populations for which it was designated and therefore establish parameters for longer term monitoring and to establish whether it is in favourable conservation status.\tPublic record\n19605\tNRW_DS161300\t2024-09-26T19:56:08.426Z\tForester Planting Plan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333030\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Planting Plan.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tThis layer holds additional restock information that is separate from the ‘Restock Areas’ layer. The records shows specific tree species that are to be planted. The data is spatially records as polygons. The data is used for coupe planning and to update the sub-compartment database.\tPrivate record\n17854\tNRW_DS111170\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.895Z\tSkomer Sublittoral Habitats and Communities 1984\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313730\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.   Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000161). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian:  Assistant Marine Conservation Officer - Skomer MNR Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  This dataset contains the results of surveys of habitats and communities from 90 previously unsurveyed or poorly described sites around Skomer Marine Reserve. The dataset includes site locations grid references site descriptions and site maps; habitat information; community categories and species distributions.  The purpose of this data capture was to add to the results of past surveys in collecting the information necessary to provide revised descriptions of habitats and communities for the whole reserve. A major aim of the survey was to produce more photographs of species and habitats in the reserve.  The survey spanned the period between the 7th of March and the 6th of September 1984 with the majority of survey work undertaken in the summer months specifically August.\tPublic record\n18069\tNRW_DS115379\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.629Z\tAll Wales Marsh Fritillary ( Euphydryas aurinia ) Habitat Quality Master Map\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333739\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Adrian_f\\MapInfo Data\\Marsh Fritillary\\Habitat Quality Surveys\\All Wales\\Surveys\\All Wales\\All_Wales_surveys_Jun2013.TAB\tInternal Contact: Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW  ( Mike Howe ) Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team or Spatial Team.\tCompiled MapInfo layer of the condition of  habitat polygons for marsh fritillaries Euphydryas aurinia. The purpose of this data capture is to ascertain the extent and condition of habitat capable of supporting populations of marsh fritillaries across Wales.\tPublic record\n18495\tNRW_DS121509\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.255Z\tWater Framework Directive Cycle 2 - Target Waterbodies Groundwater\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353039\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target Groundwater Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers; Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes wabter bodies\tPublic record\n20402\tNRW_DS161349\t2025-03-12T14:45:01.614Z\tGweunydd Nant y Twyn SSSI - National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Grassland Survey (2024)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333439\tY:\\Protected_Sites_Monitoring\\SC\\All_Area\\Nant_y_Twyn_2024_pSSSI\\Nant_y_Twyn_2024.gdb  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landmanps2/pscaer/NewPS/Forms/NRW%20Protected%20Sites%20Document%20Set/docsethomepage.aspx?ID\u003d2\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120D52000824C7CCC16790D469B8E16E1874A147100BA418224CB5E96458B8094535919D4B50074DD1354BE7A6941919D304C42B925BF\u0026amp;List\u003ddb63e9b1-3802-45a0-989c-2c3fc8864e14\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2fteams%2flandmanps2%2fpscaer%2fNewPS%2fGweunydd+Nant+y+Twyn+SSSI\u0026amp;RecSrc\u003d%2fteams%2flandmanps2%2fpscaer%2fNewPS%2fGweunydd+Nant+y+Twyn+SSSI\u0026amp;InitialTabId\u003dRibbon.Read\u0026amp;VisibilityContext\u003dWSSTabPersistence\u0026amp;xsdata\%3d\u0026amp;sdata\u003dNXVoTCtwQnA4d0J5UkJuTklodlo3dTR4S3BJei8zUUQzWmMwdmFobiszVT0%3d\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTgzMjQuMjAyNDAiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D#InplviewHasha1022bbe-43e8-4819-8a85-12eeae834e73\u003dFolderCTID%3D0x0120D52000824C7CCC16790D469B8E16E1874A147100BA418224CB5E96458B8094535919D4B50074DD1354BE7A6941919D304C42B925BF-RecSrc%3D%252Fteams%252Flandmanps2%252Fpscaer%252FNewPS%252FGweunydd%2520Nant%2520y%2520Twyn%2520SSSI-InitialTabId%3DRibbon%252ERead-VisibilityContext%3DWSSTabPersistence-WebPartID%3D%7BA1022BBE--43E8--4819--8A85--12EEAE834E73%7D-FilterField1%3DTitle-FilterValue1%3DPhase%25202%2520Survey%252C%2520Maps%2520and%2520Report%25202024\tSophie Higgett, Natura 2000 Sustainable Management Advisor - Newport, Blaenau Gwent \u0026amp; Caerphilly Environment Team\tA standard Phase 2 NVC survey was undertaken at Gweunydd Nant y Twyn as part of the Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) designation process.  Gweunydd Nant y Twyn consists of a cluster of small fields with tall wooded hedgerows. The site is of particular importance for bryophytes.  The site comprises the original Phase 2 site survey site of Mockridge et al. 1998 which has now been resurveyed (2024) and extended to include several contiguous small fields and a further extension centred on 5 small nearby fields approximately 1/3rd of a km to the north linked by a block of woodland and pasture. The total area of the Phase 2 survey site is now 39.9ha.\tPublic record\n17417\tNRW_DS46929\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.596Z\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436393239\t[A]  Original data is held within the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) CCW in Recorder 6  The data is also available as a GIS layeron the X drive (see metadata entry URI. NRW_DS99503)\tMike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW (Formerly Adrian Fowles, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, Countryside Council for Wales)\tMarsh Fritillary  (Euphydryas aurinia) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species. This dataset is a collation of all confirmed occurrence records of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) butterfly from Wales. The purpose of this data collation is to inform Local Biodiversity Action Plan  (LBAP) Partners and inform conservation decisions related to Marsh Fritillary butterflies. Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. Spatial references dates and species identifications have been validated by Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 CCW\u0027s invertebrate experts checking printed lists of records and literature references have been added where possible.\tPublic record\n17953\tNRW_DS114631\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.632Z\tCommon Standards Macrophyte Surveys: Welsh Standing Waters 2007 - 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363331\tData is archied as project no. 329, media no. 1288\tInternal custodian: Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tData collected during the survey of 41 standing water sites in Wales. With the exception of Llyn Bodlyn all the sites are designated Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) with the majority also being within Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  These surveys were undertaken as part of a wider project commissioned by the Environment Agency to meet obligations under the Water Framework Directive and provide a measure of site condition assessment for SSSI\u0027s.  The work was commissioned by the Environment Agency and funded in part by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  Work was conducted by ENSIS- ECRC. For further information please see \u0027Common Standards Macrophyte Surveys: Welsh Standing Waters 2007 - 2009. Data Summary Report to CCW as part of: Lake Macrophyte \u0026amp; Habitat Surveys for the Water Framework Directive 2007-10. Contract No: 20457 The Environment Agency\u0027  Work carried out over the course of 3 summers during 2006 - 2009. Palaeoecological and surface sediment analysis of  these sites incorporating chemical and bathymetric surveys was also conducted and this information is available in an associated report.  Please see \u0027Goldsmith B. Burgess A. Bennion H. Turner S.D. Appleby P.G. \u0026amp; Piliposian G.T. (2010). Palaeoecological and surface.\tPublic record\n17388\tNRW_DS24313\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.210Z\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Data for Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333133\t\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) data for Wales only. This data is used as historical records of the floristic data used in compiling the Nation Vegetation Classification (NVC). The quadrat data are also valuable as historical documentation of the floristic composition of geo-referenced localities in Wales about 25 years ago.\tPrivate record\n18314\tNRW_DS116442\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.909Z\tAncient Woodland Inventory\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343432\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Ancient Woodland Inventory 2011.lyr   X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Ancient Woodland Inventory 2021.lyr\tCustodian: Chris Tucker, Specialist Advisor: Technical Forestry (Resilience)\tThis dataset comprises of the boundaries of Ancient Woodland sites in Wales. Each site is categorised as either Ancient Semi Natural Woodland (ASNW) Restored Ancient Woodland Site (RAWS) Plantation on Ancient Woodland Site (PAWS) or Ancient Woodland Site of Unknown Category (AWSU).   Initially all woods greater than 2 ha shown on the Ordnance Survey (OS) 1:25 000 1st Series maps surveyed between 1880 and 1960 were considered. Evidence as to which of these were ancient was provided by presence on the earlier 19th century OS 1st Edition Maps (surveyed 1805 - 1873; scale 1:63 360). The general presumption was that woodland on 1800s maps was ancient unless there was other evidence that the wood originated between 1600AD and 1800. Supportive indications of ancient status included the wood\u0027s name its situation in the landscape and the nature of both the surrounding pattern of enclosure and the pattern of boundaries within the wood. Where available field survey data such as the presence of indicator species or other historical maps and documents were also used. In mountainous areas such as Wales the first edition Ordnance Survey 1 inch maps were hard to interpret because the heavy hatching used to indicate steep slopes tended to obscure tree symbols.  The 2021 update to the ancient woodland inventory includes changes to the 2011 version resulting from the provision of additional evidence presented to NRW through public enquiries. This includes private estate maps tithe maps management records and photographs. The evidence has been considered by a panel of NRW experts and the inventory amended if the evidence indicates that a site has had another land use for a significant period i.e. woodland is absent from the site in two consecutive epochs of OS historic maps or there has been significant ground disturbance to the site e.g. quarrying mining or development. Amendments include removal of sites from the inventory adjustments to boundaries or changes to the ancient woodland category of sites.     The 2011 version remained static until it was replaced by the 2021 version. The 2021 version is being updated at least annually as new evidence becomes available.  An older 2004 version of the dataset also exists - please see metadata for dataset number NRW_DS125363 - Ancient Woodland Inventory for Wales 2004 (AWI2004) https://metadata.naturalresources.wales/geonetwork/srv/api/records/NRW_DS125363?language\u003deng\tPublic record\n18811\tNRW_DS123478\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.920Z\tFR Peat Restoration Combined Scores\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343738\tx:\\Physical\\Peat\\FR Peat Restoration Combined Scores.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17226\tEXT_DS121976\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.436Z\tWelsh Government: Glastir Datasets (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393736\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government See all those that are marked as Glastir\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tGlastir is the Welsh Government\u0027s sustainable land management scheme through which financial support is offered to farmers and land managers. It is funded by the Welsh Government’s Rural Development Programme 2014-20. It pays for the delivery of specific environmental goods and services aimed at: - combating climate change - improving water management - maintaining and enhancing biodiversity. This data collation consists of data associated with Glastir.  Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to the following Welsh Government owned Glastir datasets: - Glastir Planted Woodland Fencing - Glastir Planted Woodland - Glastir Woodland Creation - Glastir Water Quality Parcels - Glastir Freshwater Pearls Parcels - Glastir Advanced  - Glastir Entry  - Glastir bracken spraying  - Organic Farm Schemes\tPublic record\n18107\tNRW_DS115570\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.039Z\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353730\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500) Species data held in Marine Recorder (Survey numbers  MRCCW31600000012 and MRCCW31600000013.\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Contact: Mike Kaiser or Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University.\tThis Natural Resources Wales (NRW) owned dataset contains the species data associated with drop down video survey conducted by Bangor University. Surveys of seabed in Liverpool Bay carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in June and October 2012 as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (9 tows) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 8 and 22 km offshore north east of  Anglesey.  The tow lengths ranged from 100m to 500m and seabed consisted primarily of sands gravels and pebbles with some areas of cobbles.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.  The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) seabed habitat mapping for Liverpool Bay and assign biotopes. CCW now forms part of NRW.\tPublic record\n18111\tNRW_DS115572\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.038Z\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Cardigan Bay (2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353732\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500) Data is stored in Marine Recorder (survey numbers MRCCW31600000010 and MRCCW31600000011)\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team        Part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted. The sensitive data is as follows: Pecten maximus and Aequipecten opercularis. Sensitive data must be kept confidential; general release is not permitted due to risk of harm to the environment or third parties.\tSurveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in July 2013 as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (42 tows (24 in Work Package 1b and 18 in Work Package 2b) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 2 and 22 km offshore of the coast between Cardigan and New Quay.  The tow lengths ranged from 160m to 1800m and seabed consisted primarily of sands gravels and pebbles with some areas of cobbles and boulders.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes.  The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging. The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Cardigan Bay and assign biotopes.\tPublic record\n18397\tNRW_DS118643\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.059Z\tTranquil Areas Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138363433\tPublished Dataset:  X:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas 2009.lyr   Other data related to the published data can be found in the following Folder Level: x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas  e.g. x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas 1997.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas A roads zone B.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas A roads zone C.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas active mineral zone B.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas active mineral zone C.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas airports zone B.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas airports zone C.lyr\tLead Specialist Advisor: Landscape - Jill Bullen Planning Landscape Energy and Climate Change Team, Environmental Policy and Permitting Department.\tThis data collation is made up of two datasets showing tranquil areas across Wales for 1997 and 2009. A map of the land of Wales graded to show levels of Tranquillity.  It results from combining several underlying layers of data covering different factors that either contribute to tranquillity or detract from tranquillity. Produced to provide a strategic baseline for monitoring change in tranquillity in Wales to inform strategic planning and policy-making.   Tranquillity is an aspect of the Welsh Government\u0027s Noise Action Plan (in response to the European Noise Directive) and is one of the qualitative measures of natural resources benefits provided by landscapes as used in SoNaRR 2016.  An accompanying technical report details the methodology used and provides map images of underlying data layers and the resulting Tranquil Areas Map.   Caveats:  1. The resolution of some layers that informed the map were coarse meaning that those parts of the data are unsuitable for using at a detailed scale.   2. Tranquillity was not formally defined in the accompanying technical report.  Welsh Government has more recently defined the term and Natural Resources Wales adopted this definition for SoNaRR 2016.    Tranquillity is is an untroubled state which is peaceful calm and free from unwanted disturbances. This can refer to a state of mind or a particular environment.  The 2009 dataset repeats more or less the same methodology used for a similar study in 1997.  This allows change over time to be reported. Other projects in other parts of the UK using data at better resolution and with more sophisticated analysis means that the Wales 1997 and 2009 studies are regarded as crude.  However to date the 2009 study is the most recent Tranquil Areas map available for Wales.\tPublic record\n17857\tNRW_DS111647\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.488Z\tAccess Maps - Promoted Regional Routes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131363437\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\WNAR\tInternal Custodian: Jill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis data set is one of a number of MapInfo tables that are used to deliver the Welsh National Access Register (WNAR) project.  The collation and presentation of data on a number of routes and trails throughout Wales in an easy-to-view format enables people to find out more about places of interest and their accessibility. The dataset was first created in 2005. It is maintained and added to regularly during the year due to changing partnership information as well as in response to annual agreement partnership checks. This project helps to deliver the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) \u0027PE04 Enjoying the Outdoors\u0027  programme output \u0027More people from all sectors of society will regularly be using and benefiting from widespread and equitable access to countryside coast and urban green-space\u0027.  The overall aim of the project was to get more people (1% annual increase) from all sectors of society regularly participating in open-air recreation and exercise. 1. Increase availability of high quality recreation resource including public and other urban green-space without compromising environmental and cultural interests  2. Provide readily available targeted information about recreation opportunities and encourage greater awareness of rights and responsibility that will lead to positive behaviour changes  3. Provide a means of finding accommodation alongside the recreational opportunities in discussion with our partners.\tPublic record\n19010\tNRW_DS125288\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.401Z\tBait digging evidence database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323838\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/MPANLICACT/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B8803200A-1E1E-4EA3-A959-6791FB8FBD5D%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dR3772_BaitDiggingEvidenceDatabase_100222.xlsx\u0026amp;action\u003ddefault\u0026amp;mobileredirect\u003dtrue\u0026amp;DefaultItemOpen\u003d1\u0026amp;CID\u003dE7498CFC-2686-4650-98FC-FBFA5BFF8B67\u0026amp;wdLOR\u003dcE61ECA1C-CF4D-43B9-ACF3-CADEB8A6DAE6\tLaura Grant, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe bait digging evidence database (an Excel spreadsheet) contains the detailed information which forms the rationale for the report produced by ABPmer entitled ‘West V. Robbins K. Frost N. Hull S. 2022. Bait Digging Management Options. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 602 74pp NRW Bangor’.   It displays information about each of the 11 bait digging sites in Wales in order to inform potential management measures.  This includes information such as sediment type exposure area of the site intensity of digging extent of Section 7 muddy gravels recovery times of sediment protected sites present site access and proximity of potential displacement sites.  It also contains the intensity scoring which uses proportion of the site used for bait digging and intensity of digging to rank and prioritise the sites.\tPublic record\n17836\tNRW_DS111166\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.439Z\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Biological Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313636\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The ECN data is also available from CEH. b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tInternal Contact: Victoria Bowmaker, or Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of biological data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site - Yr Wyddfa / Snowdon North Wales.The 700ha site incorporates the summit of Yr Wyddfa and includes three additional summits over 800 m it also includes 5 lakes.  Aspects of the vegetation vertebrates and invertebrates at the site are monitored following ECN protocols. As well as the ECN monitoring protocols other data collected purely for Natural Resources Wales (NRW) use and include data on the arctic alpine flora and fungi. Phenological information is also collected (information on the timing if things e.g. tadpole hatching). The purpose of this data capture is to collect data on the biodiversity of the site and how it changes over time in order to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the terrestrial environment.\tPublic record\n19023\tNRW_DS125188\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.478Z\tPolitical Boundaries of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313838\tNRWGDBV.GISDATA.WALES X:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Wales.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Olly Ishmael\tPolitical boundary of Wales\tPublic record\n17864\tNRW_DS111488\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.799Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: West Wales (West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre - WWBIC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343838\tWest Wales Biodiversity Information Centre  LRC hold the physical data set on their server. If you want extra information about the LERCs contact: Enterprise Data Management Specialist Advisor - Barny Letheren.   X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to WWBIC.lyr\tWWBIC are the custodians on behalf of NRW Ruth Harding is the main lead with WWBIC  This data will be incoprorated in the GIS layers that NRW also received\tThis dataset is made up of a collation of data (sensitive species only) derived from Natural Resources Wales (formerly Countryside Council for Wales) regional offices in West Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to mobilise a valuable data resource in order to provide baseline data to inform conservation work research site management and decision-making within NRW and externally. Records are for NRW\u0027s West region which includes Carmarthenshire Pembrokeshire and Ceredigion. Many records are for designated sites and a proportion are from the wider countryside. The majority of records have six figure grid references some are site centroids.\tPublic record\n18108\tNRW_DS115580\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.968Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Northern Atlantic Wet Heath, European Dry Heath and Raised Bog Habitats within Cernydd Carmel candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353830\tAssociated data is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\04 Erica Tetralix Cernydd Carmel cSAC\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tThis dataset includes data collected from an assessment of the extent and condition of three Annex I habitats of the EC Habitats Directive at the Cernydd Carmel cSAC; namely North Atlantic Wet Heaths with Erica tetralix European Dry Heaths (all subtypes) and Active Raised Bogs. Two principle objectives were defined: 1. To carry out an initial condition survey of the Annex I listed habitats using condition classes provided by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff 2. To monitor the condition of the Annex I listed habitats against performance indicator criteria provided by former CCW staff\tPublic record\n17230\tEXT_DS122014\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.431Z\tKeep Wales Tidy: Seaside Awards (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303134\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Seaside Award.lyr\tHelena Sykes - GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis spatial dataset show the location fo beaches in Wales that have been awarded with a Seaside Award. The Seaside Award is the nationwide standard for the best beaches across the UK. While Seaside Award beaches can be vastly different the flag is a symbol of quality which ensures visitors are guaranteed to find a clean attractive and well managed coastal stretch.\tPublic record\n17103\tEXT_DS112862\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.622Z\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Survey of Sabellaria Reefs at Criccieth North Wales (2001) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132383632\tArchived as Project 284, Media 1110.  Species data is held in Marine Recorder [MRCCW30000000039]\tGIS Service Desk  Subtidal Team Leader, MFSG, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP). The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006. Sabellaria alveolata reefs are formed by the honeycomb worm Sabellaria alveolata a polychaete which constructs tubes in tightly packed masses with a distinctive honeycomb-like appearance. These reefs can be up to 30 or even 50 cm thick and take the form of hummocks sheets or more massive formations. Reefs are mainly found on the bottom third of the shore but may reach mean high water of neap tides and extend into the shallow subtidal in places. Few studies have directly examined the S.alveolata associated communities and the majority of which were conducted abroad. Studies have been undertaken using quantitative sampling of algae and epifaunal communities studying reefs around the UK however no examination was made of infauna.  This dataset is made up of the results of an investigation into the faunal and floral communities associated with intertidal Sabellaria alveolata reefs at Criccieth North Wales.  The aims of the study were to provide further information regarding the dynamics of the flora/epifauna and infauna communities associated with S. alveolata reefs in the UK.\tPublic record\n17170\tEXT_DS121666\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.424Z\tBritish Mountaineering Council (BMC) Coastal Climbing (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363636\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\MCRA\\BMC coastal climbing lines.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\MCRA\\BMC coastal climbing points.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset was obtained in 2009 as part of the Marine Coastal Recreation Audit (MCRA).  It is thought that this data is owned by the British Mountaineering Council (BMC) and was obtained by the contractors for Natural Resources Wales under a licence. However there is also  potential that this product was derived from the BMC Data.  This dataset is for internal use only as part of work for the MCRA. For a more up to date version of the data which is advised if you are planning to use the data please contact the spatial data team who can obtain a copy from the BMC.\tPublic record\n18569\tNRW_DS122191\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.300Z\tForestry Local Area Managers\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313931\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\FCW Local Area Managers.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset orginated from Forestry Commission Wales - still being used but in need of an update. This is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Forestry local area team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for foresty managed teams.  This dataset defines the forestry team boundaries and the 14 team locations within those areas.\tPrivate record\n18113\tNRW_DS115587\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.984Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Vertigo geyeri in Waun Eurad and Cors Erddreiniog SACs (2003-2007)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353837\tAssociated data for 2003 is held within Maes-y-Ffynnon\u0027s IT systems as H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\08 Vertigo Geyeri Cors Waun Eurad \u0027ampisand\u0027 Cors Erddreiniog  Associated data for 2007 monitoring is held at Maes-y-Ffynnon at - H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\42 Vertigo Geyeri Cors Waun Eurad \u0026amp; Cors Erddreiniog\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tGeyers whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri is listed on Annex II of the European Union Habitats and Species Directive classified in the British Red Data Book as RDB1 (Endangered) and is on the list of priority species of the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP). The purpose of this study was to undertake monitoring of the Vertigo geyeri population at Waun Eurad and Cors Erddreiniog in order to carry out a condition assessment and to determine the attributes that will be assessed by future monitoring which is a requirement of the EU Habitats and Species Directive.\tPublic record\n17863\tNRW_DS111489\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.802Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: North Wales (North Wales Environmental Information Services - Cofnod)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343839\tCofnod LRC hold the physical collation on their server. If you want extra information about the LERCs contact: Enterprise Data Management Specialist Advisor - Barny Letheren.   X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to COFNOD point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to COFNOD region.lyr\tCofnod is the cutsodian Huw Jones is the main lead with Cofnod This data will be incoprorated in the GIS layers that NRW also received\tThis dataset is a collation of records derived from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) (formerly Countryside Council for Wales) North Region paper files and entered by staff of Natural Resources Wales onto the Cofnod (North Wales Environmental Information Service) Online Recording System. Records are primarily for the NRW\u0027s North Region which includes the following Local Authority areas: Anglesey Gwynedd Conwy Denbighshire Flintshire and Wrexham. However there are some records for other areas. Many records are for designated sites (including Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) Special Area of Conservation (SACs) National Nature Reserves (NNRs) etc) however there are also large numbers of records from the wider countryside. The majority of the records have grid references of at least 100m accuracy (6 figure) however some of these may be derived from site centroids rather than refer to the actual location of the species. The majority of the records were originally created by NRW staff or qualified contractors. They have been entered onto computer by trained Cofnod staff or volunteers using Cofnod\u0027s custom built Data Entry Systems which use standard lists where possible to ensure consistent terminology. Randomised Quality Assurance checks have been carried out on much of the data (apart from that entered directly by NRW staff). All records have been screened to ensure that they have valid dates species names and grid references. The majority of these records are subject to systematic verification by relevant local experts and are labelled accordingly (within an additional Attribute field). Individual records have a default verification level of \u0027Assigned\u0027 or \u0027Unassessed\u0027 until they have been looked at by a Verifier after which they may be re-labelled as \u0027Unconfirmed\u0027 or \u0027Considered Correct by Expert\u0027. Some records known to be from a trusted source are labelled as \u0027Considered Correct by Cofnod\u0027. Records identified by Verifiers as \u0027Known/Probably Incorrect\u0027 will be removed from the NBN Gateway. Further details are given within relevant Cofnod policies. Data should be checked against original sources before use in contentious circumstances.\tPublic record\n19096\tNRW_DS125453\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.535Z\tHistoric Landfill Sites points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343533\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Historic Landfill Sites Points.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning  Also could contact: Tyrone (Ty) Ward - PPC/RSR Officer 1, PPC Team\tThis dataset shows the point location historic landfill sites that are held in NRWs permitting database and have been issues under the EPR. These are landfill sites which have been taken off the Authorised Landfill Sites points dataset when the waste licence status changes to either:  - Licence expired: Some licences issued under the Control of Pollution Act 1974 were time limited and expired on the date specified in the licence.  - Licence Revoked: Whereby the licence has been entirely revoked and is no longer in force; or  - Licence Surrendered: Operator has successfully surrendered the licence which is no longer in force.\tPublic record\n18568\tNRW_DS122189\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.390Z\tGlastir Woodland Contract Managers Area\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313839\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Glastir Woodland Contract Managers Area.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team - Keith Harri Jones\tadmin boundary set up by NRW for the woodland contract managers NRW owned - subject to change with OD\tPrivate record\n18573\tNRW_DS122188\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.515Z\tGlastir Woodland Management Networks\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313838\tx:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Primary Cores.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Primary Networks.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Secondary Cores.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Secondary Networks.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Tertiary Networks.lyr\tJim Latham is the internal contact who knows about this dataset.  GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThese were created by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of assess agricultural woodland planting and habitat management teams. This dataset was derived from Phase 1 and created by Forest Research as part of a contract for Countryside Council for Wales. This dataset is now static but has been and is used for guiding the Glastir Woodland Managemnet Schemes. These datasets have been used to assess where planting woodladn could occur with those areas closer to the primary core networks scoring higher than those near the tertiary networks. This helped to identify opportunities for planting.\tPrivate record\n18548\tNRW_DS121719\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.663Z\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Community Woodlands\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373139\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Community Woodlands.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows planting opportunities for community woodlands as well as already existing accessible and inaccessible woodlands within 2km of urban areas. Each of these three categories is separated into three demand classes: areas where many children are present; where the overall deprivation level is high; and where these two factors coincide e.g. many children live in areas of high deprivation.\tPublic record\n18090\tNRW_DS115615\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.932Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the River Usk Catchment Area (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363135\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at:- Z\\:Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\19 Otter in the River Usk\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned this work to assess the condition and conservation status of the European otter Lutra lutra within the River Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and catchment. The results of this project combined with those of similar projects instigated by CCW on other SAC river catchments over the past two years and in future years will provide a valuable and unique picture of the otter in Wales.\tPublic record\n19048\tNRW_DS125297\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.618Z\tDetermining Confidence in the 2018 Indicative Condition Assessments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323937\tData currently stored on DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/mar-wq/Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fmar%2Fmar%2Dwq%2FEvidence%2FNutrient%20SAC%20project\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200078914C818B753241BAFF4899DE5F2F8F\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1FDF1E04%2D17DE%2D4770%2DBA8B%2D3EDC8394EB22%7D\t\tIn 2018 Indicative Condition Assessments were undertaken to examine the feature condition of Marine Protected Areas. Given the time available at the time the process for assigning condition was limited. Thus given the potential for marine to be taken into the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) river project whereby permits may be refused or reviewed due to water quality failures within SACs we need to determine our confidence in those failures assigned in 2018. The dataset here is that used to support the generation of confidence metrics. It comes in Excel files and GIS shapefiles.\tPrivate record\n18351\tNRW_DS116465\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.411Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Lower Wye English Boundary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343635\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\LowerWye\\Lower Wye English Boundary.lyr\tSpatial Data Team - Louise Harris\tBoundary of the English section of the Lower Wye IDD. Mapped for the purposes of the management of Flood Defences Water Levels and Flood Risk. Identifies the area which the Board is responsible for preventing flooding managing water levels and flood defences.\tPrivate record\n19025\tNRW_DS125219\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.477Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Canal Waterbodies Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323139\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Canals Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the canal waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes Surface Water Transfer (SWT) waterbodies which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline and can be linked to other Water Framework Directive (WFD) data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID). WFD River canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 3 is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.\tPublic record\n18114\tNRW_DS115584\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.979Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Monitoring Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Caron candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353834\tAssociated data is held within the folloring location within Maes-y-Ffynnon\u0027s IT Sytem; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\06 Raised mire vegetation Cors Caron cSAC\tInternal custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset was collected at Cors Caron candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) in partial fulfilment of former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Contract FC 73-05-35 \\Monitoring of Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Fochno and Cors Caron cSAC\\\". A second report outlines the results of monitoring work undertaken at Cors Fochno. The contract was commissioned to aid the assessment of the extent and condition of three Annex 1 habitats listed in the EC \\\"Habitats Directive\\\" (Council of the European Communities 1992): Degraded raised bogs still capable of natural regeneration; Transition mires and quaking bogs; Depressions on peat substrates of the Rhynchosporion;\"\tPublic record\n18622\tNRW_DS123237\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.851Z\tDrinking Water Safeguard Zones 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323337\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\Drinking Water Safeguard Zones 2020.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn Suzanne.Lawn@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tAny waterbodies in Wales that are used to provide drinking water are designated as drinking water protected areas (DrWPAs). The designation aims to protect the water and prevent any deterioration in quality that could increase the required treatment of water to meet Drinking Water Standards. Drinking Water Protected Areas are required to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017. Within these drinking water protected areas we work with water companies to identify raw water sources that are ‘at risk’ of deterioration which would result in the need for additional treatment. These areas are degsinated as Safeguard Zones where additional measures can be used to address any factors that may reduce the water quality. The SgZ have been identified based on DCWW monitoring and risk assessments and are not limited to the DWPA but based on the wider Water Company identified catchment areas.  A catchment is the term used to describe the area of land where water drains or collects. After rain water flows over the land through soil and rock into streams and eventually the main river. The boundary of a catchment follows natural slopes hills or mountains within a landscape and determines if water flows into one river or another or subsequently into a reservoir.\tPrivate record\n17435\tNRW_DS88663\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.192Z\tBat Roosts Database - Pembrokeshire (1887 - 2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838363633\t[A]  Paper records are held at the NRW Stackpole / Pembrokeshire Offices.   [B]  Digitised species data held in Recorder 6 45 VC Pembs:  Bats - Hibernacula \u0026amp; day/night roost records  CCW0000800000027\tPaul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South, NRW (Formerly Bob Haycock, NRW Senior Reserve Manager - Stackpole)\tThis dataset comprises of former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 regional bat hibernacula and day/night roost records for Pembrokeshire. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts to assist with the former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) bat conservation work. In particular to provide information when dealing with development proposals to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species across Pembrokeshire. Ad hoc survey work is continuing however the main data was collected between 1887 and 2011.\tPublic record\n17242\tEXT_DS121969\t2024-05-28T12:17:32.753Z\tWelsh Government: Agricultural Census (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393639\tx:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agricultural Census 1969.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agricultural Census 1981.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agricultural Census 1992.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agricultural Census 1997.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is supplied to Natural Resources Wales by Welsh Government. This is a statutory sample survey which is carried out annually by the Welsh Government to agricultural holdings in Wales as part of a UK wide exercise.\tPrivate record\n18776\tNRW_DS123440\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.411Z\tLIFE Project Horses for Corsydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343430\tx:\\Resource Management\\LIFE project\\LIFE H for C Access infrastructure A15 polys .lyr        x:\\Resource Management\\LIFE project\\LIFE Project. Horses for corsydd lines.lyr              x:\\Resource Management\\LIFE project\\LIFE Project. Horses for corsydd polys.lyr\tJustin Hanson, Team Leader, People \u0026amp; Places, Anglesey and Llyn Fens LIFE Project\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18944\tNRW_DS124806\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.908Z\tBait digging areas 2019/2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383036\tShapefile of digging X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Mapping bait digging\\Bait digging areas (2019-2020).lyr    Layer file for the drone photos X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Drone footage\\Intertidal Drone Photos 2019-2020.lyr   The PDFs and with processing notes and word documents are saved in DMS Advice\u0026gt;Marine\u0026gt;MPA Non-Licensable Activities\u0026gt;WNLA Phase 3 Bait Digging\u0026gt;Final Report and products July 2020  Digital terrain model outputs showing bait digging areas - Z:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Bait Digging DSMs 2019-20\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team         This dataset has been listed as sensitive because it shows the location of areas that may be targeted by bait diggers. Project Officer has identified this as data that should be kept confidential however areas suitable for bait digging are not listed on the sensitive species guidance. But the data should still be licensed and restricted under EIR. Data should not be proactively published.\tThe data has been collected under a contract run under the Wales non-licensable activities project.  Bait digging and collection of living resources was identified as an activity which is causing damage to the Marine Protection Area (MPA) network in Wales.  The data obtained from this contract provides NRW with evidence which will help inform discussions around future management of this activity.  A GIS layer was created through interpretation of the visible evidence of bait digging from the aerial images (NRW_DS124825 Bait digging areas aerial imagery 2019/2020).  The GIS layer was created by digitising around areas identified as impacted by bait digging. In addition the layer displays site name confidence in intrepretation the age of the evidence (old/new) and digging intensity (low medium and high) along with who captured the data the date and image date.\tPublic record\n17316\tEXT_DS124931\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.307Z\tMet Office Rainfall Amount Annual Average 1961-1990 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393331\tX:\\Physical\\Meteorological\\Met Office Rainfall Amount Annual Average 1961-1990.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial datasets for annual average rainfall obtained from the Met Office. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have obtained this dataset via a bespoke licence from the Met Office. The Met Office is the UK National Meteorological Service set up to research the possibilities of forecasting the weather and now uses more than 10 million weather observation from across the UK to create advanced atmospheric models to predict upcoming forecasts and longer term climate change scenarios.\tPrivate record\n17829\tNRW_DS111186\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.581Z\tSurveys of Harbours, Rias and Estuaries in Southern Britain - Loughor Estuary and the Burry Inlet\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313836\tPaper report (volume 1) is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000256). NRW only has a snapshot of this data\tData contacts: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Nature Conservancy Council commissioned the Field Studies Council to undertake studies of the marine inlets of southern Britain including harbours rias and estuaries in order to gain information on the ecosystems present. The study took place between 1985 and 1988 and covered 24 inlets. This dataset contains the results of a survey of Loughor Estuary (incorporating the Burry Inlet). The purpose of this data capture was to describe areas in terms of their physical attributes; to review the results of previous studies; to review marine resource usage e.g. fisheries diving activities etc; to catalogue available information and to collect information and photographs on the habitats and species present.\tPublic record\n17123\tEXT_DS115372\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.317Z\tAudit of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) Habitat Management in Rhondda Cynon Taff County Borough Council (CBC) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333732\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Adrian_f\\MapInfo Data\\Marsh Fritillary\\Habitat Quality Surveys\\RCT post Dec, 2004 RGS\tGIS Service Desk  Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW\tThe Marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) priority species and has been listed by the Welsh Government as species of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity in Wales (Section 74 species Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000). This species in now confined to the south-western counties of England south and west Wales western Scotland and Ireland. There is some evidence to suggest that the species range is continuing to contract along eastern edge having previously been lost in central and eastern parts of England. It has also become scarcer within this range with healthy populations now any appearing to persist where there is sufficient habitat to support their meta-populations (dynamic network of colonies) in which the species naturally occurs. Nevertheless in parts of this range its status maybe poorly known due to recording issues. For species existing in meta-populations as is the case with many of our scarce butterflies the quality and abundance of available habitat within colonising distance of local populations is extremely important. In Wales there is increasing emphasis on mapping these patches of habitat to complement surveys of marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia adults and larval webs.   This dataset consists of a MapInfo layer of the condition of habitat polygons for marsh fritillaries in Rhondda Cynon Taff. The purpose of this data capture was to ascertain the extent and condition of habitat capable of supporting populations of marsh fritillaries in order to provide scientifically-justified guidance to inform planning decisions.\tPublic record\n17137\tEXT_DS120174\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.212Z\tDefra: National Atmospheric Emissions Inventory (NAEI) (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230313734\tX:\\Resource Management\\Air Quality\tGIS Service Desk\tThe UK National Atmospheric Emissions Inventory (NAEI) is developed and maintained by Ricardo Energy \u0026amp; Environment in collaboration with Aether CEH and Gluckman Consulting. The NAEI is funded by the Department for Business Energy and Industrial Strategy (BEIS) Department for Environment Food and Rural Affairs (Defra) the Scottish Government the Welsh Government and the Northern Ireland Department of Agriculture Environment and Rural Affairs.   Natural Resources Wales (NRW) obtains the spatial layers from some of the datasets associated with the NAEI under licence from Defra. NRW only gets the Wales area for the majority of the dataset the acception being the Defra air quality management areas (2015). This includes the following:  - Modelled Air Quality Concentrations for 2008 2009 2010 2011 2012 2013 2015 2015 and 2016 - Modelled Air Quality Emissions 2008 2009 and 2010 - Air Quality Management Areas  - Defra Air Quality Management Areas 2015 - Defra Nitrogen Dioxide Concentrations - Defra Ozone Concentrations - Defra Particulate Matter Concentrations\tPublic record\n18124\tNRW_DS115775\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.870Z\tA geomorphological appraisal and options for dune rejuvenation at Pembrey Burrows (2014).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373735\tReport location, with datasets included is located on Ffynnon NRW-14-1090223\tInternal Custodian: Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the results from a geomorphological assessment of Pembrey Burrows south Wales. The datasets holds a combination of environmental data published and unpublished literature air photography LiDAR interpretation site visits and discussions with relevant personnel. The purpose of this data capture and analyses was to provide a geomorphological assessment of Pembrey Burrows sand dunes system with the aim of creating an informed management policy in the future which could include options for dune rejuvenation.\tPublic record\n18123\tNRW_DS115776\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.900Z\tKenfig Dune Rejuvenation Trials Topographic Survey Data (2012 - 2015)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373736\tArchive Project Number: 420  Media number:1438  [Data from 2012 /  2013 surveys]  1490  [Data from 2014 surveys]  1534 [data from 2015 surveys]\tInternal Contact: Marine and Coastal Physical Scientist, Evidence Analysis Group.   Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to assess the topographic mobility of the Kenfig National Nature Reserve sand dunes and to monitor the sand dune rejuvenation.  Kenfig sand dune rejuvenation trails began in December 2012. The dataset consists of points were topographic elevations were determined many of which were on profile lines which were profile lines. Key features were defined based on the limits. These features included ridge crests standing water new vegetation growth and the position of the frontal sand dunes.\tPublic record\n18886\tNRW_DS124802\t2024-05-28T12:57:55.770Z\tNRW Fisheries Teams\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383032\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Fisheries Teams.lyr\tGary Eccleston, Advisor 2, Geospatial Knowledge, Information Management\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for operational Fisheries Teams post Organisation Design July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tPrivate record\n17564\tNRW_DS102150\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.802Z\tWelsh Biodiversity Action Plan Invertebrate Species Spatial Distribution (archive dataset)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313530\tM:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Terrestrial\\BAPs\\invertebrates\tDr Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW Formerly: Adrian Fowles in CCW\tThis is an archived reference spatial dataset. It is advised that users gain a more up to date version of this datasets. UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority species were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The original list of UK BAP priority species was created between 1995 and 1999.  In 2007 however a revised list was produced following a 2-year review of UK BAP processes and priorities which included a review of the priority species and habitats lists. The UK list of priority species however remains an important reference source and has been used to help draw up statutory lists of priority species in  England  Scotland Wales and  Northern Ireland as required under Section 41 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities Act 2006 (England) Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 Section 2(4) of the Nature Conservation (Scotland) Act 2004 and Section 3(1) of the Wildlife and Natural Environment Act (Northern Ireland) 2011. Invertebrate BAP lists of priority species were produced with several important aspects such as the following: - most invertebrates have an annual life cycle and require their specific habitat conditions to be present each year;  - the ecological niches occupied by invertebrates can be highly specialised; - many insect species require different conditions for each stage of their life cycle (egg larva pupa adult); and - many species have limited powers of dispersal.  This invertebrate BAP dataset was originally created in 2004 as a spatial layer for use within Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). This data should be used as reference only as it is out of date.\tPublic record\n17234\tEXT_DS121991\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.487Z\tWelsh Government: Wales Spatial Plans (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393931\tFolder:  X:\\Social Economic\\Wales Spatial Plan\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a Welsh Government owned data collation which Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to via a licence agreement. The Planning and Compulsory Purchase Act 2004 placed a duty on the National Assembly for Wales to prepare a spatial plan. The Government of Wales Act 2006 further embeds the Wales Spatial Plan as a policy the Welsh Ministers must now agree and keep under review. For the purposes of the 2004 Act this Update constitutes the Wales Spatial Plan. This dataset details the extent and location of boundaries for each spatial plan area across Wales.\tPublic record\n18349\tNRW_DS116492\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.334Z\tAssessment of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) Habitat Quality on Castlemartin Range Pembrokeshire (2015)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343932\tSpecies data is stored in Recorder 6 (attached to the citation of the report) Report is available in PDF Format through the Reports Archive on the DMS Spatial Habitat Maps are being produced by Johnathon Rothwell.\tData Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW Jonathon Rothwell, Terrestrial Mapping Scientist  Metadata Contact: Data services Team, NRW\tThe marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia is an Annex II European protected species. The butterfly and its habitats are primary features of the Castlemartin Range Special Site of Scientific Interest (SSSI) in south Pembrokeshire. The metapopulation was discovered in 2003 too late to be included in the Limestone Coast of South West Wales Special Area of Conservation (SAC) but it is included as a key SSSI feature in the SAC Core Management Plan (CMP). Marsh fritillaries exhibit metapopulation dynamics experiencing regular extinctions and colonisations of suitable patches over time. A large network of suitable sites is essential to maintain their populations in the long term. This survey was commissioned to examine potential marsh fritillary habitat on the Range and to determine the total amount and condition of habitat available for the metapopulation. All survey sites were on MOD Castlemartin Training Area which in turn lies within the Pembrokeshire Coast National Park. The entire survey area is within the Castlemartin Range SSSI and most of it is included in the Limestone Cliffs of Southwest Wales SAC.\tPublic record\n17092\tEXT_DS108556\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.294Z\tNational Trust Coastal Risk Assessment Database - Welsh Subset Only (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038353536\tWelsh Subset of the data, and the report are held on the MFSG shared drive (File path: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Estuarine \u0026amp; Coastal Science\\NT_Coastal Risk Ass_Oct04   For full database please contact the National Trust.\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team External Custodian: Coast and Marine Support, The National Trust. (also contact: Adrian Woodhall adrianwoodhall@btinternet.com)\tNational Trust database detailing information including: Designations Habitat and Species features Geomorphology Wrecks Historical Landscapes and Features Flood Data Visitors Access Income etc. The purpose of this abstraction was to identify National Trust properties at risk from flooding due to future climate change and global warming.  The database was also collated to identify assests associated with the properties that may be at risk and to identify actions that may be taken to prevent flood damage to properties or their assets. The Welsh subset of data contains information about approximately 40 sites for the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) West Region and 30 for CCW North Region. Data was collected between August 2006 and May 2008 and will be updated as and when necessary. A high level of confidence in the source data  provided.  Ground-truthing of properties was carried out by the National Trust.\tPublic record\n17782\tNRW_DS110510\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.138Z\tCondition Monitoring of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353130\tHigh level data are held centrally by CCW Environmental Change Group.  Supporting data are held in Regional CCW offices or by Science Groups.  Published reports are available from CCWs libraries.\tInternal Custodian: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team  (Formerly: David Allen, Team Leader, Monitoring, Strategy and Planning, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW)\tThe Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) series has developed since 1949 as the suite of sites providing statutory protection for the best examples of the UK\u0027s flora fauna or geological or physiographical features. Originally notified under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 SSSIs were re-notified under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981. Improved provisions for the protection and management of SSSIs were introduced by the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. These sites are also used to underpin other national and international nature conservation designations. The SSSIs designation may extend into intertidal areas out to the jurisdictional limit of local authorities. In Wales the limit is Mean Low Water for SSSIs notified before 2002 and for more recent notifications the limit of Lowest Astronomical Tides (LAT) where the features of interest extend down to LAT.  Condition monitoring of SSSIs is essential to determine whether or not they are in satisfactory condition. This dataset contains the results of a single rapid review exercise and a programme of Common Standards Monitoring carried out by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to assess the condition of SSSI features in Wales.  The data from the rapid review monitoring was collected to provide an indicative picture of the condition of a sample of SSSI features. Common Standards Monitoring data is collected and assessed against a set of agreed criteria to provide an indication of the condition in each case. The first cycle of common standards monitoring took place between 2001 and 2006. The first rapid review exercise was carried out in 2003 and was updated in 2006.\tPublic record\n19603\tNRW_DS161298\t2024-09-26T10:46:42.933Z\tForester Heritage\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323938\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Heritage.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support\tThis layer holds information about heritage features on or near to the National Forest Estate. It is made up of data recorded by NRW staff and data imported from third parties. The records are recorded as points polygons and lines. It is collected to create a record of all the heritage features on the  Welsh Government Woodland Estate so they can be managed and improved if possible. It is also used for constraints in coupe planning and for heritage feature restoration projects.\tPrivate record\n17232\tEXT_DS122163\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.412Z\tDŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water: MCERTS Effluent Flow Monitoring Sites (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313633\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Quality\\MCerts DCWW Effluent Flow Monitoring.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\MCerts Effluent Flow Monitoring.lyr   The original source of this data is WISKI\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water and licenced to NRW for internal use. As part of a permit requirements issued by Natural Resources Wales Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water are required to monitoring the Dry Weather Flow or Maximum Daily Volume of discharge as part of their Effluent Flow Monitoring. As part of this Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water must comply with MCERTS Flow Monitoring Schemes which records the total daily volume of discharge. This dataset details the locations of these monitoring sites managed by Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water.\tPublic record\n18876\tNRW_DS124730\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.518Z\tBaseline Project – Pressures Contract\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373330\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/sites/smar/stratplan/tsg/Programmes/Terrestrial%20Transition%20Planning/Task%2002%20-%20Baseline/Baseline%20Project%20-%20Pressures%20contract/Contract%20final%20products?csf\u003d1\t\tDevelopment and application of a methodology to assess the anthropogenic pressures acting upon independently qualifying features of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales.\tPublic record\n18153\tNRW_DS115891\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.928Z\tAllis (Alosa alosa) and Twaite (Alosa fallax) shad monitoring data sampled from the rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi, using Mitochondrial DNA techniques (2014).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383931\tData is archived under project number 461 and media number 1522. Report is held on the DMS Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports.  NRW Evidence Report No. 053 - Monitoring Allis and Twaite Shad - quality assurance and species identification using molecular techniques  KNOW-1206538853-44\t\tPopulations of allis shad (Alosa alosa Lacépède) and twaite shad (Alosa fallax L.) have declined across their geographic distribution over the last century and despite being widespread around the coast of the British Isles UK spawning populations are largely restricted to the Rivers Severn Wye Usk and Tywi which drain into the Bristol Channel. As a result both species are protected by UK and international legislation. In response to a need to monitor the population status to inform conservation objectives for these species the former Countryside Council for Wales and Environment Agency Wales now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) developed a semi-quantitative and cost-effective method to assess recruitment potential by sampling shad eggs. However this method is unable to distinguish between the two Alosa species and there is a risk that eggs of other fish species may erroneously be recorded as shad eggs.   The aim of this data collection was to use genetic techniques to address the weaknesses related to the semi-quantitative and cost-effective methods adopted being unable to distinguish between the two Alosa species and the risks that other eggs related to other fish species being erroneously recorded.  The two objectives were to: (a) Determine whether the collected eggs belonged to Alosa spp.  (b) Determine the proportion of A. fallax and A. alosa using three different genetic markers – mitochondrial DNA and two types of nuclear DNA: nif1-nDNA and six microsatellite loci.  A total of 226 eggs were successfully genotyped. 85% belonged to Alosa spp. demonstrating that a good level of egg identification in the collection team. Non-Alosa eggs were identified as belonging to minnow (Phoxinus phoxinus) and chub (Squalius cephalus). As a result the extent of putative shad spawning on the Tywi is slightly less than suggested by the egg surveys alone. However the upper extent of shad spawning on the Wye was confirmed as being Brynwern Bridge about 9km further upstream than the previous known upper limit at Builth Wells.    Methods of data capture:\tPublic record\n18302\tNRW_DS116343\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.362Z\tFlood Risk Forecast Application Programming Interface (API)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333433\tHost by the EA and Made available through our API manager\tMartin Linforth: Flood and coastal risk management\tThe flood risk forecast is produced by the Flood Forecasting Centre (FFC) on a daily basis. It is issued more frequently when serious flooding is forecast. It provides the indication of the potential for flooding for five days: the day on which it is issued and the subsequent four days ahead.  The forecast highlights flood risk on a county by county basis and includes a short commentary on the situation. It covers flooding from rivers the sea surface water and groundwater for Wales and England.  This dataset is produced in partnership with the Met Office Flood Forecasting Centre and the Environment Agency and is intended to complement the Flood Warning Service provided by Natural Resources Wales.\tPublic record\n18536\tNRW_DS121852\t2024-05-28T12:51:14.789Z\tNational Coastal Erosion Risk Management (NCERM)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383532\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan .....lyr  (There are 4 layers) see below  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan Gt Orme to England.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan Severn.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan Swansea Carmarthen Bay.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan West Wales.lyr\tLouise Pennington, Senior Specialist, Strategic Planning and Investment, Flood Risk Strategy, Sustainable Communities Group\tThe National Coastal Erosion Risk Management (NCERM) map shows the spatial NCERM coastal baseline. This baseline is split to \u0027frontages\u0027. These are defined as lengths of coast with consistent characteristics based on the cliff behaviour characteristics and the defence characteristics. It is intended as an up-to-date and reliable benchmark dataset showing erosion extents and rates for three periods from a base date of 2005:   - Short Term (0 - 20yr i.e. 2005 to 2025);  - Medium Term (20 - 50yr i.e. 2025 to 2055); and  - Long Term (50 - 100yr i.e. 2055 to 2105).    The data extents are based on 50%ile confidence levels whilst the data attributes highlight the 5-95%ile confidence range of erosion predictions for the following two scenarios (NB All distances are cumulative over time and given in metres):  - No Active Intervention Policy Scenario; and  - With the implementation of Shoreline Management Plan 2 Policies.    Defence type and SMP policies for each of the three periods described above are included.\tPublic record\n18255\tNRW_DS116278\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.983Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Operational Catchments Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323738\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Operational Catchments Cycle2 Draft.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tOperational catchments are a way of grouping Water Framework Directive (WFD) waterbodies together at a meaningful scale smaller than the management catchment or River Basin District. The river waterbodies are not always hydrologically linked as they may have been grouped based on pressures and measures. This data set is based on the surface water boundaries of operational catchments and excludes the underlying Groundwater data.\tPublic record\n18574\tNRW_DS122093\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.516Z\tTerrestrial Mammals\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303933\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\tLiz Halliwell GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis spatial dataset details the extent and location of a variety of terrestrial mammals across Wales.\tPrivate record\n18346\tNRW_DS116464\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.350Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Lower Wye English Watercourses\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343634\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\LowerWye\\IDD Lower Wye English Watercourses.lyr\tSpatial Data Team, Louise Harris\tWatercourses within the English section of Lower Wye Internal Drainage Board now administered by Hoople. These watercourses drain into Lower Wye IDD watercourses within Wales. Mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for the purpose of water level management flood defence maintenance and planning operations/work programming.\tPrivate record\n18782\tNRW_DS123434\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.687Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Powysland Catchments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343334\tx:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\PowysLand\\IDD Powysland Catchments.lyr\tLouise Harris, Geospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18446\tNRW_DS121294\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.763Z\tEcological Surveys of Welsh Lakes 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323934\tAsk Tristan Hatton Ellis, or see report as alot of the data is in there: Ecological Surveys of Welsh Lakes 2017 NRW Evidence Report No. 257\tTristan Hatton Ellis\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) is repsonsible for the management and monitoring of the freshwater environment in Wales including protecting sites designated under UK and European legislation and enivronmental montoring for the Water Framework (WFD) and Nitrates Directive. This includes montioring of lakes. This project set out to collect process and supply to NRW ecological and environmental data from a network of 16 lake sites across Wales in support of NRW\u0027s integrated monitoring programme for protected sites (Special Area of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest) the Water Framework Directive Nitrates Directive Biodiversity Action Plans and other legislative and policy drivers. In particular the surveys are aimed at informing management and restoration of protected sites and facilitating delivery of River Basin Management Plans.\tPrivate record\n19027\tNRW_DS125203\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.433Z\tDuty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Flood Incidents\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323033\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Duty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Flood Incidents.lyr \"\tSally Davies - Incident Management Team\tNRW currently has Duty Tactical Manager (DTM) rotas covering the North South East and South West areas of Wales. This dataset shows the areas of DTM responsibility for Flood incidents.\tPublic record\n19051\tNRW_DS125326\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.588Z\tMetal Mine Impacted Waterbodies in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333236\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Metal Mine Impacted Waterbodies in Wales.lyr\tSophia Lennie\tThe Metal Mine Impacted Waterbodies in Wales contains waterbodies and its lengths which have been affected by metal mine either through direct or diffuse pollution. The data is collected to show the extent of downstream impacts of metal mine pollution. It should be noted that this dataset is collected in 2022. As remediation works continue the number and length of waterbodies impacted may change.\tPrivate record\n17157\tEXT_DS121850\t2024-05-28T12:15:04.672Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): 625k Onshore Geology (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383530\tx:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 625k Bedrock.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 625k Superficial.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) is a UK provider of geoscientific data information and knowledge. Natural Resources Wales has licenced access to a number of BGS datasets. This particular spatial data is a collation of licenced data from BGS Geology 625k DiGMapGB-625 all of which depict the data at a scale of 1:625000.\tPublic record\n19031\tNRW_DS125202\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.448Z\tDuty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Land Management Incidents\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323032\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Duty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Land Management Incidents.lyr\tSally Davies - Incident Management Team NRW\tNRW currently has Duty Tactical Manager (DTM) rotas covering the North South East and South West areas of Wales. This dataset shows the areas of DTM responsibility for Land Management incidents (i.e. incidents on the NRW estate).\tPublic record\n18200\tNRW_DS116210\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.714Z\tRare and Protected Species Records across Wales (1975 to 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323130\t[A] This data is now available on the NBN Gateway [B] Original data on Wiski-Kieco\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe aim of this data collection was to monitor the distribution of rare and protected species across Wales.  This dataset is primarily made up of aquatic and riparian species (marine and freshwater) including invertebrates plants algae and fish.  The data includes ad-hoc records from surveys at local offices.  Data collection started in the 1970s onwards.  This dataset does not include records of Freshwater Pearl Mussel but otherwise includes all Rare and Protected species surveyed by the former Environment Agency Wales now Natural Resources Wales.\tPublic record\n18582\tNRW_DS122099\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.919Z\tNon Native Species: New Zealand mud snail (Potamopyrgus antipodarum)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303939\tx:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Other Potamopyrgus Antipodarum.lyr\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Other Potamopyrgus Antipodarum.lyr\tThe is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of the New Zealand mud snail Potamopyrgus Antipodarum. Potamopyrgus antipodarum is an aquatic snail of the family Hydrobiidae.\tPrivate record\n18588\tNRW_DS122915\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.780Z\tNew birds point\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393135\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\New birds point.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18587\tNRW_DS122910\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.779Z\tHigh tide roosts for waterbird features in Welsh Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs) 2018/19\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393130\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Wildfowl and waders High tide roosts.lyr  Future data to be available to NRW through BTO/RSPB/JNCC Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS).\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External contact: niall.burton@bto.org        Spreadsheet names were listed but have been removed as this record is focused on the .lyr GIS layer.\tThis dataset provides detailed information on the location extent and composition of high tide waterbird roosts within Welsh protected sites (Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs)). Metadata included in the GIS shapefiles include details of: species using the roost months in which the roost is used tides on which the roost is used habitat and human activities that occur in the associated Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS) count sector.\tPublic record\n17395\tNRW_DS87091\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.226Z\tSAC Monitoring: Monitoring Great Crested Newts at Johnstown SAC and  Deeside and Buckley SAC (2003)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837303931\t[A]  Associated data is held within the Recorder 6 database. [B]  Data is also held on the H:/ at H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\01 Great crested newt at Johnstown, Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley\t\tDeeside and Buckley and Johnstown Newt Sites candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) in Flintshire; and Johnstown Newt Sites cSAC in Wrexham support some of the most important areas for newts (Triturus cristatus) in Great Britain. The main aim of the current project was to provide monitoring information on Great Crested Newts adults and juveniles and on Great Crested Newts  aquatic and terrestrial habitat within the two cSACs.   This was to appraise the condition of great crested newt populations at Johnstown Newt Sites SAC and Deeside and Buckley Newt Sites SAC and to provide monitoring data on adults and juveniles and on Great Crested Newts  aquatic and terrestrial habitat.  The dataset consists of:  1) A collation of historical Great Crested Newts surveillance data from known display ponds for the two SACs (then candidate SACs).  Records were mainly taken from Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) paper files but also from Deeside Urban Wildlife Group (DUWG) Flintshire County Council (FCC) and Wrexham County Borough Council (WCBC).  Individual CCW recorders names are not known. 2) 2003 monitoring data for presence of Great Crested Newts (Jun) and larvae/adults (Aug-Sept).\tPublic record\n17902\tNRW_DS112020\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.586Z\tCommon Land Registers Fully Attributed Dataset\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132303230\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Common Land Registers 2014.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Common Land Register 2001.lyr\t\tRegistered common land element of Section 4 Map as required by Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. This dataset contains the Common Land areas of Wales. It is a fully attributed dataset therefore due to sensitivities in the data cannot be released. Any line shown as not matching the original boundary has been copied from what is shown on the original registers and is not a digitising error.\tPublic record\n18567\tNRW_DS122205\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.277Z\tNewborough Warren and Llanddwyn Island Wind data (2018-present)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323035\tData can be downloaded from: www.minimet.co.uk.  NRW users will need to contact Suzie Jackson for Website login details and FTP login details.    Data can be accessed from this location on DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20%20Data/Wind%20data/Wind%20Data%202017-2023?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1   An NRW Evidence report is in prep (as of May 2023) to describe this dataset, link to be added when available.\tInternal Custodian: Suzie Jackson, Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes AWMA, Marine and Coastal Policy and Planning       The actual location of the Weather Station has been removed for safety purposes.  Location is 53.136004, -4.4135866 (Pilots Cottages).  The data has been confirmed as Open data and added to the NRW Open data list.\tWind data collected at Llanddwyn Island Newborough. Data is available from January 2018 to present. The data provides daily 30 minute instantaneous averaged wind speed and direction.\tPublic record\n17954\tNRW_DS114594\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.709Z\tRing ouzel (Turdus torquata) national sample survey in the UK in 1999\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353934\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and available on the NBN Gateway\tInternal Contact: Sian Whitehead, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis is the first co-ordinated census of breeding ring ouzel Turdus torquata across the UK. A random subsample of the UK breeding range was surveyed to arrive at a population estimate and as such the dataset does NOT contain all breeding locations of ring ouzel in the UK in 1999. Of the tetrads surveyed that were known to be occupied during the 1988-91 breeding bird atlas 39-43% were unoccupied in 1999. The survey highlights a continuing contraction in range. The survey was run as part of the Statutory Conservation Agencies and RSPB Annual Breeding Birds Scheme (SCARABBS) and was funded jointly by the Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB) the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) English Nature (now Natural England) and Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH). The Ring Ouzel Study Group took part in the survey and many landowners and site managers allowed access to their land.  The purpose of this data capture was to set a baseline for the future monitoring of breeding ring ouzels and to provide a systematically based estimate of the population size.\tPublic record\n18285\tNRW_DS116310\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.550Z\tRadioactive Substances Register\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333130\t\tThis data is no longer available for external users; the old EA legacy data is outdated and product is discontinued; no new data is produced due to re-use concerns by Holly Noble’s team.\t\tPrivate record\n18217\tNRW_DS116248\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.171Z\tSource Protection Zones (SPZ) Individual\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323438\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPZ Source Protection Zones Individual.lyr\tDave Jones, Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tSPZs are defined around large and public potable groundwater abstraction sites. The purpose of SPZs is to provide additional protection to safeguard drinking water quality through constraining the proximity of an activity that may impact upon a drinking water abstraction. This is part of an initial screening process in assessing impacts to groundwater resources.   Zones around location sites are defined by groundwater travel time to an abstraction. This is determined through applying groundwater flow models run at the location of abstractions inputting parameters such as flow direction geology type rainfall and hydrological boundaries. SPZs provide a visual representation of the increased risks as you get closer to the abstraction.   The following subdivisions are defined within SPZs:   Zone 1: (Inner Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by a travel time of 50-days or less from any point within the zone at or below the water table. Additionally the zone has as a minimum a 50-metre radius. It is based principally on biological decay criteria and is designed to protect against the transmission of toxic chemicals and water-borne disease.   Zone 2: (Outer Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by the 400-day travel time from a point below the water table. Additionally this zone has a minimum radius of 250 or 500 metres depending on the size of the abstraction. The travel time is derived from consideration of the minimum time required to provide delay dilution and attenuation of slowly degrading pollutants.   Zone 3: (Total catchment) - This zone is defined as the total area needed to support the abstraction or discharge from the protected groundwater source.   A further Zone 4 or ‘Zone of Special Interest’ was previously defined for some groundwater sources. These zones highlighted areas (mainly on non-aquifers) where known local conditions meant that potentially polluting activities could impact on a groundwater source even though the area is outside the normal catchment of that source. In future this zone will be incorporated into one of the other zones (1 2 or 3) whichever is appropriate in the particular case.\tPublic record\n18110\tNRW_DS115569\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.044Z\tFisher Towed Survey in Cardigan Bay 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353639\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466.\tGIS Service Desk  External Custodian: Michael Kaiser, School of Ocean Science, Bangor University Formerly: Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University\tSurveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in October 2012 as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  The main aim of the survey work was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats and primarily whether cobble reef habitat was present as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.\tPublic record\n17593\tNRW_DS109024\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.061Z\tAmphibians and Reptiles Licence Return Data  (2001 - 2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039303234\tRecorder 6 CCW0007200000002 Amphibian \u0026amp; Reptile Licence Return Data\tInternal Custodian:  Species Protection Team, Maes y Ffynnon/Ffynnon DRMS.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW / Data Services Team, NRW\tThe collation of amphibian and reptile data from licence returns was created in order to store all relevant data in one location and mobilise records which may not have otherwise been available to the public. This dataset comprises of records of amphibian and reptile distributions across Wales obtained from former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 licence returns for scheduled amphibian and reptile species. This dataset does not represent a systematic coverage or survey of Wales but enhances species distribution surveys. The geographic location of individual records is generally random depending on the requirements of the licence holders. The dataset has been subject to some basic validation to eradicate erroneous outlying records and inputting errors but has not been systematically verified.\tPublic record\n19032\tNRW_DS125201\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.582Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323031\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3 are a polygon dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams rivers and possibly lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. Since rivers are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to the WFD river waterbody to which it relates.\tPublic record\n18575\tNRW_DS121868\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.665Z\tCoastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) Gauge, Surge, Swell data UK Datasets 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383638\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB confidence intervals.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Estuary boundaries.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB extreme sea levels.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Gauge data UK.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Surge shapes.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Surge shapes UK.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Swell waves.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Swell waves UK.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis GIS dataset and supporting information providing design / extreme sea level and typical surge information around the coastline of the UK under present day conditions. The data within Wales is owned by Natural Resources Wales England\u0027s data is owned by Environment Agency and the Scottish data is owned by SEPA.   This dataset has now been superseded by NRW_DS123552 Coastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) publication 2018.\tPublic record\n19049\tNRW_DS125308\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.623Z\tPowys Woodland Survey 1982-85/86\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333038\toff-site archive storage (add number)\tData Services team (OpenData inbox)       Data Services team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tPowys County Council set up a woodland project in 1981 to discover more about the semi-natural broadleaved woodland of the County. The Powys Woodland project survey ran from 1982 to 1985 and completed an ecological classification of semi-natural woods in the county recognising fifteen woodland site types in a sample of four hundred and thirty woods surveyed either by the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) between 1970-80 or by local Nature Trusts between 1980-82.  The work explores how much exists its type and distribution how much change has occurred  and some possible reasons for the survival of long-established woods. The approach was ecological to find out the amount of hardwood timber left the irreplacable assets of the country.\tPrivate record\n18595\tNRW_DS122919\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.875Z\tBarn Owl Sites Dec 2003\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393139\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\Barn Owl Sites Dec 2003.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n19026\tNRW_DS125234\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.471Z\tRed Zones\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323334\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Red zones.lyr\tPeter van Velzen\tThe polygon theme called “RedZones” is a subjective user interpretation (based on experience) showing forest areas with high public useage. This theme is used in the Land Management Service Plan to determine which work sites require additional mitigation- and safety measures to safeguard the public.\tPrivate record\n17237\tEXT_DS122152\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.929Z\tHM Land Registry: Spatial Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313532\tX:\\Ownership\\Land Registry\\Land Registry Polygons.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is wholly owned by the Land Registry and has been licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for use. This dataset contains the extent and location of parcels of land that have been allocated a land registry title number.\tPrivate record\n18592\tNRW_DS122916\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.832Z\tNightjar\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393136\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\Nightjar.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n17128\tEXT_DS115371\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.399Z\tAudit of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) Habitat Management in Merthyr Tydfil County Borough Council (CBC) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333731\tM Drive\tGIS Service Desk  Mike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW Formerly Adrian Fowles in CCW\tThe Marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) priority species and has been listed by the Welsh Government as species of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity in Wales (Section 74 species Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000). This species is now confined to the south-western counties of England south and west Wales western Scotland and Ireland. There is some evidence to suggest that the species range is continuing to contract along eastern edge having previously been lost in central and eastern parts of England. It has also become scarcer within this range with healthy populations now any appearing to persist where there is sufficient habitat to support their meta-populations (dynamic network of colonies) in which the species naturally occurs. Nevertheless in parts of this range its status maybe poorly known due to recording issues. For species existing in meta-populations as is the case with many of our scarce butterflies the quality and abundance of available habitat within colonising distance of local populations is extremely important. In Wales there is increasing emphasis on mapping these patches of habitat to complement surveys of marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia adults and larval webs.   This dataset consists of an ArcMap layer of the condition of habitat polygons for marsh fritillaries in Merthyr Tydfil  County Borough Council (CBC) to help inform planning decisions.  The purpose of this data capture was to ascertain the extent and condition of habitat capable of supporting populations of marsh fritillaries in order to provide scientifically-justified guidance to inform planning decisions.\tPublic record\n17466\tNRW_DS99503\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.910Z\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939353033\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestial\\Invertebrates\\Insects  (multiple datasets held under this file)\tCustodian of WID - Mike Howe and therefore contact for Marsh Frillitary data as well. Custodian of GIS data - GIS and Spatial Team\tMarsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species. This dataset is a spatial collation of all confirmed occurrence records of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) butterfly from Wales.  The purpose of this data collation is to inform Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) Partners and inform conservation decisions related to Marsh Fritillary butterflies.\tPublic record\n18353\tNRW_DS116462\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.472Z\tNVC Terrestrial Plant Surveys Coastal Ecologist\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343632\t\tHeather Lewis, Coastal Ecologist\tHeather Lewis is filling in the metadata for this survey- Evidence Report 143.\tPrivate record\n19050\tNRW_DS125315\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.607Z\tWoodland Surveys in Dyfed/Powys 1988 using the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Cooke \u0026amp;Saunders - ( Paper data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333135\tpaper data currently in MYF post room floor 1\tData Services team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk (or James Latham although he has moved post and no longer the custodian)       Data Services team (OpenData@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk)\tThis survey covers 156 woods and 2200 ha throughout Dyfed and the districts of Radnor and Montgomery in Powys. Many private and public landowners granted permission to survey their wooodland. Survey results were recorded on standard NVC record cards and a 300-500 word report on each site was written.  Phase II surveys were undertaken in selected woods using standard procedures. Woodland communities were described using the National Vegetation Classification NVC.\tPrivate record\n18564\tNRW_DS122401\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.874Z\tAn assessment of the current condition of the saproxylic invertebrate assemblage at Castell y Waun a’i Barcdir/Chirk Castle and Parkland SSSI in 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343031\tData is stored in Recorder 6 as part of the Welsh Invertebrate Database\t\tMike Howe\u0027s evidence reports:   Alexander K.N.A. 2019. An assessment of the current condition of the saproxylic invertebrate assemblage at Castell y Waun a’i Barcdir/Chirk Castle and Parkland SSSI in 2018. NRW Evidence Report No: 317 XXpp Natural Resources Wales Bangor.\tPrivate record\n18678\tNRW_DS123330\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.368Z\tREGIS Waste Licences\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333330\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\REGIS Waste Licences.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n17411\tNRW_DS46133\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.411Z\tAll Wales Common Scoter ( Melanitta nigra ) Survey (1994 - 2002)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313333\tAssociated data is held as Project No. 19, media 537\tThe internal data custodians are: Marine Ornithologist, NRW and Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. The metadata point of contact: Data Services Team\tThis dataset comprises of results from an aerial survey of common scoters Melanitta nigra (seabirds) in Carmarthen Bay Cardigan Bay and Liverpool Bay. The data was collected to provide information for Special Protection Areas (SPA) designations and monitoring. Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters (less than 10 m) in Carmarthen Bay.\tPublic record\n17245\tEXT_DS121983\t2024-05-28T12:17:32.898Z\tWelsh Government: Constituency Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393833\tX:\\Social Economic\\Administrative Boundaries\\Welsh Assembly Constituency Regions.lyr  X:\\Social Economic\\Administrative Boundaries\\Welsh Assembly Members.lyr  X:\\Social Economic\\Administrative Boundaries\\Westminster MP Constituencies.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis data collation is owned and managed by Welsh Government licenced to Natural Resources Wales for use. These datasets detail the location and of constituent regional boundaries across Wales.\tPublic record\n19126\tNRW_DS125590\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.916Z\tImportant areas for ponds in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353930\tCurrently in -   Being moved to the X Drive under River Restoration Layers\tTristan Hatton-Ellis - Lead Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species\tImportant Areas for Ponds in Wales created as part of the Wales River Restoration Project and derived from UK Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology UK Lakes Portal.  https://eip.ceh.ac.uk/apps/lakes/\tPublic record\n19021\tNRW_DS125236\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.425Z\tUwch-Conwy Drone Survey October 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323336\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\Uwch-Conwy GIS layers: 1. UwchConwy_dsm 2. UwchConwy_Orthomosaic 3. UwchConwy_water 4. UwchConwy _dsm_contours_50cm 5. UwchConwywatershed_stream_segments 6. UwchConwy_hag_line 7. UwchConwy_watershed_basins 8. UwchConwy_site_boundaries 9. UwchConwy_peat 10. UwchConwy_hag GylcheddFlyby.mp4\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Esther Clews, Specialist Advisor, Data and GIS – National Peatland Action Programme  Metadata Point of Contact:  Esther Clews, Specialist Advisor, Data and GIS – National Peatland Action Programme\tThe 10 sites were flown between the 10th and 16th September 2020 over the Uwch-Conwy peatlands. All sites were surveyed using an RGB vertical camera then the data processed to produce digital surface models (DSM) and orthomosaics.  The DSMs were processed to generate catchment flow and contour vector data sets to assist with the peatbog restoration planning.\tPrivate record\n17413\tNRW_DS46136\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.415Z\tSeabird Colony Surveillance along Castlemartin Coastline, South Pembrokeshire\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313336\tRaw data are held by NRW at Stackpole and by JNCC -  electronic copies annually forwarded to their Seabird data coordinator.\tSenior Reserve Warden - Stackpole, NRW.\tThis dataset contains the results from the surveillance of breeding seabird species including: auks (guillemot razorbill puffin) large Larus gulls (herring lesser black-back great black-back) kittiwake shag and fulmar and the surveillance of productivity of kittiwakes. The overall purposes of the data capture was to contribute to the JNCC-managed Seabird Monitoring Programme. Local purposes for the data capture were to  - Assess SSSI feature condition (seabird assemblage) in two adjacent Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI); - Maintain up-to-date records of regionally high concentrations of seabirds relevent to management of recreation (sea cliff-climbing and coasteering)  - Service update/data requirements for local regional marine pollution contingency plans.\tPublic record\n19030\tNRW_DS125195\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.411Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313935\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Groundwater Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 3 (2021-2027) is a spatial (polygon) dataset which has been created for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2 clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027…all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.   For the purposes of reporting under the WFD a groundwater body represents a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries. These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties recharge and vulnerability to pollution. This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale.\tPublic record\n18259\tNRW_DS116274\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.924Z\tFlood Map for Surface Water Complex Package\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323734\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n17229\tEXT_DS121982\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.433Z\tWelsh Government: Tir Cynnal and Tir Gofal (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393832\tx:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Cynnal Agreement Areas.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Cynnal Fields.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Cynnal S15 2005 2006.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Cynnal S15 2007 2008.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Capital Works Line.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Capital Works Points.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Capital Works.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Districts.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Permissive Access Line.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Permissive Access Points.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Permissive Access.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Signed Habitats.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Points.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Poly.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis collation of datasets is owned and managed by Welsh Government. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this data via a licenced agreement. Tir Cynnal and Tir Gofal were welsh agri-environment schemes that have now been replaced by Glastir in 2013. These two schemes were aimed at improving habitat quality supporting biodiversity protecting our precious natural landscapes and historic features and adopting improved resource management to minimise water pollution. amongst other agri-environment benefits.\tPublic record\n17124\tEXT_DS115370\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.327Z\tAudit of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) Habitat Management in Neath Port Talbot (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333730\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Adrian_f\\MapInfo Data\\Marsh Fritillary\\Habitat Quality Surveys\\SWales LA surveys (RSmith) revised\tGIS Service Desk  Mike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW\tThe Marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) priority species and has been listed by the Welsh Government as species of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity in Wales (Section 74 species Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000). This species in now confined to the south-western counties of England south and west Wales western Scotland and Ireland. This dataset is a MapInfo layer of the condition of habitat polygons for marsh fritillaries in Neath Port Talbot produced to help inform planning decisions.  The purpose of this data capture was to ascertain the extent and condition of habitat capable of supporting populations of marsh fritillaries in order to provide scientifically-justified guidance to inform planning decisions. This dataset is of a high quality collected under contract by experienced surveyors.\tPublic record\n18888\tNRW_DS124796\t2024-05-28T12:57:55.993Z\tScarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula, River Dee at Bangor-on-Dee 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373936\tStored on the WID in recorder 6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tIn 2018 a survey commissioned by Natural Resources Wales across the full former range of I. nubecula confirmed that it occurred at five sites on the River Dee two of which (Bangor-on-Dee and Pickhill Meadows) appeared to support strong populations. In 2020 Natural Resources Wales commissioned a second survey to confirm the continued presence of Scarce Yellow Sally and six nymphs were found at Bangor-on-Dee on 24th March 2020\tPublic record\n18192\tNRW_DS116011\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.646Z\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Dinefwr Woodlands 1994\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303131\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000002.\tInternal Custodian: Woodland Ecologist\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 1145 observations in woodlands of conservation interest in and around Carmarthen and Dinefwr woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.    The standard woodland NVC survey methodology was used.    The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities and to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tPublic record\n17644\tNRW_DS109756\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.081Z\tExtent and Distribution of Saline Lagoons in Wales (2009)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373536\tH:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working\\Saline Lagoons. H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\XMarine Habitats GIS working\\Saline Lagoons.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services\tCoastal saline lagoons are an unusual and rare habitat in the UK where they are recognized as a \\key\\\" habitat under the Biodiversity Action Plan. The EC Council Habitats Directive on the conservation of natural habitats and of wild fauna and flora identified coastal lagoons as a priority habitat. Work carried out identified 14 saline or potential saline lagoons and ponds in Wales four of which are identified as of high conservation merit.  The extent of these lagoons have been mapped . A secondary layer showing the extent of other pools that were investigated is also available. The purpose of this digitisation was to provide one collated layer showing the extent and distribution of saline lagoons in Wales. This layer may also be used as the basis for monitoring the extent of saline lagoons and therefore as a contribution to an assessment of their conservation status and to provide one collated layer showing the extent and distribution of saline lagoons in Wales. This layer may also be used as the basis for monitoring the extent of saline lagoons and therefore as a contribution to an assessment of their conservation status. This layer was created in January 2009 based on information provided by CCW Contract Science Report No. 464 (see related titles).\"\tPublic record\n18949\tNRW_DS124902\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.478Z\tOtter Survey of Wales 2015 - 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393032\tDMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Otter Data/Distribution %26 Population/2017 Otter Survey Wales/2017 Survey Only/Master data of NRW 2020 Otter Survey and hydrology contract_FINAL.csv?d\u003dw3c7e9be636d045acb28103d6f7cd0d0f\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1  EVID-285-2145\tRebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor\tSpreadsheet detailing the 6th Otter Survey of Wales results and hydrology analysis. The data collected for the 6th Otter Survey of Wales has been presented in excel. Data was collected by expert surveyors and volunteers to enable analysis of otter presence across Wales.   The data has been used for the 6th Otter Survey of Wales but may be useful for other research purposes.  Data collected in 1991 2002 and 2009-2010 is described separately. See metadata entry 116334.\tPrivate record\n17403\tNRW_DS46211\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.261Z\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) invertebrate data for Ceredigion\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436323131\tThe dataset is held on the NBN Gateway only. The full dataset from which it was derived from is held in NRW (see separate metadata entry).\tInternal Custodian: John Bratton, Biological Data Officer Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW, Bangor  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team\tCollation of occurrence records for UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species in Ceredigion. This is a subset of  \u0027UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) invertebrate data for Wales\u0027.  In Ceredigion it covers 7 species of ant 8 species of beetle 3 species of butterfly 1 species of dragonfly 14 species of moth 5 species of true fly and 1 mollusc. The purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions.\tPrivate record\n17904\tNRW_DS111834\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.587Z\tSt Bride\u0027s Bay Sublittoral Sediment Benthic Survey (2000)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383334\tData is held as Project No. 288, Media No. 1191.  Marine Recorder survey MRCCW30000000002.\tInternal Custodian: West Region Senior Marine Conservation Officer, NRW All other data held by Anne Bunker, West Region.  Metadata custodian: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to gain an understanding of the benthic sediment ecology of St Brides Bay in order to contribute to the development of conservation objectives for the Pembrokeshire Marine candidate Special Area of Conservation (SAC). During the summer of 2000 Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) investigated St Bride\u0027s Bay using a variety of survey techniques. This dataset contains the results of the analyses of biological data from grab and anchor dredge samples and examines the community types present by comparison with those listed in the Marine Biotope Classification of Britain and Ireland.\tPublic record\n18030\tNRW_DS115034\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.712Z\tCoastal Vegetated Shingle Structures of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303334\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1348 Archived as Project 363 and media 1348.   Archived on the Z drive  (not available on x drive) Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_ M_DRIVE:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\VegetatedShingle\\\tCustodians Coastal Scientist, NRW. Marine and Freshwater Data Manager, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tA survey of Coastal Vegetated Shingle Ridges of Great Britain was commissioned in 1987 by the Nature Conservancy Council.  The project provides a classification of the main shingle plant communities found on stable or semi-stable structures in Great Britain and was published by Joint Nature Conservation Council (JNCC) in four parts.  The data was captured in an attempt to describe the size location and quality of the Vegetated Shingle Ridges.  The surveys focused on the vegetation of stony banks and did not attempt to map strandline communities; therefore the site boundary is not a true representation of the extent of the Shingle Ridge.  An overall report which combines all data to determine which plant communities are found on shingle at a national level and how these related to existing National Vegetation Classification (NVC) communities a map and a description of the major plant communities recorded at each of the sites.\tPublic record\n17856\tNRW_DS111769\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.413Z\tSite Actions held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131373639\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.  Can be accessed via extranet by third parties who are responsible for actions. This is a legacy CCW Database and therefore Access maybe limited to a certain number of people.\tChristine Edwards, Strategic Project Manager, Operational Support Group, NRW Access to the database is provided under licence only through Gill Walker, Data Manager Sites       All requests for this data should be referred to Gill Walker.   Metadata will not be set to export to data.gov due to number of restriction son the data and access to the database\tThe Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database contains the actions that need to be carried out on sites to achieve appropriate conservation management The purpose of this data capture was to document and share information on who needs to do what where in order to get special sites into favourable condition. There are numerous management units involved (7000 plus) there are many organisations whose work needs to be coordinated and there are many different types of issues and actions needed. In practice a database is the most efficient way of collection and organising this information. The Actions Database project was initiated in May 2008. Data entry began in November 2008 and is ongoing.\tPublic record\n18800\tNRW_DS124164\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.030Z\tEighteen years of capture-mark-recapture (CMR) data of grey seal pups, Halichoerus grypus, from Pembrokeshire, Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234313634\tCopies of the 2 spreadsheets, scanned paper files, detailed metadata and copy of the paper submitted (which ws not  accepted for publicatuion in the end) are stored in the DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mm/Data Publications  Presentations/Seal Data/Seal Ringing Records Skomer.xlsx?d\u003dw26b1116ca7c44a05a578350158932094    Original paper files were given to presentation to Pembrokeshire Archive on 20/1/23 (by Kate Lock)\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Tom Stringell – electronic data   Metadata Point of Contact:  Tom Stringell       This data accompanies a scientific paper to be published via the Journal of ecology.  Data to be published as part of the paper.  We need to  confirm if the data will be held on the JoE platform or whether we need to put a copy on Lle to link to. All the raw records are third party data but NRW has received permission verbally from a rep of the now obsolete West Wales Field Society Malcolm Cullen.  They have requested the paper data be lodged at the Pembrokeshire Achive and therefore become public information.  detailed metadata (required for the JoE) explains the data futher and is stored in the DMS.\tEighteen years (1954-1972) of capture-mark-recapture data of 245 grey seal pups marked with identification tags in Wales.  The data was obtained from paper files held by NRW and supplied originally West Wales Field Society and includes information on the location and number of animals marked in each year including sex and age estimations.\tPublic record\n18826\tNRW_DS124681\t2024-05-28T12:56:49.998Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Substation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363831\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Substation.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Substation. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18663\tNRW_DS123254\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.425Z\tWFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2 Interim\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323534\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2 Interim.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge\t\tPrivate record\n17868\tNRW_DS111301\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.669Z\tGrazing Marsh Species Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333031\t? This data may have been lost\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of flora and fauna species records from grazing marshes in Wales. The purpose of this dataset was to collate information on the range of species found in Welsh grazing marshes in order to provide an inventory of the species and their distribution.\tPublic record\n18371\tNRW_DS116807\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.499Z\tMarine Licences\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383037\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence applications line WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence applications points WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence applications polys WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence disposal sites WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Grab Sample Applications.lyr    The restricted munitions data is stored on restricted editing area of the X drive.  X:\\Editing Restricted\tEmmer Litt\tThe records contain coordinate data associated with current marine licence applications in Wales. The location of disposal sites is also available. This data allows the locations of licence applications to be mapped to assess their impacts on designated sites and combined effects with other proposals.   Data related to historic and expired marine licence applications are held as a separate dataset (NRW_DS119099).\tPublic record\n17940\tNRW_DS112412\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.963Z\tSubtidal Habitat Diversity Layer (2010)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343132\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the range is measured using biotope richness biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is used to present these metrics.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out taking into account the source of the data its age spatial taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tPublic record\n18034\tNRW_DS114949\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.710Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Biotope SLR.FserX.T in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393439\tData is archived as project 199, media 1352.  A working copy of the data is available at  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSandCB\\2010 ASML MSandCB\\Data\tInternal custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. This dataset is the results of an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the tide-swept boulder habitat in the Menai Strait in 2008.  Tide-swept Fucus serratus habitats is a nationally important habitat and although the habitat extent was known no detailed studies of the communities had been carried out.  Tide-swept habitats were surveyed at two locations to provide a baseline for current condition and to allow a study for potential future impacts of mussel farming in the vicinity.  All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high.\tPublic record\n18844\tNRW_DS124652\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.185Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Export Cable\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363532\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Export Cable.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows the Pen y Cymoedd Export Cable (132 kV).  Export cables relate to the 132Kv cable that leaves the Substation once it has been transformed from 33kV. This then runs down to the sub-station in Rhigos where it connects to the National Grid. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17980\tNRW_DS114744\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.159Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Marine Species Sightings and Miscellaneous Data (Extracted from Marine Recorder)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373434\t[A]  Data is held in Natural Resources Wales copy of Marine Recorder.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tTom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal ecologist, Natural Resources Planning/Marine and Freshwater Ecosytem Group\tThe dataset was set up to collate ad-hoc records of species around Wales as seen by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) staff or provided specifically to NRW for its business use.  The purpose of this collation was to record incidental records. These tend to be species of special interest such as rare or scarce species or species not considered native in Welsh waters.  Data may include incidental records collected while conducting specific scientific research that are outside of the remit of the research or methodology used. This dataset is a work in progress and is continually updated.  Sightings date from 2007 onwards however older records may be added from files and notes as required. The majority of the recorded by experienced staff or are verified with known experts and staff through the use of photographs video or specimens.\tPublic record\n18577\tNRW_DS121942\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.842Z\tAir Photos (Geomatics)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393432\tX:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Geomatics air photos  for data related to 2009 - 2017\tKeith Jones - GIS and Spatial Data team\tThis data collation was commissioned to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) as an enduring service by Environment Agency (EA) using Geomatics the surveying arm to the EA.\tPublic record\n18731\tNRW_DS123350\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.116Z\tRiver Quality Objectives (RQO) 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333530\tX:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\RQO 2010.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n17522\tNRW_DS101843\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.131Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of Red Sea Fingers Alcyonium glomeratum\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383433\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/alglom ,  Images - Dell1590 C/docs and settings/Monitoring/alglom\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the red sea fingers Alcyonium glomeratum colony area and to look for damage and disease. Alcyonium glomeratum (red sea fingers) is a Lusitanian species near to its northern limit of distribution. Colonies are long-lived and possible indicators of climate change. Several colonies have been monitored since 1982. Some colonies are very stable over time and others are variable.\tPublic record\n17889\tNRW_DS112407\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.013Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species Diversity Layer (Chao2 Analysis)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343037\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact is Data Services\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. The Chao2 estimator was applied to species data collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot) the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data for the Welsh coastline taken from the mean high water datum to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out taking into account the source of the data its age spatial taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tPublic record\n18830\tNRW_DS124680\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.233Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Step Up Transformer (EPS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363830\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Step Up Transformer (EPS).lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Step Up Transformer (EPS). These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18606\tNRW_DS122600\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.767Z\tNDF Key hotspots of biodiversity \u0026amp; ecological connectivity\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232363030\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\Key hotspots of biodiversity \u0026amp; ecological connectivity.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis data shows nationally important ecological networks where connectivity is high across a range of habitats between biodiversity hotspots. High connectivity means that ecosystems within these areas are relatively resilient to environmental changes such as climate change and also have the capacity to help build ecosystem resilience more widely.  It is important to recognise these ecologically important area both with a view to maintaining the existing habitats that contribute to the networks and also identify opportunities to improve them through targeted action such as habitat restoration and improved landscape management. Whilst the map gives a national overview it is based on more detailed data of habitat networks that need to be considered for practical implementation as the finer scale features in the lowland areas are not visible at the national scale.  Maps of ecological networks provide a practical means of considering connectivity alongside development.  Under the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 public authorities across Wales have a duty to promote ecosystem resilience in the exercise of their functions. This layer can used to help inform this work by demonstrating which broad areas contain important and diverse habitat networks that are likely to benefit from careful management in order to maintain and enhance habitat connectivity and therefore the resilience of the habitats they support. This layer shows summary data at a national level which is made up of more detailed maps that are available to be considered at whatever scale is appropriate.  Ecological connectivity operates at the national as well as local level. This map considers the broadest scale and presents a national framework of ecologically resilient networks and the most important biodiversity hotspots. This map could be used at a strategic level to identify areas which are likely to benefit from careful management to maintain and enhance ecological connectivity and therefore the resilience of the habitats they support. Implementation on the ground is likely to require much finer scale information as at a local level other networks and ecological hotspots not visible at a national scale will high importance.\tPrivate record\n17516\tNRW_DS101864\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.485Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Littoral communities Meso scale\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383634\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared docs/data/biological/littoral ,   Images - Dell1590 C/docs and settings/MONITORING/littoral.  MarClim data is also available on the NBN Gateway.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Investigation into the intertidal littoral communities around Skomer MNR.  The purpose of this data capture was assess the \"health\" and changes occurring in the intertidal communities and investigating climate change effects.\tPublic record\n18032\tNRW_DS114948\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.690Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Boulders Habitat in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393438\tData is archived as project 199, media 1352 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400) A working copy of the data is available at  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy_CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSandCB\\2010 ASML MSandCB\\Data Marine Recorder Survey: MRCCW1630000002F ArcGIS layers: Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. To do this CCW has developed a programme of feature condition monitoring. In Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide and Reefs.   The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the reporting on the favourable conservation status of Reef features in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation. This dataset comprises of the results from an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the tide-swept boulder habitat in the Menai Strait in 2008. Boulders are a reef feature of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Boulders at both Britannia Bridge and Felinheli were surveyed. The purpose of this data capture was to assist in the reporting on the favourable conservation status of Reef features in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors so data quality and confidence is considered high.\tPublic record\n19149\tNRW_DS125736\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.097Z\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of selected areas of the Dee Estuary - 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373336\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Coastal Habitats\\Dee NVC 2022\\Dee_saltmarsh_NVC2022.gdb\tData Custodian: Heather Lewis, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team, EPP\tThe report and GIS dataset present the results of a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey carried out in 2022 on selected areas of saltmarsh habitat and transitional vegetation in the Dee Estuary Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   Seven distinct areas of saltmarsh were assessed from the marshes between accreting dunes at Gronant southwards through Point of Ayr Greenfield Flushing Lagoon Bagillt Marshes Flint Marshes and Connah’s Quay Marshes to the most southerly areas included in the survey referred to as the Dee River Marshes which extend southwards to the Jubilee Lift Bridge at Queensferry.  A field based judgement was carried out on community assignment supported by quadrat samples and target notes.\tPrivate record\n17887\tNRW_DS112408\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.578Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species Diversity Layer (Neighbourhood Analysis)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343038\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of Contact: Master Reference Data Team.\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the range is measured using species richness average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 10 km is utilised to present these metrics. The purpose of this data capture was to establish diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline between the mean high-water mark up to 12 nautical miles offshore.   An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out taking into account the source of the data its age spatial taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tPublic record\n17367\tNRW_DS46083\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.239Z\tStoneworts in Wales (Recorder 6 Dataset)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436303833\t[A]  Species Records are available in Recorder 6  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tJulian Woodman, Higher Plant Specialist, Terrestrial Species, NRW\tA collation of all known records of stoneworts in Wales from 1726 up till August 2007. Stoneworts (or charophytes) are a group of algae that grow in fresh and brackish water and have a relatively complex structure. Stoneworts are good indicators of water quality due to their sensitivities towards pollution including nutrient enrichment acting as a bio-indicator.  Many are of conservation importance. 30 species have been recorded in the UK and 17 of these occur in Wales. Of the latter four are Red Data Book species six are Nationally Scarce and 9 are considered rare in Wales; three are Priority Species in the UK Biodiversity Action Plane (BAP) and are included on the National Assembly of Wales\u0027 List of Species and Habitats of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity (Section 74 list).  All data collated and verified at source by known expert.\tPublic record\n18601\tNRW_DS122909\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.441Z\tNDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through wetland creation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393039\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through wetland creation.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThe dataset shows how many ecosystem service benefits a wetland habitat could generate if created at a given location. In some areas a single wetland habitat may generate multiple ecosystem services making this site a prime opportunity area. However it is important to recognise that in some cases a wetland that only delivers a single ecosystem service can still be beneficial if the demand for that service is high at the site of interest. Services that were considered in this map are: opportunities to enhance the wetland network restoration priorities for functional peat and opportunities to generate natural flood management.  This dataset gives and overview how the opportunities for wetland habitat creation are distributed nationally which aids in identifying important opportunity spaces. Looking at the national distribution of restoration opportunities also helps to highlight that not all areas of Wales are the same with some areas being much more suited to restoration of one broad habitat than the other.\tPrivate record\n18684\tNRW_DS123325\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.544Z\tScreening protected habitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333235\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\Protected Habitats for Permitting Screening.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\Screening protected habitats.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18617\tNRW_DS123244\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.297Z\tNitrates\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323434\tx:\\EU Directives\\Surface Water Abstraction Directive\\Nitrates.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n17927\tNRW_DS112726\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.694Z\tTir Cymen Farms - Raw Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373236\tOff-site storage (contact library team)\tInternal Custodian: Buddug Jones, Common Land and Agriculture Advisor, Ecosystem Services Team/ Natural Resources Planning.  Metadata contact: Information Services Officer, Records and Information Services Team\tRaw data to support the management plans for a number of holdings throughout Wales that are part of the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme. The raw data includes macro landscape assessment records taken in each of the areas over a five year period; characterisation records; species plot records; cover plot records; boundary plot records management maps. The purpose of this data capture was to ensure selected land holdings meet and continue to meet the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme.\tPublic record\n19035\tNRW_DS125198\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.897Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313938\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 3 are a spatial (polygon) dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. \u0027WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 3\u0027 is a subset extracted from the NRW\u0027s Detailed River Network (DRN).\tPublic record\n18672\tNRW_DS123283\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.430Z\tFlood Defence Condition Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323833\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Defence Condition Survey.lyr\tWyn Davies, Manager, National Flood Risk Services, Flood and Incident Management\t\tPrivate record\n17674\tNRW_DS109893\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.688Z\tReview of Side-Scan Sonar Results from North Llyn Modiolus Reef\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383933\tAll data is archived in project no.150 (DV tapes are archived in media 482 \u0026amp; 483, project data is archived in media 484).  The original side-scan sonar data is archived under project 233 (media 602).\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tAn emergency review of side-scan sonar data collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2007 was required as the images showed marks that were potentially derived from trawling gear.  The area was approximately 10 km by 6 km and approximately 1.5 km offshore within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and is also protected under Sea Fisheries byelaws that prevent the use of trawls and dredges for the harvesting of molluscan shellfish except as authorised by the local sea fisheries committee.\tPublic record\n17822\tNRW_DS110926\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.141Z\tMonitoring of Carbonate Reef Extent and Fish Population at Holden\u0027s Reef (2003 - 2010)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393236\t[A]  Data is archived under project 222, media no. 829.  [B]  Copies of the video data is held on the Imaging drive, the tapes are held in the fireproof safe.  [C[  Images are held on the imaging computer and a copy on the MFSG drive.   [D]  A working file is held on the MFSG drive.\tInternal Custodian: Mike Camplin - Marine Monitoring Team, NRW  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tHolden\u0027s Reef is a small (approximately 40 m diameter) roughly circular reef situated in the northern sector of Cardigan Bay. It is unusual in being formed as a result of a chemical reaction between seawater and the escape of natural gas from the seabed a phenomenon generally found in much deeper waters.  Holden\u0027s Reef is one of an assemblage of methanogen-precipitated calcium carbonate reefs and was the first of the group to be discovered. This dataset contains the results of a programme of monitoring established to study fish populations and reef extent. It also includes data from an in-house survey carried out at Holden\u0027s Reef in 2003 initiated following the reef\u0027s discovery and Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) phase 2 surveys carried out in 2005 of \\Little\\\" and \\\"Big\\\" Reef.  Holden\u0027s Reef is monitored for fish abundance and diversity extent and rugosity (the link between rugosity and fish abundance has been seen in several studies). This will contribute to condition assessment of the reef feature in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  Holden\u0027s Reef is situated more than 10 km off shore north west of Barmouth and is approximately 40 m in diameter.  \"\tPublic record\n17783\tNRW_DS110514\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.231Z\tMonitoring and Surveillance of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353134\tContact - Patrick Lindley for information about where NRW store their bird monitoring data.\tMatty Murphy - Maritime Ornithologist/ Marine vertebrate team leader, Natural resources planning/Marine and freshwater ecosytem group Patrick Lindley - Maritime Ornithologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (Formerly David Allen, Environmental Monitoring Manager, CCW)\tThis dataset contains the results from on going monitoring of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) across Wales. SPAs are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive) and for regularly occurring migratory species. One of the key responsibilities of the statutory nature conservation agencies such as Natural Resources Wales (NRW) in the UK is the identification and protection of a series of sites intended to conserve important birds. Article 12 under the EC Birds Directive deals with the general reporting obligations of the Member States and Commission. Every three to four years Member States of the European Union are required to report on implementation of the Birds Directive. This reporting provides information on: the general implementation of the Directive; and the conservation status of individual species listed under the Annexes of the Directive.\tPublic record\n18206\tNRW_DS116266\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.175Z\tFlood Risk Areas (Cycle 1)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323636\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM.\tThe Cycle 1 Flood Risk Areas are those communities in Wales where most significant flood risks exist from surface water flooding only (from surface water run-off groundwater and ordinary watercourses). These areas were identified for the purpose of the first Preliminary Flood Risk Assessment under the Flood Risk Regulations 2009 using guidance produced by the Welsh Government to identify areas where flood risk is an issue for greater than 5000 people.   The Flood Risk Areas were first identified in 2011 until being replaced in 2018 by the Cycle 2 Flood Risk Areas.\tPublic record\n18668\tNRW_DS123282\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.364Z\tDPS3 Site allocations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323832\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DPS3 Site allocations.lyr\tRichard Weston, Team Leader, Flood Forecasting, National Flood Risk Services\t\tPrivate record\n17958\tNRW_DS114567\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.553Z\tDrop-Down Video of Historical Native Oyster (Ostrea edulis) Habitat in Pembrokeshire, South Wales (2010)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353637\t[A] MFSG Archive Project no. 320 and Media No. 1268 and 1397 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1268 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400) [B] Species data held in Marine Recorder survey number: MRCCW31000000002\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe native or flat oyster (Ostrea edulis) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority species and it is also listed for Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006 as being \u0027of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity in Wales.\u0027   The native oyster has a long history as an important commercial species in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to Milford Haven Swansea Bay/Mumbles and Porthcawl although at a much reduced scale. The remaining oyster population in Wales is under threat through the spread of the protozoan parasite Bonamia ostreae continued poor recruitment and competition from the alien invasive slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata. The situation is exacerbated by the continued operation of a small oyster fishery in Milford Haven and Swansea Bay.   The aim of this project was to gather information on the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Pembrokeshire coast. This information will contribute to our understanding of the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Wales coast and will inform projects to reinstate oyster habitat and stock enhancement.  This dataset collates the examination of drop down video of oyster beds.\tPublic record\n19123\tNRW_DS125603\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.475Z\tNutrient Review - Surface and Sub-Surface Phosphate and Nitrogen Loads\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363033\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Subsurface Load Nitrate.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Subsurface Load Phosphorus.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Load Phosphorus.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Load Nitrate.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tDataset shows the predicted surface and sub-surface phosphorus and Nitrogen loads in transitional surface water lake catchment and coastal catchments.  based on a land use/land cover and associated loading rates (Kg/5m grid/yr).  Estimated amount of phosphorus and nitrates for each 5m grid square that enters the soil by subsurface flow.\tPublic record\n17276\tEXT_DS124645\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.296Z\tEnvironment Agency: NRW Alternate Class Points WGS84 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363435\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh Areas\\NRW Alternate Class Points WGS84.lyr\tMarine Monitoring Officer\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. Saltmarsh points of uncertainty taken from 2018 aerial imagery. These points required ground-truthing to determine the species present. In coordinate system WGS84 for ease of use on hand held differential GPS device.  This data is Open but owned by the Environment Agency\tPrivate record\n17481\tNRW_DS100811\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.219Z\tWater Discharge ( Revoked )\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383131\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\WIMS Revoked Consents.lyr\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons.    The dataset has been supplied by the NRW and contains Revoked Water Discharge Consent Licenses point data information.  This is likely to be a duplicate of \"WIMS Revoked Consents - Aug 2016.lyr\" (NRW_DS123341) as the dataset WIMS Revoked Consents.LYR no longer exists on the NRW X drive.\tPrivate record\n19118\tNRW_DS125607\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.113Z\tNutrient Review - Phosphorus and Nitrate Loading From Consented Discharges\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363037\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nitrogen and Phosphorus Loading from Consented Discharges.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tEstimated phosphorus and nitrate loading from consented discharges for sites with a numerical limit on phosphorus (kg/yr)\tPublic record\n18173\tNRW_DS115862\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.909Z\tBeach Profile Data Swansea Bay \u0026amp; Carmarthen Bay Coastal Autumn 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383632\tProject 437 Media 1476 (File path: K\\Archive\\Media  1401to1500\\Media 1476)\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\t\tPrivate record\n17079\tEXT_DS24229\t2024-05-28T12:10:11.121Z\tBiodiversity Species Audit for Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323239\tCD ROM in NRW Maes y Fynnon Library. 2 CD’s available: Library Number:  502.74(429)\tGIS Service Desk Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\tA Species Audit produced by Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB) as part of the Biodiversity Action Plan process to inform Local Biodiversity Action Planners of the whereabouts and status of selected species considered of particular importance for Wales. It is hoped that in the longer term analysis of these data will help identify other measures to address Species Action Plan targets.  The purpose of this data capture is to create an audit of species data across Wales.\tPublic record\n18010\tNRW_DS115072\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.792Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria Reef in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303732\tArchived as Project No 199, Media No 548. Species data is also available in Marine Recorder and on the NBN Gateway.  A working copy of the data us stored at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Cardigan Bay\\2008 ASML Cardigan BayData\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats).  In Cardigan Bay SAC specific areas of interest include the biogenic Sabellaria reef.  Sabellaria reef is considered vulnerable to coastal development sea defence construction and heavy trampling. The purpose of this data capture was to re-survey the quadrat monitoring sites established in 2007. The descriptive transect surveys are not repeated in 2008. Data quality and confidence is considered high as all survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.\tPublic record\n19624\tEXT_DS161304\t2024-10-01T10:46:28.807Z\tNational Disused Coal Tip (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\National Disused Coal Tips.lyr\tMary Lane, Specialist Advisor, Land Management \u0026amp; Liabilities, Land Management Team\tThis dataset has been compiled by Welsh Government and shows the locations and boundaries of disused coal tips across Wales.  The dataset includes information on the potential to impact public safety ratings from A to R as well as source of boundary data local authority where the tip is located and additional information about the tip.\tPublic record\n19058\tNRW_DS125407\t2024-05-28T13:01:42.975Z\tElan Valley Drone Survey 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343037\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\Elan Valley - for combined .lyr  files (raw data: Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\GIS \u0026amp; Spatial Data\\From Y drive\\Peatlands\\Elan Valley)\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Esther Clews, Specialist Advisor, Data and GIS – National Peatland Action Programme\tAir Survey of 10 upland peatland sites in Mid Wales flown in January and March 2022. The 10 sites were flown between the 12th January and 25th March 2022. All sites were surveyed using an RGB vertical camera then the data processed to produce digital surface models (DSM) and orthomosaics. The DSMs were processed to generate catchment flow and contour vector data sets to assist with the peatbog restoration planning. Drone details - eBee RTK unmanned aircraft system (UAS) by Sensefly. The eBee is an electric fixed wing Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) weight 1.1 kg with a maximum speed of 110 km/h and a maximum wind capability of 12 m/s. Camera - Sensefly S.O.D.A. RGB camera: 1″ Sensor RGB lens F/2.8-11 10.6 mm resolution 5472 x 3648 px (3:2) Exposure compensation ±2.0 Global Shutter 1/30 – 1/2000s Auto White balance ISO range 125-6400. Flight path - GIS ESRI Shape or kml files provided by the client were loaded into the UAS to provide survey area coverage parameters. Data resolution - The UAV is programmed to fly the areas at a height that obtains an image resolution of between 3-4 cm/pixel with an image overlap of 75%. The exact resolution is dependent on the ruggedness of the terrain; the resolution is set by the lowest resolution area of the orthomosaic. Data capture - Once the orthomosaic sand DSMs had been created the DSMs were processed to generate catchments flow lines and 0.5m interval contour layers. This was carried out using QGIS and GRASS. The orthomosaics were then used to manually digitise peat and hag patchwork areas hags bare peat/water. The 10 sites fell into the following categories: • No visible peat bog areas or stabilised hags and gulleys. • Signs of historic peatbog degradation but currently stable/vegetated – vegetated hags and gulleys • Visibly eroding peatbog areas – exposed bare peat visible eroding hags and gulleys\tPrivate record\n18120\tNRW_DS115774\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.849Z\tSpatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) in South Wales (2013).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373734\tMaxEnt Model outputs are located within the accompaning report: NRW-14-105023 Spatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt in South Wales. NRW Science Report Series. Report 30.   Raw Data is held and distributed by ARC Trust.  Associated Report Title: Spatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt in South Wales (2013) Fletcher DH, Arnell AP, French GCA and Wilkinson JW.  NRW Science Report No.30  Ffynnon Number: NRW-14-105023\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset was compiled to build on previous related work conducted in North Wales and Powys. 12 Unitary Authorities in south Wales were assessed in terms of their Favourable Conservation Status for great crested newts (Triturus cristatus) using a habitat modelling approach.  Project specific aims included assessing: i) known distribution ii) suitable habitat iii) survey target areas iv) important populations for metapopulation connectivity v) pond and habitat creation areas and vi) Local Population Targets (LPTs) for each unitary authority in South Wales to develop a tool kit which can be used by local planners as an indication as to the presence of great crested newts. This tool kit includes spatially-explicit information in the form of GIS-based mapping (layers) and spreadsheets that will inform conservation priorities and action land-use planning and other things such as derogation licensing.  This project follows on from other recent attempts to describe the status of great crested newts (Triturus cristatus) in the UK and to apply the concept of Favourable Conservation Status (FCS). This is the third and final regional modelling exercise in Wales covering the country\u0027s core great crested newt range (Anglesey NE Wales Powys and South Wales between Gower and Monmouthshire). The integrated results will enable the first objective Wales-wide assessment of great crested newt status for the country. MaxEnt model testing produced acceptably high values (bigger than 0.7) for the area under the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve. Due to limited record numbers in individual Unitary Authorities NRW built and tested the MaxEnt model with records from all 12 Unitary Authorities.\tPublic record\n17182\tEXT_DS121966\t2024-05-28T12:15:59.324Z\tOffice of National Statistics (ONS): Police Authority Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393636\tX:\\Designations\\Emergency Services\\Police Authority Areas.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Police Authority Areas.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Office of National Statistics (ONS) and licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under an Open Government Licence (OGL).\tPublic record\n18585\tNRW_DS122146\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.980Z\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) Sampling Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313436\t[A]x:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\River Habitat Survey.lyr  [B] Original data held on Wiski-Kieco  Update from Mel Lacan, August 2021: \\When we have KiECo RHS up and running fully (and calculating HQA Class) then I would like to update this layer and make it more widely available with some comms around RHS intranet page to be produced (so not ready yet).\"\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  GIS and Spatial Data Team\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) is Natural Resources Wales standard for collecting data on the physical character and quality of river habitats across the Wales. This is a spatail dataset detailing the location of sampling points from around Wales.\tPublic record\n18584\tNRW_DS122027\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.920Z\tNationally Important Biotopes: Intertidal Habitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303237\tx:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Nationaly_Important_Biotopes\\Intertidal  Habitats 2009.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team  Internal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNationally Important Biotopes is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of intertidal nationally important biotopes across Wales. A biotope is defined as the combination of abiotive habitat and its associated community of species. It can be define at a variety of scales (with related corresponding defress of similarity) and should be a regularly occuring association to justify its inclusion within a classification system. Biotopes becoming nationally important when they become or are generally geographically restricted on a UK wide basis. Highly rated examples of nationally and internationally important biotopes should be notified features within Site of Special Scienitific Interest (SSSIs). In the UK Biotopes types include chalk biotopes soft piddock bored biotopes extremely exposed fucoid biotopes Tide-swept algal biotopes sand-influence rock biotopes extreme shelter biotopes muddy gravel biotopes rockpool biotopes under-boulder biotopes cave and overhang biotopes sublittoral fringe surge gully biotopes and seagrass biotopes.\tPrivate record\n18604\tNRW_DS122463\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.521Z\tForest Mineshafts\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343633\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest mineshafts.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset contains Mineshafts on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any Mineshafts data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured Mineshafts information.\tPrivate record\n18121\tNRW_DS115830\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.809Z\tWater Quality of Riverine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) across Wales (2006-2012).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383330\tArchive project number: 448 Archive number 2006-2012\tInternal Contacts: Freshwater Team Leader, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis data collection was required as part of the water quality assessment of various Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) for the monitoring round and report cycle 2006 to 2012. Water quality targets for designated riverine features are specified under the JNCC CSM Guidance. The targets are based on routine water quality monitoring and subsequent reporting. Water quality targets are assessed based on the chemistry biology phosphate suspended solids and un-ionized ammonia. The dataset details the results of water quality target testing for the following rivers and associated SACs: - Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn SAC - Afon Eden and Cors Goch Trawsfynydd SAC - River Dee and Bala Lake / Afon Dyfrdwy a Llyn Tegid SAC - Afon Tywi / River Tywi SAC - Afon Teifi / Afon Teifi - Afonydd Cleddau SAC - River Usk SAC - River Wye SAC - Afon Glaslyn (Meirionnydd Oakwoods SAC)\tPublic record\n18598\tNRW_DS122766\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.409Z\tHydrometry and Telemetry Data held in WISKI (WISKI 7)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373636\tAll data is stored in WISKI in the WISKI 7 Also known as WISKI Core or the Hydro and Telemetry module\tInternal Custodian:  Alison Hanson - Hydrometry and Telemetry Team Leader\tWISKI 7 holds core hydrometry and telemetry data owned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and third parties. This data includes; River Level and Flows Rainfall Groundwater levels and water temperature.\tPublic record\n18713\tNRW_DS123356\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.303Z\tMonitoring Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Lakes Species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333536\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\Monitoring SSSI Lakes Species.lyr\tVictoria Bowmaker, Officer 2 (Environmental Assessment Officer), Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team\t\tPrivate record\n19086\tNRW_DS125516\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.545Z\tCeredigion Coastal Rare Plant Survey 1975 \u0026amp; Ceredigion Rare Plants\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353136\toff-site archive ref: CCW/WRAB/024\tData Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Data Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThis is a survey of rare plants in Ceredigion coastal areas. Most of the 1700 species of British vascular plants can be found on the coast the greater part of them on the more terrestrial coastal habitats on sand dunes shingle beaches or cliffs. Also included is a register and survey of Ceredigion rare plants.\tPrivate record\n17298\tEXT_DS124594\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.348Z\tNetwork Rail: Network Route data (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353934\tonly available to Nick Pritchard through the W drive\tInternal contact: Nick Pritchard\tRail network route data from Network Rail.\tPublic record\n17810\tNRW_DS111011\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.861Z\tMonitoring of Limestone Communities and Sponge Morphology at Ynys Moelfre\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303131\tAll electronic data is archived under project 226, media no. 898. Quadrat video and incidental video is stored on the imaging machine. A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSponges are considered an important component of benthic communities in temperate waters and the community composition of subtidal limestone biotopes has been identified as a priority feature for the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   Monitoring sponge morphology diversity is used as a qualitative estimate of sponge species diversity and changes in morphological composition of sponge assemblages may also provide an indication of changes in environmental conditions.  The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Identify community composition of subtidal limestone biotopes and their favourable conservation status.   -  Establish a baseline for the community and monitor changes in the diversity of subtidal sponge morphologies.  Changes in sponge species composition may indicate a change within the sessile community as a whole.\tPublic record\n19053\tNRW_DS125330\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.674Z\tVisitor safety zones\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333330\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Visitor Safety Zones.lyr\tDavid Liddy, Specialist Advisor Recreational Safety, NRW Heather Crump, Data and Content Advisor (Recreation), NRW\tThe NRW estate zoned according to Visitor Safety Group risk categories. Although these zones appear with hard boundaries they are indicative only and care must be exercised if zooming in closely and zones are not sharply defined regarding the application of control measures. Public rights of way infoamtion is a NRW representation only. These maps should be used to compliment public safety risk assessments. Reliance is upon site managers to contact the data custodian to keep this data layer current. To edit or print a map of the zones email the Data and Content Advisor (Recreation) to ensure the correct template and formatting are used.\tPrivate record\n20260\tNRW_DS161338\t2025-02-14T11:39:33.327Z\tElement XML Match - Title1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333338\tNRW Internal Location\tNRW Internal Contact\tAbstract\tPrivate record\n17119\tEXT_DS115541\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.247Z\tBotanical Society of Britain and Ireland (BSBI): Rare Plants Register for Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353431\tX:\\Biological\\Rare plants register\\BSBI RPR 1 and 2km sq.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Rare plants register\\BSBI RPR 10km sq.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Rare plants register\\BSBI RPR points.lyr   Should contact BSBI for the most up to date information.\tGIS Service Desk  Terrestrial Environmental Evidence Manager, NRW  External Contact: Welsh Officer, BSBI\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Botanical Society of Britain and Ireland (BSBI) which has been licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for use. County Rare Plant Registers are prepared by Botanical Society of the British Isles (BSBI) using data from Vice-county recorders from a range of data sources including their own records those sent to them and those seen from other sources including Local Environmental Records Centres (LERCs). Records are validated by recorders and provide a summary of the best available data for selected species including: - Red Data species: A subset of the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red Data Book criteria and categories related to species in the UK - Section 42 species: The Section 42 list of habitats and species of principal importance in Wales is the definite list and is a key requirement of the National Environment Research Council (NERC) Biodiversity Duty.   - Nationally Rare and Scarce species: Excludes rare species qualifying under the main IUCN criteria. - Locally Rare (and sometimes Scarce) species: A species should be defined as \u0027locally rare\u0027 if it was known to be present within three or fewer \u0027sites\u0027 within a vice-county.\tPublic record\n18500\tNRW_DS121298\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.352Z\tThe status and distribution of the ground beetle Leistus montanus in 2017 at its historic sites in Wales.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323938\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tData Custodian: Mike Howe  Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThe aim of the 2017 data collection was to determine the current status and distribution of Leistus montanus in Wales by visiting all known locations and searching under stones on scree slopes. Once regularly recorded in the montane areas of Scotland and the Lake District recent records of the IUCN Endangered ground beetle Leistus montanus a species associated with high altitude scree are confined to the Langdale Pikes in 2008 and Skiddaw in 2016 both in Cumbria. In Wales it was historically recorded on Cadair Idris in 1829 and 1968 from Y Lliwedd on Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa) in 1965 and 1968 and from Carnedd y Ddelw on the Carneddau in 1971. The only recent record was thought to be from Eglwyseg Rocks in 2007.\tPublic record\n17289\tEXT_DS124598\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.872Z\tNational Flood Asset Dataset for Wales: Ordinary Water courses flood assets held in AMX–Flood (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353938\tAMX\tawaiting information from custodian Richard Kelland - now moved to new role\tmetadata to be provided by custodian.   AMX flood asset data store contains data that has been split into three types according to data owner (NRW EA others)\tPrivate record\n18467\tNRW_DS119296\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.323Z\tUnderground Telephone Lines on Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Land\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323936\tX:\\Utilities\\Telecoms\\UG Telephone Lines on NRW Land.lyr\tCustodian: GIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains Underground Telephone Lines on NRW land. Currently the data covers forestry areas but in future will expand to all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured underground telephone line information.\tPublic record\n17763\tNRW_DS110321\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.497Z\tLittoral Monitoring in Permanent Quadrats Established at West Angle Bay and Manorbier 1996-1997\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333231\tArchived as project no 126 (media no 367). File path: -Z:|\\Archive\\Media 401to500\\dia301 to 400\\Media367.  Data is also held within Marine Recorder as survey numberMRCCW10000000075.\tInternal Contact: Mike Camplin, Team Leader, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe Sea Empress oil tanker ran aground at the entrance to Milford Haven on the 15th of February 1996 and proceeded to spill over 70000 tonnes of crude oil into the sea. The purpose of data capture of this data capture was to investigate the changes in community structure following the oil spill and identify any differences between the two sites monitored. Two permanent quadrats established prior to the spill at West Angle Bay and Manorbier Bay were both severely impacted however neither were subjected to cleaning. This provided an ideal opportunity for pre and post spill monitoring the results of which are presented in this dataset.\tPublic record\n18929\tNRW_DS124804\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.583Z\tDistribution of the spider Baryphyma gowerense on Cors Bodeilio, Cors Erddreiniog, Cors Geirch and Cors Goch 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383034\tData stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder.\tMike Howe\tIn order to improve our knowledge of the ecology and distribution of the Vulnerable and Nationally Rare money spider Baryphyma gowerense survey work was conducted on Cors Bodeilio Cors Erddreiniog Cors Goch and Cors Geirch National Nature Reserves (NNRs) between June and August 2019.\tPublic record\n17687\tNRW_DS110067\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.359Z\tNutrient Modelling for Llangorse Lake\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303637\tArchived as project number 202, media number 567 H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media567\tInternal Custodian: Senior Freshwater Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tLlangorse Lake is the largest natural eutrophic lake in Wales and has been designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The lake has a history of eutrophication and so it was decided to undertake a nutrient modelling and nutrient budget exercise to determine the size and sources of nutrient loads entering the lake. Llangorse Lake has a history of eutrophication problems believed to have been caused by high nutrient loads entering the lake from the surrounding catchment. This study aimed to determine the size and main sources of those loads. Specific objectives include: -  To review existing data and information on the water quality of Llangorse Lake. -  To conduct a field study to investigate the nutrient budget of the lake. -  To model the nutrient delivery from the catchment to the lake with particular focus on phosphorus delivery to give an indication of source apportionment. -  To prepare a draft catchment management plan.\tPublic record\n17562\tNRW_DS102121\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.517Z\tSquirrels of Clocaenog Conifer Plantation in North Wales (1992-2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313231\t[A]  Species data held in Recorder 6 Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Clocaenog CCW000380000000B\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tPresence of red and grey squirrels in Clocaenog Forest based on joint contract run by Forestry Commission Research Agency (FCRA) and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) (1992-1999)  with subsequent records supplied by Sarah Cartmel (1999-2006).  The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the population ecology of red and grey squirrels and to identify their habitat requirements and to investigate the relative populations of red and grey squirrels in parts of the forest and whether they are stable or changing.\tPublic record\n17534\tNRW_DS101803\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.369Z\tFreshwater Image Library\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383033\tFreshwater Ecosystems Team Leader. N: and G: drive These images may have been transferred to the NRW Image Store\tA collection of freshwater images compiled and managed by Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader. If this data has been transferred to the image store the internal contact is Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis is a catalogue of images sourced from both slides and digital images. Approximately 75 percent are CCW 10 percent are Head of the Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group or Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader within NRW. Approximately 15 percent of the images are sources externally from volunteers or organisation - information about this is held within an excel document within the image catalogue. These images were captured for numerous purposes including training interpretation monitoring and stock images for publication.\tPublic record\n17216\tEXT_DS122465\t2024-05-28T12:17:03.561Z\tDŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water: Reservoir Storage Data held in WISKI 7 Core Hydro and Telemetry (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343635\tData is held in WISKI Hydro Module.\tAlison Hanson - Team Leader for this area of work  Liam Perrott - technical specialist for hydrometry       Data from 2014 in Water Resources Team DMS location.\tReservoir storage data is recorded by Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water. Weekly reading of reservoir storage volume (Million litres) and % storage.\tPublic record\n18780\tNRW_DS123433\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.625Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Powysland English Boundary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343333\tx:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\PowysLand\\IDD Powsland English Boundary.lyr\tLouise Harris, Geospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17498\tNRW_DS100201\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.563Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Scans\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323031\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Scans.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.   Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset of raster images of the hand-coloured Phase 1 Survey 5x5 km square 1:10000 Ordnance Survey maps. The original maps (\u0027master maps\u0027) are lodged in the National Library of Wales.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tPublic record\n17572\tNRW_DS102001\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.421Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Ruppia maritima in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Spatial Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303031\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 551 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Ruppia maritime\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Ruppia maritima is a species of aquatic plant known by the common names beaked tasselweed widgeongrass ditch-grass and tassel pondweed. Ruppia maritima (beaked tassle weed) is found in suitable habitats all around the British coast and grows in soft sediments in sheltered shallow coastal waters from full salinity to nearly fresh water.  The aim of this survey was to return to an area where Ruppia maritima had previously been identified during a Phase 1 survey carried out by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to determine whether or not the species is still present. Ruppia maritima has previously been identified by CCW in 1999 during a Phase 1 survey of the Afon Tywi. It was found at grid reference SN371122 in a pool on a track in the higher saltmarsh in the middle reaches of the river. This area was revisited and the results of the survey can be found in this dataset.\tPublic record\n17592\tNRW_DS108879\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.995Z\tTerrestrial Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitats of Wales (archived 2022)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383739\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Archive\\\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader\tThis dataset is the geographic data for Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats. The purpose of data capture was to show the distribution of BAP priority habitats across Wales and allow statistics to be derived.  Survey data initially collected between 1979 - 1997 however this dataset was compiled in February 2003. Derived from Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey and Other Datasets as required.\tPrivate record\n19121\tNRW_DS125604\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.311Z\tNutrients Review - Predicted Nitrogen and Phosphorus Source Appointments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363034\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nutrient Source Apportionment.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tTotal phosphorus and nitrogen export from t catchments by surface and subsurface flow (kg/yr). Relative contribution of each source to the overall phosphorus and nitrogen load within the transitional surface water lake catchment and coastal catchment water bodies.\tPublic record\n18650\tNRW_DS123292\t2024-05-28T12:53:50.618Z\tReservoir Velocities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323932\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Velocities.lyr\tMatthew O\u0027 Brien, Senior Specialist Advisor, Reservoir Safety Group, National Services - Reservoir Safety\t\tPrivate record\n17609\tNRW_DS107907\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.106Z\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the Rivers Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau (2004-2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037393037\t[A] Associated data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 61, Media 356.  [B] Species data available in database called Recorder 6 under the Freshwater. CCW0002800000008 Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi, Teifi \u0026amp; Cleddau Catchments 2004 - CCW - RoC Report no. 7.\t\tRiver Brook and Sea Lamprey are designated (Annex II) features of the Tywi Teifi and Cleddau River Special Areas of Conservation.  Assessments of species composition distribution abundance and size  of these species in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Tywi Teifi and Cleddau Special Areas of Conservation.  Monitoring of the status of lampreys is required to comply with the reporting requirements of the Habitats Directive.  A total of 112 sampling sites across the River Tywi Teifi Eastern and Western Cleddau were sampled of which 77 have positive records for either River or Brook Lamprey.\tPublic record\n19122\tNRW_DS125602\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.447Z\tNutrient Review - Risk Maps\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363032\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Water Phosphate Risk Map.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Water Nitrate Risk Map.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Phosphate Risk Map.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Nitrate Risk Map.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tSurface water and groundwater risk maps created by combining current and future interpolated nitrate and phosphate surface water and concentrations and the nitrate and phosphate leachate concentrations. Each component was assessed using a scoring and weighting system and the scores were combined to generate a national risk map for nitrate at a scale of 1 km2.\tPublic record\n19059\tNRW_DS125400\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.177Z\tHabitat Maps for designated sites (NNRs \u0026amp; SSSIs) - paper records\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343030\toff-site storage references: fill in once received from JILL Records team\topendata@naturalresources.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThis is a collation of habitat maps and a mixture of copies and originals from 1970s\tPrivate record\n18596\tNRW_DS122455\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.421Z\tForest Management Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343535\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Management Area.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Primary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary: Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThe Management Layer is the actual area of land NRW manages this can be larger than the legal boundary this is because we manage land that is not our own such as extra land or Partnership land.\tPublic record\n18885\tNRW_DS124688\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.530Z\tCaRR 2019 - Addresses at risk\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363838\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2019.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis). Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThis dataset may have been superseded by the dataset know as Communities at Risk Register (Metadata record NRW_DS124687).  A review of the Communities at Risk Register data is due in 2025.\tPublic record\n18241\tNRW_DS116296\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.114Z\tUrban Tree Cover 2006\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323936\tX:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2006\\Urban Tree Point 2006.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2006\\Urban Tree Poly 2006.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2006\\Urban Trees Land Categories 2006.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\Urban Extent.lyr\tCustodian:  John Briggs, Landscape Architect,   PPG Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Used to be Dafydd Fryer, Urban Forest Architect, Knowledge, Strategy and Planning, NRW. No longer works for NRW  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tThis spatial dataset relates to Phase 1 (2006) and consists of 4 individual layers being Points (individual trees) polygons (groups of trees) urban extents (the extent of the study areas) and land use classifications. The technical report (see Related Titles and lineage) shows what is covered in each Phase (page 25) and provides more detail on the methodology behind the different layers in the dataset in Appendix A2.1 (page 132).\tPublic record\n17503\tNRW_DS100124\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.911Z\tGrazing Marsh Phase 1 Habitat Data (Derived from Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313234\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1   derived data from at least 4 datasets in this folder). The grazing site boundary data is no longer available in this folder - contact GIST for further information.   Archived under Project 248, Media 900. This includes both Site Boundaries and Phase 1 Habitat Data.\tJon Rothwell?\t*** This data is no longer accessible  therefore this metadata is under review 02/08/2024 ****  This is a spatial dataset based on the Habitats of Wales Phase 1 data (1979-1997) it shows updated phase 1 polygons for Grazing Marsh and Floodplain Grasslands for Wales extracted from the all Wales Phase 1 digitisation.   This was used to produce an inventory of the priority UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat grazing marshes and flood plains for all Wales see metadata entry NRW_DS99333.\tPrivate record\n17256\tEXT_DS123335\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.237Z\tEnvironment Agency: NRW Alternate Class Area WGS84 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333335\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh Areas\\NRW Alternate Class Area WGS84.lyr\tMarine Monitoring Offcier\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. Saltmarsh areas of uncertainty taken from 2018 aerial imagery. These areas required ground-truthing to determine the species present. In coordinate system WGS84 for ease of use on hand held differential GPS device.  This data is Open but owned by the Environemnt Agency\tPrivate record\n17619\tNRW_DS109860\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.984Z\tSaltmarsh Features of the Braint Estuary 1996\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383630\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media301to400\\Media388 Archived as Project No. 131, media 388\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the results of a National Vegetation Classification as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Braint Estuary. The purpose of this data capture was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map areas of saltmarsh on the Braint Estuary- put forward as a pSAC as part of the Glannau Mon: Cors Heli/ Anglesey Coast saltmarsh.\tPublic record\n17776\tNRW_DS110516\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.984Z\tBat Licence Return Data (1996 - 2009)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353136\tExcel spreadsheet available from Environmental Information Team, CCW.  Paper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, CCW Species data is held in Recorder 6: Bat Licence Returns CCW0008000000005.\tSenior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW\tThis former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) who are now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) dataset is a collation of licence return data for work carried out on bat sites in Wales. The collation was created to add to the picture of bat distributions in Wales to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. The dataset does not represent a systematic coverage/survey of Wales. Location of records is generally random depending on the requirements of the licence holders. Records are provided by licence holders who have very variable recording ability. The dataset has been subject to some basic validation to eradicate erroneous outlying records and inputting errors.\tPublic record\n19065\tNRW_DS125401\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.387Z\tHeathland data for Pembrokeshire (paper records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343031\toff-site archive storage ref: CCW/WRPB/005\topendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThese are the various paper data survey records: 1. 1987/88 Skomer general vegetation and seacliff communities data 2. 1979 Pembrokeshire Heathland survey field notes (species recording cards) 3. 2 files of collated and original information related to all Heathland sites in Pembrokeshire file 1 all sites file 2 Dowrog Common detailed file\tPrivate record\n19055\tNRW_DS125325\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.677Z\tMetal Mine Interventions in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333235\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Metal Mine Interventions in Wales.lyr\tSophia Lennie\tMetal Mine Interventions in Wales records the locations of structures and desk studies completed by the Metal Mine Programme. These structures range from flow gauging station to surface water management to capping works. The data is collected to display where remediation works and desk studies on metal mine sites have been done. It should be noted that the dataset is collected in 2022. As the Metal Mine Programme continues new assets may be created in the future.\tPrivate record\n17622\tNRW_DS109834\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.199Z\tAcross Wales Intertidal Monitoring Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383334\tData is updated every 6 years  [A]  All data is archived under project 199.  [B]  Data for each feature is archived under separate Media numbers (Please refer to individual metadata entries).   [C]  Repeat sampling station locations is available on the UKDMOS website.   [D]  Species data is available through Marine Recorder.\tInternal custodian: Intertidal Survey Team Leader, MFSG, NRW.   Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of results from intertidal monitoring of Special Area of Conservations (SACs) that contain Wales is undertaken. Work is undertaken to enable reporting on a range of external obligations under Habitats Directive Water Framework Directive and the inter-agency agreement on Common Standards Monitoring; to harmonise with WFD monitoring in coastal and transitional waters. The work will also be used to inform management decisions on individual sites. Each sample station will be monitored for one of the following SAC sub-features;  1) Under-boulders  2) Sabellaria reef  (created by the honeycomb worm) 3) Rockpools  4) Characterizing rock or sediment habitat   5) Zostera beds  (seagrass meadows) 6) Nationally rare or scarce species. Work has been carried out from 05/07/2004 and is on-going.  The majority of sample stations are visited every 6 years although some sites where there are particular pressures are visited annually for example reef features in Pembrokeshire Marine SAC and Zostera beds at Traeth Lafan in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Confidence in certain methods and their data is high whilst the objectives of some surveys are to develop confidence in the method and data.\tPublic record\n17775\tNRW_DS110518\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.614Z\tOtter Licence Return Data (1905 - 2008).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353138\tExcel spreadsheet available from Environmental Information Team, CCW. Paper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, CCW.  Species data held in Recorder 6: Otter Licence Returns CCW0008000000007\tInternal Custodian, Senior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a former Countryside Council for Wales collation of licence return data for work carried out affecting otters in Wales.The collation was created to add to the picture of otter distributions in Wales to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.\tPublic record\n18824\tNRW_DS123507\t2024-05-28T12:56:22.139Z\tNRW Flood Risk Management (FRM) Operational Area Boundaries, post Organisational Design, July 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353037\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW FRM Operational Areas.lyr\tData custodian; Gary Eccleston, Knowledge \u0026amp; Information Management, NRW Metadata point of contact; Spatial team, NRW Business contact ; Dave Tarrant, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tMap layer giving the boundaries of the post OD FRM Operational Areas.  In MyMap this is layer at Operational Layers/Resource Management Terrestrial/NRW FRM Operational Areas It is the post OD (Organisational Design) catchment boundaries for the Flood and Water Management teams (North and South Wales). Will be used to ensure enquiries incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tPublic record\n18843\tNRW_DS124663\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.053Z\tRemaining Landfill Void in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363633\tOriginal data is held in Documents and collated into CRM (PALS) and provided as an csv export.  Extraction is undertaken annually and placed in Citrix ShareFile.\tJohn Fry Lead Specialist Advisor, Waste Policy\tPermitted landfill operators have a condition in their permits to report the remaining landfill void (capacity) of their sites at the end of the calendar year. This information although used for compliance purposes is also used by the EC Government Local Authorities and other interested parties for statutory reporting and waste planning purposes. Data is provided in cubic metres and collated into a national dataset.  There are only around 20 operational landfills in Wales. Operators can claim commercial confidentiality for their data at time of submission. To date no operator has claimed commercial confidentiality.\tPublic record\n17287\tEXT_DS124284\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.824Z\tLocal Authorities: Wrexham Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323834\tarchived from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Wrexham Wildlife Sites.lyr , retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact: Geospatial team\tWrexham Wildlife Sites 3rd party data and property of local authority. Archived 2019 the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\tPublic record\n17670\tNRW_DS109993\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.547Z\tSurvey of Lakes in the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt Proposed Site of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSI) Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Area (SPA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393933\tArchived as Project No. 30, Media 176 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Ffynnon,  Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Head Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt Proposed Site of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSI) and Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is situated in North Wales in the Snowdonia National Park. The pSSSI/SAC boundary marks the limit of a large upland block located along the eastern fringe of the National Park. The primary reasons for the site qualifying for SAC status are the large areas of blanket bog a priority habitat under the Habitats Directive and the upland European dry heath.  Other qualifying features include two Annex I habitat lake types: (1) oligotrophic to mesotrophic standing waters with vegetation of the Littorelletea uniflorae and/or of the Isoeto-Nanojuncetea and (2) natural dystrophic lakes and ponds. The limnological surveys (surveys of inland water) focus on the collation of site and catchment data and the collection of new data on lake bathymetry water quality and macrophyte assemblages. A particular focus is an assessment of the current acidification and conservation status of the four lake sites.  A preliminary assessment of whether the lakes are in favourable condition as defined in the Habitats Directive is also outlined. The primary aim of this project was to survey four lakes in the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt pSSSI/SAC in order to build up CCWs datasets on Welsh lakes.  The information collected is required for conservation site assessments ecological quality judgements for site condition reporting contributions to site management plans and assistance with casework issues.\tPublic record\n19072\tNRW_DS125389\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.090Z\tHolyhead Marina Ultrasound Trial Footage 2020-2021\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333839\tZ Drive storage requested, data currently on a memory card.\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor – Marine Ecology, NRW\tVideo (MP4) taken by a Natural Resources Wales owned Underwater Remotely Operated Vehicle (Chasing Innovation M2) at Holyhead marina. This was part of the baseline survey and monitoring of effectiveness of the ultrasound trial attempted in Holyhead marina to eradicate Didemnum vexillum. Video stills (JPG) were captured from the footage.   The data was collected between October 2020 and January 2021.\tPublic record\n18610\tNRW_DS122717\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.802Z\tRecreation Places to Go Promoted Routes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373137\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Recreation Places to Go Promoted Routes.lyr\tHeather Crump, Data and Information Advisor (Recreation), Estate Recreation Planning Team\tVectors (lines) depicting waymarked/promoted recreation routes managed or co-managed by NRW. The ‘ROUTE_DESCRIPTION’ field should be queried in order to delineate the date a route was last updated/confirmed and by whom. Information includes route name length direction grade type and the NNR or forest the route is listed against. Dataset is a resource for promotional material and visualisation rather than management. Management data associated with recreation routes is stored separately in the Forester database.\tPrivate record\n18878\tNRW_DS124733\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.522Z\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Coppice Mawr, Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373333\tData to be entered into Recorder 6 (Survey ID to be added)\tMike Howe\t\tPrivate record\n17194\tEXT_DS121867\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.084Z\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Rivers (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383637\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\CEH Rivers 250K.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\CEH Rivers 50k Shreve .lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\CEH Rivers 50k Strahler.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\CEH Rivers 50K.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\CEH Rivers.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH) is a research organisation focusing on land and freshwater ecosystems and their interaction with the atmosphere. This is a UKCEH owned dataset containing the extent and location of rivers in Wales. This dataset has been licenced to Natural Resources Wales for use.\tPublic record\n18680\tNRW_DS123327\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.448Z\tWildlife and Countryside Act Licences\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333237\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\WCA Licences.lyr\tIwan G Hughes - Team Leader, Species and Carbon Permitting\tGeospatial Layer containing Wild Bird Control Licences including the species location purpose of the licence and the licence dates.\tPrivate record\n17299\tEXT_DS124587\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.347Z\tNational Oceanography Centre (NOC): Class A tide gauge data (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353837\tData held in WISKI- Hydro module\tCustodian: Liam Perrot / Andy Wall\tNational Oceanography Centre (NOC) Class A tide gauge data\tPublic record\n19087\tNRW_DS125508\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.634Z\tMorfa Friog Monitoring Data 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353038\tZ:\\Archive\\Flood\\SPI\\NHCP\\Morfa Friog\\ \\MorfaFriog_Habitats_v2\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Heather Lewis, Lead Specialist Advisor: Coastal Habitats, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team\tMorfa Friog managed coastal realignment site situated on the Mawddach estuary was created when flood embankment was breached in November 2015. A methodology was established and baseline data collected during autumn 2018 to monitor the development of the saltmarsh vegetation on the site.\tPrivate record\n18070\tNRW_DS115409\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.673Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rocky Reefs in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007-2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135343039\tProject 199, media 1436 Species data is held in Marine Recorder: 2007 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW1630000000C 2008 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW16300000013 2009 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW16300000020 2010 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW1630000002D  The file path for the report: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\ No 101 - Pembrokeshire Marine 2007-2010 bedrock  The file path for the data and analyses: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2010 ASML Pembs\\Data H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Analyses\\Pemb bedrock 2005-\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  In Pembrokeshire Marine SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features include Reefs. The specific area sub-feature of interest covered here are intertidal rocky reefs at six sites in Milford Haven together with one site at Nolton Haven in St Bride’s Bay and another site at Peny-Holt on the Castlemartin peninsula. The purpose of this data capture was to undertake quantitative monitor of the biota colonising sloping emersed bedrock and colonising rockpools.\tPublic record\n19038\tNRW_DS125356\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.082Z\tGwent Meadows Survey 1987\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333536\toff-site storage ref: CCW/SERBC/209\t\tPaper data shetts of Gwent Meadows Survey 1987\tPrivate record\n19092\tNRW_DS125506\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.855Z\tLichen Survey of Dinefwr NNR (VC 44 Carmarthenshire): including re-monitoring the 2013 baseline and comments on air pollution and Ash Dieback.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353036\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDinefwr%20Lichen%20Surveys\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tSurvey and accompanying spreadsheet covering monitoring of the epiphytic lichens of Dinefwr Estate SSSI in Carmarthenshire including assessment impacts of Ash Dieback and ammonia pollution\tPublic record\n17607\tNRW_DS107795\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.107Z\tFreshwater Bullhead Survey Data in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373935\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Data is stored in Recorder 6:  - Bullhead Monitoring, Wye \u0026amp; Usk Catchments 2007 - CCW- Report no. 818 CCW000280000000B  - Bullhead Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2001-2004- CCW - RoC Report no. 19 CCW0002800000006\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tBullhead is listed under Annex II of the Habitat and Species Directive as a species of community interest and is a feature on the River Wye Afon Gwy and River Usk Afon Wysg Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales. Bullhead numbers have been declining across Europe in recent decades as a result of human activities. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor bullhead populations to assess whether SAC management adequately protects that species.\tPublic record\n17575\tNRW_DS102006\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.504Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Ophelia bicornis in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303036\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199,Media No 553 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Fynnon, on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Fynnon. H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Ophelia bicornis\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThe purpose of this data capture was to determine whether Ophelia bicornis found at two sites within the lower Loughor estuary during Phase 1 surveys carried out by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) were still present.  The polychaete Ophelia bicornis is known from only a few locations in the UK and is therefore of national importance. It is particularly vulnerable to changes in the granulometric composition of the sediment pollution and trampling. The purpose of this data capture to determine whether O. bicornis found at two sites within the lower Loughor estuary during Phase 1 surveys carried out by CCW were still present.\tPublic record\n17701\tNRW_DS110037\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.591Z\tBroadscale Remote Survey and Mapping of Sublittoral Habitats and Biota of Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire and South Cardigan Bay\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303337\t[A]  Data is Archived as Project 141, Media 77, (related Media numbers include 79, 505 and 506) [B]  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elyfn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tData Custodians - Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group. Metadata Point of Contact - Data Services\tThe Broadscale Mapping Project has been devised and funded by a consortium consisting of the Crown Estate the Countryside Council for Wales English Nature Scottish Natural Heritage and the SeaMap research group based at Newcastle University and undertaken by SeaMap. Three trial areas were selected around Milford Haven Pembrokeshire and South Cardigan Bay for developing and testing the methodology although experience from many other surveys conducted by SeaMap have also contributed to the development of the methodology. The aim of this data collection was to map the seafloor sediments and biota for habitats and biotope mapping. This was supplemented by maps and supporting data showing the location of ground truth samples and the positions of the acoustic tracks.\tPublic record\n17808\tNRW_DS110954\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.927Z\tLowland Grassland Areas of Search Re-visit Files\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393534\tStored on the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group. H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV\\PHASE II PHOTO AND IMAGE ARCHIVE\\Areas of Search\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tThe phase 2 lowland grassland survey of Wales came to an end in 2004; since then various lowland grassland sites within the 12 Welsh Areas of Search have been re-visited on an ad hoc basis. This dataset contains the notes from the re-visits including details of any ecological changes to have taken place since the original survey. The purpose of which was to provide a record of ecological change. Some of the revisit file notes also include information on management and ownership history records of uncommon species present and redrafted vegetation maps. The dataset includes the photographs taken during the re-visits.\tPublic record\n18964\tNRW_DS124863\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.208Z\tNRW People and Places teams\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383633\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW People and Places teams.lyr\tMike Thomson, Specialist Advisor, Incident ManagementIncident and Emergencies, Flood and Operational Risk Management\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales People and Places team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for People and Places Teams post Organisation Design July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tPrivate record\n18733\tNRW_DS123354\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.116Z\tMonitor Sites Eels\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333534\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\Monitor Sites Eels.lyr\tVictoria Bowmaker\t\tPrivate record\n19060\tNRW_DS125399\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.234Z\tSpecies \u0026amp; Habitats survey data by grid square - paper data records\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333939\toff-site archive storage ref: CCW/GRID/SH/1 CCW/GRID/SH/2 CCW/GRID/SJ/1 CCW/GRID/SJ/2 CCW/GRID/SN/1 CCW/GRID/SN/2 CCW/GRID/SN/3 CCW/GRID/SN/4 CCW/GRID/SN/5 CCW/GRID/SN/6 CCW/WRAB/027- is also SN CCC/ tbc by Jill- is also SN CCW/GRID/SO/1 CCW/GRID/SO/2 CCW/GRID/SO/3 CCW/GRID/SS/1 CCW/GRID/ST/1\tData Services Team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThis is a collation of mixed habitat \u0026amp; species data (Heath/Mires Grasslands  Mixed Habitats  Heath/Upland Wetlands and Open Water  Bird Bats Invertebrates lower plants Woodlands etc.) for the following grid squares: SH SJ SN SSST SO\tPrivate record\n19057\tNRW_DS125432\t2024-05-28T13:01:42.910Z\tDynamic Dunescapes National Vegetation Classification Survey Date\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343332\tPlanned for X:Drive with suggested filepath but needs confirming  X:\\Biological\\Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Mospoly.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Photographs.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Polygons.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Quadrats.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Target Notes.lyr\tJulie Creer - Lead Specialist Advisor: Coastal Habitats\tData from a National Vegetation Classification survey of 10 sand dune sites including shapefiles of NVC polygons vegetation mosaics target notes quadrats photograph locations and overall survey boundary. Data collected in 2020 and 2021 based on field survey of entire sites with field maps digitised to OS Mastermap and aerial photographs.\tPublic record\n19067\tNRW_DS125392\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.645Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Fixed Point Botanical Monitoring Survey Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333932\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Fixed Point Quadrat Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Fixed Point Quadrat Centres.lyr\tToni Henwood\tData from the fixed point quadrats to monitoring the effects of SoLIFE project actions\tPrivate record\n18116\tNRW_DS115839\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.464Z\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay; Caernarfon Bay and Cardigan Bay (2012 - 2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383339\tArchived as Project 439 Media 1479 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1479)   [C] Marine Recorder data to be entered (data  received 2014-09-31 - to be merged with the main database) MRCCW31700000015 - 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Liverpool Bay, August. MRCCW31700000016 - 2012 DRAFT Fisher Survey Towed Survey in Cardigan Bay, October. MRCCW31700000017 - 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Cardigan Bay, July. MRCCW31700000018 - 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Caernarfon Bay, July. MRCCW31700000019 - 2012 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay, October.\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Data Custodian: School of Ocean Science - Bangor University\tIn 2012 and 2013 video tows and photographic surveys were undertaken to ground truth the seabed around Cardigan Bay Caernarfon Bay and Liverpool Bay. The Bangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) surveyed five sites two using fishing vessels and three using the Bangor University Research Vessel (RV) Prince Madog. In total sixty video tows were undertaken within circalittoral habitats; six of these had associated photographic stills of which there were 570.  The purpose of this data capture was to ground truth areas of the seabed around Cardigan Bay Caernarfon Bay and Liverpool Bay.  Confidence in this dataset is high as data was collected by scientists. Imagery was analysed using standard methods by competent and experienced surveyors.\tPublic record\n18605\tNRW_DS122565\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.743Z\tDune Rejuvenation Trials - Newboorough Warren \u0026amp; Dunes NNR, Kenfig Dunes NNR, Merthyr Mawr Dunes NNR - Assessment 2016\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353635\tArchived as Project 481, Media 1558 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media1501to1600\\Media1558)\tInternal Custodian: Emmer Litt, Specialist Advisor - Marine and Coastal Physical Processes, All Wales Advice Team       This entry is related to metadata 114691, 115843, 115776, 115840\tTrial dune rejuvenation works have been undertaken at Kenfig Merthyr Mawr and Newborough during the winters of 2011-12 to 2014-15 with a hiatus in 2013-14 when work was undertaken only at Merthyr Mawr Warren. The data related to a geomorphological assessment of the effectiveness of these rejuvenation works which will inform the planning of further works and includes UAV data GPS data LIDAR DEMs and GIS outputs.  The accompanying report provides a summary of changes in the extent of bare sand and mobile dunes at each site discusses the factors which have resulted in differences in the impact between and within sites and comments on the relative merits of the methods which have been used to monitor geomorphological change. Proposals are also made for further intervention works at each of the sites.\tPublic record\n17454\tNRW_DS99603\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.351Z\tNatural Resources Wales Easements Over Third-Party Land\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363033\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land current line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land current poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land proposed line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land proposed poly.lyr\t\tThis is a spatial dataset showing the easements for NRW over third parties land used for Access and pipelines. Data are both completed and proposed. Includes information on site name owner scale captured expiry date unique codes. This is to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and to provide a complete table showing all proposed and completed easements across third party land in Wales.\tPublic record\n17196\tEXT_DS121891\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.233Z\tNatural England: Designated Sites (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383931\tX:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE Areas Outstanding Natural Beauty.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE Country Parks.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE Designations.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE Heritage Coast.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE Local Nature Reserves.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE National Nature Reserves.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE National Parks.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE National Trails.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE Ramsar.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE Sites Special Scientific Interest.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE Special Areas Conservation.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Natural England\\NE Special Protection Areas.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural England designate manage and monitoring a number of protected sites throughout England. This is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of these sites. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this data under an Open Government Licence.\tPublic record\n17807\tNRW_DS110956\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.765Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Species Datasets\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393536\tThis dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  Biological Species Data is also held in Recorder 6. LGSW Brecknock CCW0002600000009    LGSW Carmarthen \u0026amp; Dinefwr CCW0002600000008    LGSW Ceredigion CCW0002600000005    LGSW Clwyd CCW0002600000003    LGSW East Gwynedd CCW0002600000002    LGSW Gwent CCW0002600000012    LGSW Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan CCW0002600000011    LGSW Montgomery CCW0002600000004    LGSW Preseli \u0026amp; South Pembroke CCW0002600000007    LGSW Radnor CCW0002600000006    LGSW West Glamorgan \u0026amp; Llanelli CCW0002600000010    LGSW West Gwynedd CCW0002600000001\tCustodians: Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.  Data Manager, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW. Excel spreadsheets inputted into Recorder 6 by contractors (Charles Copp and Mike Weideli).  The restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group.    Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tA detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. Community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). At each site homogeneous stands of vegetation were identified sampled with 2 x 2 m quadrats and mapped onto Ordnance Survey base maps or aerial photographs at 1:5000 scale.  This dataset has all Wales coverage with sites varying in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha. The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and with Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and is now managed by Natural Resources Wales. This dataset contains all the floristic quadrat data and site information from the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales.  The dataset has been extensively validated and verified.  This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tPublic record\n17565\tNRW_DS102205\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.773Z\tAssessment of the Nature Conservation Value of Mussel Crumble in the Burry Inlet and the Effects of Dredging/Hand Gathering Operations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323035\t[A] Report available from NRW library (Maes Y Ffynnon) and in Ffynnon.  [B] Data is available in Marine Recorder (survey number MRCCW30000000032) [C] Archived in project 307, media1222 (K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe aim of this data capture was to identify and quantify the epifauna and infauna of the mussel crumble beds quantify the impacts of a simulated \u0027dredging\u0027 operation on the fauna of the beds and assess the stability of the beds in relation to dredging activities. Mussel Crumble are beds of mussels barnacles mussel and cockle shells bound together by the mussel byssus thread. This dataset includes sediment particle size data macrobenthic infaunal data tables raw data matrices for the epifaunal quadrats. The purpose of this data capture was to identify and quantify the epifauna and infauna of the mussel crumble beds quantify the impacts of a simulated \u0027dredging\u0027 operation on the fauna of the beds and assess the stability of the beds in relation to dredging activities.\tPublic record\n18093\tNRW_DS115608\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.174Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Petalwort Petalophyllum ralfsii at Y Twyni o Abermenai i Aberffraw cSAC (2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363038\tAssociated data is held on the Z Drive at: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\12 Petallophyllum ralfsii at Tywyni o Abermenai i Aberffraw\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tPetalwort Petalophyllum ralfsii is a pale green thalloid liverwort with erect lamellae on its upper surface that grows in open damp calcareous dune slacks often on low hummocks rather than on the very wet ground on compacted sandy/muddy bryophyte-rich turf.  The inclusion of the Petallophyllum ralfsii on Annex II of the EU Habitats and Species Directive requires NRW to identify Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) for the species and to ensure that Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) applies. Whilst some background work has enabled the identification of  key areas for Petallophyllum ralfsii on the candidate SAC further information on its current extent distribution and habitat associates are required. The purpose of this data capture was to map the distribution density and habitat characteristics of Petalophyllum ralfsii at Twyni o Abermenai i Aberffraw cSAC.  This will enable the targets within the conservation objective to be confirmed and to report on the condition of the Petalophyllum on the site.\tPublic record\n18602\tNRW_DS122763\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.414Z\tNDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through woodland creation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373633\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Opportunities to develop multiple benefits through woodland creation.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset shows how many ecosystem service benefits a woodland could generate if plated at any given location. In some areas a single woodland might generate four or more ecosystem services making the site a prime opportunity area. It is important to consider that in some cases a woodland that is only an opportunity for one or two ecosystem service can be very beneficial if the demand for that service is very high at the site of interest. Services considered in this dataset are: opportunities that would enhance the woodland ecological network natural flood management water quality provide recreation benefits or carbon sequestration.  This datasets gives an overview of how the opportunities for woodland planting are distributed nationally which aids in achieving Wales\u0027 planting target of 10000ha by 2020. Only opportunitites are shown that if realised would generate large (over 500ha) or moderately sized (over 5ha) woodlands based on the large/medium/small woodland map produced under GIS for Area Statements.\tPrivate record\n19156\tNRW_DS125730\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.444Z\tNational Peatland Action Programme (NPAP) Funded Peatland Restoration Activities 2020-2023 – GIS Dataset\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373330\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP Restoration Activities 20-23.lyr\tAlice Whittle - NPAPA Data and GIS Officer\tThe total activities footprint for NPAP funded restoration work from programme inception in 2020 to 2023.   This layer covers both NPAP direct delivery restoration work and restoration delivered by partners across several NPAP grant streams: Strategically Allocated Funds (SAF) Section 16 Land Agreements and Competitive Grants.  The activities footprint is formatted as a shapefile layer comprised of polygons representing restoration activities across Wales  Restoration Activity Footprint  This dataset shows the total area of peatland restoration across Wales up to present day. The data is presented as a polygon shapefile with the unit of measurement being hectares.  Restoration Activities  This dataset shows the total area measured in hectares of peatland restoration that carried out across Wales up to the present date. The data is presented as a polygon shapefile that captures restoration activities. Restoration activities have been divided into five broad groups: Hydrological Management Erosion Control Tree Management Vegetation Management and Grazing.  Hydrological Management aims to reverse the impact of harmful activities that have resulted in adverse changes to hydrological regimes.  Erosion Control aims to reduce the effect of erosional features such as haggs and gullies. These features expose areas of bare peat which are prone to further degradation if left uncontrolled.  Tree Management aims to reduce the impact of trees on peatland. Most Tree Management techniques for peatland restoration involve conifer plantation there are also activities addressing the presence of broadleaf trees.   Vegetation Management aims to control the dominance of small numbers of abundant species (e.g. Molinia Calluna) to ensure the recovery and persistence of mire vegetation.  Grazing activities aim to restore and manage peatlands through modifying grazing regimes.  The data is broken down into polygons with columns detailing the broad activity types sites delivery organisation start and end dates of restoration activity and the financial year it occurred in.  Further information on the structure of the dataset can be found in the\tPublic record\n19052\tNRW_DS125313\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.643Z\tWales River Restoration Plans\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333133\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Fisheries Activity Lines.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Fisheries Activity Points.r X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Line.lyr\tRhodri Powell - Senior Officer, People \u0026amp; Places\tCollection of river restoration plans presented in geodatabases identifying opportunities for river restoration within applicable waterbody. Individual river restoration plans commissioned by Natural Resources Wales delivered by external consultant  Plans are made for the following rivers:  Rhymney Tywi Roath Brook Pembroke River Pelenna Nant Y Fendrod Nant Glandulas Ely/ Nant Dowlais Dulas Cynon Clywedog Afonydd Cleddau Afon Wen\tPublic record\n18996\tNRW_DS124925\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.073Z\tOperational Small Scale Hydro Projects\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393235\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Small Scale Hydro Projects.lyr\tDave Farmery, Senior Officer, Energy Delivery Manager, NRW Enid Carrick, Project Support Officer, NRW\tThis shapefile relates to Small Scale Hydro Projects that are sited on Natural Resources Wales managed estate and facilitated by the Energy Delivery Team Data received from Project Manager and supporting officers during construction phase\tPrivate record\n17755\tNRW_DS110320\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.370Z\tAnalysis of seabird blood samples following the Sea Empress Oil Spill.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333230\tArchived as project no 123 (media no 364) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media364\tInternal Contacts: Matty Murphy, Marine an Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe results of a study that used haematological techniques to investigate the subtle sub-lethal effects of oil on seabirds (razorbills guillemots and shags) following the Sea Empress oil spill. Data includes summary statistics for the measured blood parameters.  The purpose of this data capture was to investigate subtle sub-lethal effects of oil on seabirds. Blood samples were collected from birds at two sites in Wales- Skomer Island and Middleholm Pembrokeshire and one site in Scotland- Isle of May Firth of Forth.\tPublic record\n17720\tNRW_DS110126\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.536Z\tStudies and Biometric Gut Content Analyses of Common Scoter Melanitta nigra nigra Killed During the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313236\tDraft Reports are held as archive Project No. 73, media 68 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media068).    Final Reports are held in the DMS\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Ornothologist (Matty Murphy?) or NRW. Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW (Mike Camplin or Lucie Haines?).  Work was originally commissioned by Bill Sanderson. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay is the most important wintering site for Common Scoters in the UK. It is estimated that the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996 resulted in the deaths of at least 4500 Common Scoter. This dataset contains the results of a study to describe the population structure body condition level of oiling biometrics moult diet and cause of death of Common Scoters killed by the Sea Empress oil spill and to investigate factors affecting body condition diet and cause of death. Data captured in order to describe the biometrics and moult of Common Scoters killed in the Sea Empress oil spill to determine the age structure of killed birds and to determine the diet of birds through gut contents analysis.  All data is held within the reports.\tPublic record\n18145\tNRW_DS115625\t2024-05-28T12:40:37.374Z\tGrey Seal Census for North Wales 2001\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363235\tArchived as Project 417, Media 1484.\tTom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist, Natural Resources Planning/Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group, NRW\tThe grey seal Halichoerus grypus is relatively uncommon globally with estimates of 300000 individuals across the north Atlantic in 2000. In the British Isles the grey seal is more common that other species of seal breeding. This dataset contains the results of a full census of grey seal pup production in North Wales for the 2001 season. The aim of this project was to undertake an appropriate level of survey of grey seals in North Wales from Aberystwyth to the Dee Estuary in order to lead to a clearer understanding of the abundance and distribution of grey seals and pup production and thereby to guide management. The objectives were to: - Locate and document all major sites where grey seal haul out for moulting and breeding  - Confirm the timing and extent of the breeding season - Locate and document all breeding sites in North Wales - Count the number of pups at each of these sites at regular intervals throughout that breeding season in 2001 in order to estimate pup production - Continue the daily pupping census of Bardsey Island - Take photographs of profiles to identify pelage markings of individuals for possible computer-aided matching in the future - Identify areas where seals are subject to disturbance by humans - Assess the quality of breeding haul-out environment - Advise on appropriate levels of monitoring / surveillance.\tPublic record\n18494\tNRW_DS121361\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.263Z\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Wider Catchment Woodland Potential\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333631\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Wider Catchment Woodland Potential.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Wider Catchment Woodland Potential is our best estimate of locations where there are slowly permeable soils where scrub and tree planting may be most effective to increase infiltration and hydrological losses. The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas not already wooded.\tPublic record\n19140\tNRW_DS125526\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.377Z\tThe Distribution of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior on Whiteford Burrows \u0026amp; Cwm Ivy Marsh - September 2021\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353236\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID) in Recorder 6\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tWhiteford Burrows is a dune system lying on the north-west coast of the Gower Peninsula and is part of the much larger Carmarthen Bay Dunes/Twyni Bae Caerfyrddin SAC. The Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior listed on the EC Habitats \u0026amp; Species Directive Annex IIa was first found on Whiteford Burrows in May 1983 - a new live record for the species not only in Wales but also in western Britain. Previous V. angustior studies have been carried out at this location between 1983 and 2018. It was suggested that further surveys be undertaken at the far north-east of the transition zone throughout the main body of the dune system (focussing upon areas in damp hollows and on the margins of dune slacks where the water table is near the surface) and with a survey of the Cwm Ivy Marsh marine/terrestrial transition zone recently formed with the area now subject to tidal inflow.\tPublic record\n17959\tNRW_DS114459\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.627Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera marina) Beds in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343539\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 199, Media No 1228. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW, Maes y Fynnon\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas such as lagoons and channels which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina) is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Data is captured to support reporting on conservation status of the Pen Llyn air Sarnau SAC.\tPublic record\n17563\tNRW_DS102126\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.459Z\tSquirrel Survey of Cwm-Mynach (2001 - 2002)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313236\t[A]  Species data held in Recorder 6 Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Cwm Mynach, Report 540 CCW0003800000008\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tPresence or absence or red and grey squirrels in Cwm-Mynach woods in the Snowdonia National Park.  Also a records of tree species from which the records were taken.  Red squirrels were reported in the Cwm-Mynach woods in 1998 and 1999 and the work was commissioned to establish whether there is a continued presence of red squirrels in this area. Survey took place on conifer plantations to the east of Mynydd Cwm-mynach within the Snowdonia National Park between November 2001 and January 2002. Squirrel activity was low at the time of survey which may account for the presence of no red squirrels.  Further surveys at a different time of year could be considered.  Records where a positive identification could not be made have been tagged as unidentified.  In some cases the hair was only identified to family level.\tPublic record\n17543\tNRW_DS101993\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.018Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Underboulder Communities in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004/2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393933\tThe report and data collected is archived as Project No 199, Media No 550 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  A specific feature of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC are the underboulder communities which are established in the extensive boulder areas within the SAC.  Rich underboulder communities have been recorded and mapped on the rocky intertidal areas of the Menai Strait and an area west of Llandudno.  Underboulder communities are also present where there are tide-swept communities that happen to lie on boulder habitats throughout the Menai Strait.  Sponge dominated communities have been recorded from boulders and cobbles that are nationally uncommon hosting a rich faunal turf on tide-swept substrata. The associated report combines the surveys and findings undertaking during 2004 (Boyes and Allen 2004) and 2005 (Mazik et al. 2005). The report combines both datasets and additional analaysis has been completed using both sets of data.  The purpose of this data capture was to  - Provide a reference condition on species present at a number of sites within the SAC - Evaluate the variability between boulders and between sites and attempt to correlate the variability with physical factors  - Identify the best methods for measuring the condition of the underboulder features in the SAC in the future and to evaluate the possible methods by which the impact of boulder turning can be assessed and monitored.\tPublic record\n18723\tNRW_DS123353\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.903Z\tMonitor Sites Coarse\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333533\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\Monitor Sites Coarse.lyr\tVictoria Bowmaker\t\tPrivate record\n17325\tEXT_DS125289\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.496Z\tEMFF Protecting Our Seas Project (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323839\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\EMFF Protecting our Seas project Envision contract outputs\tNatasha Lough\tIn 2020-21 seabed surveys were carried out by ENVISION for the Welsh Fishermen’s Association - Cymdeithas Pysgotwyr Cymru (WFA-CPC) in two Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) where interaction between mobile fishing gear and conservation features have been highlighted as a potential issue.   The study supported ongoing work by the Welsh Government and Natural Resources Wales to evaluate interactions between fishing activities and features of Welsh Marine Protected Areas (MPAs).  Surveys were completed in the Severn Estuary/Môr Hafren SAC to explore the location of Sabellaria alveolata reefs and in Cardigan Bay/Bae Ceredigion SAC to establish the extent and ‘reefiness’ of cobble/boulder habitats.  The data products for the Severn Estuary include the processed geophysical data processed ARIS (Sonar Camera) data data logs GIS files and reports. The data products for Cardigan Bay include processed video data data logs GIS files and reports.\tPublic record\n17091\tEXT_DS112468\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.963Z\tMarine Protected Areas (MPAs) Biophysical Data Layer Project (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343638\tArchived as Project 271, Media 983 and 1000-1013 (File paths - K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 983,  K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 1000, K:\\Archive\\Media 1001to1100\\Media 1001 etc etc.)  In addition to Related Titles: DCT-10-184068 Distribution of highly mobile species 2010 DCT-12-049886 Spawning and nursery grounds of selected fish species in UK waters 2012. DCT-10-075940 Oceanic thermal fronts from Earth observation data - a potential surrogate for pelagic diversity Summary Document 2010 DCT-10-184095 Development of marine diversity data layer: review of approaches and proposed methods 2009 DCT-10-077540 Residual current flow datalayer: Description of approach 2010 DCT-10-077274 Development of a Benthic Productivity Datalayer: Assessing the available approaches 2009 DCT-10-078178 Benthic productivity data layer development-North Sea Pilot Study 2010\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team (Formerly James Dargie who now works at NatureScot)        See Also: Report No 6, Task 1A. Report No 13, Task 1B. Report No 8, Task 2A. Report No 14, Task 2B. Report No17: Task 2D. Report No 24, Task 2i. Report No 22, Task 3.\tMarine Protected Areas (MPAs) are a clearly defined geographical space recognised dedicated and managed through legal or other effective means to achieve the long-term conservation of nature with associated ecosystem services and cultural values. This network of MPAs will help meet commitments under the Convention on Biological Diversity and contribute to the measures needed to achieve Good Environmental Status across Europe’s seas by 2020 under the EU Marine Strategy Framework Directive. The United Kingdom (UK) is committed to the establishment of a network of  MPAs to conserve marine ecosystems and marine biodiversity. Selection of MPAs should be based on best available biological physical oceanographic and socio-economic data. Defra and partners have commissioned a consortium lead by ABPmer Ltd and partners to take forward a package of work to provide this data. This dataset contains the project outcomes. The purpose of this data capture was to collect the data that will be required for designating new MPAs following the Marine Bill. The overall aim is to ensure that the best available information is available for the selection of MPAs in UK waters and that the data layers can be easily accessed and utilised by those whom will have responsibility for selecting sites. The data layers will also be of use in taking forward marine planning in UK waters. The project has been divided into a number of discrete tasks each of which has a specific temporal coverage (see separate metadata entries which will be added as and when tasks are completed). Data used is loosely taken between the following dates 1982-2008.\tPublic record\n18073\tNRW_DS115334\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.743Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Bedrock at Porth Oer in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333334\tData is archived as project 410, media 1430 and forms part of archive Project No 199. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2012 ASML PLAS\tPaul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones. Of these Estuaries and their constituent mudflats sandflats and reefs large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC.  Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC (CCW 2009).\tPublic record\n18232\tNRW_DS116237\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.529Z\tDetailed River Network\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323337\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Detailed River Network.lyr   DO NOT SET TO EXPORT or publish this data - As this data is old and has been superseded by an OS product (see metadata details)\tKeith Jones Spatial Service Team       DO NOT SET TO EXPORT - As this data is old and has been superseded by an OS product (see metadata details)\tThe Detailed River Network (DRN) is a large-scale accurate and fully attributed digital river centreline covering Wales. The DRN is captured from the water features theme of the OS MasterMap topographic layer and built into a network using automated rules. Other input datasets and extensive local staff knowledge has been used to augment the core geometry to incorporate critical spatial detail and attribution such as flow direction and path not available from the OS mapping and to verify the accuracy of the centreline itself. The dataset has full-feature network geometry cross-referenced with OS MasterMap following Digital National Framework principles.\tPublic record\n18071\tNRW_DS115304\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.696Z\tHarvest Mice (Micromys minutus) in Wales - Where Might They Be?\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333034\tAssociated data is held at; NRW corporate GIS (M:) drive (File path Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\ )  NB This data location is too imprecise. More detail is required.\tMammal Ecologist, NRW\tThe Harvest mouse (Micromys minutus) Britain’s smallest rodent is sparsely distributed in Wales. The harvest mouse is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan priority species and is also on the list of Species of Principal Importance in Wales (Section 42 Natural Environment and Rural Communities Act 2006). Thus the species is afforded a high conservation status yet very little is known about its true distribution across the UK and especially Wales.  This dataset is a collation of records obtained from a report written to question \u0027Harvest mice in Wales - where might they be?\u0027. The work carried out and demonstrated in the associated report (see related titles) was motivated by the lack of data for the harvest mouse in Wales in an attempt to try and understand whether the species was as sparsely spread as the data would suggest. The results from analysis of the data would act to target harvest mouse survey to areas most likely to discover new populations and confirm existing ones. This dataset is made up of harvest mouse data obtained from the Welsh Local Records Centres comprising 139 records dating back to 1895 with fewer than 30 records since 2000. Records are scattered across lowland areas in north west and south Wales forming obvious clusters in areas such as North Pembrokeshire Bridgend and Eastern Glamorgan. The purpose of this data capture was to try gain an understanding of the distribution of harvest mouse around Wales. Various data were analysed alongside the harvest mouse records to highlight areas where potential harvest mouse surveys could be targeted. Maps were produced to highlight sites with the highest habitat ranking around the most recent harvest mouse records.\tPublic record\n18976\tNRW_DS125183\t2024-05-28T12:59:48.892Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) UAV Survey 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313833\tX:\\Basemapping\\LiDAR\\SoLIFE File Name: Sands of Life LIDAR – Orthophotography.lyr\tSands of LIFE Team, NRW\tUnmanned aerial vehicle (drone) survey of the Sand of LIFE (SoLIFE) sand dunes sites at the Gower Newborough Warren Pembrey Coast Kenfig and Merthyr Mawr. Survey data includes high resolution aerial imagery and photogrammetry derived Digital Elevation Models.\tPrivate record\n20132\tNRW_DS161331\t2025-01-24T15:37:12.049Z\tClimate Vulnerability Assessment (CVA) for Ramsar Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333331\tX:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\CVA RAMSAR.lyr\tContact Clive Walmsley, Senior Specialist Advisor, Climate Change and Decarbonisation, SPG for further information about the data.  Data originally produced and provided by Jon Rothwell, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18095\tNRW_DS115614\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.141Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Southern Damselfly (Coenagrion mercuriale) on candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (cSAC\u0027s) in Wales (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363134\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\18 Southern damselfly on cSAC in Wales (part 2)\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tFollowing Part 1 of the project to carry out an assessment of favourable condition for Southern Damselfly Coenagrion mercuriale (Boyce 2004) the author was contracted by the former Countryside Council for Wales to continue with the condition assessment at further sites in Pembrokeshire and elsewhere in Wales on Candidate Special Areas of Conservation. Each site was assessed to examine whether Favourable Condition had been attained. Sites covered were as follows;  -  Pembrokeshire:  Mynydd Preseli Gweunydd Blaencleddau (SN13) and Waun Fawr (SN03) -  Glamorgan:  Rhossili Down and Sluxton Farm (SS4290) and Cefn Bryn (SS5090) -  Anglesey:  Cors Erddreiniog (SH4683).\tPublic record\n18406\tNRW_DS117601\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.327Z\tSpatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Wrexham, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137363031\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr E.A.Howe (Contractor: Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust)\tThis report draws on a series of previous research reports commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body on determining the conservation status of great crested newts Triturus cristatus in north Wales. The Welsh Government is obligated to maintain or restore the favourable conservation status of great crested newts within Wales and this report aims to provide practical guidance on how this can be achieved within the County Borough of Wrexham. It is one of a series of three such reports the other two covering the counties of Flintshire and Anglesey. Due to its legal status the great crested newt is the focus of considerable conservation effort from a variety of bodies. This document aims to provide an overarching strategy to guide developers conservation NGO\u0027s and local and national government in their efforts in order to achieve the greatest conservation benefit for great crested newts and ensure a unified approach to achieving Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) across the county. To this end the report is accompanied by a number of GIS layers which will be of particular practical use to those engaged in great crested newt conservation across Wrexham.  The report presents a scoring method by which the Current Conservation Status (CCS) of great crested newts can be determined at multiple geographical scales from a single pond to the entire county. It then uses predictive modelling based on geographical distributional climatic and historical data to produce habitat suitability maps and to derive survey targets and pond creation targets to enable FCS to be achieved within the county. These techniques can be applied in various land use and planning scenarios and the report provides practical guidance on identifying areas of particularly high or low value for great crested newts assessing the impacts of proposed developments designing effective mitigation and the implications of agri-environment schemes for great crested newts.   During the modelling process a total of 2593 extant ponds were identified within the County Borough of Wrexham. With a view to redressing historic loss (37% of ponds having been lost since the 1843 mapping data) FCS was considered in the light of the level of occupancy that would have occurred at the time of the original mapping relative to current levels of occupancy and Habitat Suitability of ponds to the species (Habitat Suitability Index (HSI) scores). Historically we estimate there would have been 4116 ponds in Wrexham and a corresponding 640 ponds occupied by great crested newts and 988 ponds with an HSI score \u0026gt;0.7. We propose that the 640 occupied ponds should be used to provide a target level for great crested newts (Favourable Reference Value for Range and Habitat sufficiency). This is further qualified by requiring these ponds have an HSI \u0026gt;0.7 to ensure habitat quality has at least a medium population are found at a density of at least 4 per km2 (to ensure population viability) and has a minor (or lower) level of threat (to address the species\u0027 future prospects). The Current Status of the species in Wrexham is estimated as having 403 ponds occupied by great crested newts.   The approach to identifying CCS and FCS laid out within this document is one of a number which are in the process of being developed across the UK and Europe. Whilst there is currently no single standard approach which can be applied uniformly across a range of species and habitats one may emerge in the future. With the recent increase in studies looking at European Protected Species and with developing technologies (such as remote sensing and environmental DNA analysis) we may expect that new approaches to status assessment of great crested newts will develop in the near future. We would encourage efforts to learn from this work to further develop practical tools and necessary policy measures to aid the conservation of both for this and other species. However this d ...\tPublic record\n19029\tNRW_DS125200\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.429Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323030\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 3.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\t\u0027Water Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies\u0027 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2 clause 6 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...bodies of surface water in the vicinity of river mouths which are partly saline in character as a result of their proximity to coastal waters but are substantially influenced by freshwater flows\u0027.   Transitional waterbodies were defined from Mean High Water boundaries taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2 and NRW estuarine boundaries defined for the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD). Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics risk classification and proposed objectives. These data apply to Cycle 3 of the Water Framework Directive.\tPublic record\n17778\tNRW_DS110499\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.114Z\tRegulations Governing Sea Fisheries in Welsh Waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343939\tArchived as project 332, media 1291 (K:\\Archive\\Media1201to1300\\Media 1291 Available from NRW library  Available on the hard drives of Clare Eno and Rowland Sharp, NRW Environmental Policy Group\tInternal Contact: Rowland Sharp, Senior Advisor, SW Area Operations (Marine)       This is part of a larger dataset the Sea Fishing Atlas of Wales. (which consisted of three parts) The regulations bit is just held in the marine archive and is not available on the X drive.  This is because no-one has ever asked for it and it was a snapshot and I imagine completely out of date so as long as the project and media number are there I don’t think we need a X drive location.    Removed from public view on the advice of marine fisheries experts as information is dated and could be misleading.\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.  This dataset contains a collation of all relevant regulations pertaining to Welsh water fisheries. The fisheries regulations are split according to whether they are general to Welsh waters (including relevant EU and UK regulations) Sea Fisheries Committee (SFC) districts or relate to specific locations around the coast. The relevant sections of the byelaws are quoted and correct to the end of 2008. Those fishing activities currently unregulated are also identified and mapped. Another component of the dataset is the Rainbow chart. The purpose of this data capture was to collate all relevant regulations relating to sea fishing activity in Welsh waters and to present the information spatially in the form of maps (most of the regulation information was previously available primarily within SFC byelaws but had not previously been mapped). The regulations documented are those that were in force up to December 2008 there may have been changes to some regulations or new regulations created since. For information on the current regulations the relevant organisations should be contacted.\tPrivate record\n19154\tNRW_DS125734\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.380Z\tThe status and distribution of Broom-tip Chesias rufata at Morfa Harlech and Harlech Forest in 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373334\t\tMike Howe\t\tPrivate record\n19143\tNRW_DS125529\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.416Z\tSaproxylic Invertebrates Survey Data at Gilfach Radnorshire Wildlife Trust Reserve in 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353239\tWID held on Recorder 6\tMike Howe\tA survey of the dead wood (saproxylic) invertebrate fauna of the Gilfach Nature Reserve which is owned and managed by the Radnorshire Wildlife Trust (RWT) was undertaken over four man-days in 2022. This work is part of the Natur am Byth! Partnership project which aims to promote the recovery of selected key species in Wales. The Gilfach survey involved both a range of hand-searching techniques and the installation of five vane traps that were operated between May and September. The survey has shown Gilfach to support a diverse invertebrate fauna that includes a number of scarce species.\tPublic record\n17331\tNRW_DS24304\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.442Z\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333034\tThe database is held and maintained by JNCC\t\tThe Geological Conservation Review (GCR) was a major initiative launched in 1977 to identify and describe the most important geological sites in Britain. The GCR site database contains details of Geological Conservation Review sites in Wales such as notification date and priority unit authority and GCR number.  Purpose of data capture: The GCR was designed to identify those sites of national and international importance needed to show all the key scientific elements of the Earth heritage of Britain.  Methods of data capture: Site evaluation and documentation have resulted in the creation of an inventory of approximately 3500 GCR sites selected for around 100 categories (the GCR Blocks) encompassing the range of geological and geomorphological features of Britain.  Geographic coverage: England Scotland and Wales  Temporal coverage: The site evaluation and documentation ocurred between 1977 and 1990. New sites are added to the database when necessary.\tPrivate record\n19160\tNRW_DS125698\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.409Z\tStanner Rocks National Nature Reserve meteorological data (paper data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363938\tPaper records.  Note 16 November 2023 - Donna has kept these paper records in the comms room in Bangor for now just in case she finds more to add to the box. The paper data didn’t come from archives so there is no old number. She will give it a few months before transferring, when she does she\u0027ll get a number from Jill and pass it on.\tDylan Lloyd, Lead Specialist Advisor, Monitoring Strategy and Planning Donna Muirhead, Specialist Advisor, Data Services\tHourly and daily meteorological data from Stanner Rocks National Nature Reserve (NNR) was recorded from 1976 to 1979. It included solar temperate (c) rainfall (mms) and hours of sunshine.   The data was captured in association with work of the Nature Conservancy Council.\tPrivate record\n17080\tEXT_DS24275\t2024-05-28T12:10:11.374Z\tJNCC Conservation of River Macrophyte Database (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323735\tThe master version is available through the JNCC website http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/page-5815. NRW hold a copy in archive on server-based storage as project 293, media 1196.  This is the 2011 version, where restrictions were included and is an unreacted version. NRW have also downloaded a copy of the redacted version for general use\tSenior Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Evidence Group, NRW.  Tristan Hatton-Ellis acts as the data(base) manager for the Freshwater Lead Co-ordination Network, which represents JNCC on freshwater issues.   This responsibility may have been passed to Sue Hearn       JNCC have requested permission from all agencies/contributors to make this database available as open data 2016-05-11 (Lynn Heeley is main contact).  In principal NRW have agreed providing the database does not contain personal data or sensitive species.  When confirmed we will need to amend the metadata to OGL licences, requests would be referred to JNCC and we can add the correct resource locator.   Data was released under an OGL in 2016.  Personal data (recorder names) has been redacted.  Information about the original restrictions is held in the ReadMe file in the archive media 1196\tA copy of a Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) database used in the conservation of river macrophytes containing a collation of data on: Site Information Survey Information Plants Physical Data Fish and Invertebrate data.  The database is managed by the Freshwater Lead Co-ordination Network the data is owned by individual conservation agencies or even individuals and contains NRWs River Macrophyte data.   The purpose of this data capture was to collate data on a variety of river sites across Wales. The database is not fully populated: some invert and fish sections have few records though most of the coverage is reasonably comprehensive.\tPublic record\n17591\tNRW_DS108822\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.054Z\tMonitoring invertebrates on Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSIs): Toadflax Leaf Beetle (Chrysolina sanguinolenta) on Poppit Sands, Pembrokeshire\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383232\tLibrary and Area Libraries:  H:Drive \\TSG\\ Technical Research Reports\\Fowles_A\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW\tPoppit Sands actually fall within two Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSIs) Afon Teifi SSSI in the east and Aberarth − Carreg Wylan SSSI to the west and the situation is further complicated by the boundaries of these two SSSIs overlapping. The line where the two SSSIs join almost follows a major path running from the car park to the beach 50 metres east of the RNLI buildings. The purpose of this data capture was to map the distribution of common toadflax (Linaria vulgaris) on and around poppit Sands including number of plants size of plants and numbers of leaves per plant. Count (zero) of Chrysolina sanguinolenta on and around these plants.  The purpose of this data capture was to determine a method of monitoring the Nationally Notable leaf beetle Chrysolina sanguinolenta a qualifying SSSI feature here and carry out Common Standards Monitoring.\tPublic record\n18686\tNRW_DS123310\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.985Z\tMetal Mines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333130\tx:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Metal Mines.lyr\tTrystan James and Sophia Lennie, Geoscience\tMetal Mines record locations of disused metal (non-coal) mines in Wales with each being represented by centre point of the site. The dataset is collected to display the locations of metal mines which can be used in conjunction with other datasets to provide a better understanding of the area.\tPublic record\n18417\tNRW_DS119267\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.447Z\tSeal Sites in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323637\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seal sites.lyr  X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seal haul out sites.lyr   The above are now considered old data and should be archived in favour of data published by WG on DataMapWales\tTom Stringell       This base layer was created to identify all know seal sites, a derived layer was then created to illustrate just those sites where monitoring was ongoing or that was currently suspended  X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seal Monitoring sites.lyr.  As seals were previously on the CCW sensitive features list the information was then redacted to 5km square resolution and provided to UKDMOS.  This redacted layer was also made available to WG Marine Monitoring Portal  ***to be removed when/if metadata is made publicly available**** This base layer was created to identify all known seal sites, a derived layer was then created to illustrate just those sites where monitoring was ongoing or that was currently suspended. As seals were previously on the CCW sensitive features list the information was then redacted to 5km square resolution and provided to UKDMOS. This redacted layer was also made available to WG Marine Monitoring Portal\tThis dataset shows the location of seal sites in West Wales. The original version was based upon those identified in the West Wales Grey Seal Census 1991-1994 and Grey Seal census for North Wales 2004. See the base reports for further information. This data has subsequently been updated to include sightings in 2006 (see reports listed in the lineage for further information).  The original data was derived from the two reports (from 1995 and 2004 as detailed in the lineage) to meet a need to provide information on the location of seal sites that were included in CCW\u0027s marine monitoring program.  The purpose of this was to provide information to the UKDMOS catalogue.  NRWs collated data has been superseded by a collation undertaken by Welsh Government and published in 2022 on DataMapWales\tPrivate record\n19134\tNRW_DS125570\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.299Z\tThe status and distribution of the Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata on Whiteford Burrows, Pembrey Burrows, Laugharne \u0026amp; Pendine Burrows and habitat assessment of Baglan Burrows \u0026amp; Frainslake Sands in 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353730\tData held on the WID in Recorded 6 - folder titled Natur Am Byth\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tFollowing surveys in 2016 which identified healthy populations were established on the three largest beaches at Pendine-Laugharne this survey of E. complanata was designed to inform future management of strandline habitats also to assess habitat condition of two potential reintroduction sites at Frainslake and Crymlyn.\tPublic record\n18703\tNRW_DS123305\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.389Z\tNIRS Incidents\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333035\tx:\\Incidents\\National Incident Recording System (NIRS)\\NIRS Incidents.lyr\tJohn Farnhill\t\tPrivate record\n17075\tEXT_DS99376\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.916Z\tBat Records from National Trust Properties in Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939333736\tA copy of the data is held in Recorder 6. This data in is Recorder 6 under the Third Party Data (copy) tag.\tGIS Service Desk Sam Dyer, Mammal Ecologist, bats and riparian mammals, Terrestrial Species Team, NRW       This data in is Recorder 6 under the Third Party Data (copy) tag.\tThis is a third party dataset containing records from the National Trust Recorder 2002 species database extracted in March 2007.  These datasets include records of all bat species commonly found in Wales and are split into 5 separate surveys.  The National Trust licenced these datasets for use by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW staff only under their Access Agreement. This Access Agreement has now been outdated. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct bat surveys across National Trust sites in Wales.\tPublic record\n17786\tNRW_DS110498\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.232Z\tSea Fishing Activity in Welsh Waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343938\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries_Habitat_and_sensitivity\\Fishing_activity\\ Archived as project 332, media 1291 (old version) at K:\\Archive\\Media 1291to1300\\Media 1291 or Project 376, Media 1396 (new version) at K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1396\tInternal Custodian:  Previous custodian who have now left or moved roles: Sea Fisheries Liaison Officer, NRW   Clare Eno and Rowland Sharp, NRW Environmental Policy Group  Current custodian: Internal Contact: Rowland Sharp, Senior Advisor, SW Area Operations (Marine)  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW       Removed from public view on the advice of marine fisheries experts as information is dated and could be misleading.\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.  Information has been collated to produce a series of maps of sea fishing activities in Wales complete with regulatory boundaries (12nm 6nm and 3nm). The purpose of this dataset was provide spatial information for a range of fishing activities occurring in Welsh waters. Activities mapped are: - Beam trawling and scallop dredging - Mussel oyster and hydraulic dredging - Otter trawling - Light demersal trawls and seines - Pelagic trawling drift nets and hook and line fishing - Static gear fishing- nets and long line - Static gear fishing- pots - Hand gathering - Aquaculture The dataset includes maps and a text description of each activity.\tPrivate record\n17834\tNRW_DS111180\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.976Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools, Caves and Overhangs in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313830\tData is held within the marine archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 555 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 555) [A]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\MarineMonitoring\\MarineMonitoring Reports\\No 58 - Carm Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Data [C]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder - MRCCW16300000008 [D]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\GIS [E]   Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  There were two purposes for this data capture:  - To provide an inventory and distribution map of selected biotopes present to use as a reference condition for the rocky shore habitat communities in the SAC. - To describe gross changes in these features since the Phase 1 survey (1999-2001) to give some indication of their stability.  The rocky shore areas that lies within the SAC includes a number of rockpool overhang and cave features that are considered to be nationally important biotopes and/or Rare or Scarce in Wales and others that are considered to be important for their biodiversity. These potentially species rich rocky habitats were therefore surveyed to provide an inventory of these sub features for future survey work.  The confidence in this data is high as surveys were carried out by a team of experienced marine biologists.\tPublic record\n18203\tNRW_DS116004\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.678Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Pembrokeshire 1997\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303034\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000009.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Pembrokeshire. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tPublic record\n18201\tNRW_DS116006\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.664Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Ramsey 1995\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303036\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000015.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Ramsey. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tPublic record\n17405\tNRW_DS47010\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.204Z\tBat Roosts Database - North West Wales (GWYBATS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437303130\tThis data is held and managed by Cofnod - who have the top copy. Shared drive with Dolgellau, Llys-y-bont, HQ.   MapInfo export held on the M Drive.\tInternal Custodian: NR Protected Species Officer Casework, Maes y Fynnon, NRW. Mammal Ecologist Bats and Riparian Mammals, Maes y Fynnon, NRW.  Internal Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  External Contact: Aisling May  - Rheolwr Data/Data Manager       aisling.may@cofnod.org.uk Cofnod, Intec, Ffordd y Parc, Parc Menai, Bangor, Gwynedd. LL57 4FG.  (01248) 672603\tThis GWYBATS is a Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regional bat roost site information and lesser horseshoe bat monitoring data for Caernarvonshire Anglesey and Merionethshire. The original records were derived from BATSITES-WALES and the original Bat Roosts Database - Caernarvonshire and Anglesey (GWYBATS) which were subsequently validated. New records received by the CCW Bangor (Llys y Bont) regional office have since been added. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts to assist CCW\u0027s conservation work. In particular to provide information when dealing with development proposals to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tPublic record\n19132\tNRW_DS125518\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.234Z\tNCC Biological survey of common land in wales 1989-1994 (paper data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353138\t5 boxes of paper data are currently held in the comms room F13, under the table.  Labelled as , PP19, PP20, PP21, PP22, PP23.  These are to be prepared to go to TNA (work to be carried out with Jill), Box PP23 my need some info to the disposed of as mainly supporting info or rough maps\tPossibly Buddug Jones or Stuart Smith\tIn 1986 the Countryside Commission published the Report of the Common Land Forum which resulted in the possibility of new legislation on common land. The Nature Conservancy Council subsequently commissioned the Biological Survey of Common Land in order to assess the implications for conservation agencies. Work on the Survey started in November 1987 and continued until December 1994. It was undertaken by Rural Surveys Research Unit at Aberystwyth University and following the split of NCC into English Nature Scottish Natural Heritage and the Countryside Council for Wales in 1991 the three national bodies continued to fund the research.  The aim of the survey was to record detailed information about wildlife in each of the common land areas in order to produce informed management schemes. These schemes would be operated by local management associations consisting of representatives from all interested parties.\tPrivate record\n18554\tNRW_DS121723\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.856Z\tWINS Water Quality: Opportunities for Reducing Sedimentation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373233\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Opportunities Reducing Sedimentation.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where management action could be taken to reduce sedimentation in coastal and transitional waterbodies as well as lakes and rivers. Management opportunities considered for reduction of sedimentation are habitat restoration or conversion in areas at high erosion risk; riparian buffer strips; hedgerow planting; cover crops or grass on tillage land\tPublic record\n17122\tEXT_DS115256\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.299Z\tRisso\u0027s Dolphin (Grampus griseus) Surveys Conducted in North Wales (2012) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323536\tArchived as Project 390, Media 1406   Excel spreadsheets are stored on the WDC server.\tGIS Service Desk Team Leader Marine Vertebrates, NRW. External Custodian: Sonja Mareike Eisfeld, WDC.         We have listed as not for re-supply externally.  As WG ERF grant, we are only aware of the condition that allows NRW to produce and  publish a NRW Scientific report.  It is not clear if we also have the right to distribute the data.\tA Welsh Government (ERF) grant funded project carried out by Whale and Dolphin Conservation (WDC).  WDC have studies cetaceans in the area around Ynys Enlli Bardsey for 13 years.  The long-term dataset helps describe the occurrence distribution site fidelity habitat use and behaviour of small cetaceans.  The data described here relates to the 2012 annual survey.  The collated information will help provide the basis for future surveys comparisons to determine population densities for small cetacean species and also provide information for local conservation and management plans.  The seas around Ynys Enlli/Bardsey Island and the sound between Ynys Enlli and the mainland.\tPublic record\n18015\tNRW_DS115064\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.033Z\tCoastal Saltmarsh Monitoring\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303634\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A]  Data is archived as project 370, media 1365.   [B]  Species data is also held in Recorder 6 [LC0003850000006Q]\tBianca Bacchi, Senior Env Mon Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW\tCoastal saltmarshes in the UK comprise the upper vegetated portions of intertidal mudflats lying approximately between mean high water neap tides and mean high water spring tides. The characteristic vegetation consists of a limited number of halophytic (salt tolerant) species adapted to regular immersion by the tides. This project was commissioned by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) primarily to cover the condition monitoring of the Annex I saltmarsh habitats across four Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SAC). The two habitats concerned are:   - Atlantic salt meadows (referred to as ASM) - Annexe I code 1330. Atlantic salt meadows develop when halophytic vegetation colonises soft intertidal sediments of mud and sand in areas protected from strong wave action. This vegetation forms the middle and upper reaches of saltmarshes where tidal inundation still occurs but with decreasing frequency and duration. A wide range of community types is represented and the saltmarshes can cover large areas especially where there has been little or no enclosure on the landward side. The vegetation varies with climate and the frequency and duration of tidal inundation. - Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud (referred to as \u0027Salicornia\u0027) - Annexe I code 1310. This pioneer saltmarsh vegetation colonises intertidal mud and sandflats in areas protected from strong wave action and is an important precursor to the development of more stable saltmarsh vegetation. It develops at the lower reaches of saltmarshes where the vegetation is frequently flooded by the tide and can also colonise open creek sides depressions or pans within saltmarshes as well as disturbed areas of upper saltmarshes.\tPublic record\n18436\tNRW_DS119125\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.190Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey on the River Wye (2017)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313235\tDMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitat Directive 3rd reporting cycle \u0026gt; Shad Fish Species - 3rd Reporting Cycle \u0026gt; Wye DMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 3 \u0026gt; Wye Shad \u0026gt; Workbook Wye shad egg surveys 2013 onwards.xlsx\tNRW Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator: Heather Garrett.\tThe River Wye rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains and flows in a generally south-easterly direction to enter the Severn Estuary at Chepstow. The river is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive for several features including salmon lampreys and shads: Twaite (Alosa fallax) and Allis (Alosa alosa) shad. In Britain significant populations of Twaite shad are known to spawn in the Rivers Severn Wye Usk and Tywi. The population of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and water quality issues. The purpose of this data capture was to make an assessment of Conservation Status by conducting egg deposition surveys to assess spawning extent and compare these with results from previous datasets. Surveys carried out along the length of the Welsh portion of the River Wye from Builth Wells to Monmouth. The survey was conducted in May and June of 2013 2015 \u0026amp; 2016.\tPublic record\n19142\tNRW_DS125523\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.376Z\tAfon Teifi Data Collection 1989 (paper  data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353233\tData to be sent to off-site storage. Box numbers: box 1 of 2 is CCW/WRAB/48 and box 2 of 2 is CCW/WRAB/49\tData to be stored off site.  Mobilisation would be in form of scanning as raw data may be available elsewhere.  Would only be considered if originals are found, otherwise may be disposed of\tData Collection created ahead of proposed notification of the site as a NCR.  The aim of the project was to collate a wide range of scientific information already available from NCC and others to aid the designation of the site.  data was collated from local regional NCC files FBA Welsh Water RSBP Otter Haven Trust UWIST Dyfed Wildlife Trust.  The data consists of 8 volumes.  Volumes 7 is a Report and Bibliography Volume 1: Vegetation main river Volume 2: Vegetation tributaries Volume 3: Vegetation tributaries report Volume 4:Inverts fish Volume 5: Birds Volume 6: mammals Volume 7: Final report and tributaries Volume 8: Maps\tPrivate record\n17694\tNRW_DS110036\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.565Z\tVegetation Survey of Maritime Cliff and Slopes of the Llyn Peninsula\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303336\tArchived as project number 139, media number 236.\tInternal Custodian:  Julie Creer, Coastal Ecology Specialist, Evidence and Analysis, NRW Heather Lewis, Coastal Ecology Specialist, Evidence \u0026amp; Analysis, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tMaritime cliff and slopes is a priority habitat within the UK Biodiversity Action Plan. Selected maritime sites on the Llyn Peninsula were surveyed mapped and evaluated in September/October 2000 and the range of National Vegetation Classification (NVC) vegetation types present characterized.  A number of sections of the UK coastline have been nominated as \u0027Vegetated Sea Cliffs of the Atlantic and Baltic Coasts\u0027 candidate Special Areas of Conservation under the EC Habitats Directive. This survey was undertaken as part of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCWs) commitment to undertake research and survey in order to increase knowledge of the extent and quality of this resource. Selected maritime sites on the Llyn Peninsula:  -  Porth Neigwl (Hell\u0027s Mouth) -  Porth Dinllaen (Borth Wen to Penrhyn Nefyn) -  Porth Nefyn (Penrhyn Nefyn to Penrhyn Bodeilias) -  Porth Pistyll (Penrhyn Bodeilias to the lower slopes of Carreg y Llam)\tPublic record\n18016\tNRW_DS114978\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.151Z\tDormouse Core Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393738\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Dormouse_Core_Areas.lyr\tCustodians and metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team Also contact the PTES for the most up to date version of the dataset.\tThis is a spatial dataset held by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) which holds records from the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) dormouse data sourced from areas of work such as licence returns and dormouse survey work and also The Vincent Wildlife Trust (VWT) dormouse survey data. Data collation completed by PTES under data exchange agreement with PTES. Map has been used as basis for Glastir Targeted Element - core (or mandatory) sites.  Majority of records collected by experienced surveyors or verified by experienced surveyors (e.g. nut hunt records). Licence returns have not been independently verified.  This dataset is a collation of individual records of dormouse sightings and/or evidence for Wales mostly between 1988 and 2014 although there are a few earlier records. Records originate from a variety of sources with the collation held and maintained by People\u0027s Trust for Endangered Species (PTES). It is an incomplete directory of sites due to the ad-hoc nature of data collection. Some sites are regularly monitored using nestboxes as part of the National Dormouse Monitoring Programme (NDMP) and these sites are indicated in the attributes and more detailed biometric data are held by PTES who administer the monitoring programme.   Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a copy of the GIS dataset on their internal GIS data store - however requestors are advised to obtain the most up-to-date version of the data from PTES. This dataset is also held under the National Dormouse Database (NDD) available to download from the NBN Gateway.\tPublic record\n17788\tNRW_DS110501\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.297Z\tModelling of Habitat Networks for Welsh Woodlands, Grasslands, Heathlands, Bogs and Fens\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353031\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Habitat Networks all Wales\\Lowland ,  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Habitat Networks all Wales\\Upland\tJim Latham, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team Leader\tThere is a growing emphasis on ecological connectivity in planning for effective biodiversity conservation and building ecosystem resilience through Resilient Ecological Networks (RENs). A major part of this is to counteract the negative impacts of habitat fragmentation (reduction in are and increase in isolation).  Modelled habitat networks can provide insights into how landscapes are likely to be functioning and inform the location of actions to improve the connectivity and resilience of protected sites.  This is a spatial dataset consisting of maps of habitat networks originally developed by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in collaboration with Forestry Commission Wales and Forest Research using a functional networks approach and now managed and progressively developed by NRW.  Patches of habitat and other intervening habitats through which many of their species are able to move are mapped as habitat networks.   Networks have been mapped for habitats including woodland unimproved grassland calcareous grassland marshy grassland heathland fens and bogs; in most cases these are divided into upland and lowland versions and in some cases networks that support the highest quality areas of habitat have been selected out as priority layers.   Results are available for the whole of Wales as GIS layers and include three levels of habitat networks; core networks (areas within which species that require extensive habitat and disperse poorly are able to move) focal networks (areas within which species tolerant of smaller habitat patches and with greater dispersal ability are able to move) and local networks (areas within species that can persist within small habitat patches and have very limited dispersal abilities can move).    These habitat networks  also form the basis of ‘Priority Ecological Networks’ (PENs) a subset of the ‘focal’ networks described above which have been selected against our suite of Protected Sites.  Purpose of data capture was to allow the scope and range of potential networks to be rapidly explored. Predicted habitat networks can be used to guide large-scale planning for nature conservation provide insight into how the landscape is likely to be functioning and prioritise action to improve the connectivity and viability of protected sites.  Layers are arranged in two folders: Level 1 containing all outputs across Wales and Level 2 which are selected priority networks within which action may be targeted to enhance functional networks of the best habitat areas.\tPublic record\n18737\tNRW_DS123397\t2024-05-28T12:55:19.524Z\tPhysical Characteristics\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333937\tx:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine_Habitat_Vulnerability\\Physical_Characteristics.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Internal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17772\tNRW_DS110688\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.649Z\tCountryside Council for Wales\u0027 Marine Monitoring Sample Stations for UKDMOS\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130363838\tThis data is archived under Project No. 230, Media No. 599. Raw data provided to BODC for upload to UKDMOS. This data can be accessed via the UKDMOS website.  http://www.ukdmos.org/welcome.asp.\tInternal Custodian - Marine Monitoring Data Officer, MFSG. Metadata point of contact - Data Services\tThe exact location of sampling stations that occur at each of the Marine Monitoring Sites.  The purpose of this data capture is to provide an overview of legacy Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) Marine Monitoring sample stations to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and make these available to a wider audience through a central location.  This will help facilitate a more coordinated approach to Marine Monitoring across the UK and promote the sharing of resources. Sample stations for a variety of monitoring programmes are available including: Benthic rock and sediment intertidal rock and sediment saline lagoons scoter aerial monitoring bottlenose dolphins grey seals shellfish (bird food) stocks video and acoustic monitoring. At some locations this is the same as the Marine Monitoring Site location as listed in the separate metadata entry (Marine Monitoring Sites - GIS Dataset) for others it may be a series of samples stations within the sites and for others it may be a larger area that is surveyed remotely.  The Marine Monitoring Sample locations are available online through UK Directory of Marine Observing Systems (UKDMOS).  Included are the following legacy CCW Marine Monitoring programmes; - Intertidal Monitoring Surveys - Benthic Subtidal Sediment Monitoring Survey - Aerial scoter monitoring - Acoustic Seabed Monitoring of the North Llyn Modiolus reef - Benthic Rock Monitoring - Bottlenose Dolphin Monitoring - Shellfish Stock (bird food) Monitoring - Skomer Marine Nature Reserve Monitoring - Across Wales drop-down video monitoring  - Saline lagoon soft sediment survey - Grey seal monitoring\tPublic record\n18231\tNRW_DS116244\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.331Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323434\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional (Estuarine) Waterbodies Cycle 1 (2008-2013) is a polygon dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2 clause 6 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...bodies of surface water in the vicinity of river mouths which are partly saline in character as a result of their proximity to coastal waters but are substantially influenced by freshwater flows\u0027. Transitional waterbodies were defined from Mean High Water.\tPublic record\n18204\tNRW_DS116009\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.647Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Ystwyth and Rheidol shingle heaths 1995\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303039\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000013.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Ystwyth and Rheidol. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tPublic record\n19003\tNRW_DS124922\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.473Z\tRumney Great Wharf to River Usk Drone Survey 2021\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393232\tZ:Drive Location: Archive/ Land Mgmt/ Protected Sites/ Rumney Great Wharf to River Usk Drone Survey\tSarah Revill - Senior Marine Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) wish to establish a topographical baseline and aerial imagery of the foreshore and surrounds to help inform the consideration of possible nature-based solutions at this location. The purpose of the survey was to collect topographic data of the foreshore along the 12km frontage that stretches between Cardiff in the west and Newport to the east. Vertical images were taken for the photogrammetric reconstruction. Flights were also undertaken to capture oblique imagery looking back at the coastline. A key constraint of this survey was being unable to take GPS points out in the mudflats. To overcome this a RTK capable drone was used which reduces the reliance on ground control points by ensuring that the geolocation for each image is significantly more accurate than on a standard drone.  The survey was carried out over a period of 6 days between the 3rd and 12th of February 2021.  Flights were planned to ensure lowest possible tides. In total 19 flights were completed to collect the aerial data taking a combined 285 minutes (4.75 hours). Survey deliverables comprised ASCII Grid Orthophotos and Obliques\tPublic record\n17365\tNRW_DS45660\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.061Z\tChough Breeding Population Surveillance in Pembrokeshire\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435363630\tRaw south Pembrokeshire data are held by NRW at Stackpole office;  Remainder by Pembrokeshire Coast National Park All Wales colour-ringed data are held by Tony Cross/Adrienne Stratford (and by RSPB)\tStackpole office (see contact details above); BTO/NRW annual Schedule 1 Licence reports (location unknown).\tSurveillance of breeding population (including productivity and recruitment) and non-breeding population of Chough (Pyrrhocorax pyrrhocorax) in Pembrokeshire South Wales. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) collect and collate data from the south Pembrokeshire coastline which includes Caldey Island and the Castlemartin Coast Special Protection Area (SPA).  SPAs are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive) and for regularly occurring migratory species. Castlemartin SPA is designed for breeding and over wintering choughs. This includes the Pembrokeshire Coastal National Park (PCNP) coordinate records from St Anne\u0027s Head to Cemaes Head and the offshore islands including Ramsey (SPA) and St David\u0027s Head). In this dataset some records date from early 1960s forming part of the first national survey; recording was established at the main study area in 1978 but most records date from 1982.  There were numerous national and local purposes to this data capture.   National purposes:  - To contribute to decadal national and international surveys of chough and to update JNCC records for reporting under the Birds Directive (SPAs) and Habitats Directive (Special Areas of Conservation (SAC)).   Local purposes  - To assess condition of chough as an Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) feature in Castlemartin Coast SPA; - To maintain up-to-date records of concentrations of chough relevant to management of recreation (sea cliff-climbing and coasteering); - To service update data requirements for local chough Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) in partnership with other members of a local chough study group including PCNP Authority who coordinate records for rest of Pembrokeshire.\tPublic record\n18889\tNRW_DS124795\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.056Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) bryophyte surveys\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373935\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE bryophyte surveys.lyr  Spreadsheet version also available in DMS LAND-75481194-986\tToni Henwood, Team Member 2, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South WalesSands of LIFE\tLocations of notable bryophyte species recorded during pre-intervention surveys for the Sands of LIFE project at Tywyn Aberffraw Newborough Morfa Harlech Pembrey Coast Kenfig and Merthyr Mawr. Data was collected between September and November 2019.\tPublic record\n17357\tNRW_DS24227\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.797Z\tWelsh Raptor and Schedule 1 Bird Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323237\tThis data is now part of the terrestrial archive. Project T004 Media Number T_Media004.\tRSPB contact is R Thorpe\tConfidential database of records of Welsh raptors chough and some other non-raptor Wildlife and Countryside Act Schedule 1 birds. Type of data: nest sites chick counts and grid references (although some locations are coded by the license returnee and only the area the license covers is given the exact location is unknown.)  This data is strictly confidential due to Protected Schedule 1 status of birds. Selective parts of the data (but not in full or in the same form) are shared with Reg Thorpe RSPB and Rare Breeding Birds Panel.  This dataset is used for casework survey monitoring and site designation across Wales.\tPublic record\n18194\tNRW_DS116137\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.662Z\tAcoustic multibeam survey of St Bride\u0027s Bay (MBES data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136313337\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 399, Media No 1416 (K\\:archive\\Media 1401-1500\\Media 1556) project 399 EA (Environment Agency) Acoustic multibeam survey  of St Bride\u0027s Bay [A] xyz data [B] multibeam tiffs [C] Surpporting information about any processing and source of data in form of emails saved as txt files\tInternal Custodian: Charlie Lindenbaum, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Data provided to Pete Walker from Mike Camplin (Pete thinks that it came straight from Alison Miles at the EA in 2007). There is no metadata or additional information other than that stored with the data.  Just emails from Charlie Lindenbaum and Lucie Skates.  Don\u0027t think any internal CCW processing was done\tThis dataset shows the results of a bathymetric survey of St Brides Bay Pembrokeshire. This dataset was owned by Environment Agency however with the formation of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) in 2013 ownership was transferred to NRW.\tPublic record\n17341\tNRW_DS24280\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.250Z\tWELSHNAMELIST.MDB\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323830\tUnknown\tNatasha Lough, Marine Habitats Biologist, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Science Group, CCW.\tWelsh/English dictionary of marine intertidal species names.\tPrivate record\n17313\tEXT_DS125568\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.136Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): Depth to Groundwater (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353638\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS Depth to Groundwater.lyr\tMatthew Wareham, Data Services Team\tThis dataset is owned by the British Geological Survey (BGS) who is a provides UK geoscientific data information and knowledge.  This dataset that displays probable maximum depth in metres to the phreatic water table.  This represents the likely lowest water level under natural conditions in an open well or borehole drilled into the upper parts of the bedrock.\tPrivate record\n18669\tNRW_DS123278\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.433Z\tCoastal Reaches\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323738\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Coastal Reaches.lyr\tLouise Pennington\t\tPrivate record\n17374\tNRW_DS45632\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.923Z\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435363332\tSee individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field above for more details\tInternal Custodian: Environmental Change Network Project Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW. Environmental Change Network Data Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tLong term monitoring of physical chemical and biological variables on Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon. The monitoring is designed to collect store analyse and interpret long-term datasets for physical chemical and biological variables which drive or respond to environmental change. The Yr Wyddfa monitoring contributes to the national Environmental Change Network. It is one of 12 terrestrial ECN sites across the UK and is important within the context of the network because it extends its climatic and ecological coverage as well as the geographical distribution (it\u0027s the only montane site under oceanic influence and has the highest rainfall). The ECN\u0027s objectives are to establish and maintain a network of sites from which to obtain comparable long-term datasets through the monitoring of a range of variables; to integrate and analyse these data to identify natural and man-induced environmental changes improve understanding of the causes of change distinguish short-term fluctuations from long-term trends and predict future changes.  NRW also carries out other monitoring at the site which contributes to a variety of national monitoring initiatives see lineage fields of the individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field below.\tPublic record\n18719\tNRW_DS123352\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.799Z\tMonitor Sites Salmonid\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333532\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\Monitor Sites Salmonid.lyr\tVictoria Bowmaker\t\tPrivate record\n17324\tEXT_DS125269\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.280Z\tMonmouthshire Public Rights of Way (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323639\tNRWGDBTP.GISDATA.PROW_MONMOUTHSHIRE_22_09_2021\tThomas Blythe\tPublic footpaths byways and bridleways in Monmouthshire. Provided by Monmouthshire council in 2022.\tPrivate record\n18055\tNRW_DS115166\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.560Z\tHABMAP Acoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of Selected Areas of the Southern Irish Sea (Summer 2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313636\tData is stored in the CCW / NRW marine data archive as Project 274, Media 1353\tInternal Custodians: - Subtidal Marine Ecologist, NRW. - Subtidal Team, NRW.\tThe HABMAP project was set up in response to the need for better spatial awareness of habitat distributions in the Southern Irish Sea. It was a partnership project between partners in Wales and Ireland and part funded by INTERREG IIIa European regional development fund. Data collection included acoustic data sediment data and seabed imagery. These data were used to classify the seabed and feed into a predictive modelling process to predict benthic habitat.    The multibeam data was collected in five locations throughout the Southern Irish Sea by the R/V Celtic Voyager to Irish National Seabed Survey (INSS) standard. They were then processed cleaned and classified based on acoustic classes which were then related to seabed sediment types. Bedforms were also identified using the MBES data.   The Coordinate System used for the multibeam data was UTM Zone 30N. The xyz data was usually processed to a 5 m resolution. The purpose of this data capture was to obtain multibeam coverage of the five study areas identified to cover a variety of different environments with different physical properties and biological communities. This would be used to link backscatter intensity to physical properties of the seabed. Data depth corrected for tide using POLPRED software which utilises one or more of Proudman Oceanographic Laboratory\u0027s hydrodyamic models and reduced to chart datum. CARIS HIPS / SIPS software used to create surfaces and gridded datasets by experienced surveyors.\tPublic record\n17508\tNRW_DS100170\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.791Z\tStoneworts in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313730\tTop copy as a spreadsheet in the DMS (EVID-359161902-13) https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/stnew/Stonewort%20Information/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fstnew%2FStonewort%20Information%2FStonewort%20Statuses  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestial\\Lower_plants\\Stoneworts.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Stoneworts_Grid.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Snr Habitat Survey \u0026amp; Evaluation \u0026amp; Non-Vascular Plant Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Internal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       The GIS data may need tp be updated from the sreadsheet\tThis is an excel spreadsheet that is also presented as a spatial dataset.  The datasets contains a collation of all known records of stoneworts in Wales from 1726 up till July 2018. Stoneworts (or charophytes) are a group of algae that grow in fresh and brackish water and have a relatively complex structure. They are sensitive to pollution including nutrient enrichment and are therefore good indicators of water quality. Thirty species have been recorded in the UK and seventeen of these occur in Wales. Of the latter four are Red Data Book species six are Nationally Scarce and 9 are considered rare in Wales; three are Priority Species in the UK BAP and are included on the National Assembly of Wales\u0027 List of Species and Habitats of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity (the \\Section 74\\\" list). The purpose of this data capture is to inform understanding of the distribution and status of rare an/or threatened lichen species in Wales. This in turn will facilitate decision-making on SSSI notifications species status assessments etc.\"\tPublic record\n18050\tNRW_DS115188\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.475Z\tInvestigation into Musculus discors Beds in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313838\tData is archived a project 372, media 1369  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1369  Species data is available in NRW Marine Recorder [MRCCW31600000008].\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis survey was carried out as part of a MSc thesis in September 2011. To provide baseline data on M. discors beds around the LlPeninsula with four specific aims: Establish the population structure for sample beds from around the LlPeninsula in order to investigate population structure and determine bed longevity. Identify the in-fauna/epi-fauna associated with M. discors beds. Investigate the sedimentation that occurs upon M. discors beds. Provide advice to CCW on whether M. discors should be included within a Local Biodiversity Action Plan for Gwynedd.   To determine M. discors population structure and influence on assemblage structure twenty six ten centimetre (cm) diameter cores of M. discors associated fauna and sediment were collected by Seasearch volunteers on SCUBA from four randomly chosen beds in North Wales.were rinsed through a 63 micrometre (sieve with the sediment M. discors and in-fauna retained. All liquid was retained for later filtration. M. discors were sorted from the sample and length measured to the nearest 0.1 millimetre (mm) using Vernier callipers with epifauna identified and enumerated (Rees et al. 2008). If present new recruits were sorted from the sample and measured using a calibrated microscope slide (to the nearest 0.1mm).   In cases where there were high numbers of recruits sub-sampling occurred with the abundance and size of a quarter of the total recruit population determined. All associated fauna were identified to the lowest taxonomic level possible.\tPublic record\n17898\tNRW_DS111816\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.567Z\tMapping Marine Benthic Biodiversity in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383136\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe UK is committed through international agreements and European obligations to the establishment of an ecologically coherent network of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) to conserve marine ecosystems and biodiversity. The identification of areas of high biodiversity may be helpful for planning both Marine Protected Areas and for Marine Spatial Planning and can contribute to an ecosystem-based approach to the management of our seas. Furthermore identifying which areas are most important for biodiversity not only yields benefits for the maintenance of ecosystem structure and functioning but can also enable cost effective prioritisation of areas for marine protection. Each data layer produced in relation to this project has its own metadata entry. The main objective of the project is to provide maps indicating different levels of biodiversity in Welsh waters (including the intertidal). The data covers the Welsh coastline from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.\tPublic record\n17380\tNRW_DS24307\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.044Z\tDormouse Database (1905 - 2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333037\t[A]  A welsh abstract of the species data is held in Recorder 6 Dormouse Database - NE - Welsh Extract CCW000380000000C\t\tThe database was constructed to hold data arising from the Great Nut Hunt and build a complete and up to date picture of the distribution of the dormouse. This has been used for reporting on the delivery of the Biodiversity Action Plan. England and Wales. The dormouse does not occur in Scotland or Northern Ireland. The dataset has relied largely on contributed data rather than proactive survey so it suffers from bias towards places that people visited during the Great Nut Hunts. Grid references would normally refer to the centroid of a wood where the dormouse was found rather than the actual point. Many grid references have not been checked on a GIS to confirm that they fall within the woodland boundary; this is an on-going process and would normally result in an 8-figure (10m resolution) grid reference being used.  This dataset includes the name of recorder site name grid reference and type of sighting (for example dead or alive location of nest).  Records originate from a variety of sources with the collation held and maintained by Tony Mitchell-Jones at English Nature. It is an incomplete directory of sites some of which are monitored annually as part of the national monitoring scheme. Only the most recent record for each site is included in this dataset historical data is held in the original database. The source data also includes comments and the record source. Records use 6 figure British National Grid references (precision unknown) all give the centre of the site.\tPublic record\n17336\tNRW_DS24288\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.986Z\tPhase 2 Welsh Lowland Peatland Survey (Fenbase 1977 - 1982)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323838\tOriginal paper field records held at Sheffield University.  Some copies within CCW. FENBASE: Location unknown - Access Database Site summaries (Wales Wetland Survey.xls) held by Peter Jones, TSG; Jo Hughes G:drive, TSG; Cyprian Payne N:drive (WETLSURV.XLS) Slides: Unknown\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThis dataset has now been incorporated into and superseded by the new Phase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales. The survey work was split into two time periods with the first Phase 2 lowland peatland surveys being conducted between 1977 and 1982 (this dataset). There was then a break between this and the second tranche of surveys which have occurred from 2004 onwards. These more modern surveys were conducted on sites already covered on the 1997-1982 surveys and on newer identified sites. FenBASE is an ecological database on fens maintained by the Wetland Research Group University of Sheffield. Pre-NVC survey of 4100 quadrats of vegetation data collected on paper and held in the FENBASE database. Includes site summaries (known as Wales Wetland Survey). Also includes some slides. FenBASE was created to provide a comprehensive database of information pertaining to the fens within England. FenBASE data were used to evaluate the potential issue of nutrient enrichment of basin fens at the regional level and provide an overview of mitigation option applicable to those sites at risk from nutrient enrichment. It is also used to provide indication of additional data that would be appropriate to further aid assessment and evaluation.\tPublic record\n17368\tNRW_DS45924\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.247Z\t1 Otter Survey Wales 2000 to 2004 - ***  FULL FIELD TEST NOT REAL DATA  *** update two - Third Party Dataset\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435393234\tThe location of the data internally: EXAMPLE: Data is archived as project 450, media 1372. (Or) GIS Corporate Data Store: X-Layer_Files\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Ramsar.lyr   Mammal Ecologist office Terrestrial Science Group CCW.\tData Custodian:  Jonathon Rothwell - Terrestrial Mapping Scientist NRW.  Or contact Mammal Ecologists, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\t*** This metadata entry is NOT REAL data. ***  It is just acting as a test metadata entry for use internally within NRW and as an example for staff who are entering metadata.  Description to be included in the Abstract: - Purpose of data capture. - Geographic Coverage. - Temporal Coverage. - Give context to the data collected.  - Be as detailed as possible.   EXAMPLE: The aim of this data collection was to monitoring for otter spraints holts and sightings on all major river systems and their tributaries across Wales.Surveying was carried out between April and October between 2000 and 2004. Each river was visited only once each year. The river systems covered included Usk Tywi and Wye all of which are designated under the Habitats Directive as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).\tPrivate record\n18401\tNRW_DS118724\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.093Z\tA Survey for Geyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Erddreiniog SSSI and Cors Geirch SSSI (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373234\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information \u0026gt; Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Martin Willing)\tA survey for Geyer\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Erddreiniog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)/ National Nature Reserve (NNR) and Cors Geirch SSSI/NNR in October 2016 failed to locate the snail. Survey locations were based upon previous records dating from 1985 to 2007 on Cors Erddreiniog and 1996 and 2008 on Cors Geirch. Losses from some former strongholds on Cors Erddreiniog are probably due to a lack of grazing leading to the development of blanketing tussocks of Black Bog-rush Schoenus nigricans and Blunt-flowered Rush Juncus subnodulosus which have shaded out the open \u0027Carex-lawn\u0027 habitat required by the snail. This is most pronounced on Nant Isaf spring field (Compartment 13c) which has not been grazed for over 20 months. In other compartments snail loss may be a consequence of over-grazing or periods of under-grazing followed by cattle poaching of the remaining open areas. Similarly Vertigo geyeri losses from known locations on Cors Geirch appear to be due to the development of a rank \u0027tussocky\u0027 sward that has reduced or eliminated the lightly-grazed \u0027Carex-lawn\u0027 habitat as a result of under-grazing. Voucher specimens from 2005 have been confirmed by the author of this report but no vouchers of the four specimens recorded in 2008 have been seen.       As small populations of Vertigo geyeri can be easily overlooked it is premature to declare the snail as extinct on Cors Erddreiniog and Cors Geirch. Additionally areas which previously supported very small numbers of snails and not sampled in 2016 need to be surveyed soon. More regular surveys are required in order to advise on appropriate grazing regimes if Vertigo geyeri is found to be \u0027hanging on\u0027 on either site. If this is the case then the development of simplified and affordable surveillance routines is suggested.   Even in the apparent absence of Vertigo geyeri it is vital that areas which previously supported the snail are managed appropriately. Grazing should be re-instated on Nant Isaf spring field as a matter of great urgency and the current over-grazing of Compartment 9 on Cors Erddreiniog should be relaxed. On Cors Geirch the area where Vertigo geyeri was first found in 1996 and again in 1998 and 2005 is too rank and grazing levels need to be increased to open up the seepages and the general sward.\tPublic record\n18848\tNRW_DS124629\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.071Z\tAculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera assemblages at Dyfi National Nature Reserve – Ynyslas Dunes in 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363239\tWelsh Invertebrate Database - Recorder 6\tMike Howe       Mike Howe to supply full metadata\tA survey of the aculeate bees \u0026amp; wasps and flies (Diptera) of Ynyslas Dunes was undertaken in 2019 in an attempt to determine the current condition of this site for these taxonomic groups. A total of 57 aculeate species was recorded 17 of which were to the dunes resulting in a cumulative total of 119 species now known from the site. Only 40 of the 102 previously-recorded aculeates were refound in 2019. 22 of the 38 aculeate species identified as part of the Ynyslas Dunes dune invertebrate assemblage (currently consisting of 141 invertebrate species) were recorded duringthe current survey and four recognized dune-dependent species were added to the site list. Lasioglossum xanthopus was recorded from a 2nd Welsh locality and Osmia bicolor was recorded new to VC46 both representing significant range expansions\tPublic record\n18082\tNRW_DS115312\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.831Z\tSAC Monitoring: Afon Tywi Shad Egg Survey (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333132\t[A]  Data is archived as project 408, media 1429.    [B]  Field notes are held in the Llandeilo Office and MFEG Office.   [C]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Shad egg Survey Afon Tywi 2012 - CCW - Report No. 12/8/4  CCW000800000000P\t\tThe Afon Tywi is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EC 92/43/EEC Habitats Directive for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. In the UK significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn Usk Wye and Tywi.   Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Environment Agency Wales (EAW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of shad populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. The Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) published generic guidance on \u0027Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Freshwater Fauna\u0027 (JNCC 2005) outlining the attributes (mandatory and discretionary) and targets for monitoring freshwater fish. These include targets for shad population river flow water quality and river morphology.  The purpose of this data collection was to facilitate the determination of Favourable Conservation status of Shad in the Afon Tywi. Confidence in this dataset is high as survey were undertaken by experienced staff members.\tPublic record\n18376\tNRW_DS116803\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.413Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Surface Water Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383033\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Surface Water Cycle 2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Surface Water Transfers Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRobert Blacklidge, Principal Officer IEA, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the Surface Water Transfer (SWT) waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes SWT which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline and can be linked to other Water Framework Directive (WFD) data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID). WFD River canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 2 is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.  Updated to 2018 version\tPublic record\n18704\tNRW_DS123298\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.493Z\tAvian flu winter species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323938\tx:\\Incidents\\Avian flu contingency\\Avian flu winter species.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n17184\tEXT_DS121960\t2024-05-28T12:15:59.371Z\tRoyal Commission on the Ancient and Historic Monuments of Wales (RCAHMW): National Monuments Record of Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393630\tX:\\Designations\\RCAHM\\Royal Commission Ancient Historic Monuments.lyr  X:\\Designations\\RCAHM\\WMS for Coflein National Monuments Record for Wales.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tNational Monuments Record of Wales is the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historic Monuments of Wales\u0027 (RACHMW) archive of built heritage around wales. RACHMW have licenced Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for the use of this data. There are over 110000 sites including buildings archaeological industrial and maritime sites throughout Wales.  NRW has access to both the Coflein National Monuments Records for Wales via a Web Mapping Service (WMS) and a layer file of the Royal Commission Ancient Historic Monuments which includes information on board classes for: - Agriculture and Subsistence - Civil - Commemorative - Commercial - Defence - Domestic - Education - Gardens - Health - Maritime - Religious - Water Supply and Drainage - Unassigned\tPublic record\n18738\tNRW_DS123396\t2024-05-28T12:55:19.529Z\tIntertidal and Subtidal Biotopes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333936\tx:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine_Habitat_Vulnerability\\Intertidal_and_Subtidal_Biotopes.lyr\tPaul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17337\tNRW_DS24289\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.928Z\tWelsh Geological Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323839\tFfynnon.\tRaymond Roberts, Senior Earth Scientist  Geologist/Earth Science Officer, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tAn excel spreadsheet containing the records of over 400 geological sites in Wales. Contains site names grid references block name associated SSSIs SSSI code and type of geology county area  etc.  Purpose of data capture:  Information on the spreadsheet was used to set up the Geological Conservation Review (GCR) database but has continued to be updated.\tPrivate record\n17848\tNRW_DS111154\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.661Z\tMarine Habitats and Species Spatial Layers for Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP), NERC Act, OSPAR (Archived)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313534\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP Species Archived X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\tInternal Custodian: - Natasha Lough - Marine Habitats Biologist, Knowledge Evidence and Advice,  Kirsten Ramsay - Subtidal Team Leader, Knowledge Evidence and Advice.   NRW  Metadata Contact: - Spatial Data and GIS Team, NRW\tThis archived collation of datasets has been superseded by Environment (Wales) Act Section 7: Marine Habitats and Species as part of the newer legislative requirements.    These datasets are associated with the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) was published back in 1994 and was the UK Government\u0027s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. The Convention for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic (the \u0027OSPAR Convention\u0027) was open for signature at the Ministerial Meeting of the Oslo and Paris Commissions in Paris on 22 September 1992. The new annex on biodiversity and ecosystems was adopted in 1998 to cover non-polluting human activities that can adversely affect the sea. These two conventions have been highly important for identifying statutory lists of priority species and habitats for Wales. Under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006 it is required that the a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027. The UK BAP is one of the main focuses of this work. As part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 CBD OSPAR Convention and NERC Act there have been a number of initiatives to identify those species and habitats whose UK populations may be under threat and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity.   The list includes all marine UK BAP priorities as well as additional habitats and species to reflect those in need of conservation action in Wales. For each species and habitat data provided it should include a map of the distribution around the Welsh coast and supporting text with information on the ecology rarity legislative protection occurrence in statutory sites and known threats. This dataset is made up of a collation of datasets which have been updated throughout the years. All of these are listed separately under the \u0027Contains\u0027 field below. These datasets related to the marine species and habitats which are of conservation importance for Wales.\tPrivate record\n19136\tNRW_DS125552\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.336Z\tMonitoring the Gipsywort Weevil Datonychus arquatus on Pembrey Air Weapons Range in 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353532\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database stored on Recorder6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe Black Bog Ant Formica picea is rare in the UK where it is associated with bogs and wet heath at a small number of sites in southern England including the heaths of Dorset and Surrey and the valley bogs in the New Forest with a single site in Yorkshire. In Wales it is restricted to Rhossili Down on Gower Cors Goch Llanllwch in Carmarthenshire and Cors Llanerch in Pembrokeshire. It was first recorded on Rhossili Down in 1913 and subsequently refound in 1996. A survey in July 1997 found 49 active nests along four east to west-oriented transects across the bog and flushed wet heath habitats to the east of the central ridge with a further 23 active nests elsewhere on the site. It was estimated thatbetween 840 and 4970 active nests were present across the site representing the largest population in the UK. The current survey in August 2022 repeated the 1997 work with a count of active nests along the four transects. A total of 47 active nests was recorded two less than in 1997 although numbers along each transect varied\tPublic record\n18305\tNRW_DS116443\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.467Z\tPearl Mussel Distribution for Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC) Reporting under Article 17\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343433\t[A]  This data is available on NBN Gateway [B] Collation of records extract from numerous databases and ad hoc records.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of a collation of records of pearl mussels from Welsh rivers.  This dataset is a compilation of numerous records from different sources complied for Habitats Directive Article 17 reporting related to freshwater pearl mussels Margaritifera margaritifera. Every six years Member States of the European Union are required (by Article 17 of the Directive) to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive. The reporting format set by the European Commission requires a separate analysis for each species and each habitat in each biogeographic region which that country covers.  This dataset comprises of Environment Agency and Countryside Council for Wales surveys.  The aim of this collation has been to ensure that the 10 km distribution maps on the NBN accurately reflect the known position of this species. Records have been compiled with this in mind and hence no attempt has been made to collate every single record for each 10km square within the designated time period.\tPublic record\n19159\tNRW_DS125627\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.526Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone Management Zones\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363237\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Skomer MCZ Management Zones\tKate Lock, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer) - kate.lock@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk  Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer - mark.burton@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThis dataset contains multiple layers used to recreate a map of Skomer Marine Conservation Zones with byelaws and codes of conduct zones for: vessel speeds indicative seasonal breeding zones for seals and seabirds sensitive areas for dolphins and porpoises no anchoring areas and anchoring areas.  This dataset contains the following layers:  Skomer Inshore Protection Zone Skomer Nearshore Protection Zone Skomer Seasonal Breeding Zones Seals Skomer Seasonal Breeding Zones Seabirds Skomer No Anchoring Areas Skomer Anchoring Areas Skomer Sensitive Areas for Dolphins and Porpoises\tPublic record\n19022\tNRW_DS125214\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.425Z\tAfon Iwrch Sea Trout Survey Project 1984-1994 (paper data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323134\tOffsite storage - Original Thomas archive code: B0008972 Restore code: EAWC00000000916\tUnknown custodian – could be fisheries team/lab team/EEAT?\tSeries of 7 folders containing field notes relating to Sea Trout smolt.  Sea Trout Trap Smolt visit record sheets contains information such as information about the actual trap physical data (e.g. condition temperature pH) biological data (no. of brown and sea trout species name length markings sex stage).\tPrivate record\n18651\tNRW_DS123288\t2024-05-28T12:53:50.682Z\tNational Flood Risk Assessment 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323838\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Flood Risk Assessment 2013.lyr\tMark Pugh\t\tPrivate record\n18012\tNRW_DS115048\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.864Z\tLlyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys and Gwyniad Translocation 2003\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303438\t[A]  Data is archived as Project 369, Media 1364.   [B]  Species data is available in Recorder 6 Gwyniad Gill netting survey of Llyn Tegid 2003 - CCW Science Report 597  [CCW000800000000E]\t\tThe purpose of this project was to develop a generic Whitefish sampling protocol suitable for Llyn Tegid and produce a condition assessment of the population.  A population survey of Gwyniad was recommended to be undertaken after failed attempts to establish a refuge population in 2003. Project to develop a generic protocol for the assessment of whitefish (Coregonus albula and Coregonus lavaretus) populations developed by Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH) to the Gwyniad of Llyn Tegid during July 2003 with the addition of the determination of oxygen and temperature profiles and Secchi depths.  The protocol involved hydroacoustics and limited gill netting.  This datasets contains the results of the gill netting survey only.\tPublic record\n17817\tNRW_DS111077\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.924Z\tResults of Studies into the Impact of Hydraulic Cockle Dredging on Intertidal Sediment Flat Communities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303737\tData is held within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder. NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.Paper report available from NRW library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above)\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of experimental studies investigating the impact of hydraulic cockle dredging on intertidal sediment flat communities at Lafan Sands North Wales and Blackshaw Flats Solway Firth during 1989 and 1990. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the implications of hydraulic dredging for marine nature conservation.\tPublic record\n17780\tNRW_DS110500\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.132Z\tCompetent Authorities for Fisheries in Welsh Waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353030\ta) Report available from NRW library  b) Available on the hard drives of Clare Eno and Rowland Sharp, NRW Environmental Policy Group c) Archived as project 332, media 1291 (File path: K\\Archive\\Media 1200to1300\\Media 1291\tInternal Contact: Colin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Fisheries, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Removed from public view on the advice of marine fisheries experts as information is dated and could be misleading.\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading  This dataset contains a collation of tables containing information on the competent authorities i.e. the regulatory bodies with current competence for managing fisheries (towed gears scallop fishery static gear- potting mussel fishery cockle fishery) within each marine Natura 2000 site in Welsh waters. The purpose of this data capture was to collate all relevant regulations relating to sea fishing activity in Welsh waters. Information including name of European marine site competent authority relevant legislation \u0027permit\u0027 controls and comments collated by contractor. The regulations documented are those that were in force up to December 2008 there may have been changes to some regulations or new regulations created since. For information on the current regulations the relevant organisations should be contacted.\tPrivate record\n17891\tNRW_DS112289\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.941Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Tide-swept Boulder Habitat in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323839\tThe data are archived as Project No 199, Media No 550 and is stored on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.   H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSCB\\2008 ASML MSCB\tIntertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Rocky reefs (hard substrata) are of considerable conservation importance due the high diversity of the communities they support and as such are included under Annex 1 of the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC) (Hill et al. 1998). They are an abundant feature of the UK coastline especially on western coasts where exposed bedrock predominates. In some areas the substratum may be composed entirely of bedrock whilst on less steeply shelving / gently shelving shores large boulders cobbles gravel sand and mud may be present (Hill et al. 1998). Rocky reefs are extremely variable in terms of their structure and the communities they support with reef communities being influenced by the type of rock and the microhabitats available sediment availability degree of shelter shore height and temperature regime. Communities in exposed areas are usually dominated by sponges anemones and foliose red algae with ascidians delicate filamentous algae and fan worms being more common in sheltered areas (JNCC 2005).\tPublic record\n17894\tNRW_DS112400\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.059Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal habitat diversity (neighbourhood analysis)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343030\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the intertidal range is measured using biotope richness biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is utilised to present these metrics. Biotope information for the Welsh intertidal zone was collated from CCW and is full coverage collected using standardised methods.  Establish intertidal diversity from known data.  The Welsh coastline from the mean high-water datum to the mean low-water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tPublic record\n18181\tNRW_DS116231\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.931Z\tFlood Storage Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323331\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Storage Areas.lyr  Data is extracted from Asset Management eXpert (AMX) software.\tInternal Custodian: Wyn Davies, Flood Risk Manager (Asset Management), Flood and Incident Management Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tFlood Storage Areas show those areas that act as a balancing reservoir storage basin or balancing pond. Their purpose is to attenuate an incoming flood peak to a flow level that can be accepted by the downstream channel. It may also delay the timing of a flood peak so that its volume is discharged over a longer time interval.   Flood Storage Areas that are not yet shown will be gradually added as information becomes available.\tPublic record\n18971\tNRW_DS124865\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.425Z\tCompliance Assessment of Welsh River SACs against Phosphorus Targets\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383635\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Compliance with NRW River SAC Phosphorus Targets 2017-19 by Catchment Waterbodies X:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Phosphorus Sensitive SAC Freshwater Catchments\tTristan Hatton-Ellis\tRevisions to Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) monitoring guidance have required Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to review its conservation objectives for river Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales notably with respect to phosphorus where targets have been substantially tightened.   This data was created to identify compliance with the revised phosphorus targets for Welsh river SACs. Phosphorus concentration data were extracted from the NRW water quality database (KiWQM) for a three-year period from January 2017 to December 2019 for all sample points within water bodies in the nine SACs designated for one or more river features. These were Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn; Afon Eden – Cors Goch Trawsfynydd; Meirionnydd Oakwoods (Afon Glaslyn); Afon Teifi; Afon Tywi; Afonydd Cleddau; River Dee \u0026amp; Llyn Tegid; River Usk; and River Wye. In total 125 water bodies (river sections) were in scope.   Data were excluded where (i) detection limits were inadequate to assess compliance with the target or (ii) where the sample exceeded the recommended holding time of three days prior to analysis. Outliers in the data were identified and if they were considered likely to be causing a failure on their own data from other nutrient pollution indicators (biochemical oxygen demand nitrate and ammonia) were checked to corroborate possible pollution events.   The data consists of two products: 1. An Excel spreadsheet summarising results and 2. An ArcGIS layer consisting of the WFD catchment area water bodies clipped to the Welsh border and excluding water bodies not overlapping with river SAC boundaries.\tPublic record\n18382\tNRW_DS116724\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.533Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) (Cardiff) Bat Casework Files 2001 to 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373234\tThis data is held and managed by SEWBREC - NRW own the data but SEWBREC manage it on our behalf as a top copy.  A copy of the data is also available in Recorder 6 but currently only from 2001 to 2005 (12/08/2016) We have received a whole update including all years - however this has not yet been put into Recorder 6. Instead at the moment this is on the NBN Gateway as part of the sensitive data that SEWBREC hold (see parent ID). LC0002150000001R CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2001 LC0002150000001Z CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2002 LC00021500000021 CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2003 LC00021500000028 CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2004 LC0002150000002L CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2005\tContact: Data Services Team, NRW   External Contact:  David Slade Senior IT and Records Officer,  South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre,  13 St Andrews Crescent, Cardiff CF10 3DB Tel: 029 2064 1110 Fax: 0843 2652714  Email: david.slade@sewbrec.org.uk www.sewbrec.org.uk\tThis dataset comprises of bat records extracted from casework files related to the Cardiff area. The top copy of this dataset is held and managed by South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre (SEWBReC) who manage the data on behalf of Natural Resources Wales who continue to own the data.\tPublic record\n18006\tNRW_DS115123\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.421Z\tSurveys of Potential Oystercatcher Prey, Pembrey Sands (2009)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313233\tData is archived as Project 371, media 1368\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Maritime Ornithologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) are studying the food requirements of oystercatcher and knot to develop monitoring targets and to assess the implications of shellfish fisheries on the bird populations. As part of that study surveys of the cockle and mussel stocks in the Burry Inlet have been carried out every autumn since 2004 using a standard methodology. Significant populations of oystercatcher also feed in the Three Rivers and along Pembrey Sands and it is known that they are part of the same population that feed in the Burry Inlet SPA but the information is limited. Data from the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) on oystercatcher distribution along the Pembrey coast showed that they concentrate in the central section of the Sands and confirmed that they are feeding at low tide. It is known from previous survey work that the sediment fauna found along the Pembrey coast are likely to include a variety of shellfish (but not cockles and mussels) however data is limited. The purpose of this data capture was to provide data on the abundance and distribution of the potential prey species for the oystercatcher along the Pembrey Sands due to limited data availability. This data aimed to determine the potential prey of the oystercatcher in Pembrey Sands examining the abundance and distribution of the sediment fauna.  Previous findings showed that high numbers of the main prey species of the oyster catchers (cockles and mussels) were not found in the area.\tPublic record\n18329\tNRW_DS116538\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.399Z\tRiver Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC) habitat structure condition assessment 2013 - 2015\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353338\t[A] Reports and database exports held internally on the DMS using the following pathway: Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting (SMAR) \u0026gt; freshwater monitoring Running waters Habitats Directive data Cycle 3.  [B] Spatial data currently on W drive Heather Garrett Wye RHS but waiting for guidance to move to Y drive. [C] Species data held on BIOSYS \u0026amp; Environment Agency RHS database.\tData Custodian:  Heather Garrett, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team Melissa Lacan, Senior Env Monitoring Officer (Biology) Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R   Internal Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the condition assessment of the River Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC) physical river habitat quality assessed using River Habitat Survey (RHS) and LEAFPAC (river macrophyte) survey data between 2012 and 2016. NRW is responsible for monitoring the condition of the river habitat quality on SAC rivers designated for the Ranunculion feature; more formally known as “Water courses of plain to montane levels with the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion vegetation” (EU habitat code 3260). Habitats Directive and NRW is  required to evaluate the conservation status of this river habitat. The Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance (JNCC 2014) sets out eight river habitat quality assessment attributes for the condition of this SAC feature. Specific details for RHS survey methods are detailed in Environment Agency. 2003. The River Habitat Survey manual. Details for the LEAFPAC macrophyte survey method are found in Environment Agency. 2008. Surveying freshwater macrophytes in rivers. Operational instruction 131-07.\tPublic record\n17876\tNRW_DS111197\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.871Z\tRemote Sensing Phase 1 Habitat Survey Update\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313937\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Remote sensed Phase 1\\Phase 1 remote sensed.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Senior Remote Sensing Manager, Sustainable Communities, NRW Metadata Custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUp-to-date habitat survey of Wales produced by analysis of remote sensing data. The dataset is comprised of contiguous habitat polygons classified into phase 1 categories. It is the product of a large scale remote sensing project aimed at updating the Phase 1 habitat survey carried out in Wales between 1979 and 1999. It was produced in order to have key dataset for Wales which can be used to inform the core work of former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and its partners and map specialist habitats or interpretations of habitats.  The maps will be checked through field visits and feedback from CCW staff with local knowledge.\tPublic record\n18637\tNRW_DS122923\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.648Z\tCrayfish WFD catchments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393233\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Crayfish WFD catchments.lyr\tNatasha Lough\t\tPrivate record\n17156\tEXT_DS121864\t2024-05-28T12:15:04.480Z\tMarine Conservation Society: Beachwatch data Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383634\tNon Spatial Data: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/Data-Management/dai/Third%20Party%20Data/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Marine Conservation Society\\MCS Beachwatch marine litter 2015 to17 WGS84.lyr\tBusiness Contacts are: Matt Green (marine) Harriet Robinson (marine)  GIS and Spatial Data Team for spatial layer. Data Services for Licence and non spatial layer.  Marine Conservation Society for original data.\tThis is a Marine Conservation Society non-spatial dataset the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has for internal use only. NRW has converted this non-spatial dataset into a spatial data layer for their corporate internal use. Beachwatch is Marine Conservation Society\u0027s national beach cleaning and litter survey programme helping people all around the UK to care for their coastline. This dataset contains the results of the cleaning a litter programme between 2015 and 2017.\tPublic record\n18736\tNRW_DS123418\t2024-05-28T12:55:19.355Z\tMostyn Marine Energy Park (MEP) consented\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343138\tX:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Port of Mostyn Casework\\Mostyn MEP consented.lyr ,  x:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine Reports\\Mostyn\\Mostyn MEP consented.lyr\tData originally supplied by Paul Brazier, due to role changes, now managed within the Marine Area Advice and Management Team, DPAS and Marine Services\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17321\tEXT_DS124834\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.309Z\tWales and West Utilities: Gas Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383334\tX:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Intermediate pressure gas main.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Low pressure gas main.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Medium pressure gas main.lyr\t\tWales and West Utilities gas data showing the location of Gas Pipes across Wales. NRW has access to the following:  Intermediate Pressure Gas Main Pipelines Low Pressure Gas Main Pipelines High Pressure Gas Main Pipelines\tPublic record\n17058\tEXT_DS100843\t2024-05-28T12:10:09.639Z\tWoodland Trust: Sites (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383433\tx:\\Ownership\\Woodland Trust\\Woodland Trust Sites.lyr\tGIS Service Desk External Contact: The Woodland Trust GIS Department\tThis is a GIS dataset of all Woodland Trust property boundaries. It is an external third party dataset supplied and owned by the Woodland Trust and licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use only.\tPublic record\n18099\tNRW_DS115574\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.894Z\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay (2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353734\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466) Marine Recorder Database:  2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey Liverpool Bay, August MRCCW31600000014\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: (Mike Kaiser) Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University.\tSurveys of seabed in Liverpool Bay carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in August 2013 as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (9 tows) collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 8 and 27 km offshore north of Bangor to Colwyn Bay. The tow lengths ranged from 216 m to 611 m and seabed consisted primarily of sands and gravels with some areas of pebbes.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes.  The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.\tPublic record\n18688\tNRW_DS123314\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.104Z\tPPC Installation Boundary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333134\tx:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\PPC Installation Boundary.lyr\tJulie Boswell\t\tPrivate record\n18242\tNRW_DS116289\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.181Z\tShoreline Management Plan and Coastal Erosion (archived data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323839\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk/Coastal Erosion.lyr\tLouise Pennington / Mark Pugh: Flood Risk Team.\tThis dataset has been archived and is no longer maintained please see dataset NRW_DS121852 \"National Coastal Erosion Risk Management (NCERM)\" for more up to date information.  The datasets shows the spatial coastal baseline which is split into \u0027frontages\u0027. These are defined as lengths of coast with consistent characteristics based on the cliff behaviour characteristics and the defence characteristics. It is intended as a benchmark dataset showing erosion extents and rates for three periods:    -  Short Term (0 - 20 yr)  -  Medium Term (20 - 50 yr) and  -  Long Term (50 - 100 yr).    Defence type and Shoreline Management Plan (SMP) policies for each of the three periods described above are included.\tPublic record\n17637\tNRW_DS109804\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.049Z\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Intertidal Area of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383034\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal Data is archived as project 204, media 569 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 569)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Internal Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a static spatial dataset.  The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications. This particular dataset maps the whole intertidal area of Wales as a polygon to calculate total area.\tPublic record\n17873\tNRW_DS111311\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.791Z\tEstablishment of Permanent Quadrats at Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ )\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333131\tPaper report is available from CCW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on CCWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000068)\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  A detailed survey of the shores of Skomer Marine Nature Reserve was carried out in 1982. This dataset contains the results of an exercise carried out in 1992 to repeat some of the littoral transects surveyed in 1982 and establish permanent quadrats at six sites. The purpose of this data capture was to:  - to establish six permanent littoral monitoring sites at locations surveyed in the 1982 littoral survey.  - survey the sites and where appropriate compare with the results of the 1982 littoral survey.  - photograph the general appearance of the site for relocation purposes and photograph the quadrats at specified intervals along the transects at each site.\tPublic record\n18589\tNRW_DS122722\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.835Z\tRecreation Places to Go\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373232\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Recreation Places to Go.lyr\tCustodian: Heather Crump, Advisor (2) Recreation Content, Estates Recreation Planning Team\tVectors (points) depicting the location of managed or co-managed sites by NRW which are listed on the Recreation Places to Go external map browser.  Information includes site name grid reference subtitle and facilities.\tPublic record\n18100\tNRW_DS115601\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.917Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: \u0027European Dry Heath\u0027, \u0027Northern Atlantic Wet Heath with Erica tetralix\u0027 and \u0027Blanket Bog\u0027 at the Cadair Idris candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363031\tThe associated data is on the H Drive at;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\11 European dry heath, N. Atlantic wet heath at Cadair Idris\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.\tThis dataset details the findings of a two part project to assess the condition of the European dry heath wet heath and blanket bog habitats within the Cadair Idris candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) . The work was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and carried out by Central Environmental Surveys (CES) during November 2003.  The purpose of this data capture was to map the dry heath wet heath and blanket bog habitats including condition classes of these within the Cadair Idris cSAC to a uniform specification.\tPublic record\n17240\tEXT_DS121990\t2024-05-28T12:17:05.570Z\tWelsh Government: Technical Advice Note 8 (TAN8) Wind Energy Strategic Search Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393930\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\TAN8 Areas.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is owned and managed by Welsh Government and is licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for use. Technical Advice Notes (TAN8) provides advice on areas including onshore renewable energy technologies design and energy and how renewable energy should be accounted for as part of development plans development management and monitoring processes.\tPublic record\n19054\tNRW_DS125305\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.650Z\tTranquillity and Place: Visually Tranquil Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333035\tX:Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquility and Place\tJill Bullen - Specialist Advisor Agriculture\tA nationally consistent terrestrial Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource that identifies visually tranquil areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent practice and provision for well-being benefits.   Storymap link https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/865c1876d9f64280a3dfc6e2769a46a5    Tranquillity is associated with the degree to which places and ecosystems deliver a state of quiet calm peace and well-being. This can be described as a relative abundance perception or experience of nature natural landscapes and features and/or a relative freedom from unwanted visual disturbance signs of human influence and artificial noise.\tPublic record\n17533\tNRW_DS101834\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.432Z\tLlyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys and Gwyniad Translocations 2004-2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383334\t[A]  Data is Archived under Project Number: 29, 154, 194 ,226, 227, 200 Media Numbers 169, 468, 539, 595, 232, 952   [B]  Species Data is also stored in Recorder 6: CCW000800000000D Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill netting survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2009 - CCW Science Report 904   [C]  Hardcopies are archived in CCW HQ fireproof safe: stack 05.\t\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains the results of a project established in 2004 to carry out translocation hydroacoustic surveys and measurement of environmental variables. The purpose of this data capture was to consider the implications for the status of gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) and its habitat in Llyn Tegid. The objectives of the project include both conservation actions and further research including the establishment of a refuge population in Llyn Arenig Fawr. Surveys were carried out annually from 2004 - 2009.  Following a first unsuccessful translocation attempt in 2003 three successful translocations have taken place- in early 2005 2006 and 2007. A full fish population survey of the recipient site of Llyn Arenig Fawr was undertaken in 2009.\tPublic record\n18404\tNRW_DS118585\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.223Z\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138353835\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS118585\tMetadata point of Contact: Data Services Custodian: Jane Roberts-  Environmental Reporting Team\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches.  This is now a static dataset and 2009 was the final year of the scheme.\tPublic record\n18969\tNRW_DS124869\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.435Z\tWater Resource Reliability\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383639\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Resource Reliability Wales.lyr\tEmma Amos -   Specialist Advisor Water Resources\tThe Resource Reliability data indicates the percentage of time additional consumptive abstraction may be available and what this means for abstraction licensing. The Resource Reliability data is based on a nationally consistent method. Results have been overridden to reflect local assessments in our Abstraction Licensing Strategies (ALS) published on our website. ALS outline the implications for abstraction licensing of resource reliability.\tPublic record\n17858\tNRW_DS111562\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.661Z\tFluvial Audit of the Afon Tywi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353632\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Hydrology_Reports\\fluvial_morphology\\Tywi Fluvial AuditData H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media901to1000\\Media951 Archived under Project 257 Media 951.\tInternal Custodians: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Senior Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group Rhian Thomas, Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group  Metadata Contact: Data Services for Archived Data or GIS and Spatial Data Team for GIS data locations\tThe Afon Tywi in Carmarthenshire is a large actively eroding river designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for its migratory fish and as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for various features including river habitat river shingle invertebrates and little ringed plover. The river is unusually active in the middle reaches sometimes moving as much as 50 m in a single year and the various species for which the river is designated depend on the river remaining active. In order to inform current and future river management a fluvial audit of the past and present geomorophology of the Afon Tywi was carried out. This consists of a detailed technical report including identification of the most active river sections based on historic maps a timeline of the river indicating key events such as major floods and maps showing the geomorphology and sediment character of the river. Advice is also provided on the future sustainable management of the Tywi as an active shingle river. This dataset consists of the results from the Fluvial Audit of the Afon Tywi which describes and spatially maps the past and present fluvial geomorphology of the Tywi catchment. The aim of this data capture was to:  - Develop an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the Afon Tywi. Specific aims include: - Describe and map the fluvial geomorphology of the Tywi catchment past and present using a GIS system. - Interpret the data gathered in the context of past and present human activity including a risk assessment of consented activities. - Provide advice on the future management of the Tywi SAC for use as a management and casework decision-making tool with regards to future bank reinforcement work flood defence activities aggregate extractions etc.\tPublic record\n17101\tEXT_DS111151\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.518Z\tLittoral Hard Substrata of the Menai Strait (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313531\tThe survey data has been entered into JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000129)\tGIS Service Desk\tThis survey was undertaken as part of a general study of the Menai Strait commissioned by JNCC required to assist in decisions related to its designation as one of Britain\u0027s Marine Nature Reserves. Parallel surveys of the soft substrata and sublittoral hard substrata have also been conducted. The dataset includes detailed descriptions of the flora and fauna found on the littoral hard substrate of the Menai Strait detailed site descriptions (including geological and topographical information and notes on special interest access and pressures) and flora and faunal lists covering all substrata in the straits. The purpose of this data capture was to provide a topographical and biological description of the Anglesey and Arfon sides of the Menai Strait concentrating on the hard or more particularly stable substrata and the animals and plants which live in these habitats.\tPublic record\n18356\tNRW_DS117097\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.195Z\tThe Status of the Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Yr Wyddfa (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137303937\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in the Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe\tThe Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis is restricted in the United Kingdom to Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa) although it was also found in Cwm Idwal until 1980. As recent records of the beetle have been few a survey was undertaken in 2015 (NRW_DS116513) in an attempt to determine its status and distribution. A total of five beetles (four larvae and one adult) was found following 63.5 hours of hand searching on and around thyme plants (the only recorded foodplant of the beetle in the UK) and stone-turning near patches of thyme from June to September 2015 with all specimens found in Clogwyn Coch in September. No beetles were found in Cwm du\u0027r Arddu its traditional hotspot despite exhaustive searches.   A continuation of the survey from May to October 2016 located ten adults and one larva following 69 hours of search with all adults recorded in Clogwyn Coch and the larva found under a stone in Cwm Glas Bach. The 2016 survey reinforces the suggestion that the beetle may be retreating to higher altitudes perhaps as a consequence of climate change or other factors. Over the two year period 16 beetles (11 adults and 5 larvae) have been found after more than 130 hours of search. As in 2015 no beetles were found in Cwm Idwal.\tPublic record\n18490\tNRW_DS121556\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.928Z\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Natural Processes Woodlands\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353536\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Natural Processes woodland.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\t\tPrivate record\n18106\tNRW_DS115575\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.030Z\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Caernarfon Bay (2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353735\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466) Marine Recorder survey: 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Caernarfon Bay, July MRCCW31600000016\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: (Mike Kaiser) Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences,  Bangor University.\tSurveys of seabed in Caernarfon Bay carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in July 2013 as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (11 tows) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 8 and 25 km offshore in the centre of Caernarfon Bay.  The tow lengths ranged from 150m to 500m and seabed consisted primarily of sands and gravels with some pebbles and cobbles.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.  The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Caernarfon Bay and assign biotopes.\tPublic record\n17484\tNRW_DS100779\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.536Z\tRAS Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373739\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\physical\\hydrology\\environment_agency\\industrial_sites\\ras_authorisations_sites.TAB\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to CCW for internal use only.  The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains Sites under Radioactive Substance control (point data)\tPrivate record\n18331\tNRW_DS116527\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.412Z\tA survey of calaminarian grassland in Mid-Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353237\tThe Excel sheets are on the DMS in Evidence\u0026gt;Terrestrial Habitats\u0026gt;Lowland Grassland\u0026gt;Calaminarian – survey 2014 (marked FINAL).  The ARC data is more difficult as they are currently on Stuart Smiths Y drive but I assume will need moving to a more permanent and accessible location (ie X Drive).\tInternal Custodian:  Stuart Smith Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe aims of this project were to refine guidance on calaminarian habitat definition for mid-Wales and to provide data and information on the extent of the different forms. The purpose of this data capture was to assess calaminarian grassland habitat types condition and extent for each site and to gather information on the distribution and frequency of metallophytes and other notable species. This was done by detailed survey of the plants and lichens of the calaminarian grasslands on 27 abandoned mine sites in mid-Wales 26 in Ceredigion and 1 in Montgomeryshire with mapping of the compartments in GIS. A wide variation in the character of calaminarian habitat was observed including grass-dominated examples metallophyte-rich heathland and largely-bare highly-contaminated land.  Some of the vegetation was considered distinctive in both a British and European context. A total of 90 vascular plants 38 bryophytes 227 lichens and 15 lichenicolous fungi were recorded including many species of conservation concern. Categories of calaminarian habitat types in mid Wales are presented to facilitate future mapping of the habitat.\tPublic record\n18317\tNRW_DS116335\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.977Z\tHazardous Waste Interrogator\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333335\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\tTim Morris, Senior Waste Advisor (Hazardous Waste), Waste \u0026amp; Resources Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales is legally required to monitor all movements of hazardous waste in Wales. Hazardous waste producers are required to register with us and the site where the waste is disposed or recovered is required to inform us of the details of the wastes they receive.  Hazardous waste producer data is commercially confidential. However a summary of the movements is provided in this Hazardous Waste Interrogator. Only high-level waste classification geographical locations (where the waste was produced and where the waste management facility is located) and tonnage is included. Individual site names and producers details are not included.\tPublic record\n18939\tNRW_DS124805\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.850Z\tAlien Taxa Environmental and Socio-economic impact classification\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383035\tData held in the DMS at the following locations:  EICAT spreadsheet: EVID-363697614-2272  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/EMFF%20INNS%20Project/EXTERNAL%20CONTRACTS/Marine%20Biological%20Association%20-%20Impacts%20of%20INNS/MBA%20final%20report/MBA_EICAT_042020.xlsx?d\u003dw1d3200d4e3824d73a6c21125ab1de07d    SEICAT Spreadsheet: EVID-363697614-2271: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/EMFF%20INNS%20Project/EXTERNAL%20CONTRACTS/Marine%20Biological%20Association%20-%20Impacts%20of%20INNS/MBA%20final%20report/MBA_SEICAT_assessment_042020.xls?d\u003dw11cbbbd4221242d2be394c94583c320f\tInternal Custodian: Chloe Powell-Jennings, Senior Advisor (EMFF Biosecurity Project Officer), Southwest Operations Internal Custodian: Katherine Griffith, Senior Marine Advisor, Marine Area Management \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThere are two excel spreadsheets which accompany the report titled ‘Assessing the impact of key Marine Invasive Non-Native Species on Welsh Marine Protected Area habitat features fisheries and aquaculture’. These are the Environmental Impact Classification of Alien Taxa (EICAT) and the Socio-economic Impact Classification of Alien Taxa (SEICAT) which summarise and provide additional information data for the report.  The purpose of the spreadsheets (along with the accompanying report) is to assess potential impacts of 16 invasive non-native species on Welsh Marine Protected Area features commercial fisheries and aquaculture to address knowledge gaps for biosecurity planning.   The EICAT Excel workbook consists of a summary matrix for the 16 invasive non-native species providing a summary score for likelihood of establishment and an aggregated (worst-case) score for impact on each of the 41 Marine Protected Area features assessed by the project.    The SEICAT Excel workbook consists of a summary matrix for the 16 invasive non-native species providing a summary impact score for direct impacts on fisheries and aquaculture via interactions which affect deployment and operation of gear and installations and impacts on target or cultivated species. It does not consider indirect impacts or ancillary activities (for example impacts on gaining access or impacts on bait).    A number of general limitations regarding the impact assessments should be considered in their application:  •The impact assessments are generic and NOT site-specific. They are based on the likely effects of an INNS pressure on a ‘hypothetical’ MPA feature/ biotope based on general characteristics of these features. •The impact assessments are general assessments that indicate the likely effects of a given INNS (likely to arise from one or more impact pathways) on MPA features or aquaculture and fisheries operations as represented in Wales. •The assessments have attempted where possible to take account of spatial or temporal scales.  •The significance of impacts arising from pressures also needs to take account of the scale of the features. •There are limitations in the scientific evidence on the biology and ecology of MPA features and their responses to INNS on which the impact assessments have been based. •The EICAT impact assessment methodology takes account of the level of impact and the likely recovery to differentiate between the two most severe impact ranks (massive and major). Eradication of INNS is difficult and recovery will generally only occur where management measures are implemented. There is the potential therefore for all major impacts to become massive. •As a general rule where the impact is ranked as higher the need for management measures is greater. •A rank of ‘Minor or Minimal Concern does not mean that no impact is possible from a particular ‘INNS x feature’ combination only that a limited impact was judged to be likely based on the available evidence.\tPublic record\n18369\tNRW_DS116725\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.399Z\tFreshwater pearl mussel (Margaritifera margaritifera) larval encystment of host fish species on the Afon Eden – Cors Goch Trawsfynydd Special Area of Conservation (SAC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373235\tData held on a site by site basis on word and excel documents on DMS.\tInternal Contact; Heather Garret, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, Monitoring Strategy and Planning Team, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe freshwater pearl mussel (Margaritifera margaritifera) is protected under several tiers of national and international legislation; EU Habitats and Species Directive Annexes II and V UK Biodiversity Action Plan list of priority species UK Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 Schedule 5 Bern Convention Appendix 3 IUCN Red Data List as Critically Endangered. The Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd is the only Special Area of Conservation (SAC) site in Wales where the freshwater pearl mussel Margaritifera margaritifera forms the primary reason for selection of a site. Each year during early summer and when the water temperature is suitable the female adult mussel releases larvae (glochidia) into the river where they are either ingested or inhaled by host salmonid fish. The species of host fish is specific to each pearl mussel population and prior to these encystment surveys it had been assumed that Atlantic salmon Salmo salar were the host for the Afon Eden population. The results of four annual surveys for gill encystment has proven that brown trout (including sea trout) Salmo trutta is the host species for the Afon Eden population. This dataset contains the results of extent of encystment during electrofishing surveys for salmonid species.\tPublic record\n17235\tEXT_DS122165\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.640Z\tWelsh Government: Assisted Areas Objectives (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313635\tx:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Objective 1.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Objective 2 Trans.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Objective 2.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Objective 3.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Objective 5 Tran.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Welsh Government and supplied to NRW under an licence agreement. These dataset feed into the Assisted Areas which are places where government can offer additional financial support to businesses under European Commission state aid rules. Assisted areas are recognised in European state aid rules as being less economically advantaged places that would benefit from additional support for development. As a result financial support from Government is permitted to undertakings typically businesses for new investments in these areas. Being located in an Assisted Area does not confer any right to financial assistance; rather it allows the public sector to provide certain types of assistance if it wishes. These areas have been split into regional areas where the level of funding allocated is defined by the objective number.\tPublic record\n18308\tNRW_DS116441\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.930Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Boundaries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343431\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Boundaries.lyr\tGIS Service Desk South IDDs - Louise Harris, GIS/Engineering Technician, Spatial Data, KSP Directorate, NRW North IDDs - James West, Advisor- IDB Transition, Flood Risk Strategy, NRW       19/09/2019 Latest info from James West to Data Distribution is that NRW are still waiting for WG to draft an Order confirming the Scheme submitted to them in Feb 2018 to alter the boundaries of 8 (out of 10) IDDs. Until WG do this data will remain unchanged. Metadata will need to be updated when the new boundaries are submitted Contacted James West July 2022.The data on the IDD boundaries Dataset (on the X Drive) is still current. NRW is still awaiting the authorisation of our proposed (very minor) boundary changes we submitted to WG in Feb 2018\tContains the boundaries of all 14 Internal Drainage District\u0027s within wales. Mapped for the purposes of the management of Flood Defences Water Levels and Flood Risk. Identifies the area which the Board is responsible for preventing flooding managing water levels and flood defences. Digitised as polygons.\tPublic record\n18578\tNRW_DS122202\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.839Z\tWales\u0027 grey seal photo-identification database: EIRPHOT (1992-2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323032\tReports and outputs held in the DMS.  Database archived as project 480, media 1557.  Listed on the MS Access database plan to move to new NRW MS access storage/transactional area or MUP.\tTom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist\tThis dataset is a catalogue of seal images that were collected as part of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) seal monitoring and evidence work.   Images were also provided by volunteers partners organisations and members of the public through collaboration with CCW/NRW. The images are of heads necks and flanks of grey seals (mostly female) photographed at seal haul out sites around the Celtic and Irish Seas from 1992 to 2016 and show the pelage markings of seals that can be individually recognised/identified from subsequent photographic encounters – photo-identification (Capture Mark Recapture).   The database contains over 17000 photographic images of more than 9000 grey seals. Photo-ID allows individual seals to be captured through time and space and analyses of the data provide insights into site fidelity and associations movements and connectivity and longevity/survival.  This entry supersedes metadata entry 115548 which was created in 2013.\tPublic record\n18700\tNRW_DS123313\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.296Z\tPPC Air Emissions Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333133\tx:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\PPC Air Emissions Points.lyr\tJulie Boswell\t\tPrivate record\n19017\tNRW_DS125196\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.482Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Surface Water Transfer Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313936\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Surface Water Transfers Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the Surface Water Transfer (SWT) waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes SWT which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline and can be linked to other Water Framework Directive (WFD) data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID). WFD River canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 3 is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.\tPublic record\n17297\tEXT_DS124279\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.350Z\tLocal Authorities: Conwy Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323739\tArchived from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Conwy Wildlife Sites.lyr  in 2019, retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact; Geospatial team\tConwy Wildlife Sites 3rd party data and property of local authority.  Archived 2019 the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\tPublic record\n17685\tNRW_DS110082\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.971Z\tSpatial Analysis of Intertidal and Subtidal Biotopes in Welsh Waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303832\tArchived as project 203, media number 568 on backed up server-based storage at the Countryside Council for Wales (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media501to600\\Media568)\tInternal Custodian: NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tMaps depicting the distribution of marine biotopes are now available for the whole of the Welsh intertidal and most of the seabed in Welsh Territorial Waters.  The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Phase 1 intertidal database provides the former; and the CCW HABMAP database provides the latter.    Using those databases while taking account of their limitations CCW wish to know the proportions of each major biotope group that lies within protected areas  - i.e. Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs) and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  This report provides a spatial analysis of those biotope data. Various data issues mean that these are only very rough calculations; the percentages are not definitive values.\tPublic record\n18772\tNRW_DS123453\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.098Z\tGround Water Authorised SheepDip Pesticides\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343533\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Quality\\Ground Water Authorised SheepDip Pesticides.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18697\tNRW_DS123311\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.268Z\tMine Water Discharges\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333131\tx:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Mine Water Discharges.lyr\tTrystan James\t\tPrivate record\n17744\tNRW_DS110466\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.702Z\tPickleridge Lagoon Soft Sediment Infauna Survey (2006 - 2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343636\t[A]  All data is archived under project no. 241,  media no 764.   [B]  A working copy is held on S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Lagoons\\Pickleridge\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Technical Specialist, MFSG. Assisstant Marine Conservation Officer, Skomer. Bill Sanderson, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Pickleridge lagoon which is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The purpose of this data capture was to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature.\tPublic record\n18211\tNRW_DS116257\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.465Z\tRisk of Flooding from Reservoirs\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323537\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Maximum Flood Extent.lyr\tData custodian - Phillip Morgan, Team Leader, Reservoir Regulation\tNRW is responsible for assuring the safety of the Welsh reservoirs by enforcing the Reservoirs Act 1975. Simplified inundation mapping was carried out nationally by the Environment Agency to provide a baseline assessment of all reservoirs falling within the Act. This work is now carried out by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Reservoirs Flood Maps – Spatial Data are referred to externally as Risk of Flooding from Reservoirs.\tPublic record\n18253\tNRW_DS116270\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.982Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323730\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2 X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Lakes Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS.\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 2 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive.   Article 2 clause 5 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...a body of standing inland surface water\u0027. There is data on the physical characteristics risk classification and proposed objectives that can be linked to waterbodies by their unique identifiers.   Artificial and modified lake waterbodies are included within this dataset however generally only lakes above \u0026gt; 50 hectares were assessed under the WFD except for lakes in protected areas where a minimum of 5.0 ha was used. Lakes below this threshold are not included within this dataset unless allocated as Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).   Each waterbody has been assigned \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 which is a unique identifier that enables a link to WFD attributes. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive.   Updated to 2018 version.\tPublic record\n18089\tNRW_DS115616\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.926Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Greater Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) in the Wye Valley (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363136\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\20 Greater horseshoe bats in the Wye Valley\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Wye Valley and Forest of Dean Bats Sites Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is considered one of the best areas in the UK for both Greater Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) and Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros). The activity patterns of greater horseshoe bats roosting at Newton Court Stable Block Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) were investigated over a ten-day period during August 2004 in order to ascertain key foraging areas flight paths and night roosting sites. Ten bats eight lactating females and two juvenile males were radio-tagged and followed for the duration of the study period. Greena Ecological Consultancy was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales to identify and assess the condition of principal flight routes and key foraging areas used by the colony in order to develop Performance Indicators. A condition survey of the actual and potential flight lines within 500 m of the Newton Court Stable Block was carried out a paper map and MapInfo file has been compiled allowing future condition monitoring by the lengths of flight features present.\tPublic record\n18566\tNRW_DS122192\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.163Z\tHydrological Tools Datasets\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313932\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro Rasters.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Adjoint_Catchments.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Catchments.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Drainage_Lines.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_DRN.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_DRN_Nodes.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Extent.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Terrain_5m_Terrain.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydrological Models.lyr\tBen Anscombe,  GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a collation of datasets used from hydrological modelling within Natural Resources Wales. This collation is a mixture of terrain raster and vector data which allows more advanced modelling to occur around the hydrology of a catchment and watershed.   These datasets were created by Natural Resources Wales for use in the following tools:   Generic Hydro Tools: Used to model the path of raindrops as part of watershed analyses.  Hydropower Tools: used for hydro developments to model the effects of water abstractions and input on flow speed and directions and therefore the possible effects this might have on species that are effected by these changes to the watercourse.   This helps users build a 3D profile and therefore model different scenarios.\tPrivate record\n17394\tNRW_DS87090\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.335Z\tAcross Wales Drop-down Video monitoring 2004-2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837303930\t[A]  Also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW HQ as project no. 35, Media 175.   [B]  Fire-proof storage facilities at CCW HQ on optical media. [C]  Species data held in Marine Recorder [MRCCW31600000002]\tData Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Team, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tDrop-down video surveys of Special Area of Conservation (SACs) across Wales (Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Cardigan Bay and Pembrokeshire).  Work carried out by Seastar Survey under contract to Countryside Council of Wales (CCW).  The data was collected to enable site monitoring to support management and reporting of SACs in Wales. The monitoring of subtidal aspects of SACs will indicate whether the desired condition of the feature of interest for which the site was designated is being achieved. The work will also enable judgements to be made about whether the management of the site is appropriate. Analysis of video recordings from the 2005 survey will be used to produce descriptions of the seabed environment including substrata and faunal communities as well as classifying the different sites into biotopes according to Connor et al. (2004). Survey areas within the SACs focused on north Pen Llyn Bardsey Island Tremadog Bay and Moelfre in north Wales areas off Cardigan Island Cemaes Head and between Aberaeron and Llangranog (off Newquay) within Cardigan Bay and Hats and Barrels Reef Bishops and Clerks Skokholm Island and the Castlemartin ridges in Pembrokeshire.\tPublic record\n17541\tNRW_DS101995\t2024-05-28T12:26:24.817Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata in Cardigan Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393935\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 548 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2004 IECS\\Sabellaria\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact:  Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats).  Honeycomb worms build tubes from sand and shell fragments.  They are found on the lower part of rocky seashores but they also need a sand supply for tube-building. Dense congregations of Sabellaria alveolata tubes form large biogenic reef structures which can be up to a metre thick and take the form of hummocks sheets or more massive formations.  The purpose of this data capture was to identify specific survey locations and to measure the extent of Sabellaria alveolata reef along agreed transects and assess the quality of Sabellaria alveolata reef present and map and make notes on any sightings of wireweed Sargassum muticum.   Confidence in this dataset is high as surveys were undertaken by experienced staff.\tPublic record\n18720\tNRW_DS123339\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.786Z\tWIMS DCWW Discharges\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333339\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\WIMS DCWW Discharges.lyr\tData originally created and supplied by Simon Webb, please now refer to the water quality permitting team for relevance.\tThis data was likely to have been extracted from the WIMS system in 2016 to create a spatial dataset and is currently static.  WIMS has been replaced with the NRW\u0027s Permitting And Licencing System (PALS).\tPrivate record\n18284\tNRW_DS116304\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.478Z\tEnforcement Action against Corporate Entities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333034\tData is extracted from information stored in the National Enforcement Database (NEDS) from 01/01/2001 to 31/12/2015 and from 01/01/2016 to present in the Collins Database.\tCustodian: Helen White Governance / Stuart Kay Master Reference data\tConvictions (excluding those under appeal and for avoidance of doubt not including acquittals) and formal cautions data filtered to limit information to corporate entities only. Data available commences from 1/1/2000. Updates available on a quarterly basis.\tPublic record\n20252\tNRW_DS161337\t2025-02-12T15:01:18.392Z\tMonitoring of the Atlantic Woodland Bryophyte Assemblage in Coed Graig Uchaf SSSI\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333337\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoed%20Graig%20Uchaf%20Bryophyte%20Monitoring%202024\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam, Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tCoed Graig Uchaf SSSI (32.5 ha) was designated in 1987 and was selected as an example of a woodland with a rich oceanic bryophyte flora.  The first comprehensive bryophyte survey of the site was undertaken during 2014 when baseline monitoring data was collected. The aim of this 2024 study was to undertake the first re-monitoring of the Atlantic woodland bryophyte assemblage and assess what changes may have occurred  This dataset shows bryophyte presence data from 10x10m grid squares in Coed Graig Uchaf SSSI.\tPublic record\n17060\tEXT_DS100803\t2024-05-28T12:10:09.626Z\tWelsh Government: Local Authorities in Wales - Village Greens (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383033\tX:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Village Greens area.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Village Greens line.lyr    Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\access\\commons\\village_greens\\vilage_greens.TAB\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a GIS dataset owned by the Local Authorities in Wales and has been supplied by Welsh Government. It contains the location of village greens from across Wales.\tPrivate record\n17732\tNRW_DS110140\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.757Z\tAssessment of Sublittoral Epibenthic Rock Communities and Species Following the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313430\tArchived at Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media052 .   Marine Recorder survey JNCCMNCR40000777\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tThe Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996 affected the water column seabed and shores of south Pembrokeshire.  Several rocky subtidal sites in and around the entrance to Milford Haven were studied at the time of the spill. This datasets is made up of the results obtained during a subtidal survey of South Pembrokeshire 16 months after the Sea Empress oil spill. The survey aimed to examine the open coast of South Pembrokeshire sites close the entrances of Milford Haven as far eastwards as Caldey Island.  The objective of this work was to revisit previously surveyed bedrock areas which may be have been subjected to elevated water column hydrocarbon load in order to investigate their community diversity richness and condition.\tPublic record\n17288\tEXT_DS124588\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.833Z\tJNCC: 2013 Article 17 species distribution data (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353838\tData held on DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/reporting/sr/Habitats%20Directive/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx  \"I am working with the Environment Portal team to publish the trends in a Power BI report on the NRW website. All data behind the overall results is already publically available on the JNCC website but in spreadsheet format per feature\".\tInternal Contact: Sheryl Davies, Specialist Advisor - Environmental Reporting, Environmental Reporting Team Internal Contact: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tHaitats regulations article 17 species distribution data by JNCC.\tPublic record\n17295\tEXT_DS124590\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.349Z\tMet Office: Bala, Alwen, Ness Gardens climate data (historic) (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353930\tCustodian: Andy Wall / Zhong Zhang\tCustodian: Andy Wall / Zhong Zhang\tMet Office: Bala Alwen Ness Gardens climate data (historic data held only; mutual exchange no longer taking place)\tPublic record\n18393\tNRW_DS119019\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.418Z\tThe Wales Outdoor Recreation Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303139\tNRW website and folder in the DMS  https://naturalresources.wales/media/4757/wales-outdoor-recreation-survey-2014-final-report.pdf\tSue Williams: Head of social Sciences.\tThe Welsh Outdoor Recreation Survey (WORS) provides information on participation in a wide range of outdoor activities from climbing to picnics which take place in all areas from local parks to mountains and the sea. It also covers people\u0027s attitudes towards biodiversity and details any actions they might have taken to help protect the environment. It is the largest survey of public engagement with the environment by people living in Wales.\tPublic record\n18304\tNRW_DS116446\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.469Z\tRiver Levels Application Programming Interface (API)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343436\tTelemetry system River levels online database\tCustodian: Alison Hanson Hydromentry and Telemetry\tThis dataset covers monitoring data that is updated on our systems on a minimum of a daily update cycle. This is increased during times of flooding etc. Measurements of the height (m) of water in a river lake or coastal site taken using automatic field devices are given usually every 15 minutes. Information is available for NRW river monitoring sites throughout Wales as well as some reservoirs and coastal sites.\tPublic record\n18323\tNRW_DS116340\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.320Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Risk Assessments for Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333430\tDMS spreadsheets with the WFD team\tRobert Blacklidge Environmental reporting team\tThis dataset contains risk assessment data produced to support the cycle 2 river basin management plans. It contains -   Lake water bodies  -   River water bodies  -   Transitional water bodies (estuaries) -   Groundwater bodies -   Coastal water bodies  that are assessed to be at risk of failing WFD Objectives or at risk of deteriorating from current status for a number of pressures. It also contains details of protected areas (Shellfish Waters and Bathing Waters) that are assessed to be at risk of failing their objectives or at risk of deteriorating in the future.\tPublic record\n18673\tNRW_DS123260\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.439Z\tWFD Saltmarsh\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323630\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Saltmarsh extent Cycle 3.lyr  x:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Saltmarsh zone area Cycle 3.lyr  x:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Saltmarsh zone point Cycle 3.lyr\tKathryn Borrowdale\tSee metadata entries 122088 - WFD Saltmarsh extent Cycle 3.lyr 122506 - WFD Saltmarsh zone area Cycle 3.lyr 122505 - WFD Saltmarsh zone point Cycle 3.lyr\tPrivate record\n18873\tNRW_DS124741\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.454Z\tCarbon sequestration potential of marine habitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373431\tY:\\SharedProjects\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Karen_Robinson\\Blue carbon project\tInternal Data Custodian:  Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor - Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.\tBlue carbon is the carbon stored and sequestered by marine habitats. Related terms can be defined as follows:  • A carbon sink is a natural or artificial reservoir that accumulates and stores carbon. • A carbon store encompasses the carbon stored in the water column surface sediments and floral and faunal biomass. This storage may be short or long-term.  Where carbon is stored in the long-term it can be considered to be sequestered.  In order to estimate the blue carbon potential of Welsh waters and thus allow a greater understanding of how Welsh marine ecosystems contribute to offsetting the release of carbon through human activities the following steps have been undertaken for this study:  • The best and most up to date available spatial datalayers for blue carbon habitats have been identified and combined so that approximate Welsh coverage could be determined for each. • The relevant literature has been reviewed to obtain the most relevant carbon storage and sequestration values which would then be applied to Welsh marine habitats. • A dedicated numerical model has been interrogated to obtain (1) water column carbon estimates (including plankton biomass); (2) estimates on air-sea flux of Carbon Dioxide (CO2) (i.e. how much CO2 enters the water column and is converted into various forms of carbon) as well as (3) estimates of carbon flux across the Welsh marine boundary (i.e. how much carbon leaves Welsh waters and gets transported to adjacent shelf and deeper waters). • Riverine inputs of carbon have also been estimated based on literature values and average river discharges supplied by NRW hydrologists. • Carbon values have been calculated and put into context with terrestrial (‘green carbon’) rates as well as Welsh CO2 emissions.\tPublic record\n17798\tNRW_DS111149\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.613Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Subtidal Monitoring\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313439\tA copy of the report is available in NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above).  The survey data has been entered into JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000156).  NRW only have a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Data collected during the development and implementation of low budget stereo-photographic monitoring of rocky subtidal epibenthic communities in within Skomer marine reserve. The data includes descriptions of communities present and subsequent changes in these communities over a one year period (1982 - 1983). The Skomer subtidal monitoring project was established as an ongoing project however this dataset includes the results from the first year only. Purpose of this data capture was to locate and permanently mark suitable monitoring stations and to observe and record the topography and epibenthic communities present in order to enable the assessment of the natural fluctuations in subtidal communities.\tPublic record\n19112\tNRW_DS125446\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.980Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Hydrology and Dipwells\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343436\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n18799\tNRW_DS124283\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.997Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Catchments Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323833\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Coastal Catchments Cycle 2.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Catchments Cycle 2 are a polygon dataset collated and defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams rivers and possibly lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. It is a layer of the undesignated small coastal catchments and is used alongside the Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2 dataset to produce outputs showing all the river catchments together.\tPublic record\n17107\tEXT_DS119247\t2024-05-28T12:11:56.346Z\tCranfield University: Soil Carbon Density Data Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323437\tX:\\Physical\\Soils\\Soil Carbon Density.lyr\tGIS Service Desk  Dylan L Williams, Soil Use and Management Advisor, NRW       http://www.landis.org.uk/data/nmcarbon.cfm\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds copies of Cranfield datasets which containing soil and soil-related information for Wales. This is a spatial dataset owned by Cranfield University detailing the soil\u0027s carbon density across Wales at a 1km grid cell scale. This dataset provides users with a summary of the stock carbon in the soils across three layer depths 0-30 cm (map 30) 30-100 cm (map 100) and 100-150 cm (map GT100) based on the averages associated with Cranfield University\u0027s Horizon data.\tPublic record\n18362\tNRW_DS116829\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.705Z\tAssessment of the Condition of the White-Clawed Crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation in 2014-2016\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383239\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: David Rogers Associates)\tBetween October 2014 and September 2016 work was undertaken to provide the data required to assess whether the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) was in favourable condition for the white-clawed crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes. The Afon Edw Nant yr Offeiriad Sgithwen Brook Dulas Brook (Builth Road) Clyro Brook and Afon Llynfi were surveyed to assess the condition of both crayfish populations and crayfish habitat. A further two rivers (Afon Duhonw and Afon Irfon) make up the full complement of the Wye SAC tributaries but no crayfish were found in these two rivers in 2003 and there are no subsequent records so these were not surveyed during the current survey and it is assumed that no white-clawed crayfish are present in these two rivers for this assessment.  Sampling followed the methodology developed during the 2003 monitoring programme (Rogers \u0026amp; Watson 2004) and included a combination of manual searching and trapping. During the survey white-clawed crayfish were only found in three of the eight tributaries of the River Wye SAC: Nant yr Offeiriad Sgithwen Brook and Clyro Brook (although the latter is not included as part of the SAC it is included within the condition assessment). Whilst crayfish numbers were relatively robust in Nant yr Offeiriad and Sgithwen Brook populations have now become confined to the headwaters. In 2016 Clyro Brook still supports a thriving population as it did in 2003 and this now appears to be one of the most important populations in the Wye because there is no diminution in numbers or extent as in the Sgithwen and Offeiriad. In fact both numbers and extent have increased in the Clyro. The lack of white-clawed crayfish in the Afon Edw following a mortality in 2006 is worrying given that this has been a very important waterway for this species in the recent past. Also there appeared to be no crayfish left in Dulas Brook (Builth Road) in 2015 despite supporting a good population in downstream reaches in 2003.  Using the provisional conservation objectives as part of the assessment of Favourable Conservation Status for Natura 2000 features the 2014 - 2016 survey shows the River Wye SAC does not achieve Favourable Conservation Status. Despite abundant suitable habitat (Attribute 3) and a lack of disease and the absence of aliens (Attribute 4) the density of crayfish throughout is not high enough (Attribute 1) and the distribution throughout the monitoring units is not sufficient (Attribute 2) for the River Wye SAC to achieve Favourable Status. In 2013-14 the Natural Resources Wales\u0027 Cynrig Fish Culture Unit implanted approximately 3000 crayfish into Afon Chwefru a tributary of the Irfon. As part of the present work an additional survey of a 1km stretch in two 500 metre sections at the introduction sites was included but no crayfish were found. Recommendations include a review of protection for surviving white-clawed crayfish populations and further survey work to verify the presence/absence of native and alien crayfish throughout the main River Wye and all tributaries and sub-tributaries. Given the importance of Clyro Brook for crayfish it is recommended that this tributary is notified as part of the Wye Site of Special Scientific Interest and Wye SAC. [This report provides an amalgamation of the 2014 2015 and 2016 crayfish survey results and supersedes the 2014 and 2015 survey reports (Rogers \u0026amp; Watson 2015 and 2016)].\tPublic record\n18426\tNRW_DS119153\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.002Z\tTransfrontier Shipment (TFS) of Waste and Refuse Derived Fuel (RDF) (Wales)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313533\tDMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/Permitting/International%20Waste%20Shipments/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2FRegulatory%2FPermitting%2FInternational%20Waste%20Shipments%2FTFS%20DATA%20%28external%20customers%20only%29%2FRDF%20DATA%2FRDF%20data%20%2DPublished\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000C2B903A34CC62A40BBDD00FD337BE4EC\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B6DF30B29%2D753D%2D47A5%2DA1DF%2D4DEAC78C0A06%7D\tMark Rhodes: Senior Environment Officer: Waste\tTransfrontier Shipment (TFS) of waste and Refuse Derived Fuel (RDF) waste export data (monthly tonnage weights) from Wales to non-UK countries since November 2014. TFS or International Waste Shipments are terms used for exporting and importing waste. The Transfrontier Shipment of Waste regulations govern the movement of recyclable materials across borders either by road rail ait or sea. The European Community first introduced a directive on control procedures for the shipment of hazardous waste in 1984 when it became apparent that hazardous waste from developed countries was being exported and dumped in developing countries. RDF is produced from domestic and business waste which includes biodegradable material as well as plastics.\tPublic record\n18984\tNRW_DS125076\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.396Z\tFlood Map for Planning Flood Zones 2 and 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303736\tGIS Corporate Data Store X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) includes climate change information to show how this will affect flood risk extents for rivers the sea and surface water and small watercourses over the next century. It shows the potential extent of flooding assuming no defences are in place.  It also indicates areas at risk from coastal erosion areas that have flooded in the past and key information on the location of flood defence infrastructure.     The FMfP can be considered as the best available information on flood risk and will replace the Development Advice Map for planning purposes when the Welsh Government implements the revised TAN15.  Although it currently has no official status for planning purposes it is a useful data source when considering flood risks.  The coming into force of the new TAN 15 and the Flood Map for Planning has been suspended due to a further consultation on the TAN.  Technical advice note (TAN) 15: development flooding and coastal erosion - https://www.gov.wales/technical-advice-note-tan-15-development-flooding-and-coastal-erosion   The Flood Zones within the FMfP shows the undefended risk of flooding from Rivers the Sea and from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses.    Flood Zone 3 displays the extent of flooding from:  • rivers with a 1% (1 in 100) chance or greater of happening in any given year including an allowance for climate change.  • the sea with a 0.5% (1 in 200) chance or greater of happening in any given year including an allowance for climate change.  • Surface water \u0026amp; small watercourses with a 1% (1 in 100) chance or greater of happening in any given year including an allowance for climate change.     Flood Zone 2 displays the extent of flooding from:  • Rivers with less than 1% (1 in 100) but greater than or equal to 0.1% (1 in 1000) chance of happening in any given year including an allowance for climate change.  • the Sea with less than 0.5% (1 in 200) but greater than or equal to 0.1% (1 in 1000) chance of flooding in any given year including an allowance for climate change.  • Surface water \u0026amp; small watercourses with less than 1% (1 in 100) but greater than or equal to 0.1% (1 in 1000) chance of happening in any given year including an allowance for climate change.\tPublic record\n17877\tNRW_DS111295\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.876Z\tInternational Biological Programme Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323935\tThe hardcopy raw data is to be kept in the monitoring team store room at NRW\u0027s Plas Penrhos campus. The excel spreadsheets are stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff.\tAlex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW.\tThe International Biological Programme (IBP) was an international research programme set up in the 1960s which aimed to understand the dynamics of whole ecosystems e.g. the energy flow across an ecosystem and the productivity of biological resources. Many sites were set up across the globe as part of the IBP one of which was in Snowdonia. This dataset contains raw data collected at a site in Snowdonia as part of the IBP. Data collected includes meteorological data vegetation data flora and fauna data etc. The dataset also contains some earlier data on sheep numbers. Purpose of data capture: The IBP was set up to coordinate large-scale ecological and environmental studies. The main purpose of data capture was to gain a greater understanding of whole ecosystem dynamics and how ecosystem processes may be affected by environmental change. Methods of data capture: Extensive field research was carried out at the site throughout the duration of the IBP. Soil collection vegetation sampling meteorological studies and weighing of animals were some of the methods used. Geographic coverage: The main experimental site was above Llyn Llydaw on Snowdon. Temporal coverage: The IBP ran from approximately 1964 to 1974. The main work on Snowdon took place during the late 60s; however there is also data on sheep numbers from the 1950s. Confidence in data: High. Survey work was undertaken by expert scientists.\tPublic record\n18636\tNRW_DS123226\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.621Z\tGreat crested newt habitat suitability\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323236\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Great crested newt habitat suitability (EQSS).lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Great crested newt habitat suitability.lyr\tJonathan Rothwell\t\tPrivate record\n17589\tNRW_DS108873\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.989Z\tCockle and Mussel Stock (bird food) Surveys in the Burry Inlet 2005 - 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383733\tThe data is archived under project 212, media no. 578.\tData custodian Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive) and for regularly occurring migratory species. The Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders.  It also supports an important cockle fishery. The removal of cockles and the associated disturbance caused by fishing needs to be balanced with bird food requirements. The aim of the project is to assess the food requirements of wading birds that over-winter in the Burry Inlet Special Protection Areas (SPAs).  Data Collection is repeated annually.  In the future this data may be combined with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tPublic record\n18803\tNRW_DS124625\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.914Z\tArachnids (spiders \u0026amp; harvestmen) data for Morfa Harlech National Nature Reserve (NNR) 2017-2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363235\tRecorder 6?\tMike Howe       Waiting for Mike Howe to provide information about the dataset\tMorfa Harlech NNR is a large sand dune system on the west coast of North Wales. In order to improve knowledge of the arachnid fauna of the NNR four survey visits were made to the reserve in 2017 2018 and 2019. Arachnids were surveyed from 28 different sample points across the site to gain a broad insight into the fauna. Sampling was largely undertaken using vacuum samplers (G-vac 2-stroke and E-vac Lithium-ion battery powered machines) with hand searching employed in areas with more mobile sand.\tPrivate record\n18624\tNRW_DS123228\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.953Z\tInvertebrates South\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323238\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Invertebrates\\Invertebrates South points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Invertebrates\\Invertebrates South rectangle.lyr\tJonathan Rothwell\t\tPrivate record\n18732\tNRW_DS123338\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.056Z\tWIMS Consent Outlets\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333338\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\WIMS Consent Outlets - Aug 2016.lyr\tData originally created and supplied by Simon Webb, please now refer to the water quality permitting team for relevance.\tThis data was likely to have been extracted from the WIMS system in 2016 to create a spatial dataset and is currently static.  WIMS has been replaced with the NRW\u0027s Permitting And Licencing System (PALS).\tPrivate record\n18267\tNRW_DS116328\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.187Z\tUK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme Summary Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333238\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116328\tInternal Custodian: Julie Boswell- Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS  Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThe Historic UK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) data contains statistics for a series of water quality sampling sites including annual means maximum and minimum values for each region for specified determinands from 1975 onwards. The Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) was established to provide an archive of water quality data for the UK. It is used to provide information for international obligations including the long-term trends of some determinands and the estimation of riverborne input of selected determinands to the sea.\tPublic record\n17584\tNRW_DS102004\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.748Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Hydroid-dominated Rockpools in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303034\tThe report and data are archived as Project No 199, Media No 555 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 555) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media.   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters (File path: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05  IECS\\LRH Rockpools.  Marine Recorder file:MRCCW30200000017, MRCCW31600000007  GIS data is here:- Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata Point of Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tHydroid rockpools (LR.H) are by definition species poor in comparison to other rockpool biotopes within the UK. LR.H rockpools are shallow mixed substrata pools characterised by the hydroids Obelia longissima and Kirchenpaueria pinnata ephemeral seaweeds such as Ulva sp. and Enteromorpha spp. and the common periwinkle Littorina littorea. The casts of the lugworm Arenicola marina and the sand mason worm Lanice conchilega can be found in sandier pools. Hydroid rockpools are also associated with mussel beds (SLR.MytX) with Mytilus edulis present in the pools. Barnacles (Semibalanus balanoides and Elminius modestus) and the keel worm Pomatoceros sp. may also be attached to the harder substrata. On the south side of the Burry Inlet hydroid rockpools have been identified at Whiteford Point during Phase 1 biotope surveys carried out by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2000. The rockpools are located at the tip of Whiteford Point within the vicinity of a cast iron Victorian lighthouse currently listed as an ancient monument. The purpose of this data capture was to map and assess the current extent and distribution of Hydroid rockpools within the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and evaluate the possible ephemeral nature of Hydroid rockpools by assessing the species richness of the rockpools at Whiteford Point.\tPublic record\n17074\tEXT_DS47100\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.864Z\tEffects of Air Pollution and Climate Change on Welsh Calcicolous Ecosystems (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437313030\tPaper reports: Room G4 shelves, Monitoring Team, Environmental Change Group.  Raw data: ECG R:drive.\tGIS Service Desk  Environmental Audit Group, NRW External Contact: Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)       Alternative Title: Effects of Air pollution and climate change on British Calcicolous ecosystems - Welsh sites    Alternative Title: Effeithiau llygredd aer a newid hinsawdd ar ecosystemau calchgar Cymru     Temporal Extent: 1992-12-31 ; 1990-01-01/1992-12-31    Frequency of Update: Not Planned Extent : Wales British National Grid No Spatial Element Data Format Type: Documents Site reports including maps and ground photographs in paper format.  Raw data in reports and on MS Excel file. Originator: Department of the Environment (DOE) (Air and Environmental Quality Division)    Originator: Lancaster University. Unit of Vegetation Science\tThis dataset is comprised of the welsh component of a larger dataset of 128 plots spread over 56 calcicolous grassland monitoring sites in the UK. In Wales there are 17 plots over 9 calcicolous grasslands. The sites surveyed were eight National Nature Reserves (NNR\u0027s): Cadair Idris Craig Cerrig gleisiad Craig y Cilau Cwm Idwal Ogof Ffynnon Ddu Snowdon South Gower Stackpole and one Site of Species Scientific Interest (SSSI): The Great Orme. The idea behind the work was to create a network of calcicolous grassland sites to look at the effects of air pollution and climate change on these sites.\tPublic record\n17326\tEXT_DS125192\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.508Z\tRWE Seahorse Hippocampus guttulatus records (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313932\tThe collated GIS dataset with the RWE records can be currently found here: Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Marine_Habitats_Species\\BAP_species\\Updated_records_20200701.gdb\\Hippocampus_guttalatus_20211207  It is due to go on the X drive in this location: X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Section 7 Species  Spreadsheet Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Marine_Habitats_Species\\BAP_species\\new_data\\RWE_hippocampus_data_GIS_table_confirmed_20211207.xlsx\tNatasha Lough\tExcel spreadsheet of hippocampus guttulatus Seahorse records from RWE and derived GIS.\tPublic record\n17795\tNRW_DS110479\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.561Z\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Cadair Idris National Nature Reserve (NNR)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343739\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive \u0026amp; with CCWs upland ecologist Some of this data is also held in Recorder 6 under: CCW Upland Survey CCW0007400000001\tinternal Custodian: Pete Jones, Terrestrial and Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader  Jane Stevens (TEG) Formerly Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tThis dataset contains the survey results from the Cadair Idris National Nature Reserve between August and September 1999. The survey was commissioned by former Countryside Council for Wales in order to map and describe the vegetation of Cadair Idris. The reserve comprises of approximately 450 hectares of the Cadair Idris massif some 6 km south of Dolgellau.\tPublic record\n18639\tNRW_DS123220\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.647Z\tIntertidal sensitivity\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323230\tx:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\Intertidal sensitivity\\Dee Estuary.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\Intertidal sensitivity\\Severn Estuary.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\tPrivate record\n18868\tNRW_DS124683\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.516Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Tenants Connecting Cables\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363833\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Tenants Connecting Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Tenants Connecting Cables. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18272\tNRW_DS116323\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.636Z\tWaste Carriers, Brokers and Dealers\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333233\tData taken from the Customer Relationship Management (CRM) database  Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116323\tCustodian: Stephen Badman, Customer Contact Centre\tThis dataset contains details of currently permitted waste carriers brokers and dealers.    Carrier: A person who transports controlled waste in the course of a business or otherwise with a view to profit.   Broker: Waste brokers are people who make arrangements on behalf of others to recover or dispose of waste regardless of whether or not they handle the waste themselves.   Dealer: Waste dealers are people who use an agent to buy then sell wastes regardless of whether they handle the waste themselves or not.   Historical details are not included.\tPublic record\n18623\tNRW_DS123236\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.953Z\tNRW Valleys Regional Park\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323336\tx:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Valleys Regional Park.lyr\tPeter Cloke\t\tPrivate record\n19083\tNRW_DS125363\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.056Z\tAncient Woodland Inventory for Wales 2004 (AWI2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333633\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Ancient Woodland Inventory 2004.lyr Paper copies of the tracings are stored in Maes y Ffynnon, Floor 1 Post room (Jim Latham\u0027s cabinets)\tChris Tucker, Specialist Advisor: Technical Forestry (Resilience)\tIn the 1980s the Nature Conservancy Council instigated the Ancient Woodland Inventory (AWI). In Wales the mapping baseline used was the 1st editions of the Ordnance Survey (OS) 1” maps published in the 1830s. The 1980s version of the inventory was compiled manually using tracings from paper maps. It contained a significant number of boundary discrepancies. In the late 1990s paper maps were converted to digital format. Work to reconcile boundaries with digital OS boundaries developed the first digital version of the inventory made available in 2004. This version is referred to as the AWI 2004.    Also see the metadata entry for Ancient Woodland Inventory dataset number NRW_DS116442 https://metadata.naturalresources.wales/geonetwork/srv/api/records/NRW_DS116442?language\u003deng\tPrivate record\n18714\tNRW_DS123360\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.255Z\tSheep Dip Water Sampling SE Area\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333630\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\Sheep Dip Water Sampling SE Area.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18502\tNRW_DS121295\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.259Z\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323935\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tData Custodian: Mike Howe\tThis dataset was collected to add to our current knowledge of the saproxylic invertebrate fauna found across the Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Despite the designation remarkably little is known about the saproxylic invertebrate fauna of the SAC. It is known to support significant populations of a number of nationally rare and threatened species but until now only limited investigation of the fauna had been carried out. The two outstanding species known from the SAC are the lime bark beetle Ernoporus tiliae and Cosnard\u0027s net-winged beetle Erotides cosnardi both Species of Principal Importance in England but not included on Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016.   These works will be the first phase of an intended larger body of work planned for the coming years aimed at producing a comprehensive inventory of invertebrate interest across the Wye Valley Woodlands. This inventory in the longer term will aid the process of drawing up management guidelines as a basis for the sustainable management of the woodlands within the Wye Valley Woodlands SAC and potentially the wider landscape. These preliminary surveys have demonstrated the great potential interest of the Wye Valley Woodlands SAC for saproxylic invertebrates and provide ample justification for continued survey as resources permit. They suggest that it is the extent of the woodland in the SAC that is a key factor although individual areas may have more localised special interest for particular rare and threatened species. The importance of veteran trees is emphasised as well as the value of individual open-grown trees. These are significant as they suggest a modified approach to saproxylic conservation within the SAC.\tPublic record\n18306\tNRW_DS116334\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.876Z\tOtter Survey Data 1991-2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333334\tThis data is available through Sharefile as part of a packaged licence product.\tHuw Jones, Team Leader, Natural Resource Management, NRW Janet Buckles, Technical Assistant Biodiversity, Biodiversity, NRW\tThe European otter (Lutra lutra) is widely recognised as an emblem for nature conservation in the UK because it is a top predator and an important  biological indicator of the health of our rivers and wetlands. Monitoring the status of the otter therefore gives us a valuable measure of the state of our water and wetland ecosystems. In Wales as in much of the UK it is a largely nocturnal animal and is rarely observed in the wild. It is however possible to detect its presence by searching for its distinctive droppings (spraints) and footprints. As part of a scheme to monitor the distribution of otters throughout the UK five Welsh otter survey were carried out as follows:  - 1st survey: 1977-78 run and funded by NCC. - 2nd survey: 1984-85 run and funded by Vincent Wildlife Trust - 3rd survey: 1991 run and funded by Vincent Wildlife Trust - 4th survey: 2002 run by EAW and the Wildlife Trusts and funded by EAW CCW and Welsh Water. - 5th survey: 2009-2010 run by EAW and the Wildlife Trusts IBAP officers and the Snowdonia Mammal Group.  The aim of these surveys was to monitor the distribution of otters throughout Wales. Sites were selected by accessibility and likelihood of finding signs of otter activity typically road bridges and the confluences of watercourses. The number of sites visited per hydrometric region varied. No attempt was made to randomise the sampling effort but there was some site variability due different issues when the surveys were undertaken.   This dataset is made up of two parts; the 3rd and 4th surveys (1991 and 2002) and the 5th survey 2009-2010 .The full welsh for the 1st and 2nd surveys for 1997-78 1984-85 is not currently available. The 1991 and 2002 data are currently available on the NBN Gateway (published by JNCC) as redacted open data. This raw data is currently not available however more detailed information about each surveying year can be found in the reports available through the library (see related titles). the 2009-2010 otter surveys are available thought NRW but will be issued under licence.\tPublic record\n17415\tNRW_DS46160\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.468Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313630\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Lowland grassland   a)  Reports: TSG N:drive and Archive Room.   b)  Recorder 6 database - All surveys related to this are tagged as Phase 2 lowland grassland surveys  - LGSW Brecknock CCW0002600000009     - LGSW Carmarthen \u0026amp; Dinefwr CCW0002600000008     - LGSW Ceredigion CCW0002600000005     - LGSW Clwyd CCW0002600000003     - LGSW East Gwynedd CCW0002600000002     - LGSW Gwent CCW0002600000012     - LGSW Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan CCW0002600000011     - LGSW Montgomery CCW0002600000004     - LGSW Preseli \u0026amp; South Pembroke CCW0002600000007     - LGSW Radnor CCW0002600000006     - LGSW West Glamorgan \u0026amp; Llanelli CCW0002600000010     - LGSW West Gwynedd CCW0002600000001     c)  GIS layers  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland  d)  Site packs: TSG N:drive and in files outside D. Stevens office and local team offices.  e)  Some images are stored on CCW\u0027s H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV\\PHASE II PHOTO AND IMAGE ARCHIVE.  DVDs are in the safe.  This data is now available on the NBN Gateway\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  For data held on Recorder 6 - Master Reference Data Team, NRW For spatial datasets - contact GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis project comprised of a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation across Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.   The survey locations and sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.   The datasets which were produced from this project have been extensively validated and verified. However the main limitation of these datasets is their age. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends agricultural treatments or even planned developments which means that there is no guarantee that the information within these datasets is still representative on the ground today. However they do provide an important long-term catalogue of data which can be used to monitor changes to the grassland environments overtime.    The dataset contributes to JNCC\u0027s UK Lowland Grassland Database held by the Lowland Grassland Lead Co-ordination Network at EN in Peterborough.     The original Phase 2 Grassland Survey datasets (1987 – 2004) have now been put into a folder called Archive and the whole dataset has been updated with new and re-mapped sites. These now have the suffix 2020 and cover a time period 1989 – 2018.\tPublic record\n18708\tNRW_DS123359\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.042Z\tSheep Dip Invertebrate Sampling SE Area\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333539\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\Sheep Dip Invertebrate Sampling SE Area.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18735\tNRW_DS123337\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.174Z\tWIMS Consent Discharges\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333337\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\WIMS Consent Discharges - Aug 2016.lyr\tData originally created and supplied by Simon Webb, please now refer to the water quality permitting team for relevance.\tThis data was likely to have been extracted from the WIMS system in 2016 to create a spatial dataset and is currently static.  WIMS has been replaced with the NRW\u0027s Permitting And Licencing System (PALS) and new products are available see (NRW_DS116329 and NRW_DS116215) in non-spatial format.\tPrivate record\n18033\tNRW_DS114819\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.688Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) and feeding habitat within Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion / Merionnydd Oak Woods Bat Sites SAC (2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383139\tAssociated data and reports are held at Maes-y-Ffynnon at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\33 Monitoring selected colonies of LHB and feeding habitat within Meirionnydd Oak Woods SAC.\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCoedydd Derw a Safloedd Ystlumod Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is a large composite site of international importance for lesser horseshoe bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) containing more than 15 known maternity roosts and hibernacula. Although lesser horseshoe bat annual count data are collected from roosts within the SAC by the Gwynedd Bat Group and other volunteers additional information is required regarding the condition of foraging habitat and flight lines surrounding the roosts. For the purposes of this contract an area covering a radius of two kilometres around each of the ten target roosts was selected for further study. The purpose of this data capture was to establish the condition of the lesser horseshoe bat within the Meirionydd Oakwoods and Bat Sites Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tPublic record\n17214\tEXT_DS122412\t2024-05-28T12:17:03.535Z\tCanal and River Trust: Water Level and Flow Data held in WISKI 7 Core Hydro and Telemetry (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343132\tThis data is held in the Hydro part of WISKI\tAlison Hanson - Team Leader for this area of work  Liam Perrott - technical specialist for hydrometry       Data from 2014 in Water Resources Team DMS location.\tReservoir storage data is recorded by Canal and River Trust. Weekly reading of reservoir storage volume (Million litres) and % storage.\tPublic record\n18599\tNRW_DS122469\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.424Z\tA survey of the invertebrate fauna of Marford Quarry in 2018, with a particular focus on aculeates (bees, wasps and ants).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343639\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive. Data is due to be entered into the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) held in Recorder 6\tDr Mike Howe, Lead specialist advisor; terrestrial habitats and species       Data is due to be entered into the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) held in Recorder 6 by Mike Howe, but this is not yet complete. There is no anticipated ate for input at this time.\tFollowing management work in Marford Quarry to increase the amount of bare sand habitat for psammophilic insects a survey in 2018 recorded 118 species of aculeate bees wasps and ants including 35 species new to the site. The cumulative aculeate list now stands at 171 species with 81 bees 80 wasps and 10 ants 80 wasps making Marford Quarry one of the most important site in Wales for this taxonomic group. The survey also recorded four new psammophilic aculeates - Anoplius infuscatus Arachnospila wesmaeli Coelioxys conoidea and Coelioxys inermis - and the psammophilic ground beetle Harpalus tardus at its only Welsh inland locality. The overall total for the quarry now stands at 31 psammophilic species including 26 aculeates two beetles and three flies. Six new dead-wood nesting aculeates were found – Ancistrocerus gazella Ancistrocerus scoticus Megachile ligniseca Psenulus pallipes Stigmus solskyi and the Hornet Vespa crabro – resulting in a cumulative total of 43 species (42 aculeates and one beetle). Both the psammophilic faunal assemblage and the dead-wood assemblage are considered to be currently in favourable condition although there is an urgent need to provide more deadwood habitat which is at a premium. It is also important to maintain early-successional sandy habitats by a programme of regular excavation work. The finding of the solitary wasp Gorytes laticinctus in Marford Quarry is arguably the most significant record in the 2018 survey given its UK distribution and indicates a major range extension. Other important records were made of other insects including the tachinid flies Freraea gagatea (new to Wales) Gymnosoma rotundatum and Wagneria gagatea (both at 2nd Welsh localities) and Leucostoma simplex (new to north Wales) the Alder Leaf Beetle Agelastica alni (otherwise known from Fagl Lane Quarry) and the parasitic wasps Sphecophaga vesparum and Haltichella rufipes (both new to Wales).\tPublic record\n17076\tEXT_DS87452\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.880Z\tPriority Species and Species of Local Conservation Concern for the Dyfi Biosphere Catchment Area (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837343532\tMaster copy held by BIS Local Record Centre.   Copy held by Mike Bailey (Aberystwyth/Ynys Las).\tGIS Service Desk  Mike Bailey, Dyfi Reserve, Senior Reserve Manager, NRW\tThe Dyfi Biosphere is the only Biosphere Reserve in Wales and includes the Dyfi Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Cors Fochno SSSI. This dataset is a collation of species records. Just under 5000 species records within the Dyfi catchment.  Species include all UK Species of Conservation Concern Red Data Book and Nationally Scarce species Amber List Birds and other locally important species.  Also recorded is UK and EU legally protected species Section 74 of the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 (CRoW) species UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Species and Red List Birds.  The data was collated on behalf of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to contribute to the Dyfi Biosphere project.  Biosphere Reserves are nominated by national governments and designated under the Man and the Biosphere Programme of UNESCO.\tPublic record\n17312\tEXT_DS124989\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.941Z\tSmall Sewage Discharge Risk Zone Map England (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393839\t\tDave Jones\tSmall Sewage Discharge map of the UK is a risk map covering the whole of England so it can be used to inform the potential risk of Diffuse Water Pollution for new or existing small discharges to ground for all sensitive SSSIs  Please be aware that the dataset is no longer held within NRW and has not been replaced or superseded.  The Metadata entry is a record of the existence of the dataset in NRW\tPrivate record\n18769\tNRW_DS123446\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.038Z\tNRW Flood duty officer areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343436\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Flood duty officer areas.lyr\tLucy Edwards\tMap of Wales split into 3 sections each section covers the area of responsibility for each of the Flood Duty Officer area.\tPrivate record\n18345\tNRW_DS116496\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.145Z\tMonitoring of Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) Chinese Mitten Crabs (Eriocheir sinensis) on the River Dee 2007 - 2016\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343936\tData is held in Marine Recorder - MRCCW3000000009C 2015 NRW Monitoring of Chinese Mitten Crabs (Eriocheir sinensis) on the River Dee (NNS)  Data and associated project details e.g. images also need to be pulled together and put into Project Number is 467 Media number 1540.\tInternal Custodian: Ben Wray, Marine Monitoring Group NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Chinese mitten crab (Eriocheir sinensis) is registered by the Invasive Species Specialist Group (ISSG) of the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) as one of the top 100 invasive species worldwide. Mitten crabs are a catadromous species migrating between fresh water and marine environments to reproduce. They spend the majority of their adult life in fresh water habitats where they mature to reproductive size. Depending on mitten crab population density and habitat suitability juvenile mitten crabs will migrate large distances up river to mature. The Dee Chinese mitten crab project set out to investigate the population of Chinese mitten crab in the Dee catchment and achieve a number of objectives including awareness raising trialling and evaluating potential methods to monitor and manage the population and make inferences about the current and future status of the species in the Dee catchment.\tPublic record\n17290\tEXT_DS124494\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.846Z\tRSPB marine bird data (3rd Party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234343934\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\tInternal contact; geospatial team\tSpatial layers for marine bird 95% Utilisation Distributions in 5% Bands for; Black-legged kittiwake Common Guillemot European Shag and Razorbill.\tPublic record\n17383\tNRW_DS24310\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.096Z\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Photomonitoring Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333130\tHafod Elfyn\u0027s archival store, CCW Headquarters.\tRaymond Roberts, Senior Earth Scientist  Geologist/Earth Science Officer, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tA black and white fixed point archival-quality photographic survey of most GCR sites in Wales.  The images were captured to provide a baseline when CCW was created in 1990/1991.  Work was carried out by CCW\u0027s Earth Science Officers between 1991 and 1992 to establish a baseline of site condition for GCR sites in Wales. The negatives and contact prints were specially processed to ensure the longest possible storage life.\tPrivate record\n18710\tNRW_DS123365\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.180Z\tNRW Acquisitions \u0026amp; Disposals\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333635\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\NRW Forest Deeds - Acquisitions.lyr  X:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\NRW Forest Deeds - Disposals.lyr   Acquisition and Disposal Record Map Scans archive held on G:\\FCData\\Aberystwyth\\EstateManagement\\A and D. Record Map Scans to be moved to Z drive.\tPrimary Metadata contact: Ian Medcalf, GIS Technician 4, NRW Secondary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan\tThis dataset illustrates the acquisitions \u0026amp; disposals that have created the NRW Managed Forestry Estate as we know it today. This information is obtained from the Deed Management System which has been designed for the maintenance and reporting of the NRW land holdings.\tPrivate record\n17082\tEXT_DS115118\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.461Z\tRSPB Invertebrate and Algae Inventory of the Skerries (2011) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313138\tArchived as Project 349, Media 1326 and 1366.   Species data available through Marine Recorder. We had permission to digitise and enter into Marine Recorder\tGIS Service Desk  Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, NRW External Contact: David Tompkins, RSPB (The Skerries)\tThe aim of this data capture was to develop an inventory of species for the Skerries around North Anglesey. Data was supplied to Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to provide contextual information about seal habitats on the Skerries.  In 2011 the RSPB conducted an intertidal survey of the Skerries off North Anglesey to create an inventory of species found there. 5 sites were surveyed to represent different intertidal environments with respect to exposure to wave action silt and sediment level tidal current exposure and shoreline substrata. At each location the shore was surveyed at high mid and low tide levels. This provided an inventory of algal species and invertebrate species at the 5 locations recording presence/absence. RSPB have given Natural Resources Wales (NRW) permission to include this data with its own data holdings in Marine Recorder.\tPublic record\n17352\tNRW_DS24235\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.735Z\tInventory of British Lakes (UKLakes)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323335\tThis dataset is held by CEH and will be made available through their data portal.\tExternal Custodian: Centre for Ecology and Hydrology - they are the ones that hold the data and manage the dataset.  Internal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services        15/02/2016 - This data is held by CEH and we contribute and own a stake in it. CEH are going to add it to their data portal - Tristan Hatton Ellis to send us the link when it is live so we can update the metadata - the link should be added to the additional information - or even the Resource Locator.\tThis is an inventory of lakes in Great Britain derived mainly from Geographical Information System (GIS) datasets. It is linked with a risk based prioritisation protocol for standing waters in Great Britain which will be used in Lake Habitat Action Plans (HAP) and Water Framework Directive (WFD) work. The purpose of this data capture was to develop the required lake typology under the Water Framework Directive. This dataset is held by Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH).\tPublic record\n17930\tNRW_DS112413\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.946Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal Species and Habitat Diversity Confidence Layer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343133\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Point of contact: Data Services Team\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Confidence ratings based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final species diversity analysis was calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.    An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out taking into account the source of the data its age spatial taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tPublic record\n17616\tNRW_DS109880\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.804Z\tSaltmarsh Features of the Taf, Tywi and Gwendraeth Estuaries 1997\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383830\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media101to200\\Media178 Data is held as Project No. 32, media 178\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of the results from a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Taf Twyi and Gwendraeth Estuaries- the \u0027three rivers complex\u0027. The work was carried out in preparation for notification of the saltmarshes in the complex as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tPublic record\n18979\tNRW_DS125154\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.068Z\tAn invertebrate survey of Llangennith Moors 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313534\tData stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder.\tMike Howe Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tLlangennith Moors grazing marsh lies at the extreme western end of the Gower Peninsula. Before this survey the invertebrate interest of the site was little known with the only previous survey having been carried out in July 1991 which had a focus upon Diptera. Due to the minimal invertebrate knowledge of the site and relative absence of ditch maintenance there since the 1990s Natural Resources Wales commissioned a survey of ditch and fen invertebrates and a search for two whorl snails of European importance the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior (which is found on the nearby Whiteford Burrows and Oxwich Burrows) and Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana which is found in similar habitat on the south Llŷn coast in North Wales. The surveys were aimed at determining the current invertebrate interest of Llangennith Moors following a sea wall breach and subsequent managed retreat at Cwm Ivy Marsh and the loss of grazing marsh to saltmarsh. The Llangennith surveys were undertaken simultaneously by two surveyors in October 2018.\tPublic record\n17384\tNRW_DS24311\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.097Z\tMapinfo Habitats Database on Candidate Special Areas of Conservation (cSACs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333131\t\tGIS Data Manager, NRW.\tMapinfo habitat databases on 23 candidate Special Areas of Conservation (cSACs) comprising 5 to 20000 records. Used to fulfill requirements of the Habitats Directive to set up protected areas in the marine environment. Collaborations with relevant authorities established operational management schemes on candidate marine SACs around the United Kingdom the locations of which are located on the map of projects and special features. Also available on an interactive map on the web.\tPrivate record\n17914\tNRW_DS112290\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.014Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Flats in Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323930\tThe data are archived as Project No 199, Media No 549 and is stored on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.  [A]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSCB\\2008 ASML MSCB\tIntertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW\tMenai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC)  is designated for five Annex 1 habitats: Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide Reefs Large shallow inlets and bays and Submerged or partially submerged sea caves. Of these mudflats sandflats and reefs are the main habitats of interest within intertidal areas of Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC.  The overriding objective of the survey was to establish the reference condition for the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC and develop monitoring stations for recording the level of natural change. This would enable the development of conservation objectives for the appropriate management of the features of the SAC.\tPublic record\n18396\tNRW_DS118757\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.812Z\tSpatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Flintshire, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373537\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr E.A.Howe (Contractor: Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust)\tThis report draws on a series of previous research reports commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body on determining the conservation status of great crested newts Triturus cristatus in north Wales. The Welsh Government is obligated to maintain or restore the favourable conservation status of great crested newts within Wales and this report aims to provide practical guidance on how this can be achieved within the County of Flintshire. It is one of a series of three such reports the other two covering the counties of Anglesey and Wrexham. Due to its legal status the great crested newt is the focus of considerable conservation effort from a variety of bodies. This document aims to provide an overarching strategy to guide developers conservation NGO\u0027s and local and national government in their efforts in order to achieve the greatest conservation benefit for great crested newts and ensure a unified approach to achieving Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) across the county. To this end the report is accompanied by a number of GIS layers which will be of particular practical use to those engaged in great crested newt conservation across Flintshire.  The report presents a scoring method by which the Current Conservation Status (CCS) of great crested newts can be determined at multiple geographical scales from a single pond to the entire county. It then uses predictive modelling based on geographical distributional climatic and historical data to produce habitat suitability maps and to derive survey targets and pond creation targets to enable FCS to be achieved within the county. These techniques can be applied in various land use and planning scenarios and the report provides practical guidance on identifying areas of particularly high or low value for great crested newts assessing the impacts of proposed developments designing effective mitigation and the implications of agri-environment schemes for great crested newts.   During the modelling process a total of 1116 extant ponds were identified within the County of Flintshire.  With a view to redressing historic loss (37% of ponds having been lost since the 1843 mapping data) FCS was considered in the light of the level of occupancy that would have occurred at the time of the original mapping relative to current levels of occupancy and Habitat Suitability of ponds to the species (HSI scores). Historically we estimate there would have been 1659 ponds in Flintshire and a corresponding 258 ponds occupied by great crested newts and 398 ponds with an HSI score \u0026gt;0.7.  We propose that the 258 occupied ponds should be used to provide a target level for great crested newts (FRV for Range and Habitat sufficiency).  This is further qualified by requiring these ponds have an HSI \u0026gt;0.7 to ensure habitat quality has at least a medium population are found at a density of at least 4 per km2 (to ensure population viability) and has a minor (or lower) level of threat (to address the species’ future prospects). The Current Status of the species in Flintshire is estimated as having 174 ponds occupied by great crested newts. Using the propose scoring system FCS in Flintshire requires 258 occupied ponds which form part of medium populations in high pond density areas and that have an HSI of \u0026gt;0.7: the minimum score to achieve these criteria will be 16 for each pond.  To achieve FCS the score would need to exceed is 258 x 16 \u003d 4128. With an estimated 174 ponds being occupied and if all of the ponds are in favourable condition (i.e. would score 16) then the Current Conservation Status score \u003d 2748.  The approach to identifying CCS and FCS laid out within this document is one of a number which are in the process of being developed across the UK and Europe. Whilst there is currently no single standard approach which can be applied uniformly across a range of species and habitats one may emerge in the future. With the recent increase in stud ...\tPublic record\n17145\tEXT_DS121619\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.760Z\tWelsh Government: Environmental Noise Mapping (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363139\tX:\\Resource Management\\Environmental Noise Mapping\\\tGIS Spatial Team: Keith Jones (keith.jones@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds data provided by Welsh Government giving information on noise maps for major roads across Wales and for industry in our largest towns and cities. Noise data for all the sound level contours calculated in 2017 are provided in 5-decibel \\A-weighted\\\" contour bands. The noise maps for Wales are based on calculation rather than measurement. They should only be considered a starting point for identifying areas that need priority action.\"\tPublic record\n18948\tNRW_DS124905\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.185Z\tCommunity Flood Plans\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393035\tTop Copy: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/frm/aware/Results%20%20Reporting/Reports/CFP%20Update%20Live.xls?d\u003dw671f401b42de404f81b240fe7cf67f69 A GIS version of the data is available at: X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Community Flood Plans.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Hannah Davies, Team Member 5, Community Engagement and Resilience (Flood) Kelly McLauchlan, Lead Specialist Advisor, Community Engagement and Resilience (Flood)       Although the data contains personal data, this is in the top copy and should have been redacted from the GIS layer.  providers have consented to provide their personal data\tA community flood plan is an account of information and actions agreed by communities at risk of flooding across Wales. The aim and content of the plan depends on the needs and motivations of the community. Community flood plans are volunteer and community led. Data presented is a snapshot and only based on data disclosed to NRW rather than live information managed by NRW.  This data is collected during the annual maintenance of community flood plans across Wales at the start of financial year. The data is collected by Operation Warning \u0026amp; Informing teams and collated by the EPP Community Engagement and Resilience Teams.\tPrivate record\n18344\tNRW_DS116497\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.211Z\tAd Hoc sightings of Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) chinese mitten crab (Eriocheir sinensis) 2013 to 2014\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343937\tData is held in Marine Recorder  MRCCW3000000009C 2015  NRW Monitoring of Chinese Mitten Crabs (Eriocheir sinensis) on the River Dee (NNS)   Data and associated project details e.g. images also need to be pulled together and put into Project Number is 467 Media number 1540.\tInternal Custodian: Ben Wray, Marine Monitoring Group NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Chinese mitten crab (Eriocheir sinensis) is registered by the Invasive Species Specialist Group (ISSG) of the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) as one of the top 100 invasive species worldwide. Mitten crabs are a catadromous species migrating between fresh water and marine environments to reproduce. They spend the majority of their adult life in fresh water habitats where they mature to reproductive size. Depending on mitten crab population density and habitat suitability juvenile mitten crabs will migrate large distances up river to mature. The Dee Chinese mitten crab project set out to investigate the population of Chinese mitten crab in the Dee catchment and achieve a number of objectives including awareness raising trialling and evaluating potential methods to monitor and manage the population and make inferences about the current and future status of the species in the Dee catchment. This dataset contains a subset of the monitoring data comprising of ad hoc sighting records of Chinese mitten crab surveyed between 2013 and 2014.\tPublic record\n17839\tNRW_DS111159\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.464Z\tSkomer Marine Reserve Sublittoral Habitats and Communities 1983\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313539\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.  Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR60000117).  NRW only hold a snapshot of this data\tInternal Custodian:  Assistant Marine Conservation Officer - Skomer MNR Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  This dataset contains the results of the survey of 44 sublittoral survey sites around the Skomer Marine Reserve during 1983. 111 species of plant and 276 animal species were noted (27 species of algae and 27 species of animal were previously unrecorded from the area). The purpose of this data capture was to collect information necessary to provide revised descriptions of habitats and communities for the Skomer Marine Reserve (SMR) and to continue the SMR seaweed survey (first started in 1982) in order to provide information on algal communities around the reserve and to provide quantitative information on seaweed communities.\tPublic record\n17244\tEXT_DS121970\t2024-05-28T12:17:32.806Z\tWelsh Government: Environmentally Sensitive Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393730\tx:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Environmentally Sensitive Areas Land Cover.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Environmentally Sensitive Areas.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Environmentally Sensitive Areas Farms.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is owned and managed by Welsh Government and provided to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under licence. The Environmentally Sensitive Areas were introduced in 1987 to offer incentives to encourage farmers to adopt agricultural practices which would safeguard and enhance parts of the country of particularly high landscape wildlife or historic value. The scheme has now closed to new applicants.\tPublic record\n17672\tNRW_DS109891\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.582Z\tAcoustic Monitoring of the Extent of the North Llyn Modiolus Reef\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383931\tData from monitoring trial and subsequent 1999 surveys archived under media no. 004 and 005 Annual date collection from 2006 archived in project no. 233 (media 602). Analysis of the data from 2006 archived in project no. 234 (media 306)\tInternal Custodian is: Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG, NRW. Metadata point of contact is: Data Services\tBiogenic horse mussel (Modiolus Modiolus) reef is an important feature of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  The reef has high biodiversity and is vulnerable to anthropogenic activities such as benthic fishing.  The mussel reef forms an undulating surface that is significantly different to the surrounding seabed and therefore acoustic monitoring methods can be used to monitor changes in extent and identity areas of potential damage. Work is carried out on the north Llyn horse mussel reef  in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau jointly by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and University of Wales Bangor.  The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Measure extent and temporal changes within the of the Modiolus reef in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.   -  Investigate the health of the reef and for signs of anthropogenic activity within the area.   An area approximately 10km by 6km adjacent to the Pen Llyn coast south-west of Porth Dinllaen approximately 1.5km off shore was surveyed.  All data is processed to reduce errors (e.g. positional corrections).  Acoustic data is subject to ground-truthing using drop down videos captured as part of a separate project.  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPublic record\n17800\tNRW_DS111144\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.708Z\tResults of a Study into the Use of Transects to Identify the Broad-Scale Attributes of the Mawddach Estuary. (1999 CCW Marine Monitoring Trials)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313434\t[A]  Data held in NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000795).   [B]  Paper MNCR forms are held in NRW off-site storage.   [C]  Archived as project 316, media 1240.\tContact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to investigate the feasibility of using a transect based methodology to determine the proportion of muddy communities and the zonation of muddy communities from upper to lower shore.  The Mawddach is one of the best examples of a small drying bar-built estuary in Britain and with its mountainous catchment area it has some of the lowest nutrient inputs in Wales.  In order to be able to report on the condition of the estuary a number of attributes have been identified which require monitoring.   The aims of this project was to investigate the feasibility of using a transect based methodology to determine the proportion of muddy communities and the zonation of muddy communities from upper to lower shore.\tPublic record\n18727\tNRW_DS123348\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.092Z\tWFD Transitional Fish Survey Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333438\tx:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Transitional fish\\Transitional fish survey.lyr\tKate Borrowdale Swyddog Asesu Amgylcheddol Morol / Marine Environmental Assessment Officer Tîm Asesu ac Adrodd Monitro Morol / Marine Monitoring Assessment and Reporting Team\tWater Framework Directive transitional fish survey spatial data. Data includes the location of beam nets fyke nets and seine netting.\tPrivate record\n17969\tNRW_DS114591\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.634Z\tFarmland Bird Survey North East Wales (2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353931\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and available throught the NBN Gateway\tInternal Custodian: Sian Whitehead, Terrestrial and Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA systematic regional survey of farmland birds within and around the Bettisfield and Dee Estuary (Welsh side) areas undertaken in 2008. The survey aimed to establish whether corn bunting were still breeding in Wales and to gather information on farmland bird populations in this area.\tPublic record\n17727\tNRW_DS110174\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.500Z\tMarine Habitat Video Clips Available Through YouTube\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313734\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine_Habitat_videos ,  GIS subset data for video published on YouTube is only available in the Legacy CCW Archive for MapInfo format data: M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\Marine_Habitat_Videos ,  Video files held on NRW You Tube page.\tContact Charles Lindenbaum, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting for information about dropdown video surveys\tDrop down video and hand held video clips were produced to illustrate the seabed environment and its flora and fauna across Special Areas of Conservations (SACs).   Edited clips have been produced in order to provide access for Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRWs) staff to relevant underwater video.  By disseminating the data through YouTube it becomes available to external audiences and provides an illustration of NRWs work and to increase awareness of the marine environment in Wales.    These videos were produced and filmed by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of NRW. The GIS Dataset shows the location of these video surveys.\tPublic record\n17682\tNRW_DS110074\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.859Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Grey Seals Halichoerus grypus\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303734\tData - Skomer MNR, West Region drive \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\shared documents\\data\\biological data\\seals ,  Images -  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\Seals ,   SOP - DMS:  (Location to be provided by Helen Carby) Off-site boxes of the original paper data CCW/WRPB/003 \u0026amp; 004 (1991-1994 data)\tInternal Custodian: Kate Lock, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Internal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.   Grey seals are a protected species of conservation importance which live and breed in the Skomer MNR. The West Wales population is the largest in SW Britain and is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the number and survival rate of seal pups born in the MNR.\tPublic record\n18187\tNRW_DS116215\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.554Z\tConsented Discharges to Controlled Waters (Archived)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323135\tExtracted post 26/06/2016 from PALS previously extracted from WIMS in Environment Agency Universe. WIMS data is stored in a static dataset on Y:DRive and Sharefile\tHenry Forrest, Permitting Officer, Wales Permitting Centre Cardiff (Water Resources), NRW\tThis data provides details of permit details as required under the Environmental Permit Regulations. Information is held for all permit holders and covers all substances that are controlled. This data is taken from the Permit and Licencing System (PALS)\tPublic record\n17908\tNRW_DS111800\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.619Z\tCommon Scoter Survey (Melanitta nigra) in Liverpool Bay (2000-2001)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383030\tData is held as Project 44, media number 91 Further work in Liverpool Bay has included the Development of a methodology for the assessment of cumulative effects of marine activities using Liverpool Bay as a case study, held as Media No.80.\tData Custodian: Marine Ornathologist, NRW or Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services\tThe Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. It is currently listed under the Birds Directive (Annex 2) Convention on Migratory Species (Bonn Convention Appendix 2) and is featured on the UK red-listed bird of conservation concern (for the breeding population) and a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Species for conservation and is a Schedule 1 species under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981.    The majority of wintering common scoter recorded around the Welsh coast around Carmarthen Bay North Cardigan Bay and the North Wales coast which in combination are thought to hold 30-36000 birds. A further 10 sites regularly support 1000 or more birds.  These are no known major concentrations away from these 13 sites although this may be a consequence of the lack of appropriate survey. Large numbers of birds are recorded at several sites in April and May and there are significant post-breeding gatherings at three sites including Carmarthen Bay from August onwards. Casual observations suggest that these sites are not moulting grounds for the majority of birds. However there maybe as yet undetected moulting sites further offshore as is the case in Denmark.  The purpose of this data capture was to record the numbers and distribution of common scoter in the Liverpool Bay; to determine the accuracy of the two survey methods (aerial and land-based) and the correlation between the two; and to provide recommendations on future monitoring of common scoter in Liverpool bay.\tPublic record\n17789\tNRW_DS110497\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.338Z\tSea Fishing Atlas of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343937\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries_Habitat_and_sensitivity\\Fishing_activity\\ Archived as project 332, media 1291  (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1291\tInternal Custodians: Sea Fisheries Liaison Officer, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.  An atlas of sea fishing activities around the coast of Wales. The atlas includes sections on fishing activities regulations and competent authorities (see \u0027contains\u0027 field for metadata entries for the three different sections). The purpose of this data capture was to outline general fishing patterns regulations and competent authorities around Wales in order to present a general picture of fishing activity which has previously not been publicly available.\tPublic record\n17841\tNRW_DS111160\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.502Z\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Mynydd Preseli\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313630\tH: drive, under Science/TSG/PEATSURV/Upland Survey Preseli H:\\Science\\TSG\\PEATSURV\\Upland Survey Preseli  Legacy information: Draft report is currently held by Alex Turner CCWs ECN Project Officer, once finalised this will be from CCW libraries. The quadrat data is held in an excel spreadsheet, also stored by Alex Turner, whilst the vegetation maps are available as MapInfo tables on CCW Upland Ecologists user drive.\tDataset collected by CCW Staff including Alex Turner, Sam Bosanquet DK Reed AM Averis        Sam Bosanquet said that Alex never finished the Preseli write-up - with the excel spread sheets being in Ffynnon, and the GIS data being somewhere on ArcGIS. However it could still be in MapInfo format.\tThis dataset is realated to a draft Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) report entitled \u0027A vegetation survey of Mynydd Preseli Pembrokeshire 2003-2005\u0027. The quadrat data which details the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) habitat codes has not yet been validated.\tPublic record\n19173\tNRW_DS125637\t2024-05-28T13:04:11.020Z\tAssessing Welsh Aquaculture Activities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363337\tBiotopes (DMS file: ADVI-1932381820-54280):    https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/fish/Projects/AWAA%20Project/Task%2005%20-%20Interactions%20spreadsheet/FINAL_interactions_spreadsheet_biotopes_16062023.xlsx?d\u003dw20239fd02510433d8693a44239bae8c8\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1    Species (DMS file: ADVI-1932381820-54281):    https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/fish/Projects/AWAA%20Project/Task%2005%20-%20Interactions%20spreadsheet/FINAL_interactions_spreadsheet_species_16062023.xlsx?d\u003dwea1e3c443ae2413297114697497538af\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tColin Charman - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries\tThe two AWAA Interactions Spreadsheets in Excel present the potential sensitivity of biotopes (components of habitats) and species (designated within MPAs SSSIs or included within the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 Section 7 list) to the pressures associated with different aquaculture activities. The Biotopes Interactions Spreadsheet contains the information presented in the AWAA Mapping Tool.   The AWAA Mapping Tool displays the sensitivity of marine biotopes in Welsh Waters to the potential pressures associated with 11 different aquaculture activities. The Tool allows users such as developers regulators and advisors to view the potential sensitivity of biotopes around Wales or within the vicinity of a proposed aquaculture development to the potential pressures associated with that activity.   Biotope locations have been sourced from the Marine Recorder (2020) and Intertidal Phase 1 Habitat Survey (2022). The sensitivities of the biotopes are sourced from the Marine Life Information Network (MarLIN) as part of the Marine Evidence base Sensitivity Assessment (MarESA) (Tyler-Walers et al. 2022).  Full details of data sources and acknowledgements can be found on the ‘Attributions’ button.   The data viewer was created by ABPmer Ltd for Natural Resources Wales.   The AWAA Project is supported by the EU through Welsh Ministers and is fully funded by the European Maritime and Fisheries Fund  Story Map can be found here: https://smnr-nrw.hub.arcgis.com/apps/73894fae28f64ed0b9b7344dcc667493/explore\tPublic record\n19170\tNRW_DS125707\t2024-05-28T13:04:11.020Z\tCompliance Assessment of Welsh River Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) Against Water Quality Targets Associated Data (2017-2019)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373037\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/fe/Internal%20Research/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2Ffe%2FInternal%20Research%2FCondition%20assessments%2FSAC%20River%20Water%20Quality%20Compliance%202022\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120003484E0523A301C478CE56BB727C2A9A2\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BB2119901%2DA148%2D4F26%2D93EE%2D937B564A9233%7D\tHilary Foster - Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species\tThese data identify the compliance of Welsh river Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) regarding targets for seven water quality attributes: Dissolved Oxygen Biochemical Oxygen Demand Total Ammonia Unionised Ammonia Trophic Diatom Index pH and Acid Neutralising Capacity. The targets and methods of assessment are set at a UK level by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee as published in the Common Standards Guidance for Rivers 2016 and form part of the conservation objectives for the riverine SAC features.  There are nine SACs in Wales with riverine features (known in their respective Core Management Plans as): • River Dee and Bala Lake  • Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn  • Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion (Afon Glaslyn)  • Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd • Afon Teifi  • Afonydd Cleddau  • Afon Tywi  • River Usk  • River Wye   For the purposes of water quality reporting Natural Resources Wales (NRW) divides rivers into units called water bodies. A total of 119 (of a possible 127) water bodies each with a single sampling point were assessed for one or more attribute.   Water quality data collected by NRW’s routine surveillance monitoring programme for a three-year period (1st of January 2017 – 31st December 2019) were extracted from the NRW water quality database (KiWQM) to assess compliance with targets.    Data were not available for all attributes for all water bodies due to the risk-based design of NRW’s monitoring programmes. The majority of water bodies were assessed for more than one attribute. All data were subject to robust quality assurance processes. The data consists of two products: 1. An Excel spreadsheet summarising results and 2. GIS layers consisting of the WFD catchment area water bodies clipped to the Welsh border and excluding water bodies not overlapping with river SAC boundaries.\tPrivate record\n18530\tNRW_DS121629\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.503Z\tSource Protection Zones (SPZ) Locations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363239\tx:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPZ Source Protection Zones Location.lyr\tDave Jones, Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tSPZs are defined around large and public potable groundwater abstraction sites. The purpose of SPZs is to provide additional protection to safeguard drinking water quality through constraining the proximity of an activity that may impact upon a drinking water abstraction. This is part of an initial screening process in assessing impacts to groundwater resources.   Zones around location sites are defined by groundwater travel time to an abstraction. This is determined through applying groundwater flow models run at the location of abstractions inputting parameters such as flow direction geology type rainfall and hydrological boundaries. SPZs provide a visual representation of the increased risks as you get closer to the abstraction.   The following subdivisions are defined within SPZs:   Zone 1: (Inner Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by a travel time of 50-days or less from any point within the zone at or below the water table. Additionally the zone has as a minimum a 50-metre radius. It is based principally on biological decay criteria and is designed to protect against the transmission of toxic chemicals and water-borne disease.   Zone 2: (Outer Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by the 400-day travel time from a point below the water table. Additionally this zone has a minimum radius of 250 or 500 metres depending on the size of the abstraction. The travel time is derived from consideration of the minimum time required to provide delay dilution and attenuation of slowly degrading pollutants.   Zone 3: (Total catchment) - This zone is defined as the total area needed to support the abstraction or discharge from the protected groundwater source.   A further Zone 4 or ‘Zone of Special Interest’ was previously defined for some groundwater sources. These zones highlighted areas (mainly on non-aquifers) where known local conditions meant that potentially polluting activities could impact on a groundwater source even though the area is outside the normal catchment of that source. In future this zone will be incorporated into one of the other zones (1 2 or 3) whichever is appropriate in the particular case.\tPublic record\n18213\tNRW_DS116259\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.531Z\tCoastal Design - Extreme Sea Levels (archived data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323539\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB confidence intervals.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB Estuary boundaries.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB extreme sea levels.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB Gauge data.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB Surge shapes.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM.\tThis dataset has now been superseded by data provided by the Environment Agency for England and Wales please see metadata number EXT_DS123552 - \"Environment Agency: Coastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) publication 2018 - England and Wales (3rd Party Data)\".  This is a GIS dataset and supporting information providing design / extreme sea level and typical surge information around the coastline of Wales under present day conditions. This is a specialist dataset which informs on work commenced around the coast ranging from coastal flood modelling scheme design strategic planning and flood risk assessments.   The dataset contains the following layer components:  -  Extreme Sea Level Values: Extreme sea levels for a range of return periods  -  Extreme Sea Level Confidence Level: Confidence intervals providing allowances for uncertainty  -  Estuary Boundaries: Inland estuarine boundaries of the extreme sea levels  -  Surge Shape Locations: Reference to surge shape appropriate for each Class A gauge site and where to apply it  -  Tide Gauge Locations: Location of gauging stations\tPublic record\n18154\tNRW_DS115976\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.921Z\tIntegrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393736\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tGillian Walker, Data Manager Sites, Terrestrial Management Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW. Christine Edwards, Protected Sites Team Leader, NRW.  Support Desk Team Leader, Information Group, NRW.\tThe Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) is updated continually and consists of several modules each of which relate to protected sites across Wales. These modules include: Casework - records of all consultations about development proposals and plans received by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW\u0027s responses; Consents and Assents - records for all Section 28 and Regulation 48 Consent and Assent requests received by CCW and CCW\u0027s responses; Contacts - information of individuals and organisations who did business with CCW;  Features - qualifying and non-qualifying features (species and habitats) on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs);   Land Agency - manages award and payment process for management agreements on NNRs and SSSIs including land purchases and buildings leases; Licencing - details of the species licences granted by CCW to individuals and organisations; Monitoring data - details about which SSSI Features have been monitored; Open Access -  records of consultations in relation to the Countryside and Rights of Way (CRoW) Act 2000 in relation to parcels of land known as mountain moor heath or down; Protected Sites - information on protected sites in Wales and provides historical data on designation dates aiding the reporting process for designations in Wales; Site Actions - documents and shares information on who needs to do what where in order to get special conservation sites into favourable condition; Wales Survey Inventory (WALSIN) - Index to all environmental surveys of NC/NCC since 1949 and bibliography of all files held by NCC including archives from approximately 1900 until March 1992 (No Actual Data); (See Contains for more information)\tPublic record\n18581\tNRW_DS121916\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.831Z\tSeagrass Area\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393136\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Areas\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tWater Framework Directive seagrass area data. This data was mapped by tracking the seagrass bed using a hand held GPS device either by foot or by hovercraft\tPrivate record\n17791\tNRW_DS110496\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.403Z\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of Rhinog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Adjacent Land\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343936\tHard copies in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copies \u0026amp; electronic copies of reports with CCWs upland ecologist .  Digitised versions of maps for Rhinog SSSI survey also with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tThis data collection was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in order to map describe and assess the vegetation of the Rhinog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in north Wales. Ninety-five types of vegetation and habitat were records the most extensive being lightly grazed Calluna vulgaris heaths.\tPrivate record\n17794\tNRW_DS110495\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.529Z\tUpland Vegetation Survey of the Pumlumon Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSI)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343935\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tThis dataset consists of the results from a vegetation surveys at Pumlumon Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) on the borders of Powys and Ceredigion. In September 2004 a survey of the National Vegetation Classification communities present together with an assessment of the vegetation condition of four habitat types. The work was carried out by Rigby Jerram Ecological Consultants on behalf of Countryside Council for Wales.\tPrivate record\n17734\tNRW_DS110113\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.788Z\tStatus of Breeding Common Scoter Melanitta nigra nigra in Britain and Ireland in 1996: The Effect of the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313133\tReport is held as Project No. 25, media 71 (File path: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media071),   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Headquarters, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW (At the point of collection was Bill Sanderson, now Lucie Haines) Metadata Contact: Data services Team\tFollowing concerns regarding potential effects of the Sea Empress oil spill in Carmarthen Bay February 1996 on the British and Irish populations of breeding Common Scoters Melanitta nigra nigra their status and distribution were determined from a survey in May - June 1996.  The survey was undertaken in five areas (Shetland the Flow country West and South Scotland Islay and Ireland) using methods devised during the first complete British and Irish census of this species in 1995.  Survey effort was concentrated on sites that held Common Scoters in 1995 and on sites with historical records.    The survey visited 223 sites and located 376 birds.  This was 79 fewer birds than the previous year a 17% decline in numbers (16% in Ireland and 19% in Britain) a 19% decrease in occupied 10-km squares and a 31% decrease in occupied sites compared to visits made at a similar time in 1995.  It is possible that a decline of this magnitude may be within the range of natural population fluctuations or it could be due to the late arrival of birds after the late spring in 1996.  However over the last eight years the number of Common Scoters in the Flow country has declined by 42% from an estimated 48 pairs in 1988 to only 28 in 1996.  Given the Common Scoter\u0027s threatened status in the UK that the dynamics of the population are unknown and the potential decline annual monitoring of both numbers and productivity is a high priority. Data used to assess the potential effects of the Sea Empress oil spill in Carmarthen Bay February 1996 on the British and Irish populations of breeding Common Scoters Melanitta nigra nigra.\tPublic record\n18258\tNRW_DS116288\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.980Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Recreation Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323838\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\NRW Forest Recreation Areas.lyr\tCustodian: Heather Crump, National Forest Planning Team     Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tA recreation area feature which is managed and has a physical boundary (e.g. a car park play area or building). There are a number of Asset Types: -  Vehicle:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset or regularly used natural feature which has management activity and investment e.g. Car park layby.  -  Visitor area:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset which has management activity and investment e.g. BBQ area picnic education events arboretum.  -  Sports:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset (or sub-let) which has management activity and investment e.g. Archery bike park ropes course GoApe.  -  Building:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset which has management activity and investment e.g. Visitor centre toilet cycle hire catering shelter.  -  Play:  NRW area designated for play activities which has management activity and investment e.g. Deb building play area paddling.  -  Accommodation:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset which has management activity and investment e.g. Camp site cabin site caravan site wild camp.  - Ponds/Lakes:  Natural or man-made feature which has management activity and investment (Natural fishing model boats duck flighting). -  Concert:  NRW area designated for concert activities which has management activity and investment (Stage backstage viewing area).   Additionally these asset types are expanded on using a range of Asset Subtypes;  Car Park Layby BBQ Area Picnic Archery Bike Park Water Sports Visitor Centre Toilet Cycle Hire Information Office Wildlife Hide Heritage building Shelter Den Building Play Area - Formal Play Area - Wild Paddling Camp Site Cabin Site Caravan site Natural Fishing Model Boars Duck Flighting Stage Back stage Viewing Area Woodland Bays Events Go Ape Climbing Education Ropes courses Catering Forest Classroom Retail Outlet Arboretum Memorial Trees Wild Camp Area.\tPublic record\n19171\tNRW_DS125728\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.962Z\tAlgae Raw Data Sample Forms - North Wales (paper data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373238\tPaper data records\tJenny Owen, Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis and Reporting\tThese paper data records are analysis forms of ad-hoc incident algal samples. They were taken in the area of North Wales covered by Environment Agency Wales.\tPrivate record\n17793\tNRW_DS110494\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.527Z\tUpland Vegetation Survey of parts of the Migneint-Dduallt Area\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343934\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\t(Formerly: Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tThis survey was commissioned by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW). The work was done to map and describe the vegetation of several blocks of upland in the  Migneint-Dduallt area of north-west Wales and to help define the nature and boundaries of the Migneint-Dduallt Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Special Area for Conservation (SAC). The whole survey area measures 58 square kilometres in area and has an altitudinal range of 170-854 m. It consists of various types of habitat including level valley floors steep valley sides irregularly undulating hills and plateaux and the high steep-sided mountains of Arenig Fawr and Moel Llyfnant. The bedrock is a mixture of sedimentary and igneous rocks of Ordovician and Cambrian age though much of the land is overlain by peat.\tPrivate record\n18583\tNRW_DS122102\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.860Z\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitats Cycle 2 (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313032\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2012\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.  Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset collated as part of the Habitats Directive reporting cycle 2 for Article 17 terrestrial habitats. Article 17 required a report to be sent to the European Commision every 6 years follwoing an agreed format. JNCC collate theses on behalf of the UK Country Agencies and this data was sent to JNCC for the collation. Article 17 reports are an assessment of conservation status of the habitat and species targetted by the directive. The assessment is made based on information on status and trends of species populations or habitats and on information on main pressures and threats. An important component of the Article 17 report is a map of habitat or species distribution mapped in 10x10 km grid.\tPublic record\n18745\tNRW_DS123393\t2024-05-28T12:55:19.738Z\tMussel SO HRA 2014\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333933\tx:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Fishery casework\\Mussel_SO_HRA_2014.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18370\tNRW_DS116726\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.473Z\tStackpole National Nature Reserve Species Inventory and Ad Hoc sightings from around Pembrokeshire (extracted from Recorder 6)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373236\tThis data is held in Recorder 6 under the survey tag Stackpole. In addition to this there is also a lot of data held in the Stackpole Office. These are kept with the NNR managers on hard drives and have not been entered into Recorder 6 due to technical difficulties. They are held in spreadsheets, word documents as individual records and ad hoc sightings.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas.   This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tPaul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South, NRW\tThis dataset contains species data from across Pembrokeshire and specifically Stackpole National Nature Reserve (NNR). The data was collected as part of Special Area of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest monitoring. It includes data from Stackpole Castlemartin Broomhill Burrows Corsydd Llangloffan and Freshwater East.\tPublic record\n18657\tNRW_DS123249\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.409Z\tWFD Groundwater Baseline\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323439\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Groundwater Baseline Cycle 1.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge\t\tPrivate record\n18464\tNRW_DS119299\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.196Z\tSection 42 Bryophytes Register\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323939\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophytes S42 points.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tA set of records of bryophytes listed on Section 42 of the NERC Act localised as accurately as possible and with the most recent record for a site listed rather than a full set of records from each site.\tPublic record\n19175\tNRW_DS125610\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.969Z\tNutrient Review - Lake Mean Totals and Monitoring Locations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363130\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Lake Monitoring Locations.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tLake mean total nitrogen and phosphate concentrations for monitoring sites between 2014 and 2019.\tPublic record\n17594\tNRW_DS108883\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.091Z\tInvestigation into the Role of the Ancient Woodland Inventory, Historical Maps and Indicator Species in Identifying Ancient Woodland in Wales (2007)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383833\tPaper reports - held in CCW libraries (Regional and HQ- see related titles field) [Ffynnon Reference DCT-12-243383]\tJim Latham, Woodland Ecologist, NRW\tThe results of an experimental study involving desk study field survey and statistical analysis which investigated whether a more robust basis for identifying ancient woodland in Wales could be developed. Dataset includes species records from 20 study sites a provisional list of vascular plant ancient woodland indicator species for Wales broken down by region results of statistical tests etc. Overall aim was to investigate whether a more robust basis for identifying ancient woodland in Wales can be developed. This included a comparison of the AWI and Epoc 1 County Series OS maps in terms of the identification of possible ancient woodland and a review of the use and reliability of Ancient Woodland Indicator Species in Wales.\tPublic record\n19164\tNRW_DS125726\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.626Z\tEryri snowbed bryophyte monitoring 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373236\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FEryri%20Snowbed%20Bryophytes%202023\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet : Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA survey of snowbed bryophytes in two areas of Eryri along with setting up of monitoring plots for those species\tPublic record\n17921\tNRW_DS114338\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.812Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Transects in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333338\tArchive project 199, media 1218. Data is held within Marine Recorder as No. MRCCW16300000012\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct Intertidal Special Area of Conservation monitoring.  Transect survey of sediment flats in Camarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC in June 2007. In Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Large shallow inlets and bays estuaries and Mudflats and sandflats not covered by sea water at low tide. Rockpools overhangs and caves have also been identified as important elements of the large shallow inlets and bays feature and of Sites of Special Scientific Interest and metadata for these datasets are documented elsewhere.\tPublic record\n18435\tNRW_DS119255\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.248Z\tBottlenose Dolphin Monitoring in Cardigan Bay 2014-2016\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323535\tData is archived as Project 479, Media 1556:  K:\\archive\\Media 1501 -1600\\Media 1556\tCharlie Lindenbaum, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tThis dataset contains the results of bottlenose dolphin monitoring work conducted by the Sea Watch Foundation between 2014 and 2016 on behalf of Natural Resources Wales. The semi-resident population of bottlenose dolphins found in Cardigan Bay is one of two major coastal populations in the UK. The species is the primary feature of Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and also a qualifying feature of Pen Llŷn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC in northern Cardigan Bay designated under the EC Habitats Directive. The primary objective of this contract was to monitor the bottlenose dolphin population of Cardigan Bay including both Cardigan Bay SAC and the wider Cardigan Bay area.\tPublic record\n17482\tNRW_DS100791\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.291Z\tSquirrel Survey of Aberhirnant Forest and Woodland surrounding Lake Vyrnwy (1999 - 2000)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373931\t[A] Species data held in Recorder 6\tLiz Hallewell - Mammal Ecologist\tThis dataset is made up of presence or absence records for red and grey squirrels in Aberhirnant Forest and the woodlands surrounding Lake Vyrnwy.   The purpose of this data capture was to report on the presence distribution and likely status of red squirrels in Aberhirnant Forest and the Lake Vyrnwy area.  The data is will be used as a basis to assess whether red squirrels have a future in this area that is sufficient potential to justify further monitoring and sympathetic forest management. The target sites included conifer plantations of Aberhirnant Forest and the catchment area of Lake Vyrnwy.\tPublic record\n17379\tNRW_DS24423\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.986Z\tWales Survey Inventory (WALSIN) Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343233\tThis dataset is held in the DMS: Knowledge \u0026gt; Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Records Management \u0026gt; CCW Records \u0026gt; Walsin Records  KNOW-55-75   https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/Records-Management/CCW%20Records/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?id\u003d%2Fteams%2Fkdim%2FRecords%2DManagement%2FCCW%20Records%2FWalsin%20Records\u0026amp;viewid\u003db151fb6e%2Dc7d5%2D4ca4%2Da30d%2D4d258f16d972   This is a legacy CCW dataset which used to be held in the ISIS Database before it was moved to the DMS. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files. [A] Was held in ISIS database under WALSIN ISIS database  [B] Was Held in ISIS (Oracle) database.\tData Custodian: Beth Hall - Library and Information Services Team Leader.  Support Desk Team Leader, Information Group. NRW\tIndex to all environmental surveys of NC/NCC since 1949  and bibliography of all files held by NCC including archives from approximately 1900 until March 1992. It lists every file note that holds survey information but it does not hold actual data. Every survey has a separate entry on the database consisting of two screens: the first screen contains structured data giving bibliographic geographic and scientific details for the survey the second a summary of the survey\u0027s objectives methodology results and conclusions together with notes on the number of sites in the survey maps and photographs.  Generally the records are limited to one line of information in the objectives methodology results and conclusions fields.  WALSIN can only be used for archival storage and retrieval and does not allow new data input.\tPublic record\n17740\tNRW_DS110476\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.647Z\tPhase 2 Upland Vegetation Surveys of Wales (1999 - 2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343736\tNRW\u0027s Upland Ecologist holds a copy of each of the reports; they are also available from the HQ and regional NRW libraries MapInfo tables are stored on former CCW\u0027s m:drive: M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\upland_survey  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Upland  The NVC data is stored in Recorder under one survey CCW Upland Survey CCW0007400000001. There are separate events for each geographic location.\tInternal Custodian: Jane Stevens, TEG, NRW Formerly Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tThis dataset contains both the non-spatial and spatial (GIS) outputs from a vegetation surveys of upland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales to National Vegetation Classification (NVC) level.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide data on the extent and distribution of vegetation communities in the Welsh uplands for planning notification and management purposes. Phase 1 level vegetation surveys of around 48 upland sites in Wales.   Each site has a dataset which includes a published report quadrat data tables maps (most paper maps have been digitised and are available as MapInfo tables and ESRI Feature Classes) and site descriptions.\tPublic record\n17627\tNRW_DS109818\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.789Z\tShellfish Stocks, Oystercatcher and Knot (bird) Modelling Data for the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers (2004-2007)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383138\tAll the data is archived in project 158 and media no. 594. A copy is held on the MFSG shared drive with the report.\tInternal Contact: Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders which feed on the mudflats and sandflats over the winter period. Oystercatcher feed predominantly on cockles and knot feed on a range of prey including cockle spat.  CCW commissioned a series of studies to determine the food requirements of oystercatcher and knot to develop conservation objectives monitoring targets and to assess the implications of cockle and mussel fishing scenarios on the bird populations. The latest of which is published as CCW Marine Monitoring Report no. 65. The purpose of this project was to extend previous modelling to incorporate both the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers in order to determine the inter-reliance of the sites and how environmental change (e.g. changes in shellfishing habitat loss changes in human disturbance climate change and changes in population size) affects foraging animal populations.  This information will be used to help inform management of the SPA.\tPublic record\n18137\tNRW_DS115636\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.844Z\tThe Feasibility of the Eradication of the Non Native Species Devil\u0027s Tongue Weed (Grateloupia turuturu) in Wales - Milford Haven Survey July 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363336\tArchived as project 445, media 1486. Marine Recorder,  Survey_ID: MRCCW30000000092\tCustodian Contact: Ben Wray Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecology, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Data ServicesTeam\tGrateloupia turuturu Devil\u0027s tongue weed is a Non Native Species (NNS) now found in the UK. Devil’s tongue weed grows in sheltered areas including harbours and bays attached to pontoons harbour walls shells and stones. It occurs on the lower shore in pools and subtidally to 7 m.  Due to its tolerance of low salinities it may be found in estuarine areas as well as fully marine waters.    A survey was undertaken to search for and record the presence of G. turuturu within Neyland marina and the surrounding Milford Haven area.  The results showed that 38% of the pontoons and other floating structures surveyed in Neyland marine and wider Milford Haven waterway had a presence of G. turuturu. It is postulated that Neyland marina provides a suitable environment for reproduction of G. turuturu and introduction may have occurred through ship ballast water and/or the import of live shellfish associated with local aquaculture.   The purpose of this survey was to assess the extent of dispersion of G. turuturu in Neyland marina and the wider Milford Haven waterway with the purpose of establishing whether eradication of this species was feasible. Information on the extent density and dispersion of G. turuturu in the Milford Haven waterway may also influence future management responses to this and other marine non-native species.  Recording the extent of G. turuturu informs future management response and helps decide the feasibility of eradicating the species.\tPublic record\n17359\tNRW_DS24263\t2024-05-28T12:20:41.048Z\tInventory of Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey Sites 1996 - 2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323633\tMaster copies of data is available to MFSG staff: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Ph1sites\\IntertidalPhase1SurveySiteInventory.xls\tInternal Custodian:  Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThis dataset comprises of an Microsoft Excel spreadsheet listing all intertidal sites surveyed by the Intertidal Team as part of the Marine Phase 1 Intertidal Phase 1 Mapping Survey.  It provides a summary of site code site name the year surveyed the extent of the site (grid refs) which Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) guidelines the site was assessed and qualified under if any and the original version of the biotope classification the site was surveyed under. The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the management of the of Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey and to provide manageable areas to assist with the assessment of the intertidal for the potential of designating SSSIs. Data collection started in 1996 and finished in 2005. The site names codes and boundaries remain constant but the qualifying guidelines may go out of date if further assessments are done.  The spreadsheet should be updated in line with any future assessments.\tPublic record\n17350\tNRW_DS24261\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.701Z\tMarchlyn Mawr Ozone / NOx monitoring\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323631\tAvailable to Environmental Audit Group staff on the shared drive:  R:\\Current Projects\\ECN\\EcnData\\A_AtmosChem\\Oz_data\\ OZNOX2\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) Team, Enviromental Change Group, NRW. Environmental Change Network Team, Environmental Change Group, NRW.\tMonitoring of Ozone and NOx air pollution. Work is funded by NAW. The purpose of this data capture was to collect data on air pollution levels in North Wales and contribute to the Welsh Air Pollution database which monitors changes in air pollution levels across Wales (all other stations are in South or mid-Wales).\tPrivate record\n18373\tNRW_DS116727\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.508Z\tTycanol National Nature Reserve (NNR) Species Inventory\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373237\tAll this data is available in Recorder 6 under the Tycanol Tag.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tPaul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South, NRW\tThis dataset is made up of species sightings from Tycanol National Nature Reserve (NNR). The purpose of this data capture was to collate together species sightings for Ty Canol in order to create a species inventory for the site.\tPublic record\n17311\tEXT_DS125512\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.000Z\tPackaging Regulations - Approved Reprocessors and Exporters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353132\tN/A  (not stored by NRW – data held on a stand-alone ICT system - National Packaging Waste Database.)   https://npwd.environment-agency.gov.uk/\tKate Llewellyn - Officer 2 - Once in Ops Waste Regulation\tA public register including filters such as by Regulator that produces a pdf report in that shows the details of packaging reprocessors and/or exporters that are accredited by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the Producer Responsibility (Packaging Wate) Obligation Regulations 1997.  The report is downloadable from the National Packaging Waste Database. This is  UK wide ICT system dedicated to producer responsibility that is managed by the Environment Agency (EA) on behalf of the regulators across the UK (NRW SEPA and NIEA).\tPublic record\n17378\tNRW_DS24420\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.047Z\tProtected Sites Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343230\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tSSSI and Species Protection Team Leader, Operational Support Group, CCW\tDataset providing information on Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs) Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) Special Protection Areas (SPAs) RAMSAR Sites Geological Conservation Review Sites (GCRs) Mainre Conservation Zones (MCZs) Local Nature Reserves (LNRs) Marine Nature Reserves (MNRs) National Nature Reserves (NNRs) Biogenetic reserves Biosphere reserves National Parks Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONBs). The information varies depending on the designation.  SSSIs have site description PDOs historical data on notifications site area and central gri reference cross refrence sites and responsible officers. Other designations have similar information excluding the PDO list which is only relevant to SSSIs.  There are also some Commons with limited data recorded on the system.   The purpose of this data capture was to collate information on protected sites in Wales and provide historical data on designation dates aiding the reporting process for designations in Wales. The dataset is derived from a pre Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and is now owned managed and updated by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and is an ongoing system updated as and when changes occur to a desination or when a new site is designated.\tPublic record\n17392\tNRW_DS87108\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.002Z\tGround truthing towed video images from North Pen Llyn horse mussel (Modiolus modiolus) reef\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313038\tFire-proof storage facilities at NRW, Maes y Fynnon, on optical media.  Also maintained on backed-up server based storage as Media 280-282.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) was initially selected for two of the Annex II habitat types listed in the Habitats Directive - reefs and estuaries. Monitoring trials were developed in relation to these two features. During the late 1990\u0027s there was a dynamic period where the development of marine monitoring and surveillance under the Habitats and Species Directive was undertaken. The presence of the horse mussel Modiolus modiolus off the north Pen Llyn is thought to have been known since the early 19th Century. The extent of the biogenic reef feature was considered an important attribute to monitor as part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC. This dataset consists of digital footage of the horse mussel (Modiolus modiolus) reef collected in 1999. Data was collected to validate the results of an acoustic monitoring survey of the Modiolus modiolus reefs present within the SAC.\tPublic record\n17255\tEXT_DS123030\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.004Z\tWelsh Government: WIMD Lower-layer Super Output Areas (LSOA) Rural Urban Classification 2014 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303330\tX:\\Social Economic\\Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation\\WIMD 2014 LSOA Rural Urban Classification.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management.  Business contact may be Julie Boswell as listed for other WIMD data obtained from WG. May be a spatial version of data that is available from here https://statswales.gov.wales/Catalogue/Community-Safety-and-Social-Inclusion/Welsh-Index-of-Multiple-Deprivation/Archive/WIMD-2014\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17373\tNRW_DS24341\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.922Z\tCors Fochno Invertebrates Pitfall Trapping Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333431\t[A]  Data held in Recorder 6 under Welsh Invertebrate Database: - CCW Staff- Adrain Fowles CCW000660000000C  - Cors Fochno Pitfall Survey CCW0006600000GCB.\tInternal Contact: Mike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.   Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset was collected to assess the occurrence of invertebrate species at Cors Fochno Ceredigion between 1987 and 2002.  The species were recorded in order to investigate the effect of a major fire in 1986 on the raised mire invertebrate assemblage. The data will be used for CSM assemblage monitoring.\tPublic record\n17296\tEXT_DS124491\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.339Z\tNHS health data (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234343931\tX:\\Social Economic\\Health\tInternal contact; Geospatial team\tHealth statistics by Upper / Lower Super Output Area for adult acticity diabetes heart conditions weight hospital clinics hospitals and mental components.\tPublic record\n17903\tNRW_DS111878\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.715Z\tCommunity Composition and Spatial Variation of Epifauna on the Horse Mussel Modiolus Reef (1999)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383738\t[A]  Marine Recorder survey JNCCMNCR40000775.  [B]  MNCR forms are held in CCW off-site storage.(Box CCW/MNCR/3) [C]  Report is available from CCW library (see related titles above) this survey relates to section 5.4 of the report.\tNRW Custodian Mike Camplin  Originator: Marine Monitoring Team CCW Bill Sanderson, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW Rohan Holt, Marine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to examine spatial variation in epifaunal community composition in order to inform the development of a sensitive and cost-effective future monitoring strategy.  Modiolus modiolus reef communities are comparatively rich in species and because they are also scarce in the UK it was considered important to monitor elements of community composition as an attribute that indicates the condition of these features. This dataset contains the results of in-situ sampling carried out by divers to try to determine the spatial variation in epifaunal community composition on the horse mussel reef off the coast of North Pen Llyn.\tPublic record\n18060\tNRW_DS115528\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.822Z\tDrop Down Video Data Caernarfon Bay, Tremadog Bay, Cardigan Bay and west Anglesey (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353238\tData is archived as Project No. 387, Media 1402 Working area H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\drop down video\\2012\\SOS data\tData Custodian: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW. Marine Recorder system custodian: Master Reference Data Team Metadata Point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tBangor University School of Ocean Science undertook video surveys during a research programme. This data was provided to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) so CCW could analyse the video and still images for species and habitat information. This data was then collated and entered into Marine Recorder. The purpose of this data capture and collation was to help build an evidence base on which to create informed management decisions within the marine environment. This data allows for a better Bangor University has given former CCW permission to hold reuse and release this data as appropriate.\tPublic record\n17254\tEXT_DS123460\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.908Z\tOffice of National Statistics (ONS): Built Up Area by Population Size (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343630\tx:\\Social Economic\\Office of National Statistics\\Built Up Area by Population Size.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18269\tNRW_DS116324\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.326Z\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses - Biology\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333234\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116324\tMetadata point of Contact: Data Services Custodian: Jane Roberts-  Environmental Reporting Team\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches. This datasets relates to the biological assessments. The Biology assessment gave an indicator of the overall \u0027health\u0027 of rivers. It describes water quality in terms of 83 groups of macroinvertebrates. Some of these are pollution sensitive so their presence suggested better water quality. A category was assigned to the river stretch where the monitoring point was located:  - A - Very Good: Biology similar to that expected for an average and unpolluted river of this size type and location. High diversity of groups usually with several species in each. Rare to find dominance of any one group.  - B - Good: Biology falls a little short of that expected for an unpolluted river. Small reduction in the number of groups that are sensitive to pollution. Moderate increase in the number of individuals in the groups that tolerate pollution. - C - Fairly Good: Biology worse than expected for an unpolluted river. Many sensitive groups absent or number of individuals reduced. Marked rise in numbers of individuals in groups that tolerate pollution.  - D - Fair: Sensitive groups scarce and contains only small numbers of individuals. A range of pollution tolerant groups present some with high numbers of individuals.  - E - Poor: Biology restricted to pollution tolerant species with some groups dominant in terms of the numbers of individuals. Sensitive groups rare or absent.  - F - Bad: Biology limited to a small number of very tolerant groups (such as worms midge larvae and leeches) present in very high numbers. In the worst case there may be no life present. Sample points represent one or many stretches.\tPublic record\n18337\tNRW_DS116498\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.090Z\tRiver Dee and Bala lake Special Area of Conservation (SAC) population attribute condition assessment for brook, river and sea lamprey (2014)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343938\t[A] Report Word \u0026amp; pdf format:  Reports and field note are held internally on the DMS using the following pathway Located: Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting (SMAR) \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt;  - Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 1  - Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 2 - Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 3. [B]  Field record sheets:  -Dee lamprey annual survey results 2013 – 2018.xlsx SAMP-753-222  [C]  Data:GIS data: Arc GIS Shape files 10.2.2 Spatial data is stored on NRW Y Drive. Location TBC. [D] Species Data in BIOSYS and Recorder 6\tInternal Custodian: Heather Garrett, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning team \u0026amp; Robert Evans, Senior Environmental Monitoring Officer, (A\u0026amp;R), NRW North Ops area team. Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of  lamprey populations within Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. This is in line with Habitats Directive monitoring and reporting on a 5 yearly cycle. The River Dee and Bala lake is designated under the Habitats Directive as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for several feature including brook river and sea lamprey (Lampetra planeri L. fluviatilis and Petromyzon marinus) The populations of all three species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. The River Dee and Bala lake SAC holds a significant population of these three lamprey species for Wales and Great Britain.   The aim of this data collection was to contribute towards the assessment of the condition of the shad population in the River Dee \u0026amp; Bala lake SAC as part of the Habitats Directive Report Cycles.  This dataset contains data from the following reporting cycles:  Cycle 1: 1999 – 2005  Cycle 2: 2006 – 2012  Cycle 3: 2013 – 2018 (data collection is on-going).\tPublic record\n19176\tNRW_DS125611\t2024-05-28T13:04:35.897Z\tNutrient Review - Transitional and Coastal Monitoring Locations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363131\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Transitional Monitoring Locations.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Coastal Monitoring Locations.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Coastal Water Monitoring Point Current (Winter 2019_2020) DIN WFD classification.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Transitional Water Monitoring Point Current (Winter 2019_2020) DIN WFD classification.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tThis dataset shows NRW coastal and transitional water monitoring sites where dissolved inorganic nitrogen included in assessment following data processing and application of data rules.  Current (Winter 2019/2020) dissolved organic nitrogen WFD classification for each coastal and transitional water monitoring point to understand spatial patterns in the coastal water dataset.\tPublic record\n18747\tNRW_DS123383\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.593Z\tHorseriding Access\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333833\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Horse Riding\\Horseriding Access.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18129\tNRW_DS115656\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.826Z\tApproaches for Monitoring the Effectiveness of Tir Gofal in Delivering Benefits to Farmland Birds\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363536\tAssociated data is held at ;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\16 Scoping study Tir Gofal delivering benefits to farmland birds\tInternal Custodian:  Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tTir Gofal is the main scheme in Wales within which some habitat management prescriptions are designed to provide benefits to breeding and wintering farmland birds. The aim of this project was to identify and evaluate methods of monitoring the extent to which this is achieved.  The purpose of this data capture was to assess possible approaches to monitor the effect of Tir Gofal on farmland birds.\tPublic record\n18730\tNRW_DS123344\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.990Z\tSeal monitoring sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333434\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seal monitoring sites.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s):  Monica Jones, Data Services Team Leader, NRW   Metadata Point of Contact:  Spatial and Information Services, NRW\tThis dataset shows the location of the seal sites that were monitored by legacy body CCW. The location of the sites was derived from the West Wales grey seal census and the grey seal census for North Wales. This dataset was created to support the overview of CCW’s marine SAC monitoring sites (see metadata entry NRW_DS108805).\tPrivate record\n18829\tNRW_DS124675\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.269Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Grid Poles\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363735\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Grid Poles.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Grid Cable Poles. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n19167\tNRW_DS125642\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.720Z\tDee Conservancy, Dee Estuary (Navigation)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363432\tX:\\Resource Management\\Dee Conservancy\tInternal Data Custodian: Dawn Beech, Senior Dee Conservancy Advisor, Marine Management and Advice Team, Marine Ops.  Dawn.beech@cyfoethnaturiolcymr.gov.uk Tel: 077468 742713\tMaritime map data relating to the Dee Conservancy.  Showing Natural Resources Wales’ jurisdiction as the Statutory Harbour and Local Lighthouse Authority for the Dee Conservancy including location and names of aids to navigation (buoys and markers) managed by Natural Resources Wales.  The data also shows the extent of the Port of Mostyn Harbour and the Mostyn Operational Area.\tPublic record\n18768\tNRW_DS123448\t2024-05-28T12:55:50.911Z\tNRW Hydropower teams areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343438\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Hydropower teams areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gary Ecclestone, Open Access GIS Officer, GIS and Spatial Information Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial datasets containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales hydropower team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for hydropower managed teams.  This dataset defines the hydropower team boundaries and the 18 team locations within those areas.\tPrivate record\n18625\tNRW_DS123239\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.961Z\tNon EC Bathing Waters Classification\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323339\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Non EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr   Original data is held in WISKI - KwiQM (see metadata entry116227)\tHelena Sykes?\t\tPrivate record\n18665\tNRW_DS123251\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.403Z\tWFD Rivers Baseline\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323531\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Rivers Baseline Cycle 1.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge\t\tPrivate record\n18468\tNRW_DS119300\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.325Z\tThreatened Bryophytes Species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333030\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophytes Threatened Species 2015.lyr   The spreadsheet for this dataset is available on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Bryophytes \u0026gt; Bryophyte Statuses https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Information/Bryophyte%20Statuses\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tA set of records of bryophytes listed as threatened in GB and/or Wales localised as accurately as possible with records of less than 100m resolution excluded (these are in another layer on Y:).\tPublic record\n18835\tNRW_DS124679\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.423Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Spur Road\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363739\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Spur Road.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Spur Roads. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17248\tEXT_DS123552\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.807Z\tEnvironment Agency: Coastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) publication 2018 - England and Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353532\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Estuary boundaries.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Exteam sea levels.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Exteam sea levels estuary.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Gauge data.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Surge shapes.lyr\tBen Hext\tThis metadata record is for AfA product AfA 010. This is part of Coastal Design/Extreme Sea Levels a GIS dataset and supporting information providing design / extreme sea level and typical surge information around the coastline of the UK including England Wales Scotland Northern Ireland Isle of Man and Jersey. The information is relevant under present day (year 2018) conditions and does not account for future changes due to climate change such as sea level rise. This is a specialist dataset which informs on work commenced around the coast ranging from coastal flood modelling scheme design strategic planning and flood risk assessments.\tPublic record\n17167\tEXT_DS121679\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.463Z\tThe Crown Estate: Offshore Wind Energy Site Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363739\tx:\\Resource Management\\The Crown Estate\\Offshore Wind farm cable agreements WGS84.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\The Crown Estate\\Offshore Wind Farms WGS84.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Crown Estate off shore wind farms.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Crown Estate wind farm agreement.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data team       Data can be accessed via: https://www.thecrownestate.co.uk/en-gb/resources/maps-and-gis-data/\tThis is a collation of datasets associated with the offshore wind energy.  Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to a number of Crown Estate datasets associated with the offshore renewable wind industry.  NRW can use this dataset under the terms and conditions of a bespoke Crown Estate Licence. NRW has access to \u0027Offshore Farm Cable Agreements\u0027 and \u0027Offshore Wind Farms\u0027. According to The Crown Estate: \u0027Offshore Wind Farms spatial data represent all offshore wind farms in pre-planning planning construction and operational phases in United Kingdom waters. Leasing rounds 1 2 and 3 are included along with round 1 and 2 extension Scottish exclusivity award applications Demonstration wind farms and Northern Ireland Offshore Wind Resource Area.  The boundaries are a true reflection of what has been signed in the Agreement for Lease and Lease documents\u0027\tPublic record\n17573\tNRW_DS102033\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.500Z\tCarmarthen Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Intertidal Sediment Flats Monitoring 2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303333\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 549 (File path:- K:\\Archive\\Media 501 to 600\\Media 549) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Arcive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\LSIB transects Marine Recorder file (Survey No. MRCCW3030000001E) GIS data is here:- Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to gain an understanding of the variety and species composition of intertidal communities and their macro-scale distribution within the interest features of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The present study aims to revisit the area to undertake representative sampling to be compared to the phase 1 mapping survey undertaken in 1999 and 2001 in order to assess shifts and changes in intertidal habitats within the SAC. As such the specific objectives of the current survey were therefore to:  - Undertake in-situ biotope recording along pre-defined transects to establish the extent and distribution of intertidal soft sediment biotopes - Verify field observations with quantitative sampling of the intertidal habitats using core sampling for benthic infauna (and particle size analysis for sedimentary characterisation) - Compare 2005 findings with baseline phase 1 mapping survey data with the view to detect changes or shifts in biotope distribution - Assign the 2004 biotope classification.  Between 1999 and 2001 the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) undertook a phase 1 Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) biotope mapping survey  of the intertidal areas of the Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The study identified over 80 littoral biotopes covering an area of approximately 130000 hectares and included several important sub-features such as seagrass Zostera noltii beds saltmarsh piddocks in clay hydroid rockpools tide-swept algal biotopes the presence of the polychaete worm Ophelia bicornis and the presence of the tasselweed Ruppia maritima. These habitats were resurveyed in 2004 in order to assess their current distribution and status. In 2005 a further survey was commissioned to assess the macro-scale distribution and community composition of the littoral biotopes in the SAC and determine any significant changes from the phase 1 biotope mosaic.\tPublic record\n18834\tNRW_DS124673\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.318Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Demised Premises\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363733\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Demised Premises.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Demised Premises. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18847\tNRW_DS124635\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.186Z\tAculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera assemblages at Morfa Dyffryn NNR and Morfa Harlech NNR, 2018 and 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363335\tRecorder 6 - Welsh Invertebrate Database\tDr Mike Howe, Lead specialist advisor; terrestrial habitats and species        Mike Howe to Supply full metadata\tA survey of the aculeate bees \u0026amp; wasps and flies (Diptera) of Morfa Dyffryn and Morfa Harlech dunes was undertaken in 2018 and 2019 in an attempt to determine the current condition of the two sites for these taxonomic groups.  At Morfa Dyffryn 51 aculeate Hymenoptera species were recorded during the survey including one European IUCN Vulnerable bee Colletes fodiens two Nationally Rarewasps one Nationally Rare bee three Nationally Scarce wasps and two Nationally Scarce bees. 32 aculeate Hymenoptera species of the Welsh sand dune invertebrate assemblage were recorded but seven previously-recorded species were not found.Three aculeate Hymenoptera species new to the site list of sand dune assemblage species were recorded including the Nationally Scarce spider wasp Arachnospila wesmaeli. 110 Diptera (true-flies) species were recorded including 32 recognised as part of the sand dune invertebrate assemblage. Nine dune-dependent fly species were added to the site list and nine Nationally Scarce flies were recorded of which two were new to Morfa Dyffryn. The overall totals for dune-dependent aculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera now stand at 39 and 44 species respectively.  At Morfa Harlech 57 aculeate Hymenoptera species were recorded during the survey including one European IUCN Vulnerable bee Colletes fodiens one Nationally Rare wasp one Nationally Rare bee four Nationally Scarce wasps and three Nationally Scarce bees. 29 aculeate Hymenoptera species of the Welsh sand dune invertebrate assemblage were recorded but ten previously-recorded species were not found. One Nationally Scarce species recognized as part of the dune  assemblage was recorded new to the site list. 128 Diptera (true-flies) species were recorded (including two species recorded in 2017) including 28 recognised as part of the sand dune invertebrate assemblage. Eight dune-dependent fly species were added to the site list including Six Nationally Scarce and one “Data Deficient” fly.The overall totals for dune-dependent aculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera now  stand at 38 and 41 species respectively.\tPublic record\n18667\tNRW_DS123250\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.373Z\tWFD Lakes Baseline\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323530\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Lakes Baseline Cycle 1.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge\t\tPrivate record\n18833\tNRW_DS124674\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.254Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Grid Cable\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363734\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Grid Cable.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Grid Cable. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17409\tNRW_DS46201\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.461Z\tImpacts of Atmospheric Nitrogen Deposition in Combination with Variable Grazing Pressure upon Upland Grazing\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436323031\t\t\tThis study was done to provide baseline data on nitrogen deposition. The following was monitored: Pollution habitats plant communities and species assemblages. The purpose of this data capture was to aid with the monitoring of Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI) features and allow staff to make informed decision on the conservation management of upland sites especially those in ESA or with management agreements.\tPrivate record\n18755\tNRW_DS123382\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.550Z\tForest Schools\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333832\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Forest Schools\\Forest Schools.lyr\tSue (Education) Williams\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18770\tNRW_DS123449\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.100Z\tNRW Landscape teams areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343439\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Landscape teams areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gary Ecclestone, Open Access GIS Officer, GIS and Spatial Information Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial datasets containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales landscape team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for landscape managed teams.  This dataset defines the landscape team boundaries and the 26 team locations within those areas.\tPrivate record\n18310\tNRW_DS116332\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.929Z\tExtractive Materials Management Statement Summaries – Corporate Entities Only\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333332\t[A] Extracted from Extractive Materials Management Statements.\tTim Morris: Waste Permiting       This entry is not added to Lle or Data.Gov to proactively publish it because the data is old and not something we recommend using, unless specifically requested, although it will be available to download from ShareFile as Open Data from the metadata\tExtractive Materials Management Statements are sent in by operators of mines and quarries to demonstrate that extractive materials produced on site are not extractive waste and therefore do not require a permit under the Environmental Permitting Regulations (EPR) to manage this material as would otherwise be required under the Mining Waste Directive. EMMS are not submitted under any legislative framework but are submitted as a result of an agreement between ourselves and the mines and quarries sector as a way of stating what materials are not extractive waste due to European Court of Justice rulings that define extractive waste in a more restrictive way that the Directive. The EMMS spreadsheet holds summary details of all EMMS submitted to us since September 2010. Subsequent requests for individual EMM Statements themselves will be treated on an individual basis and will be checked for relevant confidentialities in accordance with normal procedures.\tPublic record\n18025\tNRW_DS114943\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.642Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera) Beds in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393433\tArchived as project 199 media 1350.   Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data.  Species data also available through Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas such as lagoons and channels which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina) is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Data is captured to support reporting on conservation status of the piddock feature. Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae). Specific areas of interest include areas of eelgrass Zostera noltii in the Burry Inlet and the monitoring aimed to map the area covered by Zostera at the two sites; sample points on a grid across those areas identified to have Zostera to estimate the cover of surface species.\tPublic record\n17265\tEXT_DS123022\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.574Z\tESTYN: Higher Education (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303232\tX:\\Social Economic\\Education\\ESTYN Higher Education.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18767\tNRW_DS123454\t2024-05-28T12:55:50.810Z\tPreviously Licence Exempt Ground Water Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343534\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Previously Licence Exempt Ground Water Areas.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18341\tNRW_DS116505\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.145Z\tRiver habitat condition assessment using 2013 and 2014 River Habitat Survey (RHS)  data and Common Standards Monitoring guidance for River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353035\t[A] Reports and database exports held internally on the DMS using the following pathway: Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting (SMAR) \u0026gt; freshwater monitoring Running waters Habitats Directive data Cycle 3.  [B] Spatial data currently on W drive Heather Garrett Wye RHS but waiting for guidance to move to Y drive. [C] Species data held on BIOSYS \u0026amp; Environment Agency RHS database.\tInternal Custodian: Heather Garret, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team, NRW Melissa Lacan, Senior Env Monitoring Officer (Biology), Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R   Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of a condition assessment of the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) physical river habitat quality using River Habitat Survey (RHS) and LEAFPAC (river macrophyte) survey data 2012 to 2016. Former Environment Agency RHS database contains welsh data which is now held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  NRW is responsible for monitoring the condition of the river habitat quality on SAC rivers designated for the Ranunculion feature; more formally known as \"Water courses of plain to montane levels with the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion vegetation” (EU habitat code 3260). The RHS \u0026amp; LEAFPAC surveys are conducted as part of the Water Framework Directive (WFD) water body classifications. In addition NRW is required under the Habitats Directive to evaluate the conservation status of this river habitat. Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance recommends that some of this data can be used to assess the following physical habitat attributes: Habitat Modification Score; Riparian zone vegetation naturalness; Large woody debris; Fine sediment; Negative indicators: alien / locally absent species. The Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance sets out eight river habitat quality assessment attributes for the condition of this SAC feature. Specific details for RHS survey methods are detailed in The River Habitat Survey manual produced by the Environment Agency in 2003. Details for the LEAFPAC macrophyte survey method are found in Surveying freshwater macrophytes in rivers Operational instruction 131-07 produced by the Environment Agency in 2008.\tPublic record\n18161\tNRW_DS115877\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.740Z\tQuantifying the Status of Great Crested Newts (Triturus cristatus) in Wales (2014)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383737\tA pdf of the report is available in Ffynnon: NRW-15-014399\tContact: Species Team Leader and Herpetologist Associated Report Title:  Quantifying the Status of Great Crested Newts in Wales.French GCA, Wilkinson JW, Fletcher DH, and Arnell AP. 2014. NRW Science Report 31  Ffynnon Number: NRW-15-014399\tThe Great Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) is declining in the United Kingdom despite full European-level protection. Reporting on the species status to the EU is carried out at UK level but nature conservation is a devolved function. Knowledge and quantification of the Great Crested Newts status in Wales will therefore forms part of future UK wide reporting.  The purpose of this GIS based dataset was to: (i) Demonstrate the applicability of GIS techniques to quantification of conservation status parameters and predictive mapping; and (ii) Produce regional fine-scale spatial outputs (at 25 m resolution) which could be aggregated to create an objective quantification of spatial metrics for the Great Crested Newt across Wales.  Combined model outputs were good to excellent (AUC 0.71 to 0.86) and successfully quantified range (7312 km squared) population (3271 occupied ponds) and habitat (22170 km squared) parameters as well as suggesting a proxy quantification for habitat quality (810 high quality ponds). Article 17 reporting is based on quantification of four parameters: range population habitat for the species and future prospects.   Range: The defined range for the great crested newt in Wales is calculated using a convex hull algorithm in the R statistical software. The input data consisted of a collection of all known presence locations. The alpha value used was 8000 arrived at by trialling several values and selecting the one most representative of the observed core distribution in Wales.  Population: Population is number of populations equating to number of occupied ponds. To determine this number for Wales the EQSS threshold for each model was used to determine the areas in which suitable ponds might be found. The suitable habitat was then clipped to the known range of the species in Wales and subsequently used to clip a UK wide pond dataset extracted from Mastermap.   Habitat for the species: The EQSS outputs were converted into polygons from which total area in km squared was derived. This area was then clipped to the known range of Great Crested Newts in Wales to provide an estimate of the suitable habitat available to the species within their range.   Future Prospects: It theoretically includes all factors potentially impacting great crested newts at sites and/or larger scales including (but not limited to) legislation climate or site-specific factors. The UKs latest Article 17 report for the great crested newt lists Future Prospects as unknown. With application of the modelling processes described in the present report to Scotland and regions of England confidence in habitat and population metrics could be improved to allow a UK-wide assessment of Future Prospects.\tPublic record\n17088\tEXT_DS114689\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.617Z\tNational Water Vole (Arvicola amphibius) Database and Mapping Project (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363839\tThis dataset is currently managed and supplied by RSWT (hosted by Hampshire and Isle of Wight Wildlife Trust) on behalf of the Environment Agency. NRW hold this data currently on the M Drive: M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Water_Voles\tGIS Service Desk  External Contact: Helen Perkins, Water for Wildlife Project (Royal Society of Wildlife Trusts)\tIn 2007 the UK Water Vole Steering Group highlighted the need for a concerted effort to collate and map available data in order to assess the national status and trend of the species and to report against national Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) targets. It also recognised the potential of a spatial water vole layer which could be used to support conservation measures and enable more strategic working at local regional and national level. The Water Vole Database and Mapping Project was established in January 2008 with funding provided by the Environment Agency Royal Society of Wildlife Trusts (RSWT) and People\u0027s Trust for Endangered Species. Further funding was later provided by Scottish Natural Heritage. This dataset comprises a GIS of water vole alert areas local key areas and regional key areas for England Scotland and Wales 2004-2008. This GIS has been derived from water vole records provided to the project for this purpose by a total of 70 data providers. The GIS must be used in conjunction with the Guide to the Use of Project Outputs 2010 provided to recipients of the mapping outputs in 2010. The project is managed by RSWT with work delivered by a Water Vole Information Officer based at Hampshire Wildlife Trust. Variable information is from a wide range of suppliers.\tPublic record\n19108\tNRW_DS125437\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.957Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343337\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Tidal Flaps.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Watercourses.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Pumping Stations.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Boundaries.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Sluices.lyr ,\tJames West, Team Leader, Integrated Engineering NW\tDrainage districts are typically found in low-Iying land where boundaries are determined by physical not political attributes. Districts vary in nature and size from the mainly agricultural (such as Conwy Llanfrothen and Towyn) to the semi-urban (such as the Gwent Levels).    Drainage districts are at risk from various sources of flooding. Without flood risk and water level management drainage districts would be unsuitable to live in and would not protect and provide for the variety of agricultural land utilities and transport networks that have developed within their boundaries.     This dataset shows the location of the boundaries of IDD\u0027s within Wales including the maintained watercourses tidal flaps pumping stations and for for Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge Levels \u0026amp; Lower Wye and Powysland it also shows locations of sluices.     Current Drainage Districts as of 2022:   Afon Ganol Ardudwy Marsh  Borth Bog   Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge Levels \u0026amp; Lower Wye (Gwent Levels)  Dysynni Valley    Glaslyn - Pensyflog    Harlech \u0026amp; Maentwrog    Llanfrothen    Malltraeth Marsh    Mawddach \u0026amp; Wnion    Powysland    River Conwy    Towyn.\tPublic record\n18632\tNRW_DS122967\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.647Z\tSurvey of distribution and extent of Didemnum vexillum in Holyhead Harbour after Storm Emma October 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393637\tMarine Recorder Spreadsheet DM.  Document: EVID-301-367 Location:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/Monitoring/Didemnum/Holyhead%20Incident%20March%202018/D%20vex%20surveys/20181207%20didemnum%20vexillum%20storm%20emma%20survey%20data.xlsx?d\u003dwa1abdda14eec415d9647c66bc054e58e   Evidence \u0026gt; Marine \u0026gt; marine Non native Species \u0026gt; Holyhead Incident March 2018 \u0026gt; D vex Survey  Images available on https://photos.app.goo.gl/w6VvJd6eoL3YTJBk8 for download\tInternal Custodian: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External contractors: Survey carried out for NRW by CloudBase Productions Ltd (Rohan Holt) with sub-contractor surveyors Kate Northen (Blue-C-Ecology), Nick Owen, Dan Gill, Liz Morris (Marine Ecological Solutions) with funds direct from WG\tThis dataset details the presence and absence of Didemnun vexillum (an invasive non-native Carpet Sea Squirt) around North West Anglesey after March 2018 when Storm Emma destroyed Holyhead marina. Live Didemnum vexillum previously found to be established on the marina structures and nearby boat hulls and moorings was presumed to be distributed on marina debris around NW Anglesey. Shore and diving surveys were conducted in October 2018 in the marina area and where the bulk of floating material was washed ashore on the NW of Anglesey coast early in March 2018. D vexillum was only located on the various marina structures that survived the storm and on boat hulls and moorings in the areas where it had been found on previous surveys.\tPublic record\n18372\tNRW_DS116767\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.370Z\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales: Site packs, paper maps and field notes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373637\tThese paper records are currently held in Maes y Ffynnon - contact James (Jim) Latham for more information.  Likely to be held in Woodland Tambour Unit in Old Post Room F12 Scans of the maps are held as tifs on Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Woodland\\Raster Data\tInternal Custodian:  James (Jim) Latham - Woodland and Spatial Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group  Metadata contact:  Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains site packs for sites surveyed or resurveyed during the Phase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales. The site packs contain site summary sheets description maps quadrat data for each site and standard data from National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The summary sheets include general site information site descriptions vegetation maps and plant community data. The data was collected to provide supplementary information for the Phase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales.\tPublic record\n17510\tNRW_DS100196\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.901Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313936\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Vegetation.lyr .   Version 3 is on the cloud for use in ArcGIS and version 2 is on M:\\Data\\Live.   Full description can be found in M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\Lookup_tables.\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing polygon layers showing the boundaries and attributes of vegetation polygons.   In 2014 the data were cleaned by NRW\u0027s Terrestrial Mapping Specialist to rectify the line work issues and the resolution was set to 0.5 m. Polygon attributes include Phase 1 habitat code(s) information on scattered vegetation uniformly distributed across an entire polygon acid flush present in patches too small to map individually dominant species codes habitats in re-vegetated quarry and spoil whether it was apparent from the OS Landline maps at the time of digitising in 2000-2002 that habitat loss had occurred name of Upland Vegetation Survey site (or site-complex) whether polygon is upland or lowland polygon area.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales. About 30 percent of upland polygons are mixed habitat with no information on habitat proportions. A voronoi version of the vegetation layer has been created to help address this (see metadata for All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer voronoi - GIS dataset).\tPublic record\n18664\tNRW_DS123259\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.426Z\tWFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 2 Interim\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323539\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 2 Interim.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge\t\tPrivate record\n18151\tNRW_DS115999\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.476Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Gower 1997\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393939\t[A] Data stored in Recorder 6 CCW0007300000005.\t\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in the Gower south Wales. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around the Gower. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tPublic record\n17397\tNRW_DS64573\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.421Z\tCasework Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353733\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.  Reports from the database may be edited to remove information that would reveal the locations of sensitive habitats or species. Individual records may be released with the authority of the D band manager responsible for the content of the record. Data may only be released for the purpose of further analyses with the authority of the casework database manager (Richard Ninnes). Analyses of the data may only be released with the authority of the casework database manager (Richard Ninnes). The database is only accessible directly to NRW staff because it gives access to letters and documents that may contain information in all categories of restricted information.\tRichard Ninnes, North Region Operations Manager, NRW Bryn Jones, Gemma Beyton\tThe casework database records all consultations about development proposals and plans received by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW\u0027s responses from across Wales. The main content is consultations received from other regulatory authorities including planning authorities seeking CCW\u0027s advice. The database includes a record of designated sites protected species and other natural heritage interests affected by proposals.  The database cross references with CCW\u0027s response letters filed on the electronic data and records management system (Ffynnon).  As of January 2010 the casework database does not include the following:  Policy consultations; Section 28 notifications from Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) owners and occupiers (use the consents database); Section 28H and Section 28G notifications (use the consents database); Applications for licences issued by CCW (Use the Licence module of ISIS).  Recording of these will be integrated with the casework database in due course.  The casework database fulfills the following functions: a) A framework for processing consultations. b) A record of documentation for each consultation. c) A consistent approach and response to consultations throughout CCW. d) Management information about consultations\tPublic record\n18462\tNRW_DS119302\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.190Z\tSection 42 Lichens Register\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333032\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Lichens S42 Inventory 2014.lyr   Excluded records these are on another layer on Y:\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tA set of records of lichens listed on Section 42 of the NERC Act localised as accurately as possible with imprecise records excluded.\tPublic record\n17487\tNRW_DS100675\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.636Z\tEC Bathing Waters Historic Compliance 1988 to 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030363735\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters Historic Compliance 1988 to 2013.lyr\tMark Squire Reporting team\tThis dataset is an archive of data relevant to the old Bathing Water Directive (76/160/EEC) and won\u0027t be updated now the directive is no longer in force. For the latest bathing water quality information please see https://environment.data.gov.uk/wales/bathing-waters/index.html. Every week during the bathing season (15 May to 30 September 1988-2014) the Environment Agency took samples from coastal and inland designated bathing waters.\tPublic record\n18722\tNRW_DS123347\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.059Z\tWales 2012 Available Intertidal Habitat\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333437\tx:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Available Intertidal Habitat\\Wales 2012 Available Intertidal Habitat.lyr\tKathryn Borrowdale\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18642\tNRW_DS123215\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.817Z\tEnvironmentally Sensitive Permanent Grassland\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323135\tx:\\Biological\\Environmentally Sensitive Permanent Grassland.lyr\tIwan Hughes\t\tPrivate record\n17419\tNRW_DS46214\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.693Z\tBenthic Marine Data from Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Technical Support (Research and Monitoring) Contracts\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436323134\tMFSG Marine Recorder database at http://data.nbn.org.uk/\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collection of marine benthic surveys related to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRWs) technical support research and monitoring contracts.  The purpose of this data capture and collation was to aid coastal zone and sea-use management and to contribute to the identification of important areas of marine natural heritage.  The surveys focussed on benthic habitats often referred to as \u0027biotopes\u0027 in the intertidal and inshore (typically within 3 nm) subtidal areas. Original copies of all the surveys are now held separately in NRW\u0027s Marine Recorder database.\tPublic record\n18620\tNRW_DS123241\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.554Z\tDangerous Substances Directive\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323431\tx:\\EU Directives\\Dangerous Substances Directive\\Dangerous Substances Directive.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18662\tNRW_DS123256\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.285Z\tWFD River Waterbodies Catchments Cycle 2 Interim\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323536\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbodies Catchments Cycle 2 Interim.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge\t\tPrivate record\n18980\tNRW_DS125158\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.217Z\tAll Wales Non-Native Marine Species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313538\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Non Native Species All Wales Marine July 2024.lyr\tDave Tavmer: Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Mapping\tThis dataset comprises data on marine non-native species in Wales.  The purpose it to gain an up to date understanding of the distribution of marine non-native species to inform their spread and inform casework advice. The data used to build this GIS layer comes from existing relevant locations where data is held and has been amalgamated into one location in ESRI format. It contains data from the 1930s up until 2023.  No new surveys were carried out for this dataset.\tPrivate record\n19090\tNRW_DS125500\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.857Z\tDNA Barcoding Survey of Dune Slack Bryum in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353030\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDune%20Bryum%20DNA%20Barcoding\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA survey of dune slack mosses using DNA Barcoding including sampling of mosses from recently scraped slacks.   The following sites were surveyed: Tywyn Aberffraw Newborough Warren Morfa Dyffryn  Whiteford Burrows Merthyr Mawr Warren Kenfig Burrows\tPrivate record\n18721\tNRW_DS123346\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.091Z\tSkomer monitoring locations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333436\tx:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Skomer monitoring locations.lyr\tPhilip Newman\t\tPrivate record\n18074\tNRW_DS115460\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.689Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring Data for River Teifi 2012/2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135343630\tData is archived as project 422, media 1440\tInternal Custodian: Terrestrial Environmental Evidence Officer, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe River Teifi has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EU Habitats Directive. The three species of lamprey for which the data was collected are listed under Annex II of the Habitats Directive. Data was collect to determine the habitat condition and suitability of habitats for the lamprey as well as make recommendations for future surveys.  Data was collected to recognized standards and data presented using a GIS data. The data was collected to determine habitat conditions for lamprey in the Afon Teifi catchment. It involved collating information on age structure distribution within the catchment ammocoete density spawning activity and barriers to migration.\tPublic record\n18359\tNRW_DS116821\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.613Z\tMarine and Coastal Recreation Audit 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383231\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\MCRA H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1501to1600\\Media1552 Archived as project 475, media 1552.\tData: Kirsty Lindenbaum, Marine Resource Management Advisor or  Mary Lewis, Marine Strategic Plan Energy and Advice Team Leader Metadata point of contact: Monica Jones.  Original data i.e.  Natural Facilities database - contact is Sue Williams, Coastal Access Database is within Jont Bullbeck\u0027s team, Richard Deering is relevant contact, although database no longer exists other than what is available as GIS layers       The datasets have not gone through the Open Data Process and therefore should possible not be classed as open data as yet.  However it is unlikely  that there are any restriction.  The Coastal Access database does not appear to have used an OS admin boundary dataset as the background as it is too generalised for high water.\tIn 2009 an audit was undertaken of existing information and knowledge on marine and coastal recreation by Land Use Consultants on behalf of the Countryside Council for Wales.  The study was undertaken in two stages: - Identify analyse and map existing data on recreational activities including an validation with key bodies and robustness test - Review existing promotional material and guidance on recreational activities in relation to their compatibility with marine protected areas. The study looked water-based activities Intertidal activities and land-based activities.  The data described here is a series of spatial datasets.\tPrivate record\n18666\tNRW_DS123252\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.358Z\tWFD Transitional Baseline\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323532\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Transitional Baseline Cycle 1.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge\t\tPrivate record\n17837\tNRW_DS111175\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.431Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Tide-Swept Boulder Habitat in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313735\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 552. [A] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 56 - MS\\ampersand\\\"CB intertidal monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Data [C] NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder - MRCCW16300000005 [D] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\GIS [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Images\"\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThere were two purposes for this data capture. Firstly to establish the reference condition from which to establish conservation objectives; and to be able to take account of natural changes in the appropriate management of the features of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Secondly to repeat survey of sites surveyed in 2005 to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data. Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC is designated for five Annex 1 habitats; Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide reefs large shallow inlets and bays; and submerged or partially submerged sea caves. Intertidal mudflats and sandflats in this location include a variety of biotopes many of which are present on the large sediment flat of Traeth Lafan. Intertidal reefs include a large variety of biotopes with tide-swept bedrock boulder and cobble habitats particularly well represented. This dataset includes the results of an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the tide-swept boulder habitat just south of Britannia Bridge during September 2007.\tPublic record\n17843\tNRW_DS111172\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.542Z\tUpland vegetation survey of Bwlch Corog, Ceredigion (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313732\tQuadrat data is held in an excel spreadsheet, stored by Alex Turner, whilst the vegetation maps are available as MapInfo tables on CCW Upland Ecologists user drive. A paper copy of the report is available from CCW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above)\tInternal Custodian:  (used to be Alex Turner) Sam Bosenquet, Snr Habitat Survey \u0026amp; Evaluation \u0026amp; Non-Vascular Plant Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of the results from vegetation survey work carried out at Bwlch Corog. Bwlch Corog is located between two Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs): Pengarreg−gopa a Moel Hyrddod SSSI and Pencreigiau\u0027r Llan SSSI which are notified for their blanket mire dry and wet heath vegetation. The survey area can be divided into three main sub−areas: the enclosed fields of Pemprys and Cefn Coch separated by the more upland area of Bwlch Corog. The vegetation of this central area can be regarded as the most semi−natural and the principal vegetation types are M25 Molinia−Potentilla mire M17 Scirpus−Eriophorum blanket mire M15 Scirpus− Erica wet heath H12 Calluna− Vaccinium heath M6 Carex−Sphagnum mire U4 Festuca− Agrostis−Galium grassland U5 Nardus−Galium grassland and U6 Juncus−Festuca grassland. There are large expanses of species−poor Molinia−dominated vegetation pointing to a prior history of frequent burning and heavy grazing which probably ceased when the adjacent forestry was planted.\tPublic record\n17056\tEXT_DS102211\t2024-05-28T12:10:09.326Z\tCadw: Register of Historic Landscapes (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323131\tx:\\Designations\\CADW\\Register of Historic Landscapes.lyr\tGIS Service Desk\tNatural Resources of Wales (NRW) are supplied a number of historic and cultural datasets under a licence from Cadw. Cadw is the Welsh Government\u0027s historic environment service working for an accessible and well-protected historic environment for Wales. They were set up with the aim of conserving Wales\u0027s heritage helping people understand and care about their history and helping to sustain the distinctive character of Wales.   The Register of Historic Landscapes were digitised from the original hardcopy depictions taken from Registers that were created in 1998 and 2001. They are used in the production and evaluation of EIA\u0027s and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tPublic record\n19005\tNRW_DS124928\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.757Z\tWelsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393238\tX drive layer file: X:\\Physical\\Peat\\Welsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory.lyr  DMS Word document explaining the attribute domains: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/peatl/Evidence Analysis Projects/Welsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory/Welsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory ArcGIS data structure.docx?d\u003dw7065453cc6ca43d887e02948ebf77083\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tKathryn Birch- Specialist Advisor Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThis dataset maps the distribution of Lowland Raised Bogs within Wales giving details on their size elevation conservation status context and whether they have been surveyed. It was compiled using data from the Phase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales (2004-2020) and aerial photographs.\tPublic record\n18288\tNRW_DS116307\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.563Z\tDangerous Substances List II/ Annex 10 Substances under WFD\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333037\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n18779\tNRW_DS123422\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.667Z\tPort of Mostyn Boundary points of development\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343232\tX:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Port of Mostyn Casework\\PM Boundary points of development.lyr\tData originally supplied by Paul Brazier, due to role changes, now managed within the Marine Area Advice and Management Team, DPAS and Marine Services\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18296\tNRW_DS116460\t2024-05-28T12:45:26.883Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Dangerous Corners\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343630\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL dangerous corners.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tDangerous corners of watercourses within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Shows dangerous corners for flailing works. Data collected and mapped for planning operations and health and safety reasons.\tPrivate record\n17360\tNRW_DS24253\t2024-05-28T12:20:41.108Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1kmsq Access Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323533\tArchived under Project number T006 Media number T_Media006.   Also available on the H Drive: H:\\Science\\Common\\Phase 1\\1 km square database\\Habsup1\\HABSUP.mdb.   The other access databases (Habsup2 , Habsup3) within this H Drive folder: H:\\Science\\Common\\Phase 1\\1 km square database\\  were intermediaries in the creation of the GIS versions 2 and 3 of the 1kmsq data.\tCustodian:  Internal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping \u0026amp; Farmland Ecologist (Clare Burrows?) Ecosystems Team NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe data are derived from a programme of field recording that was begun by the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) in 1979 and continued by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) after restructuring in 1991.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently simplified and imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales (see All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1Kmsq .    The database was created in 1999 using data from the Phase 1 Habitat Survey of Wales undertaken between 1979 and 1999.  This table was held in Paradox database but then was converted into Access. It lists Phase 1 habitat areas in every 1 km square in Wales. Habitat area measurements are sub-divided within 1 km squares by Region District Survey programme (Terrestrial Phase 1 Survey and upland surveys) Upland and lowland and for land covered by the terrestrial Phase 1 survey statutory and non-statutory at the time of survey.\tPublic record\n17697\tNRW_DS110062\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.566Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Macrophyte Condition Assessment for the Afon Gwyrfai SAC (2007)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303632\tThe report and data collected are archived as Project 198 (Media 545) and are stored in fireproof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media545\tInternal Custodian: Rivers Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Afon Gwyrfai Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Llyn Cwellyn Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) was notified in 2003 and is considered to be one of the best examples in the United Kingdom of an oligotrophic to mesotrophic river with submerged beds of aquatic plants especially its Callitricho-Batrachion vegetation community.  The purpose of this data capture was to undertake a Condition Assessment of the river macrophyte and river macrophyte-related attributes of the former Countryside Council for Wales CCW\u0027s Management Units 2 5 6 and 7 of the Afon Gwyrfai.\tPublic record\n19119\tNRW_DS125606\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.133Z\tNutrient Review - Leachate Concentrations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363036\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nutrient Leachate Concentrations.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tThis dataset forms part of the NRW Nutrient Review of 2023. This layer shows the predicted nitrate and phosphate concentration based on total surface and subsurface nitrogen load  divided by hydrologically effective rainfall for   Surface Water Catchments (mg/l) Transitional Catchments (mg/l) Coastal Catchments (mg/l\tPublic record\n17761\tNRW_DS110347\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.475Z\tEnvironmental Impact Assessment of Borden Chemicals Long Sea Outfall (March 2001)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333437\t[A] A copy of the raw data is archived as part of the Marine Sites: Data collation project as project 27, media 81 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100) [B] Species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW90000000007]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBorden Chemicals (formerly B.P. Chemicals) is a large chemical works located to the east of Barry south Wales. Effluent is released into the Bristol Channel via a long sea outfall which discharges approximately 1 km offshore at 51degrees 23.41 N 03degrees 14.49 W. This replaced an old outfall in 1993 that discharged into the intertidal zone to the east of Barry docks at 51degrees 23.67 N 03degrees 14.88 W. The effluent is a complex mix of mostly organic compounds including phenol toluene 12-dichloroethane methanol acrylonitrile and vinyl chloride.  However the discharge generally meet the levels set by their consent for materials discharged into the waters of the Bristol Channel. This dataset contains the results of a survey of the benthic environment in the vicinity of the Borden Chemicals long sea outfall east of Barry in the Bristol Channel. The purpose of this data capture was to collect data to conduct biological analysis as well as sediment samples and physico-chemical analyses to provide information for an Environmental Impact Assessment. As of the 1st April 2013 Natural Resources Wales has taken over the functions currently carried out by the Countryside Council for Wales Environment Agency Wales and Forestry Commission Wales.\tPublic record\n18141\tNRW_DS115633\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.851Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the River Wye SAC and catchment area (2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363333\tAssociated data is held at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\30 Current \u0026amp; pot distribution, cond \u0026amp; breeding success of the otter in the R Wye SAC\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring provides an assessment of the current and potential distribution and breeding success of the otter within the Wye SAC and to identify features outside the SAC boundary whose condition may influence the viability of the otter meta-population associated with the River Wye.  Results from the most recent Otter Survey of Wales (Jones 2004) show that otter signs were found within the Welsh part of the Wye catchment at 92% of sites searched. Whilst these signs are a useful indicator of otter distribution they shed little light on otter breeding. The ability to monitor breeding activity and breeding sites in the catchment is crucial if the species and its habitats are to be maintained in a Favourable Conservation Status. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the current and potential breeding success of the otter within the Wye catchment.\tPublic record\n17081\tEXT_DS114969\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.456Z\tJNCC Acoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of Selected Sites off the North Coast of Anglesey as part of the Mapping European Seabed Habitats (MESH) Project (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393639\tArchived as Project 179, Media 1357.   Master data is stored at JNCC.    Raw multibeam data is on CD-ROM (OF1001) as is processed multibeam data (OF1049), and in GIS format with other GIS data\tGIS Service Desk Subtidal Team, MFEG, NRW\tDuring the summer of 2005 JNCC collaborated with partners of the HABMAP project to survey areas of potential Annex 1 habitat (primarily reef) in the Irish Sea. This was to support nature conservation initiatives beyond 12 nm North of Anglesey. This multibeam echosounder survey was carried out alongside single-beam data collection seabed sampling and towed video imaging. Four areas of seabed covering approximately 140 sq km were surveyed and following data analysis the area of study was broadly characterised by a complex topography and mixed sediment mosaics. The purpose of this data capture was to obtain MBES coverage of the study areas for further interpretation and analysis in conjunction with other data gathered in the area for the purpose of identifying Annex 1 subtidal habitats.  Confidence in data is high. Data depth corrected for tide using POLPRED software which utilises one or more of Proudman Oceanographic Laboratory\u0027s hydrodyamic models. CARIS HIPS / SIPS software used to create surfaces and gridded datasets by experienced surveyors.\tPublic record\n18283\tNRW_DS116303\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.405Z\tNational River Flow Archive (NRFA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333033\tWelsh NRW owned data is currently held in WISKI - and sent to CEH who collate this and put in NRFA  https://nrfa.ceh.ac.uk/data/search\tInternal Custodian of the NRW Welsh Data:  Alison Hanson, Hydrometry and Telemetry.  CEH hold the custodianship over the archived UK data that they publish on the agencies behalf.\tThe National River Flow Archive (NRFA) is the UK\u0027s focal point for river flow data and is the primary archive of daily and peak river flows for the United Kingdom. The archive incorporates daily monthly and flood peak data from over 1500 gauging stations across the UK.   The NRFA holds a wide range of hydrological information to assist in the understanding and interpretation of measured river flows. In addition to time series of gauged river flow the data centre maintains hydrometric information relating to the gauging stations and the catchments they command and data quantifying other parts of the hydrological cycle.\tPublic record\n18152\tNRW_DS115996\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.927Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Anglesey 1994\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393936\tData is stored in Recorder 6 under CCW0007300000002\tAJ Turner from the North Wales Wildlife Trust was the original contractor.  Metadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW  Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Anglesey. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Anglesey. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tPublic record\n18289\tNRW_DS116306\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.533Z\tHistorical Dangerous Substances List I and List II\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333036\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116306\tJulie Boswell / Jane Roberts: Environmental reporting team\tData was collated annually until the end of 2011 for the Dangerous Substances Directive (76/464/EEC) on the discharge of dangerous substances to surface waters. The Dangerous Substances Directive was repealed in 2012 following the introduction of the Water Framework Directive (2000/60/EC) which includes monitoring and assessment of a wide range of chemicals in surface waters.  List I covers substances that are particularly toxic or persistent or may tend to accumulate in the environment. List II covers substances whose effects are less serious. List I includes 20 substances and List II has 67 substances. Every listed dangerous substance has a concentration limit known as an Environmental Quality Standard (EQS). The EQS is usually an upper concentration with the exception of pH which has both upper and lower limits. It is set for the receiving watercourse and not the discharge itself. The dangerous substance is not believed to be detrimental to aquatic life at any concentration below its EQS limit. The EQS varies for each substance and can be different for fresh estuarine or coastal waters.\tPublic record\n17303\tNRW_DS23774\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.535Z\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3233373734\t[A] Data stored in Recorder 6 under the Welsh Invertebrate Database. Recorder 6 is currently located on the legacy Countryside Council for Wales drives, accessed through VMware.\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. (Adrian Fowles who is now retired) Custodian is the Terrestrial Ecosystems Group. Some records are collected from Adrian Fowles, an ex-CCW employee - these datasets are now managed by Mike Howe.         Data entry into the WID is on-going and is being run by Mike Howe. Some entries have their own specific metadata entry, but these are usually related to contractors work. Some records within the WID are classed as either NEGATIVE RESULT, NEEDS CONFIRMING, ERRONEOUS or DUBIOUS under the attribute Recorder Name. This means that the species was absent from survey / sample if the survey was done to purposely find that species. So when conducting an extract of the data it might be worth checking and removing these entries or making sure it is reflected in the NBN Gateway metadata.\tThe purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions.  Collation of around 500000 invertebrate occurrence records most of which relate to Wales. This is the primary database used by Natural Resources Wales; incorporates other major invertebrate datasets. Includes terrestrial freshwater and coastal airbreathing invertebrates but not marine species.  The database also contains records of associated taxa and incidental records of other species including alga vascular plants amphibians mammals birds fungi reptiles and bryophytes.  Much of the data has been sent to various UK recording schemes who may also have supplied this data to the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas.  Family groups where data may already have been uploaded to the Atlas includes Cerambycidae Ciidae. Mollusca Aquatic Heteroptera Brachycera All butterfly data Odonata Ant data Tipuloidea and Trichoptera.  Mainly Wales although there are occasional records from elsewhere in the UK. The database has been maintained since 1992. Records date from 1500s to present day.  Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. However data has been transcribed by experienced invertebrate biologists in order to research incomplete records and minimise errors. Any uncertainties about a particular record are noted in the database. Systematic verification has been carried out on significant parts of the dataset.  The database is constantly updated.  This dataset comprises of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.\tPublic record\n18718\tNRW_DS123343\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.768Z\tSea cave locations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333433\tx:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Sea cave locations.lyr\tPaul Brazier\t\tPrivate record\n17723\tNRW_DS110103\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.495Z\tCommon scoter Melanitta nigra monitoring in Carmarthen Bay following the Sea Empress oil spill.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313033\tReport is held as Project No. 24, media 70.\tInternal Custodian: Patrick Lindley, Maritime Ornithologist, Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tFollowing the Sea Empress oil spill a monitoring programme was immediately established by the Countryside Council for Wales to record numbers and distribution of Common Scoters in Carmarthen Bay. Scoters generally prefer feeding in waters less than 10 m deep and previous aerial surveys in Carmarthen Bay have shown that most birds feed inshore of the 10 m depth contour which extends some 6-10 km offshore.  The purpose of this data capture was to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on Common Scoter numbers and distribution in Camarthen Bay.  There is high confidence in this dataset as surveys undertaken by experienced staff members. However no spatial reference were provided with data for statistical analysis. It is assumed for the purpose of the metadata that samples were collected using British National Grid.\tPublic record\n18338\tNRW_DS116504\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.112Z\tThe status of Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail (Vertigo moulinsiana) at Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve (NNR) (2015)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353034\tA] The final report in Microsoft Word and Adobe PDF formats. [B] Species records, which are held on the NRW Recorder 6 database CCW000800000001J NRW Contracted Invertebrate Research.\tData Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystem Services, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  This survey was done by a contactor: MJ Willings on behalf of NRW.\tThis dataset is made up of the results of a survey for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail (Vertigo moulinsiana) in 2015 confirming it\u0027s presences at Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve (NNR) following it discovery at this location in 2007 making this one of only three sites in Wales for the snail. Desmoulin’s whorl snail Vertigo moulinsiana despite being a small snail is the largest Vertigo species with a shell height up to about 2.6 mm. V. moulinsiana is only known from three localities in Wales; Cors Geirch and Afon Penrhos near Pwllheli on the Llŷn peninsula and Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve near Hay-on-Wye in Radnorshire. All of the Welsh V. moulinsiana sites are isolated and do not closely resemble the lake and river-side base-rich fens typically associated with the snail at most of its English sites.   The conservation importance of the species has meant its inclusion in various schedules and Red Data lists. It was categorised as Rare (category 3) in the UK Red Data Books and more recently has been classed as Vulnerable in the recent IUCN-based UK Red List status review. The species is listed on Annex IIa of the European Community Habitats and Species Directive (92/43/EEC) and is also a Welsh Section 42 Species of Principal Importance (replacing the UK BAP priority species in 2006). The purpose of this data capture was to survey for V. moulinsiana focussing on areas where there was known presence in 2007 and later a wider search for potentially suitable habitat within the NNR and adjoining Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).\tPublic record\n18324\tNRW_DS116346\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.355Z\tFreshwater Shad Monitoring Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333436\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Species data is held in Recorder 6. -  Shad Monitoring, Usk, Wye \u0026amp; Tywi Catchments 2006 - CCW - Report no.40 CCW0002800000005. -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2010 - CCW - Report no.10/8/1 CCW000280000000E. -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2011 - CCW - Report no.11/8/3 CCW0008000000009. -  Shad Egg Survey River Wye 2011 - CCW - Report no. 11/8/4 CCW0008000000008.  -  Shad egg Survey Afon Tywi 2012 - CCW - Report No. 12/8/4 CCW000800000000P. -  Shad Egg Survey, River Usk and River Wye, 2012 - CCW - Report No. 13/8/3 CCW000800000000Q.\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe Rivers Wye Usk and Tywi are designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) under the EC 92/43/EEC Habitats Directive for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. In the UK significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn Usk Wye and Tywi. The River Wye Usk and Tywi have been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for shads and other features including salmon lampreys and bullhead. However it is not certain whether a breeding population of allis shad still exists. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of shad populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the assessment of Favourable Conservation Status along the Wye Usk and Tywi to supplement the main shad spawning distribution condition assessment for these rivers. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.  Detailed information about each of the survey data can be found under their individual metadata entries.\tPublic record\n18143\tNRW_DS115678\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.810Z\tLow Tide Wetland Birds Surveys (WeBS) Counts and Feeding activity of Oystercatch and Knot in the Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) and Carmarthen Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) (2013-2014).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363738\tData archived as project 447, media 1488\tInternal Custodian: Marine Ornithologist Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive 2009/147/EC which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive) and for regularly occurring migratory species.  This dataset consists of spatial information detailing Wetland Birds Surveys (WeBS) Low Tide Counts carried out various SPAs in the winter of 2013/2014. These count data details feeding and non-feeding birds. The focus of the study was on Oystercatcher and Knot however other wetland birds were also included.  The aims of this study were to support further WeBS Low Tide Counts and provide additional information on the feeding behaviour of Oystercatchers and Knots in the Burry Inlet Pembrey Coast and Three Rivers Estuary during the low tide period.\tPublic record\n19151\tNRW_DS125735\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.115Z\tTimber Transport Forum Draft Road Categorisation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373335\tX:\\Transport\\Timber Transport Forum\\Timber Transport Forum All Roads.lyr\tRob Marsh - Senior Officer, Forest Planning Mid\tShapefile (lines) derived from OS Open Roads dataset for the purpose of categorising the status of roads for timber haulage. The Timber Transport Forum is developing this dataset with a view to improving communication and consultation around timber haulage. The present dataset is draft / in development and road categorisation is currently in place for small areas of Wales only.\tPublic record\n18140\tNRW_DS115634\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.845Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) in the Wye Valley and Forest of Dean Bat Sites SAC\u0027s (2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363334\tAssociated data is held at the following location at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon- H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\31 Monitoring the Welsh colonies of Lesser horseshoe Bats in the Wye Valley.\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring of Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) in the Wye Valley commuting route and foraging areas were investigated. The study considered five nursery roosts and one hibernation roost on the Welsh side of the Wales-England border. The roosts are all subject to regular annual monitoring and it was not a requirement of this study to reproduce the monitoring effort. Primary focus of the project was to identify and verify important foraging habitats for lesser horseshoe bats and to assess the condition of commuting routes between roosts and forage areas.  The purpose of this data capture was to assist in the survey each surveyor was issued with a bat detector and each team had one Pettersson D-240x and one Skye Instruments SBR2100 bat detectors available to them. Detectors were set to a frequency of 108 kHz the \u0027peak\u0027 echolocation frequency for lesser horseshoe bats.\tPublic record\n17757\tNRW_DS110463\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.472Z\tSurveys of Sea Caves in Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SAC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343633\tAll data is archived under project 243, media no. 777 including species spreadsheets reformatted for analysis in PRIMER.   Site locations are also available on the m drive and report is available in Ffynnon.  Photographs are available on the Marine Monitoring Team catalogue (CD 21, 22, 24, 44), video grabs available on the Marine Monitoring Team catalogue (CD 40).  Specimens are held by Francis Bunker and will be transferred to the National Museum of Wales in Cardiff. Original paper MNCR forms are held in CCW off-site storage.  (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tInventorial survey as a first step for former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to develop sensitivity management and monitoring for submerged or partially submerged caves.  Submerged or partially submerged caves are an Annex 1 habitat listed under the Habitats directive. Caves vary in size from only a few metres to more extensive systems which may extend hundreds of metres into the rock. There may be tunnels or caverns with one or more entrances in which vertical and overhanging rock faces provide the principal marine habitat. Sea cave communities vary considerably depending on the structure and extent of the cave system their degree of submergence and of exposure to sand scour and wave-surge and their geology. Caves are typically colonised by encrusting animal species but may also support shade-tolerant seaweeds near their entrances. Physical conditions such as inclination wave surge scour and shade change rapidly from cave entrance to the inner parts of a cave and this often leads to a marked gradation in the communities present.  Surveys were set up to identify a baseline and trial survey methodologies in order to establish surveillance and monitoring programmes for the feature.  24 caves (and tunnels) were described. They include caves that are wholly Intertidal wholly subtidal and ones with both littoral and sublittoral elements. They range from caves that extend 1-3 meters into the cliff to ones that extend up to 100 meters into the cliff.  Inventorial survey as a first step for CCW to develop sensitivity management and monitoring for submerged or partially submerged caves.  24 caves across Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Cardigan Bay SAC Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay SAC.  All measurements were taken to a standard protocol as described in the CCW Marine Monitoring Report no. 6.  Specimens were fixed and preserved.\tPublic record\n18753\tNRW_DS123386\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.692Z\tClimate Vulnerability Assessment (CVA) for Special Protection Areas (SPAs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333836\tx:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\CVA SPA.lyr\tContact Clive Walmsley, Senior Specialist Advisor, Climate Change and Decarbonisation, SPG for further information about the data.  Data originally produced and provided by Jon Rothwell, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17425\tNRW_DS98746\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.358Z\tLocal Nature Reserves (LNRs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373436\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\LNR Local Nature Reserves.lyr\tHolly York\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Local Nature Reserves (LNRs) in Wales. Local Nature Reserves are established and managed by local authorities following consultation with Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949. For a site to become an LNR it must have natural features of special interest to the local area and the authority must either have a legal interest in the land or have an agreement with the owner to manage the land as a reserve. LNR prove to be useful not only to protect habitats and wildlife but increase people\u0027s awareness of their environment. They are places where children can learn about nature and they are often situated in or near urban areas. Please refer to Countryside Act 1949 for reasons for capture of original designation. With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making. The LNRs have been designated over a number of years from 1970 to the present day and are on-going. The data has been held digitally since mid1990s. This data can be subject to change by the local authority at anytime.\tPublic record\n19094\tNRW_DS125504\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.002Z\tInformation for each Carbon Reduction Commitment (CRC) participant\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353034\tExternal Website: Published on data.gov by the EA  DO NOT PUT ON THE WAF! (Published by the EA so for NRW internal catalogue only)\tCustodian: Elizabeth Voice, Lead Specialist Officer, Species and Carbon Permitting  DO NOT PUT ON THE WAF! (Published by the EA so for NRW internal catalogue only)       DO NOT PUT ON THE WAF! (Published by the EA so for NRW internal catalogue only)\tThe CRC Order 2013 provides for publication of information on the basis of participants\u0027 annual reports plus details submitted when they registered for the scheme. The CRC Energy Efficiency Scheme (CRC) is a mandatory scheme which aims to improve energy efficiency and cut emissions for large public and private sector energy users across the UK. The scheme aims to encourage organisations to develop energy management strategies that promote a better understanding of energy usage and lead to improved energy efficiency. The scheme is split into phases: Phase 1 from 1 April 2010 until 31 March 2014 and Phase 2 from 1 April 2014 until 31 March 2019.  Each year CRC participants have to monitor their energy consumption and report this information into the CRC Registry an online IT system which calculates their carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions. Participants must purchase and surrender \u0027allowances\u0027 to cover these emissions - one allowance for each tonne of CO2.   Further information on the scheme can be found on the CRC pages of GOV.UK. The ARP format was introduced for the 2012 to 2013 reporting year and replaces the performance league tables (PLT). Removal of the PLT was part of the government’s simplification of the CRC scheme. These annually produced data sets contain the information provided by participants to the CRC Registry including organisational type energy use and CO2 emissions. The data should be read in combination with the equivalent years ‘CRC Energy Efficiency Scheme: annual report publication which are located on GOV.UK. CRC performance league tables were published for the 2010 to 2011 and 2011 to 2012 compliance years and ranked participants according to their energy efficiency performance against a range of metrics.\tPublic record\n17390\tNRW_DS24316\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.221Z\tWater Voles Site Records\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333136\t\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team (Formerly Liz Halliwell) R. Gwynne\tThis database of water vole (Arvicola terrestris) site records (including grid references) is being developed by the North Wales Riparian Mammals Group (Menter Mon Anglesey) as part of a UK steering group stategy (CCW EA Wildlife Trust and others).\tPrivate record\n18281\tNRW_DS116311\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.075Z\tDischarges of Consented Red List Substances\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333131\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116311\tSimon Bonwick, Data Services Team, NRW\tThe UK has a list (known as the Red List) of 23 of the most dangerous substances which were selected for priority control under the Integrated Pollution Control legislation (subsequently superseded by the Pollution Prevention and Control and then Environmental Permitting Regulations). This list of substances includes EC List I substances defined under the Dangerous Substances Directive as well as certain substances listed on EC List 2. There are statutory Environmental Quality Standards (EQSs) in place for their discharge into surface waters. Statutory EQSs for a further 25 substances came into force on 1 April 1998. These deal with substances produced by manufacturing industry as well as a number of pesticides applied to crops. Dangerous Substances are toxic do not or are very slow to degrade in water and are likely to accumulate in living organisms.\tPublic record\n18278\tNRW_DS116315\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.939Z\tSummary Shellfish Directive Assessments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333135\t[A]  Data available in Biosys [B]  Packaged published version available on Sharefile.\tThere is a small risk that this data can be considered commercially confidential but is difficult to assess. However, these data would be made available under an EIR request and as such these would inevitably lead to disclosure where the public interest test has been applied.  For this reason we consider it unnecessary that this slight risk should prevent full access.\tShellfish waters are coastal and brackish waters used for commercial shell fishing. These areas have been designated under the EC Shellfish Waters Directive.  This dataset contains sample results taken within the Designated Shellfish Waters that are assessed for compliance under the standards as defined under Directive (79/923/EEC).  These sites are sampled for water quality and for faecal coliform amongst others and contain both the raw sample results as well as the summary compliance data.  Samples are taken monthly with summary data collated for compliance annually and reported to Defra and the Welsh Government.  Since these data are reported annually the dataset is available for each complete year reported. It is of note that shellfisheries no longer used on a commercial basis are still maintained.\tPrivate record\n19074\tNRW_DS125381\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.214Z\tSouth Wales Open Water Survey 1981 (paper data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333831\tOffsite archive storage box no CCW/SERC/219\tOpendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tSurvey of 94 permanent standing open water sites in Gwent 1981- areas greter than once acre in site ST and SO grid references\tPrivate record\n18725\tNRW_DS123342\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.125Z\tWIMS Revoked Discharges\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333432\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\WIMS Revoked Discharges - Aug 2016.lyr\tData originally created and supplied by John Farnhill, please now refer to the water quality permitting team for relevance.\tThis data was likely to have been extracted from the WIMS system in 2016 to create a spatial dataset and is currently static.  WIMS has been replaced with the NRW\u0027s Permitting And Licencing System (PALS) and new products are available see (NRW_DS116329 and NRW_DS116215) in non-spatial format.\tPrivate record\n18754\tNRW_DS123369\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.676Z\tAll Wales Hydrological Analysis Flow Direction Raster\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333639\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\allwales_fdr.lyr\tBen Anscombe\tThis layer was created to point to rasters used in NRW hydrological analysis tools. They are based upon the flow direction models which were created by the Environment Agency Detailed River Network which subsequently passed to NRW.  For each cell in a raster the All Wales Hydrological Analysis Flow Direction Raster assigns a value indicating which adjacent cell surface water would be expected to flow to.\tPublic record\n18749\tNRW_DS123367\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.596Z\tAll Wales Hydrological Analysis Catchments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333637\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\allwales_cat.lyr\tBen Anscombe\tThis layer was created to point to rasters used in NRW hydrological analysis tools. They are based upon the flow direction models which were created by the Environment Agency Detailed River Network which subsequently passed to NRW.  The All Wales Hydrological Analysis Catchments is generated for each area above a threshold area from the following related datasets: NRW_DS123368 NRW_DS123369 NRW_DS123370 NRW_DS123371\tPublic record\n18758\tNRW_DS123368\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.709Z\tAll Wales Hydrological Analysis Flow Accumulation Raster\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333638\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\allwales_fac.lyr\tBen Anscombe\tThis layer was created to point to rasters used in NRW hydrological analysis tools. They are based upon the flow direction models which were created by the Environment Agency Detailed River Network which subsequently passed to NRW.    The Flow Accumulation Raster is a count for each cell a count of how many other cells flow to it.\tPublic record\n17270\tEXT_DS122694\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.773Z\tWelsh Government: Estimating Carbon in Organic Soils - Sequestration and Emission (ECOSSE) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232363934\tx:\\Physical\\Soils\\ECOSSE.lyr\tThe ECOSSE model was funded by WG and Scottish Government so it is more than likely that the output data provided to NRW is WG owned. http://nora.nerc.ac.uk/id/eprint/2233/1/Ecosse_published_final_report.pdf Not including this in webview as yet until further information obtained on dataset.\tThis data was provided to NRW by Welsh Government. For internal use only. The data is derived from licensed Cranfield Soils data.\tPrivate record\n17726\tNRW_DS110102\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.485Z\tAutecological study of Thick Top Shell, Osilinus lineatus (Monodonta lineata), in the area affected by the Sea Empress oil spill (1996 and 1997).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313032\tThe report is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 23 and Media No. 62 (File path K:\\Media 001to100\\Media 62) Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor,\tMetadata Point of Contact: Data Services Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tAfter the oil spill from the Sea Empress investigations were initiated into the biota of many of the habitats affected. One of the species known to be susceptible to oil pollution that had been studied previously and that would not be adequately investigated by the proposed investigations was the toothed or thick top shell Osilinus lineatus an animal commonly found on shores in western Britain. Data collected in order to establish the population age and size structure at each study site and allow an assessment of the impact the Sea Empress oil spill has had on these populations of Osilinus linaetus.  As of 1 April 2013 the Countryside Council for Wales Environment Agency Wales and Forestry Commission Wales became Natural Resources Wales / Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru.\tPublic record\n18746\tNRW_DS123385\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.222Z\tClimate Vulnerability Assessment (CVA) for Special Areas of Conservation (SACs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333835\tx:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\CVA SAC.lyr\tContact Clive Walmsley, Senior Specialist Advisor, Climate Change and Decarbonisation, SPG for further information about the data.  Data originally produced and provided by Jon Rothwell, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18287\tNRW_DS116313\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.562Z\tReferrals of Red List Discharges to Sewers (Corporate Entities)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333133\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116313\tChris Hall (SUW Advisor - Water quality)\tThe Referrals of Red List Discharges to Sewers dataset (extracted from IPCIS) records those companies (and potentially individuals) who have applied to water undertakers for permission to discharge a Red List substance into sewers. The Water Industry Act 1991 (as amended) section 120 (Applications for the discharge of special category effluent) directs sewerage undertakers who have received a notice containing an application for consent to discharge trade effluent from a trade premise into a public sewer (section 119) to refer to the following questions: - whether the discharges to which the notice relates should be prohibited; and - whether if they are not prohibited any requirements should be imposed as to the conditions on which they are made. It is this information stored within IPCIS that makes up the Referrals of Red List Discharges to Sewers dataset.\tPublic record\n18295\tNRW_DS116314\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.565Z\tGroundwater Level Measurements\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333134\t\tCustodian: Liam Perrott       This is a large WISKI database dataset; requests are dealt with ad hoc only with usually high extraction costs regarding staff resources. We do not routinely supply this dataset as a whole. Please therefore always ask the customer for the  geographic extent they require and include this in the licence.  Due to national security reason, you must send any raw data extracts you receive from the data custodian password protected. (Please ask custodian or Simon Bonwick/Andrew Jeffery for help with this if required). Please send password and data in a separate email. Otherwise the NGRs must be abbreviated to 4 digits.\tThis dataset comprises groundwater level time series data taken at borehole monitoring stations located across Wales. Discrete station information is stored for each site including identifier spatial reference parameter type and time series type. This dataset contains sites for operational and closed monitoring stations.  Data is collected from NRW borehole monitoring stations that are collated by Area staff normally by either downloading the station \u0027Logger Data\u0027 or manually \u0027Dipping\u0027 to determine borehole water level.\tPublic record\n17561\tNRW_DS102112\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.486Z\tSquirrels of Central Wales - Collation of historical records (1950 - 1999)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313132\tSpecies data held in Recorder 6  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, West area report (56)   CCW000380000000A\tLiz Halliwell, Mammal ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe red squirrel (Sciurus vulgaris) is Britain\u0027s only native squirrel and has experienced a severe decline in England and Wales over the last 50 years. This decline is linked to the expansion of its larger relative the grey squirrel S. carolinensis which was introduced into Britain from North America in the late nineteenth century. The exact mechanisms of displacement are not fully understood but it appears that grey squirrels are better able to utilise food resources in broadleaved woodland with the result that red squirrels generally disappear from such habitats within about 15 years of the arrival of greys. This dataset is a collation of red squirrel sightings for central Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to collate all records of red squirrels from central Wales in order to identify remaining areas of refuge for this species. The data was also collated to provide the initial recommendations for conservation actions. Brief habitat assessments of the remaining red squirrels sites were provided based on forest area tree species composition and age structure. Forest configuration adjoining habitat and the presence of large−seeded broadleaves were also considered to assess the potential threat from grey squirrels. The primary aim of the present study was to collate all recent (post−1990) red squirrel records from central Wales to identify the remaining red squirrel refugia. Other key objectives were to provide a preliminary habitat assessment of the main sites where red squirrels still occur and to make initial recommendations for conservation action as appropriate.\tPublic record\n18778\tNRW_DS123444\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.529Z\tLand Management Boundaries, post Organisational Design, July 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343434\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Land management Boundaries.lyr\tData custodian; Gary Eccleston, Knowledge \u0026amp; Information Management, NRW Metadata point of contact; Spatial team, NRW\tMap layer giving the boundaries of the post OD Land Management teams.  In MyMap this is layer at Operational Layers/Resource Management Terrestrial/Land Management Boundaries. It is the post OD (Organisational Design) boundaries for the Land and Assets teams. Will be used to ensure enquiries incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tPublic record\n17821\tNRW_DS111009\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.122Z\tMarine Biological Survey of Bardsey Island 1977\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303039\tData held within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000228) Paper report available from CCW\u0027s library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 section above)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, NRW External Contact: JNCC  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe results of a survey of the littoral and sub-littoral regions around Bardsey Island carried out in 1977. Bardsey Island was being offered for sale in 1977 therefore this survey was initiated in order to gain knowledge of the littoral and sub-littoral surroundings of the island in case public access to the areas was denied by subsequent owners. The data captured was also used to detect any changes since previous surveys and to investigate whether the shores of Bardsey offered a suitable site for a Marine Nature Reserve.\tPublic record\n18157\tNRW_DS115890\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.604Z\tThe reproduction and connectivity of Sabellaria alveolata reefs in Wales - MAR4REF (2014)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383930\tData and Report are Archived as project 460, media 1521 See project 404, media 1421, 1425, 1426, 1427\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe reef building tube worm Sabellaria alveolata is an important ecosystem engineer on UK shorelines and is subsequently protected as a result of its limited spatial distribution in reef form. To effectively manage and reduce impacts on populations resulting from environmental change more information is required to understand the distribution and development of S. alveolata reefs. The purpose of this data capture was it collect information to help understand the distribution and development of Sabellaria alveolata reefs in order to effectively manage and reduce impacts of populations resulting from environmental change. The associated report presents the findings from a Welsh Government Resilient Ecosystems Fund funded project lead by Bangor University investigating the reproduction and connectivity of S. alveolata reefs along Welsh coastlines.  Three key sites for data collation Llanddulas (north Wales) Aberarth (mid-Wales) and Dunraven (south Wales) but expanded to the regional scale for modelling larval movements between major reef sites throughout the Irish Sea. The main research areas included:  1) Laboratory-based experiments to describe larval behaviour in relation to phototaxis and quantification of swimming speed throughout different larval development stages.  2) We assessed adult fecundity and egg sizes from worms collected from all three study sites every month from February-Sept 2014.  3) We measured larval densities in the water column at all three sites during the theorised reproductive period.  4) We produced estimates of population connectivity for different reef sites within Wales and determined major source and sink sites by using a biophysical model to create estimates of larval movement.\tPublic record\n17824\tNRW_DS110893\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.294Z\tRoxAnn Ground Truthing Video Drops 1997\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383933\tMarine Recorder Survey: JNCCMNCR40000960.  Original MNCR forms are held in CCW off-site storage. Data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 237, Media No\u0027s. 1, 75 and 109\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to increase the available information of species and habitats thus extending the area covered by survey within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Results of a survey carried out from the Prince Madog Research Vessel as a joint operation by the University of Wales Bangor and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). Using RoxAnn ground truthing video transects within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC were recorded.\tPublic record\n17437\tNRW_DS87460\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.175Z\tThreatened Bryophyte Database (TBDB) UK (1965 - 2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837343630\tMaster Copy held by British Bryological Society.   Recorder 6 holds some species data: Tycanol: RF36 Bryophyte records - BBS database SR0006010000000C.   An export of welsh data held as MapInfo file on m drive.  NB Update from Sam Bosanquet 2021 - The Threatened Bryophyte Database is basically defunct now - all data for Threatened Bryophytes are stored along with those for all other bryophytes by the British Bryological Society and there is no point in NRW storing them separately as that just leads to duplication and errors.\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team External Custodian: British Bryological Society\tThis metadata is derived from metadata created by British Bryological Society for the NBN gateway. The purpose of this data capture is to service bryophyte conservation by documenting detailed records of threatened species and tracking the populations of these species through time. It is a \u0027live\u0027 database feeding specialist information to UKBAP Lead Partners and conservation agencies and to feed information about bryophyte conservation programmes to bryologists. Some biological records have more precise location information than others. Often they are recorded using national grid references which identify which 10km 2km 1km or 100m square they occur in. This table shows how many records there are at these resolutions for this dataset.\tPublic record\n18392\tNRW_DS118982\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.413Z\tAnglesey Invasive Species, Non-native Species and Sabellaria spinulosa Survey 2016\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138393832\tStores as Project 477, Media 1554 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1501-1600\\Media 1554) [A] Report in PDF Format - held in the DMS (DMS: Evidence\u0026gt;Marine\u0026gt;projects\u0026gt;Contracts\u0026gt;HD INNS and Sabellaria survey October 2016 [B] Raw video files in .ASF format - held in Media 1554 [C] Still images in .JPG format - held in Media 1554  [D] Survey logs as .XLSM  and .TXT format - held in Media 1554  [E] Track GPS data in Arc .SHP and MapInfo .TAB format - held in Media 1554  [G] Marine Recorder Survey [MRCCW32600000002] - held in Media 1554 [H] Field Notes, scanned and stored as .PDF format - held in Media 1554 [H] Field Notes, scanned and stored as .PDF format - held in Media 1554\tInternal Custodian: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tOne of the aims of this survey was to collect data on Invasive Non-Native Species particularly in relation to Holyhead Deep.  This disposal site receives dredged disposal material mainly from Holyhead Port which is in close proximity to Holyhead Marina where there are known records of non-native species. Sabellaria spinulosa (Ross Worm) reef is designated by the Oslo/Paris Convention (for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic) as an threatened and declining habitat and a component of Annex I Reef.  Individuals and crusts of Sabellaria spinulosa are recorded commonly around Wales but the presence of reef has rarely been confirmed.  Previous commercial surveys off north and west Anglesey have indicated the presence of Sabellaria spinulosa reef.  This survey therefore also aimed to verify these records and give an indication of extent of the habitat.  The principal objective was to undertake a taxonomic analysis of video and still images from survey work undertaken around north and west Anglesey in October 2016.  The survey area extended from Point Lynas west to Church Bay and out to sites on the Skerries West Anglesey Reef and Holyhead Deep. The collection of underwater video using a high definition digital drop-down camera and stills for this survey was undertaken by the coastal survey vessel RV Mersey Guardian in October 2016.  The video dataset encompassed 25 tows comprising over eight hours of video in total.  A total of 3759 still images taken at approximately seven second intervals were collected simultaneously with the video tows. The entire video/ set of stills from each tow were reviewed prior to detailed assessment and recording and the boundaries between habitats (biotopes) or habitat complexes noted.  The species and substratum in each tow section or \u0027sample\u0027 was then recorded and habitats assigned. A total of 35 samples were derived across the 25 survey stations with Ross Worm reef recorded in 34 of the 35 samples.  Substantial reef over 10cm high was found at nine survey stations with the most diverse Ross worm reef habitat occurring on a stony reef of cobbles among pebbles off Wylfa.   No non-native species were recorded during the survey.  In particular there was concern that the Carpet Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) might occur at some of the sites receiving dredge material from Holyhead Port but it was not identified in any of the imagery. This species has recently been reported in natural habitats off the north Kent coast. Relatively little litter was seen in the video mostly fragments of monofilament  pieces of plastic and cloth.  Some toppled Ross worm reef was noted at single sites off Cemlyn and Church Bay. Two of the three sites in Holyhead Deep receiving dredge material from Holyhead Port appeared to be siltier than the near shore survey locations.\tPublic record\n17823\tNRW_DS110957\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.088Z\tSublittoral Surveys of Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Formally a Marine Nature Reserve ( MNR ) Boundaries 1982\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393537\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder as JNCCMNCR10000184. NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW External Contact; JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. This dataset consists of the results of a survey carried out to describe the types of sublittoral habitats and communities adjacent to the coast in the region of the eastern boundaries of the Skomer Marine Nature Reserve. The purpose of this data capture was provide a basis for assessing the adequacy of the eastern boundaries in relation to sublittoral areas and their relevance in terms of the presence or absence of unusual communities or communities not well represented within other regions of the reserve.\tPublic record\n17272\tEXT_DS124757\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.048Z\tDepartment for Transport (DfT) Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373537\tx:\\Transport\\Dept of Transport car parks.lyr  X:\\Transport\\DfT Welsh ferry terminals.lyr  X:\\Transport\\Railway\\DfT Welsh railway stations.lyr  X:\\Transport\\Bus Stops.lyr\tGeospatial team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds Department for Transport data (DfT) for Wales. This includes DfT car parks and DfT National Public Transport Access Nodes (NaPTAN) data.  The static car parks dataset includes co-ordinates of each car park and entrance/exit car park name locality and operator for Wales.   The NaPTAN data is a national dataset of public transport access points  - currently NRW have obtained data relating to Welsh bus stops Welsh ferry terminals and Welsh railway stations.\tPublic record\n17404\tNRW_DS46954\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.288Z\tAerial Photographs of the Intertidal Areas of Wales 1996 to 2002\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436393534\tHafod Elfyn Fireproof room. M:/airphotos\tInternal Custodians: Phase 1 Intertidal Team, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services / Spatial Data Team, NRW\tStereoscopic aerial photographs supplied by CUCAP (Cambridge University Centre for Aerial Photography) at scale of 1:10000 CR World (Liverpool Bay Forum) BKS Surveys Limited ExeGesIS and the Environment Agency for the coast of Wales.  The aim of this data collation was to map Intertidal Phase 1 communities (biotopes) both in the field and for the production of digital Phase 1 Intertidal maps on the GIS (MapInfo). Also used for monitoring Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) features and intertidal casework. The collation is a complete coverage of the Welsh coast from the Ribble Estuary (England) to the Severn Estuary inclusive.\tPublic record\n18147\tNRW_DS116001\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.328Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Gwynedd Supplement 1994 - 1995\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303031\tData stored in Recorder 6: Gwynedd suppliment 1994-5. Prosser \u0026amp; Wallace. CCW0007300000006\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Gwynedd. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tPublic record\n17624\tNRW_DS109832\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.276Z\tAcross Wales Bays and Banks Subtidal Soft Sediment Surveys\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383332\t[A]  All data is archived under project 240 (media no 763, 910, 1285, 1286, 1330, 1388).   [B]  A working copy is held on H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Subtidal\\Bays and Banks benthic invert data [C]  Associated Spatial Data for sampling locations are held on the X Drive: X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring points.lyr  [D]  Data is currently being entered into Marine Recorder by a contractor – Marine Ecosol\tThe internal data custodian is: Mike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge \u0026amp; Advice The metadata point of contact is: Data Services, NRW\tThe aim of this data capture was to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of subtidal soft sediment Annex I features in Wales.  Subtidal benthic soft sediment repeat sampling is carried out with Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) across Wales to monitor Large Shallow Inlets and Bays and Estuaries and Sandbank features. Some of these data are collected in conjunction with the  Water Framework Directive invertebrate monitoring programme so other data from these locations or the surrounding area may be available upon request from the EA. Surveys took place in large shallow inlets and bays (Conwy Bay Red Wharf Bay  Cardigan Bay St Brides Bay Carmarthen Bay) and designated subtidal sandbank features in Wales. Benthic invertebrate samples are processed by laboratories that are members of the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme and random samples from each survey are also selected for audit by the NMBAQC contractor. Data entry into Marine Recorder database has not been validated.\tPublic record\n17414\tNRW_DS46197\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.588Z\tCondition monitoring of National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313937\tHigh level data are held centrally by NRW Environmental Change Group.  Supporting data are held in Regional NRW offices or by Science Groups.\tCustodian: Dylan Lloyd  or   Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs) contain examples of some of the most important natural and semi-natural terrestrial and coastal ecosystems in Great Britain. They are managed to conserve their habitats or to provide special opportunities for scientific study of the habitats communities and species represented within them. In addition they may be managed to provide public recreation that is compatible with their natural heritage interests.  NNRs are declared by the statutory country conservation agencies under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 and the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981. In some cases NNRs form part of for wholly within Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) or Special Protection Areas (SPAs).  The purpose of this data capture was to assess the condition of SSSI/SAC/SPA features on NNRs in Wales. SAC features are generally monitored to the condition of the feature at the SPA level. Features on designated sites are to be assessed over a period of six years in a rolling monitoring cycle. The first cycle began in 1999 however SAC monitoring cycle began in 2001.\tPublic record\n18724\tNRW_DS123341\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.057Z\tWIMS Revoked Consents\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333431\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\WIMS Revoked Consents - Aug 2016.lyr\tData originally created and supplied by Simon Webb, please now refer to the water quality permitting team for relevance.\tThis data was likely to have been extracted from the WIMS system in 2016 to create a spatial dataset and is currently static.  WIMS has been replaced with the NRW\u0027s Permitting And Licencing System (PALS) and new products are available see (NRW_DS116329 and NRW_DS116215) in non-spatial format.\tPrivate record\n18275\tNRW_DS116325\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.723Z\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses - Chemistry\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333235\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116327\tMetadata point of contact: Data Services Custodian: Jane Roberts\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches. This datasets relates to the chemical assessments. In Wales we maintained the full GQA network until 2010 based on 800 sampling sites which provided information for approximately 4700km. Grades of river quality for the chemical GQA Chemical grade Likely uses and characteristics:en - A: Very good All abstractions Very good Salmonid fisheries Cyprinid fisheries Natural ecosystems. - B: Good All abstractions Salmonid fisheries Cyprinid fisheries Ecosystems at or close to natural.  - C: Fairly good Potable supply after advanced treatment Other abstractions Good Cyprinid fisheries Natural ecosystems or those corresponding to good Cyprinid fisheries  - D: Fair Potable supply after advanced treatment Other abstractions Fair Cyprinid fisheries Impacted ecosystems.  - E: Poor Low grade abstraction for industry Fish absent or sporadically present vulnerable to pollution Impoverished ecosystems  - F: Bad Very polluted rivers which may cause nuisance Severely restricted ecosystems *Provided other standards are met where the grade is caused by discharges of organic pollution.\tPublic record\n17625\tNRW_DS109473\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.548Z\tSite Dossier for Lichen Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Features at Pen y Gogarth/Great Orme\u0027s Head SSSI\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039343733\t[A] CCW library and Ffynnon reference DCT-09-049763 [B] Report copy on DMs\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tData collected as part of a data search and lichen survey of Pen Y Gogarth/Great Ormes Head Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). The survey was divided into two parts: Part 1 dealt with the taxa that comprise the lichen flora the origin of the Orme\u0027s older records and with the people who collected on the Great Orme prior to the current survey. Outputs include information on lichen communities and habitats and tables showing  the conservation status and rarity of each of the records including those of special importance such as Red listed species.   Part 2 involved developing proformas and establishing Common Standards Monitoring for lichens through trials at the site. Outputs include two indirect monitoring site accounts with a summary of lichen interest table of notable lichen species and management suggestions; site attribute tables for the two sites and a map showing the extent of the sites. Also included are direct monitoring proformas for 6 sites with details of location habitat abundance species present etc. The 6 sites monitored in this way are mapped to show their locations.\tPublic record\n17401\tNRW_DS47082\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.118Z\tStorm Petrel (Hydrobates pelagicus) Census of Skokholm and Skomer Special Protection Area (SPA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437303832\tH:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\MurphyM\\contracts\\Completed projects\\Skomer \u0026amp; Skokholm storm petrels  Report on H:\\TSG\\Gray_C\\Contract reports\\\tMatt Murphy, Senior Maritime Ornithologist, Natural resources planning/Marine and freshwater ecosytem group, NRW (Formerly Catherine Gray)\tPopulation estimate of storm petrels Hydrobates pelagicus on Skomer in 2003 and 2004 and Skokholm in 2003. The purpose of this data capture was to create a population estimate to provide a baseline for monitoring. As well as establishment of monitoring plots and methods for future monitoring.\tPublic record\n17666\tNRW_DS109916\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.462Z\tCoal Spoil Conservation Project\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393136\tSee individual survey metadata entries in \u0027contains\u0027 field. 109899 Assessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales. 109898 Assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales 109900 Assessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales. Phase II: Assessment of Environmental Parameters\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (Formerly Jan Sherry Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage ecology landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context.  The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales. This was intended as the first phase of a coal spoil conservation strategy which incorporated the results of additional archaeological and community assessment. The study was limited to the area covered by Torfaen Merthyr Tydfil Caerphilly Rhondda Cynon Taf and Blaenau Gwent County Borough Councils. A Coal Spoil Conservation Strategy was initiated and this dataset includes the results from different phases of the project (see \u0027contains\u0027 field). The aim of this data capture was to provide background data for the delivery of a consistent strategy for local biodiversity action and amenity enhancement. The results of an archaeological and community assessment were also incorporated. The study suggests a classification for types of coal spoil and assesses the ecological value of a large number of sites.\tPublic record\n17418\tNRW_DS46140\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.589Z\tGreater Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) breeding productivity surveillance in Pembrokeshire\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313430\tStackpole office, NRW\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South (used to be Bob Haycock, Senior Reserve Warden - Stackpole, NRW.)  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe greater horseshoe bat Rhinolophus ferrumequinum nursery roosts at Stackpole and Slebech Pembrokeshire are principal summer roosts of the West Wales population of the species which ranges through Pembrokeshire Carmarthenshire Ceredigion and Gower West Glamorgan. Monitoring the population is difficult as for much of the year only a small percentage are accessible through winter surveys of hibernacula. Monitoring the status of the population is therefore only practical at the nursery roosts. This dataset consists of Greater Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) surveillance results. The surveillance of the breeding productivity of Greater Horseshoe Bat i.e. numbers of young born annually occurs between June and August. The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Provide information and updates to the local Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) -  Assess feature conditions of greater horseshoe bats within Pembrokeshire Bat Sites Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and 3 component Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).  -  Contribute to the management database of the breeding population records for Greater Horseshoe Bats species recovers programme and the UK BAP species programmes.\tPublic record\n17304\tEXT_DS125633\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.564Z\tMet Office Wave Height - Hindcast Extract (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363333\t\tNeil Counsell, Specialist Advisor - Flood Forecasting, Flood and Operational Risk Management\tMet Office have developed a wave modelling to develop and maintain wave forecasts of sea state.  These wave models have been used as a tool to predict the sea state and wave height which can then be applied to coastal overtopping and flooding.  The forecast model for Wales has been developed from using three locations around the coastline of Wales (North South and West).  From these three locations NRW have collected observed data and the following parameters are collected.  The data stored in the archive consists of the following information:  dir: Wave Mean Direction (degree) hs: Significant Wave Height (m) tm: Mean Period Tm01 (s)  NRW have stored this information for all of the offshore locations in Wales for the period 2006 – 2020.\tPublic record\n17766\tNRW_DS110885\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.095Z\tIntertidal Monitoring Survey of the Effect of Parys Mountain Discharge around Amlwch, North Anglesey (2003)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383835\t[A]  This project is archived under project no. 253, media no 907 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 907) [B]  Survey data held in Marine recorder survey: MRCCW3000000002 [C]  Undergraduate project on copper concentrations in mussels available from Kirsten Ramsay (on paper, now send to off sire storage) [D] Paper MNCR forms in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe purpose of this data capture was to detect impacts of pumping discharge from the disused copper mines in Parys Mountain.  Intertidal surveys conducted around Amlwch North Anglesey during the early part of 2003 to detect impacts of pumping discharge from the disused copper mines in Parys Mountain into the Afon Goch (North) that flows into the sea underneath the Great Lakes plant in Amlwch. In addition there is a undergraduate project that looked at concentrations of copper in Mytilus edulis samples. These projects are both unpublished. The purpose of this data capture was to detect  impacts of pumping discharge from the disused copper mines in Parys Mountain.\tPublic record\n17850\tNRW_DS111157\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.821Z\tRegional Seascape Units\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313537\tX:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes regional units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes coast hight points.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes coast hight regions.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes index value line.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged land units single polys.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged land units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged sea units single polys.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged sea units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes regional units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes unit numbers.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes value index points.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes value weightedscore land units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes value weightedscore line.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Landscape Architect, Environmental Policy Group, NRW.\tA \u0027seascape\u0027 is an area of sea coastline and land as perceived by people whose character results from the actions and interaction of land and sea by natural and/or human factors.   This dataset is the product of the first detailed assessment of the character of Wales\u0027s seascapes. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise and define the geographical extent of the seascape units of Wales and to assess the sensitivity of each seascape to offshore developments.   The dataset contains a map images and descriptive text for each of the 50 seascapes units in Wales. Also included are maps illustrating the national and regional divisions of seascape units land with sea views relative visibility of the sea surface from land and tranquillity. There are also maps presenting the results of sensitivity assessments carried out to model the sensitivity of seascape units to offshore wind farm developments wave farm developments and tidal stream developments. Included in the dataset is a pdf file outlining what exactly the MapInfo files in the dataset show.\tPublic record\n18728\tNRW_DS123340\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.971Z\tWIMS Discharge Exemptions IR\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333430\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\WIMS Discharge Exemptions IR - Aug 2016 .lyr\tData originally created and supplied by Simon Webb, please now refer to the water quality permitting team for relevance.\tThis datasets is not available on the GIS X drive.  Please contact the Geospatial team for further information.  WIMS has been replaced with the NRW\u0027s Permitting And Licencing System (PALS) and new products are available see (NRW_DS116329 and NRW_DS116215) in non-spatial format.\tPrivate record\n18301\tNRW_DS116456\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Watercourses\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343536\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Watercoureses.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team  South IDDs - Louise Harris North IDDs - James West\tAll former Internal Drainage Board(IDB)/Internal Drainage District(IDD) maintained watercourses also known as Reens Ditches Brooks and Drains are mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for the purpose of water level management flood defence maintenance and planning operations/work programming. Identifies watercourses managed for water voles priority/critical watercourses which must be maintained and watercourses designated as \\urban\\\". Urban watercourses are those in urban areas which are given priority for Health \u0026amp; Safety and Flood Risk reasons.\"\tPublic record\n18798\tNRW_DS124197\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.014Z\tEA Environment Management Teams\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234313937\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\EA Environment Management Teams.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18801\tNRW_DS124618\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.896Z\tNRW Water Quality analytical flow and Quality Control nformation held in LIMS (Laborartory Information Management System)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363138\tLIMS - Swansea Laboratory\tAndrew Wesley, ICT and Data Specialist, Service Delivery\tTo be added when supplied from Data Custodian  Scheduling collection analysis and reporting of all routine monitoring and pollution response samples.  This includes scheduling and analysis of NRW sample as well as a commercial service for third parties (e.g. Utility companies and Environmental Agencies).  The Results of the Water Quality samples are exported to WISKI (see metadata entry NRW_DS119251)\tPrivate record\n18760\tNRW_DS123380\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.709Z\tRegional Trails\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333830\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Regional Trails.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18981\tNRW_DS125168\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.228Z\tNDF Preassessed areas for wind energy\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313638\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\NDF Preassessed areas for wind energy.lyr\tOlwen Maidment\tAreas with a presumption in favour of large-scale wind energy development.\tPrivate record\n18836\tNRW_DS124670\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.647Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Armoured Crossing Point Markers\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363730\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Armoured Crossing Point Markers.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Armoured Crossing Point Markers. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18756\tNRW_DS123384\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.648Z\tRecreation Activities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333834\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Water activities\\Recreation Activities Lakes.lyr  x:\\Recreation and Access\\Water activities\\Recreation Activities Rivers.lyr  x:\\Recreation and Access\\Water activities\\Recreation Activities Transition.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17420\tNRW_DS64557\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.645Z\tImages/Photograph Libraries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353537\tHQ and Regional offices.\tMonica\u0027s Advice:  This is a collection of images that was held by Linda Williams in CCW (also known as the CCW photo image library and identified as SOAP Ref: 003920 on the ETC).  All images are in the process of transferring to the NRW image library as part of the Image store project.  Have no idea about the hardcopy slides, which probably sit still in regional offices or have been transferred to Jill/Dwyn as part of office moves and paper archive project.  To be honest I am not sure that it needs an entry now as I don’t really consider promotional/nice images to be data and would just delete the entry. Alternatively perhaps change it for a general entry about the content of the NRW Image store in the same way we have a general entry for WISKI content or Marine Recorder content (it could include general information about the archive of slides that Jill/Dwyn currently hold too?).  Bryan Tozer would be best placed to help write a generic entry for the NRW image store (the lineage could include a sentence that described that this now contains what was previously knows as the CCW image library – a set of presentation level images used for promotional purposes). NRW holds so many images, unless they are a specifically data e.g. photo-monitoring time series of site/feature or something that is digitally analysed using software I wouldnt bother with metadata entries for them as they are not data and I don’t think they fall under our remit to give advice on.  If those that are considered data tend to be part of a larger dataset and are captures as part of the metadata for the larger dataset.\tNRW holds a large number of good quality photographs (both in digital and 35mm slide format) of biodiversity landscape countryside marine and environmental subjects. These are held in various sets in HQ and Regional offices.  Purpose of data capture: To represent species and habitats etc found in Wales which may be used for interpretational and promotional purposes eg. illustrations slide shows displays.\tPrivate record\n18135\tNRW_DS115628\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.799Z\tConservation Management of Hay Meadows in the Elan Valley\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363238\tData outputs associated with this project are archived at HP TRIM Record Number: 09/90051 on server–based storage at Natural Resources Wales. (Ffynnon record number (folder) 09/9005) Relevant NRW Evidence Report: No 8: Conservation Management of species-rich grasslands in the Elan Valley, Radnorshire Hayes, M., Lowther. R   Located on the DMS: KNOW-101-8\tContact: Stuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW [M.J. Hayes \u0026amp; R.A. Lowther - originally collected the data.]  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Elan Valley in mid-Powys includes some of the most important and richest examples of unimproved mesotrophic grasslands in Wales. The majority of these are highly species-rich upland-fringe meadows that are now mostly designated as Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) due to their national significance. In order to help safeguard the future of these important sites the Elan Valley Meadow Project was initially set up in 2004 to address both the management of the existing species-rich grasslands and their potential expansion. The project was managed and implemented by a partnership between the Elan Valley Trust Natural Resources Wales (Natural Resources Wales) (formerly Countryside Council for Wales; CCW) and independent ecological consultants. The primary objective of the project was to provide site-specific and ecologically sustainable management advice to help conserve these important and regionally distinct upland-fringe meadows. Specifically there was a need to determine what levels of nutrient inputs are required to enable reinstatement of traditional hay making practices while still conserving existing high levels of floristic diversity. To this end a long-term replicated field trial was undertaken to determine how a representative group of the Elan Valley meadows respond to different inputs of farmyard manure (FYM) and/or lime in terms of soil chemistry herbage productivity and changes in species diversity.\tPublic record\n18764\tNRW_DS123381\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.737Z\tShort local walks\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333831\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Short local walks.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18298\tNRW_DS116457\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Sluices\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343537\tX;\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Sluices.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team  South IDDs - Louise Harris North IDDs - James West\tSluices within the Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge Lower Wye and Powysland Internal Drainage Districts digitised as point features and mapped for the purpose of flood defence water level management and asset management. Information recorded includes Top Base Winter and Summer water levels status of the sluice e.g. used or disused if and when its due for conversion to a modern style sluice sluice type and material and the watercourses the sluices are located on.\tPublic record\n18748\tNRW_DS123372\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.593Z\tDEM 5m\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333732\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\dem_5m.lyr\tGeospatial Services Team, Knowledge Information Management Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.  This is the same data that is held at X:\\Basemapping\\Height\\APGB_5m_DEM.lyr (see URI EXT_DS121661) Speak to Ben Anscombe for further information\tPrivate record\n17855\tNRW_DS111158\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.917Z\tMarine Nature Conservation Review Intertidal Survey of Rhos Point to New Brighton\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313538\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on a copy of JNCC\u0027s Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000240). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data - part of All Wales snapshot)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe results of intertidal surveys carried out on the flora and fauna of sixteen sites within a survey area along the southern coast of Liverpool Bay. The shores within the survey area were mainly sediment shores however at some sites rocky substratum was present.  The purpose of this data capture was to describe a representative range of intertidal habitats and their associated flora and fauna from locations on the open coast distributed throughout the survey area. The survey was part of the Marine Nature Conservation Review which had the aims of extending our knowledge of British marine ecosystems identifying sites of nature conservation importance and providing information to support more general measures required to minimise adverse effects of development and pollution.   The sampling area extends from Rhos-on-Sea in the west to New Brighton in the east (approximately 50 km) and comprises of the majority of the southern coast of Liverpool Bay. All sites were surveyed between the 23rd - 25th of May 1990 and some sites were subject to additional visits on the 17th and 18th of September 1990.\tPublic record\n18125\tNRW_DS115828\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.041Z\tBangor University SOS Prince Madog Video and Photographic Survey Data for Habitat and Scallop Assessments (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383238\tArchived as Project 387 Media 1402 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1402) Species Data is held in Marine Recorder surveys [MRCCW3170000000D, MRCCW3170000000E, MRCCW31700000010, MRCCW31700000012, MRCCW31700000013].\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Contact: School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University\tSurveys of the seabed in Cardigan Bay Caernarfon Bay West Anglesey and Tremadog Bay were carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in 2012. The datasets  comprises of a collation of video tows photographs and subsequent species and habitat photographic analyses. The main aim of this survey work was to collect information on the characteristics and status of the offshore benthic environment with specific reference to scallop beds and habitat types at various locations around Wales.  Confidence in this dataset is high.\tPublic record\n17768\tNRW_DS110689\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.190Z\tMonitoring of Yellow Trumpet Anemone Parazoanthus axinellae at Pen Cristen, Bardsey Island\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130363839\tElectronic data (including maipulated images) is archived under project 219, media no. 800.  Copies of the video data is held on the Imaging drive, the tapes are held in the fireproof safe.  Images are held on the imaging computer and a copy on the MFSG drive.\tMarine Monitoring Officer, MFSG. Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG.\tYellow trumpet anemone Parazoanthus axinellae is primarily a south-western species found at Bardsey Island and is close to the northern boundary of its known distribution at this location.  This species has been selected as an attribute for monitoring the condition of reefs in the Special Area of Conservation (SACs).  In 1999 an assessment of appropriate methods was made to look at monitoring the abundance and population structure of Parazoanthus axinellae and the erect branching sponge Axinella dissimilis. The trials focused on comparing different techniques to find the one that was most repeatable and therefore sensitive to population change.  Trials took place at Pen Cristen on Bardsey Island.  Subsequent monitoring of the Parazoanthus axinellae assemblage has occurred annually at this location when weather conditions allow. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the numerical abundance percentage cover and distribution of individuals within the dominant Parazoanthus axinellae assemblage using a quantitative photographic approach.  This will contribute to condition assessment of the reef feature in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.\tPublic record\n18335\tNRW_DS116513\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.685Z\tThe Status of the Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Y Wyddfa (2015)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353133\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in the Recorder 6\tDr Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Services, NRW\tThe Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis is restricted in the United Kingdom to Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa) although it was also found in Cwm Idwal until 1980. As recent records of the beetle have been few this survey was undertaken in an attempt to determine its status and distribution. A total of five beetles (four larvae and one adult) were found following 63.5 hours of hand searching on and around thyme plants (the only recorded foodplant of the beetle in the UK) and stone-turning near patches of thyme from June to September 2015 with all specimens found in Clogwyn Coch in September. No beetles were found in Cwm du\u0027r Arddu its traditional hotspot despite exhaustive searches.   Further surveys were also conducted in 2016 please see -  National Museums Liverpool 2017. The status of the Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Yr Wyddfa in 2016. NRW Evidence Report No: 193 18pp. Natural Resources Wales Bangor.  - Metadata for 2016 Survey here -  NRW_DS117097\tPublic record\n18053\tNRW_DS115169\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.565Z\tBottlenose Dolphin and Harbour Porpoise Monitoring in Cardigan Bay and Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SAC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313639\tData is archived under Project No 382  Media No 1393, 1410 (2005-2012) Subsequent data for 2013 Archived as project 441, media 1481 Data for 2014-2016 archived as project 479, media 1556\tInternal Custodian is: Marine Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA population of bottlenose dolphins forms a primary interest of Cardigan Bay and it was for this that Cardigan Bay was first selected as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Cardigan Bay is known to host the largest population of semi-resident bottlenose dolphins in the UK. The data collected as part of this research is being used to monitor the population dynamics and behaviour of bottlenose dolphins and harbour porpoises in Cardigan Bay.  Cardigan Bay is known to host the largest population of semi-resident bottlenose dolphins in the UK. This data covers monitoring carried out by the Sea Watch Foundation between 2011-2013 on behalf of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and subsequently Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to monitor bottlenose dolphin and harbour porpoise populations in Cardigan Bay.  The goal of this data collection was to monitor bottlenose dolphin and harbour porpoise populations in Cardigan Bay. The aims were to: - Provide information on the condition of bottlenose dolphins and harbour porpoises in Cardigan Bay including Cardigan Bay and Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation;   - To use photo-identification techniques to evaluate dolphin movements distribution and abundance; - To assess supporting habitats.\tPublic record\n18270\tNRW_DS116326\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.228Z\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses - Nutrients\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333236\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116326\tMetadata point of contact: Data Service team Custodian: Jane Roberts\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches. This datasets relates to the nutrient assessments. In Wales we maintained the full GQA network until 2010 based on 800 sampling sites which provided information for approximately 4700km. The GQAHI/GQA scheme was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. The Nutrients GQA described quality in terms of two nutrients: nitrates (mg NO3 /l) and phosphates (mg P/l) and graded from 1 to 6.\tPublic record\n18277\tNRW_DS116320\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.964Z\tLarge Raised Reservoirs (archived data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333230\tData extracted from Cronfeydd Cymru database  Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116320\tCustodian: Matthew O\u0027Brien, Reservoir Safety Team\tThis dataset has been discontinued. Please see the datasets which have replaced it:  - Large Raised Reservoir register (Locations and undertakers) NRW_DS125215 and;   - Large Raised Reservoir (Registered information) NRW_DS125216  This dataset contains details of Large Raised Reservoirs. Natural Resource Wales (NRW) collects and maintains data on all reservoirs designed or capable of holding more than 25000 cubic metres of water above the natural level of any part of the land adjoining them defined as \u0027large raised reservoirs\u0027 under the Reservoirs Act 1975. Two types of reservoir are maintained within the register:   - Impounding (Dammed) or; - Non-Impounding (Pumped/unimpeded)\tPublic record\n17483\tNRW_DS100786\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.315Z\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Biology Spatial Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373836\tx:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Biology Network.lyr  X:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Biology Sample Points.lyr\tPlease contact Nick Davies ( Environment Agency)\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to NRW for internal use only. The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains  River quality biology. GQA covering England \u0026amp; Wales displayed in varying thickness according to flow and colour according to Biology (year 2000).\tPrivate record\n18087\tNRW_DS115618\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.783Z\tSpecial Protection Area (SPA) Monitoring: Bird features within Welsh SPAs (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363138\tThe Report, which contains the data, is held on the NRW DMS Under the Reports Archive \u0026gt; CCW Marine Environmental Monitoring Reports: -  KNOW-911039132-34 - CCW EMR 22 - A preliminary assessment of the condition of bird features within Welsh Special Protection Areas (2005)\tAdditional Information Contact: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis datasets presents a preliminary exploration of the potential use of existing data sets to inform assessments of the condition of Special Protection Area (SPA) features across Wales. The approach adopted for this study was to assess each species/assemblage in the individual SPAs. The most recent list of SPAs in Wales (UK SPA review) contains inconsistencies in the qualifying species/assemblage features when comparisons are made between the Natura 2000 datasheets the SPA review site documentation and the SPA review - species documentation.  The aim of this data capture was to: 1 To produce draft performance targets for bird features within Welsh Special Protection Areas in accordance with the JNCC Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance for birds. 2 To make a preliminary assessment of whether performance targets have been met for the 6 year JNCC report. 3 To identify where existing data are insufficient to report on the performance targets.\tPublic record\n17846\tNRW_DS111162\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.534Z\tSaltmarsh Features of Morfa Uchaf Dyffryn Conwy Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) 1990-1994\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313632\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media901to1000\\Media904 Archived as Project 251, Media 904. Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Conwy 1990 to 1994 Original paper maps, quadrat cards, and floristic tables are in North Region files (Mold Office)\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNational Vegetation Classification has been used as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the southern Conwy Estuary.  The principal objective of the survey was to provide a suitable basis for Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) designation. Data collected by trained personnel digitised by contractors and CCW staff.\tPublic record\n18271\tNRW_DS116327\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.307Z\tHistoric River Quality Objectives\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333237\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116327\tInternal Contact Dave Johnson (was Jane Roberts)  in the Evidence, Monitoring \u0026amp; Reporting.\tThe River Quality Objectives (RQO) classification was used for planning water quality improvements until 2006 when the scheme ended. The national RQO classification scheme ceased at the end of 2006 it is now a fixed table which will not be updated. RQO compliance figures are available for 1997-2006.\tPublic record\n18831\tNRW_DS124672\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.296Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Culverts\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363732\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Culverts.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Culverts. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17305\tEXT_DS125653\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.534Z\tWoodland Creation Planner Scheme (WCPS) Pre-applications (Third Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363533\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\tCarol Owen Email: Carol.Owen@Naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales provides a verification service to Welsh Government for Government funding for new woodland creation government grants. Woodland Creation Planner Scheme (WCPS) has introduced a pre-application service to inform if land is suitable.\tPublic record\n18184\tNRW_DS116227\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.180Z\tBathing Water Quality at Designated Beaches\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323237\tExtracted from the WISKI KwiQm database and available on Sharefile.  Originally this data was extracted from WIMS Following transformation from WIMS to WISKI, future data will be extracted from WISKI as follows (2017 - jointly extracted from WIMS \u0026amp; WISKI, 2018 onwards - WISKI only)    There are also 4 GIS layers that are created from the same data in WISKI KwiQM.  These have their own metadata entries:   EC sites: NRW_DS124755 X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr  Non-EC sites:  NRW_DS123239 X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Non EC Bathing Waters.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Non EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr\tCustodian: Helena Sykes Officer, Environmental Information, Environmental Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) monitors bathing water sites identified under EC Bathing Waters Directive 2006/7/EC in Wales. Samples are taken throughout the bathing season from May to the end of September and analysed for two parameters: Escherichia coli and intestinal enterococci. A classification of bathing water quality generally based on 4 years of sample results is produced for each bathing water at the end of each season.\tPublic record\n18734\tNRW_DS123336\t2024-05-28T12:54:51.099Z\tWater Resource Permitting Catchments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333336\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\Water Resource Permitting Catchments.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18685\tNRW_DS123315\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.584Z\tPPC Sewer Emission Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333135\tx:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\PPC Sewer Emission Points.lyr\tJulie Boswell\t\tPrivate record\n18051\tNRW_DS115168\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.536Z\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Usk (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313638\t[A]  Data is archived as project 357, media 1336.   [B]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Lamprey Monitoring, River Usk 2011 - CCW – Science Report No. 11/8/6 CCW000800000000M.\t\tRiver lamprey Brook lamprey and Sea lamprey are listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive (EU Directive on the Conservation of Natural Habitats of Wild Fauna and Flora 92/43/EEC). There are six rivers in Wales designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) for lamprey including the River Usk in South Wales. The Countryside Council for Wales and now Natural Resources Wales is responsible for monitoring the condition of lamprey species populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within designated sites.  The main aim of this project is to undertake a condition assessment for the population attribute only for brook river and sea lamprey designated features for the River Usk SAC for the second Habitats Directive monitoring reporting cycle (2007-2012).  A total of 35 sites distributed within the Usk catchment.  Only a few sites had optimal habitats and sub-optimal habitats were dominant throughout.  Sampling was carried out between the 10th October and 16th November 2011.  The data is used to assess the population attribute for all three lamprey species. The results of the four attributes outlined in the Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) Guidance for Freshwater Fauna give an indication of the overall condition assessment.\tPublic record\n17712\tNRW_DS110194\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.753Z\tCondition monitoring of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313934\tIn summary DMS file and data are stored under the relevant SAC protected site under ‘monitoring’. GIS data is stored under the relevant GIS drive under ‘Evidence_Monitoring’ and split into either North or South Wales depending on location. Other than the top level summary data all field data and records are held and managed by the relevant NRW area.  This has largely been replaced by the baseline dashboard.\tInternal Contact/Custodian: Dylan Lloyd Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team        Dylan Lloyd is supplying some more detailed information on Terrestrial SAC monitoring data to update the entry.  This entry is specifically about terrestrial SAC monitoring for habs directive.  Marine and Freshwater programmes should probably have a separate entry.  Dylan will consider if when data and programme reviewed additional metadata entries need to be added for each Location/feature that is monitored.  This metadata entry is the one that will be linked to from Terrestrial Monitoring SAC reports (reduced to one summary report due to staff workloads)\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive. The Habitats Directive has a list of habitat types and species that are considered to be most in need of conservation at a European level (excluding birds which are covered under the EC Birds Directive). Once sites have been designated the country agencies work with owners occupiers and others to secure their management and protection regime. One element of this work is a programme of habitat and species monitoring across the site. Habitat monitoring includes woodland uplands wetlands lowland grasslands and coastal margins. For species monitoring covers a range of taxa from invertebrates through to mammals (including bat species) and both higher and lower plant.  This dataset combines the results of SAC monitoring from across Wales. The purpose of this collation is to assess the condition of features (the reason sites are protected) across these specific sites. The condition of features is monitored using site-specific performance indicators. The scope of performance indicators includes habitats and species across the terrestrial freshwater marine and coastal environments. This data is also used as an important record for aspects of their management both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales including UK through JNCC and for contributing to UK reports on Conservation Status under the EC Habitats Directive.\tPublic record\n18297\tNRW_DS116459\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.066Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Pipelines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343539\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Pipelines.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team Louise Harris\tPipelines within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge IDD mapped in point form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for health and safety reasons and for planning operations. Enables operators to identify the location of pipelines and ensure their safety when maintaining watercourses and flood defences.\tPrivate record\n18634\tNRW_DS123209\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.647Z\tSeagrass Quadrats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323039\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Quadrats\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tWater Framework Directive seagrass quadrat data. This data shows the positions of the quadrats that were taken. Species and density information for the quadrats is available on KiECO.\tPrivate record\n18516\tNRW_DS121625\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.404Z\tWales Grassland Fungi Assessment 2017 - 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363235\tEvidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Fungi \u0026gt; Fungus Habitats \u0026gt; Grassland Fungi assessment 2017-18 David Mitchel  DMS copy https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/fun/Fungus%20Habitats/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Ffun%2FFungus%20Habitats%2FGrassland%20Fungi%20assessment%202017%2D18%20David%20Mitchel\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000A00E94777D591649A9E40A217F4AC093\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet        • Third Party Data (records from the British Mycological Society and LERCs) • Data that is release could result in environmental harm  (species records that could result in a site owner destroying a potentially important grassland) • Personal Data (names of recorders)\tGrassland fungus data for Wales compiled updated and checked by David Mitchel in 2017-18. Species names are standardised; records are assigned to sites subsites and metasites with standard names; the diversity of species on sites subsites and metasites is given for each grassland fungal genus and for the whole \u0027CHEGD\u0027 assemblage.\tPublic record\n17851\tNRW_DS111167\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.798Z\tNant Teyrn Freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Physical and Chemical data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313637\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The data also gets sent to CEH.  b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team d) ECN team members m:drives\tCustodian: Victoria Bowmarker, Alex Turner ECN Project Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) is network of sites spanning the UK where we conduct environmental monitoring using standard methods. There are currently 12 terrestrial sites and 45 freshwater sites covering a wide range of upland and lowland habitats including moorland chalk grassland woods and forests farmland small ponds and streams large rivers and lakes. ECN is the UK member of the International Long-Term Ecological Research Network (ILTER) and of LTER-Europe. Nant Teyrn is a small upland stream on the out flow from Llyn Teyrn in Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve. This freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) site is co-located with Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon terrestrial ECN site. This dataset consists of the physical and chemical data collected at the ECN freshwater site at Nant Teyrn Snowdonia. Measurements taken include surface water discharge and surface water chemistry. The physical and chemical variables measured are expected to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the aquatic environment.\tPublic record\n18766\tNRW_DS123455\t2024-05-28T12:55:50.638Z\tPreviously Licence Exempt Surface Water Area\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343535\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Previously Licence Exempt Surface Water Area.lyr\tPaula Taylor, Senior Officer - Reservoir Regulation, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18300\tNRW_DS116458\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Powerlines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343538\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Powerlines.LYR\tLouise Harris, GIS/Engineering Technician, Spatial Data, KSP Directorate, NRW\tPowerlines within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge IDD mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for health and safety reasons and planning of operations on the Gwent Levels. Contains information on the height of powerlines.\tPublic record\n17363\tNRW_DS46086\t2024-05-28T12:21:21.926Z\tContacts Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436303836\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tAndrew Peterken, Designated Sites Project Manager, South and East Region, NRW\tThe Contacts data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) database information of individuals and organisations who do business with the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) who now form part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  The purpose of this data capture was to collate information on Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 contacts.\tPublic record\n18751\tNRW_DS123377\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.551Z\tFR Peat Restoration Hydro Suitability\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333737\tx:\\Physical\\Peat\\FR Peat Restoration Hydro Suitability.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18763\tNRW_DS123371\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.709Z\tAll Wales Hydrological Analysis Stream Network\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333731\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\allwales_str.lyr\tBen Anscombe\tThis layer was created to point to rasters used in NRW hydrological analysis tools. They are based upon the flow direction models which were created by the Environment Agency Detailed River Network which subsequently passed to NRW.  The All Wales Hydrological Analysis Stream Network is based upon a threshold of the flow accumulation raster (NRW_DS123368)\tPublic record\n18716\tNRW_DS123333\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.793Z\tNALD Licence Impoundments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333333\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\NALD Licence Impoundments - Feb 2016.lyr\tCustodian: Ashley Lansdown, Team Leader, Water Resources Permitting\t\tPrivate record\n17598\tNRW_DS108573\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.912Z\tThe Distribution of Salix herbacea (tiny creeping willow) on the Glydeiriau and evaluation of habitat quality\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038353733\tNRW Upland Ecologist Officer and TSG archive\tThe contractor was Richard Leishman and the contract manager was Barbara Jones Internal Custodian - There is no longer an Upland Ecologist in NRW, so the main contact is Jane Stevens or Andy Jones (in the address book at Robert A Jones). Peter Jones or Stuart Smith could also know where the data is.        Sent an email to Jane Stevens about the location of the data.\tEryri is the most southerly mountain range in Britain with summits of over 914 metres  (3000 feet). As a consequence it is also the southern-most station for a number of  Britain\u0027s montane vegetation communities and also a range of arctic alpine plants. The importance of the montane vegetation in this area has been recognised both nationally through the notification of the three highest mountain ranges as the Eryri Site of Special Interest (SSSI) but also internationally through the designation of this site as the Eryri/Snowdonia Special Area of Conservation (SAC). In addition many of the upland habitats that are present are listed in the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP).  One of the arctic alpine plants which characterises some of Eryri\u0027s most exposed and inhospitable summits and crags is Salix herbacea the smallest of the British willows. Salix herbacea is a component species of  Carex bigelowii-Racomitrium lanuginosum moss-heath (National Vegetation Classification (NVC) code U10) chasmophytic vegetation of rock crevices and rocky tors and very rarely a component of flush/spring vegetation including (NVC code M3I) Anthelia julacea-Sphagnum auriculatum spring. The habitat that supports the largest stands of S. herbacea is the moss-heath and this occupies plateaux and ridges from 730-970 metres. Less frequently it also occurs as a component of chasmophytic communities usually with very few individuals at each site and with an altitudinal range of 670 -998 metres.   This dataset is a collation of the results from a survey of the distribution of stands of Salix herbacea on the Glydeiriau and comparisons between this data-set and a similar data-set of 1986. The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Assess the condition of those plant communities which support Salix herbacea and where these communities are found to be in unfavourable condition  -  Assess the causes and impacts that have lead to this condition;   -  Identify any changes in distribution that may have occurred during the period between the capture of data sets in 1986 and 2007 and which might be attributed to climate change;  -  Identify those stands of vegetation which might be expected to be most vulnerable to climate change and which might be monitored in future years.  All suitable habitat on the Glydeiriau above 600 metres which supported Salix herbacea included H6150: Siliceous alpine and boreal grasslands on summits summit ridges and bwlchs. H8220:  Siliceous rocky slopes with chasmophytic vegetation particularly on exposed ridges and bwlchs and high level.\tPublic record\n18072\tNRW_DS115521\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.738Z\tSwansea Bay and Carmarthen Bay Beach profiles data (Lavernock to St Anns Head Shoreline Management Plan (SMP2))\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353231\tData is archived as project 432, media 1469.\tCeri Seaton       need to understabd the T\u0026amp;C under which this data was provided.  May be for internal use only\tThe purpose of this data capture was to provide a review of the background geological factors and physical processes which is intended to inform the assessment of policy options in the second generation Shoreline Management Plan for the area between St. Ann\u0027s Head and Lavernock Point.   This dataset describes the geological and physical processes occurring at Swansea and Carmarthen Bay. Processes investigated include beach profile beach topographic survey LiDAR cross sections summary characteristics tide data and weather data. Two related reports were written within the same project Annex A and Annex B Annex B builds on the earlier review and presents further analysis of data. Particular attention has been given to the estuaries and intervening shorelines in Swansea Bay and Carmarthen Bay. Three sections of open coast associated with the estuaries were also considered:  - The Pendine to Ginst Point barrier complex (including Pendine Burrows and Laugharne Burrows) - The Tywyn Point to Pembrey Burrows barrier complex - The Hills Tor to Whiteford Point barrier complex (including Whiteford Burrows).\tPublic record\n17762\tNRW_DS110271\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.472Z\tMonitoring the Health of the North Llyn Modiolus Reef\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323731\tAll data is archived under project no.218, media no. 584. Working copies of the data is held on shared MFSG drive. Video tapes are archived in the fireproof safe.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG Metadata contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tModiolus Reefs are a feature of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  Information is gathered about the health of the reef through the recording of species present in fixed quadrats.  Live horse mussels are hard to distinguish within the quadrat however it is known that each live horse mussel supports one colony of the soft coral dead man\u0027s fingers Alcyonium digitatum and therefore these are used to count the number of live horse mussels present. This survey work is carried out as part of the Across Wales Diving Survey. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the health of the reef. This contributes to reporting of favourable conservation status of the SAC feature.  Data will be used in conjunction with acoustic data that looks at the extent of the feature (reef) and potential areas of damage.\tPublic record\n19102\tNRW_DS125494\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.797Z\tBryophyte Survey and Assessment of Cwm Rheidol NNR and Coedydd a Cheunant Rheidol SSSI\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343934\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoed%20Rheidol%20Bryophyte%20Survey\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tAn updated bryophyte survey of the Cwm Rheidol NNR and Coedydd a Cheunant SSSI. It updates a previous survey undertaken by Averis in 1998. The bryophyte assemblage was found to be of national conservation interest and is certainly within the top ten sites in Wales for Oceanic Ravine Bryophytes (a species assemblage of national conservation importance under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2006).  The survey area comprises two sections of the Afon Rheidol one running north from Devil’s Bridge for approximately 3km and one running west from Devil’s Bridge for approximately 2km.\tPublic record\n18286\tNRW_DS116301\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.543Z\tUrban Tree Cover 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333031\tX:\\Biological\\Wales Urban Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Point 2009 .lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Poly 2009.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Land Categories 2009.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\Urban Extent.lyr\tCustodian:  John Briggs, Landscape Architect, PPG Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team Used to be Dafydd Fryer, Urban Forest Architect, Knowledge, Strategy and Planning, NRW. No longer works for NRW    Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis spatial dataset relates to Phase 2 (2009) and consists of 4 individual layers being Points (individual trees) polygons (groups of trees) urban extents (the extent of the study areas) and land use classifications. The technical report (see Related Titles and lineage) shows what is covered in each Phase (page 25) and provides more detail on the methodology behind the different layers in the dataset in Appendix A2.1 (page 132).\tPublic record\n18750\tNRW_DS123376\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.586Z\tFR Peat Restoration GHG Suitability\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333736\tx:\\Physical\\Peat\\FR Peat Restoration GHG Suitability.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18865\tNRW_DS124736\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.885Z\tArachnids (spiders \u0026amp; harvestmen) data for Morfa Dyffryn National Nature Reserve (NNR) 2017-2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373336\tData to be added to Recorder 6 - WID (Survey ID to be added)\tMike Howe\t\tPrivate record\n18771\tNRW_DS123452\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.117Z\tGround Water Authorised Landspreading\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343532\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Quality\\Ground Water Authorised Landspreading.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18163\tNRW_DS115898\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.714Z\tMacrophyte Survey of Welsh Lakes 2014\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383938\tData is archived under Project number 463 Media Number 1524\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) commissioned ECUS Ltd to collect and present ecological data from twenty-five lakes across eight local authority areas in Wales. The data was needed to support NRW’s integrated monitoring programme for; the Water Framework Directive (WFD); protected sites including Special Areas of Conservation SACs Special Protection Areas (SPAs) and Site of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs) as well as other legislative and policy drivers. The lakes surveyed were selected for a number of reasons:  - SAC: Monitoring of a SAC feature (Natura 2000 Site) - SSSI: Monitoring of a Site of Special Scientific Interest feature; - WFD: Water Framework Directive surveillance network site - ND: Nitrates Directive Two notable species that were recorded during the surveys. Floating water-plantain Luronium natans a European Protected Species was recorded at seven lakes (Llyn Egnant Llyn Gynon Llyn Teifi Llyn Cerrigllwydion Isaf Gwnllyn Llyn Padarn and Llyn Tegid). All of these are known locations for the species. Starry stonewort Nitellopsis obtusa is listed as Vulnerable on the UK Red List (JNCC 2014). Starry stonewort was recorded at Llangorse Lake making it only the second recorded site in Wales and a new record for this well-studied lake.\tPublic record\n17284\tEXT_DS124289\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.767Z\tPublic rights of way (PROW) (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323839\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Public rights of way\\PROW.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team\tPublic rights of way (PROW) data. This data is related to the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Public Rights of Way (PROW) Condition Survey (98725) and covers the baseline PRoW dataset  INFORMATION WARNING this data is from 2007 and as such may be out of date.It should NOT be used for legal challenges. For anything legal related please contact the Local Authorities for the most up to date version of this dataset.\tPublic record\n17387\tNRW_DS24327\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.200Z\tWelsh Sand Dune Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333237\tThe site reports are available from NRW\u0027s library.   Biological Data held in Recorder 6 Sand Dune Survey Wales CCW0007600000001  Spreadsheets were stored on the personal drive of NRW\u0027s Coastal Ecologist (Peter Rhind, retired). There is a data set here:- Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\RhindP\\Dune Spreadsheets.  According to Julie Creer this appears to be  \u0027Peter Rhind\u0027s own interrogation of the Phase 2 sand dune survey data (both the UK and Wales), which I suspect he got from the paper versions of survey. Looking at a few of the spreadsheets it looks like he separated out the different sand dune NVC communities and then tabulated which sites had those NVC communities and then added in the quadrat data tables for each site of that NVC type. S, in short, this is his analysis of that data and not the original dataset per se. These are most useful to me, but I’m not sure useful to anyone else?\u0027\tInternal Custodian: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSand Dune National Vegetation Classification (NVC) spreadsheets containing area and DOMIN values for each NVC community. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the sand dune vegetation of Wales.\tPublic record\n18757\tNRW_DS123370\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.710Z\tAll Wales Hydrological Analysis Stream Link\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333730\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\allwales_lnk.lyr\tBen Anscombe\tThis layer was created to point to rasters used in NRW hydrological analysis tools. They are based upon the flow direction models which were created by the Environment Agency Detailed River Network which subsequently passed to NRW.  All Wales Hydrological Analysis Stream Link shows the cells representing the All Wales Hydrological Analysis Stream Network (NRW_DS123371) but aggregated together to indicate discrete dendritic areas.\tPublic record\n17721\tNRW_DS110179\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.534Z\tBaseline survey for the Afon Teifi restoration project\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313739\tArchived as project number 197 (media number 544) H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media544\tInternal Custodian:  Rivers Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Afon Teifi is the longest river to have its course entirely within Wales. Its conservation value is high the entire river having Special Area of Conservation (SAC) status. Not far below its source in Teifi Pools the river traverses the Cors Caron National Nature Reserve meandering its way between the raised bogs for which the reserve is renowned. Parts of this sinuous course were canalized about a century ago creating sections of unusually straight and deep channel between steep clay banks. It is proposed to restore these reaches of the river to their original course turning the canalized sections into new lentic water bodies. This dataset includes the results of a baseline survey conducted in 2006-2007 which focussed on sediments invertebrates and fish. Sediment sampling was conducted to determine the extent of metal contamination of the material that would be excavated in the course of the restoration. The purpose of this data capture is to contribute to the evidence base that would be essential to completing a fully documented restoration project. Sediment sampling was conducted to determine the extent of metal contamination of the material that would be excavated in the course of the restoration. The risk of metal contamination exists due to historic mining in the upper part of the catchment. Invertebrate sampling aimed to establish the potential for improvement of the river\u0027s conservation value by comparing the invertebrate assemblages of the uncanalized reaches with the canalized sections. It also aimed to establish the conservation value of the existing pond in the original channel providing both a measure of the potential loss and a benchmark for the development of the replacement ponds in the canalized sections post-restoration. Survey of fish in an upstream uncanalized reach and in two canalized sections aimed to provide a baseline for the assessment of the effect of restoration on the suitability of the river for fish.\tPublic record\n18774\tNRW_DS123451\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.114Z\tPF WM Area 10k Buffer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343531\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\PF WM Area 10k Buffer.lyr\tListed as Clare Burrows?\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.  Should the  dataset be called \"Public Facing Waste Management Area - 10k Buffer\" ??\tPrivate record\n17059\tEXT_DS102087\t2024-05-28T12:10:09.638Z\tRSPB: Important Bird Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303837\tX:\\Designations\\RSPB\\RSPB Important Bird Areas.lyr  x:\\Recreation and Access\\MCRA\\RSPB important bird areas coastal 2009.lyr   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\biological\\species\\all_wales\\rspb\\important_bird_areas.TAB\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial boundaries of Important Bird Areas (IBAs) in Wales. IBAs are a world-wide network of areas identified using internationally agreed set of criteria highlighting defined areas an being important globally for the conservation of bird populations. Important Bird and Biodiversity Areas (IBAs) are: - Places of international significance for the conservation of birds and other biodiversity - Recognised world-wide as practical tools for conservation - Distinct areas amenable to practical conservation action - Identified using robust standardised criteria - Sites that together form part of a wider integrated approach to the conservation and sustainable use of the natural environment. This is an external third party dataset owned in the UK by the Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB) and licensed to NRW for internal use only.\tPublic record\n18797\tNRW_DS124101\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.983Z\tFR Peat Restoration Climate Suitability\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234313031\tx:\\Physical\\Peat\\FR Peat Restoration Climate Suitability.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17286\tEXT_DS124593\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.883Z\tScottish Power: cable records (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353933\tWe have an access only licence for one NRW user (John A Williams) to access this data online/as a service\tinternal contact: John A Williams\tOnline Access to GIS cable records database refreshed every 24 hours\tPublic record\n18761\tNRW_DS123378\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.648Z\tNRW Forest Soils\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333738\tx:\\Physical\\Soils\\NRW Forest Soil Map Coverage.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Soils\\NRW Forest Soils Line.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Soils\\NRW Forest Soils Point.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Soils\\NRW Forest Soils Polygon.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18773\tNRW_DS123450\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.106Z\tNRW Species teams areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343530\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Species teams areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gary Ecclestone, Open Access GIS Officer, GIS and Spatial Information Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales species team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for species managed teams.  This dataset defines the species team boundaries and the 22 team locations within those areas.\tPrivate record\n18983\tNRW_DS125112\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.388Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Fen Orchid Monitoring 2021\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313132\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Fen Orchid locations 2021.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Toni Henwood, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales, Sands of LIFE Project Internal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tLocation and phenology of fen orchids in January 2016 North slack and Lpost Extension Scrape as recorded in August 2021. Data also available Excel spreadsheet\tPrivate record\n18216\tNRW_DS116254\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.174Z\tNational Receptors Database (NRD) 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323534\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2011.lyr   Reports - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/Data-Management/da/DataArchivingTNA/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?id\u003d%2Fteams%2Fkdim%2FData%2DManagement%2Fda%2FDataArchivingTNA%2FNRD%202011%20reports%2Ezip\u0026amp;parent\u003d%2Fteams%2Fkdim%2FData%2DManagement%2Fda%2FDataArchivingTNA\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors typically residential and commercial property primarily intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management. NRD is a spatial dataset which contains a number of GIS layers categorised into themes of information including; buildings environment heritage transport and utilities. The NRD includes property points that are Copy Derived from OSMM AddressLayer2 with information on property type floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centres Multi-Coloured Manual codes attributed.  It was designed to meet the needs of Preliminary Flood Risk Assessments and the Environment Agency\u0027s National Flood Risk Assessment although it can be used for other purposes.\tPublic record\n17434\tNRW_DS87117\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.191Z\tSub-surface towed video footage off North Pen Llyn, and subsequent biotope identification data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313137\tData is archived under project no. 58, media no.\u0027s 279,283-287,294,300-309,543\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of around 5 hours of digital video footage. Positional and other associated data are given in Sanderson et al. (Eds.) 2001 (CCW Marine Monitoring Report No.1 section 2.1.3 and data appendix). The purpose of the data capture was to create an evaluation of the deployment of towed video for monitoring biotope richness. Footage collected as an assessment of using towed video as part of a site-orientated monitoring technique. Following the increased commercial availability of small digital video cameras and digital video editing software data was collected to evaluate the use of these systems in marine monitoring studies. Biotope richness (ie. The number of biotopes present rather than species diversity within a biotope) was assessed from the recorded video.\tPublic record\n18752\tNRW_DS123373\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.653Z\tTerrain 5m\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333733\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Terrain_5m_Terrain.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThis dataset forms part of the Hydrological Tools Datasets  The Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any further information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18156\tNRW_DS115889\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.255Z\tSAC Monitoring: Afon Tywi Shad Egg Survey (2013 and 2014)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383839\t[A] Data is archived under Project Number: 459 Media Number: 1520.   [B] Field notes are held in Maes Newydd by the Technical Biodiversity Officer and River Officer, in Maes Ffynnon, under the Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group - Evidence and Analysis Team. [C] PDF of the report is held on the DMS Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports NRW Evidence Report No. 048 - Afon Tywi SAC shad egg surveys 2013 \u0026amp; 2014  KNOW-1206538853-57\t\tThe Afon Tywi is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. In Britain significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn Usk Wye and Tywi. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. The aim of this data collection was to contribute towards the assessment the condition of the shad population in the Afon Tywi SAC based on 2013 and 2014 egg surveys. The objectives of this study are to: 1)  Conduct a shad egg survey on the Afon Tywi in 2013 and 2014.   2)  Analyse and present the results for spawning distribution of the population attribute only.   3)  Use the data obtained from the genetic analysis of shad egg tissue to give additional context to the above analysis.  2013 Shad eggs were present at seven of 13 survey sites (54 percent). Each of the 12 survey sites were visited once. The egg sample size ranged from 1 to 100 plus eggs. The downstream limit of spawning was Tidal limit (site 16) and the upstream limit was Manordeilo (site 11). A total of 177 plus eggs were recorded. The identification of shad eggs were confirmed through genetic analysis at two of the three selected sample sites.  2014  Shad eggs were recorded at eight of the 14 survey sites (57 percent). Seven of the sample sites were visited twice (54 percent) and four sites were visited three times (29 percent). The 14 sites were surveyed between 16 May and 11 Junes over 6 days by 13 surveyors.   Purpose of data capture:\tPublic record\n18856\tNRW_DS124764\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.386Z\tCuRVe Condition INNS\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373634\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tInvasive non-native species (INNS) can pose a threat to ecosystem resilience throughout Wales.  The output shows relative resilience as high for squares where very few unique INNS were recorded.\tPrivate record\n18808\tNRW_DS123553\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.553Z\tCamping and Caravan Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353533\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Camping and Caravan Sites.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18860\tNRW_DS124760\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.686Z\tCuRVe Adaptability Overall\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373630\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows resilience based on adaptability using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tPrivate record\n18176\tNRW_DS116235\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.383Z\tFlood Map: Flood Zone 2 (archived data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323335\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Zone 2.lyr\tCustodian:  Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW\tThis dataset has been archived and has been superseded by Flood Map for Planning (NRW_DS125076).  Flood Map shows the areas across Wales that could be affected by flooding from rivers or the sea. It also shows flood defences and the areas that benefit from them.   Flood Map is designed to raise awareness among the public local authorities and other organisations of the likelihood of flooding and to encourage people living and working in areas prone to flooding to find out more and take appropriate action.   The Flood Map includes several layers of information this layer is: Flood Zone 2 which is NRWs best estimate of the areas of land between Zone 3 and the extent of the flood from rivers or the sea with a 1000 to 1 chance of flooding in any year. It includes those areas defined in Flood Zone 3.\tPublic record\n17457\tNRW_DS99644\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.425Z\tTir Cymen Farms\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363434\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_cymen\\tircymen_farms.tab\tGIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to NRW for internal use only. The dataset has been supplied by the Welsh Government and shows Tir Cymen Farm locations as a point layer.\tPublic record\n18291\tNRW_DS116318\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.549Z\tMarine Benthic Invertebrate Species (extracted from BIOSYS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333138\t[A]  Extracted from BIOSYS Packaged data available: Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116318\tBianca Bacchi / Richard West - Senior Env Mon Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R\tInformation regarding the presence and abundance of benthic invertebrate species at specific marine monitoring points held within the Biological Systems (BIOSYS). BIOSYS is a former Environment Agency Wales\u0027 (EAW)) database. This welsh extract of the data is now stored and managed by Natural Resources Wales. BIOSYS was EAW main database for storing manipulating and reporting data from freshwater and marine biological surveys at any taxonomic level and Water Framework Directive marine benthic invertebrate database. Data are laboratory assessed and quality assured following the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme. National databases are continually being updated as a result of on-going benthic invertebrate monitoring programmes. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data collected/owned/managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPublic record\n18189\tNRW_DS116005\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.609Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Powys 1999\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303035\tspecies data held on Recorder 6: CCW0007300000010.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Powys. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tPublic record\n17677\tNRW_DS110099\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.567Z\tStatus and Breeding of Cormorants Phalacrocorax carbo in Wales (1996-1997): The Effect of the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303939\tReports are archived as Project No\u0027s. 17 and 115, and Media No\u0027s. 49 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media049) and 383 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media301to400\\Media383).   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW- Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.\tInternal Contact: Marine Monitoring Team Leader (was Bill Sanderson at the point of collection, now Lucie Haines), NRW Marine Ornithologist, NRW  (Matty Murphy)  Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe Sea Empress Environmental Evaluation Committee (SEEEC) and the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned various studies of the effects of the oil spill on the wildlife of Pembrokeshire. This dataset contains the results of work carried out to investigate the effects of the spill on Cormorants Phalacrocorax carbo.    In 1996 the project aimed to carry out a survey of the numbers distribution and breeding performance of cormorants breeding in Wales including detailed case studies at colonies in the area contaminated by oil in south and west Pembrokeshire.  Monitoring of numbers and breeding performance was continued in 1997 with detailed studies of breeding phenology and breeding success at two South Pembrokeshire colonies (St Margaret\u0027s Island and Green Scar).   Data was used to assess the numbers and distribution of Cormorants breeding in Wales; and to investigate the breeding performance of Cormorants in Wales including detailed case studies at colonies in the area contaminated by oil in south and west Pembrokeshire.\tPublic record\n17443\tNRW_DS98710\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.260Z\tLocal Biodiveristy Action Plan (LBAP) Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373130\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestial\\LBAP_areas.lyr\tMetadata and Custodian Contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the locations of the Local Biodiversity Action Plan Areas.The Local Biodiversity Partnership areas across Wales were used for tragetted species and habitat Action Plans allowing for a more localised place based approach to monitoring and protecting key areas in Wales.\tPublic record\n17399\tNRW_DS64563\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.579Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Image Store\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353633\thttp://imagestore.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.internal/asset-bank/action/viewHome  NB  You need to be on Pulse Secure for the URL in internal location to work.\tInternal Contact: Bryan Tozer, Advisor 2, Master Data Team  Whilst Bryan is the main contact for Image Store - each image and image collation will have an owner in the metadata in image store itself\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a large numbers of photographic images created for the following numerous purposes including biological recording; habitat recording; land-use recording; recording events and activities; communications and public engagement.\tPublic record\n17291\tEXT_DS124288\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.933Z\tESTYN: Education data (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323838\tX:\\Social Economic\\Education\tData custodian ; Geospatial Team. Knowledge Information Management\tFour spatial layers covering the following;    1) ESTYN Higher Education; Higher Education establishments in Wales as at January 2014.    2) ESTYN annual report 2011-14; Based on the Local Authorities dataset provided by NRW this layer shows the Local Authorities ESTYN results for period 2012-2014. Fields Overall Performance and Overall Prospects have been updated based on the ESTYN results downloaded from http://www.estyn.gov.uk/english/annual-report/.   3) ESTYN Further Education; Further Education establishments in Wales as at January 2014.   4) ESTYN Independent Schools; Independant Schools in Wales as at January 2014.\tPublic record\n17885\tNRW_DS111196\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.905Z\tRemote Sensing Image Catalogue\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313936\tMost images are kept in locked storage units in CCW\u0027s Maes y Ffynnon and the Environmental Policy group\u0027s work area.  Backups are held on hard drives and stored in a safe. The metadata is stored with the images.\tHelena Skyes  Used to be Alan Brown, Remote Sensing Officer, Environmental Policy Group, NRW\tRemote sensing is any method of collecting information about objects such as lakes trees fields and habitats \u0027from a distance\u0027. This dataset contains a collection of around 300 archival images purchased by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to build up a resource of satellite remote sensing images of the Welsh countryside. The images have not all been collected for specific projects however some tasking has taken place i.e. images requested from a particular satellite or from a particular date. Each image has metadata detailing image identification number date and time type of image location etc.\tPublic record\n18640\tNRW_DS122948\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.845Z\tWales Trails Management System\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393438\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Furniture Lines.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Furniture Points.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Issue Lines.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Issue Points.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Issue Polygons.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Route Lines.lyr\tContact:  Quentin Grimely Access Delivery Team, Sustainable Communities Gruffudd Owen, Wales Coast Path Officer (North)       The data is overwritten as it is updated along the path - so start date may not relfect the date on the dataset.  Natural Resources Wales (NRW) grant fund Local Authorities to collect this data. They input the data into the CAMs system which is hosted. NRW then collate the data on behalf of the grant funded local authorities and supply it back to them as a whole dataset via a Web Mapping Service.  At the moment we arent clear over the ownership of the data, becuase it is grant aided we think that the data is owned by the council but then given to NRW for use.\tThis dataset is related to the condition of the Wales Coast Path and National Trails in Wales (with the exception of the Pembrokeshire Coast Path the data for which is held by Pembrokeshire Coast National Park Authority). It contains information relating to trail furniture and problems / issues encountered along each respective trail.\tPublic record\n18170\tNRW_DS115992\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.735Z\tWelsh Sand Lizard  (Lacerta agilis) and Natterjack Toad (Epidalea calamita) Recovery Project 2011-2014\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393932\tRaw Data is held and distributed by ARC Trust.\tContact: Liz Howe Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of field observations of adults spawn strings and habitat conditions for Natterjack Toads (Epidalea calamita) and Sand Lizards (Lacerta agilis). The data was collected for an assessment of current populations at re-introduction sites.   Natterjack Toad (Epidalea calamita) Natterjack toad breeding activity was recorded in all three sections of Gronant Dunes and Talacre Warren Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in 2013. The breeding pools were monitored and the data presented to the annual recorders meetings.  A project to establish natterjacks at the Old Colliery site was started in 2009 but the initial translocation was unsuccessful. In 2010 Environment Agency Wales created about 30 pools at the site and further modified them for natterjacks in 2012. Their hydro-periodism was observed during 2013 and the translocation programme will start afresh in 2014.  In 2011 a total of only 168 spawn strings were recorded in Wales whereas in 2012 was a record year was observed with 454 spawn strings counted. The relatively low spawn count in 2011 can be attributable to the dry weather that shortened the breeding season. In stark contrast to a dry start 2012 became very wet and the natterjacks had a long and successful breeding season.  The total recorded in 2012 was the highest ever a reflection of the strengthening population good weather conditions and an increase in the number of pools at Talacre through the Million Ponds Project funding. The population and breeding range is likely to be at its highest level since the re-introduction project began in 1995.   Sand Lizard (Lacerta agilis) The sand lizard (Lacerta agilis) was formerly known from dune systems in north and west Wales. These known populations were lost due to erosion development and increased public pressure. Natural Resources Wales (formerly CCW) has continued to provide funds to ascertain the status of the species via site monitoring and provided funding for captive breeding and translocation programmes to restore the species former range. Monitoring at all of the translocation sites continued though was constrained by poor spring weather in 2013. Positive results of both adults and juveniles have been obtained at all established translocation sites.\tPublic record\n18319\tNRW_DS116351\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.261Z\tSevern Estuary additional samples taken from Phase 1 intertidal surveys (2002)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333531\t[A] All species data is stored in Marine Recorder  2002 CCW Severn Estuary intertidal Phase 1 survey  MRCCW30000000045\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environment Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThis datasets contains the results of additional sampling taken place during phase 1 intertidal surveys around the Severn Estuary. Marine Nature conservation Review (MNCR) littoral habitat forms were completed and infauna and Particle Size Analysis (PSA) were collected but never worked up due to resource constraints.\tPublic record\n19024\tNRW_DS125197\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.477Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313937\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Lakes Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 3 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Article 2 clause 5 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...a body of standing inland surface water\u0027.   There is data on the physical characteristics risk classification and proposed objectives that can be linked to waterbodies by their unique identifiers. Artificial and modified lake waterbodies are included within this dataset however generally only lakes above \u0026gt; 50 hectares were assessed under the WFD except for lakes in protected areas where a minimum of 5.0 ha was used. Lakes below this threshold are not included within this dataset unless allocated as Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). Each waterbody has been assigned \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 which is a unique identifier that enables a link to WFD attributes.   These data apply to Cycle 3 of the Water Framework Directive.\tPublic record\n18827\tNRW_DS124676\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.216Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Innogy Maintenance Route\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363736\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Innogy Maintenance Route.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Maintenance routes. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n19088\tNRW_DS125511\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.597Z\tMonitoring of the Black Bog Ant Formica picea on Rhossili Down SSSI in 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353131\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe Black Bog Ant Formica picea is rare in the UK where it is associated with bogs and wet heath at a small number of sites in southern England including the heaths of Dorset and Surrey and the valley bogs in the New Forest with a single site in Yorkshire. In Wales it is restricted to Rhossili Down on Gower Cors Goch Llanllwch in Carmarthenshire and Cors Llanerch in Pembrokeshire.   It was first recorded on Rhossili Down in 1913 and subsequently refound in 1996. A survey in July 1997 found 49 active nests along four east to west-oriented transects across the bog and flushed wet heath habitats to the east of the central ridge with a further 23 active nests elsewhere on the site. It was estimated that between 840 and 4970 active nests were present across the site representing the largest population in the UK.   The current survey in August 2022 repeated the 1997 work with a count of active nests along the four transects.\tPublic record\n17704\tNRW_DS110035\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.631Z\tResults of a field survey following the Menai Strait oil spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303335\tArchived as project number 138 and Media No. 397 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 397)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tFormer Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) were informed at 09:13 on Sunday the 25th June 2006 that there was an oil spill in the Menai Strait. Oil had been reported to the Coastguard at around 08:15.  At the time the information available was that there was an oil/diesel slick of unknown origin spreading from Caernarfon up to the Swellies. The tide was flooding at this time with high water around 11:00. The level of the incident was thought to be minor. At 10:25 on 25th June CCW regional officers were on the shore close to the Friction Dynamics factory.  The strandline was reported to be contaminated: cobbles splattered with oil. The level of the incident was considered minor/medium at this time. The purpose of this data capture was to assess contamination of species and habitats following the oil spill on the 25th of June 2006.\tPublic record\n17439\tNRW_DS87189\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.175Z\tFood sources for Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) in Carmarthen Bay\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313839\tData and Report are archived under Project No. 1, Media 222\tInternal Custodian Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services.\tIn the UK the Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) is recognised as a nationally threatened species due to its small and declining breeding population. Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important wintering ground for the Common Scoter duck (Melanitta nigra) and is widely regarded as the most important such site in the UK causing the site to be proposed as a Special Protection Area (pSPA) under the EC Birds Directive. Carmarthen Bay is considered to support peak numbers of 17000 - 24000 birds. The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs especially during the winter although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps) worms echinoderms isopods amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs.   An extensive macrofauna sampling survey was undertaken to coincide with an aerial scoter survey. Conservation concerns for the scoter population focus on threats of hunting pressure breeding habitat disturbance and loss food chain contaminants oil spills and the threat to non-breeding habitats represented by offshore developments. The aim of this project was to investigate the biological and physical factors affecting the winter distribution of common scoter in Carmarthen Bay and to identify what component of the benthos they feed upon.  The identification of such components is crucial to the future management and monitoring of the Carmarthen Bay Special Protection Area and to some extent the Special Area of Conservation.\tPublic record\n18828\tNRW_DS124678\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.256Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Permissive Route\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363738\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Permissive Route.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Permissive Route. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17861\tNRW_DS111511\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.681Z\tAccess Maps - Permissive Access in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353131\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\Permissive Access\tInternal Custodian:  Jill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains permissive access land in Wales. This includes land under Tir Gofal agreement which give access to specified parts of the farm National Trust land and National Park access agreements and means that the landowner or tenant of the land has agreed to allow public access for open-air recreation normally on foot. The purpose of this data capture was to enable people to be confident of their access rights when visiting the Welsh countryside.\tPublic record\n17320\tEXT_DS124935\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.307Z\tEarthTrack (Living Wales) Ground Validation Points for Land Cover and Land Cover Change (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393335\tX:\\Biological\\Living Wales\\Earth Track Points in Wales (LCCS data format).lyr\tHelena Sykes and Philip Jutson - Specialist Advisors, Monitoring and Analysis\tThe EARTHTRACK app has been specifically designed to allow a wide range of users to collect ground truth data that then supports a) spatial retrieval and validation of attributes with pre-defined units (e.g. m %) or categories (e.g. species type) from EO data (by allowing measurement and recording of these on the ground) and b) validation of derived land cover and change classifications obtained from satellite and airborne data.   Earth Track points include near real time and historical records of observations of land cover and change on the ground that are geographically referenced (typically \u0026lt; 3-5 m positional accuracy) with well-defined categories that mirror those mapped at the national level through Living Wales (wales.livingearth.online).  These points have been collected by the EarthTrack mobile application which has been developed using Open Data Kit software and functionality but is being translated (in 2021/22) to a professional version available to be made available on the app stores and allowing use across major operating systems.  All points are used to validate the land cover and land cover change maps from 2018 onwards and into the future including in near real time.  The Living Wales 10 m national land cover maps for Wales are constructed from environmental descriptors with predefined units (e.g. m %) or codes (e.g. for lifeform or leaf type) retrieved or classified respectively primarily from satellite sensor data and according to the according to the United Nations (UN) Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) Land Cover Classification System (LCCS) Version 2 taxonomy (Di Gregorio 2005).  The EarthTrack mobile application exactly replicates how these land cover classes are constructed but at the ground level.    The land cover change information also replicates that of the land cover maps for Wales which is based on evidence and uses the Global Change Taxonomy (with an associated glossary). This describes change across global environments according to at least 230 classes with these defined according to the notation ‘impacts (pressures)’.   These categories are derived from the Drivers-Pressures-States-Impacts-Response framework where the states define the physical chemical and/or biological characteristics of an environment.  Impacts are discerned from state changes (either one or many) and result from pressures associated with abiotic or biotic events or processes (e.g. fires droughts) or anthropogenic activities (e.g. ploughing timber harvesting) with these driven primarily by climate/weather and economy.  Di Gregorio A.: Land Cover Classification System (LCCS). Classification concepts and user manual for software version 2. FAO Environment and Natural Resources Series 8 190 pp (2005).\tPublic record\n18364\tNRW_DS116819\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.849Z\tAfon Teifi Special Area of Conservation (SAC) population attribute condition assessment for brook, river and sea lamprey population 2014 . Habitats Directive third reporting cycle 2013 – 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383139\t- Data is stored in WISKI / KiECO  - Analysis: Workbook Teifi lamprey 2014.xlsx DMS ref: SAMP-753-136  - Report: Afon Teifi SAC population attribute condition assessment for brook, river and sea lamprey population 2014. Habitats Directive third reporting cycle 2013 – 2018. Report No. 106.\tData Custodian(s): Emma Keenan, Environmental Monitoring Officer Fisheries  Metadata Point of Contact:  Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator.\tRiver lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis) Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri) and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) are designated features of the Afon Teifi Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Assessments of species composition distribution abundance and size composition of larval lamprey (ammocoetes and transformers) populations in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Teifi catchments. This collation of lamprey data is made up of data collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW)  Environment Agency Wales and Natural Resources Wales. The collation includes information about species distribution composition and abundance for river brook and sea lampreys which are a designated (Annex II) features within SAC rivers in Wales. The majority of the data was recorded at the genus level and no distinction was made between river and brook lampreys. However for some historical data no distinction was made between sea and river/brook lampreys and these were recorded at the family level.\tPublic record\n17860\tNRW_DS111509\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.692Z\tAccess Maps - Sites and Reserves\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353039\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\WNAR\tInternal Custodian:  Jill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset is one of a number of GIS tables that are used to deliver the Welsh National Access Register (WNAR) project. The collation and presentation of data on a number of sites throughout Wales in an easy-to-view format enables people to find out more about places of interest and their accessibility. The dataset was first created in 2005. It is maintained and added to regularly during the year due to changing partnership information as well as in response to annual agreement partnership checks.  This ensures we display the information that our partners want to promote as well as keeping broken URL links to a minimum.  This project helps to deliver the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) \u0027PE04 Enjoying the Outdoors\u0027  programme output \u0027More people from all sectors of society will regularly be using and benefiting from widespread and equitable access to countryside coast and urban green-space\u0027.  The overall aim of the project was to get more people (1% annual increase) from all sectors of society regularly participating in open-air recreation and exercise. The objectives from this program output are:  1. Increase availability of high quality recreation resource including public and other urban green-space without compromising environmental and cultural interests  2. Provide readily available targeted information about recreation opportunities and encourage greater awareness of rights and responsibility that will lead to positive behaviour changes  3. Provide a means of finding accommodation alongside the recreational opportunities in discussion with our partners.\tPublic record\n17432\tNRW_DS98705\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.128Z\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Invertebrate Species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373035\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestial\\Invertebrates Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\biological\\species\\all_wales\\baps\\invertebrates\\bap_species.tab\tData Custodian: Mike Howe (legacy CCW dataset put together by Adrian Fowles) Senior Invertebrate Ecologist or John Bratton Biological Data Officer. Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons.  This purpose of this data capture was to enable Non Government Organisations (NGOs) Local Authorities (LAs) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) staff to have access to spatial data on BAP Priority invertebrates.  This dataset is a collation of all confirmed occurrence records of UK Biodiversity Action Plan ( BAP ) priority invertebrate species reported from Wales.\tPublic record\n18545\tNRW_DS121725\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.211Z\tWINS Water Quality: Opportunities for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373235\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Opportunities for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment .lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: The map shows areas where management action could be taken to reduce nutrient enrichment in coastal and transitional waterbodies as well as lakes and rivers. Management opportunities considered for reduction of nutrient enrichment are the restoration of land well-connected to the local water environment; hedgerow planting in proximity to agricultural land likely to be a diffuse source of nutrients; planting riparian woodland or wooded shelterbelts along drainage channels. For each opportunity type prioritisation is based the number of downslope waterbodies that are known to have issues with nutrient enrichment.\tPublic record\n17448\tNRW_DS87182\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.274Z\tSensitivity and mapping of inshore marine biotopes in the southern Irish Sea (SensMap): Final report\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313832\tData is archived as Project No. 181, media No\u0027s. 359 and 521 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 359 and K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 521)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThe SensMap project funded under the INTERREG programme involved survey and mapping of biotopes in the marine intertidal and subtidal in both Wales and Ireland. The project partners were the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Ecological Consultancy Services Ltd (EcoServe) and the National Parks and Wildlife Service (NPWS) in Ireland now Duchas the Heritage Service.  This project had three main objectives:  1. To develop an integrated methodology for mapping in the marine intertidal and subtidal which can link both marine and terrestrial maps.  2. To develop a protocol for determining the sensitivity of marine biotopes according to the sensitivity of marine species to a range of natural and man-induced changes including changes to water quality oil pollution fishing and recreational activities and in the longer term climate change. 3. To develop a model for a decision-making support system based on sensitivity mapping.\tPublic record\n18333\tNRW_DS116518\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.499Z\tIntertidal Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring Menai Strait and Conwy Bay (2012): Evaluating Intertidal Seagrass Beds – Field Survey Versus Remote Sensing\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353138\tArchived as project 410, media 1541.  Locally held version of data at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2012 CCW (to be cleared up)  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2012 CCW Zostera\\ Archive: Data stored as Project 410, media 1541 W:\\Media1501to1600\\Media1541\\2012ZosteraProject\\Data\\\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Paul Brazier is completing a metadata proforma.  unlikely that the data will have any restrictions.  Contracted ortho-rectified images\tSeagrass beds are of special interest in Wales as features under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 (as amended) a Section 42 habitat under the NERC Act (2006) a subfeature of Special Areas of Conservation under the Habitats Regulations and is a biological quality element (marine angiosperms) under the Water Framework Directive.  As such it is crucial that a reliable method of evaluating the quality (extent density and species richness) of the seagrass beds is used.  Traditionally field observations are made and records of the presence and extent derived from mapping using a GPS.  Until recent years the resolution of aerial photographs taken from aircraft has not been high enough to differentiate seagrass beds unless they are particularly dense with clearly defined edges.  Many of the intertidal seagrass beds in Wales are patchy and of low density (5-50% cover) and cannot be identified reliably from 1:5000 aerial photographs. This study uses an unmanned aerial vehicle to take higher resolution images by flying lower along with GPS positions allowing for the many images to be stitched together and be orthorectified.  The two methods are compared each one proving to have certain benefits and short-comings.\tPublic record\n17919\tNRW_DS114339\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.749Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools, Caves and Overhangs in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333339\tArchive project 199, media no. 1218 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1218) Species data available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW1630000001A] Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  Rockpools overhangs and caves have been identified as important elements of the \u0027large shallow inlets and bays\u0027 feature and of Sites of Special Scientific Interest. These potentially species rich rocky habitats were surveyed in Carmarthen Bay to provide an inventory and to fully characterise these sub-features.  The purpose of this data capture was to provide an inventory and distribution map of selected biotopes and species inventory across South Wales.\tPublic record\n18838\tNRW_DS124661\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.844Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Site Boundary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363631\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Site Boundary.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm Construction Site Boundary. The boundary that was granted by the Local Planning Authority and was used as the Construction Design Management (CDM) area. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18479\tNRW_DS121595\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.014Z\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G West Proposed Turbines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353935\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G West Proposed Turbines.lyr\tCustodians: James Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tPublic record\n18332\tNRW_DS116519\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.526Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Tidal Flaps\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353139\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Tidal Flaps\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset shows the location of tidal flaps across the Gwent Levels from Cardiff to Caldicot. Tidal Flaps are sea doors that allow water out of the reen/watercourse network when the tide is out and close when the tide is in to stop sea water from coming in to the reen/watercourse network.\tPublic record\n18081\tNRW_DS115277\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.811Z\tFreshwater Macrophyte Survey Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323737\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A]  Data is archived as project 401, media no. 1418.  [B]  Species and abundance data is held within Recorder 6 as Macrophyte Survey of Mid-Wales Rivers 2013- CCW Science Report 1034 No. CCW000800000000K.\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Data came from the core survey team of Sarah Clarke, Dianne Dobson and Rob Harrison.\tThe habitat \u0027H3260 Water Courses of Plain to Submontane Levels with the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-batrachion Vegetation\u0027 (henceforth \u0027Ranunculion habitat\u0027) is listed in Annexe I of the EU Habitat Directive as a habitat of European Importance. Four rivers in Wales the Afon Gwyrfai Afon Teifi River Dee and River Wye are considered of European importance for this habitat type. A gap analysis of available data has indicated that there is a shortage of recent information on macrophyte community composition and extent in the Teifi and Upper Wye catchments. Therefore these catchments have formed the focus of this study and data has been collected and analysed from these areas to determine whether the performance indicators for macrophyte community composition and extent for these rivers pass the required standard for extent and quality. The primary aim of this project is to produce an assessment of the status of the plant community component of the Ranunculion habitat on the Rivers Wye and Teifi. This dataset comprises of a survey entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway. Confidence in this dataset is high all survey work was undertaken by a team of two experienced macrophyte surveyors.\tPublic record\n18182\tNRW_DS116228\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.945Z\tRecorded Flood Extents\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323238\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Historic Floodmap.lyr\tInternal custodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tRecorded Flood Extents shows areas that have been recorded to have flooded in the past from rivers the sea or surface water. The records come from a number of evidence sources including Natural Resources Wales its predecessors or other Risk Management Authorities.    It is possible that the pattern of flooding in an area may have changed and would now flood under different circumstances. In addition the absence of a recorded flood extent does not mean that the area has never flooded only that we do not currently have records of flooding in this area.\tPublic record\n17920\tNRW_DS113480\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.783Z\tMarine Non-Native Atlas for Wales 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133343830\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species  Archived under project 286, media no. 1184. Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Marine\\NonNatives\tCustodian: Marine and Freshwater Group or GIS and Spatial Data Team. Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe dataset contains a series of records for each of the non-native marine species currently identified as occurring in Wales. The records have been extracted from Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCWs) Phase 1 Intertidal surveys Marine Recorder data holdings National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway web source MarLIN/DASSH web source DEFRA Marine Protected Area (MPA) non-native GIS layers Local Environmental Record Centres (LERC\u0027s ) and other ad-hoc records collated by CCW staff or supplied by third parties. The purpose of this data collation is to monitor the spread of non-native marine species in Wales by creating a current and meaningful atlas using GIS layers for individual layers.\tPublic record\n18832\tNRW_DS124677\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.195Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Landlords Estate\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363737\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Landlords Estate.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Landlords Estate. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17605\tNRW_DS102597\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.078Z\tGreat Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) Sites in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032353937\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Great Crested Newts.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Great Crested Newts font point.lyr\tMetadata and data custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons. Listing of great crested newt (Triturus cristatus) sites-extracted from recorder. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the distribution and abundance of an Annex II species. Records for the whole of Wales although the majority of records occur in Ynys Mon and on the borders of East Wales. The collection of records began in 1974 and is on-going.\tPublic record\n17323\tEXT_DS124854\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.328Z\tMet Office: Quality Controlled Rainfall Data (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383534\t\tH\u0026amp;T Team\tTo be completed\tPrivate record\n18671\tNRW_DS123257\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.436Z\tWFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2 Interim\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323537\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2 Interim.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge\t\tPrivate record\n18705\tNRW_DS123300\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.452Z\tChalara Working Target Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333030\tx:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Chalara Working Target Sites.lyr\tSam Milner\t\tPrivate record\n18166\tNRW_DS115954\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.795Z\tAssessment of the condition of the white-clawed crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) (2014).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393534\tData is held within the following report: Rogers D \u0026amp; Watson E 2015. Assessment of the condition of the white-clawed crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation in 2014. NRW Evidence Report No: 74. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t\tIn October and November 2014 parts of the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) on the Afon Edw Nant yr Offeiriad and Sgithwen Brook were surveyed for white-clawed crayfish to assess the condition of both the crayfish populations and crayfish habitat. The work was undertaken to provide some of the data required to assess whether the white-clawed crayfish in the River Wye SAC was in favourable condition.   Whilst further data will need to be collected before the full assessment can be made the apparent lack of white-clawed crayfish in the Afon Edw following a mortality in 2006 is worrying given that this has been a very important waterway for this species in the recent past. Whilst crayfish numbers were relatively robust in Nant yr Offeiriad and Sgithwen Brook populations were mostly confined to the headwaters.  It is important to complete survey work for white-clawed crayfish on the River Wye SAC in 2015 so that the 2014 results can be amalgamated into an overall assessment.\tPublic record\n17599\tNRW_DS108672\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.917Z\tCanal sediment and peat chemistry at Crymlyn Bog National Nature Reserve (NNR) (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363732\tThe results are held in the Report located in the NRW library: [exCCW location Ffynnon Reference DCT-10-037447]  Located in Reports Archive: NRW DMS KNOW-1375319549-766\tInternal Custodian:  Peter Jones, Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Jamie Bevan, Senior Reserves Manager, NNRs/Swansea Neath Port Talbot / Operations SW, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tAn investigation found that Crymlyn Brook was a major threat to the integrity of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) qualifying habitats at Crymlyn Bog as it was a source of nitrogen and phosphorous flowing directly in to the area of low-nutrient status transition mire and quaking bog habitats. It was suggested that the course of the brook should be altered to what it was prior to changes and siltation during the latter half of the twentieth century. This would result in it avoiding the sensitive habitats instead connecting to Glan-y-Wern canal the main route by which water leaves the mire.  This dataset contains the results of a survey of the possible route for the reinstatement of the Crymlyn Brook relating to the extant vegetation along the route and the degree and depth to which the substratum is contaminated with mineral oil. Also examined were the depth of the Glan-y-Wern canal and the degree to which the sediments in the canal are contaminated with heavy metals and mineral oil. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the depth of standing water in the canal the chemistry of it\u0027s sediments and various aspects relating to the extent of hydrocarbon pollution within peat lying along the course of a proposed drainage feature.\tPublic record\n18656\tNRW_DS123280\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.233Z\tCatchment Flood Management Plans\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323830\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Catchment Flood Management Plans.lyr\tRachel Sion\t\tPrivate record\n17867\tNRW_DS111487\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.158Z\tVolunteer Diver Seagrass Zostera marina Surveys Porth Dinllaen (2009)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343837\tData is archived as project 229, media no. 598.  A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSeagrass is the only marine flowering plant found subtidally in the UK and seagrass beds are considered of National importance.  In 2009 Zostera marina was the focus of former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) volunteer dive surveys at Porth Dinllaen in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) to estimate the extent of the bed. The survey is a continuation of a 2008 pilot and is due to be re-surveyed before 2012 to start to build up a picture of variation in the extent of the seagrass bed at the site. The main objectives of this project were to: 1. Test methods to measure extent of large seagrass beds using volunteers 2. Estimate density and measure depth of live seagrass in different parts of the bed 3. Make note of any unusual features of the seagrass bed 4. Compare with results and success of 2008 surveys\tPublic record\n18887\tNRW_DS124799\t2024-05-28T12:57:55.976Z\tCurrent Relative Value of ecosystem resilience (CuRVe)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373939\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJohnathan Rothwell -  Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping\tCuRVe is a series of interconnected web-based maps that together form an atlas. It is a tool that can help users to explore how ecosystem resilience varies across Wales and understand the underlying reasons for this variation. It can be used to support and improve decision making about the use of and investment in the sustainable management of natural resources. CuRVe stands for Current Relative Value of ecosystem resilience. The Atlas is the first step in an experimental project to reveal relative values and spatial patterns of ecosystem resilience at a landscape scale for Wales.\tPublic record\n19870\tNRW_DS161321\t2024-12-05T09:58:58.182Z\tCwm Ivy National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333231\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Coastal Habitats\\Cwm Ivy NVC 2022\\WyndrushWild_CwmIvy_NVC_2022.shp\tHeather Lewis, Lead Specialist Advisor, Coastal Habitats, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team, EPP\tThe report and this GIS dataset present the results of a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey carried out in 2022 on selected areas of saltmarsh habitat and transitional vegetation at Cwm Ivy Marsh North Gower.   Cwm Ivy is a saltmarsh creation site and the vegetation survey confirms the establishment of saltmarsh over the majority of the site.\tPublic record\n17862\tNRW_DS111508\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.793Z\tAccess Maps - Promoted Local Routes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353038\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\WNAR\tJill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW\tThis dataset is one of a number of MapInfo tables that are used to deliver the Welsh National Access Register (WNAR) project.  The collation and presentation of data on a number of routes and trails throughout Wales in an easy-to-view format enables people to find out more about routes of interest and their accessibility.  The dataset was first created in 2005. It is maintained and added to regularly during the year due to changing partnership information as well as in response to annual agreement partnership checks.   This project helps to deliver the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) \u0027PE04 Enjoying the Outdoors\u0027  programme output \u0027More people from all sectors of society will regularly be using and benefiting from widespread and equitable access to countryside coast and urban green-space\u0027.  The overall aim of the project was to get more people (1% annual increase) from all sectors of society regularly participating in open-air recreation and exercise. The objectives from this program output are:  1. Increase availability of high quality recreation resource including public and other urban green-space without compromising environmental and cultural interests  2. Provide readily available targeted information about recreation opportunities and encourage greater awareness of rights and responsibility that will lead to positive behaviour changes  3. Provide a means of finding accommodation alongside the recreational opportunities in discussion with our partners.\tPublic record\n18658\tNRW_DS123248\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.398Z\tWFD Coastal Baseline\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323438\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Coastal Baseline Cycle 1.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge\t\tPrivate record\n18139\tNRW_DS115639\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.762Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Gwyrfai and the Dyfi Estuary (2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363339\tAssociated data and reports are held electronically at Maes-y-Ffynnon at;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\36 Otters in the Afon Gwyrfai \u0026amp; Dyfi estuary\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe Afon Gwyrfai and Dyfi Estuary forms part of two Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) the Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn SAC and the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau / Llyn Peninsula and the Sarnau SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to report on the current and potential distribution and breeding success of the otter within the Gwyrfai catchment and Dyfi Estuary area; identify and map actual and potential breeding habitat and feeding ponds and ditches; and to produce performance indicators to contribute to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) reporting of the condition and conservation status of the otter.\tPublic record\n18967\tNRW_DS124868\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.349Z\tWales Water Vole Survey Data 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383638\tDMS:  South West Wales Spreadsheet: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B2F3B52BD-437F-4F34-A211-AF30841ABDAC%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dSurvey%20Records%20database%20SOUTH%20WEST%20final.xlsx  South East Wales Spreadsheet: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7BB492C1C2-7793-44A4-B1AB-CDAFB061BC1F%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dVOL5420%20Survey%20Records%20SE%20Wales%20Results.xlsx  North West Wales Spreadsheet: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B1D52AED3-6E00-4411-A0C2-BC4BBD4D9B4B%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dWater%20Vole%20Survey%20Records%20North-West%20Area.xlsx  Mid East Wales Spreadsheet https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B652E603B-A969-44EC-A40F-2F2EBFD435CC%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dSurvey%20Records%20MWT%202020-09.xlsx\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian: Jonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat \u0026amp; Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tThe Water Vole is now Wales fastest declining Mammal and has been classified as \u0027Endangered\u0027 in Wales in 2020 with the species disappearing from sites where it was previously present. Surveys were carried out in 2020 to assess the presence of Water Voles in areas which had previously recorded positive and negative sightings of the Water Voles across Wales. To accompany this the presence of Mink and Mink control were also recorded as the predator is a major cause in the decline of Water Vole in Wales.   The surveys were carried out across 4 regions in Wales - North West Mid East South East and South West which were then divided into separate sites. The Data is in a spreadsheet format to accompany four separate Natural Resources Wales Evidence Report on the 2020 Water Vole Surveys for Wales.\tPublic record\n18690\tNRW_DS123316\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.092Z\tPPC Water Emission Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333136\tx:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\PPC Water Emission Points.lyr\tJulie Boswell\t\tPrivate record\n17705\tNRW_DS110029\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.680Z\tSite Condition Assessments of Welsh Standing Water Features (2003 - 2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303239\tArchived as Project No. 84, Media No\u0027s 361 and 604.\tInternal Custodian: Freshwater Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe UK Conservation Agencies have devised a Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) protocol (JNCC 2005) for the routine monitoring and assessment of protected site condition.  The CSM lake monitoring method is based on previous survey protocols used by the conservation agencies. It aims to gather the maximum amount of information relating to the ecology of each water body in the most efficient way possible.  The purpose of this data capture was to provide an assessment of the current conservation status of water features in Wales.\tPublic record\n18815\tNRW_DS123457\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.920Z\tPotential Wetlands Mapping Stage 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343537\tx:\\Resource Management\\Wetland Opportunities\\Potential Wetlands Mapping Stage1.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17485\tNRW_DS100751\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.454Z\tIntegrated Pollution Prevention Control (IPPC) Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373531\tx:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\Active IPPC Sites.lyr  x:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\IPPC Sites.lyr   Archived Dataset: Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\physical\\hydrology\\environment_agency\\industrial_sites\\ipc_sites.TAB\tGIS Service Desk Data Custodian: Susan Freeman\tIn 1996 the EU adopted a set of common rules for permitting and controlling industrial installations in the IPPC Directive. In essence the IPPC Directive was about minimising pollution from various industrial sources throughout the EU. Operators of industrial installations whose operating activities covered by Annex I of the IPPC Directive were required to obtain an environmental permit from the authorities in the EU countries.  Integrated Pollution Prevention Control (IPPC) Sites is a spatial dataset detail the point location of industrial installations that require a permit under The IPPC Directive. Please note these points do not indicate the size or area covered by the installations they represent a general location only. The system of Integrated Pollution Prevention and Control (IPPC) set out in the IPPC Directive applies an integrated environmental approach to the regulation of certain industrial activities. This means that emissions to air water (including discharges to sewer) and land plus a range of other environmental effects must be considered together as part of a bespoke mix of regulated pollution prevention. This bespoke regulation will depend on the type location and size of the installation. IPPC aims to prevent emissions and waste production and where that is not practicable reduce them to acceptable levels.\tPublic record\n17438\tNRW_DS98725\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.188Z\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) Public Rights of Way (PROW) Condition Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373235\tThere is no spatial dataset related to the condistion status, only the base dataset of PRoW created by CCW and supplmented by Local Authority data.  PRoW base data stored at x:\\Recreation and Access\\Public rights of way\\PROW.lyr\tThis metadata may not be complete for more information please contact Jont Bulbeck, Access Team Leader, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing spatial information related to the Public Rights of Way (PROW) obtained from the PROW condition survey. The core survey assessed the condition of 3283 km of rights of way within 225 randomly selected survey squares. A sufficient number of survey squares were selected to ensure a geographically representative spread and were randomly selected within highway and national park authority areas. The length of paths surveyed was calculated to provide statistically reliable and representative information about path condition within each authority area.  The purpose of this data capture was to strategically assess the physical condition of public rights of way in Wales to inform Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s now NRW\u0027s) work including policy advice and guidance to the Welsh Assembly Government and other stakeholders. This can be used for the basis for cost estimates for improving the PROW network in Wales. Condition survey data was deemed very good [a robust sampling methodology and survey and data quality control procedures were employed]. These are detailed in an associated research report.\tPublic record\n18762\tNRW_DS123374\t2024-05-28T12:55:20.703Z\tFR Peat Restoration Current Suitability\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333734\tx:\\Physical\\Peat\\FR Peat Restoration Current Suitability.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17458\tNRW_DS99623\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.506Z\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) Landholdings\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363233\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW Land holdings current.lyr  x:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW land holdings expired.lyr  x:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW land holdings proposed.lyr\tHannah Bazier, Cartographic Officer, GIS Team,NRW Ian Medcalf, GIS Officer, Estates Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset consisting of all land currently owned or purchased by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which now forms part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  The dataset holds information such as site name scale captured and unique codes amongst others. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and provide a complete table showing all land owned by CCW in Wales.\tPublic record\n17452\tNRW_DS99635\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.207Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Target Notes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363335\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Target.lyr .   The scanned target notes in pdf format are stored on Ffynnon (CCW legacy DMS - now called Content Manager)\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tDuring the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey of Wales thousands of target note and cover notes were created. Over the last 6 years these notes have all been scanned and a layer file (ARCMAP)  has been created to record the position and reference number of Phase 1 target notes.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The target notes layer was created as a way of collating all the Phase 1 terrestrial target note information for All Wales and to allow the pdf target notes to be easily accessible to all staff. Field survey spanned from 1979 to 1997 target notes were produced between 1989 and 1997 and the scanning and digitisation took place between 2004 and 2010.\tPublic record\n19148\tNRW_DS125740\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.112Z\tThe condition of the aquatic element of the grazing levels invertebrate assemblages on Nash \u0026amp; Goldcliff SSSI and Newport Wetlands SSSI on the Gwent Levels in 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373430\tData to be loaded to the WID on recorder6\tMike Howe\t\tPrivate record\n17725\tNRW_DS110125\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.488Z\tSea Empress Rocky Shore Impact Assessment/Monitoring: Permanent Transects and Rock Pool Studies\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313235\tData are held with the Marine Archive as Project No. 72 and Media No. 63 and 64 Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.. Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media063 ,  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media064\tContact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThree permanent intertidal transects in the vicinity of the Dale Fort Field Centre were established immediately following the Sea Empress oil spill. Marine communities at these sites and in rock pools at a further site were surveyed at intervals up to fourteen months post spill. Data was collected in order to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on the rocky shore.\tPublic record\n18682\tNRW_DS123319\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.538Z\tFelling Licence Applications Pre 1 April 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333139\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Felling Licences\\Felling Licence Applications Pre 1 April 1998.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n17501\tNRW_DS100202\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.735Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - SurveyArea\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323032\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Survey area.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Habitat Survey.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis spatial dataset shows the boundaries and survey dates of the 15 different survey areas adopted for reporting purposes during the lowland phase of the All Wales terrestrial Phase 1 habitat survey. The survey areas are based on the Nature Conservancy Council\u0027s 15 Areas of Search. The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level.   An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tPublic record\n18660\tNRW_DS123281\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.440Z\tDPS3 Proposal versions\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323831\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DPS3 Proposal versions.lyr\tRichard Weston\t\tPrivate record\n17626\tNRW_DS109822\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.573Z\tShellfish Stocks, Oystercatcher and Knot (bird) Modelling Data for the Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan and the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers, Phase 2 (2003-2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383232\tThe data associated with this project is archived as Project 158 (Media number 486).  A working copy is also held on the MFSG shared drive.\tInternal Custodian: Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Dee Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet are designated Special Protection Areas (SPAs) for nationally and internationally important populations of wintering wildfowl and waders.  To set conservation objections and assess the sites for Favourable Conservation Status information is required on numbers of birds using the sites and the food supplies they require to survive the winter and attain adequate body condition for Spring Migration  This is phase 2 of a project that was started in 2002-03 and uses existing and new data to parameterise an individual behaviour-based model to answer key questions relating to target setting condition monitoring and management of each of the three sites. The purpose of this data capture was to inform the setting of conservation objectives for the SPAs and assess the favourable conservation status of bird populations.\tPublic record\n18558\tNRW_DS122446\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.580Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Ecological Data held in WISKI (KiEco)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343436\tThis module sits within WISKI - KiECO\tMetadata contact: Data Services Team  Data custodians and SME’s (Subject Matter Experts) Mel Lacan (RHS), Bianca Bacchi (Marine), Paul Hyatt (Fish) and Graham Rutt (Freshwater Ecology)\tSome of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) core ecology datasets are held in the WISKI Kisters Ecology (KiEco). KiEco provides a central repository for some of NRW\u0027s ecological monitoring data. The database holdings include data on fish species River Habitat Surveys invasive species marine benthic species and habitat monitoring and freshwater ecology. KiEco was developed in NRW to take over the required functions of the legacy Environment Agency Wales (EAW) databases such as Biosys National Fish Population Database (NFPD) and Ecosys.\tPublic record\n18172\tNRW_DS115863\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.949Z\tBeach Profile Data - Swansea and Carmarthen Bay 1998- 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383633\tArchived as Project 438 Media 1477 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media1401to1500\\Media 1477)  The data can now be accessed externally via the Wales Coastal Monitoring Centre https://www.wcmc.wales/\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tThis data  was collected by the Swansea Bay \u0026amp; Carmarthen Bay coastal group part funded by NRW/legacy bodies.    It can now be accessed externally via the Wales Coastal Monitoring Centre https://www.wcmc.wales/ We should direct customers to this resource.\tPrivate record\n18433\tNRW_DS119132\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.201Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313332\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Management Catchment Cycle2.lyr\tCustodian: Robert Blacklidge, Environmental Reporting Team\tWFD Management Catchments Cycle 2 is a dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).   Management catchments are the unit of geography for which action plans are drafted in implementing the WFD. This process was conducted by using expert judgement in consultation.   WFD Management Catchments have an action plan published that relates to all waterbodies that fall within its boundaries. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive\tPublic record\n17349\tNRW_DS24274\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.677Z\tData on RIGS (GD2 Database)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323734\tThe Program is on Tony Rogers PC. And is backed up to C.Payne G:\\drive\\docs\\databases\\geology.  Command to start the program is: AREV gd/x\t\tData on Regionally Important Geological and Geomorphological Sites (RIGS).  Including information on minerals rocks and stratigraphic units.  This database was developed from one written to handle records from the National Scheme for Geological Site Documentation (NSGSD) Project in 1991. The Lithostratigraphic table has been developed from data initially provided by the British Geological Society. The main data file contains Site data including all the main descriptive and keyword data for the Site. The Long Record is the main data entry screen containing all the fields that appear on the NSGSD Site recording form.\tPrivate record\n19162\tNRW_DS125620\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.468Z\tNational Forest for Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363230\tx:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest for Wales.lyr\tStacey Delbridge, Team Leader, National Forest for Wales  Metadata Point of Contact: Stacey.delbridge@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk  07977849229\tThe National Forest for Wales team are responsible for enabling woodland site owners to become part of the National Forest for Wales network. The extent of Prospective National Forest for Wales sites will be mapped as polygon data meta data will be gathered with relevant site details in order to inform whether they meet the outcomes necessary.   The extent of ownership will be determined through discussion with the site owner and when a site comes into the National Forest for Wales scheme this will be verified using their Land registry documents.  Contacts and events will also be logged as point data so that we have a record of the extent of engagement across Wales about the National Forest including relevant funding streams that the team can signpost to.\tPrivate record\n18191\tNRW_DS116008\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.639Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: West Gwynedd 1994\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303038\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000012.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around West Gwynedd. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tPublic record\n19020\tNRW_DS125193\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.490Z\tMynydd Du Vegetation Map 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313933\tENVI format classified image at  Z:\\Archive\\Sampling Monitoring \u0026amp; Analysis Reporting\\Remote Sensing\\Mynydd_Du\\updated_map\\Mynydd Du Vegetation Map 2019.hdr and .dat\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Philip Jutson, Monitoring Analysis Team  Metadata Point of Contact:  Philip Jutson, Monitoring Analysis Team\tThe extent of land cover classes on the common lands of Mynydd Du in the western end of the Brecon Beacons National Park derived from satellite imagery.  The classes include dominant vegetation type rock outcrop and scree road and lake.  The land cover classes follow the scheme used by the  Meithrin Mynydd 1997 survey but with adjustments where some classes could not be separated.  The dataset was derived from Sentinel 2 satellite imagery from 2019 with a pixel size of 10m by 10m on the ground.  The goal was to extend vegetation mapping of the west end of Mynydd Du from 2018 carried out by NRW\u0027s Environmental Assessment Officer and so provide up-to-date vegetation mapping of upland grassland moor and mire in an area actively managed by commoners for sheep grazing in collaboration with the BBNPA.\tPublic record\n19066\tNRW_DS125390\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.489Z\tHolyhead Harbour Carpet Sea Squirt Survey Data November 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333930\tStorage area requested on the Z drive.\tInternal Data Custodian(s):  Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology\tThis is the raw data from a dive survey of Holyhead harbour in November 2020 which focussed on mapping the distribution of the invasive non-native species Carpet seasquirt Didemnum vexillum.  It contains; • Video (MP4) and image files (JPG and ARW) of underwater habitats and species taken during the surveys • Drone aerial images and videos of the current layout of the pontoons and mooring buoys (JPG and DNG and MP4). • An Excel spreadsheet of sites surveyed   • Data recording forms – PDF copies of dive log sheets and recording forms  This data accompanies; Holt R.H.F. 2018. Didemnum vexillum: survey of distribution and extent in Holyhead Harbour – November 2020. NRW Evidence report no. 538. 29 pp.\tPublic record\n18133\tNRW_DS115640\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.821Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Woodland Habitat at four SACs in North and Mid Wales (2007)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363430\tAssociated reports and data are held at Maes-y-Ffynnon at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\38 Woodland habitats at 4 SACs in North Wales\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe four Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) surveyed were Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion/Meirionnydd Oak woods and Bat Sites; Rhinog; Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt and Cwm Doethie Mynydd Mallaen. The Annex 1 habitats surveyed were old sessile oak woods with Ilex and Blechnum (Habitat 91AO); alluvial forests with Alnus glutinosa and Fraxinus excelsior (Alno-Padion Alnion incanae Salicion albae) (Habitat 91EO); Tilio-Acerion forests of slopes screes and ravines (Habitat 9180) and bog woodland (Habitat 91DO). The project was completed in two phases a survey phase and a monitoring phase. The survey phase of the contract had two main objectives: first to assess the condition of Annex I woodland habitat within individual management compartments and second to identify and map hot-spots for lichens and bryophytes. The monitoring phase had a single principal objective: to establish permanently mark and record 28 sampling plots in areas identified by CCW; these plots will form the basis of long-term monitoring on the sites.\tPublic record\n18378\tNRW_DS116792\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.310Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373932\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Groundwater Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS.\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 2 (2014-2019) is a spatial (polygon) dataset which has been created for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2 clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027…all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.   For the purposes of reporting under the WFD a groundwater body represent a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries. These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties recharge and vulnerability to pollution. This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale.\tPublic record\n18343\tNRW_DS116461\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.249Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Safe Locations for Plant\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343631\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Safe Locations for Plant.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team, Louise Harris\tSafe locations for plant machinery to be left overnight or longer periods across the Gwent levels. Machines have been known to have fuel siphoned from them and faced other vandalism so to reduce this risk of this  safe locations where machinery can be safely left when unattended and not in use have been mapped.\tPrivate record\n18626\tNRW_DS123240\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.062Z\tNon EC Bathing Waters Historic Compliance 1988 to 2014\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323430\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Non EC Bathing Waters Historic Compliance 1988 to 2014.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n17300\tEXT_DS124285\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.338Z\tTree Health Phytophthora ramorum and Phytophthora kernoviae disease data for England \u0026amp; Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323835\tStored on the Y:/ drive for use by the tree health team only.\tData Custodian: Sam Milner/Andy Wright\tTree Health Phytophthora ramorum and Phytophthora kernoviae disease data for England \u0026amp; Wales owned by Fera Science Limited; https://www.fera.co.uk/\tPublic record\n18594\tNRW_DS122765\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.853Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Chironomid Pupal Exuvial Technique (CPET)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373635\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data.\tInternal Custodian:  Harriet Robinson - Data Services Team.  Tim Jones - Enivironmental Reporting Team  SMEs Graham Rutt and Mel Lacan       Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tChironomid are an insect family that comprises of midges. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) conducts Chironomid Pupal Exuvial Technique (CPET) surveys in lakes rivers and canals as they provide a robust assessment of the anthropogenic impacts acting on large water bodies with minimal sampling effort due to the passive nature of its sampling strategy. This dataset details the Chironomid species records from the CPET sampling of lakes rivers and canals conducted by NRW across Wales.\tPrivate record\n18022\tNRW_DS114942\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.642Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Piddock Beds in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393432\tArchived as project 199 media 1350.  Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data and species data available in Marine Recorder.  Species data also available in Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. This data was captured to support a number of reporting requirements. These were namely reporting on conservation status of the piddock feature; mapping the boundary of the clay and peat exposures; to record the density of the piddock holes; and evaluate the best sampling methods.  Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae). Specific areas of interest include the peat and clay beds burrowed by piddocks and the monitoring aimed to map the boundary of the clay/peat exposures record the density of the piddock holes and evaluate the best sampling methods.\tPublic record\n17742\tNRW_DS110464\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.702Z\tCemlyn Lagoon Soft Sediment Infauna Survey (2006 - 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343634\tAll data is archived under project no. 241 and media no. 766.   A working copy is held on S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Lagoons\\Cemlyn Lagoon\tInternal Custodians - Marine monitoring Team, NRW  - Bill Sanderson, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG  Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services\tThe aim of the EC Habitats Directive 92/43/EEC is to promote the maintenance of biodiversity by requiring Member States to take measures to maintain or restore natural habitats and wild species listed on the Annexes to the Directive at a favourable conservation status introducing robust protection for those habitats and species of European importance. Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the Habitats Directive. In the UK coastal lagoons show a wide range of geographical and ecological variation; five main sub-types have been identified in the UK on the basis of their physiography as meeting the definition of the Annex I habitat type. These are graded from A to D.  - A Outstanding examples of the feature in a European context. - B Excellent examples of the feature significantly above the threshold for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) notification but of somewhat lower value than grade A sites. - C Examples of the feature which are of at least national importance (i.e. usually above the threshold for SSSI notification on terrestrial sites) but not significantly above this. These features are not the primary reason for SACs being selected. - D Features of below SSSI quality occurring on SACs These are non-qualifying features (“non-significant presence”) indicated by a letter D but this is not a formal global grade.   Cemlyn lagoon on the north coast of Anglesey north Wales is considered to be the best example of a saline coastal lagoon in Wales (Graded A) and forms part of the Cemlyn Bay SAC. The lagoon is separated from the sea by a shingle bank with a narrow channel at the western end across which a sluice system was built in the 1930s. Seawater exchange occurs mainly through the sluice and by percolation through the shingle bank although in extreme storms coinciding with spring tides waves break over the top of the shingle bank. Cemlyn lagoon supports a relatively diverse set of species several of which are specific to lagoons including the bryozoan Conopeum seurati the lagoon cockle Cerastoderma glaucum and the lagoonal mud-snail Ventrosia ventrosa. Cemlyn lagoon is also the only site in Wales where the lagoonal isopod Idotea chelipes has been recorded. A number of uncommon plant species are found within the lagoon including the brackish water-crowfoot Ranunculus baudotii and beaked tasselweed Ruppia maritima. The purpose of this data capture is to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature. This dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Cemlyn lagoon.\tPublic record\n17612\tNRW_DS107825\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.067Z\tInvertebrate Data of Selected Coastal Soft Cliff Sites in Wales (2002 - 2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037383235\t[A] Recorder 6 - Welsh Invertebrate Database Liverpool Museum coastal soft cliff survey CCW000660000000X.\tMaster Reference Data Team\tCoastal soft cliffs have been highlighted as a priority habitat under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan as a component of the Maritime Cliff and Slope.  A total of 6 Grade 1 7 Grade 2 and 47 Grade 3 species of invertebrates that are restricted to coastal soft cliffs have been identified for Wales.   A 3 year survey jointly funded by Countryside Council for Wales National Museums of Liverpool and with logistical support from the National Trust was undertaken between 2002 and 2004 to provide more detailed information on invertebrate fauna of coastal soft cliffs at selected sites throughout Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to identify current threats and management requirements for soft cliff habitats and to attempt to rank soft cliff sites on the basis of their cliff fauna across selected sites in Wales.\tPublic record\n18472\tNRW_DS119364\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.388Z\tPowys Intensive Livestock Units 2017 only\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333634\tX:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Powys intensive livestock units 2017 onwards.lyr\tRachel Jarvis - conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team\tA comprehensive dataset of the locations of intensive livestock units (poultry and pigs) in Powys operational since 2004.  This is an ongoing working dataset and will be updated periodically to reflect any new developments across Powys. For internal staff use only.  See also Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2004 2017 - NRW_DS119362 and Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2008 2017 - NRW_DS119363.\tPublic record\n17339\tNRW_DS24276\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.983Z\tCountryside Information System (CIS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323736\t\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader \u0026amp; Herpetologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.\tThe Countryside Information System (CIS) is physical and geological data based on 1 km squares. The data is at three levels. Level 1 is \"Summary Statistics\"; Level 2 is \"Land Class Data Sets\"; and Level 3 is \"Data Access Services\".  CCW is no longer a member of CS2000 Advisory Group thus is not eligible to receive free level one and level two data in electronic format.  Consortium members receive all the land cover data sets and full licences operating in perpetuity for internal use of the data.\tPrivate record\n18706\tNRW_DS123366\t2024-05-28T12:54:49.754Z\tNRW properties\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333636\tx:\\Ownership\\NRW Properties\\NRW properties.lyr\tIan Medcalf, Technical Support (4) GIS, Estates, North \u0026amp; Mid Wales Operations\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18612\tNRW_DS122590\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.797Z\tSurvey of Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) at Welsh dredge disposal sites:  Milford Haven and the Severn Estuary 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353930\tDMS\tBen Wray\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) contracted Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd. (Marine EcoSol) to analyse species and habitats surveyed in several dredge disposal sites around Wales (Milford Haven the Severn Estuary Swansea Bay Outer and Holyhead Deed) and undertake a data and literature review to discuss the potential for marine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) transfer via dredge disposal pathways. The purpose of the surveys was to obtain information to inform decision making to minimise further spread of non-native species from dredge disposal.\tPublic record\n18618\tNRW_DS123243\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.358Z\tFreshwater Fish Sample Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323433\tx:\\EU Directives\\Freshwater Fish Directive\\Freshwater Fish Sample Points.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18616\tNRW_DS123242\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.427Z\tFisheries Stretches\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323432\tx:\\EU Directives\\Freshwater Fish Directive\\Fisheries Stretches.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n17453\tNRW_DS99662\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.412Z\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Map\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363632\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Biotopes.lyr    Derived Layer: [requestors should get this information from the original layer] Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\biological\\habitats\\marine\\phase1_intertidal\\mapbiotopes.TAB.   Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media569 Data is also archived as project 204, media 569.\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environment Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThis is a static layer. This dataset contains Phase 1 level intertidal habitat and substrata information as a seamless layer of communities within the intertidal zone. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.  All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team.  Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping.  They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.   A complete validation process has been undertaken. The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted. An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under NRW licence.\tPublic record\n18707\tNRW_DS123362\t2024-05-28T12:54:49.973Z\tWISKI Open Hydrometric Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333632\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\WISKI Open Hydrometric Sites.lyr\tAlison Hanson\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17315\tEXT_DS125135\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.199Z\tLocal Environmental Records Centres (LERCS) Wales: All Species Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313335\t\tBarnaby Letheren\tThe data are collated from species datasets held by the Welsh Local Environmental Records Centres. All species are included in this dataset. If you want just priority and protected species please refer to metadata entry 111486 or Invasive non-native species please refer to metadata entry 122097.  The purpose of this data capture was to inform conservation management and decisions.  Data being received from LERC\u0027s does vary between centre. North Wales Environmental Information Centre (COFNOD) data only contains data that has been verified by trusted sources and specialists in the field. South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre (SEWBReC) West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre (WWBIC) and Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park (BIS) data contains all protected and priority records including records that have been unverified.\tPublic record\n18570\tNRW_DS121888\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.442Z\tRegulatory Woodland Officer Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383838\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Regulatory Woodland Officer Areas.lyr\tCallum Stone, Team Leader Forest Regulation and Tree Plant Health\tMap of Wales split into 6 sections (South West South East South Central Mid North West North East) with the officer responsible for each. Purpose: Staff information\tPublic record\n18086\tNRW_DS115619\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.710Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath and Blanket Bog Habitat at the Berwyn a Mynyddoedd De Clwyd / Berwyn and South Clwyd Mountains candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363139\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\23 European dry heath at Berwyn \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 S Clwyd mountains\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset presents the findings of an investigation into the condition of key habitats in selected management units within the Berwyn and South Clwyd Mountains candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC). The purpose of this data capture was to determine the extent distribution and condition of the dry heath and blanket bog habitats.\tPublic record\n17746\tNRW_DS110345\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.173Z\tWoodland Recording Cards\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333435\tHeld in box files in Jim Latham\u0027s (woodland ecologist) office.  Relocated to Woodland Tambour Unit in Old Post Room F12.\tInternal Custodian: Jim Latham, Woodland Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Includes recently found box for Carmarthen.  Long terms plan is to digitise the habitat maps into GIS, scan the field notes and attach them to the GIS polygon via a hotlink.  Additionally species information should be transcribed to spread sheet for import to corporate biological recording database.  Could be let under data digitising \u0026amp; scanning  call-off contract when one is put in place\tApproximately 3000 recording cards from woodland surveys in Wales. Some are from structured surveys e.g. the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) surveys of woodland Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) whilst others are ad-hoc recording events. The records can include site name grid references aspect slope altitude area species lists height cover age class abundance etc. The purpose of this data capture was to gather compositional and structural information about woodlands; to gain an understanding of their species diversity and conservation importance; to influence site designation and to identify future research and survey needs.\tPublic record\n17803\tNRW_DS111141\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.952Z\tBenthic Ecology of Carmarthen Bay (1998)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313431\t[A]  Paper report is available from CCW library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above).  [B]  Report and data are held within the Marine Archive as Media No. 221 [File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 221) [C]  Sediment analysis data on CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW30000000004).\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe 1979 European Commission Directive (79/409 EEC) on the Conservation of Wild Birds requires Member States to notify the Commission of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) that are of particular importance to the conservation of wild birds.   For some time there had been an increasing body of evidence that Carmarthen Bay was an important site in Britain for wintering common scoters (Melanitta nigra) one of the species protected by the ‘Birds Directive’. This project was stimulated by concern for the Carmarthen Bay macrofaunal communities on which the wintering population of the Common scoter depend following the Sea Empress oil spill of February 1996.   This dataset contains the results of a project on the benthic ecology of Carmarthen Bay undertaken by Andrew Woolmer as part of his PhD to investigate the benthos and Scoter populations of Carmarthen Bay.   The purpose of this data capture was to describe quantitatively the distribution of the macrofaunal communities in Carmarthen Bay to provide accurate baseline data and to assess the distribution and density of potential prey species of Common scoter in an internationally important over- wintering site.   The project comprised of four main areas of study:  1) Macrofaunal communities of Carmarthen Bay  2) Growth abundance and spatial models of the Tellinacean bivalve Fabulina fibula  3) Macrofaunal response groups: a multivariate method for isolating indicator species along environmental gradients  4) Temporal variability structural redundance and the long term persistence of the Carmarthen Bay Tellina Community\tPublic record\n17247\tEXT_DS123462\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.859Z\tOffice of National Statistics (ONS): Medium super output areas 2011 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343632\tx:\\Social Economic\\Office of National Statistics\\Census 2011\\Medium super output areas 2011.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17314\tEXT_DS125322\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.117Z\tWelsh Government: Peatlands of Wales Maps (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333232\tX drive at: X:\\Physical\\Peat Peatlands of Wales  Peatlands of Wales Thickness Peatlands of Wales Evidence Score Peatlands of Wales Emissions Peatlands of Wales Carbon Stock\tEsther Clews\tThe Peatlands of Wales Map series provides an updated distribution of Welsh Peatlands (to 2022) based on current evidence sources. The data layers were created on a 50m grid whereby the presence and thickness of peat are elucidated from a range of sources for each 50m grid cell across Wales. A peatland evidence score defines the level of confidence in the presence of peat in any given grid cell with those cells scoring more than 2 on this scale of 1-10 captured in the ‘Peatlands of Wales’ peat distribution map.  Full details of the sources of data used to build this map as well as the methodologies used to define the level of evidence for:   - the presence of peat - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_scg8 - the derivation of the peat evidence score - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_evidence_2nss  - estimates of peat thickness (depth) - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_thickness  - carbon stock estimates - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_carbon_stock - greenhouse gas emissions - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_emmisions   These are presented in the Peatlands of Wales mapping methodology report.\tPublic record\n18917\tNRW_DS124850\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.303Z\tFCW Woodland Officer Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383530\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\FCW Woodland Officer Areas.lyr\tSuzanne Lilley-Owen, Permitting Team Leader Forestry, Permitting Services (Forestry)\tMap of Wales split into 8 sections each section covers the area of responsibility for each of the Regulatory Woodland Officers. The attributes are the name phone numbers email address for the area covered. Purpose: Staff information\tPrivate record\n18214\tNRW_DS116262\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.491Z\tGroundwater Vulnerability (1990 - 2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323632\tThis dataset is not available on the X drive.  But is stored in NRW\u0027s Geodatabases as a reference or archived dataset. this data can be put on the X Drive if needed, but more appropriate 2018 data is available on the X Drive. Feature Dataset: NRWGDBV.GISDATA.Designations Feature Class: NRWGDBV.GISDATA.GW_VULNERABILITY_ZONE Feature Class: NRWGDBV.GISDATA.GW_VULNERABILITY_ZONE_DRIFT\tCustodian: Dave Jones, Technical Specialist (Groundwater), Geoscience/Knowledge Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, NRW  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset of Groundwater Vulnerability between 1990 and 2008 residing as a reference or archived dataset in Natural Resources Wales\u0027 geodatabases. A newer version of this data is now available. This older 1990 - 2008 data consists of two polygon spatial layers available at a scale of 1:100000.  These are Groundwater Vulnerability Zones and Groundwater Vulnerability Zones Drift. The data broadly define areas relevant to the protection of groundwater.  The approach considers the vulnerability of the groundwater resources as a whole; and the specific importance of areas which for the catchments main sources of supply. Groundwater resources are assigned a vulnerability class [Groundwater Vulnerability 100K] based on soil type and the underlying geology only (e.g. depth to groundwater is not considered): -  Variably permeable groundwater with low leaching potential -  Variably permeable groundwater with intermediate leaching potential -  Variably permeable groundwater high leaching potential -  Highly permeable groundwater with intermediate leaching potential -  Highly permeable groundwater with high leaching potential -  Highly permeable groundwater with low leaching potential The Groundwater Vulnerability data is intended to be used to indicate where groundwater resources may be vulnerable from activities carried out on the surface land.  Other information such as depth of groundwater and thickness and type of overlying cover will always be required for a site-specific assessment. An assessment of the vulnerability of groundwaters to diffuse pollution is also included as the Groundwater Vulnerability Drift 100K spatial layer (\u0027Drift\u0027 is transported rock debris overlying the solid bedrock) - it shows the distribution of low permeability drift deposits and should be used in conjunction with Groundwater Vulnerability 100K.\tPublic record\n19013\tNRW_DS125264\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.727Z\tBenthic Core Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323634\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Benthic Cores\\Nyfer 2021 Benthic Quadrats.lyr  X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Benthic Cores\\Teifi 2021 Benthic Quadrats.lyr\tKathrynBorrowdale\tWater Framework Directive benthic core data. This data shows the positions of the cores that were taken. Species and partical size information for the cores are available on KiECO.\tPrivate record\n18921\tNRW_DS124839\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.571Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Risk Assessments for Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383339\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Risk Assessments\\2019\tInternal Data Custodian(s): David Allen, Monitoring Analysis Team Leader, NRW Metadata Point of Contact: David Allen, Monitoring Analysis Team Leader, NRW\tWe have reviewed water quality data and information on the types and magnitude of pressures affecting water bodies across Walest to assess how susceptible they are to those pressures and in particular estimate the likelihood of water bodies failing to meet their environmental quality objectives in the future or deteriorating from their current condition. The methodology for each risk assessment was tailored to the specific pressure but in general it was an assessment of the scale of the pressure and the sensitivity of the water body.   The risk assessments are available as maps for each individual pressure including: chemicals and metals dissolved inorganic nitrogen phosphates dissolved oxygen and ammonia faecal indicator organisms acidification abstraction and flow invasive non-native species and sediment. They are valid until 2027. We will use the risk assessments to identify where more data is and prioritise our monitoring strategy and support the development of local and national programmes of measures including for pressures where Water Framework Directive classification data is missing.\tPrivate record\n17506\tNRW_DS99835\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.850Z\tNatural Resources Wales Land Management Agreement Access\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939383335\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement access proposed.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement access current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement access expired.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Hannah Brazier or Pippa Jones, Cartographers, GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW  Metadata contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all current proposed and expired agreement Access Routes and includes information on site name owner scale captured expiry date unique codes. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements. and provide a complete table showing all agreement access routes in Wales. High (with less confidence in some pre 2000 data).\tPublic record\n18947\tNRW_DS124913\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.229Z\tTranquillity and Place: Dark Skies\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393133\tX:\\Physical\\Light Pollution\\Night lights.lyr  Data also includes a set of maps produced in JPEG format and an Excel spreadsheet containing the zonal statistics analysis.\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor, Landscape\tDark skies can affect our experience of nature landscapes and greenspaces and can benefit our health and wellbeing and local wildlife.   The Dark Sky and Light Pollution Dataset for Wales has been created to identify the strategic and local resource in remote rural peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent practice and provision for benefits to benefits to wellbeing.    The dataset is based upon satellite imagery taken at 1:30 A.M in December 2019 by the United States National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) Suomi National Polar-orbiting satellite with the Visible Infrared Imaging Radiometer Suite (VIIRS) capturing visible and infrared imagery used in this dataset and later processed in 2021.    A set of maps have been created to display the dark skies data against the following geographical boundaries:   - Local Authority areas   - Future Wales boundaries    - Designated landscapes (AONBs and National Parks)   - NRW Operational Areas   - National Landscape Character Areas   - LANDMAP Visual \u0026amp; Sensory aspect areas\tPublic record\n17888\tNRW_DS112405\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.597Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal species diversity layer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343035\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the intertidal range is measured using species richness average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is used to present these metrics. The purpose of this data capture was to establish intertidal diversity from known data of the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tPublic record\n17827\tNRW_DS111189\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.409Z\tSurveys of Harbours, Rias and Estuaries in Southern Britain - Milford Haven and Daugleddau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313839\t[A] Paper reports (volumes 1 and 2) are available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. [B] Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000246). Available on All Wales Snapshot.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: JNCC    Metadata Contact:Master reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Nature Conservancy Council commissioned the Field Studies Council to undertake studies of the marine inlets of southern Britain including harbours rias and estuaries in order to gain information on the ecosystems present. The study took place between 1985 and 1988 and covered 24 inlets. This dataset contains the results of a survey of Milford Haven and the estuary of the Rivers Cleddau (Daugleddau estuary). The purpose of this dataset was to describe areas in terms of their physical attributes; to review the results of previous studies; to review marine resource usage e.g. fisheries diving activites etc; to catalogue available information and to collect information and photographs on the habitats and species present. The field survey specifically aimed to collect further information on habitats and species where little or no previous work had been carried out in areas of known or likely nature conservation importance or where rare or unusual species may be present.\tPublic record\n18942\tNRW_DS124808\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.856Z\tNRW Industry Regulation Teams\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383038\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Industry Regulation Teams.lyr\tSophie Lewis, Team Member, Customer Management/Customer Hub\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Industry regulation Teams post Organisation Design July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tPrivate record\n19069\tNRW_DS125391\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.791Z\tSurvey of the caddisfly Limnephilus tauricus at Cors Erddreiniog National Nature Reserve (NNR) in 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333931\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID) – Natur Am Byth Surveys.\tMike Howe\tA survey of Cors Erddreiniog National Nature Reserve (NNR) was carried out in order to update knowledge of the status and distribution of the very rare caddisfly Limnephilus tauricus – a species known only from this reserve in Wales. The survey also aimed to locate the even rarer caddisfly Limnephilus pati which hasn’t been previously recorded at the reserve (or in Wales) but is present in similar habitats within the same flight period.\tPublic record\n18132\tNRW_DS115638\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.829Z\tFreshwater Macrophytes in Nant Teyrn - Freshwater Environmental Change Protocol (2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363338\tAssociated report and data is held at Maes-Y-Ffynnon at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\34 Freshwater Macrophytes in Nant Teyrn - Freshwater Environmental Change Protocol\tInternal Custodian:  Victoria Bowmaker, ECN Project Officer, NRW Alex Turner, ECN Project Officer, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tScott Wilson was commissioned to undertake a macrophyte survey of Nant Teyrn in Snowdonia National Park. The aim of this survey was to provide macrophyte monitoring data on a 100m stretch of Nant Teyrn within the Snowdonia Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site. The following elements were recorded: ·  Macrophyte species present and their relative abundance with reference to the check list of aquatic macrophytes used in ECN. Macrophytes include any plant observable by the naked eye and nearly always identifiable when observed.This includes all aquatic vascular plants and bryophytes together with groups of algae which can be seen to be composed predominantly of a single species ·  Species distribution along a river length and zonation at selected sample points. ·  Physical characteristics of the sampling location\tPublic record\n18699\tNRW_DS123306\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.410Z\tOil spill booming plans\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333036\tx:\\Incidents\\Oil Spills\\Oil spill booming plans.lyr\tAndrew Hill\t\tPrivate record\n18225\tNRW_DS116238\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.309Z\tFlood Warning Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323338\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Warning Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Andy Wall, Flood Risk Manager, NRW  Originators: EAW, NRW (via EA flood warning system as part of managed service)\tThis spatial Flood Warning Areas dataset are geographical areas where NRW expect flooding to occur and where NRW provide a Flood Warning Service.   Flood Warning Areas generally contain properties that are expected to flood from rivers or the sea. Specifically Flood Warning Areas define locations within the Flood Warning Service Limit that represent a discrete community at risk of flooding. A discrete community is a recognised and named geographical community which can be an urban area a significant suburb of a large city or a village or a hamlet.   The purpose of Flood Warnings is to alert people that flooding is expected and they should take action to protect themselves and their property.   An online viewable version of this dataset is available here https://naturalresources.wales/our-evidence-and-reports/maps/flood-risk-map/?lang\u003den\tPublic record\n18421\tNRW_DS119289\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.414Z\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) Sewage Treatment Works\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323839\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTD Sewage Treatment Works.lyr\tMark Squire, Team Leader Water Policy \u0026amp; Directives Implementation\tSpatial dataset containing wastewater treatment plants monitored and reported under the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) in Wales. These plants serve population equivalent (PE) greater than 2000 if discharging to freshwater or greater than 10000 if discharging to coastal/transitional waters. Details include load treatment types and performance.\tPublic record\n17319\tEXT_DS125406\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.254Z\tSevern Lower Seagrass Project - Seagrass Aerial Imagery Test 2022 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343036\t\tAdam Leyshon\tAerial imagery collected by Project Seagrass using the WingtraOne VTOL drone. Imagery gathered as a test to determine feasibility of gathering seagrass extent data.  Dataset – aerial imagery in the form of Raster files destined for ARC GIS.\tPublic record\n18224\tNRW_DS116252\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.353Z\tSaltmarsh Extents\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323532\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Saltmarsh extents.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Bianca Bacchi- Rhymney and Ebbw A\u0026amp;R Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Services Team\tPolygon data layer showing the extent of Saltmarsh in Coastal and Transitional waters for use by both Flood and Coastal Risk Management and the implementation of Water Framework Directive. At the seaward end of the transect the final demarcation will be where the saltmarsh vegetation cover has become so sparse it only covers 5% whether it is Upper Mid Lower or Pioneer saltmarsh.\tPublic record\n18683\tNRW_DS123324\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.545Z\tProtected Species for Permitting Screening\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333234\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\Protected Species for Permitting Screening.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\Screening protected species.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18199\tNRW_DS116225\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.649Z\tSpatial Flood Defences with Standardised Attributes (archived data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323235\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Defences with standardised attributes.lyr  Stored in AMX but made available on the Y drive\tData Custodian: Mark Pugh- Reservoir Safety Team Metadata Contact - Data Services Team\tThis dataset has been superseded by the dataset \"Flood Defence Structures\" (NRW_DS125266).  This dataset shows flood defences that have been built to protect against flooding from rivers and the sea. The defences shown provide different levels of flood protection and this is recognised in the risk classification shown in the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map.   Flood defences that are not yet shown and the areas that benefit from them will be gradually added.    Flood defences do not remove the risk of flooding however and can be overtopped or fail in extreme weather conditions.\tPublic record\n19084\tNRW_DS125380\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.143Z\tDerelict Land Habitat \u0026amp; Species Survey 1979 (paper data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333830\toff-site storage box ref CCW/NRM/R-52\tOpenData@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tSurvey Site reports for SJ grid ref area and Anglesey\tPrivate record\n17754\tNRW_DS110289\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.369Z\tNewborough Warren and Newborough Forest maps\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323839\tArchived as Project 159, Media 488 and Media 396\tMike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge and Advice - NRW (Used to be Gordon Bell / James Dargie)\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC) commissioned a survey of Newborough Warren and forest culminating in the production of high quality maps.\tPublic record\n17274\tEXT_DS124723\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.236Z\tWelsh Government: Multibeam, Drop-Down Video survey and Habitat Maps from St David’s Head, Pembrokeshire and West coast of Anglesey (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373233\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\EMFF SMNR Spatial Approach Data from WG\tContact: Internal:  Lee Murrary Marine Mapping Analyst\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Welsh Government. Natural Resources Wales has access to this data through an Open Government Licence. This dataset is made up of three parts: Multibeam Survey data detailing the topography of the seafloor; drop down video surveys taken at point of interest within the survey extent and broad habitat maps detailing the extent and location of habitats based on the findings from the analysis of the drop-down videos and multibeam data. This project has received funding through the European Maritime and Fisheries Fund which is funded by the European Union and the Welsh Government.\tPublic record\n18420\tNRW_DS119266\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.431Z\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Area - Shellfish Waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323636\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTR Sensitive Areas Shellfish Waters.lyr\tGeraint Weber - Team Leader Water Quality\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017 (WFD Regulations 2017) requires specification of protected areas for those areas designated as nutrient sensitive areas.     UWWTR Sensitive Areas Shellfish Waters layer has been renamed from the UWWTD Sensitive Areas Shellfish Waters and updated to only show areas which are designated as Shellfish Waters under the WFD Regulations following Welsh Government review.    This is a spatial dataset showing the extent of the Urban Wastewater Treatment Directive (UWWTD) sensitive areas (Shellfish Water) in Wales.   The Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) (91/271/EEC) is designed to reduce the pollution of freshwater estuarine and coastal waters by domestic sewage and industrial wastewater collectively known as urban waste water. These water have been identified as a sensitive because further treatment than secondary or equivalent treatment is necessary to meet other EC Directives such as the Water Framework Directive.\tPublic record\n17061\tEXT_DS100802\t2024-05-28T12:10:09.723Z\tOS Open Data: Watsonian Vice County Boundaries (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383032\tX:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Watsonian Vice County Boundaries.lyr   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\political\\boundaries\\vice_counties\\watsonian_vcb_polygon.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the boundaries of the  Watsonian Vice Counties across Wales. This is a 3rd Party dataset supplied to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under an OS Open Data Licence.\tPublic record\n19015\tNRW_DS125257\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.726Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Opportunity Catchments Layer Final 2021 - Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323537\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Opportunity Catchments Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Helen Haider, SMNR Advisor (Water), NRW\tThe Opportunity Catchment layer shows the boundaries and locations of Wales’ ten Opportunity Catchments.  The Opportunity Catchments form part of the third cycle River Basin Management Plans (2021-2027).  The layer shows the ten Opportunity Catchments in Wales.\tPublic record\n18425\tNRW_DS119263\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.611Z\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Areas (SPA) Monitoring Programme Results 2013-2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323633\tDMS\tNRW Monitoring and Surveillance Advisor: Daniel Guest. NRW Environmental Surveillance Officer: Dylan Lloyd.\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs) are protected and designated sites under the EC Habitats and Birds Directives. The Directives list habitat types and species that are considered to be most in need of conservation at a European level. An obligation for this is a programme of species and habitat monitoring across the site.   This dataset shows the results of NRWs\u0027 feature condition monitoring programme on the SACs and SPAs in Wales and contains the base data used to populate one of NRWs\u0027 corporate performance indicators. The data is updated from the active condition monitoring programme on an annual basis.    The publication of indicative condition assessments for marine features in Wales in January 2018 supersedes the information for marine features currently in this data layer. The indicative condition assessments are NRWs formal advice on condition of its European marine sites and should be used. Information on the assessments and the reports themselves can be downloaded from the following link:  https://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/environmental-topics/wildlife-and-biodiversity/find-protected-areas-of-land-and-seas/indicative-feature-condition-assessments-for-european-marine-sites-ems/?lang\u003den  Subsequent updates of the SAC and SPA table may provide updated assessments for a few marine features and as such any marine feature assessment dated after May 2017 should be considered to take precedence.\tPublic record\n18248\tNRW_DS116273\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.789Z\tRisk of Flooding from Surface Water\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323733\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Surface Water\\Surface Water Flood Risk 30.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Surface Water\\Surface Water Flood Risk 100.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Surface Water\\Surface Water Flood Risk 1000.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW       Data will not be added to Lle as the packaged data is too large and complex, will remain as a ShareFile download\tThe surface water flood maps give an indication of the broad areas likely to be at risk of surface water flooding.  This includes flooding that takes place from the surface runoff generated by rainwater (including snow and other precipitation) that: (a) is on the surface of the ground (whether or not it is moving) and (b) has not yet entered a watercourse drainage system or public sewer.  The Flood Map for Surface Water pick out natural drainage channels rivers low areas in floodplains and flow paths between buildings. But it does not indicate flooding caused by local rainfall.  It does not show flooding that occurs from overflowing watercourses drainage systems or public sewers caused by catchment-wide rainfall events or river flow.  A national model has been run for 1 in 30 1 in 100 and 1 in 1000 year rainfall events.  It has been modelled on a 2 metre square grid.  Lead Local Flood Authorities were consulted and where available locally held model outputs have been incorporated into the maps.  This dataset has been superseded by the new National Flood Hazard Maps 2019 - NRW_DS124790\tPublic record\n17750\tNRW_DS110333\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.330Z\tBiotope studies on rocky shores following the Sea Empress oil spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333333\t[A] Archived as project no 105, media no 379 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 379) [B] Report available from NRW library\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the results of a series of broadscale studies on rocky sea shores around the open coast of South Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996. The dataset includes maps and diagrams of biotopes list of regional biotopes referred to in survey and target notes. The purpose of this data capture was to help assess the effects of oil on shore biotopes communities and species over whole shore areas to provide information on biotopes habitat communities or species which will assist in the development of future monitoring or surveillance strategies and to investigate the role of Phase 1 studies in surveillance and monitoring of rocky intertidal areas. Surveys were concentrated on the open coast of South Pembrokeshire from West Angle Bay to Manorbier. All survey carried out by experienced surveyors.\tPublic record\n17520\tNRW_DS101857\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.745Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of sponge assemblages\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383537\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/sponges ,  Digital images - Dell1590 C/Documents and settings/monitoring/sponges ,   Species data held in Marine Recorder Surveys (MRCCW30900000005 / MRCCW16600000002)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  The sponge communities at Skomer MNR have been identified as rich and diverse with over 80 species. Six are nationally scarce species and eight near the limit of their distribution. Sponges are filter feeders and therefore reliant on water quality which makes them susceptible to changes in sediment deposition. They are therefore useful biotic indicators of changes in suspended sediment and surface sedimentation rates the cause of which might include dredge spoil dumping. The purpose of this data capture was to: - monitor the sponge assemblages in the MNR;  - identify natural and anthropogenic fluctuations  - identify the presence of rare scarce and edge of range species in the MNR.\tPublic record\n18862\tNRW_DS124756\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.814Z\tAssessment of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) Landscape Rhos Glyn-yr-helyg (2019)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373536\tRecorder 6 - WID\tMike Howe\tMetadata to be supplied (data will be added to the WID in Recorder 6) *************GIS layer may be supplied by Butterfly conservation for updated map of Wales - this would be new third party data********** and could potentially replace or add to data held under X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Invertebrates\\Insects\tPrivate record\n19091\tNRW_DS125502\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.857Z\tGathering Twig Lichen Data for Powys SSSIs to Investigate Ammonia Levels: Second Phase\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353032\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2FLichen%20surveys%20to%20investigate%20ammonia%20impacts\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Sam.Bosanquet@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tTwig Lichen Surveys on 9 SSSI in Powys showing that these sites are suffering significant ammonia pollution except in a few upland areas.\tPublic record\n17708\tNRW_DS110223\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.518Z\tGeneral Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Invertebrate Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323233\tRhian Thomas, CCW\u0027s Rivers Ecologist holds a copy.\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, CCW) Nick Davies, Environment Agency\tThe GQA River Biology dataset (see separate metadata entry) gives stretches of rivers a grade from A-F depending on the number of invertebrate species caught. This dataset is a more extensive version containing the names of the invertebrates caught for the Rivers Usk and Wye.  This data was captured as part of the Routine Environment Agency England and Wales General Quality Assessment monitoring programme.\tPrivate record\n19129\tNRW_DS125562\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.940Z\tLichen survey of parts of Gwydyr Forest, Glyn Lledr, Gwynedd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353632\tStored in the DMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FPAWS%20Glyn%20Lledr\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tA survey of epiphytic lichens in seven areas of the Gwydyr Forest along the Glyn Lledr valley including recommendations for forest management.\tPublic record\n18670\tNRW_DS123284\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.328Z\tFlood Defence Mapping Survey Reach Markers\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323834\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Defence Mapping Survey Reach Markers.lyr\tWyn Davies\t\tPrivate record\n19158\tNRW_DS125727\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.465Z\tBontddu Hydropower bryophyte monitoring 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373237\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2FBryophyte%20Hydropower%20Monitoring\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tMonitoring of bryophytes within plots set up before the operation of the Bontddu Hydropower commenced 10 years ago and following an initial monitoring round 3 years after operation commenced\tPrivate record\n17461\tNRW_DS99437\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.807Z\tPaper Air Photo Index\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343337\tX:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Paper air photos in NRW index.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team - Hannah Brazier\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the centre point of each paper air photo taken for the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) between 1991 and 2002. This data is now managed and held by Natural Resources Wales.\tPublic record\n18817\tNRW_DS123954\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.745Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) meteorological data 2017 to 2023 (Morfa Harlech, Merthyr Mawr, Whiteford)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233393534\tData is available on DMS here https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20%20Data/Wind%20data/Wind%20Data%202017-2023?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1   An NRW Evidence report is in prep (as of May 2023) to describe this dataset, link to be added when available.\tInternal Custodian: Iain Fairley, Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Science, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems\tMeteorological data collected at Morfa Harlech Merthyr Mawr and Whiteford from February 2017 to February 2023. The data provides wind speed and direction temperature rain and pressure at 15 minute intervals.\tPublic record\n18492\tNRW_DS121511\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.044Z\tWater Framework Directive - Cycle 2 Target Waterbodies Lakes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353131\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target Lakes Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers; Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes wabter bodies\tPublic record\n18348\tNRW_DS116463\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.340Z\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Trash Screens\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343633\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Trash Screens.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tTrash Screens on watercourses within CWL IDD digitised as point features and mapped for the purpose of flood defence and asset management. Trash screens are a type of fencing used to filter out debris in the path of a waterway. They help keep watercourses free of debris and unwanted elements.\tPrivate record\n17900\tNRW_DS112225\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.566Z\tBenthic Habitats: Maps of Sensitivity to Fishing Gears (Intertidal and Subtidal)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323235\tLegacy CCW Archive (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Users\\Sue Hearn\\\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team (Was Dr Clare Eno, Senior Fisheries Policy Officer, Envrionmental Policy Group, NRW)\tThis dataset consists of intertidal and subtidal maps of the sensitivity of benthic fisheries habitats to fishing gear types within the Welsh Territorial Seas (high water to 12 nm offshore).  Intertidal biotope mapping was carried out in order to determine the distribution and extent of seashore habitats biological communities and species on the shores of Wales. Subtidal biotopes were predicted in HABMAP in order to act as a guide for marine scientists. The biotopes were converted to fisheries habitats in order to provide a means of assessing the sensitivity of benthic habitats to the effects of fishing gear types. The sensitivity of each fisheries habitat to each fishing gear and access was determined in order to provide a basis for management advice and fisheries regulation.  The intertidal area consists of the whole of the Welsh coastline between the limits of the tidal rise and fall. The subtidal area consists of Welsh water out to 12 nm offshore which are within the Southern Irish Sea i.e. sections around the Severn in the south and the Dee in the north are not included. HABMAP modelling has been re-run to include these areas in the south and north and these biotopes are due to be included in the fisheries habitats maps in 2010.\tPublic record\n18937\tNRW_DS124827\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.886Z\tSaltmarsh Transect and Quadrat points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383237\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh\tKate Borrowdale Swyddog Asesu Amgylcheddol Morol / Marine Environmental Assessment Officer Tîm Asesu ac Adrodd Monitro Morol / Marine Monitoring Assessment and Reporting Team\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. The points in these layers show the location of the saltmarsh transects (landward seaward and zone transitions) and saltmarsh quadrats and also additional species (if spatial data was recorded).\tPrivate record\n18046\tNRW_DS115217\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.266Z\tLlyn Tegid and Llyn Arenig Fawr hydroacoustic and Gwyniad surveys 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323137\t[A]  Data is archived as project 389, media 1405.  [B]  Species data is held in Recorder 6: - Gwyniad Hydroaccoustic Survey of Llyn Tegid 2012 - CCW Science Report 1012  CCW000800000000G  - Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill Netting Survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2012- CCW Science Report 1013 CCW000800000000I.\t\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains the results of a project investigating the environmental variables. Attemps to translocate a population to Llyn Arenig Fawr have been attempted since 2003. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the status of gwyniad and its habitat in Llyn Tegid (native population) and Llyn Arenig Fawr (introduced population).\tPublic record\n18674\tNRW_DS123286\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.511Z\tFluvial Max scores\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323836\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2017.lyr\tMark Pugh\t\tPrivate record\n18321\tNRW_DS116344\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.220Z\tFreshwater Gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) Species Survey Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333434\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Species data is held in Recorder 6. -  Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill netting survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2009 - CCW Science Report 904 CCW000800000000D    -  Gwyniad Hydroacoustic Survey 2003 - 2009 - CCW Science Report 903 - Countryside Council for Wales CCW000800000000C    -  Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill Netting Survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2012- CCW Science Report 1013 CCW000800000000I     -  Gwyniad Gill netting survey of Llyn Tegid 2003 - CCW Science Report 597 CCW000800000000E\tTristan Hatton-Ellis, Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains species data from the results of projects established to carry out translocation hydroacoustic surveys and measurement of environmental variables. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas. The purpose of this survey work and collation was to develop a generic whitefish sampling protocol suitable for Llyn Tegid and to produce a condition assessment of the population. Data is collected in Llyn Tegid and Llyn Arenig Fawr. All hydroacoustic records are available for site centroid only. Gill netting results are recorded at the site of the cast where information was available.\tPublic record\n18524\tNRW_DS121654\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.485Z\tGas Pipelines on Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Land\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363534\tx:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Gas Pipelines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains gas pipelines on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured overhead telephone line information.\tPublic record\n17781\tNRW_DS110493\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.233Z\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of Eryri\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343933\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\t\tPrivate record\n18537\tNRW_DS121810\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.051Z\tWINS ONS Broad Habitats - Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383130\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\ONS Broad habitats\\ONS Broad habitats.lyr   ONS Broad Habitat report and statistics spreadsheet stored on the DMS. (https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/nrm/enpb/Project%20Charters/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fnrm%2Fenpb%2FProject%20Charters%2FA14%20%2D%20Natural%20Resource%20Evidence%20Framework%2FSCCAN%2FONS%20Broad%20Habitats\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000267983946555B041947C4D4E8269E2AF\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BB4208ECF%2D3BE5%2D4868%2DAA0B%2DE40E8633D58A%7D)\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk\tThis map shows the broad habitats throughout Wales based on the Office of National Statistics broad habitats classes. It has been created as part of the project GIS for Area Statements (GISfAS) to support land cover ecosystem accounts by providing information on current economic value and change in land cover. GISfAS is part of a suite of projects also including SoNaRR (State of National Resources Reporting) and SAfAS (Spatial Analysis for Area Statements) all of which are facilitating management of natural resources and ecosystem services within Wales.\tPublic record\n17728\tNRW_DS110145\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.554Z\tGreater Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) survey of the Castlemartin bat cave following the grounding of the Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313435\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media369\tWest Region Senior Reserve Manager, NRW (The Senior Reserve Managers in NRW include Graham Williams, Hywel Roberts, Emyr Humphreys, Huw Green, Rhodri Dafydd)\tThe Castlemartin coast holds the most important hibernacula for Greater Horseshoe Bats Rhinolophus ferrumequinum in Wales. Following the oil spill from the Sea Empress oil was dispersed along and deposited on the Castlemartin coast and concerns were raised about the contamination of Castlemartin bat cave. This dataset contains the results of surveys carried out to check for the presence of oil and bats at the cave. The purpose of this data capture was to determine whether oil pollution from the Sea Empress was adversely affecting the bats.\tPublic record\n18739\tNRW_DS123394\t2024-05-28T12:55:19.565Z\tPenrhyn Gwyn Carreg Ddu scar Bounding Box\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333934\tx:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Fishery casework\\Penrhyn_Gwyn_Carreg_Ddu_scar_Bounding_Box.lyr\tPaul Brazier\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17278\tEXT_DS124752\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.366Z\tJNCC: Marine Habitat EUNIS level 3 combined Map (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373532\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Broadscale habitats\\JNCC combined map EUNIS level 3.lyr   This is a third party dataset, the data is downloadable from the JNCC website.\tNatasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tIn some cases a full-coverage map displaying the best available data everywhere at the expense of consistency is required. The production of such a product showing EUNIS level 3 habitats requires integrating EUNIS level 3 seabed habitat data from fine and broad-scale habitat maps. The product aims to create a complete map that presents the best available information on the distribution of EUNIS level 3 habitats at any locations in UK waters. This data product is required for among other things assessments of progress towards networks of marine protected areas (MPAs) and marine spatial planning in the UK. The aim was to produce a single map layer displaying the best quality EUNIS level 3 data for any given location. Furthermore the process for producing the layer needed to be:  • Repeatable;  • Transparent; • Easy to explain and understand;  • Objective;  • Fully documented;  • Appropriate for EUNIS level 3 habitats; and  • Appropriate for the UK intertidal and subtidal areas   Any location would show only a single value describing the habitat at EUNIS level 3 i.e. no overlapping polygons leading to multiple possible values at a location. The data at any location should be the best available to describe the EUNIS level 3 habitat type (i.e. the most likely to be correct). EUNIS level 3 habitats (Appendix 1) describe physical habitats classified using biologically meaningful parameters substrate type and additionally for rock: energy and biological zone. Therefore a method was created to choose data based on their ability to describe these physical variables.\tPublic record\n18109\tNRW_DS115571\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.961Z\tBangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Cardigan Bay, West of Dyfi (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353731\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466) Species data held in Marine Recorder (Survey key:MRCCW31600000015)\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis dataset consists of the results of a surveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in June 2012 as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (3 tows) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was approximately 25 km offshore west of Dyfi.  The tow lengths ranged from 500m to 750m and seabed consisted primarily of sands gravels and cobble/boulder reef.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging. The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Cardigan Bay and assign biotopes.\tPublic record\n17220\tEXT_DS122167\t2024-05-28T12:17:03.811Z\tLocal Authority: Cycling and Walking Routes (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313637\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Denbigh Conwy Mountain Bike Routes.lyr  x:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Denbigh Cycle Routes.lyr  x:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Monmouth Cycle Routes.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis data collation is jointly owned by the Denbighshire County Council Conwy County Borough Council and Monmouthshire County Council and was provided to Natural Resources Wales for use. It details the extent and location of of cycling routes around Denbigh and Monmouth.\tPublic record\n18315\tNRW_DS116345\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.966Z\tFreshwater River lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis), Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri) and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) Survey Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333435\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Species data held in Recorder 6: - Lamprey Monitoring, Wye and Usk Catchments 2005 - CCW - Report no. 41 CCW0002800000007 - Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau Catchments 2004 - CCW - RoC Report no. 7 CCW0002800000008 - Lamprey Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2004 - CCW - RoC Report No. 18 CCW0002800000009 - Lamprey Habitat Assessment in the River Usk 1998 - CCW - Report No. 365 CCW000280000000C - Lamprey Monitoring, River Usk 2011 - CCW – Science Report No. 11/8/6 CCW000800000000M - Lamprey Monitoring, Cleddau Catchments 2012 - CCW - CCW Staff Science Report No. 13/8/1 CCW000800000000O - Lamprey Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2011 - CCW - Contract Science Report 975 CCW0008000000008 - Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi Catchment 2011 - CCW - Staff Science Report 11/8/5 CCW000800000000B\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader of Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tRiver lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis) Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri) and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) are designated features of the River Usk and Wye Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). Assessments of species composition distribution abundance and size composition of larval lamprey (ammocoetes and transformers) populations in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Usk and Wye Catchments. This collation of lamprey data is made up of data collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the Environment Agency Wales for CCW SAC monitoring reports or CCW Review of Consents reports. The collation includes information about species distribution composition and abundance for river brook and sea lampreys which are a designated (Annex II) features within SAC rivers in Wales. The majority of the data was recorded at the genus level and no distinction was made between river and brook lampreys. However for some historical data no distinction was made between sea and river/brook lampreys and these were recorded at the family level. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.\tPublic record\n18218\tNRW_DS116241\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.176Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323431\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD River Basin Districts Cycle 1\tInternal Contacts: Internal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team.\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 1 is a spatial (polygon) dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).  Article 2 clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...the area of land and sea made up of one or more neighbouring river basins together with their associated ground waters and coastal waters\u0027.\tPublic record\n17614\tNRW_DS107797\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.106Z\tBullhead Monitoring for the Dee Catchment, 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373937\t[A]  Data Archived under Project Number 295 Media Number 1198    [B]  Data is also held in  Recorder 6 CCW0002800000006 Bullhead Monitoring [C]  Dee Catchment 2001-2004- CCW - RoC Report no. 19\t\tBullhead is listed under Annex II of the Habitat and Species Directive as a \"species of community interest\".  Bullhead numbers have been declining across Europe in recent decades as a result of human activities. Bullhead is a designated feature on the River Dee and Bala Lake / Afon Dyfrdwy a Llyn Tegid  Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to assess bullhead abundances and population structure throughout the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) area. Population data for the fish bullhead (Cottus gobio) collected in the River Dee Catchment since 2001 and additional quantitative and semi-quantitative survey data to assess bullhead abundance from 2004.\tPublic record\n17179\tEXT_DS121652\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.694Z\tSEA 6 Inshore Sector Geophysical Report Summer 2004 OSIRIS Project (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363532\tStored in Project Number 170 media number 500\tDave Tavner - Marine Mapping Scientist, Evidence Analysis  OSIRIS Projects Old Contact Details - this became part of BibbyHydromap in 2012. Lockside House, 80 Eastham Village Road Eastham Wirral CH62 0AW Tel. 0151 328 1120. Fax / ISDN 0151 328 1139 e-mail:  enquires@osirisprojects.co.uk\tThe region encompassed by the strategic environmental assessment No. 6 (SEA 6) represents the central Irish Sea with specific focus given to the areas of Liverpool Bay and Tremadog Bay. At the onset of the contract the broad aim of the survey was to acquire data representative of the geology bathymetry and ecology of Tremadog Bay and surrounding areas. Osiris Projects had been contracted by Geotek Ltd on behalf of the Department of Trade and Industry (DTI) to undertake a programme of shallow marine survey work as part of the SEA 6 initiative.  This work was predominantly focussed on the Tremadog bay area of the Irish Sea as part of the inshore sector of the SEA 6 region.  Offshore Survey and Engineering (OSAE) performed survey work in the offshore sector of the SEA 6 region. The scope of the work performed by Osiris Projects as part of the SEA 6 inshore sector included the following elements: - Geophysical survey work using side scan sonar and sub-bottom profiler - Hydrographic survey work using swathe bathymetry and single beam bathymetry - Benthic survey work including grab sampling and photographic imagery\tPublic record\n17221\tEXT_DS122285\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.173Z\tRoyal Yatching Association (RYA): UK Coastal Atlas of Recreational Boating (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323835\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\RYA UK Coastal Atlas of Recreational Boating 2\tGIS and Spatial Data Team  Laura Grant was the person who orginally wanted the data. Kate Griffith has requested the 1 year extension to Feb 2021       Licencse renegotiated and signed 12 Feb 2020 to hold for 1 year  until Feb 2021\tThis dataset is wholly owned by the Royal Yachting Association (RYA). The Royal Yachting Association (RYA) UK Coastal Atlas of Recreational Boating is a GIS dataset of recreational boating activity around the UK comprising spatial data including indicators of intensity of use general boating areas offshore routes as well as the locations of clubs training centres and marinas\tPublic record\n18363\tNRW_DS116830\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.762Z\tThe Status and Distribution of the Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata on Whiteford Burrows, Cefn Sidan, Laugharne \u0026amp; Pendine Burrows and Frainslake Sands, Castlemartin in 2016.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383330\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: Barry Stewart Associates)\tThe Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata was recently classified as Endangered following a decline that has led to an apparent retreat in its Great Britain range to a few beaches in Carmarthen Bay. Even here recent counts have indicated that the population might be at a critically low level. The beetle is an indicator of the health of strandline communities which include a range of rare and scarce species likely to be similarly threatened. The main aim of this study was to survey sites in South Wales in 2016 in order to determine the current status of E. complanata and inform management of strandline habitats. Repeat visits to key sites were undertaken to assess spatial and temporal changes.   Strandline Beetle was found at just four sites all in Carmarthen Bay with peak counts in June of 328 adults on Whiteford Burrows 211 adults on Cefn Sidan 288 adults on Laugharne-Pendine Burrows and one adult on Broughton Burrows providing a total Welsh count of 828 beetles. At a landscape scale the results of the study found that of the 27.5km of beach made up by the three main occupied beach units in Carmarthen Bay only 17km (i.e. 62%) were considered as being in favourable condition for E. complanata in 2016. Perhaps given the dynamic nature of the habitat this is what might be expected for such expansive areas of mobile beach. Therefore it would appear that the bay currently provides a stable stronghold and maintaining population viability should be an urgent conservation priority. Military restrictions are likely to have been instrumental in preventing excessive loss of beach debris at Tywyn Point (Cefn Sidan) and Laugharne-Pendine Burrows whilst the comparative scarcity of debris at Whiteford suggests there is a higher level of vulnerability despite the presence of a strong population during 2016. It was found that accreting dunes supported the healthiest populations of E. complanata. The associated NVC communities of SD2 and SD4 provide very useful indicators of good habitat quality and those beaches with wider profiles of these communities invariably provided the best habitat for E. complanata. Stable dune fronts typically characterised by SD6 vegetation were also occupied by E. complanata but in lower numbers and always with good SD2 and SD4 beach sections nearby.   Wooden items made up 64% of refuges used by adult beetles with plastics making up 24%. This is in contrast to the few previous studies suggesting that the species is probably more dependent on niches provided by beach items rather than item types. Beaches dominated by shingle ridges would appear to be unsuitable for the larval stages but may be important for adults especially during periods when beach debris is scarce. Extant populations of E. complanata are likely to be sufficiently large and fluid enough to enable natural recolonisation to occur with Carmarthen Bay with the beaches of West Gower and Pembrey Burrows being particularly well placed to benefit from remedial action. Measures might include the provision of interpretation to encourage a responsible attitude (and possibly self-policing) amongst beach users; and the provision of supplementary refuges (where necessary) appropriate to each site.  It is critical to maintain populations of Strandline Beetle on Cefn Sidan Laugharne-Pendine Burrows and Whiteford Burrows in favourable condition in order to safeguard the beetle as a UK species. This will require a cessation of debris removal on Whiteford Burrows. It is recommended that colonisation of Broughton Burrows should be encouraged by providing additional refuges and informing the public as to the importance of the beetle.\tPublic record\n17239\tEXT_DS122088\t2024-05-28T12:17:05.148Z\tEnvironment Agency: All Wales 2017 Saltmarsh Extent (WFD Cycle 3) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303838\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh Areas\\All Wales 2017 Saltmarsh Extent (WFD Cycle 3).lyr\tMarine Monitoring Officer\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. Saltmarsh extents determined in a desk based study using aerial imagery. This data is Open but owned by the Environment Agency.\tPublic record\n17113\tEXT_DS116260\t2024-05-28T12:11:56.643Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): Areas Susceptible to Groundwater Flooding (2010) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323630\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Areas Susceptible to Groundwater Flooding 2010.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW\t2010 1km Gridded \u0027Areas Susceptible to Groundwater Flooding \u0027data is derived from BGS \u0027Susceptibility to Groundwater Flooding\u0027 dataset.   The data is a 1 kilometre square grid that identifies at a broad scale areas susceptible to flooding from groundwater on the basis of geological and hydrogeological conditions. It does not show the likelihood of groundwater flooding occurring and therefore is a hazard not risk-based dataset.  It does not take account areas where groundwater is likely to pond or flow but simply considers where groundwater might emerge.  Hazard is represented by one of four area categories showing the proportion of each 1 km square that is susceptible to groundwater flood emergence \u0026lt; 25% \u0026gt;\u003d 25% \u0026lt;50% \u0026gt;\u003d50% \u0026lt;75% \u0026gt;\u003d 75% Absence of a value means that no risk is anticipated to be present.\tPublic record\n17158\tEXT_DS121851\t2024-05-28T12:15:04.616Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): Mineral Resources (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383531\tx:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\\BGS Bedrock resources.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\\BGS Coal resources.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\\BGS Metallic mineral occurence.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\\BGS Mudstone and sandstone resources.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\\BGS Mudstone and slate resources.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\\BGS Slate waste resources.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\Mineral Resources\\BGS Superficial resources.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) is a UK provider of geoscientific data information and knowledge. Natural Resources Wales has licenced access to a number of BGS datasets. This particular spatial data is a collation of licenced data from BGS mineral resources depicted at a 1:50000 scale.\tPublic record\n17449\tNRW_DS87115\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.211Z\tMenai Strait turbidity and other co-variate surveillance\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313135\tData is archived as project no. 7 and 183, and media no. 132, 231 and 524 (File paths are K:\\Archive\\Media 101to200\\Media 132, K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\231 and K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 524)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Menai Strait separating the Isle of Anglesey from the north coast of Wales is part of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation. Monitoring of physical parameters in the strait including water turbidity has been carried out on a sporadic basis for the past 40 years. These parameters are of importance to the biological communities for which the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay has been designated and help to inform assessments of the condition of those communities and the effectiveness of management measures to conserve them. The observations of physical parameters in the strait have shown large long-term fluctuations in turbidity. The aim of this project was to establish an automatic system for continuous monitoring of water turbidity and other relevant physical parameters including temperature salinity and meteorological variables.\tPublic record\n18742\tNRW_DS123392\t2024-05-28T12:55:19.726Z\tSMP Policy\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333932\tx:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Coastal defence\\SMP_Policy.lyr\tRichard Park\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n19141\tNRW_DS125532\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.379Z\tSurveys for Large Mason Bee Osmia xanthomelana on Llŷn Peninsula in 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353332\tLink to Associated Evidence Report - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/rl/NRW%20Publications/Natur%20Am%20Byth%20Reports/Natur%20am%20Byth!%20Report%20No.%20007%20-%20Osmia%20xanthomelana%20report%20April%202023%20redacted.pdf  Data is contained within the Welsh Invertebrate Database - Natur Am Byth\tMike Howe\tCreated as part of the development phase of the Natur am Byth project this field survey was taken at two sites (Porth Neigwl and Porth Ceiriad) located on the Llyn Peninsula where previous records have been made of the Large Mason Bee.\tPrivate record\n18744\tNRW_DS123391\t2024-05-28T12:55:19.721Z\tShoreline Management Plan 2 (SMP2) High Water Mark (HWM) 10k 3 epochs\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333931\tX:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Coastal defence\\SMP2 HWM 10k 3 epochs.lyr\tRichard Park, Senior Specialist, Strategic Planning and Investment, Asset Investment \u0026amp; Planning\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17460\tNRW_DS99457\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.763Z\tUpland Habitat Survey - Vegetation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343537\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1\\Phase1 Vegetation.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset is based largely on vegetation maps produced by the Wales Field Unit of the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) using the Ratcliffe and Birks vegetation classification. It also contains National Vegetation Classification (NVC) maps for parts of five upland sites and Phase 1 Survey information for three areas of Common Land. The vegetation layer holds the boundaries and attributes of vegetation polygons.  The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The GIS dataset was created as part of the remit of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to identify all areas of \u0027open country\u0027 in Wales for the implementation of The Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. All major Welsh upland sites and other upland areas not mapped by the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey.\tPublic record\n18358\tNRW_DS116837\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.324Z\tPaper Data: NCC Topographical/ Terrain Survey of Sites around Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383337\tCurrently in the Shelves in Old post Room F12.  To be sent to off-site storage, Hannah/Jill to supply the archive reference\tKeith Jones, Spatial Information Team Hannah Brazier, Spatial Information Team       Long-term the data could be scanned and disposed off subject to custodian agreement. Entry should not be included in WebView as data is not suitable for public supply\tto be supplied  3 boxes of paper surveys. Contains hand annotated maps of transect lines and print out of information such as feature (trig point fence etc..)grid ref bearing distance (3 boxes).  This was field surveys carried out by the Cartography team in the early 1990\u0027s to validated and identify features and reference points that were used to accurately map the boundary of designated sites.  These are to be stored in off-site storage long-term in case the boundaries are queried\tPrivate record\n17489\tNRW_DS100389\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.724Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Quadrat\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333839\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_quadrats.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.  This dataset consists of quadrat locations recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland survey of Wales ( 1987 - 2004 ) and a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. Sites across Wales vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by CCW.\tPublic record\n17648\tNRW_DS110023\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.346Z\tWelsh Sandbank Survey: Fish Stomach Content, Fish Epibenthic Survey, and Data Analysis (2001)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303233\t[A] Data is archived as project no. 234, media no. 96 and 745 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 96 and K:\\Archive\\Media 701to800\\Media 745) [B] A copy is stored on the I drive:I:\\MMT video Archive\\Handheld surface video\\20010000 Sandbanks BS media 745.avi   [C] A clip has been extracted from the video, edited and made available on CCW\u0027s YouTube channel. See online resource below. [D] Species data on Marine Recorder (Survey numbers MRCCW30000000008, MRCCW30000000037, MRCCW30000000038).\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tSandbanks are listed as an Annex 1 habitat that requires conservation measures under the European Union Habitats Council of the European Communities (1992). Sandbanks can be categorised either by sediment type or by topography. The different sediments Annex I sandbanks can consist of are sands gravelly sands and muddy sands. This study was designed to examine the fish and epifaunal biota of sandbanks and formed part of a larger collaborative survey in which the hyperbenthos and infauna were also sampled.  As a result of their unique physical regime sandbanks may hold their own distinct flora and fauna that is particularly well adapted to such conditions or that uses these habitats in an opportunistic manner (e.g. at slack water). However the extent to which sandbanks are characterised by a distinct set of taxa remains unknown.\tPublic record\n19152\tNRW_DS125733\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.127Z\tThe current status of the spiders Callilepis nocturna, Dipoena erythropus, Erigone dentigera, Gongylidiellum murcidum, Lathys stigmatisata, Maro lepidus and Marpissa radiata on selected Welsh SSSIs in 2021 and 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373333\t\tMichael Howe\tMike Howe To supply\tPrivate record\n17238\tEXT_DS121996\t2024-05-28T12:17:05.064Z\tWoodland Trust: Woods for People (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393936\tx:\\Social Economic\\Woods for People\\Woods for People Version 11.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is owned by the Woodland Trust and Forest Enterprise England and licenced to Natural Resources Wales for use. The Woods for People project has created a UK-wide provisional inventory of accessible woodland. At present it does not include woodlands where the only access is on public rights of way. For Scotland the Land Reform Act gives a right of responsible access to almost all land but the dataset only includes woods that encourage access.\tPublic record\n18849\tNRW_DS124653\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.171Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Low Voltage Cables\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363533\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Low Voltage Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Live Cables.  This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18940\tNRW_DS124817\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.857Z\tWFD Rocky Shore Site Summary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383137\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Rocky Shore\\WFD Rocky Shore Site Summary.lyr\tKate Borrowdale Swyddog Asesu Amgylcheddol Morol / Marine Environmental Assessment Officer Tîm Asesu ac Adrodd Monitro Morol / Marine Monitoring Assessment and Reporting Team\tWater Framework Directive rocky shore site summary spatial data. Monitoring locations and years when monitoring has taken place are included in the data.\tPrivate record\n18853\tNRW_DS124650\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.273Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Access Roads\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363530\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Access Roads.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Access Roads. The access roads shows forest roads owned and maintained by NRW on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17213\tEXT_DS122466\t2024-05-28T12:17:03.533Z\tSevern Trent Water (STW): Reservoir Storage Data held in WISKI 7 Core Hydro and Telemetry (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343636\tData is held in WISKI Hydro Module.\tAlison Hanson - Team Leader for this area of work  Liam Perrott - technical specialist for hydrometry       Data from 2014 in Water Resources Team DMS location.\tReservoir storage data is recorded by Severn Trent Water (STW). Weekly reading of reservoir storage volume (Million litres) and % storage.\tPublic record\n17222\tEXT_DS122193\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.327Z\tWelsh Government: Habitat Land Cover (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313933\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Habitat Land Cover.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a Welsh Government owned dataset licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for use. This spatial dataset details the extent and location of Habitat Land Cover for Ty Gofal agri-environment schemes.\tPublic record\n18839\tNRW_DS124651\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.988Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Demised Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363531\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Demised Areas.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Demised Areas. Demised areas are areas of land ownership that have been transferred by lease over to the leaseholder (Vatenfall) this includes turbine spur roads substation turbine bases etc. Vattenfall are therefore fully responsible for the demised areas this includes maintaining repairing and granting access to or across these areas. No works can take place in these areas without full consent from Vattenfall. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18691\tNRW_DS123304\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.222Z\tHistoric NIRS Incidents (EAW) April 2001 to March 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333034\tx:\\Incidents\\National Incident Recording System (NIRS)\\Historic NIRS Incidents (EAW) April 2001 to March 2013.lyr\tJohn Farnhill\t\tPrivate record\n18491\tNRW_DS121508\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.935Z\tWater Framework Directive Cycle 2Target Catchments Rivers\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353038\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target Rivers Catchments Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers;  Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes waterbodies\tPublic record\n18240\tNRW_DS116297\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.109Z\tDee Water Protection Zone\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323937\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Dee water Protection Zone.lyr\tAngela Roberts-Custodian    *Consents relating to the DWPZ are Public Register and currently stored in the DMS and Carole Williams Environment Assistant is looking after these*\tThis dataset shows the location of the Dee Water Protection  Zone (DWPZ). A Water Protection Zone is an area where certain activities (storing or using controlled substances) are banned or restricted to reduce the risk of polluting drinking water. Under the Water Protection Zone (River Dee Catchment) (Procedural and Other Provisions) Regulations 1999 you need consent (permission) to carry out a controlled activity in the DWPZ. The aim is to prevent water pollution arising from activities that cannot be controlled using other permits. This map can be used to whether your site is within the DWPZ.\tPublic record\n18928\tNRW_DS124820\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.667Z\tNRW North Assistant Flood Incident Duty Officer Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383230\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW North Assistant Flood Incident Duty Officer Areas.lyr\tEirian Gilford, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Team Leader, NRW Gwyn Moseley, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Advisor 2, NRW\tAreas covered by the various Assisant Flood Incident Duty Officers within North Operations with regards to flood incident management and operational response.\tPrivate record\n17915\tNRW_DS111860\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.002Z\tFluvial Audit of the River Wye and Tributaries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383630\tArchived as Project 149, Media 481\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW)\tThis dataset consists of the results of a Fluvial Geomorphological Audit of the River Wye and it\u0027s Tributaries. The river Wye is one of the largest rivers in England and Wales and supports a range of rare and threatened habitats and species. The river includes several Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). It is also designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the geomorphology of the watercourses surveyed and to identify key areas for monitoring management and restoration particularly in relation to the desired physical habitats for the SAC species. The area covered included 305 km of the River Wye and selected tributaries between Pant Mawr and Hay-on-Wye. Tributaries included Aran Bachawy Bachell Brook Camddwr Cletwr Brook Clywedog Brook Colwyn Brook Duhonw Dulas Dulas Brook Dulas Llynfi Edw Gwenlas Brook Howey Brook Ithon Llaethydy Brook Llymwynt Brook Migrams Brook Mithil Brook Nant Bwch Nant yr Offeiriad rhiwiau Brook Sgithwen Brook and Triffrwd.\tPublic record\n18493\tNRW_DS121510\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.037Z\tWater Framework Directive Cycle 2 - Target Waterbodies Rivers\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353130\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target River Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers; Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes wabter bodies\tPublic record\n17488\tNRW_DS100750\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.716Z\tHabitat Agri-Environment Scheme\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373530\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\esa\\habitatbddis.TAB\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Welsh Assembly Government and licensed to CCW for internal use only. The dataset has been supplied by the Welsh Assembly Government and similar to the ESA`s the Habitat scheme is a part farm Agri-Environment scheme run by the WAG Agricultural Dept.\tPrivate record\n18859\tNRW_DS124759\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.644Z\tCuRVe Adaptability Habitat Recovery Period\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373539\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe rate at which a habitat can recover back to a steady stable state from a disturbance event is an inherent property of ecosystem resilience. Habitats that have an inherent ability to re-establish quickly following disturbance have a higher likelihood of persisting because they can re-establish rapidly and before the next disturbance event occurs. For example wet woodlands can recover relatively quickly from flood events (providing this flooding stays within the range of historic limits) whilst ancient woodlands may take many hundreds of years to re-create if lost. The effect that recovery periods may have on ecosystem resilience is explored through this factor.\tPrivate record\n17931\tNRW_DS112544\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.943Z\tDrop-Down Video Survey Location Inventory (1999 - 2010)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353434\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat video points ,  Archived under project 277, media 1057 (pathway: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1001to1100\\Media1057) Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\Marine_Habitat_Videos\tCharles Lindenbaum, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting,   Monica Jones, Data Services Team Leader.\tThis is a spatial dataset to show the location  of drop-down video surveys. This dataset acts as an inventory of dropdown video survey which enables staff to navigate drop-down video archive using and identifying specific geographic location of each video clip. The purpose of this data capture was to allow staff to use and access relevant spatial information.\tPublic record\n17109\tEXT_DS119177\t2024-05-28T12:11:56.487Z\tWelsh Government: Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation (WIMD) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313737\tX:\\Social Economic\\Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation\\WIMD 2000.lyr ,     X:\\Social Economic\\Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation\\WIMD 2005.lyr ,     X:\\Social Economic\\Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation\\WIMD 2008.lyr ,     X:\\Social Economic\\Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation\\WIMD 2011.lyr ,     X:\\Social Economic\\Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation\\WIMD 2014.lyr ,     x:\\Social Economic\\Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation\\WIMD 2019.lyr ,\tGIS Service Desk Julie Boswell, State of Natural Resources Reporting Specialist Advisor, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tThe Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation (WIMD) is the Welsh Governments official measure of relative deprivation for small areas in Wales. It is designed to identify those small areas where there are the highest concentrations of several different types of deprivation. Deprivation is the lack of access to opportunities and resources which we might expect in our society. This can be in terms of material goods or the ability of an individual to participate in the normal social life of the community. An Index is a group of separate measurements which are combined into a single number. WIMD ranks all small areas in Wales from 1 (most deprived) to 1909 (least deprived). It does not provide a measure of the level of deprivation in an area.\tPublic record\n18465\tNRW_DS120146\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.289Z\tForest Priority Catchments 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230313436\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\NRW Forestry Priority Catchments 2013.lyr\tCustodian: Dave Johnston, Team Leader Environmental Reporting Metadata Point of Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services Team\tThe dataset comprises a number of river catchments based on Water Framework Directive water body boundaries which have been identified by NRW as areas within which forestry environmental improvement works will be prioritised to reduce the impact of forestry on the water environment.\tPublic record\n18256\tNRW_DS116267\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.980Z\tInventory of Closed Mining Waste Facilities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323637\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Inventory of Closed Mining Waste Facilities.lyr\tRebecca Favager, Regulation Policy and Approach Manager Philip Goodman, Environment officer (Metal mines project manager), Environment management, NRW\tThe European Mining Waste Directive (2006/21/EC) requires Member States to create an inventory of closed or abandoned mine waste facilities causing serious environmental impacts and to make this inventory available to the public. A waste facility means any area designated for the accumulation or deposit of extractive waste.\tPublic record\n18228\tNRW_DS116249\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.368Z\tAuthorised Landfill Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323439\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Active Authorised Landfill Boundary.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning  Also could contact: Tyrone (Ty) Ward - PPC/RSR Officer 1, PPC Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW       The publish data is a redacted version of the whole dataset - as the non redacted version includes names and addresses.\tThis dataset has now been discontinued and won’t be updated. The permit information may no longer be correct and the boundary may no longer reflect the actual site anymore as sites may have expanded their perimeter to accommodate additional capacity. Also this dataset will not show landfills that have been created after 2013 or may contain landfill sites that are now expired surrendered or revoked.  The authorised landfill site is a spatial (polygon) dataset containing the boundaries of landfill sites which are currently authorised by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under The Environmental Permitting (England and Wales) Regulations 2010. The Environmental Permitting (England and Wales) Regulations provide industry regulators and others with a single extended permitting and compliance system. Landfill permits were previously authorised by a Waste Management Licence  or a Pollution Prevention and Control (PCC) Permit which have subsequently become Environmental  (EPR) Permit and are recorded within a NRW database. Pre 1996 landfill sites data was managed and collated by Local Authorities this duty was then transferred to Environment Agency and was taken over by NRW in 2013.  INFORMATION WARNING: This dataset has now been discontinued and won’t be updated. The permit information may no longer be correct and the and boundary may no longer not reflect the actual site anymore as sites may have expanded their perimeter to accommodate additional capacity. Also this dataset will not show landfills that have been created after 2013 or may contain landfill sites that are now expired surrendered or revoked.\tPublic record\n18692\tNRW_DS123308\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.216Z\tOil spill dispersant\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333038\tx:\\Incidents\\Oil Spills\\Oil spill dispersant.lyr\tAndrew Hill\t\tPrivate record\n17892\tNRW_DS112406\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.940Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species and Habitat Diversity Confidence Layer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343036\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Contact Data Services for metadata information.\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Confidence ratings based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final species diversity analysis was calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data for the Welsh coastline taken from the mean high-water datum to 12 nautical miles offshore.\tPublic record\n18394\tNRW_DS119026\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.452Z\tEnvironment Agency Wales (EAW) Landholdings\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303236\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex EAW Landholdings.lyr\tIan Medcalf, GIS Officer, NRW William Patterson, Assistant Surveyor National Services, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset for internal use only containing information on areas of land in which former Environment Agency Wales (EAW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have an interest or a right to manage. This includes freehold and leasehold land EAW land that is let out and land which has been disposed of access agreements agreements deeds of grants easements fishing rights honoraria lettings licenses sub leases and wayleaves. This provides an indicative representation land managed by EAW in Wales. The  dataset contains only polygons which includes information on site name and unique codes. Content varies for each parcel of land but often includes personal details (which are regarded as sensitive) and basic details of agreements.  The purpose of the data is to provide location and boundary information primarily for use by the estates department.  This is not an exhaustive list of all of the assets that ex-EAW own/maintain or have an interest in.\tPublic record\n18539\tNRW_DS121731\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.127Z\tEnvironmental Impact Assessment\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373331\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\EIA\\Environmental Impact Assessments.lyr\tLilley-Owen, Suzanne, Permitting Team Leader Forestry, Permitting Services (Forestry)\tAreas showing the extent of EIA forestry projects for which we have given our opinion.  The data takes the form of a polygon digitised by protected sites map.   The following attributes are recorded:  Property Name; Grid Reference; Project Type Ref number. It was captured as a decision making tool it shows where the site is and whether there are any adjacency issues which may affect our opinion.\tPrivate record\n18918\tNRW_DS124845\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.360Z\tFlood Risk Areas (Cycle 2)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383435\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Cycle 2 Flood Risk Areas are those communities in Wales where most significant flood risks exist across all sources of flood risk. They were identified for the purpose of the second Preliminary Flood Risk Assessment under the Flood Risk Regulations 2009.   The threshold for communities at significant risk of flooding was set by Welsh Government with input from Natural Resources Wales and the Welsh Local Government Association (representing Lead Local Flood Authorities).    The Cycle 2 Flood Risk Areas were identified in December 2018 and replace the Cycle 1 Flood Risk Areas.\tPublic record\n17496\tNRW_DS100237\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.422Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Upland Boundary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323337\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Upland boundary.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis spatial dataset shows the boundary between upland and lowland that was adopted during the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey and the WFU Upland Vegetation Survey of Wales.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tPublic record\n17707\tNRW_DS110218\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.412Z\tEpipelic and epilithic microalgal distribution, density and relative abundance following the Sea Empress oil spill.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323138\tArchived as project no 111 - media no 385 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Media301to400\\Media385)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThe ecological impact of a pollution event such as the Sea Empress oil spill is readily observable on macroscopic plants and animals. Although microscopic organisms are not as easily observed and studied they may be a crucial part of the ecosystem structure and function. This dataset contains the result of a study into the effects of oil contamination on epipelic and epilithic microalgal communities in coastal habitats of South West Wales. Sites contaminated by the Sea Empress oil spill were compared to a nearby adjacent uncontaminated site. Purpose of this data capture was to study the effects of oil contamination on the distribution density and abundance of epipelic and epilithic microscopic algal communities.\tPublic record\n18809\tNRW_DS123857\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.821Z\tGeneral licences - excluded areas where specific licences are required\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233383537\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Areas where bespoke licence required.lyr\tIwan G Hughes, Team Leader, Species and Carbon Permitting, Technical Services and Customer Care\tThis spatial dataset shows the areas which are excluded from General licences 001 002 and 004 issued on 1st January 2024 by Natural Resources Wales to permit the killing of certain species of wild birds. Individuals wishing to kill wild birds within these areas will need to apply to Natural Resources Wales for a specific licence using the application form which can be found on the Natural Resources website.\tPublic record\n17459\tNRW_DS99710\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.490Z\tLettings to Natural Resources Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939373130\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\Lettings to NRW current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\Lettings to NRW expired.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\Lettings to NRW proposed.lyr\tNatural Resources Wales GIS Team Hannah Brazier Cartographic Officer, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all current proposed and expired Land Agency Lettings to Natural Resources Wales (NRW).This is lease of land and sporting rights to NRW so we can influence how things are managed across this land. This is always money going out from NRW. The dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name owner scale captured expiry date and unique codes.\tPublic record\n18694\tNRW_DS123317\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.228Z\tRadioactive Sites Non Nuclear\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333137\tx:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\Radioactive Sites Non Nuclear.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18080\tNRW_DS115332\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.796Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata Reefs in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333332\tData is archived as project 410, media 1430 and forms part of archive Project No 199.   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2012 ASML PLAS.   Marine Recorder Survey MRCCW16300000038\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones listed in Table 1.  Of these Estuaries and their constituent mudflats sandflats and reefs large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata; are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC.  Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC (CCW 2009). The purpose of this data capture was to continue monitoring the intertidal area within the SAC.\tPublic record\n17292\tEXT_DS124282\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.937Z\tLocal Authorities: Flint Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323832\tArchived from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Flint Wildlife Sites.lyr , retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact; geospatial team\tFlint Wildlife Sites 3rd party data and property of local authority. Archived 2019 the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\tPublic record\n17342\tNRW_DS24262\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.250Z\tNational Peatland Resource Inventory (NPRI)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323632\tUnknown\tData Custodian: Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. Now falls under Jane Stevens, TSG, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNational Peatland Resource Inventory (NPRI) was the most comprehensive assessment of the lowland raised bog resource in Great Britain at the time of publishing (1996). The inventory lists all known existing or former lowland raised bog sites. Within the NPRI raised bog is classified according to a number of sub-categories based on land cover. Four classes of active raised bog are defined: natural or near- natural vegetation; degraded vegetation (usually burnt or dry); drained; and revegetated or regenerating cutover.  There is uncertainty about data reliability and source.\tPrivate record\n17346\tNRW_DS24148\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.677Z\tUpland Vegetation Survey and Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales Brecknock spreadsheets (1991-1995)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234313438\t[A]  This data is archived in the terrestrial archive under: Project Number T005 Media Number T_Media005.    [B]   Location of data in the CCW H Drive:  H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\PayneC\\2003\\MYeo spreadsheet data   To convert data from old format to xls, they were copied to TRIM (Location of data in Ffynnon: NRWF-15-001292), where they could be viewed and then copied to a new format.  Retention on these temporary files has passed and they have been deleted.  the converted files are retained in the archive\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems / Heathlands Ecologist, Grasslands Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecologist - Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.  Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to inform site selection and develop understanding of the semi-natural vegetation cover of Wales. The upland data covers all the major upland blocks in Wales; the grassland data is restricted to Brecknock sites.  Approximately 60 spreadsheets of Upland data (environmental habitat type statutory classifications and grid referenced sites). Data from various times between 1991 to 1995.  Collation and analysis of data collected by the Wales Field Unit Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 and a small number of subsequent upland NVC surveys in the early 1990s.\tPublic record\n18357\tNRW_DS116847\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.273Z\tBiodiversity Action Reporting System (BARS) Archived Action Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383437\tAn archive of the full data is stored as project T008, T_Media020. Copies of relevant spreadsheet will be stored in the DMS\tData and metadata: Terrestrial Mapping Specialist, Jonathan Rothewell Data and System: Specialist Advisor Enterprise Data Management, Barnaby Letheren       The GIS data should be rated as poor quality and will need cleaning up if it is to be made available as a corporate layer\tAn archive of NRWs (and legacy bodies) actions boundaries and project data downloaded December 2016 following the closure of the Biodiversity Action Reporting System.  Biodiversity actions are recorded as GIS point and polygon data with related information held in spreadsheets.  BARS was originally created to support the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UKBAP) and allowed organisations companies and partnerships to manage and share their own Biodiversity Action Plans.  The UKBAP closed in 2011 and the last version of BARS was launched in April 2012 to collate biodiversity actions in a standardised form and capture geographic locations enabling spatial analysis and reporting of activity.  The UK BARS partnership (DEFRA Natural England Northern Ireland Environment Agency Scottish Natural Heritage Wales Biodiversity Partnership and Joint Nature Conservation Committee) took the difficult decision to discontinue support for BARS in November 2016.\tPublic record\n20191\tNRW_DS161334\t2025-02-04T13:53:20.139Z\tGrizzled Skipper Survey of Wrexham Industrial Estate 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333334\t\tBethan Beech\tWaiting for metadata from custodian\tPrivate record\n18712\tNRW_DS123358\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.298Z\tSheep Dip Bryophyte Sampling SE Area\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333538\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\Sheep Dip Bryophyte Sampling SE Area.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\t\tPrivate record\n17618\tNRW_DS109879\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.893Z\tSaltmarsh Features at Burry Inlet and Loughor Estuary (1998)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383739\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media217 Data is held as Project No.52, media 217.\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Burry Inlet and Loughor Estuary. The area comprises the most extensive site of saltmarsh vegetation in Wales. The purpose of this data capture is to provide detailed information on the distribution of the key elements for which the estuary was submitted for selection as a candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) including the extent of transitional zones extent of saltmarsh communities and the distribution of the nationally scarce and locally distributed Marsh Mallow Althaea officinalis. Another aim of the survey was to provide floristic data to enable the separation of the Mediterranean Saltmeadows from their more generally distributed counterparts.\tPublic record\n18550\tNRW_DS121722\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.690Z\tWINS Water Quality: Constraints for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373232\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Constraints for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows constraints to intervention types for faecal coliforms entering the water environment. The number of constraints is combined across all land management intervention types so an areas with one constraint for one type of action and two constraints for another type of action will show as having three constraints.\tPublic record\n17451\tNRW_DS99725\t2024-05-28T12:24:08.816Z\tNatural Resources Wales Land Management Agreements\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939373235\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement expired.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement proposed.lyr   Feature class includes proposed, completed and expired Land Management Agreements\tNatural Resources Wales GIS Team\tThis is a spatial dataset containing land management agreements for Natural Resources Wales (NRW). This layer contains all current (completed / live) land management agreements as well as all currently proposed management agreements (not yet signed) Plus expired agreements. Dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name owner scale captured expiry date and unique codes. The purpose is to provide accurate legal maps for management agreements and to provide a complete table showing all live management agreements in Wales.\tPublic record\n17571\tNRW_DS102089\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.381Z\tSquirrels of Central Wales  (2001)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303839\t[A]  Recorder 6  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, Report 475  CCW0003800000009.\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tThis dataset contains the records of the presence or absence of red and grey squirrels and associated tree species in four forests Tywi Irfon Crychan and Brechfa of Central Wales.  The aim of this data capture was to determine the presence and distribution of grey and red squirrel and to make some preliminary conclusions on population viability and recommendations for conservation actions for red squirrel.\tPublic record\n18250\tNRW_DS116272\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.904Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323732\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tInternal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\t\u0027Water Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies\u0027 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2 clause 6 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...bodies of surface water in the vicinity of river mouths which are partly saline in character as a result of their proximity to coastal waters but are substantially influenced by freshwater flows\u0027.   Transitional waterbodies were defined from Mean High Water boundaries taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2 and NRW estuarine boundaries defined for the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD). Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics risk classification and proposed objectives. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive.\tPublic record\n18076\tNRW_DS115333\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.799Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Mawddach Estuary Sediments in Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333333\t1) Data is archived as project 410, media 1430 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring) and forms part of archive Project No 199. 2) Marine Recorder Survey Number (MRCCW16300000044). 3) This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2012 ASML PLAS.   4) Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Pen Llyn SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC)  is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones.  Of these Estuaries and their constituent mudflats sandflats and reefs large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC.  Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC (CCW 2009).\tPublic record\n17402\tNRW_DS64561\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.137Z\tAdmiralty Charts (Paper Records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353631\tVarious paper maps are available through HQ and Regional offices.\tInternal Custodian: Keith Jones, GIS Data Manager, Information Management Group, CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds paper copies of Admiralty Charts (maps of sea areas) covering much of the Welsh coastline.\tPublic record\n18264\tNRW_DS116279\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.061Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323739\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Basin Districts Cycle2 Draft.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts (RBD) is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2 clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...the area of land and sea made up of one or more neighbouring river basins together with their associated groundwaters and coastal waters…\u0027.   River Basin Districts have been delineated by using River Catchments as \\building blocks\\\" that have been aggregated together within a GIS ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries. Coastal and transitional waterbodies are also merged and assigned to a river basin district. River Catchments were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive.\"\tPublic record\n18233\tNRW_DS116247\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.565Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323437\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Management Catchments Cycle 1.lyr\tRob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 1 is a spatial (polygon) dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the WFD)  Management catchments are the unit of geography for which action plans are drafted in implementing the WFD. WFD Management Catchments have an action plan published that relates to all waterbodies that fall within its boundaries.\tPublic record\n19144\tNRW_DS125543\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.304Z\tA survey of rare coastal species of the moss genus Bryum in Wales in September 2015\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353433\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDune%20Bryum%20survey%202015\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA report on the monitoring of rare Bryum mosses on Welsh sand dunes.\tPrivate record\n18361\tNRW_DS116826\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.699Z\tHistoric LIDAR Archive\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383236\tx:\\Basemapping\\LiDAR\\Historic LiDAR\tBen Anscombe / Louise Harris (Spatial services team)\tLight Detection and Ranging (LIDAR) is an airborne mapping technique which uses a laser to measure the distance between the aircraft and the ground. Up to 100000 measurements per second are made of the ground allowing highly detailed terrain models to be generated at spatial resolutions of between 25cm and 2 metres. The Natural Resources Wales composite dataset contains digital elevation data derived from surveys carried out over several years and covers approximately 70% of Wales. We are making available 25cm 50cm 1m and 2m datasets supplied as terrain models (a representation of the ground level) or surface models (a representation of object heights such as vehicles buildings and vegetation). In addition to the height information geo-referenced coloured shaded relief images at the same resolution as the input LiDAR data grids are available.\tPublic record\n18709\tNRW_DS123357\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.087Z\tMonitoring SSSI Lakes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333537\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\Monitoring SSSI Lakes.lyr\tVictoria Bowmaker\t\tPrivate record\n17423\tNRW_DS98750\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.256Z\tFormer Marine Nature Reserves (MNRs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373530\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Nature Reserve.lyr\t\tIn 2015 the Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). This spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Marine Conservation Zones (MCZs) former Marine Nature Reserves in Wales. MCZs and MNRs are a way of conserving marine habitats and wildlife and other features of special importance along the shore or on the seabed. Skomer the first and only MNR in Wales was established in 1990 and is situated around the island of Skomer and the Marloes Peninsula in south west Wales. The Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 empowers NRW to apply to the First Minister for the National Assembly for Wales (NAW) to designate MNR.  MNR may be governed by byelaws created through discussions and mutual agreements with all concerned and approved by WAG. MNR make a valuable contribution to the protection of our seas by creating an awareness of the fragility and value of the natural marine heritage. In Wales the MNRs that existed have been superseded by MCZs. The Marine and Coastal Access Act 2009 allows for the creation of MCZs. MCZs protect a range of nationally important marine wildlife habitats geology and geomorphology and can be designated anywhere in English and Welsh territorial and UK offshore waters.\tPublic record\n18814\tNRW_DS123644\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.917Z\tMarine Licence Disposal Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233363434\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence disposal sites WGS84.lyr\tJoseph Thomas\tHistoric and current designated sites for disposal of dredge material at sea.\tPrivate record\n17132\tEXT_DS119898\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.048Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): Ad hoc Offshore and UK Continental Shelf (UKCS) Geological Datasets (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139383938\tx:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS Details survey UKCS WGS84.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS Boulders cobbles UKCS WGS84.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS Rockhard substrate UKCS WGS84.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS Hard Substrates_WGS84.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team       • In 2010, BGS and JNCC produced the UKSeaMap 2010 substrate layer containing the 4 broad EUNIS sediment types plus rock o The dataset has joint ownership and BGS IPR were happy for us to supply the data to anyone who made a formal request via email   • In 2011, BGS published DigHardSubstrate250 dataset which is a further improvement on MB0103 Made avaiable as an extra for people who licence digi map products from BGS  Andrew Jeffery emailing Helen Lili from JNCC to find this information- JNCC  were currently letting a contract to replace these datasets,and were written in to be made OGL.\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) is a UK provider of geoscientific data information and knowledge. Natural Resources Wales has licenced access to a number of BGS datasets. This particular spatial data is a collation of licenced data from BGS for offshore and UK Continental Shelf (UKCS) geology.\tPublic record\n18858\tNRW_DS124763\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.621Z\tNH3 Sensitive Ancient Woodlands\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373633\tX:\\Resource Management\\Air Quality\\NH3 sensitive Ancient Woodlands.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tThis layer identifies areas of Ancient Woodland and Parkland from which NRW hold recent auditable records of N-sensitive lichens. Not all lichens are sensitive to increased ammonia: the 62 species used to identify N-sensitive Ancient Woodland and Parkland are particularly sensitive and are important in the woodland/parkland ecosystem. Any area identified as sensitive in this GIS layer should be assessed using a 1µg/m3 NH3 Critical Level.\tPublic record\n17283\tEXT_DS124281\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.767Z\tLocal Authorities: Denbigh Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323831\tArchivd from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Denbigh Wildlife Sites.lyr , retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact: Geospatial team\tDenbigh Wildlife Sites 3rd party data and property of local authority. Archived 2019 the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\tPublic record\n19489\tNRW_DS161291\t2024-09-03T08:28:35.393Z\tNorth Wales Groundwater Archive Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323931\tZ:\\Archive\\Water Mgmt\\Groundwater\\Groundwater Archive (LTD)\tKay Roberts - Specialist Advisor, Hydrogeology\tData from groundwater sources (borehole wells and springs) from across North Wales and the Lower Dee in England but predominantly in the Clwyd and Lower Dee catchments.  Data includes borehole construction and geology logs (records) test pumping data ad hoc groundwater level and quality data surveying details (for elevation) maps data analysis and other correspondence and notes on the site.  Data collection started in the 1970s and continued until 2014 (when the officer colleting the data retired).\tPublic record\n17818\tNRW_DS110886\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.140Z\tEpiflora Diversity Within Eelgrass (Zostera marina) Beds on the Welsh Coast\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383836\tHeld as a Marine Recorder survey: MRCCW30000000036\tCustodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tSeagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas such as lagoons and channels which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina) is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Zostera beds is also on the OSPAR List of threatened and/or declining species and habitats. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor Zostera marine habitat and condition and this dataset is a collation results of a survey investigating the epifloral diversity within eelgrass beds around the coast of Wales.\tPublic record\n18572\tNRW_DS122028\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.515Z\tTerrestrial Broad Habitats Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303238\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Broad Habitat Combined BAP Habitats.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Broad Habitat Combined BAP Habitats Point.lyr\tGIS and Spatail Data Team  Liz Howe, Jon Rothwell, Stuart Smith, Pete Jones\tBroad habitats are a spatial datasets derived from Phase 1 Terrestrial habitat and species surveys conducted across Wales. Board habitats classifies / defines habitats as ecologically integrated units at a landscape scale rather than seeing habitats as simply distinct types of vegetation. These broad habitats were classified in responsible to the convention on Biological Diversity. In Wales there are 36 broad habitats and the terrestrial habitats are as follows: Broad leaved and yes planted coniferous woodland native pine woodland lowland wood pastures and parkland boundary features arable improved grassland unimproved neutral grassland acid grassland calcareous grassland lowland heathland grazing marsh fens carr marsh swamp and reed bed lowland raised bog standing open water rivers and streams canals montane (alpine and subalpine types) upland heathland and blanket bog.\tPublic record\n17663\tNRW_DS109885\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.385Z\tSaltmarsh Features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) - Glaslyn 1993\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383835\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Glaslyn 1993 Field Studies journal available at CCW Headquarters Library, Bangor. (see library catalogue) H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media533 Archived as project 190 media 533\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau - Glaslyn 1993.  The main objective of the survey was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map the unusual brackish water saltmarsh communities on the seaward edge of the marsh as well as characterising some of the transitional vegetation types and the glycophytic communities on the middle and upper marsh.\tPublic record\n17910\tNRW_DS112227\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.746Z\tIntertidal and Subtidal Predicted Benthic Fisheries Habitats Maps  (2009)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323237\tStated as Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Sue_Hr\\Benthic Fisheries habitats Maps but should have been moved to Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS Users\\Sue_Hr BUT THIS LOCATION DOES NOT APPEAR TO EXIST.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team (Formerly Claire Eno)\tThis dataset is consists of intertidal and subtidal maps of benthic fisheries habitats within the Welsh Territorial Seas (high water to 12 nm offshore).  Intertidal biotope mapping was carried out in order to determine the distribution and extent of seashore habitats biological communities and species on the shores of Wales. Subtidal biotopes were predicted in HABMAP in order to act as a guide for marine scientists. The biotopes were converted to fisheries habitats in order to provide a means of assessing the sensitivity of benthic habitats to the effects of fishing gear types. The intertidal area consists of the whole of the welsh coastline between the limits of the tidal rise and fall.  The subtidal area consists of Welsh water out to 12 nm  offshore which are within the Southern Irish Sea  i.e. sections around the Severn in the south and the Dee in the north are not included. HABMAP modelling has been re-run to include these areas in the south and north and these biotopes are due to be included in the fisheries habitats maps in 2010.\tPublic record\n18391\tNRW_DS119037\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.412Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Landholdings\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303337\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW Land holdings current.lyr  X:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex EAW Landholdings.lyr  X:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex FCW Landholdings.lyr\tKeith Jones, Natural Resources Wales (NRW) GIS TEAM Jonathan Rothwell, Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tThis dataset is made up of separate spatial layers from all three legacy bodies Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Environment Agency Wales (EAW) and Forestry Commissions Wales (FCW) all of whom now make up Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  Dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name scale captured unique codes. This provides accurate legal maps for management agreements and provides a complete table showing all land owned by NRW in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to provide an accurate digital legal maps for management agreements and provide a complete compilation of land owned or managed by NRW.\tPublic record\n17583\tNRW_DS102007\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.739Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera noltii) Beds in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004 and 2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303037\tThe report and data collected is archived as Project No 199, Media No 546 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon, on optical media.   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\MarineMonitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2004_05 IECS\tIntertidal Ecologist, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas such as lagoons and channels which are sheltered from significant wave action.  Zostera noltii generally occurs higher on the shore than the other two Zostera species found in the UK and occurs in low/variable salinity areas. Seagrass beds have important biological functions and are of conservation and economic importance; however human activity in the coastal zone (pollution dredging boat mooring etc) can affect its distribution or even destroy the beds completely.  The work expressed here is both to trial the methodology and to establish a reference condition for the eelgrass beds at Foryd Bay and Traeth Lafan in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to determine and map the extent density and condition of Zostera noltii identified at Traeth Lafan and Foryd bay in previous years record the presence of epiphytes and ephemeral algal species on the beds record other conspicuous biota within the Zostera beds record any damage or disease of the Zostera and develop and test an index of damage.\tPublic record\n18796\tNRW_DS124626\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.671Z\tNRW North Flood Incident Duty Officer Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363236\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW North Flood Incident Duty Officer Areas.lyr\tEirian Gilford, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Team Leader, NRW Gwyn Moseley, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Advisor 2, NRW\tAreas covered by the various Flood Incident Duty Officers with regards to flood incident management and operational response.\tPrivate record\n17347\tNRW_DS24264\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.695Z\tCoppicing Surveilance at Coeddyd Aber National Nature Reserve\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323634\tUnknown\t\tLarge spreadsheet of plant species against quadrat number and percentage cover values.  Collected as part of coppicing surveillance at Coedydd Aber National Nature Reserve (NNR).\tPrivate record\n18693\tNRW_DS123312\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.107Z\tMining Impact Non Coal\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333132\tx:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Mining Impact NonCoal.lyr\tTrystan James, Team Leader, Geoscience, Geoscience/Knowledge Strategy \u0026amp; Planning\t\tPrivate record\n17250\tEXT_DS123029\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.912Z\tWelsh Government: WIMD 2014 Indicators (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303239\tX:\\Social Economic\\Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation\\WIMD 2014 Indicators.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management.  Business contact may be Julie Boswell as listed for other WIMD data obtained from WG.   May be a spatial version of data that is available from here https://statswales.gov.wales/Catalogue/Community-Safety-and-Social-Inclusion/Welsh-Index-of-Multiple-Deprivation/Archive/WIMD-2014\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18375\tNRW_DS116805\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.389Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Species Data on the NBN Atlas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383035\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas  Top copies of data held within corporate store e.g. Recorder 6, Marine Recorder, BioSys, WISKI.   Extracted version in NBN Exchange format held Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data.  Uploads from legacy CCW held on the H drive (to me moved to the NRW archive storage area)\tJohn Beattie Advisor 2 - Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) species data held on the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas. This includes marine terrestrial and freshwater species records for a variety of taxonomic groups. Data was collected to support core NRW functions including statutory and non-statutory reporting condition monitoring and decision making. The NBN data portal is the main route for the publishing NRW species data. Details of each NRW species data can be viewed through the NBN Atlas.\tPublic record\n18422\tNRW_DS119281\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.448Z\tForest Restock Coupes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323831\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Restock Coupes.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Restock 2nd rotation.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset shows restocking coupes. These are areas of intended management and may not be implemented when indicated or at all. The attribution indicates the probable habitat or species and the year field when those will take place or be completed by. A related layer is management coupes which show what management is intended prior to restocking\tPublic record\n18083\tNRW_DS115296\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.803Z\tDrop-Down Video North Wales Survey 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323936\t[A] Associated data is held within NRW\u0027s Marine Archive as; Project 367, Media 1362 (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1362).    [B] Species data in Marine Recorder survey: MRCCW31600000009\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tDrop down video survey of seabed off the north coast of Pen Llyn and in Tremadog Bay North Wales carried out by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2012.  The dataset comprises of 26 video tows each of approximately 10 minutes in length with a set of still images that are taken approximately every 10 seconds along the length of the tow.  Videos and stills subsequently analysed by Coastal Assessment Liaison and Monitoring under contract to CCW to describe habitats species and biotopes. The purpose of this data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat maps in those areas.  4 sites off the north coast of Pen Llyn between Porth Dinllaen and Porth Colman and 22 sites distributed around the northern half of Tremadog Bay.\tPublic record\n17559\tNRW_DS102271\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.330Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Area of Search (AoS) Reports\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323731\tDigital data and reports available on the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV  of Terrestrial Science Group.  Paper versions in libraries and held by CCW HQ and district staff.  Reports are found on legacy CCW\u0027s document archive, Report Numbers in Fynnon as follows: NRWF-15-000303      Brecknock 09/27617      Dinefwr and Carmarthen (2 reports) 09/27627      Ceredigion 09/27641      Clwyd NRWF-15-000451      Gwent 09/27672      MId \u0026amp; South Glamorgan 09/89979      Pembrokeshire 09/89950      Radnor 09/89971      West Glamorgan NRWF-15-000271      Anglesey and West Gwynedd (2 reports)\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW Formerly Jane Stevens, Habitat and Ecosystem Resource Analyst, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tAreas of Search (AoS) are geographical areas used in the selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs). There are a total of 12 AoS in Wales. For each AoS there are one or two phase 2 lowland grassland AoS reports. This dataset contains the series of these reports each of which includes a detailed description of the survey undertaken within the area survey methodology and conservation assessments of each survey site.\tPublic record\n18775\tNRW_DS123447\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.199Z\tNRW Flood risk teams areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343437\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Flood risk teams areas.lyr\tLucy Edwards\tMap of Wales split into 11 sections each section covers the area of responsibility for each of the Flood risk team area. The attributes are the name email address for the area covered. Purpose: Staff information\tPrivate record\n17929\tNRW_DS112484\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.898Z\tGeological Site Condition Reports\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343834\tKept by Area Earth Science Officers. Digital photographs stored on the personal computers of the Area Earth Science Officers For instance, Bob Mathews works from home and stores his records on a work laptop that is backed-up on an external hard-drive. Paper records are also available.\tRaymond Roberts, North Region Earth Science Officer, NRW.\tShort paper forms used to record condition of geological features/ Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) accompanied by digital photographs. The forms form the basis of the geological site monitoring condition reports on the Monitoring Module of the Features Database.\tPrivate record\n17251\tEXT_DS123426\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.912Z\tFire and rescue authorities (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343236\tx:\\Resource Management\\Emergency Services\\Fire and rescue authorities.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management.   Business Contact: Lucy Edwards, Lead Specialist Advisor, Flood Warning \u0026amp; Informing\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18385\tNRW_DS116742\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.487Z\tCoastwatch Habitat Maps\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373432\tArchived as Project 470, Media 1547 (File path: K\\:Archive\\Media1501to1600\\Media1547)\tInternal Custodian: Gabrielle Wyn, Team Leader, All Wales Marine Advice Team (Gabe Wyn advised that should be kept as permanent record)  Gabe Wyn advised that should be kept as permanent record\tThis dataset consists of paper records which have been boxed and sent to offsite storage. The records relate to the Coastwatch project started during the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) days. The surveys were related to how people use the coast in terms of resources and recreation. The leaflet details some information related to the surveys as follows: Take part in Coastwatch the largest coastal survey of Great Britain ever planned. If you are interested in the coast or concerned about its protection you can help increase our knowledge of our coastal heritage.... to do the job properly we first need to know the location and size of all the different types of coastal habitat such as rocky and sandy shores saltmarshes and sand dunes. We also want to find out which human pressures are affecting these habitats and where they are concentrated.  This data is currently not known to be digitised. It has been superseded by the Phase 1 Intertidal work but still provides and important historic record of the coastline around Wales. The majority of records cover Wales however there are some ad hoc records which are located elsewhere.\tPrivate record\n17735\tNRW_DS110182\t2024-05-28T12:30:03.120Z\tFluvial Audit of the Upper Usk Tributaries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313832\tArchived as Project 59, Media 354.\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, NRW)\tThe results of a Fluvial Geomorphical Audit of the Upper Usk tributaries. The Usk system comprising the River Usk and its upper tributaries has been designated as a Special Area for Conservation (SAC) and notified as a Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) for its geomorphological and physiographic features and the associated plant and animal communities. It supports internationally important populations of otter Atlantic salmon bullhead brook lamprey and river lamprey. This survey provides a baseline source for assessing potential habitat suitability by relating the presence or absence of morphological features to the condition parameters that are identified as habitat preferences for the SAC species. The purpose of this data capture is to develop an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the Upper Usk system.  The areas surveyed included the Upper Usk system (upstream of Abergavenny) and it\u0027s tributaries which included Hydfer Crai Senni Treweryn Camlais Tarell Cynrig Menascin Caerfanell Cilieni Eithrim Bran Ysgir Ysgir Fechan Ysgir Fawr Honddu Rhiangoll Grwyne Grwyne Fechan Banw Grwyne Fawr.\tPublic record\n18212\tNRW_DS116258\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.494Z\tCatchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) Boundaries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323538\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) Boundaries.lyr\tNRW Internal Custodian: Ben Anscombe, Principal Officer, Geographic Information Systems\tCatchment Abstraction Management Strategies (CAMS) assess the amount of water available in each river catchment.   The CAMS Boundaries are an external reference dataset giving an indication of where technical assessments of water abstraction have been undertaken.   CAMS helps to look at the balance between society the economy and the environment. The technical assessment helps identify where water may be available for future use but also where water resource demands may be impacting the water balance.   The CAMS boundaries should be used on a national (Wales) scale to show the geographical distribution of CAMS and are not suitable for detailed technical assessments.\tPublic record\n18702\tNRW_DS123309\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.363Z\tWIRS\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333039\tx:\\Incidents\\Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS)\\WIRS.lyr\tSean Moore\tSee  metadata entry 117602 This GIS layer is likely to be an extract from the WIRS database\tPrivate record\n17747\tNRW_DS110336\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.312Z\tMonitoring of golden hair lichen (Teloschistes flavicans) in Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333336\tArchived as project no 99 (media no 374). Report No. 237 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media301 to 400\\Media374)\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tTeloschistes flavicans the golden haired lichen is a species formally widespread in the UK but now mainly found only in South-West England and Wales. It is included as \u0027vulnerable\u0027 in the Red Data Book for British Lichens and is included on schedule 8 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act. It is a species particularly sensitive to sulphur dioxide air pollution. This dataset includes the results of a resurvey of Teloschistes flavicans in South Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996. A comprehensive survey of all known Teloschistes flavicans sites was undertaken in Pembrokeshire in 1994. It was considered likely that the lichen might be detrimentally affected by air-borne pollutants arising from the Sea Empress oil spill and consequently a resurvey was commissioned.\tPublic record\n18701\tNRW_DS123307\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.411Z\tOil spill dispersant advice\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333037\tx:\\Incidents\\Oil Spills\\Oil spill dispersant advice.lyr\tAndrew Hill\t\tPrivate record\n17655\tNRW_DS110017\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.492Z\tTemporal variation and monitoring of important lagoonal communities and species in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303137\tData is archived as Project No. 9 and Media No. 153\tInternal Contact: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of a synthesis collation and review of knowledge pertaining to coastal saline lagoon sites in Wales and the important species recorded at these lagoons undertaken by the Natural History Museum on behalf of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The scope was to understand:  - The habitat resource requirements of the species  - The scales of natural variation in the species and in their habitat resource requirements - The scales of natural variation in the habitats themselves and   - To review monitoring and surveillance work in similar habitats and species. This work will allow the development of Standard Operating Procedures for condition monitoring the lagoons.\tPublic record\n17381\tNRW_DS24328\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.086Z\tPembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas (PMSA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333238\tData is archived under project no. 109 and 156, and media no. 382 and 394. Species Data is held in Marine Recorder.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas  Some of the paper MNCR forms are held in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/1, Box CCW/MNCR/2, Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tMonica Jones, Data Services Team Leader, EPP, NRW\tThis datasets acts as an atlas of marine species for Pembrokeshire including flora and fauna from the intertidal and sublittoral marine zones funded by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  The purpose of this data capture was to collate and make accessible all historical marine species data for Pembrokeshire between 1899 and 1998. The majority of records are from 1956 to 1998 and this dataset is now classed as static. This dataset is a collation of data from various CCW and third party sources (both published and unpublished) that cover Pembrokeshire. It is comprised of 79 surveys ( available to download on NBN Gateway see resource locator). This is a historical collation which is not being updated.  The data includes all records from the Dale Fort Marine Fauna survey plus additional data from subsequent and prior surveys.  Data that are included in the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNRC) database have been excluded from this dataset to avoid duplication. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Marine Recorder which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tPublic record\n19172\tNRW_DS125722\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.910Z\tKieco Marine Fish - Geospatial\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373232\tData is held in KiEco stored on the WISKI database - an FME process creates a GIS layer which are stored here:  X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Fish Individually Measured.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Fish Site Details.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Fish Total Counts.lyr\tPaul Hyatt, Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting\tMarine fish are used as an indicator of stock status water and habitat quality. This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the fish surveys conducted across Wales marine environment by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  This metadata was created for the geospatial data for KiEco Marine Fish - for species data that is downloadable from the NBN please see NRW_DS125741\tPublic record\n17490\tNRW_DS100503\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.733Z\tArea of Search\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030353033\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Areas of search.lyr\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a spatial dataset consisting of the former Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) categories for surveying and following vice County boundaries.\tPublic record\n18222\tNRW_DS116245\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.310Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwater bodies Cycle 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323435\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Groundwaterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwater bodies Cycle 1 is a spatial (polygon) dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).  Article 2 clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.  For the purposes of reporting under the WFD a groundwater body represent a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries.  These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties recharge and vulnerability to pollution.  This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale. Since water bodies are attributed with a unique identifier (EA_WB_ID) this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics risk classification and proposed objectives.\tPublic record\n17922\tNRW_DS114189\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.811Z\tGround Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay (2010 - 2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134313839\tData is archived under project no 301 (media 1211).\tInternal Custodians - Marine Ornithologist, NRW - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata Contact: - Data Services\tCarmarthen Bay Spatial Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16000 individuals) of the biogeographic population of Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra). The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs especially during the winter although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps) worms echinoderms isopods amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs which are found in abundance in the shallow water in Carmarthen Bay. Common scoter are also listed as a Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has supported ground based counts since 1996 following the Sea Empress oil spill. This work feeds in to the condition monitoring and surveillance of Carmarthen Bay SPA and contributes to Common Standards Monitoring.\tPublic record\n19110\tNRW_DS125448\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.037Z\tA survey and assessment of the saproxylic invertebrate fauna of Gregynog National Nature Reserve, Powys - 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343438\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID) in Recorder 6\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe Gregynog Hall Estate comprises about 300ha of ancient wood pasture habitats within the old county of Montgomeryshire. The 15ha of Great Wood were designated a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for its rich epiphytic lichen communities in 1978 although it later proved to also have significant interest for invertebrates and especially those species associated with the decaying wood (saproxylic) habitats provided by the abundance of veteran trees. The SSSI was extended in 2011 and the whole estate declared a National Nature Reserve in 2012.   A survey of the saproxylic invertebrates of the wider NNR was commissioned by Natural Resources Wales in 2022. This is the first baseline survey of the fauna previous research comprising exploratory surveys only. The aims of the survey were to determine the current condition of the saproxylic invertebrate fauna and to identify any threats.\tPublic record\n17642\tNRW_DS109614\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.022Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sea Temperature Recording\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039363134\tSkomer MNR West Region drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic/temperature\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Monitoring of the seabed and temperature at Skomer MNR.  The purpose of this data capture was to:  Provide accurate seawater temperature records for the seabed and the water column.  Record temperature as continuously as possible.  Produce an ongoing long-term data set for the site.\tPublic record\n17628\tNRW_DS109815\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.862Z\tIntertidal Climate Change Monitoring (MarClim)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383135\tThe reports and data are archived as Project No 262 to 267 and 303, Media No 958 to 962, 964, 1215 and 1409 at CCW headquarters.   A working copy of the data is held at: H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intertidal Climate Change. Since 2013, data and reports are held on the NRW Document Management System\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe MarClim project was established in 2001 to investigate changes that had occurred in rocky intertidal systems within the last 50 years around the UK. MarClim established a low-cost network of sites covering England Wales and Scotland which provided subsequent annual updates to track how climate influences the marine biodiversity of the British Isles.   This dataset consists of the Welsh coverage of MarClim surveys only. Since 2005 Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has continued working with the MBA through a Partnership Agreement. The annual survey in Wales forms part of a longer thirteen-year continuous UK survey. Geographical coverage includes sites throughout north mid and southwest Wales for which historical data dating back to the 1950s exist and additional sites where range extensions have been predicted to occur. Thirty seven sites were surveyed in north Wales and seven sites in south Wales. MarClim has been highlighted in a global scientific assessment of the need for scientific research to be appropriately designed to provide fit-for-purpose information at relevant spatial and temporal scales useful to managers of protected areas statutory bodies and policymakers.    The main aims at the outset of the MarClim project in 2001 remain as follows: - To use existing historical information and collect new data on intertidal indicator species from the last 50-100 years to develop and test hypotheses on the impact of climatic change on marine biodiversity in Britain and Ireland.  - To forecast future marine community changes on the basis of the Met Office’s Hadley Centre climate change models and the United Kingdom Climate Impacts Partnership’s climate change scenarios. The broad range of species known or likely to be temperature sensitive was covered.  - To establish low-cost fit-for-purpose methodologies and networks to provide subsequent regular updates and track how climate influences the marine biodiversity of Britain and Ireland.  - To provide general contextual time series data to support reporting on the success or otherwise of the Marine Strategy Framework Directive marine aspects of Biodiversity Action Plans European initiatives including the Habitats Birds and Water Framework Directives (through appropriate Regulations) and management and monitoring of marine activities and resources including fisheries and Special Areas of Conservation.  - To evaluate whether the climate indicator species used in this work have a wider contribution to make as part of the sustainability indicators that are needed to underpin the UK sustainable development strategy.  - To disseminate the results widely and accordingly elucidate the known impact climate has had on marine biodiversity over the last 100 years and may have in the future.  - To provide a basis for the development of a proposal for European Commission funding to establish a pan-European network with related aims.  - To assess and report on the likely consequences of the predicted changes in response to climate for society for commercial and non-commercial users of the marine environment and the policies and frameworks that conserve manage and protect marine biodiversity.\tPublic record\n17463\tNRW_DS99287\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.808Z\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Boundaries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939323837\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Geological Conservation Review sites.lyr   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\geology\\CCW\\GCR_Site_Boundaries_Original.TAB   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\geology\\CCW\\gcrsites_north.TAB Held by JNCC and CCW (in Hafod Elfyn basement registry)\tInternal Custodian: (Raymond Roberts) Regional Earth Science Officers, NRW (info on individual sites) Hannah Brazier Cartography Officer, PKCG, NRW (collation and digitisation of data)  Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe dataset contains the original site boundaries for all Geological Conservation Review (GCR) Sites in Wales.  The boundaries define the area of a particular GCR Site (usually coincident with the geological feature as defined by Common Standards Monitoring). Proposed current and denotified boundaries are included. Records originally collected to show the extent of GCR blocks.  These blocks represent important geological or geomorphological topics (e.g. Mineralogy) geological time divisions stages and series (e.g. Llandeilo) and areas of search (e.g. South Wales and the Mendips).\tPublic record\n18328\tNRW_DS116536\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.291Z\tOpen Access - Other Statutory Access Land (CRoW Act)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353336\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Other Statutory Access Land 2014.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Other Statutory Access Land 2004.lyr\tChris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access, Open Access Team / Recreation \u0026amp; Partnerships Team\tThe CRoW Act provides for public access on foot to certain types of land amends the law relating to public rights of way increases measures for the management and protection for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and strengthens wildlife enforcement legislation and provides for better management of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB).   Under Section 4 of the CRoW Act it states the following: It shall be the duty of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW now forms part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW))  to prepare in respect of Wales maps which together show; (a) all registered common land and (b) all open country.    This dataset contains all Common Land with a higher right of access (Urban Commons Commons with Deeds for Access 1899 Commons Act) for areas of Wales and Elan Valley Estate. This dataset does not include the Registered Common Land dataset.   Digitisation took place in 2014 for all Wales.   When using this data for Open Access it should be used in conjunction with Open Country Registered Common Land and Public Forests from the same years. Contact your local authority for access to and information about Registers of Common Land.\tPublic record\n17308\tEXT_DS125658\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.685Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): GeoScour Open Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363538\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS Geoscour Tier 1 and BGS Geoscour Tier 2  (7 layers)\tCustodian - GIS Team  Internal Contact - Oliver Lowe\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) is a UK provider of geoscientific data information and knowledge. Natural Resources Wales has downloaded this open data for the Wales extent to use internally in our mapping system.  The BGS GeoScour Open dataset provide a generalised overview of the natural characteristics and properties of catchment and riverine environments for the assessment of river scour in Great Britain.  The dataset comprises two different tiers of geographical information system data containing seven different data layers. Each tier represents a different scale of assessment from a high-level catchment to subcatchment data. The datasets are polygon (area) layers which are described using straightforward classifications and enabling an indicative catchment susceptibility assessment.\tPublic record\n18846\tNRW_DS124627\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.072Z\tNRW North Flood Warning Duty Officer Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363237\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW North Flood Warning Duty Officer Areas.lyr\tEirian Gilford, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Team Leader, NRW Gwyn Moseley, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Advisor 2, NRW\tAreas covered by the various Flood Warning Duty Officers with regards to flood monitoring forecasting and dissemination of alerts/warnings\tPrivate record\n19318\tNRW_DS161277\t2024-07-15T12:55:52.058Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Lake Macroinvertebrates - Geospatial\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323737\tData Held in Wiski-KiEco - a FME Report has been created to export this data to the GIS.  X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Lake Macro-invertebrates Sample Metric.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Lake Macro-invertebrates Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Lake Macro-invertebrates Taxa.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jennifer Owen, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tFreshwater macroinvertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality and Lake flow requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance.   This dataset contains site sample metric and taxa information from freshwater Lake macroinvertebrate surveys carried out  across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales since 1997 and onwards. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of Lake water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business needs.\tPublic record\n17917\tNRW_DS114388\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.566Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria reef in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333838\tData is archived under: Project 190, media 1219\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats). CCW developed a programme of intertidal monitoring work across Wales during 2004 and 2005. These surveys were managed and implemented for CCW by the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS University of Hull). These projects focused on a wide range of sensitive habitats such as Zostera muddy gravels caves rock pools algal dominated rocky shores Sabellaria reefs under-boulders and various rare habitats and species.   Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd. (ASML) have been contracted by CCW to continue development and management of the intertidal monitoring programme for each marine SAC for the period 2006 to 2011 working as a team with CCW HQ and CCW Regional staff.\tPublic record\n17787\tNRW_DS110482\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.297Z\tUpland Vegetation Survey of the Elenydd Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343832\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive \u0026amp; with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tThe purpose of this survey was to map and describe the vegetation of twelve holdings in Elenydd Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in mid Wales. These surveys were commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in order to map and describe the vegetation of two areas within Elenydd SSSI. Survey were carried out in August 1998 August 1999 and May 2002.\tPrivate record\n18740\tNRW_DS123395\t2024-05-28T12:55:19.616Z\tPenrhyn Gwyn Several Order mussel monitoring stations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333935\tX:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Fishery casework\\Penrhyn Gwyn SO mussel monitoring stations.lyr\tPaul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17073\tEXT_DS99645\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.934Z\tWelsh Government: Tir Cymen Open Access Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363435\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_cymen\\tir_cymen_open_access_areas.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tThis spatial dataset is owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to NRW for internal use only. Tir Cymen is an agri-environment scheme.\tPublic record\n17070\tEXT_DS24320\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.588Z\tFuturecoast (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333230\tArchived as Project No. 426, Media No. 1459, 1460, 1461, 1462.\tGIS Service Desk Terrestrial Science Group, NRW External Custodian: Beth Greenaway (020) 72381587 beth.greenaway@defra.gsi.gov.uk\tIn 2000 the Department for Environment Food and Rural Affairs (DEFRA) and the National Assembly for Wales commissioned Halcrow to develop Futurecoast a completely new way of predicting shoreline evolution. The analysis it contains gives a better understanding of coastal systems and their characteristics and it is now being used to create the next generation of Shoreline Management Plans.  The main format is oblique aerial photos taken from helicopter of the entire GB coastline linked to the Ordnance Survey map.  They were produced by DEFRA in conjunction with Halcro expressly to assist in the shoreline management planning process.  A \u0027behavioural systems\u0027 approach such as was adopted by the FUTURECOAST project involves the identification of the different elements that make up the coastal structure and developing an understanding of how these elements interact on a range of both temporal and spatial scales. In this exercise it is the interaction between the units that is central to determining the behaviour. Feedback invariably plays an important role and changes in energy/sediment inputs that affect one unit can in turn affect other units which themselves give rise to a change in the level of energy/sediment input.  Whilst the starting point for a behavioural system is the energy and sediment pathways it is important to identify the causative mechanism as a basis for building a robust means of predicting the response to change. This must take account of variations in sediment supply and forcing parameters such as tide and wave energy. However it is also important to look for situations where the system response is to switch to a different state for example the catastrophic failure of a spit or the switching of channels as a consequence of episodic storm events.\tPublic record\n18481\tNRW_DS121590\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.212Z\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G East Felling Plan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353930\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G East Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPublic record\n18235\tNRW_DS116240\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.617Z\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323430\tx:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NVZ Nitrate Vulnerable Zones 2013.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NVZ Nitrate Vulnerable Zones 2010.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NVZ Nitrate Vulnerable Zones 2008.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor, Water Quality Policy\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZs) were areas within Wales that contain surface water or groundwater that is susceptible to nitrate pollution from agricultural activities. They were designated in 2013 accordance with the requirements of the European Commission’s Nitrates Directive 91/676/EEC which aimed to protect water quality across Europe by preventing nitrates from agricultural sources polluting ground and surface waters and by promoting the use of good farming practices. In April 2021 the designated NVZs in Wales were revoked by the introduction of the Water Resources (Control of Agricultural Pollution)(Wales) Regulations with some measures transitioning into law over a period of time. The transition periods do not apply to those farms located in previously designated NVZ  where all the measures within CoAPR apply  immediately. This dataset is relevant until all measures transition into law.\tPublic record\n18247\tNRW_DS116283\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.703Z\tWoodland Dedications\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323833\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Dedications.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThe Dedication woodland Scheme (Basis I and II) was introduced in 1947 in order to encourage landowners to retain their land in forestry and to introduce good forestry practice. Basis III was introduced in 1974 providing grants for new planting and additional supplements for broadleaves. The Dedication Scheme was closed to new applicants in 1981. Land still under Dedication could continue to be within the scheme but Dedication would terminate on a change of ownership. Dedication schemes without a Plan of Operations and therefore receiving no grant are deemed to be under Negative Covenant.\tPublic record\n19045\tNRW_DS125346\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.325Z\tClwyd Pond Survey 1979 (paper survey records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333436\tOff site archive storage, ref: CCW-NRM/R-53\t\tThis is a paper format survey of pond species and habitat of the surrounding area which was undertaken during 1979. Photos of the ponds are also included.\tPrivate record\n17652\tNRW_DS110016\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.394Z\tBreeding Seabird Surveys of South-west Wales Colonies 1996-1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303136\tArchived as Project 66, Media 39.\tGIS Service Desk  Senior Reserve Manager, NRW\tSouth-west Wales has nationally and internationally important populations of nine species of seabirds and a total of thirteen regular breeding species. Following the Sea Empress oil spill at Milford Haven in February 1996 a census of seabird colonies in south-west Wales was initiated in order to assess the impact of the oil spill on population of seabirds breeding in the affected area. This dataset is compiled of the results of breeding seabird monitoring at colonies in south-west Wales in 1996 1997 and 1998 the three seabird breeding seasons following the Sea Empress oil spill of February 1996.  Data collected in all three years of the project have been compared with data collected in previous years in order to provide an assessment of the impact of the oil spill on local populations of ten species of breeding seabirds (Fulmar Cormorant Shag Lesser Black-backed Gull Herring Gull Great Black-backed Gull Kittiwake Guillemot Razorbill and Puffin).  Data collected for monitoring and comparison purposes to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on local populations of breeding seabirds.\tPublic record\n17790\tNRW_DS110511\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.357Z\tMonitoring and Surveillance of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353131\t\tInternal Custodian: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tA variety of monitoring and surveillance projects are carried out on SSSIs across Wales.  Geographic coverage: SSSIs throughout Wales\tPrivate record\n17494\tNRW_DS100338\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.373Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland_Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333338\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_sites.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing sets of survey site boundaries recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (1987-2004). The Phase 2 lowland Grassland Survey of Wales was a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. The chief aims of the Phase 2 survey were to characterise the semi natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed. Sites varied in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering. The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and 2013 by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPublic record\n17901\tNRW_DS112401\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.482Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal habitat diversity\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343031\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the intertidal range is measured using biotope richness biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is utilised to present these metrics. Biotope information for the Welsh intertidal zone was collated from CCW and is full coverage collected using standardised methods.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish intertidal habitat diversity from known data. The Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tPublic record\n18366\tNRW_DS116802\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.016Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Canals Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383032\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle 2\\WFD Canals Cycle 2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Canals Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS.      N.B. The cycle 1 dataset is not available on DataMapWales as unlike Cycle 2 data, cycle 1 data is derived from Third Party Data (Canals trust)\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the canal waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes canals which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline and can be linked to other WFD data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID).   \u0027WFD River canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 2\u0027 is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.   Updated to 2018 version.\tPublic record\n17913\tNRW_DS112114\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.094Z\tOpen Access - Dedicated Forests (CRoW Act)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132313134\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Dedicated Forests 2014.lyr X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Dedicated Forests 2004.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gwen Thomas, Advisor (2) Estates Business Systems   Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tDataset showing forests that comprised the freehold public forest estate dedicated by former Forestry Commission Wales (FCW) now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under section 16 of the Countryside and Rights of Way (CRoW) Act in 2005. Some forest that was dedicated has since been sold and is now in private ownership. When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access  it should be used in conjunction with Open land Open Country Registered Common Land Other Statutory Access Land and Other Dedicated Land from the same years.\tPublic record\n18698\tNRW_DS123303\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.397Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Diatoms\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333033\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data.\tInternal Custodian:  Advisor 2 - Data Services Team.  Tim Jones/Graham Rutt - Enivironmental Reporting Team  SMEs Graham Rutt and Mel Lacan       Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tFreshwater diatoms (phytobenthos) are free-living microscopic plants that form a biofilm on submerged substrates such as rock/stone or large plants. They are used as an indicator of water quality because their silica shells are easy to identify under the microscope. This dataset is made up of the relative abundance of different species within the sample from freshwater diatom surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\tPublic record\n17473\tNRW_DS99408\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.357Z\tMarine Recorder Sample Point Data (Spatial  Export)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343038\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine_Recorder\\Marine_Recorder_Biotopes_WGS84.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine_Recorder\\Marine_Recorder_Species_WGS84.lyr\tMetadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team / GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial export of all welsh sample points held within the Marine Recorder database. Not to be distributed outside NRW without the prior permission of the Master Reference Data Team. This is essentially a collation of data from many different marine biological surveys held within Marine Recorder. It contains all biotope instances and species data within the Wales Territorial Sea (12 nautical miles offshore) plus some instances beyond where relevant to boundaries such as the Dee and Severn estuaries.\tPublic record\n18177\tNRW_DS116234\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.459Z\tFlood Map: Areas Benefiting from Flood Defences (archived data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323334\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Areas Benefitting from Flood Defence.lyr  Extracted from the FloodGeodatabase\tCustodian: Wyn Davies, Flood Risk Manager (Asset Management), Flood and Incident Management, NRW\tThis dataset has been superceded by the \"Areas Benefitting from Flood Defences\" dataset (NRW_DS124843) which is displayed within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map.   The Flood Map is a spatial dataset that shows the areas across Wales that could be affected by flooding from rivers or the sea. It also shows flood defences and the areas that benefit from them.   Flood Map is designed to raise awareness among the public local authorities and other organisations of the likelihood of flooding and to encourage people living and working in areas prone to flooding to find out more and take appropriate action.   The Flood Map includes several layers of information this layer is: Areas Benefiting from Flood Defences which shows those areas that would benefit from the presence of defences in a 1 percent fluvial / 0.5 percent tidal flood event.\tPublic record\n18223\tNRW_DS116243\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.294Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323433\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the WFD. Article 2 clause 7 of the WFD defines coastal waterbodies as \u0027...a surface water on the landward side of a line every point of which is at a distance of one nautical mile on the seaward side from the nearest point of the baseline from which the breadth of territorial waters is measured extending where appropriate up to the outer limit of transitional waters\u0027. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics risk classification and proposed objectives.   This dataset covers the layer for Cycle 1 of the Water framework Directive (2008 - 2013). The equivalent layer for Cycle 2 is covered under WFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2.\tPublic record\n18482\tNRW_DS121566\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.235Z\tCarmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Atlantic Salt Meadows Monitoring (2017)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353636\tGIS Data: Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Team_SouthWest\\Lily_Pauls\\Lily_Pauls.gdb\\SaltmarshSurveys\\all_saltmarsh_points2_text\tInternal custodian: Lily Pauls, Marine Team, SW Ops, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThis dataset consists of an excel spreadsheet containing the sampling point data for Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Saltmarsh 2012 condition assessment survey. The survey covered saltmarsh in the Burry Inlet Loughor and Three Rivers. Data recorded included saltmarsh plant species % cover negative indicators and sward heights. The GIS layer shows the location of the points referred to within the Excel spreadsheet.\tPublic record\n18528\tNRW_DS121627\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.616Z\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) B Proposed Turbines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363237\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA B Proposed Turbines.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tPublic record\n18398\tNRW_DS118981\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.054Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Rhiannon Modiolus Survey 2015: Video and Still Image Analysis\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138393831\tArchived as Project 476, Media 1553 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1501-1600\\Media 1553) and as Marine Recorder Survey MRCCW31600000018 Currently all project data stored at V:\\MMT video Archive\\Drop video\\2015\\Rhiannon Modiolus DDV until moved to archive (this appears to have happened).\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tModiolus modiolus (Horse Mussel) reef is an OSPAR threatened and declining habitat and a component of Annex I Reef.  In Wales two Modiolus reefs have been well described to date off the North Lleyn Peninsula. In 2008 the EIR Grid East West Interconnector Project conducted a survey approximately 10nm North of Anglesey and identified an area of seabed with an acoustic signature indicative of biogenic Modiolus modiolus reef. The presence of reef was later confirmed by drop down video survey. In 2012 surveys for the proposed Rhiannon Offshore Windfarm in the same location also identified several areas with an acoustic signal indicative of Modiolus reef.  NRW undertook some survey work in October 2015 to collect video and still images from areas identified as potential Modiolus reef by the two previous commercial projects in welsh waters. This report provides a detailed description of the method a summary of the results and some initial conclusions from the study\tPrivate record\n17751\tNRW_DS110344\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.343Z\tBroadleaved Woodland in North Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333434\t1) Raw data- original copies held in CCW North Wales regional offices, photocopies of parts of the dataset held by Jim Latham at CCW Headquarters. 2) Paper reports- held in CCW libraries (Regional and HQ- see related titles field).\tJim Latham, Woodland and Spatial Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW\tA historically important dataset underpinning woodland site designation in North Wales. 260 woodland sites surveyed across North Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to survey and evaluate in terms of their nature conservation interest all predominantly broadleaved woods in North Wales using nationally developed standard methods and evaluation criteria. The data collected helped to identify the \u0027best\u0027 examples and assisted in management and protection of the woods by making it possible to view them in context within an overall classification.\tPublic record\n18894\tNRW_DS124791\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.203Z\tNational Flood Risk Maps 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373931\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk from Rivers.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk from Surface Water and Small Watercourses.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk from the Sea.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk CaRR.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh / GIS and Spatial Data Team   Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh / Simon James / Dave Tarrant\tThe National Flood Hazard and Risk Maps are based on generalised modelling only. The maps have been published to comply with the Flood Risk Regulations (2009) and the EU Directive (2007/60/EC) and used to inform the creation of Flood Risk Management plans across Wales. These maps have no official status for Planning or Insurance purposes therefore the public and professionals are advised to use the information contained within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Map and Development Advice Map/ Flood Map for Planning (as appropriate) for these purposes as these will be more up-to-date and incorporate improved modelling information.   The National Flood Risk Maps have been created for 3 sources of flooding namely  1. Flooding from Rivers 2. Flooding from the Sea 3. Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourse  The maps show Risk to a range of receptors grouped in categories of People Economic and Environment. Risk information is aggregated and displayed at a community scale.  For Rivers and Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses High risk is up to 1 in 30year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 and 1 in 100year; and Low risk is between 1 in 100year and 1 in 1000year Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP).  For the Sea High risk is up to 1 in 30 year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 to 1 in 200 year; and Low risk 1 in 200 year to 1 in 1000 year.\tPublic record\n18407\tNRW_DS118344\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.317Z\tA Survey for the Caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog, Anglesey (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138333434\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive  [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Ian Wallace)\tA survey of Cors Erddreiniog National Nature Reserve (NNR) from July to September 2016 failed to find the caddis Limnephilus tauricus (classified as Vulnerable in the UK) at its only Welsh locality. First found on the reserve in 2006 in a small area of permanently wet gently-flowing spring-fed fen supporting an extensive bed of Yellow Flag Iris pseudacorus small numbers of adults were also found at the same station in 2007 and 2008. No larvae have been found to date. Wider searches of apparently suitable habitat on Cors Erddreiniog in 2008 and 2016 proved fruitless. Whilst the vegetation at the original station looked very similar to the situation in 2006-08 the water flowing through the site was less clear and appeared to contain more iron flocculence. Further searches of Cors Erddreiniog NNR are required to determine the current status of Limnephilus tauricus.\tPublic record\n18804\tNRW_DS124099\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.030Z\tRoad verges Designated RVNR\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234303939\tx:\\Biological\\Road verges Designated RVNR.lyr\tIsobella Griffiths\t\tPrivate record\n19034\tNRW_DS125215\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.614Z\tLarge Raised Reservoir register (Locations and undertakers)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323135\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Reservoirs\tMatthew O\u0027Brien\tThis dataset contains details of Large Raised Reservoirs in Wales.     Natural Resources Wales (NRW) collects and maintains data on all reservoirs designed or capable of holding more than 10000 cubic metres of water above the natural level of any part of the land adjoining them defined as \u0027large raised reservoirs\u0027 under the Reservoirs Act 1975. Two types of reservoir are maintained within the register:   - Impounding; or  - Non-Impounding.    This dataset is an extract from NRW’s Cronfeydd/Reservoir Database. It contains information on the location undertakers and engineers of the reservoirs.    \u0027Undertaker\u0027 is the legal term used to identify who manages and controls the use of the reservoir and is responsible for maintaining compliance with the law. The undertaker is the person people or company that uses a reservoir for a particular purpose. If there is no use the owners or lessees are the undertakers. The undertaker may be different from the owner. If several people or organisations manage and control the reservoir there can be more than one undertaker.\tPublic record\n19016\tNRW_DS125226\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.362Z\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitat Features\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323236\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2007,   X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2012,   X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2018,\tDave Johnston Team Leader (on secondment), Environmental Reporting.   Geralene Mills, Team Leader,  Environmental Reporting.\tThe Habitats Directive Article 17 reporting maps are a snapshot of the most up to date spatial data for features listed on the various Annexes of the Directive at the time of reporting. They represent the current known extent and location of features in Wales. The latest 10km2 habitat distribution maps submitted as part of the official Article 17 reporting can be obtained from the JNCC: European reporting | JNCC - Adviser to Government on Nature Conservation.   The maps are reviewed and updated every 6 years as part of the Habitats Directive reporting process. (Since EU exit now replaced by Regulation 9a of the Conservation and Species Regulations 2017).\tPublic record\n18655\tNRW_DS123285\t2024-05-28T12:53:50.754Z\tFluvial addresses at risk\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323835\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2017.lyr\tMark Pugh\t\tPrivate record\n18741\tNRW_DS123387\t2024-05-28T12:55:19.694Z\tClimate Vulnerability Assessment (CVA) for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333837\tx:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\CVA SSSI.lyr\tContact Clive Walmsley, Senior Specialist Advisor, Climate Change and Decarbonisation, SPG for further information about the data.   Data originally produced and provided by Jon Rothwell, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17218\tEXT_DS122402\t2024-05-28T12:17:03.627Z\tDŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water: Reservoir Asset Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343032\tCronfeydd Database\tMatt O\u0027Brian is the custodian\tThis dataset is owned by Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water and licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use. Welsh Water owns 91 reservoirs varying in size from 2 acres to 1026 acres.\tPublic record\n18352\tNRW_DS116480\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.344Z\tMacrophyte Survey of Welsh Lakes 2015\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343830\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Leafpacs calculator files within folder [Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt; Internal Research \u0026gt; Lake Leafpacs data and spreadsheets \u0026gt; ENSIS Surveys 2015] in Microsoft Excel format [Lake_name_LEAFPACS 2 0_metriccalculator_Month_Year] and summary information in MS Excel format [Site_metrics_Summary_2015.xls].\tInternal: Tristan Hatton-Ellis,  Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, NRW\tThis project set out to collect process and supply to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) ecological and environmental data from a network of 19 lake sites across Wales in support of NRW\u0027s integrated monitoring programme for protected sites (Special Areas of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)) the Water Framework Directive Nitrates Directive Biodiversity Action Plans and other legislative and policy drivers. In particular the surveys are aimed at informing management and restoration of protected sites and facilitating delivery of River Basin Management Plans.  Using standard methods lakes were surveyed to assess the species and abundance of aquatic plants growing within and directly around the lake and to measure water clarity dissolved oxygen and temperature within the lakes.  The results of the aquatic plant surveys are suitable for the purposes of assessing site condition for Habitats Directive standing water features and SSSI status. The lake metrics are applicable for the production of ecological quality ratios from which the lakes may be classified in accordance with the requirements of Water Framework Directive (2000/60/EC).\tPublic record\n18246\tNRW_DS116285\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.638Z\tNational Inventory of Woodland and Trees\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323835\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Inventory of Woodland \u0026amp; Trees (GB) - 2002.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset was created by Forestry Commission Wales (FCW) and now remains as a static archive of the data. This file contains Forestry Commission\u0027s previous survey the National Inventory of Woodland and Trees and includes spatial data and associated metadata for the whole of Great Britain. This dataset shows all areas of woodland over 2ha in Great Britain and the Interpreted Forest Type (IFT) of those woodlands. The dataset includes FC new planting and new Woodland Grant Schemes as of 31st March 2002.\tPublic record\n19161\tNRW_DS125685\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.595Z\tCardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363835\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Barry and Cardiff Site Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Barry Hedgerow Survey Results.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Barry Hedgerows.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Cardiff and Caerphilly Hedherows.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Cardiff and Caerphilly Survey Results.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Invasive Species Target Notes.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Photo Points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Veteran Trees.lyr\tLiz Hancocks - Senior Officer, People and Places\tIn order to further an understanding of and improve connectivity in the landscape Natural Resources Wales commissioned a survey of the hedgerow network around Cardiff in two areas to the North and South West of Cardiff. A total of 696 hedgerows were surveyed in November and December 2022 using a bespoke survey pro forma based primarily on the DEFRA hedgerow survey handbook.  This dataset compromises of a range of maps that are based on analysis of aerial imagery using AI.\tPublic record\n18711\tNRW_DS123363\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.169Z\tGwent Levels assessed ditches\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333633\tx:\\Monitoring\\Wetland\\Gwent Levels assessed ditches.lyr\tKerry Murton\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n20190\tNRW_DS161333\t2025-02-04T10:51:51.583Z\tBaseline Monitoring of Peatland Invertebrate on the Hafod Elwy - Cord Goch Peatland Restoration Site 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333333\t\tBethan Beech\t\tPrivate record\n19169\tNRW_DS125640\t2024-05-28T13:04:11.020Z\tFindings of drop down video and stills imagery from an area of drumlins off Northwest Anglesey, July 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363430\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\North Anglesey Drumlin Survey 2019\tLaura Grant, Specialist Advisor – Marine Ecology\tThis data contains the raw products of a seabed survey of an area of drumlins off north west Anglesey.  The images and video was taken by a Natural Resources Wales owned drop down camera system to provide a better understanding of the seabed structure and biological communities at the survey sites and to investigate the presence of non-native species. The data contains Video (.asf) (and still images (.JPG) The data was collected on the 11th July 2019. The a copy of the MR snapshot produced after data analysis by the contractor is also supplied here.  The analysis of the video and stills imagery followed guidelines provided by NRW Marine EcoSol’s in-house standard operating procedures and JNCC/NMBAQC recommendations. The video camera showed a swath of seabed approximately 0.3m wide while still images showed an area estimated as about 0.13m2. All images were reviewed using appropriate high-quality imaging equipment capable of playing and displaying full HD video at 1920p x 1080p resolution.     The video and set of stills from each tow were reviewed prior to detailed assessment and recording and the boundaries between biotopes or biotope complexes noted by recording the time and positional coordinates at which major changes in seabed and habitat type occurred. The species and substratum in each habitat ‘sample’ (tow section) was then recorded and biotopes assigned.  Discrete habitat tow sections or ‘samples’ as defined above were determined using  a combination of video and stills images. The lower quality video imagery was then linked to the ‘sample’ in terms of data recording using the time stamp on the video playback. Higher quality stills images were used to identify and quantify substrates and taxa with abundances pooled to assess the abundance of the taxa over each sample.\tPublic record\n17469\tNRW_DS99411\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.050Z\tMINESCAN\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343131\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\MINESCAN.lyr ;  Minescan spreadsheet currently not located (Jun 2022) but Raymond is looking for it after discussing with Gabi Williams as part of TNA project; might be on old CCW drive??  There is also a report that seems to contain info from the spreadsheet but this is not a CCW science report and only marked as National Museum and Galleries of Wales: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Geodiversity/grr/Mineralogy/MINESCAN%20%20An%20Introduction,%20Bevins%20\u0026amp;%20Mason,%201996.PDF\tRaymond Roberts\tThis is a spatial dataset containing point data of mine sites (metaliferous non-metaliferous) in Wales. Data taken from minescan spreadsheet data.  The purpose of this data capture was to form a spatial framework for MINESCAN sites. In 1996 the Minescan project ran for four years. This was commissioned by the former Countryside Council for Wales and John S. Mason undertook the project as a consultant to the National Museum and Galleries of Wales (NMGW). The project involved field assessments of the conservation value of over 1000 old mines and other mineralogical sites throughout Wales and the data obtained permitted an objective ranking of the sites relative to one another. As a primary result a comprehensive network of SSSI and RIGS sites was identified representing all aspects of mineralisation and metallogenesis in Wales. These sites have a real chance of survival as future scientific resources.\tPublic record\n17950\tNRW_DS114672\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.354Z\tApplication of Phase 1 Intertidal Survey Techniques to Monitoring\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363732\tArchived as project 315, media 1239. The report associated with this data is available in the Reports Archive - NRW\u0027s DMS.\tInternal Custodian : Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tThis dataset is part of the 1999 monitoring trials that became the basis for Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring in Wales; Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) Marine Monitoring Report No1. \u0027The establishment of a programme of surveillance and monitoring for judging the condition of the features of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau\". Relating to section 3.4.2  This was important for the notification of protected sites and also for planning conservation management. The purpose of this data capture was to support the designation and conservation planning of protected sites.  The purpose of condition monitoring on the site is essential to provide information which enables judgements to be made about whether the favourable condition of the features of interest for which this site was designated is is being achieved and so unable judgements to be made about the appropriateness of site management. This programme of survey has been stimulated by the EC habitats directive SAC designation requirements because the implementation of the directive requires that appropriate adjacent intertidal land must be notified as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) before it can be included as part of an SAC. Historically intertidal marine SSSIs have been notified on an ad hoc basis in Wales. However it was recognised a large scale notification of SSSIs linked to the habitats directive would need to be part of a series of sites covered a range of intertidal communities in Wales. In addition to this it was recognised that map data for the intertidal within the SACs would be invaluable for site management including sensitivity mapping and as a basis from which to develop future monitoring.  This dataset forms part of the Intertidal SAC monitoring program.\tPublic record\n17792\tNRW_DS110481\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.505Z\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Mynydd Eglwyseg, Denbighshire\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343831\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive \u0026amp; with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tTemporal coverage: 2000-2001\tPrivate record\n17327\tEXT_DS124787\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.499Z\tMet Office: Flood Forecasting Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373837\tNRWs Flood forecasting Service, currently licensed until 2023\tAndy Wall\tFlood forecasting data (observed and forecast) including Atmospheric Gridded Best Data. Data is provided to NRW to Inform the NRW Flood Warning service on decision making including input into flood forecasting models. Also used for event planning informing communications and scaling up of resources for operational resilience.\tPublic record\n17475\tNRW_DS99495\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.178Z\tUpland Habitat Survey - Habitat Features\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343935\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Level 1 all networks\\Upland Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\phase_1\\upland_survey\\habitat_survey_upland_hab.TAB\tInternal Custodian: Habitat and Ecosystems Resource Analyst, NRW.\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset is based largely on vegetation maps produced by the Wales Field Unit of the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) using the Ratcliffe and Birks vegetation classification. It also contains National Vegetation Classification (NVC) maps for parts of five upland sites and Phase 1 Survey information for three areas of Common Land. The Habitat layer is a point layer that shows the location of different habitats and habitat strings mapped in mosaic polygons on the Ratcliffe and Birks vegetation maps.  The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The GIS dataset was created as part of the remit of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to identify all areas of \u0027open country\u0027 in Wales for the implementation of The Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset has been combined with digitised data from the terrestrial Phase 1 survey to form the AllWales Phase 1 GIS dataset. All major Welsh upland sites and other upland areas not mapped by the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey.\tPublic record\n18842\tNRW_DS124671\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.072Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Crane Pad\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363731\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Crane Pad.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Crane Pad. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17406\tNRW_DS46159\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.343Z\tMonitoring of Horseshoe Bats with Automatic Bat Counters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313539\tAfter preliminary analysis the data are held in spreadsheets on NRW corporate document store (Ffynnon). H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\EIT\\Staff (ALL)\\Cyprian Payne\\Bats\tStuart Kay or Barnaby Letheren for technical questions Jean Matthews, Mammal Ecologist Bats and Riparian Mammals, NRW.\tAutomated bat counters have been deployed at various important roost sites for Greater and Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum and Rhinolophus hipposideros). These collect roost entrance and exit numbers for the bats as well as some limited environmental data. The purpose of this data capture was to obtain reliable population data in order to relate the changes in bat population and behaviour to changes in the climate and environment in an attempt to understand the factors that limit the geographical ranges of the 2 species. In Wales Greater Horseshoe Bats breed at the northern limit of their distribution and Lesser Horseshoe Bats breed up to the north coast of Wales where they are fairly common but they are not found at this latitude in England or the mainland of Europe. The sample roosts are within Special Areas of Conservation. The data gives valuable information on the seasonal activity of these bats which can be directly related to management within and around these key roost sites.\tPublic record\n17408\tNRW_DS46198\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.286Z\tCommon Standards Monitoring (CSM)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313938\tHigh level data are held centrally by NRW Environmental Change Group.  Supporting data are held in Regional NRW offices or by Science Groups.\tCustodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team.  Metadata: Data Services Team\tOne of the key responsibilities of the statutory nature conservation agencies in the UK like Natural Resources Wales is the identification and protection of a series of sites intended to conserve important wildlife and Earth science features. Such sites may be designated under: - National legislation (Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs)) - European Directives (Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs)) or - An international Convention (Ramsar sites).  The scope of monitoring includes coastal freshwater lowland grassland lowland heathland lowland wetland marine upland habitats woodland earth science (geodiversity) amphibians birds fish invertebrates reptiles marine mammals terrestrial mammals vascular plants bryophytes and lichens. The purpose of this data capture is to assess the condition of features on all designated sites (SSSI SAC SPA and Ramsar) based on site-specific performance indicators and to record aspects of their management both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales including UK through JNCC and for contributing to UK reports on Conservation Status under the EC Habitats Directive.\tPublic record\n17621\tNRW_DS109859\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.080Z\tNational Landscape Character Areas (NLCA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383539\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\National Landscape Character Areas.lyr  Written reports held in paper form in NRW libraries.\tMetadata and Data Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of boundaries for the 48 regional landscape character areas for Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to geographically distinguish different regions in Wales in terms of landscape identity and what characteristics and qualities make one region different and distinct from another.    These distinctions and policy drivers for this project are described together with an illustrated narrative and published on Natural Resources Wales (NRW\u0027s) website. This dataset covers 100% of terrestrial Wales including inter-tidal areas.  Boundary data derived from source data 2007-2008 with the final version being amended October 2008. Some area names were amended in August 2023 to match written profile documents and to reflect official status change for the names Eryri and Bannau Brycheiniog.\tPublic record\n19104\tNRW_DS125451\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.796Z\tForester Management Features\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343531\tForrester database\tGeraint Morngan - Specialist Advisor, Forester System\tThese datasets show the location of features within the national forest estate that are capture and stored for forestry management purposes such as for planning safety and access. This record covers the following features within the forest estate boundaries:  Crossing points - these will generally be electricity or telecom pylons. Cattle Grid Gates and Barrier Areas of open water - these will generally be fairly natural and permanent in character and not be created by dams Walls water courses - these will be natural water courses which will usually be permanent.\tPrivate record\n18452\tNRW_DS120746\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.164Z\tKiWQM Open Stations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230373436\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Open Stations.lyr  Source data is stored in WISKI\tSimon Webb, Stuart Kay - now Martin Stephens?       MJ  - I don\u0027t think the the access and use is correct in this entry as only third party data may be restricted, should look at the access and use constraint statements for the KiWQM overarching dataset  - GIS team to check\tThe KiWQM Sampling Stations GIS layer provides a route of discovery for the spatial element of all data relating to water quality measurements that have taken place in and around Wales. It is a record of locations where water sampling has taken place.  ***Replaced by metadata entry 120006**** which has created a combined open and closed station dataset\tPrivate record\n17654\tNRW_DS110018\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.577Z\tGeomorphological Response of Welsh Sand Dunes to Sea Level Rise over the Next 100 Years\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303138\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media101to200\\Media177 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, NRW - Maes y Ffynnon Data is archived Project Number 31 Media Number 177\tInternal Custodian: Senior Coastal Scientist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tResults of an assessment into the impact of predicted of sea level rise and climate change on Welsh dune systems over the next 100 years with particular emphasis on Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and certain other dune sites of key nature conservation importance. The main purpose was to assess the impact of predictions of sea level rise and climate change on Welsh dune systems over the next 100 years with particular emphasis on cSAC sites certain Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and other important nature conservation sites. The specific objectives of the study were to: -  Estimate future erosion/accretion rates and evolution of the dune systems -  Define the extent of sand dune habitat loss -  Assess the natural and anthropogenic constraints that may limit the ability of the dune systems to readjust -  Identify future management requirements and practices that will ensure the maintenance of the sand dune cSAC resource in Wales in terms of both quantity and quality\tPublic record\n17909\tNRW_DS111945\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.715Z\tBenthos of Cardigan Bay Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131393435\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000774)\tThe Custodians are in MFSG, Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW Contact Master Reference Data Team for more information.\tThis survey was intended as a follow up excercise to the RoxAnn ground truthing video drops carried out in 1997.  This dataset comprises of the results of a survey carried out by Ivor Rees to ground truth RoxAnn data collected in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tPublic record\n17832\tNRW_DS111179\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.970Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313739\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 555 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 555) [A] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 57 - Cardigan Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Data [C] NRWs copy of Marine Recorder - MRCCW16300000004 [D] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\GIS [E] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe purpose of this data capture was to establish sites for long-term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods. Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated primarily for Bottlenose dolphins but is also designated for Sandbanks Reefs and Sea Caves. Of these Reefs and Sea Caves are present within intertidal areas of Cardigan Bay SAC. Intertidal reefs in Cardigan Bay SAC include areas of bedrock and boulder/cobble habitats and many areas are characterised by biogenic reef formed by the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria. Many intertidal rockpool biotopes are considered to be important for their biodiversity (JNCC 1996). Rockpools are also a notable feature of some intertidal reef areas in the SAC. This dataset includes the results of a survey of intertidal rockpools at two sites within Cardigan Bay SAC.\tPublic record\n17077\tEXT_DS99475\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.905Z\tWelsh Government: Tir Gofal Whole Farm Boundary (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343735\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Points.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Poly.lyr    Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_gofal\\wf_signed\\tg_whole_farm.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a spatial dataset owned by Welsh Government detailing the extent and location of Tir Gofal which is a whole farm agri-environment scheme across Wales. This third party dataset is licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use only. Tir Gofal a whole farm agri-environment scheme for Wales encourages farmers throughout Wales to maintain and enhance the agricultural landscape and its wildlife and to provide new opportunities for people to visit the countryside. It replaces the previous schemes such as Environmentally Sensitive Areas and Tir Cymen and takes forward the experience gained in a single whole farm scheme.\tPublic record\n17844\tNRW_DS111165\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.536Z\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Chemical Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313635\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff.  The ECN data is also available from CEH. b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tVictoria Bowmaker, and Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, NRW.\tThis dataset is a collation of the chemical data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site - Yr Wyddfa. The 700ha site incorporates the summit of Yr Wyddfa and includes three additional summits over 800 m it also includes 5 lakes. Measurements taken for chemistry include atmospheric chemistry and precipitation chemistry. As well as the ECN monitoring protocols other data collected at the site includes the results of ammonia monitoring taking place at Dyffryn Mymbyr and monitoring of Ozone carried out at Marchlyn Mawr.  The purpose of this data capture is to use the observed changes in chemical variables to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the terrestrial environment.\tPublic record\n17067\tEXT_DS100183\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.480Z\tWelsh Government: Land Parcel Identification System (LPIS) Crop Codes (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313833\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\live\\Land_management\\agrienvironment\\WAG_DEPC\\lpis\\crop_codes_all.TAB\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the crop code to cross reference against field boundaries for the Land Parcel Identification System (LPIS).  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use only.\tPrivate record\n18390\tNRW_DS119078\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.411Z\tFull-coverage European Nature Information System (EUNIS) level 3 layer integrating maps from surveys and broad-scale models\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303738\tLocation no longer exists (changed since entry creation) Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Marine_Habitats_Species\\Broadscale habitats\\JNCC combined map\\Version 20160427\\MPA_Network_Output_Welsh.gdb\\CombinedEUNIS_CP2_Welsh_all_attr_WGS84  Data available via Lle Portal\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: Graeme.Duncan@jncc.gov.uk\tThe European Environment Agency (EEA) developed a classification scheme for habitats as part of its European Nature information System (EUNIS) system for managing species site and habitat information. As part of the classification for EUNIS data there are different levels. Level 1 splits off marine habitats from coastal and terrestrial habitats. Level 2 uses the biological zone and the presence / absence of rock as classification criteria so A1 is littoral rock and other hard substrata while A5 is Sublittoral sediment.  Level 3 introduces depth into the classification and energy for hard substrata and splits the softer substrata by different sediment types so A1.1 \u003d High-energy littoral rock while A5.4 \u003d Sublittoral mixed sediments. Some divisions in the classification are determined by biology here such as biogenic reef and large seaweed or plant dominated sediments. References to specific taxa rather than those above are first introduced at Level 4 where major epifaunal/epifloral taxa are used to discriminate rocky habitats. At Level 5 discrimination is based on both physical and biological characteristics of the habitats. Level 6 the highest discriminant level in EUNIS frequently describes notable variations in community structure of level 5 habitats. The purpose of this data capture was to create a spatial layer that contains the best available information on European Nature Information System (EUNIS) Level 3 habitats at any position in UK waters. This dataset combines field surveys polygon habitat data and board-scale predictive maps from the EUSeaMap model.\tPrivate record\n17629\tNRW_DS109760\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.035Z\tAcross Wales Sea Bottom Temperature Time Series Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373630\t[A]  Data between 2000 and 2015 is archived in media no. 590 and the master spreadsheet held on Ffynnon.   [B]  Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive at  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Temperature \u0026amp; salinity loggers\tData Custodian is:  Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG, NRW Metadata point of contact is: Data Services, NRW\tTemperature loggers have been placed at sites across Special Areas of Conservations (SACs) in Wales to measure the bottom sea temperature.  The data is collected continuously but is processed to give the daily mean temperature as well as yearly averages.This data is collected as part of the Across Wales Diving Monitoring Survey. Additional sea temperature recordings are taken at Skomer Marine Conservation Zone but these are covered in a separate metadata entry.  Water temperature is also recorded in saline lagoons at Cemlyn and Pickleridge. The data are captured as part of the Across-Wales Diving Survey designed to gather information on Reef features across the Marine SACs in Wales.  Gathering physical data such as temperature provides context and background information for the condition assessment of each of the reef features monitored.  For example biogenic reefs and many of the species that occur around the reefs in Wales may be sensitive to fluctuations in temperature.  The data collected may provide supporting evidence for any recorded changes in species composition on sites particularly where species are known to be at the edge of their biogeographical range.  All information about the sites is in kept the Temperature loggers site location document.\tPublic record\n17847\tNRW_DS111161\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.557Z\tNant Teyrn Freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313631\tSee individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field above for more details.\tCustodian: Victoria Bowmaker and Alex Turner, ECN Project Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) is network of sites spanning the UK where we conduct environmental monitoring using standard methods. There are currently 12 terrestrial sites and 45 freshwater sites covering a wide range of upland and lowland habitats including moorland chalk grassland woods and forests farmland small ponds and streams large rivers and lakes. ECN is the UK member of the International Long-Term Ecological Research Network (ILTER) and of LTER-Europe. The ECN\u0027s objectives are to establish and maintain a network of sites from which to obtain comparable long-term datasets through the monitoring of a range of variables; to integrate and analyse these data to identify natural and man-induced environmental changes improve understanding of the causes of change distinguish short-term fluctuations from long-term trends and predict future changes.  Nant Teyrn is a small upland stream on the out flow from Llyn Teyrn in Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve. This freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) site is co-located with Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon terrestrial ECN site.  As part of the ECN project long term monitoring of physical chemical and biological variables occurs at Nant Teyrn. The monitoring is designed to collect store analyse and interpret long-term datasets for physical chemical and biological variables which drive or respond to environmental change. The data is subject to a variety of quality control procedures (QA). Each monitoring protocol has a corresponding paper form. The data is entered into an Excel spreadsheet which has background calibration graphs in order to flag any outliers within the data. These outliers can then be double checked against the paper records. Every year the spreadsheet is checked against the paper records and each line of data within the spreadsheet is assigned a quality code which helps to flag up any problems during data collation e.g. snow in the gauges. The CEH CCU also has its own QA procedures which they follow on receipt of the data.\tPublic record\n18851\tNRW_DS124658\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.147Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Permanent Masts\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363538\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Permanent Masts.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm (as built) Permanent Mast locations. A measurement tower or met mast is a free standing tower or a removed mast which carries measuring instruments with meteorological instruments such as thermometers and instruments to measure wind speed. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18028\tNRW_DS114859\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.711Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: South East Wales (South East Wales Biological Records Centre - SEWBReC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383539\tX:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to SEWBREC.lyr   SEWBREC hold and manage data on behalf of NRW. NRW still hold ownership over these dataset. All data that SEWBREC hold as a top copy on behalf of NRW is published on the NBN Gateway. In Addition to this NRW hold a copy of the data in Recorder 6 - however this can be out of date. Some of these are listed in the \u0027Contains\u0027 Field and others aren\u0027t.  NRW hold an out of date copy of some of this data in Recorder 6: These are as follows. LC00021500000049  CCW Cosmeston Park SSSI LC0002150000003Z. CCW Craig Pont Rhondda SSSI LC00021500000040   CCW Cwm Taf Fechan Woodland SSSI LC00021500000030   CCW Lesser Horseshoe Bats LC0002150000002U  CCW The River Wye Valley LC0002150000002E  CCW - MOD Caerwent    Also contains the following specific metadata \u003d 116724 - CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework Files 2001 to 2005\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Karen Wilkinson is the main lead with SewBrec SewBrec are the Custodians This data will be incoprorated in the GIS layers that NRW also received\tThis dataset is made up of a collation of species records (excluding sensitive records) derived from Natural Resources Wales (formerly Countryside Council for Wales) South and East Region paper files. The purpose of this data capture was to mobilise a valuable data resource in order to provide baseline data to inform conservation work research site management and decision-making within NRW and externally. Records are for the NRW\u0027s South and East Region which includes Blaenau-Gwent Bridgend Caerphilly Cardiff Merthyr Tydfil Monmouthshire Neath-Port Talbot Newport Rhondda-Cynon-Taff Swansea Torfaen and The Vale of Glamorgan. There are also Records from other parts of Wales. Many records are for designated sites (including Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) Special Area of Conservation (SACs) National Nature Reserves etc) however there are also large numbers of records from the wider countryside. The majority of the records have six figure grid-references but some of these may be from site centroids rather than actual location of the species in question.\tPublic record\n18715\tNRW_DS123361\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.260Z\tWISKI Historic Hydrometric Stations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333631\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\WISKI Historic Hydrometric Stations.lyr\tAlison Hanson, Team Leader, Hydrometry \u0026amp; Telemetry, Supra Area Hydrometry \u0026amp; Telemetry\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17852\tNRW_DS111164\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.918Z\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Physical Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313634\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The ECN data is also available from CEH.  b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tInternal Custodian:  Victoria Bowmaker, and Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, NRW  Metadata Contact:  Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tPhysical data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site - Yr Wyddfa.  The 700ha site incorporates the summit of Yr Wyddfa and includes three additional summits over 800m it also includes 5 lakes. It is part of Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve managed by NRW. Measurements are taken of various meteorological surface water and soil variables. The purpose of this data capture was to use the observed changes in physical variables to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the terrestrial environment. See overarching Yr Wyddfa ECN metadata entry (\u0027part of\u0027 field below) for general ECN objectives.\tPublic record\n18313\tNRW_DS116333\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.950Z\tSurface Water Temperature Archive up to 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333333\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116333\tInternal Contact: Alison Hanson\tWater temperature data is collected  for different reasons and in different locations. Time series of surface water temperatures can provide indicators of climate change and associated ecological responses. An archive was created in 2007 as part of a research project and is a unique collation of Natural Resource Wales and The Environment Agencies water temperature data from more than 30000 sites across England \u0026amp; Wales. The archive contains water temperature data (up to 2007) and site metadata. The archive contains water temperature data (up to 2007) and site metadata. Most sites have records starting from the 1980s.\tPublic record\n18322\tNRW_DS116338\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.321Z\tUK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme Detailed Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333338\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\tInternal custodian: Mark Squire / (previously) Julie Boswell\tThe Historic UK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) dataset contains individual determinand results for all sites in the UK Harmonised Monitoring Scheme network from 1974 until 2013. The Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) was established to provide an archive of water quality data for the UK. It is used to provide information for international obligations including the long-term trends of some determinands and the estimation of riverborne input of selected determinands to the sea.\tPublic record\n18234\tNRW_DS116236\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.640Z\tHistoric Landfill Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323336\tX:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\Historic Landfill Sites.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning.  Also could contact: Tyrone (Ty) Ward - PPC/RSR Officer 1, PPC Team\tThis dataset is now Static and won’t be updated. However this dataset should still be used for information on permits that were historic before came under EPR regulations.   This dataset should be used in conjunction with the Historic Landfill Sites points dataset (NRW_DS125453) which is the most comprehensive dataset for recently expired surrendered or revoked landfill sites.    Historic Landfill Sites is a spatial (polygon) dataset. These are landfill sites which have been taken off the Authorised Landfill Sites when the waste licence status changes to either:   - Licence expired: Some licences issued under the Control of Pollution Act 1974 were time limited and expired on the date specified in the licence.  - Licence Revoked: Whereby the licence has been entirely revoked and is no longer in force; or   - Licence Surrendered: Operator has successfully surrendered the licence which is no longer in force.    Under the Town and Country Planning (General Development Procedure) Order 1995 Local Planning Authorities have to consult with the former Environment Agency Wales now part of Natural Resources Wales about all applications they receive to develop land within 250 metres of landfill sites (including any land that has been used as a landfill site within the past 30 years or is likely to be used as one in the near future). The Historic Landfill dataset was created to help fulfil our statutory responsibility to Local Planning Authorities by supplying information on the risks posed by landfill sites for development within 250m.    The data is the most comprehensive and consistent national historic landfill dataset and defines the location of and provides specific attributes for known historic (closed) landfill sites i.e. sites where there is no PPC permit or waste management licence currently in force. This includes sites that existed before the waste licensing regime and sites that have been licensed in the past but where this licence has been revoked ceased to exist or surrendered and a certificate of completion has been issued.\tPublic record\n18609\tNRW_DS122580\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.745Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Lake Macro-invertebrates Taxa Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353830\tData Held in Wiski-KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data\tInternal Custodian: Jennifer Owen, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team         Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tFreshwater marco-invertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance. This dataset is made up of the species abundance values from the freshwater lake macro-invertebrates surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of lake water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\tPrivate record\n18104\tNRW_DS115560\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.035Z\tA Survey of Moorings and their Impact on Seagrass Beds at Porth Dinllaen (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353630\tData is located as Project 386, media 1475 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1475)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tPorth Dinllaen has one of the largest and densest seagrass beds within Wales. As with the majority of the United Kingdom the seagrass bed at Porth Dinllaen is typified by one species Zostera marina. The seagrass beds at Porth Dinllaen spans over 1 km in length and up to 650 m wide in places throughout the inner and outer harbour areas. Previous work has found that boat moorings create distinct radial scour patterns within the Z. marina.   As part of the management of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation(SAC) the SAC Officer has been working with the National Trust Relevant Authorities local fishermen boat owners and other stakeholders to look at options to reduce the impact of the moorings on Z. marina at Porth Dinllaen. In 2012 surveys were conducted within the outer harbour using volunteer SCUBA divers.   The aims of the 2012 surveys were to record the mooring number and designs used within Porth Dinllaen outer harbour and assess how these moorings relate to the density of Z. marina adjacent Z. marina canopy height and presence of wasting. The work was carried out under the Ecosystem Resilience and Diversity Fund.\tPublic record\n18560\tNRW_DS122417\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.869Z\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343137\t\t\tAlexander K.N.A. 2019. Saproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2018. NRW Evidence Report No: 320 xxpp Natural Resources Wales Bangor.  Contact Mike Howe / Rob Bacon for metadata details\tPrivate record\n17375\tNRW_DS24425\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.883Z\tMonitoring Data Held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database (Archived 2021)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343235\tISIS Database. Electronic. Formerly available to NRW staff over intranet and site files. This system has now been closed down and replaced by the protected sites management system. Currently there is no evidence \u0026amp; monitoring module on the system.\tInternal Custodian: Christine Edwards, Lead Specialist Advisor – Protected Sites programme       Metadata was archived 17th August 2021. Dylan Lloyd is the new custodian for this and has highlighted that after the ISIS System was decommissioned monitoring data was moved to the  protected sites baseline review data (124833) and will be used in the future possibly to populate a newer monitoring module when created.\tA collation of which SSSI Features of protected sites in Wales have been monitored. It provides historical data on designation dates and reports on designations in Wales.  The Original monitoring Data from the ISIS system has now been deleted as part of the CCW Decommissioning project. Resulting baseline data can now be found in The Freshwater and Terrestrial Protected Sites Baseline Evaluation Project (NRW_DS124833).\tPrivate record\n17653\tNRW_DS109994\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.429Z\tRestoring the Afon Teifi at Cors Caron National Nature Reserve\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393934\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media101to200\\Media179 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian:  Senior Freshwater Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Afon Teifi is one of the largest rivers in Wales and is of considerable national and international importance; designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). Approximately 15km from its source the Teifi runs through the Cors Caron National Nature Reserve (NNR). Within the NNR approximately 1.5km of the Afon Teifi has been altered by straightening and deepening of the river. The northernmost straightening is present on Ordnance Survey maps dating from 1889 whilst the remaining alterations were undertaken in the period 1891 to 1905. The present day aerial photographs show the original meandering channel as sections of open water or vegetated channel. The presence of the original channel has lead to a proposal to restore the river to its former course. A scoping study by the River Restoration Centre has indicated that restoration is a viable option for the Afon Teifi within the NNR. The scheme proposed by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) incorporated an environmental assessment stakeholder consultation pre- and post-project monitoring programmes and technical design. This dataset contains the results of the work undertaken to address the scheme\u0027s requirements (Assessment of the current baseline information relating to the existing river morphology and hydrology the in-channel vegetation and invertebrate fauna the floodplain and the stakeholder interest). It includes an environmental impact assessment of the proposed restoration and presents a detailed plan of work for the restoration of the Afon Teifi in the NNR with guidance for further research and monitoring.\tPublic record\n18504\tNRW_DS121362\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.242Z\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Runoff Attenuation Features 1% AEP\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333632\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Runoff Attenuation Features 1% AEP.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tRunoff Attenuation Features Potential is our best estimate of locations of high flow accumulation across the land surface or in smaller channels where it may be possible to temporarily store water and attenuate flooding during high flows. The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas where to target enhanced storage. It is based upon the Risk of Flooding from Surface Water datasets and identifies areas of high flow accumulations for the 1% Annual Exceedance Probability surface water maps. The areas of ponding or accumulation are between 100 and 5000 metres squared and have been tagged where they fall on an area of slope steeper than 6% as gully blocking opportunities. All the potential areas have been constrained so that they are not in urban areas or on roads rails or canals.\tPublic record\n18043\tNRW_DS115186\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.933Z\tReporting of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) Contribution to the UK Network\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313836\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive as project 383, media 1395\tInternal Custodian:  Deputy Group Head/Team Leader Marine Ecosystems, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSection 124 of the Marine and Coastal Access Act (MCA Act) requires Welsh Government to report on the extent to which they have achieved the objectives in the Act to use conservation sites to contribute to a UK network of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs).  Initial reporting takes place in 2012 and subsequently every 6 years.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide supporting information to the Welsh Government Marine Protected Areas of Wales in both the offshore and inshore marine environment. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) have provided a report and supporting information to WG which provides an assessment of the representative and replication of the suite of Welsh MPAs as well as a discussion of management and condition evidence to date.  A copy of the data is archived at CCW as a record of the evidence provided for the initial reporting.\tPublic record\n17372\tNRW_DS24426\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.836Z\tOpen Access held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343236\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tRecreation Information Development Officer, Recreation \u0026amp; Access Group, NRW\tThis dataset contains data in relation to the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 where members of the public and people with a registered interest in land known as mountain moor heath or down have consulted Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in relation to parcels of land.  The data covers enquiries about the CRoW Act land parcels requests for leaflets publications and maps and advice or guidance. The bulk of the data relates to specific land parcels in the 14 A to O consultation areas in Wales (for draft provisional and conclusive mapping) along with descriptions of the land decisions related to the land in terms of whether or not it is mountain moor heath or down and appeals based on decisions about it\u0027s land status.\tPublic record\n17899\tNRW_DS112214\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.591Z\tPalaeoecological Study of Llyn Padarn (2009)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323134\tElectronic data is archived as project 269, media no. 980.   The report is available on CCW\u0027s Electronic Documents Management System (Ffynnon: DCT-10-088698)\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW)\tLlyn Padarn is designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for several reasons including its populations of Arctic charr and floating water-plantain. However there have been several concerns of the water quality owing to the decline in adult charr population and the occurrence of the blue-green algal bloom in 2009. In light of this it was necessary to assess the recent environmental change that has occurred in the lake.  The aim of this project is to sample and analyse a lake sediment core from Llyn Padarn and relate the findings to chemical and nutrient changes over time and known events in the lake\u0027s history. Palaeolimnological methods offer a means of reconstructing the environmental history of standing waters by studying subfossil fragments of the biota preserved in the lake sediments. The project aims to reconstruct the environmental history of Llyn Padarn over approximately the last century using diatoms with particular attention to the eutrophication history of the site. The study focuses on diatom remains in sediment cores and also includes the application of an existing diatom-total phosphorus (TP) model to reconstruct the nutrient history of the lake. In addition to this X-ray fluorescence analysis was carried out to assess down core changes in chemical composition with a particular focus on iron copper and P. The analysis of a dated sediment core collected from Llyn Padarn in 2009 (PADA2) was used to assess environmental change over the last 100-150 years.  The data was also used to reconstruct the environmental history of Llyn Padarn over approximately the last century with particular attention to the eutrophication history of the site. Diatom analysis of only three samples from an undated core taken from the lake in August 2004 showed that the diatom assemblage shifted from species typically associated with nutrient-poor waters towards those associated with more productive systems. This study builds on this earlier work by analysing a new sediment core at a higher resolution and providing a chronology to determine the timing and rates of change. The study feeds into ongoing monitoring at Llyn Padarn to determine the trophic status of the lake and the health of the Arctic charr population.\tPublic record\n17386\tNRW_DS24323\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.219Z\tAcidification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333233\tStatistical data held on paper and Recorder 6 Geographic data held digitally in MapInfo\tSimon Bareham, Team Leader Environmental Futures, SPG, NRW. (Project Officer at the time)\tThe dataset was collected as part of a specific project and is no longer used by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for reporting purposes. This dataset is to be kept for reference and as part of former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 data archive. The data was collected between 1992 and 1993 and used as part of a specific data modelling exercise. Site specific information about Welsh Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) that are under threat from acidification with predicted damage under three scenarios of acid rain reduction. 218 sites are involved at the worst level of acidification and 86 sites are involved under the best scenario modelled (out of 845 SSSIs in Wales in total). The data include the area of the site at risk and the communities at risk. In the UK Critical Loads Advisory Group report on Critical and Target Load Maps for Soils in Great Britain the production of soils critical loads maps is explained as follows: The soil information is based on 1km squares of the UK Ordnance Survey national grid and on 1:250000 scale soil maps and supporting databases. The soil maps for Wales was produced and published by the Soil survey and land research centre. The critical load assigned to each 1 km square was the Critical for the soil association what occupied the largest proportion of search square. Each map unit was placed in one of five soil classes. These five soil classes were based on soil mineralogy.  This dataset was created to help describe the \u0027stock at risk\u0027 according to the Critical load exceeding limits (due to sulphur) at a 1 km resolution. The dataset was used to as an assessment of the biological SSSIs within areas where the soil\u0027s critical load for sulphur was predicted to exceed in 2005 when the agreed 60 percent reduction in SO2 from large combustion plants in Europe is implemented. The data was also used to consider the ecological benefits which may accrue if greater emission reduction targets are agreed. A possible 80 percent reduction was examined and compared with the predicted effects of the current scenario.\tPublic record\n17421\tNRW_DS98759\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.061Z\tRamsar Sites - Wetlands of International Importance\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373539\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Ramsar.lyr   x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas Ramsar.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tThe Convention on Wetlands called the Ramsar Convention is an intergovernmental treaty that provides the framework for national action and international cooperation for the conservation and wise use of wetlands and their resources. This spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Ramsar sites in Wales. In ratifying the Convention in 1976 the UK government accepted a commitment to promote the conservation of internationally important wetland sites within its territories.  Wetlands are vital for many types of birds particularly waterfowl and Wales have some prime sites that are essential to the survival of many wetland plants and animals. Wetland sites can be areas of marsh fen peatland or open water; natural or artificial; permanent or temporary; with water that is fresh brackish or salty. They can also include shallow areas of sea. All Ramsar sites are also Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). Wetlands of International Importance are identified by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) in collaboration with the UK Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) and designated by the First Minister for the National Assembly for Wales.    The Ramsars have been designated over a number of years from 1976 to the present day and are on-going.  The data has been held digitally since mid1990s. The boundaries have been checked by NRW staff. Due to scale enlargement and different editions of map a certain degree of interpretation was required.\tPublic record\n17486\tNRW_DS100749\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.579Z\tForest Enterprise Holdings\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373439\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\external_agency\\forestry\\forest_enterprise\\fe_land.TAB\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Forest Enterprise Holdings and licensed to CCW for internal use only.  The dataset has been supplied by the Forest Enterprise Holdings and contains the legal boundaries of Forestry Enterprise land as at 11/07/2001.\tPrivate record\n17474\tNRW_DS98776\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.313Z\tSites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373736\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SSSI Sites of Special Scientific Interest.lyr x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas SSSI.lyr\tHolly York\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales. SSSIs cover a wide range of habitats from small fens bogs and riverside meadows to sand-dunes woodlands and vast tracts of uplands. Most are in private ownership although some are owned and managed by local wildlife trusts or other voluntary conservation bodies.   Notification of an SSSI under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 has since been amended by the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2001 which brought about numerous changes in the way SSSI are notified managed and protected.   In order to ensure consistent favourable long-term management of these sites Natural Resources Wales (NRW) with landowners have prepared management plans for all SSSI in Wales. Local planning authorities are required to consult NRW before allowing any development to proceed that may affect an SSSI. Water gas and electricity companies must also do the same.   SSSIs have been designated from 1949 to the present day and are on-going. The data has been held digitally since the mid-1990s. This data has been checked by relevant NRW staff. Please refer to the designation map as the legal definitive boundary. For large SSSIs that were captured digitally and have been printed on a smaller scale map than OS MasterMap please refer to the OS MasterMap edition at time of capture to view the definitive boundary.\tPublic record\n18220\tNRW_DS116255\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.179Z\tAreas Affecting Bathing Waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323535\tx:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Areas Affecting Bathing Waters.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Helena Sykes, Lead Specialist Advisor, Monitoring Analysis, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning\tThis spatial dataset comprises of a polygon relating to each site identified under the Bathing Water Directive (76/160/EEC). These sites are drawn as a simple guide to aid the work of Natural Resources Wales and therefore these polygons have no formal status under the Bathing Water Directive.\tPublic record\n18011\tNRW_DS114979\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.865Z\tDormouse Outer Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393739\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Dormouse_Outer_Areas.lyr\tMetadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Team  Custodian: GIS and Spatial Team / PTES\tThis is a spatial dataset held by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) which holds records from the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) dormouse data sourced from areas of work such as licence returns and dormouse survey work and also The Vincent Wildlife Trust (VWT) dormouse survey data. This dataset is a collation of dormouse records for Wales from the People\u0027s Trust for Endangered Species (PTES) National Dormouse Database with a spatial resolution of a 2 km buffer zone. Map has been used as basis for Glastir Targeted Element - core (or mandatory) sites. Majority of records collected by experienced surveyors or verified by experienced surveyors (e.g. nut hunt records). Licence returns have not been independently verified.  Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a copy of the GIS dataset on their internal GIS data store - however requestors are advised to obtain the most up-to-date version of the data from PTES. This dataset is also held under the National Dormouse Database (NDD) available to download from the NBN Gateway.\tPublic record\n17472\tNRW_DS98778\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.153Z\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373738\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NNR National Nature Reserves.lyr\tTom Hellawell, Conservation and Recreation Management Planning Team Leader, Ecosystems Partnership \u0026amp; Planning North\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in Wales. NNRs represent the very best examples of our wildlife habitats and geological features and can range in size between five hectares to well over 2000.   NNRs are declared by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act of 1949 or under the Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981. They are owned or leased by NRW or the land is held by an approved body such as a County Wildlife Trust.   Each reserve has a programme of work to manage the site\u0027s special features. All of them are also Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and may provide places for educational projects research and management trials. Some reserves require permits to gain access to them. NNRs are designated over a number of years beginning in 1954 and on going.   This data has been checked by NRW staff. The original individual declaration paper maps are the legal documents.\tPublic record\n17333\tNRW_DS24294\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.751Z\tRegionally Important Geodiversity Sites (RIGS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323934\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\RIG Sites.lyr\tRaymond Roberts, Senior Earth Scientist  Geologist/Earth Science Officer, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  External: GeoConservation UK- The Association of UK RIGS Groups\tRegionally Important Geodiversity Sites (RIGS) are non-statutory sites selected to protect the most important places for geology geomorphology and soils complementing the network of legally protected Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs).   RIGS are selected for their scientific educational historical and aesthetic features. As stated in Planning Policy Wales Planning Authorities should protect the features and qualities for which RIGS have been designated. The impact of proposed developments will depend on the nature of the RIGS feature so early consultation with the local RIGS group or NRW is strongly recommended.\tPublic record\n17393\tNRW_DS64583\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.285Z\tConsents and Assents held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353833\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tGill Walker, Data Manager (Sites), Operational Support Group, CCW\tThis dataset contains information on consents and assents requests received by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW\u0027s responses to the requests.   The purpose of this data capture is to maintain database records for all Section 28 and Regulation 48 Consent and Assent requests received by CCW and CCW\u0027s responses. This dataset is now managed by Natural Resources Wales.\tPublic record\n18559\tNRW_DS122418\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.864Z\tEnvironment (Wales) Act Section 7 Terrestrial Habitats of Principal Importance\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343138\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\\tPete Jones is the custodian of this - although Stuart Smith is also a really good contact.  GIS and Spatial Data Team deal with the updates\tUnder the Environment (Wales) Act (2016) Section 7 requires biodiversity lists to be produced. These lists include types of habitats which are of \"Principal Importance\" for the purpose of maintain and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This list supersedes the duty in Section 42 of the NERC Act 2006. This dataset details the extent and location of those terrestrial habitats classed of \"Principal Importance\" in Wales.\tPublic record\n17641\tNRW_DS109752\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.065Z\tGround and Aerial Monitoring of Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) in Carmarthen Bay Spatial Protection Area (SPA) (2002 - 2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373532\tData is archived under project no.40, media no. 219 (data for 2002-2004), project No. 162, media 491 (data for 2004-2007) Project No. 165, media no. 494 and Project No. 205,media 570 (data for 2007-2008).\tInternal Custodians:  - Marine Ornithologist, NRW - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay Spatial Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16000 individuals) of the biogeographic population of Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra). The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs especially during the winter although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps) worms echinoderms isopods amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs which are found in abundance in the shallow water in Carmarthen Bay.  Common scoter are also listed as a Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has supported ground based counts since 1996 following the Sea Empress oil spill.  Aerial surveys have also been employed as scoter often occur out of sight of land.  This dataset is compiled from data collected by The British Trust for Ornithology with support from CCW from 2002 onwards. The purpose of this data capture was to maintain a monitoring program using a combination of ground-based and aerial surveys to assess numbers and distribution of common scoter in Carmarthen Bay and to investigate whether ground-based counts provide an appropriate index of overall numbers of scoter within Carmarthen Bay as well as update the current index.\tPublic record\n18236\tNRW_DS116299\t2024-05-28T12:43:54.738Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Team Boundaries (Archived 2019)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323939\tThis layerfile has been removed from the X: drive. The following dataset supersedes the Team Boundaries layer X:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Environment Teams.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gary Ecclestone.       This layerfile has been removed from the X: drive. The metadata for Team Boundaries can be archived. The following dataset supersedes the Team Boundaries layer X:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Environment Teams.lyr (metadata 123441)  For this reason this record has been removed from public view.\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for operational managed teams.    This dataset defines the operational team boundaries and the 18 team locations within those areas.    Information warning: This dataset has now been archived as it has been superseded by \u0027Environment Teams post Organisational Design July 2019\u0027 NRW_DS123441.\tPublic record\n18695\tNRW_DS123301\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.228Z\tVaccinnium Dominant Open Habitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333031\tx:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Vaccinnium Dominant Open Habitats.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones\t\tPrivate record\n18810\tNRW_DS123895\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.802Z\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales: Species Datasets\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233383935\tSpecies data is also stored in Recorder 6 under the Phase 2 Woodland Survey Tag. This data is now available on the NBN Atlas The species part of the dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy\tJames Latham, Woodland and Spatial Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW\tThis dataset contains species records from detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 800 woodlands of conservation interest in Wales predominantly Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSls) and pSSSI woodlands. Phase 2 woodland surveys have been carried out periodically since the late 1980s but a concentrated period of survey on SSSI and pSSSI woodland occurred from 1995 and 2000.\tPublic record\n18852\tNRW_DS124667\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.171Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Armoured Crossing Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363637\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Armoured Crossing Points.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Armoured Crossing Points. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17062\tEXT_DS100787\t2024-05-28T12:10:09.882Z\tRSPB: Lapwings (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373837\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\Lapwings.lyr    Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\biological\\species\\all_wales\\rspb\\lapwings.TAB\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Royal Society for the Protection of Birds and licensed to NRW for internal use only. The dataset has been supplied by the RSPB and contains Lapwing sites for Wales-showing status of birds sited. Supplied and updated by Tony Pickup ( RSPB ).\tPublic record\n18818\tNRW_DS123530\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.946Z\tFlood Flows Estimate Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353330\tWinFap Model run on request by the flood team\tGlenda Tudor-Ward\tPeak flood flow and/or flood hydrograph estimates for one or more locations for one or more design events usually calculated using gauged data Flood Estimation Handbook Statistical and/or Rainfall Runoff methods.\tPublic record\n17233\tEXT_DS122097\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.469Z\tLocal Environmental Records Centre (LERC) Wales: Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303937\tX:\\Biological\\Local Record Centres\\LRC Invasive non native species points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Local Record Centres\\LRC Invasive non native species polygon.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jennie Jones, Specialist Advisor, Non-Native Species Internal Custodian: Barnaby Letheren, Lead Specialist Advisor, Knowledge \u0026amp; Information Management  Contact GIS and Spatial Data Team for more info\tThis spatial data collation is supplied by the Local Environmental Records Centres in Wales who are the data collator and copyright holders. The information Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to as part of a licence with the Local Environmental Records Centre in Wales is only part of the full record. Further details on each observation can be obtained from the data collator. Ownership of the record remains with the original recorder.\tPublic record\n17211\tEXT_DS122467\t2024-05-28T12:17:03.274Z\tUnited Utilities (UU): Reservoir Storage Data held in WISKI 7 Core Hydro and Telemetry (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343637\tData is held in WISKI Hydro Module.\tAlison Hanson - Team Leader for this area of work  Liam Perrott - technical specialist for hydrometry       Data from 2014 in Water Resources Team DMS location.\tReservoir storage data is recorded by United Utilities. Weekly reading of reservoir storage volume (Million litres) and % storage.\tPublic record\n19186\tNRW_DS000004\t2024-05-28T13:14:30.956Z\t\t\t\t\t\tPublic record\n17601\tNRW_DS108654\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.946Z\tLANDMAP Cultural Landscape (archived data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363534\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap2017\\LANDMAP Cultural Landscape.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LANDMAP Cultural Landscape.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tThis dataset has been superseded by Landmap Cultural Landscape Services (metadata number:125279).  Landmap is a unique national information system devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset.   The Landmap database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    It is a geographic dataset showing cultural landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information including General Description Evaluation Recommendations Tolerance to Change Area Boundary Evaluation and Bibliography.    The dataset records human activity that can be related to landscape including major influences in art or literature which depicted or described the landscape as well as more commonplace expressions of culture like farming type or choice of building materials which creates a distinctive character in an area.   Information is gathered by LANDMAP in order to inform sustainable decision making and to enable landscape to be taken into account during decision making processes. This dataset is important as landscape not only shapes but is shaped by culture. The dataset will raise awareness and enable people to engage in landscape decision making in Wales.\tPublic record\n18374\tNRW_DS116797\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.393Z\tThe Status of the Gipsywort Weevil Datonychus Arquatus on Pembrey Air Weapons Range in 2016.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373937\tReport is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports Data is stored in the Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: Adrian Fowles)       The contact (address) details of the Originator, Adrian Fowles, are withheld as personal data.\tThe gipsywort weevil Datonychus arquatus currently classified in Britain as Red Data Book: Indeterminate is known in the British Isles only from Pembrey Air Weapons Range (SN3604) where it was first discovered in 1997. It is an Independently Qualifying Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Feature of the Arfordir Pen-bre/ Pembrey Coast SSSI and a Conservation Objective has been developed (Boyce 2007). This contract sought to repeat the monitoring of 2006 to assess the current Condition of the SSSI Feature.   Three Stations were identified in 2006 as supporting an abundance of the foodplant gipsywort Lycopus europaeus and the targets for Favourable Condition require assessment of each of these Stations. However all of the fen vegetation in Stations 1 \u0026amp; 2 had been mown in the week prior to survey which would have caused the loss of any sub-populations of D. arquatus in these areas had they been present. Survey of the adjacent grazing enclosure revealed an additional area of suitable habitat and this was assessed in addition to Station 3. A total of seven adult arquatus was recorded - five in Station 3 and two in the newly-discovered area here designated as Station 4. Ten large patches of foodplant were recorded in total but in four of these the plants were either too short or the area of the patch was insufficient. The loss of Stations 1 \u0026amp; 2 meant that an outcome of Unfavourable: Declining was inevitable and Station 4 is not big enough to compensate for the loss of habitat in those Stations.   Management recommendations are given for each Station which include the need to allow fen vegetation to recover in Stations 1 \u0026amp; 2 by stopping the current practice of mowing. In Stations 3 \u0026amp; 4 regular small-scale management of scrub encroachment will be needed to ensure that gipsywort patches are not lost to succession. Light grazing should be continued in the enclosure alongside Station 2 to help maintain open fen conditions. A visit was also made to the nearby Ffrwd Farm reserve of the Wildlife Trust of South \u0026amp; West Wales to sample gipsywort populations there for D. arquatus. Here cattle grazing is sufficiently high to restrict gipsywort to protected situations underneath established willow carr etc. although other parts of the reserve without grazing stock which weren\u0027t surveyed on this visit may have healthier populations of the foodplant. No examples of D. arquatus were found but further surveys are recommended.\tPublic record\n17700\tNRW_DS110059\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.589Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Marine Monitoring Programme Overview\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303539\t[A]  Species data held in Marine Recorder  [B]  Data held at Skomer Office on   \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\shared documents\\data\\biological data\\ [C]  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\ [D] SOPs held in the DMS   This data is now available on the NBN Atlas  back ups of Video library \u0026amp; CDs in off-site storage: CCW/WRPB/025\tPhil Newman, Senior Marine Conservation Officer, Skomer Marine Conservation Zone\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The Skomer Marine Monitoring Team has focused on developing a range of projects to monitor the health of the reserve and investigate the relevant aspects of the species life histories for baseline information.  Large scale surveys developed in the 1970\u0027s and 1980\u0027s provided the foundation for the monitoring.   This metadata details general monitoring on Skomer each datasets has its own metadata entry.  Monitoring is undertaken to provide an assessment of the condition of features against set targets and will contribute to reporting on the MNR as well as Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) within which the reserve lies.  Data is collected around Skomer Island and on the mainland between Musselwick Sands and Gateholm Island within the Marine Nature Reserve.  Data has been collected from 1975 onwards. An extensive review of images has been completed to determine the quantity and quality of information that could be extracted from these images. Species data has been collated from a number of surveys entered into Marine Recorder which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tPublic record\n17918\tNRW_DS114387\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.626Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Open Coast Sediment (Sandflats and Mudflats) in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333837\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 109, Media No 1219 and stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon, on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2009 ASML PLAS\\\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe aim of this data capture was to proceed with the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) intertidal monitoring.  Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) developed a programme of intertidal monitoring work across Wales during 2004 and 2005. These surveys were managed and implemented for CCW by the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS University of Hull). These projects focused on a wide range of sensitive habitats such as Zostera muddy gravels caves rock pools algal dominated rocky shores Sabellaria reefs under-boulders and various rare habitats and species.  Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd. (ASML) have been contracted by CCW to continue development and management of the intertidal monitoring programme for each marine SAC for the period 2006 to 2011 working as a team with CCW HQ and CCW Regional staff.\tPublic record\n17493\tNRW_DS100514\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.425Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Zostera marina (Eelgrass) Monitoring\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030353134\tSkomer MNR, West Region Drive  /shared documents/data/biological data/zostera\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Zostera marina is the only flowering plant within the British Isles that grows and produces seed entirely submerged by seawater.  Zostera populations are highly productive habitats and they provide an important stabilising function for the mobile marine sediments. The maintenance of Zostera populations directly influences the associated algal and invertebrate communities that it supports which are an important source of food for birds. Zostera marina is one of three seagrass species which are listed as nationally scarce and are included as a key habitat for conservation in the UK Biodiversity Action Plan 1994. The bed at North Haven Skomer is one of only 4 in Wales and is the deepest. It is completely sub tidal and has been well surveyed.  The purpose of this data capture was: 1.  To map the boundaries of the Zostera marina bed. 2.  To determine and identify changes in its distribution and abundance. 3.  Record conspicuous organisms associated with the Zostera population.\tPublic record\n18743\tNRW_DS123389\t2024-05-28T12:55:19.752Z\tCoastal Realignment Opportunity Confidential Stage 6\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333839\tx:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Coastal defence\\Coastal_Realignment_Opportunity_Confidential_Stage6.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Coastal defence\\Coastal Realignment Opportunities Stage6.lyr\tPaul Brazier\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18993\tNRW_DS124915\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.096Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) National Vegetation Classification Survey Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393135\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Mospoly.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Photographs.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Polygons.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Quadrats.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Target Notes.lyr\tToni Henwood, Team Member 2, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales Sands of LIFE\tData from a National Vegetetation Classificaiotn survey of 10 sand dune sites including shapefiles of NVC polygons vegetation mosaics target notes quadrats photograph locations and overall survey boundary. Data collected in 2019 based on field survey of entire sites with field maps digitised to OS Mastermap and aerial photographs.\tPrivate record\n18031\tNRW_DS114817\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.707Z\tA Preliminary Description of the Sublittoral Habitats and Associated Biota within the Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ )\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383137\t[A] Data is archived as project 352, media 1331 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1331) [B] Species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW31700000003]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team, NRW.\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  This dataset consists of the survey results from sublittoral work carried out between 1973 and 1975.  This work was the first baseline sublittoral dive survey of the Skomer Marine Nature Reserve and there is only one copy of the report currently available to NRW.  As a result the digitisation and archiving of this report in a format utilisable to ongoing monitoring work and decision makers was deemed essential. The survey work focussed on the sublittoral environment around Skomer Island Marine Nature Reserve. The locations of the stations were fixed at regular distances throughout the then proposed area of the reserve at regular depth intervals; forming a series of discontinuously sampled transects of the seabed cliff or slope. This formation of sample stations was chosen to ensure information could be obtained on substrate types and topography; water movement (exposure); and macro-flora and fauna.\tPublic record\n17962\tNRW_DS114451\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.674Z\tDiver Observations on Arctic Charr (Salvelinus alpinus) Spawning Grounds in Llyn Padarn Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) - December 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343531\tData is archived within the MFSG data archive as project no. 302 and media no\u0027s 1214 and 1227.   Video is held on the imaging archive at V:\\MMT video Archive\\Tristan Freshwater\\2010 Llyn Padarn Arctic Charr Media1214 Additional footage is archived as  project 335, media 1301 and currently held on:  V:\\MMT video Archive\\Tristan Freshwater\\2011 Llyn Padan (Arctic Char) Media 1301 V:\\MMT video Archive\\Tristan Freshwater\\2011 1221 Llyn Padarn Arctic Charr RH Media 1301\tInternal Custodian: Rivers Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Contact:  Data Services for metadata information\tLlyn Padarn is a large natural lake located at the foot of Snowdon alongside the village of Llanberis North Wales. The lake is designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for its rare genetically distinct Arctic charr (Salvelinus alpinus) population the presence of the nationally scarce Annex II aquatic macrophyte Luronium natans L. (Floating Water-plantain) and the exposure of geologically important Cambrian rock sequences. The Arctic charr population is one of only three naturally occurring populations in Wales and one of the southernmost in the UK. There has been concern in relation to the declining Arctic charr population in Llyn Padarn and the potential impact of the water quality of the lake on the population. This study was undertaken to investigate potential spawning areas in the lake for Arctic charr by a SCUBA diving survey. Knowledge of the location of the spawning grounds of charr in Llyn Padarn is important to inform management of the site for conservation of the species. The purpose of this data capture was to collected information so that any additional spawning grounds (to the one location known) could be identified along with any potentially important spawning sites.\tPublic record\n17759\tNRW_DS110337\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.458Z\tInfauna of heavily oiled shores in Milford Haven and Carmarthen Bay following the Sea Empress oil spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333337\tArchived as project no 102 (media no 377). Report No. 244 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 377) Report available in CCW  library, see library catalogue/ \u0027related titles\u0027 field. CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey no.  MRCCW10000000083)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team   Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe sediment shores of Pembrokeshire are of many types from coarse sand storm beaches to the soft fine muds of the Milford Haven Estuary. Following the grounding of the Sea Empress it became obvious that several sediment shores particularly in the lower reaches off the Milford Haven were vulnerable to serious damage as a results of large quantities of crude oil spilt. The physical characteristics of beaches determine to a large extent the types and range of biota present. This dataset includes the results of an assessment of the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the infauna of sediment shores in Milford Haven and Carmarthen Bay. The purpose of this data capture was to carry out biological and hydrocarbon analyses to assess the impact of the oil spill incident on sediment infauna.\tPublic record\n18893\tNRW_DS124790\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.196Z\tNational Flood Hazard Maps - Present Day\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373930\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard River Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard Rivers Extent.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard Rivers Hazard.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard Rivers Velocity.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Hazard.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Velocity.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from the Sea\\Flood Hazard from the Sea Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from the Sea\\Flood Hazard from the Sea Extent.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from the Sea\\Flood Hazard from the Sea Hazard.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh / GIS and Spatial Data Team   Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh / Simon James / Dave Tarrant       Surface Water Catchments (subset dataset NRW_DS124811): Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from Environment Agency © EA and Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited [2015].\tThe National Flood Hazard Maps for Present day  are based on generalised modelling only. The maps have been published to comply with the Flood Risk Regulations (2009) and the EU Directive (2007/60/EC) and used to inform the creation of Flood Risk Management plans across Wales. These maps have no official status for Planning or Insurance purposes therefore the public and professionals are advised to use the information contained within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Map the Development Advice Map/ Flood Map for Planning (as appropriate) for these purposes as these will be more up-to-date and incorporate improved modelling information.   The National Flood Hazard Maps for Present Day have been created for 3 sources of flooding namely   1. Flooding from Rivers  2. Flooding from the Sea  3. Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourse    The Maps show flood depth velocity (speed and direction) hazard and extent for High Medium and Low risk scenarios without raised flood defences.    For Rivers and Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses High risk is up to 1 in 30year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 and 1 in 100year; and Low risk is between 1 in 100year and 1 in 1000year Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP).    For the Sea High risk is up to 1 in 30 year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 to 1 in 200 year; and Low risk 1 in 200 year to 1 in 1000 year.    The Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses dataset is a direct replacement for the earlier Updated Flood Map for Surface Water (uFMfSW) 2013 and is based on the modelling of Surface Water Catchments.  Details of the modelling work to produce the new National Flood Hazard Maps are available in the supporting Information sheets.\tPublic record\n17604\tNRW_DS108704\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.073Z\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of Woodland Site of Special Scentific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales - (E3)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038373034\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland\\PH2_Woodland_Sites.lyr  Archived as Project No. 206, Media No. 571\tMetadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, EKIM, NRW Internal Custodian: Woodlands Ecologist, NRW\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of woodland at 171 sites (E3) on 132 Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) throughout Wales covering a total of 3806 ha.  The purpose of this data capture was to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information.\tPublic record\n19445\tNRW_DS161284\t2024-08-19T09:23:40.090Z\tUnderstanding the likely scale of deterioration of Marine Protected Area features due to coastal squeeze: Part 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323834\tY:\\Marine\\Coastal Squeeze geospatial files awaiting to be assessed and moved to X:Drive Spreadsheets found in DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/CSMPA/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fmar%2FMNN22%2D25%2FCSMPA%2FFinal%20Data\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BFEAFDBF3%2DFEA3%2D4A4D%2DA512%2DE1FB3CF2337E%7D\tNicola Rimington - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Process and Coastal Management\tTo complete the coastal squeeze assessments a series of geospatial layers and accompanying spreadsheets were created as part of a project to understand the likely scale of deterioration of Marine Protected Area features due to coastal squeeze  Coastal squeeze and natural squeeze  was calculated for habitats at a national scale for the whole of the Welsh coastline as well as for smaller delineations of the coastline. The potential scale of habitat deterioration due to coastal squeeze was then reported:  · Nationally; · Against individual MPAs; and · Against MPA designations amalgamated across Wales: o SACs; o SSSIs; o SPAs; and o Ramsar sites.  Coastal squeeze has been assessed for three timeframes (epochs): · 2025 to 2055 (30 years) – Equivalent to SMP2 medium-term epoch; · 2055 to 2105 (50 years) – Equivalent to SMP2 long-term epoch; and · 2105 to 2155 (50 years) – New long-term epoch.\tPrivate record\n17343\tNRW_DS24176\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.521Z\tMarine Monitoring Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234313736\t[A] Location is determined with each of the individual survey projects metadata.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Sciences Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis data provides the baseline for the marine monitoring.   Marine baseline monitoring data (initially from collection trials of monitoring procedures) collected at locations in Wales. The trials were to determine the best combination of data collection for monitoring. This metadata refers to the project as a whole however each individual survey may have metadata of its own. The surveys took place over Penllyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation and Pembrokeshire Islands Special Area of Conservation between 1999 and  2001.\tPublic record\n18445\tNRW_DS119134\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.456Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313334\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Rivers Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Environmental Reporting Team\tWFD River Waterbodies is a polyline Shapefile dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). The river polylines were defined by using of the Environment Agency - General Quality Assessment (GQA) River Stretches dataset this is copied directly from the CEH 1:50K River Network with some additional stretches added in by the Environment Agency. Additional stretches were added to cover all stretches designated under the Fresh Water Fish Directive all stretches designated within riverine SSSIs and ensuring that all riverine abstractions which contributed to the designation of a Drinking Water Protected Area were sited on a designated river waterbody. In addition to this some stretches were added where the upstream catchment size was \u0026gt; 10km2 but no other river stretch was designated (10km2 was the original cut-off catchment size form defining a waterbody under the WFD).   The resultant WFD river waterbody datasets is a sub-set of the CEH network including only stretches that meet any of the criteria outlined above.WFD River waterbodies share the same \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 as their river waterbody catchment allowing these 2 datasets to be linked. All river waterbodies are associated with a waterbody catchment. Not all catchments have a designated WFD river waterbody within them. These data apply to Cycle 1 of the Water Framework Directive. The equivalent layer for Cycle 2 is covered by WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2 AfA292.\tPublic record\n17556\tNRW_DS102568\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.158Z\tLakes ( 1:50,000 )\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032353638\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Lakes\\os_lakes.TAB\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons. Lakes and large ponds in Wales digitised at 1:50000. The primary objective of this project is to amalgamate  Quattro spreadsheet data extracted from the OS 1:50000 by the Ordnance Survey and the OS Lakes GIS layer available as a standard GIS dataset to produce a comprehensive GIS Welsh Lakes layer for use within NRW.\tPublic record\n17567\tNRW_DS102086\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.094Z\tSquirrel survey of Aberhirnant Forest and woodland surrounding Lake Vyrnwy (2001)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303836\t[A] Species Data held in Recorder 6   - Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Aberhirnant, Report 589 CCW0003800000007\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tPresence or absence or red and grey squirrels in Aberhirnant Forest and the woodlands surrounding Lake Vyrnwy. This is a follow-up to the \"Squirrel survey of Aberhirnant Forest and woodland surrounding Lake Vyrnwy 1999 - 2000\". The purpose of this data capture is to inform work to remove grey squirrels in the area as well as gather further information about red squirrel populations. The survey locations were conifer plantations of Aberhirnant Forest and the catchment area of Lake Vyrnwy.\tPublic record\n18854\tNRW_DS124649\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.285Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm 33 kV Cables\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363439\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\33KV Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows the Pen y Cymoedd windfarm 33 kV cables. 33kV cables are internal electric cabling come from the wind turbine power systems. They terminate at the sub-station on site. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18726\tNRW_DS123355\t2024-05-28T12:54:50.994Z\tMonitoring SAC Lakes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333535\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\Monitoring SAC Lakes.lyr\tVictoria Bowmaker\t\tPrivate record\n17104\tEXT_DS112025\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.619Z\tArchaeological Trust Spatial Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132303235\tX:\\Designations\\Archaeological Trust\\Archaeological Trust Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Designations\\Archaeological Trust\\Archaeological Trust Points.lyr  x:\\Designations\\Archaeological Trust\\Archaeological Trust Polygons.lyr  x:\\Designations\\Archaeological Trust\\Archaeological Trust Lines.lyr    Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\historic_landscapes\\CPAT_SMR.TAB\tGIS Service Desk  John Briggs, Landscape Architect, Environmental Policy Group, NRW\tThe four Welsh archaeological trusts which were established in the mid-1970s are independent limited companies with charitable status providing a uniform local archaeological service across the whole of Wales. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has licenced access to the Archaeological Trusts spatial data. In Wales there are four Archaeological Trusts: - Clwyd-Powys Archaeological Trust - Dyfed Archaeological Trust - Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust - Gwynedd Archaeological Trust\tPublic record\n18644\tNRW_DS123208\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.804Z\tOpportunistic Macroalgae Quadrats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323038\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Macroalgae Quadrats\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tWater Framework Directive opportunistic macroalgae quadrats. This data shows the positions of the quadrats that were taken for the following waterbodies from 2007 onwards; Alaw Artro Braint Dyfi Erch Ffraw Foryd Holyhead Straits MHI MHO Nyfer and Teifi. Species density and biomass information for these quadrats can be found on KiECO.\tPrivate record\n17576\tNRW_DS102009\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.512Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Mawddach Estuary Biotopes in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303039\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 549 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\MawddachEstuary\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The Afon Mawddach (Mawddach estuary) is a small drying bar-built estuary and it is one of the best examples of its type in the UK. With its mountainous catchment area it has some of the lowest nutrient inputs of any estuary in Wales.  For this reason the Mawddach estuary has been included in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The Mawddach estuary is predominantly a sandy/sandy mud estuary and contains communities characteristic of these conditions. The Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS) as part of the 2004 intertidal monitoring programme undertook a Phase 1 biotope survey of the Mawddach estuary following previous monitoring at the site. Specific aims of the survey included to carry out a Phase 1 biotope survey along predefined transects noting infaunal species and sediment characteristics to digitally record photographic evidence along each transect and to assign biotopes to each of the sampling locations. This in turn would allow information to be provided on conservation objectives of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC estuary feature.\tPublic record\n17470\tNRW_DS98777\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.137Z\tNational Parks\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373737\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\National Parks.lyr\tCarole Thornley, Lead Specialist Adviser Landscape, Designated Landscapes Programme\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of National Parks which are statutory landscape designations in Wales. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has a legal duty to maintain and make available National Park boundaries.  National Parks are designated for the purposes of conserving and enhancing the natural beauty wildlife and cultural heritage and for the public to enjoy the special qualities of these nationally important landscapes.   NRW is the designating authority for any new National Parks in Wales or boundary variations to existing ones however these must be confirmed by Welsh Ministers. NRWs statutory powers derive from the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act (NPAC) 1949 and Environment Act 1995. The Environment Act (1995) established National Park Authorities as independent Special Purpose Local Authorities to administer the National Park area in order to deliver their statutory purposes.   Three National Parks have been designated in Wales: Snowdonia 1951 (variation 1974); Pembrokeshire Coast 1952 (Variation 1989); and Brecon Beacons 1955 (Variation 1966).   The data has been held digitally since the mid-1990s. The boundary has been transferred to OS MasterMap by NRW and has been subject to joint checking by NRW staff and the relevant Park authority staff.\tPublic record\n17947\tNRW_DS114688\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.215Z\tBarbastelle (Barbastella barbastellus) Bat Distribution Model (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363838\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Bats\\Matt_Zeale_Barbastelle_Model\tInternal Custodian: Jean Matthews or Liz Halliwell - Mammal Ecologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group External Custodian: Bristol University / Matt Zeale  Ownership could be split between Bristol Uni, Matt Zeale and NRW at the moment it says various  Metadata point of Contact: GIS and Spatial Team.\tThe purpose of this data capture was to use a multidisciplinary approach to fill current knowledge gaps in the ecology and conservation requirements of a rare bat the barbastelle (Barbastella barbastellus).  Spatial modelling was used to evaluate the environmental parameters that dictate where the species is able to persist in the UK and to identify areas of high conservation value. The purpose of this data capture for Countryside Council for Wales was to assess and predict the distribution of the species around Wales. The models were created and used my Matthew Zeale a PhD student at the time. The PhD findings from the models indicated that B. barbastellus is highly dependent on large areas of native mature woodland and is limited in its distribution predominantly by summer climate although a number of other environmental factors also appear to be important. The data collation and survey undertaken by experienced bat biologist. The results were later submitted as part of a PhD Thesis.\tPublic record\n17645\tNRW_DS109743\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.077Z\tAcross Wales Diving Monitoring Project (2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373433\tData is stored in Marine Recorder. Data is archived  (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\) as project 54 media 335-353; and projects 215-225, 229, media numbers 581-591 and 598 and in the fireproof safe but currently the data is held by contractors and on CD.  Video data on the Z marine archive drive (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Video), still images on DVD.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, NRW.  Contact is probably Harry Goudge or Lucie Haines.\tThe initial 2 year project was a developmental trial for establishing  (i) Methods  (ii) Recording protocols.  (iii) Deployment strategies  (iv) Repeatability  (v) Site marking and relocation  (vi) Quality Control for site monitoring.   Following on from the initial 2 year project a longer-term project has been established to gather data in order to assess the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) sites for their \u0027favourable conservation status\u0027.  The concept of favourable conservation status is central to the EC Habitats Directive. These SAC sites included five marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) around the Welsh coast though only four of these (Menai Strait and Conwy Bay; Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau; Cardigan Bay; and Pembrokeshire Marine) have \u0027reefs\u0027 listed as Annex I \u0027features\u0027. An ongoing programme of monitoring the reefs in each of these SACs follows the establishment programme.\tPublic record\n17985\tNRW_DS114753\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.243Z\tMonitoring of a Maerl Bed in the Milford Haven Waterway, Pembrokeshire 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373533\tData is archived as project 337, media 1303.   Voucher specimens are to be held by the National Museums of Wales, Cardiff.\tMarine Biodiversity Ecologist, NRW. Team Leader - Marine Monitoring, NRW.\tMaerl is a collective term for several species of red seaweed with hard chalky skeletons. It is rock hard and unlike other seaweeds it grows as unattached rounded nodules or short branched shapes on the seabed.  Like all seaweeds maerl needs sunlight to grow and it only occurs to a depth of about 20 m. Maerl can form large beds when the conditions are right – a fast tidal flow or sufficient wave action to remove fine sediments but not strong enough to break the brittle maerl branches.  Within these beds layers of dead maerl build up with a thin layer of pink living maerl on the top. Maerl beds are classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitat listed under the Annex 1 of the Habitats Directive is on the list of OSPAR\u0027s List of Threatened and/or Declining Species and Habitats (Region 3 - Celtic Sea) and the habitat supports a number of UKBAP priority species and species of principal important for the purpose of conservation of biodiversity under the Natural Environment and Rural Communities Act 2006. This data contains the results of a monitoring exercise undertaken to assess the status of the maerl bed in the Haven for the reporting purposes and also to compare with baseline data gathered in 2005 to identify any trends. The information contributes to UK BAP monitoring and reporting obligations and to the management of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). This dataset forms part of repeated monitoring of 6 sites within the maerl bed in the Milford Haven waterway. The initial surveys were carried out in 2005.\tPublic record\n17099\tEXT_DS110030\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.552Z\tEnvironment Agency (EA): Catalogue of Light Detection and Ranging (LIDAR) Coverage (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303330\t\tGIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tLight Detection and Ranging (LIDAR) is an airborne mapping technique. The Environment Agency\u0027s LIDAR data archive contains digital elevation data derived from surveys carried out by the Environment Agency and represents over 10 years of survey work carried out by a specialist remote sensing team based in Bath and Coventry. Depending on the area data is available at 25cm 50cm 1m and 2m resolution which can be supplied as a Digital Surface Model (includes heights of objects such as vehicles buildings and vegetation as well as terrain surfaces) and a Digital Terrain Model (a \\bare earth model\\\" with surface objects filtered out of the DSM by applying bespoke software techniques). The purpose of this data capture was to produce accurate elevation data and highly detailed digital surface and terrain models.\"\tPrivate record\n18265\tNRW_DS116271\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.066Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323731\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2 clause 7 of the WFD defines coastal waterbodies as \u0027...a surface water on the landward side of a line every point of which is at a distance of one nautical mile on the seaward side from the nearest point of the baseline from which the breadth of territorial waters is measured extending where appropriate up to the outer limit of transitional waters\u0027. Coastal waters were defined by territorial waters 1 nautical mile from the Mean High Water coastline taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2. The delineation between coastal and estuarine waters was delineated by the Environment Agency defined transitional waterbodies. Waterbodies are also split and assigned to River Basin Districts. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics risk classification and proposed objectives. This dataset covers the layer for Cycle 2 of the Water framework Directive.\tPublic record\n18063\tNRW_DS115526\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.002Z\tMarine Monitoring Drop-Down Video around North Llyn Special Area of Conservation ( SAC ) 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353236\tData is Archived as Project No. 365, Media 1360.  (Full pathway: K:\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1360) All species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder [ MRCCW31700000005 \u0026amp; MRCCW3170000000B].   This monitoring is a follow on from data collected under archive project No. 35.\tInternal Custodian - CCW Marine Monitoring Ecologist.  Suggested NRW contact is Charles Lindenbaum, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team.   NRW Metadata contact - Data Services\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive. The Habitats Directive includes a list of habitat types and species that are considered to be most in need of conservation at a European level (excluding birds which are covered under the EC Birds Directive). Once sites have been designated the country agencies work with owners occupiers and others to secure their management and protection regime.    This dataset provides a continuation of data collected from Drop Down Video Monitoring in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.  The purpose of this data capture was to inform management decisions within the SACs. QA was undertaken including 5% of tows being re-analysed by a different surveyor and QA of at least 10% of data entry. Analysis and QA undertaken by Marine EcoSol.\tPublic record\n18416\tNRW_DS119291\t2024-05-28T12:48:34.967Z\tTotal Peatland\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323931\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\Total Peatland.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset comprises the total peatland for Wales. The peat is categorised as Deep Peaty Soil Modified Deep Peaty Soil Shallow Peaty Soil and Soil with Peaty Pockets.\tPublic record\n17322\tEXT_DS125220\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.247Z\tWelsh Government: Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) data on NRW land (3rd Party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323230\t\tFull resolution data for NRW land only.  NRW has just negotiated access to all the data 22/06/2022.  Matt is going to discuss with Barny if we need to keep the NRW land version or whether the full data will replace it or whether we will need metadata entries for all three Reduced resolution data downloaded from CEH Full resolution data for NRW land Full resolution data for all Wales\tGlastir is the Welsh Government\u0027s sustainable land management scheme which pays for environmental goods and services aimed at:  Combating climate change Improving water quality and managing water resources Improving soil quality and management Halting biodiversity loss Managing landscapes and historic environment and improving public access to the countryside Woodland creation and management  The Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) has been commissioned by the Welsh Government to assess the performance of Glastir. GMEP was launched at the same time as the Glastir scheme. This provides fast policy feedback allowing for the scheme to be modified to improve efficiency and effectiveness.\tPrivate record\n17784\tNRW_DS110509\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.233Z\tMonitoring and Surveillance of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353039\tMarine Monitoring is stored W: Drive  Terrestrial SAC Monitoring Paper Records: A filing cabinet of paper records which have not been digitised and is current stored with Tom Harrison.    Electronic Data: Our electronic data is stored in the following locations: Pre 2013 - On Ffynnon, SAC monitoring data is stored under the Monitoring folder for each SAC, e.g. Protected Sites - North Region - North West - Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn SAC ( SH55.11.100 ) – Monitoring Within each monitoring folder there will be a folder for each monitoring cycle of each feature, e.g. for the Gwyrfai there are folders for “Luronium natans 99 to 06”, “Luronium natans 07 to 12”, “Lutra lutra 99 to 06”, “Lutra Lutra 07 to 12”, etc.  There will also be a folder called “SAC Monitoring reports” which should contain the final SAC monitoring reports for each feature.  Post 2013 where projects were started once the DMS had been set up. This folder system is recreated on DMS for monitoring undertaken in the last 18 months or so.  It is all stored under the Land Management part of DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/SitePages/Home.aspx\tInternal Custodians: North Region: Tom Harrison, Environmental Assessment Officer, NE EAAT. South Region: Karen Wilkinson, Environmental Assessment Officer, SE EAAT South Region: Dan Guest, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SW EAAT South Region: Alex Harding, Team Leader, SW EAAT Used to be: David Allen, Environmental Monitoring Manager, CCW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results from on going monitoring of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) across Wales. One of the key responsibilities of the statutory nature conservation agencies such as Natural Resources Wales (NRW) in the UK is the identification and protection of a series of sites intended to conserve important wildlife and earth science features. These sites include SACs which are designated as part of the EC Habitats Directive.  Every six years Member States of the European Union are required to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive under Article 17 of the Directive. This reporting provides information on: the general implementation of the Directive; and the conservation status of individual habitats and species listed under the Annexes of the Directive.\tPublic record\n18044\tNRW_DS115216\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.953Z\tAerial Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay 2010 - 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323136\tData is archived under Project No 301, media 1404\tInternal Custodian:  Maritime Ornithologist, Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tCarmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16000 individuals: Wetlands International 2006) of the biogeographic population of common scoter Melanitta nigra. Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters (less than 10 m) in Carmarthen Bay.   Common scoter are also listed as a UK BAP species. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has supported ground based counts since 1996 following the Sea Empress oil spill. The purpose of this data capture was to collect information regarding the abundance and distribution of common scoters in the Carmarthen Bay SPA.\tPublic record\n18441\tNRW_DS119129\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.315Z\tGrey Seal Halichoerus grypus Phenology at Skomer and Marloes Peninsula\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313239\tData is held in DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Marine \u0026gt;Marine Mammals \u0026gt; Evidence Base / Contracts \u0026gt; Seal Monitoring and Research \u0026gt; Grey Seal phenology reports Referenced in Marine Archive as project 478, media 1555. • Marloes Peninsula census data.xlsx (EVID-307-372) • phenology basic analysis marloes.R (EDIV-307-371) • Data_Skomer.xls (EVID-307-375)  and metadata Data_Skomer_Notes.xls (EVID-307-377) • Skomer_daily_births.csv (EVID-307-376) • Skomer analysis (EVID-307-373)\tOriginator: NRW Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist: Tom Stringell (contractor: James Bull, Dept. Biosciences, Swansea University)\tThe grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) is listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive and is a qualifying feature of three Special Areas of Conservation in Wales. Pembrokeshire colonies represent the largest breeding population in southwest Britain. The aim of this study was to provide evidence on the status of grey seal pup numbers around the Island of Skomer and the Marloes Peninsula Pembrokeshire based on over 30 years of survey data collected from 1983-2015. This report quantifies recent population growth analyses the timing of the pupping season and makes recommendations for the future of this survey.  Logistical constraints at the end of the observing season required data modelling to estimate both the mid-point and length of the pupping season.\tPublic record\n18380\tNRW_DS116788\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.434Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Acid Sensitive Waterbodies Cycle 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373838\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 2 2016.lyr\tRob Blacklidge- Reporting Team WFD Specialist\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Acid Sensitive Waterbodies Cycle 2 (2014 - 2019) is a spatial (polygon shapefile) dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). To implement the guidance on managing forests in order to minimise adverse impacts in acid-sensitive areas two categories of acid sensitivity have been identified. These are defined as \u0027failing due to acidification\u0027 and \u0027at risk of failing due to acidification\u0027.   Water bodies identified as currently \u0027failing\u0027 have been classified through the Water Framework Directive monitoring as being of less than \u0027good\u0027 status and the cause of that failure has been attributed to acidification. The \u0027at risk of failing\u0027 water bodies have been identified via the findings of the Welsh acidification risk assessment as being at risk of failing to achieve \u0027good\u0027 status by 2027 due to acid deposition. The risk assessment combines chemical and biological data and an assessment of the environment\u0027s ability to neutralise the predicted acid pollutants.\tPublic record\n18188\tNRW_DS116014\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.510Z\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Welsh Woodlands (1996 - 1997)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303134\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000001 Welsh Woodlands.\tWoodland Ecologist, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 11833 observations in woodlands of conservation interest in across Wales. The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities and to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tPublic record\n17578\tNRW_DS102011\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.509Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy Gravel Community (IMX.VsenMtru) in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303131\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-291 is archived as Project No 199,Media No 557 (K:\\Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 557) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon. (New file path: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\MuddyGravels Marine Recorder file: MRCCW3030000000B\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThe results of monitoring the extent and species richness of a distinct area of mixed sediment that lies east of Llanbedrog beach. The muddy gravel community is - IMX.VsenMtru (Venerupis senegalensis and Mya truncata in lower shore or infralittoral muddy gravel) and has a high biodiversity of epifauna flora and infauna due to the admixture of cobbles pebbles gravel sand and mud. This provides many different habitat niches. The habitat is located within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) on the lower shore at Llanbedrog. In fully marine conditions on the lower shore this habitat can be particularly species rich due to the complex nature of the substratum supporting a high diversity of infauna and epifauna however many factors affect the habitat physical disturbance bait digging organic enrichment introduction of non-native species etc. The purpose of this data capture was to map the current extent of muddy gravel define the character of the muddy gravel for in situ recognition identify current known species composition and identify location for representative core sampling and Particle Size Analysis.\tPublic record\n18837\tNRW_DS124669\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.842Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Bridges\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363639\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Bridges.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Bridges. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18841\tNRW_DS124654\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.048Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Spur Roads\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363534\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Spur Roads.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Spur Roads. Spur roads are all roads that are used to access the wind turbine power systems. These roads tend to divert from the original forest road system and in places they enter the demised areas. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17065\tEXT_DS100762\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.029Z\tNational Trust: Ownership (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373632\tX:\\Ownership\\National Trust\\National Trust Ownership.lyr   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\external_agency\\national_trust\\nt_ownership_wales.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a spatial dataset that has been supplied by the National Trust and contains digital boundaries of the National Trust land ownership within Wales. Natural Resources Wales has access to this is data through an agreed National Trust Licence Agreement which states we can use this data for internal business use only.\tPublic record\n17083\tEXT_DS115163\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.433Z\tEIRGrid plc East West Interconnector Project in the Irish Sea (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313633\tArchived as Project 374, Media 1384 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1384)  [A] Reports and appendices in .pdf format [B] Report Images/charts for each of the above [C] GIS data in ArcView and for use with ERMapper. Include habitat data (grab Photo locations, contours, seabed photo locations, side Scan locations, Video footage locations) [D] SideScan images in GeoTiff format [E] Source data.  unprocessed XYZ data for Bathymetry and sidescan data. [F] Processed gridded xyz data in tiff form [G] Images of sediment grabs and seabed [H] Video data in .VOB format (for use with DVD) [I] Supporting information as txt and pdf files.  Information about data and permissions for use\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team GIS Service Desk  External Custodian: EirGrid Plc\tFUGRO OSAE GmbH was contracted by EirGrid to survey an electrical cable route for the East West Interconnector Project which links the Republic of Ireland and the United Kingdom by a power cable.  The final selected route links Rush North Beach in Ireland and Barkby Beach in North Wales.  JP Kenny acted as marine engineering advisor.  This route was fully surveyed with bathymetric environmental geophysical and geotechnical methods from landing site to landing site. The total length of the final selected route from Rush North Beach to Barkby Beach is approximately 186 km and between 400 to 500 m wide. This data was required to plan the most favourable route option for installation of the cable link between Ireland (Rush North Beach) to North Wales (Prestatyn Barkby Beach).\tPublic record\n17072\tEXT_DS100758\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.871Z\tWelsh Government: Less Favoured Areas (LFA) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373538\tx:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Less Favoured Areas.lyr\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a GIS dataset owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use only.  In recognition of the different physical and socio-economic characteristics across the regions the European Union introduced the Less Favoured Area (LFA) designation to support farming where production conditions are difficult. The criteria for LFA designation were first established in European legislation in 1975 (Directive 75/268 EEC and accompanying measures).\tPublic record\n17385\tNRW_DS24421\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.138Z\tLand Agency Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343231\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tWest Region Regional Admin Manager, NRW\tManages award and payment process for management agreements on National Nature Reserves (NNRs) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) including land purchases and buildings leases.\tPublic record\n17398\tNRW_DS86828\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.312Z\tSurvey of North Wales and Pembrokeshire tide influenced communities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3836383238\t[A] Data is archived as project 79, media 223 and 224 (File paths  K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 224) [B]Species and habitat data held in Marine Recorder (MRCCW80000000002, MRCCW80000000003, MRCCW80000000004, MRCCW80000000005). [C] Paper MNCR forms in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. Tidal Rapids is one of those habitats. Tidal Rapids are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan feature and rocky Reefs are listed as an Annex I feature under the Habitats Directive (1992). These features are of conservation importance and the data collected during this survey is used to help define conservation objectives and inform decisions regarding protection and management.\tPublic record\n17519\tNRW_DS101856\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.701Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Subtidal Algal Assemblages\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383536\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/algae.    2005 Data also available in HQ Marine Recorder (Survey number MRCCW16800000008)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Skomer MNR\u0027s algal communities have been identified as being rich and diverse with 241 species of red green and brown algae recorded. This represents 34% of the British marine flora and 21% of North Atlantic marine flora including two nationally scarce species five near their limit of distribution four species with specialised habitat preferences and five deep water algal species. Skomer MNR has been identified as a Criteria B European IPA (important plant area) for marine algae. The area has a wide range of habitats including excellent examples of algal communities on bedrock boulders and cobbles. It has a very high diversity of algae with over 240 species recorded. Rare or threatened species include: Atractophora hypnoides Sphacelaria mirabilis Hydrolithon cruciatum and Hinksia ovata. In situ recording and collection is used to survey the algae at various habitats around the Reserve. A herbarium is kept for all notable species found. Methods have varied from year to year. The whole project was reviewed in 2007. There were numerous aims for this project and data capture.  1. To monitor temporal change in algal communities; 2. To monitor algal species of recognised importance; 3. To investigate species diversity using taxonomic distinctness analysis; 4. To establish a monitoring protocol for the future that is both achievable and worthwhile.\tPublic record\n18029\tNRW_DS114836\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.693Z\tCollection Activity in the Menai Strait and Pilot Methods to Assess Frequency of Boulder Turning Activity (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383336\tArchived as project 355, media 1334.\tData Custodian:  Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive. Reporting on the condition of the intertidal features of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) depends upon an understanding of the features and the threats of damage by regulated and unregulated activities.The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) run an intertidal SAC monitoring program which is undertaken to evaluate the quality of the biological communities associated with under-boulder and muddy gravel habitats. The biological data must be placed in context with the levels of collecting activity on the shore specifically bait and winkle collecting. CCW contracted Marine EcoSol to better assess the intensity of collecting activity in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC in order to establish the levels that might be considered  acceptable in terms of their impact on the intertidal (Reef) features. Both muddy gravel and underboulder communities are Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats and the 2011 field surveys are within or adjacent to intertidal Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and marine SAC.  The project was to investigate the feasibility of developing rapid visual assessment methods for use by non-specialists and to provide contextual information for status reporting of reef features within the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay SAC.\tPublic record\n18175\tNRW_DS115886\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.892Z\tMenai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) muddy gravel survey (2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383836\tCurrently stored at: he data are archived as Project No 411, Media No xxx and is stored on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor office.  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSCB\\2013 ASML MSCB  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder\\MarineRecorderData\\MRCCW163_JenJones-original\\From Jen Jones 2014\\NBNData CCW163 JJ Pembs \u0026amp; Menai muddy gravel.mdb    Marine Recorder Survey MRCCW16300000031 2013 ASML /NRW Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC muddy gravel survey  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2013 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tSeveral rich marine mud and muddy gravel habitats were identified within the Menai Strait by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Marine Intertidal Phase 1 survey team and a sampling programme initiated in order to better characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats. Marine mud and muddy gravel habitats are an important feature of several Special Area of Conservation (SACs) including the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SACs. The purpose of this data capture was to carry out a repeat survey of sites previously surveyed to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data. In fully marine conditions on the lower shore sheltered muddy gravel habitats can be particularly species rich due to the complex nature of the substratum supporting a high diversity of infauna and epifauna.\tPublic record\n17268\tEXT_DS122505\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.759Z\tEnvironment Agency: All Wales 2017 Saltmarsh Zonation Points (WFD Cycle 3) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353035\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh Areas\\All Wales 2017 Saltmarsh Zonation Points (WFD Cycle 3).lyr\tMarine Monitoring Officer\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. Saltmarsh zonation points determined in a desk based study using aerial imagery. This data is Open but owned by the Environment Agency.\tPublic record\n18367\tNRW_DS116814\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.360Z\tLIDAR terrain and surfaces models Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383134\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\LiDAR\\Current LiDAR\tBen Anscombe / Louise Harris (Spatial Services Team).\tThis dataset shows the extent of data in each individual tile that went into the merging process to create the LIDAR Composite DSM \u0026amp; DTM.   Light Detection and Ranging (LIDAR) is an airborne mapping technique which uses a laser to measure the distance between the aircraft and the ground. Up to 100000 measurements per second are made of the ground allowing highly detailed terrain models to be generated at spatial resolutions of between 25cm and 2 metres.   The Natural Resources Wales composite dataset contains digital elevation data derived from surveys carried out over several years and covers approximately 70% of Wales. We have made available 25cm 50cm 1m and 2m datasets supplied as terrain models (a representation of the ground level) or surface models (a representation of object heights such as vehicles buildings and vegetation).   In addition to the height information georeferenced coloured shaded relief images at the same resolution as the input LiDAR data grids are available.\tPublic record\n18428\tNRW_DS119161\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.083Z\tMain Rivers\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313631\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Main Rivers.lyr\tRoss Akers/ Mark Pugh       Have based start date on the date that EA have recorded in their metadata on data.gov\tThe statutory main river map is a 1:10000 scale spatial (polyline) dataset that defines statutory watercourses designated by Natural Resources Wales as main rivers. The data is for Wales only. \u0027Main rivers\u0027 are usually larger streams and rivers but some of them are small watercourses of significance. They include certain structures that control or regulate the flow of water in into or out of the channel.\tPublic record\n18290\tNRW_DS116312\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.546Z\tHazardous Waste Registrations with Standard Industrial Classification (SIC) Code\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333132\tData is held in the NRW Customer Relationship Management Database.\tCustodian: Stephen Badman Customer Contact Centre\tThe Hazardous Waste Regulations 2005 require that anyone who produces or holds hazardous waste at any premises in Wales must register the premises with Natural Resources Wales each year unless the total quantity of hazardous waste is less than 500 kg each year.    There is an online public register that allows searches to be run on registrations which shows the business name registration reference address  postcode registration start date and registration end date. The dataset contains information about live registrations.\tPublic record\n17089\tEXT_DS114950\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.677Z\tIMAGIN Acoustic Multibeam Survey of the North Coast of Wales (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393530\tArchived as Project 312, Media 1235.   Reports available online through http://imagin.ucc.ie.   Master copy of data stored by the IMAGIN project and available on the IMAGIN website.\tGIS Service Desk  Marine Mapping Scientist, NRW\tMultibeam data for the North Wales part of the Irish Sea Marine Aggregates Initiative (IMAGIN) project. This includes GeoTiffs (Black and white) navigation data from the vessel and raw data in CARIS HIPS format. Collect multibeam data to support the IMAGIN project (Geological appraisal of marine aggregate resources in the Southern Irish Sea).\tPublic record\n18408\tNRW_DS117602\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.342Z\tEnvironmental Incident data held in Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137363032\tData is stored in WIRS. WIRS is stored on its own server.\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Farnhill, Data Quality Officer, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Data Services Team\tDetails of all environmental incidents that are reported to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) via the environmental incident hotline or by internal NRW staff via the online reporting form are held on the Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS). WIRS in one of NRW\u0027s centralised storage databases. It hold information related to but not restricted too incidents such as; environmental pollution (land water and air); illegal dumping of waste (including fly-tipping); damage destruction and removal of sensitive habitats and species; and incidents relating to the increased risk of flooding. Approximately 9000 calls reporting incidents in Wales are received and processed each year. A risk based approach is used to assess response and is proportionate to our ability to take action to prevent or mitigate impact to the environment; NRW\u0027s role within the incident: and NRW\u0027s professional reputation in influencing the greater public interest. Response time to all major and significant incidents is within 2 hours during office hours and 4 hours outside office hours. Other incidents are assessed based on the risk based approach.  As of 2016-03-01 data is recorded and stored on the Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS). Prior to this between 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-29 data was stored on the National Incident Recording System (NIRS). The system allows user to manage and track the progress of incidents.  All pertinent details relating to an incident should be recorded as soon as possible. The data recorded over time relating to incidents can be used to provide management information determine trends and highlight areas of concern amongst other uses.\tPublic record\n18339\tNRW_DS116508\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.134Z\tIntertidal Monitoring Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353038\tThe data are archived as The Project Number is 411, Media number 1525   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.   H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2013 ASML PLAS\tInternal Custodian - Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tPen Llyn a’r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for its estuaries and their constituent mudflats sandflats and reefs; large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds; populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus piddocks in intertidal clay and lower shore boulder communities and reefs particularly the biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata.  These features are monitored by ASML on behalf of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) which has a statutory duty to produce advice under Regulation 35 (formerly Regulation 33) of the Habitats Regulations 1994. This dataset is a compilation of a number of intertidal research and monitoring programmes conducted in 2013.  This included intertidal surveying of the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata at West Afon Dwyfor and Llandanwg; the surveying of the population status of Pectenogammarus populations (LGS.Pec) a scarce shingle shore specific amphipod found near Pwllheli; sediment analyses of biotopes of the Dyfi and Glaslyn/Dwyryd estuaries were conducted and subsequently the Glaslyn/Dwyryd infaunal samples were analysed; and finally a species list was created using fixed monitoring quadrats in the upper mid and lower shore at Porth Oer the results of which were compared to 2012 survey results.\tPublic record\n18924\tNRW_DS124841\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.720Z\tFlood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383431\tX\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Assessment Wales\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tFlood Risk Assessment Wales provides a national assessment of risk flooding from Rivers the Sea and Surface Water and Small Watercourses. The assessment takes into account flood defences and combines new national-scale modelling with detailed local-scale models to categories risk into 3 bands labelled ‘High’ ‘Medium’ and ‘Low’ risk.\tPublic record\n18168\tNRW_DS115887\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.868Z\tComprehensive reassessment of Non Native Species in Welsh marinas (Marine Recorder dataset)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383837\t[A] Species data is held in Marine Recorder: 2014 NRW Comprehensive reassessment of NNS in Welsh marinas MRCCW32900000002 [B] Copy of the source NBN file currently stored at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder\\MarineRecorderData\\MRCCW329_BishopsGroup_MBA\\MRCCW329_NBNDATA.mdb  [C] A copy of the Marine Recorder species data is included on the NBN Gateway  Archived as project 457 Media 1518 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1501to1600\\Media 1518) A copy of the MEDIN format dataset is lodged at DASSH (likely to be condition of the WG funding).  NRW do not hold a copy in this format.  We are not publishing the metadata to MEDIN as we assume DASSH are.  DASSH identifier DASSHDT00000083\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team       We have not set this metadata entry to harvest to MEDIN portal because NRW only hold a copy of MR data, DASSH have the rest of the data and we assume that they are publishing metadata for the full data outputs. DASSH identifier DASSHDT00000083\tThe dataset is part of an ongoing rapid assessments for presence/absence of marine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) in Welsh marinas.  Survey undertaken to assess the presence of non-native species in 15 marinas throughout Wales. Funding from obtained by Welsh Government Resilient Ecosystems Fund which is administered by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Comprehensive reassessment of non-native species on floating pontoons in Welsh marinas. The purpose of this data capture was to look at the spread of invasive non-natives in Wales and provide early warnings for new introductions with a possible view to employing eradication techniques.  This metadata only covers the data held in NRWs copy of marine recorder.\tPublic record\n17842\tNRW_DS111177\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.525Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313737\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 1221. [A]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 60 - Pembroke Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML[B]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs[C]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder[D]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\GIS[E]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\Images\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) was selected for eight Habitats Directive Annex I habitat types and five Annex II species. The SAC is considered to be one of the best areas in the UK for marine reef habitats a category within the Annex I list. High habitat and biological diversity is of great importance throughout the site particularly the best documented reefs habitat in the Milford Haven ria-estuary. This dataset includes the results of a survey of intertidal rockpools at Pen-y-Holt on the Castlemartin peninsula.\tPublic record\n17577\tNRW_DS102008\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.533Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata Reefs in Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) (2004/2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303038\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 548 (File Path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 548) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maesy Ffynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maesy Ffynnon.(File paths:Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Sabellaria2004 and Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Sabellaria2005 Marine Recorder Survey numbers: MRCCW30200000010, MRCCW30200000019\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats). Dense congregations of S. alveolata tubes form large biogenic reef structures which can be up to a metre thick and take the form of hummocks sheets or more massive formations. This dataset combines the results of two monitoring events carried out on Sabellaria alveolata reef features within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC during 2004 and 2005.  In 2004 the purpose of this data capture was to identify specific survey locations measure the extent of S. alveolata reef along agreed transects assess the quality of S. alveolata reef present.  Map and make notes on any sightings of wireweed Sargassum muticum.  In 2005 the purpose of this data capture was more detailed monitoring of selected reef areas to thoroughly characterise small patches of reef and make an assessment of the impacts of anthropogenic disturbance on reef diversity.\tPublic record\n19157\tNRW_DS125741\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.465Z\tKieco Marine Fish\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373431\t\t\tMarine fish are used as an indicator of stock status water and habitat quality. This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the fish surveys conducted across Wales marine environment by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\tPublic record\n17845\tNRW_DS111176\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.530Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rocky Reefs in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313736\t[A] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 60 - Pembroke Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs [C] NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey No. MRCCW1630000000C) [D] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\GIS [E] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tPembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is considered to be one of the best areas in the UK for marine reef habitats a category within annex 1 of the Habitats Directive. This dataset comprises of the results of re-surveys of the six Milford Haven rocky intertidal transects first established in 2005 and the additional establishment of a similar transect at Nolton Haven in the north of Pembrokeshire.  The purpose of this data capture was to repeat a set of six permanent monitoring sites representative of the rocky shore intertidal habitats within the Haven Waterway and last surveyed in 2005 and to present data on the current status of the sites. This data was also used to establish another site outside the Haven to the north at Nolton Haven.\tPublic record\n19163\tNRW_DS125721\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.609Z\tNational Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373231\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Management National Receptors Database 2023 (NRD 2023).lyr\tData Custodian: Dave Tarrant, Lead Specialist Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors typically residential and commercial property intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management. It is a spatial dataset produced from Ordnance Survey (OS) AddressBase® Premium (ABP) and OS MasterMap® (MM) data with information on property type floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centre’s Multi-Coloured Manual (MCM) codes attributed.    The version of the NRD described here is the NRD 2023 created as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Data Management (FRAW DM) process. The NRD 2023 builds on the same methodology as NRD 2018 with a refresh of input datasets.    NRD 2023 comprises of two layers NRD 2023 created from the OS AddressBase Premium dataset and NRD 2023 from OSMM which is created from OS MasterMap.  Both datasets should be viewed together to give a complete view of the NRD 2023 dataset.      List of improvements from NRD 2018 to NRD 2023 include:  1. Main input datasets have been updated including; OS ABP OS MM NRW Flood Alert and Warning Areas NRW Flood Warning Uptake Defended Areas.  2. Over 52000 Static Caravans are now included in the OS MM datasets therefore these have now been included in the NRD 2023 from OSMM Layer.  3. The building footprint threshold within the NRD 2023 from OSMM layer has been increased from 30m² to 60m².  This is to remove unwanted features like garages being classified as “Residential” dwelling.  4. Ordnance Survey have significantly improved their classification of properties across Wales.  For example end of terrace houses in some location were called “semi-detached” previously these have now been correctly assigned as “end of terrace” in NRD 2023.\tPublic record\n17849\tNRW_DS111178\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.709Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria Reef in Cardigan Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313738\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No, 199, Media No. 548. [A] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 57 - Cardigan Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Data [C] CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [D] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\GIS [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Images\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats).  Cardigan SAC is designated for a number of features including Bottlenosed dolphins Sandbanks Reefs and Sea Caves and some intertidal features including areas of bedrock and boulder/cobble habitats and many areas are characterised by biogenic reef formed by the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata. This dataset includes the results of a survey of the biogenic reef formed by the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata at two sites within Cardigan Bay SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to establish sites for long term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods. This is a high quality survey carried out by an experienced team of marine biologists.\tPublic record\n18336\tNRW_DS116511\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.016Z\tMacrofaunal Community Monitoring on Saline Lagoons across Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353131\tData is currently being entered into Marine Recorder by a contractor – Marine Ecosol\tInternal Custodian: Mike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge \u0026amp; Advice  The data was entered into Marine Recorder by Harry Godge from Marine Ecosol.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team - NRW\tShallow coastal lagoons are designated as a priority Annex I Habitat under the EC Habitats Directive. This dataset includes the infaunal community and species populations and sediment grain distribution data for five Welsh lagoons. Infaunal communities of three lagoons (Cemlyn Anglesey; Pickleridge Pembrokeshire; and Morfa Gwyllt Gwynedd) have been annually monitored since 2006 and two lagoons (Neyland and Carew Pembrokeshire) have been monitored since 2011. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the ecological health of invertebrate communities and population dynamics across lagoon environments in order to infer the health and functionality of the ecosystems at each lagoon.\tPublic record\n18411\tNRW_DS118494\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.333Z\tAfon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Freshwater Pearl Mussel Population Condition Assessment Report - Habitats Directive Reporting Cycle 3 (2013 - 2018)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138343934\tReport is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports\tNRW Rivers Ecologist: Sue Hearn (Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW)\tFreshwater pearl mussel counts were carried out in specific monitoring sections and transects on the Afon Eden. The methodology followed protocol devised for the Afon Eden in 2003. The data was collected to feed into condition assessment of the pearl mussel population on the Afon Eden according to Common Standards Monitoring guidance for Freshwater fauna for the following attributes: Spatial extent Population density Age structure Dead shells Suitable habitat\tPublic record\n18388\tNRW_DS119101\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.272Z\tMarine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Features\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313031\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\SAC features X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\SAC Features Archive   There are 43 datasets\tKaren Robinson- Marine Ecologist - EVIAN       This metadata entry covers both the live data and the archive. Although current regulation is Reg 37, marine team have not updated any of the exiting layers.  Therefore the latest data is Reg 35 in the X drive data and is supplied in response to request for this dataset.  It is classed as Reg 37 data and a copy may be renamed to separate between the two.  Each layer will be updated as new evidence becomes available. The published data is technically the dataset generated for Reg35 but is the most recent information to inform Reg 37\tThe Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2010 provide for the designation and protection of \u0027European sites\u0027 the protection of \u0027European protected species\u0027 and the adaptation of planning and other controls for the protection of European Sites. This dataset is made up of 43 datasets showing the extent of features for which our marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) are designated and forms part of the advice packages Natural Resources Wales (NRW) prepares for our marine SACs under Regulation 37 (formerly Regulation 33 and 35) of the Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations. They show the presence of (extent) of features within our marine SACs at the time of site designation against which statutory advice can be given. They also act as a baseline for feature extent against which changes can be measured during reporting under Article 17 of the Habitats Directive.\tPublic record\n17252\tEXT_DS123364\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.957Z\tForestry Commission Great Britain (FCGB) Forest Research Experiment Sites (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333634\tx:\\Ownership\\Monitoring Equipment\\FCGB Forest Research Experiment Sites.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17131\tEXT_DS120291\t2024-05-28T12:14:09.830Z\tAir Quality Concentrations (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230323931\tx:\\Resource Management\\Air Quality\\\t\tThis is a duplicate entry that should be deleted.  Please refer to metadata records EXT_DS120174 Defra: National Atmospheric Emissions Inventory (NAEI) (3rd party data)\tPrivate record\n17087\tEXT_DS114487\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.604Z\tMarine Aggreggates Levy Sustainability Fund (MALSF) Outer Bristol Channel Marine Habitat Study (Biomor 4 report) - Multibeam Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343837\tArchived as Project 308, Media 1229 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1229) MALSF data is managed by BGS as a MEDIN Data Archive Centre (DAC).  The data is available through the DAC network.\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team       Vertical Extent:  -10. -60; metres (Tidal corrections carried out on the data during post processing)\tThe Outer Bristol Channel Marine Habitat Study was a multifaceted project involving scientific investigation Interpretation and education in an area with potential marine aggregate resources. The Bristol Channel and Severn Estuary boarded by Wales and England has the largest tidal range in UK waters and is a multiple-use environment. Natural resource activities include aggregate extraction fisheries and wind-farm developments. Marine nature conservation is prominent with Lundy Marine Nature Reserve in the south and under the EC Habitats Directive a Special Area of Conservation. The purpose of this study was to undertake a variety of work to collect and provide data and interpretations on the current physical state of the sea bed environment in terms of biology sediment geology and morphology. The study was jointly lead by the British Geological Survey (BGS) and Amgueddfa Cymru - National Museum Wales (NMW) and supported by core funding from the Aggregate Levy Sustainable Fund (ALSF) in Wales and England. Additional support came from The Crown Estate and the British Marine Aggregate Producers Association (BMAPA). There were numerous aims to this project as outlined in the report (see related titles). This dataset just covers one specific aim: To undertake a marine geophysical survey utilising multibeam sidescan sonar and sub-bottom seismic reflection systems. This dataset comprises of multibeam bathymetry data for the Outer Bristol Channel including raw data files cruise logs calibration data processed data and geotiffs. As well as the sidescan sonar and sub bottom profile data.  The purpose of this data capture was to collect multibeam data for the Biomor project to give a 3D image of the seabed and enable display of sandbanks ascertain slope steepness and identify other seabed features. Additional backscatter and acoustic data along with benthic sampling enabled the multibeam data to be used to classify and map the seabed.\tPublic record\n18891\tNRW_DS124794\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.036Z\tMarine Protected Areas in Welsh waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373934\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\tNatasha Lough.  Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tThe Marine protected area (MPA) network is a subset of existing protected sites that have marine features. Sites include: SACs SPAs Ramsars SSSIs and MCZs.     What features are considered marine is determined through expert groups of SNCBs led by JNCC. The list of MPA network features are hosted by JNCC https://hub.jncc.gov.uk/assets/8ee15786-510b-44e4-819e-e6681a1abd96.     The need for a coherent and well managed MPA network is underpinned by the legislation Marine and Coastal access act and MFSD (UK Marine Strategy). This has been driven by our commitment to OSPAR (Oslo Paris convention) and CBD (Convention on biological diversity). NRW are the custodians of MPAs within 12nm (Welsh territorial waters) sites that are found in out offshore waters are hosted by JNCC and can be downloaded from their website https://jncc.gov.uk/mpa-mapper/.\tPublic record\n17389\tNRW_DS24333\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.219Z\tMarine Phase 1 Intertidal Habitat Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333333\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal Data is archived under project no. 204, media no\u0027s 34-35,148,194-195,569,1157-1183,1185,1187-1190 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media etc.)  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThis is a dataset compiled of spatially-referenced survey data obtained from different intertidal communities (biotopes) in Wales. Maps include information on biotope location species composition access and photographs points.  Additional information is recorded as target notes.  Further site information including site descriptions  geomorphology uses impacts target notes and habitat information is held in an accompanying database  Intertidal mapped data provides an integrated information resource for Local Authority structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.   The maps give important regional information about habitat and biotope distribution. They are used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) as a basis for the development of sensitivity mapping and as a baseline for developing monitoring of intertidal features in marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC). Sites are mapped once and therefore are a \u0027snapshot\u0027 in time.  All data has been subject to a validation process. The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted.  An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under NRW licence.\tPublic record\n18047\tNRW_DS115165\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.345Z\tMarine Habitat Vulnerability Assessment of the Welsh Coast - Phase 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313635\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat vulnerability.lyr   Archived as project 378, media 1389 (K:\\Archive\\Media\\Media 1301-1400\\Media 1389. The database is held in Z:\\Archive\\CCW_H_Drive\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Ph1sites\\Intertidal Site Assessments\\Intertidal Site Assessment Contract\\ExeGesIS Products\\Assessment Database V10 The database is called: IntertidalPhase1Analysisv10.mde In this folder on the Z:\\ drive are also ArcGIS format maps covering the assessments of biotopes and units, and also summary report tables.\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe coastline of Wales supports a wide variety of important marine ecological systems habitats and species. A range of human activities such as coastal developments and defences can result in the loss of these important coastal and marine habitats. These human activities coupled with future climate change in particular sea level rise could have significant impacts upon the natural coastal and marine environments.  Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) require a way to model the vulnerability of coastal habitats to these impacts.  The following metadata describes phase 1 of this project. The purpose of this data capture was to produce a marine habitat vulnerability assessment of the Welsh Coast to consider those habitats/biotopes that are vulnerable to coastal squeeze as a result of coastal management and other natural constraints for the coast of Wales.\tPublic record\n19166\tNRW_DS125704\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.656Z\tLichen Survey of Baron Hill Park SSSI\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373034\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FBaron%20Hill%20Park\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tA survey of epiphytic lichens on parkland trees at Baron Hill Park SSSI\tPublic record\n19043\tNRW_DS125335\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.325Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Operational Catchments Cycle 3 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333335\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Operational Catchments Cycle 3 2022.lyr\tRob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting Team\tWFD Operational Catchments’ is a polygon Shapefile dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).  Operational catchments are the unit of geography that group river water bodies into smaller sub-catchments.    These units support the management and understanding of the river network at a local scale.\tPublic record\n18056\tNRW_DS115553\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.551Z\tFishMap Môn Generalised Fishing Intensity Levels\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353533\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists). Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRWCrown Copyright and Database Right.\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW) the Welsh Government and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities. The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey.   Fishing Intensity is described as the effort of a fishing activity in a given area. For example this could be the number of pots hauled per day per hectare. Fishing intensity is described as one of three levels but the actual intensity value is derived differently for each fishing type. The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging Queen Scallop Dredging Mussel Seed Dredging Light Otter Trawling Bottom Set Nets Potting Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering.   There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors From the attribute \\_overlaps\\\"  it is possible to determine the number of vessels/ fishers operating in a particular area. Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the FishMap Môn intensity level data were created both a detailed and generalised version. The detailed version is sensitive and therefore has restrictions accompanying the dataset however this generalised version does not.\"  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning\tPublic record\n19135\tNRW_DS125549\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.337Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Catchments Cycle 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353439\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Coastal Catchments Cycle 3.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge - Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Catchments Cycle 3 are a polygon dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams rivers and possibly lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. It is a layer of the undesignated small coastal catchments and is used alongside the Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3 dataset to produce outputs showing all the river catchments together.\tPublic record\n17932\tNRW_DS112682\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.908Z\tCommon Scoter (Melanitta nigra) Summary Statistics and Distribution Analysis for Carmarthen Bay 1998 - 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132363832\tSummary data is archived under project 213, media 579 within the Marine Data Archive (W drive).   Source raw data is not archived with this project as the data is already in the archive with the project it was collected under\tData Custodians Marine Ornithologist, NRW or Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay an extensive shallow embayment off the south coast of Wales UK encompasses a wide range of rich and diverse coastal and marine habitats. Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important wintering ground for the Common Scoter duck (Melanitta nigra) and is widely regarded as the most important such site in the UK causing the site to be proposed as a Special Protection Area (pSPA) under the EC Birds Directive. Former Countryside Council for Wales (now part of Natural Resources Wales) have supported ground and aerial based surveys for a 10 year period since the Sea Empress oil spill in 1996. The purpose of this data capture was to collate and synthesise records on common scoter populations and distributions for a 10 year period. Data was collated from old CCW reports. A summary of the data  has been produced to cover the period 1998 to 2010.\tPublic record\n18785\tNRW_DS123441\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.703Z\tNRW Environment Team Boundaries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343431\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Environment Teams.lyr\tData custodian; Gary Eccleston, Knowledge \u0026amp; Information Management.\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Environment Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for operational Environment Teams post Organisation Design July 2019.    In MyMap (internal mapping system) this is layer at Operational Layers/Resource Management Terrestrial/Environment Teams. It is the post OD (Organisational Design) boundaries for the Environment Teams (previously NRM teams). It will be used to ensure enquiries incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tPublic record\n17840\tNRW_DS111168\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.513Z\tNant Teyrn Freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Biological data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313638\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The data also gets sent to CEH.  b) Paper forms are filed in NRWs ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tData Custodians: Victoria Bowmaker, or Alex Turner - ECN Project Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) is network of sites spanning the UK where we conduct environmental monitoring using standard methods. There are currently 12 terrestrial sites and 45 freshwater sites covering a wide range of upland and lowland habitats including moorland chalk grassland woods and forests farmland small ponds and streams large rivers and lakes. ECN is the UK member of the International Long-Term Ecological Research Network (ILTER) and of LTER-Europe.  Nant Teyrn is a small upland stream on the out flow from Llyn Teyrn in Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve. This freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) site is co-located with Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon terrestrial ECN site. This dataset is made up of the biological data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) freshwater site at Nant Teyrn Snowdonia. Measurements and observations are made on phytoplankton aquatic macrophytes epithilic diatoms and macro-invertebrates.  The biological variables measured are expected to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the aquatic environment.\tPublic record\n18103\tNRW_DS115554\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.045Z\tFishMap Môn Detailed Habitat Sensitivity Levels\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353534\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Habitat\\\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists). Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRWCrown Copyright and Database Right.\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW) the Welsh Government and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.   This data is related to habitat sensitivity. Habitat Sensitivity is based on the resistance and resilience of a habitat to a particular fishing intensity from a fishing gear type. Sensitivity is recorded as Low Medium or High. The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging Queen Scallop Dredging Mussel Seed Dredging Light Otter Trawling Bottom Set Nets Potting Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering. There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors.    The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey. A 12 nautical mile buffered zone around the Isle of Anglesey / Ynys Mon extending from Nefyn in the West to Colwyn Bay in the East.    Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the Fishmap Môn habitat sensitivity level data were created both a detailed and generalised version. This detailed version of the dataset shows the precise locations of the fishing activities and the data also contains specific information about the fishing activities and so is restricted to the FishMap Môn Team and certain partners - contact the dataset owner for more information.\tPublic record\n17084\tEXT_DS114968\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.549Z\tBangor University Acoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of the North Llyn Modiolus Bed 2008 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393638\tArchived as Project 234, Media 1354,1355,1356.   Raw data is stored at the School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University\tGIS Service Desk  Subtidal Team, MFEG, NRW External Custodian:  Dr. Jim Burnell (Experimental Physicist), Ocean Sciences, Craig Mair Building, Bangor University The data was then processed by Christian Wilson at CEFAS\tThis is a multibeam echosounder (MBES) dataset covering the extent of the original horse mussel (Modiolus modiolus) bed off the north coast of the Llyn Peninsular just west of Porth Dinllaen. The area surveyed is approximately 13 km long and 4 km wide.  Additional boat time between British Geological Survey (BGS) surveys was utilised to provide coverage of the North Llyn Modiolus bed. The dataset has been subsequently used by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for modiolus monitoring work; and by Bangor University for teaching and Master of Science (MSc) project work.  The confidence in this dataset is high. The data has been tidally corrected although a couple of small gaps exist.  It is not clear whether the data meets International Hydrographic Organisation (IHO) order 1 standards.\tPublic record\n17400\tNRW_DS64572\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.625Z\tForestry Grant Scheme Recording\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353732\t\t\tPurpose of data capture: To keep a record of FC grant aided schemes\tPrivate record\n17330\tEXT_DS125070\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.574Z\tCardiff Bay Barrage Fisheries Monitoring Programme (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303730\tZ:\\Archive\\Access Recreation \u0026amp; Education\\Access \u0026amp; Recreation Programmes \u0026amp; Projs\\Cardiff Bay Fisheries Monitoring Project\t\tThe data sets include fish telemetry data fish trapping data rod and line caught fish data broodstock and juvenile stocking data and returning fish data. All data was collected to assess the impact of Cardiff Barrage on the runs of sea trout and salmon on the rivers Taff and Ely. An Annual Report was produced on a yearly basis from 1990 to 2019. A range of supporting information and data was also produced such as weir information.\tPublic record\n17108\tEXT_DS119264\t2024-05-28T12:11:56.345Z\tCranfield University: Land Information System (LandIS) Soil data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323634\tSpatial Data: X:\\Physical\\Soils\\NATMAP vector Wales.lyr   Non spatial data: Y:\\NATMAP SOIL\\HORIZONfundamentalsV2.csv\tGIS Service Desk Keith H. Jones, GIS and Spatial Information Services Team Leader, Evidence Knowledge and Advice, NRW\tCranfield University maintains all the published and unpublished paper soil reports and maps for England and Wales and market and lease a wide body of digital soil information. These datasets are held in the LandIS relational \u0027land information system\u0027 and National Soils Inventory (NSI) both are maintained by Cranfield.   Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds copies of these datasets which contain soil and soil-related information for Wales. These include both spatial and non spatial data as follows:   Spatial Data:  NATMAP vector: Derived from the National Soil Map this displays the 300 mapped soil associations at a scale of 1:250000 and urban soil linework to 1:50000.    NSI - point set data covering England and Wales on a 5km grid providing detailed information including erosion landuse lithological information.  Originally sampling from the 1980 with some re-sampling in the mid 1990s.   NSI Topsoil - includes information on Erosion landuse and lithological information  NSI Profile - Detailed soil description and boundary information.   NSI Topsoil - Detailed measures of over 20 elements in the soil including pH  NSI Textures - soil texture  NSI Features - Provides depth data  Non spatial datasets:  HORIZON Fundamentals: Includes very detailed descriptions of the texture and other properties pertaining to the soils. This dataset provides the most detailed description of each horizon or horizontal layer determined for the soil series. It is based heavily on laboratory based analyses. -   HORIZON Hydraulics: Includes very detailed descriptions of the hydraulic properties pertaining to each layer or horizon for each of the soils. This data is based heavily on laboratory based analyses. -   HORIZON Structure: This product contains soil series-layer properties which describe the respective structural properties of four successive nominal layers within each soil topsoil upper subsoil lower subsoil and substrate.  -   NAPMAP Associations: List of soil series components of soil associations with proportions (%). - NATMAP Legend: Text descriptions of Geology Soil and Site characteristics and of Cropping and Land Use for each map unit of the National Map. -   Soil Series Hydrology: This product contains many properties of soil series most of which are hydrological in nature.  - Soil Series Info: List of the basic soil types (or SERIES) which are components of the soil associations on the National Soil Map. - Soil Series Pesticide:  Includes information useful for pesticide control such as pesticide leaching and runoff classes.\tPublic record\n18861\tNRW_DS124750\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.814Z\tSouthern Damselfly Coenagrion mercuriale Habitat Creation and Restoration, Pembrokeshire from 2020 onwards\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373530\tData to to provided\tData Custodian:  Mike Howe\tData is likley to be a CD with QGIS files showing the location of all recommended and potential habitat restoration areas Needs to be transformed and placed on the X drive  *****************report number to be provided******************************\tPrivate record\n17328\tEXT_DS124812\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.611Z\tCefas: WaveNet data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383132\t\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tWaveNet is Cefas’ strategic wave monitoring network for the United Kingdom and provides a single source of real-time wave data from a network of wave buoys located in areas at risk from flooding.  The network has been in operation since 2002 WaveNet collects and processes data from the Cefas-operated Datawell Directional Waverider buoys tethered at strategic locations around the UK coastline. The WaveNet system also gathers wave data from a variety of third party platforms and programmes (industry and public sector-funded).  Cefas has been contracted to make WaveNet data available  in XML format to UK Coastal Flood Forecasting Service (UKCFF) through the Environment Agency or through the Met Office. Such data are collated through a number of contracts with third parties.\tPrivate record\n20116\tNRW_DS161330\t2025-01-22T10:27:04.920Z\tCommunities at Risk Register 2024 – Present Day (CaRR)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333330\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Communities at Risk Register (CaRR) has been developed to provide an objective means of identifying risk and prioritising flood risk management activities at a Wales-wide community level. It applies a standard methodology across all flood sources to calculate a theoretical ‘danger score’ that allows comparative risks to be quantified and ranked (from High to Low).    The CaRR comprises a spreadsheet that identifies and ranks individual communities for  1. a natural ‘undefended’ scenario and  2. a managed scenario (based on the presence of defences and flood warning).   It is complemented by a GIS layer which defines the individual Community polygons and is attributed with danger scores community rankings and other metric information.\tPrivate record\n18879\tNRW_DS124685\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.493Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Turbine Location\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363835\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Turbine Location.lyr\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Turbine Location.lyr\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Turbine Location. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18207\tNRW_DS116263\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.229Z\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas - Eutrophic\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323633\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTD Sensitive Areas - Eutrophic.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Geraint Weber Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017 (WFD Regulations 2017) requires specification of protected areas for those areas designated as nutrient sensitive areas.   The Urban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas Eutrophic layer is a replacement layer of the Welsh only sites previously designated under the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive. It shows the extent of Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulation  (UWWTR) sensitive areas (eutrophic).     The UWWTR describes eutrophication as \u0027the enrichment of water by nutrients especially compounds of nitrogen and/or phosphorous causing an accelerated growth of algae and higher forms of plant life to produce an undesirable disturbance to the balance of organisms present in the water and to the quality of the water concerned\u0027.   The UWWTR regulates the collection and treatment of waste water from homes and from industry. In the UK the Directive is implemented through the Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulations 1994. Under these Regulations water bodies that are (or may soon become) eutrophic should be designated as sensitive areas by Defra. This applies to still fresh waters rivers estuaries and coastal waters.\tPublic record\n17833\tNRW_DS111183\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.977Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy Gravel in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313833\tData is held within Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 557 [A] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 59 - PLAS Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Data [C] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\GIS [D]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Images  The report and data collected under NRW contract FC 73-02-342 is archived as Project No 199, Media 549 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist\tThe purpose of this data capture was to carry out a repeat survey of sites previously surveyed to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data.    In fully marine conditions on the lower shore sheltered muddy gravel habitats can be particularly species rich due to the complex nature of the substratum supporting a high diversity of infauna and epifauna. Analysis of survey records held on the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) database suggest that fully saline sheltered muddy gravel communities are scarce in their British distribution with available descriptions lacking detail and comprehensive data. This has been attributed to the habitats being difficult to survey and monitor due to the large quantities of coarse material which require sampling and subsequent analysis. Similarly historical data on the distribution of muddy gravel beds appears limited.   This data set includes results of sediment coring and sampling undertaken at one site in Porthmadog during intertidal monitoring of the muddy gravel in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tPublic record\n17739\tNRW_DS110474\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.527Z\tImpact of Grazing and Nitrogen Deposition on the Condition of Racomitrium lanuginosum on the Carneddau Mountains\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343734\ta) Hardcopy of report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). Word version is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist b) Raw data is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist\tPete Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader Jane Stevens, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Formerly, Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tResults of work conducted on the moss heath habitat of the Carneddau massif to establish whether the overall condition of the dominant woolly fragile moss Racomitrium lanuginosum was associated with grazing pressure. The purpose of this data capture was to: - Determine whether the condition and growth of R. lanuginosum (on the basis of tissue chemistry physiology and growth rate) differs in areas of differing grazing intensity.  - Investigate the recovery potential of R. lanuginosum under the current pollution climate if grazers were removed.  - Find out whether membrane potassium leakage from R. lanuginosum shoots increases under increased levels of trampling damage and whether the depth of R. lanuginosum is affected by increased levels of trampling damage.\tPublic record\n18243\tNRW_DS116292\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.261Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Roads and Planned Forest Roads\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323932\tX:\\Transport\\Forest Roads\\NRW Forest Roads.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan   Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tForest Roads is compiled from Forest road network data managed by Forestry Civil Engineering. The data relates to forest road Classification.\tPublic record\n18784\tNRW_DS123443\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.743Z\tFormer EA PF Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343433\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Former EA PF Areas.lyr\tBen Anscombe\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18101\tNRW_DS115555\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.911Z\tFishMap Môn Generalised Habitat Sensitivity Levels\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353535\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists). Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRWCrown Copyright and Database Right.   Metadata Custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW) the Welsh Government and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.    Habitat Sensitivity is based on the resistance and resilience of a habitat to a particular fishing intensity from a fishing gear type. Sensitivity is recorded as Low Medium or High. The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging Queen Scallop Dredging Mussel Seed Dredging Light Otter Trawling Bottom Set Nets Potting Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering. There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors.   Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the FishMap Môn habitat sensitivity level data were created both a detailed and generalised version. The detailed version of the dataset shows the precise locations of the fishing activities and the data also contains specific information about the fishing activities and so is restricted to the FishMap Môn Team and certain partners. However this generalised version is shown at a coarser scale and is available for release.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning\tPublic record\n19073\tNRW_DS125385\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.238Z\tNant Irfon NNR Plant Species Survey 1974 and 1991/2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333835\toffisite storage -box number CCW/SERN/149\tNo know custodian,contact Opendata@naturalreosueceswales.gov.uk to discuss       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNant Irfon NNR Plant Species Quadrat Survey 1974 and 1991/2\tPrivate record\n17115\tEXT_DS119070\t2024-05-28T12:11:56.644Z\tWelsh Government: Agricultural Land Classification (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303730\tX:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Agricultural Land Classification.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Predictive Agricultural Land Classification Map.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Predictive Agricultural Land Classification Map Attributed.lyr   Version 2 layers: X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Soil Series on 50m Grid.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Soils of Wales.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Climatic Limitation Grade.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Depth to Slowly Permeable Layer (SPL).lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Droughtiness Limitation Grade.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Field Capacity Days.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Grade – No masking layers applied.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC grade for each 50m point.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Gradient Limitation Grade.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Overall Predicted ALC Grade.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Soil Depth Limitation Grade.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Soil Wetness Class.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Stoniness Limitation Grade.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Topsoil Texture.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Agri Land Class V2\\ALC Wetness and Workability Limitation Grade.lyr\tKeith Jones, Spatial Evidence Manager, Evidence, Knowledge and Advice\tThese are Welsh Government owned datasets licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under and Open Government Licence (OGL). The Agricultural Land Classification (ALC) system is used to grade the quality of agricultural land. The Predictive ALC map for Wales is based on the principles of the Agricultural Land Classification System of England and Wales the Revised Guidelines and Criteria for Grading the Quality of Agricultural Land. The ALC system classifies land into one of the following five grades:  Grade 1 - excellent quality agricultural land;  Grade 2 - good quality agricultural land;  Grade 3a - good to moderate quality agricultural land;  Grade 3b - moderate quality agricultural land;  Grade 4 - poor quality agricultural land;  Grade 5 - very poor quality agricultural land.  Planning policy defines the top three grades (1-3a) as the \u0027Best and Most Versatile\u0027 agricultural land. Best and Most Versatile agricultural land accounts for approximately 7% of total land in Wales.\tPublic record\n17933\tNRW_DS112903\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.834Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132393033\tThe data and the report are archived as Project No 199, Media no. 548 and 555.  [A] This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters and stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media. [B]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Cardigan Bay\\2008 ASML Cardigan BayData\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata point of contact:  Master Reference Data Team.\tIntertidal surveys provide comprehensive baseline data for monitoring change or for the identification of locations sensitive to certain anthropogenic impacts. The data also provides information to support a notification programme or intertidal Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) thereby providing a series of intertidal designated sites across Wales.  Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation  Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated primarily for Bottlenose dolphins but is also designated for Sandbanks Reefs and Sea Caves. Of these Reefs and Sea Caves are present within intertidal areas of Cardigan Bay SAC. Rockpools are also a notable feature of some intertidal reef areas in the SAC. Phase 1 surveys of intertidal habitats in the SAC were carried out in the SAC in 1996 and 2003 (Brazier et al. 2007) providing detailed mapping of the intertidal biotopes with some information on characterizing species. The data represents the results of a survey of intertidal rockpools at Aberporth one of two sites surveyed during a similar survey undertaken in 2007 within the Cardigan Bay SAC.\tPublic record\n18529\tNRW_DS121633\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.538Z\tMultibeam Acoustic Data Index\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363333\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\Marine\\Marine Acoustic Data\\Multibeam acoustic data index.lyr.   On CCW M drive it is stored as M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\Marine_Acoustic_data\\multibeam_data\\acoustic_data_index.tab.It is also archived as  project 379, media 1390.\tDave Tavner Marine Mapping Scientist, Evidence Analysis  GIS and Spatial data team\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the location of multibeam data Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to. This was done as a collation exercise to produce a spatial (GIS) index of all multibeam data held by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The Index shows the loaction of surveys and points to where the raw data and/or processed data is held.\tPublic record\n18551\tNRW_DS121715\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.673Z\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373135\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where woodland could be planted and deliver carbon sequestration benefits.\tPublic record\n17407\tNRW_DS64560\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.414Z\tNRW Aerial Photographs of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353630\t\t\tCCW has commissioned aerial photographs which are held in various offices.\tPrivate record\n17210\tEXT_DS121887\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.676Z\tNational Grid: Electricity and Gas Infrastructure (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383837\tX:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\National Grid cable fibre lines.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\National Grid high voltage lines.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\National Grid Line towers.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\National Grid overhead line routes.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\National Grid Substations.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\National Grid Underground cable lines.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Gas\\National Grid Gas sites.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Gas\\National Grid High pressure gas mains.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThese datasets detail the extent and location of various electricity and gas infrastructure that are managed and owned by the National Grid. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to these datasets through a licence agreement.\tPublic record\n18262\tNRW_DS116276\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.060Z\tRisk of Flooding from Rivers and Seas (RoFRS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323736\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Sea (RoFRS).lyr\tDatasets custodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW\tThis is a national assessment of flood risk for Wales produced using local expertise. The dataset shows the chance of flooding from rivers and/or the sea based on cells of 50 m. Each cell is allocated one of four flood risk categories taking into account flood defences and their condition. No more detailed resolution is provided.  An online Flood Risk Map Browser is available via the NRW website.\tPublic record\n17329\tEXT_DS124837\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.617Z\tWales and West Utilities: Major Accident Hazard Pipeline (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383337\tX:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Major Accident Hazard Pipeline.lyr\t\tWales and West Utilities gas data showing the location of Major Accident Hazard Pipelines across Wales.\tPublic record\n18877\tNRW_DS124684\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.533Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Turbine Foundation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363834\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Turbine Foundation.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Turbine Foundation. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18850\tNRW_DS124668\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.186Z\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Blade Pad\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363638\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Blade Pad.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Blade Pad. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n18365\tNRW_DS116820\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.848Z\tBullhead (Cottus gobio) population condition assessment for River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Habitats Directive reporting cycle 3 2013 – 2018.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383230\tOriginal data is stored in KiECO / WISKI with the exception of the shad data Analysis (when completed)  for each bullhead SAC river is held in a separate spreadsheet on DMS.  Wye: SAMP-753-344\tData Custodian(s): Emma Keenan, Environmental Monitoring Officer Fisheries  Metadata Point of Contact:  Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Coordinator\tBullhead (Cottus gobio) is a designated feature of the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Assessments of bullhead distribution population demographic and density in this river is of particular interest as an indication of condition assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of bullhead in the River Wye catchments. This collation of bullhead data is made up of by-catch records collected by Natural Resources Wales during salmonids eel and coarse fish surveys. In addition a small number of by-catch records are also made during river macroinvertebrate and shad egg kick sampling surveys. The collation includes information about species distribution size and abundance for bullhead which are a designated (Annex II) features within SAC rivers in Wales. The first 50 fish are measured and the remainder of the catch at a survey point are only counted.\tPublic record\n18546\tNRW_DS121714\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.530Z\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Risk of Pluvial Flooding\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373134\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Risk of Pluvial Flooding.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: The two maps of pluvial flooding show:  [A] the risk of standing water accumulating during heavy rainfall on a bright colour ramp from green to purple and areas at risk of strong overland flow on a darker colour ramp from green to purple with purple areas being at highest risk in both cases. The separation into standing water and overland flow can help to target intervention – the dark purple area connected to a bright purple area shows where the water flooding the light purple area originates and hence where interventions could be located to disrupt flow paths. [B] the demand based on population density and the Welsh index of multiple deprivation on a gradient from light to dark brown with darker areas having a higher demand. A purple semi-transparent overly indicates the areas at highest risk from the left-hand map to make the maps spatially comparable.\tPublic record\n18230\tNRW_DS116239\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.339Z\tFlood Alert Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323339\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Alert Areas.lyr\tCustodian: Andy Wall, Flood \u0026amp; Operational Risk Management\tFlood Alert Areas are geographical areas where it is possible for flooding to occur from rivers or the sea. A single Flood Alert Area may cover the floodplain within the Flood Warning Service Limit of multiple catchments of similar characteristics containing a number of Flood Warning Areas. A Flood Alert Area may also match that of a corresponding Flood Warning Area and warn for the possibility of flooding in that area. In some coastal locations a Flood Alert may be issued for spray or overtopping and be defined by a stretch of coastline. Practical and administrative factors may also influence the exact extent of a Flood Alert Area.    An online viewable version of the dataset is available here: https://naturalresources.wales/flooding/check-your-flood-risk-on-a-map-flood-risk-assessment-wales-map/?lang\u003den\tPublic record\n17938\tNRW_DS112566\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.889Z\tSarn Badrig Reef Sublittoral Survey 1993\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353636\tThe MNCR survey results have been entered into Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000498) Paper copies of the report are available in NRW\u0027s library and the original report is held by Lucy Kay in NRW\u0027s North Region.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW North Region Marine Specialist, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of a survey aiming to trial the three main methods used to describe biotope recognition biotope mapping determining species presence determining species abundance and cost effectiveness.  The three methods trialled were the BIOMAR/Roxanne methodology the MNCR method and the Seasearch method.\tPublic record\n18552\tNRW_DS121716\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.688Z\tWINS Woodland Planting: Constraints for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373136\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Constraints for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows the number of constraints an area has to woodland planting. The constraints map gives an indication for the number of reasons for not planting woodland in an area. The following types of constraints for woodland planting have been considered: biophysical practical preservation of existing urban areas conservation cultural economic and legislative.\tPublic record\n19130\tNRW_DS125572\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.084Z\tPriority Ecological Networks\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353732\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Priority Ecological Networks\tJon Rothwell - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping\tPriority Ecological Networks (PENs) in the terrestrial environment are versions of the all-Wales habitat networks that show areas of connectivity between Protected Sites and as such provide a framework to inform the location of action to build functional resilient ecological networks based on our most important places for biodiversity.  Typical actions would be habitat improvement restoration or creation located within the boundaries of a PEN or situated at its margins to consolidate extend or link different PENs. These PENs supersede the GIS layers that were previously known as ‘Level 2 networks’ and are available for:   Native woodland; Semi-natural grassland; Heathland; Bog; Fen and Sand Dune.   All these priority networks are also represented as a ‘PEN Hotspot’ map which is a simple count of all available networks within a given 1km square for an ‘at a glance’ view across Wales.    In addition the ‘native woodland’ and ‘semi-natural grassland’ PENs have both received updates in the form of additional networks to  accommodate our latest protected sites series and survey information and expand beyond what was originally available. Heathland Bog and Fen have been merged from their originally separate upland and lowland versions into singular datasets and a previously unreleased ‘sand dune’ network is also now included.  A Freshwater PEN is planned.   At sea an ecologically coherent network of marine protected areas (MPAs) has been established which when measures are in place to bring about good condition will form the marine Resilient Ecological Network.   Connectivity within the MPA Network is inherently good however some of the features (species and habitats for which the sites are designated) are known to be in unfavourable condition.    The Marine Priority Ecological Network (MPEN) map shows the full extent of the MPA network highlighting the sites where one or more features are known to be in unfavourable condition. In the highlighted areas action is needed to deliver measures that restore features reduce pressures to enable their recovery or gather evidence needed to support effective management.  NRW continues to gather and assess information about the condition of habitats and species across the MPA network.  Future iterations of the MPEN will incorporate new evidence and understanding as it becomes available.\tPublic record\n18084\tNRW_DS115311\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.810Z\tMorphodynamic Modelled Outputs for the Dyfi Estuary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333131\tData is archived as project 407, 1428 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1401to1500\\Media1428)\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tA 4-month study conducted by Bangor University for the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to develop a high-resolution morphodynamic model of the Dyfi Estuary and surrounding area. The purpose of this data capture was to collect data for modelling used to investigate the response of the estuary to future tidal and atmospheric changes. The model has also been used to investigate the response of the estuarine dynamics to potential management strategies. The work leads on from a previous study which developed an initial version of the Dyfi Model (Robins 2009 hereafter denoted the 2009 Dyfi Model). It was possible to improve the model resolution due to ever-increasing computer capabilities and data storage at Bangor University. There is a need for this Dyfi Estuary study to keep track with technological advances in modelling and to improve the accuracy of the simulations broadening the possible applications of the model to coastal processes and coastal management scenarios.  Further information can be found in CCW Contract science report 1033.\tPublic record\n18965\tNRW_DS124867\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.311Z\tExtended Phase 1 Habitat Survey of the Gwent Levels Sea Wall October 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383637\tAvailable from NRW Library. DMS location: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/manbus/ManagingRegionsAndGroups/nrmu/Work%20Planning/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fmanbus%2FManagingRegionsAndGroups%2Fnrmu%2FWork%20Planning%2FProjects%2FSea%20Wall%20Biodiversity%20Project%2FSea%20Wall%20Botanical%20Survey%20%2D%20Map%20Files%20and%20Photos\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC854CF9F65FC344AFC2099255D45C99\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B21D5C4CC%2D0AD6%2D4D1D%2D8624%2DC4E9BB1EBEA0%7D\tInternal Contact: Catrin Grimstead, Senior Officer, Environment Team (Usk)\tThis GIS dataset maps an Extended Phase 1 Habitat Survey of the Gwent Levels Sea Wall between Cardiff and Chepstow completed in summer 2020. Red Data Book (RDB) species invasive species notable arable weeds and other notable species are also recorded. Habitats have also been assigned provisional NVC communities. Suggested management prescriptions to improve the value of the sea wall for Shrill Carder Bee and general biodiversity are described and an evaluation of ‘landscape potential’ for Shrill Carder Bee is given.  There are five datasets:  SeaWall_Photo_point  SeaWall_Summary  SeaWall_Phase1  SeaWall_Phase1_polyline  SeaWall_LandscapeHabitatPotential\tPrivate record\n18510\tNRW_DS121710\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.507Z\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Noise Pollution\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373130\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Noise Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows demand areas for greenspace based on locations affected by noise pollution with noise pollution in decibel sound level contours. Along a colour gradient from dark blue to dark brown the demand increases based on a combination of three factors: the intensity of noise pollution problem population density and deprivation.\tPublic record\n18586\tNRW_DS122922\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.284Z\tCrayfish All Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393232\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Crayfish All Data.lyr\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species, Terrestrial Species Team\t\tPrivate record\n18591\tNRW_DS122911\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.876Z\tNDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through grassland/heathland creation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393131\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through grassland_heathland creation.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset shows how many ecosystem service benefits a woodland/grassland type habitat could generate if created at any given location. In some areas a single grassland/heathland type habitat might generate multiple ecosystem services making this site a prime opportunity area. However it is important to recognise that in some cases a grassland/ heathland that will only deliver a single ecosystem services can still be beneficial if the demand for that services is high at the site of interest. Services that were considered for this map are: Opportunities to enhance the grassland/heathland network accessible greenspace for recreation benefits and surface roughness for natural flood management.  This dataset gives an overview of how the opportunities for grassland/heathland type habitat creation are distributed nationally which aids in identifying important opportunity spaces. Looking at the national distribution of restoration opportunities also helps to highlight that not all areas of Wales are the same with some areas being much more suited to restoration of one broad habitat than the other.\tPrivate record\n18210\tNRW_DS116261\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.438Z\tShoreline Management Plan Policies\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323631\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Management plan (SMP) 2.lyr\tInternal Custodians  Mark Pugh ,  FCRM Louise Pennington FCRM Nich Pritchard, FCRM\tA Shoreline Management Plan (SMP) is a large-scale assessment of the risks associated with coastal processes and helps reduce these risks to people and the developed historic and natural environments. Coastal processes include tidal patterns wave height wave direction and the movement of beach and seabed materials.   The SMPs set the strategic policy direction for coastal management and identify the most sustainable approaches to managing the risks to the coast in the short term (Epoch 1 0-20 years) medium term (Epoch 2 20-50 years) and long term (Epoch 3 50-100 years).   A set of preferred policies are identified which are assigned to \u0027policy units\u0027 for each SMP epoch. A \u0027policy unit\u0027 (PU) is a length of shoreline where a separate shoreline management policy applies. PU\u0027s are defined by coastal areas that have similar characteristics in terms of coastal processes and assets at risk that can be managed efficiently.   Four policy options are available for SMPs:  - Hold the Line (HTL): an aspiration to build or maintain artificial defences so that the current position of the shoreline remains. This can involve maintaining or changing the standard of protection.   - Advance the Line (ATL): by building new defences on the seaward side of the original defences. This is rarely used and is limited to policy units where there is significant land reclamation is considered. There are no policy units in Wales assigned ATL.  - Managed Realignment (MR): by allowing the shoreline to move backwards or forwards naturally but managing the process to direct it in certain areas.  - No Active Intervention (NAI): where there is no planned investment in coastal defences or operations regardless of whether or not an artificial defence has existed previously.     The coastal management approach for a certain section of coast may change from the status quo where a policy may no longer be practical or acceptable over 100 years. Therefore a combination of policies may be proposed over the length of the SMP.   The dataset identifies which of the second-generation Shoreline Management Plans are applicable to a particular stretch of the Welsh coastline and the policies assigned to policy units related to that particular area. This dataset is a polyline spatial data layer for the Welsh coast only.\tPublic record\n17293\tEXT_DS124591\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.999Z\tMet Office: Soil Moisture Index (MORECS) Weekly and monthly data (3rd party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353931\tData held on DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/wrmr/ww/Morecs%20Data/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tCustodian: Andy Wall/Zhong Zhang\tMet Office Soil Moisture Index (MORECS) Weekly and monthly data\tPublic record\n18509\tNRW_DS121701\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.491Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Floodplain Woodland Planting\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373031\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Floodplain Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.\tPublic record\n17440\tNRW_DS87141\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.212Z\tResults of a study into the use of airborne remote sensing to identify habitats and biotopes in the Mawddach estuary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313431\tData is archived as Project No. 82, Media No.\u0027s 165 and 272.  Data is also held on backed-up server based storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of a study into the use of airborne remote sensing to produce a comprehensive thematic map of biotope and sediment type distribution in the Mawddach estuary. The purpose of this data capture was to be able to report on the condition of the Mawddach estuary a number of provisional attributes have been identified which may require monitoring e.g. the relative proportion of sandy and muddy biotopes and distribution of major communities.  This project investigated the possibility of using remotely sensed data to provide a contextual overview of broad-scale attributes in a monitoring programme.\tPublic record\n19046\tNRW_DS125332\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.438Z\tCurlew GPS Data: Curlew Movements\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333332\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/birds/Taxa/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbirds%2FTaxa%2FBreeding%20Waders%20%2D%20Curlew%2FCurlew%20GPS%20tagging%20contract\tPatrick Lindley - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tTo improve our understanding of how Curlew use structurally different landscapes in the uplands and lowlands and under different land management regimes in order to aid Natural Resources Wales’ work in agri-environmental policy design.  Data is collected and transferred to an Excel spreadsheet.\tPublic record\n18499\tNRW_DS121347\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.340Z\tWind Energy Development Boundary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333437\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy Development Boundary.lyr\t\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of the proposed windfarm development area as defined by the Developer. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tPublic record\n18424\tNRW_DS119273\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.514Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Bridges\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323733\tX:\\Transport\\Bridges\\NRW Forest Bridges.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan    Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNational Forest Estate Bridges are managed by Forestry Civil Engineering in one of NRW\u0027s Forester GIS modules. This data set comprises location and category of construction.\tPrivate record\n18410\tNRW_DS118345\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.344Z\tA Survey for the Sandhill Rustic Luperina nickerlii gueneei on Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138333435\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive  [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Ian Wallace)\tA total of 149 adults of the Sandhill Rustic Luperina nickerlii gueneei were recorded on Newborough Warren between 29th August and 2nd September 2016. This included 121 adults on saltmarsh and dune edge at Penllyn Pysgod and Braich Abermenai and 28 adults on Cefni Dunes \u0026amp; saltmarsh. Numbers indicate robust populations at these two sites. They have survived the 2013-14 winter storms which eroded large areas of the fore dunes on Newborough Warren and may have responded to developing Sand Couch habitat following the event. The total count contrasts sharply with the previous survey in 2010 which recorded just twelve adults. However seaward-facing populations in front of Newborough Forest at Ro Bach and Traeth Llanddwyn and at Gwddw Llanddwyn on Ynys Llanddwyn where the moth was recorded in small numbers in 2010 seem to have been lost. It is suggested that areas of suitable breeding habitat are mapped as they develop to determine their extent and that the Sandhill Rustic population is monitored at regular intervals depending upon the frequency of severe winter storms.\tPublic record\n17507\tNRW_DS99807\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.760Z\tNatural Resources Wales Land Management Plans\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939383037\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement management proposed poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement management proposed line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement management current line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement management current poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement management expired line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement management expired poly.lyr\tInternal Custodian and metadata contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW (Pippa Jones / Hannah Brazier)\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all currently proposed completed and expired Land Management Data. This is the information about what management needs doing within the agreement area e.g. fences ditches areas to be burnt etc. It includes information on site name owner scale captured expiry date and unique codes. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and provide a complete table showing all management information for management agreements in Wales ( except text and symbols ).\tPublic record\n19165\tNRW_DS125699\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.650Z\tRed Squirrel Focal Sites Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363939\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Buffer Zone April 2023.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Buffer Zone December 2010.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Focal Site April 2023.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Focal Site December 2010.lyr\tChris Tucker - Specialist Advisor: Technical Forestry (Resilience)\tThis spatial dataset comprises the boundaries of Red Squirrel Focal sites in Wales. This includes the core areas and the buffers.  There are three red squirrel focal sites in Wales identified by the Wales Squirrel Forum (WSF) as part of the Wales Red Squirrel Conservation Plan 2009.\tPublic record\n18412\tNRW_DS118108\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.345Z\tInvestigating the Behaviour and Ecology of the Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa in Field Conditions in Llyn Tegid (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138313038\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive  [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Ian Hughes)\tThe Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa is an aquatic species restricted to a single known population in Britain located in Llyn Tegid.  Following on from successful captive breeding experiments and their revelations proposals were made to use the knowledge acquired to begin field experiments that might lead to improved understanding of Myxas natural ecology and appropriate management procedures. This report comprises the instigation of that work. The experiments proposed are a tentative method to probe into the life of Myxas and answer the following questions:  Does Myxas migrate up and down the shore as lake levels recede and advance?  Do changes in water level benefit or harm the snail population or are they benign?  What substrates favour Myxas?  What physicochemical differences occur across Llyn Tegid and what effect do they have on Myxas?  Would Myxas benefit from the placement of refuges at particular sites around the lake and if so where and of what material?  Is it possible to manage habitats with simple methods that benefit Myxas and could be employed to return Myxas to its former range?   We do not know exactly what the experiments will show us and they may disprove some existing theories but the author feels sure they will show us more than we already know and will help engage local workers land-owners and others in Myxas conservation and study. The first stage has been completed with the installation of bespoke concrete refuges and a brief second inspection of these has already begun to provide some insight into the life of wild Myxas in the lake and an opportunity to begin mapping areas of the lake bed where Myxas occurs.\tPublic record\n18079\tNRW_DS115314\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.804Z\tInfaunal Sampling of Sediment around the North Llyn Modiolus Bed (Additional Area) 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333134\t[A] Data is archived as project 402, media 1419.   [B] Species data held in Marine Recorder [survey number:MRCCW3000000006C]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tInfaunal and granulometery samples collected from sediment around the North Llyn modiolus beds.  Sampling was completed to inform decisions on the location of a potential fishing exclusions zone to prevent accidental incursions by fishing gear and smothering by resettlement of sediments suspended by fishing gear operating close to the bed. The work was mainly carried out to collect information about the sediment type in the area surrounding the \u0027new\u0027 Modiolus bed.  The data was also collected to inform discussions over the size of buffer area needed around the Modiolus bed for the towed fishing gears (trawls and dredges) exclusion area.\tPublic record\n17570\tNRW_DS102034\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.380Z\tCarmarthen Bay Intertidal Attributes and Monitoring Techniques - Saltmarsh Boundaries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303334\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 549.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Saltmarsh Edge\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW\tThe Burry Inlet is the large estuary of the River Loughor which separates the Gower Peninsula from the mainland. The Burry Inlet is a Special Protection Areas (SPA) and Ramsar site and its Atlantic and Mediterranean Saltmeadows have contributed to its designation as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The saltmarsh habitats here correspondingly determine the border of important mudflat and sandflats within the SAC. The area of saltmarsh within the Burry Inlet is regarded the most extensive site of such vegetation in Wales. In 1998 a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey was carried out by Prosser and Wallace (1999) for the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to provide more detailed information on the distribution of key elements of saltmarsh within the estuary. They mapped an area of 1879 ha of saltmarsh vegetation. As part of the ongoing monitoring program for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS) were commissioned to carry out further monitoring at key sites in order to provide an understanding of the dynamics of the saltmarsh and mudflat/sandflat habitats. The purpose of the data capture was to map the seaward boundary of saltmarsh at Llanrhidian Marshes between Great Pill and Salthouse Point; map the seaward boundary of saltmarsh between Penrhyn Gwyn and Loughor Bridge and record positions of outlying Spartina anglica tufts at both locations.\tPublic record\n19095\tNRW_DS125498\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.042Z\tSurveillance of the montane macrolichen Thamnolia vermicularis in Eryri, 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343938\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FThamnolia%20vermicularis%20in%20Eryri\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tResurveys of all four Welsh sites where the snowbed lichen Thamnolia vermicularis has been recorded found no extant colonies. Climate Change Nitrogen pollution and heavy sheep grazing are implicated in its extinction in Wales.\tPublic record\n18519\tNRW_DS121648\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.335Z\tRiver Habitat Survey in Wales: Analysis for Area Statements 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363438\tThe excel spreadsheet is saved on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt; Area Based Statements \u0026gt; River Habitat Survey Contract for Area Based Statements https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/fe/Area%20Based%20Statements/River%20Habitat%20Survey%20Contract%20for%20Area%20Based%20Statements  The Access database is saved on Sue Hearn\u0027s desktop until such as time as it can be moved to the Azure File Storage Solution.\tInternal Data Custodian(s):  Internal: Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning  Metadata Point of Contact: Internal: Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning\tData collation and indices derived from 704 River Habitat Survey sites in Wales surveyed 2007-8. This dataset comprises of an excel spreadsheet of basic attribute data plus calculated indices for Welsh statement areas including Habitat Modification score Habitat Quality Assessment score and River Habitat Quality score plus an interactive access database of above.\tPublic record\n17155\tEXT_DS119894\t2024-05-28T12:14:11.175Z\tWelsh Government: Unified Peatland (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139383934\tx:\\Physical\\Peat\\Unified Peatland.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team Colin Chapman from WG confirmed this recently and James.Skates@gov.wales is the WG contact\tThis dataset was created by CEH on behalf of Welsh Government therefore this data is wholly owned by Welsh Government and licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for use. Peatlands (defined here as deep (\u003d/\u0026gt;0.4 m) peat soils and supporting both mire habitats and other habitat/land-use types) cover 4.3% of Wales and represent Wales largest terrestrial ecosystem store of carbon as well as important reservoirs of biodiversity. This work in creating this dataset was undertaken as part of the Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) in order to provide improved data on the extent and condition (in terms of broad habitat cover and drainage as opposed to the nature conservation definition) of Welsh Peatlands as a basis for: - prioritising areas for restoration - monitoring long-term change in the status of the Welsh peat resource - estimating current greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions from Welsh peatlands as a result of drainage and land-use change and  - estimating the future climate mitigation potential of peat restoration.\tPublic record\n19630\tEXT_DS161309\t2024-10-04T10:41:54.105Z\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2023 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2023 10m Classified Pixels.lyr X:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2023 25m Rasterised Parcels.lyr\tGIS Team\tLand Cover Map 2023 (LCM2023) is the eleventh UK land cover map produced by UKCEH (others include LCM1990 2000 2007 2015 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022). Land cover is given as 21 UKCEH Land Cover Classes based on Biodiversity Broad Habitats (BAP see Jackson 2000).   LCM2023 was created by classifying Classification Scenes comprising Sentinel-2 Seasonal Composite Images combined with a series of Context Layers designed to reduce spectral confusion. The classification process is automatic using a technique we call Bootstrap Training combined with a Random Forest classifier.    NRW has access to the 10m classified Pixel and the 25m Pixel Rasterised Parcel Datasets\tPublic record\n18608\tNRW_DS122762\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.751Z\tNDF Greenspace Provision in Main Urban Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373632\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Greenspace Provision in Main Urban Areas.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset shows areas where access to greenspace is limited either because very little greenspace is available in the area to because access to greenspace is difficult. The larger the circle the more greenspace is available in the area relative to the size of settlement. Accessibility of greenspace is shown in colour with green meaning that greenspace is easily accessible and red that most people need to walk far to reach it.   This dataset can be sued to identify areas with a significant shortfall in the provision and accessibility of greenspace. While addressing these types of issues is arguably best done at the local planning level this dataset gives a national overview in areas where greenspace provision is an issue this information could be used to steer development away from existing greenspace and/or identify areas where greenspace provision could usefully be improved.   This dataset shows greenspaces and all areas that are within 400m walking distance of at least one greenspace It also shows how high the demand for greenspace is in areas that fall outside the catchments for these greenspaces.  NRW has a number of other datasets that can be used to refine this information further for use at the local planning level.\tPrivate record\n17517\tNRW_DS101866\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.484Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Stereo photographic monitoring of rocky reef communities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383636\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/stereo , Skomer MNR office fire proof cabinet 1 draw 4 (bottom)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Investigating rocky reef communities at Skomer MNR. Very little is known about the community dynamics on sublittoral habitats. The epibenthic communities are one of the most abundant and important features in the Reserve. In order to make decisions about the conservation status of these communities it is vital to have some understanding of how these communities change over time. Therefore the aim of the stereo photographic monitoring is to monitor how species and communities vary over time. Possible measurements include species richness / diversity growth rates longevity and competitive interactions over time.\tPublic record\n17416\tNRW_DS47079\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.577Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog at Eryri candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437303739\tData held on the H Drive at;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\26 Heaths and blanket bogs at Eryri cSAC\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tMonitoring to assess the condition of three European Annex 1 habitats; Dry Heath Wet Heath and Blanket Bog at Eryri. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the condition of features on the Eryri cSAC designated site based on site-specific performance indicators and to record aspects of their management both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales. The areas of Eryri targeted for survey lie in four separate compartments and their approximate locations are around Aber Falls Llyn Cowlyd Tryfan and an area to the south and west of Bethesda.\tPublic record\n18244\tNRW_DS116290\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.349Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Legal Boundary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323930\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\NRW Forest Legal Boundary.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Ian Medcalf Secondary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan\tThis layer shows the external legal boundaries of land within Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Forestry ownership. It does not show the internal deed boundaries.  It has been designed to:   - enable the mapping and Geographic Information Units to interrogate and maintain NRW land holdings records   - provide land ownership data to  staff with GIS   - produce updated polygons to maintain legal boundary information in the \u0027Forester\u0027 GIS system.\tPublic record\n18045\tNRW_DS115243\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.979Z\tMilford Haven Waterway: Benthic and Intertidal Sediment Macrofauna Data Collation 2006\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323433\tData is archived as project 391, media 1407 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1407)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe Milford Haven Waterway is arguably the most intensively studied coastal region of the UK with respect to the soft sediment macrobenthos. These studies have largely been motivated by concern for the environmental effects of the oil industry since the diversity and composition of the macrobenthos have become one of the mainstays of marine biological effects monitoring. The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Evaluate the existing macrobenthic and environmental data.  -  Undertake new statistical analyses of the data and to interpret the findings of these analyses.  -  Make recommendations for a meaningful and cost effective monitoring programme that might best be employed for regular wide-scale evaluation of biological quality in relation to a range of anthropogenic impacts.\tPublic record\n18112\tNRW_DS115556\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.064Z\tFishMap Môn Fishing Activity Combined with Seabed Habitat Sensitivity\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353536\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Combined Seabed Habitat Lines.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Combined Seabed Habitat Regions.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW) the Welsh Government and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors.   Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers. The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.    The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey. Confidence in the data used for habitat types varied depending on the source of the data. The confidence in the Phase 1 habitat data is good however confidence in subtidal data from the HABMAP program was assigned a confidence level of Very Poor Poor Fair and Good.\tPublic record\n18553\tNRW_DS121721\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.731Z\tWINS Water Quality: Opportunities for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373231\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Opportunities for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where management action could be taken to reduce the risk for faecal coliforms entering into coastal and transitional waterbodies as well as lakes and rivers. Management opportunities considered are fencing and/or hedges to prevent direct access to water courses by livestock and woodland planting to form riparian or drainage channel shelter belts. For all opportunity types those falling into upslope areas of sites with a known problem with faecal coliforms are considered priority opportunities.\tPublic record\n18195\tNRW_DS116012\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.602Z\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Dinefwr Woodlands (1993 - 1994)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303132\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000005 ET Dinefwr Woodlands.\tInternal Custodian: Woodland Ecologist, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 7140 observations in woodlands of conservation interest across Dinefwr woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.     The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tPublic record\n18532\tNRW_DS121646\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.567Z\tHydroelectric Power (HEP) - Sensitive Bryophytes, Lichens and Ferns\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363436\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophyte_Lichen_HEP_Sensitive.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tSites considered to be sensitive to Hydroelectric Power (HEP) abstraction by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) because of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) bryophyte features the presence of desiccation-sensitive bryophytes the presence of rare or scarce riverine lichens or the presence of Killarney Fern gametophyte. This layer was designed to aid consultation between Environment Agency Wales (EAW) and CCW.  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPublic record\n18292\tNRW_DS116300\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.536Z\tMarine Conservation Zones (MCZ)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333030\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\MCZ Marine Conservation Zones.lyr\tKirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Marine Conservation Zones (MCZs) former Marine Nature Reserves in Wales.   MCZs are a way of conserving marine habitats and wildlife and other features of special importance along the shore or on the seabed. The Marine and Coastal Access Act 2009 allows for the creation of MCZs. MCZs protect a range of nationally important marine wildlife habitats geology and geomorphology and can be designated anywhere in English and Welsh territorial and UK offshore water.\tPublic record\n17853\tNRW_DS111174\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.917Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Flats in Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313734\t[A]  Data is held within the marine archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 549 [B]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 56 - MS\\ampersand\\\"CB intertidal monitoring 2007 ASML [C]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Data [D]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [E]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\GIS [F]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Images\"\t\tThe purpose of this data capture was to establish the reference condition from which to establish conservation objectives; and to be able to take account of natural changes in the appropriate management of the features of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). In addition to this the data collected to establish sites for long term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods in accordance with the conservation objectives.  Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC is designated for five Annex 1 habitats: Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide Reefs Large shallow inlets and bays and Submerged or partially submerged sea caves. This dataset includes the results of an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the sediment flats at Traeth Lafan during September 2007. The intertidal sediment communities across Traeth Lafan are considered to be of particular nature conservation importance and their good condition (Favourable status) is listed as a Conservation Objective of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Performance Indicators were still being developed for this feature so this survey focuses on the richness distribution and abundance of sediment biotopes.\tPublic record\n17068\tEXT_DS99640\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.481Z\tWelsh Government: Tir Cymen Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363430\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_cymen\\tircymen_area.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tThis dataset is wholly owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use only. Tir Cymen Areas details the extent and location of areas that come under the Tir Cymen agri-environment schemes.\tPublic record\n18802\tNRW_DS124436\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.965Z\tAsh Lichen Alert\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234343336\tX:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Ash lichens alert.lyr X:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Ash lichens alert 1km.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tThese data identify 10x10m 100x100m and 1000x1000m areas where a Threatened Lichen has been recorded on Ash. The dataset is designed to allow land managers to rapidly identify whether they need to consider rare lichens when working with Ash trees especially if they are considering felling a tree because of Ash Dieback.\tPublic record\n18330\tNRW_DS116580\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.411Z\tPotential Accessible Natural Greenspaces for Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353830\tX:\\Social Economic\\Greenspaces\\Potential Greenspaces.lyr    Pete Frost’s Greenspaces Toolkit CD (https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/are/commu/gi/_layouts/15/WopiFrame.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d{71B1EAFE-DA54-4340-980B-6E3AE44FCA6C}\u0026amp;file\u003dGreenspace%20Toolkit%20version%202%202011.DOC\u0026amp;action\u003ddefault\u0026amp;DefaultItemOpen\u003d1)\tInternal Contacts: Pete Frost, Senior Urban Advisor, NRW  Metadata; Pippa Jones, GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of polygon areas showing the potential accessible natural greenspaces for the entire of Wales input by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) with the assistance of Unitary Authorities and Planning areas and their original Urban Greenspace data. This was mapped against Mastermap 2009 mostly but amendments will be ongoing and will be mapped against the latest version of Mastermap that NRW holds at the time of digitisation.  This data will be used by Unitary Authorities and Planning Areas and NRW staff to help make informed decisions on how best to change/create urban and rural areas incorporating greenspaces for the health and wellbeing of the community they are surrounded by. This will also impact on the Welsh Index of Multiple Depravation and the Good for People toolkit.  Warning: The Column titled \u0027Status\u0027 is to be used when running any form of statistics as this is the column that says whether the polygon has been checked by the Local Authority \u0027Draft\u0027 means it has not been checked and should be ignored for any statistical analysis \u0027No Ver LA\u0027 Is to signify that the Local Authorities don\u0027t know anything about that parcel of land and should be used cautiously in any statistical analysis. \u0027Completed\u0027 are the polygons the Local Authorities have checked and are happy that the information is correct to the best of their knowledge.\tPublic record\n18696\tNRW_DS123299\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.393Z\tAsh DieBack\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323939\tx:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Ash DieBack Monitoring Squares.lyr  x:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Ash Dieback Working Target Sites.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones\t\tPrivate record\n17241\tEXT_DS122161\t2024-05-28T12:17:31.921Z\tDŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water (DCWW): Asset Management Plans (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313631\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Quality\\AMP2 DCWW Improvements.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Water Quality\\AMP3 DCWW Improvements.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Water Quality\\AMP3 DCWW sewage works continuous.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Water Quality\\AMP3 DCWW sewage works intermittant.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Water Quality\\AMP4 DCWW Improvements.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Water Quality\\AMP4 Removed Schemes.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Water Quality\\AMP5 DCWW Improvements.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\AMP2 DCWW (1995-2000).lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\AMP3 DCWW (2000-2005).lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\AMP4 DCWW (2005-2010).lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\AMP4 Removed Schemes.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\AMP5 DCWW (2010-2015).lyr\tGIS and Saptial Data Team\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water and is licenced to Natural Resources Wales for internal use only. Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water is a regulared water and sewerage company.\tPublic record\n17743\tNRW_DS110470\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.686Z\tCwm Idwal Surveillance data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343730\t[A]  Hardcopy of report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). Word version is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist [B]  Raw data is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist [C]  Digital images on CCWs upland ecologist hardrive and on CD in TSG Archives\tPete Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, NRW  Jane Stevens, Habitat and Ecosystem Resource Analyst, NRW (Formerly: Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tThis dataset currently contains the first year\u0027s recorded data following the establishment of a long-term vegetation surveillance system at Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR). An earlier programme of intensive surveillance was undertaken at Cwm Idwal during 1999-2004 in response to the long term exclusion of grazing. This current surveillance project involves the establishment of a new less intensive surveillance programme based on just eleven of the original plots which will be repeated at three year intervals. The dataset includes a summary of plot frequency data summary statistics for each species/feature digital photographs general observations on the eleven plots and plot identification sketches. The purpose of this data capture was to inform large-scale restoration projects in other parts of the Welsh uplands and provide information on the effects of such management decisions for the restoration of upland sites.\tPublic record\n18413\tNRW_DS118342\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.321Z\tThe Status of the Caddis Adicella filicornis in Wales (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138333432\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Ian Wallace)\tA survey for the Endangered caddis Adicella filicornis (Trichoptera) in 2016 found pupal cases at all four Welsh sites with modern records and a single adult. Populations at Coed Dol-goch Halton Wood Prisk Wood and Wilderness Wood appear to be small and the habitat used by the caddis - very small permanent streams on quite steep slopes close to their origin from groundwater springs - is also highly localised and very fragile. Only Coed Dol-goch supports a reasonable area of habitat with several stations being occupied. None of the sites has any statutory protection although Prisk Wood is owned by the Gwent Wildlife Trust and Coed Dol-goch is a community woodland. Consideration should be given to notifying one if not all sites as Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for Adicella filicornis. Prisk Wood and Wilderness Wood also support the only Welsh populations of the rare cranefly Ellipteroides alboscutellatus a species associated with tufa-depositing seepages. Further surveys for Adicella filicornis at all four occupied sites are required to determine its status and distribution and to identify threats to its habitat.\tPublic record\n17206\tEXT_DS121879\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.399Z\tCowi: Welsh Digital Aerial Photography 2006 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383739\tX:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Aerial Images 2006.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Air photo index 2006.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tIn 2006 the Aerial Images were produced by Cowi. GetMapping and Bluesky International supplied NRW this data under licence for the following years; 2000 2009 2013-14 2013-17 2015 and 2017 (see metadata number 121661 for more information about these datasets).   This Cowi raster dataset contains aerial imagery for 2006. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to the Welsh extent of this dataset as part of a Public Sector Mapping Agreement (PSMA). The licencing conditions mean that NRW can only use this data for internal use.\tPublic record\n18096\tNRW_DS115594\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.845Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Vertigo moulinsiana in Cors Geirch, Corsydd Llyn/Lleyn Fens cSAC (2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353934\tThe associated data is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\09 Vertigo moulinsiana  in Cors Geirch, Corsydd Llyn\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tDesmoulin\u0027s whorl snail Vertigo moulinsiana is listed in the British Red Data Book as an RDB3 (Rare) species is scheduled on Annex II of the European Habitats and Species Directive and is included on the UK Biodiversity Action Plan short-list of priority species. The purpose of this study was to undertake monitoring of the Vertigo moulinsiana population at Cors Geirch in order to determine the thresholds necessary to carry out a condition assessment which is a requirement of the EU Habitats and Species Directive. The only data held aside from the associated report is photographic images that are also held within the report itself.\tPublic record\n17512\tNRW_DS101869\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.134Z\tSand dune survey of Great Britain\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383639\tSpreadsheet stored on personal drive of NRW\u0027s Coastal Ecologist (Peter Rhind, retired). However, the data is on the X: drive as a GIS layer, so the \u0027spreadsheet\u0027 could be derived from that layer:- X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Sand Dunes.  JNCC report available in CCW Ffynnon: NRW-14-059263\tInternal Contact: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tSand dunes are one of a series of terrestrial habitats that in Britain are almost entirely restricted to the coast. The others are saltmarshes maritime cliffs and grasslands vegetated shingle and strandlines. This dataset is made up of the results from vegetation surveys covering 49 sand dune systems in Wales. The specific objectives of the sand dune survey are:  - To review existing knowledge of British dune vegetation; - To compile an inventory of the range and extent of dune vegetation throughout Britain; - To allow the national and regional importance of each individual site to be assessed; - To provide vegetation maps and descriptions for each site in sufficient detail to support site specific casework and conservation management and to act as a baseline for future monitoring.\tPublic record\n17610\tNRW_DS107789\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.080Z\tDormouse Licence Return Data (1992 - 2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373839\t[A] Species data is held in Recorder 6 Dormouse - CCW - HQ Terr - Licence Returns CCW000380000000E\tInternal Custodian: - TEG, NRW -  Master Reference Data Team, NRW  Metadata Contact:  - Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of a collation of records from Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) licence returns for work carried out on dormouse sites across Wales. The collation was created to add to the dormouse distribution in Wales to collect relevant data in once place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. The dataset does not represent a systematic coverage of survey of Wales. The location of records is generally random depending on the requirements of the licence holders.\tPublic record\n18777\tNRW_DS123445\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.386Z\tNRW Area of interest\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343435\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Area of interest.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n19128\tNRW_DS125556\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.960Z\tThe effects of topography and rainfall on the distribution of Oceanic/Atlantic bryophytes in Wales – revised 2015\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353536\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FAtlantic%20Bryophytes%20vs%20Topography%20and%20Rainfall\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA review of studies around the effects of rainfall and waterfall flows on oceanic bryophyte distribution.\tPrivate record\n18186\tNRW_DS116211\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.434Z\tNon Native Species Across Wales (1989 to 2012) (Extracted from BIOSYS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323131\t[A] This data is now available on the NBN Atlas [B] X:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\BIOSYS Invasive non native species.lyr  [C] X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Crayfish Non Native Species.lyr  [D] Original data is on Wiski -Kieco database\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe aim of this data capture was to monitor the distribution of non-native species across Wales. Any non-native species present in a sample were recorded when they were of the target group for example Signal Crayfish (Pacifastacus lenisculus) in a benthic invertebrate sample or Canadian pondweed (Elodea Canadensis) in a plant survey. Other non-native species are recorded when seen at or around monitoring sites. At the time of surveying between 1970s and 2012 non-native species did not have a specific monitoring programme.\tPublic record\n17555\tNRW_DS101913\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.587Z\tCollation of Images for Use in the Promotion of Marine Biodiversity in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393133\tNRW Pembroke Dock Office, Archived as Project No. 172, Media Nos 502-504. Report is located in the NRW Reports Archive on the DMS.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Team Leader, Pembroke Dock Office, NRW. ( Ex-CCW Aethne Cooke)  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tA significant remit of the Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) process is to raise awareness of biodiversity both with the public and other stakeholders. Raised awareness leads to better understanding appreciation and ultimately the public taking greater responsibility for biodiversity. Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) partnerships in particular are well placed for this role as they operate on a local scale able to engage local communities partners and visitors to an area. Inability to easily access high quality marine images is often a hindrance in this process. The purpose of this project was to collate high quality images of marine BAP habitats and species (both Welsh and local priorities) and also images of general Welsh marine biodiversity.\tPublic record\n17514\tNRW_DS101870\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.098Z\tMaritime Cliff and Slope Inventory for Wales (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383730\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Maritime_Cliff_and_Slope.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis, Julie Creer, Coastal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tMaritime cliff and slopes are a feature of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) listed under annex 1 of the EC Habitats Directive form part of some Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) across Wales and are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitat. This habitat encompasses a variety of vegetation types ranging from saxicolous lichen communities in the spray zone through to sparsely vegetated cliff-faces and ledges to coastal grassland heath and scrub on the deeper soils of the cliff-top. This is a spatial datasets which outlines 1 km square where maritime cliff or slope habitat is found across Wales.\tPublic record\n17112\tEXT_DS116795\t2024-05-28T12:11:56.601Z\tForestry Commission England (FCE): Forest SubCompartments (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373935\tx:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\FC England Woodland Comp_Subcomp.lyr\tCustodian: Heather Crump, National Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of forest sub-compartments in England. Forestry Commission England (FCE) own this data and have licenced Natural Resources Wales to use it under an OGL. The Forestry Commission holds information on trees and forests. The role of the Forest Inventory (the Sub-compartment Database (SCDB) and the stock maps) is to be their authoritative data source giving them information for recording monitoring analysis and reporting.\tPublic record\n17895\tNRW_DS112403\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.160Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal species diversity layer (chao2 analysis)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343033\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956.\tSubtidal Team Leader, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. The Chao2 estimator was applied to intertidal species data collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot) the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses. The purpose of the data capture was to establish species diversity from known data of the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tPublic record\n17587\tNRW_DS108947\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.978Z\tHydrological Investigations to Underpin Mire Restoration Work at Cleddon Bog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), Monmouthshire, SE Wales (2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038393437\tReport held in Ffynnon reference (DCT-09-011958)\tPete Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group  Senior Conservation Officer and Team Leader Monmouthshire CCW and Peatland Ecologist CCW\tCleddon Bog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) is located around 9 km south of Monmouth Monmouthshire SE Wales. The site has a valley-head mire which has developed within two connected valley-head troughs.  The small catchment of which the site forms the upper reaches is a hanging valley system to the River Wye which runs north-south around 1 km to the east. There is a comprehensive body of evidence which shows that the condition of the mire feature at the site is deteriorating. Over the past few decades this has been marked by a decline in the abundance of key mire species at the expense of purple moor-grass and scrub. It is thought that the causes of this deterioration include drying of the mire surface cause by catchment afforestation and a range of peripheral and internal drainage measures coupled with the cessation of historical management practices such as grazing and burning. This dataset was collected to assess the current hydrological regime of the site and provide specific detailed recommendations to support a future programme of hydrological restoration.  The objectives of this project were to: - Develop an initial conceptual understanding of the principle water supply mechanisms to the site with particular reference to valley-flank communities influence by lateral seepage. - Based on the initial conceptual model undertake a limited programme of field investigation to secure a better understanding of water flow pathways into and within the site.  - Identify a range of hydrological restoration options for the site.\tPublic record\n17698\tNRW_DS110058\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.582Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Commercial Shipping Occurring in and around Skomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303538\tSkomer MNR office,West Region drive/shared docs/data/commercial/Tankers\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. This dataset consists of records of all the Tankers using St brides bay including details of record numbers names and movements of vessels. The purpose of this data capture was to document the usage of St Brides Bay as a Tanker anchoring area.  Information may also provide context for any changes in the habitat and species composition in the area.\tPublic record\n18781\tNRW_DS123439\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.530Z\tWater Catchment Strahler Order\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343339\tx:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Water Catchment Strahler Order.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17638\tNRW_DS109800\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.076Z\tNon Native Species Data - Common Slipper Shell (Crepidula fornicata) around Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383030\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Invasive Crepidula Fornicata.lyr  Most Current Data: S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Species Records - New\\Non-native records\\AlienCrepidula\\MostCurrentData.\tData Custodian: Intertidal Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe dataset contains a series of records of the non-native slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata around Wales.  The records have been extracted from the Phase 1 Intertidal survey from target notes and site species lists and collated with ad-hoc records recorded by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff or supplied by third parties along with records extracted from CCW\u0027s Marine Recorder data holdings. The purpose of this collation is to monitor the spread of non-native marine species across Wales.\tPublic record\n17542\tNRW_DS101994\t2024-05-28T12:26:24.885Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Pectenogammarus planicrurus in Cardigan Bay and Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SACs) 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393934\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 554 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\PectenogammarusH:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2004 IECS\\Pectenogammarus\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThere were numerous aims of this data capture. - To establish the presence and extent of Pectenogammarus planicrurus at two sites where it had previously been found.  - To establish whether the amphipod was restricted to a localised habitat at the sites or whether it was present along the entire length of the shoreline identify and map its presence and extent along the surf zone estimate its abundance.  - To take representative core samples for enumeration of P. planicrurus and Particle Size Analysis across the full extent of the biotope.  The results of a Pectenogammarus planicrurus monitoring programme carried out at two sites- Pwllheli Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SACs) and Gilfach yr Halen Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SACs). P. planicrurus had previously been found at these sites during Phase 1 surveys undertaken by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) so the data collected contributed to an investigation into their continuing existence. P. planicrurus is the only amphipod which is a permanent resident of shingle beaches and is found in the intertidal zone of clean shingle beaches often in the lee of obstacles such as rocky outcrops and groynes.\tPublic record\n17227\tEXT_DS122164\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.399Z\tGwent Wildlife Trust: Vole Raft Locations (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313634\tX:\\Biological\\Gwent Wildlife Trust\\GWT Water Vole Raft Locations.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tThis dataset is wholly owned by the Gwent Wildlife Trust and licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use. This dataset details the location of vole rafts collected by the Gwent Wildlife Trust pre 2015.  Water Voles (Arvicola amphibius) are protected in Wales under Schedule 5 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act making them a \u0027species of principal importance to Wales\u0027 and they are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority species. As part of their feed habits Voles create feeding rafts. These are piles of cut vegetation that will be think enough for a vole to site on in the water. These are most typically found next to areas where suitable vegetation emerges directly from the water.\tPrivate record\n18675\tNRW_DS123255\t2024-05-28T12:53:51.412Z\tWFD Lakes Waterbodies Cycle 2 Interim\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323535\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Lakes Waterbodies Cycle 2 Interim 2018.lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, Environment Reporting Team\t\tPrivate record\n17635\tNRW_DS109566\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.064Z\tNon Native Species data - Distribution of Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) in Holyhead Marina\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039353636\tGIS Dataset: X:\\Biological\\Non_Native_Species\\Invasive Didemnum Vexillum.lyr  All data is archived under media no 580, 593 \u0026amp; 592 (Project 214). Working copies of the data is held on MFSG shared Drive S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Didemnum vexillum Photos also archived on MMT DVD 17 in the fireproof safe and video archived on the I drive I:\\MMT video Archive\\Handheld Diver video\\2008\\Didemnum vexillum.\tData Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Custodian: Data Services\tDidemnum vexillum is an invasive colonial ascidian that has become established world-wide having been identified in Japan North America northern Europe and New Zealand following a global expansion since the 1970’s. It forms sheet like colonies on natural and artificial hard substrata as well as benthic organisms including other ascidians and algae. The serious ecological and economic damage experienced in New Zealand and other temperate regions has led to a large investment in on-going research into the biological tolerances and spread of D. vexillum as well as rapid response monitoring and management following introductions.  In summer 2008 an MSc student from Bangor University carrying out a survey of non native species in marinas in North Wales found a sea squirt in Holyhead Harbour that looked like the invasive non native species D. vexillum. A specimen was sent to a UK expert who confirmed in November 2008 that it was very likely to be D. vexillum. Specimens were confirmed by Gretchen Lambert (pers. com. University of Washington USA) as being D. vexillum. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the distribution and spread of the non-native species and possible threat to native species and biodiversity of the area.\tPublic record\n20279\tNRW_DS161339\t2025-02-17T17:15:05.609Z\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) and Plant Community survey data of the River Wye SAC 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333339\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/CARWC4/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Ffm%2FCARWC4%2FWye%20SAC%20RHS%20and%20macrophyte%20contract%2FRHS%20Macrophyte%20Report%20and%20Data\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200042A0D276A0ABB34DACF1ADA432CBE918  Z:\\Archive\\Sampling Monitoring \u0026amp; Analysis Reporting\\Freshwater Monitoring\\River Habitat Survey (RHS) and Plant Community surveys of the River Wye SAC (Wales) carried out in 2024  No additional open data assessment were undertaken as will be covered by the published kieco datasets\tSuzanne Hearn, Freshwater Ecosystems and Fisheries Management Team Leader\tIn 2024 a River Habitat Survey (RHS) Simplified Phase 1 survey and LEAFPAC aquatic macrophyte survey was carried out on the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC). This was to contribute to the condition assessment of features in the River Wye SAC as well as provide information to support the management and restoration of protected sites and the delivery of River Basin Management Plans.   Twenty-two monitoring sites out of thirty-five possible sites were surveyed in the River Wye catchment between 5th August and 28th September 2024.   At each site the following surveys were conducted:  • River Habitat Surveys (RHS) to assess the quality of physical habitat supported by  • Simplified Phase 1 survey and • Aquatic plant surveys using the rivers LEAFPACS method along with  • Field observations of habitat quality to assist with interpretation of the data.  The principal outputs of this project are digital copies of field records including photographs access and parking along with a description of each site.  These surveys were conducted on the Welsh section of the River Wye SAC.\tPublic record\n18451\tNRW_DS121121\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.095Z\tThe status and distribution of the ground beetle Harpalus melancholicus at Stackpole Warren in 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231313231\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tData Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist  Metadata contact: Data Services\tStackpole Warren supports the only known British population of the Endangered ground beetle Harpalus melancholicus the Stackpole Seed-eater. This data collection was commissioned to attempt to determine its current status and distribution on Stackpole Warren.    This survey has done much to characterise the habitat occupied by H. melancholicus with adults recorded only from two small patches of habitat where the toe of a vegetated sand-dune encroaches over outcropping limestone. The survey has also confirmed that H. melancholicus is a seed-eater with Lesser Hawkbit Leontodon saxatilis and probably thyme Thymus sp. as food items. It is hypothesised that H. melancholicus is ecologically highly specialised with a patchy occurrence at Stackpole Warren but that additional occupied habitat patches remain to be discovered. Alternative hypotheses are also presented.\tPublic record\n18057\tNRW_DS115551\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.627Z\tFishMap Môn Habitat Sensitivity Confidence\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353531\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Sensitivity ,   Legacy CCW Archive:  M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team,\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW) the Welsh Government and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.   Habitat Sensitivity Confidence represents the confidence NRW has had in the expert judgement of the Habitat Sensitivity Assessment from a fishing gear type.  The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging Queen Scallop Dredging Mussel Seed Dredging Light Otter Trawling Bottom Set Nets Potting Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning Portal.\tPublic record\n17568\tNRW_DS102010\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.300Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera marina) Beds in Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in 2004/2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303130\tThe report and data is archived as Project No199 Media No 547 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Z_marina2004H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Z_marina2005\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas such as lagoons and channels which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina) is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat.  In particular seagrass meadows are used as a refuge area by several juvenile commercially important fish species and similarly provide an important food source for wildfowl such as Brent geese. As a result of their dense root networks together with a certain degree of wave attenuation and the trapping of sediment particles which further encourages sedimentation seagrass beds help to stabilise the underlying substratum thereby playing an important role in terms of coastal defence and the reduction of coastal erosion (Davison and Hughes 1998). As such in addition to their biological function and conservation importance they are also of considerable economic importance. Human activity in the coastal zone (pollution dredging boat mooring etc) can affect seagrass distribution or even destroy the beds completely.  The purpose of this data capture was to map the current known intertidal extent of the Zostera biotope develop a survey methodology to determine grass density record damage to the beds caused by physical impacts develop an index of disease and record species (epibiota) on the Zostera and adjacent sediment.\tPublic record\n17939\tNRW_DS112571\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.966Z\tLamprey Habitat Assessement in the River Usk 1999\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353731\t[A]  Associated is data is held within the Marine data Archive as project 280, media 1076.  [B]  Biological Data is held in Recorder 6 Lamprey Habitat Assessment in the River Usk 1998 - CCW - Report No. 365 CCW000280000000C\t\tThe River Usk has been notified as a site of Special Site of Scientific Interest (SSSI) and a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The three lamprey species which occur there are all listed in Annex II of the EU Species and Habitat Directive. The aim of this study was to investigate the status of lampreys in the Usk and to consider matters relevant to their conservation. Specific objectives of the study have been.  -  To define the characteristics of lamprey spawning and nursery habitat -  To obtain and summarise information on the distribution of lampreys and lamprey spawning and nursery habitat in the Usk -  To investigate the possibility of using information from RHS to predict the occurrence or lampreys in the Usk.  -  To make recommendations to assist with the monitoring and conservation of lampreys and their habitat. -  To monitoring the status of lampreys required to comply with the reporting requirements of  the Habitats Directive.\tPublic record\n18409\tNRW_DS118712\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.306Z\tSpatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Anglesey, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373132\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr E.A.Howe (Contractor: Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust)\tThis report draws on a series of previous research reports commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body on determining the conservation status of great crested newts Triturus cristatus in north Wales. The Welsh Government is obligated to maintain or restore the favourable conservation status of great crested newts within Wales and this report aims to provide practical guidance on how this can be achieved within the County of Anglesey. It is one of a series of three such reports the other two covering the counties of Flintshire and Wrexham. Due to its legal status the great crested newt is the focus of considerable conservation effort from a variety of bodies. This document aims to provide an overarching strategy to guide developers conservation NGO\u0027s and local and national government in their efforts in order to achieve the greatest conservation benefit for great crested newts and ensure a unified approach to achieving Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) across the county. To this end the report is accompanied by a number of GIS layers which will be of particular practical use to those engaged in great crested newt conservation across Anglesey.  The report presents a scoring method by which the Current Conservation Status (CCS) of great crested newts can be determined at multiple geographical scales from a single pond to the entire county. It then uses predictive modelling based on geographical distributional climatic and historical data to produce habitat suitability maps and to derive survey targets and pond creation targets to enable FCS to be achieved within the county. These techniques can be applied in various land use and planning scenarios and the report provides practical guidance on identifying areas of particularly high or low value for great crested newts assessing the impacts of proposed developments designing effective mitigation and the implications of agri-environment schemes for great crested newts.    During the modelling process a total of 2146 extant ponds were identified within the County of Anglesey.  With a view to redressing historic loss (37% of ponds having been lost since the 1843 mapping data) FCS was considered in the light of the level of occupancy that would have occurred at the time of the original mapping relative to current levels of occupancy and Habitat Suitability of ponds to the species (Habitat Suitability Index (HSI) scores). Historically we estimate there would have been 2983 ponds in Anglesey and a corresponding 464 ponds occupied by great crested newts and 716 ponds with an HSI score \u0026gt;0.7.  We propose that the 464 occupied ponds should be used to provide a target level for great crested newts (Favourable Reference Value for Range and Habitat sufficiency).  This is further qualified by requiring these ponds have an HSI \u0026gt;0.7 to ensure habitat quality has at least a medium population are found at a density of at least 4 per km2 (to ensure population viability) and has a minor (or lower) level of threat (to address the species\u0027 future prospects). The Current Status of the species in Anglesey is estimated as having 334 ponds occupied by great crested newts. Using the proposed scoring system FCS in Anglesey requires 464 occupied ponds which form part of medium populations in high pond density areas and that have an HSI of \u0026gt;0.7: the minimum score to achieve these criteria will be 16 for each pond.  To achieve FCS the score would need to exceed is 464 x 16 \u003d 8352. With an estimated 334 ponds being occupied and if all of the ponds are in favourable condition (i.e. would score 16) then the Current Conservation Status score \u003d 5344.  The approach to identifying CCS and FCS laid out within this document is one of a number which are in the process of being developed across the UK and Europe. Whilst there is currently no single standard approach which can be applied uniformly across a range of species and habitats one may emerge in the ...\tPublic record\n18565\tNRW_DS122293\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.096Z\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitats Cycle 3 (2018)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323933\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2018\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.  Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset collated as part of the Habitats Directive reporting cycle 3 for Article 17 terrestrial habitats. Article 17 required a report to be sent to the European Commission every 6 years following an agreed format. JNCC collate theses on behalf of the UK Country Agencies and this data was sent to JNCC for the collation.   Article 17 reports are an assessment of conservation status of the habitat and species targeted by the directive. The assessment is made based on information on status and trends of species populations or habitats and on information on main pressures and threats. An important component of the Article 17 report is a map of habitat or species distribution mapped in 10x10 km grid.\tPublic record\n17424\tNRW_DS98745\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.322Z\tHeritage Coast\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373435\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\Heritage Coasts.lyr\tCarole Thornley, Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape Planning, Landscape and Energy\tThis spatial dataset contains digitised boundaries of heritage coasts in Wales. Heritage Coasts occupy about a third of the Welsh coastline that is 500 km (300 miles). These sites were set up to protect our coastlines from insensitive developments. Most are defined simply by the coastline between two named points however some have clearly defined inland boundaries. Their status carries no legal protection but planning authorities must take the designation into account when making decisions on development. Management of Heritage Coasts is the remit of the local authority and is generally carried out by Heritage Coast Officers with some practical tasks done by volunteers. Most of the Heritage coastal strips were defined in 1973 1974 and one in 1984. The descriptions and paper maps associated with this dataset are the legal documents.\tPublic record\n17936\tNRW_DS112410\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.870Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal Habitat Diversity Layer (Neighbourhood Analysis)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343130\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tThe metadata point of contact is: Data Services Team Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the range is measured using biotope richness biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 10 km is utilised to present these metrics. The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out taking into account the source of the data its age spatial taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tPublic record\n18882\tNRW_DS124737\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.533Z\tSeascape and visual sensitivity to Offshore wind farms in Wales: Low and Medium effect buffers\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373337\tTo be stored on the X drive.  Has been emailed to Keith Jones 03/0382020 My understanding is that the data is only partially loaded onto the X drive now (as per email from John Briggs in Nov 2020), Low and Medium buffer layers have been uploaded but not Seascape visual sensitivity layer. All layers are otherwise stored in https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/advice/mar/ind/Impact%20Assessment/OWF%20-%20Visual%20Impacts%20Assessment/Seascapes%20contract%202018/GIS%20files?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1  X:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascape and visual sensitivity to Offshore Windfarms\\Buffers for Low magnitude of effect.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascape and visual sensitivity to Offshore Windfarms\\Buffers for Medium magnitude of effect.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Lucie Skates (Lead Specialist Advisor Marine Planning Implementation - Sustainable Places Land \u0026amp; Sea) and John Briggs (Specialist Advisor, Landscape - Planning, Landscape, Energy,De-carbonisation)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Lucie Skates\tThese GIS files were produced as part of a project looking at seascape and visual sensitivity to future offshore windfarm development off the coast of Wales. The aim of the project was to improve the evidence base and update our advice to reflect the likely scale of new offshore windfarm developments. The project had three stages which looked at the following topics:  Stage 1 - The relationship between distance of offshore turbine away from a sensitive receptor and magnitude of visual effects Stage 2 - Offshore wind farm siting and design guidelines in relation to seascapes Stage 3 – Visual sensitivity of the marine settings of Wales’s Designated Landscapes to offshore wind farms  As part of stage 1 GIS maps of the Welsh marine area were produced which showed the proposed buffers for offshore windfarms with low and medium magnitude visual effects. As part of stage 3 the seascape was divided into 15 zones and a summary of the sensitivity of each zone to offshore windfarm development provided. A GIS map shows the location and boundaries of these zones.\tPrivate record\n17499\tNRW_DS100182\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.564Z\tLocal Access Forums\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313832\t\t\tThis is a spatial Dataset\tPrivate record\n17428\tNRW_DS98740\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.469Z\tBiogenetic Reserves\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373430\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Biogenetic Reserves.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the boundaries of Biogenetic Reserves in Wales.  Biogenetic Reserves aim to conserve European flora fauna and natural areas especially heathlands and dry grasslands that although common in one country may be scarce in another. In this way a store of genetic material - the genes of plants and animals - is kept for the future hence the term Biogenetic.   To qualify for this designation sites in the UK must first be Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) or otherwise designated. The Bern Convention - signed by the UK government in 1982 led to the establishment of this European network of Biogenetic Reserves.\tPublic record\n17893\tNRW_DS112402\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.951Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal Species Diversity Confidence Layer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343032\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tContact Data Services for metadata information. Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Confidence ratings based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final species diversity analysis was calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tPublic record\n17765\tNRW_DS110241\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.653Z\tMean Trophic Rank Data for rivers in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323431\tThe most up-to-date version of the data can be extracted from the BIOSYS database.\tInternal Custodian:  For this extract contact Rhian Thomas, Team Leader WFD, Water Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains Mean Trophic Rank data for a selection of rivers in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the trophic status and eutrophication impact on rivers and streams using biotic indices. This is an old methodology and has now been superseded by LEAFPACKS. This dataset used to be supplied to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) by former Environment Agency Wales (EAW) both of whom now for part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW). This data is now currently held in the BIOSYS database where it can be extracted for internal use.\tPublic record\n18432\tNRW_DS119168\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.191Z\tPhytophthora Working Target Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313638\tX:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Phytophthora Working Target Sites.lyr   Also available on the Y Drive: Y:\\Tree Health\\P ramorum\\FRWTS\\   where the tree health officers access the most up to date data. They also archive the older versions of this data in this folder as well.\tInternal Custodian: Callum Stone       Contains personal information in the comments fields which is why we cannot just give it out.\tPhytophthora ramorum (P. ramorum) is a fungus-like pathogen called a water mould. This is working data for the management of Phytophthora ramorum surveillance.  This is a spatial dataset which is generally updated weekly from information held by Forest Research on Natural Resources Wales (NRWs) behalf as part of their Service Level Agreement. The data is very rough and is to be used internally primarily by the Tree Health Team. This is not to be released externally and the use of internally without understanding of the terminology may result in misinformation.\tPublic record\n17706\tNRW_DS110240\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.199Z\tJuvenile salmonid survey data for Special Area of Conservation (SAC) rivers.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323430\tLegacy CCW M:drive: M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Freshwater\\Freshwater_Fish Rhian Thomas, CCW\u0027s Rivers Ecologist holds copies of the spreadsheets.\tInternal custodian: Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, CCW  Metadata contact: Data services Team, NRW\tThe Environment Agency monitors juvenile salmon and trout populations nationally by fishing a limited number of sites on an annual basis to obtain meaningful temporal data for trends in fish populations. A more comprehensive spatial monitoring survey programme is carried out every 5 years to investigate differences between groups of sites within particular river catchments. Other species caught in the surveys are also recorded e.g. bullhead lampreys eels etc. This dataset contains juvenile salmon data for the 8 rivers designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) in Wales.  The purpose of this dataset is to obtain temporal data for trends in fish populations and to investigate differences between groups of sites within river catchments.\tPublic record\n18786\tNRW_DS123436\t2024-05-28T12:55:51.734Z\tForest Compartments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343336\tx:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Compartments.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17205\tEXT_DS121878\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.484Z\tForestry Commission Great Britain (FCGB): National Forest Inventory (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383738\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory 2012.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\FCE National Forest Inventory 2014.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory England 2015.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory 2016.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory 2017.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory 2018.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory 2018 (Service).lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory 2020.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory 2021.lyr X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest Inventory 2022.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is the Forestry Commission National Forest Inventory (NFI) Map for Great Britain and is wholly owned by the Forestry Commission Great Britain (FCGB).   The NFI programme monitors woodland and trees across Great Britain. The NFI provides an extensive and unique record of key information about our forests and woodlands.   This dataset includes Interpreted Forest Types (IFTs) for all woodland over 0.5ha and Interpreted Open Area (IOA) information for areas over 0.5ha that are completely surrounded by woodland.\tPublic record\n18855\tNRW_DS124659\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.307Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Main Access\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363539\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Main Access.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows the Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm Main Access. The Main Access shows the agreed access route used by Vattenfall and their contractors/visitors to get to the Vattenfall control centre (the HWB). The control centre is in the vicinity of the substation.  This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17758\tNRW_DS110245\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.507Z\tFish Data for Transitional Waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323435\tA static archive copy is held on  Z:\\Archive \\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\WFD  Live data is held in the fisheries component of Wiski-Kieco.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNB FULL DATA FOR WALES IS NOW HELD ON WISKI-KIECO  Abundance data of fish caught in Welsh estuaries during Environment Agency Wales Water Framework Directive monitoring for the Transitional Fish biological quality element in 2007 onwards. For reporting the ecological quality status of Welsh estuaries under the EU Water Framework Directive.\tPrivate record\n18449\tNRW_DS120730\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.086Z\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) F Felling Plan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230373330\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA F Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development.\tPublic record\n17603\tNRW_DS107949\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.082Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Dee (2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037393439\t[A]  Data is stored as in the data Archive under Project Number 174, 294  Media Number 508, 1197.  [B]  Data is stored in Recorder Lamprey Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2004 - CCW - RoC Report No. 18 CCW0002800000009.   [C]  GIS and Spreadsheet data available.\t\tRiver Brook and Sea Lamprey are a designated (Annex II) feature of the River Dee SAC.  Assessments of species composition distribution abundance and size class composition are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment.  The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Dee Catchment.  Monitoring of the status of Lampreys is required to comply with the reporting requirements of the Habitats Directive. The dataset is made up of a collation of historical data recorded during the Environment Agency Wales juvenile salmon surveys from 1985 - 2004 and surveys conducted as part of the Countryside Council for Wales project 2002 - 2004.  Within the historical data information has not been broken down to a species or genus level and is only recorded at the family level. Historical records collected 1985 - 2002 were sampled by electro-fishing according to protocols laid out by the \\Environment Agency Electric Fishing - Theory and Practice Manual\\\" (Environment Agency 1998). Sampling was either Quantitative or Semi-quantitative or Timed surveys; precise dates are not given and records are assigned a year only.\"\tPublic record\n18866\tNRW_DS124734\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.267Z\tSurveys for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch SSSI, Geyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Geirch SSSI \u0026amp; Cors Erddreiniog SSSI, 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373334\tAdded to Recorder 6 on the Welsh Invertebrate Database\tMike Howe\tGeyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri has been recorded from three sites in Wales. However surveys of Cors Erddreiniog SSSI in 2016 and of Cors Geirch SSSI in 2016 and 2017 failed to locate the snail. It was feared that V. geyeri had been lost from both sites but as small populations of this tiny snail can easily be overlooked it was felt premature to declare the snail as extinct and additional surveys were therefore undertaken at both sites in October 2019. The survey focussed upon two areas of potentially suitable habitat that had been recently surveyed; (1) an area of horse-grazed ‘Carex-lawn’ within the National Nature Reserve where V. geyeri had been reported in 1998 and (2) a series of base-rich flushes lying towards the western end of the National Nature Reserve last surveyed in 2016.\tPublic record\n18105\tNRW_DS115577\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.031Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: White Clawed Crayfish in River Wye SAC (2003)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353737\tHeld at H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports and Datasets\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe inclusion of the white-clawed crayfish on Annex II of the EU Habitats and Species Directive requires Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to identify Special Areas of Conservation for the species and to ensure that Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) applies. The purpose of this data capture was to undertake monitoring of the white-clawed crayfish and its habitat on the River Wye candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) in order to report on condition as part of the assessment of Favourable Conservation Status for Natura 2000 features.\tPublic record\n18068\tNRW_DS115378\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.672Z\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the Afon Cleddau (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333738\t[A]  Data is archived as project 415, media 1433. [B]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Survey Number CCW000800000000O.\t\tRiver lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis) Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri) and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) are listed in the Annex II of EU Habitats Directive (EU Directive on the Conservation of Natural Habitats of Wild Fauna and Flora 92/43/EEC) and 6 rivers in Wales are designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) for this feature.  The River Cleddau is one of the westernmost rivers in Britain and can be broadly divided into Eastern and Western arms. Assessments of species composition distribution abundance and size class composition are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The sampling strategy for the monitoring programme for sea river and brook lamprey followed the protocols developed for the LIFE in UK Rivers project was aimed at undertaking a condition assessment for the population of brook river and sea lamprey designated features for the second Habitats Directive monitoring reporting cycle (2007-2012). 12 sites in the Eastern Cleddau and 13 sites in the Western Cleddau Pembrokeshire.\tPublic record\n17970\tNRW_DS114563\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.671Z\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Plants)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353633\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tThis is a collated list of terrestrial plant species names in English Welsh and scientific Latin.  Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff.  Methods of data capture: Most of the translations have been created and/or verified by Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd for CCW the records have been extensively cleaned by the Natural History Museum  Confidence in data: All translations have been carefully checked and approved however with the English and Welsh names as in any language there will be multiple derivations and regional variations. Also the scientific terminology changes periodically as work on taxonomy progresses.\tPrivate record\n17529\tNRW_DS101844\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.248Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Territorial Fish Species Monitoring\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383434\tSkomer MNR office, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological/FISH.     Dataset is updated every 5 years  2005 and 2009 data also held in Marine Recorder (surveys MRCCW16800000002, MRMCS00500000036)  Data for 2013 held by Assistant Marine Conservation Officer, Pembrokeshire Team.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Monitoring of territorial fish is part of an on-going survey was carried out in the Skomer Marine Conservation Zone using volunteer divers to assess the abundance of territorial fish species and the habitat characteristics of these populated areas.  The survey methods were developed through trials in 1997 and the first survey was completed in 2001 this was affected by heavy plankton bloom and therefore repeat survey was completed in 2002.  In 2005 survey reviewed the methods in order to allow improved statistical analysis but allowed comparison of the 2002 data.  The methods were also developed to be suitable for volunteer dive teams.  Seabed and Habitat data were collected for each site using Seasearch methods to support the fish data. The fish data entered is an average for two habitats (10m and 15m) and represents the number of fish per 90 m2.  In 2009 Habitat Descriptions were created using Phase 1 SeaSearch habitat description methods. These surveys were done by NRW and volunteers as in previous year. 2013 data is currently held by the Skomer Island Marine Team (Assistant Marine Conservation Officer Pembrokeshire Team) and is to be entered into Marine Recorder.\tPublic record\n17777\tNRW_DS110515\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.057Z\tLicencing Data for Welsh Assembly Government (WAG) and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Licensing Authorities.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353135\ta) Archives and Dylan Lloyds many filing cabinets (\u0026gt;10) in OSG b) Tracking of activity spreadsheet is currently stored on CCW\u0027s Species Protection forum. Other electronic data is currently stored on Dylan Lloyds user drive, in copies of Recorder or in ISIS.\tDylan Lloyd, Senior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW\tThe relevant licensing authority depends on the legal purpose of each licence. For licences for which former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is the licensing authority data includes electronic or hardcopy licence information e.g. historic archive of licence applications copies of licences licence return forms and correspondence and electronic data such as copies of licences.  Where WAG is the licensing authority consultation with CCW occurs and a tracking of activity is kept on an electronic spreadsheet.\tPublic record\n18469\tNRW_DS119974\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.200Z\tForest Priority Catchments 2016\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139393734\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW Forestry Priority Catchments 2016.lyr\tCustodian: Dave Johnston, Team Leader Environmental Reporting Metadata Point of Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services Team\tThe dataset comprises a number of river catchments based on Water Framework Directive water body boundaries which have been identified by NRW as areas within which forestry environmental improvement works will be prioritised to reduce the impact of forestry on the water environment.\tPublic record\n17504\tNRW_DS100031\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.712Z\tLand Agency - Proposed Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Holdings\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030303331\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW land holdings proposed.lyr\tInternal Custodian and Metadata contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW ( Hannah Brazier or Pippa Jones )\tThis is a GIS dataset containing all land proposed to be owned (purchased) by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  The dataset includes information on site name scale captured and unique codes. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and provide a complete table showing all land proposed to be owned by NRW in Wales.\tPublic record\n18395\tNRW_DS119005\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.514Z\tMarine Licensing: Band 1 Sensitive Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303035\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licensing Band 1 access sensitivity habitats.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licensing Band 1 construction.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licensing Band 1 SPA sensitivity.lyr  for the coprporate versions  Working Version - updated regularly by the Marine Team Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Laura_Grant\\Low_impact_activities\\Final shapefile copies for MLT use\\\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.       Data will be updated 6 monthly.  The subtidal team will retain an archive, dated version as previous version as we may need to go back to any layers which were issued to marine licence applicants in the past in case there is a dispute.               Update from Laura Grant April 2025: The published dataset hasn\u0027t been updated since 2018. The schedule for 6 month updates was probably overoptimistic when this was said.  However they do intend to update it sometime this year (2025) and they are also hoping eventually to set up something in FME to improve the update process as it is taking a long time to do this manually.\tNRW agreed identified and documented which habitats and SAC features are sensitive to a range of Marine Licencing Band 1 activities.   This dataset consists of two layers detailing information about sensitive habitats.  These datasets constitute polygons derived from NRW held BAP (Section 7) / OSPAR layers Regulation 35 SAC habitat layers and Phase 1 intertidal survey.  All the habitats included in these layers were deemed to be sensitive to Marine Licencing Band 1 activities.     The data includes records of protected or sensitive habitats collected as part of baseline survey monitoring programmes and ad-hoc records.  A third layer is derived from the Special Protection Area (SPA) designated sites dataset and identifies SPA sensitive to Marine Licencing Band 1 Activities.    The publicly available dataset has some environmentally sensitive information redacted.  An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under a NRW licence.\tPublic record\n17690\tNRW_DS110042\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.445Z\tModelled biomass, production and species richness of benthic invertebrate communities in Welsh coastal waters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303432\tArchived at K:\\Archive\\Media 401to493\\Media493\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW  The PhD was being undertaken by Liz Richardson - former Bangor University\tSoft-sediment benthic invertebrate communities in the coastal zone of Wales were modelled using a size-based model. Biomass production and species richness were modelled as a function of local environmental conditions and bottom trawling disturbance. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) contributed to a PhD at the School of Ocean Sciences Bangor University entitled. The socio-economic and ecological implications of inshore fisheries management (project FC 73-02-740). The final part of this PhD includes an evaluation of the economic impacts of different types of fisheries management including No Take Zones (NTZs) and areas where no fishing with towed gears is permitted.  In order to base this economic analysis on realistic scenarios data was needed concerning the locations which would most benefit from the protection described.  The state of the benthic community was modelled using bottom trawl data up to 2003 and therefore represents the state in 2003. Predictions for a situation without trawling are not time specific. Values represent a yearly average.  The model that was used was validated for the North Sea and explained 39% of the observed variation there. No data was available for validation of model predictions in the Irish Sea.\tPublic record\n17203\tEXT_DS121866\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.235Z\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Hydrological and Catchment Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383636\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\CEH Catchments 10k.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\CEH Catchments 50k.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\CEH Hydrological Areas.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data team\tThis is a UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH) owned dataset containing the extent and location of catchment and hydrological areas in Wales. This dataset has been licenced to Natural Resources Wales for internal use and can be sub-licenced to NRW contractors.\tPublic record\n17090\tEXT_DS114820\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.846Z\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2000 and 2007 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383230\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2000.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2007.lyr    Used to be in: Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\Land_Cover_Map_2007\\\tGIS Service Desk Subject Matter Experts: Helena Sykes, David Allen\tLand Cover Map is a UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH) spatial dataset which NRW acquires under licence. There are two datasets that NRW get from UKCEH these being Land Cover Map 2000 and Land Cover Map 2007. NRW only has a segment of the data and not the whole of GB. This segment covers the whole of Wales cross boarder catchments and coastal areas around the Bristol Channel.   Land Cover Map 2000 (LCM2000) updates and substantially upgrade UKCEH\u0027s Land Cover Map of Great Britain (LCMGB) 1990 and also cover Northern Ireland. This was the first UKCEH all UK dataset. LCM200 was classified in relation to JNCC\u0027s Broad Habitats and so encompasses the entire range of UK Habitats. LCM2000 is used for a wide range of applications including environmental character assessments environmental protection transport planning animal disease distribution bird and habitat relationships and forestry research. Land Cover Map 2007 (LMC2007) was released in July 2011 and is a parcel-based classification of UK land cover. LCM2007 builds upon the successes of the parcel-based mapping of LMC2000 and employs similar but enhanced classification techniques.\tPublic record\n17935\tNRW_DS112415\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.871Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal Species Diversity Layer (Chao2 Analysis)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343135\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. The Chao2 estimator was applied to species data collated from the CCW and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot) the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses. The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out taking into account the source of the data its age spatial taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tPublic record\n17136\tEXT_DS120732\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.234Z\tEnvironment Agency (EA): KiWQM English Stations (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230373332\tX:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM English Stations.lyr\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is the English locations for stations or monitoring locations held within WISKI\u0027s Water Quality Module - KiWQM. This data is owned by the Environment Agency and comes in as part of a data exchange agreement.\tPublic record\n17871\tNRW_DS111298\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.709Z\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of Proposed Extensions to the Eryri SAC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323938\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist.\tBarbara Jones, Upland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW\tThis data collation is the results from two studies researching a proposed extension to Eryri Special Area of Conservation (SAC) conducted in 2001 and 2002. The first survey was commissioned by Countryside Council for Wales in order to map and describe the vegetation of six upload areas adjacent to the existing Carneddau and Clydeiriau Site of Special Scientific Interest. The second survey built on the results from the 2001 studies and evaluated these against the requirements for SPA’s.\tPrivate record\n17643\tNRW_DS109658\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.057Z\tCollated Zostera Seagrass Bed Habitat Boundaries for Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039363538\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Seagrass Beds Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Seagrass Beds Polygon.lyr  Master copy stored on S:\\Users\\lough_n\\OSPAR species and habitats\\Zostera Beds\\Currentlayer_2008 Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\Zostera beds\tMetadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team Custodian: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group\tSeagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas such as lagoons and channels which are sheltered from significant wave action. The three species of Zostera that occur in the UK Dwarf eelgrass (Zostera noltii) narrow-leaved eelgrass (Zostera angustifolia) and  eelgrass (Zostera marina)  are all considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) and have some protection through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Zostera beds is also on the OSPAR List of threatened and/or declining species and habitats. The OSPAR Convention is the Convention for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic. This is a collation of Zostera habitats mapped by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Phase 1 Intertidal Survey Intertidal Monitoring Contracts Fisheries Sensitivity maps as well as point sources derived from casework site visits CCWs data holdings in Marine Recorder and Environment Agency Wales (EAW) Water Framework Directive (WFD) Monitoring Results. The purpose of this data capture was to create a spatial representation for Zostera habitat across Wales. This collation was carried out for reporting on the distribution of OSPAR Habitats in Wales.\tPublic record\n17532\tNRW_DS101806\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.252Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of King Scallop Pecten maximus\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383036\tSkomer MNR, West Region Drive /shared documents/data/scallops\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  The scallop population in the Reserve is of conservation value because of its small size and its remoteness from any substantial population.  Scallops were collected commercially and recreationally in the area of the Reserve until 1990.  In 1990 the South Wales Sea Fisheries Committee (SWSFC) introduced a byelaw prohibiting scallop collecting by any means. The population of scallops needs to be monitored in order to assess the effectiveness of this byelaw. Surveys have been carried out since 1979 looking at scallop density and size.  The purpose of this data capture was to estimate the density of scallops within suitable habitats in the Reserve and to assess the age structure in the population.\tPublic record\n17661\tNRW_DS109899\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.467Z\tAssessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383939\ta) Paper report held by CCW HQ library, digital version on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) b) MapInfo layers on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) c) Excel files on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) d) Photographs on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry)\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (Formerly Jan Sherry Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems/ Heathland Ecologist, NRW)\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage ecology landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales.  This dataset compiles of the results of an National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of coal spoil habitats on 15 sites in the South Wales valleys. It contains quadrat and species data for heath grassland mire and associated habitats and mapped habitat polygons.  The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a strategic assessment of the conservation value of coal spoil in the South Wales valleys to be used to identify important sites which require protection through the planning process and as possible use for designation of sites. It has also been used to aid in identifying action by Local Biodiversity Action Plans (LBAPs).  The area covered includes South Wales valley; Torfaen Rhondda Cynon Taf Caerphilly and Merthyr County Borough Council (CBCs).\tPublic record\n17926\tNRW_DS112416\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.825Z\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal species diversity layer (neighbourhood analysis)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343136\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the range is measured using biotope richness biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.   An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out taking into account the source of the data its age spatial taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tPublic record\n17943\tNRW_DS112723\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.968Z\tTir Cymen Farm Management Plans for Wales (Paper Records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373233\tOff-site storage (contact library team)\tBuddug Jones, Common Land and Agriculture Advisor, Ecosystem Services Team/ Natural Resources Planning, NRW\tManagement plans for a number of holdings throughout Wales that are part of the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme. Each plan consists of personal details including the names and address of the owner and tenant of the holding; the type of farm enterprise; the general management practice of the holding including livestock type use of common land and the growth of crops; the changes in management to conform to Tir Cymen guidelines; schedules of annual management payments and standard capital payments; paper maps showing the extent of the holding and surrounding area the different habitat types surrounding the holding and the capital works being completed around the holding. The plans also list the whole farm management code which includes listing the archaeological features land forms and water and wetlands found around the holding. The purpose of data capture was to ensure selected land holdings meet the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme.\tPublic record\n19127\tNRW_DS125554\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.954Z\tWelsh Dune Fungi: Data Collation, Evaluation and Conservation Priorities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353534\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/fun/Fungus%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Ffun%2FFungus%20Evidence%2F0%20Fungi%20Evidence%20Report%20Data%2FWelsh%20Dune%20Fungi%20Data%20Collation\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200083EEAA977243FD40886539B9E121A6E5\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B3D1F5A48%2D0066%2D4C30%2DAF53%2D7534D1DCD95A%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA report and data spreadsheet covering analysis of historic data on dune fungi which subsequently led to the development of a scoring system for SSSI selection.\tPrivate record\n17688\tNRW_DS110039\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.511Z\tThe Rare Red Alga Anotrichium barbatum:  UK Species Action Plan and its Implementation (1998 - 1999)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303339\t[A] Archived as project 153, media 251 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 251) [B] Marine Recorder survey (JNCCMNCR40000738).   [C] Paper MNCR forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe marine red alga Anotrichium barbatum is one of two rare seaweeds in the UK with a Species Action Plan. The alga was detected in Tremadog Bay in 1998. The site was resurveyed in 1999 and Anotrichium barbatum was again recorded. The dataset contains information on the geographical distribution of the alga and density estimates. In 1997 an Action Plan was prepared for English Nature but downgraded to a Species Statement because there were no known extant UK populations. The discovery of Anotrichium barbatum in Cardigan Bay in 1998 permitted the completion in 1998 of the Action Plan with target. The minimum objective is to stabilize the species\u0027 status i.e. to maintain the net population size and distribution against baseline figures set at the time of Action Plan publication.\tPublic record\n17906\tNRW_DS111924\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.627Z\tSublittoral Survey of North Pen Llyn 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131393234\tMarine Recorder survey JNCCMNCR40000771. Report is available from NRW library - Maes y Fynnon. Original MNCR forms are maintained at Maes y Fynnon off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe aim of this survey was to gather detailed information on reef habitats and their associated communities in the northern part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau cSAC and to provide a photographic and video record of the habitats and communities surveyed.  The geographic coverage included The North Pen Llyn coast and concentrated on the section between Penrhyn Mawr and Porth Dinllaen. Surveys took place over a period between the 18th and the 22nd of July 1998.\tPublic record\n19098\tNRW_DS125462\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.660Z\tA survey for larvae of the caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog - Winter 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343632\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe ‘Natur am Byth!’ partnership unites nine environmental charities and Natural Resources Wales to deliver the country’s largest natural heritage and outreach programme to save species from extinction and reconnect people to nature.  Following the rediscovery of adults of the Vulnerable caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog in July and August 2022 its only Welsh locality an extensive search of 26 locations for larvae was undertaken. The opportunity was taken to look for the Critically Endangered L. pati O’Connor at the same time as the two species are known to occur together although it has never been recorded from Wales (Wallace 2016).\tPublic record\n17937\tNRW_DS112727\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.940Z\tSAC Monitoring: Twaite (Alosa fallax) and Allis (Alosa alosa) Shad egg survey of the River Usk (2010-2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373237\t[A]  2010 survey data is held in the Marine Data Archive as Project 281 and Media 1096.  [B]  2011 survey data is held in the Marine Data Archive as Project 326, Media 1283.  [C]  Biological Data is held in Recorder 6: -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2010 - CCW - Report no.10/8/1 CCW000280000000E   -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2011 - CCW - Report no.11/8/3  CCW0008000000009\t\tThe River Usk rises in the Black Mountain range in the west of the Brecon Beacons National Park and flows east and then south to enter the Severn Estuary at Newport.  The river is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for several features including salmon lampreys and shads.  Twaite (Alosa fallax) and allis (Alosa alosa) shad are anadromous members of the Family Clupeidae (Herring).  In Britain twaite shad is known to spawn in the Rivers Severn Wye Usk and Tywi.  However it is not certain whether a breeding population of allis shad still exists.  The fomer Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of shad populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. The JNCC \u0027Common Standards Methodology Guidance for Freshwater Fauna\u0027 (2005) gives a target for the spawning distribution of shad.  It states there should be \u0027no decline in spawning distribution\u0027 assessed by kick sampling during May and June.  There are two main data sources of shad spawning in the River Usk.  The Environment Agency Wales (EAW) now Natural Resources Wales recorded the presence or absence of shad eggs at a number of sites from 1990 to 2005 although surveys have only been conducted annually since 2003.\tPublic record\n17771\tNRW_DS110519\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.510Z\tWild Bird Licence Return Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353139\tData is currently held and custodianship rests with the RSPB\tDylan Lloyd, Environmental Surveillance Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW (Formerly Sian Whitehead, Senior Ornithologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tA collation of licence return data for bird species listed under the EC Directive on the conservation of wild birds and schedule 1 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act. Excludes ringing licences which are handled by British Trust for Ornithology (BTO). The collation was created to add to the picture of wild birds distributions in Wales to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.\tPublic record\n18571\tNRW_DS122094\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.255Z\tMarine Non Native Species Spatial Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303934\tx:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\  NB MORE UP TO DATE DATA IS NOW AVAILABLE AT THIS LOCATION\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNB THIS DATA SET HAS BEEN REPLACED BY A MORE UP TO DATE DATA SET (116352) N.B. 116352 is not a replacement as it specifically documents data exyracted  This is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location on marine non native species across Wales. These have been split out into invasive low risk other and potential. This data was collected from a variety of ad hoc surveys conducted by Natural Resource Wales staff or contractors.\tPrivate record\n17114\tEXT_DS119113\t2024-05-28T12:11:56.601Z\tKeep Wales Tidy: Blue Flag Awards (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313133\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Blue Flag Awards.lyr\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a spatial dataset showing the location of Blue Flag beaches across Wales. 2018 marked the 30th year of the Blue Flag Award in Wales which is generally considered the \u0027gold standard\u0027 for beaches across the world. The Blue Flag Programme is owned by the non-governmental non-profit organisation \u0027Foundation for Enivronmental Education\u0027 (FEE). The Blue Flag Programme was started in France in 1985. It has been operating in Europe since 1987 and in areas outside of Europe since 2001. The programme is currently in operation in 46 countries across the world. In Wales the award is managed by Keep Wales Tidy.\tPublic record\n17509\tNRW_DS100391\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.908Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Vegetation\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333931\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1\\Phase1 Vegetation.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.  This is a GIS dataset containing vegetation types recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland survey of Wales (1987 - 2004). A detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation.  Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha. The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).\tPublic record\n17869\tNRW_DS111406\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.670Z\tSkomer Island Eunicella verrucosa Survey 1985\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343036\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000029)\tContact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of the results of a survey aimed at studying the population of the broad sea fan Eunicella verrucosa at Skomer Marine Reserve. The dataset also contains data on a number of other species of interest and previously unsurveyed areas of the reserve. The purpose of this data capture was: -  Provide information on the distribution and abundance of E. verrucosa around Skomer and determine habitat preferences.  -  Investigate the size of individual E. verrucosa and the population age structure. -  Study the epibionts of E. verrucosa.  -  Study orientation and growth form of E. verrucosa colonies in a variety of environmental conditions. The survey also aimed to tag and photograph E. verrucosa for future monitoring record the occurrence and abundance of various species of interest and survey some additional areas around Skomer Marine Reserve for which there was little or no information.\tPublic record\n17345\tNRW_DS24234\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.609Z\tOtter Sightings in Wales (OTTERSURV)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323334\tSee the Terrestrial Archive:  Project Number T003 Media T_Media003 (File Path: K:\\Archive\\Terrestrial Archive\\T_Media001to100\\T_Media003)\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA database of otter sightings in Wales. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Regional consultations and for information and reporting at Head Quarters.\tPublic record\n17972\tNRW_DS114561\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.683Z\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Vertebrates)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353631\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tThis is a collated list of terrestrial vertebrate species names in English Welsh and scientific Latin.  Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff.  Methods of data capture: Most of the translations have been created and/or verified by Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd for CCW the records have been extensively cleaned by the Natural History Museum  Confidence in data: All translations have been carefully checked and approved however with the English and Welsh names as in any language there will be multiple derivations and regional variations. Also the scientific terminology changes periodically as work on taxonomy progresses.\tPrivate record\n17523\tNRW_DS101852\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.162Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of Parazoanthus axinellae\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383532\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/parazoo\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Internal Custodian: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The population of Parazoanthus axinellae (yellow trumpet anemone) is an important feature of Skomer MNR.  P. axinellae is a Lusitanian (south-western) species near to the edge of its range and may act as an indicator of climatic change. Colonies have been photographed from fixed locations since 2001 and colony growth has been monitored. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor P. axinellae colonies for changes in polyp density and colony area.\tPublic record\n18402\tNRW_DS118975\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.113Z\tMapping the Extent of Calaminarian Grassland at Mwyngloddfa Cwmystwyth SSSI 2016/2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138393735\tY drive:  Shared_Projects\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Phase 2 Grassland\\Phase 2 Grassland.gdb\\Cwmystwyth_veg GIS data stored at above location, rest of the information is stored DMS\u0026gt;Evidence\u0026gt;Terrestrial Habitats\u0026gt;Lowlands Grassland\u0026gt;Projects\u0026gt;Cwm Ystwyth calaminarian survey 2017\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems, NRW.\tThe project aims to map the extent of the Annex 1 type \u0027H6130 Calaminarian grasslands of the Violetalia calaminariae\u0027 (Calaminarian grassland) on Mwyngloddfa Cwmystwyth Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the adjacent area of Elenydd SSSI in Ceredigion.  The habitat here includes examples where Calaminarian grassland is characterised largely by the presence of metallophyte lichen species although metallophyte bryophytes and vascular plants also indicate some forms of the community here. This survey built on previous work (Simkin 2014) that sought to produce a more detailed categorisation of the habitat in mid Wales.  This established that different forms of the habitat existed which were variously typified by metallophyte-rich spoil grassland and heathland as well as areas that were largely denuded of vegetation (bare highly contaminated spoil).\tPublic record\n17500\tNRW_DS100390\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.664Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Target Notes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333930\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1\\Phase1 Target.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.  This is a GIS dataset containing target notes recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (1987 - 2004). The Welsh Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey was a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW).\tPublic record\n17971\tNRW_DS114573\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.650Z\tMarine Algae Sites in Wales: Survey and Inventory of Important and Rare Algae\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353733\tData is archived as project no. 325, media 1282 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1200to1300\\Media 1282)  Species data is also available through Marine Recorder (survey no. MRCCW31200000002)   The herbarium is stored at the National Museum of Wales, Cardiff.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services\tSeveral sites around Wales have been highlighted as being of interest for their marine seaweeds either as species rich sites or for supporting rare scarce or Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Many of these sites were designated in 2007 as provisional Important Plant Areas for Algae and all the sites are situated within marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) or Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs).  This study was designed to provide an inventory of species at selected sites which were highlighted as the result of previous surveys and also to recommend methodologies for future monitoring.\tPublic record\n18059\tNRW_DS115548\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.820Z\tSeal pelage and Photo ID Monitoring Wales (2005-2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353438\tData is archived as Project No. 395, Media No. 1412  Other information: The majority of the images are created by CCW.  Some images are suppplied to CCW for use within the project by volunteers and other third parties/partners. Images are also supplied to CCW through Flicker.  The ExtractCompare software used as part of this project is held on DELL1407.\tInternal Custodian: Tom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal ecologist, Natural resources planning/Marine and freshwater ecosystem group,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW       This entry will be taken out of public view and a new metadata entry will be created for the latest version of the database.  This entry is retained internally as the data is retained as a snapshot in time.  New metadata entry 122202.\tThis dataset is a catalogue of seal images were collected as part of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) seal monitoring work. Specific seal pelage surveys and images provided by volunteers and members of the public were also collected through NRWs Flicker account. These images were used to monitor behaviour and populations of seals around north Wales.  Photo-ID allowed these pelages to be captured and analysed which allows a catalogue of individuals to be constructed. This was then used to estimate the population size and movements. By repeating the photo-ID on an annual basis matches can be re-made giving an indication of a seals fidelity to a particular site and associations between individuals.\tPrivate record\n17391\tNRW_DS87114\t2024-05-28T12:22:31.952Z\tDrop-down Video Footage and Biotope Identification Data 1999\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313134\ta) Fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes-y-Ffynnon on optical media. Also maintained on backed-up server based storage as project 56 and 351, Media Nos 275-278, 290-293, 307, 1044-1056,1094,1100,1329 b) Sanderson et al., (Eds.) 2001, section 2.1.2, and data appendix. See related titles field. c) Marine Recorder [MRCCW31600000003] d) Original species and habitats data held in Marine Recorder mdb file in the archivee) Paper MNCR Survey forms held in NRW off-site storage\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW Metadata Point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to create a dataset to help with an evaluation of the deployment of drop-down video for identifying biotopes. Footage collected as an assessment of using drop-down video as part of a site-orientated monitoring technique.  Data was gathered as a result of the development and trial of a remote drop-down video-based monitoring method to measure biotope richness. Data was collected from two sites that presented sufficiently different physical conditions and biological conditions- north Pen Llyn reefs and Sarn Badrig reef.\tPublic record\n18019\tNRW_DS114970\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.522Z\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Tywi (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393730\t[A]  Associated data is held within the Marine Archive as Project 356, Media 1335.  [B]  Biological Data is held in Recorder 6 Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi Catchment 2011 - CCW - Staff Science Report 11/8/5 CCW000800000000B.\t\tRiver lamprey Brook lamprey and Sea lamprey are listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive (EU Directive on the Conservation of Natural Habitats of Wild Fauna and Flora 92/43/EEC). There are six rivers in Wales designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) for lamprey including the Afon Tywi in West Wales. Natural Resources Wales is responsible for monitoring the condition of lamprey species populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within designated sites.  The data collected and contracted out by former Countryside Council for Wales is used to assess the population attribute for all three lamprey species.  Sampling was carried out between the 5th October and 22nd November 2011.  A total of 30 sites distributed within the Tywi catchments were selected between Llandovery and Carmarthen.\tPublic record\n17202\tEXT_DS121882\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.221Z\tMet Office: Met Office Rainfall and Evapo-transpiration Calculation System (MORECS) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383832\tX:\\Physical\\Meteorological\\Met Office Rainfall Evaporation Calc Sys (MORECS) squares.lyr   More up to date data is held in the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/wrmr/ww/Morecs%20Data/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is the Met Office Rainfall and Evapo-transpiration Calculation System (MORECS) 40km x 40km squared averages. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have access to a licenced welsh cut of this data from the Met Office. The Met Office is the UK National Meteorological Service set up to research the possibilities of forecasting the weather and now uses more than 10 million weather observation from across the UK to create advanced atmospheric models to predict upcoming forecasts and longer term climate change scenarios.\tPublic record\n17714\tNRW_DS110213\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.656Z\tPolycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon (PAH) and N-Alkane Residues in Seabird Eggs after the Grounding of the Sea Empress.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323133\tArchived as project no 93 (media no 368) - File path K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 368 NB The report won\u0027t open in Adobe acrobat or Word.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe grounding of the Sea Empress in February 1996 resulted in large-scale oil pollution of the water around the Pembrokeshire coast and its associated island seabird colonies. The accident occurred at about the time that seabirds were returning to their breeding colonies. Therefore it was possible that breeding birds might transfer oil directly to their eggs through contact with oiled nesting material feathers and feet or oral ingestion of sub-lethal amounts of hydrocarbons and subsequent transfer to yolk. Exposure of adult birds and eggs to crude oil or polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) which are present in crude oil can have adverse effects on egg structure and quality embryo development and hatching success. This dataset contains the results of an investigation into the PAH and n-alkane content of seabird eggs on the coast of Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill. The aim of the study was to investigate whether there was any evidence of hydrocarbon contamination of seabird eggs resulting from the Seam Empress spill.\tPublic record\n17874\tNRW_DS111300\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.792Z\tTir Gofal Species Package Maps\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333030\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users/clare/TG targeting/final versions\tInternal Custodian: Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset contains maps of key areas for species that are targeted in Tir Gofal (the Welsh Government\u0027s agri-environmental programme) and information sheets describing the prescriptions for each species.  The maps are designed to be used by Tir Gofal project officers in order to inform them of the distribution of species targeted by Tir Gofal. Data collation and map production exercise carried out during 2007.\tPrivate record\n17197\tEXT_DS121881\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.237Z\tLocal Environmental Records Centres (LERCS) Wales: Boundaries (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383831\tX:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\Local Record Centre Boundary.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the areas covered by each Local Environmental Record Centre in Wales. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this dataset through a licence set up under the Local Environmental Records Centres Wales (LERC) Wales.\tPublic record\n17948\tNRW_DS114673\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.346Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey on the River Wye (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363733\t[A]  Data is archived on NRW Marine Archive server as project 330, media 1289. [B]  Species data is available in Recorder 6: Shad Egg Survey River Wye 2011 - CCW - Report no. 11/8/4  CCW0008000000008\tInternal Custodian: Rivers Ecologist, MFEG, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThe River Wye rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains and flows in a generally south-easterly direction to enter the Severn Estuary at Chepstow.  The river is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive for several features including salmon lampreys and shads.  Twaite (Alosa fallax) and allis (Alosa alosa) shad. In Britain significant populations of twaite shad are known to spawn in the Rivers Severn Wye Usk and Tywi.  However it is not certain whether a breeding population of allis shad still exists. The population of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and water quality issues. The purpose of this data capture was to make an assessment of Conservation Status by summarising previous data held at the site conducting a baseline survey and compare these with results from previous datasets.  Along the length of the welsh portion of the River Wye from Builth Wells to Monmouth. The survey was conducted in May June and July of 2011.\tPublic record\n17527\tNRW_DS101853\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.238Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Ross Coral Pentapora foliacea\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383533\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/pentapora ,  Images - dell1590 C/doc \u0026amp; settings/MONITORING/pentapora   CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000057)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Colonies of the bryozoan Pentapora foliacea are fragile structures thought to be moderately slow growing and long lived. They are important microhabitats for mobile species and are regarded as useful indicators of anthropogenic activity such as mobile fishing gear and anchoring. Stereo photographs of colonies have been taken from 1984. Image analysis methods have been tested and currently a morphological class system is used to classify and monitor the colonies. Alongside the basic monitoring various research projects are in progress looking at; the genetics of the species Anti-microbial zone assays and palaeoclimate using two different techniques: Variation in zooid size and isotopic signature of the carbonate skeleton. The purpose of this data capture was to: - monitor the numbers and growth rate of Ross coral colonies.   - monitor the amount of damage occurring to the colonies.\tPublic record\n18136\tNRW_DS115702\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.851Z\tMonitoring invertebrates by pitfall trapping after excavation of the surface vegetation in two slacks at Newborough Warren, Ynys Llanddwyn Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in 2013.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373032\t[A]  All data is currently held in the report, and is being transferred onto Recorder 6. [B]  The final report Located on the DMS: Reports Archive . NRW Evidence Reports KNOW-1206538853-14  NRW Evidence Report No. 022 - Monitoring~ace vegetation in two slacks at Newborough Warren - Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI in 2013\t\tThis dataset holds information regarding the monitoring of excavated dune slacks in Newborough Warren north Wales. The monitoring consisted on invertebrate surveys focussing on the two excavations sites further inland as well as an adjacent control site. The dataset hold 12838 individual invertebrates records made up of 298 species. The aim of the current study is to monitor by pitfall trapping the effects upon selected dune invertebrates of the excavation of the surface vegetation of two slacks and the dunes bordering their NE ends.  Some voucher specimens have been retained and are in the authors\u0027 private collections.\tPublic record\n17444\tNRW_DS87116\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.274Z\tPembrokeshire Marine / Sir Benfro Forol Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Grey Seal (Halichoerus grypus) Monitoring 2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313136\tStored as Project No. 60, media 355\tInternal Custodians:  Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Grey seals (Halichoerus grypus) are among the rarest seals in the world: the UK population represents about 40 percent of the world population and 95 percent of the EU population. Pembrokeshire Marine / Sir Benfro Forol SAC is home to one of the largest populations of grey seals around the west coast of the UK. The purpose of this data capture was to record document and report indicators of the condition of grey seals within the SAC. In addition to this monitoring is also used to document and report on the impact of human activities assessment of supporting habitat and used in the estimations related to the population and structure (age and sex).  The data collected will contribute to an estimate of whole site (SAC) production using indices developed following the 1992-94 seal census.\tPublic record\n17433\tNRW_DS87152\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.165Z\tMaritime Supralittoral Lichen Survey of the Portmeirion Promontory, Morfa Harlech National Nature Reserve (NNR), Site of Special Sceintific Importance (SSSI), and Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313532\tData is held as Project No.83, Media 358.\tCustodian: Intertidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is made up of the results of a supralittoral lichen survey on coastal rocks around the Portmeirion headland within the Morfa Harlech. Morfa Harlech is designated as a National Nature Reserve (NNR) Site of Special Sceintific Importance (SSSI) and Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC). The development and diversity of the supralittoral lichen communities is this area is one of great importance and is of conservation significance in this context. The purpose of this data capture was to produce an inventory of supralittoral lichen species highlighting and localising any noteworthy taxa and assemblages.\tPublic record\n17366\tNRW_DS24715\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.254Z\tTerrestrial Phase 1 Habitat Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234373135\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestrial\\Original_Phase_1    Original UVS maps stored NRW Maes y Fynnon.  Copies held in site reports.   Original NVC maps held in site reports.  Ph1 Survey \u0027master maps\u0027 archived at National Library of Wales; raster and vector data and 1km sq table are stored in the Geospatial Central Data Store.\tFor scientific issues; Jim Latham, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team Leader.     For data specification issues \u0026amp; Metadata; Jon Rothwell,  Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping.  Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     For additional information on the \u0027master map\u0027 archive contact Paul Joyner, NLW, Aberystwyth, Ceredigion SY23 3BU; tel 01970 632800\tThis dataset holds comprehensive habitat cover data for the whole of Wales derived from a programme of field recording that was begun by the Wales Field Unit (WFU) of the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) in 1979 and continued by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) after restructuring in 1991.   The purpose of this data capture was to inform site selection casework and policy decisions. The lowland survey was particularly used to locate grassland sites suitable for more in-depth survey for notification purposes.  Habitat extent data has been derived to inform the Local Biodiversity Action Plan process.   The digitisation was carried out in the first instance for the drafting of maps for open country and registered common land as required by the CROW Act 2000. For reporting purposes during the lowland phase of the surveys Wales was divided up into 15 different areas based on the Nature Conservancy Council\u0027s (NCC) Areas of Search (AoS).  The dataset is unique in that it holds full census field survey habitat data for the whole of terrestrial Wales. It has been used widely for research advice and planning both within and outside CCW.     The majority of the fieldwork was carried out \u0027in house\u0027 by closely co-ordinated teams of trained surveyors. Every effort was made to ensure consistency of habitat mapping.  A check on surveyor accuracy during the early stages of the Phase 1 Survey revealed comparatively high repeatability levels (Kerry S. M. Rimes C. A. Smith S. L. N. Williams A. M. 1994. An assessment of errors in Phase 1 habitat survey in Wales.  CCW report no. 94/4/1 Countryside Council for Wales Bangor; Stevens J.P. Blackstock T.H. Howe E.A. Stevens D.P. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73 53-59) and checks on the Upland Vegetation Survey data were carried out between 1994-1996 (Yeo M.J.M. Blackstock T.H. 2002.   A vegetation analysis of the pastoral landscapes of upland Wales UK. J. Veg. Sci. 13 803-816).    Digital maps were checked for accuracy at three stages: by contractor (Cynefin Environmental) and by CCW (C. Burrows L. Howe J. Stevens J. Rothwell). 1km square table: the data were input and checked by a contractor (C. Copp) and re-checked by CCW (C. Burrows and J. Stevens). For a detailed overview of the background methods and outputs of the Survey see Blackstock T. H. Howe E. A. Stevens J. P. Burrows C. R. Jones P. S. 2010. Habitats of Wales. A comprehensive field survey 1979 -1997.  University of Wales Press Cardiff.\tPublic record\n17753\tNRW_DS110334\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.354Z\tEffects of oil and clean-up of oil from the Sea Empress on the nationally rare and scarce vascular plants of Pembrokeshire\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333334\tArchived as project no 110 (media no 384). Report No. 289 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 384. Media 379 also mentioned originally in this entry but seems to have no connection.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is made up of the results of an investigation into the impact of the Sea Empress oil spillage and its clean-up on populations of some of the rare and scarce vascular plants that were well recorded in Pembrokeshire prior to the pollution incident. The purpose of this data capture was to reassess the population size of selected nationally rare and nationally scarce vascular plants of the Milford Haven waterway and the South Pembrokeshire coast.\tPublic record\n17134\tEXT_DS121601\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.101Z\tCadw: Designated Shipwreck Sites (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363031\tX:\\Designations\\CADW\\Designated Shipwreck Sites.lyr\tInternal Custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources of Wales (NRW) are supplied a number of historic and cultural datasets under a licence from Cadw. Cadw is the Welsh Government’s historic environment service working for an accessible and well-protected historic environment for Wales. They were set up with the aim of conserving Wales’s heritage helping people understand and care about their history and helping to sustain the distinctive character of Wales.  \u0027Wrecks\u0027 are designated under the 1973 Protection of Wrecks Act. There are numerous maritime wrecks in the seas around Wales. Although all of them have historic value six currently have legal protection. These six are known as \u0027designated wrecks\u0027 or \u0027protected wrecks\u0027.\tPublic record\n18813\tNRW_DS123529\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.841Z\tWaste Electrical and Electronic Equipment Approved Authorised Treatment Facility and Approved Exporter\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353239\tlist of current approvals on Public Register Copies of all historic reports are on the DMS.\tJohn Davies (weee@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk)\tPublic Register showing details of reprocessors and exporters of Waste Electrical and Electronic (WEEE) approved under the Waste Electrical and Electronic Regulations. A Spreadsheet showing the details of companies in Wales currently approved as an  Approved Authorised Treatment Facility (AATF) or Approved Exporter (AE) available on NRWs Online Public register. Only public register data specified in the WEEE Regulations are included.\tPrivate record\n18052\tNRW_DS115180\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.475Z\tSite Condition Assessment of Welsh Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Standing Water features (2007 - 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313830\tData is archived as project 385, media 1399.\tInternal Custodian: Dr Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Freshwater team leader, CCW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA report and supporting data was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2011 to provide an assessment of the current conservation status of 23 mostly oligotrophic Welsh standing waterbodies. These lie within six different Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). The data have been used to generate site-specific condition assessments. The collection of survey data and preparation of the site condition assessments broadly follows the methods detailed in the Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) Guidance (JNCC 2005). The lakes are assessed every 6 years.  The data contributes the the report \\Site condition assessments of Welsh SAC and SSSI standing water features 2007-2012\\\".\"\tPublic record\n18102\tNRW_DS115583\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.025Z\tDistribution of the Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) American slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata in Conwy Estuary (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353833\tData is archived as project 442, media 1482 The file path for the report: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Non-native species\\Alien Crepidula   The file path for the data and analyses: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Non-native species\\Alien Crepidula\\Data and GIS  The file path for the archive: W:\\Media1401to1500\\Media1482\tInternal Custodian: Ben Wray, Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Menai Strait and Conwy estuary area is a designated Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in Wales. The Habitats Directive establishes that the management of SACs should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I habitats and Annex II species. Crepidula fornicata the American slipper limpet is an invasive non native species in Wales. The slipper limpet occurs on a variety of substrates it is often found attached to the shells of mussels Mytilus edulis as well as oysters stones and other shells around the low water mark. This species is found in south Wales especially the Milford Haven area with few records north of the Haven. The Menai Strait mussel fishery in north Wales is the largest in the UK. The mussel ‘seed’ is imported from outside the area. The problem with importing the seed from elsewhere is that there is the risk of accidentally introducing the invasive slipper limpet. This was the case in 2006 when C. fornicata escaped into the Menai Strait. The purpose of this data capture was to assess spread of Crepidula fornicate in North Wales by assessing the Conwy estuary.\tPublic record\n17191\tEXT_DS121885\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.230Z\tMet Office: Rain Gauge (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383835\tx:\\Physical\\Meteorological\\Met Office Raingauge Closed.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Meteorological\\Met Office Raingauge Open.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThese spatial datasets show the location of open and closed rain gauges from across Wales. This data is owned by the Met Office and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have access to a licenced welsh cut of this data from the Met Office. The Met Office is the UK National Meteorological Service set up to research the possibilities of forecasting the weather and now uses more than 10 million weather observation from across the UK to create advanced atmospheric models to predict upcoming forecasts and longer term climate change scenarios.\tPublic record\n17595\tNRW_DS108800\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.080Z\tPhase 2 Saltmarsh Survey of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383030\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Saltmarsh\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM  (was originally managed by Peter Rhind, Coastal Ecologist, ex-CCW).\tThis National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Phase 2 project and corresponding datasets are a collation of mapped saltmarsh habitats across Wales.  This data was collected to provide an overview of saltmarshes across Wales.  The surveys were conducted between 1990 and 2003. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified.   However habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends land management or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type or species in the digital data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.  Coastal saltmarshes in the UK comprise of the upper vegetated portions of intertidal mudflats lying approximately between mean high water neap tides and mean high water spring tides.  Saltmarshes are usually restricted to comparatively sheltered locations in five main physiographic situations: in estuaries in saline lagoons behind barrier islands at the heads of sea lochs and on beach plains.  The development of saltmarsh vegetation is dependent on the presence of intertidal mudflats.  Communities are additionally affected by differences in climate the particle size of the sediment and within estuaries by decreasing salinity in the upper reaches.\tPublic record\n17442\tNRW_DS88241\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.213Z\tFreshwater Site Visits and Ad-hoc Sightings\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838323431\t[A]  Species Data held in Recorder 6: -  Freshwater Sites - Occasional Reconnaissance Surveys - CCW CCW0002800000001    -  Freshwater ad-hoc sightings collected by CCW staff - CCW CCW0002800000003      This data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader of Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tRecords of species and habitat ad hoc sightings collected in the freshwater environment.  This includes but it not limited to aquatic plants amphibians and certain easily identified invertebrates.  Data is collected in order to assist with targeted monitoring and site management of freshwater environments across Wales.  Data collection will be on-going during the monitoring and other survey work.  This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tPublic record\n17756\tNRW_DS110332\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.461Z\tRocky Shore Transect Monitoring in Milford Haven following the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333332\tArchived as project no 101 (media no 376) and project no 108 (media no 381 )  H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media376 H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media381 CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Marine Vertebrates, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThree months before the Sea Empress oil spill baseline surveys of rocky shore fauna and flora were undertaken at fixed transect sites throughout Milford Haven and the Daugleddau Estuary. Following the spill Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned a full re-survey of the fixed transects in 1996 and 1997. The purpose of this data capture was to identify the effects of the oil spill on the flora and fauna of the rocky shore at Milford Haven through comparisons with baseline surveys and to provide quantitative data on important species for future analysis of recovery. This dataset contains the results of the survey work.\tPublic record\n18066\tNRW_DS115520\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.452Z\tAcross Wales Lagoon Monitoring - Carew Mill Pond (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353230\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 241 and Media No\u0027s 1337.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting (Matt Green?),  NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Carew Mill Pond lagoon which is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The data is unpublished. The purpose of this data capture is to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature.\tPublic record\n17881\tNRW_DS111297\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.886Z\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of the Carneddau (2001-2002)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323937\tHard copy reports are available in CCW libraries and TSG archive.\tBarbara Jones, Upland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW\tThis survey was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales in order to map describe and assess the vegetation of the western part of the Carneddau. The study forms part of the much larger Eryri Site of Special Scientific Interest Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).\tPrivate record\n17105\tEXT_DS111486\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.651Z\tLocal Environmental Records Centre (LERC) Wales: Welsh Protected and Priority Species (PPS) Collation (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343836\tX:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC priority and protected species points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC priority and protected species regions.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS all bats points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS all bats regions.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS All WCA points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS Badgers points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS Common Toad.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS Dormice points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS EPS points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS Great Crested Newt points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS Marsh Fritilary points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS Otter points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS Red Squirrel points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS Section 42 points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS Water Vole points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS WCA Sch1 Birds points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS WCA Sch5 Animals points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Local Record Centers\\LRC PPS WCA Sch8 Plants points.lyr\tInternal Custodian: GIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Barnaby Letheren, Lead Specialist Advisor, Knowledge \u0026amp; Information Management\tThe data are collated from species datasets held by the Welsh Local Environmental Records Centres. Species are selected to form part of this dataset if they have international/national protection or national priority status (UKBAP or Section 42 of the NERC Act). The purpose of this data capture was to inform conservation management and decisions.   Data being recieved from LERC\u0027s does vary between centre. North Wales Environmental Information Centre (COFNOD) data only contains data that has been verified by trusted sources and specialists in the field.  South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre (SEWBReC) West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre (WWBIC) and Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park (BIS) data contains all protected and priority records including records that have been unverified.\tPublic record\n17681\tNRW_DS110097\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.782Z\tSediment shore impact assessment- monitoring: strandline fauna\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303937\t[A] Report is archived as Project No. 14, Media No. 40 (K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 040) [B] Fireproof safe - Stack 01, Hafod Elfyn, NRW-Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales. [C] CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey number:MRCCW10000000078)\tInternal Contact: Master Reference Data Team Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe present study investigates the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the invertebrate populations of the strandline on two selected soft sediment shores Pembrey and Pendine in Carmarthen Bay South Wales.  It represents one of four sampling protocols initially proposed by University of Wales Swansea (March 1996) and is the protocol selected by CCW (September 1996).  A comparison can be made between the findings for one of the areas studied (Pembrey) with the results of a previous study on the strandline fauna of the same area (Llewellyn 1996). Data was used to investigate the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the invertebrate populations of strandlines.\tPublic record\n17579\tNRW_DS102035\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.542Z\tCarmarthen Bay Intertidal Attributes and Monitoring Techniques - Monitoring of Piddocks\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303335\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 199, Media No 558.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Piddocks\tData Custodian - Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata point of contact - Master Reference Data Team\tUnder the 1992 EC Habitats and Species Directive the UK submitted a list of candidate  Special Areas of Conservation (cSACs) to the European Commission (EC). Each cSAC is  selected for one or more of the habitat types and species listed in Annexes I and II of the  Directive. This process was undertaken largely in 1995-1996. The piddock Pholas dactylus occurs in Britain from Kent along the south and south-west coasts including south Wales Anglesey and Solway (Hill 2004). It has also been recorded from several sites on the east coasts of Yorkshire and Northumbria and southwest Ireland. The surface of the peat or clay bed is characterised by dense algal mats and pools of standing water (JNCC 2004). The presence of piddocks is instantly identified by holes in the substratum.  The aim of this data capture was to map the current known extent and density of boring molluscs piddocks across the biotope at Marros Sands and Whiteford Point and record any mussel spat or seed on the biotope.  Piddocks are bivalves that bore clay and peat exposures. The survey work is largely a trial to determine the best means by which to record the extent of the exposures as well as the populations of piddocks. Sand levels are found to change over time seasonally annually and as a result of one off weather events (storms) and thus the areas of populated peat and clay also vary. A non-destructive means of assessing the piddock population is considered.\tPublic record\n18681\tNRW_DS123329\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.463Z\tPollution Inventory\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333239\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Pollution Inventory.lyr\tSusan Freeman\t\tPrivate record\n17195\tEXT_DS121926\t2024-05-28T12:15:59.848Z\tEnvironment Agency: Severn Lower 2017 Saltmarsh Extent (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393236\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh Areas\\Severn Lower 2017 Saltmarsh Extent.lyr\tMetadata Point of contact - Geospatial Team Internal Custodian - Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. Saltmarsh extents determined in a desk based study using aerial imagery. This data is Open but owned by the Environment Agency.\tPrivate record\n18342\tNRW_DS116494\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.170Z\tOpen Access - Other Dedicated Land (CRoW Act)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343934\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Other Dedicated Land 2014.lyr\tChris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access, Open Access Team / Recreation \u0026amp; Partnerships Team\tThis dataset shows the area of land that has been dedicated under Section 16 of the CRoW Act by organisations/individuals other than Natural Resources Wales (NRW).   When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access it should be used in conjunction with Open Land Open Country Registered Common Land Other Statutory Access Land and Other Dedicated Land from the same years.\tPublic record\n18978\tNRW_DS125172\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.053Z\tAssessing the vulnerability of Annex I marine habitats to climate change in Wales (2021)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313732\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability Assessment\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dr. Karen Robinson  Metadata Point of Contact:  Dr. Karen Robinson\tThis project assessed the potential impact of climate change pressures on SACs and their Annex 1 features in Wales under two different climate change emission scenarios from 2025 – 2100. The outputs from the project were a report GIS layers showing vulnerability levels for features within each site (based on their component biotopes) and a series of excel workbooks summarising vulnerabilities of each site and feature by pressure type.\tPublic record\n17711\tNRW_DS110209\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.657Z\tEstablishment of a Permanent Monitoring Site at Mid Channel Rocks, Milford Haven\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323039\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media053 Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elyfn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Metadata contact and Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tData from a study undertaken to establish a permanent monitoring site on Mid Channel Rocks Milford Haven. The work was commissioned following the grounding of the Sea Empress oil tanker on the Mid Channel Rocks Milford Haven on February 15th 1996.  On the four days following the grounding the tanker remained in the immediate vicinity of these rocks grounding and floating free several times.  An estimated 70000 tonnes of oil were spilled within one nautical mile of the Mid-Channel Rocks.  A considerable volume of dispersant was applied from the air in the nearby area.  This is expected to have led to significantly elevated hydrocarbon levels in the water column possibly including the more toxic oil fractions where fresh oil was dispersed. The objectives of this project were to establish a permanent sublittoral monitoring site at a rock substrate location close to the source of the spilled oil and which was amongst the most likely to have been subjected to elevated water column hydrocarbon load and to undertake a baseline survey to document epibenthic rock community and species composition diversity richness and condition. Data was captured in order to establish a permanent sublittoral monitoring site at a rock substrate location close to the source of the spilled oil and to undertake a baseline survey to document epibenthic rock community and species composition diversity richness and condition.\tPublic record\n17880\tNRW_DS111299\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.900Z\tHedgerow Database for Angelsey North Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323939\tNeeds secure location. Currently on a cd with Claire Burrows\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tThe Hedgerow Database contains data from hedgerow surveys undertaken on Anglesey looking at the structure and composition of the hedgerows.  In 2002 a Hedgerow Survey Handbook was produced setting a standard procedure for local hedgerow surveys in the UK. It was proposed that a series of hedgerow surveys were to be undertaken in Wales using this standard procedure however as yet only the Anglesey survey has taken place due to funding constraints and therefore the database only currently contains data from this one survey. The purpose of this data capture was to build up a detailed body of information on the current status of hedgerows across the UK thus enabling appropriate action to be taken to safeguard the most important hedgerows and to monitor any changes in the quality and quantity of the hedgerow network over time.\tPublic record\n17204\tEXT_DS121928\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.462Z\tEnvironment Agency: Severn Lower 2017 Saltmarsh Zonation Polygon (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393238\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh Areas\\Severn Lower 2017 Saltmarsh Zonation Polygons.lyr\tMetadata contact - Geospatial Team Internal Custodian - Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. Saltmarsh zonation polygons are determined in a desk based study using aerial imagery. This data is Open but owned by the Environment Agency.\tPrivate record\n17678\tNRW_DS110098\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.695Z\tAppraisal of Environmental Impact and Recovery Using Laminaria Holdfast Faunas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303938\tThe report and data are held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 15, media No.41 (pathway: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media041).   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder. [MRCCW10000000093]\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW.   Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Sea Empress spill provided an opportunity to test the applicability of Laminaria holdfast faunal communities for detecting the pollution effects of a putative oil contamination gradient and to assess the immediate response and consequent recovery of components of the holdfast fauna to oil pollution.   Data captured to test the applicability of Laminaria holdfast faunal communities for detecting the pollution effects of a putative oil contamination gradient and to assess the immediate response and consequent recovery of components of the holdfast fauna to oil pollution.\tPublic record\n17185\tEXT_DS121927\t2024-05-28T12:15:59.542Z\tEnvironment Agency: Severn Lower 2017 Saltmarsh Zonation Points (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393237\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh Areas\\Severn Lower 2017 Saltmarsh Zonation Points.lyr\tMetadata contact - Geospatial Team Internal Custodian - Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. Saltmarsh zonation points determined in a desk based study using aerial imagery. This data is Open but owned by the Environment Agency.\tPrivate record\n18977\tNRW_DS125181\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.054Z\tScallop Fisheries Orders (closed and unrestricted areas)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313831\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries Orders\\Scallop order closed areas.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries Orders\\Cardigan Bay Unrestricted Scallop Area.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries Orders\\Cardigan Bay Unrestricted Scallop Area Points.lyr\tData Custodian: Alexander Scorey Possibly Colin Charman.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17344\tNRW_DS24269\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.513Z\tBird Spreadsheet Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323639\tSian Whitehead\u0027s G:drive.\tSian Whitehead: Ornithologists, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\tThis is a collation of spreadsheets containing bird data and 1 containing rainfall data as follows: -  Numbers of lesser black backed gulls occurring on Welsh Islands Numbers of curlew recorded in Welsh tetrads 1988 - 1991 Summary of surveys undertaken at each Welsh bird SSSI Summary of upland bird data from site surveys in Wales. Summary of bird species surveys in Wales. Pied flycatcher (and other species) breeding data for nest box colony  Enlli-skylark\u0027s bird days\u0027 column: Skylark days on Ynys Enlli Wye rainfall data 1944 - 1996 Number of raptors only no site details for Elenydd. Sian also holds  Numbers of dead and RSPCA recovered sea birds recorded by date  This is in Quattro Pro version 5. Numbers of black grouse breeding on Marine Number of common scoter recorded in Carmarthen Bay 1977 - 1995 Numbers of lapwing recorded in Welsh tetrads 1988 - 1991 Number of ring ouzels recorded on Spring and Autumn passage at UK Bird Number of ring ouzel days recorded on Spring and Autumn passage at Number of ring ouzels recorded in Welsh tetrads 1995 \u0026amp; 1996 Number of pairs of roseate terns in Wales 1960 -1995 (The file name for each of these surveys is available on Cyprian Payne\u0027s original Access metadatabase.) Number of pairs of artic terns on the Skerries 1980 -1995 Number of pairs of roseate terns on Ynys Feurig and in UK and Ireland Number of pairs of gannets on Grassholm 1850 - 1995 Five spreadsheets containing data from previous spreadsheets plus common Marine date is in format 10 -Sep -95. Numbers of common scoter  recorded Number of hen harrieres recorded at individual locations 1978 -1986 Raven breeding data for some sites 1986 - 1992 (data from AVC)\tPrivate record\n19131\tNRW_DS125534\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.099Z\tA survey for Short-necked Oil Beetle Meloe brevicollis at Castlemartin Range SSSI\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353334\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tFollowing the recording of an adult at Linney Head in June 2015 a survey was conducted at Castlemartin Range SSSI in Pembrokeshire in 2022 to clarify the  conservation status of the Nationally Rare Short-necked Oil Beetle Meloe brevicollis. A single dead adult and numerous triungulins were found at Pen-y-holt Bay confirming that Short-necked Oil Beetle is both present and breeding within the SSSI. This is believed to be the first record of wild larvae of Short-necked Oil Beetle in Wales and suggests that triungulin surveys can be a successful tool in identifying ‘new’ locations for the beetle at times when adults are less active.  Short-necked Oil Beetle exhibits a restricted distribution at Castlemartin Range SSSI increasing its vulnerability to local and national extinction. At present Pen-y-holt Bay is the only location within the SSSI supporting recent records of the beetle (and indeed the only breeding evidence). The conservation of this population is therefore of upmost importance.\tPrivate record\n17669\tNRW_DS109980\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.551Z\tCoastal Saline Lagoons and Pools in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393830\tData is archived as project 12, media 22 and 23 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 22 and K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 23) Species data held in Marine Recorder (survey number JNCCMNCR40000799) Original survey forms are stored in NRW off-site storage. Fireproof safe - Stack 01, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe Natural History Museum was asked by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to undertake a survey of all potential lagoonal or coastal brackish pond sites in Wales. This dataset presents the results of that survey conducted during December 1998 as well as the results of the review and proposals for the monitoring and conservation of the coastal saline lagoons in Wales. Lagoons are an unusual and rare habitat both in the UK (total resource ca 5000 ha) and elsewhere. The characteristic lagoon community includes species almost or entirely restricted to lagoonal habitats in the United Kingdom where they represent some of our rarest plants and animals; nine lagoonal species are currently protected under Schedules 5 and 8 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act (1981).  Survey of lagoonal habitats in Wales to identify lagoons and pools and carry out an assessment of condition and monitoring.\tPublic record\n17412\tNRW_DS46955\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.473Z\tSAC Monitoring: Great Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) Populations at Halkyn Mountain / Mynydd Helygain Special Area of Conservation (SAC) (2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436393535\t[A]  Associated data is held in the following location; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\27 GCN at Halkyn Mountain SAC   [B]  Species Data held in Recorder 6: CCW - HQ Main Assessment of Great Crested Newts at Halkyn Mountain SAC CCW000480000000E\t\tMonitoring of Great Crested Newts (GCNs) on Halkyn Mountain Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under contract. The monitoring consisted of the following:  a) Survey of 45 ponds where GCN had previously been recorded for eggs larvae and juveniles and other environmental data.   b) Mapping of terrestrial habitat within 250m of pond to assess the value of the habitat for GCNs (total of 183.9ha). c) Collation of existing GCN count data from 1985 to 2005 in known display ponds in the SAC. d) Condition assessment of GCN as a SAC feature.  This dataset forms part of the Wales-wide Common Standards Monitoring. The aim of this data capture was to assess the condition of designated SAC features based on site-specific performance indicators and to record aspects of their management both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales.\tPublic record\n18966\tNRW_DS124881\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.206Z\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Developer Access\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383831\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Developer Access to Site.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThese shapefiles relate to Developer access to Cefn Croes Windfarm for construction maintenance and decommissioning of site as per Lease Agreement dated 20.11.2003.\tPrivate record\n17294\tEXT_DS124237\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.130Z\tMet Office: Numerical Weather Prediction (NWP) System data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323337\t\tJiping Shi, Team Leader Air Quality Modelling and Risk Assessment Team, Integrated Evidence Group\tNumerical weather prediction (NWP) is the process of obtaining an objective forecast of the future state of the atmosphere by running a computer model.\tPublic record\n17650\tNRW_DS110022\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.429Z\tZooplankton analysis\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303232\tData is held as Project No. 41, media 94 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 94) Fireproof safe - Stack 03, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tPlankton samples were collected from during a sandbank survey at nine sites in four Candidate Special Areas of Conservation (cSAC) using a plankton net positioned above a beam trawl. The zooplankton was analysed my Emu Ltd and found to contain common Irish Sea species.   Classification analysis revealed that four of the sites (Red Wharf Bay Four Fathom Helwick and Cardigan) each supported distinct communities based on their faunal composition and the distinguishing features of each were identified. Samples from the remaining sites did not fall into site-specific groups indicating that the fauna in these areas was less distinct.   Data used to sort and identify the planktonic animals from samples taken as part of the sandbank survey in four SACs along the Welsh coastline.\tPublic record\n18867\tNRW_DS124695\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.406Z\tAnchoring, mooring and launching spatial distribution in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363935\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Anchoring Mooring and Launching\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tAnchoring mooring and launching of recreational vessel has the potential to damage benthic marine habitats. The impact and significance of these activities depend on a number of factors including the intensity of the activity and the sensitivity of underlying habitats. Defining the specific locations in which these activities occur is integral to determining the potential for interactions with sensitive features. The data collection has been commissioned under the Wales non-licensable activities project and provides a robust dataset from a wide range of sources on the location of anchoring mooring and launching sites in Wales from the high-water mark to 12nm offshore.  The collated data was sent to a range of knowledgeable marine stakeholders for consultation to ensure that the polygons accurately represent the anchoring mooring and launching areas and that no additional areas had been missed.  A range of intensity metrics were used to allocate a value of intensity to each polygon for anchoring mooring and launching.  This includes extracting values from AIS data from the Royal Yachting Association proximity to sailing clubs and centres and consultation with stakeholders.   The dataset comprises 4 GIS layers; • Anchoring • Mooring • Launching • No-anchoring\tPublic record\n18470\tNRW_DS119295\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.356Z\tWater Pipelines on NRW Land\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323935\tX:\\Utilities\\Water\\Water Pipelines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains water pipelines on NRW land. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured water pipeline information\tPublic record\n18995\tNRW_DS124949\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.106Z\tClocaenog Forest Electrical Marker Posts\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393439\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Electrical Marker Post.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm (as built) electrical marker post locations. This shows all 382 electrical marker posts on the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm. Electrical marker posts are located along the line of the 33kV cables for operational health and safety ground truthing. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPrivate record\n17450\tNRW_DS87120\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.420Z\tNon Native Species Data - Sargassum muticum around Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313230\t[A]  Data is archived under Project No. 151, media no\u0027s. 230 and 485 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\230 and K:\\Archive\\Media 401to500\\Media 485) [B]  Species Data is held in Marine Recorder: 1998-ongoing CCW Sargassum muticum records for Wales MRCCW30000000047\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to collate and monitor the spread of non-native species in Wales. This survey contains a series of records of the non-native marine alga Sargassum muticum around Wales. Many of the records were obtained during the intertidal phase 1 survey while others are anecdotal records from both Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff and various other recorders surveying the shoreline for both work or recreationally. The dataset has been fully validated.  All grid references have been cross-referenced against site name.  The majority of records were recorded by specialists.  The data was collated in order to monitor the spread of non-native species in Wales.\tPublic record\n17709\tNRW_DS110217\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.588Z\tPolycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon composition of herbage samples following the Sea Empress oil spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323137\tArchived as project no 98 (media no 373) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media373\t\tDataset contains the results of an investigation into the Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon (PAH) composition of grass collected from the South Pembrokeshire coast following the Sea Empress oil spill. Data includes site location and grid references herbage type PAH concentrations and n-alkane content. Purpose of this data capture was to determine the concentration of PAH and aliphatic hydrocarbon in herbage samples following the oil spill and detect any patterns/trends.\tPublic record\n18963\tNRW_DS124859\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.221Z\tMonitoring the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae at Dale \u0026amp; South Marloes Coast SSSI in Pembrokeshire in 2020 and a survey for further populations on nearby beaches\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383539\tData held on the WID in Recorded 6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae is found in Wales only within Dale \u0026amp; South Marloes Coast SSSI where it was first recorded in 1999 and again surveyed in 2006. The 2020 survey found that suitable habitat exists along approximately just 262 metres at Marloes Sands and 40 metres at West Dale Bay. A total of 287 adults (male and female) and nymphs of Scaly Cricket was recorded in 2020 indicating that breeding populations exist at both Marloes Sands and West Dale Bay. However Scaly Cricket was found at just four monitoring plots at Marloes Sands and suitable habitat was present in just six plots.\tPublic record\n18126\tNRW_DS115679\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.466Z\tInsect Survey Results for Two Brown Field Sand Quarry Sites, Borras Quarry and Marford Quarry, in North-East Wales (2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363739\t[A]  Recorder 6 database.\t\tThe dataset contains the results from insect surveys conducted on two sand quarries in north-east Wales Borras Quarry (162 species recorded) and Marford Quarry (145 species recorded). The data collected from Borras Quarry includes 14 psammophilic 70 aculeate species. The data collected from Marford quarry includes 8 psammophilic and 69 aculeate species. The aim of this data collection was to identify whether the sand quarries supported suitable conditions for invertebrate species in order to make recommendations for securing long-term sympathetic management of key sites. The diversity of aculeate species recorded from both quarries to date (135 species in total) ranks these areas as the most species-rich sites in Wales.\tPublic record\n17086\tEXT_DS114814\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.565Z\tLuftwaffe Aerial Photographs for Anglesey (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383134\tPrints are held in the aerial photo archive in North Region (Mike Willis room).   Negatives are held in the samples fridge in MYF Labroratory.   A crude CCW produced flight diagram is available on MapInfo.\tGIS Service Desk Sally Ellis, Conservation Officer, North West Wales Sites Team, NRW\tA copy of prints and negatives of the Luftwaffe aerial photography of Anglesey.  Flight diagrams are also available as negatives. The purpose of this data capture was to covert aerial photography of the UK before the outbreak of World War 2 (WW2).  Photos are available for areas that were considered possible military targets. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) acquired a copy of the photos as reference to changes in the environment and to support conservation aims.\tPublic record\n18041\tNRW_DS115268\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.870Z\tDistribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activity Project - Revised Fishing Atlas Activity Layers (Task 2)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323638\tAssociated data is held within the Marine Archive as; Project 376, Media 1396\tData Custodian - Senior Fisheries Advisor, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tThe objective of the associated project was to summarise how the fisheries habitats developed during the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) habitat sensitivity project intersected with Special Area of Conservation (SACs) SAC features and fishing activity. This sets the fisheries sensitivity work in the context of the Habitats Directive. As part of the project the Fishing Atlas activity layers were processed by the toolkit to remove areas where the activity was unlikely to occur due to the habitats present.  The outputs were to be used by CCW to determine whether sensitive habitats were subject to fishing activity.\tPublic record\n18127\tNRW_DS115635\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.710Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Mawddach catchment area (2006)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363335\tAssociated data is held at Maes y Ffynnon at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\32 Current \u0026amp; Pot dist, condition of the otter in the Afon Mawddach catchment\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Afon Mawwdach catchment forms part of two Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) the Afon Eden SAC and the Pen Llyn a Sarnau SAC. Current and potential distribution and breeding success of the otter were investigated as well as identifying and mapping potential and actual breeding habitat and feeding ponds and ditches. A major focus of this project is an assessment of the current and potential breeding success of the otter within the catchment so that conservation objectives can be set and future trends can be identified.\tPublic record\n18263\tNRW_DS116280\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.059Z\tNational Receptors Database (NRD) 2014\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323830\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2014.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors primarily intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management. The NRD includes property points that are Copy Derived from Ordnance Survey (OS) AddressBase Premium with information on property type floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centres Multi-Coloured Manual codes attributed.  It is designed as the consequence information (particular properties) for risk assessments with flood hazards. The version of the NRD described here is the NRD2014. This version of the NRD supersedes NRD2011 which was released in November 2011.\tPublic record\n18526\tNRW_DS121676\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.537Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Catchment Woodland Planting\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363736\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Catchment Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: This map shows the opportunity space for planting catchment woodland for NFM benefits. It is important to note that most effective use of these opportunity spaces would be to plant deep-rooting species particularly broadleaved species in order to fully realise the NFM potential.\tPublic record\n17181\tEXT_DS121968\t2024-05-28T12:15:58.964Z\tWelsh Government: Conservation Zones (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393638\tX:\\Designations\\Welsh Government\\Conservation Zones.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tBoundaries of Conservation Areas in Wales to conform with s.70(5) of the Planning (Listed Buildings and Conservation Areas) Act 1990.  This dataset provides information on the map boundaries only. For the full information including information on designations individuals should contact the appropriate Local Authority. This dataset is owned and managed by Welsh Government and supplied to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under an Open Government Licence (OGL).\tPublic record\n17193\tEXT_DS121876\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.229Z\tForestry Commission England (FCE): Forest Roads (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383736\tx:\\Transport\\Forest Roads\\FCE Forest Roads.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a Forestry Commission England (FCE) spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of forest roads in England. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this data via a OGL.\tPublic record\n19139\tNRW_DS125517\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.379Z\tA survey of hedges and their woody species in Ceredigion 1974-1979\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353137\tCCW/WRAB/026\tData Services Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk Data Services Team\tA survey of the woody species and physical attributes of 2223 30 m sample lenths of 752 hedges in Ceredigion took plce between 1974-1979. Most hedges ocurred on banks. Lane hedges were in the best condition; the hedge conditions and proportion managed declined significantly with altitude. As each hedge in this survey has been mapped there is the potential for the survey to be repeated in the future.\tPrivate record\n18677\tNRW_DS123328\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.376Z\tEPS IROPI Licences\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333238\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\EPS IROPI Licences\\EPS IROPI Licences All.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\EPS IROPI Licences\\EPS IROPI Licences Bats.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\EPS IROPI Licences\\EPS IROPI Licences Dormouse.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\EPS IROPI Licences\\EPS IROPI Licences Great crested newt.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\EPS IROPI Licences\\EPS IROPI Licences Natterjack toad.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\EPS IROPI Licences\\EPS IROPI Licences Otter.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\EPS IROPI Licences\\EPS IROPI Licences Sand lizard.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Iwan G. Hughes, Team Leader Species \u0026amp; Carbon Permitting\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18350\tNRW_DS116473\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.349Z\tMarine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Habitats in Wales (archived 2018)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343733\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived  Consists of 37 datasets\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis archived dataset consists of 37 datasets and has been superseded by \u0027Environment (Wales) Act Section 7: Marine Habitats\u0027 as part of the newer legislative requirements.   This dataset is associated with the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) which was published back in 1994 and was the UK Government\u0027s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006 a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027 is required.   The NERC Act in Wales is being superseded by the Environment Act (Wales) 2016. This dataset will provided a basis on which to create a biodiversity list to meet the requirements of the Environment Act (Wales) set out under Section 6 and 7 which require biodiversity and resilience of ecosystem duty and biodiversity lists and duty to take steps to maintain and enhance biodiversity are required respectively. This includes the publishing of a list of types of habitats of principal importance in Wales for the purpose of maintaining and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This dataset captured will contribute towards the identification of marine habitats of principal importance in Wales and will be kept under review in order to keep the list up-to-date as per Section 7 of the Environment Act (Wales).   These habitats include: blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Native oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh Sabellaria spinulosa (Ross worm) reefs Seapens and burrowing megafauna communities and Subtidal sands and gravels.\tPrivate record\n17951\tNRW_DS114632\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.445Z\tBeach Sediment Particle Size for 10 Welsh Beaches (Collected 2010/2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363332\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 319, Media No. 1267 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1267) HQ library and electronic version held in DMS.\tInternal Custodian: Emmer Litt, Specialist Advisor - Marine and Coastal Physical Processes, All Wales Advice Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tBeaches in Wales are important for several reasons including as a major \u0027attractor\u0027 of relevance to the tourist industry and local regional and national economies. The Welsh Coast is vitally important to the tourism industry in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to provide details of the types and properties of sediment on Welsh beaches to inform the Beach Nourishment in Wales Phase 2 project. 10 beaches were chosen during the Beach Nourishment in Wales Pilot Study project which included both sand and mixed sand/shingle systems located around the coasts of North West and South Wales and which reflect varying relative importance of touristic / recreational flood and erosion risk management and nature conservation interests.\tPublic record\n17710\tNRW_DS110210\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.571Z\tEffectiveness of the rehabilitation of oiled birds with reference to the Sea Empress oil spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323130\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media073 Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Patrick Lindley or Matty Murphy, Marine Ornithologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis datasets comprises of the results from a review of the rescue and rehabilitation of oiled birds following the Sea Empress oil spill. This data was used in the evaluation of the effectiveness of rehabilitation efforts and suggested management procedures.\tPublic record\n17676\tNRW_DS110100\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.409Z\tOrigins, population structure, pathology and diet samples of diver and auk casualties of the Sea Empress oil spill\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313030\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media055 Data is stored within the report. Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset provides data on the examination of dead oiled seabirds from southwest Wales collected after the February 1996 Sea Empress oil spill in order to assess impact and damage done by the spill. Data was collected in order to determine origins pathology and diet of casualties (seabirds affected by the Sea Empress oil spill).\tPublic record\n17992\tNRW_DS114730\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.877Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Intertidal Mudflats (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373330\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Intertidal Mudflats.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to intertidal mudflats only. Mudflats are sedimentary intertidal habitats created by deposition in low energy coastal environments particularly estuaries and other sheltered areas. Their sediment consists mostly of silts and clays with a high organic content. Towards the mouths of estuaries where salinity and wave energy are higher the proportion of sand increases. Mudflats are intimately linked by physical processes to and may be dependent on other coastal habitats such as soft cliffs and saltmarshes. They commonly appear in the natural sequence of habitats between subtidal channels and vegetated saltmarshes. In large estuaries they may be several kilometres wide and commonly form the largest part of the intertidal area of estuaries. However in many places they have been much reduced by land claim. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n17987\tNRW_DS114732\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.648Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Maerl (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373332\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Maerl Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to maerl only. Maerl is a collective term for several species of red seaweed with hard chalky skeletons. It is rock hard and unlike other seaweeds it grows as unattached rounded nodules or short branched shapes on the seabed. Maerl beds are an important habitat for many different types of marine life which live amongst or are attached to the surface of Maerl or burrow in the coarse gravel of dead maerl beneath the top living layer. Maerl beds can be of importance to sustainable fisheries providing nursery grounds for commercial species of fish and shellfish. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n18822\tNRW_DS123582\t2024-05-28T12:56:22.028Z\tMarine Habitat Vulnerability\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353832\tx:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat vulnerability.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Abrasion and physical disturbance.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Decrease in emergence regime.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Decrease in suspended sediment.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Decrease in turbidity.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Decrease in water flow rate.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Decrease in wave exposure.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Increase in emergence regime.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Increase in suspended sediment.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Increase in turbidity.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Increase in water flow rate.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Increase in wave exposure.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Overall Biotope Vulnerability V2.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Overall Biotope Vulnerability.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Smothering.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Substratum loss.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Abrasion and physical disturbance.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Decrease in Emergence Regime.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Decrease in Suspended Sediment.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Decrease in Turbidity.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Decrease in Water Flow Rate.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Decrease in Wave Exposure.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Increase in Emergence Regime.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Increase in Suspended Sediment.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Increase in Turbidity.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Increase in Water Flow Rate.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Increase in Wave Exposure.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Overall Biotope Vulnerability V2.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Overall Biotope Vulnerability.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Smothering.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Substratum Loss.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP3\\EP3 Abrasion and Physical Disturbance.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP3\\EP3 Decrease in Emergence Regime.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP3\\EP3 Decrease in Suspended Sediment.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP3\\EP3 Decrease in Turbidity.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnera\tPaul Brazier\t\tPrivate record\n19076\tNRW_DS125375\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.253Z\tOcean Literacy Survey of Wales 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333735\t\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Kathryn Hughes, Senior Advisor: Marine Area Statement   Metadata Point of Contact: Kathryn Hughes\tThis data provides the evidence for the report “Survey on Ocean Literacy in Wales”. The survey was commissioned as part of the project “Understanding Ocean Literacy and Ocean Climate-related Behaviour Change in the UK” by Defra in collaboration with the Ocean Conservation Trust Marine Scotland and Natural Resources Wales. The main aim of the survey was to better understand the extent and current level of Ocean Literacy in the England and Wales. The survey defines ‘Ocean Literacy’ as an understanding of the ocean’s influence on a person and their influence on the ocean. The survey explored different dimensions of ‘Ocean Literacy’: including information on public awareness knowledge attitudes communication activism and behaviours related to the marine environment. The survey also explored barriers to promoting Ocean Literacy in the population.\tPrivate record\n17990\tNRW_DS114733\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.750Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Horse Mussel (Modiolus modiolus) Beds (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373333\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Horse Mussel Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to horse mussel Modiolus modiolus only and was originally collated to assist in the identification of Highly Protected Marine Conservation Zones (HPMCZ\u0027s) in Wales however it was recognized that the data would have wide uses for conservation in Wales and therefore information about the habitats was as encompassing as possible. The horse mussel Modiolus modiolus forms dense beds at depths of 5-70 m in fully saline often moderately tide-swept areas off northern and western parts of the British Isles. M. modiolus can occur as relatively small dense beds of epifaunal mussels carpeting steep rocky surfaces but is more frequently recessed at least partly into mixed or muddy sediments in a variety of tidal regimes. Extensive expanses of seabed are covered in scattered clumps of semi-recessed M. modiolus on muddy gravels.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n19080\tNRW_DS125377\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.183Z\tPembrokeshire Rare Plant Register proformas (Paper Records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333737\tOff site storage CCW/WRPB/33  Some info to Julian Woodman?\tJulian Woodman, off-site storage reference number CCW/WRPB/33\tCopies of handwritten Rare Plant Register Index proformas information includes species name and locality coverage associates and conservation status.  Although all original information supplied to BSBI to collate CCW and subsequently NRW retained a copy of the paper proformas for internal use.  Information was collected by NRW staff and therefore is not classed as third party data.\tPrivate record\n17693\tNRW_DS110068\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.553Z\tRiver Dee Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Comparative Macrophyte Surveys (2008)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303638\tThe report and data collected are archived as Project 211 as Media 576 and are stored in fireproof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Ffynnon. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.\tInternal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group. NRw  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned ECUS Ltd. to undertake macrophyte survey work on the River Dee Special Area of Conservation (SAC) to inform a larger project aiming to determine the main variability components for macrophyte communities in rivers. The objective of the larger project a partnership between CCW Natural England the Environment Agency (EA) Scottish Environmental Protection Agency (SEPA) and Scotland and Northern Ireland Forum For Environmental Research (SNIFFER) is to develop and test a statistically robust standardised field sampling method for river macrophytes to be used by the agencies for assessment of ecological and conservation status of rivers.\tPublic record\n17521\tNRW_DS101782\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.140Z\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373832\tSpecies data stored in Recorder 6 all surveys are tagged as being Phase 2 Woodland Survey. a)  Woodland Ecologist\u0027s G:drive b)  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\EIT\\Staff\\Cyprian Payne\\2009\\P2only.zipc)  M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\phase_2\\Woodland   c)  Spatial data available to internal NRW staff on the corporate spatial data store.  GIS layers are stored on the X Drive: X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland d) copy of the scanned paper maps are available on legacy CCW M drive at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Woodland\\Raster Data  Species data is also stored in Recorder 6 under the Phase 2 Woodland Survey Tag. This data is now available on the NBN Atlas The species part of the dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe primary aim of the project was to complete woodland community descriptions of all Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSls) in Wales that have semi-natural woodland as a qualifying feature. There was also a need for some survey of woodland on SSSls where woodland is not the outright qualifying feature and some areas of non-SSSI woodland (primarily pSSSI and areas of woodland adjacent to existing SSSls). Sites and areas of woodland to be included in the survey were selected by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). National Vegetation Classification (NVC) field survey methodology followed procedures.    This dataset contains detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 800 woodlands of conservation interest in Wales predominantly SSSI and pSSSI woodlands.   The purpose of this data capture was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites. Phase 2 woodland surveys have been carried out periodically since the late 1980s but a concentrated period of survey on SSSI and pSSSI woodland occurred from 1995 and 2000.  Data was collected by trained personnel digitised by contractors validated by CCW Knowledge Management Group corrections made by contractors validated again by CCW Knowledge Management Group corrections made in-house in conjunction with Woodland Ecologist.\tPublic record\n17623\tNRW_DS109831\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.232Z\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383331\tArchived at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Phase1_Database\\Phase1Acc2k.mdb.   The database is available through the intranet.   Data is also archived as project 204, media 569 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to500\\Media 569 Also under construction on NRW intranet: https://apex.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.internal/apex/f?p\u003d116:1::::::  (see online resource)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis is a database of Marine intertidal Phase 1 survey data which can be queried to provide information on biotope species distribution site details uses and impacts target notes and conservation interests. Following the completion of the intertidal project in 2007 the database was transferred to Apex software to be made available internally on the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) intranet. The intertidal phase 1 survey was undertaken to provide an integrated information resource for Local Authority structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications. The data collected is also used to provide regional information about habitat and biotope distribution assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs) a basis of sensitivity mapping and in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIAs).    The database itself aims to provide a storage system to effectively store the survey results whilst providing a convenient way to view and manipulate the data collected. Data validated to ensure it\u0027s entered onto the database correctly. The database has been designed to keep data entry errors to a minimum e.g. majority of data entry are taken from drop down menus percentage values must add up to 100% many fields are mandatory and duplicate entries are not allowed.   Each record in the database is checked against the data collection form by the data imputer and by a member of the survey team. A further validation is carried out when the data is cross checked with the digital map to ensure that the database and maps agree.\tPublic record\n17997\tNRW_DS114724\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.072Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Carbonate Reefs (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373234\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Carbonate Reefs.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working. Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tCustodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Spatial Data and GIS Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.  The data is related to carbonate reefs only. The reef structure that was identified is known as \u0027Holden\u0027s Reef\u0027 – it is composed of a particular type of calcium carbonate formed by methane gas leaking from the seabed. This and the associated reefs are the first known example of this habitat in UK inshore waters.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n17994\tNRW_DS114727\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.023Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Estuarine Rock (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373237\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC Estuarine Rock Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC Estuarine Rock Polygon.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Data Services Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to estuarine rock only. Estuaries are usually soft muddy places so rock and stable boulders in estuaries are rare and occur mostly in drowned river valleys and fjords.  They form a small proportion of estuarine seascapes but are important because they contribute a lot to the richness of life within estuaries.  The rich and sheltered waters of estuaries provide nursery grounds for fish and the rocky areas are an important part of these. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n19967\tNRW_DS161323\t2024-12-23T14:25:17.508Z\tLlyn Horse Mussel Multibeam Echosounder Survey data 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333233\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine acoustic bathymetric data\\Multibeam\\Modiolus 2024\\H23034_Hydrofix_2024_Deliverables\tHarriet Lincoln, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Projects Team\tThis data is from the 2024 Llyn Horse Mussel (Modiolus modiolus) Multibeam Echosounder Survey (MBES).   The work was carried out by Binnies/Hydrofix funded by Nature Networks as part of an investigation into the decline observed at the reef. Both reefs were mapped to International Hydrographic order (IHO) 1a or higher.   The data was processed and analysed to produce bathymetric surfaces; backscatter mosaics; a seabed classification including the extents of the mussel beds; and measurements of the mussel bioherms.  The results from the 2024 survey were then compared to previous datasets provided by NRW from 2005 2008 and 2015 all of which were analysed and reprocessed.\tPublic record\n17925\tNRW_DS113460\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.071Z\t2008 CCW / ASML Carmarthen Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Cockle Bed Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133343630\tThe data are archived as Project No. 199, Media No. 549 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 549) whilst the report is held within Media No. 1218 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201-1300\\Media 1218.This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Fynnon. [A]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2008 ASML Carm [B]  Stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon on optical media.  [C]  GIS data Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb [D] Marine Recorder file (survey number MRCCW16300000012)\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Internal Metadata point of contact - Master Reference Data Team  or Data Services\tThe Habitats Directive requires that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II.  For SACs in Wales the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  To do this the former CCW developed a programme of feature condition monitoring.  Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd. (ASML) have been contracted by CCW to develop and manage the monitoring programme for the intertidal features in marine SACs for the period 2006 to 2011; working as a team with CCW HQ and CCW Regional staff.  In Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Large shallow inlets and bays estuaries and Mudflats and sandflats not covered by sea water at low tide.  Specific areas of interest include the extensive sediment flats of the 3 rivers (Taf Tywi and Gwendraeth) Loughor estuary (Burry inlet) open coast sands and notable rocky habitats particularly in west Carmarthen Bay.  This report and dataset contains the results of the surveys carried out in those areas in June 2007.   The forms part of the monitoring that CCW also developed on intertidal monitoring across Wales during 2004 and 2005.  These surveys were managed and implemented for CCW by the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS University of Hull).  These projects focused on a wide range of sensitive habitats such as Zostera muddy gravels caves rockpools algal-dominated rocky shores Sabellaria reefs under-boulders and various rare habitats and species.  In Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC IECS surveyed Ruppia maritima (Boyes 2008a) Zostera noltii beds (Mazik \u0026amp; Boyes 2009) the biotope SLR.FserX.T (Boyes 2008b) the polychaete Ophelia bicornis (Mazik 2008) hydroid dominated rockpools (Boyes et al. 2004) saltmarsh boundary (Boyes \u0026amp; Brazier 2008) and the sediment flats across the SAC (Allen et al. 2008).\tPublic record\n17151\tEXT_DS119298\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.838Z\tOffice of National Statistics (ONS): Built Up Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323938\tX:\\Social Economic\\Office of National Statistics\\ONS Built up areas.lyr\tCustodian: GIS Service Desk Business contact: NRW statistician\tThis dataset is owned by the Office of National Statistics (ONS). Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to the Welsh extent (plus some border areas) for this dataset. This spatial data provides information on the villages towns and cities where people live and allows comparisons between people living in built-up areas and those living elsewhere. Census data for these areas (previously called urban areas) has been produced every 10 years since 1981.\tPublic record\n19081\tNRW_DS125376\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.239Z\tClwyd Woodland Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333736\toff-site storage box ref: CCW/NRW/R51 \u0026amp;52\t\tClwyd woodlland survey of 1981 and preliminary survey of 1979\tPrivate record\n19284\tNRW_DS161274\t2024-07-05T07:56:03.299Z\tTranquillity and Place Wales Sound Environment Part 2 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323734\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Indicator T4-2-A Low Flying Military Aircraft.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Road \u0026amp; Rail.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Combined.lyr\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape\tThe nationally consistent terrestrial Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource identifies the strategic and local resource in remote rural peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent practice and provision for well-being benefits. There are 6 themes.   Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Part II delivers Theme 4 where natural sounds may be expected to be more prominent than noise and are appropriate to context building upon Part I to incorporate Welsh Government Strategic Noise Mapping for Wales 2022 Road and Rail.\tPublic record\n18954\tNRW_DS124883\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.563Z\tCefn Croes Windfarm - BT Fibre Optic Cable\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383833\t*X:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\BT Fibre Optic Cable.lyr\t\tThese shapefiles relate to the Cefn Croes Windfarm BT Fibre Optic Cable within Welsh Government Woodland Estate This area forms part of the existing data held for ‘UG Telephone Lines on NRW Land’ – within the Utilities layer of MyMap\tPrivate record\n18883\tNRW_DS124689\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.530Z\tCaRR 2019 - Wales CaRR Town Areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363839\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2019.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis). Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThis dataset may have been superseded by the dataset know as Communities at Risk Register (Metadata record NRW_DS124687).  A review of the Communities at Risk Register data is due in 2025.\tPrivate record\n19041\tNRW_DS125353\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.288Z\tDenbigh Grassland survey 1979/1980\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333533\toff-site storage, ref: CCW/NRM/R-53\tData Services Team\tThis is a grassland survey of Denbigh area in paper format undertaken during 1979 and 1980\tPrivate record\n18653\tNRW_DS123293\t2024-05-28T12:53:50.682Z\tWales C town areas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323933\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2017.lyr\tMark Pugh\t\tPrivate record\n18652\tNRW_DS123291\t2024-05-28T12:53:50.692Z\tReservoir Depths\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323931\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Depths.lyr\tMatthew O\u0027 Brien, Senior Specialist Advisor, Reservoir Safety Group, National Sevices - Reservoir Safety\t\tPrivate record\n19490\tNRW_DS161292\t2024-09-03T10:01:29.031Z\tUrban Gull Surveys 2021\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323932\tStored on the DMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mneb/Liaison%20%20SMP/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTc3MjYuMjAyMDYiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D\u0026amp;CID\u003d6ae24ca1%2D20b6%2D0000%2D1b3d%2Db9a684ddd4c9\u0026amp;cidOR\u003dSPO\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FMarine%2DEvidence%2Fmneb%2FLiaison%20%20SMP%2FUK%20Wide%20Seabird%20Census%20%2D%20Urban%20Gulls\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120005ECAE68515E2714E8923C04099DEA67D\tMatthew Murphy: Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Species\tFollowing evidence of widespread decline in the productivity of Herring Gulls and less black backed gulls this survey compromises of a ground based survey and aerial survey of gulls to estimate the number of black-back  and herring gulls breeding in urban environments in Wales.\tPublic record\n18973\tNRW_DS124876\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.507Z\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Underground Cable 33kV\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383736\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\33KV Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThese shapefiles relate to Cefn Croes Windfarm Underground Cables – 33kV This area forms part of the existing data held for ‘Electricity Powerlines on NRW Land’ – Legend: ‘Underground’ within the Utilities layer of MyMap.\tPrivate record\n17649\tNRW_DS110021\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.375Z\tBaseline Survey of Natura 2000 Standing Water Habitats in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303231\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media201to300\\Media205 Fireproof safe - Stack 06, Hafod Elyfn, NRW Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Senior Freshwater Ecologist, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Environment Agency coordinated macrophyte and physico-chemical surveys on over 50 lake sites in England and Wales during 2003 including a number of lake sites within Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs). In an effort to update existing information on the site condition of these lakes and provide additional information on other Welsh lakes in SACs former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned surveys on a further 19 lake sites in 2004. This dataset brings together data collected in 2003 and 2004 from 28 Welsh lakes in 11 SACs.  Limnological data collected for the purpose of site condition assessment and to provide a series of lake data reports on each of the 11 specified SACs.\tPublic record\n17662\tNRW_DS109887\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.397Z\tSaltmarsh Features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2003\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383837\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media216 Archived as project 39 media number 216\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau.  The purpose of the data capture was to map the location and extent of saltmarsh and associated vegetation types to classify the stands in relation to the National Vegetation Classification to apprise the quality of the vegetation and to produce information relevant to the completion of a conservation assessment of the constituent sites.\tPublic record\n18160\tNRW_DS115876\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.723Z\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mosaic Layer Voronoi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383736\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Mosnotes voronoi.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Mospolys voronoi.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\allwales_ph1 \\phase1_mosaic.tab\tInternal Custodian: Terrestrial mapping Scientist Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is a GIS browser table that contains information on the habitats present and their proportions and areas in mosaic polygons. This method of dealing with mosaics is based on the approach used for mosaics in Phase 2 vegetation survey GIS datasets.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales 1km square dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage and the maps were digitised as part of the project to map Open Access land.  The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales. Voronoi was carried out to enable full representation and visualisation of the components of complex mixed-habitat upland polygons and the mosaic browser lists habitats in mosaic polygons and gives proportions and area data.   Habitat mixtures were treated as equal splits e.g. D.1.1 and E.2.1 in the string \u0027D.1.1 + E.2.1\u0027 were allocated proportions 1 : 1 and mosaics were treated as subsidiary divisions e.g. the components of the string \u0027D.1.1 + D.2/E.2.1\u0027 were allocated proportions 2 : 1 : 1. Habitats in brackets were allocated proportions of 2 percent. The Phase 2 Tools were used to create a mospolys (mosaic polygons) layer to which the Phase 1 legend can be applied for better visualisation in GIS.\tPublic record\n17657\tNRW_DS109889\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.074Z\tSaltmarsh Features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) - Dyfi 1993\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383839\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Dyfi 1993 H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media901to1000\\Media903 Archived as project 250 media 903\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau - Dyfi 1993. Dyfi Junction saltmarsh survey. The main objective of the survey was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map the unusual brackish water saltmarsh communities as well as characterising some of the transitional vegetation types and the glycophytic communities present.\tPublic record\n18988\tNRW_DS124950\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.723Z\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Site Boundary\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393530\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Development Boundary.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm Construction Site Boundary. The boundary that was granted by the Local Planning Authority and was used as the Construction Design Management Regulations 2015 (CDM 2015) area. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n17993\tNRW_DS114729\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.862Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Honeycomb Worm (Sabellaria) Reefs (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373239\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Sabellaria Alveolata Reef.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive). Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Honeycomb worm (Sabellaria) reefs only. Sabellaria spinulosa reefs comprise of dense subtidal aggregations of this small tube-building polychaete worm. Sabellaria spinulosa can act to stabilise cobble pebble and gravel habitats providing a consolidated habitat for epibenthic species. They are solid (albeit fragile) massive structures at least several centimetres thick raised above the surrounding seabed and persisting for many years. As such they provide a biogenic habitat that allows many other associated species to become established. The S. spinulosa reef habitats of greatest nature conservation significance are those which occur on predominantly sediment or mixed sediment areas. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n17995\tNRW_DS114728\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.017Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Fragile Sponge and Anthozoan (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373238\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Fragile Sponges Anthozoan.lyr    x:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Fragile Sponges Biotopes.lyr    Data is archived as project 282, media 1098 Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.   Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (X drive).  .\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to fragile sponge and anthozoan only. Anthozoans are a group of soft animals with feathery tentacles which includes soft corals sea-fans cup corals and anemones.  Members of this group can be found together with branching sponges on steeply sloping bedrock or large boulders in depths from about 50m to just below low tide. These sponge and anthozoan communities require sufficient water movement to bring a ready supply of food but not too much as they are vulnerable to breakage.  As a result they are found on shores which are exposed to moderate or strong waves and possibly quite strong tidal currents but only in areas of those shores where there is some local shelter from the full force of the waves and tides.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n17153\tEXT_DS121606\t2024-05-28T12:14:11.076Z\tNational Trust Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363036\tVarious Locations (see specific metadata for further details) Spatial Data is held on the X Drive.\tJoe Bloggs\tIn Natural Resources Wales we obtain a variety of data from the National Trust. These relate to a wide variety of topics such as coastal management land and property ownership and species data.\tPrivate record\n17988\tNRW_DS114741\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.604Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Subtidal Mixed Muddy Sediments (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373431\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Subtidal Mixed Muddy Sediment WGS84.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to subtidal mixed sediments only. Subtidal mixed seabeds can have a range of different types of sediment from muddy gravely sands to mosaics of cobbles and pebbles in or on a sand gravel or mud seabed. Mixed areas also include seabeds where waves or ribbons of sand form on the surface of a gravel bed.  They may support a wide range of animals both on and in the sediment.  Animals found here include worms bivalves (with their paired hinged shells) starfish and urchins anemones sea firs and sea mats.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n17991\tNRW_DS114740\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.876Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Sheltered Muddy Gravel (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373430\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Sheltered Muddy Gravels.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working. Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Sheltered Muddy Gravel only. Muddy gravels occur mainly in estuaries drowned river valleys and sea lochs in areas protected from wave action and strong tidal streams. They can be found both on the shore and in the shallows.  Which plants and animals occupy the gravels depends on the composition of the seabed and the influence of freshwater. Where the seawater is undiluted muddy gravel seascapes tend to be dominated by worms such as delicate peacock worms with their fan of feathery tentacles which is used for trapping food from the passing seawater.  Burrowing anemones are found in muddy gravels as are burrowing bivalves (with their paired hinged shells) including ridged carpet shells and blunt gapers. Coarse gravel and stones on surface of the muddy gravel provide a hard substrate for seaweeds to attach to.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n17658\tNRW_DS109888\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.170Z\tSaltmarsh Features of the Conwy Estuary 2001\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383838\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Conwy 2001 H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media532 Archived as project 189 media 532\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the results from a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey used as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the North Conwy Estuary.  The principal objective of the survey was to provide a suitable base for future monitoring and also as part of CCW\u0027s commitment to reporting on the suitability of the estuary description as a Site of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI) and inclusion into the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay possible Special Area of Conservation (pSAC).\tPublic record\n17981\tNRW_DS114742\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.162Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Subtidal Sand and Gravels (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373432\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\Marine Bap Habitats Subtidal Sands and Gravels BGS.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Subtidal sand and gravels only. Sand and gravel seabeds are widespread.  Those found to the west of the UK include a high proportion of shell fragments whereas those from the North Sea are mainly formed from rock.  They occur in a range of environmental conditions from wave-sheltered enclosed bays and estuaries to highly exposed open coasts. The mix of sand or gravel and any sand waves or ripples present on the surface of the seabed depend on factors such as the strength of the waves and tides. The communities of animals that live in this habitat are also determined by the environmental conditions. There tend to be fewer animals where waves and tides are strong and the communities there will be characterised by rapid burrowers such as bristleworms tube worms bivalve shells and shrimp-like creatures and perhaps by crabs hermit crabs and sea snails on the surface of the seabed.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n18448\tNRW_DS121238\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.832Z\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Floodplain Reconnection Potential\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323338\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Floodplain Reconnection Potential .lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Floodplain Reconnection Potential is our best estimate of locations where it may be possible to establish reconnection between a watercourse and its natural floodplain especially during high flows. The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas where there is currently poor connectivity such that flood waters are constrained to the channel and flood waves may therefore propagate downstream rapidly.\tPublic record\n18254\tNRW_DS116281\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.972Z\tBetter Woodlands for Wales Case Boundaries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323831\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Better Woods For Wales Boundary.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Better Woods for Wales SCDB.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tBetter Woodlands for Wales (BBW) is the Forestry Commission Wales (FCW) grant scheme designed to meet the Woodland Strategy vision. The vision of the strategy for Wales to be known for its high-quality woodlands that enhance the landscape are appropriate to local conditions and have a diverse mixture of species and habitats. The BBW scheme has been developed in consultation with woodland owners and their agents as well as our partner organisations. As a result the new scheme places greater emphasis on good quality woodland management and offers grants specially designed for Welsh woodlands. Grant aid is now based upon an approved long term management plan. This plan must meet the minimum standards under the UK Woodland Assurance Scheme. BWW was closed to new applications in April 2012.\tPublic record\n18476\tNRW_DS121599\t2024-05-28T12:50:12.804Z\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353939\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\ X:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive.lyr\tMark Squire, Team Leader Water Policy \u0026amp; Directives Implementation (mark.squire@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)       Metadata contact: Mark Squire\t\u0027Urban waste water\u0027 is defined in the Urban Waster Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) as the mixture of domestic waste water from kitchens bathrooms and toilets the waste water from industries discharging to sewers and rainwater run-off from roads and other impermeable surfaces such as roofs pavements and roads draining to sewers. Urban waste water is often referred to as ‘sewage’. The UWWTR  is intended to protect the water environment for the animals and plants that live in and around water as well as for recreational purposes and use as a resource for drinking water sanitation industry and commerce.   Under the UWWTR further sensitive areas have been identified because further treatment than secondary or equivalent treatment is necessary to meet other EC Directives such as the Water Framework Directive. Areas that come under as sensitive areas include Bathing Waters Shellfish Waters and waters that are or soon to be Eutrophic.  This entry refers to a series of spatial datasets that can used in Urban Waste Water management.  In 2023 the dataset was updated and renamed from the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD). Please see the child metadata entries for more information on these updates.\tPublic record\n17188\tEXT_DS121959\t2024-05-28T12:15:59.685Z\tPlantlife International: Broad Soil Types (3rd Party data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393539\tX:\\Biological\\Plant Life\\Plant Life Soils 2km.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tPlantlife International collects and holds a large quantity of data on plants and habitats of conservation importance throughout the UK from a network of individuals and organisations. This data collations is owned by Plantlife International and has been licence to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for use. Dominant soil types have been determined to ascertain the Important Arable Plant Area  (IAPA) score for each tetrad used in the Welsh Important Arable Plant Area (IAPA) Status dataset.\tPublic record\n19056\tNRW_DS125436\t2024-05-28T13:01:42.865Z\tTerrestrial Priority Habitat Surveys\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343336\tX-Layer_Files\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Mapping.lyr  Associated survey reports will be saved in different places on the DMS depending on the location or type of site surveyed\tKathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Habitats\tThis dataset contains Priority Habitat Surveys of individual sites (not Wales-wide coverage) focusing on terrestrial habitats listed under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 known as Priority Habitats. It consists of detailed surveys of plant communities in mixed habitat context at a coarser scale than Phase 2 (National Vegetation Classification) habitat surveys.    The primary objective of these surveys is to identify and map areas of Priority Habitat of nature conservation importance which can then feed into existing Wales-wide Priority Habitat extent and distribution data. The survey data can be used to identify areas of important habitat to inform schemes and projects such as woodland creation schemes developments or agricultural improvement and can inform conservation management and restoration work to improve habitat condition and connectivity and enhance biodiversity.    The dataset is not the product of a co-ordinated Survey Programme like the Lowland Peatland and Lowland Grassland Surveys of Wales but is instead designed to be a single place to store ad-hoc site surveys of mixed Priority Habitat under a common data structure so they can be easily analysed and shared. Only surveys that have been extensively validated and verified by NRW vegetation and cartographic specialists will be added to the dataset.    This dataset is not to be confused with layer files derived from the ‘Environment (Wales) Act Section 7 Terrestrial Habitats of Principal Importance’ dataset which is largely based on the Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey (1987-1997). These layer files are split into Wales-wide coverage for individual habitats for example ‘Priority Habitat Blanket Bog.lyr.\u0027 See metadata NRW_DS122418 for more information.    Habitat composition may change over time for example due to successional trends land management or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type or species in the digital data is therefore no guarantee of it still being extant on the ground.  Individual site reports for most sites surveyed are also available upon request.\tPrivate record\n18557\tNRW_DS122445\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.555Z\tScarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula, River Dee 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343435\tdata should be entered into Recorder 6 - Mike to confirm R6 SurveyID\t\tthis metadata relates to:  Davy-Bowker J. Mauvisseau Q. Hammett M. Sweet M. \u0026amp; Troth C. 2019. A survey of the River Dee for the Scarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula in spring 2018. NRW Evidence Report No. 362  Contact Mike Howe for metadata proforma\tPrivate record\n18002\tNRW_DS114722\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.052Z\tSeabirds at Sea Evidence Base\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373232\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird coverage 3KM WGS84.lyr,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird WWT ESAS merged flying 3KM WGS84.lyr,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird WWT ESAS merged sitting 3KM WGS84.lyr,   The source data used to create the grid cell maps is also stored at: X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird WWT SAS OBS merged WGS84.lyr   however this is not covered by this metadata entry as restrictions may apply and only available internally.    Archived data in original MapInfo format is held within the Marine archive as project 333, media no. 1292\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy Marine Ornithologist, MFEG, NRW Metadata contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tGIS layers showing the abundance and distribution of seabirds in welsh waters.  The datasets consists of the raw data showing observations of all seabirds and derived grids showing the density of flying and sitting species on a 3km grid scale and the survey coverage. The purpose of this data capture was to collate data from ESAS and WWT Consulting databases to produce combined database of birds recorded from surveys of Welsh waters.  Other sources identified were also consulted to produce the most complete representation of  the observed distribution of seabirds.\tPublic record\n17178\tEXT_DS121638\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.668Z\tCefas: Spawning and Nursery Grounds of Selected Fish in UK Waters (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363338\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\CEFAS\\CEFAS Fisheries nursery grounds WGS84.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\CEFAS\\CEFAS Fisheries nursery points WGS84.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\CEFAS\\CEFAS Fisheries spawning grounds WGS84.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\CEFAS\\CEFAS Fisheries spawning points WGS84.lyr\tInternal Contact: Colin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Fisheries, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tThis is a third party spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of spawning and nursery ground of selected fish in UK waters. NRW holds data from Cefas on the spawning and nursery grounds of selected fish in UK waters on a OGL licence.  NB While we have the data set under general licence it is preferable users go to Cefas direct for the dataset.  This dataset was created by Cefas for additional analyses to complement the original maps produced in \u0027Coull K.A. Johnstone R. and S.I. Rogers. 1998. Fisheries Sensitivity Maps in British Waters\u0027 (published and distributed by UKOOA Ltd.) and to: (a)  Provide supporting evidence for the maps of spawning and nursery grounds; (b) Provide some of the more recent data that has become available since the original Fisheries Sensitivity Maps were produced; (c)  Increase the number of species for which maps are available;(d) Plot the spawning areas as rectangles so that the user is more likely to infer that the boundaries of such areas are not fixed delineations between spawning and non-spawning areas; (e) Provide more information on those grounds likely to be of greater importance to the stock(s).\tPublic record\n18180\tNRW_DS116229\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.822Z\tEmissions Inventory\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323239\tZ:\\Archive\\Sampling Monitoring \u0026amp; Analysis Reporting\\Reporting\\Statutory Reporting\\Industrial Emissions\tCustodian: Amy Healey, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting\tThe Emissions Inventory (EI) dataset collects data from regulated industrial activities and includes annual emissions to air and controlled waters off-site transfers of waste and transfers of pollutants to wastewater.   The NRW EI includes all of the data from Wales that is published on the UK Pollutant Release and Transfer Register (UK PRTR) but also includes further emissions that are under the UK PRTR threshold.   The published dataset has been redacted to not include confidential permits.\tPublic record\n19568\tNRW_DS161296\t2024-09-20T10:05:57.369Z\tThe Status and Distribution of Broom-tip Chesias rufata at Morfa Harlech and Harlech Forest in 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database which is stored on the Recorder 6 Database\tDr Michael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA survey for Broom-tip Chesias rufata at Morfa Harlech   The aims of the current survey were to map the distribution of the larval foodplant on Morfa Harlech and Harlech Forest and to assess the distribution of Broom-tip by searching for larvae. This would provide a truer reflection of the exact distribution of the moth rather than recording the more mobile adult stage. Surveys were undertaken from July to September to avoid confusion with larvae of the Streak moth Chesias legatella which have a similar appearance and also feed on Broom in May and June.and Harlech Forest the key locality for the moth in north Wales from July to early September 2023.\tPublic record\n18950\tNRW_DS124900\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.556Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Great Crested Newt\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393030\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Aberfraw Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Aberfraw Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Aberfraw Terrestrial Refuges - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Harlech Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Harlech Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Kenfig Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Kenfig Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Newborough Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Newborough Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Newborough Terrestrial Refuges - Great Crested Newt.lyr\tToni Henwood, Team Member 2, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales Sands of LIFE\tResults of pond surveys to look for Great Crested Newt breeding. Results of habitat assessment for areas of suitable terrestrial habitat for Great Crested Newt\tPrivate record\n19320\tNRW_DS161278\t2024-07-15T14:26:12.215Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macroinvertebrates - Geospatial\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323738\tData Held in KiEco - a FME Report has been created to export this data to the GIS.   X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macro-invertebrates Taxa.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macro-invertebrates Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macro-invertebrates Sample Metric.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Jennifer Owen Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer) Analysis and Reporting\tFreshwater macroinvertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality and river flow requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance.   This dataset contains site sample metric and taxa information from freshwater river macroinvertebrate surveys carried out  across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales since 1978 and onwards. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\tPublic record\n18974\tNRW_DS124858\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.490Z\tDistribution data for spider Mioxena blanda at Newborough Warren – Ynys Llanddwyn (SSSI) 2019-2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383538\tStored on the WID in Recorder 6\tMike Howe\tA survey to improve our understanding of the ecology and distribution of the Nationally Rare money spider Mioxena blanda was conducted at Newborough Warren – Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI. The survey ran between 28th September 2019 and 7th March 2020 employing a variety of survey techniques: terrestrial and aerial pitfall traps vacuum sampling grubbing sieving rock turning and hand sampling.\tPublic record\n17096\tEXT_DS111953\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.432Z\tCadw: Listed Buildings (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131393533\tX:\\Designations\\CADW\\Listed Buildings.lyr   Used to be: Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\historic_landscapes   \u0026gt;\u0026gt;\u0026gt; various MapInfo tables\tGIS Service Desk  John Briggs, Landscape Architect, NRW  For further information please contact Cadw at Plas Carew, Unit 5/7 Cefn Coed, Park Nantgarw, Cardiff, CF15 7QQ, www.cadw.wales.gov.uk.\tNatural Resources of Wales (NRW) are supplied a number of historic and cultural datasets under a licence from Cadw. Cadw is the Welsh Government’s historic environment service working for an accessible and well-protected historic environment for Wales. They were set up with the aim of conserving Wales’s heritage helping people understand and care about their history and helping to sustain the distinctive character of Wales.  Buildings and structures of national importance are given legal protection by being placed on a ‘List’ of Buildings of Special Architectural or Historic Interest. This is a third party spatial dataset containing information on the CADW designated sites and listed buildings in Wales. In Wales there are over 29000 buildings and structures on the list. Listed buildings range from Medieval Churches to modern commercial premises etc structures can include railings gate piers walls war memorials gravestones post boxes and telephone boxes.\tPublic record\n18303\tNRW_DS116445\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\tEnd of life vehicles - Authorised Treatment Facilities - Public Register\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343435\tData is extracted from spread sheets stored in Local Offices\tData Custodian: Lisa McDonald SE Ops / National Waste Audit Team\tPermitted End-of-Life Vehicle (ELV) sites that can meet the required standards in the ELV Directive for de-polluting vehicles. Authorised sites can have access to the DVLA Certificate of Destruction (CoD) scheme.\tPublic record\n17986\tNRW_DS114739\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.596Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Seagrass Beds (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373339\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Seagrass Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to seagrass beds only. Seagrasses (also known for their long thin leaves as eel grass) are grass-like flowering plants with dark green long narrow ribbon-shaped leaves. They are one of the very few groups of flowering plants that live in the sea. Common eel grass is the only species that occurs below low water mark and it can form dense underwater lawns.  It grows mainly on sand but also fine gravel typically down to a depth of 4 m. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n18039\tNRW_DS115270\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.776Z\tDistribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activity Project - Output Layers by Gear Category (Task 4)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323730\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive as Project 376, Media 1396.\tData Custodian: Senior fisheries Advisor, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tThe objective of the associated project was to summarise how the fisheries habitats developed during the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) habitat sensitivity project intersected with Special Area of Conservation (SACs) SAC features and fishing activity. This sets the fisheries sensitivity work in the context of the Habitats Directive. The outputs for Task 4 included one layer per gear category for each SAC feature area outside SAC features and 12 nm zone outside SACs over which the activity occurred. The outputs were to be used by CCW to determine whether sensitive habitats were subject to fishing activity and whether these situations occurred within SACs and SAC features. The data could also be used as a baseline for discussions with fishermen.    As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPublic record\n18864\tNRW_DS124755\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.886Z\tEC Bathing Waters Classification\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373535\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr    Original data is held in WISKI - KwiQM (see metadata entry116227)\tHelena Sykes?\t\tPrivate record\n18930\tNRW_DS124832\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.571Z\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ) Surface Water Points\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383332\tX:\\EU Directives\\Nitrates Directive\\NVZ Surfacewater Points.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n18447\tNRW_DS121241\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.811Z\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323431\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential.lyr X:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\EA Potential Floodplain Woodland.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential is our best estimate of locations where tree planting on the floodplain may be possible and effective to attenuate flooding.   The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas of floodplain not already wooded. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) also has access to some Environment Agency data which we are licenced to have as part of our data exchange agreement.\tPublic record\n18000\tNRW_DS114736\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.145Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Oyster Beds (Ostrea edulis) (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373336\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\OSPAR Oyster Beds Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\OSPAR Oyster Beds Polygon.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Oyster (Ostrea edulis) beds only. Native Oyster (Ostrea edulis) beds occur in shallow water (usually less than about 10 m deep) on fine muddy sand native oysters can be found in large numbers.  Where this happens the oysters themselves and the dead shells amongst them form extensive beds which become home to many other marine creatures including crabs worms sea snails sponges sea urchins and seaweed.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n17989\tNRW_DS114735\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.694Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Musculus discors Habitats (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373335\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Musculus Discors Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Musculus discors habitats only. This biotope typically occurs on the upper faces of moderately exposed moderately tide-swept bedrock boulders and cobbles in slightly silty conditions. The mussel Musculus discors occurs in dense mats and occasionally completely coats all available surfaces.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n17689\tNRW_DS110064\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.523Z\tInitial hydroacoustic mapping of macrophyte communities in the southern end of Llyn Tegid\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303634\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-304 and is archived as Project 196 (Media 542) and is stored in fireproof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Ffynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage. H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media542\tInternal Custodian:  Rhian Thomas, Team Leader WFD, Water Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is made up of the results of a hydroacoustic survey to map the extent of submerged macrophyte communities and major substrate types in the southern end of Llyn Tegid.  The objectives of the present part of the project were to use hydroacoustics to map the current extent of submerged macrophyte communities and major substrate types in the southern end of Llyn Tegid and to present the findings of such work at the Countryside Council for Wales Marine and Freshwater Workshop 2007. This works was undertaken as a small work package added during the summer of 2007 to a much larger project concerned with the hydroacoustic survey and translocation of the gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus).\tPublic record\n17999\tNRW_DS114738\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.128Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Saline Lagoons (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373338\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Saline Lagoons.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Saline Lagoons 2009.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Saline Lagoons only. Lagoons in the UK are essentially bodies natural or artificial of salinewater partially separated from the adjacent sea. They retain a proportion of their seawater at low tide and may develop as brackish full saline or hyper-saline water bodies.  Lagoons can contain a variety of substrata often soft sediments which in turn may support tasselweeds and stoneworts aswell as filamentous green and brown algae. In addition lagoons contain invertebrates rarely found elsewhere. They also provide important habitat for waterfowl marshland birdsand seabirds. The flora and invertebrate fauna present can be divided into three main components: those that are essentially freshwater in origin those that are marine/brackish species and those that are more specialist lagoonal species. The presence of certain indigenous and specialist plants and animals make this habitat important to the UK\u0027s overall biodiversity. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n19283\tNRW_DS161273\t2024-07-02T08:35:13.664Z\t3.0 Otter TEST Data Discovery *** This is not a real dataset *** FULL FIELD EXAMPLE\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323733\tINTERNAL LOCATION TEST:   Archived as Project 360, Media #1590.   GIS layer:  X:\\Layer_Files\\Biology\\Species\\Example_MEDIN_Compliant_Full_Field.lyr.\tData Custodians \u0026amp; SME:  John Smith, Otter specialist.   Geospatial Custodian: Joe Bloggs, Geospatial Officer.\tTHIS IS NOT REAL DATA.    THE FOLLOWING ELEMENTS HAVE NOT BEEN TESTED  Vertical Datum - we are waiting on encoding advice from MEDIN   THE FOLLOWING ELEMENT IS STORED IN THE METADATA and XML BUT IS NOT VISIBLE iN MAIN VIEW/PDF/PERMALINK.  However this will be addressed at a later point with the Datum issue. Vertical CRS type e.g. EPSG 5701  • This is NRW\u0027s test dataset to ensure staff can refer back to a fully valid record to help themselves solve validation errors • It is also to ensure all elements are displaying and exporting to multiple XML schemas.  The Metadata is written to be compliant with the NRW MEDIN and GEMINI Standard.   • It may temporarily be in public view when checking view formats for the public but will be removed later.\tPublic record\n19153\tNRW_DS125723\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.109Z\tImportant Curlew Areas (ICAs) in Wales Map\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373233\tY:\\Knowledge Strategy Planning\\Natural Resource Planning\\Biodiversity Fisheries Geodiversity\\Jon Rothwell\\Curlew 2021\\Final ICA.GDB\tData Custodian: Bethan Beech, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Species Recovery, Biodiversity and Ecosystem Resilience Group\tThe 12 Important Curlew Area (ICA) boundaries have been drawn up by the members of Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales. Glyfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales is a joint working group between organisations that are committed to Curlew recovery in Wales including representatives from the government conservation farming and game management sectors.    Some of the original hand drawn maps were passed to British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) to draw then passed to NRW to finalise. The others were sent to NRW to check. If they were correct they were uploaded to the Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales web site. If they were incorrect they were sent back to the originator.\tPublic record\n18932\tNRW_DS124814\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.653Z\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Pierce, Alcove \u0026amp; Piercefield Woods SSSI, Coppice Mawr WGWE and Dingestow Court in SE Wales 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383134\tTo be entered into Recorder 6 - contact Mike Howe for R6 SurveyID\tMike Howe\t****Metadata to be supplied by Mike Howe************  Have asked Mke if should be combined with Metadata entry 124733 which seems like it could be same of similiar dataset.  Saproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Coppice Mawr Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2019\tPrivate record\n18037\tNRW_DS115274\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.621Z\tSediment Core Sampling of Seagrass (Zostera) Bed and Sediment Areas in Porth Dinllaen\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323734\t[A] Archived as project 386, media 1401 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1401) [B] Marine Recorder (MRCCW31700000008) NBN file and Snapshot  This project follows on from volunteer P. Dinllaen work - carried out under Across Wales Diving, Project 229, media 598, 878 (File paths: K\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 598 and K:\\Archive\\Media 801to900\\Media 878) Also additional work carried out by Gwynedd Country Council (Lucy to send details) that will be archived separately\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tAs part of the management of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) the SAC Officer has been working with the National Trust local fishermen boat owners and others to look at options to reduce the impact of moorings on the seagrass (Zostera marina) bed at Porth Dinllaen North Wales.  This is a joint project between Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Gwynedd County Council as part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC. As part of the Porth Dinllaen seagrass project a variety of studies are being undertaken to secure an appropriate baseline to establish a detailed picture of the current condition of the seagrass bed that can be used as a reference point to monitor recovery in the future as the project proceeds.  These were conducted to aid in the management of the seagrass bed (Zostera marina) present listed as an important feature under Regulation 35 for the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation. This project was a component of a much larger venture which aims to better understand the local ecology and biology of Porth Dinllaen seagrass bed with an ultimate goal of developing more seagrass friendly moorings and management techniques.   The project has been let in 4 stages: 1) The core and sediment samples and field notes were collected by MES on the 10th October  2) The cores were sent to Thomson Unicomarine for infauna and sediment analysis (By CCW)  3) The species data was sent to MES analysed in PRIMER  (Paid by GCC)  4) The analysed data was entered into Marine Recorder under the PKC data capture contract\tPublic record\n18989\tNRW_DS124933\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.806Z\tUpland Framework: Pilot projects to prove concept and approach – GIS dataset\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393333\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Eryri Upland Options.shp Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Mynydd Du Trial Upland Options.shp Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Eryri Upland Options.lyr   Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Mynydd Du Trial Upland Options.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Ratcliffe, Specialist Adviser, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team.  Metadata Point of Contact: John Ratcliffe, Specialist Adviser, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team.\tIn a pilot project two maps of upland areas; Eryri in North Wales and Mynydd Du in West Wales were created to indicate the preferred habitat cover over the long-term. This was undertaken using available habitat and land cover data (Phase 1 / Habitat Survey of Wales Phase 2 / National Vegetation Survey unified peatland data aerial imagery Environmental Record Centre species data BSBI vascular plant data RSPB data etc.) consideration of designated features and conservation objectives for protected sites along with local expert knowledge. In the case of Eryri extensive local expert knowledge was relied on; in contrast in the case of Mynydd Du consultation with graziers on the Common Land led to a suggested approach covering part of the site for further consultation with Commoners. Both maps are recommendations for future habitat but should be used as a starting point in discussion with other interests to determine the practical objectives for particular parcels of land.\tPrivate record\n17187\tEXT_DS121945\t2024-05-28T12:15:59.647Z\tOrdnance Survey and Landmark Information Group: Historic Mapping (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393435\tX:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS MasterMap\\Historic Mapping\\County Series 10560 epoch 1 to 4 X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS MasterMap\\Historic Mapping\\County Series 2500 epoch 1 to 4 X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS MasterMap\\Historic Mapping\\Historic maps indices X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS MasterMap\\Historic Mapping\\National grid 1250 town plans X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS MasterMap\\Historic Mapping\\National grid series 10000 epoch 5 to 7  Historic mapping is also available as a service and on MyMap X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\Historic Mapping\\OS Historic maps service\\OS Historic maps WMS.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tHistorical Map Data is owned and supplied by Landmark Information Group and Ordnance Survey (OS) and supplied to Natural Resource Wales (NRW) via an OS licence.  Landmark Information Group Limited holds the most extensive digital collection of historical Ordnance Survey mapping of Great Britain. This has been taken from Ordnance Survey’s archive of Town Plans County Series and National Grid mapping covering the period from 1841 to 1996. Historical mapping provides a unique insight into our past and is a fascinating journey through history in itself. Particularly when displayed with contemporary mapping or aerial photography. Historical Map Data is available for different time periods referred to as epochs.  Epoch 1: the first County Series survey; published dates 1843 to 1893 Epoch 2: the first County Series revision; published dates 1891 to 1912 Epoch 3: the second County Series revision; published dates 1904 to 1939 Epoch 4: the third County Series revision; published dates 1919 to 1939\tPublic record\n18990\tNRW_DS124939\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.826Z\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm 33 kV Cables\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393339\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\33KV Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team       To be reviewed by Data Services\tThis dataset shows the Clocaenog windfarm 33 kV cables. 33kV cables are the six underground electric cable circuits coming from the wind turbine power systems. They terminate at the sub-station on site. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPrivate record\n18517\tNRW_DS121669\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.321Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Agricultural Management Opportunities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363639\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Agricultural Management Opportunities.lyr\tRussell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows:  The map shows areas where agricultural management measures could contribute positively to natural flood management. Measures considered are the planting or widening of existing hedgerows (all existing hedgerows are displayed in this map) as well as retention of winter stubbles. Hard engineering solutions are not considered in the map set for natural flood management.\tPublic record\n18293\tNRW_DS116305\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.536Z\tWaste Management Licence Current Exemptions\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333035\tData extracted from CRM and is then stored in Sharefile\tCustodian: Helen Florek Oughton - Customer Care Centre Stephen Badman\tCurrent Waste Management Licence Exemption data as required by Schedule 3 of the Waste Management Licensing (WML) Regulations 1994 (as amended) lists the activities which are normally exempt from WML in Wales. Circular 26/94 in Wales provides additional information.\tPublic record\n17560\tNRW_DS102203\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.434Z\tWelsh Lakes Bathymetry Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323033\tArchived in project 297 Media 1207 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1201to1300\\Media1207),  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Welsh lakes bathymetry.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Wales lakes bathymetry points.lyr  Tristan has a copy held on the Y drive Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Monitoring\\Tristan_Hatton_Ellis\\Corporate_data\\CCW_Final_Data\tInternal Custodian:   Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, NRW\tBathymetric data from Welsh lakes collated from documentary and electronic sources and re-formatted into a consistent and accessible form and structure.  The data includes transformed raw coordinate and depth data to bathymetric surface rasters at 0.5 m interval polygon shapefiles that can be placed into GIS software with volume/depth and area/depth data for the lakes at the same 0.5 m interval.  The data supplied collates existing bathymetric data to produce a series of GIS layers that display detailed bathymetric maps of Welsh lakes.\tPublic record\n17085\tEXT_DS114947\t2024-05-28T12:10:54.547Z\tUKHO/Royal Navy Acoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of Outer Cardigan Bay and Caernarfon Bay, Irish Sea. Summer 2010 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393437\tArchived as Project 364, Media 1351.   Any processed data will be unique to NRW. Source data is available from UK Hydrographic Office.\tGIS Service Desk  Marine Mapping Specialist, NRW External Contact: UKHO       We do have an MOU for this dataset currently saved in Ffynnon: ref DCT-11-107096 .\tIn summer 2010 the Royal Navy Survey vessel HMS ECHO carried out a multibeam echosounder survey of outer Cardigan Bay and Caernarfon Bay. This dataset is the multibeam data coverage from the voyage surveyed to IHO standards and released by the UKHO after verification. The purpose of this data capture was to obtain bathymetry and backscatter datasets of the study area with 100% coverage.\tPublic record\n18150\tNRW_DS116000\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.456Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Gwynedd 1994\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303030\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000014.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Gwynedd. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tPublic record\n18533\tNRW_DS121626\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.540Z\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) B Felling Plan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363236\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA B Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPublic record\n18058\tNRW_DS115549\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.673Z\tFishMap Môn Detailed Fishing Intensity Levels\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353439\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Intensity\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW) recreational and commercial fishing sectors and the Welsh Government. The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and combining it with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activity.   Fishing Intensity is described as the effort of a fishing activity in a given area. For example this could be the number of pots hauled per day per hectare. It is also described as one of three levels but the actual intensity value is derived differently for each fishing type.  The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey.   The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging Queen Scallop Dredging Mussel Seed Dredging Light Otter Trawling Bottom Set Nets Potting Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering. There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors. From the attribute \\_overlaps\\\"  it is possible to determine the number of vessels/ fishers operating in a particular area.   Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the FishMap Môn intensity level data were created both a detailed and generalised version. This detailed version of the dataset shows the precise locations of the fishing activities and the data also contains specific information about the fishing activities and so is restricted to the FishMap Môn Team and certain partners - contact the dataset owner for more information. \"\tPublic record\n18576\tNRW_DS122291\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.688Z\tArt17 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323931\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2018\tInternal Custodian 1: Heather Lewis, Lead Specialist Advisor -Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Internal Custodian 2: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tData collected for Article 17 Habitats Directive reporting 2018. Collection of Point and polygon datasets. This is mapped to standards required by Article 17. The data has been produced through manual survey referencing relevant documentation and third party data.  101 files containing distribution and extent data for Article 17 Habitats Directive 2018 reporting:  Art17 2018 H1210 Annual Vegetation of Drift Lines 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H1210 Annual Vegetation of Drift Lines.lyr Art17 2018 H1220 Perennial Vegetation Stony Banks.lyr Art17 2018 H1230 Vegetated Sea Cliffs 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H1230 Vegetated sea cliffs.lyr Art17 2018 H1310 Salicornia 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H1310 Salicornia.lyr Art17 2018 H1330 Atlantic Salt Meadow.lyr Art17 2018 H1330 Atlantic Salt Meadows 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H1420 Mediterranea Saltmarsh Scrub.lyr Art17 2018 H1420 Mediterranean Saltmarsh Scrub10km.lyr Art17 2018 H2110 Embryonic Shifting Dunes 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H2110 Embryonic Shifting Dunes.lyr Art17 2018 H2120 Shifting Dunes with Marram 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H2120 Shifting Dunes with Marram.lyr Art17 2018 H2130 Fixed Dunes with Herbaceous Veg 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H2130 Fixed Dunes with Herbaceous Veg.lyr Art17 2018 H2150 Decalcified Fixed Dunes 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H2150 Decalcified Fixed Dunes.lyr Art17 2018 H2170 Dunes with Salix.lyr Art17 2018 H2170_Dunes with Salix 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H2190 Humid Dune 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H2190 Humid dunes.lyr Art17 2018 H3130 Oligotrophic to Mesotrophic Standing Waters 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H3130 Oligotrophic to Mesotrophic Standing Waters.lyr Art17 2018 H3140Hard Oligo Mesotrophic Water Point.lyr Art17 2018 H3140 Hard Oligo Mesotrophic Waters with Chara Calcium 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H3150 Natural Eutrophic Lakes 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H3150 Natural Eutrophic Lakes Point.lyr Art17 2018 H3160 Natural Dystrophic Lakes 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H3180 Turloughs 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H3260 Watercourses with Ranunculion Rivers 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H4010 Wet Heath with Cross Leaved Heath 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H4030 Dry Heaths 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H4060 Alpine \u0026amp; Subalpine Heaths 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H5130 Juniper on Heaths or Calcareous Grasslands 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H6130 Calaminarian Grassland 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H6130 Calaminarian Grassland.lyr Art17 2018 H6130 Calaminarian Grasslands point.lyr Art172018 H6150 Siliceous Alpine and Boreal Grasslands 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H6170 Alpine \u0026amp; Subalpine Calcareous Grasslands 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H6210 Semi Natural Dry Grasslands 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H6210 Seminaturaldry grasslands.lyr Art17 2018 H6410 Molinia Meadows 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H6430 Hydrophilious Tall Herb Communities 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H6510 Lowland Hay Meadows 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H7110 Active Raised Bogs 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H7110 Active Raised Bogs.lyr Art17 2018 H7120 Degraded Raised Bog 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H7130 Blanket Bog 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H7140 Very Wet Mires 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H7150 Depressions on Peat Substrates 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H7210 Calcareous Fen Calcium Rich Fen 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H7220Petrifying and Hardwater Springs Depositing Lime 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H7230 Alkaline Fen Calcium Rich Springwater 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H7230 Alkaline Fen Calcium Rich Springwater.lyr Art17 2018 H7240 Alpine Pioneer Formations 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H8110 Siliceous and Acidic Scree 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H8120 Calcareous and Calcshist Screes Base Rich Scree 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H8210 Calcareous Rocky Slopes 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H8220 Siliceous Rocky Slopes 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H8240 Limestone Pavements 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H8310 Caves Not Open to the Public 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H9120 Beech Forests on Acid Soils 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H9130 Beech Forests on Neutral to Rich Soils 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H9180 Tilio AcerionMixed Woodland 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H91A0 Western Acidic Oak Woodland 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H91D0 Bog Woodland 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H91E0 Alluvial Forest Alder10km.lyr ...\tPrivate record\n17525\tNRW_DS101840\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.195Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of Nudibranch Species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383430\t[A] Skomer MNR, West Region Drive /shared documents/data/biological data/nudibranch ,   [B] Species data in Marine Recorder (survey numbers MRCCW30000000052,MRCCW30000000055, MRCCW3000000005C)  NB this data set appears to have been extended to include nudibranch data from 2014 and 2018. There are surveys in Marine Recorder. Metadata may need updating to reflect this.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Nudibranchs are predators on a wide range of epibenthic species. Most are seasonal and reliant on their prey for food shelter and a place to lay their eggs. Being near the top of the food chain they can act as an indicator of the health of the communities they rely on.  Nudibranchs are a feature of the Skomer marine Nature Reserve (MNR) for which species diversity and the presence of reare or scarce species are attributes used to assess conservation status.  As top predators they can act as an indicator of the health of the communities they rely on.   Between 1975 and 1991 surveys of nudibranch diversity in the Skomer MNR were conducted as a series of identification courses/workshops at Dale Fort Field Centre and these showed species diversity to be high.  61 species of nudibranchs were recorded (Bunker Picton and Morrow 1993).  The Skomer MNR team complete nudibranch species surveys every four years these have been completed in 2002 2006 and 2010. The Skomer MNRs nudibranch population have been identified as being rich and diverse with over 62 species including one nationally rare species and four nationally scarce species. The purposes for this data capture were to: - to produce a species list for the entire Reserve on a 4 yearly basis.  - to record a minimum of 16 common species annually in the Skomer MNR from a check list.\tPublic record\n17715\tNRW_DS110219\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.660Z\tEffects of the Sea Empress oil spillage on sensitive sessile marine invertebrate communities\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323139\t[A] Archived as project no 107 (media no 380). File path K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 380. [B] Marine Recorder survey MRCCW10000000090\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe grounding of the Sea Empress at the mouth of Milford Haven in February 1996 resulted in oil polluted shorelines from the south of St Brides Bay to Pendine Sands. This dataset contains the results of an investigation into the effects of the oiling and its clean-up on sensitive sessile intertidal communities of marine invertebrates and the results of an investigation into possible effects of the oil/clean up on the biology of Alcyonidium species at Dale and Angle. The purpose of these investigations were to survey the lower shore sessile fauna on the north side of St Catherine\u0027s Island Tenby documenting the present composition of the sessile fauna to assess what changes had occurred since the 1950s and whether any of these might be attributable to the oiling an clean up following the Sea Empress grounding; and investigate the effect of the oil on the reproduction and recruitment of Alcyonidium species (Bryozoa) on Fucus serratus at Dale and Angle in Milford Haven and at control sites in Solva Harbour.\tPublic record\n17608\tNRW_DS107902\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.106Z\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring Data for the Rivers Wye and Usk (2007)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037393032\t[A]  Data is archived under Project 358 Media Number 1339.   [B]  Data is also held in Recorder 6 Lamprey Monitoring, Wye \u0026amp; Usk Catchments 2005 - CCW -  Report no. 41 CCW0002800000007.\t\tRiver Brook and Sea Lamprey are designated features of the River Usk and Wye Special Areas of Conservation (SAC).  Assessments of species composition distribution abundance and size composition of larval lamprey (ammocoetes and transformers) populations in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment.  The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Usk and Wye Catchments.  Monitoring of the status of lampreys is required to comply with the reporting requirements of the Habitats Directive. 54 Sites on the River Wye (of which 30 contained positive records) and 35 sites in the River Usk (of which 10 contained positive records) were sampled.\tPublic record\n17524\tNRW_DS101841\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.235Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Echinus esculentus and other echinoderm species surveys\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383431\tSkomer MNR, West Region Drive /shared documents/data/biological data/urchins.   This dataset is updated 4-yearly.  2003 data also available in Marine Recorder survey number MRCCW16800000005.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  The purpose of this data capture was to assess the abundance of an important habitat grazer. The dataset includes data related to the abundance data for common sea urchin Echinus esculantus Spiny starfish Martasterias glacialis Seven armed starfish Luidia ciliaris and Common sunstar Crossaster papposus.  The data also contains the density of each species and depth band at which recorded and the presence of \\bald urchin disease\\\".  From 2007 the data includes a qualitative assessment of the larvae in plankton samples.\"\tPublic record\n18065\tNRW_DS115525\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.016Z\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring: Additional North Llyn Ground Truthing (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353235\t[A]  Data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 366, Media No. 1361 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400),   [B]  Videos are maintained in the archive  [C]  Marine Recorder Survey  - MRCCW3170000000A\tInternal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.     NRW.Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDrop down video ground-truthing of additional Modiolus bed discovered partially outside the Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  This  follows on from previous ground-truthing carried out in 2011 (Marine Archive project 313). Analysis and Marine Recorder entry to be undertaken later in the year under Data Capture call-off contract.    The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a ground truthing exercise through which the extent and quality of Modiolus mussel beds could be assessed in an area of the coast of the Llyn Peninsula North Wales.\tPublic record\n18521\tNRW_DS121674\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.405Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Enhancing Surface Roughness\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363734\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Enhancing Surface Roughness.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: The maps shows the number of constraints to enhancing surface roughness for NFM.\tPublic record\n19042\tNRW_DS125355\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.283Z\tGwent Grassland Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333535\toff-site storage ref: CCW/SERBC/209 / CCW/NRM/R-53\tData Services Team\tGwent Grassland Survey 1983\tPrivate record\n18933\tNRW_DS124813\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.639Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Invertebrate Surveys\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383133\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Invertbrate points May.lyr X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Invertbrate points July.lyr X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Invertbrate polygons    https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/Protected-Sites/so-life/D1%20Physical%20Monitoring/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTgyMjcuMjAyMjIiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D\u0026amp;CID\u003df14f7ca1%2D20e6%2D0000%2D415a%2D9e4a3862147a\u0026amp;cidOR\u003dSPO\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Flandman%2FProtected%2DSites%2Fso%2Dlife%2FD1%20Physical%20Monitoring%2F00%20SoLIFE%20Data%20Archive%2FInvertebrates\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000DC881C721DC6C9439F0968E82C157F72  Final combined dataset for post and pre surveys in Darwin Core Format found here - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Data Services Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data\\NRW_DS124813_SoLIFE Inverts\tInternal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tThis report presents the results of surveys carried out for the Sands of LIFE project to understand the impact of dune slack rejuvenation and other sand dune restoration work on the invertebrate fauna. Dune slacks are known to support a range of rare and specialist invertebrate species that are expected to benefit from the works.  Pitfall trapping timed sweep netting and visual searches were undertaken across seven project sites: Merthyr Mawr Kenfig Whiteford Burrows Pembrey Burrows Morfa Harlech Newborough and Tywyn Aberffraw. Morfa Dinlle was also surveyed but only in 2019. Surveys were carried out in May July and August in three separate years 2019 2021 and 2022. At each project site survey plots were identified in 2019 at locations where future conservation management interventions were planned as well as nearby control areas.   The data includes invertebrates (Coleoptera Diptera Araneae Opiliones Hymenoptera Hemiptera (Heteroptera) identified to species level together with their frequency for each survey plot in each project site.\tPublic record\n18048\tNRW_DS115145\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.442Z\tSeasearch Diver and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Snorkel Seagrass Zostera Marina Surveys Rhoscolyn 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313435\tData is archived as project 375, media 1385 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1385) A working copy is stored at Z:\\Archive\\CCW_H_Drive\\Science\\MFSG\\Users\\Ramsay_K\\rhoscolyn seagrass\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSeagrass is the only marine flowering plant found subtidally in the UK and seagrass beds are considered of National Importance. In 2012 Seasearch volunteers undertook a one day survey to verify the presence of seagrass beds at Rhoscolyn Anglesey North Wales.  Following the successful Seasearch search for seagrass CCW undertook a snorkel survey to establish the size of the larger bed to the west of the bay. 1. Confirm the presence of seagrass beds at Rhoscolyn. 2. Note estimates of density and depth of live seagrass in different parts of the bed 3. Make note of any unusual features of the seagrass bed\tPublic record\n17664\tNRW_DS109884\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.372Z\tSaltmarsh Features at Milford Haven 1996 - Sea Empress Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383834\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Milford Haven 1996 Data also archived as project 47 media numbers 42, 54, 215 at  H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media042 (report)   , H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media054  ,    H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media201to300\\Media215\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThe Sea Empress released approximately 72000 tonnes of crude oil and about 480 tonnes of heavy fuel oil in the period 15 to 21 Feb 1996 when grounded off Milford Haven and later while at the jetty.  The coastline within Milford Haven was heavily oiled and a major clean-up operation was mounted.  However to avoid further damage oil was not cleaned from the saltmarsh. Many of the affected saltmarshes in Milford Haven lie within Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and also form components of candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) under the Habitats Directive.  As part of the response to the oil spill the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) commissioned a damage assessment survey of impacted saltmarshes in Milford Haven. Carry out an assessment of the possible damage to saltmarsh habitat and the associated scarce species Salicornia pusilla and Limonium humile affected by the Sea Empress oil spillage and to establish a base-line against which future changes may be kept under surveillance - this included mapping the saltmarshes using National Vegetation Classification (NVC) methodology. The survey also aimed to assess the levels of hydrocarbon pollution within the sediments of the affected saltmarshes.\tPublic record\n18518\tNRW_DS121672\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.358Z\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Catchment Zones\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363732\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Catchment Zones.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: The map shows the area split into seven separate catchment zones in which different types of NFM intervention can be used to achieve benefits. The catchment zones are:  floodplains in valley bottom areas; floodplains in the mid-reaches; gentle and steeply sloping mid-reaches; floodplains in the headwaters regions as well as gently and steeply sloping headwater regions.\tPublic record\n18687\tNRW_DS123318\t2024-05-28T12:54:22.128Z\tWater Quality Exemptions\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333138\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\Water quality exemptions.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Water Quality Policy Team, Natural Resources Wales waterqualitypolicyteam@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThe Environmental Permitting Regulations contains exemptions from needing an environmental permit for certain water discharges and groundwater activities. Exemptions cover the discharge of treated domestic sewage to either surface or groundwater managing vegetation near/on inland water substances to ground for scientific purposes and discharges from open loop heating and cooling systems.    These exemptions must be registered with Natural Resources Wales. The Water Quality Exemptions dataset contains the locations for these registered exemptions in Wales.\tPublic record\n17558\tNRW_DS102209\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.379Z\tLANDMAP Geological Landscape\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323039\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap Download\\LandMap Geological Landscape (Boundaries).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap Download\\LandMap Geological Landscape (Classification).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap Download\\LandMap Geological Landscape (Evaluation).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset. The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    This is a geographic dataset showing geological landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information including General Description Evaluation Recommendations Tolerance to Change Area Boundary Evaluation and Bibliography.    Information is gathered by LANDMAP in order to inform sustainable decision making and to enable landscape to be taken into account during decision making processes. The Geological Landscape dataset is important as geology is often the strongest influence on the landscape affecting drainage patterns vegetation cover the human environment and so on. These effects in turn determine the possibilities for agriculture water sources transport networks etc.  2003 to 2008 then updated at regular intervals.   This dataset maps geological geomorphological and hydrological features of an area. The geographical and topographical characters of areas are noted as are significant active geological and geomorphological processes and landscape - forming pedological processes.\tPublic record\n18895\tNRW_DS124788\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.213Z\tDeterioration of Natura 2000 Sites in Wales due to Coastal Squeeze Losses\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373838\tSpreadsheets- DMS (record numbers to be confirmed) GIS data - X drive - location to be confirmed  Currently zipped in the DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:u:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20NRW%20projects/Article%206%20-2%20Deterioration%20due%20to%20Coastal%20Squeeze%20Project/Data_20190710.zip?csf\u003d1  And copy (to be deleted) in  K:\\Archive\\Temp_forArchive\\Report 355\\Data\tInternal Contact: Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Process and Coastal Management, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tThe data resource includes NRW Evidence Report 355 plus associated Excel and GIS files. The project has calculated potential coastal squeeze losses under a range of different No Active Intervention and Managed Realignment management Scenarios climate change predictions and Shoreline Management Plan Epochs. The study covers the Pen Llŷn a’r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation. The excel files contain coastal squeeze calculations which are summarised within the report and GIS files used to undertake analysis and produce maps provided within the evidence report.\tPrivate record\n18970\tNRW_DS124861\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.486Z\tIndicative feature condition assessments and reasons for failure for Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) habitats in Wales.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383631\tY:\\Marine\\Marine and Coastal Policy and Planning Team\\MPA CIP Mapping\\FINAL Welsh SAC Designated Habitat Features Condition and Reasons for Failure.gdb  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\Indicative Assessments 2018.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\Indicative Assessments 2018\\Features.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\Indicative Assessments 2018\\Sites.lyr   Further spreadsheet located in the DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/MPAImpProg/ProgDocs/Features_Sites_Reasons_for_failure_final.csv?d\u003dwdf33cfb0e9e74aedabbc8318ff0b5c78\tInternal Contact: Harriet Robinson, Specialist Advisor - Marine Systems, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the location of Special Area of Conservation (SAC) habitat features their condition and reasons for failure where applicable.\tPublic record\n18899\tNRW_DS124786\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.959Z\tAll Wales Slope 5m2 (degrees)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373836\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\All Wales Slope 5m2 (degrees).lyr\tPaul Challender, Lead Specialist - Industry and Waste Regulation, Industry Regulation NE\t\tPrivate record\n17198\tEXT_DS121869\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.213Z\tEnvironment Agency (EA): Water Resource Permit Locations (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383639\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\EA Water quality permits.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\EA Water resource permits area.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\EA Water resource permits points.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\EA Water resource permits reach.lyr\tHenry Forest - Permitting Officer, Wales Permitting Centre Cardiff (Water Resources)  GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is an Environment Agency (EA) collection of datasets related to the location of Water Resource Permits in England. Natural Resource Wales (NRW) has access to this data via a data exchange agreement with EA. This is for internal use only.\tPublic record\n17318\tEXT_DS124855\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.257Z\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2015 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383535\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2015.lyr\t\tLand Cover Map is a UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH) spatial dataset which NRW acquires under licence. NRW only has a segment of the data and not the whole of GB. This segment covers the whole of Wales cross border catchments and coastal areas around the Bristol Channel.  The dataset is an update from the Land Cover Map 2007 (LCM2007).\tPublic record\n17530\tNRW_DS101842\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.208Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Condition Monitoring of Eunicella verrucosa\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383432\tSkomer MNR office, V drive/ shared documents/ data/ biological/seafans.    Data also available in HQ Marine Recorder (survey number MRCCW16800000004)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Eunicella verrucosa is a feature of the marine nature reserve it is at the edge of it\u0027s distribution range and is a potential indicator of climate change. Eunicella verrucosa colonies around the Skomer MNR have been photographed and assessed for condition since 1992. Colonies are revisited every year and assessed for: Presence damage epi-foulants entanglement growth necrotic tissue and the presence of Tritonia nilsodherni and Simnia patula. Eunicella verrucosa is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species and information is used for BAP reporting.\tPublic record\n18619\tNRW_DS123227\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.737Z\tNatterjack font\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323237\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Natter_Jack_font_point.lyr\tLiz Halliwell\t\tPrivate record\n19464\tNRW_DS161288\t2024-08-27T08:40:25.194Z\tNational Flood Hazard Maps - Climate Change\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323838\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh / GIS and Spatial Data Team   Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh / Simon James / Dave Tarrant       Surface Water Catchments (subset dataset NRW_DS124811): Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from Environment Agency © EA and Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited [2015].\tThe National Flood Hazard Climate Change maps are based on generalised modelling only.  These maps use the same base information as the Present Day Hazard Maps but include an allowance for Climate Change (2120).  These maps have no official status for Planning or Insurance purposes therefore the public and professionals are advised to use the information contained within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Map the Development Advice Map / Flood Map for planning (as appropriate) for these purposes as these will be more up-to-date and incorporate improved modelling information.    The National Flood Hazard Map for Climate Change have been created for 3 sources of flooding namely 1. Flooding from Rivers 2. Flooding from the Sea 3. Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses  The Maps show flood depth velocity (speed) hazard and extent for High Medium and Low risk scenarios with raised flood defences.    For Rivers and Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses High risk is up to 1 in 30year plus climate change uplift (CC); Medium risk is between 1 in 30 plus CC and 1 in 100year plus CC and Low risk is between 1 in 100 year plus CC and 1 in 1000year plus CC Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP).  For the Sea High risk is up to 1 in 30 year plus CC; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 plus CC to 1 in 200 year plus CC; and Low risk 1 in 200 year plus CC to 1 in 1000 year plus CC.  The Climate Change uplift values use the “Central” estimate for the year 2120 from the Welsh Government guidance available at the time of modelling (2018).\tPublic record\n18679\tNRW_DS123331\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.455Z\tNALD Dereg Licence Abstraction\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333331\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\NALD Dereg Licence Abstraction Areas.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\NALD Dereg Licence Abstraction Point.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\NALD Dereg Licence Abstraction Reach.lyr\tCustodian: Ashley Lansdown, Team Leader, Water Resources Permitting\t\tPrivate record\n18064\tNRW_DS115523\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.022Z\tAcross Wales Lagoon Monitoring - Neyland Wier (Westfield Pill) Pool 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353233\t[A] Data is archived as Project No. 241, Media No. 1338.    [B] For further details of analyses see: Analysis of macrofaunal and sediment samples. Analytical Report Report Number: J36221-1 available in NRW Marine Archive: Project Number 241 Media Number 1338. [C] Species and PSA data is also available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW3000000007A]\tInternal Custodian: NRW Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team (Matt Green?)  NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Neyland Wier lagoon which is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   The purpose of this data capture was to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature\tPublic record\n17660\tNRW_DS109883\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.417Z\tSaltmarsh Features of the Dee and Clwyd Estuaries 2000\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383833\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media101to200\\Media180 Data held as Project No. 129, media 180\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results from a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey used as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Dee and Clwyd Estuaries. The purpose of this dataset was to provide systematic descriptions at the plant community level of saltmarsh and associated habitats in the Dee and Clwyd estuaries. The objective was to provide an essential basis for monitoring facilitate management planning and improve our ability to evaluate development proposals.\tPublic record\n18676\tNRW_DS123332\t2024-05-28T12:54:21.287Z\tNALD Licence Abstraction\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233333332\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\NALD Licence Abstraction Areas - Feb 2016.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\NALD Licence Abstraction Points - Feb 2016.lyr  x:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\NALD Licence Abstractions Reach - Feb 2016.lyr\tCustodian: Ashley Lansdown, Team Leader, Water Resources Permitting\t\tPrivate record\n19077\tNRW_DS125378\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.252Z\tNCC Powys Local Woodland surveys up to 1976/77 (Paper records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333738\toff-site archive storage. box no: CCW/SERN/149\t\tPaper record cards - Woodland surveys for Powys - SO 05-08 14-18 25 26/36 27/37 and SN 97 grid squares\tPrivate record\n19137\tNRW_DS125521\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.366Z\tPhase 2 Survey of Selected Grassland Sites in Ceredigion 1987\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353231\toffi-site archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/023\tData Services Team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk, off-site archive box ref:CCW/WRAB/023        Data Services team Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThe objective of the filed work were: Determine the nature and distribution of plant comunities within the site. Collect detailed records from a representative sample of plant communities. Note the distribution and abundance within the site of any plant species known to be rare or uncommon in Ceredigion\tPrivate record\n17138\tEXT_DS121393\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.243Z\tJNCC Management Units for Cetaceans in UK waters (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333933\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\ X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Bottlenose dolphin management units.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Common dolphin management units.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Harbour porpoise management units.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Minke whale management units.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Rissos dolphin management units.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Whitebeaked dolphin management units.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Whitesided dolphin management units.lyr  Data is stored in the 3rd Party geodatabase.\tTom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist (tom.stringell@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk) Ceri Morris,  Marine Mammal Ecologist (ceri.morris@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)       The data can be downloaded from: http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/page-6943 which also has some more information attached as well.\tThis is a collation of spatial datasets detailing the extent and location of the final agreed Management Units (MUs) for the seven most common cetacean species in UK waters. This dataset is wholly owned by JNCC and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) can access the data through an Open Government Licence.   Almost all species of cetacean found in UK waters are part of larger biological populations whose range extends into the waters of other European States and/or the High Seas. Equally the number of individuals present at any one time may be only a small proportion of those that make use of UK waters throughout the year. In order to obtain the best conservation outcomes for many species it is necessary to consider the division of populations into smaller management units. Management Units (MUs) should be based on our best understanding of the structure of biological populations and any ecological differentiation within such populations (as outlined in the Appendix). However the boundaries between MUs may be determined by either political boundaries (e.g. the boundary between Ireland and the UK) or the management of human activities (e.g. ICES divisions used for the collection of fisheries data and management of fisheries). As far as possible the management units defined in this report have been based on the presence of known populations with divisions proposed on the basis of ecological evidence and/or divisions used for the management of human activities. Therefore whilst being consistent with the best biological understanding an MU refers to the animals of a particular species in a geographical area to which management of human activities is also applied. As such these MUs comprise partially artificial divisions of often slightly uncertain biological populations.\tPublic record\n19071\tNRW_DS125379\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.095Z\tForestry Comission Woodland Survey  - Gwent 1987 (paper records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333739\toff-site archive box FCW/FELL/5\topendata@naturalresources.gov.uk\tIn 1987 NCC Woodland survey cards were completed for FC broad-leafed woodland likely to be semi-natural .  Area: Ebbw area of Gwent.\tPrivate record\n17713\tNRW_DS110203\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.661Z\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Nutrients Spatial Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323033\tx:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Phosphate Network.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Nitrate Network.lyr\tPlease contact Jane Roberts ( Environment Agency)\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons. It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to NRW for internal use only.  The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains River Nutrient data (nitrate and phosphate) - GQA nitrate and phosphate grades achieved for each stretch in the 10k network.\tPrivate record\n17422\tNRW_DS98751\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.123Z\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs) Declarations\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373531\tX:\\Resource Management\\NNR Declarations\\NNR Declarations.lyr\tThomas Hellawell, Lead Specialist Advisor Estates\tThis is a spatial dataset which shows National Nature Reserves (NNRs) with their different declarations. NNRs represent the very best examples of our wildlife habitats and geological features and can range in size between five hectares to well over 2000.   NNRs are declared by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act of 1949 or under the Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981. They are owned or leased by NRW or the land is held by an approved body such as a County Wildlife Trust. Each reserve has a programme of work to manage the site\u0027s special features. All of them are also Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and may provide places for educational projects research and management trials. Some reserves require permits to gain access to them.    Please refer to National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 and Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981 for reasons of creation of the original designation. With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making.    This data has been checked by NRW staff. The original individual declaration paper maps are the legal documents. It should be pointed out that some of the oldest declaration maps.\tPublic record\n17703\tNRW_DS110026\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.576Z\tDevelopment of a Methodology for the Assessment of Cumulative Effects of Marine Activities Using Liverpool Bay as a Case Study (2002)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303236\tData held as Project No. 75, media 80\tData Custodian - Maritime Policy Section Head, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tFormer Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) encourages good practice in undertaking Environmental Impact Assessments (EIAs) for developments and activities including those located in Welsh Territorial waters (up to 12 nm offshore). Experience has shown that EIAs carried out in the marine environment often address impacts of the development in isolation from other similar developments or other developments having similar impacts. A review of environmental statements produced for offshore oil and gas developments found that none addressed cumulative impacts over the projects lifetime or with regards to adjacent existing or proposed developments.  It can therefore be said that cumulative impacts from marine activities have seldom been adequately addressed in EIAs for offshore projects. The purpose of this data capture is to: -  Review current understandings of potential cumulative effects from offshore industries and develop a proposed methodology for cumulative effects assessment of offshore industries from a nature conservation and landscape / seascape perspective;  -  Produce a GIS database and analysis tool for offshore Cumulative Effects Assessment (CEA);  -  Identify current and predict likely future cumulative effects from offshore industries in Liverpool Bay on habitats and species of importance; and  -  Identify data gaps and recommend further data and research requirements for undertaking CEA for offshore activities.\tPublic record\n18614\tNRW_DS122461\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.876Z\tForest Mine Area\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343631\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest mine area.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset contains Mine Areas on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any Mine Area data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured Mine Area information.\tPrivate record\n17717\tNRW_DS110183\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.422Z\tFluvial Audit of the River Dee\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313833\tArchived as Project 161, Media 490\tRhian Thomas, Env Mon Officer, Dadansoddi a Hysbysu, NRW ? Formerly Rhian Thomas in CCW\tThe results of a CCW commissioned Fluvial Geomorphological Audit of the River Dee to record the morphological features and to identify and understand the geomorphological processes which influence and control the channel activity and the morphological quality of the river system.  The River Dee is a dynamic gravel-bed river with evidence of active channel migration in specific locations. The river is of high conservation status being designated a Special Area for Conservation by virtue of its Ranunculus community and in its lower reaches the river channel and its floodplain are designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest for its morphological and physiographic features.  This survey provides a baseline source for assessing site suitability by relating the presence or absence of morphological features to the condition parameters that are identified as habitat preferences for the components of the Ranunculus community. The purpose of this data capture was to develop an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the River Dee. The area used for this study included A 130 km length of the Main River of the River Dee between the Bala Lake (Llyn Tegid) outflow and the Normal Tide Limit (NTL) at the Chester Weir (SJ 408659).\tPublic record\n18144\tNRW_DS115704\t2024-05-28T12:40:37.344Z\tCondition assessment of 8 standing water Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and 24 unprotected lakes across Wales (2012-2014).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373034\tData outputs associated with this project are archived as Media Number: 1493 Project Number: 449 (Condition Assessment of eight standing waters in Welsh SSSIs) 450 (The BAP lake surveys) 451 (Lake Ecological Surveys)\tInternal Contact: Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader - or Tristan Hatton-Ellis Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tLake ecosystems are threatened both nationally and globally. Commitments under European legislation and international treaties require the UK to protect and enhance these in a conservation conscience way taking account of the culturally important habitats. This data collection forms part of an ecological and limnological survey. Limnology is the study of inland waters as ecological systems interact with their drainage basins and the atmosphere. The lakes surveyed were as follows:  - Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Lake Surveys: Llynnau Cregennen Llyn Dinas Llyn Mair Llyn Cerig-y-Myllt Llyn Gwernan Llyn Gwyddior and Llyn Coch-hwyad.   - Lake Ecological Surveys: Llyn Cadarn Llyn yr Wyth-Eidion Llyn Coron Llyn Goddionduon Llyn Anafon Gloyw Lyn Llyn Cwm Bychan Llyn Eiddew-mawr Llandgorse Lake Llyn Mawr Cosmoeston Lake Llyn Eiddwen Llyn Fanod Upper Talley Lake Lower Talley Lake Llyn Penrhyn.    - Condition Assessment of Eight standing waters in Welsh SSSIs: Pysgodlyn Mawr Llyn Llech Owain Llyn Pencarreg Witchett Pool Llyn Treflesg Llyn Cerrig-bach Llyn Traffwll and Llyn Glasfryn.  Data from this network of lakes supports an integrated monitoring programme for protected sites the Water Framework Directive and other legislative and policy driven initiatives. The Welsh Environment Strategy has imposed a target of 95% of all Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) to be in favourable condition by 2015. All the SSSIs assessed in this dataset were classes as being in unfavourable condition. Therefore another objective of this dataset is to provide initial monitor the condition of a series of SSSIs lakes in order to assess the need for improvement or increased monitoring in the future to reach the targets set by the Welsh Environmental Strategy. The data was also collected to fill the gaps in the quality and condition of unprotected sites in Wales by targeting select lakes of potential biodiversity importance in Wales. This forms part of an on-going strategic survey programme designed by NRW to improve the understanding of the distribution and quality of different lake types in Wales.\tPublic record\n17094\tEXT_DS110041\t2024-05-28T12:10:55.429Z\tSeasearch Marine Surveys in Wales 1995 onwards (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303431\tNB - More recent data is available via the NRW All Wales snapshot for Marine Recorder.  Archived as Project 22, Media 135 (File path: K\\Archive\\Media 101to200\\Media 135) SeaSearch snapshot data is merged with NRW\u0027s and available to view through the Marine Wizard.   Related titles above for Seasearch reports available in NRW libraries.\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Contacts: - Seasearch National Coordinator. chris@seasearch.org.uk. Marine Conservation Society, Unit 3 Wolf Business Park, Alton Road, Ross on Wye, Herefordshire, HR95NB - Liz Morris, North Wales Local Seasearch Organiser. liz@marine-ecosol.com - Kate Lock, West and South Wales Local Seasearch Organiser. rumbalock@tiscali.co.uk\tSeasearch is a national project for volunteer sports divers who have an interest in what they\u0027re seeing under water want to learn more and want to help protect the marine environment around the coasts of Britain and Ireland. This dataset contains the results of a number of Seasearch surveys carried out around the coast of Wales from 1995.  A snapshot of the SeaSearch data is merged with the NRW Marine Recorder snapshot. The main aim is to map out the various types of sea bed found in the near-shore zone. The data collected is also used to fill gaps in our knowledge about what lives where what affect human activities are having on marine life and to help advise government and policy makers on actions needed to protect the marine wildlife and habitats e.g. by identifying sites where better protection may be needed.\tPublic record\n19078\tNRW_DS125360\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.237Z\tUnofficial Mountain Bike Trails (2021/22 Snapshot)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333630\tZ:\\Processing\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Management\\1 - Geospatial\\SM\\Unofficial Mountain Bike Trails (2021_2 Snapshot) - Heather Crump\\Unofficial Mountain Bike Trails (2021_2 Snapshot).gdb\tHeather Crump - Advisor (2) Recreation Content\tThis \u0027Wild MTB trails\u0027 data was collected by contractors and covers NRW managed land. The survey began with Strava Heat Maps and other user driven websites and was validated on site. It looks at the busiest trails and sites and covers most but certainly not all of the forests or all of the trails. It represents the 2021/2 snapshot. Data includes trail routes (line data) and hazard points (point data) associated with a set of photographs stored on the DMS. Data regarding rider numbers was manually written down trail by trail. THIS DATA IS FOR INTERNAL USE ONLY.\tPublic record\n17718\tNRW_DS110193\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.461Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Passive Sediment (Collector) Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313933\tSkomer MNR, V drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic data/sediment\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton Assistant Marine Conservation Officer - Skomer MNR Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Passive sediment traps have been deployed at 2 sites intermittently since 1994. Sediment dropping out of the water column is collected into a pot. The sample pots are changed every 2 weeks during the field season and the sediment samples are frozen. The purpose of this data capture was to record the depositional material occurring in the MNR.\tPublic record\n18138\tNRW_DS115631\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.780Z\tFine Grain Vegetation Monitoring of Yr Wyddfa / Snowdon ECN Site\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363331\tAssociated data is held at H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\28 Fine-Grain Vegetation at Snowdon ECN Site at NRW, Maes y Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, Maes y Ffynnon, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Environmental Change Network (ECN) is the UK\u0027s long-term integrated environmental monitoring and research programme. It gathers information on the responses to environmental change in physical chemical and biological systems. This is done through a network of UK monitoring sites from which long-term datasets can be used to identify environmental changes and improve understanding of the causes of change. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate environmental change across the ECN site.\tPublic record\n20189\tEXT_DS161332\t2025-02-04T10:25:43.747Z\tNational Soil Resources Institute (NSRI) : Anglesey 63K Soil Series Map (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333332\tx:\\Physical\\Soils\\NSRI Anglesey 63K Soil Series Map.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.  National Soil Resources Institute has been incorporated into LandIS or the Land Information System which is one of the offerings of the Cranfield Environment Centre (CEC) the largest UK national and international centre for research and development consultancy and training in soils and their interaction with the atmosphere land use geology and water resources.\tPrivate record\n18274\tNRW_DS116321\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.710Z\tLicenced Water Abstractions\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333231\tPermitting and Licencing System (PALS) - also know as Dynamix 365 Data stored from 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-26 in WISKI and from 2016-02-27 to present in PALS (Also know as Dynamix 365)\tData Custodian: Ashley Lansdown, Team Leader, Water Resources Permitting, Future Regulation\tThe water abstractions dataset details all sites in Wales covered under the Water Act 2003 where all abstractions of 20 cubic metres or more require an abstraction licence\tPublic record\n17702\tNRW_DS110060\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.542Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ )  - Commercial Fishing Activities Occurring in and around Skomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303630\tSkomer MNR office,West Drive drive/shared docs/data/commercial/potting\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The purpose of this data capture was to document the usage of the MNR and to create GIS maps of fishing effort and types of gear used in the reserve. The dataset contains records of all the fishing activity in the reserve.  This includes potting effort position and length of strings of pots position of nets number of boats using the area and number of dredgers in the St Brides area.\tPublic record\n18638\tNRW_DS123018\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.816Z\tActivity Centres\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303138\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Activity Centres.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n19075\tNRW_DS125371\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.238Z\tThe status and distribution of Lilljeborg’s Whorl Snail Vertigo lilljeborgi at Pont Croesor on the Afon Glaslyn in 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333731\tWelsh Invertebrate Database held in Recorder 6\tMike Howe - NRW Invertebrate Specialist\tResults of a monitoring survey in August 2022 for the Near Threatened Lilljeborg’s Whorl Snail Vertigo lilljeborgi within Glaslyn SSSI are presented. The area surveyed is north of Pont Croesor on the Afon Glaslyn. Judged to be in Unfavourable condition after the last survey in July 2008 (Willing 2009) the present survey over two days in August repeated the previous survey. The wetland habitats remain visually very similar to those described in 2008 and weather and river level conditions were similar.  All 8 sites were revisited and sampled by visual search beating vegetation and collecting bulk samples of decaying plant matter. A 9th site was sampled near Site 7 the point at which V. lilljeborgi was last recorded in 2008. A suction sampler was used in 5 additional locations between sites on the east bank. A total of 12 adults of V. lilljeborgi was found at Site 7 and Site 8 confirming that the species survives on both sides of the Afon Glaslyn although it is still apparently restricted to small areas. Two juvenile Vertigo were also found at Site 6. These could not be unambiguously identified as V. lilljeborgi owing to the presence of another species Vertigo antivertigo nearby. All Vertigo specimens were found in large bulk samples that had been dried and sieved. No Vertigo were observed in the field emphasising the importance of multiple sampling methods.   Using the Conservation Objective Lower Limit of 10 individuals set by Willing (2009) V. lilljeborgi is judged to be in Favourable condition within Glaslyn SSSI in August 2022. Nonetheless the population only just exceeds the Lower Limit and remains small and potentially vulnerable. Further sampling in the areas around Pont Croesor could help determine whether V. lilljeborgi is more widespread on the Afon Glaslyn floodplain.\tPublic record\n19082\tNRW_DS125362\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.248Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) At Risk and Failing Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333632\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD At Risk Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Failing Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022.lyr\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment\tAt Risk Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022 and Failing Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022 are spatial (polygon shapefile) datasets containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). To implement the guidance on managing forests in order to minimise adverse impacts in acid-sensitive areas two categories of acid sensitivity have been identified. These are defined as \u0027failing due to acidification\u0027 and \u0027at risk of failing due to acidification\u0027.  Water bodies identified as currently \u0027failing\u0027 have been classified through the Water Framework Directive monitoring as being of less than \u0027good\u0027 status and the cause of that failure has been attributed to acidification. The \u0027at risk of failing\u0027 water bodies have been identified via the findings of the Welsh acidification risk assessment as being at risk of failing to achieve \u0027good\u0027 status by 2027 due to acid deposition. The risk assessment combines chemical and biological data and an assessment of the environment\u0027s ability to neutralise the predicted acid pollutants.\tPublic record\n18534\tNRW_DS121653\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.627Z\tElectricity Powerlines on Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Land\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363533\tx:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Electricity Powerlines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains electricity powerlines on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured electricity powerline information.\tPublic record\n18613\tNRW_DS122456\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.815Z\tForest Quarries\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343536\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest quarries.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset contains Quarries on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any quarry data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured quarry information.\tPrivate record\n18627\tNRW_DS123225\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.226Z\tMalltraeth cockle survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323235\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\WG_Malltraeth_cockle_survey\\All Stations WGS84.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\WG_Malltraeth_cockle_survey\\District Low Water Marks.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\WG_Malltraeth_cockle_survey\\Most Accurate District Coastline.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n18615\tNRW_DS122464\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.908Z\tForest Third Party Access\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343634\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest third party access.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset contains Third Party Access on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any Third Party Access data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured Third Party Access information.\tPrivate record\n18215\tNRW_DS116256\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.766Z\tPotential Sites of Hydropower Opportunity\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323536\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\River Obstructions.lyr\tGideon Carpenter, Hydrology and Water Resources Management, NRW\tThese data show the location of opportunities for hydropower and the basic environmental sensitivity associated with exploiting them in Wales. Barriers are identified and assessed in this project. The term barriers is used to describe sites with sufficient drop to provide a hydropower opportunity. They are mostly weirs but could also be other man-made structures or natural features such as waterfalls. The average maximum power generation capacity on a barrier was 45Kw with a total potential capacity of 1178Mw. Environmental sensitivities were assessed. This assessment considered the presence of fish species and whether the site has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Almost half (46%) of these barriers are classified as highly sensitive mostly because of the presence of migratory fish species such as salmon and eel. 27% are medium and high sensitivity and the remainder are unclassified due to a lack of data. When it is assumed that a new scheme has a fish pass built into it the environmentally compatible opportunities increase considerably. Given the scale of the project and the data used the results are not intended to replace any part of an individual site assessment. Instead the dataset gives national and regional level overviews of the potential opportunities available their locations and their relative environmental sensitivity to exploitation.\tPrivate record\n19145\tNRW_DS125522\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.421Z\tSurvey of Broadleaved Woodlands in Ceredigion 1978\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353232\toff-site archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/023\tData Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk, Off-site archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/023       Data Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNCC Survey to study the extent and state of semi-natural broadleaved woodland in Ceredigion (SN grdi squares)\tPrivate record\n17355\tNRW_DS24270\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.812Z\tRare Flowering Plant and Fern Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323730\t[A]  Species data is held in Recorder 6. [B]  Data held in database: Rec2002 - Higher Plants in Aberystwyth.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tJulian Woodman, Higher Plant Specialist, Terrestrial Species, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of locality information conservation status and dates of most recent records for Red Data Book Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) and Nationally Extinct vascular plants in Wales.  This dataset is separate and different from JNCCs Threatened Plants Database but there is likely to be considerable duplication of records between them.  The purpose of this data capture was to inform survey monitoring and conservation management of sites for rare plants. The records span the period 1639 (one record extracted from literature) to 1999.  The majority of records occur after 1950.  Not all dates are given to the precise day or month most are only known to be within a particular year.\tPublic record\n17566\tNRW_DS102082\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.169Z\tSquirrel Collation for Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303832\t[A]  Stored in Recorder 6. -  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, Report 475  CCW0003800000009  (See Metadata Entry:  102089) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, West area report (56)  CCW000380000000A   (See Metadata Entry:  102112) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Aberhirnant \u0026amp; Lake Vyrnwy, Report 241  CCW0003800000005   (See Metadata Entry:  100791) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Aberhirnant, Report 589  CCW0003800000007   (See Metadata Entry:  102086) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Clocaenog  CCW000380000000B   (See Metadata Entry:  102121) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Cwm Mynach, Report 540  CCW0003800000008   (See Metadata Entry: 102126)  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tLiz Halliwell, Mammal Ecologist Woodland and Farmland Mammals, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tRecords presence and absence of red and grey squirrels in Wales.  The collation was created to give a picture of squirrels distributions in Wales and to collect all relevant data in one place 1951 to 2006. The majority of the data is fully validated and verified.\tPublic record\n17306\tEXT_DS125585\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.639Z\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Landcover Crops (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353835\tArchived\tMatthew Wareham Data Services\tDeveloped in collaboration with Remote Sensing Applications Consultants Ltd (RSAC) the maps exploit satellite data - Copernicus Sentinel-1 C-band SAR (Synthetic Aperture Radar) and from 2016 onwards Sentinel-2 optical data.   The crops from 2016 onwards are winter wheat (including oats) spring wheat winter barley spring barley oilseed rape field beans potatoes sugar beet maize and improved grass. Other cereals root crops early potatoes and vegetables are grouped in a class called ‘other’ together with a small number of parcels which could not be classified. From 2020 solar fields (sites with solar panels at ground level) are also included.  UKCEH Land Cover® plus: Crops is based on the Land Cover Map parcel framework. Every parcel which is larger than 2ha and categorised as agricultural land is coded with crop type information. Where parcels contain two or more crops preliminary processing is applied to subdivide parcels into separate cropping units.\tPrivate record\n17310\tEXT_DS125587\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.756Z\tTraws Eryri long distance cycle trail – Cycling UK (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353837\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Traws Eryri cycle trail.lyr  \"X:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Traws Eryri cycle trail fallback route.lyr \"\tJohn H Taylor - Team Leader, Recreation - NW Wales\tLong distance off road cycle trail promoted by Cycling UK and supported by NRW. Route runs from Machynlleth to Conwy primarily on existing public rights of way - unclassified council roads bridleways tarmac roads and forest roads. Some short sections use private land where an agreement for access is in place with the landowner.\tPublic record\n18119\tNRW_DS115829\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.790Z\tUnderwater photographs and video of aquatic flora, habitat structure and water transparency from Llynnau Cregennen.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383239\tProject Number 455 Media 1516\tInternal Custodian: Freshwater Team Leader  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe data supports a condition assessment of Llynnau Cregennen on the northern slope of Cadair Idris above Dolgellau. The dataset supports an assessment of both the water clarity and diversity of aquatic plants in the lake. The dataset is made up of a number of underwater video and photography surveys which were conducted by Marine Ecosol. This data was collected in order to have representative samples images and video footage of macrophyte and faunal community assemblages within Llynnau Cregennenn.\tPublic record\n17078\tEXT_DS64566\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.911Z\tOrdnance Survey Basemaps (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353636\tX:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping   X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\Grids   X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS MasterMap   X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Products  X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Raster maps  X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Strategi Vector 200k\tGIS Service Desk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds copies of Ordnance Survey (OS) basemaps for the whole of Wales in paper and digital format. NRW obtain much of it\u0027s OS data initially as part of the Public Sector Mapping Agreement (PSMA) and from 2020 as part of the Public Sector Geospatial Agreement (PSGA) which is a collective agreement between OS and the government.\tPublic record\n17209\tEXT_DS121886\t2024-05-28T12:16:00.467Z\tMinistry of Defence (MoD): Land Ownership (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383836\tX:\\Ownership\\MOD\\MOD Land.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis spatial dataset details the extent and location of land owned by the Ministry of Defence (MoD) in Wales. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to this data through a licence agreement with the MoD.\tPublic record\n17974\tNRW_DS114540\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.679Z\tDrop-Down Video of Historical Native Oyster (Ostrea edulis) Habitat in Swansea Bay, South Wales (2010)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353430\tData is archived at project no. 317, media nos. 1252 and 1397 (File paths (K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1252 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1397) Species data in NRW copy of Marine Recorder (Survey Number: MRCCW31000000003)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team (used to be Aethne Cooke)   Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe native or flat oyster (Ostrea edulis) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority species and it is also listed for Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006 as being \u0027of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity in Wales.\u0027   The native oyster has a long history as an important commercial species in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to Milford Haven Swansea Bay/Mumbles and Porthcawl although at a much reduced scale. The remaining oyster population in Wales is under threat through the spread of the protozoan parasite Bonamia ostreae continued poor recruitment and competition from the alien invasive slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata. The situation is exacerbated by the continued operation of a small oyster fishery in Milford Haven and Swansea Bay.   The aim of this project was to gather information on the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Pembrokeshire coast. This information will contribute to our understanding of the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Wales coast and will inform projects to reinstate oyster habitat and stock enhancement. This dataset collates the examination of drop down video of oyster beds. This project used video data was examined to identify broad seabed type and evidence of oysters.\tPublic record\n18646\tNRW_DS123297\t2024-05-28T12:53:50.137Z\tAvian flu seabird colony\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323937\tx:\\Incidents\\Avian flu contingency\\Avian flu seabird colony text.lyr  x:\\Incidents\\Avian flu contingency\\Avian flu seabird colonys.lyr\t\t\tPrivate record\n19079\tNRW_DS125393\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.237Z\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Sand Dune Soil Baseline Monitoring 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333933\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Soils Slacks.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Soils Transects.lyr\tToni Henwood\tThis survey focuses on baseline pre-intervention monitoring in relation to two of the planned intervention types: notch creation and dune slack re-profiling. The monitoring covers six sites in Wales: Tywyn Aberffraw Newborough and Morfa Harlech in north Wales and Kenfig Merthyr Mawr and Pembrey Burrows in south Wales.  At each site baseline monitoring was set up for the planned interventions and for control locations where no intervention will take place in order to clearly show the effect of the restoration measures.  For both intervention types soil profiles were taken at each monitoring location. Soil profiles were recorded to a depth of 50 cm and soil samples were taken to a depth of 20 cm. In addition the baseline monitoring for the notches involved setting up sand-rain collectors at transects away from the proposed notch and control locations downwind in the direction of the prevailing winds. Each transect started at 50 m distance from the inland end of the notch and then at two further points (100 and 200 m). Soil samples were analysed for bulk density electrical conductivity pH organic matter content by loss on ignition (LOI) calcium carbonate content carbon (C) and nitrogen (N) content.\tPublic record\n19037\tNRW_DS125358\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.081Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Fish Monitoring Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333538\tWISKI KIECO\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Paul Hyatt\tFreshwater fish are used as an indicator of stock status water and habitat quality artificial obstacles to migration and river flow requirements due to specific life-cycle requirements. This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the freshwater river fish surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  For the geospatial version of this data please see NRW_DS125743 \"KiEco: Freshwater Fish - Geospatial\".\tPublic record\n18229\tNRW_DS116251\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.377Z\tLive Flood Warnings and Alerts Application Programming Interface (API)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323531\tLive feed only: https://api-portal.naturalresources.wales/products/\tAndrew Wall Flood  Flood \u0026amp; Operational Risk Management, National Services\tFlood Warnings are provided by the constant monitoring of stations and forecasting flooding from rivers or the sea:    - Severe Flood Warning: Severe flooding. Danger to life.   - Flood Warning: Flooding is expected. Immediate action required.   - Flood Alert: Flooding is possible. Be prepared.   - Warning no longer in force: Flood warnings and flood alerts that have been removed in the last 24 hours.     Flood Warnings are available on our website and as a Live Feed. The live feed provides a summary of flood warnings (Alert Warning and Severe) that are sent externally. The Flood Warnings Live Feed provides current status update of every Flood Warning Area in England and Wales. This is updated on the Flood Warning Information System (FWIS) service every minute although it is presented for external users every 15 minutes via the Data Distribution Hub where the XML can be securely downloaded (sFTP feed).\tPublic record\n19068\tNRW_DS125394\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.670Z\tMonk\u0027s Trod and Hengae Drone Survey 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333934\tZ:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\GIS \u0026amp; Spatial Data\\Drone data\tMichael Sneade - Advisor 2, Natura 2000 Sustainable Management\tGIS layers from Monks Trod and Hengae survey areas: • full site orthomosaic • tiled orthomosaic • full site digital surface model • tiled digital surface model  Used to survey the following: Ascertaining a baseline status of the highway. Seeing how far the traffic \"drifts\" from the highway.\tPublic record\n20472\tEXT_DS161351\t2025-03-25T11:43:47.919Z\tGB1900 Filtered Mines and Quarries (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333531\tY:\\Metal Mines\\NWCRO data\tDave Johnston - Senior Specialist Advisor, Abandoned Metal Mines\tThe GB1900 project computerised all the place names and other text on the Second Edition County Series six-inch-to-one-mile maps covering the whole of Great Britain published by the Ordnance Survey between 1888 and 1914. This dataset is a filtered version containing elements identified as related to mining and quarrying\tPrivate record\n18959\tNRW_DS124871\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.068Z\tGrey Seal Census for North Wales 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383731\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mm/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7BF756400B-FB4D-4155-8CC5-BAAFB9FF1B70%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dOEL_NRW_SLP0817_DTA_FULL.xlsx\u0026amp;wdLOR\u003dc7EB90253-6835-4F64-B333-E6F032772D01\u0026amp;_DSL\u003d1\u0026amp;action\u003ddefault\u0026amp;mobileredirect\u003dtrue\tTom Stringell (Lead specialist Advisor: Marine Species)\tFull-ground based survey of Grey Seals undertaken between Aberystwyth and the Dee Estuary aimed to identify potential and active pupping sites throughout the North Wales and to estimate pup production for the 2017 season. All active and newly identified potential pupping sites were surveyed in 7 to 10 day cycles by boat sea kayak and on foot. A drone was used to supplement pup counts and to quantify sampling error at The Skerries. A total of 126 potential and previously recorded pupping sites along the North Wales coast were defined\tPublic record\n18067\tNRW_DS115512\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.662Z\tHistoric Aerial Imagery to Monitor Temporal Change in Intertidal Habitats\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353132\tData archived as project 430, media 1467\tPaul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tLong-term and broad-scale datasets are essential to any investigation of change in natural communities. This dataset includes the collation of aerial imagery for 44 sites around the Welsh coastline selected to contain rocky shore saltmarsh or Sabellaria alveolata across temporal scales providing suitable data for comparisons of change through time both anthropogenic and biological.   In order for comparisons to present day imagery current aerial imagery was also obtained in this study. Photographic aerial images containing features of conservation value (specifically rocky shores Sabellaria alveolata inhabited shores and saltmarsh areas) within Wales were obtained. Historic photographic aerial images that contained features of conservation value (specifically rocky shores Sabellaria alveolata inhabited shores and saltmarsh areas) within Wales were selected from expert knowledge and Countryside Council for Wales\u0027s collection of habitat maps.    The aim of this project was to collate digitise and georeference aerial photographic imagery for sites that had multi-year overlap. Accuracies of less than 5 m RMS was obtained (with the exception of Treath Lafan 1995 and the Dyfi Estuary 1975). At these saltmarsh locations there were too few ground control points to achieve this accuracy.\tPublic record\n17667\tNRW_DS109992\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.510Z\tRe-examination of distribution data for the harbour porpoise (Phocoena phocoena) on the North West European Continental Shelf\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393932\tData is within the Marine archive as Project No. 28 and 169, and Media163 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.\tMarine and freshwater Evidence Manager, NRW\tThe Joint Cetacean Database contributed to by Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) Sea Watch Foundation (SWF) and Sea Mammal Research Unit (SMRU) contained most but not all effort-related cetacean data for North-west European waters up to 1998. Effort-related data from this database have been used to produce maps of cetacean distributions for north-west European waters for a Cetacean Atlas at the scale of 1/4 ICES (International Council for the Exploration of the Sea) rectangles (c. 25 x 25 km - 625 km2) due to be published by the JNCC. Some initial modelling of distribution data for harbour porpoise had not indicated any discrete areas of concentration. However the spatial analyses conducted to derive species distributions were at too coarse a scale to detect concentrations. The UK has undertaken to re-examine distribution data for harbour porpoise with a view to site selection for this species. During 2001 a number of new effort-related surveys of harbour porpoise distribution were conducted in Welsh waters.  These included:  1) surveys of southern Cardigan Bay with emphasis upon the Special Area of Conservation established for bottlenose dolphins conducted by Sea Watch Foundation between May and October and funded by EU INTERREG Programme with support from the Countryside Council for Wales (Baines et al. 2002);  2) a parallel acoustic and visual survey of harbour porpoises in South-west Welsh waters with emphasis west of Pembrokeshire conducted by Chris Pierpoint with support from the International Fund for Animal Welfare (Pierpoint 2001); surveys of the waters around Bardsey Island and the Llyn Peninsula North Wales conducted by Friends of Cardigan Bay and the Whale and Dolphin Conservation Society (WDCS) with support from CCW; and land-based and offshore surveys off the coast of Anglesey undertaken by Marine Awareness North Wales also with support from CCW.\tPublic record\n18607\tNRW_DS122760\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.739Z\tNDF Hotspots of resilient ecosystem service provision\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373630\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\Hotspots of resilient ecosystem service provision.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset shows an overview of where areas with hotspots of ecological networks important for maintaining biodiversity into the future spatially coincide with areas delivering a number of key ecosystem services form the list considered.   The highest values are areas that are delivering a wide range of ecosystem services and are also significant in their provision of biodiversity and ecological networks. These areas therefore are hotspots for nature and areas which should be carefully managed to enhance their ecological and ecosystem service functions.  This dataset deals with a number of key issues that have been identified as important in planning policy including understanding risks addressing landscape change and adapting to the effects of climate change. Under the environment Act (Wales) 2016 public authorities across Wales also have a duty to promote ecosystem resilience in the exercise of their functions.  This dataset can help to inform this work at both a national and local scale by identifying the areas that are important habitat networks which also provide a range of ecosystem services.\tPrivate record\n18400\tNRW_DS118898\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.054Z\tA Survey for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve / Site of Special Scientific Interest and the Afon Penrhos Floodplain (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138383938\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information \u0026gt; Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Martin Willing)\tA survey for Desmoulin\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve (NNR)/ Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the Afon Penrhos floodplain was undertaken in October 2016 to determine its current status and distribution. Sampled locations were based upon the results of previous surveys at Cors Geirch (1999-2008) and Afon Penrhos (2002-2008).   The population at Cors Geirch was found to have declined considerably with a complete loss of the snail from former stronghold areas and just 17 shells recorded from two small areas at the southern end of the site. Assessments in 2003 and 2008 had considered the population to be in favourable condition. With an eight-year monitoring hiatus the causes of the sharp decline are unclear but are likely to include the spread of uncut and un-grazed rank fen vegetation combined with encroaching birch and sallow. Dense vegetation growth at the site was such that the former ditch-lines used as features for site monitoring were almost completely obscured and difficult to reach.  At Afon Penrhos the snail was found in large numbers in short mechanically-cut and lightly horse-grazed \u0027fen-meadow\u0027; tall uncut fen supported significantly lower numbers. Elsewhere in the UK the snail is typically associated with tall fen. Vertigo moulinsiana was recorded on about 19 acres (8 hectares) of the site but population boundaries were not established on the eastern and south-east borders of the surveyed area. It is estimated that a further 75 acres (30 hectares) of potentially-suitable habitat exists which should be surveyed to determine if it supports the snail. The Afon Penrhos population is not only the largest in Wales but also probably one of the largest in mainland Britain. The significance of the population here is further enhanced by recent considerable population losses at several Special Areas of Conservation(SAC) in southern England. Once the full extent of the population on Afon Penrhos is determined it is recommended that the site should be notified as a SSSI to reduce any risk of loss or damage due to changes in site management.  In light of the snail\u0027s association with short \u0027fen-meadow\u0027 at Afon Penrhos it is recommended that rank areas which previously supported Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch are opened up to provide short fen habitat to assist the recovery of the remnant population.\tPublic record\n18320\tNRW_DS116342\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.220Z\tHistoric Flood Warnings and Flood Alerts\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333432\tEmailed every quarter from EA, but in 2016 the data will come via our own service. Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116342\tAndrew Wall - Flood Forecasting Metadata Contact - Data Services Team\tListing of Severe Flood Warnings Flood Warnings Update Flood Warnings and Flood Alerts issued since the Floodline Warnings Direct service went live on January 26th 2006 to the present.   This dataset includes flood warnings issued by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) from 1 April 2013.   Flood Alerts and Warnings are issued for flooding from rivers and the sea.  For Flood Alerts this is generally river catchments or stretches of coastline. For Flood Warnings and Severe Flood Warnings this is specific communities or parts of communities.\tPublic record\n17236\tEXT_DS122162\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.822Z\tDŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water (DCWW): Utilities Water Assets and Ownership (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313632\tx:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Foul above ground assets.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Groundwater abstraction catchments.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Potable above ground assets.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Potable Line.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Reservoir Catchments.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW River Intake Catchments.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Sewer AGA.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Sewer Catchments.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Sewer Chambers.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Sewer Discharge Points.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Sewer Network.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Sewer Structures.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Water Control Valves.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Water Hydrants Washouts.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Water Network Structures.lyr  x:\\Utilities\\Water\\DCWW Water Network.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water and licenced to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use. This details the extent and location of various utilities managed or owned by Dŵr Cymru Cyf / Welsh Water.\tPublic record\n17716\tNRW_DS110192\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.357Z\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Salinity Records\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313932\tSkomer MNR, V drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic data/sea water/ salinity\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Salinity is recorded from the sea bed and in a water column profile from a single site in the MNR. Records began in Jul 1992 and have continued to date. The purpose of this data capture was to record the oceanographic qualities of the surrounding sea water.\tPublic record\n17699\tNRW_DS110027\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.567Z\tCarmarthen Bay: Evolution of Estuarine Morphology and Consequences for Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Management\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303237\t[A] Report is held as Project No. 76, media 93 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 93) [B] Also available on OLIB\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay has a total of four important estuaries comprising the Loughor Estuary and the Taf Towy and Gwendraeth. Between 16th and 19th century these estuaries lost around 2400 Ha of saltmarsh due to land claim. Between the end of the 19th century and end of the 20th the estuaries gained 1136 Ha. The changes in the saltmarsh environment may impact shellfish invertebrates and wading birds. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the history of evolution and current trends in the geomorphological development of the estuaries of the then Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC). Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries is now designated fully as an Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for numerous species and habitats including estuaries mudflats sand flats and lagoons which make up 13.7% of the site.\tPublic record\n17118\tEXT_DS115879\t2024-05-28T12:11:57.247Z\tAcoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of Ramsey Sound, Pembrokeshire November 2008 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383739\tArchived as Project 400, Media 1417 Marine networked external hard drive available through VMWare P:\\TEL multibeam Ramsey - The physical hard drive is located with the Marine Team contact Kirsten Ramsey.\tGIS Service Desk  Kirsten Ramsey, Marine Ecosystems (subtidal) Team Leader, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, or James Moon (South Ops) External Custodian: TEL (Tital Environmental Ltd) or Longdin and Browning (who commission Clydeside Surveys Ltd).        According to the Marine Archive Access Database Project Number 400 - No information supplied about this data or where it came from.  Thought that this data was provided by Tital Environmental Ltd (along with the Swansea Bay data), however report that saved with it indicates that it was commissioned by Longdin \u0026amp; Browning.  The Ramsay data is potentially confidential.\tA multibeam bathymetric survey was commissioned by Longdin and Browning Ltd and carried out by Clydeside Surveys Ltd to carry out a cable route survey in Ramsey Sound Pembrokeshire. This was carried out to assist the planning of a cable route and any further survey work. The main objectives of the survey were to provide the following: - Accurate and repeatable Bathymetric survey - Acoustic imaging of the seabed to identify seabed features. The most important aspect of this survey was to obtain accurate bathymetric survey data over the entire survey area and also to check the digital imaging data for changes in seabed type.\tPublic record\n18562\tNRW_DS122295\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.876Z\tThe status of the blackfly Simulium morsitans on the Afon Teifi at Cors Caron National Nature Reserve - 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323935\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe Afon Teifi at Cors Caron supports the only recently recorded UK population of the blackfly Simulium morsitans Edwards (Diptera: Simuliidae). The true extent of its UK distribution and status remain uncertain although Simuliidae are increasingly identified to species level during routine river quality monitoring.   This survey shows the results of samples and observations of simuliid larvae and pupae collected in early August 2018 and compares the results with a similar survey undertaken in 2008.\tPrivate record\n18023\tNRW_DS114944\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.643Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Communities in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393434\tArchived as project 199 media 1350. Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data. Species data also available in Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae). Specific areas of interest include the range of intertidal sediment communities in the bay and the monitoring aimed to collect core samples along transects established in previous years.  Data is captured to support reporting on conservation status of the piddock feature. All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high. Piddocks can be described as a biotope that occurs on circalittoral soft rock such as soft chalk or clay most often in moderately exposed tide-swept conditions. As soft chalk and firm clay are often too soft for sessile filter-feeding animals to attach and thrive in large numbers an extremely impoverished epifauna results on upward-facing surfaces although vertical faces may be somewhat richer. The rock is sufficiently soft to be bored by bivalves.\tPublic record\n17279\tEXT_DS124611\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.481Z\tEnvironment Agency (EA): Integrated Height Model data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363131\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\EA Integrated Height Model 2016 DTM 02m.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Height\\EA Integrated Height Model 2016 DTM 02m Hillshade.lyr\tBen Anscombe\tData supplied by the Environment Agency.   Data was derived from 2016 NRW and EA LIDAR with gaps derived from licenced APGB DTM data.\tPublic record\n18580\tNRW_DS122098\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.914Z\tInvasive Non Native Species Records obtained from the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303938\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Invasive Non Native Species.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jennie Jones, Specialist Advisor, Non-Native Species  GIS and Spatial Data Team       This dataset needs updating with this search... https://wales-species-inns.nbnatlas.org/?\u0026amp;fq\u003dlistMembership_m_s:%22NRW Sustainable Management of Natural Resources (SMNR)%22#tabs-1\tThis data collation contains a variety of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) data and 3rd party owned data and has been obtained from the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas.\tPrivate record\n18649\tNRW_DS123294\t2024-05-28T12:53:50.496Z\tWales T address at risk\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323934\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2017.lyr\tMark Pugh\t\tPrivate record\n18654\tNRW_DS123290\t2024-05-28T12:53:50.644Z\tPluvial Max scores\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323930\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2017.lyr\tMark Pugh\t\tPrivate record\n17246\tEXT_DS123682\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.444Z\tRSPB: UK and Eire 95% Utilisation Distributions in 5% Bands (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233363832\t\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Matty Murphy  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Services Team\tThis is a spatial data collation wholly owned by RSPB. From 2010 to 2015 the RSPB and partners undertook a series of large-scale seabird tracking studies across the UK during the late incubation / early chick rearing period of the breeding season using cutting-edge GPS tracking technology. For four of the species tracked there was sufficient data coverage to map their UK-wide at sea distributions using habitat selection models. These four species were European shag Phalacrocorax aristotelis black-legged kittiwake Rissa tridactyla common guillemot Uria aalge and razorbill Alca torda. Habitat selection models were based upon all GPS locations and therefore included coverage of all behaviours (e.g. foraging commuting resting etc.). This dataset uses the UK distribution maps of these four species to identify important areas of high seabird density at sea based on hotspot mapping techniques. Utilisation distributions are 2-dimensional probability distributions that represent the time spent in a specific area and thus the probability of encountering an animal in that location during a future observation period.\tPublic record\n18185\tNRW_DS116226\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.200Z\tRecorded Flood Outlines (archived data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323236\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\ Recorded flood outlines.lyr\tCustodian: Nick Steel, Flood Risk Analysis/FCRM / (Previously) Mark Pugh, Flood \u0026amp; Operational Risk Management, NRW\tThis dataset has been archived and has been superceded by  NRW_DS116228 Historic Flood Outlines.  Recorded Flood Outlines contains the individual location outline and approved attributes for records of historic flooding. Please note that these records show flooding to the land and do not necessarily indicate that properties within the historic flood extents were flooded internally.   It is also possible that the pattern of flooding in this area has changed and that this area would now flood under different circumstances. In addition absence of a historic flood event for an area does not mean that the area has never flooded only that we do not currently have records of flooding in this area.\tPublic record\n19063\tNRW_DS125397\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.398Z\tRare Plant Species survey of Wales 1987-88 (paper records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333937\tOff site storage box ref:  CCW/WRAB/025\tstored in offsite archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/025,  internal contact  Data Services team\tSurveys of South Wales including Dyfed/Powys and West Glamorgan/Llanelli. Reference copies of species found by 10km grid squares not known where originals are kept\tPrivate record\n17266\tEXT_DS123007\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.597Z\tESTYN: Annual report 2011-14 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303037\tX:\\Social Economic\\Education\\ESTYN annual report 2011-14.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n19062\tNRW_DS125398\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.408Z\tPowys Woodlands Old Maps and woodland survey (Paper records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333938\toff-site archive ref CCW/SERN/30\tOpendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tpaper copies of old maps of Powys Woodlands and woodland species no year mentioned\tPrivate record\n17260\tEXT_DS123008\t2024-05-28T12:18:11.597Z\tDepartment for Communities and Local Government (DCLG) Fires 2013 (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303038\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\DCLG Fires 2013.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tPrivate record\n17656\tNRW_DS109921\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.103Z\tCoastal Sensitivity Atlas\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393231\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Emergencies_Data\\oil_spill\\WalesMarineResourceAtlas. Maes y Fynnon and regional offices. MESG Oil Spill Contigency Plan - Fynnon numbers DCT-09-103877, DCT-09-103876.\tCustodians - GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW Jennie Jones was involve din updating the data, Mike Thomson will be taking over as business lead as hold  role of Duty dispersant officer Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW       This metadata entry need re-writing as the product has been updated and renamed to reflect NRW current role to provide dispersant advice only and that the data has been transferred to ArcGIS from MapInfo and may have been updated in some cases.  The new name is Coastal Sensitivity Atlas (previously known as Atlas of Coastal and Marine Resources Sensitive to Oil Pollution (Wales) - GIS Dataset Jennie Jones has taken a lead on the updating the Atlas, working with Phil Jutson. Mike Thomson (Incidents and Emergencies Training and Systems Manager) will become the business lead as the Dispersant Duty Officer\tThis spatial dataset is an Atlas of Coastal and Marine Resources Sensitive to Oil Pollution in Wales. This data capture aims to highlight sensitive and vulnerable areas across Wales which maybe sensitive to oil pollution. It contains but is not limited to information on seabirds scoter wetland birds grey seals cetaceans booming plans protected sites sand dunes saline lagoons sea grass beds and biotopes.  This is intended for use by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) staff but is a legacy Countryside Council for Wales’ dataset. It should be used when providing environmental advice during an oil spill response operation. It\u0027s primarily intended as a memory jogger during the early stages of operation. It provides information on the location of environmental resources only background knowledge of the relative sensitivity and vulnerability of the resources is required when providing advice during marine pollution incidence. Covers the coast of Wales and adjacent coasts of England (east coast of Dee estuary to Hoylake and south coast of Bristol Channel to Porlock Bay). 1st edition published March 2003; currently being updated and 2nd edition to be published early 2009.\tPublic record\n17675\tNRW_DS109881\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.690Z\tSaltmarsh Features at Milford Haven 2002\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383831\tArchived as Project 132 Media 389.  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh  Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW.          Although this dataset is 3rd party, we have decided not to include it as strictly 3rd party, because we were heavily involved in the validation process and we are a partner of the owner.\tThis dataset is the results of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats at Milford Haven. The purpose of this data capture was to survey and map the saltmarsh units within the Milford Haven Waterway within the context of the National Vegetation Classification and to repeat the recording of the permanent quadrats established in 1996 to assess change in the floristics of these areas.  Other purposes include the erection of a series of new permanent quadrats within the eastern section of the estuary as a baseline for the monitoring of future changes and to record and map the distribution of notable species of the saltmarshes and their associated vegetation. This dataset has been amalgamate with saltmarsh data from across Wales to form the Phase 2 Saltmarsh Survey data for Wales.\tPublic record\n18009\tNRW_DS115081\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.624Z\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Biotope SLR.FserX.T in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303831\tData is archived as project 199 and media 552.  Species data is also available in Marine Recorder.  A working copy of the data is available at  :\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2009 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  In the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide and reefs. Specific areas of interest include areas of tide-swept bedrock and boulders and areas of tide-swept Fucus serratus on mixed sediment along the Menai Strait. The purpose of this in-situ survey was to locate specific tide-swept Fucus serratus zones and measure the width of the zone percentage of Fucus serratus within the zone and information on epibionts. This in turn provided a baseline for future condition monitoring and helped establish a methodology for intertidal surveying.\tPublic record\n18648\tNRW_DS123296\t2024-05-28T12:53:50.332Z\tHostile Sites Database\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323936\tX:\\Incidents\\Hostile sites.lyr  L Drive - Please contact the custodian or ICC Team for access\tSean Moore, Team Leader, Incident Communications Centre, Incident and Emergencies, Flood and Operational Risk Management  Please note that metadata should also be left for internal view only.\tThis database contains information on people or sites that are a health and safety risk to NRW staff. It contains names locations reasons for concern and actions to be taken if a site visit is required.   This data should only be available for staff who administer the database (currently ICC) and relevant information will be provided to individuals seeking to make site visits.   The Grid Reference for each site is provided to the Geospatial Team for upload to GIS no other data is provided. This data is also restricted and should not be shared externally.\tPrivate record\n18117\tNRW_DS115838\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.555Z\tTowed Video Surveys off the Coastline of the Llyn Peninsula North Wales (2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383338\tMarine Recorder,  Survey Code: MRCCW31700000014.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tSurveys of the seabed were carried out using towed dropdown video surveys were taken aboard Lara B and William Stanley both fishing vessels operating around Llyn Peninsula Gwynedd North Wales.   A total of 38 video tows were collected from infralittoral and circalittoral habitats at the following five locations offshore from the Llyn Peninsular North Wales; Llyn-Hells Mouth Ynys Gwlan Porth Meudwy North Llyn Modiolus and North Llyn sea Grass. Where available HD GoPro video footage was used in the analyses however if this was not possible a lower quality standard definition footage was used. The locations of the video tows were made into GIS files as a spatial representation of the location of the tow surveys. Video surveys were conduct to conduct ground-truthing surveys through the seabed around the Llyn Peninsula Gwynedd North Wales.\tPublic record\n17069\tEXT_DS100191\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.646Z\tWelsh Government: Land Parcel Identification System (LPIS) Farm and Field Boundaries (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313931\tx:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\LPIS Farm and field boundary join unredacted.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\LPIS Farm Boundaries.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\LPIS Field Boundaries Redacted.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\LPIS Field Boundaries Unredacted.lyr  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\lpis\\lpis_farm_bdy.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent and location of farm boundaries for the Land Parcel Identification System (LPIS). It is an external third party dataset owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use only.   This dataset includes Land Parcel Identification System (LPIS) Field and Agricultural/Crop Information. The dataset has been supplied by the Welsh Government and contains Whole Farm and Field Boundaries Customer Details with the appropriate Reference Number (CRN) and Crop Information for each Field Boundaries.\tPublic record\n18501\tNRW_DS121359\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.326Z\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Woodland Constraints\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333539\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Natural Processes woodland.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tThis dataset comprises a range of land cover types where the woodland potential layers have been masked owing to existing woodland watercourses peat roads rail and urban areas. Other constraints data such as protected habitats and high grade agricultural land should also be considered.\tPublic record\n19061\tNRW_DS125396\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.299Z\tGwent Level Plant Species Survey 1991 (Paper Records )\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333936\toff-site paper storage CCW/SERC/1463  - RF24454\tnot known stored in off-site archive box ref: CCW/SERC/143 (RF24454)\t\tPrivate record\n19070\tNRW_DS125395\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.852Z\tPhase 2 Grassland survey 1986 Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan (paper data records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333935\toff-site archive storage ref: CCWRF43501 \u0026amp; CCWRF43502\tData Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tPhase 2 Grassland survey of SN SO SS and ST  Mid\u0026amp;South Glamorgan\tPrivate record\n17600\tNRW_DS108557\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.993Z\tBullhead Monitoring for the Usk and Wye Catchments 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038353537\t[A]  Data is Archived as Project No. 201, Media No. 566.  [B]  CRF Hard Drive.  [C]  Species data available in Recorder 6 CCW000280000000B Bullhead Monitoring, Wye \u0026amp; Usk Catchments 2007 - CCW- Report no. 818.\tCustodian - Master Reference Data Team - species Data is held in Recorder 6. Project Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis / Heather Garret. Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team.  Rivers Ecologist, NRW Area Officer, Llandrindod Wells, NRW\tThe bullhead Cottus gobio is declining throughout Europe (although still apparently common over its native range in the British Isles) and is listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive as a \\species of community interest\\\". Member states have the duty to designate Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) for such species and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within the SACs. There are six Welsh rivers designated as SACs for bullhead including the Wye and Usk. The Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) (now part of Natural Resources Wales) is responsible for monitoring the condition of bullhead populations within them and this study represents the first systematic quantitative sampling for bullhead in Welsh SACs for this purpose. The bullhead is a feature on the River Wye / Afon Gwy SACs and River Usk / Afon Wysg  SACs in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor bullhead populations to assess whether Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) management adequately protects that species. This project assessed the population attribute only. No judgement can be made as to whether bullhead is in favourable or unfavourable condition on the Rivers Wye and Usk until the three other mandatory attributes of water quality flow and river morphology have been assessed.  The monitoring was of populations of the freshwater fish Bullhead (Cottus gobio) on the River Usk and Wye SAC\u0027s in south-east Wales.\"\tPublic record\n17282\tEXT_DS124614\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.708Z\tRSPB: Marine Mammal Data (Cetaceans and seals) Holy Island, Anglesey (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363134\tCetaceanSightingsRecords2019_RSPB.xlsx. held in DMS as document  EVID-446-321 Seal survey 2019_RSPB.xlsx held in DMS as document EVID-446-320\tTom Stringell, Marine Mammal Specialist\tAd-hoc sighting records of cetaceans from Ellins Tower South Stack Anglesey. All records are taken from the window looking west.  Additional information such as behaviour direction of travel are also recorded and provided to SeaWatch Foundation. Ad-hoc sighting records of Grey Seals and total number of pups from North Stack to the far end of The Range also known as Penrhosfeilw.\tPublic record\n17430\tNRW_DS98737\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.479Z\tPhase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373337\tSpatial data available on X Drive: X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_peatland  and Y:\\\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\ Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Phase_2_Peatlands [Working versions, and draft data. Moved to X-drive when finished. GIS data also on DataMapWales].     Some Species Data is held in Recorder 6.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas. This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  01/01/2020 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).     [A]  Stored in Terrestrial Science Group on the server (Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\ PEATSURV)      [B]  Biological Data held in Recorder 6: See Data Format for more information (between 2004 to 2011)  - LPSW Brecknock CCW0007100000005     - LPSW Carmarthen and Dinefwr CCW0007100000006   - LPSW Ceredigion CCW0007100000007    - LPSW Clwyd CCW0007100000008  - LPSW East Gwynedd CCW0007100000009    - LPSW Gwent CCW0007100000010   - LPSW Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan CCW0007100000001   - LPSW Montgomeryshire CCW0007100000011    - LPSW Preseli \u0026amp; South Pembrokeshire CCW0007100000012     - LPSW Radnorshire CCW0007100000002    - LPSW West Glamorgan \u0026amp; Llanelli CCW0007100000003   - LPSW West Gwynedd CCW0007100000004     [C] All survey reports and quadrat data in spreadsheets are on DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/SitePages/Home.aspx; species records (quadrat data) https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/Data%20Tables/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx.     [D] Most photos are on Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\ PEATSURV , but those from last 2 years are in a series of OneDrive folders, as NRW still doesn\u0027t have a workable solution for data photos.\tInternal Custodian: Kathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species Team\tThis dataset is part of the Phase 2 Habitats surveys conducted across Wales. It consists of a detailed survey of plant communities in lowland peatland vegetation.  The survey work was split into two time periods with the first Phase 2 lowland peatland surveys being conducted between 1977 and 1982. There was then a break between this and the second tranche of surveys which have occurred from 2004 onwards. These more modern surveys were conducted on sites already covered on the 1997-1982 surveys and on newer identified sites. The surveys were conducted at Phase 2 (plant community) level of herbaceous mire habitats predominantly within the lowland environment of Wales.   Key project outputs include site-based assessments of community type distribution and ecology and evaluative accounts of vegetation and its condition.   Strategic outputs include Area of Search/regional/all Wales synthesis and analysis of data to inform site selection conservation and restoration.   This part of the Phase 2 survey just includes species datasets collected by Countryside Council for Wales and Natural Resources Wales in-house expert Phase 2 survey team.  Peer-based quality assurance is carried out within the team and by CCW\u0027s Peatland Specialist. Regular meetings are held to ensure consistent community and species determination and survey protocol application.      The primary objective of the survey is to characterise and evaluate the vegetation cover of lowland peatlands in Wales to ensure the identification protection and management of important peatland sites.  All Wales coverage although sites vary in size and complexity.   The survey programme is almost wholly confined to lowland mires i.e. those below the upper functional limit of agricultural enclosure. A few mires above this limit may be considered where they display essentially lowland characteristics or where survey would provide additional critical information for rare communities. The survey encompasses all lowland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) supporting at least one independently qualifying peatland feature. The only exceptions to this are (i) the largest of the raised mires (namely Cors Fochno Cors Caron and Fenns Whixall Bettisfield Wem and Cadney Mosses) and (ii) any sites for which recent reliable information collected in a manner broadly comparable to that of the current survey is already available. Non-statutory sites are included if they have peatland features considered likely to be of individually qualifying status or if they have a notable mire element. A 0.5 ha threshold for individual or groups of communities has been used to help prioritise sites for survey.\tPublic record\n18131\tNRW_DS115641\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.831Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Assessing Water Courses of Plain to Montane Levels with the Ranunculion Fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion Vegetation of the River Wye SAC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363431\tAssociated data and reports are held in the NRW DMS Reports Archive \u0026gt; CCW Environmental Monitoring Reports KNOW-911039132-53  CCW EMR 39 - Assessment of the condition of water courses of plain to montane levels with the ranunculion fluitantis and callitricho batrachion vegetation of the River Wye SAC  On the DMS:  KNOW-911039132-53  CCW EMR 39 - Assessment of the condition of water courses of plain to montane levels with the ranunculion fluitantis and callitricho batrachion vegetation of the River Wye SAC Held on the CCW H Drive:  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\39 Ranunculion of the River Wye SAC\\Report\tInternal custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe single habitat listed in Annex I of the Habitats Directive which is a primary reason for selection of the River Wye as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is of Water courses of plain to montane levels with submerged or floating vegetation of the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion (Habitat 3260). The purpose of this data capture was to assess the condition of the Water Courses of Plain to Montane Levels with the Ranunculion Fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion Vegetation. The aim of the work was to assess the conservation condition of this habitat. This dataset presents the results of the Common Standards Monitoring Assessment (CSM) of the River Wye and selected tributaries. The assessment was performed based on data collected in 1995 and in 2005. The CSM assessment was based purely on macrophyte survey data as these were the only comparable data available from the 1995 surveys.\tPublic record\n17673\tNRW_DS109882\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.673Z\tSaltmarsh Features of the Severn Estuary 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383832\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media214 Archived as project 130 media 214\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Severn Estuary. The purpose of this data capture was to provide a detailed NVC description of Severn Estuary saltmarsh and an account of distribution and NVC associations of several nationally scarce and one locally scarce species. The survey also provided a preliminary conservation assessment for the Severn Estuary potential Special Area of Conservation (pSAC) and line transects as baseline data for future remote sensing studies.\tPublic record\n17174\tEXT_DS121682\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.652Z\tThe Crown Estate: Offshore Sand and Gravel Resources (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363832\tX:\\Physical\\Minerals\\Offshore Sand and Gravel Resources.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of aggregates across the welsh waters. Natural Resources Wales has access to this dataset through a bespoke licence issued by The Crown Estate as part of this access to the dataset.  The UK has one of the largest dredging industries in the world. Marine-sourced aggregates make a vital contribution to UK aggregate supply for the construction sector and for coastal protection and land reclamation. However activities on the UK Continental Shelf (UKCS) are currently undergoing significant change due to a changing planning regime brought about by the Marine and Coastal Access Act 2009 and the Marine (Scotland) Act 2010 and increased pressure on the sea bed from new developments such as renewable energy. As a result to enhance understanding of the distribution of marine mineral resources The Crown Estate (TCE) as owners of these resources has commissioned British Geological Society (BGS) to produce maps of sand and gravel as well as other mineral resources such as coal metallic minerals and evaporites for the UKCS. These maps provide spatial data for use in strategic planning for marine minerals and form an accessible information-base to assist protection of these non-renewable resources against sterilisation.\tPublic record\n17668\tNRW_DS109886\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.548Z\tSaltmarsh features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) - Dyfi 1996\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383836\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Dyfi 1996 H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media901to1000\\Media902 Archived as project 249 media 902\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau - Dyfi 1996. The main objective of the survey was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map the unusual brackish water saltmarsh communities on the seaward edge of the marsh as well as characterising some of the transitional vegetation types and the glycophytic communities on the middle and upper marsh.  Data collected by trained personnel digitised by contractors validated by NRW corrections made by contractors validated again by NRW corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist.\tPublic record\n18955\tNRW_DS124903\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.559Z\tNRW Open Water Quality Archive\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393033\tKiWQM\tMartin Stephens\tNRW’s water quality archive provides data from samples collected around Wales including coastal estuary rivers lakes ponds canals sewage discharges trade discharges pollution investigation points and waste sites.   NRW holds data goes back to 1962 whereas the open data product contains data from 2000 onwards. The open data product is extracted from NRW’s Water Quality database (KiWQM) and updated every month.   Some field measurements are taken in situ at the sampling station whereas some samples are analysed in laboratories to measure aspects of the water quality. Once samples are taken they must be processed and verified before they are added to the water quality archive. Therefore there is a delay between samples being taken and the data being made available. Data may also be subject to change after publication but the archive will be updated each month to include corrections.\tPublic record\n18389\tNRW_DS119099\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.342Z\tMarine Licence Archive\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303939\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine licence archive lines WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine licence archive points WSG84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine licence archive poly WGS84.lyr  Corporate Version Y:\\Marine_Marine Licence apps_Marine Licence apps.gdb Working Version - updated regularly by Marine Team\tEmmer Litt\tThe records contain coordinate data associated with historic and expired marine licence applications in Wales. The data allows the location of applications to be mapped to assess any in combination effects with other proposals.   Data related to current marine licence applications are held as a separate dataset (NRW_DS116807)\tPublic record\n18863\tNRW_DS124753\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.885Z\tNational Flood Asset Dataset for Wales: NRW flood Assets held in AMX-Flood\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373533\tAMX\tRichard Kelland - now moved to new role\tmetadata to be provided by custodian.   AMX flood asset data store contains data that has been split into three types according to data owner (NRW EA others)\tPrivate record\n18405\tNRW_DS117519\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.325Z\tUrban Tree Cover 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137353139\tX:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2013\\Urban Trees Point 2013.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2013\\Urban Trees Poly 2013.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Land Categories 2009.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\Urban Extent 2013.lyr\tCustodian:  John Briggs, Landscape Architect, PPG Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW.\tThis spatial dataset relates to Phase 3 (2013) and consists of 3 individual layers being Points (individual trees) polygons (groups of trees) and urban extents (the extent of the study areas). The technical report (see Related Titles and lineage) shows what is covered in each Phase (page 25) and provides more detail on the methodology behind the different layers in the dataset in Appendix A2.1 (page 132).\tPublic record\n18183\tNRW_DS116230\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.199Z\tSource Protection Zones (SPZ) Merged\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323330\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPZ Source Protection Zones Merged.lyr\tDave Jones- Technical Specialist GW\tSource Protection Zones [Merged] have been created as public facing boundaries where individual groundwater bodies within Source Protection Zones (SPZ) have been dissolved so only the outer boundaries are shown. SPZs are defined around large and public potable groundwater abstraction sites. The purpose of SPZs is to provide additional protection to safeguard drinking water quality through constraining the proximity of an activity that may impact upon a drinking water abstraction.\tPublic record\n18460\tNRW_DS119292\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.088Z\tPeat Top Ten Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323932\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\Peat Top Ten Sites.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset comprises the National Assessment top 10 sites for potential peat restoration.  Peatland in pristine or good condition provides a range of critical ecosystem services including biodiversity carbon storage and sequestration regulation of stream base flows water runoff and downstream flood peaks and nutrient regulation and retention. Peatlands are also sinks and sources of several natural greenhouse gases particularly carbon dioxide (CO2) and methane (CH4). More than 75% of deep peat soils in Wales are covered in semi-natural vegetation. Most of this is upland blanket bog with significant amounts of fen and flush and locally lowland raised bog. These are all UK Biodiversity Action Plan priority habitats with UK and Welsh targets for habitat management and restoration.   In order to deliver the habitat restoration targets set out in the Welsh Environmental Strategy there is a need to progressively restore semi-natural habitat in afforested areas. Restoration measures should seek to better integrate the key objectives of nature conservation soil carbon protection carbon sequestration and water quality.\tPublic record\n19625\tNRW_DS161305\t2024-10-04T07:36:33.264Z\tSurvey of the Black Bog Ant Formica picea on Cors Goch, Llanllwch SSSI in 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333035\tData Stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder6\tMichael Howe - Michael.Howe@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThis survey was commissioned to determine the current status and distribution of Black Bog Ant on Cors Goch Llanllwch south of the railway line in August 2024 by undertaking a count of solaria.\tPublic record\n17557\tNRW_DS102416\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.239Z\tCeredigion Phase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032343136\tKathryn Birch Sept 2021 advised Part of Phase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales, so doesn\u0027t need a separate OLIB entry\tInternal Custodian: Kathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species Team\tSo far (2007) only 20 survey sites with 200-300 quadrats and roughly 35-40 field aerials and the same for field notes.  Temporal coverage: 2003 to present (2007) ongoing.\tPrivate record\n19007\tNRW_DS125285\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.362Z\tFirebreaks in NRW South Central Region\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323835\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Firebreaks in NRW South Central Region.lyr\tGeraint Price Senior Officer Forest Operations – South Central (+ Rebekah Davies – Technical Support Officer Forest Operations)\tDataset showing the location of firebreaks created and managed by Natural Resources Wales operational staff in the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. The data covers the NRW South Central Operational Region.  Data was collected so firebreak locations can be shared with South Wales Fire and Rescue Service to aid in the tackling wildfires.\tPublic record\n18209\tNRW_DS116265\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.307Z\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) Sensitive Areas - Nitrates\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323635\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTD Sensitive Areas - Nitrates.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Julie Boswell, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tThis dataset is a shapefile showing the extent of Urban Wastewater Treatment Directive (91/271/EEC) (UWWTD) sensitive areas (nitrate).   The Urban Wastewater Treatment Directive (91/271/EEC) regulates the collection and treatment of waste water from homes and from industry. In the UK the directive is implemented through the Urban Wastewater Treatment regulations 1994. Under these regulations water bodies that are used as sources for drinking water and which have high nitrate concentrations (as defined by Council Directive 75/440/EEC of 16th June 1975) should be designated as sensitive areas by Defra.   This dataset consists of rivers currently designated as UWWTD nitrate sensitive areas.\tPublic record\n20547\tNRW_DS161354\t2025-04-08T12:38:59.712Z\tRapid Evidence Assessment of the Impacts of Organic Manures on the Water Quality of Rivers\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333534\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/gs/wg/SACWQP/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fwaterman%2Fgs%2Fwg%2FSACWQP%2FPhase%202%20products%2FTechnical%20Products%2FWorkstream%204%2FTask%203%20Evidence%20Needs%2FRapid%20Evidence%20Assessment%2FProduct%203%20%2D%20Final%20Report%20%28Mar%202024%29\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000289C178199C7C343A0E69569780F56CD\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BF267B241%2DA6BD%2D465C%2D9E90%2D12B892E2F18D%7D\tTimothy Jones. Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting\tThe principal aim of this study was to improve Natural Resources Wales’ understanding of how organic manures particularly from the poultry sector can impact the nutrient status of rivers with a focus on phosphorus (P). The methodology used to produce this Rapid Evidence Assessment (REA) included a systematic review of relevant literature to establish an evidence base which was used to address primary and secondary Research Questions (RQs). The scope of the study was restricted to answering the RQs only and as a REA the literature searches and critical appraisal of the literature were not as comprehensive as a full systemic review.  This metadata is created for the finalised evidence base created from evidence reviews and research questions.\tPublic record\n17480\tNRW_DS99415\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.324Z\tNational Trails\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343135\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\National Trails (Walked line).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Chris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tNational Trails are long distance walking cycling and horse riding routes through the best landscapes in Wales. After World War II the desire to keep areas of Britain \u0027special\u0027 and to protect them from post-war development led to the establishment of Long Distance Routes (now called National Trails).  Each Trail has a Trail Partnership made up of the local authority or national park responsible for the path on the ground. There is a dedicated National Trail Officer with responsibility for keeping the Trail up to the high standards set for National Trails. Maintenance work is carried out by the local highway authorities together with landowners often with the help of volunteers.\tPublic record\n17477\tNRW_DS98766\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.385Z\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373636\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SAC Special Areas of Conservation.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\cSAC Candidate Special Areas of Conservation.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas SAC.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\pSAC Proposed Special Areas of Conservation.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tThis spatial dataset contains digital boundaries of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales. The EC Habitats and Species Directive came into effect in 1992 with the aim of maintaining a rich variety of wildlife by protecting vulnerable habitats and the plants and animals they support. The Habitat Directive together with the 1979 Birds Directive will lead to the establishment of a series of sites throughout Europe that will be known as \u0027Natura 2000\u0027. This represents the cream of European sites and will ensure their survival for future generations. SACs have been designated over a number of years from 1993 to the present day and are on-going.  These sites have been mapped on paper since the mid 1990s and then there was a major review from 2000 with the \u0027SAC Moderation Project\u0027 when the data was captured digitally for the first time.\tPublic record\n17957\tNRW_DS114460\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.325Z\tNatural Heritage Evidence to Support Strategic Planning for Marine Renewable Energy\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343630\tThe data is archived as project No.309 and media No.1230\tInternal Custodian: Marine Spatial Planning, Energy and Infrastructure Team (Strategic Planning Group), NRW        Alternative Title: Tidal mapping project    Alternative Title: Tystiolaeth treftadaeth naturiol i gefnogi cynllunio strategol ar gyfer ynni adnewyddadwy\u0027r mor yng Nghymru.     Temporal Extent: 2011-06-30 ; 2010-03-01/2011-02-28    Frequency of Update: As Needed Extent: Wales Universal Transverse Mercator (WGS84) No Spatial Element Data Format Type: Documents [A] The final report and summary leaflet in Microsoft Word and Adobe PDF formats.  [B] A series of final GIS layers on which the maps in the report are based in MapInfo format.  These are accompanied with series of word documents detailing the individual layers and their attributes.   [C] A full set of images produced in jpg format. Originator: Countryside Council for Wales (CCW)    Originator: Sea Watch Foundation    Originator: Joint Nature Conservancy Committee (JNCC)    Originator: ABP Marine Environmental Research Ltd    Originator: Wildfowl And Wetlands Trust (WWT)     Originator: Land Use Consultants\tThe Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is committed to working with regulators and developers to support the development of marine renewable energy in locations and using technologies that avoid significant adverse environmental impacts. The UK government is committed to producing 15 percent of all energy requirements from renewable sources by 2020. With approximately 1.5 percent of UK energy currently sourced from renewables significant expansion will be needed to meet this target. As far as marine sources of renewable energy are concerned UK government has to date relied on deployment of offshore wind to contribute to UK renewable energy targets and indications suggest that commercial scale (10 to 100MW) arrays of wave and tidal technologies are expected to commence largely post 2015 (DECC 2010). CCW have undertaken to produce and share key synthesised evidence relating to Welsh natural heritage interests in the form of GIS-based data layers to be taken into account by strategic planning process for marine energy devices (wave and tidal stream).  The baseline layers highlight the breadth of natural heritage interests that need to be taken into account in strategic planning processes.  The interpreted layers communicate the relative levels of risks to Wales\u0027 marine natural heritage from device deployment.  Evidence layers have been produced for Welsh inshore territorial waters from Mean High Water Spring baseline out to 12nm. The metadata describes the collation exercise which was undertaken between Mar 2010 and Feb 2011.  Temporal coverage of each of the baseline datasets is documented elsewhere. The environmental effects of marine renewable energy devices are not well understood and information about the marine environment is often limited so this work is based on the best available information at the time of the study.\tPublic record\n18845\tNRW_DS124656\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.186Z\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Turbines\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363536\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Turbines.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm (as built) wind turbine locations. This shows all 76 turbines on the Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm. Each turbine is 2MW in power. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tPublic record\n19033\tNRW_DS125216\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.482Z\tLarge Raised Reservoir (Registered information)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323136\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Reservoirs\tMatthew O\u0027Brien, Senior Specialist Advisor, Reservoir Safety Group\tThis dataset contains details of Large Raised Reservoirs.    Natural Resources Wales (NRW) collects and maintains data on reservoirs designed or capable of holding more than 10000 cubic metres of water above the natural level of any part of the land adjoining them defined as \u0027large raised reservoirs\u0027 under the Reservoirs Act 1975. Two types of reservoir are maintained within the register:    - Impounding; or  - Non-Impounding.    This dataset is an extract from NRW’s Cronfeydd/Reservoir Database. It contains registered information about the physical characteristics and inspection regimes of the dam.\tPublic record\n17859\tNRW_DS111625\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.693Z\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: Mid-Wales ( Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons - BIS )\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131363235\tX:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to BIS point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to BIS region.lyr   BIS LERC hold and manage the top copy of some NRW owned species data. Some of this data has its own metadata field (Contains Field) and other data doesn\u0027t. All NRW owned data which is managed by BIS is available to download on the NBN Gateway.   116719 - CCW Montgomeryshire General Bat Files WA0001500000001O\tInternal Custodian: Monica Jones, Team Leader, Data Services External Custodian: BIS  Additional information is available from BIS www.b-i-s.org  Janet Imlach janet@bis.org.uk Unit 4, 6 The Bulwark, Brecon, Powys www.bis.org.uk   Andrew Lucas is the NRW contact with BIS\tAll records collated from species records mobilised from former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regional files held at Newtown Abergavenny and Llandrindod Wells offices. This dataset was created from species records extracted computerised and collated from paper records held at former CCW\u0027s Regional office files covering the Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park area. The work was carried out under contract by ten Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park (BIS) staff members within the NRW offices between 2003 and 2009 as part of CCW Data Mobilisation Project. However coverage was not complete as follows:- Newtown: completed 10Km square files and partial completed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) files Llandrindod Wells: General files complete. All SSSI files complete Abergavenny: 10km square files complete. SSSI complete. Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and National Nature Reserve (NNR) files not completed Records were entered into Recorder 2002 or Recorder 6 databases.  The majority of records are from Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park or within a 5km buffer. Many records are for designated sites (including SSSIs SACs National Nature Reserves etc) however there are also large numbers of records from the wider countryside.  The grid references entered will be at resolution of original record and could be 2 or 4 figures however the majority of the records have 6 figure grid-references. Some grid references may be from site centroids rather than actual location of the species in question or may have been estimated from map. The purpose of this data capture was to create an electronic archive of species data held on CCW regional files and mobilise this data through BIS.  Confidence in data: The source of the original data has not been checked however much of the data originates from CCW staff or other trusted sources. Most of this data has not gone through external verification process but BIS will continue to work with VC recorders to verify records. Recorder has its own validation checks and will not accept data without who what where when present. Some of the grid references entered could be \u0027estimated from map\u0027 if they were not provided. The grid references entered will be at resolution of original record and could be 2 or 4 figures. To avoid duplication each record was checked to see if already in BIS database using MapInfo GIS layer. At this time the operator could check that the grid reference matches location name and is within designated site boundary if appropriate. Operators checked their own entries for accuracy as resources did not allow for a second operator check. CCW staffs were on hand to help with handwriting or taxonomic issues. Most of the operators on this project originally trained in BIS entering data and had biological or ecological backgrounds. BIS has recently worked through the surveys to remove outlier records and resolve grid reference issues where possible and will continue to work to remove any duplication.\tPublic record\n17180\tEXT_DS121664\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.880Z\tWelsh Government: Active Travel Designated Localities (Wales) (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363634\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Active Travel\\Active Travel Designated Localities.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the location of designated localities in relation to active travel routes. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have been granted access to this dataset from Welsh Government under and Open Government Licence.\tPublic record\n17965\tNRW_DS114562\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.562Z\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Moths and Butterflies)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353632\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tThis is a collated list of moth and butterfly species names in English Welsh and scientific Latin. Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff. Methods of data capture: Most of the translations have been created and/or verified by Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd for CCW the records have been extensively cleaned by the Natural History Museum Confidence in data: All translations have been carefully checked and approved however with the English and Welsh names as in any language there will be multiple derivations and regional variations. Also the scientific terminology changes periodically as work on taxonomy progresses.\tPrivate record\n20332\tNRW_DS161344\t2025-02-25T14:51:01.372Z\tNorth Wales Operations Flooded Properties\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333434\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flooded Properties North.lyr\tGwenno Talfryn - Advisor 1, Warning \u0026amp; Informing\tThis data is compiled from all reports of property flooding from Main Rivers and the Sea in North Wales from 1993 that we have within our records and reports from North Powys from 2019 onwards.\tPublic record\n17111\tEXT_DS119114\t2024-05-28T12:11:56.507Z\tKeep Wales Tidy: Green Coast Awards (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313134\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Green Coast Awards.lyr\tHelena Skyes\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of beaches with Green Coast Awards across Wales. The Green Coast Award recognises those beaches that are \u0027hidden gems\u0027 - the beautiful untouched rugged beaches that line so much of the Welsh coast.  The award recognises the excellent water quality and unspoiled environment but unlike a Blue Flag Award beach these sites won\u0027t have the infrastructure and intensive management generally associated with more traditional seaside resorts. The Green Coast Award is important in recognising all of Wales\u0027 top beach destinations with our small (but mighty!) country boasting 19 Green Coast Awards during 2018.\tPublic record\n18294\tNRW_DS116308\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.483Z\tEnvironmental Permitting Regulations - Industrial Sites\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333038\tPermitting and Licencing  System (PALS)\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest - Permitting Centre Wales, Cardiff\tThe Environmental Permitting Regulations amongst other things implement the IPPC (Integrated Pollution Prevention and Control) Directive (EC/61/96) in England and Wales.  Facilities covered by this legislation are known as Installations and generally have significant releases to air land or water or carry out certain larger scale waste management activities.\tPublic record\n17335\tNRW_DS24303\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.875Z\tSaltmarsh Survey of Great Britain\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333033\tThese are stored in the Maes-y-Ffynnon library (Classmark Research and Survey 17).\tInternal Custodian: Peter Rhind. Coastal Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSalt marshes in Great Britain represent one of a series of habitats confined largely to the coastline. The usually develop on intertidal land within the range of the normal spring tides where colonisation by halophytic plants i.e. plants which are adapted to high salinity is and able to withstand immersion in seawater takes place. The extent of the saltmarsh also depends on the amount of sediment available for the position together with a combination of physical factors such as degree of exposure tidal range and the slope of the land. Salt marsh communities may also be found in the splash same along clifftops. However the salt marsh types are not included in this project. This dataset consists of inventories of sites and vegetation each the major coastal habitats Great Britain specifically salt marshes. Salt marshes were selected for the first study in this series for two reasons. At the time the habitat was rare and not well studied and was one of the most threatened of coastal habitats. Consequently the salt marsh survey of Great Britain was set up in 1981 with the following objectives: 1. To review existing information on the distribution and abundance of salt marshes Great Britain 2. To identify omissions in our knowledge of the resource as a whole; and 3. To fill the gaps in order to increase our overall knowledge of the salt marsh resource and therefore to produce as complete as possible a description of the resource to a minimum standard.\tPublic record\n18280\tNRW_DS116309\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.202Z\tOSPAR River Monitoring Programme\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333039\tData is stored in WISKI Database\tCustodian: Claire Popp Environmental Reporting Team\tThe Convention for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic know as the OSPAR Convention 1998 (Oslo Convention 1972 and Paris Convention 1974) is an agreement signed by a number of European countries (including the United Kingdom) to protect the quality of the North East Atlantic.   The objective of the OSPAR Convention is to take all possible steps to prevent and eliminate pollution entering the sea. The OSPAR Convention provides standard methods for estimating the inputs of selected pollutants to the sea by using fixed sampling schedule and a standard input calculation.     In Wales all principal rivers are sampled monthly (12 times a year) just upstream of their tidal limits. For those rivers carrying the heaviest contaminant loads the sampling frequency maybe increased beyond the minimum of 12. Major trade effluents and sewage effluents to estuaries or coastal waters are also sampled monthly to assess direct discharges to marine waters. Flow values of discharges direct to estuary or sea are sometimes provided by the operators themselves. This is usually part of statutory monitoring arrangements. The aim of these programmes is to assess the level of contamination entering the sea from Wales (the \u0027load\u0027) and to chart the progress in the reduction of this load.   This dataset contains the results and data obtained from these programmes.\tPublic record\n18257\tNRW_DS116275\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.982Z\tRisk of Flooding from Rivers and Seas (RoFRS) Property Flood Likelihood Category\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323735\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116275\tCustodian: Nick Steele, Technical Specialist (FRA), Flood Risk Analysis/FCRM / (previously) Mark Pugh, Flood Risk Manager – Assets and Reservoir Enforcement, FORM Metadata: Data services team\tThis dataset is a product of a national assessment of flood risk for Wales produced using local expertise. This dataset is produced using [Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Sea] which shows the chance of flooding from rivers and/or the sea based on cells of 50m. Each cell is allocated one of four flood risk categories taking into account flood defences and their condition. This dataset uses OS data to assign one of four flood risk categories to each property based simply on the category allocated to the cell that the property is in. Individual addresses are not provided but OS referencing is included to enable the data to be linked to address databases.\tPublic record\n18128\tNRW_DS115630\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.698Z\tFishMap Môn Project - Anglesey\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363330\tAssociated data is held at:   X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fishmap_Mon  ,  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Analysis .   Generalised Data may only be available on the legacy CCW GIS archive (M:\\ drive).   For further details please refer to metadata for each of the individual output layers.\tInternal Custodian:  Colin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW) the Welsh Government and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities. It was jointly funded 75% / 25% between the European Fisheries Fund and the Welsh Government.   The pilot project focuses on an area around Anglesey in North Wales covering the waters out to 12 nautical miles. The Project area is located between Nefyn to the west and the Great Orme to the east and extends out to the Welsh 12 nautical mile limit. It encompasses an area of approximately 265 kmsq of which approximately 3% are Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) and 3% are Special Protection Areas (SPAs).   The main aim of the project is to survey commercial recreational and charter boat fishermen map the distribution and intensity of fishing activity around Anglesey and combine it with seabed habitat maps and their sensitivities to fishing activity.  The project aimed to deliver the following outputs:  - Mapped fishing activity for a range of commercial and recreational fishing activity in the Project area. Maps have been created following surveys of approximately 50 commercial fishers and 600 recreational fishers.  - The FishMap Môn Guidance Tool; an easy to use web based mapping application allowing stakeholders to view fishing activity data or hypothetical fishing scenarios in combination with seabed habitats and their sensitivity.  - An evaluation of how learning from FishMap Môn could be applied to managing the marine environment in Wales.  - A literature review to explore global case studies where an Ecosystem Approach has been applied to managing the marine environment. The review will identify lessons learnt and how they relate to Wales  - Lessons in stakeholder engagement and collaborative working with the fishing industry The project also aimed to develop a trial method for gathering fishing activity data from recreational and commercial fishing communities; and to develop and test an indicative web based guidance tool for the pilot area using GIS Techniques.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning Portal.\tPublic record\n17671\tNRW_DS109900\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.473Z\tAssessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales. Phase II: Assessment of Environmental Parameters\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393030\ta) Paper report held by CCW HQ library, digital version on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) b) MapInfo layer on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) c) Excel files on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) d) Photographs on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry)\tContact: Liz Howe (used to be Jan Sherry Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage ecology landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales.  This datasets consists of the soil chemical analysis soil physical analysis and soil biological analysis on 8 sites chosen from the 15 sites surveyed in the Assessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales 2006 survey.  The purpose of this data capture was to look at edaphic factors underlying the development of coal spoil communities of high conservation value. Edaphic factor can be defines as an abiotic factor relating to the physical or chemical composition of the soil found in a particular area. For example very alkaline soil may be an edaphic factor limiting the variety of plants growing in a region. South Wales valley; current dataset covers Torfaen Rhondda Cynon Taf Caerphilly and Merthyr County Borough Councils (CBCs).\tPublic record\n17956\tNRW_DS114565\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.256Z\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Dragonflies)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353635\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tThis is a collated list of dragonfly species names in English Welsh and scientific Latin. Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff.\tPrivate record\n19124\tNRW_DS125591\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.583Z\tRiver Restoration Project - Protected Area Lake Catchments\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353931\tCurrently Stored at Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Tristan_Hatton-Ellis\\Lakes_Catchments\\Lakes Catchments.gdb\\Protected_Area_Lake_Catchments   # Being relocated to X:Drive under river restoration\tTristan Hatton-Ellis - Lead Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species\tDervied from Freshwater Habitats Trust Project (Important Areas for Ponds (IAPs) - Freshwater Habitats TrustFreshwater Habitats Trust). The Important Areas for Ponds (IAP) concept was proposed and developed by Freshwater Habitats Trust to raise awareness of geographic regions that support ponds of national or international biodiversity importance.\tPublic record\n17224\tEXT_DS121984\t2024-05-28T12:17:04.413Z\tWelsh Government: Strategic Regeneration Areas (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393834\tX:\\Social Economic\\Regeneration Areas\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis data collation is owned and managed by Welsh Government and licenced to Natural Resources Wales for use. There are various areas across Wales that have been identified as Strategic Regeneration Areas. Strategic Regenerations Areas site under the Regeneration Framework which has invested in regeneration schemes. Strategic Regeneration Areas fit in with the Wales Spatial Plan and the Well-being of Future Generations Targets which are about the sustainable development and improving wellbeing and quality of life by integrating social economic and environmental objectives.\tPublic record\n18997\tNRW_DS124916\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.114Z\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) TRaC Waterbody Phytoplankton data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393136\tData is stored in WISKI\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Bianca Bacchi, Senior Marine Monitoring Officer  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer\tThis data collates all the Phytoplankton samples collected within Wales from 2007 onwards that have been used for the WFD TRaC Phytoplankton assessment tool. This methodology is based on the principle that additional nutrients (especially nitrogen) can alter the amount of phytoplankton in transitional waters. Persistent and high counts of phytoplankton taxa are also seen as a measure of disturbance in the phytoplankton community.  Phytoplankton samples are usually sent to a specialist marine contractor for Species Identity and abundance (two labs have been used in this time period; CEFAS 2007 – 2009 \u0026amp; APEM 2010 – onwards). Phytoplankton samples are analysed (identified and counted) using the Utermöhl method.  Phytoplankton cells are identified to the lowest practical taxonomic level possible. UK WFD authorities use a revised phytoplankton list (refer to the relevant WFD Agency for the current list). The resultant data is processed through a phytoplankton tool to provide a phytoplankton assessment that can be used to WFD Classification of TraC water bodies. Further information is available from the UK-TAG WFD website.\tPublic record\n17154\tEXT_DS119283\t2024-05-28T12:14:11.154Z\tSustrans: The National Cycle Network (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323833\tData is stored in the 3rd Party geodatabase.  It may be accessed through:  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Sustrans Cycle Network Link.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Sustrans National Route.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Sustrans Regional route.lyr\tGIS Service Desk   Was - Jill Meyer, Recreation and Data Access Advisor, PPG, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset wholly owned by Sustrans Cymru showing the location of their cycle network national routes and regional routes. Collectively these are known as The National Cycle Network. The National Cycle Network is a series of safe traffic-free paths and quiet on-road cycling and walking routes that connect to every major town and city. The National Cycle Network is made up of 13400 miles of walking and cycling routes across the UK. This includes a mixture of quiet lanes on-road routes and traffic-free paths. Natural Resources Wales has access to this data under licence.\tPublic record\n19331\tNRW_DS161279\t2024-07-16T11:39:04.105Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macrophytes - Geospatial\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323739\tData Held in KiEco - a FME Report has been created to export this data to the GIS.   X:drive locations   X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macrophyte Sample Metric.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macrophyte Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macrophyte Taxa.lyr\tJennifer Owen - Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis and Reporting\tMacrophytes (water plants visible to the naked eye) can indicate the impact of pressures such as increased nutrients flow pressures channel alterations etc. The species present and their abundance give an indication as to the health of a river.   This dataset is made up of  site information sample metrics and taxa details for each survey conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales since 1993. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\tPublic record\n18098\tNRW_DS115581\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.909Z\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Teifi Catchment Area (2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353831\tData is held at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\05 Otter in the Afon Teifi\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Modified to test overnight export to XML from OLIB\tThis dataset was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (Aberystwyth) to investigate the current and potential breeding success of the otter (Lutra lutra) in the Afon Teifi Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and catchment; report on the present condition and conservation status of the species; define conservation objectives; and to design an efficient monitoring methodology for future reporting. The aims of the project are: a) To report on the current and potential distribution and breeding success of the otter in the Afon Teifi cSAC and catchment; b) To produce conservation objectives using data obtained from the project; c) To design an efficient monitoring methodology for future reporting.\tPublic record\n20046\tEXT_DS161326\t2025-01-10T13:52:27.289Z\tHistoric Floodplain Meadows along the River Wye (3rd Party Date)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333236\tWaiting for X:Drive load\tStuart Smith - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA shapefile comprising a single layer containing polygons each representing a floodplain meadow (FPM) as spatial information with attributes. The shapefile covers a study area comprising part of the catchment of the River Wye. the selected stretch covers the main channel of the Wye from Builth Wells to Llangurig. The study area was defined by a 1km buffer along the River Wye excluding minor tributaries.\tPrivate record\n17942\tNRW_DS112503\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.970Z\tHabitat Mapping for Conservation and Management of the Southern Irish Sea (HABMAP)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353033\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\HABMAP\\   or z:\\Archive\\legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\HABMAP   or Archived as Project 275 and Media 1029 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1001to1100\\Media 1029)\tInternal Contact: Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe HABMAP project was set up in response to the need for better spatial awareness of habitat distributions in the Southern Irish Sea. This work produced habitat maps of the seabed using novel predictive modelling techniques. This dataset is related to the predictive modelling only.    The HABMAP Extension Project has built on the methods developed during the original project and has repeated the modelling work using higher resolution / improved input datasets to help increase the accuracy of the predictive map outputs. The modelling work has also been extended to cover all of Welsh waters (previously cut-off at the Interreg funding boundary) notably including the Dee and Severn estuaries.    The purpose of this data capture was to provide seabed habitat maps that could be used for conservation and management. Project outputs might be used in strategic planning decision making for offshore developments Marine Protected Area selection sensitivity mapping and mapping essential fish habitats. However because of the way the  has been produced and the fact that some data has been modelled and derived the maps are not appropriate to act as the sole evidence for any specific planning or regulatory decision or assessment without further supporting studies or evidence.    The project boundaries were as follows: Southern Irish Sea- land-based boundaries include the whole Welsh coast to the English border on the east side of the Dee Estuary in the north and the whole Severn Estuary and Bristol Channel coastline in the south extending as far as Morte Point (east of Ifracombe) in England. The southern project boundary then extends offshore (skirting the northern tip of Lundy) across to a point approximate 60km west of Waterford on the Irish coast including the whole SE Ireland coastline and offshore banks as well as parts of the Celtic Sea. The boundary then extends northwards along the Irish coast to a point approximately 40 km north of Dublin.\tPublic record\n18219\tNRW_DS116253\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.241Z\tEnvironmental Pollution Incidents\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323533\tNIRS up to 2016-02-29 WIRS from 2016-03-01 til present\tSean Moore, Incident Communications Centre Team Leader\tThis dataset contains details pollution incidents reported to Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Only substantiated and closed environmental protection incidents are included. Where these data indicate an incident occurred on a particular site or property no inference should be drawn that the site or property owner necessarily was responsible.\tPublic record\n19036\tNRW_DS125359\t2024-05-28T13:00:55.922Z\tNature and Us / Natur am Ni Phase 1 Survey Responses\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333539\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/manbus/PMO/V2050/Phase%201%20outputs/EDITED_Nature%20and%20Us%20survey%20responses_English%20Edited%20Copy.xlsx?web\u003d1     (Secure DMS Location)\tSerinde van Wijk, Lead Specialist Advisor Nature and Us,\tThis data was collected as part of the Nature and Us national conversation between March and May 2022. As part of this compain an online survey was available for any one in Wales to complete that sought to address five research outcomes: • what people identify as the critical issue for the future • if things went well being optimistic but realistic what would be a desirable  outcome • if things went wrong what factors would people worry about • what people think about different scenarios of the future • what people are willing to change. The survey asked individuals about what concerned them about the environment what action should be taken to help address climate change and the nature crises in Wales what actions they would like to prioritise and what changes they would like to see. A total of 3069 survey responses was received which are included in this dataset.\tPublic record\n17550\tNRW_DS101977\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.250Z\tHABMAP Predictive Habitat Maps of the Irish Sea\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393737\tData is archived as project number 274, media number 1014 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1001to1100).   Available on legacy drive: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\HABMAP\\Maps2).   Paper report available from NRW library (see library catalogue/related title field above).    Also available as PDF files on legacy drive: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\HABMAP\\Interreg Report3) HABMAP final report as above;  tif registered files on legacy shared drive: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Scienc\\MFSG\\Users\\HABMAP4).  MapInfo files Intended for CCW internal use only: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Users\\HABMAP\\ .   Image files available to download from HABMAP website (see online resource below).\tInternal Contact: Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe HABMAP project was set up in response to the need for better spatial awareness of habitat distributions in the Southern Irish Sea. This work produced habitat maps of the seabed using novel predictive modelling techniques. Two main components of the project were the mapping of seabed habitats using data collation and predictive modelling and the collection of new survey data. This was used to increase the knowledge of Irish Sea habitats and validate the maps produced by predictive modelling. There have be subsequent project developed from this HABMAP project which includes the re-modelling and larger project area is being covered to include all Welsh waters. The purpose of this data capture is to provide seabed habitat maps that could be used for conservation and management. Project outputs might be used in strategic planning decision making for offshore developments sensitivity mapping and mapping essential fish habitats. Confidence of predicted biotope maps was assessed and rated as explained on individual maps produced. The area covered in this project related to the southern Irish Sea.\tPublic record\n19099\tNRW_DS125471\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.718Z\tSurvey and Monitoring of Epiphytic Lichens around PAWS Sites in Brechfa Forest\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343731\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FPAWS%20Brechfa%20Forest\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tThis survey was designed to look at four sections of the forest not previously recorded for lichens. Oak ash and birch were selected to be surveyed and monitored in PAWS (Planted Ancient Woodland Sites) and AW (Ancient Woodland) areas of the forest.\tPublic record\n17163\tEXT_DS121846\t2024-05-28T12:15:05.036Z\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): 250k Onshore and Offshore Geology (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383436\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 250k Bedrock.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 250k Bedrock Off shore WGS84.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 250k Fault Line.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 250k Irishsea geology.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 250k Linear Bedrock Offshore WGS84.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 250k SeaBed Sediments WGS84  RCS.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS 250k SeaBed Sediments WGS84 FOLK.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) is a UK provider of geoscientific data information and knowledge. Natural Resources Wales has licenced access to a number of BGS datasets. This particular spatial data is a collation of licenced data from DiGMapGB-250 DigSBS250 and DigRock250 all of which depict the data at a scale of 1:250000.\tPublic record\n18603\tNRW_DS122460\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.519Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macroinvertebrates\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343630\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data. A copy of the latest upload file is held on the Y drive (to me moved to the NRW Archive storage location).  Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data\tInternal Custodian:  Harriet Robinson - Data Services Team.  Tim Jones - Enivironmental Reporting Team  SMEs Graham Rutt and Mel Lacan       Extraction complete, Data Sent to NBN Team 12/11/2019.  When confirmation that uploaded, add resource locator and put in public view\tFreshwater macroinvertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality and river flow requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance. This dataset is made up of the species abundance values from the freshwater river macroinvertebrates surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\tPublic record\n19064\tNRW_DS125404\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.409Z\tIthon river valley survey (paper records)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343034\toff-site archive box ref: CCW/SERNB/41\topendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThis is a paper record survey of 1980 of the river bank and aquatic habitat\tPrivate record\n18916\tNRW_DS124851\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.254Z\tUrban Tree Cover 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383531\tX:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\\tJohn Briggs\tThis study updates the data on Urban Tree Canopy Cover in Wales highlighting changes in the extent location and provision of urban tree cover since 2013. This spatial dataset relates to Phase 4 and consists of 2 individual layers being Points (individual trees) and polygons (groups of trees).\tPublic record\n17949\tNRW_DS114674\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.368Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363734\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived.   Data is archived as project 282, media 1098 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1001to1100)\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) was published back in 1994 and was the UK Government’s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro.  Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006 it is required that the a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027.   Priority marine habitats in Wales are those identified as BAP habitats Section 42 habitats or OSPAR habitats.  These habitats include: blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.  These datasets have been superseded.\tPrivate record\n17979\tNRW_DS114743\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.060Z\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Tideswept Channels (Archived 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373433\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Tidal Swept Channels.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working. Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities Tide swept channels Horse mussel beds Maerl beds Honeycomb worm reefs Seagrass beds Oyster beds Intertidal boulder communities Intertidal mudflats mud habitats in deep water Sheltered muddy gravels subtidal mixed muddy sediments Estuarine rocky habitats Peat and clay exposures Carbonate reefs Musculus discors beds Saline lagoons Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Tide-swept channels only. Tide-swept channels occur where the constricted coastline acts as a funnel.  They are found at the entrances to fjords lochs and lagoons between individual islands and between islands and the mainland.  The plentiful supply of food brought in on each tide supports rich and varied communities of marine life. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tPrivate record\n18611\tNRW_DS122454\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.800Z\tEnvironment (Wales) Act Section 7 and OSPAR: Marine Species\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343534\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Section 7 Species\tInternal Custodian 1: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Internal Custodian 2: Dave Tavner, Specialist Advisor – Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Mapping, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tUnder the Environment (Wales) Act (2016) Section 7 requires biodiversity lists to be produced. These lists include types of species which are of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 for the purpose of maintain and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This list supersedes the duty in Section 42 of the NERC Act 2006. In addition to this marine species can be protected under the OSPAR Convention.    The OSPAR convention has established a list of \u0027threatened and/or declining species and habitats\u0027 in the North-East Atlantic. This spatial dataset details the location of those marine species classed of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 under Section 7 and are regarded as \u0027threatened or declining\u0027 under OSPAR in Wales. These species include: Alkmaria Romijni tentacled lagoon worm Anotrichium barbatum bearded red seaweed Cruoria Cruoriiformis Edwardsia Timida burrowing anemone Eunicella Verrucosa pink sea fan  Grateloupia Dermocorynus montagnei Haliclystus auricula stalked jellyfish Hippocampus sp. Lithothamnion coralloides Coral maerl Lucernariopsis Campanulata stalked jellyfish Arctica Islandica Ocean Quahog Ostrea Edulis Padina Pavonica Palinurus Elephas Crawfish Phymatolithon calcareum common maerl and Tenellia Adspersa.\tPublic record\n19187\tNRW_DS000002\t2024-05-28T13:15:20.379Z\t\t\t\t\t\tPrivate record\n18621\tNRW_DS123234\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.582Z\tDrinking Water Protected Areas Lakes and River catchments 2021\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323334\tx:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Protected Areas Lakes.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Protected Areas River Catchments.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn, Specialist Advisor WQ\tAny waterbodies in Wales that are used to provide drinking water are designated as drinking water protected areas (DrWPAs). The designation aims to protect the water and prevent any deterioration in quality that could increase the required treatment of water to meet Drinking Water Standards. Drinking Water Protected Areas are required to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017.\tPrivate record\n18922\tNRW_DS124842\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.661Z\tRisk Levels Under Review\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383432\tCentral GIS Store X:\\Layer_Files\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tMark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)   Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Risk Level Under Review identifies locations where model data incorporated to the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map is under review and potentially liable to change within the next 6 months.\tPublic record\n18936\tNRW_DS124807\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.866Z\tMarine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) Pathways Heatmaps for Wales 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383037\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Marine INNS Pathway Heatmaps\tInternal Custodian: Chloe Powell-Jennings, Senior Advisor (EMFF Biosecurity Project Officer), Southwest Operations.   Internal Custodian: Katherine Griffith, Senior Marine Advisor, Marine Area Management \u0026amp; Advice Team\tHeatmaps (geodatabases) were created for invasive non-native species pathways in Welsh waters including commercial shipping recreational boating aquaculture fishing and offshore activities. Heatmaps were produced at both  2km x 2km and 5km x 5km resolutions and have associated symbology layers. An overall heatmap was also produced to highlight key areas which are likely most at risk from invasion of invasive non-native species. This assessment provides a valuable insight into hotspots related to each potential pathway of introduction the areas where the main areas of risk overlap for the different pathways and their overlap with protected areas. The outputs from this assessment can form the basis to underpin biosecurity planning.       It should be noted however that this was a national scale mapping exercise based only on freely available datasets which themselves have limitations and possible inaccuracies due to incomplete coverage both temporally and spatially. The scorings applied to the input datasets and weightings applied to the introduction pathways are also based on available literature and expert judgement.  Site-specific activities and conditions will always be an important consideration as well as temporal variations.\tPublic record\n19150\tNRW_DS125742\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.026Z\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Diatoms - Geospatial\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373432\tX:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Diatom Sample Metrics.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Diatom Site Details.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Diatom Taxa.lyr\tJennifer Owen Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer) Analysis and Reporting\tFreshwater diatoms (phytobenthos) are free-living microscopic plants that form a biofilm on submerged substrates such as rock/stone or large plants. They are used as an indicator of water quality because their silica shells are easy to identify under the microscope.   This dataset is made up of the relative abundance of different species within the sample from freshwater diatom surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\tPublic record\n18991\tNRW_DS124923\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.954Z\tRestoring marine and coastal habitats in Wales: identifying spatial opportunities and benefits\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393233\tMain GIS Data Layers are located in:   • X:\\Biological\\Habitat Restoration\\Marine Habitat   Further Mapping Packages for the report are stored in:   • Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Restoration\\ABPMer Restoration opportunities GIS data\\March\\Geodatabase\\Report_figure_mxd\tKate Griffith - Specialist Advisor Marine Sustainable Management of Natural Resources\tIn order to better understand opportunities to build resilience in the Welsh marine environment a report was commissioned by Natural Resources Wales to identify potential spatial opportunities to restore valuable marine habitats.     The report created spatial products (Arc Geo database) for: areas in the floodplain that might be suitable for the creation of mudflat and saltmarsh through managed realignment and areas where the right conditions might exist for native oyster habitat and horse mussel bed restoration. The horse mussel data was provided from JNCC. The report also addresses restoration potential for Sabellaria alveolata reefs although no spatial data was created for this habitat and seagrass habitat although this was provided as a map image from JNCC.    These spatial Data layers should be considered as initial aides to identifying potential locations or areas but come with several limitations which have been discussed in the report. Any restoration projects should undergo further site validation and local engagement before proceeding with any specific site.    The following GIS datasets were created for this:  Saltmarsh_Mudflat_Opportunities_Opportunities_Floodplains_SMPpolicy  Native_Oyster_Opportunities   Wave_Height_Grid_ABPmerSeastates  Current_Speed_Grid_ABPmerSeastates  Historic_Saltmarsh_Extents  S_alveolata_records_historic_and_current  O_edulis_records_historic_and_current  M_modiolus_records_historic_and_current  Potential_Beneficial_Use_Sources.\tPublic record\n18919\tNRW_DS124843\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.439Z\tAreas Benefiting From Flood Defences\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383433\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Areas Benefiting From Flood Defences\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Areas Benefitting from Flood Defences show areas in Wales that benefit from flood defences that protect against flooding from Rivers and the Sea.  The areas shown benefit from different levels of flood protection and this is recognised in the risk classification shown in the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map.\tPublic record\n19014\tNRW_DS125237\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.727Z\tSpecial Landscape Areas Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323337\tx:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Special Landscape Areas.lyr\tJill Bullen, Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape, Strategic Planning Group.\tSpecial Landscape Areas (SLAs) are a non-statutory designation applied by a local authority to define areas of high landscape importance within their administrative boundary.  This data resource is a collation of local authority derived SLAs and is intended to help users identify whether a location of interest is in an SLA (or not) the boundaries and extent of an SLA and the SLA reference or name to help sourcing the additional information and relevant policies. It is correct as of April 2022.\tPublic record\n18935\tNRW_DS124825\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.694Z\tBait digging areas aerial imagery 2019/2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383235\tX:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Drone footage\\Intertidal Drone Photos 2019-2020.lyr\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team       (Bait Digging Areas 2019_2020 NRW_DS124806)\tThe data has been collected under a contract run under the Wales non-licensable activities project.  Bait digging and collection of living resources was identified as an activity which is causing damage to the Marine Protection Area (MPA) network in Wales.  The data obtained from this contract provides NRW with evidence which will help inform discussions around future management of this activity.  The data contains 15 aerial images (.tiff format) of 12 separate priority intertidal sites in Wales which were suspected of having bait digging occurring.  The images were captured between October 2019 and March 2020 with a drone.  They display visual evidence of bait digging that can be picked up by sediment disturbance and patterning which indicates digging activity.  From these aerial images a GIS layer (Bait Digging Areas 2019_2020 NRW_DS124806) was created through interpretation of the visible evidence of bait digging from the aerial images.\tPublic record\n18992\tNRW_DS124932\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.047Z\tCrymlyn Bog SSSI; Rapid appraisal of Himalayan balsam Impatiens glandulifera distribution: 2020 – GIS dataset\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393332\tX:\\Biological\\LIFE\\Crymlyn Impatiens glandulifera 2020.lyr  X:\\Biological\\LIFE\\Crymlyn Impatiens Survey Route 2020.lyr\tJohn Ratcliffe - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA rapid survey of Himalayan balsam Impatiens glandulifera on Crymlyn Bog SSSI and Pant y Sais SSSI was undertaken 7-9 Sept 2020 using free-survey method. Observations were made from the defined route and estimates of extent marked on a 1:10000 scale field map and later transcribed to ArcGIS.\tPublic record\n17752\tNRW_DS110433\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.315Z\tNatural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) Upland Framework\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343333\tX:\\Biological\\Upland Framework Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\Upland_Framework\tCustodian: Liz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (ex-CCW custodian Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tA framework developed to improve the quality and diversity of habitats on Wales\u0027 statutory upland sites.  The essence of the framework is a map based overview of statutory sites in the Welsh uplands outlining the future long-term conservation ideals for each upland statutory site. This dataset is made up of two layers Upland Framework and the Upland Framework font point. The purpose of this data capture was to develop national and regional conservation priorities by highlighting important features and prioritising their conservation within particular area; and provide guidance for management planning including setting long-term objectives and steering management to achieve these objectives on individual sites.\tPublic record\n19008\tNRW_DS125279\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.366Z\tLANDMAP Cultural Landscape Services\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323739\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap\\LANDMAP Cultural Landscape.lyr\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor Landscapes\tLANDMAP is a complete All-Wales GIS based landscape resource where landscape characteristics qualities and influences on the landscape are recorded and evaluated into a nationally consistent data set. LANDMAP comprises five spatially related datasets known as the Geological Landscape Landscape Habitats Landscape Habitats the Historic Landscape and the Cultural Landscape Services.    The LANDMAP Cultural Landscape Services layer provides information relating to cultural services and the connections between landscape the natural environment and place. Including natural settings aesthetic appreciation tranquillity cultural heritage inspiration and spiritual and identity and sense of place. The existing Visual \u0026amp; Sensory boundaries are used as context and all data is calculated to these boundaries.   Cultural Landscape Services replaces the LANDMAP Cultural Landscape dataset.  This new dataset (which supersedes NRW_DS108654) will focus mapping on some key cultural services particularly linked to landscape but will in time include richer information in the surveys. This will be a significant step forward in delivering reliable usable data that can be built upon and continually improved.\tPublic record\n19174\tNRW_DS125641\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.969Z\tEmissions of radioactive substances from non-nuclear sites - OSPAR Report Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363431\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/reporting/sr/Industrial%20Emissions/Non-nuclear%20Radioactive%20Substances\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Toby Griffiths, Radioactivity and Industry Policy Karl Lombardi, Radioactivity and Industry Policy  Susan Freeman, Specialist Advisor Environmental Reporting does the annual reporting of this data to OSPAR.\tHolders of an RSR (Radioactive Substances Regulation) open source permit including disposal to the environment are required to report annually to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) emissions of certain non-nuclear radioactive substances. This emissions data is collated as part of a UK dataset and is reported to the OSPAR Commission. (OSPAR is the mechanism by which 15 Governments and the EU cooperate to protect the marine environment of the North-East Atlantic.)\tPublic record\n18325\tNRW_DS116350\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.561Z\tMarine data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Technical Support (Research and Monitoring) Contracts in Wales\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333530\t[A] All species data is stored in Marine Recorder   This data is now available on the NBN Gateway\tMonica Jones, Data Services Team Leader, EPP, NRW\tThis dataset is comprised of a collection of marine species sightings collated since 1993 from marine surveys technical support contracts and research and monitoring contracts. This dataset is currently maintained and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). The surveys focus on benthic habitats (often referred to as \u0027biotopes\u0027) in intertidal inshore (typically within 3 nm) and subtidal areas. Original copies of all the surveys are now held in NRW\u0027s Marine Recorder database. The purpose of this data capture was to aid coastal zone and sea-use management and to contribute to the identification of areas of important marine natural heritage sites. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Marine Recorder which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tPublic record\n17135\tEXT_DS121611\t2024-05-28T12:14:10.166Z\tCoal Authority: Past and Current Mining Data (3rd Party Data)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363131\t\tNo current data custodian identified  Internal Custodian: GIS Service Desk  External Contact: Louise Tipper, Information Management, The Coal Authority mailing and physical address: 200 Lichfield Lane Mansfield Nottinghamshire NG18 4RG England +44(0) 345 762 6848       Louise Tipper, Information Management, The Coal Authority mailing and physical address: 200 Lichfield Lane Mansfield Nottinghamshire NG18 4RG England +44(0) 345 762 6848\tThe Coal Authority (CA) works to resolve the impacts of mining by managing the effects of past coal mining and dealing with mine water pollution and other mining legacy issues. This spatial dataset provided to Natural Resources Wales by the CA under and Open Government Licence is available through a Web Mapping Service to enable analysis of data. The datasets are provide in raster format and does not include full vector information. Datasets available via WMS include:  - Mine Entry - Abandoned Mines Catalogue - Development High Risk Area - Surface Coal Resource Areas - Mine Entry Potential Zone of Influence - Fissures and Breaklines - Surface Mining (Past and Current) - Past Shallow Coal Mine Workings - Coal Outcrops - NE Mining \u0026amp; Groundwater Constraints - Coal Mining Reporting Area\tPublic record\n"
          }
        ]
      },
